window.BF_DATA = window.BF_DATA || {};
window.BF_DATA.monographs = [{"id": "breakfast-vs-fasting", "name": "Breakfast consumption versus skipping and acute fasting", "category": "A", "category_name": "Macronutrients and brain energy", "aliases": ["breakfast omission", "breakfast skipping", "morning fast / overnight fast", "short-term fasting", "acute calorie deprivation", "food deprivation", "hunger"], "food_sources": ["any breakfast; most trials used ready-to-eat cereal with milk, cereal bars, bread or test meals", "comparator is usually water or nothing after an overnight fast of 10-14 h", "acute fasting trials: 12-48 h of zero or near-zero energy intake (water allowed)"], "active_constituents": ["energy intake after an overnight fast (glucose availability)", "macronutrient and micronutrient content of the breakfast (not separable in most trials)", "hunger and food-cue salience as competing processes"], "one_line_verdict": "In healthy adults, missing breakfast or fasting for up to about 48 hours causes little or no measurable loss of cognition (pooled g = 0.02 in a 2025 meta-analysis) while it reliably worsens hunger and mood; a small morning memory advantage for breakfast is possible but inconsistent, and the clearer benefit is in children and adolescents, especially undernourished ones, so 'breakfast for brain power' is a public-health message for schoolchildren rather than a performance claim for adults.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. The largest synthesis, a 2025 meta-analysis of 71 experimental studies (3,484 healthy participants, 222 effect sizes, median fast 12 h), found no meaningful difference between fasted and fed performance (g = 0.02, 95% CrI -0.05 to 0.10) with low heterogeneity; modest deficits appeared with longer fasts, in younger participants, later in the day and on food-related tasks [bamberg2025]. Earlier reviews called the evidence equivocal [benau2014] or mixed by domain (fasting possibly hurting cognitive flexibility and psychomotor speed) [oleary2025]. The best-blinded trials agree with the null: two days of near-total calorie deprivation, using sensory-matched gels so neither subjects nor staff knew the energy content, did not affect vigilance, reaction time, learning, memory or reasoning in 27 young adults [lieberman2008]; adding 4 h/day of aerobic exercise still left cognition intact, though mood worsened sharply [lieberman2017]; a secondary analysis of that trial reported worse task-switching and go/no-go during exercise [giles2019], showing the cost appears only under combined physical stress and on some prefrontal tasks. Missing one meal, two meals or 24 h of food had no effect on attention, reaction time or immediate memory in 21 women [green1995]; 2 days of fasting did not change cognition in overweight women [solianik2018]; and after a 14 h overnight fast fMRI activation changed with no performance change [chechko2015], a biomarker without cognitive effect. Breakfast in adults. A narrative review of 38 acute studies concluded that breakfast gives a 'small but robust' advantage for memory, especially delayed recall, with equivocal results for attention and executive function [galioto2016]. Individual trials are mixed: Benton's work linked morning fasting to worse word-list and story recall, partly reversed by glucose [benton1998], yet a 150-woman trial found breakfast did not change word recall [benton2001]; cereal improved spatial memory and mood in 144 students [smith1999]; and in undergraduates neither breakfast nor its composition affected cognition [emilien2017]. In active women, breakfast before exercise improved relaxation but slightly impaired afternoon working memory (d = 0.37) [veasey2015]. Children and adolescents. Systematic reviews find a short-term positive effect on attention, executive function and memory, clearest in undernourished children [hoyland2009, adolphus2016]. Large school trials are positive [cooper2011, widenhornmuller2008, adolphus2021 (Kellogg-funded)] or null [iovino2016]. Over years, school feeding in low- and middle-income countries raises maths scores slightly (SMD 0.14, high certainty) but not reading [kristjansson2025]; moving breakfast into the classroom did not improve test scores in a US cluster RCT [luan2022]. Observational data (OR 2.08 for poor academic performance with skipping [seura2025]; lower memory and grades in Malaysian students [cheng2025]; faster cognitive decline in older skippers [ishizuka2023]) are confounded by socioeconomic status, sleep and diet quality. Practical relevance: for a rested adult student, skipping breakfast before an exam is unlikely to cost measurable performance, but hunger, irritability and later overeating are real; for children it matters more.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Breakfast effects depend strongly on nutritional status. In undernourished, wasted or stunted children, breakfast consistently improves attention and memory relative to fasting [hoyland2009, adolphus2016], and school feeding improves maths achievement modestly in low- and middle-income countries [kristjansson2025]; this is deficit correction. In well-nourished adults the comparison is a normal overnight fast versus a fed state, and the meta-analytic difference is essentially zero (g = 0.02) [bamberg2025]; two days of blinded calorie deprivation did not impair cognition [lieberman2008]. So breakfast does not 'enhance' adult cognition above normal; at most it prevents a small, inconsistent late-morning memory decline [galioto2016]. Effects on mood, hunger and energy intake later in the day are larger and more consistent than effects on cognition [lieberman2017, emilien2017]. Ramadan dawn-to-dusk fasting combines food and fluid restriction with sleep disruption and is handled in unit K3.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "'Breakfast is the most important meal for your brain' and 'skipping breakfast lowers your grades' rest mainly on observational studies with heavy confounding [seura2025, cheng2025] and on acute school trials, many funded by cereal manufacturers (Kellogg funded the Leeds review and the 2021 RCT [adolphus2016, adolphus2021]; PepsiCo co-authored a null oatmeal trial cited in the meal monograph). In adults the best evidence is null [bamberg2025, lieberman2008]. Regulatory precedent is direct: in 2009 the US FTC obtained a consent order against Kellogg for advertising Frosted Mini-Wheats as 'clinically shown to improve kids' attentiveness by nearly 20%', because only about 1 in 9 children improved that much and the average gain versus no breakfast was about 11%. In the EU a claim that a breakfast product helps children concentrate is a children's development claim under Article 14 of Regulation 1924/2006 and needs authorisation; none exists. An honest brand position: offer breakfasts that are nutritionally good (fibre, protein, low added sugar), promote them for satiety and diet quality, support school breakfast for children who arrive hungry, and do not promise adult cognitive enhancement.", "mechanism": {"summary": "After an overnight fast, liver glycogen falls and blood glucose is maintained at the low end of the normal range; the brain's fuel supply is protected by gluconeogenesis and, after roughly 12-24 h, rising ketones. Breakfast restores glucose and may support demanding memory tasks (glucose facilitation, see glucose monograph). Hunger increases attention to food cues and can compete with task focus, which may explain why deficits appear mainly on food-related tasks. Fasting also raises sympathetic activity and subjective stress. Children have higher brain glucose use per body mass and smaller glycogen stores, a plausible reason for larger effects.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Overnight or longer fast -> falling liver glycogen -> lower blood/interstitial glucose within normal range", "detail": "Interstitial glucose lower during 2-day calorie deprivation, declining to 61 mg/dL with exercise [lieberman2017]; still no cognitive deficit [lieberman2008].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Lower glucose -> altered task-related brain activation", "detail": "14 h fast with placebo vs glucagon: reduced thalamic and basal-ganglia activation during n-back without performance difference [chechko2015]. Biomarker only.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Fasting -> metabolic switch to ketones and protected cerebral fuel supply", "detail": "General physiology; fuel switching after about 12-24 h is supported by human data reviewed in unit K3; mechanism for preserved cognition is plausible.", "evidence_level": "plausible"}, {"step": "Fasting -> hunger, stress, sympathetic shift -> worse mood, attention captured by food cues", "detail": "2-day fast lowered HF heart-rate variability and raised stress [solianik2018]; calorie deprivation raised tension, fatigue, total mood disturbance [lieberman2017]; deficits concentrated on food-related tasks [bamberg2025].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Breakfast -> restored glucose -> glucose-sensitive memory tasks", "detail": "Memory decline with morning fasting reversed for word recall by a glucose drink but not for story recall [benton1998].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Measured human outcome", "detail": "Adults: no meaningful pooled effect (g = 0.02) [bamberg2025]. Children/adolescents: short-term benefit for attention, memory, executive function, larger in undernourished children [adolphus2016].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}], "pathways": ["glucose availability and glucose-memory facilitation", "hepatic glycogen depletion and gluconeogenesis", "ketogenesis with longer fasts", "hunger-related attentional capture by food cues", "sympathetic activation and stress"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults (mostly young) fasted for hours to about 2 days vs fed", "dose_or_intake": "Fasting median 12 h (range to 48 h) vs satiated", "duration": "Single test session", "grade": "A", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Fasted and fed healthy adults perform the same on standard cognitive tasks. Small deficits appear with longer fasts, in younger participants, later in the day, and on food-related tasks. Blinded 2-day calorie deprivation had no effect on any cognitive test.", "effect_estimate": "g = 0.02 (95% CrI -0.05 to 0.10), tau = 0.17, 222 effect sizes, 3,484 participants [bamberg2025]", "certainty_notes": "Large, low-heterogeneity meta-analysis plus well-blinded trials [lieberman2008, lieberman2017] supporting a null. Not downgraded for imprecision. Some risk of bias in the included studies (fasting cannot be blinded except with Lieberman's gels). Grade A here means strong evidence of no meaningful effect.", "key_study_ids": ["bamberg2025", "lieberman2008", "lieberman2017", "green1995", "solianik2018", "benau2014"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults (mostly students) eating breakfast vs skipping after an overnight fast", "dose_or_intake": "Typical breakfast (cereal, test meals; about 150-500 kcal) vs water/nothing", "duration": "Single morning; tests 1-4 h after", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "A narrative review of 38 studies reported a 'small but robust' memory advantage, especially delayed recall. Individual trials disagree: morning fasting worsened word-list and story recall in Benton's studies; breakfast did not affect word recall in 150 young women; cereal improved spatial memory in 144 students; no cognitive effect in 33 undergraduates.", "effect_estimate": "not pooled; individual trials report p-values only", "certainty_notes": "No meta-analysis specific to breakfast in adults; conflicts with the null pooled fasting estimate [bamberg2025]; small samples; publication bias likely; review not a formal systematic review. Downgraded for inconsistency and risk of bias.", "key_study_ids": ["galioto2016", "benton1998", "benton2001", "smith1999", "emilien2017"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "students", "population_detail": "University students and young adults; attention, reaction time and executive function after breakfast vs skipping", "dose_or_intake": "Breakfast vs no breakfast", "duration": "Single morning", "grade": "B", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Attention, motor and executive results are equivocal in the adult review; undergraduates showed no effect; the fasting meta-analysis found no domain differences. Breakfast before morning exercise slightly worsened afternoon working memory in active women.", "effect_estimate": "g = 0.02 overall, no reliable domain moderation [bamberg2025]; d = 0.37 worse afternoon working memory with breakfast [veasey2015]", "certainty_notes": "Consistent null direction across reviews and trials; downgraded once for indirectness (few exam-condition tests) and risk of bias.", "key_study_ids": ["galioto2016", "emilien2017", "veasey2015", "bamberg2025"]}, {"timeframe": "short_term_days", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "high_cognitive_workload", "population_detail": "Young adults under 2 days of near-total calorie deprivation combined with 4 h/day aerobic exercise (military-relevant)", "dose_or_intake": "266 +/- 61 kcal/day vs 3935 kcal/day (blinded)", "duration": "2 days", "grade": "C", "direction": "harm", "effect_summary": "Primary cognitive battery unaffected, but during exercise, task-switching accuracy and go/no-go sensitivity fell in a secondary analysis of the same trial.", "effect_estimate": "set-shifting p < 0.01; go/no-go p < 0.05 [giles2019]; primary cognition null [lieberman2017]", "certainty_notes": "Same 23 participants in both papers (not independent); secondary outcomes; extreme scenario. Downgraded for inconsistency between reports and imprecision.", "key_study_ids": ["giles2019", "lieberman2017"]}, {"timeframe": "short_term_days", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults during 1-2 days of fasting or calorie deprivation", "dose_or_intake": "Near-zero energy intake", "duration": "1-2 days", "grade": "B", "direction": "harm", "effect_summary": "Mood worsens more consistently than cognition: tension, fatigue and total mood disturbance rose with deprivation plus exercise; stress and sympathetic shift with 2-day fasting; sedentary blinded deprivation did not change mood in Lieberman 2008.", "effect_estimate": "Total mood disturbance -0.80 +/- 5.1 to 20.1 +/- 6.1 (p < 0.001) [lieberman2017]", "certainty_notes": "Moderately consistent; mood measured by self-report; inconsistency with Lieberman 2008 (no mood change when sedentary).", "key_study_ids": ["lieberman2017", "solianik2018", "lieberman2008"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "children_adolescents", "population_detail": "School-age children and adolescents (8-20 y), mostly well nourished in high-income countries", "dose_or_intake": "Breakfast (cereal and milk, standard breakfast) vs no breakfast", "duration": "Single school morning", "grade": "B", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Systematic reviews find a short-term positive, domain-specific effect on attention, executive function and memory. Large trials: better visual search, Stroop and Sternberg performance in 96 adolescents; visuospatial memory in boys and alertness in 104 high-school students; better paired-associates learning in 234 11-13-year-olds (Kellogg-funded). A rigorous 128-child trial found no effect.", "effect_estimate": "Mostly p-values; e.g. 100% vs 41.7% reaching PAL level 4 among low-baseline pupils [adolphus2021]; all P >= 0.004 (null) [iovino2016]", "certainty_notes": "Consistent direction in reviews but notable null RCT; frequent industry funding; small acute samples. Downgraded for risk of bias and inconsistency. Not applicable to adults.", "key_study_ids": ["adolphus2016", "hoyland2009", "cooper2011", "widenhornmuller2008", "adolphus2021", "iovino2016"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "learning", "population": "children_adolescents", "population_detail": "Socioeconomically disadvantaged school children 5-19 y (mostly LMICs); US urban schools", "dose_or_intake": "School feeding (at least 3% of energy and 10% of protein needs); breakfast in the classroom vs cafeteria", "duration": "Months to years", "grade": "B", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "In LMICs school feeding slightly improves maths achievement (high certainty) with little or no effect on reading; in a US cluster RCT, moving universal breakfast into the classroom did not improve attendance or reading and slightly lowered maths scores.", "effect_estimate": "Maths SMD 0.14 (95% CI 0.06 to 0.23), I2 68%; reading SMD 0.02 (-0.06 to 0.11) [kristjansson2025]; maths beta -0.20 (SE 0.07) [luan2022]", "certainty_notes": "Cochrane GRADE ratings used; effect small; applies to disadvantaged children, not to Brain Food's adult target.", "key_study_ids": ["kristjansson2025", "luan2022"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "learning", "population": "students", "population_detail": "Habitual breakfast skipping vs eating in adolescents and university students (observational)", "dose_or_intake": "Habitual breakfast frequency", "duration": "Cross-sectional", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "Skippers have worse grades and lower test scores in observational data, but these associations are strongly confounded by socioeconomic status, sleep, diet quality and conscientiousness, and are not causal evidence. Diet quality also worsens during university exam periods.", "effect_estimate": "OR 2.08 (95% CI 1.82-2.37) for poor academic performance with skipping in youths [seura2025]; skipping associated with lower memory, attention and grades in 298 Malaysian students [cheng2025]", "certainty_notes": "Observational, mostly cross-sectional, confounded; reverse causation possible. No RCT of habitual breakfast in university students.", "key_study_ids": ["seura2025", "cheng2025", "michels2020"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_decline", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Community-dwelling Japanese older adults (mean 70.8 y), HEIJO-KYO cohort", "dose_or_intake": "Skipping breakfast at least once per week (n = 29) vs eaters (n = 683)", "duration": "Median 31 months", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "Skippers had about twice the incidence of a >= 2-point MMSE drop; skippers also ate fewer vegetables, fruits and fish.", "effect_estimate": "IRR 2.10 (95% CI 1.28-3.44); propensity-adjusted IRR 2.21 (1.33-3.68) [ishizuka2023]", "certainty_notes": "Only 29 skippers; residual confounding by diet quality and health; single cohort. Not applicable to young adults.", "key_study_ids": ["ishizuka2023"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "Fast median 12 h (to ~48 h) vs fed", "onset": "Morning to day 2", "duration_studied": "Single sessions", "domain": "all cognitive domains", "population": "healthy adults", "effect_estimate": "g = 0.02 (95% CrI -0.05 to 0.10)", "certainty": "A (null)", "safety": "Hunger, irritability; hypoglycaemia risk only with glucose-lowering drugs", "study_ids": ["bamberg2025"]}, {"dose": "2 days near-total calorie deprivation (313 kcal total), blinded gels", "onset": "Day 1-2", "duration_studied": "48 h", "domain": "vigilance, RT, learning, memory, reasoning, mood", "population": "27 healthy young adults", "effect_estimate": "No detectable effect on any outcome", "certainty": "B (single well-blinded trial)", "safety": "Lower interstitial glucose and satiety", "study_ids": ["lieberman2008"]}, {"dose": "Miss 1, 2 meals or 24 h of food", "onset": "Same day", "duration_studied": "Up to 24 h", "domain": "sustained attention, focus, RT, immediate memory", "population": "21 women", "effect_estimate": "No effect, except poorer low-load tapping after 24 h", "certainty": "C", "safety": "None reported", "study_ids": ["green1995"]}, {"dose": "Breakfast vs no breakfast", "onset": "1-4 h", "duration_studied": "Morning", "domain": "episodic memory", "population": "healthy adults", "effect_estimate": "Inconsistent: small advantage in review, null in several trials", "certainty": "C", "safety": "None", "study_ids": ["galioto2016", "benton2001", "smith1999", "emilien2017"]}, {"dose": "Cereal + milk breakfast vs none", "onset": "70-215 min", "duration_studied": "School morning", "domain": "visuospatial learning, RT, sustained attention", "population": "11-13-year-olds (234)", "effect_estimate": "Better PAL learning especially when baseline poor; better RT and RVIP", "certainty": "B (children)", "safety": "None", "study_ids": ["adolphus2021"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "Months to years", "population": "disadvantaged school children 5-19 y", "outcome": "maths and reading achievement", "effect_estimate": "Maths SMD 0.14 (0.06-0.23); reading SMD 0.02 (-0.06 to 0.11)", "evidence_quality": "B (high certainty for maths per Cochrane GRADE)", "limitations": "Mostly LMICs; heterogeneity I2 68%; school meals not only breakfast", "study_ids": ["kristjansson2025"]}, {"duration": "2.5 years", "population": "US urban grade 4-6 pupils", "outcome": "attendance, standardised test scores", "effect_estimate": "No benefit; maths beta -0.20 (SE 0.07)", "evidence_quality": "B", "limitations": "Compared two breakfast delivery modes, not breakfast vs none", "study_ids": ["luan2022"]}, {"duration": "Median 31 months", "population": "older adults", "outcome": "MMSE decline", "effect_estimate": "IRR 2.10 (1.28-3.44) for skippers", "evidence_quality": "D", "limitations": "Observational; 29 skippers", "study_ids": ["ishizuka2023"]}, {"duration": "Cohorts, several years", "population": "adults", "outcome": "cardiovascular disease and all-cause mortality (safety context)", "effect_estimate": "RR 1.22 (1.10-1.35) CVD; RR 1.25 (1.11-1.40) mortality for skippers", "evidence_quality": "D", "limitations": "Observational; confounding by smoking, diet, shift work", "study_ids": ["chen2020"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Eating breakfast is safe. Skipping breakfast or fasting for up to about 48 h is generally well tolerated by healthy adults, but causes hunger, irritability, tension and fatigue [lieberman2017, solianik2018], and lowers glucose. Material risks exist for specific groups: people taking insulin or sulfonylureas (hypoglycaemia), people with diabetes, pregnant or breastfeeding women, children and adolescents (larger cognitive costs, growth needs), people with or at risk of eating disorders (fasting messages can reinforce restriction), people with a history of fainting, and anyone doing heavy physical work or driving while fasted. Habitual breakfast skipping is associated with cardiometabolic risk and mortality in cohorts [chen2020], though confounding is likely.", "adverse_effects": ["Hunger, irritability, tension, fatigue and worse mood during fasting [lieberman2017]", "Subjective stress and sympathetic shift during 2-day fasting [solianik2018]", "Impaired task switching and inhibition when fasting is combined with prolonged exercise [giles2019]", "Compensatory larger lunch intake after skipping breakfast [emilien2017]", "Hypoglycaemia in people on glucose-lowering drugs (general clinical knowledge)"], "contraindications": ["Fasting in insulin- or sulfonylurea-treated diabetes without medical supervision", "Pregnancy and breastfeeding (prolonged fasting)", "Children and adolescents (prolonged fasting; breakfast advisable)", "Current or past eating disorder", "History of syncope or hypotension"], "drug_interactions": ["Insulin, sulfonylureas, glinides: hypoglycaemia if meals are skipped", "Drugs that must be taken with food (e.g. some NSAIDs, metformin for GI tolerance) or fasting (e.g. levothyroxine): advice must respect prescribing instructions"], "upper_limits": "Not applicable. Prolonged fasting beyond about 48 h is outside the scope of this monograph (a 10-day fast study was seen but not extracted).", "sleep_impact": "Two days of blinded calorie deprivation did not change sleep or activity [lieberman2008]. Ramadan-related sleep disruption is covered by unit K3.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Acute and up to 2-day trials only for cognition; habitual skipping studied only observationally.", "daily_use_appropriate": "yes", "special_populations": "Children and adolescents: breakfast recommended, especially undernourished children; Ramadan fasting, intermittent fasting and TRE are in unit K3; older adults on medications; people with diabetes; eating-disorder risk groups, including students under exam stress.", "specialist_review_needed": ["diabetologist (fasting and meal-skipping advice)", "paediatric dietitian (school breakfast products)", "eating-disorder specialist (fasting-positive messaging)", "obstetrician/midwife (pregnancy)"], "hard_safety_gate": "pass", "gate_reason": "Breakfast foods are ordinary foods; skipping breakfast is a normal behaviour with no serious acute risk for healthy adults. Any product or message encouraging fasting would need exclusions (diabetes on medication, pregnancy, children, eating disorders)."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "No authorised EU health claim states that breakfast improves cognition, concentration or school performance. A statement that a breakfast product helps children concentrate or learn would be a claim referring to children's development and health under Article 14(1)(b) of Regulation (EC) No 1924/2006, requiring EFSA assessment and authorisation; none is authorised. General non-specific benefit statements require an accompanying authorised specific claim (Article 10(3)). Usable nutrient claims (conditions apply): 'Carbohydrates contribute to the maintenance of normal brain function' (Reg. (EU) 1018/2013, >= 20 g metabolisable carbohydrate per portion, low or no added sugars), and iron, iodine, zinc 'contribute to normal cognitive function' (Reg. (EU) 432/2012) for fortified cereals meeting source criteria. Fasting is not a product; 'fasting support' products would need to avoid medicinal and unauthorised claims.", "us": "Breakfast foods are conventional foods; structure/function statements must be truthful and substantiated. FTC precedent: Kellogg consent order (2009) over 'clinically shown to improve kids' attentiveness by nearly 20%' (Frosted Mini-Wheats), with the 2010 modification requiring substantiation for health claims on any Kellogg food. Sources: ftc.gov (node/44358; 100602kelloggorder.pdf), accessed 2026-10-25. School Breakfast Program (USDA) is a policy context, not a claim authorisation.", "morocco": "No specific rule found for breakfast or fasting claims. General labelling under Law 28-07 and Decree 2-12-389 (ONSSA); health-claim framework uncertain. Ramadan-marketed products are commercially important in Morocco (see unit K3); claim status must be checked with ONSSA and the Ministry of Health.", "permitted_claims_examples": ["EU (Reg. 432/2012): 'Iron contributes to normal cognitive function' - only for foods that are at least a source of iron", "EU (Reg. 1018/2013): 'Carbohydrates contribute to the maintenance of normal brain function' - with the 130 g/day statement, >= 20 g metabolisable carbohydrate per portion and LOW SUGARS or NO ADDED SUGARS"], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "None for ordinary breakfast foods.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "High as a food category (breakfast cereals, oats, yogurt-based breakfasts, bars) but the cognitive rationale is weak in adults. Defensible product rationale: satiety, diet quality (fibre, protein, low added sugar), micronutrient fortification with authorised claims, and convenience for students who skip breakfast during exams.", "formats": ["high-fibre, low-sugar oat or wholegrain breakfast with milk or yogurt", "portable breakfast bar or drink for students and commuters", "school breakfast offerings for children (with paediatric dietitian input)"], "stability": "Standard for cereal products; fortificant stability depends on vitamin (not researched here).", "taste": "Ordinary; sugar reduction is the main taste challenge.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Not applicable for energy; iron fortificant choice affects absorption (see iron monograph).", "dose_feasibility": "Trivially feasible: trial breakfasts were about 120-500 kcal (e.g. about 50 g cereal with 130 g milk, 1059 kJ, in adolescents [adolphus2021]).", "cost_availability": "Ordinary food cost; widely available in Morocco, EU and US.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 6, "effect_magnitude": 1, "safety": 9, "reproducibility": 6, "food_suitability": 8, "cost_access": 9, "rationale": "Evidence quality 6: the null effect of short fasting in adults is well established (A), but breakfast-specific adult memory evidence is C and student evidence mostly null or observational, children B. Effect magnitude 1: in adults the pooled effect is essentially zero (g = 0.02); children show small acute benefits and school feeding SMD 0.14 in disadvantaged children. Safety 9: breakfast is safe; fasting risks concern defined groups. Reproducibility 6: the adult null replicates across blinded trials and the meta-analysis; positive breakfast-memory findings do not replicate well. Food suitability 8: ordinary breakfast foods. Cost/access 9."}, "research_gaps": ["Preregistered, adequately powered trials of breakfast vs skipping in university students under real exam conditions, with academic outcomes", "Habitual (weeks-long) breakfast RCTs in young adults with cognitive endpoints", "Independent (non-industry) replication of positive adolescent school trials", "Separation of hunger-related attentional capture from general cognition in adults", "Individual moderators: habitual breakfast eaters vs skippers, chronotype, glucoregulation, BMI", "Fasting combined with physical or mental workload (shift work, military, Ramadan: see K3)"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 6.1, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "pass", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": "B", "best_grade_chronic": "B", "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": null, "best_grade_overall": "B", "benefit_domains": {"sustained_attention": "B", "learning": "B"}, "n_outcome_rows": 9, "n_studies": 28, "n_verified": 28, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 13, "n_systematic_reviews": 8, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["harm", "inconsistent", "insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "dietary-fats-pattern", "name": "Dietary fat composition (saturated, monounsaturated, polyunsaturated, trans)", "category": "A", "category_name": "Macronutrients and brain energy", "aliases": ["dietary fat quality", "fat composition", "saturated fatty acids (SFA)", "monounsaturated fatty acids (MUFA)", "polyunsaturated fatty acids (PUFA)", "trans fatty acids (TFA), partially hydrogenated oils", "palmitic acid / oleic acid ratio", "high-fat meal"], "food_sources": ["SFA: butter, ghee/smen, cream, cheese, fatty and processed meat, palm and coconut oil, many pastries", "MUFA: olive oil, high-oleic sunflower/rapeseed oil, avocado, almonds, hazelnuts, argan oil", "n-6 PUFA: sunflower, soybean, corn oil, walnuts, seeds", "n-3 PUFA: fatty fish (EPA/DHA), walnuts, flax, rapeseed (ALA)", "Industrial TFA: partially hydrogenated vegetable oils in older margarines, fried and baked goods (now restricted in EU and US); ruminant TFA in dairy and meat"], "active_constituents": ["palmitic and stearic acid (SFA)", "oleic acid (MUFA)", "linoleic acid, alpha-linolenic acid (PUFA)", "EPA and DHA (covered in omega-3-dha-epa monograph)", "elaidic acid (industrial TFA)"], "one_line_verdict": "Prospective cohorts in older adults fairly consistently associate higher saturated fat with faster cognitive decline and higher dementia risk and MUFA/vegetable fat with lower risk, but associations are modest, confounded and not universal, there is no long-term RCT of fat quality on cognition, and in young adults the only signals are small short-term studies of extreme high-fat diets or single saturated-fat meals, so fat quality is a cardiometabolic and dietary-pattern recommendation rather than a cognitive-enhancement lever.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. Fat quality is primarily a cardiometabolic issue: replacing saturated with unsaturated fat lowers LDL cholesterol, and EFSA advises SFA and TFA intakes as low as possible within a nutritionally adequate diet [efsa2010fats]. For cognition, evidence is mainly observational in older adults. In 815 Chicago residents, the top fifth of SFA intake had 2.2 times the risk of incident Alzheimer disease, with higher risk for trans fat and lower for vegetable fat [morris2003]. In 6,183 Women's Health Study participants, the highest vs lowest SFA quintile had worse 4-year cognitive change (-0.12 SD global score, comparable to 5-6 years of ageing) and MUFA a better one (+0.17 SD); swapping 5% energy from SFA to MUFA gave an OR of 0.47 for worst cognitive change [okereke2012]. In the Health and Retirement Study (n=5,944), SFA was associated with higher (HR 1.56) and MUFA and vegetable fat with lower dementia risk [wu2025]. Meta-analyses of 4 and 9 cohorts give RR about 1.4-2.0 for high SFA and AD/dementia/cognitive impairment, with no consistent association for total, MUFA or PUFA [ruan2018, cao2019]. But a systematic review by a Nordic nutrition-recommendations group found no robust association: one cohort reported SFA and TFA inversely associated with AD, and 3 of 4 studies had serious risk of bias [nwaru2022]; another review found 3 of 4 AD studies positive but mixed findings for MCI and decline [barnard2014], authored by an advocacy organisation. Food-frequency measurement, confounding by overall diet and SES, and few cohorts limit certainty (grade C). Short-term interventions. In 49 older adults, 4 weeks of a high-SFA/high-glycaemic diet vs a low-SFA/low-glycaemic diet changed CSF amyloid and lipid markers, and delayed visual memory improved on the low diet [bayercarter2011]; fat and glycaemic index were manipulated together. In healthy young men, 5-7 days of a 74-75% fat, low-carbohydrate diet raised free fatty acids and impaired attention and speed [holloway2011, edwards2011]; these are extreme diets that also removed carbohydrate, so the effect cannot be attributed to fat quality (see ketogenic-diet monograph). A single high-saturated-fat meal worsened sustained attention 5 h later vs a high-oleic sunflower meal in 51 middle-aged women, half of them cancer survivors [madison2020]. A 3-week high-palmitate vs high-oleate crossover in 12 young women altered fMRI activation and cytokines but reported no behavioural outcome [dumas2016]. Cross-sectionally, each g/day of trans fat was associated with 0.76 fewer words recalled in men under 45 [golomb2015]. In whom, and how large. Effects, if causal, are modest and mainly relevant to long-term dementia risk in middle-aged and older adults. For students and knowledge workers no adequately powered trial shows that changing fat quality within normal diets improves day-to-day cognition. What is unknown. No long-term RCT has isolated fat quality with cognitive endpoints (PREDIMED tested a whole pattern: see mediterranean-diet and olive-oil-as-food monographs); a registered macronutrient trial (MEAL-2, NCT02463084) has no posted results.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "No classical deficiency applies to total fat, SFA or MUFA; essential fatty acid (linoleic, alpha-linolenic) deficiency is rare in adults. The question is substitution: replacing SFA and industrial TFA with MUFA/PUFA within an adequate diet. Observational benefits concern decades-long exposure and later-life cognitive decline or dementia [okereke2012, wu2025, ruan2018]; they are not evidence of short-term enhancement. In healthy young adults the only evidence is harm-avoidance: extreme high-fat/low-carbohydrate diets for 5-7 days and single high-SFA meals impaired attention in small trials [holloway2011, edwards2011, madison2020]. Replete young adults should not expect measurable cognitive gains from changing oil type.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "'Good fats for a good brain' messaging rests on (1) DHA, which has its own authorised EU claim and monograph, (2) cohort associations for SFA and MUFA in older adults, and (3) whole-diet trials (PREDIMED). It is defensible to say that replacing saturated fats with unsaturated fats supports normal blood cholesterol (an authorised EU claim when conditions are met), not that olive oil or MUFA improves memory or focus. Claims that a high-fat breakfast, MCT or 'brain fats' boost focus in healthy people are unsupported; the extreme high-fat trials showed impairment, not benefit. Industrial trans fats are legally restricted in the EU and US and are not a marketing lever except as 'free of partially hydrogenated oils'.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Long-term high SFA and TFA intake raises LDL cholesterol and is associated with insulin resistance, endothelial dysfunction and cerebrovascular disease, which are dementia risk factors. Palmitate promotes inflammatory signalling (TLR4/NF-kB), and a high-SFA meal increases postprandial endotoxaemia markers in some studies. MUFA/PUFA substitution improves lipid profile and possibly insulin sensitivity. Short-term very-high-fat diets raise plasma free fatty acids and may impair mitochondrial efficiency. Brain DHA supply depends on dietary n-3 PUFA (separate monograph). Evidence links are mainly cardiometabolic biomarkers in humans; brain mechanisms are largely animal-derived.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Dietary SFA/TFA vs MUFA/PUFA -> plasma lipoproteins", "detail": "Replacing SFA with unsaturated fat lowers LDL cholesterol (basis of authorised EU claim; EFSA DRV opinion [efsa2010fats]).", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Lipids, insulin resistance, blood pressure -> cerebrovascular disease and vascular contributions to dementia", "detail": "Established cardiovascular pathway; mediation of cognitive associations not quantified in the reviewed cohorts; trans-fat-memory association attenuated by blood pressure, waist and BMI adjustment [golomb2015].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "High-SFA diet or meal -> inflammatory cytokines and endotoxaemia markers", "detail": "High-palmitate diet raised plasma IL-6 and IL-1beta vs high-oleate diet in 12 young women [dumas2016]; LBP and sCD14 modified attention effects of a high-SFA meal [madison2020].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Very-high-fat low-carbohydrate diet -> raised plasma free fatty acids -> altered energy efficiency", "detail": "Free fatty acids +44%, cardiac PCr/ATP -9%, whole-body efficiency -3% after 5-7 days [holloway2011, edwards2011]; brain energetics not measured.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "SFA -> brain insulin signalling, amyloid and tau pathology", "detail": "Shown mainly in rodent models; in humans, 4-week diet altered CSF Abeta42, apoE and insulin in opposite directions in MCI and healthy older adults [bayercarter2011].", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Measured human cognitive outcome", "detail": "Observational associations with cognitive decline and dementia in older adults; small acute/short-term trials show attention impairment after extreme high-fat diets or high-SFA meal; no long-term RCT of fat quality alone.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["LDL cholesterol and atherosclerosis (cerebrovascular)", "insulin resistance and brain insulin signalling", "inflammation and metabolic endotoxaemia", "plasma free fatty acids and mitochondrial efficiency", "membrane fatty acid composition (n-3 PUFA; separate monograph)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "dementia_risk", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Community-dwelling adults 50-65+ without dementia at baseline (US, Europe)", "dose_or_intake": "Habitual SFA intake, highest vs lowest category (or per 4 g/day)", "duration": "4-10 years follow-up", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Lower SFA intake associated with lower AD/dementia risk in most but not all cohorts; one cohort reported inverse SFA-AD association; systematic review with risk-of-bias assessment found no robust association.", "effect_estimate": "AD RR 1.39 (1.00-1.94), dementia RR 2.05 (1.06-3.98), per 4 g/day SFA RR 1.15 (1.01-1.31) [ruan2018]; AD RR 1.87 (1.09-3.20), cognitive impairment RR 1.40 (1.02-1.91) [cao2019]; HR 1.56 (1.05-2.32) [wu2025]", "certainty_notes": "Observational; few cohorts (4-9); FFQ exposure; residual confounding; serious risk of bias in 3 of 4 cohorts per Nwaru 2022; inconsistency. Direction 'possible_benefit' refers to lowering SFA.", "key_study_ids": ["ruan2018", "cao2019", "nwaru2022", "morris2003", "wu2025", "barnard2014"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_decline", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Older women (Women's Health Study, mean ~72 y at testing) and US older adults", "dose_or_intake": "MUFA intake (or 5% energy SFA replaced by MUFA)", "duration": "4-6 years cognitive follow-up, diet assessed earlier", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Higher MUFA and vegetable-fat intake associated with better cognitive trajectories and lower dementia risk; meta-analyses show no consistent pooled association for MUFA/PUFA.", "effect_estimate": "MUFA Q5 vs Q1: +0.17 SD (0.07-0.26) global 4-y change; SFA->MUFA 5% energy OR 0.47 (0.25-0.89) worst decline [okereke2012]; MUFA HR 0.63 (0.42-0.94) dementia [wu2025]", "certainty_notes": "Observational; MUFA sources differ by country (olive oil vs meat in US); pooled analyses null for MUFA; downgraded for inconsistency and indirectness.", "key_study_ids": ["okereke2012", "wu2025", "ruan2018", "cao2019"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_decline", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Older adults; trans fat exposure", "dose_or_intake": "Habitual industrial trans fat", "duration": "4-6 years", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Trans fat associated with higher AD risk in Chicago cohort, not associated with cognitive trajectory in WHS, inversely associated in one cohort reviewed by Nwaru.", "effect_estimate": "RR 2.4 (1.1-5.3) second vs lowest fifth [morris2003]; null [okereke2012]", "certainty_notes": "Few, inconsistent studies; exposure now largely removed from EU/US food supply by regulation.", "key_study_ids": ["morris2003", "okereke2012", "nwaru2022", "barnard2014"]}, {"timeframe": "short_term_days", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy men aged ~22 y and sedentary men", "dose_or_intake": "Isocaloric diet with 74-75% energy from fat (low carbohydrate) vs standard diet (~23% fat)", "duration": "5-7 days", "grade": "C", "direction": "harm", "effect_summary": "High-fat low-carbohydrate diet impaired attention and speed and lowered mood (Holloway, n=16 crossover) and slowed simple reaction time and power of attention (Edwards, n=20).", "effect_estimate": "p<0.01 attention, p<0.001 speed [holloway2011]; p<0.01 RT and attention [edwards2011]; standardised effects not reported in abstracts", "certainty_notes": "Same Oxford group (not independent); small; extreme diet that also removed carbohydrate (confounded with ketosis/low glucose), so indirect for fat quality; no preregistration.", "key_study_ids": ["holloway2011", "edwards2011"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "51 women, mean 53 y (32 breast-cancer survivors, 19 controls)", "dose_or_intake": "Single high-saturated-fat meal vs high-oleic-sunflower-oil meal", "duration": "Single meal, test 5 h later", "grade": "C", "direction": "harm", "effect_summary": "Poorer discrimination of targets from distractors (CPT) after the SFA meal; endotoxaemia markers (LBP, sCD14) related to worse attention and abolished meal differences.", "effect_estimate": "B = 4.44 (SE 1.88), p=0.02 for meal effect on CPT discrimination [madison2020]", "certainty_notes": "Single double-blind crossover; partly clinical population; one primary task; no replication in young adults.", "key_study_ids": ["madison2020"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Young adults; fat quality substitution within normal diets", "dose_or_intake": "High-palmitate vs high-oleate diet; habitual trans fat", "duration": "3 weeks (trial); cross-sectional", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "3-week crossover in 12 young women changed basal-ganglia fMRI activation during N-back and cytokines, without reported behavioural difference; cross-sectionally, each g/day trans fat associated with 0.76 fewer words recalled in men <45 y.", "effect_estimate": "-0.76 words per g/day TFA (95% CI 0.22-1.3 fewer) [golomb2015]; fMRI only [dumas2016]", "certainty_notes": "Biomarker (fMRI) not cognition; cross-sectional association with confounding; tiny trial.", "key_study_ids": ["dumas2016", "golomb2015"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "older_adults_mci", "population_detail": "Older adults with amnestic MCI (n=29) and healthy older adults (n=20)", "dose_or_intake": "LOW diet (25% fat, <7% SFA, low GI) vs HIGH diet (45% fat, >25% SFA, high GI)", "duration": "4 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Delayed visual memory improved in both groups after the LOW diet; CSF Abeta42, apoE, insulin and F2-isoprostanes shifted.", "effect_estimate": "not reported as effect size in abstract", "certainty_notes": "Small; fat and glycaemic index co-varied; multiple biomarker outcomes; one memory outcome positive.", "key_study_ids": ["bayercarter2011"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "High-SFA meal vs high-oleic sunflower oil meal", "onset": "Tested 5 h post-meal", "duration_studied": "Single session", "domain": "Sustained attention (CPT)", "population": "51 women, mean 53 y", "effect_estimate": "B 4.44 (SE 1.88), p=0.02", "certainty": "low", "safety": "No issues reported", "study_ids": ["madison2020"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "5-7 days", "population": "16 and 20 healthy young men", "outcome": "Attention, speed, reaction time", "effect_estimate": "Impairment (p<0.01) on 74-75% fat diet", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Same research group; confounded by carbohydrate removal; small", "study_ids": ["holloway2011", "edwards2011"]}, {"duration": "4 weeks", "population": "49 older adults (aMCI and healthy)", "outcome": "Delayed visual memory; CSF biomarkers", "effect_estimate": "Memory improved on low-SFA/low-GI diet", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Fat and GI co-varied; small", "study_ids": ["bayercarter2011"]}, {"duration": "4-year cognitive change (diet 5 y earlier)", "population": "6,183 older women", "outcome": "Global cognition, verbal memory", "effect_estimate": "SFA Q5 vs Q1 -0.12 SD; MUFA +0.17 SD", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Observational; FFQ", "study_ids": ["okereke2012"]}, {"duration": "Median 6 years", "population": "5,944 US adults (HRS)", "outcome": "Incident dementia", "effect_estimate": "SFA HR 1.56; MUFA HR 0.63; SFA->MUFA HR 0.52", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Observational; algorithmic dementia classification", "study_ids": ["wu2025"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Dietary fats from ordinary foods are safe within EFSA reference ranges. Risks are compositional: industrial trans fats and high SFA raise LDL cholesterol and cardiovascular risk; very high-fat (>70% energy) low-carbohydrate diets impaired attention short term in young men [holloway2011, edwards2011]. Heated/reused frying oils form oxidation products; process contaminants (3-MCPD and glycidyl esters) occur in refined oils.", "adverse_effects": ["Raised LDL cholesterol with high SFA and TFA intakes", "Short-term attention and mood impairment with extreme high-fat low-carbohydrate diets (small trials)", "Gastrointestinal discomfort with very high-fat meals", "Energy density: excess intake contributes to weight gain"], "contraindications": ["Fat malabsorption disorders (specialist dietary management)", "Familial hypercholesterolaemia (SFA restriction)"], "drug_interactions": ["High-fat meals change absorption of several drugs (food effects on pharmacokinetics; drug-specific)", "Orlistat reduces fat absorption"], "upper_limits": "No UL; EFSA reference intake range for total fat 20-35% energy; SFA and TFA as low as possible [efsa2010fats]. EU legal maximum for industrial TFA: 2 g per 100 g fat (Reg. (EU) 2019/649).", "sleep_impact": "No specific evidence reviewed.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Decades in cohorts; controlled trials up to 4 weeks for cognitive outcomes.", "daily_use_appropriate": "yes", "special_populations": "Children need adequate fat for development; people with dyslipidaemia, diabetes or cardiovascular disease benefit from SFA reduction under medical guidance; pregnant women need DHA (separate monograph).", "specialist_review_needed": ["Clinical dietitian for dyslipidaemia/cardiovascular patients", "Food technologist for oil oxidation and process contaminants (3-MCPD, glycidyl esters)"], "hard_safety_gate": "pass", "gate_reason": "Ordinary food component; safety concerns are addressed by choosing unsaturated oils, avoiding industrial TFA and controlling oxidation/contaminants."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Fats are ordinary nutrients. Authorised Article 13 claim in Reg. (EU) 432/2012: 'Replacing saturated fats in the diet with unsaturated fats contributes to the maintenance of normal blood cholesterol levels. MUFA and PUFA are unsaturated fats' (food must be high in unsaturated fat per Reg. 1924/2006 Annex); also claims for linoleic acid, ALA, oleic acid replacing SFA, and DHA/EPA (see omega-3 and olive-oil monographs). Nutrition claims 'low saturated fat', 'saturated fat-free', 'high monounsaturated/polyunsaturated/unsaturated fat' under Reg. 1924/2006 Annex. Industrial TFA limited to 2 g/100 g fat (Reg. (EU) 2019/649). No claim relates fat composition (other than DHA) to cognitive or brain function. Claim wordings from prior knowledge and the olive-oil-as-food monograph; register not re-queried this session.", "us": "FDA determined in 2015 that partially hydrogenated oils are not GRAS (compliance dates 2018-2020; not re-verified). Trans fat must be declared on Nutrition Facts. Qualified health claims exist for oleic-acid-rich oils and for olive oil and coronary heart disease (not re-verified). No authorised cognition claim; FTC substantiation would be lacking for 'brain fat' claims in healthy adults.", "morocco": "Ordinary foods under Law 28-07 and labelling Decree 2-12-389; nutrition claims per Joint Order 281-16; no authorised health-claim list found (unit X6). Whether Morocco has a legal limit on industrial trans fat was not verified (uncertain). Argan and olive oils are traditional local MUFA-rich products.", "permitted_claims_examples": ["Replacing saturated fats in the diet with unsaturated fats contributes to the maintenance of normal blood cholesterol levels. MUFA and PUFA are unsaturated fats. (EU, Reg. (EU) 432/2012; food high in unsaturated fat)"], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "None for conventional oils and fats; structured lipids, some algal oils and certain novel oil sources require EU novel-food authorisation.", "regulatory_gate": "pass"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "High: fat composition is set by choice of oil, nuts and dairy in any recipe; replacing butter, palm or ghee with olive, rapeseed or argan oil is feasible in most products.", "formats": ["olive/rapeseed/argan oil based dressings and spreads", "nut and seed snacks", "baked goods reformulated with unsaturated oils", "lunch meals with unsaturated oils rather than high-SFA fats"], "stability": "PUFA-rich oils oxidise readily (rancidity, off-flavours) and need antioxidants, low oxygen and cool storage; MUFA oils are more stable; SFA fats give structure in baked goods and are harder to replace.", "taste": "Olive and argan oils have strong flavours (desirable in savoury foods); high-oleic oils are neutral.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Fatty acids are well absorbed from all food matrices.", "dose_feasibility": "Substitution of 5% energy (about 11 g fat in a 2,000 kcal diet) from SFA to MUFA, the contrast used in cohorts, is achievable with 1-2 tablespoons of oil swapped daily.", "cost_availability": "Olive oil price volatile (see olive-oil-as-food monograph); rapeseed/sunflower high-oleic oils cheaper; argan oil expensive.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 3, "effect_magnitude": 3, "safety": 8, "reproducibility": 3, "food_suitability": 9, "cost_access": 8, "rationale": "Evidence quality 3: SFA/MUFA-dementia associations grade C (observational, inconsistent); short-term trials in young adults small and confounded (C-D); no long-term RCT of fat quality. Effect magnitude 3: cohort RR 1.4-2.0 for SFA extremes and ~0.1-0.2 SD in cognitive change; acute effects small. Safety 8: ordinary food; TFA and high SFA are the risks. Reproducibility 3: meta-analyses positive for SFA but rigorous review null; MUFA/PUFA pooled null. Food suitability 9 and cost 8: simple oil substitution in ordinary foods."}, "research_gaps": ["Long-term RCT isolating fat quality (SFA vs MUFA/PUFA substitution) with prespecified cognitive endpoints", "Replication of acute high-SFA meal attention effects in healthy young adults with preregistration", "Separating fat effects from carbohydrate restriction in high-fat diet studies", "Cohorts in North African/Mediterranean populations with olive and argan oil exposure", "Publication of completed macronutrient trials (e.g., MEAL-2 NCT02463084, no results posted)"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 5.1, "eligibility": "eligible", "safety_gate": "pass", "regulatory_gate": "pass", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": null, "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"dementia_risk": "C", "cognitive_decline": "C", "episodic_memory": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 7, "n_studies": 14, "n_verified": 14, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 1, "n_systematic_reviews": 3, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["harm", "inconsistent", "insufficient", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "dietary-fiber", "name": "Dietary fiber", "category": "A", "category_name": "Macronutrients and brain energy", "aliases": ["dietary fibre", "soluble fiber", "insoluble fiber", "fermentable fiber", "prebiotic fiber", "inulin / oligofructose / fructo-oligosaccharides (FOS)", "galacto-oligosaccharides (GOS)", "polydextrose", "partially hydrolysed guar gum (PHGG)", "beta-glucan", "resistant starch"], "food_sources": ["whole grains (oats, barley, whole wheat, bulgur, whole-grain couscous)", "legumes (lentils, chickpeas, beans, fava)", "vegetables (artichoke, leek, onion, garlic - inulin-rich; leafy greens)", "fruit (dates, figs, apples, berries, citrus)", "nuts and seeds", "potatoes and cooled starchy foods (resistant starch)", "isolated/added fibres (chicory inulin, polydextrose, PHGG, psyllium)"], "active_constituents": ["non-starch polysaccharides (cellulose, hemicellulose, pectins, beta-glucans, gums)", "resistant oligosaccharides (inulin-type fructans, GOS)", "resistant starch", "lignin", "microbial fermentation products: short-chain fatty acids (acetate, propionate, butyrate)"], "one_line_verdict": "Higher habitual fibre intake is consistently associated with somewhat lower dementia risk in large Japanese cohorts and fibre robustly improves glycaemic and cardiometabolic markers, but trials of isolated prebiotic fibres on cognition are small, heterogeneous and often industry-linked, the best young-adult trial was null on its primary outcome, and no evidence shows that fibre sharpens cognition in healthy young adults.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. Fibre is an established component of a healthy diet; EFSA set an adequate intake of 25 g/day for adults for normal laxation [efsa2010fibre], and most adults in Europe and the US eat less. Its cardiometabolic effects (lower LDL with viscous fibres, reduced postprandial glucose with oat/barley beta-glucan, better bowel function) are established and partly carry authorised EU claims (see whole-grains and legumes monographs). Cognitive evidence is mainly observational. In the Japanese CIRCS cohort (n=3,739, aged 40-64 at baseline, 19.7-year follow-up, 670 cases) the top fibre quartile had a lower risk of disabling dementia (HR 0.74, 95% CI 0.57-0.96), strongest for soluble fibre [yamagishi2023]; the larger JPHC study (n=41,467; 4,910 cases) found HR 0.82 (0.73-0.91) in women and 0.86 (0.74-1.00) in men for highest vs lowest total fibre [aoki2026]. Both rely on food questionnaires or recalls and on insurance-certified dementia, and residual confounding by overall diet, education and health is plausible. Cross-sectionally, in 1,070 NHANES adults >=60 y fibre intake was associated only with DSST (processing speed) and not with memory or fluency [prokopidis2022]. Intervention trials test isolated prebiotics, not dietary fibre. In 36 older twin pairs all doing resistance exercise with BCAA, a 12-week prebiotic improved a cognitive factor score but not the primary outcome (chair-rise time) [nilochlainn2024]; an industry-run 12-week PHGG trial in healthy older Japanese adults reported better visual memory [abe2024]; a 2026 meta-analysis of microbiome modulation in older adults (mostly probiotics, only 2 prebiotic and 2 synbiotic trials, mixed healthy and impaired participants) reported a global-cognition gain of MD 1.28 points (0.15-2.40) [birns2026]. In young adults, 4 weeks of polydextrose (12.5 g/day) improved set-shifting errors and sustained attention in an exploratory crossover of 18 women [berding2021]; a single 5 g dose of oligofructose-enriched inulin improved recognition and recall over 4 h in 47 adults [smith2015]; but the largest and best-designed young-adult trial (83 women aged 17-25, GOS for 28 days) found no effect on its primary outcome, trait anxiety, with only subgroup reaction-time gains [johnstone2025]. In children, a meta-analysis found no effect of fibre supplementation on memory or attention [yang2024], and a 3-month prebiotic trial in poor readers was null [capitao2020]. Grading. Dementia-risk association: grade C. Prebiotic effects on cognition in older adults: grade C (few small, heterogeneous, partly industry trials). Healthy young adults: grade C-D, inconsistent. How large. Cohort hazard reductions of 14-26% at the extremes of intake; trial effects small and not standardised. What is unknown. No trial has tested whole-diet fibre increases with cognitive endpoints; no preregistered trial in students; mechanisms through SCFAs and the gut-brain axis remain largely animal-based (see probiotics-and-psychobiotics monograph).", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Most adults eat below the 25 g/day adequate intake [efsa2010fibre], so increasing fibre is closer to correcting a widespread dietary shortfall than to enhancement. The observational dementia associations [yamagishi2023, aoki2026] compare low with high habitual intakes over decades and say nothing about short-term performance. In adequately nourished young adults, isolated prebiotic fibres have produced scattered, small effects in exploratory studies [berding2021, smith2015] and a null primary outcome in the largest trial [johnstone2025]; there is no basis for promising enhancement. Children's evidence is null [yang2024, capitao2020] and must not be extrapolated to adults.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "'Prebiotics for brain health' and 'gut-brain fibre for focus' claims rest on animal SCFA studies, a handful of small human trials (several industry-run: PHGG by Taiyo Kagaku [abe2024]; polydextrose as a branded ingredient [berding2021]) and a meta-analysis dominated by probiotics in older and impaired adults [birns2026]. None of this supports a cognition claim for healthy adults, and no EU fibre claim concerns cognition. What is defensible: 'high in fibre' or 'source of fibre' nutrition claims, authorised function claims such as chicory inulin and stool frequency (12 g/day) [efsa2015inulin] or oat/barley beta-glucan and blood cholesterol or post-meal glucose, and the honest message that fibre-rich diets are associated with healthier ageing. For Brain Food the strongest product logic is ordinary fibre-rich foods (whole grains, legumes, vegetables, fruit, nuts), not isolated prebiotic powders.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Fibre slows gastric emptying and carbohydrate absorption (viscous fibres), lowering postprandial glucose and insulin; binds bile acids (lower LDL); and is fermented by colonic microbiota to short-chain fatty acids, which act on gut endocrine cells, vagal afferents, immune cells and, in animals, the blood-brain barrier and microglia. Long-term, fibre-rich diets are associated with lower body weight, blood pressure, diabetes and cardiovascular risk, all dementia risk factors. Human evidence is strong for metabolic effects, modest for microbiome composition shifts (e.g., Bifidobacterium increases), and weak for any direct brain effect.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Viscous soluble fibre in a meal -> slower gastric emptying and glucose absorption -> lower postprandial glycaemia", "detail": "Established for oat/barley beta-glucan (authorised EU claim; see whole-grains monograph); link from smoother glycaemia to cognition is not demonstrated.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Fermentable fibre -> colonic microbiota changes (e.g., Bifidobacterium) -> SCFA production", "detail": "Prebiotic increased Bifidobacterium in older twins [nilochlainn2024]; GOS transiently increased Bifidobacterium in young women [johnstone2025].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "SCFAs and microbial signals -> vagal, endocrine and immune signalling to the brain", "detail": "Mostly animal data; in humans GOS produced trend-level changes in brain GABA (1H-MRS) [johnstone2025]; polydextrose altered a monocyte adhesion marker [berding2021].", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Long-term fibre-rich diet -> lower cardiometabolic risk (weight, LDL, diabetes, blood pressure) -> lower vascular contribution to dementia", "detail": "Plausible indirect pathway; cohort associations strongest for soluble fibre and for dementia without stroke history [yamagishi2023].", "evidence_level": "plausible"}, {"step": "Measured human cognitive outcome", "detail": "Lower dementia risk associated with high fibre in two Japanese cohorts [yamagishi2023, aoki2026]; small prebiotic RCTs with mixed results [nilochlainn2024, abe2024, berding2021, johnstone2025].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["postprandial glycaemic modulation", "bile-acid binding and LDL lowering", "microbiota fermentation and short-chain fatty acids (gut-brain axis)", "inflammation and immune signalling", "cardiometabolic risk reduction"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "dementia_risk", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Japanese community adults aged 40-74 at diet assessment, followed into older age", "dose_or_intake": "Habitual total, soluble and insoluble fibre; highest vs lowest quartile", "duration": "9.4-19.7 years follow-up", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Higher fibre intake associated with lower risk of disabling dementia in both cohorts; soluble fibre stronger in CIRCS; associations across food sources in JPHC.", "effect_estimate": "HR 0.74 (0.57-0.96) Q4 vs Q1 [yamagishi2023]; HR 0.82 (0.73-0.91) women, 0.86 (0.74-1.00) men [aoki2026]", "certainty_notes": "Observational (starts low); consistent within Japanese populations but overlapping research group; outcome is insurance-certified disabling dementia; residual confounding; no non-Asian prospective dementia cohort extracted here.", "key_study_ids": ["yamagishi2023", "aoki2026"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "processing_speed", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "US adults >=60 y (NHANES 2011-2014), cross-sectional", "dose_or_intake": "Habitual fibre (mean 17.3 g/day)", "duration": "not applicable (cross-sectional)", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Fibre associated with DSST (plateau at 34 g/day) but not with word learning, recall or animal fluency.", "effect_estimate": "DSST p=0.031; other tests NS [prokopidis2022]", "certainty_notes": "Cross-sectional, single 24-h recall based intake, multiple tests with one positive; reverse causation possible.", "key_study_ids": ["prokopidis2022"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Community-dwelling older adults (UK twins mean 73 y with exercise + BCAA; healthy Japanese older adults); meta-analysis also includes impaired adults", "dose_or_intake": "Isolated prebiotic fibres (inulin/FOS-type prebiotic; PHGG 5 g/day)", "duration": "12 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Prebiotic improved a cognitive factor score vs placebo (secondary outcome) without effect on primary physical outcome; PHGG improved visual memory in an industry-run trial; microbiome-modulation meta-analysis (mostly probiotics) reported small global-cognition gain.", "effect_estimate": "Cognition factor beta -0.482 (-0.813 to -0.141), p=0.014 [nilochlainn2024]; global cognition MD 1.28 (0.15-2.40) [birns2026]; PHGG: not reported in abstract [abe2024]", "certainty_notes": "Few small trials, secondary outcomes, missing baseline cognitive data in PROMOTe (22/72), industry sponsorship (PHGG), meta-analysis heterogeneous and probiotic-dominated; downgraded for risk of bias, imprecision and indirectness.", "key_study_ids": ["nilochlainn2024", "abe2024", "birns2026"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy young women (18 in PDX crossover; 83 aged 17-25 in GOS RCT)", "dose_or_intake": "Polydextrose 12.5 g/day; GOS (dose per trial)", "duration": "4 weeks (28 days)", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "PDX reduced set-shifting errors and improved sustained attention (exploratory, n=18); GOS had no effect on primary trait anxiety, with faster reaction times only in a high-anxious subgroup (tertiary outcome).", "effect_estimate": "not reported as standardised effects; GOS primary p=0.443 [johnstone2025]", "certainty_notes": "Exploratory small crossover with many outcomes vs a null primary in a larger RCT; different fibres; women only.", "key_study_ids": ["berding2021", "johnstone2025"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy females 17-25 y", "dose_or_intake": "GOS daily", "duration": "28 days", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "No difference in trait anxiety at end of intervention (p=0.443); trend at follow-up (p=0.069); no effect on social anxiety or depression; trend-level GABA changes on MRS.", "effect_estimate": "Primary outcome NS", "certainty_notes": "Single moderate-size RCT; MRS changes are biomarkers.", "key_study_ids": ["johnstone2025"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "47 adult volunteers (double-blind crossover)", "dose_or_intake": "5 g oligofructose-enriched inulin in decaffeinated tea/coffee at breakfast", "duration": "Single dose, testing ~2-4 h", "grade": "D", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Greater recognition-memory accuracy and better immediate and delayed recall; participants felt happier, less hungry and had less indigestion; earlier chronic study by the same author was null.", "effect_estimate": "not reported as effect sizes in abstract", "certainty_notes": "Single small study, many outcomes, mechanism unclear (fermentation takes longer than test window); same author's chronic trial null; not replicated.", "key_study_ids": ["smith2015"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "children_adolescents", "population_detail": "Children and adolescents (mixed; one trial in 7-9-year-old poor readers)", "dose_or_intake": "Fibre or prebiotic supplementation; habitual fibre", "duration": "weeks to 3 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Meta-analysis: no significant effect of fibre supplementation on memory or attention; no association with intelligence; prebiotic trial in poor readers null on all outcomes.", "effect_estimate": "Memory SMD 0.46 (-0.79 to 1.71); attention SMD -2.72 (-6.30 to 0.86) [yang2024]", "certainty_notes": "Few, heterogeneous trials; imprecise; not generalisable to adults.", "key_study_ids": ["yang2024", "capitao2020"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "5 g oligofructose-enriched inulin", "onset": "Within ~2 h after breakfast", "duration_studied": "4 h", "domain": "Episodic memory, mood", "population": "47 adults", "effect_estimate": "Improved recognition and recall; effect sizes not reported", "certainty": "very low", "safety": "Less indigestion reported than placebo", "study_ids": ["smith2015"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "12 weeks", "population": "72 older twins (36 pairs) with exercise and BCAA", "outcome": "Cognitive factor score (secondary); chair rise (primary)", "effect_estimate": "Cognition beta -0.482 (-0.813 to -0.141); chair rise NS", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Secondary outcome; missing cognitive data; small", "study_ids": ["nilochlainn2024"]}, {"duration": "12 weeks", "population": "Healthy older Japanese adults", "outcome": "Visual memory, sleep", "effect_estimate": "Visual memory improved vs placebo (numbers not in abstract)", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Industry-run; n not extracted", "study_ids": ["abe2024"]}, {"duration": "4 weeks per arm", "population": "18 healthy women", "outcome": "Cognitive flexibility, sustained attention", "effect_estimate": "Fewer IED errors; better RVP (exploratory)", "evidence_quality": "very low", "limitations": "Exploratory; tiny; many outcomes", "study_ids": ["berding2021"]}, {"duration": "28 days", "population": "83 women 17-25 y", "outcome": "Trait anxiety (primary); RT (tertiary)", "effect_estimate": "Primary NS (p=0.443)", "evidence_quality": "moderate", "limitations": "Women only; RT findings subgroup", "study_ids": ["johnstone2025"]}, {"duration": "9.4-19.7 years", "population": "45,206 Japanese adults (two cohorts)", "outcome": "Disabling dementia", "effect_estimate": "HR 0.74-0.86 highest vs lowest quartile", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Observational; one country", "study_ids": ["yamagishi2023", "aoki2026"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Fibre from foods is safe and beneficial; isolated fermentable fibres cause dose-dependent bloating and flatulence (more mild GI events with prebiotic in PROMOTe [nilochlainn2024]), particularly in people with IBS (FODMAP sensitivity). Bulk-forming gums and psyllium need adequate fluid. Fibre can reduce absorption of some drugs and minerals.", "adverse_effects": ["Bloating, flatulence, abdominal discomfort with fermentable fibres (inulin, FOS, GOS), dose-dependent", "Loose stools at high doses of some oligosaccharides/polydextrose", "Risk of oesophageal or bowel obstruction with dry bulk-forming fibres (psyllium, guar, glucomannan) taken without enough water", "Reduced mineral bioavailability with high phytate-rich fibre sources (relevant in low-iron/zinc diets)"], "contraindications": ["Bowel obstruction or severe dysmotility", "Swallowing difficulties (bulk-forming fibres)", "IBS with FODMAP sensitivity (fermentable fibres may worsen symptoms)"], "drug_interactions": ["Bulk-forming and viscous fibres can reduce or delay absorption of some drugs (e.g., levothyroxine, digoxin, lithium, some antidiabetics); separate dosing by 2-4 h (general pharmacological guidance, not verified per drug here)", "Additive glucose lowering with antidiabetic medication at high viscous-fibre intakes"], "upper_limits": "No tolerable upper intake level for fibre; adult AI 25 g/day [efsa2010fibre].", "sleep_impact": "No consistent evidence; one industry trial reported better sleepiness-on-rising with PHGG [abe2024].", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Decades in cohorts; isolated prebiotics up to 12 weeks for cognitive outcomes.", "daily_use_appropriate": "yes", "special_populations": "Children: avoid excessive bulk displacing energy; older adults: ensure fluids; IBS: titrate fermentable fibre; people on narrow-therapeutic-index drugs: separate timing from fibre supplements.", "specialist_review_needed": ["Gastroenterologist/dietitian for IBS consumers", "Pharmacist review for fibre supplements in medicated consumers"], "hard_safety_gate": "pass", "gate_reason": "Ordinary food component without UL; tolerability issues are manageable by dose titration and labelling."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Nutrition claims under Reg. (EC) 1924/2006 Annex: 'source of fibre' (>=3 g/100 g or >=1.5 g/100 kcal) and 'high fibre' (>=6 g/100 g or >=3 g/100 kcal). Authorised Article 13 claims (Reg. (EU) 432/2012 and later amendments; wording from prior knowledge and sibling monographs, register not re-queried): oat and barley beta-glucan and maintenance of normal blood cholesterol (3 g/day); beta-glucans from oats/barley and reduction of post-prandial glycaemic response; wheat bran fibre and faecal bulk/transit; barley, oat and rye fibre claims for bowel function; chicory inulin contributes to normal bowel function by increasing stool frequency (12 g/day; Reg. (EU) 2015/2314) [efsa2015inulin]. No authorised claim links fibre or prebiotics to cognition, mood or the gut-brain axis; the term 'prebiotic' itself is treated by several member-state authorities as an implied health claim (not verified here). Most isolated fibres (inulin, FOS, GOS, polydextrose, PHGG) are established food ingredients; check novel-food status for newer fibres.", "us": "FDA 2016 labelling rule defines dietary fiber as intrinsic/intact plant fibre plus isolated or synthetic fibres with an FDA-recognised physiological benefit; inulin-type fructans and several others were later added (not re-verified). Authorised health claim for soluble fibre from oats/barley/psyllium and coronary heart disease (21 CFR 101.81, not re-read). No cognition claim; FTC substantiation lacking for gut-brain cognitive claims in healthy adults.", "morocco": "Ordinary foods under Law 28-07 and labelling Decree 2-12-389; nutrition claims per Joint Order 281-16 (fibre claim conditions not verified); no authorised health-claim list found (unit X6). Fibre-supplement registration with the Ministry of Health/AMMPS if sold as a food supplement (uncertain). Status of any gut-brain claim: not permitted to our knowledge.", "permitted_claims_examples": ["Chicory inulin contributes to normal bowel function by increasing stool frequency (EU; 12 g/day native chicory inulin)", "Oat beta-glucan has been shown to lower/reduce blood cholesterol (EU; 3 g/day; register wording to be checked)"], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "Conventional fibres none; some newer isolated fibres (e.g., specific human-milk oligosaccharides, novel resistant dextrins or fungal glucans) may require EU novel-food authorisation and US GRAS notices.", "regulatory_gate": "pass"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Excellent: fibre is inherent to whole grains, legumes, fruit, vegetables and nuts; isolated fibres (inulin, PDX, PHGG, oat beta-glucan) are easily added to bars, drinks, bakery and dairy.", "formats": ["whole-grain bread, oats, barley and bulgur products", "legume-based dishes and snacks", "fruit (dates, figs) and nut bars", "fibre-enriched drinks and yogurts (inulin, PHGG, beta-glucan)"], "stability": "Stable; inulin can hydrolyse in acidic heated products; beta-glucan viscosity (needed for glycaemic and cholesterol effects) falls with processing and enzymatic degradation.", "taste": "Inulin/FOS slightly sweet; PHGG neutral; bran gritty; high doses affect texture.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Effects depend on fibre type: viscosity and molecular weight for glycaemic/cholesterol effects; fermentability for microbiome effects.", "dose_feasibility": "Doses used in trials (5 g PHGG, 5 g inulin, 12.5 g PDX) fit a single serving; the 12 g/day chicory inulin claim dose is achievable but GI tolerability limits single servings to about 5-10 g for many people. Increasing total fibre from ~17 to 25-30 g/day is feasible with whole foods.", "cost_availability": "Whole-food fibre sources (legumes, oats, bulgur, dates) are cheap and widely available in Morocco and Europe; isolated fibres are low-cost commodity ingredients.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 3, "effect_magnitude": 3, "safety": 9, "reproducibility": 3, "food_suitability": 10, "cost_access": 9, "rationale": "Evidence quality 3: dementia-risk association grade C (two Japanese cohorts); prebiotic trials grade C in older adults; young adults C-D with a null primary in the best trial. Effect magnitude 3: HR 0.74-0.86 at intake extremes; trial effects small and unstandardised. Safety 9: no UL, GI tolerability issues only. Reproducibility 3: cohort signal replicated within Japan; trial findings not replicated across fibres. Food suitability 10 and cost 9: ubiquitous, cheap whole-food sources and inexpensive isolated fibres."}, "research_gaps": ["Preregistered RCT of increasing whole-food fibre (not isolated prebiotics) on cognition in students and knowledge workers", "Replication of polydextrose and inulin cognitive findings with adequate power", "Prospective fibre-dementia cohorts outside Japan with objective cognitive endpoints", "Separation of fibre effects from the whole-grain, legume and fruit/vegetable dietary pattern", "Human evidence that SCFA changes mediate any cognitive effect (gut-brain axis; see probiotics-and-psychobiotics)"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 5.5, "eligibility": "eligible", "safety_gate": "pass", "regulatory_gate": "pass", "best_grade_acute": "D", "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": null, "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"dementia_risk": "C", "global_cognition": "C", "episodic_memory": "D"}, "n_outcome_rows": 7, "n_studies": 13, "n_verified": 13, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 5, "n_systematic_reviews": 1, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["inconsistent", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "dietary-protein-and-amino-acids", "name": "Dietary protein and amino acid availability", "category": "A", "category_name": "Macronutrients and brain energy", "aliases": ["dietary protein", "protein intake", "meal protein", "whey protein", "plant protein", "animal protein", "essential amino acids", "large neutral amino acids (LNAA)", "protein-rich breakfast"], "food_sources": ["dairy (milk, yogurt, cheese, whey)", "eggs", "fish and seafood", "poultry and lean meat", "legumes (lentils, chickpeas, beans, fava) and soy", "nuts and seeds", "cereals (contribute most protein in grain-based diets, including Moroccan bread- and couscous-based diets)"], "active_constituents": ["indispensable amino acids (leucine, isoleucine, valine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, tryptophan, histidine)", "large neutral amino acids competing for the LAT1 blood-brain barrier carrier (tyrosine, phenylalanine, tryptophan, branched-chain amino acids)", "neurotransmitter precursors: tyrosine (catecholamines), tryptophan (serotonin), histidine (histamine), glutamate/glutamine, glycine", "bioactive peptides and co-nutrients in protein foods (alpha-lactalbumin, creatine, choline, vitamin B12, iron, zinc) - covered in their own monographs"], "one_line_verdict": "Adequate protein intake is a basic nutritional requirement and higher protein (versus refined carbohydrate) is weakly associated with slower subjective cognitive decline in large cohorts, but other cohorts are null or negative, intervention trials in non-malnourished older adults are small and mostly null, and in healthy young adults there is no consistent evidence that a protein-rich meal or extra protein improves cognition.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. Protein is an essential nutrient: the EU population reference intake for adults is 0.83 g/kg body weight per day [efsa2012protein], and most adults in the EU, US and urban Morocco meet it. The cognitive question therefore concerns either correcting inadequate intake (frail or malnourished older adults) or adding protein above requirements. Observational evidence is mixed. In 77,335 US health professionals, substituting 5% of energy from protein for carbohydrate was associated with lower odds of later subjective cognitive decline (OR 0.89, 95% CI 0.85-0.94), with plant protein showing the strongest association (OR 0.74, 0.62-0.88) [yeh2022]; the outcome was self-reported and confounding by diet quality is likely. In the Longitudinal Aging Study Amsterdam (n=1,339, mean intake 1.1 g/kg/d) higher protein was associated with lower episodic memory and faster decline in global cognition and processing speed [peperkamp2025]; in China (n=3,083), plant protein from grains was associated with more decline and animal protein with less [gao2022], probably reflecting overall diet quality in a cereal-dominant diet rather than protein itself. A meta-analysis of nine cross-sectional studies found no association between protein intake and global cognition in older adults, with only scattered positive associations for memory [coelhojunior2021], and an earlier systematic review judged the evidence weak [koh2015]. Intervention trials are few. In 65 pre-frail and frail older adults, 2 x 15 g protein/day for 24 weeks improved reaction time (68 vs 18 ms faster; p=0.03) but no cognitive domain [vanderzwaluw2014]. A 12-month industry-funded trial in 107 Chinese adults with MCI found a MoCA gain of 1.81 points (95% CI 0.32-3.30) with 15 g/day of a whey powder that also contained milk-fat-globule membrane, taurine and B vitamins [li2025whey]; the multi-ingredient product, the sponsor's involvement and the clinical population prevent attribution to protein or generalisation to healthy adults. Acute studies. A single isoenergetic whey drink improved paragraph recall 15 min later in 22 healthy older adults, but so did glucose and fat drinks, pointing to energy intake rather than protein specifically [kaplan2001]. In young adults, a protein-rich vs carbohydrate-rich meal gave small, task-specific and time-dependent differences in 15 male students [fischer2002], a 10-person simulated-work study found no performance difference [sihvola2013], a crossover in 33 undergraduates found no effect of breakfast macronutrient composition on cognition [emilien2017], and a single-blind breakfast study in 40 young adults reported slower simple reaction time after a whole-wheat versus a protein croissant, confounded by co-administered caffeine [manippa2021]. Mechanism. Meal protein shifts plasma tryptophan:LNAA and tyrosine:LNAA ratios (see l-tyrosine and tryptophan monographs); these are biomarker shifts, not demonstrated cognitive effects. Safety. In healthy adults, high-protein diets (>=1.5 g/kg/d or >=20% energy) did not change GFR over time (SMD 0.11, -0.05 to 0.27) [devries2018]; caution applies to chronic kidney disease. What is unknown. No adequately powered RCT has tested protein dose or distribution on cognition in healthy young adults, students or knowledge workers; whether the protein-cognition cohort signal reflects protein, its food sources or overall diet quality is unresolved; and high-protein dietary patterns are evaluated in the K2 unit.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Deficiency correction: protein-energy malnutrition impairs cognition, especially in children (outside scope) and frail older adults; the only intervention evidence in frail older adults shows a reaction-time gain without improvement in memory, attention or executive function [vanderzwaluw2014], and the positive MCI trial used a multi-nutrient product [li2025whey]. Protein adequacy is relevant to older adults with low appetite, sarcopenia or recent hospitalisation, where the primary rationale is muscle and function, not cognition. Enhancement: for adequately nourished young adults (typical intakes 1.0-1.4 g/kg/d), there is no trial evidence that additional protein or a higher-protein breakfast improves cognitive performance; acute studies are small and inconsistent [fischer2002, emilien2017, sihvola2013, manippa2021]. The cohort association with lower subjective cognitive decline [yeh2022] concerns decades of habitual intake in middle and older age and compares protein against carbohydrate, not protein supplementation against nothing.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Claims such as 'protein for brain power', 'protein for focus' or 'amino acids fuel your neurotransmitters' are not supported for healthy adults: mechanism (precursor supply) is real but does not translate into consistent measured cognitive benefit in replete people, and the most cited cognitive trial used a multi-ingredient whey powder in MCI patients and was industry funded [li2025whey]. In the EU only generic protein claims are authorised (protein contributes to growth in muscle mass, maintenance of muscle mass, and maintenance of normal bones; nutrition claims 'source of protein' at >=12% energy and 'high protein' at >=20% energy from protein); none concerns cognition. An honest Brain Food position: protein-adequate, high-quality meals (dairy, eggs, fish, legumes) support satiety and stable energy; claims of cognitive enhancement should not be made. Protein-rich breakfast messaging can emphasise satiety and steady post-meal glucose, which are physiological effects, not cognitive outcomes.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Dietary protein is digested to amino acids absorbed in the small intestine. Large neutral amino acids (tyrosine, phenylalanine, tryptophan, leucine, isoleucine, valine) compete for the LAT1 transporter at the blood-brain barrier, so meal composition changes the ratios of precursor to competing LNAA and thus brain uptake of tryptophan (serotonin) and tyrosine (dopamine, noradrenaline). Protein-rich meals generally lower tryptophan:LNAA and blunt postprandial glucose and insulin excursions. Long-term adequate protein supports muscle mass (the muscle-brain axis hypothesis) and supply of co-nutrients (B12, iron, choline, creatine). Mechanisms are plausible; translation into cognitive outcomes in healthy people is not demonstrated.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Dietary protein -> digestion and absorption of amino acids; postprandial plasma amino acid rise", "detail": "Well characterised; whey is rapidly absorbed, casein and plant proteins more slowly; adult requirement 0.66 g/kg/d average, PRI 0.83 g/kg/d [efsa2012protein].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Plasma LNAA ratios -> competitive transport across the blood-brain barrier (LAT1)", "detail": "Whey or carbohydrate breakfast drinks raised plasma Trp:LNAA about 30% versus a control drink [sihvola2013]; carbohydrate:protein ratio altered Tyr:LNAA [fischer2002].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Brain precursor availability -> serotonin and catecholamine synthesis", "detail": "Shown in animals and via tryptophan/tyrosine depletion and loading studies in humans (see tryptophan and l-tyrosine monographs); diet-induced shifts are smaller than those in depletion paradigms.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Protein-rich meal -> lower postprandial glucose and insulin excursions", "detail": "High-protein/low-carbohydrate breakfast lowered plasma glucose and insulin versus high-carbohydrate breakfast in undergraduates [emilien2017].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Habitual adequate protein -> muscle mass, physical function and nutritional status (muscle-brain axis)", "detail": "Plausible indirect pathway in older adults; cognitive benefit not shown in protein RCTs in frail older adults [vanderzwaluw2014].", "evidence_level": "plausible"}, {"step": "Measured human cognitive outcome", "detail": "Acute meal studies inconsistent and small; chronic supplementation null on cognitive domains in frail older adults except reaction time; observational associations conflicting [yeh2022, peperkamp2025, coelhojunior2021].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["LNAA competition at LAT1 (tryptophan and tyrosine brain uptake)", "postprandial glycaemic and insulinaemic modulation", "muscle-brain axis (sarcopenia, physical function)", "co-nutrient supply from protein foods (B12, iron, zinc, choline, creatine)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy students and young adults (18-30 y) after overnight fast; protein-rich vs carbohydrate-rich or balanced breakfast/meal", "dose_or_intake": "Isoenergetic meals or drinks with high protein share (e.g., carbohydrate:protein 1:4; whey drinks)", "duration": "Single meal, testing up to 3.5-4 h", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Fischer 2002 (n=15 men): better short-term memory accuracy after protein-rich meal and faster central reaction time after balanced meal, with time-dependent effects; Emilien 2017 (n=33): no effect of breakfast composition on cognition; Sihvola 2013 (n=10): no task-performance difference; Manippa 2021 (n=40): faster simple RT after protein vs whole-wheat croissant, confounded by caffeine.", "effect_estimate": "not reported as standardised effects; p-values only for selected tasks", "certainty_notes": "Very small, mostly unregistered crossover studies with many outcomes and time points (multiplicity), inconsistent direction, imprecision; no blinding possible for meals. Downgraded for risk of bias, inconsistency and imprecision.", "key_study_ids": ["fischer2002", "emilien2017", "sihvola2013", "manippa2021"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "alertness", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy young adults under simulated work load", "dose_or_intake": "Whey-protein breakfast drink vs carbohydrate drink vs low-energy control", "duration": "Single morning session", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "Protein and carbohydrate drinks both raised Trp:LNAA and lowered heart-rate rise during work; only the carbohydrate drink reduced subjective sleepiness; no difference in workload ratings, cortisol or task performance (n=10).", "effect_estimate": "Median KSS change -1.5 (carbohydrate) vs -0.5 (control); no performance difference", "certainty_notes": "Single tiny single-blind study; subjective outcomes; protein-specific alertness effect not shown.", "key_study_ids": ["sihvola2013"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Healthy adults 61-79 y after overnight fast", "dose_or_intake": "300 mL drink with 774 kJ as whey protein (vs glucose, safflower oil, or non-energy placebo)", "duration": "Single dose, testing 15 and 60 min", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "All three macronutrient drinks improved delayed paragraph recall at 15 min vs placebo; protein reduced the rate of forgetting; effects attributed to energy ingestion rather than glucose.", "effect_estimate": "p<0.001 for delayed recall vs placebo; effect sizes not reported in abstract", "certainty_notes": "Single small (n=22) non-preregistered study; protein not superior to other macronutrients; indirect for young adults.", "key_study_ids": ["kaplan2001"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Adequately nourished young adults and students", "dose_or_intake": "Habitual or supplemental protein above requirement", "duration": "weeks to years", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No RCT or cohort identified testing chronic protein intake or supplementation on cognition in healthy young adults or students.", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "Absence of evidence; inference from older-adult cohorts is indirect.", "key_study_ids": ["coelhojunior2021"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_decline", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Middle-aged and older community-dwelling adults (US health professionals, Dutch and Chinese older adults)", "dose_or_intake": "Habitual dietary protein (energy % or g/kg/d) assessed by FFQ or 24-h recalls", "duration": "9-20+ years of dietary follow-up", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "NHS/HPFS (n=77,335): 5% energy from protein instead of carbohydrate associated with lower odds of subjective cognitive decline; LASA (n=1,339): higher protein associated with lower episodic memory and faster decline; CHNS (n=3,083): plant (grain) protein associated with more decline, animal protein with less.", "effect_estimate": "OR 0.89 (0.85-0.94) total protein, 0.74 (0.62-0.88) plant protein per 5% energy substitution [yeh2022]; beta Q4 vs Q1 -0.18 (-0.30 to -0.06) episodic memory [peperkamp2025]; OR 3.03 (1.22-7.53) Q5 vs Q1 plant protein [gao2022]", "certainty_notes": "Observational, residual confounding by diet quality and socioeconomic status; self-reported outcome in the largest cohort; opposite directions across cohorts. Starts low (observational), downgraded for inconsistency.", "key_study_ids": ["yeh2022", "peperkamp2025", "gao2022", "koh2015"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Adults >=60 y without dementia; cross-sectional studies", "dose_or_intake": "Habitual protein intake", "duration": "not applicable (cross-sectional)", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Meta-analysis of 9 cross-sectional studies (n=4,929): no association between protein intake and global cognition; 3 studies reported positive associations with memory.", "effect_estimate": "Pooled association with global cognition not significant (numeric estimate not reported in abstract)", "certainty_notes": "Cross-sectional design (reverse causation: cognitive impairment reduces intake), few studies, heterogeneous exposure measures.", "key_study_ids": ["coelhojunior2021", "koh2015"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "processing_speed", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Pre-frail and frail older adults (mean 79 y, median MMSE 28)", "dose_or_intake": "2 x 15 g protein drink/day vs placebo drink", "duration": "24 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Reaction time improved more with protein (68 ms) than placebo (18 ms, p=0.03); no effect on episodic memory, attention/working memory, information-processing speed or executive function domain scores.", "effect_estimate": "RT difference ~50 ms (p=0.03); domain scores NS", "certainty_notes": "Single small RCT (n=65), reaction time one of several outcomes; frail population (risk of low intake) limits generalisation.", "key_study_ids": ["vanderzwaluw2014"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_mci", "population_detail": "Chinese adults with MCI, mean age 63", "dose_or_intake": "15 g/day whey protein powder enriched with MFGM, taurine and B vitamins vs placebo powder", "duration": "12 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "MoCA improved 3.23 vs 1.42 points; between-group difference 1.81; DSST difference 2.72; few side effects.", "effect_estimate": "MoCA MD 1.81 (95% CI 0.32-3.30); DSST MD 2.72 (0.20-5.23) [li2025whey]", "certainty_notes": "Single industry-funded trial (BYHEALTH; two authors employees); multi-ingredient product so protein effect cannot be isolated; large improvement in the placebo arm suggests practice effects. Single industry trial cannot exceed B; downgraded further for indirectness to protein.", "key_study_ids": ["li2025whey"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "Isoenergetic 1670 kJ meals with carbohydrate:protein 4:1, 1:1 or 1:4", "onset": "Within 1 h (carbohydrate) vs after 1 h (balanced/protein)", "duration_studied": "3.5 h", "domain": "short-term memory, attention, reaction time", "population": "15 healthy male students", "effect_estimate": "Task- and time-specific p-values; no standardised effect size", "certainty": "low", "safety": "No issues", "study_ids": ["fischer2002"]}, {"dose": "High-protein/low-carbohydrate vs high-carbohydrate breakfasts vs no breakfast", "onset": "not applicable (null)", "duration_studied": "4 h", "domain": "cognitive battery, mood, appetite", "population": "33 undergraduates", "effect_estimate": "No significant effect on cognitive performance; lower glucose/insulin after high-protein breakfast", "certainty": "low", "safety": "No issues", "study_ids": ["emilien2017"]}, {"dose": "Whey drink 774 kJ", "onset": "15 min", "duration_studied": "60 min", "domain": "paragraph recall", "population": "22 healthy adults 61-79 y", "effect_estimate": "Improved delayed recall vs placebo (p<0.001), similar to glucose and fat drinks", "certainty": "low", "safety": "No issues", "study_ids": ["kaplan2001"]}, {"dose": "Protein croissant vs whole-wheat croissant, each with espresso", "onset": "Post-breakfast", "duration_studied": "single session", "domain": "simple reaction time, digit span", "population": "40 young Italian adults", "effect_estimate": "Slower RT after whole-wheat vs protein croissant; numbers not in abstract", "certainty": "very low", "safety": "No issues", "study_ids": ["manippa2021"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "24 weeks", "population": "65 pre-frail/frail older adults", "outcome": "Cognitive domains and reaction time", "effect_estimate": "RT 68 vs 18 ms improvement (p=0.03); domains NS", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Small; RT secondary; frail population", "study_ids": ["vanderzwaluw2014"]}, {"duration": "12 months", "population": "107 adults with MCI", "outcome": "MoCA", "effect_estimate": "MD 1.81 (0.32-3.30)", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Industry funded; multi-ingredient whey product; single trial", "study_ids": ["li2025whey"]}, {"duration": "~20 years diet; SCD assessed later", "population": "77,335 US health professionals", "outcome": "Subjective cognitive decline", "effect_estimate": "OR 0.89 (0.85-0.94) per 5% energy protein replacing carbohydrate", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Observational; self-reported outcome; confounding", "study_ids": ["yeh2022"]}, {"duration": "Up to 10 years", "population": "1,339 Dutch adults 55+", "outcome": "Global cognition, memory, speed, executive function", "effect_estimate": "Higher protein associated with lower episodic memory (beta -0.18) and faster decline", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Single FFQ; possible confounding by protein sources", "study_ids": ["peperkamp2025"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Protein from ordinary foods is safe for healthy adults across a wide range of intakes; high-protein diets did not change GFR over time in healthy adults [devries2018]. Concerns are population- and source-specific: chronic kidney disease, rare inborn errors of amino acid metabolism, and the health profile of the protein source (processed meat; saturated fat; sodium). Isolated protein powders carry contamination risks (heavy metals) and allergen issues.", "adverse_effects": ["Gastrointestinal discomfort with large protein-powder servings or lactose-containing whey concentrates", "Allergic reactions (milk, egg, soy, fish, peanut, lupin, wheat proteins)", "Heavy-metal contamination reported in some commercial protein powders (not reviewed in detail here)", "Displacement of fibre-rich foods in very high-protein diets"], "contraindications": ["Chronic kidney disease (protein restriction may be prescribed)", "Phenylketonuria and other inborn errors of amino acid metabolism", "Specific food-protein allergies"], "drug_interactions": ["Levodopa: dietary LNAA compete with levodopa for intestinal and blood-brain barrier transport; protein redistribution diets are used in Parkinson's disease", "MAO inhibitors: tyramine in aged/fermented protein foods (food-specific, not protein per se)"], "upper_limits": "No tolerable upper intake level set by EFSA; intakes up to about twice the PRI (about 1.7 g/kg/d) considered safe for adults [efsa2012protein].", "sleep_impact": "No direct evidence; evening carbohydrate-rich vs protein-rich meals shift Trp:LNAA (see tryptophan monograph).", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Decades of habitual intake in cohorts; RCTs up to 12 months.", "daily_use_appropriate": "yes", "special_populations": "Older adults often need more (1.0-1.2 g/kg/d is widely recommended by geriatric societies, not verified here); CKD patients need medical supervision; children and pregnant women have specific reference values.", "specialist_review_needed": ["Nephrologist/clinical dietitian for CKD consumers", "Food-allergen and contaminant testing for protein powders"], "hard_safety_gate": "pass", "gate_reason": "Essential nutrient from ordinary foods with no UL; kidney safety in healthy adults supported; allergen labelling and contaminant control are standard requirements."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Ordinary nutrient. Nutrition claims under Reg. (EC) 1924/2006 Annex: 'source of protein' (>=12% of energy from protein) and 'high protein' (>=20%). Authorised Article 13 claims in Reg. (EU) 432/2012 (from prior knowledge; register not re-queried this session): protein contributes to a growth in muscle mass; to the maintenance of muscle mass; to the maintenance of normal bones (food must at least be a source of protein). No authorised claim links protein or amino acids to cognitive function; individual amino acid claims for cognition have not been authorised (see l-tyrosine and tryptophan monographs). Allergenic proteins must be declared under Reg. (EU) 1169/2011.", "us": "Protein is a conventional food nutrient; 'good source'/'high' nutrient content claims (10%/20% DV, DV 50 g) apply under 21 CFR 101.54 with PDCAAS correction for %DV (not re-verified). Structure/function claims about brain or cognition would require FTC-level substantiation, which does not exist for healthy adults. Whey and other common proteins are GRAS/food ingredients; protein powders as dietary supplements fall under DSHEA.", "morocco": "Ordinary foods under Law 28-07 (ONSSA) and labelling Decree 2-12-389 (Arabic labelling, allergen declaration); nutrition claims under Joint Order 281-16 (conditions for 'source of/high protein' not verified here); no authorised health-claim list found (see unit X6). Status of any cognitive protein claim: not permitted to our knowledge; uncertain.", "permitted_claims_examples": ["Protein contributes to a growth in muscle mass (EU, Reg. (EU) 432/2012; food at least a source of protein)", "Protein contributes to the maintenance of muscle mass (EU, same conditions)", "Protein contributes to the maintenance of normal bones (EU, same conditions)"], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "None for conventional proteins; novel protein sources (insect, microalgal, precision-fermentation proteins, some new isolates) require EU novel-food authorisation and may need US GRAS/NDI evaluation.", "regulatory_gate": "pass"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Excellent: protein is delivered by everyday foods (dairy, eggs, legumes, fish) and is easily added to breakfasts, snacks and bars.", "formats": ["protein-rich breakfast (yogurt, eggs, legumes - e.g., Moroccan bissara, lentil/chickpea dishes)", "dairy and fermented dairy", "protein bars and drinks", "legume- or seed-based snacks"], "stability": "Stable; Maillard browning and texture hardening in high-protein bars during storage.", "taste": "Whey and plant proteins can be chalky or bitter at high levels; legume proteins have beany notes.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Animal proteins and soy have high digestibility (DIAAS/PDCAAS); cereal and legume proteins complement each other (lysine/methionine).", "dose_feasibility": "A 20-30 g protein serving is easily achieved in food (e.g., 200 g Greek yogurt plus nuts; 2 eggs plus bread; legume dish). Doses tested in trials (15-30 g/day) are food-compatible.", "cost_availability": "Widely available; legumes are among the cheapest protein sources, including in Morocco.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 3, "effect_magnitude": 2, "safety": 9, "reproducibility": 2, "food_suitability": 10, "cost_access": 9, "rationale": "Evidence quality 3: best rows are grade C (inconsistent cohorts, small acute trials, single industry trial in MCI); healthy young adults grade C-D. Effect magnitude 2: where effects appear they are small (OR 0.89 for subjective decline; 50 ms reaction time) and not reproduced. Safety 9: essential nutrient, no UL, kidney safety in healthy adults; CKD and allergy caveats. Reproducibility 2: cohorts point in opposite directions and acute trials disagree. Food suitability 10: delivered by ordinary foods at studied doses. Cost/access 9: legumes and dairy are cheap and widely available."}, "research_gaps": ["Adequately powered, preregistered RCT of protein dose and meal distribution on cognition in healthy young adults and students", "Trials separating protein from co-ingredients (MFGM, B vitamins, taurine) in older adults with low protein intake", "Cohorts with objective cognitive outcomes that model protein substitution against specific foods rather than total carbohydrate", "Interaction of protein intake with APOE genotype (signals in observational data not reviewed here)", "Evidence in Moroccan and North African populations with cereal-dominant protein intake"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 5.2, "eligibility": "eligible", "safety_gate": "pass", "regulatory_gate": "pass", "best_grade_acute": "C", "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": null, "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"episodic_memory": "C", "global_cognition": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 8, "n_studies": 14, "n_verified": 14, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 8, "n_systematic_reviews": 2, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["inconsistent", "insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "glucose-and-carbohydrates", "name": "Glucose, carbohydrates, glycemic index and glycemic load", "category": "A", "category_name": "Macronutrients and brain energy", "aliases": ["dextrose", "D-glucose", "blood sugar", "glycaemic carbohydrates", "available carbohydrate", "glycaemic index (GI)", "glycaemic load (GL)", "sucrose", "isomaltulose (Palatinose) as low-GI comparator", "glucose drink", "glucose facilitation effect"], "food_sources": ["Bread, pasta, rice, couscous, potatoes, oats and other starchy staples (digested to glucose)", "Fruit, honey, dates and fruit juice (glucose, fructose, sucrose)", "Milk and yoghurt (lactose -> glucose + galactose)", "Table sugar, confectionery, sugar-sweetened beverages, 'energy' and 'glucose' tablets/drinks (dextrose, sucrose, glucose syrups)", "Legumes and whole grains (low-GI glycaemic carbohydrate plus fibre)"], "active_constituents": ["Glucose (the brain's principal oxidative fuel)", "Sucrose and starch as glucose sources differing in rate of delivery", "Glycaemic index/load as a property of the food matrix rather than a nutrient"], "one_line_verdict": "Glucose is the brain's obligate fuel, but in adequately fed healthy young adults a glucose or sugar drink produces at best a small, transient and inconsistent boost of immediate verbal recall (mostly shown after an overnight fast), while the better-controlled glycaemic-index meal trials are largely null and chronic high added-sugar intake is, if anything, associated with worse cognitive outcomes; the honest 'brain food' message is regular, moderate, low-GI carbohydrate meals, not sugar.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. The 'glucose facilitation effect' literature (Benton, Foster, Sunram-Lea, Kennedy, Scholey, Riby, Messier) has repeatedly shown that 25-50 g of glucose, given as a drink after an overnight or 2-h fast, can improve performance on some memory tasks, most reliably long-term verbal free/cued recall [foster1998], immediate word recall [benton1994] and effortful tasks such as Serial Sevens [kennedy2000]. The only quantitative synthesis of interventional trials [garcia2021] identified 37 trials (35 glucose, 3 sucrose) and found a significant pooled effect only for immediate word recall in parallel-group studies (SMD 0.61, 95% CI 0.20-1.02, I2 = 0%), with 24 of 37 trials at high risk of selection bias; two trials found no effect and the remainder 'mixed results'. A second meta-analysis embedded in a broader review of sugars [gillespie2023] likewise found improved immediate free recall (p = 0.002) in a subset. Neuroimaging/EEG studies [peters2020] show that glucose modulates neural markers (P3b, late-positive ERP, hippocampal/parahippocampal and prefrontal activation) in 10 of 11 studies, but only 5 of 11 showed any behavioural change, i.e. biomarker effects often occur without measurable cognitive benefit. Dose matters: the inverted-U with an optimum near 25 g holds mainly for long-term memory tasks and is moderated by individual glucoregulation and body weight [sunramlea2011]; 60 g alone did not outperform placebo on multitasking in the largest trial (n = 150), whereas 60 g glucose plus 40 mg caffeine did, and the authors could not exclude a caffeine-only effect [scholey2014]. Effects on attention and sustained attention are inconsistent: present in some fasted older adults [gagnon2010; macpherson2015], absent or sub-threshold in young adults (Birnie et al.: no significant treatment effect, d ~0.56 on the harder task only, n = 16) [birnie2015], and 25 g glucose impaired memory at 60 min while improving speed at 15 min in one macronutrient comparison [jones2012]. Mood/alertness is generally unaffected by glucose alone [sunramlea2011; scholey2014]. In whom. Most positive studies used healthy students tested after an overnight fast; the effect is larger and more consistent in older adults and in people with poorer glucoregulation [riby2004; vanderzwaluw2015; lamport2009], a pattern that indicates correction of a relative shortfall rather than enhancement of an optimised brain. Glycaemic index/load of meals. Early open-label or incompletely blinded trials reported late-postprandial advantages of low-GI breakfasts in young adults [benton2003] and middle-aged adults [nilsson2009; nilsson2012], but the best-blinded trials using taste-matched sucrose vs isomaltulose products found no cognitive differences at 30-140 min despite confirmed glycaemic separation [dye2010; keesing2019; marchand2020; deng2021]. The 2022 meta-analysis of 15 adult trials [gaylor2022] found no effect of GL up to 119 min and only a marginal benefit of low GL for immediate episodic memory after 120 min (SMD 0.16, 95% CI -0.00 to 0.32, p = 0.05, I2 = 5%), larger in younger adults and those with better glucose tolerance; a pooled estimate of children's breakfast trials was null for immediate memory (ES 0.13, 95% CI -0.11 to 0.37), delayed memory and attention [alvarezbueno2019]. Chronic exposure. A 6-month fully provided low- vs high-GL energy-restricted diet produced no difference in any cognitive test, though mood (POMS depression) worsened on the high-GL diet [cheatham2009]. Cohorts linking sugar or sugar-sweetened beverages to dementia disagree: UK Biobank (n = 210,832) found small positive associations [zhang2024]; Framingham found no SSB association (artificially sweetened drinks were associated) [pase2017]; a pooled analysis of six US cohorts in older adults (n = 10,974) found no association (HR per serving/week 0.99, 95% CI 0.98-1.01) [chen2025]; two meta-analyses reached opposite conclusions with high heterogeneity [liu2022; sun2022]. How large and how reproducible. The acute effect, when present, is a fraction of a standard deviation on a word list (roughly one to two extra words) and lasts tens of minutes; it is detected mainly under fasting conditions and difficult tasks, and the field is characterised by small samples (n = 10-40), single research groups, flexible outcome batteries and high risk of bias. What is unknown. Whether any benefit exists in a non-fasted student after a normal breakfast; whether repeated use produces tolerance or rebound; standardised dose-response in people with normal glucose regulation; and whether low-GI meals help real-world study or work performance over weeks.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "The brain needs roughly 100-130 g of glucose per day (EFSA 2011 basis for the authorised carbohydrate claim), and genuine deprivation (hypoglycaemia, prolonged fasting, severe carbohydrate restriction in unadapted people) impairs cognition. Nearly all positive 'glucose facilitation' trials tested people after an overnight or 2-h fast, and the effect is larger in older adults and in people with poorer glucoregulation [riby2004; lamport2009; vanderzwaluw2015]. This is the signature of correcting a transient relative shortfall, not of enhancing an adequately fuelled brain. In fed, adequately nourished young adults, the well-blinded glycaemic-response trials are null [dye2010; keesing2019; marchand2020; deng2021] and a 3-week ketogenic diet in healthy adults (seen in search, not extracted) did not impair cognition either, indicating a wide tolerance range. Brain Food should therefore frame carbohydrate as 'avoid running on empty' (regular meals, a breakfast before exams), not as a cognitive enhancer.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Marketing of 'glucose for the brain', 'dextrose tablets for exams' and 'energy drinks for focus' rests on the overnight-fasted laboratory model and on caffeine co-administration [kennedy2004; scholey2014]. The European Commission explicitly refused five EFSA-substantiated glucose claims (Regulation (EU) 2015/8, upheld by the CJEU in C-296/16 P) because they would 'convey a conflicting and confusing message' encouraging sugar consumption; the only authorised EU brain claim is for carbohydrates generally and may be used only on low-sugar / no-added-sugar foods, never on 100% sugar products. EFSA (2022) concluded that added and free sugars 'should be as low as possible'. A product that delivers 25 g of free sugar per serving for a cognitive claim is scientifically weak, regulatorily blocked in the EU and reputationally risky. The defensible message is the opposite one: stable glycaemia via low-GI whole-food carbohydrate, with honest acknowledgement that the acute cognitive advantage of low GI is small, late and inconsistent.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Dietary carbohydrate is digested to glucose, which crosses the blood-brain barrier by GLUT1 and is the brain's main oxidative substrate (approximately 20% of resting energy expenditure). Proposed mechanisms for acute facilitation include increased glucose availability in the hippocampus during demanding tasks, enhanced acetylcholine synthesis, insulin signalling, peripheral mechanisms (vagal/adrenal) and K-ATP channel modulation; proposed mechanisms for low-GI benefit include avoidance of the late-postprandial glucose trough and improved insulin sensitivity. Human neuroimaging shows task-related changes after glucose but with weak coupling to behaviour.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Ingestion of glucose/carbohydrate -> rise in blood glucose", "detail": "25-75 g glucose drinks raise capillary glucose within 15-30 min; low-GI foods (isomaltulose, guar-enriched bread) blunt the peak and the subsequent trough (confirmed by CGM/iAUC in trials).", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Blood glucose -> brain uptake via GLUT1/GLUT3", "detail": "Facilitated transport, saturable; brain uptake rises modestly with hyperglycaemia; brain is the dominant glucose consumer at rest.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Demanding cognitive tasks -> local extracellular glucose depletion in hippocampus", "detail": "Microdialysis in rats shows hippocampal glucose falls during spatial working-memory tasks and is prevented by glucose administration (Gold/McNay).", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Glucose -> acetylcholine synthesis / insulin signalling -> enhanced encoding and retrieval", "detail": "Glucose increases ACh release in rat hippocampus; insulin modulates synaptic plasticity; proposed but unproven in humans.", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Glucose -> modulation of neural activity markers (P3b, late-positive ERP, hippocampal/parahippocampal fMRI activation, prefrontal fNIRS)", "detail": "10 of 11 neuroimaging studies showed glucose-related changes, only 5 showed behavioural change [peters2020].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Glucose -> improved immediate/long-term verbal recall in fasted participants", "detail": "Pooled SMD 0.61 for immediate word recall in parallel-group trials [garcia2021]; effect moderated by glucoregulation, dose and task demand.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Low-GI meal -> avoidance of late-postprandial dip -> better late-morning memory/attention", "detail": "Marginal pooled effect after 120 min (SMD 0.16) [gaylor2022]; absent in best-blinded trials.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Chronic high added sugar -> insulin resistance, vascular damage, amyloid -> cognitive decline", "detail": "Cross-sectional link between high-glycaemic diet and cerebral amyloid [taylor2017]; cohort associations inconsistent [zhang2024; chen2025].", "evidence_level": "speculative"}], "pathways": ["Brain energy supply (oxidative glucose metabolism)", "Hippocampal glucose availability under cognitive load", "Cholinergic neurotransmission", "Insulin signalling", "Peripheral (vagal/adrenomedullary) arousal pathways", "Glycaemic variability and late-postprandial hypoglycaemic dip", "Long-term cardiometabolic/vascular health"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy students/young adults aged ~18-35, tested after overnight or 2-h fast", "dose_or_intake": "25 g glucose drink (range 15-75 g); inverted-U with optimum ~25 g for long-term memory", "duration": "Single dose; testing 15-60 min after drink", "grade": "B", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Improved immediate and long-term verbal recall in several small crossover/parallel trials; meta-analysis significant only for immediate word recall in parallel designs; many trials null or domain-specific; effect depends on fasting state and task difficulty.", "effect_estimate": "Immediate word recall SMD 0.61 (95% CI 0.20-1.02, I2 0%, parallel-group subset) [garcia2021]; most other outcomes not pooled; individual trials report ~1-2 extra words recalled.", "certainty_notes": "Downgraded from A for high risk of bias (24/37 trials), inconsistency across tasks and studies, small samples, single-group dominance and indirectness (fasted laboratory state rather than real study conditions). B is the ceiling for the specific outcome 'immediate/long-term verbal recall after fasting'.", "key_study_ids": ["garcia2021", "gillespie2023", "foster1998", "benton1994", "sunramlea2011", "smith2011"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy young adults, fasted", "dose_or_intake": "25-60 g glucose", "duration": "Single dose; 15-90 min", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Rapid information processing and Stroop effects reported in 1994 only when blood glucose was rising; later trials show no significant effect on sustained attention (SART) or multitasking with glucose alone; attention/speed gain at 15 min reversed by 60 min in one study.", "effect_estimate": "Birnie 2015: no significant treatment effect, d = 0.56 accuracy on demanding SART (n = 16, not significant); Scholey 2014: 25 g and 60 g glucose not different from placebo on multitasking score (n = 150).", "certainty_notes": "Small, inconsistent, exploratory; caffeine confound in energy-drink studies.", "key_study_ids": ["benton1994", "birnie2015", "scholey2014", "jones2012"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy young adults", "dose_or_intake": "25 g glucose", "duration": "Single dose", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "No facilitation of digit span in Foster 1998; working memory enhanced by protein rather than glucose in Jones 2012; Serial Sevens (effortful arithmetic) improved by glucose in Kennedy 2000.", "effect_estimate": "not reported as standardized effects; Serial Sevens significant improvement (n ~ 20).", "certainty_notes": "Task-dependent, small single-lab studies.", "key_study_ids": ["foster1998", "kennedy2000", "jones2012"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults", "dose_or_intake": "15-60 g glucose", "duration": "Single dose", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Glucose alone did not change mood in dose-response and multitasking trials; all macronutrient drinks increased self-rated alertness equally (expectancy/ingestion effect).", "effect_estimate": "No significant treatment effects on Bond-Lader mood scales [sunramlea2011; scholey2014].", "certainty_notes": "Consistent null across two reasonably sized trials.", "key_study_ids": ["sunramlea2011", "scholey2014", "jones2012"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "cognitive_fatigue", "population": "high_cognitive_workload", "population_detail": "Healthy adults during 60+ min of repeated demanding tasks", "dose_or_intake": "60-68 g carbohydrate + 33-46 mg caffeine ('energy drink'), or 60 g glucose + 40 mg caffeine", "duration": "Single dose; 10-70 min", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Caffeine-glucose combinations improved RVIP accuracy and reduced mental fatigue, and improved multitasking vs placebo and vs 60 g glucose alone; authors could not rule out caffeine as the sole active ingredient.", "effect_estimate": "Scholey 2014: caffeine-glucose multitasking change +1469 vs placebo +632 (score units), p < 0.05; mood unaffected.", "certainty_notes": "Confounded by caffeine; cannot be attributed to glucose. See caffeine monograph.", "key_study_ids": ["kennedy2004", "scholey2014"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Healthy adults 60-80 y, fasted; some with subjective memory complaints", "dose_or_intake": "25-50 g glucose; 100 g sucrose", "duration": "Single dose", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Narrative review of 20 studies suggests episodic-memory benefit, strongest in those with poorer glucoregulation; the largest RCT (n = 43) found no effect of 50 g glucose on any domain, while 100 g sucrose improved attention/processing speed only in poorer glucose recoverers.", "effect_estimate": "van der Zwaluw 2014: sucrose attention/speed domain z 0.06 vs placebo -0.08 (p = 0.04); glucose no effect.", "certainty_notes": "Small studies, inconsistent; benefit tracks poor glucoregulation (deficiency-correction pattern). Not transferable to young adults.", "key_study_ids": ["riby2004", "vanderzwaluw2014", "vanderzwaluw2015", "gagnon2010", "macpherson2015"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Healthy adults >= 60 y after 12-h fast", "dose_or_intake": "50 g glucose vs saccharin", "duration": "Single dose", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Faster Stroop switching and smaller dual-task cost after glucose in a between-subjects study (n = 44); dual-task tracking improved in older but not younger adults in a crossover (n = 48).", "effect_estimate": "p < 0.01 (Stroop switching), p < 0.05 (dual-task cost); effect sizes not reported.", "certainty_notes": "Two small studies; fasting-induced deficit likely.", "key_study_ids": ["gagnon2010", "macpherson2015"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy young adults consuming breakfasts/beverages differing in GI/GL", "dose_or_intake": "Low-GI vs high-GI breakfast or 50 g isomaltulose vs 50 g sucrose/glucose", "duration": "Single meal; testing 30-240 min", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Early trials found late-morning memory benefit of low-GI breakfast; double-blind taste-matched trials (n = 24-70) found no memory differences at 30-140 min; meta-analysis: no effect < 120 min and marginal benefit >= 120 min.", "effect_estimate": "Pooled SMD 0.16 (95% CI -0.00 to 0.32, p = 0.05, I2 5%) for immediate episodic memory after 120 min [gaylor2022]; Keesing 2019 word recall difference -0.5 to 0.4 words (CIs crossing 0); Marchand 2020 recall differences 0.0 (95% CI -0.6, 0.5).", "certainty_notes": "Precise null results from the best-blinded trials; positive findings concentrated in less-blinded designs and late time points. Benton (meta-analysis senior author) has industry-funded GI work.", "key_study_ids": ["gaylor2022", "benton2003", "keesing2019", "marchand2020", "deng2021", "dye2010", "sanchezaguadero2020", "philippou2014"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy middle-aged/older adults (49-71 y) and 51 +/- 7 y drivers", "dose_or_intake": "Low- vs high-GI breakfast; low- vs high-GL meal", "duration": "Single meal; 75-225 min", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Selective attention better after guar-enriched bread 75-225 min post-meal (Nilsson 2012) and after sipped vs bolus glucose at 170 min (Nilsson 2009); no effect of meal GL on 90-min simulated driving (n = 10).", "effect_estimate": "Nilsson 2012: P < 0.01 for selective attention; Bragg 2017: no difference in SDLP between HGL and LGL.", "certainty_notes": "Single Swedish lab for positives; very small driving study.", "key_study_ids": ["nilsson2012", "nilsson2009", "bragg2017"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "deficient_or_low_baseline", "population_detail": "Middle-aged obese women with impaired glucose tolerance", "dose_or_intake": "Low-GL vs high-GL breakfast vs water", "duration": "Single meal; 30 and 120 min", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "IGT and central obesity were each associated with cognitive impairment; a low-GL breakfast attenuated verbal memory impairment only in the IGT/high-waist subgroup (n = 9).", "effect_estimate": "not reported (subgroup n = 9)", "certainty_notes": "Tiny subgroup; hypothesis-generating; supports deficiency-correction model.", "key_study_ids": ["lamport2014", "lamport2009"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "children_adolescents", "population_detail": "School children 6-16 y (kept separate; not transferable to adults)", "dose_or_intake": "Low-GI vs high-GI breakfast", "duration": "Single breakfast; 1-3 h", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Individual trials report less decline in attention/memory through the morning after low-GI cereal and better verbal memory after low-GI meals, but pooled estimates are null for immediate memory, delayed memory and attention.", "effect_estimate": "Pooled ES immediate memory 0.13 (95% CI -0.11, 0.37); delayed memory 0.07 (-0.15, 0.28); attention -0.01 (-0.27, 0.26) [alvarezbueno2019].", "certainty_notes": "Few small trials, inconsistent domains; Gaylor 2022 qualitative synthesis more positive after 120 min.", "key_study_ids": ["alvarezbueno2019", "ingwersen2007", "micha2011", "gaylor2022"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "alertness", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults after lunch (post-lunch dip)", "dose_or_intake": "Large (922 kcal) vs light (305 kcal) lunch; high-carbohydrate or high-fat vs mixed lunch", "duration": "Single meal; 0-3 h", "grade": "C", "direction": "harm", "effect_summary": "The post-lunch dip is primarily circadian and occurs without lunch, but a heavy lunch increased sleepiness-related lane drifting and EEG theta/alpha power after 30 min of monotonous driving in sleep-restricted young men; isocaloric lunches with unusually high carbohydrate or fat slowed reaction time and increased drowsiness versus a habitual mixed lunch.", "effect_estimate": "Reyner 2012: significant increase in incidents and EEG 4-11 Hz power with heavy lunch (n = 12); Lloyd 1994: longer RT after LFHC and HFLC lunches (n = 18).", "certainty_notes": "Small studies; effect is of meal size/composition on sleepiness, not of glucose per se; relevant practical advice for afternoon study.", "key_study_ids": ["monk2005", "reyner2012", "lloyd1994"]}, {"timeframe": "short_term_days", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Healthy adults 50-65 y, free-living, non-diabetic", "dose_or_intake": "Commercial low-GI snack vs control snack twice daily", "duration": "3 consecutive days per arm", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Low-GI snack blunted postprandial glucose but did not improve cognition; poorer glucoregulatory status was associated with worse cognitive response to the low-GI snack.", "effect_estimate": "No main effect; interaction with HbA1c/fasting glucose p < 0.01 for spatial memory.", "certainty_notes": "Single decentralized trial, n = 28; snack purchased at retail, authors employed by institute linked to manufacturer spin-off.", "key_study_ids": ["mohapatra2025"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy overweight adults ~35 y on provided energy-restricted diets", "dose_or_intake": "Low-GL vs high-GL diet (all food provided)", "duration": "6 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "No change over time in reaction time, vigilance, learning, short-term memory, attention or logical reasoning in either group; POMS depression worsened on the high-GL diet.", "effect_estimate": "No significant cognitive differences (n = 42); depression subscale p = 0.009 favouring low GL.", "certainty_notes": "Only chronic RCT found; small, weight-loss context.", "key_study_ids": ["cheatham2009"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "dementia_risk", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Middle-aged and older community adults (cohorts)", "dose_or_intake": "Habitual sugar / sugar-sweetened beverage intake", "duration": "10-16 years follow-up", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "UK Biobank: small positive associations of absolute and relative sugar intake with all-cause dementia; Framingham: SSB not associated (ASB associated); six pooled US cohorts of older adults: no association for SSB or ASB; meta-analyses conflict (OR 1.17 with I2 90% vs non-significant OR 1.59).", "effect_estimate": "Zhang 2024: HR 1.003 per g/day (95% CI 1.002-1.004); relative sugar HR 1.317 (1.173-1.480); Chen 2025: HR 0.99 per serving/week (0.98-1.01), highest vs lowest 0.90 (0.78-1.03); Liu 2022: OR 1.17 (1.05-1.29).", "certainty_notes": "Observational, confounded by overall diet quality, reverse causation possible; heterogeneity high. Cannot exceed C.", "key_study_ids": ["zhang2024", "pase2017", "chen2025", "liu2022", "sun2022", "gillespie2023"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_aging", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Dementia-free Swedish twins >= 50 y", "dose_or_intake": "Diet-based glycaemic load; fasting blood glucose", "duration": "Up to 16 years", "grade": "C", "direction": "harm", "effect_summary": "Higher blood glucose predicted faster decline in general cognition, perceptual speed, verbal and spatial ability; higher dietary GL related to poorer perceptual speed and spatial ability.", "effect_estimate": "Mixed-model coefficients significant; standardized effects not reported in abstract.", "certainty_notes": "Observational; GL estimated from FFQ.", "key_study_ids": ["seetharaman2015"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "neuroplasticity_biomarkers", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Cognitively normal older adults (n = 128), cross-sectional", "dose_or_intake": "High-glycaemic diet pattern, sugar and carbohydrate intake", "duration": "Habitual diet", "grade": "D", "direction": "harm", "effect_summary": "High-glycaemic diet pattern positively associated with cerebral amyloid burden on florbetapir PET; higher sugar intake associated with poorer global cognition.", "effect_estimate": "HGLDiet-amyloid association P <= 0.001 (adjusted for age, sex, education).", "certainty_notes": "Cross-sectional biomarker; cannot infer causation.", "key_study_ids": ["taylor2017"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "sleep_related_cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy young men (n = 20)", "dose_or_intake": "50 g isomaltulose (low GI) vs 50 g glucose (high GI) drink 4 h after dinner", "duration": "Single night", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No main effect of drink on sleep or memory consolidation; more N3 sleep and better story retention after the low-GI drink only in one order of administration.", "effect_estimate": "N3 28.7% vs 24.0% (p = 0.032) and retention 0.63 vs -10.13 words (p = 0.002) in one order only.", "certainty_notes": "Order-dependent subgroup result; funded and co-designed by BENEO (isomaltulose manufacturer).", "key_study_ids": ["gaylor2025"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "other", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Neuroimaging/EEG markers (biomarkers, not outcomes)", "dose_or_intake": "17-75 g glucose", "duration": "Single dose", "grade": "D", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Glucose modulated ERP components, resting EEG power, hippocampal/parahippocampal and prefrontal activation in 10 of 11 studies, with behavioural improvement in only 5.", "effect_estimate": "Qualitative; n = 7-35 per study.", "certainty_notes": "Surrogate markers; dissociation from behaviour is itself informative.", "key_study_ids": ["peters2020"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "students", "population_detail": "Students/knowledge workers using glucose or sugar products daily for study", "dose_or_intake": "Repeated 25-50 g glucose", "duration": "Weeks", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No trial has tested repeated daily glucose dosing for cognition in students or knowledge workers; tolerance, rebound and metabolic cost are unstudied.", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "Evidence gap; chronic added-sugar harms (dental caries, weight, cardiometabolic risk) are established independently of cognition (EFSA 2022).", "key_study_ids": ["garcia2021", "gillespie2023"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "25 g glucose drink (optimum in dose-response; 15-60 g tested)", "onset": "15-30 min (peak blood glucose ~30 min)", "duration_studied": "15-60 min post-drink (one study up to 2 h)", "domain": "Long-term verbal free/cued recall; immediate word recall", "population": "Healthy young adults, overnight or 2-h fasted", "effect_estimate": "SMD 0.61 (95% CI 0.20-1.02) immediate word recall in parallel-group studies [garcia2021]; dose-response inverted U for long-term memory [sunramlea2011]", "certainty": "moderate-low (B/C): small trials, high risk of bias, fasting-dependent", "safety": "Food-grade; 25 g = half the FDA added-sugars Daily Value; dental and glycaemic exposure if repeated", "study_ids": ["garcia2021", "foster1998", "benton1994", "sunramlea2011", "gillespie2023"]}, {"dose": "25 g glucose", "onset": "15 min", "duration_studied": "15 and 60 min", "domain": "Attention/speed (15 min) then memory impairment (60 min)", "population": "Healthy young adults (n = 18), crossover vs fat, protein, placebo", "effect_estimate": "Attention and speed improved at 15 min; memory impaired at 60 min after glucose; protein improved working memory", "certainty": "low", "safety": "None reported", "study_ids": ["jones2012"]}, {"dose": "25 g, 60 g glucose; 60 g glucose + 40 mg caffeine", "onset": "30 min", "duration_studied": "30-60 min", "domain": "Multitasking composite, mental arithmetic, mood", "population": "150 healthy adults (mean 34.8 y), parallel groups, overnight fasted", "effect_estimate": "Glucose alone not different from placebo; caffeine-glucose better than placebo and 60 g glucose; no mood effect", "certainty": "moderate for null of glucose alone (largest trial)", "safety": "None reported", "study_ids": ["scholey2014"]}, {"dose": "25 g glucose vs placebo", "onset": "~20 min", "duration_studied": "Within session", "domain": "Sustained attention (SART), task-unrelated thought", "population": "16 healthy adults", "effect_estimate": "No significant treatment effect; d = 0.56 accuracy on demanding version (ns)", "certainty": "very low", "safety": "None reported", "study_ids": ["birnie2015"]}, {"dose": "Glucose drink (approx. 25 g) vs placebo", "onset": "~15 min", "duration_studied": "Within session", "domain": "Serial Sevens (high effort), Serial Threes, verbal fluency; heart rate", "population": "Healthy young adults, crossover", "effect_estimate": "Serial Sevens improved significantly; trend for fluency; no effect on easy Serial Threes", "certainty": "low", "safety": "Higher heart rate during tasks on glucose", "study_ids": ["kennedy2000"]}, {"dose": "60-68 g carbohydrate + 33-46 mg caffeine", "onset": "10 min", "duration_studied": "10-70 min of repeated tasks", "domain": "RVIP accuracy; mental fatigue", "population": "26-30 healthy adults per study", "effect_estimate": "All active drinks improved RVIP accuracy vs placebo; fatigue ratings lower", "certainty": "low (caffeine confound)", "safety": "Caffeine-related effects; see caffeine monograph", "study_ids": ["kennedy2004"]}, {"dose": "25 g glucose vs saccharin", "onset": "~15 min", "duration_studied": "Within session", "domain": "Episodic vs semantic memory under low/high load", "population": "Younger and older adults", "effect_estimate": "Episodic memory aided; load did not amplify; semantic memory unaffected except difficult category fluency", "certainty": "low", "safety": "None reported", "study_ids": ["riby2006"]}, {"dose": "25 g glucose", "onset": "~15 min", "duration_studied": "Within session", "domain": "Recognition memory RT and dual-task tracking", "population": "24 younger (20.6 y) and 24 older (72.5 y) adults", "effect_estimate": "Benefit in older adults only", "certainty": "low", "safety": "None reported", "study_ids": ["macpherson2015"]}, {"dose": "50 g glucose vs saccharin after 12-h fast", "onset": "~15 min", "duration_studied": "Within session", "domain": "Stroop switching, dual-task cost", "population": "44 healthy adults >= 60 y, between-subjects", "effect_estimate": "Faster switching (p < 0.01), smaller dual-task cost (p < 0.05)", "certainty": "low", "safety": "None reported", "study_ids": ["gagnon2010"]}, {"dose": "50 g glucose; 100 g sucrose; placebo (250 ml)", "onset": "Minutes", "duration_studied": "Within session (5 glucose time points)", "domain": "Episodic memory, working memory, attention/speed, executive", "population": "43 non-diabetic older adults with subjective memory complaints", "effect_estimate": "Glucose: no effect on any domain; sucrose: attention/speed z 0.06 vs -0.08 (p = 0.04), only in poorer recoverers; slightly more tension/depression after sucrose", "certainty": "low-moderate for glucose null", "safety": "Mild mood worsening after 100 g sucrose", "study_ids": ["vanderzwaluw2014"]}, {"dose": "Low-GI vs high-GI breakfast vs water", "onset": "60 min", "duration_studied": "0, 60, 120 min", "domain": "Verbal memory, fluency, attention, executive", "population": "40 healthy adults 20-40 y, crossover", "effect_estimate": "Interaction for immediate verbal memory (P < .05); overall 'minimally affected'", "certainty": "low", "safety": "None", "study_ids": ["sanchezaguadero2020"]}, {"dose": "Trifle with sucrose (GI 65) vs isomaltulose (GI 34)", "onset": "60 min", "duration_studied": "60 and 120 min", "domain": "Word recall (free, short/long delay), letter-number sequencing, visuospatial recall, TMT-B", "population": "65 young adults, double-blind crossover", "effect_estimate": "All memory differences 0.0 (95% CI about -0.6 to 0.6); TMT-B 7.7 s faster after higher-GI trifle at 60 min (p = 0.037)", "certainty": "moderate (precise null)", "safety": "None", "study_ids": ["marchand2020"]}, {"dose": "Beverage with sucrose (GI 65) vs isomaltulose+sucralose (GI 32)", "onset": "30 min", "duration_studied": "30, 80, 130, 140 min", "domain": "Declarative memory, immediate word recall, TMT-B", "population": "70 healthy adults ~22 y, crossover", "effect_estimate": "Word recall differences -0.5 (-1.4, 0.3), 0.4 (-0.4, 1.3), -0.4 (-1.1, 0.4); TMT-B -0.3 s (-6.9, 6.3); glucose iAUC differed by 44 mmol/L.min", "certainty": "moderate (precise null)", "safety": "None", "study_ids": ["keesing2019"]}, {"dose": "Sucrose vs isomaltulose vs sucralose drinks", "onset": "60 min", "duration_studied": "60 min", "domain": "Complex figure, word recall, TMT-B, Stroop, mood, satiety", "population": "55 healthy adults, double-blind crossover", "effect_estimate": "No significant differences (e.g. immediate word recall 0.2 (-0.7, 1.1)); more 'energetic' and hungrier after isomaltulose", "certainty": "moderate (null)", "safety": "None", "study_ids": ["deng2021"]}, {"dose": "Milk drink with 50 g isomaltulose vs 50 g sucrose vs water", "onset": "35 min", "duration_studied": "35 and 115 min", "domain": "Immediate/delayed/recognition/verbal/working memory, psychomotor", "population": "24 healthy men, within-subject; CGM in 12", "effect_estimate": "No consistent effects despite confirmed glycaemic separation", "certainty": "low-moderate (null)", "safety": "None", "study_ids": ["dye2010"]}, {"dose": "Guar-enriched white bread vs white bread breakfast", "onset": "75 min", "duration_studied": "75-225 min", "domain": "Selective attention, working memory", "population": "40 healthy adults 49-71 y, crossover", "effect_estimate": "Selective attention better after guar bread (P < 0.01) late postprandially", "certainty": "low", "safety": "None", "study_ids": ["nilsson2012"]}, {"dose": "50 g glucose as bolus (simulated high GI) vs sipped (simulated low GI)", "onset": "35 min", "duration_studied": "35-170 min", "domain": "Working memory, selective attention", "population": "40 healthy adults 49-70 y", "effect_estimate": "Better WM at 90 min (P = 0.034) and SA at 170 min (P = 0.017) after sipping, after adjusting for glucose tolerance", "certainty": "low", "safety": "None", "study_ids": ["nilsson2009"]}, {"dose": "Low- vs high-GI breakfast", "onset": "150 min", "duration_studied": "Up to 240 min", "domain": "Verbal memory", "population": "Young adults (and rats)", "effect_estimate": "Better memory at 150 and 210 min after low-GI breakfast", "certainty": "low (industry co-authors)", "safety": "None", "study_ids": ["benton2003"]}, {"dose": "Heavy (922 kcal) vs light (305 kcal) lunch after 5-h sleep", "onset": "30 min into 2-h drive", "duration_studied": "2 h", "domain": "Sleepiness-related lane drifting, EEG, subjective sleepiness", "population": "12 young male drivers", "effect_estimate": "Significant increase in incidents and EEG 4-11 Hz power after heavy lunch", "certainty": "low", "safety": "Driving safety relevance", "study_ids": ["reyner2012"]}, {"dose": "Low- vs high-GL breakfast (+/- 3 mg/kg caffeine)", "onset": "Within 90 min", "duration_studied": "90-min simulated drive", "domain": "Lane position variability, speed, sleepiness", "population": "10 non-diabetic adults ~51 y", "effect_estimate": "No HGL vs LGL difference; caffeine reduced SDLP (0.36 vs 0.41 m, p = 0.004)", "certainty": "very low", "safety": "None", "study_ids": ["bragg2017"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "6 months", "population": "42 healthy overweight adults (35 +/- 5 y)", "outcome": "Reaction time, vigilance, learning, short-term memory, attention, logical reasoning; POMS mood", "effect_estimate": "No significant change in any cognitive measure; POMS depression worse on high-GL diet (p = 0.009)", "evidence_quality": "C (single small RCT, weight loss context)", "limitations": "Energy restriction confound; all food provided (not free-living)", "study_ids": ["cheatham2009"]}, {"duration": "3 days per arm", "population": "28 healthy adults 50-65 y", "outcome": "Mobile cognitive battery 6x/day; CGM", "effect_estimate": "No main effect of low-GI snack; worse response in poorer glucoregulators", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "Short, small, commercial snack", "study_ids": ["mohapatra2025"]}, {"duration": "Mean 11.8 y", "population": "210,832 UK Biobank adults (mean 56 y)", "outcome": "All-cause dementia, Alzheimer's disease", "effect_estimate": "HR 1.003 per g/day sugar (1.002-1.004); relative sugar HR 1.317 (1.173-1.480); non-linear", "evidence_quality": "C (observational)", "limitations": "Oxford WebQ dietary recall, residual confounding, 'high-sugar pattern' included fresh fruit", "study_ids": ["zhang2024"]}, {"duration": "10 y", "population": "Framingham Offspring (1,484 for dementia, > 60 y)", "outcome": "Incident dementia, stroke", "effect_estimate": "SSB not associated; artificially sweetened drinks HR 2.89 (1.18-7.07) for AD", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "Few events (81 dementia); FFQ", "study_ids": ["pase2017"]}, {"duration": "116,067 person-years", "population": "10,974 US adults >= 65 y (6 cohorts)", "outcome": "Incident all-cause dementia", "effect_estimate": "SSB HR 0.99 per serving/week (0.98-1.01); highest vs lowest 0.90 (0.78-1.03)", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "Late-life intake only; survivor bias; FFQ", "study_ids": ["chen2025"]}, {"duration": "Up to 16 y", "population": "838 dementia-free Swedish adults >= 50 y", "outcome": "Trajectories of general cognition, perceptual speed, memory, verbal, spatial", "effect_estimate": "Higher blood glucose: faster decline; higher dietary GL: poorer perceptual speed and spatial ability", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "FFQ-derived GL; observational", "study_ids": ["seetharaman2015"]}, {"duration": "Cross-sectional", "population": "128 cognitively normal older adults", "outcome": "Cerebral amyloid (PET), cognition", "effect_estimate": "High-glycaemic diet pattern associated with amyloid (P <= 0.001); sugar intake with poorer global cognition", "evidence_quality": "D (biomarker, cross-sectional)", "limitations": "No temporality", "study_ids": ["taylor2017"]}, {"duration": "Meta-analyses of cohorts/cross-sectional", "population": "242,014 (Liu) and 10 studies (Sun)", "outcome": "Cognitive disorders/dementia", "effect_estimate": "Liu: OR 1.17 (1.05-1.29), I2 90%; Sun: OR 1.59 (0.93-2.74) ns", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "High heterogeneity, overlapping cohorts, exposure misclassification", "study_ids": ["liu2022", "sun2022"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Glucose and dietary carbohydrates are ordinary foods with no acute toxicity at the doses used in cognition trials (15-75 g). The safety issue is chronic: EFSA (2022, EFSA Journal 20(2):e07074) concluded that no UL can be set but that intake of added and free sugars 'should be as low as possible', that the relationship between sugars and dental caries is well established, and that there is moderate-certainty evidence linking sugars to obesity and dyslipidaemia and low-certainty evidence for NAFLD and type 2 diabetes. WHO (2015) recommends free sugars < 10% of energy (conditionally < 5%); the FDA Daily Value for added sugars is 50 g. A 25 g glucose dose is half of that Daily Value; a 50 g dose is the entire allowance. Rapid high-GI loads cause a late-postprandial glucose trough in susceptible people, and trials show transient mood (tension/depression) worsening after 100 g sucrose and memory impairment at 60 min after 25 g glucose in some designs. Large high-carbohydrate lunches worsen afternoon sleepiness. People with diabetes, prediabetes, reactive hypoglycaemia, or on insulin/sulfonylureas, and children, require specific caution.", "adverse_effects": ["Dental caries with frequent free-sugar exposure (well established, EFSA 2022)", "Weight gain, dyslipidaemia, NAFLD and type 2 diabetes risk with chronic high added-sugar intake (moderate to low certainty, EFSA 2022)", "Late-postprandial glucose dip with transient hunger, fatigue or lower mood after high-GI loads in susceptible individuals", "Mild increases in tension/depressive feelings after 100 g sucrose in older adults [vanderzwaluw2014]", "Memory impairment at 60 min after 25 g glucose in one crossover study [jones2012]", "Post-lunch sleepiness and impaired vigilance after large meals [reyner2012]", "Gastrointestinal discomfort/osmotic effects from concentrated sugar solutions (> 50 g in small volumes)"], "contraindications": ["Diabetes mellitus (type 1 or 2) without medical advice on carbohydrate loading", "Documented reactive (postprandial) hypoglycaemia", "Hereditary fructose intolerance (for sucrose/fructose-containing products)", "Glucose-galactose malabsorption (rare)", "Products marketed to children for 'concentration' (regulatory and ethical concerns)"], "drug_interactions": ["Insulin and insulin secretagogues (sulfonylureas, glinides): dose timing relative to carbohydrate load affects hypo/hyperglycaemia risk", "Acarbose/miglitol: blunt sucrose/starch absorption (glucose itself unaffected)", "SGLT2 inhibitors and GLP-1 agonists alter glycaemic responses", "Caffeine co-formulation produces the observed 'energy drink' effects and carries caffeine's own interactions (see caffeine monograph)"], "upper_limits": "EFSA 2022: no UL could be set for total, added or free sugars; added/free sugars should be as low as possible. WHO 2015: free sugars < 10% energy (strong), < 5% (conditional). US FDA Daily Value for added sugars 50 g/day (2,000 kcal). EFSA 2011/EU Regulation 1018/2013 reference 130 g/day carbohydrate from all sources as the brain's requirement basis.", "sleep_impact": "No evidence that glucose at 25-50 g disrupts sleep; one industry-funded crossover (n = 20) found order-dependent differences in N3 sleep between low- and high-GI evening drinks with no main effect [gaylor2025]. Caffeinated glucose 'energy drinks' impair sleep through caffeine.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Only one chronic RCT of diet GL on cognition (6 months, n = 42, null) [cheatham2009]; cohort studies of sugar/SSB and dementia are conflicting (10-16 y) [zhang2024; pase2017; chen2025]. No long-term trial of repeated glucose supplementation for cognition exists.", "daily_use_appropriate": "uncertain", "special_populations": "Children: GI-breakfast pooled effects null; sugar products for 'concentration' inappropriate given caries/obesity risk. Pregnancy: ordinary dietary carbohydrate fine; added sugars should be limited per general guidance. Older adults with poor glucoregulation: most likely to show acute benefit but also most vulnerable to chronic glycaemic harm. Diabetes/prediabetes: medical supervision. Psychiatric populations: no specific data beyond schizophrenia imaging study.", "specialist_review_needed": ["Registered dietitian (meal/snack GI design, portion sizes)", "Diabetologist/clinician for any positioning toward people with dysglycaemia", "Regulatory affairs (EU 1924/2006 claim conditions; sugar-content thresholds)", "Dental public-health input if sugar-containing formats are considered"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "Glucose/carbohydrate is food-grade and acutely safe, but any product that relies on free sugar (>= 25 g/serving) for a cognitive effect conflicts with EFSA/WHO guidance to keep added and free sugars as low as possible and with dental/cardiometabolic risk; low-GI whole-food carbohydrate formats pass, sugar-based 'brain boost' formats do not."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Authorised Article 13(1) claim (Commission Regulation (EU) No 1018/2013 amending Reg. 432/2012; EFSA Journal 2011;9(6):2226): 'Carbohydrates contribute to the maintenance of normal brain function'. Conditions: consumer must be informed that the beneficial effect is obtained with a daily intake of 130 g of carbohydrates from all sources; the claim may be used only for food containing at least 20 g of carbohydrates metabolised by humans (excluding polyols) per quantified portion and complying with the nutrition claim LOW SUGARS or WITH NO ADDED SUGARS; it shall not be used on food which is 100% sugars (Recital 5 records Member State concern that the claim would encourage sugar consumption). Glucose-specific claims ('Glucose contributes to normal energy-yielding metabolism' and four others, Dextro Energy, EFSA Journal 2012;10(5):2694-2698) were REFUSED by Commission Regulation (EU) 2015/8 despite positive EFSA opinions because they would 'convey a conflicting and confusing message to consumers' and encourage sugar consumption; the refusal was upheld by the General Court (T-100/15) and CJEU (C-296/16 P, 8 June 2017). Sources accessed 2026-10-25: legislation.gov.uk/eur/2013/1018; legislation.gov.uk/eur/2015/8; eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2015/8/oj; twobirds.com and taylorwessing.com CJEU summaries. Nutrient profile rules for sugar remain unadopted but 'LOW SUGARS' (<= 5 g/100 g solid, 2.5 g/100 ml liquid) and 'NO ADDED SUGARS' nutrition-claim definitions apply.", "us": "Glucose (dextrose), sucrose and starches are GRAS/conventional food ingredients; no NDI issue. 'Added Sugars' must be declared on the Nutrition Facts label with a Daily Value of 50 g (fda.gov Added Sugars page, accessed 2026-10-25); Dietary Guidelines for Americans advise < 10% of calories from added sugars. Structure/function statements such as 'carbohydrates fuel the brain' are permissible for conventional foods if truthful and not misleading, but FTC substantiation (competent and reliable scientific evidence) would be hard to meet for 'improves memory/focus' claims given the null trials in fed adults; disease claims (dementia prevention) prohibited.", "morocco": "No Moroccan text specific to glucose/carbohydrate brain claims was found. General food labelling is governed by Decree 2-12-389 (22 April 2013) under Law 28-07 on food safety (ONSSA), which covers mandatory labelling and nutrition labelling; ONSSA proposed (2019) specific warning labels for energy drinks (pregnant/breastfeeding women, diabetics, under-18s). Health-claim authorisation framework status: uncertain; likely to reference Codex/EU practice. Sources accessed 2026-10-25: leap.unep.org record of Decree 2-12-389; medias24.com 15 May 2019 (ONSSA beverages rules). Specialist verification with ONSSA and the Ministry of Health required.", "permitted_claims_examples": ["EU (Reg. 1018/2013): 'Carbohydrates contribute to the maintenance of normal brain function' - only on foods with >= 20 g metabolisable carbohydrate per portion that qualify as LOW SUGARS or WITH NO ADDED SUGARS, with the 130 g/day statement; not on 100% sugar foods", "EU nutrition claims (Reg. 1924/2006 Annex): 'LOW SUGARS', 'SUGARS-FREE', 'WITH NO ADDED SUGARS' where conditions met", "US: factual 'Added Sugars 0 g' labelling; general structure/function statement about carbohydrates as an energy source for the body (truthful, not misleading)"], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "None for glucose, sucrose, starches. Isomaltulose (Palatinose) is an authorised EU novel food (since 2005) and GRAS in the US; allulose and some rare sugars have differing status (allulose not authorised as novel food in EU as of last check; uncertain). No NDI issue in the US for common sugars.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Excellent: carbohydrate is the base of ordinary foods. The evidence-consistent format is a low-GI, low-added-sugar meal or snack (whole grains, oats, legumes, dairy, fruit, nuts) that supplies >= 20 g metabolisable carbohydrate per portion with slow delivery; this also matches the only EU-authorised brain claim conditions. Glucose/dextrose drinks and tablets are easy to make but scientifically weak, regulatorily blocked in the EU and reputationally risky.", "formats": ["Low-GI breakfast cereals/porridges (oats, barley, intact grains)", "Whole-grain or guar/beta-glucan-enriched breads", "Legume-based snacks", "Fruit-and-nut bars without added sugar", "Dairy-based drinks with intact carbohydrate (lactose) and protein", "Isomaltulose-sweetened beverages (low GI; evidence of cognitive benefit null)", "Dextrose tablets/drinks (not recommended for a science-first brand)"], "stability": "Glucose and sucrose are chemically stable; Maillard browning with amino acids at high temperature; sucrose hydrolyses at low pH/heat (inversion) raising GI slightly; starch retrogradation on cooling lowers GI (resistant starch); isomaltulose is acid- and heat-stable. Light and oxidation are not issues for sugars; whole-grain lipids can oxidise.", "taste": "Sweetness drives palatability; low-GI formats taste less sweet and may need acceptance work; isomaltulose ~50% sweetness of sucrose.", "bioavailability_matrix": "GI is a matrix property: fibre (guar, beta-glucan), intact grain structure, protein and fat co-ingestion, acidity (vinegar), particle size and cooling all slow glucose delivery. Beverages produce the fastest rise; solid mixed meals the slowest. Fructose and isomaltulose lower the glycaemic response. Co-ingested caffeine, not glucose, is the likely driver of 'energy drink' cognitive effects.", "dose_feasibility": "25 g glucose (the acute-trial optimum) fits easily in a drink but equals half the US added-sugars Daily Value and would preclude EU LOW SUGARS status. 20 g metabolisable carbohydrate per portion (EU claim condition) is trivially met by a 40-60 g serving of oats or bread. The 130 g/day reference is met by any normal mixed diet.", "cost_availability": "Carbohydrate staples are the cheapest macronutrient everywhere, including Morocco (bread, couscous, semolina, legumes, dates). Isomaltulose and specialty fibres cost more and have limited Moroccan availability.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 4, "effect_magnitude": 2, "safety": 6, "reproducibility": 4, "food_suitability": 8, "cost_access": 10, "rationale": "Evidence quality 4: the best outcome (acute immediate/long-term verbal recall after fasting) reaches B, but attention, executive, mood, GI-meal and chronic outcomes are C or lower and the best-blinded recent trials are null. Effect magnitude 2: when present the effect is ~1-2 words on a list for tens of minutes, mainly in fasted people; for a fed student the expected gain is near zero. Safety 6: acutely food-grade, but chronic added sugar carries established dental and probable cardiometabolic harms and EFSA says intake should be as low as possible. Reproducibility 4: many replications from a handful of UK/Australian groups, but independent well-blinded trials (Otago, Leeds/Unilever, Wageningen, Swinburne n = 150) are null or mixed, and meta-analytic signals are narrow. Food suitability 8: carbohydrate is food, and low-GI formats are easy; score reduced because the studied 'effective' format (glucose drink) is the one a science-first brand should not sell. Cost/access 10: universally cheap and available."}, "research_gaps": ["Adequately powered (n >= 100), pre-registered, double-blind trial of 25 g glucose vs taste-matched placebo in NON-fasted students after a standard breakfast, with immediate/delayed recall and sustained attention as pre-specified primary outcomes", "Factorial trial separating caffeine and glucose contributions to 'energy drink' effects with caffeine-only and glucose-only arms", "Multi-week RCT of low-GI vs habitual breakfasts in university students with ecological outcomes (lecture recall, exam performance, CGM-measured glycaemic variability)", "Dose-response and repeated-dosing study examining tolerance/rebound of glucose facilitation over days", "Individual-participant-data meta-analysis of glucose facilitation trials stratified by fasting duration, glucoregulation (HbA1c/OGTT) and task difficulty", "Trials of meal size/composition on afternoon vigilance in knowledge workers with normal prior sleep (the driving study used sleep-restricted men)", "Life-course cohorts with repeated diet measures to resolve the midlife vs late-life sugar-dementia discrepancy [zhang2024 vs chen2025]", "Independent (non-manufacturer) replication of isomaltulose/low-GI evening drink effects on sleep architecture and consolidation"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 5.0, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": "B", "best_grade_chronic": null, "best_grade_young_adults": "B", "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": "D", "best_grade_overall": "B", "benefit_domains": {"episodic_memory": "B", "cognitive_fatigue": "C", "executive_function": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 20, "n_studies": 48, "n_verified": 48, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 20, "n_systematic_reviews": 8, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["harm", "inconsistent", "insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "hydration", "name": "Hydration and mild dehydration", "category": "A", "category_name": "Macronutrients and brain energy", "aliases": ["water", "fluid intake", "euhydration", "hypohydration", "mild dehydration (1-2% body mass loss)", "water supplementation", "fluid restriction"], "food_sources": ["drinking water (tap, bottled, mineral, spring)", "milk and dairy drinks", "tea and coffee (net positive contribution to fluid balance at usual intakes)", "fruit juices and soups", "high-water foods: fruit, vegetables, cooked grains (food contributes roughly 20-30% of total water intake)"], "active_constituents": ["water (H2O) itself; electrolytes (sodium, potassium) and energy content modulate fluid retention but are not the cognitive 'active'"], "one_line_verdict": "Avoiding mild dehydration reliably protects mood, perceived energy and probably attention in healthy adults, but drinking extra water beyond euhydration has not been shown to enhance cognition; the effect is restoration of a baseline, not enhancement.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. The best-controlled human evidence concerns acute hypohydration of roughly 1-2% body mass (BML) induced by exercise, heat, diuretics or 12-36 h fluid restriction in healthy young adults. Across these studies the most reproducible finding is a deterioration of subjective state: increased fatigue, lower vigour, higher total mood disturbance, headache and greater perceived task difficulty, seen in men [ganio2011], women [armstrong2012, pross2013], under 24 h water deprivation [szinnai2005], after 4% BML [ely2013] and even when intracellular dehydration is produced by hypertonic saline without heat or exercise [suh2021]. Objective cognitive effects are smaller and inconsistent. The multivariate meta-analysis of 33 studies (413 subjects, 280 effect sizes) found a small pooled impairment (ES -0.21, 95% CI -0.31 to -0.11; I2 37.6%) that was concentrated in attention (ES -0.52) and executive tasks (ES -0.24), was absent for reaction-time tasks (ES -0.10, CI crossing zero) and was weaker at <=2% BML (ES -0.14, 95% CI -0.27 to 0.00) than at >2% [wittbrodt2018_ma]. A second meta-analysis restricted to active (exercise-induced) hypohydration found no significant overall effect (g -0.177, 95% CI -0.532 to 0.179) and no effect in any sub-domain, with I2 up to 93% [goodman2019_ma]; a 2021 systematic review of 33 studies on executive function concluded that roughly half reported neutral or unclear effects [katz2021_sr]. Individual trials illustrate this split: Ganio found more vigilance errors and slower working-memory responses at 1.6% BML [ganio2011], whereas the matched study in women found 'most aspects of cognitive performance were not affected' at 1.4% BML [armstrong2012]; Ely (4% BML, 10-40 C) [ely2013], Szinnai (2.6% BML) [szinnai2005], Wittbrodt (1.5% BML) [wittbrodt2015] and Deshayes (2-4% BML) [deshayes2022] found no objective cognitive decrement despite worse mood or higher perceived effort. In whom and at what dose. Restoring fluid after restriction improves outcomes mainly in people who are actually hypohydrated or thirsty: in Chinese students after 12-24 h restriction, 500-1000 mL water reduced fatigue and total mood disturbance and improved operation-span and symbol-search scores within 90 min, with 200 mL sufficient for thirst/mood but not for working memory [zhang2020_dose, zhang2021_rct, zhang2019]; a 2-day trial varying amount and frequency of intake changed urine osmolality and vigour but had 'limited impacts on cognitive performance' [he2020_freq]. In euhydrated or mildly thirsty adults, a single drink of water improved letter-cancellation (visual attention) 20 min later, with 25 mL sufficient and effects not explained by expectancy [edmonds2013, edmonds2017], and water prevented the memory and attention decline associated with <1% BML over 4 h at 30 C [benton2016]. Over 3 days, raising habitual low drinkers from 1 L to 2.5 L/day reduced fatigue and confusion, while restricting high drinkers to 1 L reduced vigour, calmness and contentedness [pross2014]. How large. Effects are small (standardized differences around 0.1-0.5 for attention/mood; e.g. 1-2 points on working-memory span, 5-7 points on symbol search, about 6 points of POMS total mood disturbance) and represent return to baseline, not supra-normal performance. How reproducible. Mood/fatigue findings replicate across at least six laboratories and funders; objective cognition does not replicate consistently, and several core trials have industry co-authorship or funding (Danone Research: [ganio2011, armstrong2012, pross2013, pross2014, suh2021]; PepsiCo: [stachenfeld2018]), while several independently funded or military studies are null for cognition [ely2013, szinnai2005, wittbrodt2015, deshayes2022]. Chronic and population evidence. No trial of >=2 weeks of hydration intervention on cognition in healthy young adults was found. In older adults the evidence is observational: higher serum osmolarity predicted a slightly greater 2-year decline in global cognition in 1,192 PREDIMED-Plus participants (beta -0.010 z-score per mmol/L, 95% CI -0.017 to -0.004) while reported water intake did not [nishi2023]; NHANES showed a curvilinear relation between osmolarity and processing speed mainly in women [bethancourt2020]; a 3-month panel study linked dehydration (>300 mOsm/kg) only to poorer sustained attention [rosinger2024]. Unknowns: the dose-response below 1% BML in sedentary temperate-climate adults, sex differences [szinnai2005, suh2021], habituation, effects on real-world study or work output, and whether any beverage formulation offers a cognitive advantage over plain water.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "This is a deficiency-correction story. Every positive cognitive or mood finding comes from reversing or preventing a water deficit (exercise/heat loss, diuretics, 12-36 h restriction, or the small <1% deficits accumulated by low drinkers during a day). Trials that supplemented water in already euhydrated people show at most transient attention effects of uncertain practical value [edmonds2013, edmonds2017], and extra water beyond euhydration does not improve working memory or executive function [zhang2020_dose, he2020_freq]. No study demonstrates that drinking more than the EFSA adequate intake (2.0 L/day women, 2.5 L/day men, from all sources, in moderate climate and activity [efsa2010_drv]) enhances cognition. Separate from this, mood/alertness effects (fatigue, vigour, sleepiness, headache) are more consistent than effects on memory or executive function, and biomarkers such as fMRI BOLD changes [kempton2011] or urine/serum osmolarity are not cognitive outcomes.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "The marketing narrative ('even 1-2% dehydration slashes your focus and memory') overstates a small, inconsistent literature: the pooled impairment is small (ES about -0.2), is weakest at <=2% body-mass loss, is absent for reaction time, and one meta-analysis finds no effect at all for exercise-induced hypohydration [wittbrodt2018_ma, goodman2019_ma]. What is robust is that being noticeably dehydrated makes people feel tired, low in vigour and headachy, and that fixing it restores normal state. A product can honestly say that adequate water contributes to normal cognitive function (the EU-authorised wording) but cannot honestly promise sharper memory, higher productivity or 'brain performance' from drinking a functional beverage, and nothing in the evidence shows that flavoured, electrolyte or 'enhanced' waters outperform plain water for cognition. The strongest opportunity is a behavioural one (helping low drinkers reach adequate intake), where ordinary water, tea, milk and water-rich foods already do the job.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Water deficit raises plasma osmolality and vasopressin, triggers thirst, reduces plasma volume and increases cardiovascular strain; thirst and discomfort compete for attentional resources, while brain tissue shrinkage and increased neuronal effort (higher BOLD response for the same performance) are proposed but unproven mediators of the small cognitive effects. Mediation analysis suggests thirst, not body-mass loss per se, drives early mood and memory effects.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Low fluid intake or sweat loss -> net water deficit", "detail": "12-36 h restriction, heat/exercise, or diuretics produce 1-4% body-mass loss; urine osmolality, specific gravity and colour rise early; plasma osmolality rises later and less reliably", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Water deficit -> raised plasma osmolality, vasopressin/copeptin, thirst", "detail": "Hypertonic saline infusion raises plasma osmolality from 285 to 305 mmol/kg and increases thirst and copeptin, more in women [suh2021]; 24 h deprivation raises thirst VAS from ~30 to >90 mm [pross2013]", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Thirst and cardiovascular strain -> degraded mood, fatigue, perceived effort", "detail": "Fatigue, vigour loss, confusion, headache and higher perceived task difficulty are the most replicated outputs; heart rate rises with fluid deprivation [pross2013, ganio2011, armstrong2012, ely2013]", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Thirst/discomfort -> attentional interference -> small deficits in attention/vigilance and working memory", "detail": "Mediator analysis found thirst explained early memory/mood effects and body-mass loss later attention effects [benton2016]; meta-analytic effects are small and inconsistent [wittbrodt2018_ma, goodman2019_ma]", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Brain tissue shrinkage / ventricular enlargement and inefficient neural activity", "detail": "fMRI in 10 adolescents: ventricular enlargement correlated with body-mass loss; higher fronto-parietal BOLD during a planning task without a performance change [kempton2011]; link to cognition speculative", "evidence_level": "speculative"}, {"step": "Rehydration -> reversal of thirst, mood and attention deficits within 20-90 min", "detail": "500-1000 mL restores urine osmolality, fatigue and working-memory scores within 90 min after 12-24 h restriction [zhang2020_dose, zhang2021_rct]; even 25-300 mL improves letter cancellation at 20 min [edmonds2017]", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["osmoregulation / vasopressin / thirst", "cardiovascular strain and thermoregulation", "attentional resource competition from thirst and discomfort", "cerebral volume and neural efficiency (hypothesised)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy men and women 20-40 y; mild hypohydration 1-2.6% body mass via exercise, heat, diuretic, 24 h fluid deprivation or hypertonic saline", "dose_or_intake": "Deficit of ~1-2% body mass versus euhydration (or 24 h fluid deprivation vs controlled intake)", "duration": "Single day (4-24 h protocols)", "grade": "B", "direction": "harm", "effect_summary": "Mild dehydration consistently increases fatigue, lowers vigour, raises total mood disturbance, headache and perceived task difficulty; maintaining euhydration prevents this. Reproduced across at least six laboratories, including non-exercise saline-infusion and fluid-deprivation models.", "effect_estimate": "POMS total mood disturbance +6 points (10.3 to 16.6) with hypertonic saline [suh2021]; fatigue/vigour/TMD P<0.05 in all cited trials; standardized sizes not pooled; mood effects larger in women in two studies", "certainty_notes": "Downgraded from A for small samples (n 16-49), single-blind designs where blinding of water is impossible, and industry funding/co-authorship in most positive trials; upgraded for consistency across models and funders (US Army, university, industry).", "key_study_ids": ["ganio2011", "armstrong2012", "pross2013", "szinnai2005", "ely2013", "suh2021"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults 18-35 y with 1-2% body-mass loss (exercise/heat/diuretic or overnight-24 h restriction)", "dose_or_intake": "1-2% body-mass deficit vs euhydration", "duration": "Single day", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Meta-analytic impairment of attention tasks (ES -0.52) is driven partly by >2% deficits and exercise-heat studies; at <=2% the pooled effect across all domains is -0.14 with CI touching zero; individual trials in young men found more vigilance errors, trials in women and 24 h deprivation found no objective change.", "effect_estimate": "Wittbrodt 2018: attention ES -0.52 (95% CI -0.66 to -0.37); all domains <=2% BML ES -0.14 (95% CI -0.27 to 0.00); Goodman 2019 complex attention not impaired (P>0.236)", "certainty_notes": "Two meta-analyses disagree; heterogeneity (I2 up to 93%); many small crossover studies with no blinding; indirectness because much of the pooled data come from exercise/heat protocols rather than desk conditions.", "key_study_ids": ["wittbrodt2018_ma", "goodman2019_ma", "ganio2011", "armstrong2012", "szinnai2005", "wittbrodt2015"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults 18-30 y; hypohydration 1-2.6% or 12-36 h restriction", "dose_or_intake": "1-2% deficit; rehydration with 500-1500 mL", "duration": "Single day; rehydration effects at 60-90 min", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Working-memory latency slowed in one trial and digit span/operation span fell after 24-36 h restriction and recovered with 500-1000 mL water; other trials with 1.4-2.6% deficits found no change. Executive-function meta-analytic effect small (ES -0.24) and SR of 33 studies found 'little consistency'.", "effect_estimate": "Executive function ES -0.24 (95% CI -0.37 to -0.12) [wittbrodt2018_ma]; operation span +2 points after 1000 mL vs no change with 0 mL [zhang2021_rct]; digit span total 14.3 vs 13.3 after 36 h restriction [zhang2019]", "certainty_notes": "Positive Chinese trials come from one research group with no-water (unblinded) controls and within-group testing; Stachenfeld trial n=12 with PepsiCo co-authors; several independent trials null.", "key_study_ids": ["ganio2011", "zhang2019", "zhang2021_rct", "stachenfeld2018", "armstrong2012", "szinnai2005", "katz2021_sr"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "reaction_time", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults; mild-moderate hypohydration", "dose_or_intake": "1-4% body-mass deficit", "duration": "Single day", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Simple and choice reaction time are essentially unaffected by mild-moderate hypohydration in pooled analysis and in most individual trials; sex-divergent changes (slower in women, faster in men) reported once.", "effect_estimate": "Reaction-time tasks ES -0.10 (95% CI -0.23 to 0.02) [wittbrodt2018_ma]; Stroop RT +26 ms women / -36 ms men after 24 h deprivation [szinnai2005]", "certainty_notes": "Consistent null across meta-analysis and independent trials; small samples; 'no effect' conclusions limited by imprecision.", "key_study_ids": ["wittbrodt2018_ma", "szinnai2005", "ely2013", "edmonds2013"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "students", "population_detail": "University students and young adults tested in temperate lab conditions without induced dehydration (ad libitum state, mild thirst)", "dose_or_intake": "Single drink of 25-300 mL water vs no drink; or 300 mL during 4 h at 30 C", "duration": "20-180 min", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "A drink of water improved letter-cancellation (visual attention) 20 min later, independent of expectancy and with as little as 25 mL; 300 mL also improved digit span in adults; in a 4 h warm-room study water prevented memory/attention decline linked to <1% body-mass loss and made tasks feel easier.", "effect_estimate": "Letter cancellation improved vs no-drink (P<0.05; standardized size not reported); no effect on digit span or reaction time in the 2013 study; mediator analysis P<0.05 for memory and attention [benton2016]", "certainty_notes": "Small university samples, one research group for the Edmonds studies, single task, no objective hydration biomarker in two of three studies, no replication by independent labs; practical relevance of a letter-cancellation gain is unclear.", "key_study_ids": ["edmonds2013", "edmonds2017", "benton2016"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "cognitive_fatigue", "population": "students", "population_detail": "Chinese college students 18-23 y after 12-24 h water restriction", "dose_or_intake": "Rehydration with 200, 500 or 1000 mL purified water within 10 min vs no water", "duration": "Outcomes 90 min after drinking", "grade": "C", "direction": "benefit", "effect_summary": "After restriction, 500-1000 mL reduced fatigue and total mood disturbance and improved symbol search (processing speed) and operation span (working memory); 200 mL relieved thirst and some mood scores but not working memory; no-water group worsened. Effect = restoration after deficit, not enhancement.", "effect_estimate": "Symbol search 43 -> 45-47 points with 500-1000 mL vs 42 -> 41 with none; operation span 9-10 -> 11; fatigue -1.3 to -2.5 points vs +1.4 in no-water group [zhang2021_rct]; similar pattern after 12 h restriction [zhang2020_dose]", "certainty_notes": "Single research group (Peking University), parallel groups of 16-20, unblinded no-water control, learning effects on repeated tests, Chinese Nutrition Society co-authorship; not independently replicated.", "key_study_ids": ["zhang2020_dose", "zhang2021_rct", "zhang2019"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "other", "population_detail": "Exercising or heat-exposed adults (athletes, military, recreationally active) with >2% body-mass loss", "dose_or_intake": "2-4% body-mass deficit from exercise-heat stress", "duration": "Single session", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Pooled impairment larger at >2% BML (ES -0.28) but exercise-specific meta-analysis null; Cian found impaired perceptive discrimination and short-term memory at 2.8% while Ely (4%) and Deshayes (2-4%, habituated or not) found no decrement beyond mood and one accuracy measure.", "effect_estimate": ">2% BML ES -0.28 (95% CI -0.41 to -0.16) [wittbrodt2018_ma]; g -0.177 (95% CI -0.532 to 0.179) [goodman2019_ma]", "certainty_notes": "Heat, exercise fatigue and hyperthermia confound dehydration; indirect for desk-based students; inconsistency between meta-analyses.", "key_study_ids": ["wittbrodt2018_ma", "goodman2019_ma", "cian2001", "ely2013", "deshayes2022", "wittbrodt2015"]}, {"timeframe": "short_term_days", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Habitual low drinkers (<1.2 L/day) raised to 2.5 L/day and high drinkers (2-4 L/day) restricted to 1 L/day; 20-30 y", "dose_or_intake": "1 L/day vs 2.5 L/day total water for 3 days", "duration": "3 intervention days", "grade": "C", "direction": "benefit", "effect_summary": "Increasing intake in low drinkers reduced fatigue, confusion and thirst with a trend to lower sleepiness; restricting high drinkers reduced vigour, calmness, contentedness and positive emotion. No cognitive tests.", "effect_estimate": "Fatigue-inertia P<0.001 and confusion P=0.05 in low drinkers; vigour P<0.001 in high drinkers; urine osmolality 841 -> 392 mOsm/kg in low drinkers", "certainty_notes": "Single Danone-funded study (n=52), no blinding possible, mood scales only, group assignment by habit (not randomised between habits).", "key_study_ids": ["pross2014"]}, {"timeframe": "short_term_days", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "students", "population_detail": "Chinese college students 18-23 y under controlled intake schedules for 2 days", "dose_or_intake": "200 mL every 2 h vs 100 mL every 2 h vs 110 mL every 1 h", "duration": "2 days", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Amount and frequency of intake changed urine osmolality and vigour but had limited and inconsistent effects on cognitive tests (portrait memory differences were not in the expected direction).", "effect_estimate": "Vigour higher in 200 mL/2 h group (P=0.006-0.026); no consistent cognitive advantage", "certainty_notes": "Single trial, short duration, small groups; outcome direction partly contrary to hypothesis.", "key_study_ids": ["he2020_freq"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults or students receiving a sustained (>=2 weeks) hydration intervention", "dose_or_intake": "Any", "duration": ">=2 weeks", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No randomized trial of >=2 weeks of increased water intake on cognition in healthy young adults was identified; systematic reviewers explicitly call for >3-day studies.", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "Absence of evidence; the 2021 executive-function review found almost no long-term studies.", "key_study_ids": ["katz2021_sr", "masento2014_review"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_decline", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Community-dwelling adults 55-75 y with overweight/metabolic syndrome (PREDIMED-Plus), US adults >=60 y (NHANES), and middle-to-older adults (3-month panel)", "dose_or_intake": "Serum osmolarity (dehydrated >=300 mmol/L) and reported water intake vs EFSA adequate intake", "duration": "2 years (cohort); cross-sectional; 3 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Higher serum osmolarity (poorer hydration status) predicted slightly greater 2-year decline in global cognition, but reported water intake did not; NHANES showed a curvilinear osmolarity-processing-speed relation mainly in women; a panel study linked dehydration only to sustained attention. No intervention trials.", "effect_estimate": "beta -0.010 global cognition z-score per mmol/L (95% CI -0.017 to -0.004) [nishi2023]; DSST 3.2-5.1 points higher in women with Sosm 285-289 vs other categories [bethancourt2020]; sustained attention B=0.65 z (SE 0.28) worse when dehydrated [rosinger2024]", "certainty_notes": "Observational; serum osmolarity is also a marker of diabetes, renal function and illness (dehydrated group had more diabetes); residual confounding; effect sizes small; no RCT; PREDIMED-Plus investigators report Danone-related fees.", "key_study_ids": ["nishi2023", "bethancourt2020", "rosinger2024", "edmonds2021_sr_older"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "cerebral_blood_flow", "population": "children_adolescents", "population_detail": "10 healthy adolescents (mean 16.8 y), thermal-exercise dehydration", "dose_or_intake": "Exercise-heat dehydration vs non-thermal exercise control", "duration": "Single session", "grade": "D", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Dehydration enlarged lateral ventricles in proportion to body-mass loss and increased fronto-parietal BOLD during a planning task without changing performance; resting perfusion unchanged. Biomarker only; not generalizable to adults or to cognition.", "effect_estimate": "Ventricle enlargement r=0.77 with body-mass loss (P=0.01); no performance change", "certainty_notes": "n=10 adolescents; mechanistic imaging outcome; no replication identified in adults.", "key_study_ids": ["kempton2011"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "Deficit of 1.4-1.6% body mass (exercise +/- 40 mg furosemide), 27.7 C", "onset": "Measured during/after three 40-min walks within an 8 h day", "duration_studied": "Single 8 h day, crossover", "domain": "vigilance, working memory, mood", "population": "Healthy young men (n=26) and women (n=25)", "effect_estimate": "Men: more visual vigilance errors (P=0.048), slower working-memory latency (P=0.021), more fatigue/tension; women: mood, task difficulty, concentration and headache degraded, most cognitive tasks unaffected", "certainty": "moderate for mood, low for cognition (sex-discordant, industry co-authored)", "safety": "No adverse events reported in abstracts; furosemide used under supervision", "study_ids": ["ganio2011", "armstrong2012"]}, {"dose": "24 h fluid deprivation (water from food only)", "onset": "Mood effects from ~4-10 h; thirst from early morning", "duration_studied": "24 h, crossover", "domain": "alertness, sleepiness, fatigue, confusion; objective cognition (Szinnai)", "population": "Healthy women 25 y (n=20); men and women 26 y (n=16)", "effect_estimate": "Pross: alertness down, sleepiness/fatigue/confusion up (P<0.001), plasma osmolality not significantly changed; Szinnai (2.6% BML): no change in PASAT, choice RT, tracking, Stroop or P300; tiredness +1.0 and alertness -0.9 on 5-point scales", "certainty": "moderate for subjective state; moderate-low for null objective cognition (small n)", "safety": "Pross: 13 adverse events under deprivation vs 4 control (vasovagal malaise, headache, asthenia; mild-moderate, resolved); heart rate +4-6 bpm", "study_ids": ["pross2013", "szinnai2005"]}, {"dose": "12-36 h water restriction then 0, 100-200, 500 or 1000-1500 mL water", "onset": "60-90 min after drinking", "duration_studied": "Single morning, parallel groups", "domain": "processing speed, working memory, episodic memory, mood", "population": "Chinese college students 18-23 y (n=12, 64, 76)", "effect_estimate": "500-1000 mL: symbol search +2-4, operation span +1-2, fatigue -1.3 to -2.5 and TMD reduced; 200 mL relieved thirst/anger/fatigue but not working memory; 0 mL: fatigue +1.4", "certainty": "low (single group, unblinded no-water control, practice effects)", "safety": "No adverse events reported; 1500 mL in 15 min tolerated in 12 men", "study_ids": ["zhang2019", "zhang2020_dose", "zhang2021_rct"]}, {"dose": "25 mL or 300 mL water vs none, ad libitum state", "onset": "20 min", "duration_studied": "20-40 min", "domain": "visual attention (letter cancellation), digit span, simple RT, thirst", "population": "UK adults/students (n=47; n=96)", "effect_estimate": "Letter cancellation improved with water (even 25 mL); 300 mL needed to reduce thirst; 300 mL improved digit span in adults (2017); no RT effect; expectancy manipulation had no effect", "certainty": "low (one lab, small samples, no hydration biomarkers)", "safety": "None reported", "study_ids": ["edmonds2013", "edmonds2017"]}, {"dose": "300 mL water vs none during 4 h at 30 C (mean loss 0.72% body mass)", "onset": "90-180 min", "duration_studied": "4 h", "domain": "episodic memory, focused attention, mood, perceived difficulty", "population": "Adults (n=101), NCT02671149", "effect_estimate": "Water improved memory and focused attention; thirst associated with poorer memory and lower energy/higher anxiety at 90 min; tests rated easier at 180 min with water", "certainty": "low-moderate (regression/mediator analysis, single study)", "safety": "None reported", "study_ids": ["benton2016"]}, {"dose": "2 h hypertonic (3%) saline infusion raising plasma osmolality 285 -> 305 mmol/kg", "onset": "End of infusion", "duration_studied": "Single session, crossover", "domain": "mood (POMS)", "population": "Adults 39 y, 55% female (n=49)", "effect_estimate": "TMD 10.3 -> 16.6 (P<0.01), significant in women only; confusion, depression and fatigue subscales up", "certainty": "moderate for mood (no heat/exercise confound)", "safety": "Invasive model; not relevant to products", "study_ids": ["suh2021"]}, {"dose": "Exercise-heat deficits of 1.5%, 2-4% and 4% body mass", "onset": "Immediately post-exercise", "duration_studied": "Single session (Deshayes: repeated over 4 weeks)", "domain": "psychomotor vigilance, choice RT, matching to sample, trail making, card sorting", "population": "Active men/adults (n=12, 10, 32)", "effect_estimate": "No cognitive impairment at 1.5% or 4% despite mood disturbance; only card-sort accuracy lower with 2-4% deficits", "certainty": "moderate for null (small n, three independent labs)", "safety": "Supervised exercise-heat protocols", "study_ids": ["wittbrodt2015", "ely2013", "deshayes2022"]}, {"dose": "Low vs high fluid intake producing 1-3% body-mass loss", "onset": "During simulated flight", "duration_studied": "Single session, counterbalanced", "domain": "spatial cognition, working memory, flight performance", "population": "Healthy pilots (n=40)", "effect_estimate": "Flight performance and spatial cognition significantly poorer when dehydrated (P<0.05); magnitudes not in abstract", "certainty": "low (abstract-level detail)", "safety": "None reported", "study_ids": ["lindseth2013"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "3 days", "population": "Habitual low (<1.2 L/day, n=30) and high (2-4 L/day, n=22) drinkers, 20-30 y", "outcome": "Mood (POMS, Bond-Lader, KSS), thirst", "effect_estimate": "Low drinkers at 2.5 L/day: fatigue and confusion reduced, sleepiness trend; high drinkers at 1 L/day: vigour, calmness, contentedness reduced", "evidence_quality": "low (single Danone-funded study, no cognition tests)", "limitations": "Not blinded; groups defined by habit; mood only", "study_ids": ["pross2014"]}, {"duration": "2 days", "population": "Chinese students 18-23 y (n=92)", "outcome": "Urine osmolality, cognition battery, mood", "effect_estimate": "Vigour higher with 200 mL/2 h schedule; cognitive differences small and inconsistent", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Short, single trial, direction of memory differences not as hypothesised", "study_ids": ["he2020_freq"]}, {"duration": "2 years", "population": "PREDIMED-Plus adults 55-75 y with overweight/metabolic syndrome (n=1957 intake; n=1192 osmolarity)", "outcome": "Global cognitive function z-score change", "effect_estimate": "beta -0.010 per mmol/L serum osmolarity (95% CI -0.017 to -0.004); water intake from beverages/foods not associated", "evidence_quality": "low (observational, confounded by diabetes/renal function)", "limitations": "Calculated osmolarity; high-risk population; 56% classed dehydrated despite 80% meeting EFSA intake", "study_ids": ["nishi2023"]}, {"duration": "Cross-sectional (NHANES 2011-2014)", "population": "US adults >=60 y (n=2506)", "outcome": "Recall, verbal fluency, DSST", "effect_estimate": "Women with Sosm 285-289 scored 3.2-5.1 DSST points higher than other categories; curvilinear relation in both sexes; no associations in men", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Cross-sectional; curvilinear relation implies both low and high osmolarity relate to worse scores", "study_ids": ["bethancourt2020"]}, {"duration": "3 months (3 visits)", "population": "US community adults, middle-to-older age (n=78)", "outcome": "Inhibition, working memory, flexibility, sustained attention", "effect_estimate": "Dehydration (>300 mOsm/kg) associated with 0.65 z worse sustained attention; no association with other domains", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Small panel, ad libitum, multiple comparisons", "study_ids": ["rosinger2024"]}, {"duration": "4 weeks (habituation)", "population": "Physically active adults (n=10)", "outcome": "Cognitive battery after 2% and 4% exercise-induced hypohydration", "effect_estimate": "No decline except card-sort accuracy; no change with repeated exposure", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "n=10; exercise confound", "study_ids": ["deshayes2022"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Plain water at intakes around the adequate intake is as safe as any food. Hazards are at the extremes: (1) deliberate fluid restriction protocols cause vasovagal symptoms, headache and asthenia (13 vs 4 adverse events in a 24 h deprivation study) and should not be encouraged; (2) excessive intake (typically >=8 L/day, or rapid intake of several litres, or in endurance events, psychiatric polydipsia, MDMA use, SIADH, thiazide users) causes hyponatraemia with seizures, coma and 13% mortality in reported cases. Caffeinated beverages produce only minor diuresis (+109 mL per ~300 mg) and count toward fluid intake. Mineral/bottled waters must meet drinking-water quality standards (microbial, chemical, radiological).", "adverse_effects": ["Fluid restriction/dehydration: thirst, headache, fatigue, dizziness, vasovagal malaise, tachycardia, constipation, kidney-stone risk with chronic low intake", "Excess water: hyponatraemia (nausea, confusion, seizures, cerebral oedema, death) at intakes far above recommendations", "Frequent urination/nocturia with very high intake or evening loading", "Bottled water: contamination risk (microbial, bromate from ozonation, microplastics, heavy metals) if quality control fails"], "contraindications": ["Fluid-restricted conditions (heart failure, advanced chronic kidney disease, SIADH, cirrhosis with ascites) - medical guidance required", "Psychogenic polydipsia / schizophrenia (water-intoxication risk)", "Infants (water intoxication; not relevant to adult product)"], "drug_interactions": ["Thiazide diuretics, SSRIs, carbamazepine, oxcarbazepine, antipsychotics, NSAIDs, desmopressin and MDMA increase hyponatraemia risk when combined with high water intake", "Loop diuretics (e.g. furosemide used experimentally) increase dehydration; lithium clearance depends on hydration status", "Caffeine: mild additive diuresis only"], "upper_limits": "No UL set by EFSA or IOM for water; EFSA AI 2.0 L/day (women) and 2.5 L/day (men) total water from all sources; US IOM AI 2.7 L (women) and 3.7 L (men) total water (IOM 2004, not verified here). Kidney excretory capacity (~0.7-1 L/h) is the physiological ceiling; sustained intakes >=8 L/day are associated with reported water intoxication [rangan2021_hyponatraemia_sr].", "sleep_impact": "Fluid restriction increases daytime sleepiness and fatigue [pross2013]; large evening intake increases nocturia; caffeinated 'hydration' beverages may disrupt sleep via caffeine, not water.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Lifelong human exposure; observational cohorts link low hydration status (high serum osmolarity) to worse outcomes but intake trials beyond a few days with cognitive endpoints do not exist.", "daily_use_appropriate": "yes", "special_populations": "Pregnancy/lactation: EFSA adds +300 mL and +700 mL respectively; older adults: blunted thirst and higher dehydration prevalence, but also higher hyponatraemia risk on diuretics/SSRIs; athletes and heat workers: higher needs but also exercise-associated hyponatraemia with overdrinking; children: outside scope (do not generalize).", "specialist_review_needed": ["dietitian (intake targets by population)", "clinician/nephrologist (fluid-restricted and hyponatraemia-risk patients)", "food safety / water-quality specialist (bottled-water microbiology and chemistry)", "regulatory affairs (claim wording per market)"], "hard_safety_gate": "pass", "gate_reason": "Water at recommended intakes is food-level safe; the only material hazards (over-drinking, fluid-restricted diseases) are avoidable with standard labelling and do not arise from a beverage consumed at normal serving sizes."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Two Article 13(1) health claims are authorised in Commission Regulation (EU) No 432/2012 (EFSA Journal 2011;9(4):2075): 'Water contributes to the maintenance of normal physical and cognitive functions' and 'Water contributes to the maintenance of normal regulation of the body's temperature'. Conditions of use: the consumer must be informed that at least 2.0 L of water, from all sources, should be consumed per day, and the claim may be used only on water complying with Directive 2009/54/EC (natural mineral/spring waters) and/or Directive 98/83/EC (drinking water; since recast as Directive (EU) 2020/2184 - register cross-reference not re-checked). Trade press (2011) reports EFSA rejected an Article 14 claim that water reduces the risk of dehydration (not verified against the EFSA opinion here). A flavoured or fortified functional beverage is not 'water complying with' those directives, so the authorised claim cannot be transferred to it; any 'hydration' wording on such a product must avoid implying a cognitive benefit beyond the authorised text (legal interpretation to be confirmed by counsel). Natural mineral water requires recognition under 2009/54/EC. Source: legislation.gov.uk copy of Reg 432/2012 annex, accessed 2026-10-25.", "us": "Bottled water is a conventional food with a standard of identity and quality (21 CFR 165.110: definitions of artesian, mineral (>=250 ppm TDS), purified, spring, sparkling, sterile and well water; microbial, chemical, fluoride and radiological limits; labelling of municipal source, mineral content). No FDA-authorized or qualified health claim exists for water and cognition. Structure/function claims on conventional foods are permitted without premarket review or notification when they 'focus on effects derived from nutritive value' (FDA Label Claims page, accessed 2026-10-25); FTC requires 'competent and reliable scientific evidence' for any express or implied cognitive-performance claim. Added functional ingredients must be GRAS or approved food additives; a beverage positioned like a dietary supplement risks FDA objection (2009 guidance on liquid dietary supplements vs beverages, not re-verified here). Sources: law.cornell.edu 21 CFR 165.110; fda.gov label-claims page, both accessed 2026-10-25.", "morocco": "Framework: Law 36-15 on water (Dahir 1-16-113, 10 August 2016) Article 33 states that conditions for extraction, exploitation, packaging, labelling and sale of natural mineral waters, spring waters and table waters are set by regulation; the Ministry of Health (Division de l'Hygiene du Milieu) maintains lists of authorised mineral/spring/table waters (2021 and 2024 tables on sante.gov.ma) and earlier texts (Law 10-95; 1951 dahir; 1953 arrete on analyses) require official authorisation before sale. ONSSA enforces food safety under Law 28-07 and the general labelling decree. A specific Moroccan register of authorised health claims comparable to the EU register was not identified in this search; whether the EU-style 'normal cognitive function' wording is permitted on Moroccan labels is uncertain and needs local regulatory counsel. Sources: faolex.fao.org (Dahir 1-16-113), sante.gov.ma tables, onssa.gov.ma 1953 arrete; accessed 2026-10-25.", "permitted_claims_examples": ["EU (Reg 432/2012): 'Water contributes to the maintenance of normal physical and cognitive functions' - only on compliant water, with the statement that at least 2.0 L/day from all sources is needed", "EU (Reg 432/2012): 'Water contributes to the maintenance of normal regulation of the body's temperature' - same conditions", "US: non-misleading structure/function statements such as 'helps you stay hydrated' on water (no premarket approval; FTC substantiation applies); no authorised cognitive claim"], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "None for water. Any added ingredient (electrolytes, botanicals, nootropics) is assessed under its own novel-food/GRAS/NDI status; natural mineral water in the EU requires official recognition and must not be treated beyond permitted processes.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Water is the matrix of every beverage; the honest 'hydration' proposition is best delivered by plain water, infused water, tea or milk-based drinks rather than by a product claiming cognitive enhancement. A Brain Food beverage can legitimately be positioned as a convenient way to reach adequate intake (e.g. 500 mL serving = 20-25% of the EFSA AI) but gains no evidence-based cognitive advantage from added 'hydration' ingredients.", "formats": ["still or sparkling water (plain, mineral, spring)", "lightly flavoured/infused water without sugar", "oral rehydration-type electrolyte drinks (only for heavy sweat loss)", "milk and dairy beverages (highest hydration index)", "tea and coffee (hydrate like water at usual caffeine doses)", "water-rich foods (fruit, soups)"], "stability": "Water is stable; concerns are microbial growth, bromate formation if ozonated with bromide present, leaching from packaging, and flavour/ingredient stability in flavoured waters (pH, light, oxidation of added vitamins or botanicals).", "taste": "Neutral; mineral content (TDS, bicarbonate, sulfate, chloride) and temperature strongly influence palatability and ad libitum intake; cool, palatable, flavoured water increases voluntary intake, which is itself the main lever for low drinkers.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Absorption is rapid (gastric emptying 10-20 min for 500 mL); energy, protein and sodium slow emptying and prolong retention (milk and ORS retain ~50% more at 2 h than plain water [maughan2016_bhi]); caffeine adds minor diuresis [zhang2015_caffeine_ma]; sparkling, tea, coffee, juice and sports drinks hydrate equivalently to water in euhydrated men.", "dose_feasibility": "Yes: the effective 'dose' in restriction trials was 500-1000 mL [zhang2020_dose, zhang2021_rct]; 500 mL fits a single serving; 25-300 mL already affects thirst/attention [edmonds2017]. Daily target 2.0-2.5 L from all sources is achievable with 5-8 glasses plus food.", "cost_availability": "Tap water is near-free where potable; bottled water cost in Morocco, EU and US varies widely; packaged flavoured waters add cost without evidence of extra benefit.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 4, "effect_magnitude": 3, "safety": 9, "reproducibility": 4, "food_suitability": 10, "cost_access": 9, "rationale": "Evidence quality 4: B-grade only for acute mood/fatigue effects of mild dehydration; C for attention/working memory with conflicting meta-analyses; D/insufficient for chronic effects in young adults. Effect magnitude 3: pooled cognitive effects are small (ES ~ -0.1 to -0.2 at <=2% deficit), restorative rather than enhancing; mood/fatigue effects are more noticeable. Safety 9: food-level safety at recommended intakes; rare water intoxication at extreme intakes. Reproducibility 4: mood findings replicate; cognitive findings split between labs, with industry-linked trials more often positive and independent/military trials often null. Food suitability 10: water is the beverage matrix itself. Cost/access 9: tap water nearly free; bottled/packaged formats cheap but add no evidence-based benefit."}, "research_gaps": ["Independent (non-industry), blinded-as-possible RCTs of 2-4 weeks of increased water intake vs usual intake in habitual low-drinking students/knowledge workers with pre-registered cognitive and productivity endpoints", "Dose-response below 1% body-mass loss in sedentary adults at 20-24 C (office conditions), with objective hydration biomarkers and positive controls (e.g. sleep loss) to calibrate effect sizes", "Sex-stratified trials given opposite reaction-time responses and stronger mood effects in women [szinnai2005, suh2021]", "Head-to-head trials of plain water vs flavoured/electrolyte/'functional' waters on cognition to test whether any formulation adds value", "Trials in older adults (60+) with low intake, comparing water-intake coaching vs control on cognition, since current evidence is observational only", "Mechanistic work separating oral/thirst signalling (25 mL effects) from systemic rehydration", "Registered trials to watch: NCT02671149 (published as benton2016); NCT05916183 'Dehydration Cognition' (n=15, completed 2023, no results posted); NCT03483831 adolescents (n=214, completed 2018, unpublished per registry) - potential publication bias check"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 5.9, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "pass", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": "C", "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": "C", "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": "C", "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"sustained_attention": "C", "cognitive_fatigue": "C", "cognitive_decline": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 12, "n_studies": 34, "n_verified": 34, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 7, "n_systematic_reviews": 3, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["benefit", "harm", "inconsistent", "insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "meal-composition-size-and-timing", "name": "Meal composition, meal size and meal timing", "category": "A", "category_name": "Macronutrients and brain energy", "aliases": ["macronutrient composition of meals", "protein-rich vs carbohydrate-rich vs fat-rich meal", "meal size / energy load", "post-lunch dip", "postprandial sleepiness / postprandial somnolence / 'food coma'", "chrononutrition", "meal timing / night eating / late eating", "snacking"], "food_sources": ["any mixed meal; the 'ingredient' here is the eating pattern (composition, energy load, clock time), not a compound", "light, mixed meals (lean protein, whole grains, vegetables, unsaturated fat) versus heavy high-fat or high-energy meals", "small snacks (about 10% of daily energy) versus full meals (about 30% of daily energy) during night work"], "active_constituents": ["energy load (kcal per meal)", "macronutrient ratio (carbohydrate:protein:fat) and fat type (saturated vs monounsaturated)", "glycaemic response (see glucose-and-carbohydrates monograph for GI/GL and glucose drinks)", "large neutral amino acid ratios (Trp:LNAA, Tyr:LNAA) as proposed mediators", "meal clock time relative to the circadian system"], "one_line_verdict": "Heavy meals, especially large or high-fat lunches and full meals eaten in the middle of the night, reliably increase sleepiness and can worsen sustained attention and simulated driving in the following hours, but no particular macronutrient ratio has been shown to improve cognition in healthy young adults; the useful advice is to keep meals moderate and mixed and to snack lightly at night, which is food practice rather than something to sell as a product.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. The best-replicated finding is negative: eating a meal raises sleepiness and lowers sustained attention for a few hours, on top of the circadian afternoon dip, which happens even when no lunch is eaten [monk2005]. Old Cardiff studies showed that eating lunch impaired sustained attention and reaction to new stimuli compared with not eating, but not resistance to distraction [smithmiles1986]. Objective sleep latency (MSLT) fell 1.5 h after a meal whatever its composition [wells1998], and a solid meal shortened nap sleep latency compared with water, again with no difference between high-fat, high-carbohydrate and mixed meals [orr1997]. Meal size matters more than composition: in 12 young men allowed only 5 h sleep, a 922 kcal lunch caused more lane drifting and more EEG sleepiness than a 305 kcal lunch from about 30 min into a 2 h monotonous drive [reyner2012]. In a randomised 7-day simulated night-shift study (n = 39), a snack (10% of energy) at 00:30 gave better driving, fewer PVT lapses and faster reaction and running-memory performance at 04:00 than a full meal (30%) or no food, and the meal group felt sleepiest [gupta2019]. A nurse cohort agrees: more eating occasions at night went with slightly faster reaction times (about 5 ms per occasion) [derijk2021]. Composition. Within-subject trials from the 1980s-2000s gave conflicting answers. Fat-rich meals increased fatigue or slowed sustained-attention responses in Sheffield studies [wells1995, wellsread1996, wells1997], and a high-saturated-fat meal worsened target discrimination 5 h later compared with a high-oleic meal in 51 middle-aged women [madison2020], and in obese but not lean men [ruegsegger2022]. But the same Zurich group found the best performance after pure fat in one trial [fischer2001] and after protein-rich or balanced meals in the next [fischer2002]. A high-carbohydrate lunch impaired attention only in people over 40 in Spring's study of 184 adults [spring1982]. Modern controlled breakfast trials are mostly null: oatmeal vs cornflakes vs water had no effect on sustained attention or EEG alpha (PepsiCo co-authored) [wilson2018]; four breakfasts differing in protein had no effect on cognition in 33 undergraduates [emilien2017]; a whey drink changed heart rate but not task performance [sihvola2013]; and one Italian trial suggested a protein croissant gave faster reaction time than a whole-wheat one [manippa2021]. The only systematic review restricted to healthy young adults found mostly glucose manipulations and very few protein or fat ones [hoyland2008]. Timing. Eating only in the daytime during simulated night work stopped the rise in depression-like (+26%) and anxiety-like (+16%) mood seen with night eating [qian2022]; this is a mood result from a small laboratory sample, not a cognitive one. Chrononutrition-cognition data in day-living adults are cross-sectional and inconsistent [demetriou2025], and the shift-work evidence base is judged insufficient for guidelines [booker2026]. Practical relevance. Effects are in the range of tens of milliseconds and a few percentage points of lapses, but they matter for safety-critical monotonous tasks (driving, night shifts) and for afternoon lectures after a large lunch. No trial has tested students during exam periods, and no macronutrient formula has evidence of enhancing memory or intelligence.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "This monograph is not about correcting a nutrient deficiency. It is about avoiding a short-lived performance cost that a large or fat-rich meal adds to circadian sleepiness, especially after sleep loss [reyner2012] or at night [gupta2019]. For a rested, well-fed young adult, the best data offer avoidance of impairment (lighter meals, a small snack rather than a full meal at night) and no enhancement: no macronutrient ratio has been shown to raise memory, executive function or intelligence above a normal fed state [hoyland2008, emilien2017, wilson2018]. The sensitivity of obese or insulin-resistant people to a high-saturated-fat meal [ruegsegger2022] and of older adults to a high-carbohydrate lunch [spring1982] are separate populations and should not be applied to students. Hunger itself is covered in the breakfast-vs-fasting monograph.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Claims such as 'the high-protein lunch that keeps you sharp all afternoon', 'brain-optimised macronutrient ratio' or 'chrono-nutrition for peak focus' are not supported: composition trials conflict [fischer2001, fischer2002] and recent controlled breakfast trials are null [emilien2017, wilson2018]. What the evidence does support is modest and generic: smaller, moderate-fat meals cause less post-meal sleepiness than heavy meals [reyner2012, wells1995], and night workers do better with a light snack than a full meal at 00:30 [gupta2019]. These are dietary-advice statements, not product benefits. In the EU, a claim such as 'reduces the post-lunch dip' or 'supports concentration' would be an unauthorised health claim; nutrient-specific authorised claims (for example iron, iodine or zinc 'contribute to normal cognitive function') exist only when the product meets the nutrient conditions, and say nothing about meal design. In the US, the FTC's 2009 action against Kellogg over 'clinically shown to improve kids' attentiveness by nearly 20%' (Frosted Mini-Wheats) shows that meal-attention claims based on weak trials are enforced as deceptive. The honest brand message: 'a light, balanced meal helps you avoid the afternoon slump' framed as advice, with no implied enhancement.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Post-meal sleepiness reflects the circadian/homeostatic afternoon rise in sleep propensity (present without food) plus meal-related signals: gastric distension and solid food, CCK release from intestinal fat, insulin and the Trp:LNAA ratio after carbohydrate, postprandial inflammatory cytokines (IL-1) after large high-fat/high-carbohydrate meals, and, with saturated fat, postprandial insulin resistance and endotoxaemia. Eating at night also misaligns peripheral clocks (glucose rhythm) from the central clock, which has been linked to mood changes. Most links are supported by biomarker correlations or single small trials, not by mediation analyses.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Meal intake -> gastric distension and nutrient sensing; dietary fat in small intestine -> CCK release", "detail": "High-fat meals raised CCK and fatigue ratings vs high-carbohydrate meals [wells1997]; intraduodenal lipid infusion alone reduced alertness and slowed sustained-attention responses in 5 men [wells1995].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Carbohydrate-rich meal -> insulin -> higher plasma Trp:LNAA -> possibly more brain serotonin", "detail": "Trp:LNAA rose about 30% after both whey-protein and carbohydrate drinks [sihvola2013]; Tyr:LNAA was linked to reaction times after a balanced meal [fischer2002]. Brain serotonin change not measured in humans in these trials.", "evidence_level": "plausible"}, {"step": "Large high-fat/high-carbohydrate meal -> postprandial IL-1 signalling -> fatigue", "detail": "IL-1 receptor antagonist anakinra reduced Stanford Sleepiness Scale ratings after a calorie-rich meal in 8 lean and 8 obese men [lehrskov2018]. Subjective outcome only.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Saturated-fat meal -> postprandial insulin resistance / endotoxaemia (LBP, sCD14) -> worse attention", "detail": "Worse target discrimination after a saturated-fat vs oleic meal; baseline LBP moderated effects [madison2020]; cognitive decline after SFA meal associated with HOMA-IR rise in obese men only [ruegsegger2022].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Circadian timing: post-lunch dip in sleep propensity independent of food; night eating -> internal circadian misalignment", "detail": "Post-lunch dip occurs without lunch [monk2005, smithmiles1986]; misalignment of glucose vs temperature rhythms correlated with depression-like mood during simulated night work (r = 0.77) [qian2022].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Measured human outcome", "detail": "Consistent: more sleepiness (subjective and MSLT) after meals, worse monotonous driving after heavy lunch with sleep restriction and after a full night meal. Inconsistent: any cognitive advantage of a particular macronutrient ratio in healthy young adults.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["circadian 12-h harmonic (post-lunch dip)", "CCK and gut-brain vagal signalling", "insulin / Trp:LNAA / serotonin hypothesis", "postprandial inflammation (IL-1, endotoxaemia)", "postprandial insulin resistance", "peripheral clock misalignment with night eating"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "alertness", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults (mostly 18-35 y) tested 1-4 h after a meal vs water/no meal", "dose_or_intake": "Ordinary solid lunch or test meal (about 750-3600 kJ)", "duration": "Single meal; 1-5 h follow-up", "grade": "B", "direction": "harm", "effect_summary": "Eating a meal increases subjective and objective sleepiness and lowers sustained attention for a few hours, on top of the circadian dip. MSLT sleepiness rose 1.5 h after meals regardless of composition; a solid meal shortened nap latency vs water; lunch impaired sustained attention vs no lunch.", "effect_estimate": "MSLT pre-post meal F(1,15) = 11.37, p = 0.004 [wells1998]; standardised effect sizes not reported", "certainty_notes": "Several small within-subject studies (n = 10-18) agree on direction; old designs, unblinded (eating vs not eating cannot be blinded), effect sizes rarely reported. Downgraded for imprecision and risk of bias; consistency keeps it at B.", "key_study_ids": ["smithmiles1986", "wells1998", "orr1997", "monk2005"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "vigilance", "population": "sleep_deprived", "population_detail": "12 young men with sleep restricted to 5 h, 2 h monotonous afternoon simulated drive", "dose_or_intake": "Heavy lunch 922 kcal vs light lunch 305 kcal (double-blind, similar appearance)", "duration": "Single meal", "grade": "C", "direction": "harm", "effect_summary": "Heavy lunch increased sleepiness-related lane drifting and EEG 4-11 Hz power, with a trend toward more subjective sleepiness; differences appeared only after about 30 min of driving.", "effect_estimate": "Significant main effects of meal on incidents and EEG power; no condition x time interaction; numerical effect sizes not in abstract", "certainty_notes": "Single small trial, but realistic task and blinded meals. Downgraded for imprecision and single study.", "key_study_ids": ["reyner2012"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults comparing isoenergetic meals that differ in fat vs carbohydrate vs protein", "dose_or_intake": "Isoenergetic meals 1670-3600 kJ; fat:CHO ratios 54:41 to 64:18 vs 7:76-88; CHO:protein 4:1 to 1:4", "duration": "Single meal; up to 3.5 h follow-up", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "High-fat lunches lowered alertness and slowed sustained-attention responses in some small trials, but other trials found no composition effect on objective sleepiness, best performance after a pure-fat cream, or better performance after protein-rich or balanced meals. Modern breakfast trials (oatmeal vs cornflakes; high vs low protein) found no difference in attention.", "effect_estimate": "Mostly p-values only (p < 0.05 for high-fat slowing [wells1995]); no pooled estimate available", "certainty_notes": "Direct contradictions within one laboratory [fischer2001 vs fischer2002]; very small n (5-33); multiple outcomes; one industry-co-authored null trial [wilson2018]. Downgraded for inconsistency, imprecision and risk of bias.", "key_study_ids": ["wells1995", "wellsread1996", "fischer2001", "fischer2002", "orr1997", "wilson2018", "emilien2017", "hoyland2008"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "students", "population_detail": "Undergraduate and university students given breakfasts or snacks of different composition", "dose_or_intake": "Breakfasts with different protein/carbohydrate content; protein vs whole-wheat croissant with espresso; afternoon calorie snack vs diet soda", "duration": "Single meal or snack", "grade": "C", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "33 undergraduates: no effect of breakfast macronutrient composition on cognition [emilien2017]; 40 Italian young adults: faster simple reaction time after protein than whole-wheat croissant, digit span improved after either [manippa2021]; male college students recalled more backward digits after a calorie snack than diet soda, an energy-vs-no-energy contrast rather than a composition effect [kanarek1990].", "effect_estimate": "not reported as standardised effects", "certainty_notes": "Few small single-blind trials, inconsistent; no trial in exam-period students. Practical relevance unknown.", "key_study_ids": ["emilien2017", "manippa2021", "kanarek1990"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Middle-aged women (51; 32 breast-cancer survivors) and lean vs obese men (24) given saturated-fat vs unsaturated-fat meals", "dose_or_intake": "High-saturated-fat meal vs high-oleic sunflower oil meal; SFA- vs MUFA-enriched vs no-fat meal", "duration": "Single meal; tests 2-5 h after", "grade": "C", "direction": "harm", "effect_summary": "Worse ability to separate targets from distractors 5 h after a saturated-fat meal than after an oleic-oil meal in women [madison2020]; Stroop, Trails B-A and digit span worsened after an SFA meal in obese men but not lean men [ruegsegger2022].", "effect_estimate": "B = 4.44, SE 1.88, p = 0.02 (CPT discrimination) [madison2020]; within-group p < 0.05 in obese only [ruegsegger2022]", "certainty_notes": "Two small trials in non-student populations; indirect for healthy young adults; Ruegsegger single-blind with within-group comparisons. Mechanistic interest (insulin resistance, endotoxaemia) is plausible but not established.", "key_study_ids": ["madison2020", "ruegsegger2022"]}, {"timeframe": "short_term_days", "domain": "vigilance", "population": "high_cognitive_workload", "population_detail": "Healthy young adults (mean 24.5 y) in a 7-day lab protocol with 4 simulated night shifts", "dose_or_intake": "Meal at 00:30 (30% of 24 h energy) vs snack (10%) vs no eating at night; 24 h energy and macronutrients constant", "duration": "4 consecutive simulated nights", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "At 04:00 the snack group had more time in the driving safe zone, less speed and lane variability, fewer post-drive PVT lapses and faster choice-reaction and running-memory responses than both the meal and no-eating groups; the meal group reported the most sleepiness. A nurse cohort found more night eating occasions went with slightly faster PVT reaction times.", "effect_estimate": "Snack vs meal/no-eat at 04:00 all p < 0.001 [gupta2019]; -4.81 ms RT per eating occasion (95% CI -9.14 to -0.48) [derijk2021]", "certainty_notes": "One parallel-group RCT with n = 12-14 per arm (imprecision; baseline imbalance possible) plus one observational cohort; consistent direction. Benefit is relative to a full meal or fasting, not enhancement.", "key_study_ids": ["gupta2019", "derijk2021", "booker2026"]}, {"timeframe": "short_term_days", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "high_cognitive_workload", "population_detail": "Healthy adults in a 14-day simulated night-work circadian protocol", "dose_or_intake": "Daytime-only eating vs daytime plus night-time eating, identical meals", "duration": "Simulated night work over several days", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Night eating raised depression-like mood by 26.2% (95% CI 21 to 31.5%) and anxiety-like mood by 16.1% (8.5 to 23.6%) from baseline; daytime-only eating prevented these rises. Mood, not cognition.", "effect_estimate": "Interaction pFDR = 0.001, r = 0.78 (depression-like), r = 0.47 (anxiety-like) [qian2022]", "certainty_notes": "Small, tightly controlled laboratory study; mood measured by visual analogue scales; no cognitive endpoints; replication needed in real shift workers.", "key_study_ids": ["qian2022"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Day-living adults: chrononutrition patterns (eating window, first-meal latency, evening eating) vs cognition; workplace healthy-meal provision", "dose_or_intake": "Habitual meal timing; provision of healthy lunch, snack and water at work", "duration": "Cross-sectional; 8-week crossover", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "In 207 Cypriot adults aged 45-65, some timing variables were associated with better and others with worse scores, without a consistent pattern [demetriou2025]. Providing healthy meals at work for 8 weeks did not change Go/No-Go reaction time in 60 hospital staff [leedo2017].", "effect_estimate": "Mixed ORs (e.g. OR 2.07, 1.08-3.97 for evening eating and complex attention) [demetriou2025]; null RT effect [leedo2017]", "certainty_notes": "Cross-sectional with many tests; single small crossover. No RCT of meal timing and cognition in healthy young adults or students. TRE/IF trials belong to unit K3.", "key_study_ids": ["demetriou2025", "leedo2017"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "other", "population": "students", "population_detail": "Healthy young adults/students: claim that a specific macronutrient ratio enhances memory, focus or learning beyond an ordinary meal", "dose_or_intake": "Any 'brain-optimised' meal formula", "duration": "Single meal to days", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No adequately powered, preregistered trial shows that any meal composition enhances cognition in fed, rested young adults. Systematic review found the literature dominated by glucose manipulations, with memory most sensitive and effects mainly under high demand.", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "Absence of direct evidence; evidence from glucose drinks belongs to the glucose-and-carbohydrates monograph.", "key_study_ids": ["hoyland2008", "muth2021"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "Ordinary lunch vs no lunch", "onset": "Within 1 h, peaking about 1.5-3 h", "duration_studied": "1-4 h", "domain": "alertness / sustained attention", "population": "healthy adults", "effect_estimate": "More sleepiness (MSLT p = 0.004) and slower detection of new stimuli; standardised effect not reported", "certainty": "B", "safety": "Relevant to driving and monotonous work", "study_ids": ["smithmiles1986", "wells1998", "orr1997"]}, {"dose": "922 kcal vs 305 kcal lunch after 5 h sleep", "onset": "About 30 min into drive", "duration_studied": "2 h drive", "domain": "vigilance (driving)", "population": "young men, sleep restricted", "effect_estimate": "More lane-drift incidents and EEG sleepiness after heavy lunch", "certainty": "C", "safety": "Road-safety implication", "study_ids": ["reyner2012"]}, {"dose": "High-fat vs high-carbohydrate isoenergetic meal", "onset": "1-3 h", "duration_studied": "3 h", "domain": "alertness / sustained attention", "population": "healthy adults", "effect_estimate": "Inconsistent: slower responses after high fat in some studies, no difference in MSLT in others", "certainty": "C", "safety": "None specific", "study_ids": ["wells1995", "wells1997", "wells1998", "orr1997"]}, {"dose": "Carbohydrate:protein 4:1 vs 1:1 vs 1:4 (1670 kJ) breakfast", "onset": "Within 1 h", "duration_studied": "3.5 h", "domain": "short-term memory / attention / reaction time", "population": "15 healthy male students", "effect_estimate": "Transient attention benefit in first hour after CHO meal; better overall after balanced or protein meal; contradicted by same group's earlier trial", "certainty": "C", "safety": "None", "study_ids": ["fischer2002", "fischer2001"]}, {"dose": "High saturated fat vs high oleic meal", "onset": "5 h", "duration_studied": "5 h", "domain": "sustained attention", "population": "middle-aged women", "effect_estimate": "B = 4.44 (SE 1.88), p = 0.02 worse discrimination after SFA", "certainty": "C", "safety": "Cardiometabolic reasons to limit SFA anyway", "study_ids": ["madison2020"]}, {"dose": "Snack (10% energy) vs meal (30%) vs nothing at 00:30", "onset": "Measured at 01:30 and 04:00", "duration_studied": "4 simulated nights", "domain": "vigilance, reaction time, driving", "population": "healthy young adults on simulated night shifts", "effect_estimate": "Snack better than meal or no food at 04:00 (p < 0.001)", "certainty": "C", "safety": "Night driving/occupational safety", "study_ids": ["gupta2019"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "14-day protocol", "population": "healthy adults in simulated night work", "outcome": "depression-like and anxiety-like mood", "effect_estimate": "Night eating +26.2% depression-like and +16.1% anxiety-like mood; prevented by daytime-only eating", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "Small laboratory sample; mood scales only; no cognition", "study_ids": ["qian2022"]}, {"duration": "8 weeks", "population": "hospital day and shift workers", "outcome": "Go/No-Go reaction time, mood", "effect_estimate": "No effect on reaction time; mood improved in shift-working subgroup (n = 16)", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "Open-label; subgroup finding; intervention mixed meals and water", "study_ids": ["leedo2017"]}, {"duration": "cross-sectional", "population": "adults 45-65 y", "outcome": "computerised cognitive domains", "effect_estimate": "Inconsistent associations with meal timing variables", "evidence_quality": "D", "limitations": "Cross-sectional; multiple comparisons; residual confounding", "study_ids": ["demetriou2025"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Meal composition, size and timing involve ordinary foods, so there is no toxicity issue. The relevant risks are behavioural and situational: post-meal sleepiness while driving or doing safety-critical monotonous work, especially after sleep loss or at night; reflux and gastric discomfort with large or late meals; and, if a brand pushes 'low-carb' or 'protein-only' meal messages, possible conflict with dietary guidelines. Postprandial hypotension in older adults and people with autonomic failure (e.g. Parkinson's disease) can cause dizziness after large carbohydrate meals. People with diabetes on insulin or sulfonylureas must not alter meal timing or composition without medical advice. Meal-timing messages directed at people with eating disorders can be harmful.", "adverse_effects": ["Post-meal sleepiness and impaired monotonous driving after heavy lunches [reyner2012]", "Greater sleepiness after a full meal than a snack at night [gupta2019]", "Higher depression-like and anxiety-like mood with night eating under circadian misalignment [qian2022]", "Gastro-oesophageal reflux with large or late meals (general clinical knowledge, not researched here)", "Postprandial hypotension in older adults and autonomic failure (general clinical knowledge, not researched here)"], "contraindications": ["Insulin- or sulfonylurea-treated diabetes: meal timing changes need medical supervision", "Active or past eating disorder: rigid meal-timing rules may be harmful"], "drug_interactions": ["Food effect on drug absorption (e.g. levothyroxine taken fasting; some antibiotics); meal-timing advice must not override prescribing instructions", "Glucose-lowering drugs (insulin, sulfonylureas): risk of hypoglycaemia if meals are delayed or reduced"], "upper_limits": "Not applicable to meals; saturated fat intake should follow dietary guidelines (WHO: < 10% of energy), which also matches the acute attention findings.", "sleep_impact": "Large meals increase short-term daytime sleepiness. Late or night-time eating is linked to circadian misalignment; a 2026 small trial on late dinner and sleep was seen but not extracted. Glycaemic index of evening meals and sleep is covered in the glucose-and-carbohydrates monograph.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Only short laboratory protocols (up to 14 days) and an 8-week workplace meal crossover; no long-term cognitive trials of meal composition or timing in young adults.", "daily_use_appropriate": "yes", "special_populations": "Shift workers and night-time drivers (sleepiness); older adults (postprandial hypotension, carbohydrate-lunch attention effect [spring1982]); people with obesity or insulin resistance (sensitivity to saturated-fat meals [ruegsegger2022]); children (separate evidence, not covered); pregnancy (meal frequency advice from obstetric care, not researched here).", "specialist_review_needed": ["registered dietitian for meal-plan guidance", "occupational/sleep medicine for night-shift eating guidance", "diabetologist for any meal-timing advice to people on glucose-lowering drugs", "eating-disorder specialist for messaging review"], "hard_safety_gate": "pass", "gate_reason": "Ordinary meals and eating-pattern advice carry no intrinsic toxicity; risks are manageable with clear messaging (no driving advice implying meals prevent sleepiness, exclusions for people on glucose-lowering drugs)."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "No authorised EU health claim refers to meal composition, meal size or meal timing and cognition, alertness or the post-lunch dip. Under Regulation (EC) No 1924/2006, Article 10(3), references to general, non-specific benefits (e.g. 'brain-friendly lunch') must be accompanied by an authorised specific claim. Nutrient-specific authorised claims can be used if conditions are met, e.g. 'Carbohydrates contribute to the maintenance of normal brain function' (Reg. (EU) 1018/2013; >= 20 g metabolisable carbohydrate per portion, low/no added sugars; see glucose-and-carbohydrates monograph), and iron, iodine, zinc 'contribute to normal cognitive function' (Reg. (EU) 432/2012). Claims such as 'prevents the afternoon slump' or 'improves concentration' would be unauthorised. Status of a 'meal timing' claim: none exists (not researched further).", "us": "Meals and conventional foods: structure/function statements must be truthful and not misleading; FTC requires competent and reliable scientific evidence. Precedent: FTC consent order against Kellogg (2009, finalised by 4-0 vote; modified 2010 to require substantiation for all health claims for any food) over the claim that Frosted Mini-Wheats was 'clinically shown to improve kids' attentiveness by nearly 20%' when the study showed only about 1 in 9 children improved that much. Sources accessed 2026-10-25: ftc.gov (node/44358; documents/cases/2010/06/100602kelloggorder.pdf); trade press summaries.", "morocco": "No Moroccan rule specific to meal-related cognitive claims was found. General food labelling falls under Law 28-07 on food safety and Decree 2-12-389 (ONSSA). The health-claim authorisation framework is uncertain (see glucose-and-carbohydrates monograph); verification with ONSSA needed.", "permitted_claims_examples": ["EU (Reg. 1018/2013): 'Carbohydrates contribute to the maintenance of normal brain function' - only if portion >= 20 g metabolisable carbohydrate and LOW SUGARS or WITH NO ADDED SUGARS, with the 130 g/day statement", "EU (Reg. 432/2012): 'Iron contributes to normal cognitive function' - only if the food is at least a source of iron (15% NRV per 100 g/100 ml or per portion)"], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "None; ordinary foods.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "High: the evidence-based intervention is the meal itself (portion size, fat quality, snacking pattern). A product could be a portion-controlled, moderate-energy lunch or a 10%-of-energy night snack, but the benefit is avoiding a heavy meal, not an active ingredient.", "formats": ["portion-controlled light lunch boxes (about 400-600 kcal, unsaturated fats, protein, whole grains)", "night-shift / all-nighter snack packs (about 200 kcal, roughly 10% of daily energy)", "educational meal-planning guidance for students around lectures and exams"], "stability": "Standard food stability; no special issues.", "taste": "No constraint; ordinary foods.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Not applicable; effects relate to energy load, fat type and timing.", "dose_feasibility": "Trivially feasible: the 'doses' studied are ordinary meals (305 vs 922 kcal lunch; 10% vs 30% of daily energy at night).", "cost_availability": "Ordinary food cost; widely available.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 4, "effect_magnitude": 3, "safety": 9, "reproducibility": 4, "food_suitability": 8, "cost_access": 9, "rationale": "Evidence quality 4: post-meal sleepiness is consistent (B) but composition and timing effects on cognition in the target population are C/D. Effect magnitude 3: effects are tens of ms and a few lapses, relevant mainly to monotonous or night tasks; no enhancement. Safety 9: ordinary foods; minor situational risks. Reproducibility 4: meal-size and post-meal sleepiness reproduce; macronutrient results contradict each other. Food suitability 8: the intervention is the meal itself. Cost/access 9: no special ingredients."}, "research_gaps": ["Preregistered, adequately powered crossover trials of realistic lunches (light vs heavy; saturated vs unsaturated fat) in students with afternoon lectures or exams", "Trials of meal timing and cognition in day-living young adults (late dinner, eating window) with objective sleep", "Night-snack composition trials (protein vs carbohydrate) for shift workers and students studying overnight", "Replication of Gupta 2019 in real shift workers and of Qian 2022 with cognitive endpoints", "Standardised, sensitive cognitive batteries and reporting of effect sizes with CIs in meal studies", "Individual moderators: chronotype, insulin sensitivity, habitual diet, sleep debt"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 5.7, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "pass", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": "C", "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": "C", "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"vigilance": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 9, "n_studies": 28, "n_verified": 28, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 14, "n_systematic_reviews": 2, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["harm", "inconsistent", "insufficient", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "biotin-b7", "name": "Biotin (vitamin B7)", "category": "B", "category_name": "Vitamins", "aliases": ["biotin", "vitamin B7", "vitamin H", "coenzyme R", "d-biotin", "MD1003 (pharmaceutical high-dose biotin)"], "food_sources": ["eggs (cooked; raw egg white contains avidin, which binds and blocks biotin absorption)", "nuts and seeds", "liver and organ meats", "dairy products", "salmon", "sweet potatoes", "endogenous synthesis by gut bacteria contributes an uncertain but probably modest amount"], "active_constituents": ["biotin, the covalently bound coenzyme for five carboxylases (acetyl-CoA carboxylase 1 and 2, pyruvate carboxylase, propionyl-CoA carboxylase, methylcrotonyl-CoA carboxylase) involved in fatty-acid synthesis, gluconeogenesis and amino-acid catabolism"], "one_line_verdict": "Biotin deficiency is genuinely rare and its neurological signs (lethargy, depression, hallucinations) resolve with repletion, but no RCT shows biotin improves cognition in anyone, a large phase 3 trial of high-dose biotin in a neurological disease was clearly negative, and the single most important fact about biotin for a food/supplement company is that it causes dangerous false laboratory test results, not any claimed brain benefit.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. Biotin is the covalently bound coenzyme for five carboxylases central to fatty-acid synthesis, gluconeogenesis and branched-chain amino-acid catabolism; this biochemistry is established. True dietary biotin deficiency is rare because requirements are low, food sources are widespread, and gut bacteria likely contribute some biotin, but it can occur with prolonged raw-egg-white consumption (avidin binding), biotinidase deficiency (a genetic disorder), prolonged anticonvulsant use, or long-term total parenteral nutrition without biotin supplementation; neurological signs of deficiency include lethargy, depression, hallucinations and paresthesias, alongside the classic dermatological triad (alopecia, periorificial dermatitis, conjunctivitis), and these reverse with biotin repletion (general clinical nutrition knowledge; specific case-series/deficiency sources were not independently re-verified with primary citations in this unit beyond the searches below, which found essentially no indexed literature directly on biotin deficiency case series). Pharmacological high-dose biotin in neurological disease. The most substantial body of RCT evidence on biotin and the nervous system comes from trials of MD1003 (pharmaceutical-grade biotin, 100 mg three times daily, i.e. 300 mg/day - roughly 10,000x the adult adequate intake) in progressive multiple sclerosis, a neurodegenerative condition, not a cognitive-enhancement context. An earlier smaller trial (MS-SPI) reported improved disability outcomes, but the larger, industry-funded, phase 3 confirmatory trial (SPI2, n=642, 15-20 months) found no significant benefit: 12% of the MD1003 group improved on the composite disability/walking-speed endpoint versus 9% on placebo (OR 1.35, 95% CI 0.81-2.26, not significant), with similar rates of adverse events (84% vs 85%) and serious adverse events (26% vs 26%) in both groups [cree2020spi2]. This is a clear null/negative result for a specific neurological disability outcome in a clinical population, and it does not inform cognition in healthy adults; it must not be cited as evidence that biotin benefits the nervous system or brain function more generally. Safety signal of overriding practical importance. The SPI2 trial explicitly reported that despite mitigation strategies, high-dose biotin caused inaccurate laboratory results in tests using biotinylated antibodies (immunoassays for thyroid hormones, troponin, hormones and other analytes), a well-recognised clinical laboratory hazard that has caused real-world diagnostic errors, including at least one reported death associated with a missed diagnosis due to biotin interference in the broader clinical literature (general knowledge; the SPI2 trial itself is the directly verified source for this unit) [cree2020spi2]. A single RCT (PMID 33269113) also documented biotin interference specifically in a point-of-care HIV immunoassay. Healthy young adults. No RCT isolating biotin with a cognitive, mood or alertness endpoint in healthy young adults, students or knowledge workers was identified in this search. Hair/skin/nail marketing claims. Biotin is heavily marketed for hair, skin and nail quality in healthy (non-deficient) people; no RCT in biotin-replete healthy adults supporting these claims was identified in this search (a dedicated search for such trials returned zero PubMed hits), consistent with the clinical consensus that biotin supplementation benefits only genuinely deficient individuals. What is unknown. Whether biotin at any dose affects mood, attention or energy in replete adults; the true population prevalence of marginal biotin status in Morocco/EU/US.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Biotin deficiency is rare in people eating a varied diet and causes well-described neurological and dermatological signs that reverse with repletion; this is legitimate deficiency-correction nutrition and underlies the EU-authorised 'normal psychological function'/'normal functioning of the nervous system' claims at source level. There is no credible human evidence that biotin enhances cognition, mood, hair, skin or nails in adequately nourished people: the only large, rigorous RCT evidence available (SPI2, in a neurological disease population at a massive pharmacological dose) was negative for its primary neurological outcome [cree2020spi2], and no RCT supporting hair/skin/nail or cognitive enhancement claims in healthy biotin-replete adults was found. This is one of the clearest deficiency-vs-enhancement gaps among the B vitamins: deficiency correction is real and important in rare cases; enhancement claims common in consumer marketing have essentially no supporting trial evidence.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Biotin is one of the most heavily marketed 'beauty vitamin' ingredients (hair growth, nail strength, skin glow) and is sometimes folded into 'brain/energy' B-complex blends. The evidence does not support cognitive, mood, hair, skin or nail enhancement claims in biotin-replete healthy adults; a dedicated search for RCTs of biotin on hair/nails in healthy adults returned no results, and the only large controlled trial of high-dose biotin on a nervous-system outcome (multiple sclerosis disability) was negative. The single most commercially and legally important fact about biotin is not a benefit claim at all: high-dose biotin supplementation causes false results in biotinylated-antibody-based laboratory immunoassays (thyroid function, troponin, hormone panels, point-of-care HIV tests), a recognised source of diagnostic error; this has prompted FDA safety communications (general regulatory knowledge; not independently re-fetched live in this session). Any Brain Food product containing biotin, even at modest doses, should carry a clear consumer warning to inform lab personnel/physicians before blood testing, and should never market biotin using unsupported hair/skin/cognition claims beyond the narrow authorised nutritional-adequacy wording.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Dietary biotin is released from protein by biotinidase, absorbed in the small intestine via a sodium-dependent multivitamin transporter, and covalently attached (via holocarboxylase synthetase) to five carboxylase enzymes essential for fatty-acid synthesis, gluconeogenesis and branched-chain amino-acid catabolism. Deficiency impairs these pathways and produces neurological and dermatological signs; the exact mechanism linking biotin deficiency to lethargy, depression and hallucinations is not fully elucidated but is thought to involve disrupted fatty-acid and energy metabolism in the nervous system. There is no established or even plausible mechanism by which biotin above adequacy would enhance cognition in a replete brain, and the SPI2 neurological trial result is consistent with that absence.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Dietary biotin -> biotinidase-mediated release from protein -> sodium-dependent multivitamin transporter absorption", "detail": "Established pharmacokinetics; biotinidase deficiency (genetic) blocks this step and causes a treatable deficiency syndrome.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Free biotin -> covalent attachment to apo-carboxylases via holocarboxylase synthetase -> active acetyl-CoA carboxylase, pyruvate carboxylase, propionyl-CoA carboxylase, methylcrotonyl-CoA carboxylase", "detail": "Textbook biochemistry; these carboxylases are essential for fatty-acid synthesis, gluconeogenesis and branched-chain amino-acid catabolism.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Severe biotin deficiency -> impaired fatty-acid/energy metabolism -> neurological signs (lethargy, depression, hallucinations, paresthesias) and dermatological signs, reversible with repletion", "detail": "Established clinical syndrome in biotinidase deficiency, prolonged raw-egg-white ingestion, and long-term unsupplemented parenteral nutrition.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "High-dose pharmacological biotin (300 mg/day, MD1003) hypothesised to enhance oligodendrocyte/neuronal energetics and remyelination in multiple sclerosis", "detail": "Hypothesis tested directly in the SPI2 phase 3 RCT (n=642) and found not significantly different from placebo on the primary disability/walking-speed composite endpoint (OR 1.35, 95% CI 0.81-2.26) [cree2020spi2].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Biotin supplementation in replete healthy adults -> measured cognitive, mood or hair/skin outcome", "detail": "No RCT evidence found; this step is untested and the negative neurological-disease trial result provides no support for extrapolating a benefit to healthy brains.", "evidence_level": "speculative"}], "pathways": ["carboxylase coenzyme function (fatty-acid synthesis, gluconeogenesis, amino-acid catabolism)", "sodium-dependent multivitamin transporter (biotin/pantothenate/lipoate shared transporter)", "biotinidase/holocarboxylase synthetase cycle", "hypothesised oligodendrocyte energetics pathway (MS trials, not confirmed)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_decline", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Adults aged 18-65 with primary or secondary progressive multiple sclerosis, EDSS 3.5-6.5 (a motor-disability population, outcome is disability/walking speed, not cognition per se)", "dose_or_intake": "300 mg/day biotin (MD1003, 100 mg three times daily)", "duration": "15-20 months", "grade": "E", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Large, industry-funded, randomized, double-blind phase 3 trial (n=642) found no significant difference between MD1003 and placebo on the composite disability-improvement/walking-speed endpoint; adverse event rates were similar between groups.", "effect_estimate": "12% (39/326) improved on MD1003 vs 9% (29/316) on placebo; OR 1.35 (95% CI 0.81-2.26), not significant [cree2020spi2]", "certainty_notes": "Single large, well-conducted, pre-registered confirmatory trial; contradicts an earlier smaller positive trial (MS-SPI), illustrating the value of confirmatory phase 3 testing; graded E (not supported) for this specific disability outcome. Not a cognitive outcome and not a healthy-adult population; included to document the largest biotin RCT body of evidence and its key safety finding.", "key_study_ids": ["cree2020spi2"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "other", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "No RCT identified testing biotin on cognition, mood, hair, skin or nails in biotin-replete healthy adults", "dose_or_intake": "not applicable", "duration": "not applicable", "grade": "E", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "A dedicated search for biotin RCTs on hair/nails/cognition in healthy adults returned zero PubMed hits; this documents an absence of supporting evidence for widely marketed consumer claims, not a tested-and-refuted effect.", "effect_estimate": "not applicable (no trials found)", "certainty_notes": "Absence of evidence reported honestly; consistent with clinical consensus that biotin supplementation benefits only deficient individuals.", "key_study_ids": []}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "not tested", "onset": "not applicable", "duration_studied": "not applicable", "domain": "other", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "effect_estimate": "no acute biotin cognition trial identified", "certainty": "insufficient data", "safety": "not applicable", "study_ids": []}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "15-20 months", "population": "clinical_population", "outcome": "disability/walking-speed composite in progressive multiple sclerosis (motor, not cognitive)", "effect_estimate": "OR 1.35 (95% CI 0.81-2.26), not significant", "evidence_quality": "high-quality null result (large RCT) for this specific disease outcome; not applicable to cognition", "limitations": "Clinical disease population, pharmacological mega-dose, industry-funded (MedDay Pharmaceuticals), motor/disability not cognitive endpoint", "study_ids": ["cree2020spi2"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Biotin at nutritional doses (food level, a few to tens of micrograms) is extremely safe with no known toxicity. The overriding safety issue is not toxicity but laboratory test interference: biotin supplementation, even at doses well below the 300 mg/day pharmacological MS dose, can cause clinically significant false results in biotinylated-antibody-based immunoassays (thyroid function tests, troponin, hormone panels, some point-of-care tests), which has caused real diagnostic errors.", "adverse_effects": ["No established direct toxicity at nutritional or typical supplemental doses (up to several mg/day)", "At the 300 mg/day pharmacological MS dose, adverse event rates were similar to placebo in a large RCT, so direct toxicity even at mega-doses appears limited [cree2020spi2]", "Laboratory immunoassay interference causing false test results (thyroid, cardiac troponin, hormones, some point-of-care tests) - the principal practical safety issue"], "contraindications": ["None established for direct toxicity"], "drug_interactions": ["Long-term anticonvulsant use (phenytoin, carbamazepine, primidone) can lower biotin status - relevant to deficiency risk in epilepsy patients, not a toxicity interaction"], "upper_limits": "No UL has been set by EFSA or US NASEM for biotin due to lack of demonstrated toxicity even at high doses; this does not mean high doses are risk-free, given the laboratory-interference issue, which is a functional/diagnostic risk rather than a classic toxicological one.", "sleep_impact": "No evidence of sleep disruption from biotin at any studied dose.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "The SPI2 trial studied 300 mg/day for 15-27 months with safety monitoring and found adverse event rates similar to placebo [cree2020spi2]; nutritional-dose long-term exposure is presumed safe based on widespread dietary and supplement use with no safety signal, though not addressed by a dedicated long-term low-dose RCT in this search.", "daily_use_appropriate": "yes", "special_populations": "Anyone undergoing blood tests that use biotinylated-antibody immunoassays (common for thyroid panels, cardiac troponin, some hormone tests) should be advised to inform their clinician/lab of biotin supplement use and may need to stop supplementation for 1-2 days (or longer for very high doses) before testing; pregnant/lactating women can use standard nutritional-dose biotin without specific concern; biotinidase-deficient individuals require clinical, not food-level, management.", "specialist_review_needed": ["clinical laboratory medicine / endocrinology awareness messaging if any Brain Food product contains biotin, to prevent consumer harm from undetected lab test interference"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "No direct toxicity concern at food-relevant doses, but the well-documented laboratory-test-interference risk (confirmed in two independent sources in this unit) requires a clear consumer warning on any biotin-containing product; this is a real-world harm pathway distinct from classic toxicology and must not be omitted."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Biotin is an authorised vitamin for supplements/fortification (Directive 2002/46/EC). EFSA-authorised claims (Regulation (EU) 432/2012 register, as commonly published; not re-fetched live in this session) include 'biotin contributes to normal psychological function', 'normal functioning of the nervous system', 'normal macronutrient metabolism', and 'maintenance of normal hair/skin/mucous membranes', usable at >=15% NRV per portion - note the hair/skin wording is an authorised nutritional-adequacy claim, not evidence of benefit in replete people.", "us": "Biotin is GRAS and widely used in fortification and dietary supplements under DSHEA; it is heavily marketed for hair/skin/nails using structure/function claims; the FDA has issued public safety communications about biotin interfering with laboratory tests (general regulatory knowledge; not independently re-fetched live in this session).", "morocco": "Biotin is included in some fortified food and supplement products; ONSSA governs food-grade additive and supplement status; specific claim wording and any biotin-specific lab-interference consumer-warning requirement were not independently verified in this session - uncertain.", "permitted_claims_examples": ["EU: 'Biotin contributes to the maintenance of normal hair' (>=15% NRV per portion) - wording as commonly published in the EU claims register; not re-verified live in this session; this is a nutritional-adequacy claim, not an enhancement claim, and should not be presented as such in marketing"], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "None; biotin is a conventional, long-established vitamin. MD1003 (pharmaceutical-grade high-dose biotin) is a distinct, regulated drug product and is out of scope for a food formulation.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Good at nutritional/claim-level doses (micrograms per serving); any product containing biotin above trace/claim levels should carry a lab-interference consumer warning regardless of food-safety status.", "formats": ["d-biotin added to fortified foods, beverages, bars at microgram-level claim doses"], "stability": "Biotin is relatively stable to heat and light at food-processing conditions compared to thiamine and riboflavin (general food-science knowledge; not independently re-verified with a primary source in this unit).", "taste": "No significant taste issues at claim-level doses.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Free d-biotin from food and supplements is well absorbed via the sodium-dependent multivitamin transporter; protein-bound biotin requires biotinidase for release, which is normally efficient in healthy adults.", "dose_feasibility": "The EU claim-level dose is a tiny fraction of a food serving and easily achievable; the 300 mg/day MS-trial dose is roughly 10,000x the claim-level dose and is not relevant to or feasible within a food product.", "cost_availability": "Biotin is an inexpensive, widely available commodity ingredient.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 1, "effect_magnitude": 0, "safety": 6, "reproducibility": 2, "food_suitability": 7, "cost_access": 9, "rationale": "evidence_quality very low (1): the largest, best-quality RCT body of evidence for biotin and the nervous system is a clear null result in a clinical disease population (grade E for that outcome); no supporting RCT exists for cognition, mood, hair, skin or nails in healthy adults. effect_magnitude near zero (0): no positive effect size exists anywhere in the human literature reviewed for any brain-relevant or marketed outcome. safety moderate (6), not high, specifically because of the well-documented and clinically consequential laboratory-interference hazard, which is a real, actionable risk distinct from classic toxicity. reproducibility low (2): the one large body of RCT evidence (MS trials) shows an earlier positive trial failing to replicate in a larger confirmatory trial - a textbook non-replication. food_suitability good (7), deducted slightly to flag the mandatory consumer lab-interference warning. cost_access high (9): cheap commodity ingredient."}, "research_gaps": ["No RCT isolating biotin with a cognitive, mood or alertness endpoint in healthy young adults, students or knowledge workers.", "No RCT confirming or refuting hair/skin/nail benefits of biotin supplementation in biotin-replete (non-deficient) healthy adults - this is a major, commercially relevant gap.", "No modern prevalence data located in this search on marginal biotin status in Morocco, EU or US populations.", "The MS trial literature (SPI2 null result) cannot resolve whether biotin has any role in healthy-brain function; a dedicated, lower-dose, healthy-population trial has not been done.", "No systematic quantification found in this search of how common or severe biotin-related laboratory misdiagnoses are at the population level, beyond the SPI2 trial's own finding and one HIV point-of-care case."], "_derived": {"composite_score": 3.3, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": null, "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": null, "best_grade_overall": null, "benefit_domains": {}, "n_outcome_rows": 2, "n_studies": 2, "n_verified": 2, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 1, "n_systematic_reviews": 0, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["insufficient", "no_benefit"]}}, {"id": "folate-b9", "name": "Folate / folic acid (vitamin B9)", "category": "B", "category_name": "Vitamins", "aliases": ["folate", "vitamin B9", "folic acid", "pteroylmonoglutamic acid", "5-methyltetrahydrofolate (5-MTHF, L-methylfolate, calcium L-methylfolate, (6S)-5-MTHF glucosamine salt)", "folinic acid", "dietary folate equivalents (DFE)"], "food_sources": ["dark green leafy vegetables (spinach, chard), asparagus, broccoli", "pulses: lentils, chickpeas, beans", "liver (also very high in vitamin A)", "citrus fruit, avocado", "fortified flour/bread and breakfast cereals (folic acid)"], "active_constituents": ["5-methyltetrahydrofolate (main circulating form; methyl donor for remethylation of homocysteine to methionine via B12-dependent methionine synthase)", "tetrahydrofolate derivatives for purine/thymidylate synthesis", "folic acid: synthetic oxidised form requiring reduction by dihydrofolate reductase"], "one_line_verdict": "Folate adequacy matters for pregnancy, blood and possibly for cognition in older adults with raised homocysteine or low folate status, but in folate-replete people, and in young adults and students specifically, folic acid supplements do not improve cognition, and excess folic acid without B12 carries a masking and possibly cognitive risk.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. Folic acid (0.4-2.5 mg/day, usually with B12) lowers plasma homocysteine by about 25-30% [clarke2014]. Raised homocysteine and low folate are associated with dementia and cognitive decline in cohorts (folate intake HR 0.61, 95% CI 0.47-0.78 for dementia [wang2022]), but prospective associations are not robust in other syntheses [zhang2020], and lowering homocysteine does not reliably change cognition. Replete older adults. An individual-participant meta-analysis of 11 trials (about 22,000 people, mean 2.3-5 years) found no effect on memory, speed, executive function or global cognition (z difference 0.00, 95% CI -0.05 to 0.06), excluding a slowing of cognitive aging greater than one month per year [clarke2014]; the Cochrane review rated this 'probably little or no effect' with moderate certainty [rutjes2018]. Large individual trials agree: B-PROOF (n=2,919, high homocysteine) [vanderzwaluw2014], WAFACS [kang2008], VITATOPS [hankey2013], SU.FOL.OM3 [andreeva2011] and McMahon [mcmahon2006] were null. A pooled MMSE gain of 0.14 points [wang2022] is statistically significant but clinically trivial. Where effects appear. FACIT, in a non-fortified Dutch population with raised homocysteine, found that 800 ug/day folic acid for 3 years improved memory (z difference 0.132, 95% CI 0.032-0.233) and processing speed [durga2007]; a distressed older Australian sample improved memory slightly (d 0.15-0.18) [walker2012]; in WAFACS benefit was confined to women with low dietary B intake [kang2008]. In MCI, VITACOG slowed brain atrophy (0.76 versus 1.08 %/year) and benefited cognition only in participants with homocysteine above about 11 umol/L, possibly only with good omega-3 status [smith2010, dejager2012, douaud2013, oulhaj2016], but an independent replication was null [kwok2020] and the Cochrane MCI review finds no evidence of cognitive benefit [mccleery2018]; very large effects reported in Chinese MCI/AD trials [xu2024, ma2019] carry high risk of bias. The pattern is consistent: benefit, if any, is limited to people with poor folate status or high homocysteine, over years, with small effects. Omega-3 status modifies the effect in post hoc analyses, but in opposite directions: FACIT benefited only participants with low omega-3 [vansoest2021], whereas VITACOG and B-PROOF benefits appeared with high DHA [oulhaj2016, vansoest2022]; this inconsistency does not support a B-vitamin plus omega-3 product on cognitive grounds. Young adults and students. The only trial covering young adults (35 days, 750 ug folate, small arms) reported slight memory effects and no mood effect [bryan2002]. No trial in students exists, and no acute effect is expected. Safety. Folic acid intakes above 1000 ug/day (EFSA UL, applies also to 5-MTHF salts [efsa2023folate]) can mask B12 deficiency, and high folate with low B12 is associated with worse cognitive test scores [morris2007, bailey2020]. Five-year cancer risk was not increased (RR 1.06, 0.99-1.13) [vollset2013]. Claims that methylfolate is superior rest on lower unmetabolised folic acid, not on clinical outcomes [frye2026].", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "This is a textbook deficiency-correction nutrient. Folate deficiency causes megaloblastic anaemia and, in pregnancy, neural tube defects; low folate and high homocysteine are risk markers for cognitive decline. Trials that suggest cognitive benefit enrolled people with raised homocysteine in non-fortified populations [durga2007], low dietary intake [kang2008 subgroup] or MCI with high homocysteine [dejager2012]. Trials in replete or fortified populations are null [clarke2014, rutjes2018, vanderzwaluw2014]. There is no evidence of enhancement in adequately nourished young adults [bryan2002]. Young women of reproductive age are the one young-adult group where folate adequacy is a strong public-health priority (neural tube defect prevention), which is an authorised EU claim context ('maternal tissue growth during pregnancy') but not a cognitive one.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Folate is marketed for 'brain health', 'memory', 'mood' and, increasingly, as 'active methylfolate' for 'MTHFR' carriers. Evidence supports only adequacy: the EU allows 'contributes to normal psychological function' and 'normal homocysteine metabolism' at source-level amounts (>=15% NRV, i.e. >=30 ug). 'Supports memory' or 'prevents cognitive decline' are not authorised in the EU and would not meet FTC substantiation for healthy adults. 'Methylfolate is better absorbed/safer' is not established clinically [frye2026]; EFSA applies the same UL to 5-MTHF salts [efsa2023folate]. Leafy greens, pulses and fortified bread provide adequate folate; for most consumers in fortifying countries a supplement adds nothing cognitively.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Food folates (polyglutamates) are deconjugated and absorbed in the jejunum; folic acid is absorbed more efficiently but must be reduced by dihydrofolate reductase, which saturates at a few hundred micrograms, producing unmetabolised folic acid. 5-MTHF donates its methyl group to homocysteine via B12-dependent methionine synthase, forming methionine and S-adenosylmethionine, the universal methyl donor for DNA, phospholipid and neurotransmitter methylation. Folate is actively transported into CSF by folate receptor alpha. Proposed cognitive mechanisms are lower homocysteine (vascular and neurotoxic effects), better methylation and reduced brain atrophy; these are supported at biomarker level in high-homocysteine MCI but do not translate into cognitive benefit in replete people.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Folic acid / food folate -> intestinal absorption -> DHFR reduction -> 5-MTHF in plasma", "detail": "Folic acid 0.8 mg/day raised serum folate ~576% [durga2007]; supplemental folic acid quadruples plasma folic acid (UMFA) [vollset2013]; 5-MTHF lowers UMFA vs folic acid (SMD 0.99) [frye2026].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "5-MTHF + B12 (methionine synthase) -> homocysteine remethylation -> lower plasma tHcy", "detail": "tHcy lowered 25-30% in trials [clarke2014, smith2010, durga2007].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Lower tHcy -> slower brain atrophy in AD-vulnerable regions", "detail": "One MCI trial and its secondary analysis, confined to high tHcy; possibly dependent on omega-3 status [smith2010, douaud2013, oulhaj2016].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Lower tHcy / better methylation -> slower cognitive decline", "detail": "Positive only in high-tHcy or low-status subgroups [durga2007, dejager2012]; null in replete populations [clarke2014, vanderzwaluw2014].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "High folate with low B12 -> worsened neurocognitive effects of B12 deficiency", "detail": "Cross-sectional NHANES associations [morris2007, bailey2020]; model-system mechanisms reviewed in the vitamin-b12 monograph.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Measured human cognitive outcome", "detail": "No effect on global cognition in replete older adults (z 0.00) [clarke2014]; no data in students; small, unreplicated effects in high-tHcy groups.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}], "pathways": ["one-carbon metabolism and methionine cycle (SAM-dependent methylation)", "homocysteine remethylation (B12-dependent methionine synthase)", "purine/thymidylate synthesis", "folate receptor alpha transport into CSF", "DHFR saturation and unmetabolised folic acid"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Cognitively healthy adults mostly 60-80 y, many with vascular disease; mostly folate-replete or fortified", "dose_or_intake": "folic acid 0.4-2.5 mg/day, usually + B12 (+/- B6)", "duration": "2-7 years", "grade": "B", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "No effect on global cognition, memory, speed or executive function; effect on cognitive aging excluded beyond 1 month per year.", "effect_estimate": "domain-composite z 0.00 (-0.05 to 0.06); MMSE-type z -0.01 (-0.03 to 0.02) [clarke2014]; SMD -0.03 to 0.06 [rutjes2018]", "certainty_notes": "Large consistent IPD evidence; downgraded one level for indirectness (insensitive MMSE-type tests, cognition often secondary, vascular populations), matching Cochrane 'moderate'.", "key_study_ids": ["clarke2014", "rutjes2018", "vanderzwaluw2014", "kang2008", "mcmahon2006", "hankey2013", "andreeva2011"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Healthy older adults with raised tHcy (FACIT, B-PROOF, McMahon) or psychological distress (Beyond Ageing)", "dose_or_intake": "folic acid 0.4-1 mg/day (+/- B12)", "duration": "2-3 years", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "FACIT and Beyond Ageing showed small memory benefits; B-PROOF and McMahon (also high tHcy) were null.", "effect_estimate": "memory z 0.132 (0.032 to 0.233) [durga2007]; d 0.15-0.18 [walker2012]; NS [vanderzwaluw2014, mcmahon2006]", "certainty_notes": "Inconsistency between similar trials; small effect sizes; positive results in non-fortified/low-status settings.", "key_study_ids": ["durga2007", "walker2012", "vanderzwaluw2014", "mcmahon2006", "vansoest2021", "vansoest2022"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_decline", "population": "deficient_or_low_baseline", "population_detail": "Older adults with low dietary B intake or low folate status / non-fortified diets", "dose_or_intake": "folic acid 0.4-2.5 mg/day", "duration": "2-5 years", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Benefit appears concentrated in low-status subgroups; supported by observational folate-dementia associations.", "effect_estimate": "WAFACS low-intake subgroup benefit (no effect size in abstract) [kang2008]; FACIT [durga2007]; folate intake and dementia HR 0.61 (0.47-0.78) (cohorts) [wang2022]", "certainty_notes": "Subgroup and observational evidence; prospective associations not robust in [zhang2020].", "key_study_ids": ["kang2008", "durga2007", "wang2022", "zhang2020"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_mci", "population_detail": "MCI, mostly >65-70 y; subgroups with high tHcy", "dose_or_intake": "folic acid 0.4-0.8 mg/day +/- B12/B6", "duration": "6-24 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "VITACOG benefit limited to high-tHcy subgroup; independent placebo-controlled replication null; Chinese trials positive but high risk of bias.", "effect_estimate": "subgroup only [dejager2012]; CDR-SOB NS [kwok2020]; Cochrane: no evidence of benefit [mccleery2018]; MCI SMD 0.38-1.10 (high RoB) [xu2024]", "certainty_notes": "Downgraded for inconsistency, risk of bias and selective subgroups; patient population.", "key_study_ids": ["dejager2012", "kwok2020", "mccleery2018", "xu2024", "ma2019", "oulhaj2016"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "neuroplasticity_biomarkers", "population": "older_adults_mci", "population_detail": "MCI >70 y (VITACOG MRI subset)", "dose_or_intake": "FA 0.8 mg + B12 0.5 mg + B6 20 mg", "duration": "2 years", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Slower whole-brain and regional grey-matter atrophy, confined to high tHcy.", "effect_estimate": "0.76 vs 1.08 %/y (p=0.001) [smith2010]; up to 7-fold regional reduction [douaud2013]", "certainty_notes": "Single trial, surrogate outcome, investigator patents; never independently replicated.", "key_study_ids": ["smith2010", "douaud2013"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "dementia_risk", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Non-demented adults >=50 y (cohorts); trials rarely report dementia", "dose_or_intake": "dietary folate intake / supplementation", "duration": "years", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Higher folate intake associated with lower dementia risk in some cohorts, not robust in others; trial dementia data sparse and null.", "effect_estimate": "HR 0.61 (0.47-0.78) [wang2022]; prospective NS [zhang2020]", "certainty_notes": "Observational confounding (diet quality, socioeconomic status), reverse causation.", "key_study_ids": ["wang2022", "zhang2020"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy younger and middle-aged women (small folate arm)", "dose_or_intake": "750 ug/day folate", "duration": "35 days", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "Slight selective memory effects across vitamin arms; no mood effect.", "effect_estimate": "not reported [bryan2002]", "certainty_notes": "Single small multi-arm trial; no replication.", "key_study_ids": ["bryan2002"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "students", "population_detail": "University students", "dose_or_intake": "not studied", "duration": "not studied", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No trial in students; in fortifying countries most students are folate-replete, so no benefit is expected.", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "Absence of data.", "key_study_ids": []}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "alertness", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "No acute folate cognition trials identified", "dose_or_intake": "not studied", "duration": "single dose", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No acute effect expected (folate acts through slow tissue and methylation pools).", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "Absence of data.", "key_study_ids": []}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "deficient_or_low_baseline", "population_detail": "Safety: older adults with low B12 status and high folate/UMFA (US, fortified)", "dose_or_intake": "serum folate >59-74 nmol/L or UMFA >1 nmol/L (fortification + supplements)", "duration": "cross-sectional", "grade": "C", "direction": "harm", "effect_summary": "High folate combined with low B12 associated with more cognitive impairment on some tests; protective when B12 normal.", "effect_estimate": "OR 2.6 (1.1-6.1) [morris2007]; OR 2.87 (1.85-4.45) for low DSST [bailey2020]", "certainty_notes": "Cross-sectional, inconsistent across tests; mechanistic support mostly from model systems (see vitamin-b12 monograph).", "key_study_ids": ["morris2007", "bailey2020"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "not studied", "onset": "not applicable", "duration_studied": "no acute trials identified", "domain": "any", "population": "healthy young adults", "effect_estimate": "no data", "certainty": "D (insufficient)", "safety": "food-level doses safe", "study_ids": []}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "2-7 years", "population": "replete older adults (vascular risk)", "outcome": "global cognition", "effect_estimate": "z 0.00 (-0.05 to 0.06)", "evidence_quality": "B (no benefit)", "limitations": "insensitive tests; secondary outcomes", "study_ids": ["clarke2014", "rutjes2018", "kang2008", "hankey2013", "andreeva2011"]}, {"duration": "3 years", "population": "older adults, raised tHcy, non-fortified", "outcome": "memory, processing speed", "effect_estimate": "z 0.132 memory; 0.087 speed", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "single trial; contradicted by B-PROOF", "study_ids": ["durga2007", "vanderzwaluw2014", "mcmahon2006"]}, {"duration": "2 years", "population": "older adults with distress", "outcome": "memory (TICS-M)", "effect_estimate": "d 0.15-0.18", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "telephone tests; multiple outcomes", "study_ids": ["walker2012"]}, {"duration": "2 years", "population": "MCI", "outcome": "brain atrophy; cognition in high-tHcy subgroup", "effect_estimate": "atrophy 0.76 vs 1.08 %/y; cognition subgroup only", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "single trial; COI; failed replication", "study_ids": ["smith2010", "dejager2012", "douaud2013", "kwok2020", "mccleery2018"]}, {"duration": "35 days", "population": "women incl. young adults", "outcome": "memory, mood", "effect_estimate": "slight memory; mood null", "evidence_quality": "D", "limitations": "tiny arms", "study_ids": ["bryan2002"]}, {"duration": "~5 years", "population": "trial participants (50,000)", "outcome": "cancer incidence (safety)", "effect_estimate": "RR 1.06 (0.99-1.13)", "evidence_quality": "A (no substantial harm over 5 y)", "limitations": "short for cancer", "study_ids": ["vollset2013"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Food folate has no known toxicity. Supplemental folic acid and 5-MTHF salts are safe up to the EFSA/IOM UL of 1000 ug/day for adults; the main risks above it are masking the anaemia of B12 deficiency (allowing neurological damage to progress) and a possible adverse interaction of high folate with low B12 on cognition. Five-year cancer risk in trials was not increased.", "adverse_effects": ["masking of haematological signs of B12 deficiency at high folic acid intakes (basis of UL) [efsa2023folate]", "association of high folate + low B12 with cognitive impairment and anaemia (observational) [morris2007, bailey2020]", "unmetabolised folic acid in plasma at intakes above a few hundred ug; clinical harm not established [frye2026]", "no significant cancer increase over ~5 years (RR 1.06, 0.99-1.13) [vollset2013]", "high-dose B-vitamin combination (FA 2.5 mg) associated with faster GFR decline in diabetic nephropathy [house2010]"], "contraindications": ["undiagnosed B12 deficiency or pernicious anaemia (do not use high-dose folic acid without B12 assessment)", "people treated with methotrexate or other antifolates for cancer (medical supervision; general pharmacology, not re-verified)"], "drug_interactions": ["methotrexate, pemetrexed (antifolates): folate may reduce efficacy in oncology; folic acid is co-prescribed in low-dose methotrexate for arthritis (general pharmacology; not re-verified)", "antiepileptics (phenytoin, phenobarbital, primidone, valproate): reduce folate status, and high folic acid may lower phenytoin levels (general pharmacology; not re-verified)", "sulfasalazine, trimethoprim: reduce folate status"], "upper_limits": "EFSA 2023: UL 1000 ug/day for adults (including pregnancy and lactation) for combined folic acid and 5-MTHF salts; natural food folate excluded [efsa2023folate]. US: 1000 ug/day synthetic folate (IOM 1998; NIH ODS fact sheet could not be fetched, HTTP 403; value from agent knowledge).", "sleep_impact": "None known.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Up to 5-7 years in cardiovascular trials with 0.4-2.5 mg/day [vollset2013, kang2008, clarke2014].", "daily_use_appropriate": "yes", "special_populations": "Women who could become pregnant: 400 ug/day folic acid is standard public-health advice for neural tube defect prevention (beneficial, not a risk). Older adults, vegans, metformin/PPI users: check B12 before high folate. Children: lower ULs (EFSA age-specific; not extracted). Kidney disease: avoid high-dose combinations [house2010]. Cancer patients and people with colorectal adenomas: specialist advice.", "specialist_review_needed": ["haematologist/physician for B12-masking risk", "clinical pharmacist (antifolate and antiepileptic interactions)", "obstetric review for any pregnancy-related messaging"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "Pass at <=200-400 ug folic acid (or 5-MTHF equivalent) per day, always combined with B12 at >=NRV; fails above the 1000 ug UL or if sold as high-dose folic acid without B12."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Permitted vitamin; forms permitted for supplements and fortification include pteroylmonoglutamic acid (folic acid), calcium-L-methylfolate and (6S)-5-methyltetrahydrofolic acid glucosamine salt (the latter authorised under the novel food framework; form list from general knowledge, not re-fetched). Authorised claims (Regulation (EU) 432/2012): normal psychological function, normal homocysteine metabolism, reduction of tiredness and fatigue, maternal tissue growth during pregnancy, normal amino acid synthesis, normal blood formation, immune function, cell division; condition: at least a 'source of' folate (15% NRV; NRV 200 ug, so 30 ug; NRV from general knowledge). A disease risk-reduction claim on supplemental folate and neural tube defects exists under Article 14 (not re-verified here). No authorised claim mentions memory, cognition or cognitive decline. National maximum levels for supplements vary; EFSA UL 1000 ug/day.", "us": "Dietary supplements: a lawful dietary ingredient marketed before 15 Oct 1994, so no NDI notification is needed for the standard forms; structure/function claims (e.g. 'supports normal nervous system function', 'supports energy metabolism') are allowed with the DSHEA disclaimer and FDA notification within 30 days, but under FTC standards claims of improved memory, focus or 'brain power' in non-deficient adults would need competent and reliable scientific evidence that the trials reviewed here do not provide. Conventional foods: fortification must respect FDA's fortification policy (21 CFR 104.20) and the ingredient's food-additive/GRAS status. IOM/Food and Nutrition Board ULs (1998): folic acid 1000 ug/day for adults (agent knowledge). Folic acid fortification of enriched cereal grains is mandatory in the US; folate must be labelled in mcg DFE with folic acid in parentheses (2016 labelling rule). The only folate health claim is 21 CFR 101.79 on neural tube defects (US regulatory draft of this project). Folic acid as a food additive has use limits (21 CFR 172.345, from agent knowledge; not re-fetched).", "morocco": "Uncertain. Food supplements in Morocco have been regulated as foodstuffs under ONSSA (law 28-07 on food safety) with product registration; 2026 Moroccan press reports describe a pending reform (draft decree postponed in July 2026) that would reserve some high-risk supplement categories to pharmacies and reportedly applies national maximum doses (e.g. professionals cite 10 ug vitamin D and 180 mg vitamin C caps). No official Moroccan maximum level for vitamin B6, folic acid or B12 and no Moroccan list of authorised health claims was found in this unit; Moroccan practice is commonly assumed to follow Codex/EU references, but this is not verified. Mandatory wheat-flour fortification exists in Morocco (iron; premix composition, including whether folic acid is currently added, not verified here). Sources: hespress.com/medias24.com/le360.ma press search results (accessed 2026-10-25; medias24 article returned HTTP 403). A Moroccan regulatory consultant and ONSSA/Bulletin officiel check are required.", "permitted_claims_examples": ["Folate contributes to normal psychological function", "Folate contributes to normal homocysteine metabolism", "Folate contributes to the reduction of tiredness and fatigue", "Folate contributes to maternal tissue growth during pregnancy"], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "Folic acid and calcium L-methylfolate: no issue. (6S)-5-MTHF glucosamine salt is a novel-food-authorised source in the EU (specification-bound; not re-verified). In the US, methylfolate salts are marketed as dietary ingredients; NDI status of specific salts not checked.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Excellent: folic acid is cheap, stable and used in mass fortification; 5-MTHF salts are less stable (oxidation, heat).", "formats": ["fortified bread, flour or cereal", "bar or drink powder (B-vitamin premix)", "tablet/capsule"], "stability": "Folic acid stable in dry foods and moderately heat-stable; losses in baking and in acidic liquids exposed to light; 5-MTHF degrades faster (industry knowledge; not verified here).", "taste": "None at microgram levels; yellow colour at high concentration.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Folic acid with food is roughly 1.7x as available as food folate (basis of the DFE convention: 1 ug DFE = 1 ug food folate = 0.6 ug folic acid with food; IOM convention from agent knowledge). Natural folates are unstable to cooking.", "dose_feasibility": "Trial doses (400-800 ug) fit easily in a serving but in fortifying markets would add to intakes from bread/cereal; a source-claim needs only 30 ug. A Brain Food serving should stay at 60-200 ug folic acid with B12.", "cost_availability": "Commodity vitamin; 5-MTHF salts more expensive.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 3, "effect_magnitude": 1.5, "safety": 7, "reproducibility": 3, "food_suitability": 9, "cost_access": 9, "rationale": "evidence_quality 3: grade B evidence exists but for NO benefit in replete older adults; benefit rows are C (high-tHcy/low-status older adults, MCI) and D for young adults/students. effect_magnitude 1.5: best positive trial z 0.13 over 3 years; pooled MMSE 0.14 points; nil in replete people. safety 7: very safe at food levels; UL 1000 ug, B12 masking and high-folate/low-B12 cognitive signal require pairing with B12 and capping dose. reproducibility 3: FACIT and VITACOG findings not replicated by B-PROOF, McMahon or Kwok. food_suitability 9: proven fortificant. cost_access 9: very cheap; Morocco status unverified."}, "research_gaps": ["Trials of folate in young adults with measured low folate status (e.g. non-fortified populations, including Morocco) on cognition", "Replication of VITACOG in high-tHcy MCI with DHA stratification (prespecified)", "Long-term (>5 y) cancer and cognitive safety of chronic UMFA exposure", "Moroccan population folate/B12/homocysteine status data", "Head-to-head 5-MTHF vs folic acid on clinical outcomes"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 4.7, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": "C", "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"cognitive_decline": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 10, "n_studies": 28, "n_verified": 28, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 13, "n_systematic_reviews": 5, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["harm", "inconsistent", "insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "multivitamin-mineral-supplements", "name": "Multivitamin/multimineral supplements", "category": "B", "category_name": "Vitamins", "aliases": ["multivitamin", "MVM", "multivitamin-multimineral", "multinutrient supplement", "A-Z supplement", "Centrum Silver (brand used in COSMOS/PHS II)", "Berocca (brand)", "Supradyn (brand)", "Swisse Ultivite (brand)"], "food_sources": ["Not a food: the nutrients in an MVM are supplied by a varied diet (vegetables, fruit, whole grains, legumes, dairy, eggs, fish, meat)", "Fortified foods (breakfast cereals, fortified flour - mandatory iron/folic acid in Morocco - iodised salt, fortified plant drinks) supply part of the same nutrients"], "active_constituents": ["Typically 10-13 vitamins (A as retinyl ester and/or beta-carotene, C, D, E, K, B1, B2, B3, B5, B6, B12, folic acid, biotin) and 8-15 minerals (iron, zinc, iodine, selenium, copper, magnesium, calcium, manganese, chromium, molybdenum) at roughly 50-300% of reference intakes; no legal definition of 'multivitamin' in the EU or US; 'high-potency B' products (e.g. Berocca) contain several-fold the RDA of B vitamins"], "one_line_verdict": "A standard ~100%-RDA multivitamin slightly slows cognitive ageing in adults over 60 (about 0.07 SD over 2-3 years in COSMOS, not seen in the earlier Physicians' Health Study II), but in healthy, adequately nourished young adults and students the trials show biomarker changes and small, mostly subjective mood effects, not reliable cognitive gains; its honest role is filling dietary gaps, not enhancement.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. The strongest evidence comes from the COSMOS trial in US adults aged 60 and older. Three cognitive substudies using one commercial MVM (Centrum Silver) all favoured MVM: global cognition z 0.07 (95% CI 0.02-0.12) over 3 y by telephone [baker2023], better immediate recall at 1 y by web testing (P=0.025) with null secondary outcomes [yeung2023], and better episodic memory over 2 y in person (0.12 SD) [vyas2024]. Pooled within COSMOS, the effect was 0.07 SD for global cognition and 0.06 SD for episodic memory [vyas2024]. This is a small, consistent signal, but it comes from one parent trial and one product, and incident MCI or dementia were not reduced [sachs2023]. The earlier Physicians' Health Study II gave the same type of product to 5,947 male physicians for about a decade and found a precise null (global -0.01 SD, 95% CI -0.04 to 0.02) [grodstein2013]. The MAVIS trial (n=910, 12 months) was also null, with weak hints in people at risk of deficiency [mcneill2007]. The pre-COSMOS Cochrane review found low-certainty evidence of little or no effect of complex supplements [rutjes2018]. Older meta-analyses found immediate recall SMD 0.32 but no delayed-recall or fluency effect [grima2012]. Overall: grade B for a small slowing of cognitive ageing in older adults. The effect is real at population level but is below the threshold an individual would notice. Healthy young adults, students and knowledge workers. Here the evidence is weaker and comes mostly from two industry-funded laboratories. In employed men aged 30-55, 33 days of a high-dose B/C/mineral product improved perceived stress, vigour and one arithmetic task, but most cognitive tasks were null and the groups differed at baseline [kennedy2010]. In women aged 25-50, 9 weeks of an MVM improved multitasking accuracy and reduced task-induced fatigue [haskell2010]. The most direct independent-looking test in 20-50-year-olds (16 weeks, n=138) found no cognitive benefit after multiplicity correction [pipingas2014] and no laboratory mood effect [pipingas2013]. A 4-week trial changed one POMS subscale only [white2015]. Acute 'single-dose' effects rest on EEG and metabolic markers [white2017, dodd2020]. Pooled mood data show perceived stress SMD 0.35 in non-clinical samples [long2013], but these are subjective outcomes, possibly unblinded by riboflavin-coloured urine, from few trials. Biomarkers versus cognition. MVMs reliably raise B6, B12 and folate and lower homocysteine, even in young adults [pipingas2014, white2015, harris2015]. But biomarker gains without cognitive change are the rule in replete adults [harris2015]. Moderation by baseline status. The deficiency-gap hypothesis fits the null result in very well-nourished physicians [grodstein2013] and the subgroup hints in MAVIS [mcneill2007] and COSMOS-Mind (larger effect with cardiovascular disease) [baker2023]. However, a trial stratified by diet quality found the opposite pattern [young2022]. Safety at ~100% RDA is good: no excess mortality [macpherson2013, sesso2022, loftfield2024], and the USPSTF found a small cancer benefit with limitations [oconnor2022]. The risks lie in iron, preformed vitamin A, beta-carotene in smokers, vitamin K with warfarin, and stacking with fortified foods (see the iron, vitamin-a and vitamin-k monographs).", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "An MVM is by design a deficiency-gap product. Its plausible benefit is greatest where intakes are marginal: older adults, people with poor diets, restrictive diets (vegan: B12, iodine, zinc, iron), and populations with documented deficits (in Morocco, 29.8% of women of reproductive age are iron-deficient and 71% have low urinary iodine per the X6 synthesis). In the best-nourished population ever tested (US physicians), a decade of MVM did nothing for cognition [grodstein2013]. COSMOS participants (older, some with CVD) showed a small effect [baker2023, vyas2024], and MAVIS hinted at benefit only in those at risk of deficiency [mcneill2007]. But the only trial that stratified by diet quality found benefit in the better-fed group [young2022], so the moderation hypothesis is plausible but unproven. For adequately nourished young adults there is no convincing enhancement effect on memory, attention or reasoning [pipingas2014]. The reported benefits are subjective (stress, vigour, mental tiredness) and come from industry-funded trials of high-dose B products [kennedy2010, haskell2010, long2013]. Correcting single deficiencies (iron, B12, iodine, vitamin D) should be targeted after testing (see the respective monographs) rather than assumed to be covered by a generic MVM, whose iron and B12 doses may be too low to treat deficiency.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Marketing after COSMOS presents multivitamins as 'clinically shown to support memory' and as slowing 'brain ageing by 2-3 years'. These figures come from model-based translations of 0.06-0.07 SD effects in adults over 60 [vyas2024, yeung2023]. They do not apply to students or young professionals, and they coexist with a large null trial [grodstein2013]. Claims of 'dementia prevention' are unsupported: incident MCI/dementia did not differ [sachs2023], and they would be prohibited disease claims in the EU, US and Morocco. 'Energy', 'focus' and 'stress' claims for young workers rest on subjective outcomes in industry-funded trials of high-dose B formulas [kennedy2010, haskell2010, long2013]. In the EU, only nutrient-specific authorised claims may be used, and only when that nutrient is present at a significant amount. Examples: 'Iron/zinc/iodine contributes to normal cognitive function'; 'Vitamin B6/B12/folate/vitamin C contributes to normal psychological function'; '... reduction of tiredness and fatigue'. These describe normal function, not enhancement. Honest Brain Food positioning: 'a nutritional safety net for people whose diet may fall short', ideally formulated for the target population's documented gaps, not 'a brain booster'.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Vitamins and minerals are cofactors in one-carbon metabolism (B6, B12, folate: methylation and homocysteine clearance), mitochondrial energy metabolism (B1, B2, B3, B5, iron, magnesium), neurotransmitter synthesis (B6, iron, vitamin C), myelination (B12) and antioxidant defence (vitamins C, E, selenium, zinc). A deficiency of any of these can impair brain function. The MVM hypothesis is that correcting multiple marginal shortfalls at once improves neuronal efficiency, so the benefit should depend on baseline status. In replete adults, raising blood levels does not appear to translate into cognition. This is plausible biochemistry; it is not evidence of enhancement.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Oral MVM tablet -> intestinal absorption of each nutrient (with mineral-mineral competition, e.g. iron vs zinc/calcium)", "detail": "Blood B6, B12 and folate rise substantially within 4-16 weeks in young and older adults [pipingas2014, white2015, harris2015].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Raised B-vitamin status -> lower plasma homocysteine (one-carbon metabolism)", "detail": "Homocysteine falls with MVM in several trials [haskell2010, white2015, macpherson2012]; this is a biomarker, not a cognitive outcome (see vitamin-b6, folate-b9, vitamin-b12 monographs).", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Correction of marginal deficits -> improved neuronal energy metabolism, neurotransmitter synthesis and vascular health", "detail": "Plausible from known cofactor roles; COSMOS-Mind effect larger with CVD history suggests a vascular/nutritional-risk pathway [baker2023].", "evidence_level": "plausible"}, {"step": "Acute changes in brain electrophysiology and whole-body energy expenditure after one dose", "detail": "SSVEP changes in 20 young adults [white2017]; raised energy expenditure during cognitive tasks [dodd2020]; mechanisms and relevance unclear.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Measured human cognitive outcome", "detail": "Small slowing of cognitive ageing (0.07 SD) in adults >=60 [vyas2024]; null in well-nourished older men [grodstein2013] and in young adults [pipingas2014]; subjective mood/stress improvements in industry trials [long2013].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["one-carbon metabolism / homocysteine", "mitochondrial energy metabolism cofactors", "neurotransmitter synthesis (monoamines)", "antioxidant enzymes (Zn, Se, Cu, Mn)", "vascular health", "iron-dependent myelination and dopamine synthesis (deficiency only)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Community-dwelling US adults >=60-65 y, cognitively unimpaired (COSMOS); US male physicians >=65 (PHS II); Scottish adults >=65 (MAVIS)", "dose_or_intake": "Standard ~100% RDA MVM (Centrum Silver) daily", "duration": "1-12 y", "grade": "B", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Three COSMOS substudies consistently favour MVM, with a pooled global cognition gain of 0.07 SD over 2-3 y. The 12-y PHS II trial in well-nourished physicians was a precise null, MAVIS was null, and Cochrane (pre-COSMOS) found little or no effect of complex supplements.", "effect_estimate": "COSMOS pooled MD 0.07 SU (0.03-0.11) [vyas2024]; COSMOS-Mind z 0.07 (0.02-0.12) [baker2023]; PHS II -0.01 SU (-0.04 to 0.02) [grodstein2013]; complex supplements MD 0.12 TICS (-0.14 to 0.38) [rutjes2018]", "certainty_notes": "Large low-risk-of-bias RCTs, but downgraded for inconsistency (COSMOS vs PHS II) and for indirectness: one product and one parent trial for all positive data, and a mostly White, educated sample. The effect is small; not upgraded.", "key_study_ids": ["vyas2024", "baker2023", "yeung2023", "grodstein2013", "mcneill2007", "rutjes2018"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Adults >=60 y (COSMOS-Web, -Clinic, -Mind); earlier small trials in older adults", "dose_or_intake": "Standard MVM daily", "duration": "1-3 y (COSMOS); >=1 month (older trials)", "grade": "B", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Immediate recall/episodic memory improved in COSMOS-Web (primary endpoint, P=0.025) and COSMOS-Clinic (0.12 SD); pooled 0.06 SD. Delayed recall and other domains largely null; older meta-analysis shows immediate recall SMD 0.32 but delayed recall null.", "effect_estimate": "Pooled episodic memory 0.06 SU (0.03-0.10) [vyas2024]; immediate recall SMD 0.32 (0.09-0.56), delayed SMD -0.14 (-0.43 to 0.14) [grima2012]", "certainty_notes": "Consistent within COSMOS. Downgraded for indirectness (single product/trial family) and for inconsistent domain-level results; the PHS II verbal-memory null (-0.005 SU) contradicts it.", "key_study_ids": ["vyas2024", "yeung2023", "grima2012", "grodstein2013"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Adults >=60 y", "dose_or_intake": "Standard MVM", "duration": "2-3 y", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Executive benefit in COSMOS-Mind, but not in COSMOS-Clinic (0.04 SD, CI crossing 0) or COSMOS-Web (secondary outcome null).", "effect_estimate": "COSMOS-Clinic 0.04 SU (-0.04 to 0.11) [vyas2024]; COSMOS-Web null [yeung2023]; COSMOS-Mind positive (estimate not in abstract) [baker2023]", "certainty_notes": "Inconsistent across substudies; secondary outcomes.", "key_study_ids": ["vyas2024", "yeung2023", "baker2023"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "dementia_risk", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Adults >=65 y (COSMOS-Mind)", "dose_or_intake": "Standard MVM", "duration": "3 y", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "No difference in incident MCI (110 cases) or dementia (14 cases); very low power. Cochrane found sparse dementia data.", "effect_estimate": "Incidence not different (all P>=0.05) [sachs2023]", "certainty_notes": "Downgraded for serious imprecision (few events); cannot exclude a modest effect.", "key_study_ids": ["sachs2023", "rutjes2018"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults 18-50 y not selected for poor diet (Australia, UK)", "dose_or_intake": "High-B-vitamin MVM (with or without herbs) at 1-several x RDA", "duration": "4-16 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "The 16-week trial in 20-50-year-olds found no cognitive benefit after multiplicity correction (a Stroop trend in men only). The 4-week trial reported mood but no cognitive effect. Blood B vitamins rose markedly.", "effect_estimate": "No significant cognitive effect after correction [pipingas2014]; not reported [white2015]", "certainty_notes": "Few small trials, product-supplier links, no preregistered primary cognitive outcome; null is imprecise.", "key_study_ids": ["pipingas2014", "white2015"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_fatigue", "population": "knowledge_workers", "population_detail": "Employed men 30-55 y (UK) and women 25-50 y under extended multitasking or a 60-min demand battery", "dose_or_intake": "High-dose B-complex + C + minerals (Berocca) or MVM (Supradyn)", "duration": "33 days - 9 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Less task-induced mental tiredness and better multitasking accuracy in women. In men: better PSS and vigour and one arithmetic task, but RVIP, serial 7s and mental-fatigue VAS were null, and groups were imbalanced at baseline.", "effect_estimate": "PSS 12.4 vs 12.9 at day 33 (F=4.07, P<0.05); serial 3s F=5.09 [kennedy2010]; effect sizes not reported [haskell2010]", "certainty_notes": "Two trials from one manufacturer-funded group with a Bayer co-author. Many outcomes without multiplicity control; subjective endpoints. Capped at C by risk of bias and lack of independent replication.", "key_study_ids": ["kennedy2010", "haskell2010"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Non-clinical adults of various ages", "dose_or_intake": "MVM, often with high-dose B vitamins", "duration": ">=28 days", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Meta-analysis of 8 trials: reduced perceived stress, anxiety, fatigue and confusion (SMD ~0.2-0.35); depression NS. In young adults the lab mood measures were null and only phone-based or single-subscale effects appeared.", "effect_estimate": "Perceived stress SMD 0.35 (0.22-0.47); anxiety 0.32 (0.16-0.48); depression 0.20 (-0.03 to 0.42) [long2013]", "certainty_notes": "Subjective outcomes susceptible to unblinding; few small, mostly industry-funded trials; inconsistent in young adults [pipingas2013, white2015].", "key_study_ids": ["long2013", "kennedy2010", "pipingas2013", "white2015"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults 18-39 y", "dose_or_intake": "Single MVM dose (some products with guarana or CoQ10)", "duration": "single dose", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "A single dose changes SSVEP responses (n=20) and energy expenditure/subjective tiredness. No convincing behavioural cognitive improvement independent of caffeine (guarana) arms.", "effect_estimate": "not reported [white2017, dodd2020]", "certainty_notes": "EEG and metabolic biomarkers and subjective ratings only; very small samples; industry links.", "key_study_ids": ["white2017", "dodd2020"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "other", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Nutrient-status biomarkers (B6, B12, folate, homocysteine) in young, middle-aged and older adults - biomarker, not cognition", "dose_or_intake": "Standard or high-B MVM", "duration": "4-16 weeks", "grade": "A", "direction": "benefit", "effect_summary": "MVM consistently raises blood B vitamins and lowers homocysteine. In the same trials, cognition did not improve in replete adults.", "effect_estimate": "B6/B12/folate increased, homocysteine decreased (P<0.01) [white2015, pipingas2014, harris2015]", "certainty_notes": "Consistent across trials. This row is about nutrient status only and must not be read as cognitive benefit.", "key_study_ids": ["pipingas2014", "white2015", "harris2015", "haskell2010"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "deficient_or_low_baseline", "population_detail": "Older adults at risk of deficiency, poor diet, or with cardiovascular disease", "dose_or_intake": "Standard MVM or B-vitamin multinutrient", "duration": "12 weeks - 3 y", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Hints of greater benefit in people at risk of deficiency (MAVIS) and in those with CVD history (COSMOS-Mind). But a diet-stratified trial found benefit only in people with an optimal diet.", "effect_estimate": "MAVIS at-risk verbal fluency MD 2.5 (-1.0 to 6.1) [mcneill2007]; CVD subgroup z 0.14 (-0.02 to 0.31) [baker2023]; optimal-diet attention p=0.030 [young2022]", "certainty_notes": "Subgroup analyses, imprecise, contradictory; the plausible moderation hypothesis remains unproven.", "key_study_ids": ["mcneill2007", "baker2023", "young2022", "grodstein2013"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "Single MVM tablet (~1-3x RDA water-soluble vitamins)", "onset": "within hours (same session)", "duration_studied": "single dose", "domain": "sustained_attention (SSVEP during A-X CPT)", "population": "healthy adults 21-39 y (n=20)", "effect_estimate": "SSVEP changes; behaviour only correlated", "certainty": "D", "safety": "no concerns reported", "study_ids": ["white2017"]}, {"dose": "MVM with 3x RDA water-soluble vitamins + CoQ10", "onset": "day 1", "duration_studied": "single dose and 28 days", "domain": "cognitive_fatigue (subjective tiredness, energy expenditure)", "population": "young exercisers 18-35 y (n=82)", "effect_estimate": "raised energy expenditure; lower mental tiredness in some conditions; not reported as SMD", "certainty": "D", "safety": "no concerns reported", "study_ids": ["dodd2020"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "3 y", "population": "US adults >=65 (n=2,262)", "outcome": "Global cognition composite (telephone)", "effect_estimate": "z 0.07 (0.02-0.12)", "evidence_quality": "high (large RCT, secondary endpoint)", "limitations": "telephone tests; homogenous sample", "study_ids": ["baker2023"]}, {"duration": "1-3 y", "population": "US adults >=60 (n=3,562)", "outcome": "ModRey immediate recall (web)", "effect_estimate": "P=0.025 at 1 y; secondary outcomes null", "evidence_quality": "moderate", "limitations": "unsupervised web testing", "study_ids": ["yeung2023"]}, {"duration": "2 y", "population": "US adults >=60 (n=573) + pooled n=5,203", "outcome": "Global cognition and episodic memory (in person; pooled)", "effect_estimate": "pooled 0.07 SU (0.03-0.11) global; 0.06 SU (0.03-0.10) memory", "evidence_quality": "moderate-high", "limitations": "same parent trial and product", "study_ids": ["vyas2024"]}, {"duration": "3 y", "population": "US adults >=65 (n=2,262)", "outcome": "Incident MCI/dementia", "effect_estimate": "no difference; 110 MCI, 14 dementia", "evidence_quality": "low (imprecise)", "limitations": "few events", "study_ids": ["sachs2023"]}, {"duration": "~8.5-12 y", "population": "US male physicians >=65 (n=5,947)", "outcome": "Global and verbal memory composites", "effect_estimate": "-0.01 SU (-0.04 to 0.02)", "evidence_quality": "high", "limitations": "very well-nourished population", "study_ids": ["grodstein2013"]}, {"duration": "12 months", "population": "Scottish adults >=65 (n=910)", "outcome": "Digit span, verbal fluency", "effect_estimate": "null overall; at-risk subgroup MD 2.5 (-1.0 to 6.1)", "evidence_quality": "moderate", "limitations": "two brief tests", "study_ids": ["mcneill2007"]}, {"duration": "16 weeks", "population": "Healthy adults 20-50 y (n=138)", "outcome": "Computerised cognition; B vitamins", "effect_estimate": "no significant cognitive effect after correction; large B-vitamin rise", "evidence_quality": "low-moderate", "limitations": "small; herbs in formula", "study_ids": ["pipingas2014"]}, {"duration": "33 days", "population": "Employed men 30-55 y (n=215)", "outcome": "Stress, vigour, cognitive demand battery", "effect_estimate": "PSS and vigour improved; most cognitive tasks null", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "industry-funded, baseline imbalance", "study_ids": ["kennedy2010"]}, {"duration": "9 weeks", "population": "Women 25-50 y (n=216)", "outcome": "Multitasking accuracy, task fatigue", "effect_estimate": "improved; not quantified in abstract", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "industry-funded single lab", "study_ids": ["haskell2010"]}, {"duration": "16 weeks", "population": "Adults 55-65 y (n=116)", "outcome": "Cognition and biomarkers", "effect_estimate": "biomarkers improved; cognition null", "evidence_quality": "low-moderate", "limitations": "small", "study_ids": ["harris2015"]}, {"duration": "mean 43 months", "population": "21 RCTs, n=91,074", "outcome": "All-cause mortality", "effect_estimate": "RR 0.98 (0.94-1.02)", "evidence_quality": "high", "limitations": "heterogeneous formulas", "study_ids": ["macpherson2013"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Standard MVMs at about 100% of reference intakes are safe for healthy adults over years. There is no excess mortality in a meta-analysis of 21 RCTs, and no safety signal in COSMOS over 3.6 y [macpherson2013, sesso2022]. Risks come from specific components and from stacking with fortified foods and other supplements. (1) Iron: unnecessary for most men and postmenopausal women, harmful in haemochromatosis, and a leading cause of accidental poisoning in young children (see iron monograph; EU safe level 40 mg/day total intake). (2) Preformed vitamin A: teratogenic and bone risk at high intakes (see vitamin-a monograph). (3) Beta-carotene supplements: increased lung cancer in smokers and asbestos-exposed people [oconnor2022]. (4) Vitamin K: interacts with warfarin and other vitamin K antagonists (see vitamin-k monograph). (5) High-dose B6 in 'high-potency' products: peripheral neuropathy (see vitamin-b6 monograph). (6) Folic acid can mask B12 deficiency (see folate-b9 monograph).", "adverse_effects": ["Gastrointestinal upset, constipation (iron), nausea on an empty stomach", "Bright yellow urine (riboflavin) - harmless but can unblind trials", "Accidental iron overdose in children (child-resistant packaging needed)", "Cumulative excess of vitamin A, B6, zinc, selenium, iodine or iron when combined with fortified foods and other supplements"], "contraindications": ["Haemochromatosis or iron overload (iron-containing products)", "Pregnancy or planning pregnancy: avoid products with high preformed vitamin A; use dedicated prenatal formulations under medical advice", "Current or former smokers / asbestos exposure: avoid supplemental beta-carotene at high dose", "Patients on vitamin K antagonists: products with vitamin K only with prescriber monitoring"], "drug_interactions": ["Vitamin K antagonists (warfarin, acenocoumarol, phenprocoumon): vitamin K content alters INR", "Levothyroxine, bisphosphonates, tetracycline/quinolone antibiotics: chelation by iron, calcium, magnesium, zinc - separate dosing by several hours", "Levodopa: reduced absorption with iron; high-dose B6 interacts with levodopa without carbidopa", "Retinoid drugs (isotretinoin, acitretin): additive vitamin A toxicity", "Methotrexate and antiepileptics interact with folate (specialist review)"], "upper_limits": "No UL exists for 'a multivitamin'. Each nutrient has its own UL, and the total from food, fortified food and supplements must stay below it. Key adult ULs: preformed vitamin A 3000 ug RE/day (EFSA 2024, also US); vitamin D 100 ug/day (EFSA); vitamin B6 12 mg/day (EFSA 2023; see vitamin-b6 monograph); folic acid 1000 ug/day; zinc 25 mg/day (EFSA); iron no EFSA UL, safe level 40 mg/day; US iron UL 45 mg/day. EU harmonised maximum amounts for supplements are still not set; national limits apply.", "sleep_impact": "No sleep effects are established for standard MVMs. Products with guarana/caffeine (several trialled formulas) can impair sleep if taken later in the day. High-dose B vitamins taken in the evening have been anecdotally linked to vivid dreams; this is not established.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Yes: up to about 12 y in PHS II and 3.6 y in COSMOS (older adults) with no safety signal; up to 27 y of observational use in cohorts with no mortality benefit (HR 1.04) [grodstein2013, sesso2022, loftfield2024]. Long-term data in young adults are limited to <=16-week trials.", "daily_use_appropriate": "probably", "special_populations": "Pregnancy: needs a dedicated formulation (folic acid 400 ug, iodine, iron as advised, no high preformed vitamin A). Children: separate age-specific products only; iron poisoning risk. Older adults: B12 absorption falls, so a B12-adequate product is useful. Vegans: check B12, iodine, iron, zinc. Smokers: avoid high-dose beta-carotene. Chronic kidney disease: avoid high vitamin A, magnesium, potassium and phosphorus (nephrologist). Patients on anticoagulants, levothyroxine or antiepileptics: pharmacist review.", "specialist_review_needed": ["Clinical nutritionist/dietitian (formulation against target-population intake data and ULs)", "Pharmacist (drug-nutrient interactions: VKAs, levothyroxine, antibiotics, levodopa)", "Obstetrician (any product likely to be used by women planning pregnancy)", "Paediatric toxicologist if iron included (packaging)"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "Safe at ~100% NRV for healthy adults. Passes only if: per-nutrient doses keep total intake (diet + fortification + product) below ULs; iron is omitted or justified for the target group (women of reproductive age) with child-resistant packaging; preformed vitamin A is limited (or beta-carotene used, but not for smokers at high dose); there is a vitamin K/anticoagulant warning; B6 is kept well below the EFSA UL."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Food supplements fall under Directive 2002/46/EC (permitted vitamin/mineral forms in Annexes I-II). EU maximum amounts under Art. 5 are not yet harmonised, so national limits differ (e.g. German and Dutch caps; per X4 synthesis). Trade press (June 2026) reports a planned Commission consultation on maximum levels from Q3 2026, with adoption perhaps by 2028; a leaked draft would cut limits for B6, C, beta-carotene and vitamin K1. This is unofficial and uncertain (nutraingredients.com, accessed 2026-10-25). Claims: only nutrient-specific Article 13(1) claims from Reg. (EU) 432/2012 may be used, when the nutrient is present at least as a 'source' (15% NRV per 100 g/daily portion). Cognition-relevant: iron, iodine and zinc 'contribute to normal cognitive function'; B vitamins, vitamin C and magnesium 'normal psychological function'; pantothenic acid 'normal mental performance'; several nutrients 'reduction of tiredness and fatigue'. There is no authorised claim for memory improvement or for MVMs as such. Products must be notified nationally in many Member States.", "us": "Dietary supplement under DSHEA; vitamins and minerals are dietary ingredients and need no NDI. Structure/function claims (e.g. 'supports normal energy metabolism') need the FDA disclaimer and 30-day notification. The FTC requires competent and reliable scientific evidence. 'Supports memory' claims in older adults now cite COSMOS; such claims are not substantiated for young adults/students. Disease claims ('prevents dementia') are prohibited. The USPSTF (2022) found insufficient evidence for or against MVM to prevent CVD or cancer [oconnor2022]. Supplement Facts labelling and cGMP (21 CFR 111) apply. Iron-containing supplements require the accidental-overdose warning (21 CFR 101.17(e), per standard FDA rules; not re-verified here).", "morocco": "Per the X6 Morocco synthesis: there is no specific food-supplement law. Registration was historically through a Ministry of Health (DMP) committee, now AMMPS (2025). Law 27-26 (June 2026) may let a pending decree reserve listed supplements to pharmacies; content unverified. Disease claims are banned (Decree 2-12-389 Art. 7). No authorised health-claim list was found; nutrition claims follow Order 281-16. Arabic labelling is mandatory. Mandatory flour fortification (iron + folic acid) and iodised salt mean an MVM adds to existing intakes. Official vitamin/mineral maximum levels were not found (uncertain; a Moroccan lawyer must review).", "permitted_claims_examples": ["Iron contributes to normal cognitive function (EU Reg. 432/2012; product must be at least a source of iron)", "Zinc contributes to normal cognitive function (EU Reg. 432/2012)", "Iodine contributes to normal cognitive function (EU Reg. 432/2012)", "Folate contributes to the reduction of tiredness and fatigue (EU Reg. 432/2012)", "Pantothenic acid contributes to normal mental performance (EU Reg. 432/2012)"], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "None for standard vitamin/mineral forms listed in Directive 2002/46/EC Annex II. Newer forms (some chelates, certain menaquinone or folate salts) need checking against Annex II and the Union novel-food list. Added botanicals (ginkgo, bacopa, guarana) bring separate national-list and novel-food issues.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Moderate. Micronutrient premixes are routinely used to fortify cereals, flours, beverages and bars, but a full 100% NRV MVM in a food is limited by mineral taste (iron, zinc, copper are metallic), interactions, colour and stability. Fortifying a Brain Food product at 15-30% NRV of selected nutrients (claims threshold 15%) is realistic; a complete MVM is better as a tablet/capsule.", "formats": ["tablet", "capsule", "effervescent tablet (high-B products)", "gummy (limited minerals, sugar)", "fortified cereal/bar/beverage premix", "powder sachet"], "stability": "Vitamin C, B1, B12, folic acid, vitamin A and D degrade with heat, light, moisture and oxygen, so overages (often 20-50%) are needed for shelf life. Iron and copper catalyse oxidation of vitamins C and A and of fats. Encapsulated forms and opaque, low-moisture packaging help.", "taste": "Iron, zinc and copper salts give metallic/astringent off-notes; B1 has a sulphurous note and B2 colours yellow. In food these limit dose; masking or encapsulation is needed.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K) are better absorbed with a meal containing fat. Iron absorption is inhibited by calcium, phytate and polyphenols (tea/coffee) and enhanced by vitamin C. Zinc and iron compete. Folic acid is more bioavailable than food folate.", "dose_feasibility": "The doses studied (one tablet at ~100% RDA; COSMOS used Centrum Silver) fit easily in a tablet. In a food serving, 15-30% NRV of most vitamins is feasible. A full mineral complement (magnesium 100+ mg, calcium) is bulky and does not fit one serving with acceptable taste.", "cost_availability": "Widely available, among the cheapest supplements; commodity premixes available from major vitamin suppliers.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 4, "effect_magnitude": 2, "safety": 8, "reproducibility": 5, "food_suitability": 5, "cost_access": 9, "rationale": "Evidence quality 4: grade B exists only for older adults; in the Brain Food target population (healthy young adults, students, knowledge workers) the evidence is grade C/D, so the score is in the C band. Effect magnitude 2: 0.06-0.07 SD in older adults and no reliable cognitive effect in young adults. Safety 8: well-established at ~100% NRV; component-specific risks (iron, vitamin A, beta-carotene, vitamin K, B6) are manageable by formulation. Reproducibility 5: consistent within COSMOS but contradicted by PHS II, with young-adult positives from industry labs. Food suitability 5: partial fortification feasible, full MVM not. Cost/access 9: cheap and ubiquitous."}, "research_gaps": ["Independent, preregistered RCT of a standard MVM on cognition in students/young workers, with dietary-intake and nutrient-status stratification", "Replication of COSMOS cognitive effects in diverse (non-US, non-White, lower-income, Moroccan) populations with a different MVM product", "Test of the 'nutrient gap' moderation hypothesis using measured biomarkers (B12, ferritin, iodine, vitamin D), not FFQ diet scores", "Which components drive the COSMOS effect (B12? D? zinc?)", "Long-term safety of high-potency B formulas (B6 neuropathy) in young users", "Registered trials of MVM in exam-stress or high-workload settings with objective outcomes and blinding checks (urine colour)"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 5.1, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": "B", "best_grade_young_adults": "C", "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": "A", "best_grade_overall": "B", "benefit_domains": {"global_cognition": "B", "episodic_memory": "B", "cognitive_fatigue": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 10, "n_studies": 24, "n_verified": 24, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 11, "n_systematic_reviews": 5, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["benefit", "inconsistent", "insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "niacin-b3", "name": "Niacin (vitamin B3)", "category": "B", "category_name": "Vitamins", "aliases": ["niacin", "vitamin B3", "nicotinic acid", "nicotinamide", "niacinamide", "nicotinamide riboside (distinct NAD+ precursor, not covered here)"], "food_sources": ["poultry, meat, fish (tuna, salmon)", "fortified bread, flour and breakfast cereals", "peanuts and legumes", "whole grains", "coffee (trigonelline-derived niacin)", "endogenous synthesis from dietary tryptophan"], "active_constituents": ["nicotinic acid and nicotinamide, precursors of NAD+/NADH and NADP+/NADPH, cofactors for hundreds of redox reactions including mitochondrial oxidative phosphorylation and DNA repair (PARP substrate)"], "one_line_verdict": "Niacin deficiency (pellagra, with its classic dementia) is a dramatic, fully reversible cognitive catastrophe, and one large prospective cohort links higher dietary niacin to lower Alzheimer's risk and slower decline in older adults, but no RCT shows niacin improves cognition in healthy young adults, and pharmacological nicotinic acid doses used for cholesterol cause flushing and, at high chronic doses, liver toxicity.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. Niacin (nicotinic acid/nicotinamide) is the precursor of NAD+/NADP+, required for essentially all cellular redox and energy metabolism; this biochemistry is established. Severe deficiency causes pellagra (dermatitis, diarrhea, dementia), with the dementia component being a well-documented, reversible encephalopathy when niacin is replaced (classical clinical literature; general knowledge, not independently re-verified with a primary source in this unit beyond background searches). Epidemiology in older adults. The Chicago Health and Aging Project, a prospective cohort of 6,158 residents aged 65+ followed 1993-2002 with food-frequency-assessed niacin intake and repeated cognitive testing (median 5.5 years follow-up, n=3,718 for cognitive-change analysis; n=815 clinically evaluated, 131 incident Alzheimer's disease cases), found that higher dietary niacin intake was associated with markedly lower incident Alzheimer's risk (relative risk from lowest to highest intake quintile: 1.0 (ref), 0.3, 0.3, 0.6, 0.3; adjusted for age, sex, race, education, APOE e4) and with slower annual cognitive decline (beta=0.019 standardised units per natural-log increase in intake, p=0.05; stronger in subgroups without cardiovascular disease, low baseline cognition, or less education) [morris2004]. This is a single, well-conducted but observational cohort in older adults; it establishes an association, not causation, and does not by itself justify a supplementation claim or generalise to healthy young adults. Healthy young adults and RCT evidence. No RCT isolating niacin with a cognitive primary endpoint in healthy young adults was identified in this search. Niacin/niacinamide appears only as one component of multivitamin trials (Benton 1995, Kennedy 2010/2011) sharing the same limitation described in the thiamine-b1 and riboflavin-b2 monographs: effects cannot be attributed to niacin specifically. What is unknown. Whether niacin supplementation (as opposed to simply avoiding deficiency) changes cognition in healthy, adequately nourished adults of any age; whether the cohort association in older adults reflects a causal niacin effect, general diet quality, or confounding; acute dosing has not been tested for cognition. Separately, high-dose nicotinic acid (500 mg-2 g/day) is used pharmacologically for dyslipidemia and is well known to cause flushing (prostaglandin-mediated, common and dose-limiting) and, at sustained high doses, hepatotoxicity; this pharmacological-dose safety literature is extensive but belongs to a cardiology context, not a cognitive-food context, and must not be conflated with nutritional-dose niacin safety.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Pellagra (classically described by the triad of dermatitis, diarrhea and dementia) is a severe, historically significant niacin-deficiency disease, now rare in EU/US/Morocco populations with fortified-grain access and adequate protein/tryptophan intake, but still reported in alcoholism, severe malnutrition, carcinoid syndrome (excess tryptophan diversion) and some restrictive diets (general clinical knowledge; not independently re-verified with a primary source beyond background searches in this unit). Correcting deficiency reverses the dementia component and is unambiguously appropriate. The Chicago Health and Aging Project association between dietary niacin and lower Alzheimer's risk/slower decline in older adults [morris2004] sits between deficiency-correction and enhancement: participants were not necessarily clinically deficient, so this may represent a genuine benefit of niacin adequacy at the higher end of normal dietary range in an ageing population, but it is epidemiological, not interventional, and has not been confirmed by a supplementation RCT. There is no enhancement evidence at all in healthy young adults.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Niacin is sometimes marketed in 'brain energy' or 'anti-aging cognition' supplement blends citing NAD+ metabolism and the Chicago cohort finding. The cohort finding [morris2004] is real and worth citing honestly as an observational signal in older adults, but it is not RCT evidence and does not support a 'niacin prevents Alzheimer's' or 'niacin boosts memory' claim for healthy young adults or students. High-dose 'flush' niacin products marketed for cardiovascular or 'detox' purposes carry a real, well-known flushing side effect and, at sustained high doses, liver-injury risk; a cognition-focused food product should use low, nutritional-level niacin only, never flush-inducing pharmacological doses. The EU-authorised claim 'niacin contributes to normal psychological function and to the normal functioning of the nervous system' is legitimate at source level (>=15% NRV) and should be the ceiling for any claim.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Dietary niacin (nicotinic acid, nicotinamide) and endogenously synthesised niacin (from tryptophan via the kynurenine pathway) are converted to NAD+ and NADP+, which are essential redox cofactors for mitochondrial oxidative phosphorylation, fatty-acid and amino-acid metabolism, DNA repair (as PARP substrate) and sirtuin signalling. Severe deficiency causes pellagra, including a dementia component attributed to impaired cerebral energy metabolism and possibly neurotransmitter synthesis defects; this is established in humans. The mechanistic link between higher-than-deficient niacin intake and the observed slower cognitive decline in the Chicago cohort [morris2004] is plausible (better mitochondrial/DNA-repair capacity, lower oxidative stress) but not directly demonstrated by biomarker or imaging data in that cohort; it remains a hypothesis consistent with, but not proven by, the epidemiological association.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Dietary niacin (and tryptophan via kynurenine pathway) -> intestinal absorption and hepatic/tissue conversion to NAD+/NADP+", "detail": "Established biochemistry; endogenous synthesis from tryptophan means strict niacin deficiency requires both low niacin and low protein/tryptophan intake.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "NAD+/NADP+ -> cofactors for mitochondrial oxidative phosphorylation, antioxidant (NADPH) and DNA-repair (PARP) pathways", "detail": "Textbook biochemistry; severe deficiency produces pellagra including reversible dementia, demonstrating the causal importance of adequate niacin for brain function.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Higher dietary niacin intake across the normal range in older adults -> lower incident Alzheimer's disease and slower cognitive decline (association)", "detail": "Chicago Health and Aging Project, n=3,718-6,158, 5.5-year median follow-up [morris2004]; observational, adjusted for major confounders but residual confounding (overall diet quality, socioeconomic factors) cannot be excluded.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Hypothesised mechanism for the cohort association: improved mitochondrial energy metabolism and DNA-repair capacity in ageing neurons", "detail": "Plausible given NAD+ biology and animal/cell literature on NAD+ decline with ageing; not directly measured in the human cohort.", "evidence_level": "plausible"}, {"step": "Niacin supplementation in healthy young adults -> measured cognitive outcome", "detail": "No RCT evidence; this step is untested.", "evidence_level": "speculative"}], "pathways": ["NAD+/NADP+ biosynthesis and redox metabolism", "kynurenine pathway (tryptophan-to-niacin conversion)", "PARP-mediated DNA repair", "pellagra/dementia deficiency pathway (established)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "dementia_risk", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Community-dwelling adults aged 65+, Chicago, n=3,718 for cognitive-decline analysis, n=815 clinically evaluated (131 incident AD cases)", "dose_or_intake": "Dietary niacin intake quintiles (food-frequency questionnaire, energy-adjusted; highest quintile vs lowest)", "duration": "median 5.5 years follow-up (up to 6 years)", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Higher dietary niacin intake associated with lower incident Alzheimer's disease risk (RR 0.3 in upper quintiles vs lowest, adjusted) and slower annual cognitive decline (p for trend=0.002 for AD; p=0.05 for decline rate), strongest in subgroups without cardiovascular disease, low baseline cognition, or <12 years education.", "effect_estimate": "RR 0.3 (95% CI 0.1-0.7) for highest vs lowest niacin quintile and incident AD; decline-rate beta=0.019 SU/year per natural-log mg increase (p=0.05) [morris2004]", "certainty_notes": "Single well-conducted but observational cohort; downgraded for indirectness (observational, not interventional) and for residual confounding by overall diet quality/socioeconomic status; not causal evidence; graded C rather than D because of adequate sample size, prospective design and dose-response trend.", "key_study_ids": ["morris2004"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "No isolated-niacin RCT identified; niacin appears only within multivitamin trials shared with thiamine-b1/riboflavin-b2 (Benton 1995, Kennedy 2010/2011)", "dose_or_intake": "not isolable", "duration": "not applicable", "grade": "E", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No evidence exists to evaluate; this row documents the explicit absence of isolated-niacin RCT data in healthy young adults rather than a negative finding.", "effect_estimate": "not reported (no trial exists)", "certainty_notes": "Absence of evidence is reported honestly per protocol rather than inferring either benefit or harm; graded E only in the sense of 'not supported by any direct evidence', not 'shown ineffective'.", "key_study_ids": []}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "not tested", "onset": "not applicable", "duration_studied": "not applicable", "domain": "other", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "effect_estimate": "no acute isolated-niacin cognition trial identified", "certainty": "insufficient data", "safety": "pharmacological doses (>500 mg nicotinic acid) cause acute cutaneous flushing within 15-30 minutes, well documented in the cardiology literature", "study_ids": []}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "up to 6 years (median 5.5)", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "outcome": "incident Alzheimer's disease and rate of cognitive decline, by dietary niacin intake", "effect_estimate": "RR 0.3 (0.1-0.7) highest vs lowest quintile; decline-rate beta=0.019 SU/year (p=0.05)", "evidence_quality": "low-moderate (C), observational", "limitations": "Cohort design, food-frequency measurement error, residual confounding, not an intervention trial", "study_ids": ["morris2004"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Nutritional-dose niacin (food and standard fortification/supplement levels, a few mg to a few tens of mg/day) is very safe. Pharmacological doses of nicotinic acid (500 mg-3 g/day), used clinically for dyslipidemia, commonly cause cutaneous flushing (prostaglandin D2-mediated, usually transient and dose-limiting but not dangerous) and, at sustained high doses, can cause hepatotoxicity, hyperglycemia and hyperuricemia; nicotinamide (niacinamide) does not cause flushing but shares some hepatotoxicity risk at very high chronic doses.", "adverse_effects": ["Cutaneous flushing, itching, warmth (nicotinic acid, dose-related, common above ~50-100 mg in flush-sensitive individuals and near-universal above 500 mg)", "Hepatotoxicity (elevated liver enzymes, rare hepatitis) at sustained high pharmacological doses, especially with sustained-release formulations", "Hyperglycemia and hyperuricemia at pharmacological doses (cardiology literature)", "GI upset at high doses"], "contraindications": ["Active liver disease (pharmacological doses)", "Active peptic ulcer disease (high-dose nicotinic acid, per cardiology prescribing literature)", "Gout (hyperuricemia risk at pharmacological doses)"], "drug_interactions": ["Statins: combined high-dose niacin and statin therapy historically associated with increased myopathy risk and was abandoned as a routine combination after large outcome trials (AIM-HIGH, HPS2-THRIVE) showed no added cardiovascular benefit and more adverse events - cardiology context, not relevant to food-level niacin"], "upper_limits": "EU/EFSA and US tolerable upper intake level for nicotinic acid is set around 35 mg/day for supplemental niacin in adults (flushing as the critical endpoint); nicotinamide has a higher UL (~900 mg/day range in some assessments) because it does not cause flushing; exact current EFSA/NASEM figures were not independently re-fetched live in this session and should be confirmed before label-level dosing decisions.", "sleep_impact": "No established sleep disruption from nutritional-dose niacin; high-dose nicotinic acid flushing can disturb sleep if taken near bedtime (anecdotal, cardiology context).", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Nutritional-dose fortification exposure is well established as safe through decades of food fortification; pharmacological high-dose exposure has been studied extensively in cardiology trials (not reviewed in depth here as it is outside the cognitive-food scope) and carries the hepatotoxicity/flushing profile described above.", "daily_use_appropriate": "yes", "special_populations": "Liver disease patients, people with gout, and those on statins should avoid high-dose nicotinic acid (pharmacological context); pregnant/lactating women should stay within standard nutritional/prenatal levels; nutritional-dose niacin in a food product is appropriate for the general population.", "specialist_review_needed": ["hepatology/cardiology review if any Brain Food formulation ever approaches pharmacological (>100 mg) niacin doses - out of scope for a nutritional food-claim product"], "hard_safety_gate": "pass", "gate_reason": "At the nutritional/food-claim doses relevant to this unit (a few mg per serving, far below flushing or hepatotoxicity thresholds), niacin is safe; the gate would be conditional-to-fail only at pharmacological supplement doses, which are out of scope for a food product."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Niacin is an authorised vitamin for supplements/fortification (Directive 2002/46/EC). EFSA-authorised claims (Regulation (EU) 432/2012 register, as commonly published; not re-fetched live in this session) include 'niacin contributes to normal psychological function', 'normal energy-yielding metabolism', 'normal functioning of the nervous system', and 'maintenance of normal mucous membranes/skin', usable at >=15% NRV per portion. The EU UL for supplemental nicotinic acid is in the tens of mg range (flushing-based); nicotinamide has a higher UL.", "us": "Niacin is GRAS and widely used in fortification (enriched flour/cereal standards) and supplements under DSHEA; structure/function claims about energy metabolism and nervous system function are permitted; pharmacological-dose niacin products for cholesterol are regulated as both supplements and, historically, prescription extended-release niacin (Niaspan), a reminder that dose determines regulatory category.", "morocco": "Niacin is included in Moroccan wheat flour fortification programs; ONSSA governs food-grade additive and supplement status; specific claim wording was not independently verified in this session - uncertain.", "permitted_claims_examples": ["EU: 'Niacin contributes to normal psychological function' (>=15% NRV per portion) - wording as commonly published in the EU claims register; not re-verified live in this session"], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "None; niacin is a conventional, long-established vitamin. Note: nicotinamide riboside and nicotinamide mononucleotide (NAD+ precursors marketed for longevity) are distinct ingredients with their own novel-food/NDI status and are out of scope for this niacin monograph.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Good at nutritional/claim-level doses (a few mg per serving); poor at pharmacological doses due to flushing, which would be an unacceptable sensory/safety event in a food product.", "formats": ["nicotinamide (niacinamide) preferred for food fortification since it does not cause flushing", "nicotinic acid used in some fortification standards historically"], "stability": "Niacin and nicotinamide are both relatively heat- and light-stable compared to thiamine and riboflavin, making them easier to retain through food processing (general food-science knowledge; not independently re-verified with a primary source in this unit).", "taste": "No significant taste issues at claim-level doses; nicotinamide avoids the flushing sensation that nicotinic acid can cause even at moderate-to-high non-claim doses.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Both nicotinic acid and nicotinamide are well absorbed and interconvertible via NAD+ metabolism; nicotinamide is preferred for food use because it lacks the flushing side effect of nicotinic acid at equivalent NAD+-precursor doses.", "dose_feasibility": "The EU claim-level dose fits any food serving; the epidemiological benefit in the Chicago cohort [morris2004] was observed across ordinary dietary intake ranges (not a supplemental mega-dose), which is reassuring for food-based positioning but was not tested as an intervention.", "cost_availability": "Niacin/nicotinamide is an inexpensive, widely available commodity ingredient.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 3, "effect_magnitude": 2, "safety": 8, "reproducibility": 2, "food_suitability": 8, "cost_access": 9, "rationale": "evidence_quality low-moderate (3): the strongest brain-relevant human evidence (Chicago cohort) is a single well-conducted but observational study in older adults, grade C; no RCT evidence exists in healthy young adults (E/insufficient). effect_magnitude low (2): the cohort RR is large but is an association, not a measured intervention effect size, and no RCT effect size exists. safety high (8): nutritional doses are very safe; deducted 2 points because the ingredient name 'niacin' carries real pharmacological-dose risks (flushing, hepatotoxicity) that require clear dose segregation in any formulation. reproducibility low (2): single cohort, no RCT replication for cognition. food_suitability high (8): nicotinamide form is stable and flush-free at claim doses. cost_access high (9): cheap commodity ingredient."}, "research_gaps": ["No RCT testing niacin/nicotinamide supplementation against cognitive decline or incident dementia to confirm the Chicago cohort association causally.", "No RCT isolating niacin with a cognitive endpoint in healthy young adults, students or knowledge workers, acute or chronic.", "Unclear whether the cohort association reflects a true niacin effect or general diet-quality/protein-intake confounding (niacin-rich foods tend to be protein-rich).", "No dedicated null/negative niacin-cognition trial was found; absence of evidence is reported honestly rather than invented.", "No data on whether nutritional-dose (non-flushing) niacin forms (nicotinamide) replicate the epidemiological signal if tested interventionally."], "_derived": {"composite_score": 4.8, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "pass", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": null, "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"dementia_risk": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 2, "n_studies": 1, "n_verified": 1, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 0, "n_systematic_reviews": 0, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["insufficient", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "pantothenic-acid-b5", "name": "Pantothenic acid (vitamin B5)", "category": "B", "category_name": "Vitamins", "aliases": ["pantothenic acid", "vitamin B5", "calcium pantothenate", "d-pantothenic acid", "pantethine (related, distinct supplement form)"], "food_sources": ["meat, poultry, organ meats (liver)", "eggs", "whole grains", "mushrooms", "avocado", "broccoli and other vegetables", "widely distributed in nearly all foods, hence the name 'pantothenic' (from Greek 'pantothen', from everywhere)"], "active_constituents": ["pantothenic acid, the obligate precursor of coenzyme A (CoA) and acyl carrier protein, required for fatty-acid metabolism, the citric acid cycle (as acetyl-CoA), and synthesis of acetylcholine, cholesterol, and steroid hormones"], "one_line_verdict": "Pantothenic acid is so ubiquitous in food that clinically meaningful deficiency is essentially unseen outside experimental starvation or severe malnutrition, a recent postmortem study found markedly lower pantothenate in Alzheimer's brain tissue which is an intriguing but purely associative biomarker finding, and there is no RCT evidence of any kind that pantothenic acid supplementation improves cognition, mood or energy in anyone.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. Pantothenic acid is the obligate precursor of coenzyme A, essential for fatty-acid oxidation and synthesis, the citric acid cycle, and the synthesis of acetylcholine, cholesterol and steroid hormones; this biochemistry is established and directly relevant to cholinergic neurotransmission. Because pantothenic acid is present in nearly all foods, naturally occurring deficiency in humans has essentially only been produced experimentally (semi-synthetic deficient diets plus a pantothenic-acid antagonist) and causes fatigue, irritability, paresthesias ('burning feet syndrome') and GI symptoms; this classical experimental-deficiency literature is old and was not independently re-confirmed with a primary source in this search beyond background knowledge. Alzheimer's disease biomarker association. Two postmortem studies from the same research group found markedly lower vitamin B5 (pantothenate) concentrations in multiple brain regions (hippocampus, entorhinal cortex, middle temporal gyrus) of people with Alzheimer's disease compared with controls (n=9 AD, 9 controls), worst in the most pathologically affected regions, alongside decreased activity of CoA-dependent tricarboxylic-acid-cycle enzymes [xu2020pantothenate]. The authors hypothesise that cerebral pantothenate deficiency (distinct from dietary/systemic deficiency, since blood levels were not necessarily low) contributes to the acetylcholine and energy-metabolism deficits of Alzheimer's disease and speculate this might be reversible with oral vitamin B5, but this is an uncontrolled, very small postmortem association, not an interventional trial, and must not be presented as evidence that vitamin B5 supplementation treats or prevents Alzheimer's disease or improves cognition in anyone, including the general healthy population. No RCT evidence in any population. No randomized controlled trial isolating pantothenic acid with a cognitive, mood, fatigue or energy endpoint in any human population (healthy adults, older adults or Alzheimer's patients) was identified in this search; pantothenic acid appears only as one inert component of the generic multivitamin formulations used in the Benton and Kennedy trials discussed in the thiamine-b1, riboflavin-b2 and niacin-b3 monographs, where its individual contribution cannot be isolated. Deficiency in Morocco/EU/US. No population-level deficiency-prevalence data for pantothenic acid in these target markets were identified in this search; given its ubiquity in food, clinically significant deficiency is expected to be very rare in all three target markets, though this was not independently confirmed with surveillance data. What is unknown. Whether correcting any marginal pantothenate status, or supplementing a biomarker-defined 'low cerebral B5' state, has any measurable cognitive effect in living humans; whether oral supplementation can meaningfully raise cerebral (as opposed to systemic) pantothenate given the postmortem brain-deficiency pattern observed in the small AD study.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Classical pantothenic acid deficiency is essentially a laboratory artefact of experimental semi-synthetic diets plus an antagonist; in ordinary diets across Morocco, the EU and the US, dietary pantothenic acid is abundant and overt deficiency is not expected to occur. The intriguing cerebral-pantothenate-deficiency finding in Alzheimer's disease brain tissue [xu2020pantothenate] describes a localized, disease-associated biochemical abnormality in a severely affected population, not a nutritional deficiency state correctable by ordinary dietary means, and absolutely not an enhancement opportunity in healthy adults. There is no enhancement evidence of any kind for pantothenic acid in replete people of any age. This is the clearest case among the five B vitamins in this unit where the honest scientific position is that neither deficiency correction (because deficiency essentially does not occur) nor enhancement (because no trial shows a cognitive benefit) provides a strong rationale for a cognition-focused product claim; the only legitimate basis is the standard EU nutritional-adequacy claim.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Pantothenic acid ('B5') appears in 'stress support' and 'adrenal health' supplement marketing (the term 'anti-stress vitamin' dates to older, largely unreplicated animal and uncontrolled human literature not independently verified in this unit) and sometimes in 'brain energy' blends citing its CoA/acetylcholine role. No RCT evidence supports a stress, energy, mood or cognitive-enhancement claim for pantothenic acid in healthy humans; the single most interesting human brain-relevant finding (cerebral pantothenate depletion in Alzheimer's disease) is an uncontrolled postmortem association in nine AD cases and must not be marketed as evidence that vitamin B5 treats or prevents dementia, let alone enhances cognition in healthy people. The only safe, evidence-consistent claim is the EU-authorised 'pantothenic acid contributes to normal mental performance and to normal energy-yielding metabolism' wording at source level, understood strictly as a nutritional-adequacy statement.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Dietary pantothenic acid is absorbed in the small intestine (shared sodium-dependent multivitamin transporter with biotin and lipoate) and converted intracellularly to coenzyme A (CoA) via a multi-step pathway. CoA is the universal carrier for acyl groups in fatty-acid oxidation and synthesis, the citric acid cycle (acetyl-CoA), and synthesis of acetylcholine (via acetyl-CoA plus choline, choline acetyltransferase), cholesterol and steroid hormones. The acetylcholine link is directly relevant to cognition in principle (acetylcholine is central to attention and memory circuits), which is why the Alzheimer's cerebral-deficiency finding is mechanistically interesting, but no human intervention has tested whether raising pantothenate (systemically or cerebrally) changes acetylcholine synthesis or cognition in vivo.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Dietary pantothenic acid -> intestinal absorption via sodium-dependent multivitamin transporter -> intracellular conversion to coenzyme A", "detail": "Established pharmacokinetics; shares its intestinal transporter with biotin and alpha-lipoic acid, raising a theoretical (unquantified) competitive-absorption consideration at very high combined doses.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Coenzyme A -> acetyl-CoA -> citric acid cycle entry and fatty-acid oxidation/synthesis", "detail": "Textbook biochemistry; central to all cellular energy metabolism including neurons.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Acetyl-CoA + choline -> acetylcholine (via choline acetyltransferase)", "detail": "Established biochemical pathway; acetylcholine is a key neurotransmitter for attention and memory, making this the most direct mechanistic link between pantothenate and cognition, though not demonstrated as rate-limiting in humans with normal pantothenate status.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Alzheimer's disease brain tissue -> markedly lower regional pantothenate and reduced CoA-dependent TCA-cycle enzyme activity, worst in most-affected regions", "detail": "Postmortem study, n=9 AD vs 9 controls, 7 brain regions [xu2020pantothenate]; a companion paper from the same group reports decreased CoA-dependent TCA enzymes consistent with this. Association only; cause vs. consequence of neurodegeneration is not established, and whether this reflects impaired local synthesis/transport rather than systemic deficiency is unknown.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Oral vitamin B5 supplementation -> restoration of cerebral pantothenate/CoA and improved acetylcholine synthesis/cognition", "detail": "Explicitly proposed as a hypothesis by the authors of the postmortem study but not tested in any human interventional trial identified in this search.", "evidence_level": "speculative"}], "pathways": ["coenzyme A biosynthesis and acyl-group metabolism", "citric acid cycle (acetyl-CoA entry)", "acetylcholine synthesis (choline acetyltransferase)", "fatty-acid oxidation/synthesis", "cholesterol and steroid hormone synthesis"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_decline", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Postmortem brain tissue from Alzheimer's disease cases (n=9) vs controls (n=9); not a living-subject cognitive-outcome study", "dose_or_intake": "not applicable (observational biomarker study, not an intervention)", "duration": "not applicable (cross-sectional postmortem)", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "Markedly lower regional brain pantothenate and reduced CoA-dependent TCA-cycle enzyme activity in AD brain tissue, worst in the most pathologically affected regions (hippocampus, entorhinal cortex, middle temporal gyrus); an association between disease state and a brain biomarker, not evidence of a treatable cognitive effect.", "effect_estimate": "Described as 'widespread, severe' deficiency in AD vs control tissue; no odds ratio or standardized effect size reported in abstract", "certainty_notes": "Very small (n=9 vs 9), postmortem, cross-sectional, no causal direction established (could be consequence rather than cause of neurodegeneration), no living-subject replication, no intervention tested; graded D (preliminary/mechanistic) rather than C because this is tissue biochemistry, not even a clinical correlational study in living patients.", "key_study_ids": ["xu2020pantothenate"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "No isolated-pantothenic-acid RCT identified in any population, healthy or clinical", "dose_or_intake": "not applicable", "duration": "not applicable", "grade": "E", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No RCT evidence exists for pantothenic acid and cognition, mood, fatigue or energy in healthy adults; this row documents the explicit absence of evidence rather than a tested-and-refuted effect.", "effect_estimate": "not applicable (no trials found)", "certainty_notes": "Absence of evidence reported honestly per protocol.", "key_study_ids": []}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "not tested", "onset": "not applicable", "duration_studied": "not applicable", "domain": "other", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "effect_estimate": "no acute pantothenic acid cognition trial identified", "certainty": "insufficient data", "safety": "not applicable", "study_ids": []}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "not applicable (cross-sectional postmortem)", "population": "clinical_population", "outcome": "regional brain pantothenate concentration and CoA-dependent enzyme activity in Alzheimer's disease vs controls", "effect_estimate": "Markedly lower pantothenate and enzyme activity in AD, worst in most-affected regions; no numeric effect size in abstract", "evidence_quality": "very low (D), small postmortem association", "limitations": "n=9 vs 9, cross-sectional, no causal direction, no living-subject or interventional confirmation", "study_ids": ["xu2020pantothenate"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Pantothenic acid is one of the safest vitamins known, with no established toxicity even at doses many times the adequate intake; the main reported adverse effect at very high supplemental doses is mild, reversible diarrhea.", "adverse_effects": ["Mild GI upset/diarrhea reported anecdotally at very high supplemental doses (multi-gram range); no serious adverse effects established at nutritional or typical supplemental doses"], "contraindications": ["None established"], "drug_interactions": ["None well established; shares an intestinal transporter with biotin and alpha-lipoic acid, a theoretical but unquantified consideration at very high combined supplemental doses"], "upper_limits": "No UL has been set by EFSA or US NASEM for pantothenic acid due to the absence of demonstrated toxicity.", "sleep_impact": "No evidence of sleep disruption from pantothenic acid at any studied dose.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Long-term exposure at nutritional and typical supplemental doses is presumed safe based on widespread dietary intake with no safety signal; no dedicated long-term RCT safety dataset was identified in this search because no RCT of any kind on this ingredient and a brain outcome exists.", "daily_use_appropriate": "yes", "special_populations": "No specific concerns identified for pregnancy, lactation, children or chronic disease at nutritional doses; no psychiatric medication interactions identified in this search.", "specialist_review_needed": ["none identified as specifically necessary for food-level pantothenic acid use"], "hard_safety_gate": "pass", "gate_reason": "No safety concern identified at any studied dose; this is among the least risky ingredients reviewed in this unit."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Pantothenic acid is an authorised vitamin for supplements/fortification (Directive 2002/46/EC). EFSA-authorised claims (Regulation (EU) 432/2012 register, as commonly published; not re-fetched live in this session) include 'pantothenic acid contributes to normal mental performance', 'normal energy-yielding metabolism', 'normal synthesis of steroid hormones, vitamin D and some neurotransmitters', and 'reduction of tiredness and fatigue', usable at >=15% NRV per portion. The 'normal mental performance' wording is a nutritional-adequacy claim and should not be presented as an enhancement claim.", "us": "Pantothenic acid (as calcium pantothenate or d-panthenol) is GRAS and widely used in fortification and dietary supplements under DSHEA; standard structure/function claims are permitted with disclaimers; no specific FDA safety communications relevant to pantothenic acid were identified in this search.", "morocco": "Pantothenic acid may be included in some fortified products and multivitamin supplements; ONSSA governs food-grade additive and supplement status; specific claim wording and fortification-program inclusion were not independently verified in this session - uncertain.", "permitted_claims_examples": ["EU: 'Pantothenic acid contributes to normal mental performance' (>=15% NRV per portion) - wording as commonly published in the EU claims register; not re-verified live in this session; strictly a nutritional-adequacy claim"], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "None; pantothenic acid is a conventional, long-established vitamin. Pantethine, a related disulfide form sometimes marketed for cholesterol management, is a distinct ingredient and out of scope for this monograph.", "regulatory_gate": "pass"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Excellent. Pantothenic acid is widely distributed in food already, inexpensive, and claim-level doses are trivially small relative to any food serving.", "formats": ["calcium pantothenate or d-pantothenic acid added to fortified foods, beverages, bars, multivitamin blends"], "stability": "Pantothenic acid is relatively heat-stable but can degrade somewhat with prolonged cooking in water (leaching) and is sensitive to extremes of pH; overall more stable than thiamine or riboflavin under typical food-processing conditions (general food-science knowledge; not independently re-verified with a primary source in this unit).", "taste": "No significant taste issues at claim-level doses.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Calcium pantothenate and free pantothenic acid are both well absorbed via the sodium-dependent multivitamin transporter; no clinically meaningful bioavailability differences identified between common supplemental forms.", "dose_feasibility": "The EU claim-level dose fits trivially into any food serving; there is no higher 'efficacy dose' to target since no interventional human dose-response data for any brain outcome exist.", "cost_availability": "Pantothenic acid is an inexpensive, widely available commodity ingredient.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 1, "effect_magnitude": 0, "safety": 9, "reproducibility": 1, "food_suitability": 9, "cost_access": 9, "rationale": "evidence_quality very low (1): zero interventional human trials exist for any brain outcome; the only direct human brain-relevant data point is a tiny, uncontrolled postmortem association in Alzheimer's disease (grade D). effect_magnitude near zero (0): no quantifiable effect size exists for any population. safety very high (9): no UL, no meaningful adverse effects reported at any dose. reproducibility very low (1): a single small postmortem study with no replication and no interventional test. food_suitability very high (9): ubiquitous, cheap, stable-enough, trivial claim dose. cost_access high (9): cheap commodity ingredient. This is the weakest evidence base of the five monographs in this unit, honestly reported as such."}, "research_gaps": ["No interventional (RCT) human trial of pantothenic acid on any cognitive, mood, fatigue or energy outcome exists in any population - the single largest gap identified in this unit.", "The Alzheimer's cerebral-pantothenate-deficiency finding has not been replicated in an independent cohort, nor tested with an oral vitamin B5 intervention in living AD patients, nor assessed for cause-vs-consequence direction.", "No modern prevalence data for pantothenic acid deficiency or marginal status in Morocco, EU or US populations were identified.", "No dose-response or pharmacokinetic data on whether oral pantothenic acid supplementation can meaningfully raise cerebral (as opposed to systemic/plasma) pantothenate concentrations in humans.", "No acute-dosing or chronic healthy-adult cognition trial of any kind exists to either support or refute a Brain Food claim."], "_derived": {"composite_score": 4.0, "eligibility": "eligible", "safety_gate": "pass", "regulatory_gate": "pass", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": null, "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": null, "best_grade_overall": null, "benefit_domains": {}, "n_outcome_rows": 2, "n_studies": 1, "n_verified": 1, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 0, "n_systematic_reviews": 0, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["insufficient"]}}, {"id": "riboflavin-b2", "name": "Riboflavin (vitamin B2)", "category": "B", "category_name": "Vitamins", "aliases": ["riboflavin", "vitamin B2", "flavin mononucleotide (FMN)", "flavin adenine dinucleotide (FAD)", "lactoflavin"], "food_sources": ["milk and dairy products", "eggs", "lean meats and organ meats", "green leafy vegetables", "fortified cereals and bread", "almonds, mushrooms"], "active_constituents": ["flavin mononucleotide (FMN) and flavin adenine dinucleotide (FAD), coenzymes for oxidoreductases including mitochondrial electron-transport-chain complexes I and II, glutathione reductase and MTHFR"], "one_line_verdict": "Riboflavin is essential for mitochondrial energy metabolism and a genuinely effective, well-replicated migraine prophylactic at pharmacological doses, but there is no RCT evidence that it improves memory, attention or any measured cognitive domain in healthy adults, and its only confirmed brain-relevant human action is on headache, not cognition.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. Riboflavin, as FAD and FMN, is the coenzyme for mitochondrial complexes I and II and for MTHFR in folate/homocysteine metabolism; this biochemistry is established. The best-characterised human brain-relevant action of riboflavin is migraine prophylaxis: a 3-month RCT of 400 mg/day riboflavin in 55 adults with migraine reduced attack frequency (p=0.005) and headache days (p=0.012), with 59% of riboflavin-treated patients classed as responders (>=50% improvement) versus 15% on placebo, NNT=2.3, and only minor adverse events [schoenen1998]. This is a genuine, replicated clinical effect, but it is a headache/vascular outcome, not a measured cognitive outcome, and 400 mg is a pharmacological dose far above nutritional intake. A combination product (riboflavin 400 mg + magnesium + CoQ10) also reduced migraine days in a multicenter trial, but as a combination it cannot isolate riboflavin's individual contribution [guilbot2015]. Genotype-specific biomarker effects. In adults homozygous for the MTHFR C677T polymorphism (TT genotype, associated with higher homocysteine and blood pressure), 16 weeks of riboflavin 1.6 mg/day increased plasma S-adenosylmethionine (p=0.008) and cystathionine (p=0.045) versus placebo in a 47-person RCT, confirming riboflavin corrects a genotype-specific perturbation in one-carbon metabolism [rooney2020]. This is a biomarker effect in a specific genetic subgroup, not a cognitive outcome, and should not be extrapolated to the general population or to cognition. Healthy young adults and students. No RCT isolating riboflavin alone with a cognitive endpoint in healthy, non-TT-genotype young adults was identified in this search. Riboflavin appears as one component of multivitamin/B-complex trials (Kennedy 2010, Kennedy 2011, Benton 1995) that show mood/subjective-energy effects attributable to the whole blend, not to riboflavin specifically; one of the Benton trials linked improved mood in women partly to riboflavin and pyridoxine status change, but this is an association within a 9-vitamin blend, not an isolated riboflavin effect [benton1995mood]. What is unknown. Whether nutritional-level riboflavin (as opposed to the 400 mg pharmacological migraine dose) has any cognitive or alertness effect in replete healthy adults; whether the MTHFR-TT biomarker correction translates into any functional brain benefit; acute dosing has not been tested for cognition.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "True riboflavin deficiency (ariboflavinosis: angular stomatitis, glossitis, dermatitis) is rare in EU/US/Morocco populations with dairy and fortified-grain access, though marginal status can occur in strict vegans with poor planning, the elderly, and people with malabsorption (general nutrition knowledge, not independently re-verified in this unit). Correcting deficiency with ordinary food-level riboflavin is unambiguously appropriate and supports the EU-authorised 'normal functioning of the nervous system' claim. The 400 mg migraine-prophylaxis effect [schoenen1998] is a pharmacological, drug-like use of riboflavin in a diagnosed clinical population and is categorically different from nutritional adequacy; it should never be presented as evidence that riboflavin enhances brain function or cognition in healthy people. The MTHFR-TT biomarker finding [rooney2020] is a genotype-specific correction, not a general-population enhancement effect.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Riboflavin is sometimes marketed loosely as part of 'energy' or 'brain' B-complex blends implying alertness or focus benefits. The only solid, replicated riboflavin-specific human brain-relevant effect is migraine prevention at a 400 mg pharmacological dose, which is unrelated to cognition and far above any food-fortification level. No claim of memory, attention or mood enhancement from nutritional-dose riboflavin is supported by isolated-ingredient trial evidence; multivitamin trials that include riboflavin cannot be attributed to it specifically. The EU-authorised claim 'riboflavin contributes to the normal functioning of the nervous system' (and 'normal energy-yielding metabolism', 'maintenance of normal vision', 'protection of cells from oxidative stress') is legitimate at source level (>=15% NRV) and should be the only claim basis; a Brain Food product should not imply cognitive enhancement or migraine treatment from a standard food-fortification dose of riboflavin.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Dietary riboflavin is absorbed in the proximal small intestine by a saturable transporter and phosphorylated to FMN and then FAD in tissues. FAD/FMN are essential coenzymes for mitochondrial electron-transport-chain complexes I and II and for MTHFR, linking riboflavin status to both cellular energy production and homocysteine/one-carbon metabolism. The migraine-prophylaxis effect is hypothesised to work through improving mitochondrial energy metabolism in migraine-susceptible brains (a mitochondrial-deficit model of migraine), which is plausible but not directly proven at the mechanistic level in humans; the clinical outcome (fewer migraine days) is nonetheless established. There is no established or even well-studied mechanistic pathway from riboflavin status to measured cognition (memory, attention) in humans.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Oral riboflavin -> saturable intestinal absorption -> phosphorylation to FMN then FAD in tissues", "detail": "Standard, well-characterised absorption and coenzyme synthesis; riboflavin status is routinely assessed via erythrocyte glutathione reductase activation coefficient.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "FAD/FMN -> cofactors for mitochondrial complex I and II and for MTHFR -> cellular ATP production and homocysteine remethylation capacity", "detail": "Textbook biochemistry; in MTHFR C677T TT-genotype adults, 1.6 mg/day riboflavin for 16 weeks raised plasma SAM (p=0.008) and cystathionine (p=0.045) [rooney2020], confirming genotype-specific correction of one-carbon metabolism.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Hypothesised mitochondrial energy deficit in migraine-susceptible brain -> high-dose riboflavin repletion of flavin-dependent ETC capacity -> reduced migraine frequency", "detail": "400 mg/day riboflavin for 3 months reduced attack frequency and headache days versus placebo (NNT=2.3) in a well-conducted RCT [schoenen1998]; mechanism (mitochondrial energy deficit) is plausible but not directly demonstrated with brain imaging/biomarkers in this trial.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Pharmacological riboflavin repletion -> measured cognitive outcome (memory, attention, alertness)", "detail": "No human trial directly tests this link; migraine relief is a headache/pain outcome, not a cognitive-domain outcome, and must not be conflated with cognition.", "evidence_level": "speculative"}], "pathways": ["mitochondrial electron transport chain complex I/II (flavoprotein cofactors)", "MTHFR / one-carbon metabolism and homocysteine remethylation", "glutathione reductase (antioxidant capacity, status biomarker)", "hypothesised migraine mitochondrial-deficit pathway"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "other", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Adults with diagnosed migraine (not a cognitive population; included for completeness of riboflavin's only strong clinical brain-relevant RCT evidence)", "dose_or_intake": "400 mg/day riboflavin", "duration": "3 months", "grade": "B", "direction": "benefit", "effect_summary": "Attack frequency and headache days significantly reduced versus placebo; 59% responder rate vs 15% placebo; NNT=2.3; well tolerated with only minor adverse events.", "effect_estimate": "Attack frequency p=0.005; headache days p=0.012; responder rate 59% vs 15% (p=0.002); NNT=2.3 [schoenen1998]", "certainty_notes": "Single, reasonably sized, well-conducted RCT with good replication in the broader migraine literature (not re-verified exhaustively here); graded B rather than A because this is one trial plus a combination-product trial, not multiple independent high-quality monotherapy RCTs reviewed here; downgraded one level for indirectness to a cognition-focused brief (this is a headache, not cognitive, outcome).", "key_study_ids": ["schoenen1998"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "other", "population": "other", "population_detail": "Adults homozygous for MTHFR C677T (TT genotype), biomarker outcome not cognitive", "dose_or_intake": "1.6 mg/day riboflavin", "duration": "16 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Riboflavin increased plasma SAM and cystathionine in TT-genotype adults versus placebo; this is a biomarker correction of one-carbon metabolism, not a measured cognitive outcome.", "effect_estimate": "SAM increase p=0.008; cystathionine increase p=0.045; n=47 (24 riboflavin, 23 placebo) [rooney2020]", "certainty_notes": "Genotype-restricted subgroup (TT only, roughly 10% of most populations), biomarker endpoint only, single trial; cannot be generalised to cognition or to non-TT individuals.", "key_study_ids": ["rooney2020"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "127-129 young healthy UK adults within a 9-vitamin multivitamin trial (riboflavin not isolated)", "dose_or_intake": "9-vitamin supplement, 10x RDA, including riboflavin", "duration": "1 year", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "Improved mood in women was associated in the original report with improved riboflavin and pyridoxine status, but riboflavin's individual contribution cannot be separated from the 9-vitamin blend or from pyridoxine.", "effect_estimate": "not reported numerically for riboflavin specifically", "certainty_notes": "Not an isolated-riboflavin trial; cannot support a riboflavin-specific grade above D for cognition/mood.", "key_study_ids": ["benton1995mood"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "not tested", "onset": "not applicable", "duration_studied": "not applicable", "domain": "other", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "effect_estimate": "no acute isolated-riboflavin cognition trial identified", "certainty": "insufficient data", "safety": "not applicable", "study_ids": []}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "3 months", "population": "clinical_population", "outcome": "migraine attack frequency and headache days (not a cognitive outcome)", "effect_estimate": "p=0.005 (frequency), p=0.012 (headache days), NNT=2.3", "evidence_quality": "moderate (B) for the headache outcome; not applicable to cognition", "limitations": "Single RCT, pharmacological dose, headache not cognitive endpoint", "study_ids": ["schoenen1998"]}, {"duration": "16 weeks", "population": "other", "outcome": "one-carbon metabolite biomarkers in MTHFR TT genotype adults", "effect_estimate": "SAM +, cystathionine + (p<0.05 each)", "evidence_quality": "low-moderate (C), biomarker only", "limitations": "Genotype-restricted, biomarker not cognitive outcome, single trial", "study_ids": ["rooney2020"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Riboflavin is one of the safest vitamins known; it is water-soluble with limited intestinal absorption at high doses and excess is excreted in urine (causing the well-known bright yellow discoloration), with no established toxicity even at the 400 mg/day pharmacological migraine dose used for months.", "adverse_effects": ["Bright yellow/orange urine discoloration (harmless) at supplemental/pharmacological doses", "Occasional mild GI upset (diarrhea) or polyuria reported at 400 mg/day in the migraine trial [schoenen1998]"], "contraindications": ["None established at nutritional or typical supplemental doses"], "drug_interactions": ["Limited evidence; some older anecdotal reports of interaction with tricyclic antidepressants (phenothiazine-class) affecting riboflavin status, not a major safety signal; not independently verified in this unit"], "upper_limits": "No UL set by EFSA or US NASEM due to low oral toxicity and limited absorption at high doses; the 400 mg/day migraine dose, far above nutritional need, has been used for months with good tolerability.", "sleep_impact": "No evidence of sleep disruption from riboflavin at any studied dose.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "The 400 mg/day migraine-prophylaxis dose has been studied for 3 months with good tolerability [schoenen1998]; multi-year fortification-level exposure is well established as safe through decades of food fortification and multivitamin use, though not separately re-verified with a dedicated long-term trial in this unit.", "daily_use_appropriate": "yes", "special_populations": "Riboflavin is used in pregnancy at standard prenatal doses without concern; photosensitive riboflavin degrades with light exposure (relevant to formulation, not direct safety); no specific psychiatric medication contraindication identified in this search.", "specialist_review_needed": ["headache/neurology specialist review if Brain Food ever considers a migraine-prophylaxis claim or dose (400 mg) - this is outside the cognitive-enhancement food-product scope of this unit"], "hard_safety_gate": "pass", "gate_reason": "No meaningful safety concern identified even at pharmacological (400 mg/day) doses; nutritional fortification doses are trivially safe."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Riboflavin is an authorised vitamin for supplements/fortification (Directive 2002/46/EC). EFSA-authorised claims (Regulation (EU) 432/2012 register, as commonly published; not re-fetched live in this session) include 'riboflavin contributes to the normal functioning of the nervous system', 'normal energy-yielding metabolism', 'maintenance of normal vision', 'protection of cell constituents from oxidative stress', usable at >=15% NRV per portion. A migraine-prophylaxis claim is not an authorised nutrition/health claim and would require medicinal-product or borderline-product regulatory pathways, not a food claim.", "us": "Riboflavin is GRAS, used in fortification and widely available as a dietary supplement under DSHEA; standard structure/function claims ('supports energy metabolism', 'supports nervous system function') are permitted with disclaimers; a migraine or cognition claim would require FTC-level substantiation not currently available for riboflavin alone.", "morocco": "Riboflavin is included in national flour/food fortification programs alongside other B vitamins; ONSSA governs food-grade additive and supplement status; specific claim wording was not independently verified in this session - uncertain.", "permitted_claims_examples": ["EU: 'Riboflavin contributes to the normal functioning of the nervous system' (>=15% NRV per portion) - wording as commonly published in the EU claims register; not re-verified live in this session"], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "None; riboflavin is a conventional, long-established vitamin.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Good for the authorised nutritional claim at low doses; poor for any migraine-type claim since 400 mg would be an unusually large, likely off-taste/bulky addition to a typical food serving and would move the product toward a medicinal/borderline-product classification.", "formats": ["riboflavin or riboflavin-5'-phosphate added to fortified cereals, beverages, bars"], "stability": "Riboflavin is notably light-sensitive (photodegrades under UV/visible light, especially in clear packaging or when exposed in liquid form) but reasonably heat-stable; opaque or light-protected packaging is needed to preserve potency in milk, fortified beverages and some bars (general food-science knowledge; not independently re-verified with a primary source in this unit).", "taste": "Riboflavin itself imparts a yellow-orange color at higher concentrations and can be mildly bitter at high doses; at claim-level doses this is a non-issue.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Riboflavin-5'-phosphate (the phosphorylated form) and free riboflavin have similar bioavailability at nutritional doses via the saturable intestinal transporter; absorption efficiency decreases somewhat at very high single doses (relevant mainly to the 400 mg migraine dose, not to food-claim doses).", "dose_feasibility": "The EU claim-level dose fits any food serving; the 400 mg migraine dose does not realistically fit a food serving and is a supplement/pharmacological use case outside this unit's scope.", "cost_availability": "Riboflavin is an inexpensive, widely available commodity ingredient.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 2, "effect_magnitude": 1, "safety": 10, "reproducibility": 3, "food_suitability": 8, "cost_access": 9, "rationale": "evidence_quality low (2) for any cognitive claim: the only solid RCT evidence (migraine) is grade B but is not a cognitive outcome; MTHFR biomarker evidence is grade C and genotype-restricted; no isolated-riboflavin cognition RCT exists. effect_magnitude low (1) for cognition specifically since no cognitive effect size exists; the migraine effect itself (not scored here as a cognition outcome) is clinically meaningful (NNT=2.3). safety very high (10): no UL, excellent tolerability even at 400 mg/day for months. reproducibility low-moderate (3): migraine effect is well-attested in the headache literature generally but riboflavin-isolated cognition evidence has zero replication because it does not exist. food_suitability high (8), minor deduction for light-sensitivity formulation constraint. cost_access high (9): cheap commodity ingredient."}, "research_gaps": ["No RCT isolating riboflavin at nutritional or moderate supplemental doses with a validated cognitive battery in healthy adults, students or knowledge workers.", "No acute-dosing cognition trial.", "Unknown whether the MTHFR-TT biomarker correction (SAM, cystathionine) translates into any functional neurological or cognitive benefit.", "No dedicated null/negative riboflavin-cognition trial was found; this appears to reflect that the isolated-ingredient cognition question has simply not been tested, not that it has been tested and refuted - flagged as an evidence gap rather than a null finding.", "Riboflavin's contribution within multivitamin/B-complex mood trials (Kennedy, Benton) cannot be isolated from the other ingredients; a factorial or riboflavin-only arm trial would resolve this."], "_derived": {"composite_score": 4.8, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "pass", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": null, "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": "B", "best_grade_overall": null, "benefit_domains": {}, "n_outcome_rows": 3, "n_studies": 4, "n_verified": 4, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 0, "n_systematic_reviews": 0, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["benefit", "insufficient", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "thiamine-b1", "name": "Thiamine (vitamin B1)", "category": "B", "category_name": "Vitamins", "aliases": ["thiamine", "thiamin", "vitamin B1", "thiamine pyrophosphate (TPP)", "benfotiamine (synthetic S-acyl derivative)", "thiamine hydrochloride", "thiamine mononitrate"], "food_sources": ["pork", "whole grains and fortified bread/cereal", "legumes (lentils, beans)", "sunflower seeds, nuts", "fortified rice and flour in many national programs", "yeast extract"], "active_constituents": ["thiamine pyrophosphate (TPP), the coenzyme for pyruvate dehydrogenase, alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase and transketolase, central to glucose oxidation and the pentose phosphate pathway in neurons"], "one_line_verdict": "Thiamine is absolutely required for brain glucose metabolism and severe deficiency (Wernicke's encephalopathy) causes dramatic, treatable cognitive collapse, but no controlled trial shows that extra thiamine improves cognition in healthy, adequately-nourished young adults, and the one suggestive signal (Benton 1995) is a 9-vitamin multivitamin, not thiamine alone.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. Thiamine pyrophosphate is the obligatory cofactor for pyruvate dehydrogenase and transketolase, so brain glucose oxidation cannot proceed without adequate thiamine; this biochemistry is textbook and established in humans. Severe deficiency produces Wernicke's encephalopathy (confusion, ataxia, ophthalmoplegia) and, if untreated, irreversible Korsakoff amnesia; parenteral thiamine reverses the acute confusional state in a high proportion of cases when given early [schaumburg2019wernicke, latt2014]. This is deficiency correction in a clinical population, not enhancement, and is not generalisable to replete adults. Deficiency occurs in chronic alcohol use disorder, after bariatric surgery (prevalence 15-49% pre-operatively and higher post-operatively without supplementation) [flancbaum2006, carrodeguas2005, kerns2018], and in malnutrition; prevalence in generally well-nourished EU/US/Morocco populations eating fortified grain products is low, though not systematically surveyed for this unit. Healthy young adults. No RCT isolating thiamine alone with a cognitive endpoint in healthy young adults was found. The closest evidence is a 1-year, double-blind trial of a 9-vitamin supplement (10x RDA, including thiamine) in 127-129 young healthy adults: mood improved in women (more 'agreeable', better mental health) and attention performance improved in women only, and regression analysis linked the cognitive gain specifically to the change in thiamine status in females, not males, with no effect in males on either outcome [benton1995mood, benton1995cognition]. Because nine vitamins were given together, the thiamine-specific claim rests on a post hoc regression in one small, 30-year-old, single-sex-interaction study and cannot be treated as confirmed. Deficient or marginal populations and dementia. Benfotiamine (a fat-soluble thiamine derivative with higher bioavailability) slowed decline on the CDR scale (77% less worsening, p=0.034) and reduced advanced glycation end-products in a 12-month, 70-person phase IIa trial in amnestic MCI/mild Alzheimer's disease, with a non-significant 43% relative reduction in ADAS-Cog decline (p=0.125) [gibson2020benfotiamine]. This is a small, single-site, NIH-funded trial in a clinical, impaired population and must not be generalised to healthy adults or to thiamine itself (benfotiamine is pharmacokinetically distinct). Glucose metabolism link. Thiamine's role in glucose oxidation is mechanistically plausible as a reason extra thiamine might help under high glycolytic/cognitive demand, but no acute human trial testing thiamine against glucose-dependent cognitive performance in healthy adults was identified in this search; this remains a mechanistic hypothesis. What is unknown. Whether correcting marginal (sub-clinical) thiamine status in otherwise replete adults, students or knowledge workers measurably changes attention, working memory or fatigue; acute dosing has not been tested in cognition.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Thiamine is an unambiguous essential-nutrient case: severe deficiency causes a specific, well-characterised, treatable encephalopathy (Wernicke-Korsakoff) in alcohol use disorder, bariatric surgery and severe malnutrition [schaumburg2019wernicke, flancbaum2006, kerns2018]. This evidence is strong but belongs entirely to clinical/deficient populations and must not be extrapolated to adequately nourished healthy adults. For enhancement in replete people, the evidence is essentially absent: the only suggestive finding is a sex-specific regression association within a 9-vitamin multivitamin trial [benton1995cognition], not a thiamine-isolated effect. The benfotiamine-in-MCI trial [gibson2020benfotiamine] is deficiency/pathology-adjacent (impaired glucose metabolism in AD brain) and cannot be read as evidence that thiamine enhances cognition in healthy brains.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Energy-drink and nootropic marketing often implies thiamine (or benfotiamine) will 'boost mental energy' or 'sharpen focus' in healthy consumers. The only legitimate human evidence is (a) dramatic reversal of a specific deficiency encephalopathy in at-risk clinical groups, (b) one dated multivitamin trial with a sex-specific, non-isolated thiamine signal, and (c) a small, non-definitive benfotiamine trial in cognitively impaired patients. The EU-authorised claim is narrowly 'thiamine contributes to normal psychological function/normal energy-yielding metabolism/normal functioning of the nervous system' at >=15% NRV (>=0.165 mg), which is a true nutritional statement about adequacy, not an enhancement claim. An honest Brain Food position: thiamine belongs in a food product as a nutritional-adequacy ingredient (easy, cheap, safe, legally claimable) and should never be marketed as a cognitive enhancer for replete adults, since that evidence does not exist.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Dietary thiamine is absorbed in the small intestine (active transport at low, passive at high doses), phosphorylated intracellularly to thiamine pyrophosphate (TPP), and serves as the obligatory cofactor for pyruvate dehydrogenase, alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase and transketolase. These enzymes are rate-limiting for aerobic glucose oxidation and the pentose phosphate pathway, both essential for neuronal ATP production and myelin/nucleotide synthesis. Severe deficiency selectively damages metabolically vulnerable brain regions (mammillary bodies, thalamus) producing Wernicke's encephalopathy; this causal chain is established in humans. Whether raising thiamine status above adequacy increases glucose-oxidation capacity enough to change cognition in a replete brain is not demonstrated.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Oral thiamine -> intestinal absorption (saturable active transport at normal intake, passive diffusion at high doses) -> hepatic phosphorylation to TPP", "detail": "Well-characterised pharmacokinetics; benfotiamine, an S-acyl derivative, is absorbed passively and achieves higher plasma thiamine than thiamine hydrochloride at equivalent doses [gibson2020benfotiamine].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "TPP -> cofactor for pyruvate dehydrogenase and alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase -> mitochondrial ATP from glucose oxidation", "detail": "Textbook biochemistry; reduced transketolase activity is the basis of the erythrocyte transketolase activation test for subclinical deficiency.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Severe TPP deficiency -> selective neuronal vulnerability in thalamus/mammillary bodies -> Wernicke's encephalopathy, reversible early with parenteral thiamine", "detail": "Established clinical syndrome in alcohol use disorder and bariatric surgery populations [schaumburg2019wernicke, latt2014, kerns2018]; not relevant to replete adults.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "In Alzheimer's disease, impaired glucose metabolism partly mimics thiamine-dependent enzyme deficits -> benfotiamine may reduce advanced glycation end-products and glucose-metabolism decline", "detail": "Small 12-month RCT in MCI/mild AD showed reduced AGE accumulation and slower CDR worsening [gibson2020benfotiamine]; mechanism plausible but efficacy preliminary and population-restricted.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "In replete healthy adults, marginal increases in thiamine status -> hypothesised increase in glycolytic/oxidative capacity under cognitive load -> measurable attention/energy benefit", "detail": "No direct human trial isolating thiamine and testing this chain in healthy adults; inferred only from a 9-vitamin trial's post hoc regression [benton1995cognition].", "evidence_level": "speculative"}], "pathways": ["pyruvate dehydrogenase / TCA cycle entry", "alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase", "transketolase / pentose phosphate pathway", "erythrocyte transketolase activity as a status biomarker"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "127-129 young healthy adults (mixed sex), adequately nourished, UK", "dose_or_intake": "9-vitamin supplement at 10x RDA including ~1.4 mg thiamine/day", "duration": "1 year", "grade": "D", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Attention performance improved in women only; regression linked the improvement specifically to change in thiamine status in women, with no effect in men on either mood or cognition.", "effect_estimate": "Not reported numerically in abstract; association via regression equations, p-values not given for the thiamine-specific term", "certainty_notes": "Downgraded for indirectness (multivitamin, not thiamine alone), small single-site sample, sex-specific subgroup finding, 30-year-old methodology, no replication found.", "key_study_ids": ["benton1995cognition", "benton1995mood"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Same cohort as above", "dose_or_intake": "9-vitamin supplement, 10x RDA", "duration": "1 year", "grade": "D", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Women on supplement reported better mood ('agreeable', 'composed', better mental health) after 12 months; baseline and improved thiamine status associated with mood in women.", "effect_estimate": "Not reported numerically in abstract", "certainty_notes": "Same limitations as above; subjective mood scale, multivitamin, not thiamine-isolated.", "key_study_ids": ["benton1995mood"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_mci", "population_detail": "Amnestic MCI or mild Alzheimer's disease, n=70 (benfotiamine-derivative, not thiamine salt)", "dose_or_intake": "Benfotiamine, dose per protocol (phase IIa)", "duration": "12 months", "grade": "D", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "ADAS-Cog decline 43% lower with benfotiamine (p=0.125, not significant); CDR worsening 77% lower (p=0.034); FDG-PET pattern score showed treatment effect (p=0.002, exploratory).", "effect_estimate": "ADAS-Cog p=0.125 (NS); CDR p=0.034; exploratory PET p=0.002 [gibson2020benfotiamine]", "certainty_notes": "Small (n=70), single trial, impaired population only; benfotiamine is not thiamine salt and must not be generalised to thiamine supplementation or to healthy adults; primary endpoint missed significance.", "key_study_ids": ["gibson2020benfotiamine"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Alcohol use disorder / Wernicke's encephalopathy patients", "dose_or_intake": "Parenteral thiamine 200-500 mg/day (clinical treatment dose, far above food/supplement levels)", "duration": "acute treatment, days", "grade": "A", "direction": "benefit", "effect_summary": "Early parenteral thiamine reverses confusion, ataxia and ophthalmoplegia in a majority of Wernicke's encephalopathy cases; delay risks irreversible Korsakoff amnesia. This is deficiency-correction evidence, not enhancement evidence, and must not be used to support claims in healthy adults.", "effect_estimate": "Not reported as a pooled numeric estimate here (clinical practice evidence, reviews); see [schaumburg2019wernicke, latt2014]", "certainty_notes": "Strong clinical/historical evidence for the deficiency syndrome itself; not applicable to replete populations; included for completeness of the deficiency-vs-enhancement distinction required by this unit.", "key_study_ids": ["schaumburg2019wernicke", "latt2014"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "not tested", "onset": "not applicable", "duration_studied": "not applicable", "domain": "other", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "effect_estimate": "no acute human trial of isolated thiamine on cognition in healthy adults was identified", "certainty": "insufficient data", "safety": "not applicable", "study_ids": []}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "1 year", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "outcome": "attention and mood, within a 9-vitamin supplement", "effect_estimate": "Improved attention and mood in women only; thiamine-status change associated with cognitive gain in women by regression", "evidence_quality": "low (D)", "limitations": "Multivitamin, not thiamine-isolated; sex-specific; small, old study, unreplicated", "study_ids": ["benton1995cognition", "benton1995mood"]}, {"duration": "12 months", "population": "older_adults_mci", "outcome": "clinical dementia rating and ADAS-Cog in amnestic MCI/mild AD", "effect_estimate": "CDR worsening reduced 77% (p=0.034); ADAS-Cog decline reduced 43% (p=0.125, NS)", "evidence_quality": "low-moderate (D, preliminary) and population-restricted", "limitations": "Benfotiamine not thiamine salt; n=70; single trial; primary endpoint not significant", "study_ids": ["gibson2020benfotiamine"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Thiamine from food and standard supplement doses (up to tens of mg/day) is very safe; no tolerable upper intake level has been set by EFSA or US NASEM because of a lack of adverse effects at high oral intakes, oral thiamine being poorly absorbed above typical doses. Rare anaphylactoid reactions have been reported with large parenteral doses historically, not with oral food-level intake.", "adverse_effects": ["Oral thiamine: essentially none reported at nutritional/supplemental doses (mild GI upset occasionally)", "Parenteral high-dose thiamine: rare anaphylactoid/allergic reactions (historical reports; dose and route-specific, not relevant to food products)"], "contraindications": ["None established for oral thiamine at nutritional doses"], "drug_interactions": ["Limited; furosemide and other loop diuretics can increase urinary thiamine loss (relevant to thiamine status monitoring, not a supplement-drug toxicity interaction)"], "upper_limits": "No UL set by EFSA (2006 opinion) or US NASEM due to low oral toxicity; this is not the same as 'any dose is beneficial' - it reflects absence of a defined upper hazard from oral intake, not evidence of extra benefit at high doses.", "sleep_impact": "No evidence of sleep disruption from oral thiamine at nutritional or typical supplemental doses.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Long-term exposure at food-fortification and typical multivitamin doses is well studied via decades of fortification programs with no safety signal; the 1-year 10x-RDA multivitamin trial reported no adverse events in the abstract [benton1995cognition, benton1995mood].", "daily_use_appropriate": "yes", "special_populations": "Bariatric surgery patients, chronic alcohol users, hyperemesis gravidarum and severely malnourished individuals are at risk of deficiency and may need clinical-dose repletion under medical supervision, not a consumer food product; pregnant/lactating women need adequate but not excessive intake (standard prenatal levels); no specific psychiatric medication interactions identified in this search.", "specialist_review_needed": ["clinical nutrition/addiction medicine review for deficiency-risk populations (bariatric surgery, alcohol use disorder) if Brain Food ever targets at-risk groups"], "hard_safety_gate": "pass", "gate_reason": "No meaningful safety concern at food/fortification-level doses; deficiency-treatment doses are a separate, medically supervised context not relevant to a consumer cognition product."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Thiamine is an authorised vitamin for food supplements and fortification (Directive 2002/46/EC annex). EFSA-authorised health claims include 'thiamine contributes to normal energy-yielding metabolism', 'normal functioning of the nervous system' and 'normal psychological function', usable at >=15% NRV (>=0.165 mg) per labelled portion (Regulation (EU) 432/2012 claims register; wording verified against general knowledge of the register, not re-fetched live in this session - mark as uncertain pending a live EUR-Lex check).", "us": "Thiamine is GRAS and widely used in fortification (enriched flour/bread standards) and dietary supplements under DSHEA; structure/function claims about 'energy metabolism' and 'nervous system function' are permitted with standard disclaimers, but a specific 'improves memory/focus' claim would need FTC-level competent and reliable scientific evidence that does not currently exist for healthy adults.", "morocco": "Morocco mandates wheat flour fortification with thiamine (among other B vitamins and iron) under national fortification programs; ONSSA regulates food-grade thiamine addition and supplement registration; specific claim wording rules were not independently verified in this session - uncertain.", "permitted_claims_examples": ["EU: 'Thiamine contributes to normal psychological function' (conditions: source per Annex to Reg 1924/2006, >=15% NRV per portion) - wording as commonly published in the EU claims register; not re-verified live in this session"], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "None; thiamine is a conventional, long-established vitamin with no novel food or NDI status issues.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Excellent. Thiamine is heat- and water-soluble and already used at scale in flour/cereal fortification; doses needed for the authorised EU claim (>=0.165 mg) are trivially small and compatible with any food serving.", "formats": ["thiamine hydrochloride or mononitrate added to flour, cereal, bars, beverages", "benfotiamine (higher bioavailability, used mainly in supplement/pharma contexts, not typically food fortification)"], "stability": "Thiamine is heat-labile and partially destroyed by cooking/baking and leaches into cooking water; alkaline pH and sulfite preservatives accelerate degradation; this is a known food-science constraint for fortified products (general food-science knowledge, not separately verified with a primary source in this unit).", "taste": "No significant taste or odor issues at claim-level doses.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Thiamine hydrochloride/mononitrate: standard oral bioavailability, saturable active transport at normal intakes. Benfotiamine: higher plasma thiamine achieved at equivalent doses due to passive absorption, used in the Alzheimer's trial [gibson2020benfotiamine], but not needed for the small doses required by claim-level food fortification.", "dose_feasibility": "The claim-level dose (0.165 mg/portion) fits easily into any food serving; this is far below any dose tested for cognitive/dementia effects (which used clinical/pharmacological doses), so a food product can carry the authorised nutritional claim but cannot honestly claim the dementia-trial or multivitamin-trial outcomes.", "cost_availability": "Thiamine hydrochloride/mononitrate is a commodity ingredient, inexpensive and widely available globally.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 2, "effect_magnitude": 1, "safety": 9, "reproducibility": 2, "food_suitability": 9, "cost_access": 9, "rationale": "evidence_quality low (2): no isolated-thiamine RCT in healthy adults with a cognitive primary endpoint; strongest human evidence is for deficiency correction in clinical/at-risk groups (not applicable to target population) and one dated, sex-specific multivitamin regression finding. effect_magnitude low (1): no quantifiable cognitive effect size exists for healthy replete adults. safety high (9): no UL, excellent tolerability at all studied doses. reproducibility low (2): the only enhancement-adjacent finding (Benton trials) has not been replicated with thiamine isolated. food_suitability high (9): cheap, stable-enough for fortification programs, trivial claim-level dose. cost_access high (9): commodity ingredient."}, "research_gaps": ["No RCT isolating thiamine alone (vs. placebo) on a validated cognitive battery in healthy young adults, students or knowledge workers, acute or chronic.", "No acute-dosing cognition trial testing the glucose-metabolism mechanistic hypothesis under high cognitive load.", "No replication of the Benton 1995 sex-specific thiamine-status/attention association with thiamine isolated and modern instruments.", "No dose-ranging data on what (if any) thiamine intake above RDA changes brain glucose metabolism biomarkers in replete adults.", "Benfotiamine-in-MCI findings need replication with larger samples and a significant primary endpoint before any claim beyond 'preliminary'."], "_derived": {"composite_score": 4.6, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "pass", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": "A", "best_grade_young_adults": "D", "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": "D", "best_grade_overall": "A", "benefit_domains": {"sustained_attention": "D", "global_cognition": "A"}, "n_outcome_rows": 4, "n_studies": 5, "n_verified": 5, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 1, "n_systematic_reviews": 0, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "vitamin-a", "name": "Vitamin A and provitamin A carotenoids", "category": "B", "category_name": "Vitamins", "aliases": ["vitamin A", "retinol", "retinyl acetate", "retinyl palmitate", "retinoids", "retinoic acid (active metabolite)", "beta-carotene", "provitamin A carotenoids (alpha-carotene, beta-cryptoxanthin)", "RAE / RE (retinol activity/equivalents)"], "food_sources": ["Preformed vitamin A: liver and liver products (very high), cod-liver oil, eggs, butter, whole milk and cheese, oily fish", "Provitamin A: carrots, sweet potato, pumpkin, dark leafy greens (spinach), mango, apricots, red palm oil", "Fortified foods: margarine/oils (Morocco: voluntary vitamin A/D enrichment of table oil), some milks and cereals"], "active_constituents": ["all-trans-retinol (transported on RBP4) -> retinal (vision) and all-trans/9-cis-retinoic acid (ligands of RAR/RXR nuclear receptors)", "beta-carotene and other provitamin A carotenoids, converted to retinal by BCO1 in a regulated, status-dependent way (conversion falls when status is adequate)"], "one_line_verdict": "Vitamin A is essential for vision, immunity and retinoid signalling in the brain, but no trial shows that extra vitamin A or beta-carotene improves cognition in adequately nourished adults (the only signal is a 0.05 SD difference after 18 years of beta-carotene in older men), while preformed vitamin A has a narrow safety margin (teratogenic, bone) and supplemental beta-carotene harms smokers, so it is not a cognitive ingredient.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. Vitamin A is essential. Its active metabolite, retinoic acid, regulates gene transcription via RAR/RXR receptors, including in the hippocampus, and animal work links vitamin A deprivation to impaired synaptic plasticity and memory [fragoso2012]. Human cognitive evidence, however, is almost absent. A systematic review found that the retinoid-memory literature consists mostly of birds and rodents, with human data limited to autopsy and drug reports [fragoso2012]. The only randomised cognitive evidence concerns provitamin A. In the Physicians' Health Study, men given 50 mg beta-carotene on alternate days for a mean of 18 years scored 0.047 SD higher on a global composite (P=0.03) and 0.063 SD higher on verbal memory than placebo at a single late assessment. About 1 year of treatment had no effect [grodstein2007]. The Cochrane review graded this as low-certainty evidence of a tiny effect (0.18 TICS points) from one trial [rutjes2018], and investigators describe it as a lead to revisit, not a finding [kopec2023]. Observational data are inconsistent and confounded. One meta-analysis found lower blood beta-carotene in dementia (SMD -0.48) [wang2023]; another found no difference for beta-carotene in AD (SMD 0.04) [qu2021]. Dietary beta-carotene was not significantly associated with AD incidence (RR 0.88, 0.73-1.03) [li2012]. A carotenoid-cognition association appeared only in APOE e4 carriers [liu2025]. Low carotenoid status is a marker of fruit/vegetable intake and of illness, so these data cannot support a supplement. In whom. No trial has tested vitamin A or beta-carotene on cognition in healthy young adults, students or knowledge workers. No trial has tested whether correcting vitamin A deficiency (common in parts of Africa and South Asia, affecting mainly children) improves adult cognition. Deficiency correction is justified for vision and child survival, not as a cognitive intervention. Safety dominates the assessment. Preformed vitamin A is teratogenic: supplemental intakes above about 10,000 IU/day (3000 ug) in early pregnancy were associated with a 4.8-fold higher prevalence of cranial-neural-crest defects [rothman1995]. EFSA retained an adult UL of 3000 ug RE/day, with teratogenicity as the critical effect [efsa2024]. Chronic retinol intakes of 2000 ug/day or more were associated with RR 1.89 for hip fracture [feskanich2002]. Supplemental beta-carotene increased lung cancer in smokers by 18-28% and mortality by 17% [atbc1994, omenn1996]; it modestly raises mortality in low-bias trials (RR 1.05) [bjelakovic2012] and is advised against by the USPSTF [oconnor2022]. Pharmacological retinoids (isotretinoin) act on the brain and carry psychiatric warnings, though pooled prospective data do not show more depression [li2019iso]. Conclusion. Vitamin A should be supplied by a normal diet (vegetables and dairy/eggs). It has no place as an active cognitive ingredient, and any inclusion in a multinutrient product should stay at or below the NRV.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Vitamin A deficiency is a major public-health problem in low-income settings (night blindness, xerophthalmia, child mortality from infections), and correcting it there is essential. No trial has shown cognitive benefits from correcting deficiency in adults. National Moroccan data on vitamin A status were not found here; the voluntary vitamin A enrichment of table oil suggests authorities consider intake a concern, but this is unverified. In adequately nourished adults, intake above requirement brings no demonstrated cognitive benefit. Short-term beta-carotene was null and long-term beta-carotene gave only a 0.05 SD difference [grodstein2007, rutjes2018], while risk rises (teratogenicity, bone, and lung cancer with beta-carotene in smokers) [rothman1995, feskanich2002, omenn1996]. Enhancement is not a valid concept for vitamin A.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Vitamin A is sometimes marketed with 'brain', 'memory' or 'neuroprotection' language because of retinoic-acid neurobiology and antioxidant claims for beta-carotene. The human evidence does not support this: one secondary 18-year signal of 0.05 SD in older men [grodstein2007], inconsistent observational data [wang2023, qu2021, li2012], and no young-adult trials. In the EU, the only authorised vitamin A claims concern iron metabolism, mucous membranes, skin, vision, immune function and cell specialisation (Reg. 432/2012). There is no cognitive claim. Marketing must also avoid presenting beta-carotene as an 'antioxidant for the brain', given the trial harms. Honest position: 'contributes to normal vision and immune function' at NRV levels from food sources such as carrots, sweet potato and leafy greens.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Dietary retinyl esters and provitamin A carotenoids are absorbed with fat into chylomicrons and stored in the liver. Retinol circulates on RBP4 and is converted in target cells to retinal (rhodopsin in vision) and retinoic acid, which activates RAR/RXR nuclear receptors that regulate many genes, including in the hippocampus and in adult neurogenesis. Animal models show that vitamin A deprivation impairs hippocampal long-term potentiation and memory, reversible by retinoic acid. Homeostatic control (liver stores, regulated beta-carotene conversion) means extra intake in replete people does not raise brain retinoic acid signalling in a dose-dependent way. Excess retinoids are teratogenic, act through the same receptors, and isotretinoin can cause neuropsychiatric effects at drug doses.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Retinyl esters / beta-carotene -> absorption with dietary fat -> hepatic storage; beta-carotene cleaved by BCO1 (down-regulated when status is adequate)", "detail": "Well established human physiology; underpins RAE conversion factors and EFSA DRVs [efsa2024].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Retinol-RBP4 -> cellular uptake -> retinoic acid -> RAR/RXR nuclear receptor signalling in hippocampus and cortex", "detail": "Demonstrated in animals; human brain data limited to autopsy reports [fragoso2012].", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Retinoic acid -> synaptic plasticity (LTP), adult neurogenesis, amyloid processing", "detail": "Rodent deprivation/repletion studies and in-vitro data [fragoso2012]; untested in humans.", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Beta-carotene as antioxidant -> reduced oxidative stress in the ageing brain", "detail": "Proposed explanation for the long-term PHS signal [grodstein2007, kopec2023]; antioxidant trials show no mortality benefit and some harm [bjelakovic2012].", "evidence_level": "speculative"}, {"step": "Measured human cognitive outcome", "detail": "No effect after ~1 y of beta-carotene; +0.047 SD global cognition after 18 y in older men [grodstein2007]; no trials in young adults or with preformed vitamin A.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["RAR/RXR nuclear receptor signalling", "visual cycle (retinal/rhodopsin)", "hippocampal plasticity and neurogenesis (animal)", "antioxidant hypothesis (carotenoids)", "teratogenic retinoid signalling in cranial neural crest"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "US male physicians >65 y, long-term (mean 18 y) beta-carotene", "dose_or_intake": "Beta-carotene 50 mg alternate days", "duration": "mean 18 y", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Single late assessment after 18 y showed slightly better global cognition and verbal memory; Cochrane rates this as low-certainty evidence of a tiny effect.", "effect_estimate": "Global z +0.047 (P=0.03); verbal memory +0.063 (P=0.007) [grodstein2007]; MD 0.18 TICS (0.01-0.35) [rutjes2018]", "certainty_notes": "One trial, secondary analysis, no baseline cognition, men only; downgraded for imprecision and indirectness; harms of beta-carotene in smokers weigh against use.", "key_study_ids": ["grodstein2007", "rutjes2018", "kopec2023"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Newly recruited older male physicians (shorter exposure)", "dose_or_intake": "Beta-carotene 50 mg alternate days", "duration": "~1 y", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "No difference in cognition after about 1 year; Cochrane found no benefit at earlier time points.", "effect_estimate": "No difference (estimate not in abstract) [grodstein2007]", "certainty_notes": "Single trial, short exposure, imprecise.", "key_study_ids": ["grodstein2007", "rutjes2018"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "dementia_risk", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Observational: blood carotenoids or dietary beta-carotene vs dementia/AD", "dose_or_intake": "Habitual diet / blood levels", "duration": "cross-sectional to multi-year cohorts", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Lower beta-carotene in dementia in one meta-analysis, no difference in another; dietary beta-carotene not significantly associated with AD incidence; genotype-specific association in a trial cohort.", "effect_estimate": "SMD -0.48 (-0.78 to -0.17) [wang2023]; SMD 0.04 (-0.57 to 0.65) [qu2021]; RR 0.88 (0.73-1.03) [li2012]; beta 0.13 SU in APOE e4 carriers [liu2025]", "certainty_notes": "Observational, reverse causation and diet-quality confounding; inconsistent; indirect to supplementation.", "key_study_ids": ["wang2023", "qu2021", "li2012", "liu2025"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults 18-45, students, knowledge workers", "dose_or_intake": "any", "duration": "not studied", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No human trials identified; the only support is animal retinoid neurobiology.", "effect_estimate": "not reported (no studies)", "certainty_notes": "Mechanistic/animal only; cannot be graded above D.", "key_study_ids": ["fragoso2012"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "deficient_or_low_baseline", "population_detail": "Adults with vitamin A deficiency", "dose_or_intake": "repletion doses", "duration": "not studied", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No trials of cognitive outcomes after correcting vitamin A deficiency in adults were found; repletion is justified on vision/immune grounds.", "effect_estimate": "not reported (no studies)", "certainty_notes": "Evidence gap; plausibility from animal deprivation models only.", "key_study_ids": ["fragoso2012"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "other", "population": "other", "population_detail": "Smokers, former smokers and asbestos workers given supplemental beta-carotene (+/- retinol) - harm outcome (lung cancer, mortality)", "dose_or_intake": "beta-carotene 20-30 mg/day +/- retinyl palmitate 25,000 IU/day", "duration": "4-8 y", "grade": "A", "direction": "harm", "effect_summary": "Two large RCTs and meta-analyses show increased lung cancer and mortality.", "effect_estimate": "Lung cancer RR 1.28 (1.04-1.57) [omenn1996]; +18% (3-36%) [atbc1994]; OR 1.20 (1.01-1.42) [oconnor2022]; mortality RR 1.05 (1.01-1.09) [bjelakovic2012]", "certainty_notes": "Consistent large low-bias RCTs; harm outcome, not cognition.", "key_study_ids": ["omenn1996", "atbc1994", "oconnor2022", "bjelakovic2012"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "other", "population": "other", "population_detail": "Pregnant women (teratogenicity) and postmenopausal women (hip fracture) with high preformed vitamin A intake - harm outcomes", "dose_or_intake": ">3000 ug (10,000 IU)/day supplemental (pregnancy); >=2000 ug retinol/day (bone)", "duration": "first trimester; 18 y", "grade": "B", "direction": "harm", "effect_summary": "Cohort data show cranial-neural-crest defects at high supplemental intakes and higher hip-fracture risk with high retinol; EFSA uses teratogenicity to set the UL.", "effect_estimate": "PR 4.8 (2.2-10.5) [rothman1995]; RR 1.89 (1.33-2.68) [feskanich2002]; UL 3000 ug RE/day [efsa2024]", "certainty_notes": "Observational but consistent with animal teratology and drug retinoids; accepted by regulators.", "key_study_ids": ["rothman1995", "feskanich2002", "efsa2024"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Acne patients on isotretinoin (pharmacological retinoid; not nutritional vitamin A)", "dose_or_intake": "isotretinoin drug doses", "duration": "months", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Depression symptoms improve with acne treatment; depression risk raised in retrospective but not prospective studies.", "effect_estimate": "RR 1.15 (0.60-2.21); retrospective RR 1.39 (1.05-1.84) [li2019iso]", "certainty_notes": "Observational, confounded; shows retinoids are CNS-active only at drug doses.", "key_study_ids": ["li2019iso"]}], "acute_matrix": [], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "mean 18 y", "population": "US male physicians >65 (n=4,052)", "outcome": "Global cognition, verbal memory (single assessment)", "effect_estimate": "+0.047 SD (P=0.03); +0.063 SD (P=0.007)", "evidence_quality": "low-moderate", "limitations": "no baseline cognition; secondary analysis", "study_ids": ["grodstein2007"]}, {"duration": "~1 y", "population": "US male physicians >65 (n=1,904)", "outcome": "Global cognition", "effect_estimate": "no difference", "evidence_quality": "moderate", "limitations": "short exposure", "study_ids": ["grodstein2007"]}, {"duration": "4-8 y", "population": "Smokers/asbestos workers (n=18,314 and 29,133)", "outcome": "Lung cancer, mortality (harm)", "effect_estimate": "RR 1.28 lung cancer; +18% lung cancer", "evidence_quality": "high", "limitations": "high-risk populations", "study_ids": ["omenn1996", "atbc1994"]}, {"duration": "18 y", "population": "Postmenopausal women (n=72,337)", "outcome": "Hip fracture (harm)", "effect_estimate": "retinol RR 1.89 (1.33-2.68)", "evidence_quality": "moderate (observational)", "limitations": "FFQ, confounding", "study_ids": ["feskanich2002"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Preformed vitamin A (retinol and its esters) has a narrow margin between requirement (EU NRV 800 ug; adult PRI ~650-750 ug RE/day) and the UL (3000 ug RE/day) [efsa2024]. Excess causes teratogenicity, hepatotoxicity, bone loss/fracture and, acutely or chronically, raised intracranial pressure. Liver eaten weekly plus supplements can exceed the UL. Beta-carotene is not toxic in the vitamin A sense, because conversion is regulated, but high-dose supplements increase lung cancer and mortality in smokers [omenn1996, atbc1994] and are not recommended for the general population beyond meeting requirements [efsa2024, oconnor2022]. Carotenodermia (yellow skin) is benign.", "adverse_effects": ["Teratogenicity (cranial-neural-crest defects) at high preformed intake in early pregnancy", "Hepatotoxicity with chronic high intake", "Reduced bone mineral density / hip fracture with chronic high retinol", "Raised intracranial pressure (headache, nausea, papilloedema) with toxicity", "Increased lung cancer and mortality with high-dose beta-carotene in smokers", "Carotenodermia (benign) with high carotenoid intake"], "contraindications": ["Pregnancy or planning pregnancy: no supplemental preformed vitamin A above NRV unless prescribed", "Smokers, former smokers, asbestos exposure: avoid beta-carotene supplements", "Liver disease, high alcohol intake", "Concurrent retinoid drug therapy"], "drug_interactions": ["Isotretinoin, acitretin, alitretinoin, bexarotene: additive retinoid toxicity - avoid vitamin A supplements", "Orlistat and bile-acid sequestrants reduce absorption of fat-soluble vitamins", "Tetracyclines with high vitamin A: case reports of intracranial hypertension (specialist review)", "Hepatotoxic drugs: theoretical additive liver risk"], "upper_limits": "EU: preformed vitamin A UL 3000 ug RE/day for adults incl. pregnant/lactating women; 600 ug RE/day (4-11 months) rising to 2600 ug RE/day (15-17 y) [efsa2024]. No UL for beta-carotene; EFSA advises supplemental beta-carotene only to meet requirements. US: UL 3000 ug/day preformed vitamin A for adults (IOM 2001; NIH ODS page returned 403, not re-verified). EU labelling NRV 800 ug.", "sleep_impact": "None known at nutritional intakes.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Beta-carotene up to 18 y (PHS) and 4-8 y (CARET/ATBC); preformed vitamin A via cohorts (18 y bone). No long-term RCT of preformed vitamin A supplementation for cognition.", "daily_use_appropriate": "uncertain", "special_populations": "Pregnant women: teratogenic risk, so prenatal products limit preformed vitamin A. Children: lower ULs; young children are vulnerable to toxicity from high-dose products. Older adults: bone risk. Smokers: avoid beta-carotene supplements. Liver disease and alcohol misuse: increased hepatotoxicity. People in deficient settings: public-health high-dose programmes for children are separate medical interventions.", "specialist_review_needed": ["Obstetrician/teratology review for any product that may be used by women of reproductive age", "Hepatologist for users with liver disease", "Pharmacist (retinoid drugs, orlistat)", "Regulatory toxicologist for total-intake modelling (diet + fortified oil + supplement)"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "Acceptable only at or below the NRV (800 ug RE) as part of a balanced micronutrient formula, ideally as beta-carotene or mixed sources with total preformed vitamin A well below the 3000 ug UL. High-dose beta-carotene must be avoided because of smoker harm. Use as a stand-alone 'brain' ingredient fails: no efficacy, and risk increases with dose."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Vitamin A (retinol, retinyl acetate, retinyl palmitate, beta-carotene) is a permitted vitamin form for food supplements (Directive 2002/46/EC) and for fortification (Reg. 1925/2006). The NRV is 800 ug. Authorised claims (Reg. 432/2012, verified on legislation.gov.uk annex 2026-10-25): normal iron metabolism; maintenance of normal mucous membranes; maintenance of normal skin; maintenance of normal vision; normal function of the immune system; role in cell specialisation. No cognitive or brain claim exists. EFSA 2024 retained the UL to inform future EU maximum levels; trade press reports a draft lowering limits for beta-carotene (unofficial). National maximum levels apply meanwhile (e.g. Germany/Netherlands caps reported at 3000 ug RE; uncertain).", "us": "Dietary ingredient under DSHEA; GRAS/approved for food fortification in specified forms. DV 900 ug RAE. Structure/function claims such as 'supports healthy vision' are possible with the FDA disclaimer; cognition claims lack competent and reliable evidence (FTC). The USPSTF recommends against beta-carotene supplements for prevention of CVD/cancer [oconnor2022].", "morocco": "Per the X6 synthesis: table oil is enriched with vitamins A and D3 under a voluntary framework agreement (legal instrument not found). Supplements fall under the Ministry of Health/AMMPS registration route; no authorised health-claim list; disease claims banned (Decree 2-12-389). Maximum levels for vitamin A in supplements were not found (uncertain).", "permitted_claims_examples": ["Vitamin A contributes to the maintenance of normal vision (EU Reg. 432/2012; food at least a source of vitamin A)", "Vitamin A contributes to the normal function of the immune system (EU Reg. 432/2012)", "Vitamin A contributes to normal iron metabolism (EU Reg. 432/2012)"], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "None for listed forms. Some algal/fungal beta-carotene sources and new carotenoid preparations may need novel-food checks.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "High as a food nutrient (carrots, sweet potato, leafy greens, eggs, dairy), so a Brain Food product can deliver provitamin A naturally. As an added ingredient it is suitable only at NRV-level fortification.", "formats": ["whole foods rich in beta-carotene", "retinyl palmitate/acetate beadlets for fortification", "beta-carotene emulsions/beadlets (also used as colour E160a)", "component of MVM tablets"], "stability": "Retinol and beta-carotene are sensitive to light, oxygen and heat; commercial forms are microencapsulated with antioxidants; overages needed for shelf life.", "taste": "Neutral at fortification levels; beta-carotene colours products orange-yellow.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Requires dietary fat; beta-carotene from raw vegetables is poorly absorbed (cooking and oil improve it); conversion is regulated by status and BCO1 genotype (12:1 food beta-carotene to retinol in RAE terms).", "dose_feasibility": "NRV-level doses fit easily in any food or tablet. The only cognitive-trial dose (50 mg beta-carotene alternate days) is a high supplemental dose that EFSA advises against for the general population.", "cost_availability": "Inexpensive and widely available commodity vitamin.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 1, "effect_magnitude": 1, "safety": 4, "reproducibility": 2, "food_suitability": 6, "cost_access": 9, "rationale": "Evidence quality 1: grade D in the target population (no trials), with only a grade C single-trial signal in older men. Effect magnitude 1: at most 0.05 SD after 18 y. Safety 4: narrow margin for preformed vitamin A (teratogenic, bone, liver) and grade A harm for supplemental beta-carotene in smokers. Reproducibility 2: one unreplicated trial; inconsistent observational data. Food suitability 6: abundant in ordinary foods, fortification feasible but unnecessary. Cost/access 9: cheap."}, "research_gaps": ["Any RCT of vitamin A status correction on cognition in deficient adults or adolescents", "Replication of the long-term beta-carotene cognitive signal in non-smokers with baseline cognitive testing", "Moroccan population vitamin A status data (serum retinol, intake from enriched oil)", "Whether dietary carotenoid-rich patterns (not supplements) causally affect cognition (trial with vegetable intake as intervention)"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 3.0, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": null, "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"global_cognition": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 8, "n_studies": 16, "n_verified": 16, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 4, "n_systematic_reviews": 2, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["harm", "inconsistent", "insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "vitamin-b12", "name": "Vitamin B12", "category": "B", "category_name": "Vitamins", "aliases": ["vitamin B12", "cobalamin", "cyanocobalamin", "methylcobalamin (mecobalamin)", "hydroxocobalamin", "adenosylcobalamin (5'-deoxyadenosylcobalamin)", "holotranscobalamin (active B12, biomarker)"], "food_sources": ["meat, liver, fish and shellfish (clams very high)", "eggs", "milk, yoghurt, cheese", "fortified plant drinks, breakfast cereals and nutritional yeast (main reliable vegan sources besides supplements)"], "active_constituents": ["methylcobalamin (cofactor of methionine synthase: homocysteine -> methionine, regenerating tetrahydrofolate)", "adenosylcobalamin (cofactor of methylmalonyl-CoA mutase; deficiency raises methylmalonic acid and impairs myelin lipid metabolism)"], "one_line_verdict": "Vitamin B12 deficiency causes serious, partly irreversible neurological and cognitive damage and must be prevented in vegans, older adults and metformin or acid-suppressant users, but in people who are not deficient, including healthy students, B12 supplements do not improve cognition, mood, energy or fatigue.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. B12 deficiency causes megaloblastic anaemia and neuropsychiatric damage (neuropathy, subacute combined degeneration, cognitive and mood change); oral 1-2 mg/day is as effective as injection for correcting anaemia and neurological symptoms [langan2017]. Risk groups are well characterised: unsupplemented vegans have lower serum B12 (SMD -0.72, 95% CI -1.26 to -0.18) and higher homocysteine than omnivores, corrected by supplements [niklewicz2024]; metformin lowers B12 by 19% and raises the absolute risk of deficiency by 7.2 percentage points over 4.3 years [dejager2010], with risk rising 13% per year of use [aroda2016]; two or more years of PPIs raise the odds of deficiency (OR 1.65, 1.58-1.73) [lam2013]. Non-deficient adults. A meta-analysis of 16 RCTs (6,276 participants) found no effect of B12 alone or B-complex on any cognitive subdomain or on depressive symptoms, with moderate-certainty evidence, no funnel asymmetry and no modification by baseline B12, dose or duration [markun2021]. Even correcting biochemical deficiency in asymptomatic older adults did not help: 1 mg/day for 24 weeks in mild deficiency produced no cognitive gain (memory improved more on placebo) [eussen2006], and 1 mg/day for 12 months in moderate deficiency changed neither nerve conduction nor cognition [dangour2015]. In homocysteine-lowering combinations, B12 + folic acid had no effect in 2,919 high-homocysteine older adults [vanderzwaluw2014] or in IPD meta-analysis [clarke2014, rutjes2018]; MCI benefits are confined to one trial's high-homocysteine subgroup and perhaps to good omega-3 status [smith2010, dejager2012, oulhaj2016, vansoest2022], with a null independent replication using methylcobalamin [kwok2020] and a Cochrane verdict of no evidence of benefit [mccleery2018]. Young adults and students. In 478 young adults, 1000 ug/day methylcobalamin for a month did not change anxiety relative to placebo (interaction p=0.81) and produced only trends in visual processing [field2022]; a 35-day trial of 15 ug B12 in women showed at most slight memory effects [bryan2002]. Effects on 'energy' come only from multi-ingredient products with small subjective outcomes [kennedy2010, young2019]. No acute cognitive effect of B12 has been shown; a completed 'vitamin energy shot' trial (NCT06019858) has no posted results. In children, B12 supplementation has little or no effect on cognition (SMD 0.09, -0.10 to 0.28) [larvie2026]. Interactions. Low B12 combined with high folate is associated with worse cognitive test scores [morris2007, bailey2020]; B12 should accompany any folic acid. The honest product role for B12 is deficiency prevention in at-risk groups, especially vegans.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "This is the clearest deficiency-versus-enhancement split among brain nutrients. Deficiency causes neurological and cognitive harm that may become irreversible; preventing it in vegans (including young vegan students), older adults (reduced gastric acid), metformin users, long-term PPI/H2RA users and people after gastric surgery is a legitimate and important public-health goal [niklewicz2024, dejager2010, lam2013, langan2017]. But enhancement does not occur: in non-deficient adults B12 does not improve cognition, mood or fatigue [markun2021, field2022], and even correcting asymptomatic biochemical deficiency in older adults does not improve cognition within 6-12 months [eussen2006, dangour2015]. Brain Food should frame B12 as 'insurance against deficiency' for plant-based consumers, not as an energy or focus booster.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "B12 is heavily marketed for 'energy', 'focus', 'brain fog', 'memory' and in 'energy shots' with doses of 1000-5000 ug. The evidence shows no benefit of these doses in non-deficient people [markun2021] and no acute effect. The EU authorises 'contributes to normal psychological function', 'normal functioning of the nervous system', 'reduction of tiredness and fatigue' and 'normal energy-yielding metabolism' at source-level amounts (>=15% NRV, 0.375 ug); using them to imply that extra B12 gives extra energy to replete consumers is misleading in spirit even if legal in wording. 'Methylcobalamin is the superior/active form' is not supported by clinical outcome trials (the null Kwok and Field trials used methylcobalamin). For vegans, fortified foods or a supplement are essential; this is the defensible message.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Food B12 is released by gastric acid and pepsin, binds haptocorrin, then intrinsic factor, and is absorbed in the terminal ileum via the cubilin receptor (saturable, ~1.5-2 ug per meal); about 1% of large oral doses is absorbed by passive diffusion, which is why 1 mg oral doses can treat deficiency (absorption figures are general physiology, not re-verified in this unit). In cells, methylcobalamin supports methionine synthase (homocysteine remethylation, SAM production, folate recycling) and adenosylcobalamin supports methylmalonyl-CoA mutase. Deficiency impairs myelin synthesis and methylation, causing neuropathy and cognitive change; folate can correct the anaemia but not the neurological lesion. In replete people, extra B12 saturates tissues and is excreted, so no further brain effect is expected.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Oral B12 -> intrinsic-factor-mediated ileal absorption (saturable) plus ~1% passive diffusion at high doses -> raised serum B12/holoTC", "detail": "1 mg/day raised serum B12 by 177% and lowered tHcy in deficient older adults [dangour2015]; oral high dose effective for deficiency [langan2017]; acid suppression and metformin impair status [lam2013, dejager2010].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Methylcobalamin -> methionine synthase -> homocysteine remethylation and SAM", "detail": "B12 + folic acid lowered tHcy 30-36% [eussen2006, smith2010].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Adenosylcobalamin -> methylmalonyl-CoA mutase -> myelin integrity", "detail": "Deficiency raises MMA and causes demyelination; vegans show raised tHcy and borderline raised MMA [niklewicz2024].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Correction of deficiency -> prevention/reversal of neuropathy and neuropsychiatric symptoms", "detail": "Clinical consensus for symptomatic deficiency [langan2017]; asymptomatic moderate deficiency: no measurable neurological or cognitive benefit within 12 months [dangour2015, eussen2006].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "High folate with low B12 -> worse neurocognitive outcomes", "detail": "Observational NHANES and model-system data [morris2007, bailey2020, castillo2025].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Measured human cognitive outcome in non-deficient people", "detail": "No effect on any cognitive subdomain (16 RCTs) [markun2021]; young adults: no anxiety effect vs placebo [field2022].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}], "pathways": ["methionine synthase / one-carbon metabolism (shared with folate)", "methylmalonyl-CoA mutase / myelin lipid metabolism", "intrinsic factor-cubilin absorption and passive diffusion", "folate trap and folate masking"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Adults without overt deficiency or advanced neurological disease (mean age 66-82; baseline B12 186-385 pmol/L)", "dose_or_intake": "0.1-1 mg/day oral (B12 alone or B complex)", "duration": "4-117 weeks", "grade": "B", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "No effect on any cognitive subdomain or on depressive symptoms; not modified by baseline B12, dose or duration.", "effect_estimate": "all subdomain pooled estimates non-significant [markun2021]; combination IPD z 0.00 (-0.05 to 0.06) [clarke2014]", "certainty_notes": "GRADE moderate per review; one positive outlier (FA 400 ug + B12 100 ug, memory d 0.15-0.18 in distressed older adults [walker2012]) does not change the pooled null; indirect for young adults (all older).", "key_study_ids": ["markun2021", "clarke2014", "rutjes2018", "vanderzwaluw2014", "walker2012"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "deficient_or_low_baseline", "population_detail": "Older adults with asymptomatic mild-to-moderate biochemical B12 deficiency (no anaemia, no neurological signs)", "dose_or_intake": "1 mg/day oral", "duration": "24 weeks to 12 months", "grade": "B", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Correction of biochemical deficiency did not improve cognition or nerve function.", "effect_estimate": "no cognitive domain improved; memory favoured placebo (p=0.0036) [eussen2006]; CMAP MD -0.2 mV (-0.8 to 0.3), cognition NS [dangour2015]", "certainty_notes": "Two well-conducted RCTs agree; limited duration (<=1 y) and modest size keep it at B.", "key_study_ids": ["eussen2006", "dangour2015"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "other", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Symptomatic B12 deficiency (anaemia and/or neurological signs; pernicious anaemia, malabsorption)", "dose_or_intake": "oral 1-2 mg/day or intramuscular", "duration": "weeks to months", "grade": "B", "direction": "benefit", "effect_summary": "Treatment corrects anaemia and neurological symptoms; prevents progression of neurological damage.", "effect_estimate": "not quantified in the source (clinical consensus) [langan2017]", "certainty_notes": "Strong clinical consensus but based on a narrative review here (no RCT of cognitive outcomes in symptomatic deficiency retrieved); cognitive reversal after long-standing deficiency is uncertain.", "key_study_ids": ["langan2017"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_mci", "population_detail": "MCI with raised tHcy", "dose_or_intake": "B12 0.5 mg + folic acid 0.4-0.8 mg (+/- B6 20 mg)", "duration": "2 years", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "VITACOG subgroup benefit (high tHcy, good omega-3) versus null independent replication with methylcobalamin; Cochrane finds no evidence of benefit.", "effect_estimate": "subgroup p-values [dejager2012]; CDR-SOB 0.36 vs 0.22 NS [kwok2020]", "certainty_notes": "Inconsistency, post-hoc subgroups, single-centre COI.", "key_study_ids": ["dejager2012", "kwok2020", "mccleery2018", "oulhaj2016", "vansoest2022", "smith2010"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "UK young adults, mean 23 y", "dose_or_intake": "1000 ug/day methylcobalamin", "duration": "about 1 month", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "No anxiety or depression effect versus placebo; only trends in visual contrast thresholds.", "effect_estimate": "anxiety interaction F=0.06, p=0.81; within-group d=0.19 (NS) [field2022]", "certainty_notes": "Single trial with blinding weaknesses; no cognitive tasks.", "key_study_ids": ["field2022"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Young and middle-aged women (small B12 arm)", "dose_or_intake": "15 ug/day", "duration": "35 days", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "Slight selective memory effects across vitamin arms; mood null.", "effect_estimate": "not reported [bryan2002]", "certainty_notes": "Single small multi-arm trial.", "key_study_ids": ["bryan2002"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_fatigue", "population": "knowledge_workers", "population_detail": "Employed men 30-55 y; products with B12 plus other B vitamins, vitamin C and minerals", "dose_or_intake": "multi-ingredient (B12 not isolated)", "duration": "4-5 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Less self-rated mental tiredness and stress; one task improved; no B12 attribution.", "effect_estimate": "PSS ANCOVA p<0.05; vigour p<0.05 [kennedy2010]; stress SMD 0.23 (0.02-0.45) [young2019]", "certainty_notes": "Indirect (multi-ingredient), subjective outcomes, likely industry involvement; Markun found no fatigue data for B12 alone.", "key_study_ids": ["kennedy2010", "young2019", "markun2021"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "alertness", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "No acute B12 cognition/energy trials found; one registered energy-shot trial (NCT06019858, completed 2023) has no posted results", "dose_or_intake": "not studied", "duration": "single dose", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No evidence that a dose of B12 increases alertness or energy within hours.", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "Absence of data; possible unpublished null result.", "key_study_ids": []}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "vegetarian_vegan", "population_detail": "Adult vegans and vegetarians", "dose_or_intake": "supplements or fortified foods vs none", "duration": "habitual", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "Vegans have worse functional B12 status, corrected by supplements, but no cognitive trials in young vegans were found.", "effect_estimate": "serum B12 SMD -0.72 (-1.26 to -0.18) vs omnivores [niklewicz2024] (biomarker, not cognition)", "certainty_notes": "Biomarker evidence only; cognition untested.", "key_study_ids": ["niklewicz2024"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "other", "population": "vegetarian_vegan", "population_detail": "Functional B12 status (serum B12, holoTC, MMA, tHcy) in adult vegans", "dose_or_intake": "B12 supplement users vs non-users", "duration": "habitual", "grade": "B", "direction": "benefit", "effect_summary": "Supplement use associated with better status on all biomarkers.", "effect_estimate": "subgroup differences significant (values not in abstract) [niklewicz2024]", "certainty_notes": "Observational comparison but consistent with physiology; biomarker outcome.", "key_study_ids": ["niklewicz2024"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "children_adolescents", "population_detail": "Children <12 y in 10 countries", "dose_or_intake": "0.9-150 ug/day", "duration": "28 days to 31 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Little to no effect on cognition.", "effect_estimate": "SMD 0.09 (-0.10 to 0.28) alone; 0.00 (-0.13 to 0.13) with other micronutrients [larvie2026]", "certainty_notes": "Low certainty per Cochrane; not applicable to adults.", "key_study_ids": ["larvie2026"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "deficient_or_low_baseline", "population_detail": "Safety/interaction: low B12 status with high folate (US older adults)", "dose_or_intake": "high serum folate/UMFA with low B12", "duration": "cross-sectional", "grade": "C", "direction": "harm", "effect_summary": "Low B12, especially with high folate, associated with more cognitive impairment on some tests.", "effect_estimate": "low vs normal B12 OR 2.5 (1.6-3.8); high folate + low B12 OR 2.6 (1.1-6.1) [morris2007]; OR 2.87 (1.85-4.45) [bailey2020]", "certainty_notes": "Observational; supports pairing folate with B12.", "key_study_ids": ["morris2007", "bailey2020", "castillo2025"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "not studied (energy shots typically 1000-5000 ug)", "onset": "not applicable", "duration_studied": "no published acute trials; NCT06019858 completed without posted results", "domain": "alertness/energy", "population": "healthy adults", "effect_estimate": "no data", "certainty": "D (insufficient)", "safety": "single high oral doses well tolerated", "study_ids": []}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "4-117 weeks", "population": "non-deficient older adults", "outcome": "cognitive subdomains, depression", "effect_estimate": "no effect (16 RCTs)", "evidence_quality": "B (no benefit)", "limitations": "older populations only", "study_ids": ["markun2021"]}, {"duration": "24 weeks-12 months", "population": "older adults with asymptomatic mild/moderate deficiency", "outcome": "cognition, nerve conduction", "effect_estimate": "no benefit; CMAP MD -0.2 mV (-0.8 to 0.3)", "evidence_quality": "B (no benefit)", "limitations": "<=1 year", "study_ids": ["eussen2006", "dangour2015"]}, {"duration": "2 years", "population": "older adults, raised tHcy", "outcome": "cognitive domains (B12 + folic acid)", "effect_estimate": "NS; MMSE -0.1 vs -0.3", "evidence_quality": "B (no benefit)", "limitations": "cognition secondary", "study_ids": ["vanderzwaluw2014", "vansoest2022"]}, {"duration": "2 years", "population": "MCI", "outcome": "cognition, atrophy", "effect_estimate": "subgroup benefit vs null replication", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "COI; inconsistency", "study_ids": ["smith2010", "dejager2012", "kwok2020", "oulhaj2016"]}, {"duration": "~1 month", "population": "young adults", "outcome": "anxiety, sensory processing", "effect_estimate": "anxiety p=0.81 vs placebo", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "single trial", "study_ids": ["field2022"]}, {"duration": "4.3-13 years", "population": "metformin users", "outcome": "B12 deficiency (risk factor)", "effect_estimate": "B12 -19%; ARD +7.2%; OR 1.13/year", "evidence_quality": "A (risk established)", "limitations": "biochemical outcomes", "study_ids": ["dejager2010", "aroda2016"]}], "safety": {"summary": "B12 has very low toxicity; neither EFSA nor the US Food and Nutrition Board set a UL (agent knowledge; EFSA B12 UL status not re-fetched in this unit). The main safety issues are indirect: high folate can mask B12 deficiency, and B12 supplementation can delay diagnosis of an underlying cause (pernicious anaemia, malabsorption) if used without testing. A high-dose B-vitamin combination including 1 mg B12 worsened kidney function and vascular events in diabetic nephropathy [house2010].", "adverse_effects": ["generally none at oral doses up to 1-2 mg/day in trials [dangour2015, eussen2006]", "acneiform eruptions and rare hypersensitivity reported with high-dose/injected B12 (case literature; not re-verified here)", "faster GFR decline and more vascular events with FA 2.5 mg + B6 25 mg + B12 1 mg in diabetic nephropathy (combination; B12 role unknown) [house2010]"], "contraindications": ["Leber's hereditary optic neuropathy: cyanocobalamin traditionally avoided (general pharmacology; not re-verified)", "use as a substitute for medical diagnosis of suspected deficiency (neurological symptoms need testing and possibly injections)"], "drug_interactions": ["metformin lowers B12 (dejager2010, aroda2016)", "proton pump inhibitors and H2 blockers lower B12 absorption (lam2013)", "nitrous oxide (including recreational use) inactivates B12 and can precipitate neurological deficiency (general knowledge; not re-verified)", "chloramphenicol may blunt haematological response (general pharmacology; not re-verified)"], "upper_limits": "No UL set by IOM (1998) or EFSA (agent knowledge; not re-fetched). Doses up to 1 mg/day used for 12 months without adverse effects in trials [dangour2015].", "sleep_impact": "None established.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Up to 2-5 years in combination trials [vanderzwaluw2014, clarke2014]; 13 years of observational data on deficiency risk with metformin [aroda2016].", "daily_use_appropriate": "yes", "special_populations": "Vegans and vegetarians (including pregnant/lactating vegans and their infants): supplementation or fortified foods essential. Older adults >50-75 y, metformin users, long-term PPI/H2RA users, post-bariatric or gastrectomy patients, people with pernicious anaemia: need testing and medical management, not a food product. Kidney disease: avoid high-dose combinations. Children: no cognitive benefit shown [larvie2026].", "specialist_review_needed": ["physician/haematologist for suspected deficiency (diagnosis before supplementation)", "clinical pharmacist (metformin, PPI interactions)", "registered dietitian for vegan product messaging"], "hard_safety_gate": "pass", "gate_reason": "Very low toxicity at any food-realistic dose; conditions: pair with folate, avoid implying treatment of deficiency symptoms, and include advice to seek testing when neurological symptoms are present."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Permitted vitamin; forms permitted (Directive 2002/46/EC Annex II, as generally reported; not re-fetched): cyanocobalamin, hydroxocobalamin, 5'-deoxyadenosylcobalamin, methylcobalamin. Authorised claims (Regulation (EU) 432/2012): normal psychological function, normal functioning of the nervous system, reduction of tiredness and fatigue, normal energy-yielding metabolism, normal homocysteine metabolism, normal red blood cell formation, immune function, cell division; condition: at least a 'source of' B12 (15% NRV; NRV 2.5 ug, i.e. 0.375 ug; NRV from general knowledge). No authorised cognition or memory claim. Harmonised maximum levels not set; national maxima vary (not checked).", "us": "Dietary supplements: a lawful dietary ingredient marketed before 15 Oct 1994, so no NDI notification is needed for the standard forms; structure/function claims (e.g. 'supports normal nervous system function', 'supports energy metabolism') are allowed with the DSHEA disclaimer and FDA notification within 30 days, but under FTC standards claims of improved memory, focus or 'brain power' in non-deficient adults would need competent and reliable scientific evidence that the trials reviewed here do not provide. Conventional foods: fortification must respect FDA's fortification policy (21 CFR 104.20) and the ingredient's food-additive/GRAS status. IOM/Food and Nutrition Board ULs (1998): no UL for B12. Cyanocobalamin is affirmed GRAS for food use (21 CFR 184.1945, cited from agent knowledge; not re-fetched). 'Energy' structure/function claims for B12 in non-deficient adults are poorly substantiated and attract FTC/class-action risk.", "morocco": "Uncertain. Food supplements in Morocco have been regulated as foodstuffs under ONSSA (law 28-07 on food safety) with product registration; 2026 Moroccan press reports describe a pending reform (draft decree postponed in July 2026) that would reserve some high-risk supplement categories to pharmacies and reportedly applies national maximum doses (e.g. professionals cite 10 ug vitamin D and 180 mg vitamin C caps). No official Moroccan maximum level for vitamin B6, folic acid or B12 and no Moroccan list of authorised health claims was found in this unit; Moroccan practice is commonly assumed to follow Codex/EU references, but this is not verified. Mandatory wheat-flour fortification exists in Morocco (iron; premix composition, including whether folic acid is currently added, not verified here). Sources: hespress.com/medias24.com/le360.ma press search results (accessed 2026-10-25; medias24 article returned HTTP 403). A Moroccan regulatory consultant and ONSSA/Bulletin officiel check are required.", "permitted_claims_examples": ["Vitamin B12 contributes to normal psychological function", "Vitamin B12 contributes to normal functioning of the nervous system", "Vitamin B12 contributes to the reduction of tiredness and fatigue", "Vitamin B12 contributes to normal homocysteine metabolism"], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "None for the four listed forms in the EU or as pre-DSHEA dietary ingredients in the US.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Good in dry foods; microgram doses are trivial to add as a 0.1% triturate. Degradation with vitamin C, heat, light and moisture requires overages.", "formats": ["fortified plant drinks and yoghurts", "fortified cereal, bar or nutritional yeast", "drink powder premix", "chewable/sublingual tablet"], "stability": "Cyanocobalamin is the most stable form; methylcobalamin and adenosylcobalamin are light-sensitive; ascorbic acid accelerates degradation (general food-science knowledge; not verified here).", "taste": "None at microgram levels; red colour at high concentration.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Intrinsic-factor absorption saturates at about 1.5-2 ug per meal, so several small doses or one large dose (passive ~1%) both work; crystalline B12 in fortified foods does not need gastric acid release, which suits older adults and PPI users (general physiology; not re-verified).", "dose_feasibility": "Source-claim 0.375 ug, NRV 2.5 ug; vegan-protective intakes of several ug/day or a weekly large dose fit easily in any food serving. A 2 ug B12-fortified yogurt and a 1.8 ug fortified bar raised plasma B12 and lowered homocysteine over 120 days in double-blind RCTs [yajnik2021], showing that food-level fortification works for status. Trial doses (0.5-1 mg) also fit physically but add nothing cognitively in replete people.", "cost_availability": "Commodity vitamin; methylcobalamin costlier than cyanocobalamin.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 3, "effect_magnitude": 1, "safety": 9, "reproducibility": 6, "food_suitability": 8, "cost_access": 9, "rationale": "evidence_quality 3: grade B evidence exists, but it shows no cognitive benefit in non-deficient or asymptomatically deficient people; deficiency-treatment benefit (B) applies to clinical patients; young-adult rows are C (null) or D. effect_magnitude 1: no enhancement effect; deficiency prevention is important but is not a performance effect. safety 9: no UL, minimal toxicity; one point off for masking/diagnostic-delay issues and the kidney-disease combination signal. reproducibility 6: the null result is highly reproducible across independent trials; positive signals are not. food_suitability 8: easy to add, stability manageable. cost_access 9: cheap; Moroccan status unverified."}, "research_gaps": ["Cognitive outcomes of correcting B12 insufficiency in young vegans (RCT with holoTC/MMA-defined deficiency)", "B12 status of Moroccan students and plant-based consumers", "Publication of registered 'energy shot' B12 trials (NCT06019858)", "Long-term (>2 y) trials in symptomatic or early deficiency with sensitive cognitive measures", "Prespecified trials of B12/folate with omega-3 stratification in high-tHcy MCI"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 5.2, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "pass", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": "C", "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": "B", "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"cognitive_fatigue": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 12, "n_studies": 28, "n_verified": 28, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 11, "n_systematic_reviews": 5, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["benefit", "harm", "inconsistent", "insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "vitamin-b6", "name": "Vitamin B6", "category": "B", "category_name": "Vitamins", "aliases": ["vitamin B6", "pyridoxine", "pyridoxine hydrochloride", "pyridoxal", "pyridoxamine", "pyridoxal 5'-phosphate (PLP, P5P)", "pyridoxine 5'-phosphate"], "food_sources": ["poultry, fish (tuna, salmon), organ meats", "potatoes and other starchy vegetables", "chickpeas and other pulses", "bananas, non-citrus fruit", "fortified breakfast cereals", "nuts and seeds (pistachios, sunflower seeds)"], "active_constituents": ["pyridoxal 5'-phosphate (PLP), the coenzyme form for >100 enzymes including aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase (serotonin, dopamine synthesis), glutamate decarboxylase (GABA synthesis), cystathionine beta-synthase (homocysteine transsulfuration) and serine hydroxymethyltransferase (one-carbon metabolism)"], "one_line_verdict": "Vitamin B6 is essential and correcting deficiency is worthwhile, but no adequately powered trial shows that extra B6 improves memory, attention or any cognitive outcome in healthy young adults; the most-cited young-adult trial reports an anxiety signal that did not beat placebo at a dose eight times the EU safety ceiling, and chronic high-dose B6 causes sensory neuropathy.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. B6 (as PLP) is a cofactor for the synthesis of serotonin, dopamine and GABA and for homocysteine transsulfuration; this mechanism is established biochemistry, but it does not by itself imply that intakes above requirement change cognition. No trial has isolated B6 at food-compatible doses with a cognitive primary endpoint in healthy young adults. Healthy young adults. The largest trial (n=478, mean age 23) gave 100 mg/day pyridoxine for about a month: anxiety scores fell within the B6 group (d=0.37 versus 0.12 with placebo) but the group-by-time interaction, the correct test of a treatment effect, was not significant (p=0.14); visual surround suppression was stronger after B6 (d=-0.32, post-test only); depression, binocular rivalry and tactile measures were null, tablets were not visually identical, and no cognitive tasks were measured [field2022]. A 35-day four-arm trial in women of different ages (75 mg B6 arm) found selective effects on some memory measures and none on mood, with very small arms [bryan2002]. Multinutrient trials in working-age adults that include B6 show biomarker changes (higher B6, folate and B12; lower homocysteine) without convincing cognitive benefit [pipingas2014, white2015] and small subjective effects on stress or vigour [kennedy2010]; meta-analyses of B-complex products report a small stress effect (SMD 0.23, 95% CI 0.02-0.45) with no effect on anxiety or depression and industry-linked authors [young2019, long2013]. None of these allow attribution to B6. Older adults. The only B6-alone cognition review found two small trials and no significant benefit [malouf2003]; one of them reported improved long-term memory after 20 mg/day in 76 older men, which the Cochrane re-analysis did not confirm [deijen1992]. In homocysteine-lowering combinations, which contain B6 at 10-50 mg, large trials and an individual-participant meta-analysis of about 22,000 people show no effect on global cognition (z difference 0.00, 95% CI -0.05 to 0.06) [clarke2014, rutjes2018, ford2010, kang2008, mcmahon2006]; the only positive signals are brain-atrophy and subgroup effects in one MCI trial [smith2010, dejager2012] that the Cochrane MCI review considers unreplicated [mccleery2018]. Observationally, B6 status is not associated with cognitive decline [zhang2020]. Safety dominates the risk-benefit balance. Sensory neuropathy from high-dose pyridoxine is established [schaumburg1983, berger1992, muhamad2023]; EFSA set the adult UL at 12 mg/day in 2023 [efsa2023b6], and Dutch pharmacovigilance still records neuropathy reports after a 21 mg/day cap [vanhunsel2025]. Most 'brain' trial doses (20-100 mg) exceed the EU UL. What is unknown. Whether marginal B6 status (common in some older and some young women) impairs cognition reversibly, and whether any acute effect exists (no acute trials found).", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Clinical B6 deficiency is uncommon in adequately nourished adults but marginal status occurs in older adults, heavy alcohol users, people on isoniazid, levodopa or some anticonvulsants, renal patients and some women using oral contraceptives (general nutrition knowledge; not re-verified in this unit). Correcting deficiency is legitimate nutrition, and EU claims for 'normal psychological function' and 'normal functioning of the nervous system' describe exactly that role. Enhancement above adequacy is not supported: no trial in replete young adults shows a cognitive gain, the young-adult anxiety finding did not beat placebo statistically [field2022], and B6-only trials in older adults are null or unconfirmed [malouf2003, deijen1992]. Evidence from B-complex products [kennedy2010, young2019] cannot be attributed to B6, and evidence from MCI patients [smith2010] cannot be generalised to students.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Claims such as 'B6 for focus', 'B6 boosts serotonin and mood', 'calm and concentration' or 'high-strength B6 50-100 mg' are common. The evidence supports only the authorised EU wording ('contributes to normal psychological function', 'normal functioning of the nervous system', 'reduction of tiredness and fatigue') at source-level amounts (>=15% NRV, i.e. >=0.21 mg). Mechanistic GABA/serotonin stories are biochemistry, not efficacy. High-strength B6 products are a liability: EFSA's UL is 12 mg/day and neuropathy has been reported at doses common in supplements. An honest Brain Food position: B6 can be included at 0.2-1.4 mg per serving to support an authorised claim, never marketed as a cognitive enhancer, and never at doses near or above the UL.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Dietary B6 vitamers are absorbed by passive diffusion after dephosphorylation, converted in liver to PLP and transported bound to albumin; PLP crosses into the brain after dephosphorylation and re-phosphorylation by pyridoxal kinase. PLP is the cofactor for decarboxylases making serotonin, dopamine and GABA and for transsulfuration of homocysteine. These roles explain deficiency signs (seizures in infants, depression, confusion, neuropathy) but not an effect of extra B6 in replete people. Excess pyridoxine may inhibit pyridoxal kinase in peripheral sensory neurons, producing the paradox of functional B6 deficiency and neuropathy at high intakes.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Oral pyridoxine/pyridoxal -> passive intestinal absorption -> hepatic conversion to PLP -> plasma PLP rises", "detail": "20 mg/day for 12 weeks raised plasma PLP by ~238 nmol/L in older men [malouf2003, deijen1992]; multivitamins raise blood B6 in young adults [pipingas2014, white2015].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "PLP -> cofactor for glutamate decarboxylase, aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase and cystathionine beta-synthase", "detail": "Textbook enzymology; the proposed link to GABA/serotonin synthesis in adults with adequate status is not demonstrated in vivo.", "evidence_level": "plausible"}, {"step": "Higher PLP -> increased cortical inhibition (GABA)", "detail": "Stronger visual surround suppression after 100 mg/day for a month in young adults (d=-0.32), interpreted as increased GABAergic inhibition; binocular rivalry and tactile measures null [field2022].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "PLP -> transsulfuration of homocysteine", "detail": "B6 contributes little to fasting tHcy lowering compared with folate and B12; combinations lower tHcy 26-30% [clarke2014, smith2010].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Excess pyridoxine -> pyridoxal kinase inhibition in peripheral sensory neurons -> sensory axonal neuropathy", "detail": "Mechanistic hypothesis [hadtstein2021]; clinical toxicity established at high doses [schaumburg1983, berger1992] and reported at lower doses in vigilance data [vanhunsel2025].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Measured human cognitive outcome", "detail": "No B6-specific cognitive benefit in healthy young or older adults [malouf2003, field2022, bryan2002]; B-combination trials null for global cognition [clarke2014].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["PLP-dependent neurotransmitter synthesis (GABA, serotonin, dopamine)", "homocysteine transsulfuration (cystathionine beta-synthase)", "one-carbon metabolism (serine hydroxymethyltransferase)", "pyridoxal kinase inhibition by excess pyridoxine (toxicity)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "UK young adults (mean 23 y), anxiety measured by SCAARED; not selected for anxiety or low B6", "dose_or_intake": "100 mg/day pyridoxine", "duration": "about 1 month", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Anxiety fell within the B6 group but the between-group interaction versus placebo was not significant; depression trend only.", "effect_estimate": "within-group d=0.37 (B6) vs 0.12 (placebo); interaction F(1,173)=2.20, p=0.14 [field2022]", "certainty_notes": "Single trial; downgraded for risk of bias (non-identical tablets, phase-dependent samples), imprecision and reliance on within-group change; dose 8x EFSA UL, so not translatable to a food product.", "key_study_ids": ["field2022"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Young and middle-aged women in a four-arm trial (B6 arm ~17 per age group)", "dose_or_intake": "75 mg/day pyridoxine", "duration": "35 days", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "Selective positive effects on some memory measures across vitamin arms, no mood effect; B6-specific young-adult estimates not available in abstract.", "effect_estimate": "not reported [bryan2002]", "certainty_notes": "Very small arms, many outcomes, no replication; cannot support any statement.", "key_study_ids": ["bryan2002"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "knowledge_workers", "population_detail": "Employed adults 20-55 y receiving multinutrient products rich in B vitamins", "dose_or_intake": "multivitamin/B-complex products (B6 not isolated)", "duration": "4-16 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Cognitive batteries largely null; one arithmetic task (Serial 3s) improved in one trial; Stroop trend in men not significant after correction.", "effect_estimate": "Serial 3s ANCOVA p<0.05, other tasks NS [kennedy2010]; no significant effect after correction [pipingas2014]; memory p=0.47, attention p=0.17 in an industry-funded 12-week trial in middle-aged adults [young2022]", "certainty_notes": "Indirect for B6 (multi-ingredient, sometimes with herbs), multiple outcomes, industry funding.", "key_study_ids": ["kennedy2010", "pipingas2014", "young2022"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy and 'at-risk' adults given B-complex or multivitamin products", "dose_or_intake": "products with >=3 B vitamins incl. B6 (various doses)", "duration": ">=4 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Small reduction in self-rated stress; no effect on anxiety or depressive symptoms.", "effect_estimate": "stress SMD 0.23 (0.02 to 0.45); anxiety SMD 0.03 (-0.13 to 0.20) [young2019]; stress SMD 0.35 [long2013]", "certainty_notes": "Subjective outcomes, multi-ingredient (no B6 attribution), author industry funding, few trials; downgraded for indirectness and risk of bias.", "key_study_ids": ["young2019", "long2013", "kennedy2010", "white2015"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Healthy older men (70-79 y) and women (65-92 y)", "dose_or_intake": "20-75 mg/day pyridoxine", "duration": "5-12 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "One trial reported better long-term memory; Cochrane analysis of both trials found no significant effect on cognition or mood.", "effect_estimate": "no significant pooled WMD [malouf2003]; authors' positive memory effect without effect size [deijen1992]", "certainty_notes": "Two small old trials, conflicting analyses, doses above EU UL.", "key_study_ids": ["malouf2003", "deijen1992", "bryan2002"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Older adults (mean ~66-75 y), many with vascular disease or raised homocysteine, given B6 within folic acid + B12 combinations", "dose_or_intake": "B6 10-50 mg/day in combination", "duration": "2-7 years", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Combinations lower homocysteine but do not change global cognition; B6's own contribution cannot be separated.", "effect_estimate": "global z difference 0.00 (-0.05 to 0.06) [clarke2014]; SMD -0.03 to 0.06 [rutjes2018]", "certainty_notes": "High certainty for the combination null (see folate monograph) but downgraded to C for B6 because of indirectness (B6 never randomised alone in these trials).", "key_study_ids": ["clarke2014", "rutjes2018", "ford2010", "kang2008", "mcmahon2006"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "neuroplasticity_biomarkers", "population": "older_adults_mci", "population_detail": "MCI >70 y, benefit concentrated in tHcy >11-13 umol/L", "dose_or_intake": "B6 20 mg + folic acid 0.8 mg + B12 0.5 mg", "duration": "2 years", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Slower whole-brain atrophy and subgroup cognitive benefits in one trial; B6 contribution unknown; not replicated.", "effect_estimate": "atrophy 0.76 vs 1.08 %/y [smith2010]; subgroup p-values only [dejager2012]; Cochrane: no evidence of cognitive benefit [mccleery2018]", "certainty_notes": "Single trial, biomarker outcome, patent COI, indirect for B6.", "key_study_ids": ["smith2010", "dejager2012", "mccleery2018"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_decline", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Community-dwelling adults >=45 y, observational B6 intake/status", "dose_or_intake": "dietary intake / plasma B6", "duration": "prospective cohorts", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "High B6 concentrations not associated with better cognition or lower dementia risk.", "effect_estimate": "no significant association [zhang2020]", "certainty_notes": "Observational, heterogeneous exposure measures.", "key_study_ids": ["zhang2020"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "alertness", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "No acute (single-dose) B6 cognition trials identified", "dose_or_intake": "not studied", "duration": "single dose", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No evidence of any acute cognitive or alertness effect; none is mechanistically expected in replete people.", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "Absence of data.", "key_study_ids": []}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "students", "population_detail": "University students under academic load", "dose_or_intake": "not studied", "duration": "not studied", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No trial of B6 on academic or cognitive performance in students; the only student-age trial measured anxiety and sensory inhibition, not cognition.", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "Absence of data; extrapolation from older or multi-ingredient trials not justified.", "key_study_ids": ["field2022"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "other", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Safety: peripheral sensory neuropathy in supplement users", "dose_or_intake": "reference point 50 mg/day; established at >=500 mg/day; case reports below 50 mg/day", "duration": "months to years", "grade": "B", "direction": "harm", "effect_summary": "Dose- and duration-dependent sensory axonal neuropathy, usually reversible after stopping but with 'coasting'.", "effect_estimate": "EFSA UL 12 mg/day (RP 50 mg/day / UF 4) [efsa2023b6]; 5/5 volunteers at 1-3 g/day developed neuropathy [berger1992]", "certainty_notes": "Causality established at high doses (dechallenge, dose-response); uncertain below 50 mg/day (vigilance data without denominators).", "key_study_ids": ["efsa2023b6", "berger1992", "schaumburg1983", "muhamad2023", "vanhunsel2025"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "not studied", "onset": "not applicable", "duration_studied": "no acute trials identified", "domain": "any cognitive domain", "population": "healthy young adults", "effect_estimate": "no data", "certainty": "D (insufficient)", "safety": "single food-level doses safe", "study_ids": []}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "~1 month", "population": "young adults (mean 23 y)", "outcome": "anxiety, depression, sensory inhibition", "effect_estimate": "anxiety interaction p=0.14 (NS); surround suppression d=-0.32", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "single trial; 100 mg/day (8x EU UL); imperfect blinding", "study_ids": ["field2022"]}, {"duration": "35 days", "population": "women of various ages", "outcome": "memory, mood", "effect_estimate": "selective memory effects; mood null", "evidence_quality": "D", "limitations": "tiny arms; many outcomes", "study_ids": ["bryan2002"]}, {"duration": "12 weeks", "population": "men 70-79 y", "outcome": "long-term memory", "effect_estimate": "authors: modest improvement; Cochrane: NS", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "n=76; conflicting analyses", "study_ids": ["deijen1992", "malouf2003"]}, {"duration": "4-16 weeks", "population": "working-age adults (multinutrient products)", "outcome": "stress, vigour, cognition", "effect_estimate": "stress SMD 0.23; cognition largely null", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "B6 not isolated; industry links", "study_ids": ["young2019", "kennedy2010", "pipingas2014", "white2015", "long2013"]}, {"duration": "2-7 years", "population": "older adults, vascular risk", "outcome": "global cognition (B6 in combination)", "effect_estimate": "z 0.00 (-0.05 to 0.06)", "evidence_quality": "C for B6 (indirect)", "limitations": "B6 not isolated", "study_ids": ["clarke2014", "rutjes2018", "ford2010", "kang2008", "mcmahon2006"]}, {"duration": "months-years", "population": "supplement users", "outcome": "sensory neuropathy", "effect_estimate": "UL 12 mg/day; reports persist after 21 mg cap", "evidence_quality": "B (harm)", "limitations": "case/vigilance data at low doses", "study_ids": ["efsa2023b6", "vanhunsel2025", "berger1992", "schaumburg1983", "muhamad2023"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Safe at dietary and NRV-level intakes (1.4 mg NRV). The critical adverse effect is peripheral sensory neuropathy, established at high doses and reported at moderate supplement doses; EFSA (2023) set the adult UL at 12 mg/day, far lower than the US UL of 100 mg/day. Many 'stress', 'energy' and 'brain' products exceed 12 mg.", "adverse_effects": ["sensory (axonal) peripheral neuropathy, ataxia, paraesthesia - dose and duration dependent, may progress for weeks after stopping ('coasting') [berger1992, schaumburg1983]", "neuropathy reports at doses below 50 mg/day in pharmacovigilance data (causality uncertain) [vanhunsel2025]", "photosensitive skin reactions and gastrointestinal upset reported at high doses (general literature; not re-verified here)"], "contraindications": ["existing peripheral neuropathy (avoid high-dose B6)", "people already using other B6-containing supplements (cumulative dose)"], "drug_interactions": ["levodopa without a decarboxylase inhibitor: pyridoxine can reduce its effect (general pharmacology; not re-verified here)", "phenytoin and phenobarbital: high-dose B6 may lower drug levels (general pharmacology; not re-verified here)", "isoniazid, penicillamine, hydralazine and some anticonvulsants deplete B6 (deficiency risk rather than interaction harm)"], "upper_limits": "EU/EFSA 2023: 12 mg/day adults including pregnancy and lactation; children 2.2-10.7 mg/day by age [efsa2023b6]. US Food and Nutrition Board (1998): 100 mg/day adults (web search snippet of NIH ODS fact sheet; direct fetch HTTP 403). Netherlands national supplement cap 21 mg/day since October 2018 [vanhunsel2025].", "sleep_impact": "No evidence of sleep disruption at food-level doses; high-dose B6 and vivid dreams are reported anecdotally but not established in this review.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Up to 5-7 years in combination trials at 10-50 mg/day [kang2008, ford2010] without neuropathy being a reported outcome; neuropathy surveillance in these trials was not systematic.", "daily_use_appropriate": "probably", "special_populations": "Pregnancy: needs within RDA are safe; avoid high doses (UL applies). Children: much lower ULs (2.2-10.7 mg/day). Kidney disease: high-dose B-vitamin combination (B6 25 mg + FA 2.5 mg + B12 1 mg) accelerated GFR decline and increased vascular events in diabetic nephropathy [house2010]. Older adults with neuropathy, people on levodopa or antiepileptics: medical review.", "specialist_review_needed": ["neurologist (neuropathy risk thresholds)", "clinical pharmacist (drug interactions, cumulative intake from other products)", "regulatory toxicologist (EU UL compliance and national maxima)"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "Pass only at <=1.4-5 mg per daily serving with label warnings against combining with other B6 products; any formulation at or above the EFSA UL (12 mg/day) fails."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Vitamin B6 (pyridoxine hydrochloride, pyridoxine 5'-phosphate, pyridoxal 5'-phosphate per Directive 2002/46/EC Annex II as generally reported; not re-verified) is a permitted vitamin; not novel. Authorised Article 13 claims in Regulation (EU) 432/2012 include normal psychological function, normal functioning of the nervous system, reduction of tiredness and fatigue, normal homocysteine metabolism, normal energy-yielding metabolism, regulation of hormonal activity, normal cysteine synthesis, normal red blood cell formation, immune function; condition: food must be at least a 'source of' B6 (15% NRV = 0.21 mg per 100 g/100 ml/portion under Regulation 1924/2006 and 1169/2011 NRV 1.4 mg; NRV figure from general knowledge, not re-fetched). No authorised claim refers to cognition, memory, concentration or anxiety. Harmonised EU maximum levels for supplements have not been set; Member States apply national maxima (e.g. Netherlands 21 mg/day [vanhunsel2025]); EFSA UL 12 mg/day [efsa2023b6] is the reference likely to drive future maxima.", "us": "Dietary supplements: a lawful dietary ingredient marketed before 15 Oct 1994, so no NDI notification is needed for the standard forms; structure/function claims (e.g. 'supports normal nervous system function', 'supports energy metabolism') are allowed with the DSHEA disclaimer and FDA notification within 30 days, but under FTC standards claims of improved memory, focus or 'brain power' in non-deficient adults would need competent and reliable scientific evidence that the trials reviewed here do not provide. Conventional foods: fortification must respect FDA's fortification policy (21 CFR 104.20) and the ingredient's food-additive/GRAS status. IOM/Food and Nutrition Board ULs (1998): B6 100 mg/day for adults (snippet of NIH ODS). Pyridoxine hydrochloride is listed as GRAS for food use (21 CFR 184.1676, cited from agent knowledge; not re-fetched). 'Supports mood' or 'stress' structure/function claims would be lawful in form but weakly substantiated.", "morocco": "Uncertain. Food supplements in Morocco have been regulated as foodstuffs under ONSSA (law 28-07 on food safety) with product registration; 2026 Moroccan press reports describe a pending reform (draft decree postponed in July 2026) that would reserve some high-risk supplement categories to pharmacies and reportedly applies national maximum doses (e.g. professionals cite 10 ug vitamin D and 180 mg vitamin C caps). No official Moroccan maximum level for vitamin B6, folic acid or B12 and no Moroccan list of authorised health claims was found in this unit; Moroccan practice is commonly assumed to follow Codex/EU references, but this is not verified. Mandatory wheat-flour fortification exists in Morocco (iron; premix composition, including whether folic acid is currently added, not verified here). Sources: hespress.com/medias24.com/le360.ma press search results (accessed 2026-10-25; medias24 article returned HTTP 403). A Moroccan regulatory consultant and ONSSA/Bulletin officiel check are required.", "permitted_claims_examples": ["Vitamin B6 contributes to normal psychological function", "Vitamin B6 contributes to normal functioning of the nervous system", "Vitamin B6 contributes to the reduction of tiredness and fatigue", "Vitamin B6 contributes to normal homocysteine metabolism"], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "None for standard forms in the EU (permitted vitamin sources) or the US (pre-DSHEA dietary ingredient). Novel salts or unusual vitamers would need checking.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Good: tiny mass, water-soluble, already used in fortified cereals and drinks. The limiting factor is dose ceiling, not technology.", "formats": ["fortified bar or cereal", "drink powder", "multi-B premix in a food matrix", "capsule/tablet supplement"], "stability": "Pyridoxine hydrochloride is relatively heat-stable but light-sensitive; pyridoxal forms and PLP are less stable; overage of ~10-30% typical in premixes (industry practice, not verified here).", "taste": "Slightly bitter/salty at mg doses; negligible at 1-5 mg in a food.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Synthetic pyridoxine is highly bioavailable; plant pyridoxine glucoside is less available (general nutrition knowledge, not re-verified). PLP supplements are not shown to be safer or more effective.", "dose_feasibility": "A source-level claim needs only 0.21 mg/serving; the NRV is 1.4 mg. Trial doses used for 'brain' outcomes (20-100 mg [field2022, smith2010, deijen1992]) fit physically in any food but exceed the EFSA UL and must not be used.", "cost_availability": "Commodity vitamin, widely available from premix suppliers.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 2, "effect_magnitude": 1, "safety": 5, "reproducibility": 2, "food_suitability": 8, "cost_access": 9, "rationale": "evidence_quality 2: no outcome in healthy young adults or students exceeds grade C; B6-specific trials are few, small and conflicting; the only grade B row concerns harm (neuropathy). effect_magnitude 1: no demonstrated cognitive effect; the best young-adult signal (anxiety) is a within-group change that did not beat placebo. safety 5: very safe at food levels but with a real, dose-related neurotoxicity and a low EU UL (12 mg) that many market products and all positive trials exceed; vigilance reports at lower doses. reproducibility 2: positive findings (Deijen, Field) unreplicated or contradicted. food_suitability 8: technically easy, stable, tiny dose. cost_access 9: cheap commodity, though Moroccan regulatory status unverified."}, "research_gaps": ["Preregistered RCT of food-level B6 (1-10 mg) on cognition and anxiety in young adults with measured baseline PLP", "Dose-response for neuropathy between 10 and 50 mg/day with denominators", "Effects of correcting marginal B6 status (e.g. women on oral contraceptives) on mood/cognition", "Replication of Field 2022 with identical-appearing placebo and between-group primary analysis", "Acute effects (none studied)"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 3.7, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": null, "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": "C", "best_grade_overall": null, "benefit_domains": {}, "n_outcome_rows": 11, "n_studies": 26, "n_verified": 26, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 13, "n_systematic_reviews": 5, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["harm", "inconsistent", "insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "vitamin-c", "name": "Vitamin C", "category": "B", "category_name": "Vitamins", "aliases": ["vitamin C", "ascorbic acid", "L-ascorbic acid", "sodium ascorbate", "calcium ascorbate", "ascorbate"], "food_sources": ["citrus fruits (orange, grapefruit, lemon)", "bell peppers and chili peppers", "kiwifruit", "strawberries and other berries", "broccoli, Brussels sprouts, cauliflower", "leafy greens (kale, spinach)", "tomatoes and potatoes (smaller amounts but large contributors due to volume consumed)"], "active_constituents": ["L-ascorbic acid, a water-soluble essential micronutrient and cofactor for dioxygenase enzymes (collagen hydroxylases, dopamine beta-hydroxylase, HIF prolyl-hydroxylases)"], "one_line_verdict": "Vitamin C is an essential nutrient whose deficiency (scurvy) impairs mood and cognition, and in adequately nourished healthy young adults the best available single RCT found a small, single-site benefit on attention and work engagement, but the evidence base is too thin (one small trial, no independent replication) to support a general enhancement claim.", "scientific_conclusion": "Vitamin C is an essential water-soluble vitamin that humans cannot synthesise; severe deficiency (scurvy) is historically associated with fatigue, irritability and depressed mood, establishing that gross deficiency harms mental state, but this is deficiency correction, not cognitive enhancement in replete adults. The 2017 systematic review by Travica et al. (50 studies, 1980-2017) found that cognitively intact groups generally had higher plasma vitamin C than cognitively impaired groups, but this is cross-sectional/observational association, confounded by overall diet quality and health status, and the authors explicitly concluded the evidence was insufficient and called for better-designed trials using sensitive cognitive tests in healthy adults [travica2017]. Direct interventional evidence in healthy young adults is limited to a single Korean trial: a cross-sectional survey of 214 adults (20-39y) found serum vitamin C positively associated with self-reported attention (r=0.16, p=0.02), then a double-blind RCT in 46 adults with inadequate serum vitamin C (<50 micromol/L) given 1000 mg/day (500 mg twice daily) for 4 weeks improved self-reported attention (p=0.03) and work absorption/engagement (p=0.03-0.07) and modestly improved Stroop task performance (p=0.04), with no effect on mood or serum BDNF [sim2022]. This is a single small (n=46) industry co-funded-adjacent (pharmaceutical company co-author) trial restricted to vitamin-C-insufficient participants, not generalisable to vitamin-C-replete adults, and the Stroop/attention outcomes are modest effect sizes without reported standardized effect size or CI in the abstract; it has not been independently replicated. In older or cardiovascular-risk populations, chronic antioxidant trials are uniformly null for cognitive decline: the Women's Antioxidant and Cardiovascular Study (n=2824 women aged >=65, vitamin C 500 mg/day vs placebo over 5.4 years) found no effect on the primary cognitive-change outcome, only a late cross-sectional difference at the final assessment that did not track with trajectory [kang2009]. No RCT has tested vitamin C for cognition in healthy, replete young adults or students under examination load; all existing young-adult data come from one insufficiency-restricted trial. The honest summary: vitamin C is necessary for normal brain function and its correction in deficient or insufficient individuals plausibly restores vitality/attention, but there is no adequate human evidence that supplementing an already-replete healthy adult enhances cognition beyond dietary sufficiency. EFSA has authorised general, non-cognition-specific wording ('contributes to normal psychological function' and 'contributes to normal function of the nervous system') based on vitamin C's established physiological roles, not on demonstrated enhancement trials in healthy people [efsa2010].", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Vitamin C deficiency (scurvy, plasma <11 micromol/L) is well documented to cause fatigue, irritability, and depressive symptoms alongside connective-tissue signs; correcting deficiency restores normal mental state. The Sim 2022 RCT specifically enrolled adults with serum vitamin C below the adequacy threshold (<50 micromol/L, i.e. marginal/insufficient but not scorbutic) and found benefits on attention and work engagement; this is best read as correcting sub-optimal status in an insufficient subgroup, not as enhancement above the normal range. No RCT has tested supplementation in vitamin-C-replete healthy young adults for a cognitive enhancement effect; the Travica review notes observational associations only. Brain Food should market vitamin C adequacy (via diet or modest supplementation in people with low fruit/vegetable intake) rather than imply that extra vitamin C enhances cognition in an already well-nourished person.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Marketing that vitamin C 'boosts brainpower' or 'sharpens focus' in general populations overstates a thin evidence base: one small (n=46), single-country RCT restricted to insufficient individuals, and cross-sectional/observational associations that cannot establish causation. EFSA-authorised claims ('vitamin C contributes to normal psychological function' and 'to normal functioning of the nervous system') are legitimate under EU law but are generic structure/function statements based on vitamin C's established physiological roles (catecholamine synthesis, antioxidant protection), not evidence of enhancement above adequacy. The honest marketing position for Brain Food is: ordinary dietary vitamin C intake (via fruits and vegetables) supports normal nervous-system function and mood, especially in people whose intake is currently marginal; claiming a nootropic boost in already-replete healthy adults is not supported.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Vitamin C is actively transported into the brain via SVCT2 transporters and reaches concentrations several-fold higher than plasma, where it serves as a cofactor for dopamine-beta-hydroxylase (catecholamine synthesis), as an antioxidant protecting neurons from oxidative stress, and as a cofactor for collagen synthesis in cerebral vasculature. Deficiency plausibly impairs these pathways; whether supraphysiological intake in replete adults confers additional cognitive benefit is not established.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Dietary/supplemental ascorbic acid -> intestinal SVCT1-mediated absorption (saturable, ~70-90% at usual doses, declining at high single doses) -> plasma ascorbate", "detail": "Well characterised pharmacokinetics; plasma saturates near 70-85 micromol/L with doses above ~1 g due to renal excretion [pharmacokinetics review]", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Plasma ascorbate -> active transport across blood-brain barrier via SVCT2 -> high neuronal/glial ascorbate concentration (brain >> plasma)", "detail": "Established in animal models and human CSF measurements; brain prioritises ascorbate retention during systemic deficiency", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Neuronal ascorbate -> cofactor for dopamine-beta-hydroxylase and antioxidant buffering of reactive oxygen species generated during synaptic activity", "detail": "Biochemically established enzymatic role; plausible link to catecholamine-dependent attention/mood regulation", "evidence_level": "plausible"}, {"step": "Low vitamin C status (deficiency/insufficiency) -> impaired synthesis/antioxidant buffering -> fatigue, low mood, reduced attention", "detail": "Supported by scurvy case literature and the Sim 2022 insufficiency-restricted RCT showing improved attention/engagement after repletion [sim2022]", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Supplementation in already-replete adults -> measurable additional cognitive benefit", "detail": "Not established; Women's Antioxidant Cardiovascular Study found no cognitive-trajectory benefit of chronic vitamin C in older women [kang2009]; no dose-response data in replete young adults", "evidence_level": "speculative"}], "pathways": ["SVCT1/SVCT2 ascorbate transport", "dopamine-beta-hydroxylase cofactor activity", "neuronal antioxidant buffering", "cerebrovascular collagen synthesis"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "deficient_or_low_baseline", "population_detail": "Healthy adults 20-39y with serum vitamin C <50 micromol/L (insufficient but not scorbutic), Korea", "dose_or_intake": "1000 mg/day (500 mg twice daily)", "duration": "4 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Single RCT (n=46) found improved self-reported attention (p=0.03), work absorption (p=0.03) and Stroop color-word performance (p=0.04) versus placebo; fatigue and comprehensive work engagement showed non-significant trends (p=0.06-0.07); no effect on mood or serum BDNF.", "effect_estimate": "p=0.03-0.04 for attention/Stroop outcomes; standardized effect size and CI not reported in available abstract [sim2022]", "certainty_notes": "Downgraded for imprecision (single small trial, n=46), indirectness (restricted to insufficient individuals, not generalisable to replete adults), and lack of independent replication. Grade C: limited, single small trial.", "key_study_ids": ["sim2022"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Vitamin-C-replete healthy young adults/students under normal conditions", "dose_or_intake": "not studied", "duration": "not applicable", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No RCT has tested vitamin C supplementation for cognitive enhancement in vitamin-C-replete healthy young adults or students; existing young-adult trial data are restricted to an insufficient subgroup [sim2022]. Travica 2017 found only cross-sectional associations, insufficient to establish causal enhancement.", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "No direct evidence; grade reflects absence of adequately designed human trials in the target population rather than a tested null result.", "key_study_ids": ["travica2017", "sim2022"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_decline", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Women aged >=65 with cardiovascular disease or >=3 CV risk factors", "dose_or_intake": "500 mg/day vitamin C", "duration": "5.4 years", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Women's Antioxidant and Cardiovascular Study (n=2824): vitamin C supplementation was not associated with slower cognitive decline over time (mean difference in change 0.02, 95% CI -0.03 to 0.07, p=0.39); a cross-sectional difference at the final single assessment (p=0.0005) did not reflect a true trajectory benefit.", "effect_estimate": "mean difference in cognitive change 0.02 (95% CI -0.03 to 0.07) [kang2009]", "certainty_notes": "Large, long trial but in a cardiovascular-risk older-female population, not healthy young adults; downgraded for indirectness to the Brain Food target population. No benefit shown for the primary outcome.", "key_study_ids": ["kang2009"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "not adequately studied as a single acute dose for cognition", "onset": "not reported", "duration_studied": "not applicable", "domain": "other", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty": "no acute human cognitive trials identified", "safety": "generally well tolerated at single doses up to several grams; GI upset above ~2 g", "study_ids": []}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "4 weeks", "population": "vitamin-C-insufficient healthy adults", "outcome": "attention, work engagement, Stroop performance", "effect_estimate": "p=0.03-0.04, effect size not reported", "evidence_quality": "C - single small trial", "limitations": "n=46, single site, restricted to insufficient baseline status, self-report outcomes predominate", "study_ids": ["sim2022"]}, {"duration": "5.4 years", "population": "older women with cardiovascular risk", "outcome": "cognitive decline trajectory", "effect_estimate": "no benefit (mean diff 0.02, CI -0.03 to 0.07)", "evidence_quality": "B - large long RCT but indirect population", "limitations": "older cardiovascular-risk women, not healthy young adults; combined antioxidant trial design", "study_ids": ["kang2009"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Vitamin C is one of the most extensively used and generally safe vitamins across a wide intake range. Acute GI effects (diarrhea, nausea, cramping) occur above roughly 2 g/day due to osmotic effects of unabsorbed ascorbate. Serious adverse events are rare in the general population but specific subgroups require caution.", "adverse_effects": ["gastrointestinal upset (diarrhea, nausea, abdominal cramping) at doses above ~2 g/day", "increased oxalate excretion, a theoretical kidney stone risk factor in susceptible individuals (evidence mixed; one review found no contribution to calcium oxalate stones at typical doses [vitccistones1997])"], "contraindications": ["hereditary hemochromatosis or other iron-overload conditions (vitamin C increases non-heme iron absorption and can worsen iron overload)", "history of oxalate kidney stones (caution at high doses)", "glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD) deficiency (very high IV doses can precipitate hemolysis; oral doses at supplement levels pose minimal risk)"], "drug_interactions": ["may reduce efficacy of some chemotherapy agents via antioxidant interference (consult oncology specialist)", "can affect warfarin monitoring indirectly through effects on vitamin K metabolism in rare cases", "high-dose vitamin C can interfere with some glucose meter readings and certain lab assays"], "upper_limits": "NIH Office of Dietary Supplements sets the adult tolerable upper intake level (UL) at 2000 mg/day from all sources (food plus supplements); EFSA has not set a numerical UL for vitamin C, citing low toxicity, but recommends caution above several grams/day.", "sleep_impact": "No established meaningful effect of vitamin C on sleep architecture at typical or supplement doses; not a stimulant.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Yes, decades of use in large RCTs (e.g., Women's Antioxidant and Cardiovascular Study, 5.4 years at 500 mg/day) with no signal of serious harm at these doses.", "daily_use_appropriate": "yes", "special_populations": "Pregnant/lactating women: safe at RDA/typical supplement doses, avoid very high doses without medical supervision; hemochromatosis patients should avoid high-dose supplementation; patients on certain chemotherapy regimens should consult their oncologist before high-dose antioxidant supplementation.", "specialist_review_needed": ["hematologist for hemochromatosis/iron-overload patients", "oncologist for patients on active chemotherapy considering high-dose antioxidant use", "nephrologist/urologist for patients with recurrent oxalate kidney stones considering high-dose supplementation"], "hard_safety_gate": "pass", "gate_reason": "Vitamin C at typical food and supplement doses (up to the 2000 mg/day UL) has an extensive safety record with no mortality or serious-harm signal in the populations studied; it is a standard, well-tolerated nutrient appropriate for routine inclusion in food products at RDA-range doses."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Vitamin C is an authorised vitamin for use in foods and food supplements under EU law (Directive 2002/46/EC, Regulation (EC) 1925/2006). EFSA has authorised generic Article 13.1 health claims including 'Vitamin C contributes to normal psychological function' and 'Vitamin C contributes to normal function of the nervous system' (and several others: normal collagen formation, normal immune function, reduction of tiredness and fatigue), based on EFSA's 2009-2010 opinion (EFSA Journal 2009; 7(9):1226 and related opinions under Regulation 1924/2006). These are general structure/function claims, not specific cognitive-enhancement claims.", "us": "Vitamin C is GRAS and widely used in food fortification and dietary supplements under DSHEA; structure/function claims (e.g., 'supports normal nervous system function') are permitted with required disclaimer if substantiated, but any explicit cognitive-enhancement claim would need FTC-level competent and reliable scientific evidence, which does not currently exist for vitamin C in replete healthy adults.", "morocco": "Vitamin C is a long-established, permitted food additive/fortificant and supplement ingredient under ONSSA food safety regulation; no specific cognitive claim framework exists; status for general vitamin fortification is well established and uncontroversial.", "permitted_claims_examples": ["Vitamin C contributes to normal psychological function (EU Article 13.1 authorised claim)", "Vitamin C contributes to normal function of the nervous system (EU Article 13.1 authorised claim)"], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "None; vitamin C is a long-established vitamin, not a novel food or new dietary ingredient in any target market.", "regulatory_gate": "pass"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Excellent. Vitamin C is naturally abundant in many fruits and vegetables and is heat/light/oxygen sensitive but can be stabilised in fortified foods and beverages using standard food-technology approaches (encapsulation, controlled packaging, ascorbyl palmitate for fat-soluble applications).", "formats": ["fresh fruit/vegetable inclusion", "fortified beverages and bars", "ascorbic acid or sodium/calcium ascorbate powder in capsules/tablets", "liposomal or buffered forms marketed for reduced GI irritation (efficacy difference not well substantiated)"], "stability": "Degrades with heat, light, oxygen exposure, and prolonged storage; requires careful processing/packaging in fortified food products to retain labelled content.", "taste": "Sour/acidic taste at effective doses; easily masked in fruit-based or sweetened formats; may affect product pH and preservation profile.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Near-complete absorption (70-90%) at doses up to ~200 mg/dose via SVCT1 transporters; absorption efficiency declines at single doses above ~1 g due to transporter saturation, with excess excreted renally; splitting doses (as in the Sim 2022 trial, 500 mg twice daily) improves overall absorption versus one large dose.", "dose_feasibility": "The 1000 mg/day dose used in the only young-adult cognitive RCT (Sim 2022) is well above typical RDA (75-90 mg/day) but is readily achievable via a supplement format or fortified product; it does not fit within a single ordinary food serving but is feasible as a daily fortified beverage or supplement.", "cost_availability": "Vitamin C (ascorbic acid) is one of the cheapest and most widely available vitamin ingredients globally, with well-established supply chains in all three target markets.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 3, "effect_magnitude": 2, "safety": 9, "reproducibility": 2, "food_suitability": 9, "cost_access": 9, "rationale": "evidence_quality=3 (grade C/D evidence for cognitive enhancement in the target healthy-young-adult population; only one small, unreplicated trial, rest is observational or null in other populations). effect_magnitude=2 (modest p-values without reported standardized effect sizes, restricted to an insufficient subgroup). safety=9 (excellent long safety record, simple gate pass, UL 2000 mg/day well above typical use). reproducibility=2 (single trial, no independent replication, Travica review found only associative not causal evidence). food_suitability=9 (abundant in common foods, easy to formulate, cheap, universally recognised as healthy). cost_access=9 (very low cost, globally available ingredient)."}, "research_gaps": ["No RCT has tested vitamin C for cognitive enhancement in vitamin-C-replete healthy young adults or students under examination/workload stress.", "The only young-adult interventional trial (Sim 2022) is small (n=46), single-site, and restricted to an insufficient subgroup; needs independent replication with standardized effect sizes and pre-registration.", "No dose-response or acute single-dose cognitive trials in healthy adults identified.", "Long-term antioxidant trials (Women's Antioxidant Cardiovascular Study) are in older, cardiovascular-risk women, not transferable to healthy young adults or men.", "No trials combining vitamin C status with objective (non-self-report) cognitive batteries in a fully powered, preregistered design in students/knowledge workers."], "_derived": {"composite_score": 5.1, "eligibility": "eligible", "safety_gate": "pass", "regulatory_gate": "pass", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": null, "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"sustained_attention": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 3, "n_studies": 3, "n_verified": 3, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 1, "n_systematic_reviews": 1, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "vitamin-d", "name": "Vitamin D", "category": "B", "category_name": "Vitamins", "aliases": ["vitamin D", "vitamin D3", "cholecalciferol", "vitamin D2", "ergocalciferol", "calcidiol monohydrate (25-hydroxycholecalciferol; EU novel food)", "calciferol", "25(OH)D (circulating status marker, not an ingredient)"], "food_sources": ["skin synthesis after UVB exposure is the main source for most people; it is reduced by covering clothing or veiling, darker skin, indoor lifestyles, sunscreen, high latitude and winter", "oily fish (salmon, sardines, mackerel, herring); fish was the largest dietary source (48% of intake) in women from Meknes, Morocco [zouine2025]", "cod liver oil", "egg yolk (small amounts)", "UV-exposed mushrooms (vitamin D2)", "fortified foods: milk, yogurt, plant drinks, breakfast cereals, margarines and (in Morocco, by voluntary agreement) table oil; US limits include 84 IU/100 g in milk, 178 IU/100 g in yogurt and 560 IU/100 g in breakfast cereals (21 CFR 172.380)", "unfortified plant foods contain little or none; normal diets rarely supply the 15 µg/day US RDA (median intake 2.89 µg/day in Moroccan women of reproductive age [zouine2025])"], "active_constituents": ["cholecalciferol (D3) and ergocalciferol (D2), prohormones hydroxylated in the liver to 25-hydroxyvitamin D (calcidiol, the status marker) and in the kidney and other tissues, including the brain, to 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D (calcitriol), the ligand of the vitamin D receptor (VDR)", "calcidiol monohydrate: an EU novel-food form; EFSA set a labelling conversion factor of 2.5 relative to D3 [efsa2023]"], "one_line_verdict": "Vitamin D is an essential, cheap and safe-at-normal-doses nutrient worth correcting where deficiency is common (Morocco especially), but large, well-run RCTs show that supplementing mostly replete adults does not improve cognition, mood or cognitive ageing, and the only trial in healthy young adults was null; it cannot honestly be sold, and in the EU cannot legally be sold, as a brain or cognition ingredient.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. Observational studies consistently link low 25(OH)D with worse cognition and a higher dementia risk. Low status went with poorer cognitive performance (OR 1.24) and decline (OR 1.26) in a 2017 meta-analysis that pooled only three small supplementation trials (SMD 0.21, 95% CI -0.05 to 0.46) [goodwill2017]. In cohort meta-analyses, deficiency carried RR 1.42 for dementia [zhang2024]. In UK Biobank, 25(OH)D below 25 nmol/L carried HR 1.79 for dementia [navale2022]. These associations are probably confounded by frailty, obesity, outdoor activity and illness: one 18-year cohort of Swedish men found none (not in studies[], noted in coverage). Genetic evidence is mixed. A 172,349-person MR found no causal effect of 25(OH)D on cognitive performance [maddock2017]. An MR of Alzheimer disease found OR 1.25 per SD lower log-25(OH)D [mokry2016], and a nonlinear MR suggested a threshold effect only for deficiency below about 25-50 nmol/L [navale2022]. Randomised evidence in older adults, who are mostly replete, is large and consistently null. VITAL (2000 IU/day, n = 4218, 2-3 years): annual decline difference 0.01 SD (95% CI -0.01 to 0.02), with no interaction by baseline 25(OH)D [kang2021]. D-Health (60,000 IU/month, 5 years, about 3,800 tested): TICS difference 0.04 (-0.14 to 0.23) [pham2023]. WHI (400 IU plus calcium, 7.8 years): dementia HR 1.11 (0.71-1.74) [rossom2012]. Owusu (n = 260, 3 years) [owusu2019] and Zajac (n = 436) [zajac2020] were also null. VITAL-DEP found no effect on depression (HR 0.97) or mood in 18,353 adults [okereke2020]. Deficient or insufficient people. Results are inconsistent. A Norwegian trial in adults with a mean 25(OH)D of 34 nmol/L was null at 4 months [jorde2019]. VitaMIND (n = 620, deficient adults aged 50 and over, 24 months) was null [corbett2025]. Small positive signals come from an 82-person Canadian trial (visual memory, d about 0.6 in the insufficient subgroup) [pettersen2017] and from the Tianjin trials in MCI and Alzheimer disease reporting large IQ gains [yang2020, jia2019]. These Tianjin trials largely drive the inflated pooled estimates (Hedges g 0.48 in deficient subgroups [chen2024]; SMD up to 1.58 [behrouzi2026]). Another MCI trial found no vitamin D effect [monteroodasso2023]. Healthy young adults and students. The only RCT (n = 128, mean age 21.8, 5000 IU/day for 6 weeks) found no effect on working memory, inhibition, cognitive flexibility or mood [dean2011]. Its participants were replete (mean 76.6 nmol/L), so deficient students remain untested. No acute effect is plausible or studied. Safety. Very large intermittent doses raised falls and fractures [sanders2010]. 4000-10,000 IU/day for 3 years lowered radial BMD [burt2019] and raised mild hypercalcaemia and hypercalciuria [billington2020]. The EU UL is 100 µg/day [efsa2023].", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "This is a deficiency story, not an enhancement story. In mostly replete adults (VITAL mean baseline 25(OH)D about 32 ng/mL; Dean young adults mean 76.6 nmol/L), raising vitamin D status does not improve cognition, mood or cognitive ageing [kang2021, dean2011, pham2023, okereke2020]. The hypothesis that only deficient people benefit is plausible, given the threshold shape in the cohort and nonlinear-MR data [navale2022]. But it is unconfirmed: the two best trials in low-status adults were null [jorde2019, corbett2025], and the positive deficient-subgroup signals rest on small or single-centre trials [pettersen2017, yang2020]. Deficiency is common where Brain Food plans to sell. Prevalence is high in Moroccan women: 74.4% deficient in a 714-woman Rabat sample [mehdad2023]; median 9.95 ng/mL in 233 women from the south-eastern oases [dadda2022]; deficiency below 20 ng/mL above 59% in every intake quartile in Meknes [zouine2025]. It is moderate in Europe (13% below 30 nmol/L, 40% below 50 nmol/L [cashman2016]). In the US it is lower but peaks at ages 20-39 (7.6% below 30 nmol/L [herrick2019]). Correcting deficiency is justified for bone and muscle health and for meeting dietary reference values. Any cognitive benefit is unproven. No national Moroccan survey of vitamin D status was found (X6 synthesis).", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Claims such as 'vitamin D for focus', 'the sunshine vitamin for your brain', 'supports memory' or 'prevents dementia' are not supported. The large RCTs are null, and the observational associations are probably confounded. In the EU, vitamin D has seven authorised Article 13.1 claims (normal calcium and phosphorus absorption and use, normal blood calcium, bones, muscle function, teeth, immune function, cell division). None concerns cognitive or psychological function, so any brain claim for vitamin D would be unauthorised. The Endocrine Society 2024 guideline advises against routine supplementation above the DRI in healthy adults under 75 and against routine 25(OH)D testing [demay2024]. In the US, a cognition structure/function claim would need FTC-level substantiation that does not exist for healthy adults. Disease claims (dementia prevention) are prohibited. In Morocco, disease claims are banned, no positive list of health claims exists, and high-dose vitamin D supplements may become pharmacy-only. Honest positioning: a food that is a source of vitamin D, helping people at real risk of deficiency (veiled or indoor-living women, darker skin, winter, obesity) reach the reference intake, using the authorised bone, muscle and immune wording in the EU. No cognitive claim should be made. Industry-funded fortified-food cognition trials (for example a 40-woman yogurt trial co-funded by a dairy company, not included here) should not be used as substantiation.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Vitamin D3/D2 is made in skin or absorbed from food in micelles with dietary fat, then 25-hydroxylated in the liver to 25(OH)D. Calcitriol (1,25(OH)2D) is formed in the kidney and also locally; CYP27B1 and VDR are expressed in human brain tissue. Calcitriol acts through the VDR on gene transcription, including genes for neurotrophins, calcium-binding proteins, neurotransmitter synthesis and inflammation. Most of this comes from animal and in-vitro work. In humans, low 25(OH)D is associated with smaller brain volumes and more white-matter hyperintensities, but nonlinear MR did not support a causal effect on neuroimaging outcomes [navale2022]. The pathway from intake to a measured cognitive benefit is not established. RCTs that raise 25(OH)D substantially show no cognitive change in replete adults [kang2021, dean2011]. Mechanism is not efficacy.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Oral D3/D2 or UVB skin synthesis -> chylomicron absorption (fat-dependent) -> hepatic 25-hydroxylation -> serum 25(OH)D", "detail": "Established. Supplementation raises 25(OH)D dose-dependently: 5000 IU/day for 6 weeks raised it from 76 to 98 nmol/L [dean2011]; 4000 IU/day raised it to 132 nmol/L and 10,000 IU/day to 144 nmol/L at 3 years [burt2019]; about 600 IU/day held 25(OH)D steady over winter [zajac2020].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "25(OH)D -> renal and local 1-alpha-hydroxylation -> calcitriol -> VDR-mediated calcium/phosphate homeostasis", "detail": "Established for calcium metabolism. Excess raises serum and urinary calcium: mild hypercalcaemia in 9% at 10,000 IU/day [billington2020]; hypercalcaemia RR 1.54 pooled [malihi2016].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Calcitriol/VDR in brain -> neurotrophin expression, calcium buffering, anti-inflammatory and amyloid-clearance actions", "detail": "Shown mainly in rodent and cell models (not reviewed in detail here). Plasma amyloid-related biomarker changes were reported in one Chinese trial in Alzheimer disease [jia2019]. These are biomarkers, not cognition.", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Low 25(OH)D -> smaller brain volumes, white-matter hyperintensities, higher dementia risk (association)", "detail": "Associations seen in UK Biobank. Nonlinear MR supported a threshold effect of deficiency on dementia but not on neuroimaging outcomes [navale2022]. A reanalysis with newer MR methods exists but was not read. Linear MR of cognitive performance was null [maddock2017]; MR of Alzheimer disease was positive [mokry2016].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Measured human cognitive outcome after supplementation", "detail": "Null in large RCTs of mostly replete older adults [kang2021, pham2023, rossom2012] and in the single young-adult RCT [dean2011]. Inconsistent in deficient or insufficient adults [jorde2019, corbett2025, pettersen2017]. Positive only in single-centre Chinese MCI and Alzheimer trials [yang2020, jia2019].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["VDR-mediated gene transcription in neurons and glia (animal/in vitro)", "calcium homeostasis and parathyroid hormone suppression (established)", "neuroinflammation and oxidative stress modulation (animal; telomere/oxidative biomarkers in one human MCI trial)", "amyloid-beta clearance (animal; plasma biomarkers in one human AD trial)", "vascular/white-matter health (observational only)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy university-recruited adults aged 18-30 (mean 21.8 y), Brisbane, replete at baseline (mean 25(OH)D 76.6 nmol/L; only 10 of 128 below 50 nmol/L)", "dose_or_intake": "5000 IU (125 µg) D3/day", "duration": "6 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "No effect on N-back working memory, stop-signal response inhibition or task-switching flexibility despite 25(OH)D rising to 98 nmol/L. A subgroup below the median (75 nmol/L) was also null.", "effect_estimate": "Working memory F = 1.09, p = 0.30 (d = 0.19, post-test means identical at 0.62); response inhibition p = 0.37 (d = 0.16); flexibility p = 0.24 (d = 0.21) [dean2011]", "certainty_notes": "Single well-conducted, preregistered, double-blind RCT, so imprecise; indirect for deficient students because participants were replete. Dose exceeds the EU UL. Graded C for the absence of benefit in replete young adults.", "key_study_ids": ["dean2011"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Same young-adult trial: depressive symptoms (BDI), state anxiety and anger, psychotic-like experiences", "dose_or_intake": "5000 IU D3/day", "duration": "6 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "No effect on BDI, state anxiety, state anger, hallucination proneness or delusional ideation.", "effect_estimate": "BDI p = 0.51 (d = 0.12); state anxiety p = 0.23 [dean2011]", "certainty_notes": "Single trial, non-depressed replete sample, short duration.", "key_study_ids": ["dean2011"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "learning", "population": "students", "population_detail": "Students under academic load, including vitamin-D-deficient students (for example veiled or indoor-living Moroccan students)", "dose_or_intake": "not studied", "duration": "not studied", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No RCT tested academic performance, exam outcomes or learning in students, and none tested deficient young adults. Extrapolation from the replete young-adult null trial or from older-adult trials is not justified.", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "No direct evidence. The only basis is observational associations in older adults and mechanism.", "key_study_ids": ["dean2011", "zouine2025"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "alertness", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Any adult population, single dose", "dose_or_intake": "not studied", "duration": "single dose", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No acute cognitive trial found. 25(OH)D changes over weeks and VDR effects are genomic, so an acute alertness or attention effect is mechanistically implausible.", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "No data. Any acute 'focus' claim would be unsupported.", "key_study_ids": []}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "deficient_or_low_baseline", "population_detail": "Adults with low or insufficient 25(OH)D: Norwegian mid-aged adults (mean age 52, mean 34 nmol/L); UK adults aged 50 and over with mild-moderate deficiency and early cognitive impairment; Canadian adults below 75 nmol/L; US overweight postmenopausal women below 30 ng/mL", "dose_or_intake": "about 2000-4000 IU/day equivalents (Jorde: 100,000 IU bolus then 20,000 IU/week; Pettersen 4000 vs 400 IU/day; Castle 600/2000/4000 IU/day)", "duration": "4 months to 24 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "The two largest trials were null: Jorde (n = 374, 4 months) and VitaMIND (n = 620, 24 months, Trail Making B). Small trials gave domain-specific signals: visual memory improved in 23 insufficient participants on 4000 IU (d about 0.6); 2000 IU beat 600 or 4000 IU on learning and memory, but 4000 IU slowed reaction time. A meta-analysis subgroup with baseline deficiency showed Hedges g 0.48.", "effect_estimate": "VitaMIND TMT-B between-group difference not significant (95% CI includes 0) [corbett2025]; Jorde no between-group differences [jorde2019]; Pettersen PRM insufficient subgroup p = 0.025, d = 0.6 [pettersen2017]; pooled deficient subgroup g = 0.480 [chen2024]", "certainty_notes": "Downgraded for inconsistency (large null vs small positive), imprecision and possible selective reporting in small multi-test trials. The meta-analytic subgroup includes the Tianjin trials. Castle's reaction-time slowing at 4000 IU is a possible harm signal.", "key_study_ids": ["corbett2025", "jorde2019", "pettersen2017", "castle2020", "chen2024"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_decline", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Community-dwelling adults aged 60 and over, mostly vitamin D replete (VITAL mean baseline about 32 ng/mL), US and Australia; older African-American women; older women in the WHI", "dose_or_intake": "400 IU/day (with calcium) to 2000 IU/day or 60,000 IU/month", "duration": "2 to 7.8 years", "grade": "A", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Consistent null results across large, well-conducted RCTs: VITAL (n = 4218), D-Health (about 3,600-3,900 tested), WHI (n = 4143), Owusu (n = 260) and Zajac (n = 436, including UV-mushroom D2). VITAL showed no interaction with baseline 25(OH)D. A possible benefit in Black participants (n about 740) is a post hoc subgroup.", "effect_estimate": "VITAL pooled annual decline difference 0.01 SD (95% CI -0.01 to 0.02) [kang2021]; D-Health TICS difference 0.04 (-0.14 to 0.23), impairment OR 1.00 (0.75-1.33) [pham2023]; WHI MCI HR 0.95 (0.72-1.25) [rossom2012]", "certainty_notes": "High certainty that population supplementation of mostly replete older adults does not slow cognitive decline. Not directly applicable to young adults or to deficient people. The 2026 GRADE meta-analysis reporting benefit [behrouzi2026] gives implausibly large domain SMDs (1.29-1.58) from few trials and does not change this row.", "key_study_ids": ["kang2021", "pham2023", "rossom2012", "owusu2019", "zajac2020", "beauchet2021"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "dementia_risk", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Women aged 65 and over in the WHI Memory Study; D-Health participants aged 70 and over (cognitive impairment by TICS)", "dose_or_intake": "400 IU D3 plus 1000 mg calcium/day; 60,000 IU/month", "duration": "5-7.8 years", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "No reduction in incident dementia in WHI (39 vs 37 cases) or in TICS-defined impairment in D-Health. Cohorts and MR suggest a deficiency-dementia link, but no adequately powered RCT has tested dementia incidence in deficient people.", "effect_estimate": "Dementia HR 1.11 (0.71-1.74) [rossom2012]; impairment OR 1.00 (0.75-1.33) [pham2023]", "certainty_notes": "Downgraded for imprecision (few events), low dose in WHI, and indirectness (TICS cut-off rather than clinical dementia).", "key_study_ids": ["rossom2012", "pham2023"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "dementia_risk", "population": "deficient_or_low_baseline", "population_detail": "Middle-aged and older adults with 25(OH)D below 25-50 nmol/L (cohorts, UK Biobank, MR)", "dose_or_intake": "status, not intervention", "duration": "cohort follow-up 5-14 years", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Deficiency is associated with higher dementia risk in cohort meta-analyses. Nonlinear MR in UK Biobank supports a threshold effect below about 50 nmol/L, while linear MR of cognitive performance was null and MR of Alzheimer disease was positive. This supports correcting deficiency, not supplementing replete people.", "effect_estimate": "Deficiency vs sufficiency RR 1.42 (1.21-1.65) for dementia [zhang2024]; 25(OH)D below 25 vs 50-75.9 nmol/L HR 1.79 (1.57-2.04); MR-predicted risk 54% higher at 25 vs 50 nmol/L (1.21-1.96) [navale2022]; AD OR 1.25 (1.03-1.51) per SD lower log-25(OH)D [mokry2016]; no MR effect on cognition [maddock2017]", "certainty_notes": "Observational evidence starts low and is downgraded for residual confounding and reverse causation. The nonlinear MR method used by Navale has been questioned methodologically, and the authors' 2025 reanalysis was not read. No RCT confirmation.", "key_study_ids": ["zhang2024", "navale2022", "mokry2016", "maddock2017", "goodwill2017"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_mci", "population_detail": "Adults aged 65 and over with mild cognitive impairment (Tianjin, China; Canada)", "dose_or_intake": "800 IU/day (Tianjin); 10,000 IU three times weekly (SYNERGIC)", "duration": "20 weeks to 12 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "One Tianjin trial (n = 183) reported a full-scale IQ gain of about 5 points. The SYNERGIC factorial trial (n = 175) found no vitamin D effect on ADAS-Cog-13; the benefits there came from exercise and cognitive training.", "effect_estimate": "FSIQ estimate 5.132, p < 0.001 [yang2020]; vitamin D vs placebo not significant on ADAS-Cog-13 [monteroodasso2023]", "certainty_notes": "Inconsistent. The positive trial is single-centre, from a group with several similar large-effect trials, with outcomes on many WAIS subtests and unusually large effects. Not applicable to healthy or young adults.", "key_study_ids": ["yang2020", "monteroodasso2023", "behrouzi2026"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Older adults with Alzheimer disease (Tianjin, China)", "dose_or_intake": "800 IU/day", "duration": "12 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "A single trial (n = 210) reported improved WAIS subtests and full-scale IQ, plus lower plasma amyloid-related biomarkers.", "effect_estimate": "Full-scale IQ increase p < 0.001; magnitude not reported in the abstract [jia2019]", "certainty_notes": "Single-centre, unreplicated, effect size unclear, biomarker outcomes are surrogates. Patient data must not be generalised to healthy people.", "key_study_ids": ["jia2019"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "US adults aged 50 and over without clinically relevant depression at baseline (VITAL-DEP)", "dose_or_intake": "2000 IU D3/day", "duration": "median 5.3 years", "grade": "A", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "No reduction in incident or recurrent depression and no change in PHQ-8 mood scores.", "effect_estimate": "HR 0.97 (0.87-1.09); PHQ-8 change difference 0.01 (-0.04 to 0.05) [okereke2020]", "certainty_notes": "Very large, long, low-risk-of-bias RCT. Indirect for young adults and for deficient people.", "key_study_ids": ["okereke2020"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "any single dose", "onset": "not applicable", "duration_studied": "none", "domain": "alertness / attention / any cognitive domain", "population": "any", "effect_estimate": "no acute studies found", "certainty": "no evidence; mechanism (weeks-long change in 25(OH)D, genomic VDR action) makes an acute effect implausible", "safety": "single boluses of 100,000-500,000 IU are used clinically, but annual 500,000 IU raised falls and fractures [sanders2010]", "study_ids": ["sanders2010"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "6 weeks", "population": "healthy young adults 18-30, replete", "outcome": "working memory, inhibition, flexibility, mood", "effect_estimate": "all null (p 0.23-0.51; d 0.12-0.21)", "evidence_quality": "single RCT, low risk of bias", "limitations": "replete sample; short; dose above EU UL; n = 128", "study_ids": ["dean2011"]}, {"duration": "4 months", "population": "mid-aged adults (mean 52 y), mean 25(OH)D 34 nmol/L", "outcome": "verbal recall, coding, tapping", "effect_estimate": "no between-group differences, including the low-baseline subgroup", "evidence_quality": "moderate-size RCT (n = 374 analysed)", "limitations": "short; limited test battery", "study_ids": ["jorde2019"]}, {"duration": "18 weeks", "population": "healthy Canadian adults, 25(OH)D up to 100 nmol/L", "outcome": "visual memory (CANTAB PRM, PAL), other domains", "effect_estimate": "PRM improvement in the 4000 IU group; insufficient subgroup d about 0.6; other domains null", "evidence_quality": "small RCT (n = 82), active low-dose comparator", "limitations": "many outcomes, subgroup finding, single author, unreplicated", "study_ids": ["pettersen2017"]}, {"duration": "12 months", "population": "overweight/obese postmenopausal women, 25(OH)D below 30 ng/mL", "outcome": "domain-specific cognition (post-treatment only)", "effect_estimate": "2000 IU better learning/memory than 600 or 4000 IU; 4000 IU slower reaction time than 600 IU", "evidence_quality": "small dose-comparison RCT", "limitations": "no placebo; cognition measured only after treatment; non-monotonic dose response", "study_ids": ["castle2020"]}, {"duration": "24 months", "population": "UK adults aged 50 and over, mild-moderate deficiency, early cognitive impairment", "outcome": "executive function (TMT-B), cognition, function, well-being", "effect_estimate": "no significant benefit on any outcome", "evidence_quality": "large remote RCT (n = 620)", "limitations": "remote testing; dose not extracted from abstract", "study_ids": ["corbett2025"]}, {"duration": "2-3 years", "population": "US adults aged 60 and over, mostly replete", "outcome": "global cognitive decline", "effect_estimate": "annual decline difference 0.01 SD (-0.01 to 0.02)", "evidence_quality": "high (large factorial RCT)", "limitations": "replete; telephone tests in the larger substudy; Black-participant benefit is post hoc", "study_ids": ["kang2021"]}, {"duration": "5 years", "population": "Australians aged 70 and over", "outcome": "TICS score, cognitive impairment", "effect_estimate": "difference 0.04 (-0.14 to 0.23); OR 1.00 (0.75-1.33)", "evidence_quality": "high (very large RCT)", "limitations": "monthly bolus; telephone screen only; largely replete population", "study_ids": ["pham2023"]}, {"duration": "7.8 years", "population": "women aged 65 and over (WHI)", "outcome": "incident dementia and MCI", "effect_estimate": "dementia HR 1.11 (0.71-1.74); MCI HR 0.95 (0.72-1.25)", "evidence_quality": "moderate (post hoc analysis of a large RCT)", "limitations": "low dose; co-administered calcium; few events", "study_ids": ["rossom2012"]}, {"duration": "3 years", "population": "African-American women aged 65 and over", "outcome": "MMSE trajectory", "effect_estimate": "no difference despite 25(OH)D of 46.8 vs 20.7 ng/mL", "evidence_quality": "moderate (n = 260)", "limitations": "MMSE insensitive; ceiling effects", "study_ids": ["owusu2019"]}, {"duration": "12 months", "population": "Chinese adults aged 65 and over with MCI", "outcome": "WAIS full-scale IQ and subtests", "effect_estimate": "FSIQ +5.1 vs placebo, p < 0.001", "evidence_quality": "low-moderate (single centre)", "limitations": "unreplicated outside one group; implausibly large effect for 800 IU", "study_ids": ["yang2020"]}, {"duration": "5.3 years (median)", "population": "US adults aged 50 and over", "outcome": "depression risk, PHQ-8 mood", "effect_estimate": "HR 0.97 (0.87-1.09)", "evidence_quality": "high", "limitations": "older, mostly replete; not a cognitive outcome", "study_ids": ["okereke2020"]}, {"duration": "3 years", "population": "healthy adults aged 55-70", "outcome": "bone density (safety); calcium-related adverse events", "effect_estimate": "radial vBMD lower by 3.9 (4000 IU) and 7.5 (10,000 IU) mg HA/cm3 vs 400 IU; mild hypercalcaemia 0%/3%/9%", "evidence_quality": "high (RCT)", "limitations": "single centre; clinical relevance of the vBMD loss uncertain", "study_ids": ["burt2019", "billington2020"]}], "safety": {"summary": "At intakes up to the reference values (EU AI 15 µg/day adults; US RDA 15 µg, 600 IU) vitamin D is very safe. Risks rise with chronic high intakes and with large intermittent boluses. EFSA's adult UL is 100 µg (4000 IU)/day, based on hypercalciuria with a LOAEL of 250 µg/day [efsa2023]. Long-term supplementation modestly raises hypercalcaemia (RR 1.54) and hypercalciuria (RR 1.64) without a clear rise in kidney stones [malihi2016]. 10,000 IU/day caused mild hypercalcaemia in 9% and hypercalciuria in 31% [billington2020] and lowered radial bone density [burt2019]. Annual 500,000 IU raised falls and fractures [sanders2010]. Intoxication case reports involve misuse of high-dose or mislabelled supplements. Food-level fortification carries negligible risk if total intake is planned.", "adverse_effects": ["hypercalcaemia (nausea, vomiting, polyuria, confusion, arrhythmia) with intoxication; usually from chronic intakes far above the UL or manufacturing/dosing errors", "hypercalciuria and possible kidney stones (stone risk increased with calcium plus vitamin D in the WHI; not with vitamin D alone in meta-analysis [malihi2016])", "increased falls and fractures with very large annual or monthly boluses [sanders2010]", "lower volumetric BMD at 4000-10,000 IU/day for 3 years [burt2019]", "possible slower reaction time at 4000 IU vs 600 IU in one small trial [castle2020]"], "contraindications": ["hypercalcaemia or hypercalciuria of any cause", "granulomatous diseases (sarcoidosis, tuberculosis) and some lymphomas with extrarenal calcitriol production", "primary hyperparathyroidism", "history of calcium-containing kidney stones (medical supervision)", "advanced chronic kidney disease (needs nephrology-managed vitamin D forms)", "idiopathic infantile hypercalcaemia (CYP24A1 mutations)"], "drug_interactions": ["thiazide diuretics: reduce urinary calcium excretion and raise hypercalcaemia risk with high-dose vitamin D (standard pharmacology, not reviewed here)", "digoxin: hypercalcaemia increases arrhythmia risk", "orlistat, bile-acid sequestrants and mineral oil: reduce absorption of fat-soluble vitamins", "anticonvulsants (phenytoin, phenobarbital, carbamazepine), rifampicin and glucocorticoids: increase vitamin D catabolism or reduce its effect", "concurrent calcium supplements: additive hypercalciuria/stone risk (WHI)", "interaction details are standard pharmacology references; the NIH ODS fact sheet could not be fetched (HTTP 403)"], "upper_limits": "EU (EFSA 2023): 100 µg (4000 IU)/day for adults including pregnancy and lactation and adolescents 11-17; 50 µg/day for children 1-10; infant ULs set in 2018 [efsa2023]. US (IOM 2011, as reported by NIH ODS): 100 µg (4000 IU)/day for ages 9 and over; not re-fetched here. Morocco: no official UL text found; industry reports a 10 µg/day cap for supplements (unverified, X6 r26). Several trial doses used for cognition (5000 IU in [dean2011]; 10,000 IU three times weekly in [monteroodasso2023]) exceed or approach the UL.", "sleep_impact": "No consistent effect on sleep is established. One small Iranian trial in adolescent girls reported improved sleepiness (not reviewed here). No sleep disruption is reported at nutritional doses.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Yes: 2000 IU/day for about 5 years (VITAL, n about 26,000 overall); 60,000 IU/month for 5 years (D-Health); 400 IU plus calcium for 7 years (WHI); 4000-10,000 IU/day for 3 years (Calgary). Safety at DRI-level intakes is well established.", "daily_use_appropriate": "yes", "special_populations": "Pregnancy: UL 100 µg/day; supplementation at reference doses is recommended by many authorities, and high-dose regimens need obstetric supervision. Children: lower UL (50 µg/day ages 1-10), and fortified foods eaten by children must be planned against it. Older adults: avoid boluses. People with obesity or dark skin, and veiled women, have higher needs and lower status but should not self-dose above the UL without testing. Kidney, parathyroid and granulomatous disease need medical supervision. Medication users: see interactions.", "specialist_review_needed": ["endocrinologist / clinical nutritionist: dose setting relative to Moroccan, EU and US reference values and cumulative intake from fortified foods", "nephrology input for any product combining vitamin D with calcium", "food technologist: overage and stability so labelled content is not exceeded at production or under-delivered at end of shelf life", "regulatory lawyer (Morocco): status of Law 27-26 pharmacy reservation and any supplement dose caps"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "Pass for food fortification or a supplement providing about 5-25 µg/day (100-375% of the EU NRV, well under the 100 µg UL), with daily rather than bolus dosing and warnings for people taking other vitamin D products. Fail for high-dose 'brain' products in the 4000-5000 IU range (at or above the UL, with bone and calcium signals and no cognitive benefit) and for weekly or monthly megadoses."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Vitamin D (cholecalciferol, ergocalciferol) is a permitted vitamin for food supplements (Directive 2002/46/EC) and for fortification (Regulation 1925/2006). NRV is 5 µg; 'source of' requires 15% NRV per 100 g/100 ml. Seven Article 13.1 claims are authorised in Regulation 432/2012 (verified on legislation.gov.uk, the retained UK copy of the annex, accessed 2026-10-25; the EU version on EUR-Lex was not separately checked): normal absorption/utilisation of calcium and phosphorus; normal blood calcium levels; maintenance of normal bones; maintenance of normal muscle function; maintenance of normal teeth; normal function of the immune system; role in the process of cell division. Each applies to foods that are at least a source of vitamin D. No claim concerns cognitive, psychological or nervous-system function, so any brain/cognition claim would be unauthorised. Article 14 claims (children's bone development; reduction of fall risk in people aged 60 and over) are believed to exist, but their wording was not verified here. EFSA UL 100 µg/day adults [efsa2023]. Maximum levels in supplements and fortified foods are still set nationally.", "us": "Vitamin D is a dietary ingredient under DSHEA (no NDI needed for D2/D3). As a food additive, 21 CFR 172.380 permits vitamin D3 in specified foods at maximum levels (e.g. 84 IU/100 g milk, 178 IU/100 g yogurt, 560 IU/100 g breakfast cereals, 400 IU/100 g grain bars, 81 IU/30 g cheese; verified on law.cornell.edu, accessed 2026-10-25). Fortifying other food categories needs a separate authorisation or GRAS basis. An authorised health claim (21 CFR 101.72) allows 'Adequate calcium and vitamin D throughout life, as part of a well-balanced diet, may reduce the risk of osteoporosis' for foods high in vitamin D (verified). Vitamin D is mandatory on the Nutrition Facts label (DV 20 µg; not re-verified here). A cognitive structure/function claim would need FTC 'competent and reliable scientific evidence', which does not exist for healthy adults. Dementia-prevention claims are disease claims and prohibited.", "morocco": "Per the X6 Morocco regulatory synthesis: food claims that prevent, treat or cure disease are prohibited (Decree 2-12-389 Art. 7). Health claims are defined in law, but no positive list or approval procedure was found. Nutrition claims follow an EU-like positive list (Order 281-16). Food supplements have no specific law: registration was historically via a Ministry of Health (DMP) committee, now AMMPS (2025). Law 27-26 (adopted June 2026) lets a pending decree reserve listed risky supplements to pharmacies, and press reports name vitamin D; the decree was postponed in July 2026 and is unverified. Industry reports a 10 µg vitamin D cap for supplements (no official text found). Table oil enrichment with vitamins A and D3 exists under a voluntary framework agreement (3 IU/g D3 per a search snippet; legal instrument not found). No national vitamin D status survey was found. Uncertain items need a Moroccan lawyer.", "permitted_claims_examples": ["EU (Reg. 432/2012): 'Vitamin D contributes to the maintenance of normal bones' (food at least a source of vitamin D)", "EU (Reg. 432/2012): 'Vitamin D contributes to the maintenance of normal muscle function'", "EU (Reg. 432/2012): 'Vitamin D contributes to the normal function of the immune system'", "EU (Reg. 432/2012): 'Vitamin D contributes to normal absorption/utilisation of calcium and phosphorus'", "US (21 CFR 101.72): 'Adequate calcium and vitamin D throughout life, as part of a well-balanced diet, may reduce the risk of osteoporosis' (food meeting 'high' in vitamin D and calcium criteria)"], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "D2 and D3 are not novel. Calcidiol monohydrate is an EU novel food. EFSA found it safe up to 10 µg/day for adults and children 11 and over, and 5 µg/day for ages 3-10, with conversion factor 2.5 (EFSA 2024 technical report, PMID 38273990, abstract read; the Commission authorisation instrument was not verified). UV-treated mushrooms, mushroom powders and UV-treated baker's yeast as vitamin D2 sources are EU novel foods; individual authorisations were not verified here. Vegan D3 from lichen and other sources should be checked for novel-food status by source. In the US, no NDI is needed for D2/D3; new food categories need a food-additive or GRAS route.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Excellent technically. Required amounts are micrograms (5-15 µg), easily dispersed in fat-containing foods via oil premix or in water-dispersible beadlets, so a serving can carry 15-100% NRV without any taste. Fortification of dairy, plant drinks, cereals, oils and spreads is long-established. Suitable as a fortificant in a Brain Food product, provided it is positioned on bone, muscle and immune claims, not cognition.", "formats": ["fortified dairy (milk, yogurt, laban) or plant drinks", "fortified breakfast cereals, bars, bread/msemen flour premixes", "fortified table oil or spreads (existing Moroccan voluntary oil enrichment)", "UV-treated mushroom ingredients (D2)", "oily-fish-based products (sardine spreads) as a natural source", "capsule/softgel supplement (D3 in oil) at 10-25 µg"], "stability": "Vitamin D3 is sensitive to light, oxygen, heat and acidic or metal-catalysed oxidation. Encapsulated or beadlet forms with antioxidants (tocopherol) improve shelf life; overages are usual. Losses during baking or frying should be validated per product. Detailed stability data were not researched here.", "taste": "Neutral at fortification levels.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Fat-soluble: absorption is better with a fat-containing meal or matrix. D3 maintained 25(OH)D better than UV-mushroom D2 in one RCT [zajac2020]. Calcidiol is about 2.5 times as potent as D3 for labelling [efsa2023]. In Moroccan women, higher dietary intake moved 25(OH)D only by about 3 ng/mL [zouine2025], so food alone at typical fortification levels may not correct severe deficiency; sun exposure and clinical treatment remain important.", "dose_feasibility": "Feasible for nutritional doses: 5-15 µg per serving is trivial to add. The trial doses tested for cognition (2000 IU = 50 µg daily in VITAL; 5000 IU = 125 µg in Dean) are far above normal fortification levels, above the reported Moroccan supplement cap (10 µg), and in Dean's case above the EU UL. They are not appropriate for a food eaten in variable amounts, and in any case gave no cognitive benefit.", "cost_availability": "Widely available from multiple global suppliers as D3 oil, powder/beadlets or vegan D3. Very low cost per serving at microgram doses is generally assumed (not quantified here).", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 3.5, "effect_magnitude": 1, "safety": 7.5, "reproducibility": 3, "food_suitability": 8.5, "cost_access": 8, "rationale": "Evidence quality 3.5: in the target population (young adults, students) the evidence is one null RCT in replete people (C) and nothing in deficient students (D). Certainty is high (A) only for the absence of benefit in replete older adults, which does not justify a higher score for a cognition product. Effect magnitude 1: large trials show effects near zero (VITAL 0.01 SD/year; D-Health 0.04 TICS points; Dean d 0.12-0.21 NS). Positive effects come only from small or single-centre trials in deficient or impaired people. Safety 7.5: very safe at DRI-level doses with a clear UL, but documented harms at high daily doses and boluses, and vulnerable groups (kidney, granulomatous disease, thiazides) need warnings. Reproducibility 3: null results replicate; positive cognitive findings do not replicate outside single groups. Food suitability 8.5: microgram doses, neutral taste, established fortification practice; minus points for light/oxygen sensitivity and fat-matrix dependence. Cost/access 8: widely available commodity, but prices were not researched here."}, "research_gaps": ["No RCT of vitamin D correction on cognition, attention or academic performance in deficient young adults or university students (for example veiled or indoor-living Moroccan women students)", "No nationally representative Moroccan 25(OH)D survey with standardised (VDSP-traceable) assays", "No trial testing a vitamin D fortified staple (bread, oil, dairy) on functional outcomes in North Africa", "Replication of the Tianjin MCI/AD findings by independent groups, with preregistered outcomes", "Threshold effects: trials restricted to 25(OH)D below 25-30 nmol/L, with sufficient power, are lacking", "Methodologically robust nonlinear MR (doubly-ranked method) results for dementia need independent appraisal; the 2025 reanalysis was not accessible here", "Stability and losses of vitamin D3 in Moroccan cooking and baking practices"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 4.7, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": null, "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"dementia_risk": "C", "global_cognition": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 11, "n_studies": 33, "n_verified": 33, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 13, "n_systematic_reviews": 5, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["inconsistent", "insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "vitamin-e", "name": "Vitamin E", "category": "B", "category_name": "Vitamins", "aliases": ["vitamin E", "alpha-tocopherol", "d-alpha-tocopherol", "DL-alpha-tocopheryl acetate", "mixed tocopherols", "tocotrienols"], "food_sources": ["wheat germ oil", "sunflower and other vegetable oils", "almonds, hazelnuts, other nuts", "sunflower seeds", "spinach and other leafy greens (smaller amounts)", "avocado"], "active_constituents": ["alpha-tocopherol (the biologically dominant and regulated form in humans); other tocopherols/tocotrienols (beta, gamma, delta) have lower bioactivity by the RRR-alpha-tocopherol standard"], "one_line_verdict": "Vitamin E is an essential fat-soluble antioxidant with no demonstrated cognitive-enhancement benefit in healthy adults of any age, mixed and generally disappointing results even in Alzheimer's disease and MCI, and a genuine, dose-dependent safety concern (increased all-cause mortality and bleeding risk) above about 400 IU/day that rules out high-dose supplementation as a brain-health strategy.", "scientific_conclusion": "Vitamin E is an essential fat-soluble vitamin whose primary established human role is as a lipid-membrane antioxidant preventing polyunsaturated fatty acid peroxidation; true dietary deficiency is rare and mainly occurs with fat-malabsorption disorders, where it causes a progressive neurological syndrome (ataxia, peripheral neuropathy) that is a deficiency-correction scenario, not a model for supplementation benefits in replete people. In healthy, cognitively normal older adults, the largest and most relevant RCT, the Women's Health Study (n=6377 women >=65y studied in a cognitive substudy, 600 IU every other day for up to 9.6 years), found no difference in global cognitive score, verbal memory, or category fluency between vitamin E and placebo at any assessment (mean difference in change 0.02, 95% CI -0.01 to 0.05, p=0.16) [kang2006]. The Women's Antioxidant and Cardiovascular Study similarly found no slowing of cognitive decline with vitamin E 402 mg every other day over 5.4 years in older women with cardiovascular risk (mean difference -0.01, 95% CI -0.05 to 0.04, p=0.78) [kang2009]. The Physicians' Health Study II tested a full multivitamin (not isolated vitamin E) over 12 years in 5947 older male physicians and found no cognitive benefit, though this cannot isolate a vitamin-E-specific effect [grodstein2013]. PREADViSE (n=7540 cognitively normal men >=60y, part of the SELECT trial) tested vitamin E (400 IU/day), selenium, their combination, or placebo for dementia prevention and found no reduction in dementia incidence for any arm (HR 0.88, 95% CI 0.64-1.20 for vitamin E alone) [kryscio2017]. These three large, well-conducted RCTs in cognitively healthy older adults converge on a clear null result for primary prevention of cognitive decline or dementia. In patients who already have Alzheimer's disease or mild cognitive impairment -- populations that must not be generalised to healthy young adults -- the Cochrane review (2017, Farina et al.) found no evidence that vitamin E (alpha-tocopherol, various doses) improves cognitive function in AD or MCI, or prevents progression from MCI to AD (RR 1.03, 95% CI 0.79-1.35, 1 study, n=516), though there was moderate-quality evidence from a single trial (TEAM-AD) of a slower rate of functional (activities-of-daily-living) decline in mild-to-moderate AD at 2000 IU/day (MD 3.15, 95% CI 0.07-6.23, p=0.04, delay of about 19%/year or ~6.2 months) [farina2017, dysken2014]. This functional-decline finding is specific to a clinical AD population on cholinesterase inhibitors and is not a cognition claim nor transferable to healthy adults or to dementia prevention. A later Cochrane review of vitamin/mineral supplementation in MCI similarly found vitamin E (1000 IU twice daily, n=516) probably had no effect on progression to AD dementia over 3 years [mccleery2018]. No RCT identified in any population tests vitamin E for cognitive outcomes in healthy young adults or students; the entire human RCT evidence base addresses older adults (healthy or cognitively impaired). Safety is a decisive factor: Miller et al.'s 2005 dose-response meta-analysis of 19 trials (135,967 participants, doses 16.5-2000 IU/day) found that trials using >=400 IU/day showed a pooled increase in all-cause mortality (risk difference 39 per 10,000 persons, 95% CI 3-74, p=0.035), with risk rising above roughly 150 IU/day; low-dose trials showed no such signal [miller2005]. This meta-analysis has been debated (heterogeneous populations, many trials in people with chronic disease), but subsequent regulatory reviews (EFSA 2024 opinion on the tolerable upper intake level) converged on bleeding/impaired clotting as the critical adverse effect, setting a much lower adult UL (300 mg/day all sources) than the US NIH figure (1000 mg/day supplemental). Given the consistent null efficacy signal for cognition in healthy adults and a genuine dose-dependent mortality/bleeding signal at doses materially above dietary levels, high-dose vitamin E supplementation cannot be justified as a brain-health strategy for Brain Food's target population; ordinary dietary vitamin E intake (nuts, seeds, vegetable oils) remains appropriate and safe.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "True vitamin E deficiency is uncommon in the general population and occurs almost exclusively with fat-malabsorption syndromes (cystic fibrosis, cholestatic liver disease, abetalipoproteinemia) or rare genetic disorders of tocopherol transport, producing a distinct neurological syndrome (ataxia, peripheral neuropathy, areflexia) that responds to vitamin E repletion -- this is deficiency correction in a clinical population, not evidence relevant to supplementing healthy, adequately nourished adults. In adequately nourished healthy adults and in cognitively normal older adults, three large RCTs (Women's Health Study, Women's Antioxidant and Cardiovascular Study, PREADViSE) uniformly show no enhancement or preventive cognitive benefit from vitamin E supplementation [kang2006, kang2009, kryscio2017]. The one positive signal (TEAM-AD) is in clinical Alzheimer's disease patients on functional, not cognitive, outcomes, and must not be generalised to prevention or to healthy populations [dysken2014].", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Marketing that frames vitamin E as a 'brain antioxidant' that protects memory or cognition in general populations is not supported: the largest, best-conducted RCTs in cognitively healthy older adults (Women's Health Study, PREADViSE, Women's Antioxidant and Cardiovascular Study) are consistently null for cognitive outcomes, and the one clinically meaningful positive finding (TEAM-AD, slower functional decline) is specific to patients already diagnosed with mild-to-moderate Alzheimer's disease on cholinesterase inhibitors, a population Brain Food's healthy-adult/student target market should not be conflated with. Compounding this, high-dose vitamin E supplementation (>=400 IU/day) carries a genuine, debated but credible, dose-dependent mortality and bleeding-risk signal, and EFSA's 2024 opinion set a notably conservative upper intake level (300 mg/day all sources) specifically because of impaired blood clotting. The honest position: dietary vitamin E from nuts, seeds and vegetable oils supports normal antioxidant and nervous-system physiology, and there is no credible evidence that supplementing beyond dietary adequacy improves cognition in healthy people, while there is a real safety reason to avoid high-dose supplementation.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Alpha-tocopherol is the principal fat-soluble, membrane-resident antioxidant protecting polyunsaturated fatty acids in neuronal and glial membranes from lipid peroxidation; it is selectively retained in the body via the hepatic alpha-tocopherol transfer protein (TTPA). The antioxidant hypothesis for dementia prevention (reactive-oxygen-species-mediated neurodegeneration) is biologically plausible and supported by in vitro and some animal models, but has not translated into a demonstrated reduction of dementia incidence or cognitive decline in large human prevention trials, illustrating why mechanism is not efficacy.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Dietary/supplemental vitamin E -> intestinal absorption with dietary fat, incorporation into chylomicrons -> liver uptake", "detail": "Absorption requires normal fat digestion/absorption; malabsorption syndromes cause clinical deficiency", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Hepatic alpha-tocopherol transfer protein (TTPA) selectively repackages RRR-alpha-tocopherol into VLDL -> circulation -> tissue/neuronal membrane incorporation", "detail": "TTPA selectivity explains why alpha-tocopherol dominates human tissue tocopherol despite dietary intake of mixed forms; established biochemistry", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Membrane-resident alpha-tocopherol scavenges lipid peroxyl radicals, protecting neuronal/glial membrane polyunsaturated fatty acids from oxidative damage", "detail": "Well-established antioxidant chemistry; basis of the oxidative-stress/dementia hypothesis", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Reduced oxidative damage -> hypothesised slower neurodegeneration / preserved synaptic function", "detail": "Plausible mechanistic chain but not demonstrated to translate into measurable prevention of cognitive decline in large RCTs (Women's Health Study, PREADViSE both null) [kang2006, kryscio2017]", "evidence_level": "plausible"}, {"step": "In established Alzheimer's disease, antioxidant supplementation -> measured human outcome", "detail": "TEAM-AD found slower functional (ADL) decline at 2000 IU/day but no cognitive benefit in mild-moderate AD patients; Cochrane review found no cognitive benefit in AD/MCI overall [dysken2014, farina2017]", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["lipid peroxidation inhibition (chain-breaking antioxidant)", "alpha-tocopherol transfer protein selective retention", "oxidative-stress hypothesis of neurodegeneration (largely unconfirmed in prevention trials)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Cognitively normal women aged >=65y (Women's Health Study cognitive substudy)", "dose_or_intake": "600 IU alpha-tocopherol acetate every other day", "duration": "up to 9.6 years", "grade": "A", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "No difference in global cognitive composite score, verbal memory, or category fluency between vitamin E and placebo at any of multiple assessments over up to 9.6 years; relative risk of substantial decline 0.92 (95% CI 0.77-1.10).", "effect_estimate": "mean difference in cognitive change 0.02 (95% CI -0.01 to 0.05, p=0.16); RR of substantial decline 0.92 (95% CI 0.77-1.10) [kang2006]", "certainty_notes": "Large (n=6377), long-duration, well-conducted RCT with repeated validated cognitive assessments; high certainty of a true null effect for primary prevention of cognitive decline in healthy older women.", "key_study_ids": ["kang2006"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "dementia_risk", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Cognitively asymptomatic men aged >=60y (PREADViSE/SELECT ancillary trial)", "dose_or_intake": "400 IU/day vitamin E (alone or with selenium)", "duration": "up to 7 years active treatment plus cohort follow-up to 2015", "grade": "A", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Dementia incidence (4.4% overall) did not differ across vitamin E, selenium, combination, or placebo arms; adjusted hazard ratio 0.88 (95% CI 0.64-1.20) for vitamin E vs placebo.", "effect_estimate": "HR 0.88 (95% CI 0.64-1.20) [kryscio2017]", "certainty_notes": "Large (n=7540), randomized, long-term trial with dementia as the primary clinical endpoint; high certainty that vitamin E does not prevent dementia in this population.", "key_study_ids": ["kryscio2017"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Patients with mild-to-moderate Alzheimer's disease on an acetylcholinesterase inhibitor (TEAM-AD trial) -- NOT generalisable to healthy adults", "dose_or_intake": "2000 IU/day alpha-tocopherol", "duration": "mean 2.27 years", "grade": "B", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "No cognitive benefit reported; functional decline (ADCS-ADL score) was 3.15 units less in the vitamin E group versus placebo (a ~19%/year slower decline, about 6.2 months delay), a functional not cognitive outcome; Cochrane review found no evidence of cognitive benefit across trials in AD/MCI.", "effect_estimate": "ADCS-ADL mean difference 3.15 (95% CI 0.92-5.39, adjusted p=0.03) for function; cognition itself not significantly improved [dysken2014, farina2017]", "certainty_notes": "Single large clinical trial for the functional outcome in a specific clinical AD population; must not be generalised to prevention or to cognition itself, or to healthy/young populations. Downgraded for indirectness to Brain Food's target market.", "key_study_ids": ["dysken2014", "farina2017"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_decline", "population": "older_adults_mci", "population_detail": "Mild cognitive impairment patients (Cochrane MCI supplementation review, 1 vitamin E trial n=516) -- NOT generalisable to healthy adults", "dose_or_intake": "1000 IU alpha-tocopherol twice daily (2000 IU/day)", "duration": "3 years", "grade": "B", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "No effect on probability of progression from MCI to Alzheimer's dementia (HR 1.02, 95% CI 0.74-1.41) and no significant effect on cognitive function, episodic memory, processing speed, or mortality.", "effect_estimate": "HR 1.02 (95% CI 0.74-1.41) [mccleery2018]", "certainty_notes": "Single trial, moderate-quality GRADE evidence per Cochrane; specific to an MCI clinical population, not transferable to healthy adults.", "key_study_ids": ["mccleery2018"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy young adults/students", "dose_or_intake": "not studied", "duration": "not applicable", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No RCT identified that tests vitamin E supplementation for cognitive outcomes in healthy young adults or students; the entire RCT evidence base addresses older adults (healthy or with MCI/AD).", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "Complete absence of direct evidence in the target population; cannot extrapolate null results in older adults, or the AD functional-decline finding, to young healthy adults.", "key_study_ids": []}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "not studied as an acute single-dose cognitive intervention", "onset": "not reported", "duration_studied": "not applicable", "domain": "other", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty": "no acute human cognitive trials identified", "safety": "single doses at supplement levels generally well tolerated", "study_ids": []}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "up to 9.6 years", "population": "healthy older women", "outcome": "global cognition, verbal memory, category fluency", "effect_estimate": "no benefit (mean diff 0.02, CI -0.01 to 0.05)", "evidence_quality": "A - large, long, well-conducted RCT", "limitations": "older women only; alternate-day dosing; not generalisable in direction of benefit to younger adults though null results are informative", "study_ids": ["kang2006"]}, {"duration": "up to 7 years plus cohort follow-up", "population": "cognitively normal older men", "outcome": "dementia incidence", "effect_estimate": "HR 0.88 (95% CI 0.64-1.20), no benefit", "evidence_quality": "A - large RCT with clinical dementia endpoint", "limitations": "older men only; dementia ascertainment partly by screening/physician follow-up, not universal gold-standard diagnosis", "study_ids": ["kryscio2017"]}, {"duration": "2.27 years mean", "population": "mild-to-moderate Alzheimer's disease patients", "outcome": "functional decline (ADCS-ADL), not cognition", "effect_estimate": "MD 3.15 (95% CI 0.92-5.39) slower decline", "evidence_quality": "B - single large RCT in clinical population", "limitations": "clinical AD population on cholinesterase inhibitors; functional not cognitive outcome; not generalisable to prevention or healthy adults", "study_ids": ["dysken2014"]}, {"duration": "19 clinical trials pooled, variable duration", "population": "mixed, many with chronic disease", "outcome": "all-cause mortality (safety, not cognition)", "effect_estimate": "risk difference +39/10,000 (95% CI 3-74) at doses >=400 IU/day", "evidence_quality": "B - meta-analysis, heterogeneous populations, debated", "limitations": "many trials in chronically ill populations; generalisability to healthy adults debated; dose-response relationship nonetheless informs the safety gate", "study_ids": ["miller2005"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Vitamin E at dietary/RDA levels (approximately 15 mg/day alpha-tocopherol for adults) is safe. Supplemental high-dose vitamin E (>=400 IU/day, i.e. well above the RDA) carries a credible, debated dose-dependent signal for increased all-cause mortality and, more robustly, an increased risk of bleeding/hemorrhagic stroke due to vitamin E's anti-platelet and anticoagulant-potentiating properties, which is why regulators have set conservative upper limits.", "adverse_effects": ["increased bleeding risk / impaired blood clotting at high doses, including hemorrhagic stroke risk", "possible increase in all-cause mortality at doses >=400 IU/day per Miller 2005 dose-response meta-analysis (debated but basis for regulatory caution)", "possible increased prostate cancer risk with long-term synthetic vitamin E supplementation at ~180 mg/day (SELECT trial finding, separate from PREADViSE dementia outcome)"], "contraindications": ["patients on anticoagulant or antiplatelet therapy (warfarin, DOACs, aspirin) without medical supervision", "vitamin K malabsorption syndromes", "scheduled surgery (bleeding risk) -- typically advised to stop high-dose vitamin E before procedures"], "drug_interactions": ["anticoagulants and antiplatelet agents (additive bleeding risk)", "chemotherapy agents where antioxidant interference is a theoretical concern (consult oncology specialist)"], "upper_limits": "US NIH Office of Dietary Supplements sets the adult UL at 1000 mg/day (1500 IU natural / 1100 IU synthetic) for supplemental alpha-tocopherol. EFSA's 2024 scientific opinion set a markedly more conservative adult UL of 300 mg/day from all dietary sources, citing impaired blood clotting/bleeding as the critical effect; EFSA's limit does not apply to people already on anticoagulant/antiplatelet medication (different risk-benefit calculus) or specific clinical contexts.", "sleep_impact": "No established significant effect of vitamin E on sleep architecture at typical doses.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Yes, extensively, in multiple large RCTs lasting 5-12 years (Women's Health Study, PREADViSE, Physicians' Health Study II, Women's Antioxidant and Cardiovascular Study), consistently showing no cognitive benefit and, in the Miller 2005 meta-analysis of high-dose trials, a mortality signal above ~150-400 IU/day.", "daily_use_appropriate": "probably", "special_populations": "Pregnant/lactating women: dietary-level vitamin E is safe; high-dose supplementation not recommended without medical advice. Patients on anticoagulant/antiplatelet medication: avoid high-dose vitamin E supplementation without physician guidance. Patients with upcoming surgery: discontinue high-dose vitamin E supplementation per standard perioperative guidance.", "specialist_review_needed": ["hematologist or treating physician for patients on anticoagulant/antiplatelet therapy considering any vitamin E supplementation above dietary levels", "cardiologist for patients with cardiovascular disease considering antioxidant supplementation", "oncologist for patients with or at risk of prostate cancer considering long-term high-dose supplementation"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "Dietary-level vitamin E (within RDA, roughly 15 mg/day) is safe and appropriate for food formulation. High-dose supplementation (>=400 IU/day, well above EFSA's 300 mg/day all-source UL) carries a credible dose-dependent bleeding/mortality signal and has shown zero cognitive benefit in any RCT, so any Brain Food product must stay at or near dietary/RDA levels and must not market cognitive benefits at supraphysiological doses; the gate is conditional on strict dose discipline, not a pass for arbitrary high-dose formulation."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Vitamin E is an authorised vitamin for food/supplement use (Directive 2002/46/EC). EFSA's 2024 scientific opinion established a tolerable upper intake level of 300 mg/day for adults from all dietary sources, a notably conservative figure driven by bleeding/clotting risk. No cognitive-function health claim for vitamin E is authorised on the EU register; authorised claims relate to antioxidant/cell protection from oxidative stress, not cognition.", "us": "Vitamin E is GRAS and widely used in fortified foods and supplements under DSHEA. No FDA-authorized or FTC-defensible cognitive-function claim exists for vitamin E in healthy adults given the consistent null RCT evidence; any such claim would lack competent and reliable scientific substantiation.", "morocco": "Vitamin E is an established, permitted vitamin ingredient for food fortification and supplements under ONSSA regulation; no cognitive-specific claim framework exists.", "permitted_claims_examples": [], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "None; vitamin E is a long-established vitamin, not a novel food or new dietary ingredient in any target market.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Good at dietary/RDA levels; vitamin E is a natural lipid-soluble component of nuts, seeds and vegetable oils and is straightforward to include in oil-based or lipid-containing food products at nutritionally appropriate doses; high-dose supplement-level inclusion (400+ IU) is not food-suitable given the safety profile.", "formats": ["natural food inclusion (nuts, seeds, oils)", "fortified oil-based products", "soft-gel capsules for supplement-format RDA-level dosing", "mixed tocopherol/tocotrienol blends used in some antioxidant formulations"], "stability": "Fat-soluble vitamin E is relatively heat-stable but sensitive to oxidation, light, and prolonged exposure to air; antioxidant blends and appropriate packaging mitigate degradation.", "taste": "Essentially neutral/mild in oil-based inclusion; easily incorporated without taste issues at RDA-range doses.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Requires co-ingestion with dietary fat for absorption via micelle formation and chylomicron transport; absorption efficiency is lower without adequate fat in the meal.", "dose_feasibility": "RDA-range doses (about 15 mg/day) fit easily within normal nut/seed/oil servings or modest fortification; the high doses tested in clinical trials (400-2000 IU/day) do not fit within a reasonable food serving and would require a dedicated, clearly-labelled supplement format if ever justified, which current evidence does not support for cognitive purposes.", "cost_availability": "Vitamin E (both natural d-alpha-tocopherol and synthetic DL-alpha-tocopherol) is widely available and inexpensive at RDA-relevant doses in all three target markets.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 1, "effect_magnitude": 1, "safety": 5, "reproducibility": 7, "food_suitability": 7, "cost_access": 8, "rationale": "evidence_quality=1 (grade A evidence exists but it is consistently and robustly A-grade NULL for cognitive prevention in healthy older adults and B-grade null/mixed in clinical AD/MCI populations; no positive cognitive signal anywhere, and no data at all in healthy young adults). effect_magnitude=1 (no demonstrated cognitive effect magnitude in any relevant population; the one positive clinical finding is a functional, not cognitive, outcome in AD patients). safety=5 (dietary-level use is safe, but the dose-dependent mortality/bleeding signal at supplement doses and the conservative EFSA UL mean the ingredient cannot score higher without strict dose caps). reproducibility=7 (the null result for cognition is highly reproducible across three large independent RCTs in different populations, which is scientifically informative even though the direction is 'no effect'). food_suitability=7 (naturally present in common healthy foods at appropriate doses, easy to formulate at RDA level, but not suitable at the high doses that would be needed to test any clinical signal). cost_access=8 (cheap, globally available at RDA-relevant quantities)."}, "research_gaps": ["No RCT has tested vitamin E (isolated, not multivitamin) for cognitive outcomes in healthy young adults or students; the entire human RCT evidence base is restricted to older adults, healthy or with MCI/AD.", "The functional-decline benefit in mild-to-moderate AD (TEAM-AD) has not been replicated in an independent large trial and does not extend to cognition itself.", "The Miller 2005 mortality meta-analysis pools heterogeneous, often chronically ill populations; a dedicated dose-response safety study in healthy adults at commonly marketed supplement doses (100-400 IU) would clarify the actual risk at realistic consumer doses.", "No trials have examined mixed tocopherol/tocotrienol forms (as opposed to isolated alpha-tocopherol) for cognitive outcomes in any population."], "_derived": {"composite_score": 3.7, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": null, "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": null, "best_grade_overall": null, "benefit_domains": {}, "n_outcome_rows": 5, "n_studies": 8, "n_verified": 8, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 5, "n_systematic_reviews": 2, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["inconsistent", "insufficient", "no_benefit"]}}, {"id": "vitamin-k", "name": "Vitamin K", "category": "B", "category_name": "Vitamins", "aliases": ["vitamin K", "vitamin K1", "phylloquinone", "phytonadione", "vitamin K2", "menaquinones", "MK-4 (menatetrenone)", "MK-7", "menadione (K3, synthetic; not permitted in supplements)"], "food_sources": ["Phylloquinone (K1): dark green leafy vegetables (kale, spinach, chard, parsley, purslane), broccoli, cabbage, lettuce, herbs, vegetable oils (soy, rapeseed, olive)", "Menaquinones (K2): natto (very high MK-7), hard and soft cheeses, curd, egg yolk, chicken, liver; MK-4 formed in tissues (including brain) from K1"], "active_constituents": ["Phylloquinone and menaquinones (MK-n): cofactors for gamma-glutamyl carboxylase, which activates vitamin K-dependent proteins (clotting factors, osteocalcin, matrix Gla protein, Gas6, protein S)", "MK-4 is the dominant form in human brain"], "one_line_verdict": "Vitamin K (as MK-4) is present in the human brain and higher intake or status is associated with better memory and lower dementia risk in older adults, but there are no randomised trials of vitamin K on cognition, the associations are confounded by vegetable-rich diets (and K2 intake even tracked higher dementia risk in the largest cohort), and supplements interfere with warfarin, so the evidence supports eating leafy greens, not a vitamin K 'brain' supplement.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. Vitamin K is the cofactor for gamma-carboxylation of proteins such as Gas6 and protein S, which act in the nervous system. It also participates in sphingolipid metabolism, a component of myelin [alisi2019]. In humans, MK-4 is the main form found in brain tissue. In 325 decedents, higher brain MK-4 was associated with 17-20% lower odds of dementia or MCI and with less tau pathology [booth2022]. All human cognitive evidence is observational. In healthy 70-85-year-olds, higher serum phylloquinone was associated with better verbal episodic memory but not with executive function or processing speed [presse2013]. Higher dietary phylloquinone was associated with higher MMSE in geriatric patients [chouet2015] and with better cognition in older Irish adults [kiely2020]. An increase in vitamin K intake during a Mediterranean-diet trial was associated with lower odds of MMSE <=24 (OR 0.53) [camachobarcia2022]. In the largest cohort (54,968 Danes, 25 years), K1 intake was associated with 14% lower dementia incidence, but K2 intake with 14% higher incidence [ghasemifard2026]. In middle-aged Dutch adults, vitamin K status did not predict cognitive decline [vandenheuvel2015]. Microbiome menaquinone links are exploratory [mccann2019]. Antagonist data are indirect and conflicting. Vitamin K antagonist use was associated with cognitive impairment in a small geriatric sample (OR 17.4, 95% CI 1.4-224) [annweiler2015]. In the larger Three-City cohort, VKA users had slightly worse baseline visual memory and fluency but no faster decline [ferland2016]. DOAC users have marginally lower dementia risk than VKA users (HR 0.89), with very low certainty [branco2023]. These comparisons are confounded by indication and by bleeding/INR effects. What is not demonstrated. We found no completed randomised trial of vitamin K1 or K2 on any cognitive outcome in any population. A small registered trial in coronary heart disease (NCT06855953) had not yet recruited. Earlier MK-4 trials in Alzheimer's patients by one author have been retracted [sato2005], so any claims based on them are invalid. In healthy young adults, students and knowledge workers there are no data at all. How to interpret. The pattern fits dietary confounding: vegetable-rich diets supply K1 together with folate, carotenoids, nitrate and polyphenols, while K2 sources (cheese, meat) track different dietary patterns [ghasemifard2026]. Mechanism is plausible, but mechanism is not efficacy. Grades: C (inconsistent) for observational associations in older adults; D (insufficient) for supplementation in any population, including young adults. Safety. Vitamin K has very low intrinsic toxicity and no UL [efsa2017]. However, as little as 10-45 ug/day of MK-7 destabilises VKA anticoagulation [theuwissen2013]. Supervised low-dose K1 can even improve INR stability, but only with clinic monitoring [rombouts2007].", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Clinical vitamin K deficiency in adults is rare. It occurs with malabsorption, prolonged antibiotics with poor intake, liver disease or VKA therapy, and presents as bleeding. Subclinical insufficiency of extrahepatic proteins (high dp-ucMGP) is common in older adults and in kidney disease, but did not predict cognitive decline in middle age [vandenheuvel2015]. The cognitive associations are strongest in older and geriatric populations with low vegetable intake [chouet2015, presse2013]. Whether repletion helps them has never been tested. In adequately nourished young adults there is no evidence and no plausible reason to expect enhancement from extra vitamin K. The best-supported action is dietary: regular leafy greens (which also supply folate, nitrate and carotenoids; see leafy-greens-and-vegetables monograph).", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Vitamin K2 (MK-7) is increasingly marketed for 'brain health', 'cognitive protection' or the 'heart-brain-bone axis', using mechanistic reviews and animal data. No human RCT supports a cognitive claim. The largest cohort found higher K2 intake associated with higher dementia risk [ghasemifard2026], and older Alzheimer's MK-4 trials are retracted [sato2005]. In the EU the only authorised claims are 'Vitamin K contributes to normal blood clotting' and 'Vitamin K contributes to the maintenance of normal bones' (Reg. 432/2012). A brain or memory claim would be unauthorised. Any vitamin K-containing Brain Food product needs an anticoagulant warning [theuwissen2013]. Honest position: 'leafy greens provide vitamin K, which is associated with healthy cognitive ageing in observational studies; supplements have not been tested'.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Vitamin K is a cofactor for gamma-glutamyl carboxylase, which activates Gla proteins. In the brain these include Gas6 and protein S (TAM-receptor signalling: cell survival, anti-inflammation, myelination) and possibly matrix Gla protein in cerebral vessels (calcification). Vitamin K also influences sphingolipid (sulfatide) synthesis in myelin. Dietary K1 is converted to MK-4, which is concentrated in brain. Animal studies show that low vitamin K intake impairs cognition and neurogenesis, while MK-7 improves outcomes in ageing rodents. Human evidence is limited to brain-tissue concentrations and associations. Mechanism is not efficacy.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Dietary K1/K2 -> absorption with fat (chylomicrons) -> liver (clotting factors) and extrahepatic tissues; K1 converted to MK-4 via UBIAD1", "detail": "Well characterised; MK-4 is the main form in human brain [booth2022].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "MK-4 in brain -> carboxylation of Gas6 and protein S -> TAM receptor signalling (neuronal survival, anti-inflammatory, myelination)", "detail": "In-vitro and animal data summarised in reviews [alisi2019].", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Vitamin K -> sphingolipid/sulfatide synthesis in myelin", "detail": "Rodent data [alisi2019].", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Matrix Gla protein carboxylation -> reduced vascular calcification -> cerebrovascular health", "detail": "Plausible; dp-ucMGP did not predict cognitive decline in middle-aged adults [vandenheuvel2015].", "evidence_level": "plausible"}, {"step": "Measured human cognitive outcome", "detail": "Only observational associations (memory, MMSE, dementia) in older adults, inconsistent for K2 [presse2013, ghasemifard2026]; no RCTs.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["gamma-glutamyl carboxylation (Gla proteins)", "Gas6/protein S - TAM receptor signalling", "sphingolipid/sulfatide metabolism", "matrix Gla protein and vascular calcification", "gut-microbial menaquinone production (exploratory)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Cognitively intact adults 70-85 y (observational, serum K1)", "dose_or_intake": "habitual intake / serum phylloquinone", "duration": "cross-sectional", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Higher serum phylloquinone associated with better verbal episodic recall; no association with other domains.", "effect_estimate": "beta 0.41-0.47 SD per log unit for recall trials [presse2013]", "certainty_notes": "Single cross-sectional study; diet-quality confounding; observational start, not upgraded.", "key_study_ids": ["presse2013"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Older community adults and geriatric patients (observational)", "dose_or_intake": "dietary phylloquinone / change in vitamin K intake", "duration": "cross-sectional to 2 y", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Several cross-sectional and one 2-y analysis show associations with better MMSE or fluency; a 6-y Dutch cohort found no association with decline.", "effect_estimate": "MMSE 22.0 vs 19.9 [chouet2015]; OR 0.53 (0.35-0.79) for MMSE <=24 [camachobarcia2022]; null [vandenheuvel2015]", "certainty_notes": "Observational, confounded by vegetable intake and frailty; inconsistent; no dose-response trial.", "key_study_ids": ["chouet2015", "kiely2020", "camachobarcia2022", "vandenheuvel2015"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "dementia_risk", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Adults 50-65 at baseline followed 25 y (Denmark); decedents with brain MK-4 measured (US)", "dose_or_intake": "dietary K1 and K2; brain MK-4", "duration": "up to 28 y", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "K1 intake associated with lower dementia incidence, but K2 intake with higher; brain MK-4 associated with lower odds of dementia/MCI and less tau pathology.", "effect_estimate": "K1 HR 0.86 (0.78-0.93); K2 HR 1.14 (1.04-1.24) [ghasemifard2026]; 17-20% lower odds per MK-4 unit [booth2022]", "certainty_notes": "Large cohorts but food-source confounding and opposite directions for K1 and K2.", "key_study_ids": ["ghasemifard2026", "booth2022"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Any population - vitamin K supplementation trials", "dose_or_intake": "K1 or K2 supplements", "duration": "not studied", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No completed RCT of vitamin K supplementation on cognition found (PubMed, Europe PMC, ClinicalTrials.gov); prior MK-4 trials in AD patients retracted.", "effect_estimate": "not reported (no trials)", "certainty_notes": "Only mechanistic/animal and observational data.", "key_study_ids": ["alisi2019", "sato2005"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults 18-45, students, knowledge workers", "dose_or_intake": "any", "duration": "not studied", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No human data of any design in young adults.", "effect_estimate": "not reported (no studies)", "certainty_notes": "Extrapolation from older adults not permitted.", "key_study_ids": ["alisi2019"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_decline", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Older adults on vitamin K antagonists (atrial fibrillation etc.)", "dose_or_intake": "VKA therapy (functional vitamin K antagonism)", "duration": "cross-sectional to several years", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Small cross-sectional study: strong but extremely imprecise association with impairment. Three-City: worse baseline visual memory and fluency, but no faster decline. DOAC vs VKA: slightly lower dementia with DOAC, very low certainty.", "effect_estimate": "OR 17.4 (1.4-224.2) [annweiler2015]; Isaacs MD -1.37, no difference in decline [ferland2016]; HR 0.89 (0.80-0.99) [branco2023]", "certainty_notes": "Confounding by indication, bleeding/microbleeds; indirect for nutritional vitamin K.", "key_study_ids": ["annweiler2015", "ferland2016", "branco2023"]}], "acute_matrix": [], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "median 25.5 y", "population": "Danish adults 50-65 (n=54,968)", "outcome": "Incident dementia", "effect_estimate": "K1 HR 0.86 (0.78-0.93); K2 HR 1.14 (1.04-1.24)", "evidence_quality": "low (observational)", "limitations": "single FFQ; food-source confounding", "study_ids": ["ghasemifard2026"]}, {"duration": "6 y", "population": "Dutch adults 55-65 (n=599)", "outcome": "Cognitive decline", "effect_estimate": "no association", "evidence_quality": "low (observational)", "limitations": "dp-ucMGP marker non-specific", "study_ids": ["vandenheuvel2015"]}, {"duration": "2 y", "population": "Spanish older adults at high CV risk", "outcome": "MMSE <=24, verbal fluency", "effect_estimate": "OR 0.53 (0.35-0.79)", "evidence_quality": "low (observational within trial)", "limitations": "confounded by diet intervention", "study_ids": ["camachobarcia2022"]}, {"duration": "years (Three-City follow-up)", "population": "French adults >=65 on VKA", "outcome": "Cognitive decline", "effect_estimate": "no faster decline", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "few VKA users", "study_ids": ["ferland2016"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Phylloquinone and menaquinones have very low toxicity; no UL has been set in the EU or US, and adverse effects at food or supplement doses are not documented in healthy people (EFSA 2017 context [efsa2017]). The major hazard is the interaction with vitamin K antagonists (warfarin, acenocoumarol, phenprocoumon, fluindione): MK-7 at 10-45 ug/day lowered INR in many anticoagulated volunteers [theuwissen2013], risking thrombosis. Synthetic menadione (K3) is toxic (haemolysis, liver) and not permitted in human supplements. Newborn vitamin K prophylaxis is a separate medical issue.", "adverse_effects": ["Loss of anticoagulant control (lower INR) in VKA users", "No documented toxicity of K1/K2 at nutritional or typical supplement doses", "Menadione (K3): haemolytic anaemia, hyperbilirubinaemia - not used in supplements"], "contraindications": ["Patients on vitamin K antagonists unless supervised by their anticoagulation clinic"], "drug_interactions": ["Warfarin, acenocoumarol, phenprocoumon, fluindione: antagonises effect (INR falls) [theuwissen2013, rombouts2007]", "DOACs (apixaban, rivaroxaban, dabigatran, edoxaban): no vitamin K interaction", "Orlistat, bile-acid sequestrants, long-term broad-spectrum antibiotics: reduce vitamin K absorption/status", "High-dose vitamin E may antagonise vitamin K (reported in literature; not examined here)"], "upper_limits": "No UL in the EU (EFSA/SCF) or US (IOM 2001). EU AI for adults about 70 ug/day phylloquinone (1 ug/kg bw) [efsa2017]; EU labelling NRV 75 ug. US AI 120 ug (men) and 90 ug (women) per IOM 2001 (NIH ODS page returned 403; not re-verified).", "sleep_impact": "None known.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Long-term (up to 3 y) MK-7 and K1 bone/vascular trials exist (not reviewed here) without major safety signals; no long-term cognitive trials.", "daily_use_appropriate": "probably", "special_populations": "Anticoagulated patients (VKA): avoid unsupervised supplements and keep leafy-green intake consistent. Newborns: medical prophylaxis. Chronic kidney disease and liver disease: altered status, specialist input. Pregnancy: no specific concerns at NRV-level intakes.", "specialist_review_needed": ["Haematologist/anticoagulation clinic (VKA interaction)", "Pharmacist (labelling warning, drug interactions)"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "Safe for the general population at NRV-to-moderate doses, but any vitamin K-containing product must carry a clear warning for people on vitamin K antagonists, because doses as low as 10-20 ug MK-7 alter INR. There is no efficacy basis for doses above the NRV."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Phylloquinone (phytomenadione) and menaquinone forms are permitted in food supplements (Directive 2002/46/EC Annex II). Some synthetic MK-7 preparations are covered by novel-food authorisations; check the Union list for the specific source (uncertain). The NRV is 75 ug. Authorised claims (Reg. 432/2012, verified on legislation.gov.uk annex 2026-10-25): 'Vitamin K contributes to normal blood clotting'; 'Vitamin K contributes to the maintenance of normal bones'. There is no cognitive claim. Trade press reports a draft EU maximum-level proposal that would lower vitamin K1 limits (unofficial). EU harmonised maximum levels are not set.", "us": "Dietary ingredient under DSHEA; DV 120 ug. Structure/function claims about bone or blood clotting are possible with the disclaimer. Brain/cognition claims lack competent and reliable evidence (FTC). Labelling for anticoagulant users is advisable.", "morocco": "No vitamin K-specific rules found. General supplement registration (Ministry of Health/AMMPS), disease-claim ban (Decree 2-12-389 Art. 7) and absence of an authorised claims list apply per X6 synthesis (uncertain items need a Moroccan lawyer).", "permitted_claims_examples": ["Vitamin K contributes to normal blood clotting (EU Reg. 432/2012; food at least a source of vitamin K)", "Vitamin K contributes to the maintenance of normal bones (EU Reg. 432/2012)"], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "Phylloquinone and natto-derived MK-7 have long use. Synthetic MK-7 and some new menaquinone sources have specific novel-food entries/specifications in the EU (verify source-specific status). No NDI is needed for vitamin K in the US.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "High via whole foods: a single serving of leafy greens (spinach, kale, purslane, parsley) or a salad exceeds the NRV. Added vitamin K is easy to include at NRV levels in fortified foods or capsules but brings an anticoagulant labelling burden.", "formats": ["leafy-green ingredients (spinach, kale, herbs)", "MK-7 oil or powder (microencapsulated)", "phylloquinone premix", "component of MVM or vitamin D3+K2 products"], "stability": "Phylloquinone is light-sensitive (degrades in clear packaging) and fairly heat-stable; MK-7 all-trans isomer can isomerise to inactive cis forms, so supplier specification matters.", "taste": "Neutral at supplement doses; leafy greens add green/bitter notes.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Fat-soluble: absorption from vegetables is low (~5-15%) unless eaten with fat/oil; MK-7 has a long half-life and higher steady-state levels than K1.", "dose_feasibility": "There is no cognitive efficacy dose to target. NRV-level (75 ug) doses fit trivially in food or tablets.", "cost_availability": "Phylloquinone is cheap; MK-7 is more expensive but widely available.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 1.5, "effect_magnitude": 1, "safety": 7, "reproducibility": 2, "food_suitability": 7, "cost_access": 8, "rationale": "Evidence quality 1.5: no trials (grade D for supplementation and for young adults); only grade C inconsistent observational data in older adults. Effect magnitude 1: no causal effect estimate exists; associations are small and possibly confounded. Safety 7: non-toxic, but a clinically important VKA interaction. Reproducibility 2: K1 and K2 associations point in opposite directions; null in middle age. Food suitability 7: easily supplied by leafy greens, fat-soluble and light-sensitive as an additive. Cost/access 8."}, "research_gaps": ["Any adequately powered RCT of vitamin K1 or MK-7 on cognition in older adults with low status (dp-ucMGP high) - none completed", "Mendelian-randomisation or trial evidence separating vitamin K from leafy-green dietary patterns", "Explanation of the opposite K1 vs K2 dementia associations (food sources, MK isoforms)", "Brain MK-4 response to supplementation in living humans (imaging/CSF)", "Any data in young adults (none)"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 3.75, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": null, "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"episodic_memory": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 6, "n_studies": 16, "n_verified": 16, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 4, "n_systematic_reviews": 2, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["inconsistent", "insufficient", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "calcium", "name": "Calcium", "category": "C", "category_name": "Minerals and trace elements", "aliases": ["calcium carbonate", "calcium citrate", "calcium gluconate", "Ca2+", "dietary calcium", "calcium supplements"], "food_sources": ["dairy (milk, yogurt, cheese) - the most bioavailable and best-studied source", "fortified plant milks and juices", "small fish eaten with bones (sardines, canned salmon)", "tofu set with calcium sulfate", "leafy greens (kale, bok choy; spinach is high in calcium but oxalate reduces absorption)", "almonds, sesame/tahini", "fortified cereals and breads"], "active_constituents": ["calcium ion (Ca2+), the species relevant to all physiological action regardless of the salt or food matrix supplying it"], "one_line_verdict": "Calcium is essential for normal physiology but has no demonstrated role in enhancing cognition in healthy adults, and calcium supplements (not dietary calcium) carry a plausible, not yet confirmed, dementia and cardiovascular safety signal that argues against a calcium-supplement cognition product.", "scientific_conclusion": "Calcium is an essential mineral required for bone mineralisation, neuromuscular signalling, and intracellular second-messenger function; its relevance to brain function in the general scientific literature is almost entirely indirect, via bone, vascular, and hormonal pathways rather than direct cognitive enhancement. No systematic search returned a randomised trial testing calcium supplementation for cognitive enhancement in healthy young adults, students, or knowledge workers; the literature on calcium and brain outcomes is concentrated in older women and is about dementia risk, not performance. The single most relevant and most frequently cited finding is a 5-year longitudinal study from the Prospective Population Study of Women/H70 cohort in Gothenburg (n=700 dementia-free women aged 70-92): calcium supplement users (n=98) had higher odds of incident dementia overall (OR 2.10, 95% CI 1.01-4.37, p=0.046) and markedly higher odds of stroke-related (vascular/mixed) dementia (OR 4.40, 95% CI 1.54-12.61, p=0.006), with the association concentrated in women who already had a stroke history (OR 6.77, 95% CI 1.36-33.75) or white-matter lesions on CT (OR 2.99, 95% CI 1.28-6.96) and absent in women without these conditions [kern2016]. This is an observational study in elderly women with pre-existing cerebrovascular disease, not a trial, and the authors explicitly caution that the small, non-randomised sample means the finding needs confirmation; it supports a hypothesis (calcium supplements may be harmful specifically in a cerebrovascular-compromised subgroup) rather than a conclusion. This pattern is consistent with, and plausibly mechanistically connected to, an independent and larger body of meta-analytic evidence that calcium supplementation (as pills, not dietary calcium) modestly but significantly raises cardiovascular and particularly myocardial infarction risk: a widely cited meta-analysis found an increased stroke/MI signal with calcium-only supplements [reid2015], and more recent systematic reviews/meta-analyses of both cohort and RCT data give an inconsistent but not reassuring picture - some report increased cardiovascular risk primarily with supplemental (not food) calcium and especially when taken without vitamin D or at high doses [chung2016; yang2020], while dietary calcium from food shows no such signal and may even be protective at moderate intakes. Because cerebrovascular disease (small-vessel disease, stroke) is a major contributor to vascular and mixed dementia, a plausible mechanistic chain (calcium supplements -> vascular calcification/thrombotic risk -> cerebrovascular events -> dementia) exists but is not established in humans; grade D/C mechanistic plausibility, not grade A causal evidence. No systematic review or RCT was found testing calcium for memory, attention, processing speed or any cognitive-enhancement outcome in healthy, non-deficient adults of any age; the honest conclusion is 'insufficient evidence' for enhancement and a possible, unconfirmed, harm signal restricted to a vulnerable subgroup (older women with pre-existing cerebrovascular disease) taking calcium as a supplement. Dietary calcium from food has not shown this signal and is supported by abundant bone-health evidence; this monograph does not find grounds to recommend calcium supplementation for cognitive purposes in any population, and flags the Kern 2016 / cardiovascular meta-analysis signal as a reason for caution rather than alarm in the specific subgroup of older women with cerebrovascular disease.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "True dietary calcium deficiency is uncommon in populations with adequate dairy or fortified-food access but is a real concern for those avoiding dairy without compensatory sources (some vegans, lactose-intolerant individuals, parts of Morocco's rural population with low dairy intake); deficiency manifests as impaired bone mineralisation (osteoporosis risk), not primarily as a cognitive syndrome, except indirectly through severe hypocalcaemia causing confusion, seizures or tetany - a medical emergency, not a nutrition-product target. There is no human evidence that raising calcium intake above adequacy enhances cognition in healthy, replete adults; the only cognition-adjacent signal found (Kern 2016) points toward possible harm from supplemental calcium in a specific at-risk subgroup, not benefit from enhancement. Brain Food's honest position is that calcium belongs in the nutrition conversation as a bone-health and food-formulation nutrient, not as a cognitive-enhancement ingredient.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Calcium is sometimes marketed alongside magnesium or vitamin D in 'brain and bone' combination products implying a cognitive benefit; no evidence base supports a direct cognitive claim for calcium in healthy adults. The only calcium-cognition signal in the literature (Kern 2016) is a safety concern in a vulnerable elderly subgroup, which is the opposite of a marketing asset and should be disclosed, not omitted, if calcium supplementation were ever included in a product aimed at older consumers with cardiovascular risk factors. The scientifically honest message is that calcium matters for bone and general health via food, and that calcium supplements - as opposed to dietary calcium - have an open, unresolved cardiovascular/cerebrovascular safety question that argues for food-first formulation over isolated supplement dosing.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Calcium absorption occurs via active transcellular transport (vitamin D-dependent, saturable, dominant at low-moderate intakes) and passive paracellular diffusion (dominant at high intakes); absorbed calcium is tightly homeostatically regulated by parathyroid hormone, calcitriol and calcitonin, with bone as the main reservoir. Calcium ion signalling is fundamental to neurotransmitter release, synaptic plasticity, and neuronal excitability at the cellular level (established pharmacology), but normal extracellular calcium is maintained within a narrow range regardless of modest dietary variation, so this mechanism does not translate into a dose-responsive 'more calcium, better cognition' pathway in replete people. The proposed harm pathway - supplemental calcium causing transient post-dose hypercalcaemia, vascular calcification, and thrombotic/cerebrovascular events that secondarily affect cognition via stroke and small-vessel disease - is plausible and supported by cardiovascular meta-analyses but not directly demonstrated for brain outcomes beyond the single observational dementia study.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Dietary/supplemental calcium -> intestinal absorption (vitamin D-dependent active transport plus passive diffusion)", "detail": "Absorption efficiency is 25-35% from typical diets, lower with high doses taken as a single bolus versus spread food intake; well characterised in humans.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Absorbed calcium -> tight homeostatic regulation (PTH, calcitriol) -> bone deposition as primary sink", "detail": "Plasma ionised calcium is held in a narrow range; excess dietary calcium is largely excreted or deposited in bone, not left circulating.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Bolus supplemental calcium -> transient post-dose rise in serum calcium -> possible vascular calcification / pro-coagulant effect over years", "detail": "Mechanism proposed to explain the cardiovascular signal from supplement meta-analyses [reid2015, chung2016]; dietary calcium spread across meals does not produce the same acute spike.", "evidence_level": "plausible"}, {"step": "Vascular calcification / cerebrovascular events -> stroke-related and mixed dementia", "detail": "Supported only by one observational cohort in women with pre-existing cerebrovascular disease [kern2016]; not replicated by an independent cohort or RCT.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Intracellular Ca2+ signalling -> neurotransmitter release, synaptic plasticity", "detail": "Fundamental, well-established cell physiology, but not dose-responsive to dietary calcium variation in replete adults; relevant to severe deficiency/hypocalcaemia, not enhancement.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}], "pathways": ["vitamin D-dependent intestinal absorption", "PTH/calcitriol homeostasis", "vascular calcification (supplement-specific)", "intracellular calcium signalling in neurons"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "dementia_risk", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Women aged 70-92 with pre-existing cerebrovascular disease (stroke history or white-matter lesions) taking calcium supplements, versus without this vulnerability", "dose_or_intake": "Calcium supplement use (dose/formulation not standardised in the cohort; typical Swedish OTC doses ~500-1000 mg/day)", "duration": "5-year follow-up", "grade": "C", "direction": "harm", "effect_summary": "In a 700-woman cohort, calcium supplement users had higher odds of dementia overall (OR 2.10) and much higher odds of stroke-related dementia (OR 4.40); the association was present only in women with stroke history (OR 6.77) or white-matter lesions (OR 2.99), not in women without these conditions.", "effect_estimate": "OR 2.10 (95% CI 1.01-4.37) all dementia; OR 4.40 (95% CI 1.54-12.61) stroke-related dementia [kern2016]", "certainty_notes": "Single observational study, small subgroup numbers, no randomisation, residual confounding by indication (women prescribed calcium may differ in frailty/osteoporosis severity) cannot be excluded; downgraded for risk of bias, imprecision and lack of replication. Authors themselves call for confirmation.", "key_study_ids": ["kern2016"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults/students, adequately nourished, no cerebrovascular disease", "dose_or_intake": "not studied", "duration": "not studied", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No randomised controlled trial of calcium supplementation for cognitive enhancement in healthy young or middle-aged adults was located; the calcium-cognition literature is confined to elderly, often vulnerable populations and to bone/cardiovascular endpoints.", "effect_estimate": "not reported - no trials identified", "certainty_notes": "Absence of evidence, not evidence of absence; graded D (effectively no human enhancement data) pending any future trial.", "key_study_ids": []}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "vascular_health", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Adults taking calcium-only supplements (with or without vitamin D) versus controls, cardiovascular endpoints", "dose_or_intake": "typically 500-1200 mg/day calcium supplement", "duration": "years (meta-analyses of RCTs and cohorts, follow-up 2-10+ years)", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Meta-analyses find a modest increase in myocardial infarction/cardiovascular events with calcium-only supplements relative to no supplement, while dietary calcium from food shows no such association and co-administration with vitamin D attenuates or nullifies the signal in some analyses; results vary by analysis and inclusion criteria.", "effect_estimate": "e.g., relative risk increase of roughly 20-30% for MI in supplement-only analyses in some meta-analyses; heterogeneous across reviews [reid2015, chung2016, yang2020]", "certainty_notes": "Downgraded for inconsistency across meta-analyses, risk of confounding by indication in cohort components, and differing calcium/vitamin D co-dosing across included trials; not a cognitive outcome but relevant upstream of vascular dementia risk.", "key_study_ids": ["reid2015", "chung2016", "yang2020"]}], "acute_matrix": [], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "5 years", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "outcome": "incident dementia (all-cause and stroke-related) in women with pre-existing cerebrovascular disease taking calcium supplements", "effect_estimate": "OR 2.10 (1.01-4.37) all dementia; OR 4.40 (1.54-12.61) stroke-related dementia [kern2016]", "evidence_quality": "low (single observational cohort, small subgroup)", "limitations": "Observational, no randomisation, confounding by indication possible, small subgroup sizes, unreplicated.", "study_ids": ["kern2016"]}, {"duration": "2-10+ years (pooled)", "population": "mixed_adults", "outcome": "cardiovascular events (myocardial infarction, stroke) with calcium-only supplementation", "effect_estimate": "Modest relative risk increase for MI in several supplement-focused meta-analyses; dietary calcium not implicated [reid2015, chung2016, yang2020]", "evidence_quality": "moderate but inconsistent across meta-analyses", "limitations": "Meta-analyses combine heterogeneous trial populations, doses, and vitamin D co-administration; publication and selection differences across reviews produce non-identical conclusions.", "study_ids": ["reid2015", "chung2016", "yang2020"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Dietary calcium from food is safe at typical intakes and is essential for bone health. Calcium supplements (isolated pills, often without the co-nutrients present in dairy) have an unresolved but recurrent cardiovascular safety signal in meta-analyses, and one small observational study links calcium supplement use to increased dementia risk specifically in older women with pre-existing cerebrovascular disease. Hypercalcaemia (from excessive intake, usually supplement- or disease-driven, not food) causes confusion, lethargy and in severe cases coma; hypocalcaemia causes tetany, seizures and confusion.", "adverse_effects": ["Constipation and gastrointestinal discomfort with calcium carbonate supplements", "Kidney stone risk increase with supplemental calcium at high doses, especially without adequate fluid intake", "Possible increased myocardial infarction risk with calcium-only supplements in some meta-analyses", "Possible increased dementia/stroke-related dementia risk with calcium supplements in women with pre-existing cerebrovascular disease (one study, unconfirmed)", "Hypercalcaemia (rare from diet alone; more relevant with high-dose supplements, vitamin D excess, or underlying disease such as hyperparathyroidism) causing confusion, lethargy, cardiac arrhythmia"], "contraindications": ["Hypercalcaemia or conditions predisposing to it (primary hyperparathyroidism, sarcoidosis, some cancers)", "History of calcium-containing kidney stones (caution with supplements)", "Pre-existing cerebrovascular disease (caution with supplemental, as opposed to dietary, calcium per Kern 2016 - unconfirmed but prudent)"], "drug_interactions": ["Reduces absorption of certain antibiotics (tetracyclines, fluoroquinolones) and levothyroxine if taken concurrently", "Can interact with thiazide diuretics to raise serum calcium", "May affect absorption of iron and zinc when taken together in supplement form"], "upper_limits": "EU and US tolerable upper intake level for calcium is 2500 mg/day for adults (2000 mg/day for adults over 50 in some US guidance); typical supplement doses (500-1200 mg/day) are well below this but add to dietary intake and total daily exposure should be considered.", "sleep_impact": "No established direct effect of calcium intake on sleep architecture at nutritional doses; not a stimulant or sedative pathway.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Long-term calcium supplementation has been studied for years to decades primarily in osteoporosis prevention trials (e.g., Women's Health Initiative); cardiovascular and now a single dementia signal have emerged from secondary/observational analyses of these and other cohorts, not from trials designed to test these endpoints.", "daily_use_appropriate": "probably", "special_populations": "Pregnant and lactating women have increased calcium needs (food-first approach preferred); postmenopausal women and older adults with cerebrovascular disease or risk factors should discuss supplement use with a physician given the unresolved cardiovascular and dementia signals; patients with hyperparathyroidism, sarcoidosis, or calcium-containing kidney stones should avoid supplementation without medical guidance; vegans/lactose-intolerant individuals need non-dairy calcium sources.", "specialist_review_needed": ["Cardiology/vascular medicine review of the calcium-supplement cardiovascular literature before any supplement-based (as opposed to food-based) calcium product is marketed to older adults", "Geriatric medicine or neurology input on the cerebrovascular-dementia signal before any claim-adjacent product targeting older consumers"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "Dietary/food-based calcium is safe and appropriate (pass); isolated calcium supplementation, especially at high doses or in older adults with cerebrovascular risk, carries an unresolved cardiovascular and possible dementia signal that warrants conditional status and specialist review before any supplement-format product."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Calcium has an EU Nutrient Reference Value (NRV 800 mg/day) and several EFSA-authorised health claims under Regulation (EC) 1924/2006 relating to bone, teeth, muscle function, and neurotransmission ('calcium is needed for normal neurotransmission' and 'normal function of digestive enzymes' are authorised general claims, but no authorised claim exists for cognitive performance or memory). Calcium supplements are regulated as food supplements under Directive 2002/46/EC with permitted forms listed in its annexes.", "us": "Calcium is a recognised essential nutrient with an established Daily Value (1300 mg for adults) under FDA labeling rules; an FDA-authorised health claim links adequate calcium intake to reduced osteoporosis risk. No FDA-reviewed or qualified health claim exists for cognitive function; any cognitive claim would require independent FTC substantiation that does not currently exist.", "morocco": "Calcium-fortified foods and supplements are regulated by ONSSA under general food-fortification and food-supplement rules; Morocco follows Codex Alimentarius nutrient reference values for labeling purposes. No specific cognitive claim framework exists; status for novel formulations should be confirmed directly with ONSSA.", "permitted_claims_examples": ["EU: 'Calcium is needed for the maintenance of normal bones' (authorised under Regulation (EC) 1924/2006)", "EU: 'Calcium contributes to normal neurotransmission' (authorised, but this is a mechanistic/physiological claim, not a cognitive-performance claim)", "US: FDA-authorised claim linking adequate calcium and vitamin D intake with reduced risk of osteoporosis"], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "Calcium in its common forms (carbonate, citrate, gluconate, lactate) is not a novel food/NDI issue; well established in food law in all three markets.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Excellent as a food ingredient (dairy, fortified plant milks, tofu, fortified cereals); calcium carbonate and citrate are common, inexpensive, food-grade forms.", "formats": ["Fortified beverages (plant milks, juices)", "Dairy-based products", "Fortified bars/cereals", "Tablets/capsules (less preferred given the food-vs-supplement safety distinction in this monograph)"], "stability": "Calcium salts are generally stable in food processing and storage; citrate and carbonate are heat-stable.", "taste": "Calcium carbonate can impart a chalky taste at higher concentrations; citrate and lactate are better tolerated organoleptically.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Absorption is higher from dairy and calcium citrate (acid-independent) than from calcium carbonate taken without food (requires stomach acid); oxalate- and phytate-rich foods (spinach, whole grains) reduce absorption from the same meal.", "dose_feasibility": "A bone-health-relevant dose (200-500 mg per serving) fits easily into a fortified food or beverage serving; no cognitive dose exists to target since no cognitive benefit is established.", "cost_availability": "Calcium carbonate and citrate are low-cost, widely available commodity ingredients globally, including in Morocco.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 1, "effect_magnitude": 0, "safety": 6, "reproducibility": 2, "food_suitability": 9, "cost_access": 9, "rationale": "evidence_quality near-zero because no human cognitive-enhancement trial exists and the only cognition-relevant finding (Kern 2016) points to possible harm in a subgroup, not benefit; effect_magnitude 0 because there is no measured enhancement effect to size; safety scored moderate (6) rather than high because of the unresolved cardiovascular and dementia safety signals specific to supplemental calcium, despite food calcium being very safe; reproducibility low because the dementia signal is a single unreplicated observational study; food_suitability and cost_access high because calcium is a ubiquitous, cheap, well-tolerated food ingredient independent of any cognitive claim."}, "research_gaps": ["No RCT has tested calcium supplementation for cognitive enhancement, attention, memory or any performance outcome in healthy young adults or students.", "Kern 2016's dementia/cerebrovascular signal has not been replicated in an independent cohort or confirmed in a trial; replication in a larger, prospective, randomised design is needed before any firm conclusion.", "The food-versus-supplement cardiovascular distinction needs clarification with mechanistic studies (e.g., post-dose calcium kinetics comparing bolus supplement versus food-matrix calcium).", "No data exist on whether moderate dietary calcium intake (as opposed to supplementation) has any relationship, positive or negative, with cognitive aging trajectories."], "_derived": {"composite_score": 3.5, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": null, "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": null, "best_grade_overall": null, "benefit_domains": {}, "n_outcome_rows": 3, "n_studies": 4, "n_verified": 4, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 1, "n_systematic_reviews": 2, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["harm", "inconsistent", "insufficient"]}}, {"id": "copper", "name": "Copper", "category": "C", "category_name": "Minerals and trace elements", "aliases": ["cupric sulfate", "copper gluconate", "copper bisglycinate", "cuprum"], "food_sources": ["shellfish (oysters, crab)", "organ meats (liver)", "nuts and seeds (cashews, sunflower seeds)", "whole grains", "legumes", "dark chocolate/cocoa", "mushrooms"], "active_constituents": ["cupric (Cu2+) and cuprous (Cu+) copper, cofactor for cuproenzymes including ceruloplasmin, cytochrome c oxidase, superoxide dismutase (SOD1/SOD3), dopamine beta-hydroxylase, tyrosinase and lysyl oxidase"], "one_line_verdict": "Copper is essential for brain enzymes including dopamine synthesis and antioxidant defense, and frank deficiency (rare, usually iatrogenic from zinc excess or bariatric surgery) causes neurological disease, but there is no RCT evidence that supplementing copper above adequacy improves cognition, and observational data instead flag a possible harm signal when high copper intake combines with a high-saturated-fat diet in older adults.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. Copper is an obligatory cofactor for cuproenzymes central to brain function: dopamine beta-hydroxylase (catecholamine synthesis), cytochrome c oxidase (mitochondrial ATP production) and superoxide dismutase (antioxidant defense). True dietary copper deficiency is rare in people eating a varied diet but is well documented in specific clinical settings: bariatric surgery malabsorption, excessive zinc supplementation (zinc induces metallothionein, which sequesters copper and blocks its absorption), and total parenteral nutrition without copper. Copper deficiency causes a recognised myeloneuropathy (sensory ataxia, spasticity, optic neuropathy) that closely mimics subacute combined degeneration from B12 deficiency, confirmed across case series [goodman2009, altarelli2019]; zinc-induced copper deficiency is a well-described mechanism, including case reports in the context of Wilson disease zinc therapy and denture-cream zinc exposure [horvath2010]. This establishes that copper deficiency has serious neurological consequences, but these are disease-level findings in deficient patients, not evidence that supplementing copper-replete healthy people enhances cognition. In whom, and how large, in the Brain Food target population (healthy young adults/students). No RCT has tested copper supplementation on cognition in healthy, copper-replete or mildly-low young adults or students; the entire direct-cognition evidence base is observational, cross-sectional, or in older adults. The most load-bearing observational finding is from the Chicago Health and Aging Project (3718 community-dwelling older adults, 6-year follow-up): among people whose diets were also high in saturated and trans fat, higher total copper intake was associated with faster cognitive decline (difference between highest vs lowest copper quintile: -6.14 standardized units per year, p<0.001, equivalent to about 19 additional years of age-related decline); critically, copper intake was not associated with cognitive change among people whose diets were not high in these fats, indicating an interaction rather than a main effect of copper alone [morris2006]. A more recent NHANES cross-sectional analysis (2483 US adults 60+) found a different, non-monotonic (threshold) pattern: copper intake below an inflection point (0.8-1.4 mg/day depending on the test) was positively associated with cognitive test scores (e.g. DSST beta=6.0, 95% CI 3.8-8.3), with a flattening or non-significant downward trend above the threshold, and found that high dietary fat/saturated fat intake blunted rather than worsened the decline at higher copper intake in this dataset, a pattern that appears to diverge from the original Morris finding and highlights inconsistency across studies [wang2021]. Multiple meta-analyses of serum/plasma non-ceruloplasmin ('free') copper in Alzheimer's disease patients versus controls consistently find elevated free copper in AD patients [squitti2014, meta-analyses cited therein], but these are case-control biomarker studies in diagnosed AD patients, reflecting disease-associated copper dysregulation (impaired ceruloplasmin binding), not a demonstration that higher copper intake in healthy people causes decline, nor that lowering copper treats AD. What is unknown/inconsistent. The two population-level observational studies on dietary copper intake and cognitive decline in older adults [morris2006, wang2021] disagree on the direction and nature of the fat-copper interaction, which substantially lowers confidence in any single practical takeaway beyond 'the copper-fat-cognition relationship in older adults is complex and not simply dose-proportional, and diets that are both high in copper and high in saturated/trans fat merit caution.' No study in any population has shown that supplementing dietary copper above typical intake improves cognition; the evidence, where it exists, points toward possible harm under specific dietary conditions in older adults, not benefit. Conclusion for Brain Food: correcting genuine copper deficiency (rare, mostly iatrogenic) is medically important, but marketing added copper as a cognitive enhancer for a general, already-adequately-nourished population is not supported and carries a plausible-harm signal under certain dietary patterns that should be disclosed rather than ignored.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "This is a deficiency-and-disease-state story with a possible-harm enhancement story, not a benefit-enhancement one. Frank copper deficiency (rare; mainly bariatric surgery, high-dose zinc supplementation, or parenteral nutrition without copper) causes a well-documented, serious myeloneuropathy [goodman2009, altarelli2019, horvath2010], and correcting it is clearly indicated in those specific patients. In the general healthy adult population, dietary copper intake is rarely frankly deficient, and there is no human trial evidence that raising intake above adequacy improves cognition. The only chronic, population-level signal relevant to 'more copper' comes from two inconsistent observational analyses in older adults, one of which found higher copper intake associated with *faster* cognitive decline specifically when combined with a high-saturated/trans-fat diet [morris2006], and the other finding a threshold/non-monotonic pattern with some protective association below roughly 0.8-1.4 mg/day and attenuation above it [wang2021]. Neither supports a 'more copper equals sharper mind' marketing narrative; if anything, the honest message is that copper status should sit within normal dietary range, not be pushed upward, especially in a product also containing saturated fat.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Marketing that frames copper as a 'brain-boosting' mineral typically points to its role in dopamine synthesis and antioxidant enzymes (true mechanistically) while omitting that the best available population studies on dietary copper intake and cognitive decline in older adults found either no benefit of higher intake, or harm when combined with a high-fat diet [morris2006], or a threshold pattern that does not support simply adding more copper [wang2021]. The EU has authorised the general-function claim 'Copper contributes to the normal functioning of the nervous system' under Commission Regulation (EU) No 432/2012 (Regulation (EC) No 1924/2006 claims register), alongside other authorised copper claims (normal energy metabolism, normal connective tissue, normal hair/skin pigmentation, normal iron transport, protection of cells from oxidative stress, normal function of the immune system), conditional on the product being a significant source of copper; this is a maintenance-of-normal-function claim at adequate intake, not licence to claim that additional copper improves cognitive performance, and this wording should be reconfirmed against the live EU register before use. Honest positioning for Brain Food: copper from varied whole foods (nuts, seeds, legumes, shellfish, whole grains) supports normal nervous system and mitochondrial function at ordinary dietary levels; copper is not a cognitive enhancer, zinc supplements should not be marketed or dosed in ways that risk inducing copper deficiency, and any product pairing copper with a high-fat formulation should be reviewed against the Morris et al. interaction signal.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Dietary copper is absorbed in the stomach and proximal small intestine via copper transporter CTR1, bound to albumin/transcuprein for transport, taken up by the liver, and incorporated into ceruloplasmin (the main plasma copper-carrying ferroxidase) and other cuproenzymes. In the brain, copper is a required cofactor for dopamine beta-hydroxylase (converts dopamine to norepinephrine), cytochrome c oxidase (mitochondrial electron transport/ATP synthesis) and superoxide dismutase (antioxidant defense against superoxide radicals). Copper also binds amyloid-beta and alpha-synuclein in vitro, and dysregulated 'non-ceruloplasmin' (free) copper is consistently elevated in Alzheimer's disease case-control biomarker studies, a disease-associated dysregulation of copper handling rather than evidence that dietary copper intake causes the disease. Excess copper, particularly combined with a high-saturated-fat diet, is hypothesized to promote oxidative stress and amyloid aggregation in animal and in vitro models, providing a plausible but not definitively established mechanism for the Morris et al. interaction finding in humans.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Dietary copper -> absorption via CTR1 in stomach/proximal intestine -> portal transport to liver", "detail": "Well-characterised human absorption pathway; copper homeostasis is tightly regulated via biliary excretion, making true toxicity from food rare absent liver disease or genetic disorders (Wilson disease).", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Hepatic copper -> incorporation into ceruloplasmin and release to peripheral tissues, including the brain via specific transporters (e.g., ATP7A/ATP7B)", "detail": "Established human physiology; genetic defects in these transporters cause Wilson disease (copper excess/toxicity) or Menkes disease (copper deficiency), both with severe neurological consequences, illustrating the narrow functional range required.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Brain copper -> cofactor for dopamine beta-hydroxylase, cytochrome c oxidase, superoxide dismutase", "detail": "Established biochemistry; deficiency impairs these enzyme activities and is linked to the myeloneuropathy syndrome seen in copper-deficient patients [goodman2009, altarelli2019].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Copper dyshomeostasis (elevated non-ceruloplasmin/'free' copper) -> amyloid-beta/alpha-synuclein interaction and oxidative stress", "detail": "Demonstrated in vitro and in animal models; in humans, elevated free copper is a consistent case-control biomarker finding in Alzheimer's disease [squitti2014] but causality (copper dysregulation causing AD vs. being a consequence of the disease process) is not established.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "High dietary copper intake combined with high saturated/trans fat intake -> accelerated cognitive decline in older adults", "detail": "Demonstrated as a statistical interaction in one well-known prospective cohort (Chicago Health and Aging Project) [morris2006]; a more recent NHANES cross-sectional analysis found a different threshold pattern and did not replicate the same fat-interaction direction [wang2021], so this pathway is not consistently established.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Copper supplementation above dietary adequacy -> improved cognition in healthy adults", "detail": "No mechanism is proposed or supported by any human trial; cuproenzyme activity is saturable at normal intake, so no dose-response cognitive benefit above adequacy is biologically expected.", "evidence_level": "speculative"}], "pathways": ["CTR1 intestinal copper transporter / ceruloplasmin synthesis", "dopamine beta-hydroxylase (catecholamine synthesis)", "cytochrome c oxidase (mitochondrial ATP production)", "superoxide dismutase (antioxidant defense)", "ATP7A/ATP7B copper-transporting ATPases (brain copper delivery, dysfunctional in Wilson/Menkes disease)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_decline", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Community-dwelling Chicago residents aged 65+, stratified by dietary saturated/trans fat intake", "dose_or_intake": "highest quintile median 2.75 mg/day vs lowest quintile median 0.88 mg/day total copper intake", "duration": "chronic (6-year follow-up, cognitive testing every 3 years)", "grade": "C", "direction": "harm", "effect_summary": "Among people whose diets were also high in saturated and trans fat, higher total dietary copper intake was associated with substantially faster global cognitive decline; no association was seen in people whose diets were not high in these fats, indicating an interaction rather than a copper main effect.", "effect_estimate": "difference in decline rate highest vs lowest copper quintile: -6.14 standardized units/year (p<0.001), described by authors as equivalent to ~19 additional years of age-related decline; food-only copper highest quintile (median 1.51 mg/day) showed a marginal association (p=0.07) [morris2006]", "certainty_notes": "Large, well-conducted prospective cohort with repeated cognitive testing, but observational; residual confounding by overall diet quality plausible; graded C because the effect is conditional on a dietary interaction not independently confirmed by a more recent dataset [wang2021], which found a different pattern.", "key_study_ids": ["morris2006", "wang2021"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_decline", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "US NHANES 2011-2014 cross-sectional sample, adults 60+", "dose_or_intake": "total copper intake with inflection points around 0.8 mg/day (CERAD Word Learning) and 1.4 mg/day (Animal Fluency, DSST)", "duration": "cross-sectional (single time-point intake and cognitive assessment)", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Nonlinear/threshold association: copper intake below the inflection point was positively associated with cognitive test scores; above it, scores plateaued or trended down (not significant). High total fat/saturated fat intake was associated with a flattening rather than worsening of the decline at higher copper intake, a pattern differing from the Morris 2006 interaction direction.", "effect_estimate": "DSST beta=6.0 (95% CI 3.8-8.3) below inflection point (1.4 mg/day); CERAD Word Learning beta=3.9 (1.2-6.5) below inflection point (0.8 mg/day); Animal Fluency beta=1.7 (0.9-2.6) below inflection point [wang2021]", "certainty_notes": "Cross-sectional design cannot establish temporal or causal direction; graded C for indirectness (cross-sectional, self-reported dietary recall) and for inconsistency with the earlier prospective Morris 2006 interaction pattern.", "key_study_ids": ["wang2021", "morris2006"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Diagnosed Alzheimer's disease patients versus healthy controls, biomarker (serum/plasma non-ceruloplasmin copper) case-control studies pooled in meta-analyses", "dose_or_intake": "not applicable (endogenous biomarker, not intake or supplementation)", "duration": "cross-sectional biomarker comparison", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Meta-analyses consistently find elevated non-ceruloplasmin ('free') copper in AD patients versus controls, but this reflects disease-associated copper-handling dysregulation (reduced ceruloplasmin binding), not a demonstration that dietary copper intake causes or worsens AD, nor that healthy people's cognition is affected by their copper intake.", "effect_estimate": "elevated non-ceruloplasmin copper in AD vs controls, effect size not re-extracted here beyond the qualitative meta-analytic conclusion [squitti2014]", "certainty_notes": "Biomarker/mechanism evidence, not a measured cognitive-outcome trial; must not be read as evidence that dietary copper intake modification would alter AD risk or progression; mechanism is not efficacy.", "key_study_ids": ["squitti2014"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy, copper-replete young adults and students", "dose_or_intake": "not studied", "duration": "not applicable", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No identified RCT or cohort has tested copper supplementation or intake variation specifically in healthy young adults or students; all cognition-relevant data are in older adults (observational) or AD patients (biomarker case-control).", "effect_estimate": "not reported (no trial identified)", "certainty_notes": "Complete absence of direct evidence in this population; given the possible-harm signal in older adults under certain dietary conditions, there is no basis to assume benefit, and caution rather than enhancement framing is warranted.", "key_study_ids": ["morris2006", "wang2021"]}], "acute_matrix": [], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "6 years", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "outcome": "global cognitive decline, conditional on dietary fat intake", "effect_estimate": "-6.14 standardized units/year highest vs lowest copper quintile among high-saturated/trans-fat consumers (p<0.001) [morris2006]", "evidence_quality": "moderate (large, well-conducted prospective cohort; observational)", "limitations": "observational, residual confounding possible, interaction not independently replicated with the same direction in a later dataset", "study_ids": ["morris2006"]}, {"duration": "cross-sectional (NHANES 2011-2014)", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "outcome": "cognitive test scores (CERAD, Animal Fluency, DSST) by copper intake threshold", "effect_estimate": "positive association below ~0.8-1.4 mg/day inflection point; flattening/non-significant trend above it [wang2021]", "evidence_quality": "low-moderate (cross-sectional, self-reported diet)", "limitations": "cannot establish causality or temporal sequence; pattern diverges from the Morris 2006 interaction direction", "study_ids": ["wang2021"]}, {"duration": "chronic (case series, variable duration)", "population": "clinical_population", "outcome": "myeloneuropathy from copper deficiency", "effect_estimate": "sensory ataxia, spasticity, optic neuropathy resolving partially with copper repletion in case series [goodman2009, altarelli2019]", "evidence_quality": "low (case series/reviews, not controlled trials)", "limitations": "rare condition, mostly iatrogenic (bariatric surgery, excess zinc, parenteral nutrition); not generalizable to healthy adults", "study_ids": ["goodman2009", "altarelli2019", "horvath2010"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Copper toxicity from food alone is rare in people with normal liver function and biliary excretion; most copper safety concerns in practice are either (a) deficiency from excess zinc, bariatric surgery, or parenteral nutrition, or (b) toxicity in the context of genetic copper-handling disorders (Wilson disease) or very high-dose supplement/contaminated water exposure. An observational interaction with high-saturated/trans-fat diets raising cognitive-decline risk at higher copper intake [morris2006] is a plausible-harm signal requiring disclosure, though not independently replicated in the same direction by a later analysis [wang2021].", "adverse_effects": ["gastrointestinal upset (nausea, vomiting) at high acute copper intake", "liver toxicity at chronic high intake, especially in those with undiagnosed Wilson disease or other copper-handling disorders", "possible acceleration of cognitive decline when high copper intake is combined with a high-saturated/trans-fat diet in older adults (observational, not independently replicated) [morris2006]", "zinc-induced copper deficiency myeloneuropathy if copper is displaced by excess zinc intake (relevant to combination products) [horvath2010]"], "contraindications": ["Wilson disease or other known disorders of copper metabolism/excretion", "cholestatic liver disease (impaired biliary copper excretion)"], "drug_interactions": ["zinc supplements at high dose (antagonize copper absorption; relevant for any combined mineral product)", "penicillamine and other copper-chelating medications (used to treat Wilson disease; would counteract any added copper)"], "upper_limits": "EFSA/EU tolerable upper intake level for adults is 5 mg/day; US NIH ODS UL for adults is 10 mg/day; both are well above the RDA (~0.9 mg/day adults), giving a wider margin than iodine or selenium, but the Morris 2006 cognitive-decline signal occurred within intake ranges (up to ~2.75 mg/day median in the highest quintile, including some supplement users) that are still below these formal ULs, indicating the Morris finding (if causal) is not simply a toxicity-threshold effect.", "sleep_impact": "No established direct sleep effect at nutritional doses.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "The Morris 2006 and Wang 2021 analyses provide multi-year/cross-sectional population data on dietary copper intake and cognition in older adults, but no long-term RCT of copper supplementation and cognition exists in any population.", "daily_use_appropriate": "probably", "special_populations": "People taking high-dose zinc supplements (common in 'immune support' products) are at risk of copper deficiency and should ensure adequate copper intake or monitoring; people with Wilson disease or other copper-handling disorders should avoid copper supplementation entirely; older adults eating a diet high in saturated/trans fat should be a specific caution group for any product adding copper, given the Morris 2006 interaction signal, pending further research.", "specialist_review_needed": ["clinical nutrition/hepatology for any product with added copper intended for older adults, given the unresolved copper-fat-cognition interaction literature", "hematology/neurology referral pathway awareness for any product also containing high-dose zinc, given zinc-induced copper deficiency risk"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "Copper at normal dietary levels is safe for the general population and has a wider margin to the formal upper limit than iodine or selenium, but the unresolved and inconsistent observational signal linking higher copper intake (combined with high dietary fat) to faster cognitive decline in older adults [morris2006, wang2021] means a cognition-focused copper product aimed at older adults, or any high-fat formulation with added copper, needs explicit caution and further evidence rather than an unqualified safety claim."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Copper is an authorised EU vitamin/mineral nutrient. Under Commission Regulation (EU) No 432/2012 (EU Register of nutrition and health claims, Regulation (EC) No 1924/2006), authorised Article 13(1) general-function claims for copper include 'Copper contributes to the normal functioning of the nervous system', 'Copper contributes to normal energy-yielding metabolism', 'Copper contributes to the maintenance of normal connective tissue', 'Copper contributes to normal hair and skin pigmentation', 'Copper contributes to normal iron transport in the body', 'Copper contributes to the protection of cells from oxidative stress', and 'Copper has a role in the normal function of the immune system', conditional on the product being a significant source of copper. This is reported from standard knowledge of the EU claims register as of the research date and should be re-verified against the live EUR-Lex consolidated Annex before use in label copy.", "us": "Copper is a required Nutrition Facts label nutrient with an established RDA/DV; no FDA-authorised health claim for cognition exists for copper. Any structure/function claim referencing cognition would require FTC competent-and-reliable-scientific-evidence substantiation; given the absence of healthy-adult trial evidence and the possible-harm signal under high-fat diets, a cognition-enhancement claim for copper is not well substantiated for US marketing, and a nervous-system 'normal function' structure/function claim would need to be carefully worded to avoid implying enhancement.", "morocco": "Copper-specific supplement regulation in Morocco should be confirmed directly with ONSSA/Ministry of Health; this was not independently verified in this research pass (not researched here beyond general nutrient-fortification frameworks).", "permitted_claims_examples": ["Copper contributes to the normal functioning of the nervous system (EU, conditional on significant-source threshold; verify current EUR-Lex wording before use)"], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "Standard copper forms (copper sulfate, copper gluconate, copper bisglycinate) are established, non-novel supplement/fortification ingredients in the EU and have long-standing NDI status in the US; no special novel-food issues identified.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Copper fits naturally into food via nuts, seeds, legumes, shellfish, whole grains and cocoa; it does not require isolation to be delivered at adequate doses and is already present in many whole-food formulations Brain Food might use for other ingredients.", "formats": ["copper sulfate, copper gluconate or copper bisglycinate as standardized supplement/fortification forms", "whole-food sources (cashews, dark chocolate, legumes, whole grains) for a food-first formulation"], "stability": "Copper is a stable trace element in food matrices; not meaningfully degraded by normal cooking/storage, though copper can catalyze oxidation of some other sensitive nutrients (e.g., vitamin C) in a formulation, a formulation-compatibility consideration rather than a copper-safety one.", "taste": "Food-source copper (nuts, cocoa, legumes) carries its own natural flavor profile and is well accepted; isolated copper salts can impart a metallic taste at higher concentrations, relevant to supplement/fortified-food palatability.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Copper bioavailability from food is moderate (~30-40%) and is reduced by high dietary zinc, iron, or phytate (relevant in plant-heavy formulations); copper bisglycinate and other chelated forms are generally better absorbed than inorganic salts and may reduce GI irritation.", "dose_feasibility": "The RDA (~0.9 mg/day adults) is easily delivered by a modest serving of nuts, seeds, legumes, or a small, well-controlled supplement dose, well within the UL (5-10 mg/day); dose feasibility is not a barrier, but given the unresolved high-fat interaction signal, any product combining copper with a high-fat formulation (e.g., a nut-butter or chocolate product) should be reviewed with this in mind.", "cost_availability": "Copper salts and copper-rich whole foods (cashews, legumes, cocoa) are inexpensive and widely available globally.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 2, "effect_magnitude": 1, "safety": 6, "reproducibility": 3, "food_suitability": 8, "cost_access": 9, "rationale": "evidence_quality=2: no RCT evidence of any kind for cognitive benefit; the only chronic human cognition data are observational and inconsistent across two studies (C-grade at best), with the deficiency-disease literature being case-series level (not controlled trials). effect_magnitude=1: no demonstrated benefit; the only clear directional signal in a decent-quality cohort points toward possible harm under specific dietary conditions, not benefit. safety=6: copper has a wider formal margin to toxicity than iodine/selenium (RDA to UL ratio much larger), but the unresolved fat-interaction cognitive-decline signal and the real risk of zinc-induced copper deficiency in combination products keep this from scoring higher. reproducibility=3: the two key observational studies on copper-fat-cognition interaction disagree with each other in direction and pattern, which is a reproducibility problem, not a confirmation. food_suitability=8: copper is naturally present in many appealing whole foods already relevant to a cognitive-nutrition brand (nuts, legumes, cocoa). cost_access=9: inexpensive and globally available."}, "research_gaps": ["No RCT has tested copper supplementation on cognitive outcomes in any population, including healthy young adults, students, or older adults.", "The Morris 2006 and Wang 2021 observational analyses disagree on the direction of the copper-fat-cognition interaction and have not been reconciled by a third independent study.", "It is unknown whether the Morris 2006 harm signal reflects a true causal copper-fat interaction, confounding by overall diet quality, or chance in a single cohort.", "No data on copper status or intake relevant to cognition were located for Moroccan or North African populations specifically."], "_derived": {"composite_score": 4.0, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": null, "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": null, "best_grade_overall": null, "benefit_domains": {}, "n_outcome_rows": 4, "n_studies": 6, "n_verified": 6, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 1, "n_systematic_reviews": 0, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["harm", "inconsistent", "insufficient"]}}, {"id": "iodine", "name": "Iodine", "category": "C", "category_name": "Minerals and trace elements", "aliases": ["iodide", "potassium iodide", "iodate", "iodized salt"], "food_sources": ["iodized table salt", "seaweed (kelp, nori, kombu)", "fish and other seafood", "dairy products (iodine-based sanitizers in dairy farming raise milk iodine)", "eggs", "bread made with iodized salt/iodate dough conditioners in some countries"], "active_constituents": ["iodide (I-)", "incorporated into thyroxine (T4) and triiodothyronine (T3) via thyroid peroxidase"], "one_line_verdict": "Iodine is unambiguously essential for fetal and infant brain development and correcting deficiency in that window raises IQ, but once a person's thyroid status is normal there is no credible human evidence that extra iodine improves cognition in healthy children, students or adults, and the margin between helpful and harmful intake is narrow.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. Iodine is incorporated into thyroid hormones (T3, T4), which are required for normal fetal and infant neurodevelopment; this causal chain is established beyond dispute, and severe maternal iodine deficiency causes cretinism and profound intellectual disability (reviewed in [taylor2014]). The relevant open question for Brain Food is whether correcting *mild-to-moderate* deficiency, the situation in much of Europe and the UK, changes measurable cognitive outcomes, and whether any effect exists in adults rather than only the fetus/infant. In the UK ALSPAC cohort, first-trimester maternal urinary iodine-to-creatinine ratio below 150 microg/g (median population UIC 91 microg/L, i.e. mild-moderate deficiency) was associated with higher odds of the child scoring in the lowest quartile for verbal IQ at age 8 (OR 1.58, 95% CI 1.09-2.30, p=0.02) and for reading accuracy and comprehension at age 9 [bath2013]; this is a large, well-conducted observational cohort but cannot prove causation and does not itself test supplementation. The only adequately powered RCT of maternal iodine supplementation in mild-moderate deficiency (Thai mothers, median UIC 112 microg/L, 200 microg/day iodine vs placebo from early pregnancy, n=514 enrolled, n=157 children assessed at 5.7 years) found no benefit on child cognitive, language or motor development at any age, and a borderline worse fine/gross motor score at age 2 (p=0.05) [verhagen2020]. Pooling this and other RCTs, a 2020 systematic review (37 studies, 10 RCTs) found only 3 RCTs had assessed child neurodevelopment, only 1 was adequately powered, and meta-analysis of 2 RCTs showed no effect on cognitive (MD -0.18, 95% CI -1.22 to 0.87), language or motor scores, concluding evidence is insufficient to support supplementation recommendations in mild-moderate deficiency [dineva2020]. An older meta-analysis pooling 2 smaller RCTs in school-age children (a different population and a different, more deficient setting) reported a benefit on perceptual reasoning (SMD 0.55, 95% CI 0.05-1.04) and global cognitive index (SMD 0.27, 95% CI 0.10-0.44) but with significant heterogeneity [taylor2014]. In whom, and how large, in the Brain Food target population (healthy young adults/students). No RCT has tested iodine supplementation on cognition in euthyroid, iodine-replete or mildly-deficient healthy adults or students; the entire supplementation evidence base is in pregnant women and children. For adults with normal thyroid function, the only established effect of iodine is on thyroid hormone synthesis, and thyroid dysfunction (hypo- or hyperthyroidism) is well known to impair processing speed, memory and mood as a secondary consequence [cleveland2017] - but this is a disease-state effect, not a nootropic effect of iodine itself. Morocco context. Morocco mandated universal salt iodization in 1995; household coverage studies found 76-86% of sampled table salt adequately iodized but important regional and brand gaps remain, with some samples below or above the WHO target range [zahidi2016]. Historical northern-Morocco intervention studies (Rif region, historically a severe-deficiency goiter belt) confirmed introducing iodized salt to severely iodine-deficient schoolchildren corrected thyroid indices without inducing autoimmunity [zimmermann2003]. These are deficiency-correction data in a historically iodine-deficient population, not evidence that iodine enhances cognition above adequacy. Excess and safety. Iodine intake has a U-shaped, narrow safe range: thyroglobulin elevation occurs at both low and high intakes in infants [farebrother2019], and excess iodine (from kelp supplements, contrast media, amiodarone, or overcorrection of deficiency) can precipitate iodine-induced hyperthyroidism (Jod-Basedow) or hypothyroidism, both of which have cognitive/neuropsychiatric consequences. Conclusion for Brain Food: iodine adequacy is a prerequisite for normal brain development and function, and population-level correction of deficiency (iodized salt) remains a genuine public-health good, but no study supports marketing iodine as a cognitive enhancer for healthy, iodine-replete adults or students, and the dose-response margin is too narrow for aggressive fortification of a discretionary product.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "This is almost entirely a deficiency-correction story, not an enhancement story. The causal, high-grade evidence (fetal/infant thyroid hormone dependency for brain development) applies to the prenatal and early postnatal window; evidence of benefit from supplementing already-mild-moderate deficient pregnant women on later child cognition is weak and the best RCT is null [verhagen2020, dineva2020]. In healthy, iodine-replete children, students or adults there is no evidence base at all for an enhancement effect; thyroid hormone synthesis is maximal once iodine intake clears a modest threshold (around 100-150 microg/day), so there is no mechanistic reason to expect a dose-response cognitive benefit from supplementing beyond adequacy, and only a risk of harm from excess (Jod-Basedow, autoimmune thyroiditis flare).", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Marketing that implies iodine or iodine-rich foods (kelp, seaweed snacks) 'boost brain power' or improve IQ in adults or students conflates two very different things: (1) the well-established, legally recognised dependence of fetal/infant brain development on maternal thyroid hormone, which requires iodine, and (2) a claim that more iodine improves cognition in people who are not deficient, for which there is no supporting trial and one adequately powered RCT is null even in mildly deficient pregnant women [verhagen2020]. The EU has authorised two cognition-adjacent claims for iodine under Regulation (EC) No 1924/2006/Commission Regulation (EU) No 432/2012 (as amended): 'Iodine contributes to normal cognitive function' and 'Iodine contributes to normal functioning of the nervous system' (and 'normal thyroid function', 'normal energy-yielding metabolism'), conditioned on the product being a significant source of iodine per Annex of Regulation (EC) No 1924/2006 (at least 15% of the Nutrient Reference Value, 22.5 microg per 100 g/100 mL/serving); these claims are about maintaining normal physiology at adequate intake, not about enhancing cognition above normal, and the wording should not be read (or marketed) as implying a nootropic effect. Honest positioning for Brain Food: iodine adequacy (via iodized salt or seafood) is foundational nutrition, especially relevant to pregnant/lactating women and to vegans avoiding iodized salt and dairy; it is not a performance or focus ingredient for already-replete healthy adults.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Dietary iodide is absorbed almost completely in the small intestine, taken up by the thyroid sodium-iodide symporter (NIS) and organified by thyroid peroxidase into thyroxine (T4) and triiodothyronine (T3). T4 and T3 cross the blood-brain barrier and bind nuclear thyroid hormone receptors that regulate genes controlling neuronal migration, myelination and synaptogenesis, an established dependency in the fetal and early postnatal period. In children and adults, thyroid hormone also sets basal metabolic rate and modulates neurotransmission broadly; disease-level hypo- or hyperthyroidism (not iodine status per se, once intake is adequate) is linked to impaired processing speed and memory. There is no established mechanism by which iodine intake above the amount needed to saturate thyroid hormone synthesis would further change cognition.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Dietary iodide/iodate -> near-complete intestinal absorption -> plasma iodide", "detail": "Absorption is efficient and well characterised in humans; iodate is reduced to iodide before uptake.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Plasma iodide -> active uptake into thyroid follicular cells via NIS -> organification by thyroid peroxidase -> T4/T3 synthesis", "detail": "Core, long-established human endocrine physiology; deficiency raises TSH and thyroglobulin, and both low and high intakes raise infant thyroglobulin, indicating a narrow optimal range [farebrother2019].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Maternal/fetal T4 -> crosses placenta and blood-brain barrier -> drives fetal neuronal migration, myelination, synaptogenesis", "detail": "Established from severe-deficiency cretinism studies and supported by the ALSPAC association between first-trimester maternal iodine status and child IQ/reading [bath2013], though this is observational.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Postnatal/child/adult T4/T3 -> maintenance of basal metabolism and neurotransmitter turnover in the mature brain", "detail": "Overt hypo/hyperthyroidism from any cause impairs processing speed, memory and mood [cleveland2017]; this is a disease-state mechanism, not a graded response to iodine intake above adequacy.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Iodine supplementation in mild-moderate deficiency -> measurable improvement in offspring/child cognition", "detail": "Mechanistically plausible but not demonstrated: the only adequately powered RCT in mildly-deficient pregnant women found no child cognitive benefit at 2 or 5.7 years [verhagen2020]; pooled RCT evidence is insufficient [dineva2020].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Iodine intake above thyroid-saturating threshold -> further cognitive change in euthyroid adults", "detail": "No plausible or demonstrated mechanism; excess iodine instead risks precipitating thyroid dysfunction (Jod-Basedow or autoimmune hypothyroidism), which would impair rather than improve cognition.", "evidence_level": "plausible"}], "pathways": ["sodium-iodide symporter (NIS) thyroid uptake", "thyroid peroxidase organification / T4-T3 synthesis", "thyroid hormone nuclear receptor signalling in developing and mature neurons", "hypothalamic-pituitary-thyroid axis feedback (TSH, thyroglobulin)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "children_adolescents", "population_detail": "Offspring of mothers with mild-to-moderate iodine deficiency during pregnancy (UK ALSPAC cohort), IQ measured at 8 years", "dose_or_intake": "not applicable (observational; maternal urinary iodine-to-creatinine ratio <150 microg/g used as deficiency marker)", "duration": "chronic (prenatal exposure, outcome measured in childhood)", "grade": "B", "direction": "harm", "effect_summary": "Large UK cohort: maternal first-trimester iodine-to-creatinine ratio <150 microg/g associated with higher odds of child verbal IQ in lowest quartile at age 8 (OR 1.58) and worse reading accuracy/comprehension at 9, with a dose-response gradient across deficiency severity.", "effect_estimate": "OR 1.58 (95% CI 1.09-2.30, p=0.02) for lowest-quartile verbal IQ; OR 1.69 (1.15-2.49) reading accuracy; OR 1.54 (1.06-2.23) reading comprehension [bath2013]", "certainty_notes": "Large, well-adjusted (21 confounders) prospective cohort but observational; cannot exclude residual confounding by maternal diet/socioeconomic status; this is a child/prenatal-exposure finding and must not be generalized to supplementation benefit in adults or to non-deficient pregnancies.", "key_study_ids": ["bath2013"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "learning_retention", "population": "children_adolescents", "population_detail": "Offspring of mildly iodine-deficient Thai mothers supplemented in pregnancy (not healthy adults); cognitive/language/motor development assessed at 1, 2 and 5.7 years", "dose_or_intake": "200 microg/day oral iodine from early pregnancy to delivery", "duration": "chronic (prenatal, 5.7-year follow-up)", "grade": "B", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Only adequately powered RCT of maternal iodine supplementation in mild-moderate deficiency: no significant difference in child cognitive, language or motor scores at 1, 2 or 5.7 years; borderline worse combined fine/gross motor score at 2 years (p=0.05).", "effect_estimate": "Pooled 2-RCT meta-analysis: cognitive MD -0.18 (95% CI -1.22 to 0.87); language MD 1.28 (-0.28 to 2.83); motor MD 0.28 (-1.10 to 1.66) [dineva2020]", "certainty_notes": "Downgraded for imprecision (wide CIs, small effective sample after attrition: 157/514 assessed at final follow-up) despite being the best-powered trial; directly contradicts the assumption that correcting mild deficiency improves offspring cognition.", "key_study_ids": ["verhagen2020", "dineva2020", "taylor2014"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Euthyroid, non-pregnant healthy adults and students with adequate or mildly low iodine intake", "dose_or_intake": "not studied", "duration": "not applicable", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No identified RCT has tested iodine supplementation on cognitive outcomes in healthy, non-pregnant adults or students; the entire human intervention evidence base concerns pregnant women and infants/children. Mechanistic reasoning (thyroid hormone saturation at adequate intake) predicts no further benefit once euthyroid.", "effect_estimate": "not reported (no trial identified)", "certainty_notes": "Absence of evidence, not evidence of absence, but the prior probability of benefit in replete adults is low given the saturable, threshold nature of thyroid hormone synthesis; this row should not be upgraded without a dedicated adult RCT.", "key_study_ids": ["taylor2014", "cleveland2017"]}], "acute_matrix": [], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "pregnancy to 5.7 years", "population": "children_adolescents", "outcome": "child cognitive/language/motor development", "effect_estimate": "no significant benefit of 200 microg/day maternal iodine vs placebo; pooled MD -0.18 (-1.22 to 0.87) cognitive [dineva2020]", "evidence_quality": "moderate (best-available RCT, underpowered at final follow-up)", "limitations": "high attrition (157/514 to final follow-up), single setting (Thailand), may not generalise to UK/EU populations or to adult supplementation", "study_ids": ["verhagen2020", "dineva2020"]}, {"duration": "decades (population-level, observational)", "population": "mixed_adults", "outcome": "thyroid function maintenance in historically iodine-deficient region (northern Morocco)", "effect_estimate": "iodized salt corrected thyroid volume/TSH in previously deficient schoolchildren without inducing thyroid autoimmunity over 1 year", "evidence_quality": "moderate (prospective controlled field trial)", "limitations": "children, not adults; deficiency-correction setting, not enhancement; 1-year follow-up only", "study_ids": ["zimmermann2003"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Iodine has a narrow optimal intake range: both deficiency and excess raise thyroglobulin and risk thyroid dysfunction, which secondarily impairs cognition and mood. Excess iodine (from kelp/seaweed supplements, iodine-containing contrast media, amiodarone, or aggressive fortification) can precipitate iodine-induced hyperthyroidism (Jod-Basedow phenomenon) in susceptible individuals (nodular goiter, elderly, autoimmune thyroid disease) or hypothyroidism/autoimmune thyroiditis flare in others.", "adverse_effects": ["Jod-Basedow iodine-induced hyperthyroidism in susceptible individuals (pre-existing nodular goiter, elderly)", "iodine-induced hypothyroidism or thyroiditis flare, particularly in those with underlying Hashimoto's thyroiditis", "acute iodism (metallic taste, sialadenitis, GI upset) at very high doses", "thyroglobulin elevation at both low and high intake extremes in infants, indicating a narrow optimal intake window [farebrother2019 cited in mechanism discussion]"], "contraindications": ["known iodine hypersensitivity", "active Graves' disease or toxic nodular goiter (risk of exacerbation with excess iodine)", "caution in autoimmune (Hashimoto) thyroiditis with high-dose supplementation"], "drug_interactions": ["amiodarone (additive iodine load)", "lithium (additive hypothyroid risk)", "iodinated contrast media (additive load, relevant for repeated imaging)", "antithyroid medications (methimazole, propylthiouracil) effect may be altered by iodine intake changes"], "upper_limits": "EU/EFSA tolerable upper intake level for adults is 600 microg/day; US NIH ODS UL for adults is 1100 microg/day; both are far above the 150 microg/day (adult) to 220-250 microg/day (pregnancy/lactation) recommended intakes, but kelp-based supplements can deliver several mg per serving, far exceeding the UL.", "sleep_impact": "No established direct sleep effect at nutritional doses; untreated thyroid dysfunction (hypo- or hyperthyroidism) from iodine excess/deficiency can disrupt sleep as a downstream effect.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Population-level iodized salt programs have decades of surveillance data (including Morocco and other historically deficient regions); chronic supplementation RCTs beyond pregnancy/lactation in adults are not well studied.", "daily_use_appropriate": "probably", "special_populations": "Pregnant and lactating women need adequate iodine (deficiency risk for offspring) but should not exceed recommended prenatal doses without guidance; individuals with any thyroid disease (hypo-, hyperthyroid, nodules, autoimmune) should not supplement iodine without endocrinology input; vegans/vegans avoiding iodized salt and dairy are at higher risk of low intake and may benefit from modest supplementation or seaweed in moderation (watching for excess from high-iodine seaweed species like kelp).", "specialist_review_needed": ["endocrinology (thyroidology) for any product delivering iodine near or above upper limits, or marketed to populations with unknown thyroid status", "obstetric nutrition specialist for any prenatal iodine positioning"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "Iodine at nutritionally adequate doses (meeting but not substantially exceeding RDA) is safe for the general population, but the margin to excess is narrow (kelp supplements can deliver multi-milligram doses), and individuals with thyroid disease are at real risk of harm from supplementation; the gate is conditional on dosing within RDA/AI ranges and clear labeling against use by those with known thyroid conditions."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Iodine is an authorised 'vitamin/mineral' nutrient under EU food law. Under Commission Regulation (EU) No 432/2012 (the EU Register of nutrition and health claims made on foods, adopted under Regulation (EC) No 1924/2006), iodine carries authorised Article 13(1) general-function claims including 'Iodine contributes to normal cognitive function', 'Iodine contributes to normal functioning of the nervous system', 'Iodine contributes to normal thyroid function' and 'Iodine contributes to normal energy-yielding metabolism', conditional on the food being at least a 'significant source' of iodine (>=15% NRV per 100 g/100 mL/serving) per Annex of Regulation (EC) No 1924/2006. These are maintenance-of-normal-function claims, not enhancement claims, and should not be marketed as implying above-normal cognitive benefit. This wording is reported from standard knowledge of the EU claims register as of the research date and should be re-verified against the live EUR-Lex consolidated Annex before use in any label copy.", "us": "Iodine is a required nutrient on the US Nutrition Facts label with an established RDA/DV; no FDA-authorised health claim for cognition exists. Any structure/function claim ('supports healthy cognitive function') referencing iodine would need to meet FTC competent-and-reliable-scientific-evidence substantiation and DSHEA disclaimer requirements; given the null adult evidence base, a cognition-specific claim for iodine in replete adults is not well substantiated for US marketing.", "morocco": "Morocco has mandated universal salt iodization since 1995 (ONSSA/Ministry of Health public health program) to address historical endemic goiter in regions such as the Rif. Household coverage surveys show incomplete compliance (not all salt adequately iodized) [zahidi2016]. Novel-food or supplement-specific iodine dosing regulation in Morocco should be confirmed directly with ONSSA before product launch; this entry reflects public-health program status, not confirmed supplement-category rules.", "permitted_claims_examples": ["Iodine contributes to normal cognitive function (EU, conditional on >=15% NRV per serving; verify current EUR-Lex wording before use)", "Iodine contributes to normal functioning of the nervous system (EU, same condition)"], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "Iodine itself (as iodide/iodate salts) is an established, non-novel nutrient in the EU and has an NDI-exempt history of use in the US (standard RDA nutrient). Seaweed-derived iodine sources (certain algae species) may raise novel-food or heavy-metal-contamination considerations depending on species and processing; these should be reviewed case by case.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Iodine is already a standard food fortificant (iodized salt) and is naturally present in seafood and some dairy; it fits easily into a food matrix at RDA-aligned doses.", "formats": ["iodized salt", "potassium iodide/iodate fortification of baked goods or snacks", "seaweed-derived (kelp, nori) food ingredients", "standard mineral premix in fortified foods or capsules"], "stability": "Iodine (as iodate, more than iodide) is reasonably stable to heat, light and moisture in salt and food matrices, though some loss occurs with iodide forms during cooking/storage; iodate is preferred for fortification robustness.", "taste": "Iodized salt is essentially indistinguishable from plain salt at standard fortification levels; kelp/seaweed ingredients impart a distinct marine/umami flavor that may not suit all food formats.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Iodide and iodate are both well absorbed (>90%) from food and supplement matrices in the small intestine; bioavailability is not a significant formulation barrier.", "dose_feasibility": "The RDA (150 microg/day adults; 220-250 microg/day pregnancy/lactation) is easily delivered in a normal food serving (e.g., a few grams of iodized salt, a seaweed snack, or a modest fortified product) without approaching the UL (600-1100 microg/day); dose feasibility is not a barrier, but oversupplementation via concentrated kelp extracts is a real formulation risk to avoid.", "cost_availability": "Iodine (as potassium iodide/iodate) is inexpensive and globally available as a food-grade fortificant; cost is not a barrier.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 3, "effect_magnitude": 2, "safety": 6, "reproducibility": 4, "food_suitability": 9, "cost_access": 9, "rationale": "evidence_quality=3: strong causal biology for fetal/infant development but grade B/C human evidence for supplementation benefit even in mildly deficient pregnant women, and D (insufficient) for adults/students, the core Brain Food audience. effect_magnitude=2: best adult-relevant RCT is null; child effect sizes are modest and heterogeneous. safety=6: safe within RDA range but narrow margin to excess with kelp-type sources and real risk in thyroid-disease subgroups, hence not higher. reproducibility=4: the one adequately powered RCT contradicts the premise that correcting mild deficiency helps cognition, so the 'positive' literature is not well replicated. food_suitability=9: fits easily and cheaply into food via iodized salt. cost_access=9: inexpensive, globally available fortificant."}, "research_gaps": ["No RCT has tested iodine supplementation on cognitive outcomes in healthy, non-pregnant, iodine-replete or mildly-deficient adults or students.", "No adequately powered RCT confirms that correcting mild-to-moderate maternal iodine deficiency improves child cognition; the best trial is null.", "No dose-titration study links a specific adult iodine intake to a specific cognitive outcome short of overt thyroid dysfunction.", "Morocco-specific individual iodine status (urinary iodine surveys) in adults/students is not well characterized beyond household salt surveys."], "_derived": {"composite_score": 4.7, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": null, "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": null, "best_grade_overall": null, "benefit_domains": {}, "n_outcome_rows": 3, "n_studies": 7, "n_verified": 7, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 1, "n_systematic_reviews": 2, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["harm", "insufficient", "no_benefit"]}}, {"id": "iron", "name": "Iron", "category": "C", "category_name": "Minerals and trace elements", "aliases": ["iron", "Fe", "haem iron / heme iron", "non-haem iron / nonheme iron", "ferrous sulfate (sulphate)", "ferrous fumarate", "ferrous gluconate", "ferrous bisglycinate (iron bisglycinate chelate)", "ferric pyrophosphate", "sodium iron EDTA (NaFeEDTA)", "electrolytic / elemental iron", "carbonyl iron"], "food_sources": ["haem iron: red meat, liver and offal, poultry, fish and shellfish (haem iron is better absorbed and less affected by meal inhibitors)", "non-haem iron: legumes (lentils, chickpeas, beans), whole grains, nuts and seeds, dark leafy vegetables, dried fruit; bioavailability strongly modified by the meal", "fortified foods: wheat flour (national fortification programme in Morocco), breakfast cereals", "biofortified staples studied in trials: iron-rich pearl millet, beans; iron-fortified lentils", "estimated bioavailability 14-18% from mixed diets and 5-12% from vegetarian diets in people without iron stores [hurrell2010]"], "active_constituents": ["elemental iron (Fe2+ ferrous / Fe3+ ferric); in the brain a cofactor for tyrosine hydroxylase and tryptophan hydroxylase (dopamine/serotonin synthesis), myelin-producing oligodendrocytes and mitochondrial cytochromes", "haem iron (from haemoglobin/myoglobin) absorbed via a separate pathway from non-haem iron"], "one_line_verdict": "Correcting iron deficiency (with or without anaemia) in women, adolescents and children probably improves attention, intelligence-test scores and fatigue modestly (SMD about 0.4-0.6, low-to-moderate certainty), but there is no evidence that extra iron helps iron-replete adults, and unneeded supplemental iron carries real risks, so iron is a screening-and-correction nutrient, not a cognitive enhancer.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. Iron is essential for brain function, and iron deficiency is common: in a 2,012-woman national Moroccan survey, 34.3% of non-pregnant women aged 15-49 were anaemic, 29.8% iron deficient and 16.4% had iron-deficiency anaemia despite flour fortification [mekkaoui2025]; an earlier survey of urban women around Rabat found CRP-corrected iron deficiency in 45.2% [gartner2014]. In iron-deficient groups, oral iron reliably corrects iron status and anaemia (Cochrane: anaemia RR 0.39, Hb +5.3 g/L) [low2016]. Whether it improves cognition is less certain. Meta-analysis of 14 small, mostly weak RCTs found attention/concentration SMD 0.59 (95% CI 0.29-0.90) regardless of baseline status, and IQ gains only in anaemic participants (about 2.5 points); in premenopausal women, intelligence SMD 0.62 (0.15-1.10, 2 trials, n=76) but memory SMD 0.09 (-0.31 to 0.50) [falkingham2010]. The Cochrane review could not pool cognitive data and called the effect uncertain [low2016]. A 2025 meta-analysis of non-anaemic iron-deficient youth and menstruating adults reported intelligence d 0.46 and short-term memory d 0.53 but no effect on attention, and no effect when iron-deficient participants were excluded [fiani2025]. Key trials: 16 weeks of iron in US women 18-35 y linked ferritin gain to better accuracy [murraykolb2007]; 8 weeks of ferrous sulphate improved verbal learning in non-anaemic iron-deficient adolescent girls (n=73) [bruner1996]; biofortified pearl millet (Indian adolescents) and beans (Rwandan female students) improved reaction-time and memory-retrieval measures [scott2018, wenger2019]. Null or conflicting results: IV iron repletion in iron-deficient blood donors did not change NIH Toolbox cognition [hod2022], with only biomarker (brain iron, myelin, fMRI) signals in an ancillary study [hod2025]; non-anaemic iron deficiency was not associated with attention in 299 Australian women [cook2017]; adolescent SR found no effect on memory or intelligence [samson2022]. In whom. Benefit is plausible only for people who are actually iron deficient. For iron-replete healthy young adults and students there is no trial evidence of benefit [falkingham2010, fiani2025], and in iron-replete infants iron-fortified formula was followed by poorer cognitive scores at 16 years [gahagan2019]. Subjective fatigue is reduced in non-anaemic iron-deficient women (SMD -0.38) [houston2018, vaucher2012], which is not a cognitive outcome. Reproducibility. Most cognitive trials are small, short, single-site, and several fortification trials come from one research network with HarvestPlus co-authors; funnel asymmetry suggested publication bias [falkingham2010]. Unknown. No acute-dose cognition data exist (not expected biologically), and no large preregistered trial in iron-deficient young women with cognition as primary outcome; two completed registered trials of iron bisglycinate plus vitamin C on cognition and cerebral blood flow have no posted results.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Iron is a textbook deficiency-correction nutrient. Every positive cognitive trial enrolled people with low ferritin and/or anaemia (adolescent girls with ferritin <=12 ug/L [bruner1996]; women with iron deficiency or anaemia [murraykolb2007]; low-ferritin Rwandan students, 29-44% anaemic [wenger2019]; Indian adolescents in a high-deficiency setting [scott2018]). Where iron-replete people were analysed separately, no benefit appeared: Falkingham found no IQ effect in non-anaemic participants [falkingham2010], and Fiani found no effect once iron-deficient participants were excluded [fiani2025]. Even among the deficient, the evidence is mixed: a well-run IV repletion trial in iron-deficient blood donors was null on cognition [hod2022]. For iron-replete students and knowledge workers, extra iron is not an enhancer. It adds GI side effects [tolkien2015] and, in genetically susceptible people (C282Y homozygosity, about 0.44% of non-Hispanic whites [adams2005]), accelerates iron overload. The honest product logic is therefore: identify women and adolescents at risk (menstruating, vegetarian, frequent blood donors, high tea intake with meals), encourage testing (ferritin, Hb), and provide iron-rich or fortified foods at modest levels, rather than selling iron as a brain booster.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "The EU authorises 'Iron contributes to normal cognitive function' for any food that is at least a source of iron (15% NRV, i.e. 2.1 mg per 100 g/100 ml or per single-portion package). That claim is legally usable at food-level doses far below those tested in cognitive trials (60-80 mg elemental iron/day, or 15-20 mg/day from biofortified staples). It is a 'maintenance of normal function' claim based on the consequences of deficiency. It does not say iron improves cognition in adequately nourished people, and Brain Food must not imply that it does ('boosts focus', 'smarter', 'enhanced memory' would be misleading and outside the authorised wording). In the US, structure/function statements such as 'iron supports normal cognitive function' are possible under DSHEA with the FDA disclaimer, but FTC substantiation for any enhancement claim in replete people does not exist. The truthful positioning is 'helps prevent the cognitive and fatigue consequences of iron deficiency in people at risk', paired with advice to test iron status. For many consumers the most effective intervention is dietary: haem-iron foods or legumes eaten with vitamin-C-rich foods, and tea taken between rather than with meals. That is not a supplement opportunity.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Dietary iron is absorbed in the duodenum (non-haem via DMT1 after reduction to Fe2+; haem via a separate pathway), with uptake regulated by hepcidin, which rises for about 24 h after a 60 mg dose and reduces absorption of the next dose [stoffel2017]. Iron crosses the blood-brain barrier via transferrin receptors. In the brain it is needed for myelination, dopamine and serotonin synthesis (tyrosine and tryptophan hydroxylases) and mitochondrial energy metabolism. Animal models and infant studies show that deficiency during development alters myelin and dopamine signalling; in adults, iron repletion changes QSM-MRI brain-iron and myelin estimates and EEG measures [hod2025, wenger2019], but these biomarker shifts have not consistently translated into better measured cognition [hod2022]. Anaemia additionally reduces oxygen delivery, which may explain slower processing in anaemic people [murraykolb2007]. Excess iron catalyses oxidative stress (Fenton chemistry), which is why higher stores are a hypothesised risk in older age [andreeva2013].", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Oral iron -> duodenal absorption (non-haem via DMT1; haem separately), modulated by meal inhibitors (polyphenols, phytate, calcium) and enhancers (ascorbic acid, meat)", "detail": "Isotope studies: tea, peppermint tea, coffee and cocoa reduce non-haem iron absorption from a bread meal by 47-94% [hurrell1999]; mixed-diet bioavailability 14-18% vs 5-12% vegetarian [hurrell2010].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Hepcidin regulation -> fractional absorption falls after doses >=60 mg and when stores are replete", "detail": "Alternate-day 60 mg dosing gave higher cumulative fractional absorption (21.8% vs 16.3%) than consecutive days in iron-depleted women [stoffel2017].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Plasma transferrin -> transferrin-receptor-mediated transport across the blood-brain barrier -> brain iron in basal ganglia, oligodendrocytes", "detail": "IV repletion in iron-deficient donors produced trends toward higher whole-brain QSM iron (p=0.04) and myelin (p=0.02) [hod2025].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Brain iron -> myelination, dopamine/serotonin synthesis, mitochondrial energy metabolism", "detail": "Established in animal and developmental models; adult human evidence is indirect (EEG amplitude changes with biofortified beans [wenger2019]).", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Haemoglobin -> oxygen delivery to brain (anaemia pathway)", "detail": "Hb improvement associated with faster task completion; ferritin improvement with accuracy [murraykolb2007] (correlational within an RCT).", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Measured human outcome: attention, intelligence-test scores, memory in iron-deficient groups; subjective fatigue", "detail": "SMD ~0.5-0.6 in pooled small trials of deficient groups [falkingham2010, fiani2025]; null in iron-deficient blood donors [hod2022]; no benefit in replete people.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Excess iron -> oxidative stress, systemic overload (haemochromatosis), possible adverse brain-iron accumulation in ageing", "detail": "Observational inverse association of midlife ferritin with later cognition in women [andreeva2013]; organ toxicity in overload acknowledged by EFSA [efsa2024].", "evidence_level": "plausible"}], "pathways": ["oxygen transport (haemoglobin)", "myelination (oligodendrocyte iron)", "monoamine neurotransmitter synthesis (tyrosine/tryptophan hydroxylase)", "mitochondrial electron transport", "hepcidin-ferroportin regulation of absorption", "oxidative stress via Fenton chemistry (excess)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "deficient_or_low_baseline", "population_detail": "Iron-deficient or anaemic non-pregnant women of reproductive age (mostly 18-35 y), high-income and LMIC settings", "dose_or_intake": "Oral elemental iron approx. 60-80 mg/day (ferrous salts) or IV iron", "duration": "8-16 weeks (oral); ~4-5 months (IV)", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Pooled attention effect in women rests on one trial (SMD 0.53, CI crosses zero). 2025 MA: no attention effect in non-anaemic ID adults. Murray-Kolb: ferritin gain tracked better accuracy, Hb gain faster speed (dose-response, not ITT). Pilot RCT (n=24): attention changed with ferritin. IV repletion in ID blood donors: no cognitive effect.", "effect_estimate": "Attention SMD 0.53 (95% CI -0.06 to 1.12), 1 trial, n=47 [falkingham2010]; attention not significant [fiani2025]; NIH Toolbox fluid cognition no difference [hod2022]", "certainty_notes": "Downgraded for imprecision (few small trials), inconsistency (oral trials positive, IV donor trial null), and risk of bias (analyses by change in iron status rather than randomised arm in Murray-Kolb and Leonard). Women-only evidence; not transferable to men or replete women.", "key_study_ids": ["falkingham2010", "murraykolb2007", "leonard2014", "fiani2025", "hod2022", "low2016"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "deficient_or_low_baseline", "population_detail": "Iron-deficient (anaemic or non-anaemic) menstruating women and youth; intelligence-test outcomes", "dose_or_intake": "Oral iron, typically 60-160 mg elemental/day", "duration": "8-16 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Intelligence-type scores improve modestly in iron-deficient women and youth: Falkingham premenopausal-women subgroup SMD 0.62 (2 trials); 2025 MA RCT pool d 0.46 for intelligence and 0.53 for short-term memory, absent when ID participants excluded. Cochrane review of menstruating women could not pool cognition: uncertain.", "effect_estimate": "SMD 0.62 (95% CI 0.15 to 1.10), n=76 [falkingham2010]; intelligence d 0.46, short-term memory d 0.53 (CIs not in abstract) [fiani2025]", "certainty_notes": "Small, short, mostly unregistered trials; Fiani mixes RCTs and pre-post studies and populations 5-50 y; Falkingham noted funnel asymmetry (publication bias). A practical gain of roughly 0.5 SD would be meaningful for someone who is truly deficient, but is unconfirmed by a large trial.", "key_study_ids": ["falkingham2010", "fiani2025", "low2016", "murraykolb2007"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "children_adolescents", "population_detail": "Non-anaemic iron-deficient adolescent girls (US high school), and adolescents in LMIC", "dose_or_intake": "Ferrous sulphate 650 mg twice daily (Bruner); various", "duration": "8 weeks (Bruner); up to 6 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Bruner: iron improved verbal learning and memory (one of four tests) in 73 girls. Adolescent SR (24 intervention studies): iron may improve school performance and attention, but did not improve memory/recall or intelligence; most trials moderate-to-high RoB.", "effect_estimate": "Verbal learning p<0.02 (regression), effect size not reported in abstract [bruner1996]; qualitative synthesis only [samson2022]", "certainty_notes": "Single small positive trial with multiple outcomes versus a systematic review finding no memory effect; adolescent data must not be extrapolated to adults.", "key_study_ids": ["bruner1996", "samson2022"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "children_adolescents", "population_detail": "School-age children 6-12 y (mostly LMIC), anaemic subgroup driving effect", "dose_or_intake": "Oral iron supplements, varied doses", "duration": "Weeks to months", "grade": "B", "direction": "benefit", "effect_summary": "In school-age children, iron improved intelligence (SMD 0.46), attention (0.44) and memory (0.44), with larger IQ effects in anaemic children (0.79); no effect on school achievement. Falkingham found IQ benefit only in anaemic groups (SMD 0.54), not in replete children.", "effect_estimate": "IQ SMD 0.46 (95% CI 0.19-0.73); anaemic subgroup 0.79 (0.41-1.16); school achievement 0.06 (-0.15 to 0.26) [gutema2023]; anaemic SMD 0.54 (0.26-0.81) [falkingham2010]", "certainty_notes": "Consistent direction across two independent meta-analyses for anaemic children; downgraded for risk of bias and heterogeneity in non-anaemic groups (I2 81-82%). Deficiency correction in children; NOT evidence for adult enhancement.", "key_study_ids": ["gutema2023", "falkingham2010"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "processing_speed", "population": "deficient_or_low_baseline", "population_detail": "Food-based iron: biofortified pearl millet (Indian adolescents 12-16 y), biofortified beans (Rwandan female university students 18-27 y, ferritin <20 ug/L), fortified lentils (Bangladeshi adolescent girls), beef lunches (US young women)", "dose_or_intake": "About 15-20 mg/day extra iron from staple foods; fortified lentils ~200 g cooked 5 d/week", "duration": "4-6 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Biofortified millet: reaction time fell about twice as much as control (e.g. SRT -123 vs -63 ms). Biofortified beans: faster attention and memory responses (partial eta2 0.04-0.29), but GNG and most ANT scores null. Fortified lentils: better endline scores on some measures, with mixed directions. Beef vs non-beef lunches: no group difference.", "effect_estimate": "SRT change -123 vs -63 ms, p<0.01 [scott2018]; partial eta2 0.04-0.55 for selected RT/memory measures [wenger2019]; mixed task-level effects [barnett2023]; null group effect [blanton2013]", "certainty_notes": "Same research network across 3 trials with HarvestPlus co-authorship; many outcomes without multiplicity correction; subsamples of larger feeding trials; LMIC deficient populations. Supports the food-first concept but would not survive GRADE as more than low certainty.", "key_study_ids": ["scott2018", "wenger2019", "barnett2023", "blanton2013"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "other", "population": "deficient_or_low_baseline", "population_detail": "Non-anaemic iron-deficient adults (mainly menstruating women with ferritin <50 ug/L); outcome is SELF-REPORTED FATIGUE, not measured cognition", "dose_or_intake": "80 mg elemental iron/day oral (Vaucher); oral or IV in SR", "duration": "12 weeks", "grade": "B", "direction": "benefit", "effect_summary": "Iron reduced self-reported fatigue: SR SMD -0.38 (I2 0%, 4 trials, 714 participants). Vaucher RCT: fatigue score fell 47.7% vs 28.8% with placebo; no effect on quality of life, depression or anxiety. Objective physical capacity unchanged.", "effect_estimate": "SMD -0.38 (95% CI -0.52 to -0.23) [houston2018]; difference -18.9% (95% CI -34.5 to -3.2) [vaucher2012]", "certainty_notes": "Consistent, low heterogeneity, but subjective outcome susceptible to unblinding (GI side effects). This is fatigue, not cognition; matches the EU claim on tiredness and fatigue.", "key_study_ids": ["houston2018", "vaucher2012", "low2016"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Iron-deficient regular blood donors (ferritin <15 ug/L), 19-73 y, both sexes, IV iron", "dose_or_intake": "1 g IV low-molecular-weight iron dextran (single infusion)", "duration": "~4-5 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Correcting donation-induced iron deficiency did not change NIH Toolbox fluid cognition or well-being. Ancillary MRI: trends to higher brain iron and myelin and altered fMRI network engagement, no between-group cognitive difference.", "effect_estimate": "No significant group difference on any cognitive measure (numbers not in abstract) [hod2022, hod2025]", "certainty_notes": "Well-designed, double-blind, NIH-funded, no COI, but n=79 limits precision; IV route and mixed-sex donor population differ from menstruating women with chronic deficiency.", "key_study_ids": ["hod2022", "hod2025"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "neuroplasticity_biomarkers", "population": "deficient_or_low_baseline", "population_detail": "Iron-deficient adults: blood donors (QSM-MRI) and Rwandan female students (EEG)", "dose_or_intake": "1 g IV iron; biofortified beans", "duration": "4-5 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Brain iron and myelin estimates trended up after IV repletion (p=0.04, p=0.02). EEG amplitude and spectral power changed with biofortified beans. These are biomarkers, not cognitive outcomes.", "effect_estimate": "Whole-brain QSM iron p=0.04, myelin p=0.02 [hod2025]; EEG partial eta2 0.08-0.49 [wenger2019]", "certainty_notes": "Biomarker only; exploratory, small; must not be presented as cognitive improvement.", "key_study_ids": ["hod2025", "wenger2019"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "replete_or_high_baseline", "population_detail": "Iron-replete, non-anaemic adults and adolescents (the bulk of Brain Food's well-nourished target market)", "dose_or_intake": "Any supplemental iron", "duration": "Weeks to months", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "No IQ effect of iron in non-anaemic participants (Falkingham). Effects disappeared when iron-deficient participants were excluded (Fiani). Cross-sectionally, non-anaemic iron deficiency was not associated with attention in young women; only anaemia was.", "effect_estimate": "No significant pooled effect in non-anaemic subgroups (estimates not given in abstract) [falkingham2010, fiani2025]; ID vs replete attention p=0.34 [cook2017]", "certainty_notes": "Indirect (subgroup and observational data, no dedicated trial in replete adults). The absence of benefit plus known harms makes enhancement use unjustified.", "key_study_ids": ["falkingham2010", "fiani2025", "cook2017"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "learning", "population": "students", "population_detail": "University/high-school students in high-income settings with unknown or adequate iron status", "dose_or_intake": "Not studied", "duration": "Not studied", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No trial has tested iron on learning or exam performance in adequately nourished students. The only student trial (Rwanda) enrolled low-ferritin women, a third or more of them anaemic.", "effect_estimate": "not reported (no studies)", "certainty_notes": "Evidence gap; extrapolation from deficient groups is not allowed.", "key_study_ids": ["wenger2019", "samson2022"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "alertness", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy young adults, single dose", "dose_or_intake": "Not studied", "duration": "Single dose", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No acute cognitive studies were found. An acute effect is biologically implausible because brain iron and haemoglobin change over weeks to months. A single 60 mg dose raises hepcidin for about 24 h.", "effect_estimate": "not reported (no studies)", "certainty_notes": "Any 'instant focus' iron claim lacks evidence.", "key_study_ids": ["stoffel2017"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_aging", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Middle-aged and older French adults (SU.VI.MAX cohort), iron status measured in 1995, cognition ~13 years later", "dose_or_intake": "Iron status (ferritin), not supplementation", "duration": "~13 years follow-up", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "Higher midlife ferritin was associated with slightly worse later composite cognition in premenopausal women aged >=46 and with worse phonemic fluency in postmenopausal women. No association in men. No supplementation trials in older adults.", "effect_estimate": "Composite beta -0.09 (95% CI -0.17 to -0.00) per ferritin unit (premenopausal >=46 y) [andreeva2013]", "certainty_notes": "Observational, residual confounding; signals that 'more iron' is not better with age; no trial evidence of benefit.", "key_study_ids": ["andreeva2013"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "children_adolescents", "population_detail": "Iron-replete term infants (Chile) randomised to iron-fortified (12 mg/L) vs low-iron formula 6-12 months, followed to 16 y", "dose_or_intake": "12 mg/L vs 2.3 mg/L formula iron", "duration": "6 months exposure; 16-year follow-up", "grade": "C", "direction": "harm", "effect_summary": "Adolescents fed iron-fortified formula as infants scored lower on visual memory, arithmetic and reading comprehension. Infants with high 6-month Hb did worse with fortified formula; those with low Hb did better. This is a harm signal from iron given to replete individuals.", "effect_estimate": "Lower scores in fortified group (effect sizes not in abstract) [gahagan2019]", "certainty_notes": "Single cohort, long follow-up attrition, multiple outcomes. Infant population, cited only as a caution against giving iron to replete people, not for adult inference.", "key_study_ids": ["gahagan2019"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "60 mg elemental iron as ferrous sulfate, single morning dose", "onset": "Not applicable for cognition; hepcidin rises within 24 h", "duration_studied": "14-28 days of absorption measurement (no cognitive testing)", "domain": "No acute cognitive outcome studied; absorption only", "population": "Iron-depleted Swiss women 18-40 y", "effect_estimate": "Cumulative fractional absorption 16.3% (consecutive days) vs 21.8% (alternate days); serum hepcidin higher with consecutive dosing (p=0.0031)", "certainty": "high for absorption pharmacology; no cognitive data", "safety": "No grade 3-4 adverse events; GI effects common with ferrous salts generally", "study_ids": ["stoffel2017"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "16 weeks", "population": "149 US women 18-35 y of varied iron status (113 completed)", "outcome": "8 tasks of Detterman's Cognitive Abilities Test (accuracy, speed)", "effect_estimate": "Baseline: iron-sufficient women outperformed IDA (P=0.011) and were faster (P=0.038); after treatment, ferritin improvement associated with 5-7-fold improvement in performance, Hb improvement with speed", "evidence_quality": "moderate-to-low (randomised but key analyses by change in iron status)", "limitations": "24% attrition; analyses not by randomised arm; dose not stated in abstract; multiple tasks", "study_ids": ["murraykolb2007"]}, {"duration": "8 weeks", "population": "81 non-anaemic iron-deficient US adolescent girls (73 per-protocol)", "outcome": "4 attention/memory tests", "effect_estimate": "Verbal learning and memory better with iron (p<0.02); ferritin 27.3 vs 12.1 ug/L", "evidence_quality": "moderate (double-blind RCT) but single small trial", "limitations": "Per-protocol emphasis, one of four tests positive, high dose (650 mg FeSO4 twice daily)", "study_ids": ["bruner1996"]}, {"duration": "4-29 weeks (14 RCTs)", "population": "Children 6+, adolescents and women (no men or older people)", "outcome": "Attention, IQ, memory, psychomotor, scholastic", "effect_estimate": "Attention SMD 0.59 (0.29-0.90); IQ in anaemic SMD 0.54 (0.26-0.81), about 2.5 IQ points; no effect on memory, psychomotor, scholastic or in non-anaemic", "evidence_quality": "low (small, short, weak trials; publication bias suspected)", "limitations": "Allocation concealment reported in 1/14 trials; funding bias risk moderate-high in 13/14", "study_ids": ["falkingham2010"]}, {"duration": "Various (12 RCTs + 6 pre-post)", "population": "1,408 non-anaemic participants: 1,176 menstruating adults (mean 34.6 y), 204 youth", "outcome": "Psychiatric and cognitive outcomes", "effect_estimate": "RCTs: intelligence d 0.46, short-term memory d 0.53, anxiety d 0.34, fatigue d 0.34; attention and depression null; no effect when ID participants excluded", "evidence_quality": "low (mixed designs and ages)", "limitations": "Abstract only; CIs not available; pooling across 5-50 y and designs", "study_ids": ["fiani2025"]}, {"duration": "12 weeks", "population": "198 French non-anaemic menstruating women 18-53 y with fatigue and ferritin <50 ug/L", "outcome": "Fatigue (Current and Past Psychological Scale), QoL, mood", "effect_estimate": "Fatigue -47.7% vs -28.8% (difference -18.9%, 95% CI -34.5 to -3.2); no effect on QoL, depression, anxiety", "evidence_quality": "moderate", "limitations": "Subjective outcome; no cognitive tests", "study_ids": ["vaucher2012"]}, {"duration": "~5 months", "population": "79 iron-deficient US blood donors", "outcome": "NIH Toolbox fluid cognition composite, well-being", "effect_estimate": "No effect of IV iron repletion on any cognition or well-being measure", "evidence_quality": "moderate-high internal validity, imprecise", "limitations": "Small n; IV route; donors not typical consumers", "study_ids": ["hod2022"]}, {"duration": "6 months", "population": "140 Indian adolescents 12-16 y", "outcome": "Simple RT, Go/No-Go, ANT, face and cued recognition memory", "effect_estimate": "RT decreased about twice as much with biofortified millet (SRT -123 vs -63 ms; GNG -67 vs -30 ms; all p<0.01); iron intake 19.6 vs 4.8 mg/d from millet", "evidence_quality": "low-moderate", "limitations": "Multiple outcomes, practice effects large in both arms; HarvestPlus co-author", "study_ids": ["scott2018"]}, {"duration": "18 weeks", "population": "55 Rwandan female university students 18-27 y, ferritin <20 ug/L (cognitive/EEG subsample)", "outcome": "5 computerised tasks + EEG", "effect_estimate": "Hb eta2 0.16, ferritin 0.17; SRT eta2 0.11; memory-search and cued-recognition eta2 0.12-0.55; GNG and most ANT scores null", "evidence_quality": "low (subsample, many outcomes)", "limitations": "Subsample of 150 tested; multiplicity; ceiling accuracy; HarvestPlus co-author", "study_ids": ["wenger2019"]}, {"duration": "16 years after 6-month infant exposure", "population": "405 Chilean adolescents randomised as iron-replete infants", "outcome": "IQ, visual memory, achievement", "effect_estimate": "Iron-fortified formula group scored lower on visual memory, arithmetic, reading comprehension", "evidence_quality": "low-moderate (randomised exposure, long follow-up)", "limitations": "Attrition; multiple outcomes; infant exposure only", "study_ids": ["gahagan2019"]}], "safety": {"summary": "At food-fortification levels (about 2-5 mg per serving) iron is safe for most people. At supplement doses (>=45-60 mg elemental/day) it commonly causes GI side effects (OR 2.32 vs placebo [tolkien2015]; GI symptoms RR 1.99, constipation RR 2.07 in menstruating women [low2016]). Because the body has no regulated excretion route [hurrell2010], chronic excess accumulates, especially in hereditary haemochromatosis (C282Y homozygosity ~0.44% of non-Hispanic whites [adams2005]). Iron given to iron-replete infants was followed by poorer adolescent cognition [gahagan2019]. Higher midlife iron stores were associated with slightly worse later cognition in women [andreeva2013]. Accidental ingestion of iron tablets is a classic cause of fatal paediatric poisoning, which is why the US requires a warning label. Brain Food should not add iron to products for men, postmenopausal women or unscreened general audiences beyond source-of levels.", "adverse_effects": ["Gastrointestinal: nausea, epigastric pain, constipation, diarrhoea, black stools (OR 2.32 vs placebo for ferrous sulfate [tolkien2015])", "Metallic taste; tooth staining from liquid iron preparations", "Iron overload and organ toxicity (liver, heart, pancreas) with chronic excess, mainly in haemochromatosis or with repeated transfusion", "Acute poisoning in young children (potentially fatal)", "Possible adverse cognitive outcomes when iron is given to iron-replete infants [gahagan2019]"], "contraindications": ["Hereditary haemochromatosis or other iron-overload states (thalassaemia major, transfusion dependence, sideroblastic anaemia)", "Anaemia of unknown cause (needs diagnosis: GI blood loss, coeliac disease, thalassaemia trait common in North Africa)", "Known iron-replete status (ferritin normal/high) for supplemental doses", "Active inflammatory bowel disease flare (oral iron poorly tolerated)"], "drug_interactions": ["Levothyroxine: iron reduces absorption; separate by several hours (standard pharmacological guidance; not re-verified here)", "Tetracycline and fluoroquinolone antibiotics: chelation reduces absorption of both (standard guidance; not re-verified here)", "Levodopa, methyldopa, bisphosphonates, penicillamine: reduced absorption (standard guidance; not re-verified here)", "Proton pump inhibitors/H2 blockers: reduce non-haem iron absorption (standard guidance; not re-verified here)", "Calcium supplements and polyphenol-rich beverages (tea, coffee, cocoa, peppermint tea) taken with iron reduce absorption [hurrell1999, hurrell2010]"], "upper_limits": "EU: EFSA 2024 could not set a UL; safe level of intake 40 mg/day from all sources for adults including pregnant and lactating women; 10 mg/day (1-3 y) rising to 35 mg/day (15-17 y) [efsa2024]. US: IOM 2001 UL 45 mg/day for ages 14+ and 40 mg/day for 0-13 y, based on GI distress (National Academies text via web search, 2026-10-25). Reference intakes for context: US RDA 18 mg/day premenopausal women, 8 mg/day men and postmenopausal women; EU labelling NRV 14 mg.", "sleep_impact": "No direct effect at nutritional doses. Iron deficiency is associated with restless legs syndrome and poorer sleep (a scoping review was identified in searches but not extracted). Correcting deficiency may help sleep; supplementing replete people is not expected to.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Months-long supplementation trials in women (up to ~6 months) and decades of population fortification. Long-term safety of chronic supplemental iron in replete adults has not been demonstrated, and overload is a known risk in genetically susceptible people.", "daily_use_appropriate": "uncertain", "special_populations": "Pregnancy: needs are high, but supplementation should follow antenatal care protocols, not a functional food. Children: products with meaningful iron must be child-resistant; accidental overdose is dangerous. Men and postmenopausal women are rarely deficient and are at higher overload risk. Haemochromatosis: avoid added iron. Thalassaemia trait (prevalent in North Africa) can mimic iron-deficiency anaemia, so testing should come before supplementing. Vegetarians, frequent blood donors, adolescents and menstruating women with heavy losses are at higher risk of deficiency.", "specialist_review_needed": ["Haematologist/clinical nutritionist: screening-first protocol (ferritin, Hb, CRP) and dose levels", "Paediatrician/toxicologist: child-safety packaging if any product exceeds food-level iron", "Regulatory specialist: national maximum levels (EU member states, Morocco)"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "Food-level fortification (15-30% NRV per serving) passes for a general adult audience. Supplement-level iron (>=18 mg/day, especially >=45 mg) is appropriate only for people with confirmed or high-risk deficiency, and should not be sold as a cognitive enhancer to men, postmenopausal women, people with haemochromatosis or iron-replete consumers. Child-poisoning risk requires packaging and warnings."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Iron is a permitted mineral in food supplements (Directive 2002/46/EC) and for addition to foods (Regulation (EC) 1925/2006), with listed compounds (e.g. ferrous sulfate, fumarate, gluconate, bisglycinate, ferric pyrophosphate, NaFeEDTA for certain uses; full list not re-checked here). Seven Article 13(1) claims are authorised in Regulation (EU) 432/2012, including 'Iron contributes to normal cognitive function' (EFSA Journal 2009;7(9):1215, ID 253) and 'Iron contributes to the reduction of tiredness and fatigue' (EFSA Journal 2010;8(10):1740). Condition: the food must be at least a 'source of' iron (15% of the 14 mg NRV, i.e. 2.1 mg per 100 g/100 ml or per single-portion package) (wording via retained UK copy of the annex, legislation.gov.uk, accessed 2026-10-25). An Article 14 children's development claim about iron and cognitive development exists per EFSA opinions found in search; its exact authorised wording and conditions were not verified here (uncertain). No harmonised EU maximum level; national maxima for supplements vary. EFSA 2024 safe level is 40 mg/day [efsa2024].", "us": "Iron is a lawful dietary ingredient under DSHEA; common iron salts are GRAS/permitted food additives for fortification (specific CFR listings not re-checked here). Structure/function claims such as 'supports normal cognitive function' or 'helps maintain healthy iron levels' are permissible with the DSHEA disclaimer and FTC-level substantiation. Substantiation exists only for deficient populations, so enhancement claims for the general population would be unsubstantiated. Mandatory warning for solid oral dosage forms containing iron (21 CFR 101.17(e)): 'WARNING: Accidental overdose of iron-containing products is a leading cause of fatal poisoning in children under 6. Keep this product out of reach of children. In case of accidental overdose, call a doctor or poison control center immediately.' (Cornell LII copy, accessed 2026-10-25). IOM UL 45 mg/day (14+ y).", "morocco": "Uncertain. Wheat-flour fortification with iron has run since the 2000s. Sources disagree on the start date (2002 decision vs implementation from ~2006) and on the fortificant: the 2025 national survey describes electrolytic iron [mekkaoui2025], while an IAEA page reports a 2016 switch to NaFeEDTA (IAEA, accessed 2026-10-25). ONSSA controls millers and reported irregularities and withdrawn authorisations in 2024-2025 (press, accessed 2026-10-25). Food supplements fall under food-safety law 28-07 (ONSSA) and labelling decree 2-12-389. Products with therapeutic positioning engage the Ministry of Health/AMMPS, and a supplements reform was in progress in 2026 (see other monographs). No Moroccan claims register or iron-specific maximum level for supplements was found. Local regulatory counsel is required.", "permitted_claims_examples": ["EU (Reg. 432/2012): 'Iron contributes to normal cognitive function' - food at least a source of iron", "EU (Reg. 432/2012): 'Iron contributes to the reduction of tiredness and fatigue' - food at least a source of iron", "EU (Reg. 432/2012): 'Iron contributes to normal formation of red blood cells and haemoglobin' - food at least a source of iron"], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "None for established iron salts. Newer forms (e.g. novel iron complexes, micro-encapsulated or nano forms) may need EU novel-food or food-additive assessment and a US NDI notification; check case by case.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Feasible at source-of levels (2.1-4.2 mg per serving). Iron is reactive: soluble forms (ferrous sulfate, gluconate) cause metallic taste, fat oxidation and grey/black discolouration with polyphenols (tea, cocoa, fruit). Low-reactivity forms (micronised ferric pyrophosphate, electrolytic iron) are sensory-neutral but less bioavailable. NaFeEDTA and ferrous bisglycinate keep bioavailability in high-phytate matrices. Pairing with vitamin C (ascorbic acid) enhances absorption [hurrell2010].", "formats": ["Fortified flour, bread, couscous or semolina (staple vehicle in Morocco)", "Legume-based snacks or soups (fortified lentil model [barnett2023])", "Fortified breakfast cereals or bars with added vitamin C", "Iron bisglycinate capsules for screened deficient users (supplement, not functional food)"], "stability": "Iron itself is stable. It catalyses lipid oxidation and vitamin C degradation and reacts with polyphenols (colour change), so encapsulation or ferric pyrophosphate is needed in fat- or polyphenol-rich foods.", "taste": "Metallic or astringent at higher levels of soluble iron salts; acceptable at fortification levels with ferric pyrophosphate or encapsulated forms.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Haem iron is better absorbed than non-haem iron. Mixed-diet bioavailability is 14-18% vs 5-12% for vegetarian diets in people without stores [hurrell2010]. Tea, peppermint tea, coffee and cocoa inhibit non-haem absorption by 47-94% in single meals [hurrell1999], which matters for Moroccan mint tea with meals; drinking tea between meals is the practical mitigation (common advice; timing not tested in the studies extracted). Phytate (whole grains, legumes) and calcium also inhibit; ascorbic acid and meat enhance. Absorption rises when stores are low and falls after recent large doses because of hepcidin [stoffel2017].", "dose_feasibility": "Cognitive trials used 60-80 mg elemental iron/day (or 650 mg ferrous sulphate twice daily [bruner1996]). These are therapeutic doses that do not belong in a food and exceed the EU safe level and the US UL. Food-based trials delivered about 15-20 mg/day extra iron from staples eaten daily for 4-6 months [scott2018], which a fortified staple can match but a single snack serving cannot. A realistic functional food provides 2-5 mg per serving and serves prevention, not treatment.", "cost_availability": "Iron salts are commodity ingredients and widely available; Morocco already fortifies flour.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 4, "effect_magnitude": 3, "safety": 5, "reproducibility": 4, "food_suitability": 6, "cost_access": 8, "rationale": "evidence_quality 4: grade B only for anaemic children's intelligence and for subjective fatigue in deficient women; grade C for cognitive outcomes in deficient women and adolescents (small, weak trials, one null rigorous adult trial); no benefit or no data in iron-replete young adults and students, the core market. effect_magnitude 3: SMD ~0.4-0.6 in deficient groups would matter for them, but the effect in replete people is zero and the evidence is imprecise. safety 5: safe at food levels, but GI effects (OR 2.3), overload risk in haemochromatosis, child-poisoning risk and harm signals in replete infants and high-ferritin adults lower the score for supplement doses. reproducibility 4: oral trials in women and biofortification trials point the same way but come from few groups (several with HarvestPlus links); independent IV trial null; publication bias suspected. food_suitability 6: easy at source-of levels with appropriate fortificant, but sensory/oxidation trade-offs and inhibitors (tea, phytate) limit efficacy. cost_access 8: commodity, cheap, already in the Moroccan food supply."}, "research_gaps": ["Adequately powered, preregistered RCT of oral iron in iron-deficient non-anaemic young women (ferritin <30 ug/L) with cognition as primary outcome; the pilot estimated 26-84 per arm [leonard2014]", "Results of completed registered trials of iron bisglycinate +/- vitamin C on cognition and cerebral blood flow in women (NCT04469010, NCT04477018; completed 2019, no results posted): possible publication bias", "Moroccan data: cognitive or academic consequences of iron deficiency in Moroccan women and students; effectiveness of current flour fortificant", "Effect of mint green tea (Moroccan style), as opposed to black or peppermint tea, on iron absorption from typical Moroccan meals", "Dose-response for cognitive benefit vs ferritin threshold (at what ferritin does benefit stop?)", "Safety of chronic low-dose supplemental iron in iron-replete adults on brain iron and cognitive ageing", "Trials in men, postmenopausal women and older adults (none in Falkingham 2010)"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 4.6, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": "B", "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": "B", "best_grade_overall": "B", "benefit_domains": {"global_cognition": "B", "processing_speed": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 13, "n_studies": 28, "n_verified": 28, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 5, "n_systematic_reviews": 6, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["benefit", "harm", "inconsistent", "insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "magnesium", "name": "Magnesium", "category": "C", "category_name": "Minerals and trace elements", "aliases": ["magnesium", "Mg", "magnesium citrate", "magnesium glycinate / bisglycinate", "magnesium oxide", "magnesium L-threonate (MgT, Magtein, MMFS-01, ClariMem)", "magnesium chloride", "magnesium lactate"], "food_sources": ["pumpkin, sunflower, chia and sesame seeds", "nuts (almonds, cashews)", "legumes (chickpeas, lentils, beans - staples in Moroccan cuisine)", "whole grains and bran (whole-wheat bread, oats, barley, bulgur)", "dark green leafy vegetables", "cocoa and dark chocolate", "mineral-rich waters (Mg content varies by brand)", "fish, dairy and bananas in smaller amounts"], "active_constituents": ["Mg2+ ion (essential macromineral; cofactor for >300 enzymes incl. ATP-dependent reactions; voltage-dependent NMDA-receptor channel blocker)", "counter-anion varies by salt (citrate, glycinate, L-threonate, oxide); L-threonate is marketed as brain-penetrant but this is shown mainly in rodents"], "one_line_verdict": "Magnesium is an essential nutrient whose adequate intake matters for the brain, but no robust trial shows that supplementing well-nourished young adults improves memory, focus or learning; the eye-catching magnesium L-threonate results come from small manufacturer-funded trials, and sleep or anxiety benefits are low-certainty and modest.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. Magnesium is essential and its EU 'normal psychological function' and 'normal functioning of the nervous system' claims are authorised for foods that are at least a source. Beyond that, human cognitive evidence is thin. The 2024 preregistered meta-analysis found only three RCTs on cognition, judged them insufficient, and found inconsistent dose-response for dietary Mg in cohorts, but a consistent U-shaped association of serum Mg with dementia (low <0.75 mmol/L HR 1.43, 1.05-1.93; high >0.95 mmol/L HR 1.30, 1.03-1.64) [chen2024], echoing the Rotterdam [kieboom2017] and ARIC [alam2020] cohorts; ARIC found no association with cognitive decline itself, and Mendelian randomisation found no causal Mg-Alzheimer link [meng2022mr]. In WHIMS, intakes between EAR and RDA were associated with lower MCI risk, with no gain from higher intakes [lo2019]. This supports avoiding deficiency, not supplementing for enhancement. Magnesium L-threonate. The marketing narrative rests on rat data [slutsky2010] (one related mouse paper is retracted [li2013retracted]) and on manufacturer-funded trials: 44 completers aged 50-70 with complaints (d=0.91, sponsor-employed authors, uneven dropout) [liu2016]; a 30-day multi-ingredient formula trial reporting a ~2 SD memory-quotient gain, an effect not credible [zhang2022]; and the only trial in 18-45-year-olds, which found a borderline NIH Toolbox total-cognition benefit (+8.4 vs +5.6 points, p=0.043, ~0.19 SD, uncorrected) with null fluid composite and Raven's [lopresti2026mgt]. A null MgT trial in breast-cancer patients is reported in [chen2024]. Two nights of ZMA had no effect on Stroop or sleep in replete young men [edwards2024]. Sleep, stress and mood. In older insomniacs sleep-onset latency fell ~17 min (low/very-low certainty) [mah2021, abbasi2012]; the best adult trial at 250 mg/day bisglycinate showed d=0.2 on insomnia severity, possibly concentrated in low-intake people [schuster2025]; the 2026 systematic review rated certainty low to very low and inconsistent across objective measures [lopresti2026sr]. Anxiety evidence is poor quality [boyle2017, rawji2024]; the largest 'stress' trial had no placebo arm and was sponsor-run [pouteau2018, noah2021]. Depression meta-analysis (SMD -0.92) concerns clinical patients and small trials [moabedi2023]. What is unknown. No adequately powered, independent trial in students or knowledge workers; no acute cognitive effect has been tested; whether low-intake or low-status young adults benefit is untested. Practical relevance of current effects for a student is negligible.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Magnesium deficiency (from low intake of whole grains, legumes, nuts and greens; PPIs, diuretics, alcohol, diabetes, GI loss) plausibly impairs neuromuscular and possibly mood and sleep function, and cohorts associate low serum Mg with dementia [chen2024, kieboom2017, alam2020]. Correcting low intake is a legitimate nutritional goal; the hints of benefit come from low-intake or low-status groups (older adults with high Ca:Mg ratio [zhu2020], poor sleepers with low dietary Mg [schuster2025]). Enhancement in replete healthy young adults is not demonstrated: the only relevant trial is small, industry-funded and borderline [lopresti2026mgt], and ZMA was null [edwards2024]. High serum Mg is also associated with dementia risk, so 'more is better' is not supported.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Claims such as 'magnesium for brain power', 'the only magnesium that crosses the blood-brain barrier', 'reverses brain age by 7-9 years' or 'clinically proven memory boost' rely on rat studies, a retracted mouse paper and manufacturer-funded trials with completers analyses or implausible effects [slutsky2010, li2013retracted, liu2016, zhang2022, lopresti2026mgt]. The 'cognitive age' figure is an extrapolation from a 2.8-point composite difference. Sleep and calm claims are low certainty and modest [lopresti2026sr, schuster2025]. In the EU only the Reg. 432/2012 wording is lawful (e.g. 'Magnesium contributes to normal psychological function'; 'Magnesium contributes to normal functioning of the nervous system'; 'Magnesium contributes to a reduction of tiredness and fatigue'), on foods that are at least a source; none of these permits sleep, stress, memory or focus wording. Honest positioning: a nutrient to get adequately, ideally from legumes, whole grains, nuts and seeds.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Mg2+ is absorbed mainly in the small intestine (fractional absorption ~30-50%, higher when status is low), regulated by the kidney; serum Mg is tightly controlled and reflects stores poorly. In the brain Mg2+ blocks the NMDA-receptor channel in a voltage-dependent manner, modulates synaptic plasticity, and acts as a cofactor for ATP. Rodent work suggests raising CSF/brain Mg increases hippocampal synapse density; in humans, brain Mg changes after oral supplementation are not demonstrated in the cited trials. Proposed sleep effects involve GABAergic and HPA-axis modulation; human evidence is limited to small trials with hormone changes.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Oral Mg salt -> intestinal absorption (paracellular and TRPM6/7) -> renal regulation", "detail": "Absorption depends on dose and status; RBC Mg rose ~3% with MgT over 12 weeks [liu2016]; EFSA considered Mg bioavailable from L-threonate [efsa2024mgt].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Plasma Mg -> CSF/brain Mg", "detail": "L-threonate raised brain Mg in rats [slutsky2010]; no human brain-Mg measurement in the cited trials.", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Brain Mg -> NMDA receptor modulation, synapse density, LTP", "detail": "Rodent hippocampal data [slutsky2010]; one related AD-mouse paper retracted [li2013retracted].", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Mg status -> sleep regulation, HPA axis (cortisol, melatonin)", "detail": "Small elderly RCT reported melatonin up and cortisol down [abbasi2012]; sleep effects low certainty [lopresti2026sr].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Long-term Mg status -> vascular and metabolic health -> dementia risk", "detail": "U-shaped serum Mg-dementia associations [chen2024, kieboom2017]; MR null for AD [meng2022mr].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Measured human cognitive outcome", "detail": "Small borderline effect in one industry trial in 18-45 y [lopresti2026mgt]; null ZMA [edwards2024]; positive older-adult trials at high risk of bias [liu2016].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["NMDA receptor voltage-dependent block", "ATP-Mg cofactor / energy metabolism", "synaptic plasticity and synapse density (rodent)", "HPA-axis and sleep regulation", "vascular/metabolic (blood pressure, insulin sensitivity) pathways to dementia risk"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Adults 18-45 (mean ~37 y) with self-reported dissatisfied sleep; 14% students", "dose_or_intake": "2 g/day magnesium L-threonate", "duration": "6 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "One manufacturer-funded RCT (n=100): NIH Toolbox total cognition +8.4 vs +5.6 points (p=0.043), working-memory subtest p=0.033; fluid composite, attention, executive tasks and Raven's matrices null. A 30-day multi-ingredient MgT+phosphatidylserine trial in 18-65-year-olds reported an implausible ~2 SD memory gain.", "effect_estimate": "~0.19 SD on total composite (agent-computed; p=0.043, no multiplicity correction) [lopresti2026mgt]; Raven's p=0.953", "certainty_notes": "Single small industry-funded trial (cap B) downgraded for imprecision, multiplicity, indirectness (poor sleepers) and risk of bias; Zhang 2022 excluded from weight because of high risk of bias and confounding by phosphatidylserine.", "key_study_ids": ["lopresti2026mgt", "zhang2022"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "students", "population_detail": "University students and exam-period learners; standard Mg salts (citrate, glycinate, oxide)", "dose_or_intake": "not studied", "duration": "not studied", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No RCT of any magnesium form in students or knowledge workers measuring learning, memory or exam performance was found; meta-analysis identified no trials in healthy young adults.", "effect_estimate": "not reported (no trials)", "certainty_notes": "Absence of evidence; inference only from mechanism.", "key_study_ids": ["chen2024"]}, {"timeframe": "short_term_days", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Recreationally trained young men with adequate Zn/Mg intake", "dose_or_intake": "ZMA (Zn + Mg aspartate + B6) at bedtime", "duration": "2 nights", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "No effect on Stroop performance or sleep after two nights.", "effect_estimate": "p>0.05 (no estimate reported) [edwards2024]", "certainty_notes": "n=19, short exposure, combination product; downgraded for imprecision and indirectness.", "key_study_ids": ["edwards2024"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "alertness", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Single-dose effects on alertness/attention in rested adults", "dose_or_intake": "not studied", "duration": "single dose", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No acute single-dose cognitive trial of magnesium was found; magnesium has no plausible acute stimulant mechanism.", "effect_estimate": "not reported (no trials)", "certainty_notes": "No data.", "key_study_ids": ["chen2024"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_mci", "population_detail": "Adults 50-70 with subjective memory complaints, anxiety and poor sleep (MMSE>=24)", "dose_or_intake": "MMFS-01 (MgT) ~1.5-2 g/day", "duration": "12 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Composite cognition improved vs placebo (d=0.91) in 44 completers; sleep and anxiety no different from placebo.", "effect_estimate": "d=0.91, p=0.003 (completers) [liu2016]", "certainty_notes": "Single, small, sponsor-run trial with uneven dropout and per-protocol analysis; not replicated; high risk of bias.", "key_study_ids": ["liu2016"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Adults >65 with high dietary Ca:Mg ratio within a colorectal-cancer prevention RCT", "dose_or_intake": "Personalised Mg glycinate (mean ~216 mg/day)", "duration": "12 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Cognition +9.1% vs placebo in >65 y subgroup (MoCA change 2.3 vs 0.5); little effect in <=65 y.", "effect_estimate": "p=0.03 subgroup [zhu2020]", "certainty_notes": "Secondary subgroup analysis; imprecise; low-intake population (deficiency correction rather than enhancement).", "key_study_ids": ["zhu2020", "chen2024"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "dementia_risk", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Middle-aged and older community cohorts (Netherlands, USA, others)", "dose_or_intake": "Serum Mg or dietary Mg intake", "duration": "8-24 years follow-up", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Serum Mg is U-shaped with dementia (both low and high associated with risk); dietary-intake associations inconsistent; no association with cognitive decline in ARIC; MR null for Alzheimer's.", "effect_estimate": "Low vs mid serum Mg HR 1.43 (1.05-1.93); high vs mid HR 1.30 (1.03-1.64) [chen2024]; ARIC lowest vs highest HR 1.24 (1.07-1.44) [alam2020]", "certainty_notes": "Observational, residual confounding (kidney function, illness), single measures; MR null; cannot support supplementation.", "key_study_ids": ["chen2024", "kieboom2017", "alam2020", "lo2019", "meng2022mr"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "deficient_or_low_baseline", "population_detail": "Otherwise healthy stressed adults with low serum Mg; anxiety-vulnerable groups (mild anxiety, PMS)", "dose_or_intake": "300 mg Mg/day +/- B6; various", "duration": "4-8 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Subjective anxiety improved in about half of trials in vulnerable groups (poor quality); the largest stress trial compared Mg+B6 with Mg alone without placebo, so the ~42-45% stress reduction cannot be attributed to Mg.", "effect_estimate": "No placebo-controlled estimate; severe-stress subgroup Mg+B6 vs Mg 3.16 DASS points (0.50-5.82) [pouteau2018]", "certainty_notes": "Risk of bias (no placebo, sponsor), inconsistency, subjective outcomes.", "key_study_ids": ["boyle2017", "pouteau2018", "noah2021", "rawji2024"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Adults with diagnosed depressive disorder", "dose_or_intake": "Various Mg supplements", "duration": "varied", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Pooled depression score reduction SMD -0.92 across 7 small RCTs.", "effect_estimate": "SMD -0.919 (95% CI -1.443 to -0.396) [moabedi2023]", "certainty_notes": "Small trials, likely small-study effects and heterogeneity; clinical population, not transferable to consumers; medical supervision domain.", "key_study_ids": ["moabedi2023"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "other", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Sleep quality (not cognition) in older adults with insomnia", "dose_or_intake": "Up to ~500 mg/day oral Mg", "duration": "3-8 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Sleep onset latency shortened by ~17 min; total sleep time not significantly changed.", "effect_estimate": "SOL MD -17.36 min (-27.27 to -7.44) [mah2021]", "certainty_notes": "3 trials, 151 participants, moderate-high risk of bias; low to very-low certainty per review authors.", "key_study_ids": ["mah2021", "abbasi2012"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "sleep_related_cognition", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Adults 18-65 reporting poor sleep; outcome is subjective sleep and daytime functioning, not tested cognition", "dose_or_intake": "250 mg/day Mg bisglycinate; 1-2 g/day MgT", "duration": "3-8 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Bisglycinate: small ISI improvement (d=0.2) at week 4, other outcomes null; MgT: improved subjective sleep-related impairment but not objective sleep in one trial, wearable sleep scores in another industry-run trial. 2026 systematic review: inconsistent, low to very-low certainty.", "effect_estimate": "ISI -3.9 vs -2.3, d=0.2 [schuster2025]; no pooled estimate [lopresti2026sr]", "certainty_notes": "Inconsistency between subjective and objective outcomes, industry funding, imprecision.", "key_study_ids": ["schuster2025", "lopresti2026sr", "hausenblas2024", "lopresti2026mgt", "zhang2022cardia"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "not studied", "onset": "not applicable", "duration_studied": "none", "domain": "alertness / attention", "population": "healthy young adults", "effect_estimate": "no acute trials found", "certainty": "insufficient (D)", "safety": "single doses >250 mg supplemental Mg commonly cause loose stools", "study_ids": ["chen2024"]}, {"dose": "ZMA bedtime dose (Zn + Mg aspartate + B6)", "onset": "next morning", "duration_studied": "2 nights", "domain": "Stroop (executive) and sleep", "population": "young trained men, replete", "effect_estimate": "no effect (p>0.05)", "certainty": "C, single small trial", "safety": "no AEs reported", "study_ids": ["edwards2024"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "6 weeks", "population": "adults 18-45 with poor sleep", "outcome": "NIH Toolbox total cognition; Raven's", "effect_estimate": "+2.8 points (~0.19 SD), p=0.043; Raven's null", "evidence_quality": "C (single industry-funded RCT)", "limitations": "multiplicity, practice effects, sponsor involvement", "study_ids": ["lopresti2026mgt"]}, {"duration": "30 days", "population": "healthy Chinese adults 18-65", "outcome": "Clinical Memory Test MQ", "effect_estimate": "+21.5 vs +1.0 MQ points (implausible)", "evidence_quality": "very low (high risk of bias, multi-ingredient)", "limitations": "manufacturer-funded, phosphatidylserine co-intervention", "study_ids": ["zhang2022"]}, {"duration": "12 weeks", "population": "adults 50-70 with cognitive complaints", "outcome": "composite cognition", "effect_estimate": "d=0.91 (completers)", "evidence_quality": "C/low", "limitations": "n=44, sponsor-employed authors", "study_ids": ["liu2016"]}, {"duration": "12 weeks", "population": "adults >65 with high Ca:Mg intake", "outcome": "MoCA-based cognition", "effect_estimate": "+9.1% (p=0.03) subgroup", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "post-hoc age subgroup", "study_ids": ["zhu2020"]}, {"duration": "4-8 weeks", "population": "adults with poor sleep / older insomniacs", "outcome": "insomnia severity, sleep onset latency", "effect_estimate": "d=0.2 (ISI); SOL -17 min (older)", "evidence_quality": "C (low/very-low certainty)", "limitations": "subjective outcomes, small trials", "study_ids": ["schuster2025", "mah2021", "lopresti2026sr"]}, {"duration": "8-24 years", "population": "middle-aged/older cohorts", "outcome": "incident dementia", "effect_estimate": "U-shaped; HR 1.43 low, 1.30 high vs 0.85 mmol/L", "evidence_quality": "C (observational)", "limitations": "confounding, reverse causation; MR null", "study_ids": ["chen2024", "kieboom2017", "alam2020", "meng2022mr"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Magnesium from food is not limited (kidneys excrete excess). Supplemental magnesium causes dose-dependent osmotic diarrhoea, nausea and cramps; the EU (SCF 2001) UL for readily dissociable supplemental Mg salts and MgO is 250 mg/day for adults (and children >=4 y) and the US UL for supplemental Mg is 350 mg/day; food Mg is excluded from both. Severe, occasionally fatal, hypermagnesaemia occurs with laxative or antacid doses, mainly in kidney impairment, the elderly and bowel dysmotility [hypermag2019, fatalmg2018]. Trials at 250-500 mg/day and MgT 1-2 g/day reported no serious AEs over 3-12 weeks.", "adverse_effects": ["diarrhoea, loose stools, abdominal cramps, nausea (dose-dependent; oxide and chloride most laxative)", "hypermagnesaemia (hypotension, lethargy, arrhythmia) at high doses, especially with renal impairment", "L-threonate form: up to ~30 mg/day oxalic acid at max use, judged not of concern by EFSA [efsa2024mgt]"], "contraindications": ["chronic kidney disease / reduced eGFR (no supplements without medical advice)", "heart block or myasthenia gravis (medical supervision)", "pregnancy and lactation for magnesium L-threonate (excluded from EU novel food authorisation)"], "drug_interactions": ["chelation reduces absorption of tetracyclines, fluoroquinolones, bisphosphonates, levothyroxine and some HIV integrase inhibitors - separate dosing by several hours [mgdrugs2019]", "PPIs, loop/thiazide diuretics, some chemotherapeutics cause Mg loss (may justify monitoring, not self-dosing) [mgdrugs2019]", "potassium-sparing diuretics may raise Mg", "additive effects with Mg-containing antacids/laxatives"], "upper_limits": "EU/SCF (2001): 250 mg/day supplemental Mg from readily dissociable salts and MgO (supplements, water, fortified foods; food Mg excluded). US IOM (1997): 350 mg/day supplemental Mg for adults. EFSA 2015 adequate intake: 350 mg/day men, 300 mg/day women (total diet). EU NRV 375 mg; 'source' = 15% NRV (56.25 mg) per 100 g/100 ml or portion. US RDA 400-420 mg (men), 310-320 mg (women) (ODS page returned HTTP 403; values from IOM DRIs via secondary sources).", "sleep_impact": "No sleep disruption; possible small improvement in subjective sleep in poor sleepers (low certainty).", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Up to ~12 weeks in cognition/sleep RCTs; longer exposure only in cardiometabolic trials and observational cohorts; no long-term data for L-threonate beyond EFSA's toxicology review.", "daily_use_appropriate": "yes", "special_populations": "Kidney disease and elderly with constipation (hypermagnesaemia risk); medication users (chelation, diuretics, PPIs); pregnancy/lactation (L-threonate excluded; standard salts within UL acceptable but needs clinician input); children (UL 250 mg applies >=4 y in EU; products should not target children).", "specialist_review_needed": ["nephrologist/clinical pharmacist for renal and drug-interaction warnings", "regulatory affairs for L-threonate novel-food conditions", "food technologist for laxative effect at portion level"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "Safe within 250 mg/day supplemental Mg (EU UL) and outside kidney disease; requires label warnings on laxative effect, kidney disease and spacing from interacting medicines."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Essential nutrient; magnesium salts listed in Annexes of Directive 2002/46/EC and Reg. 1925/2006 (forms permitted for supplements and fortification). Ten authorised Art. 13 claims in Reg. (EU) 432/2012 (verified on legislation.gov.uk 2026-10-25), condition: food at least a source of magnesium. EU maximum levels in supplements still not harmonised (national limits) [X4 r45]. Magnesium L-threonate: EFSA 2024 safety opinion positive (max 3000 mg ingredient = 250 mg Mg/day, adults, not pregnant/lactating) [efsa2024mgt]; trade press reports novel-food authorisation in Oct 2024 and listing in Directive 2002/46 Annex II in July 2025 (Union list entry not read directly). Magnesium orotate dihydrate: EFSA could not establish safety (2025, trade press). No authorised claim for sleep, stress, memory or focus.", "us": "Dietary ingredient under DSHEA; common Mg salts are long-marketed (not new dietary ingredients) and several are GRAS for food use (specific CFR sections not re-verified here). Structure/function claims (e.g. 'supports healthy nervous system function') allowed on supplements with DSHEA disclaimer and FTC competent-and-reliable substantiation; cognitive-enhancement claims based on MgT trials would be weakly substantiated. NDI/GRAS status of magnesium L-threonate: uncertain (not checked).", "morocco": "Law 28-07 (ONSSA) and labelling Decree 2-12-389 apply; disease claims banned; nutrition claims limited to the positive list of Joint Order 281-16 (EU-like 'source of'); no authorised health-claim list or procedure found, so EU-style magnesium function claims are legally uncertain; no specific food-supplement decree found [X6]. Mg-specific Moroccan intake or status data were not found (regional EMR review indicates Mg intakes below recommendations in several countries) [doggui2021].", "permitted_claims_examples": ["Magnesium contributes to normal psychological function", "Magnesium contributes to normal functioning of the nervous system", "Magnesium contributes to a reduction of tiredness and fatigue", "Magnesium contributes to normal energy-yielding metabolism", "Magnesium contributes to electrolyte balance", "Magnesium contributes to normal muscle function"], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "Standard salts: none. Magnesium L-threonate: EU novel food (authorised after EFSA 2024 opinion; adults only, max 250 mg Mg/day, excludes pregnancy/lactation - verify Union list text before use). Magnesium orotate dihydrate: not authorised (EFSA safety not established). US NDI status of MgT not verified.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Good for 'source' level (>=56 mg Mg per portion) in cereals, bars, nut/seed products and legume snacks, often achievable naturally with seeds, cocoa, oats and pulses. Reaching the 250-300 mg doses used in sleep/stress trials requires added salts and approaches the EU supplemental UL in one portion; MgT doses (1-2 g ingredient) are capsule or powder-sachet territory.", "formats": ["whole-food based snacks (pumpkin seeds, nuts, cocoa, oats, chickpeas)", "fortified cereal or bars with Mg citrate/carbonate", "mineral water naturally rich in Mg", "capsules or sachets (citrate, bisglycinate, L-threonate)"], "stability": "Mineral salts are chemically stable; hygroscopic forms (chloride) need moisture control; may interact with other ingredients (pH, colour).", "taste": "Bitter/metallic and astringent (chloride, sulphate strongest); citrate sour; oxide chalky; glycinate milder.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Organic salts (citrate, glycinate) generally more soluble than oxide; absorption is higher when status is low; phytate and very high zinc or calcium doses may reduce absorption; MgT brain-targeting claim rests on rodent data.", "dose_feasibility": "Source claim (56 mg) feasible in a 30-50 g portion of seed/nut/cocoa-rich food. 250 mg/day bisglycinate or 1-2 g/day MgT not realistic in a normal food portion without off-taste and laxative effects; MgT limited to food supplements in the EU.", "cost_availability": "Magnesium salts are widely available commodity ingredients; L-threonate is a trademarked, single-source ingredient (Magtein/Threotech/AIDP).", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 3, "effect_magnitude": 2, "safety": 8, "reproducibility": 2, "food_suitability": 6, "cost_access": 7, "rationale": "Evidence quality 3: best target-population evidence is grade C (single industry-funded trial), cohorts observational. Effect magnitude 2: largest credible effect ~0.2 SD on a composite or d=0.2 on insomnia severity. Safety 8: well characterised, laxative effect and renal caution, clear UL. Reproducibility 2: positive MgT results are from sponsor-linked groups and unreplicated, with a retracted related animal paper. Food suitability 6: source-level easy from whole foods, trial doses not. Cost/access 7: standard salts cheap and widely available (no prices collected), MgT single-source."}, "research_gaps": ["Independent, preregistered RCT of standard Mg salts (250 mg/day) in students/knowledge workers stratified by baseline intake and RBC/serum Mg, with cognitive and sleep outcomes", "Replication of the Lopresti 2026 MgT result by an independent group with multiplicity-controlled primary outcome", "Human evidence that any oral Mg form changes brain Mg (MRS) at supplement doses", "Publication of registered MMFS/MgT trials with posted results (NCT02237235 schizophrenia n=60; NCT02210286 dementia n=17) and completed 2026 MgT trials (NCT07706283, NCT07015047)", "Moroccan population Mg intake and status data", "Validated Mg status biomarker for responder selection"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 4.4, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": "C", "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": "C", "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"global_cognition": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 11, "n_studies": 28, "n_verified": 28, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 7, "n_systematic_reviews": 6, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["inconsistent", "insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "other-trace-elements", "name": "Other trace elements (manganese, chromium, lithium, boron)", "category": "C", "category_name": "Minerals and trace elements", "aliases": ["manganese", "Mn", "chromium picolinate", "Cr", "trace lithium", "Li", "boron", "B"], "food_sources": ["manganese: whole grains, nuts, legumes, tea, leafy greens, shellfish", "chromium: broccoli, whole grains, nuts, meat; content highly variable and poorly characterised in food composition databases", "lithium: present only at trace, highly variable levels in drinking water and some foods (grains, vegetables grown in lithium-containing soil); not a deliberate food-fortification target", "boron: fruits (especially raisins, prunes, avocado), nuts, legumes, wine, coffee"], "active_constituents": ["manganese (Mn2+, essential cofactor for several enzymes)", "trivalent chromium (Cr3+, proposed insulin-sensitising cofactor)", "lithium (Li+, trace environmental element distinct from pharmaceutical lithium carbonate doses)", "boron (B, not yet officially classified as an essential nutrient for humans)"], "one_line_verdict": "These four trace elements are a mixed bag that should not be marketed together: manganese is essential but neurotoxic well before any cognitive benefit appears, chromium and boron have only small, old, unreplicated human signals, and trace lithium in drinking water is an interesting epidemiological association, not a food ingredient or supplement strategy.", "scientific_conclusion": "Manganese, chromium, lithium and boron are grouped in this monograph because each is a minor dietary mineral with some link to brain function, but the quality, direction and relevance of evidence differs sharply between them and they must not be presented as a single 'trace mineral cognition' story. Manganese is an essential nutrient (cofactor for manganese superoxide dismutase, glutamine synthetase and other enzymes) with a genuinely narrow safety margin for the brain: manganese neurotoxicity is well documented in occupational cohorts (welders, ferroalloy smelters) and in populations exposed to contaminated drinking water, producing a dose-related, Parkinsonism-like motor and cognitive syndrome (manganism) at chronic high exposure, with subtler neuropsychological and executive-function deficits detectable at lower occupational exposure levels using biomarkers of exposure (blood/urine manganese) and functional MRI showing altered executive-network activation in welders [hassani2016; seo2016]. This is deficiency-irrelevant, toxicity-relevant evidence: there is no human trial in this search showing that manganese supplementation above adequate dietary intake improves cognition in healthy adults, and the operative safety message is that the window between adequate and neurotoxic manganese intake is narrow, especially via inhalation or parenteral routes, making manganese fundamentally unsuitable as an 'more is better' cognitive-enhancement ingredient. Chromium (as chromium picolinate) has one small, dated, methodologically limited human signal: a placebo-controlled, double-blind trial in 26 older adults with age-related cognitive decline found 12 weeks of chromium picolinate supplementation did not improve learning rate or retention but reduced semantic interference on memory tasks and altered fMRI activation in thalamic/temporal/parietal/frontal regions in a subsample [krikorian2010]. This is a very small trial (n=26), in older adults with pre-existing cognitive decline (not healthy young adults or students), with a mixed primary result (most measures null, a secondary measure positive), and no systematic review or larger replication was located; grade C at best, and chromium's proposed mechanism (improving insulin sensitivity/glucose disposal) is itself only indirectly connected to cognition. Trace lithium in drinking water represents an entirely different evidence type: ecological/geographic correlation studies, not dietary interventions. A Texas county-level ecological study found trace lithium concentration in public well water inversely correlated with the rise in age-adjusted Alzheimer's disease mortality over time (r=-0.20, p=0.01), robust to adjustment for several but not all risk factors [fajardo2018], consistent with an earlier, well-known Danish nationwide cohort reporting a non-linear (U-shaped or threshold) association between municipal drinking-water lithium levels and dementia incidence, and with more recent US ecological work continuing to probe this association [hwang2026]. These are hypothesis-generating ecological correlations: they cannot establish that an individual drinking more lithium-containing water, let alone taking a lithium supplement, would reduce their personal dementia risk, because exposure is measured at the geographic/municipal level, not the individual level, and residual confounding by unmeasured regional factors (healthcare access, socioeconomic status, water hardness, other trace minerals) is a major limitation explicitly noted in this literature. Critically, trace environmental lithium (microgram-to-low-milligram per litre in water) is not a plausible food-fortification or supplement ingredient path for Brain Food: therapeutic lithium (carbonate, used in bipolar disorder at hundreds of milligrams per day) operates at doses roughly 100-1000x higher than trace environmental exposure and has a narrow therapeutic index with real toxicity risk (thyroid, renal, neurological), so this line of evidence is reported as a scientifically interesting but currently non-actionable epidemiological curiosity, not a candidate ingredient. Boron has the oldest and most specific human cognitive dataset of the four: three within-subject dietary-manipulation studies in healthy older men and women (approximately 0.25 vs 3.25 mg boron/2000 kcal/day) found that low boron intake was associated with poorer performance on tasks of attention, encoding/short-term memory, manual dexterity and eye-hand coordination, and with EEG changes (more low-frequency, less high-frequency activity, a pattern seen in general malnutrition and heavy-metal toxicity) [penland1994]. This single-author dataset from 1994 has not been independently replicated in the three decades since, used small within-subject convenience samples, and boron is still not formally recognised as an essential nutrient for humans by major regulatory bodies, so despite being statistically significant within its own studies it remains grade C/D (small, old, single-laboratory, unreplicated) rather than an established basis for a boron cognitive-enhancement claim. None of the four trace elements has evidence supporting direct cognitive enhancement in healthy young adults or students; the most actionable messages are safety-related (manganese's neurotoxic margin) and research-interest-related (lithium's ecological dementia association), not enhancement-related.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Manganese deficiency is essentially unseen in normal human diets (it is one of the few minerals where toxicity, not deficiency, is the practical concern); chromium deficiency is a disputed and probably rare clinical entity, historically described only in a few cases of long-term parenteral nutrition without chromium; lithium is not classified as an essential nutrient for humans at all, so 'deficiency' is not a meaningful framework, and trace dietary/water lithium exposure is categorically different from the pharmaceutical doses used therapeutically; boron is not yet officially essential, though Penland's studies suggest a function. For all four, the honest framing is that there is no demonstrated enhancement benefit from supplementing beyond typical dietary intake in healthy, adequately nourished adults - the chromium and boron human cognitive signals that do exist are small, old or confined to older/cognitively-declining populations, and must not be generalised to healthy young adults or students.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Chromium picolinate is sometimes marketed for 'brain and metabolic health' based on one small 2010 trial in older adults with existing cognitive decline, a population and context that cannot be generalised to healthy young consumers; the trial's primary memory outcomes were in fact null, with only a secondary interference measure positive. Trace lithium in water is occasionally invoked in wellness commentary as 'lithium protects the brain,' which conflates an ecological, municipal-level epidemiological correlation with an individual dietary or supplement intervention, and ignores that therapeutic-dose lithium is a regulated psychiatric medication with real toxicity - this should never be marketed as a food or supplement strategy. Boron's EEG and cognitive findings from a single 1994 USDA dataset are occasionally cited as if settled; they are suggestive but unreplicated for three decades. Manganese should never be marketed as 'more is better' for the brain given its well-documented occupational and water-borne neurotoxicity. The honest position for Brain Food is that none of these four trace elements currently supports a cognitive-enhancement product claim; manganese and boron may have a legitimate place in general micronutrient-adequacy formulation (not cognition-specific), and lithium and chromium should be excluded from cognitive-product consideration pending stronger human evidence.", "mechanism": {"summary": "The four elements have unrelated mechanisms. Manganese is an enzymatic cofactor (manganese superoxide dismutase, glutamine synthetase, pyruvate carboxylase) absorbed via DMT1 and other transporters; it accumulates preferentially in the basal ganglia at high exposure, producing a Parkinsonism-like motor/cognitive syndrome. Chromium(III) is proposed to potentiate insulin signalling, indirectly relevant to glucose-dependent neuronal and cerebrovascular function, particularly in insulin-resistant states. Trace lithium's proposed neuroprotective mechanism borrows from pharmacological lithium's known inhibition of GSK-3beta and promotion of neurotrophic/neuroprotective signalling, but this has not been demonstrated at trace environmental exposure levels in humans - it is inferred from pharmacology, not shown at trace dose. Boron's mechanism is the least understood; proposed roles in mineral metabolism (calcium, magnesium handling) and membrane function are plausible but not mechanistically resolved for the brain-specific EEG/cognitive findings.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Dietary/inhaled manganese -> absorption (DMT1 transporter, saturable) -> preferential accumulation in basal ganglia", "detail": "Well established in toxicology and occupational medicine; inhalation (occupational) bypasses hepatic first-pass regulation and is far more neurotoxic than oral intake at equivalent mass.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Chronic high manganese exposure -> basal ganglia dysfunction -> Parkinsonism-like motor syndrome and executive/attention deficits (manganism)", "detail": "Demonstrated in welders and ferroalloy smelters via neuropsychological testing and biomarkers [hassani2016] and altered executive-network fMRI activation [seo2016].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Chromium(III) -> potentiation of insulin receptor signalling -> improved glucose disposal in insulin-resistant states", "detail": "Established in metabolic physiology for chromium's canonical role; the extension to cognition in insulin-resistant older adults is proposed but only weakly tested [krikorian2010].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Trace environmental lithium exposure -> (proposed, extrapolated from pharmacological lithium) GSK-3beta inhibition and neurotrophic support -> population-level dementia risk association", "detail": "The mechanism is borrowed from pharmacological-dose lithium pharmacology; it has not been shown to operate at trace environmental concentrations in humans. The human evidence is purely ecological/geographic correlation [fajardo2018, hwang2026].", "evidence_level": "speculative"}, {"step": "Dietary boron -> proposed role in membrane function and divalent-cation (Ca, Mg) metabolism -> EEG spectral changes and cognitive/psychomotor performance", "detail": "Demonstrated as an association in three small within-subject USDA studies [penland1994]; the causal mechanism connecting boron to the specific EEG and cognitive changes observed is not resolved.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["manganese basal ganglia accumulation and neurotoxicity", "chromium-insulin signalling", "pharmacological-lithium-derived GSK-3beta hypothesis (unconfirmed at trace dose)", "boron-divalent cation/membrane function (mechanism unresolved)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Occupationally manganese-exposed adults (welders, ferroalloy smelters) versus unexposed controls", "dose_or_intake": "Occupational airborne/biomarker exposure (blood/urine manganese elevated versus reference ranges); not a dietary or supplement dose", "duration": "chronic (years of occupational exposure)", "grade": "B", "direction": "harm", "effect_summary": "Manganese biomarkers correlate with neuropsychological deficits in welders/smelters, and fMRI shows altered executive-network activation versus controls.", "effect_estimate": "Correlational biomarker-neuropsychological associations reported in [hassani2016]; altered fMRI executive-network activation in [seo2016]; exact effect sizes not extracted from abstracts", "certainty_notes": "Consistent across multiple occupational cohorts internationally, biologically plausible dose-response pattern, but cross-sectional/observational design limits causal inference strength; graded B for occupational toxicity evidence, not applicable to dietary intake in the general population.", "key_study_ids": ["hassani2016", "seo2016"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "older_adults_mci", "population_detail": "Older adults with age-related cognitive decline (not formally diagnosed MCI/dementia but below-normal baseline cognition)", "dose_or_intake": "Chromium picolinate (dose not extracted from abstract; standard trial doses are typically 200-1000 mcg/day for this type of study)", "duration": "12 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "In a 26-person placebo-controlled double-blind trial, chromium picolinate did not improve learning rate or retention but reduced semantic interference on memory tasks; fMRI showed increased activation in several regions in a subsample.", "effect_estimate": "Primary learning/retention outcomes not significant; secondary semantic-interference measure improved (exact statistics not reported in available abstract) [krikorian2010]", "certainty_notes": "Very small sample (n=26), single trial, mixed primary/secondary results, population is older adults with existing cognitive decline, not healthy adults; no independent replication found; downgraded for imprecision and indirectness to Brain Food's healthy-adult target population.", "key_study_ids": ["krikorian2010"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "dementia_risk", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Geographic/municipal populations (county level, not individuals) with varying trace lithium concentration in drinking water", "dose_or_intake": "Trace environmental lithium in public water supply (microgram-to-low-milligram per litre range); not an individual dose", "duration": "chronic (population-level trends over years)", "grade": "D", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Ecological studies find an inverse correlation between municipal drinking-water lithium levels and rising Alzheimer's disease mortality/dementia incidence trends; evidence is geographic/correlational, not an individual-level intervention.", "effect_estimate": "r = -0.20, p = 0.01 (Texas county-level correlation, robust to some but not all covariate adjustment) [fajardo2018]; additional nationwide US ecological analysis ongoing/recent [hwang2026]", "certainty_notes": "Ecological fallacy risk is substantial (county-level exposure assigned to individuals is not valid inference); residual confounding by unmeasured regional factors is likely; not a dietary intervention and not actionable as a food/supplement strategy; graded D (exploratory, non-individual-level evidence) despite statistical significance at the ecological level.", "key_study_ids": ["fajardo2018", "hwang2026"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Healthy older men and women in within-subject dietary boron manipulation studies", "dose_or_intake": "Approximately 0.25 mg boron/2000 kcal/day (low) versus approximately 3.25 mg boron/2000 kcal/day (high)", "duration": "within-subject dietary periods (exact duration not specified in abstract, consistent with controlled feeding study design typically weeks per period)", "grade": "C", "direction": "benefit", "effect_summary": "Low boron intake, compared to high intake, was associated with poorer performance on attention, encoding/short-term memory, manual dexterity and eye-hand coordination tasks, and with EEG changes (increased low-frequency, decreased high-frequency activity) in two of three studies.", "effect_estimate": "p < 0.05 for attention (all three studies), encoding/short-term memory (all three studies), manual dexterity (studies II and III), eye-hand coordination (study II); exact magnitudes not reported in available abstract [penland1994]", "certainty_notes": "Single-author, single-laboratory dataset from 1994, small within-subject samples, not independently replicated in three decades; boron not formally recognised as essential; downgraded for risk of bias (small samples, single lab) and lack of replication despite statistical significance within its own studies.", "key_study_ids": ["penland1994"]}], "acute_matrix": [], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "years (occupational)", "population": "mixed_adults", "outcome": "Neuropsychological/executive function deficits with chronic occupational manganese exposure", "effect_estimate": "Correlational, biomarker-based; see [hassani2016, seo2016]", "evidence_quality": "moderate (occupational cohort evidence, consistent across studies)", "limitations": "Cross-sectional/observational, dose-response inferred, not generalisable to dietary manganese intake in the general population.", "study_ids": ["hassani2016", "seo2016"]}, {"duration": "12 weeks", "population": "older_adults_mci", "outcome": "Chromium picolinate and memory/interference measures in older adults with cognitive decline", "effect_estimate": "Primary null, secondary positive [krikorian2010]", "evidence_quality": "low (single small trial)", "limitations": "n=26, mixed outcome pattern, no replication, not generalisable to healthy adults.", "study_ids": ["krikorian2010"]}, {"duration": "population trends over years", "population": "mixed_adults", "outcome": "Municipal drinking-water trace lithium and Alzheimer's/dementia incidence trends", "effect_estimate": "r = -0.20, p = 0.01 [fajardo2018]", "evidence_quality": "low (ecological correlation, not individual-level)", "limitations": "Ecological fallacy risk, residual confounding by regional factors, not an individual dietary intervention.", "study_ids": ["fajardo2018", "hwang2026"]}, {"duration": "within-subject dietary periods", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "outcome": "Dietary boron level and attention/memory/psychomotor performance, EEG spectral pattern", "effect_estimate": "p<0.05 across multiple measures [penland1994]", "evidence_quality": "low (single-author, unreplicated, small samples)", "limitations": "Not independently replicated in three decades; small within-subject samples; boron not formally essential.", "study_ids": ["penland1994"]}], "safety": {"summary": "The four elements have very different safety profiles. Manganese has a genuinely narrow margin between dietary adequacy and neurotoxicity, especially via inhalation (occupational) or parenteral (long-term IV nutrition) routes; oral dietary manganese toxicity in healthy people eating normal food is rare but manganese-contaminated well water and parenteral exposure are recognised causes of manganism. Chromium picolinate has a generally favourable short-term safety record in small trials but long-term safety data are limited and there are case reports of renal and other toxicity at high doses. Trace environmental lithium is not a supplement-dose exposure and carries no dietary safety concern at environmental levels, but pharmaceutical-dose lithium (a different exposure entirely) has a narrow therapeutic index with thyroid, renal and neurological toxicity risks that must not be confused with trace water lithium. Boron has a reasonable margin of safety at studied dietary levels but is not an established essential nutrient and high-dose boron exposure (well above dietary range) is associated with reproductive toxicity in animal studies.", "adverse_effects": ["Manganese: neurotoxicity (Parkinsonism-like syndrome, executive/attention deficits) with chronic high exposure, primarily occupational (inhalation) or via contaminated water or long-term parenteral nutrition", "Chromium picolinate: case reports of renal impairment and rhabdomyolysis at high doses; generally well tolerated short-term in trials", "Trace lithium (environmental): no established dietary safety concern at trace levels; pharmaceutical lithium (unrelated exposure level) has narrow therapeutic index with thyroid/renal/neurological toxicity", "Boron: possible reproductive/developmental toxicity in animal studies at high doses, well above typical dietary range"], "contraindications": ["Manganese: occupational exposure settings requiring exposure limits; liver disease (impaired biliary manganese excretion increases toxicity risk); long-term parenteral nutrition without manganese monitoring", "Chromium: chronic kidney disease (impaired clearance)", "Lithium: pharmaceutical lithium is contraindicated or requires monitoring in renal impairment, thyroid disease, pregnancy; trace environmental lithium has no established contraindication at normal exposure", "Boron: pregnancy (as a precaution given animal reproductive toxicity data at high doses, though human dietary exposure is far below those doses)"], "drug_interactions": ["Manganese: can interact with iron absorption pathways (shared transporter, DMT1)", "Chromium: may affect insulin/diabetes medication dosing requirements (potentiates insulin sensitivity)", "Lithium (pharmaceutical only, not trace): NSAIDs, diuretics, ACE inhibitors raise lithium levels; not relevant to trace environmental exposure", "Boron: no major established drug interactions at dietary levels"], "upper_limits": "Manganese: EFSA and US tolerable upper intake levels for oral manganese are not formally set by all bodies but WHO/EPA guidance values for drinking water manganese exist (WHO health-based value 400 mcg/L); adequate intake (AI) for oral manganese is approximately 1.8-2.3 mg/day for adults, with occupational airborne exposure limits set far lower in effective neurotoxic-dose terms. Chromium: no formal UL set by EFSA/IOM for chromium picolinate due to low absorption and apparent low toxicity at studied doses, though case reports of high-dose toxicity exist. Lithium: no UL for trace dietary/water lithium; pharmaceutical lithium has a narrow therapeutic range requiring blood monitoring, an entirely separate framework. Boron: US EPA reference dose approximately 0.2 mg/kg/day; EFSA has set a tolerable upper intake level of approximately 10 mg/day for adults.", "sleep_impact": "No established direct sleep effects for manganese, chromium or boron at dietary levels; pharmaceutical lithium has known effects on sleep architecture, not relevant to trace environmental exposure.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Manganese long-term occupational exposure is extensively studied (decades of occupational health literature); chromium picolinate long-term (multi-year) safety data in humans are limited; trace environmental lithium long-term ecological exposure is studied at the population level only; boron long-term human dietary exposure safety data are limited beyond the Penland studies and general nutrition surveys.", "daily_use_appropriate": "uncertain", "special_populations": "Occupationally manganese-exposed workers require medical monitoring regardless of diet; people with liver disease should avoid excess manganese supplementation; pregnant women should avoid boron supplementation beyond normal dietary levels as a precaution; anyone on pharmaceutical lithium therapy should not conflate trace water lithium messaging with their medication regimen and should follow their psychiatrist's guidance exclusively.", "specialist_review_needed": ["Occupational/environmental toxicology review before any manganese-containing product communication, given the narrow safety margin", "Psychiatry/nephrology review before any mention of lithium in consumer-facing material, to avoid confusion with pharmaceutical lithium dosing and risk", "Endocrinology review if chromium picolinate is ever considered for a product given its insulin-sensitising mechanism and interaction potential with diabetes medications"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "Manganese and chromium require conditional status and specialist review given narrow safety margins (manganese) or limited long-term human data and drug-interaction potential (chromium); boron is conditional pending stronger essentiality/safety consensus; trace water lithium is not a viable ingredient path at all and is excluded from any supplement/food formulation regardless of gate status."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Manganese and chromium have EU Nutrient Reference Values and authorised physiological claims (manganese: contributes to normal energy-yielding metabolism, normal bone formation; chromium: contributes to normal macronutrient metabolism, maintenance of normal blood glucose). No EFSA-authorised cognitive-performance claim exists for manganese or chromium. Boron is not classified as an essential nutrient in the EU and has no NRV or authorised claim; it is permitted in some food supplement forms subject to member-state rules. Lithium is not an authorised food ingredient at supplement doses in the EU and trace water lithium is addressed only via drinking-water quality standards, not food law.", "us": "Manganese and chromium have FDA Daily Values and are permitted supplement ingredients; chromium picolinate is a widely marketed OTC supplement ingredient in the US, generally for glucose-metabolism/weight-management claims rather than cognition. Boron is sold as a dietary supplement in the US under DSHEA but is not an FDA-recognised essential nutrient with a Daily Value. Lithium orotate/lithium supplements are sold in the US under DSHEA in very low doses as a 'trace mineral' despite lacking the quality evidence base and regulatory recognition that would support such marketing; FDA has issued warnings regarding unapproved lithium-containing products in some contexts.", "morocco": "ONSSA regulates manganese and chromium as general food/supplement minerals following Codex Alimentarius norms; boron and lithium supplement products would require case-by-case ONSSA assessment given the lack of established essentiality (boron) or food-ingredient status (lithium) in most regulatory frameworks.", "permitted_claims_examples": ["EU: 'Manganese contributes to normal energy-yielding metabolism' and 'normal formation of connective tissue' (authorised, non-cognitive)", "EU: 'Chromium contributes to normal macronutrient metabolism' and 'maintenance of normal blood glucose concentrations' (authorised, non-cognitive)"], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "Manganese and chromium (as common salts/picolinate) are established, not novel. Boron compounds used in supplements (e.g., boron citrate, sodium borate) vary in regulatory status by jurisdiction and form. Lithium as a food ingredient/supplement at any dose raises both novel-food/NDI-type questions and consumer-safety-communication concerns given confusion risk with pharmaceutical lithium; not recommended as a Brain Food ingredient path.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Manganese and chromium are feasible as fortification-level trace minerals in food matrices at adequate-intake doses (not therapeutic/supplement-superdoses); boron is feasible as a food-derived trace element (fruit/nut-based formulations) but has no clear functional dose target given unreplicated evidence; lithium is not suitable for food formulation at all given the dose/confusion/regulatory issues above.", "formats": ["Manganese/chromium: micronutrient premix in fortified foods or multivitamin-style formats at RDA/AI-level doses only", "Boron: naturally present via fruit/nut-forward formulation rather than isolated boron compound addition", "Lithium: excluded from formulation consideration"], "stability": "Manganese and chromium salts are generally stable through food processing; boron compounds vary by form.", "taste": "Manganese and chromium at RDA-level doses are not expected to impart noticeable taste; boron compounds can have mild taste effects at higher concentrations.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Chromium picolinate was specifically developed for improved absorption versus inorganic chromium salts, but overall intestinal chromium absorption remains low (<2%) regardless of form; manganese absorption is tightly regulated and inversely related to iron status; boron is well absorbed as borate/boric acid regardless of food form.", "dose_feasibility": "RDA/AI-level manganese (1.8-2.3 mg/day) and chromium (20-35 mcg/day, far below the 200+ mcg doses used in the Krikorian trial) fit easily into a food serving as general micronutrient adequacy, not as a cognitive dose; boron has no established functional dose; lithium dose feasibility is not applicable given exclusion from formulation.", "cost_availability": "Manganese and chromium salts are low-cost, widely available commodity micronutrient ingredients; boron compounds are moderately available; lithium is not sourced for food formulation.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 2, "effect_magnitude": 1, "safety": 4, "reproducibility": 1, "food_suitability": 4, "cost_access": 6, "rationale": "evidence_quality very low across all four elements for cognitive enhancement in healthy adults (manganese evidence is toxicity-focused and strong but not enhancement-relevant; chromium and boron have small, old, unreplicated single studies; lithium evidence is ecological, not individual-level); effect_magnitude scored 1 reflecting the small, inconsistent signals in chromium (mostly null) and boron (old, unreplicated) rather than any robust enhancement effect; safety scored low-moderate (4) reflecting manganese's genuinely narrow margin and lithium's confusion/regulatory risk, dragging down an otherwise more benign chromium/boron profile; reproducibility very low given single, old, small, unreplicated studies for three of the four elements; food_suitability and cost_access moderate, reflecting that manganese/chromium/boron can be formulated at adequate-intake levels in food but with no cognitive dose target, while lithium is excluded entirely from formulation."}, "research_gaps": ["No modern, adequately powered RCT has tested chromium picolinate for cognitive outcomes in any population since the small 2010 Krikorian trial (n=26); replication in a larger sample, including healthy adults, is needed before any conclusion.", "Boron's cognitive/EEG findings (Penland 1994) have not been independently replicated by any other laboratory in three decades; this is the single biggest evidence gap for boron.", "Trace lithium and dementia research is entirely ecological to date; no individual-level dietary or water-intake cohort with measured lithium exposure and prospective dementia outcomes was located, and such a study (or, more ethically complex, a trial) would be needed to move beyond hypothesis-generation.", "No study examines manganese, chromium, lithium or boron intake and cognitive performance in healthy young adults, students or knowledge workers specifically; all available human cognitive data are either occupational-toxicity (manganese), older/declining-cognition (chromium), ecological (lithium) or decades-old single-lab (boron)."], "_derived": {"composite_score": 2.7, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": null, "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"dementia_risk": "D", "sustained_attention": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 4, "n_studies": 6, "n_verified": 6, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 1, "n_systematic_reviews": 0, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["benefit", "harm", "inconsistent", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "potassium", "name": "Potassium", "category": "C", "category_name": "Minerals and trace elements", "aliases": ["K+", "dietary potassium", "potassium chloride", "potassium citrate"], "food_sources": ["bananas, oranges, dates", "potatoes (especially with skin)", "legumes (beans, lentils)", "leafy greens (spinach, chard)", "avocado", "dairy products", "fish (salmon, halibut)", "dried fruit (apricots, raisins)"], "active_constituents": ["potassium ion (K+), the principal intracellular cation, central to neuronal resting membrane potential and action potential repolarisation"], "one_line_verdict": "Potassium is indispensable for neuronal electrical signalling and is consistently associated with cardiovascular benefit as part of a high-potassium dietary pattern, but no trial isolates potassium itself as a cognitive enhancer in healthy adults, and the one dedicated cognition study found potassium intake unrelated to cognitive decline or brain imaging indices.", "scientific_conclusion": "Potassium is the principal intracellular cation and is mechanistically fundamental to the resting membrane potential and repolarisation of every neuron; this is textbook, established human physiology, but it does not imply that varying dietary potassium intake within or above the normal physiological range changes measurable cognition in healthy, non-deficient people. The DASH (Dietary Approaches to Stop Hypertension) eating pattern, which is relatively high in potassium (alongside magnesium, calcium, fibre and low sodium), has been studied for cognitive outcomes, but DASH is a multi-nutrient, multi-food dietary pattern, not a potassium intervention, so any cognitive signal from DASH or DASH-like trials (e.g., the MIND diet trial [mindtrial2023]) cannot be attributed to potassium specifically; this monograph treats DASH-cognition findings as background context, not as potassium evidence, consistent with the honesty rule against conflating mechanism/pattern evidence with ingredient-specific efficacy. The most directly relevant study found is a prospective analysis from the Health, Aging and Body Composition (Health ABC) cohort (n=1,194 community-dwelling older adults, mean age 74, 6.9-year follow-up): dietary potassium intake alone was not associated with odds of cognitive decline (assessed via the modified Mini-Mental State Exam), and neither sodium nor potassium alone was associated with structural or microstructural brain MRI indices; only the sodium-to-potassium ratio (not potassium alone) showed a weak positive association with cognitive decline (OR 2.02, 95% CI 1.01-4.03 per unit increase) [nowak2018]. This is a single cohort using food-frequency-questionnaire-derived intake (a method with substantial measurement error) and the authors explicitly concluded their findings 'do not support' dietary sodium reduction or potassium increase as a strategy to prevent cognitive decline. No RCT supplementing potassium specifically for cognitive enhancement in healthy adults, students or knowledge workers was located in this search. The clinical literature on potassium and brain function is dominated by disorders of extreme imbalance: hypokalemia and hyperkalemia both cause neuromuscular and, in severe cases, central nervous system disturbance (weakness, confusion, arrhythmia-related hypoperfusion), which are medical emergencies unrelated to ordinary dietary variation in healthy people. In summary: potassium is essential, population-level higher-potassium dietary patterns associate with cardiovascular benefits that could plausibly protect brain health over decades via blood-pressure and vascular pathways, but the one cognition-specific study of potassium intake itself was null, and there is no human enhancement evidence to support potassium supplementation as a cognitive intervention in Brain Food's target populations.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Clinically meaningful potassium deficiency (hypokalemia, typically from diuretic use, vomiting/diarrhea, or eating disorders, rather than simple low dietary intake in a healthy person) causes muscle weakness, cardiac arrhythmia and, in severe cases, confusion or lethargy - a medical condition requiring correction, not a performance-enhancement target. Modern Western and increasingly urban Moroccan diets tend to run low in potassium relative to recommended intakes (more processed food, less fruit/vegetable/legume intake) and high in sodium, which is the basis for public-health sodium-reduction/potassium-increase dietary advice (reflected in DASH), but this is a cardiovascular and blood-pressure rationale, not a cognitive-enhancement rationale. In adequately nourished healthy adults, no evidence supports that potassium intake above typical dietary levels enhances cognition; the one study that tested this directly (Nowak et al., Health ABC cohort) found no relationship between potassium intake and cognitive decline.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Potassium is sometimes marketed within 'electrolyte for brain function' or hydration-cognition products by inference from its undisputed role in neuronal signalling and from population-level DASH/cardiovascular-cognition associations. The honest position is that mechanism (potassium is needed for every neuron to fire) is not evidence of enhancement from supplementation in replete people, and DASH-pattern cognitive benefits (if any) cannot be attributed to potassium in isolation given the pattern includes many co-varying nutrients and behaviours. The directly relevant dietary-potassium-and-cognition study found no benefit. Any Brain Food communication about potassium should rely on its established cardiovascular/blood-pressure rationale and food-source messaging (fruit, vegetables, legumes), not an implied direct cognitive-enhancement claim.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Potassium is the dominant intracellular cation (~98% of body potassium is intracellular), maintained by the Na+/K+-ATPase pump against a steep concentration gradient; this gradient sets the neuronal resting membrane potential and its restoration (repolarisation) after each action potential, making adequate potassium status a precondition for all neuronal signalling. At the whole-diet level, higher potassium intake (as part of a high-fruit/vegetable, low-sodium pattern) is associated with lower blood pressure, which is plausibly protective for cerebrovascular and small-vessel brain health over decades, but this is an indirect, cardiovascular-mediated pathway, not a direct cognitive mechanism, and has not been isolated from co-occurring dietary factors in a potassium-specific human cognitive trial.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Dietary potassium -> intestinal absorption (highly efficient, ~85-90%) -> renal excretion as the primary regulator of balance", "detail": "Potassium homeostasis in healthy kidneys is tightly regulated; dietary variation within normal ranges produces minimal change in plasma potassium in people with normal renal function.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Na+/K+-ATPase pump maintains the intracellular/extracellular potassium gradient -> neuronal resting membrane potential and action-potential repolarisation", "detail": "Fundamental, universally established cellular electrophysiology; disruption (hypo/hyperkalemia) impairs neuromuscular and cardiac function.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Higher dietary potassium (as part of high-potassium, low-sodium dietary pattern) -> lower blood pressure", "detail": "Well-established in the DASH and sodium-potassium blood pressure literature; a population/pattern effect, not isolated potassium supplementation in most trials.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Lower blood pressure / better vascular health over years -> reduced cerebrovascular and small-vessel disease risk -> plausibly preserved cognitive function in aging", "detail": "Plausible epidemiological chain; the one study testing dietary potassium and cognitive decline directly found no association [nowak2018], so this chain is not demonstrated for potassium specifically.", "evidence_level": "plausible"}, {"step": "Severe potassium imbalance (hypokalemia/hyperkalemia) -> neuromuscular and CNS disturbance", "detail": "Clinically established in medicine (confusion, weakness, arrhythmia-related hypoperfusion) but reflects pathological imbalance, not a target for enhancement in healthy people.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}], "pathways": ["Na+/K+-ATPase and neuronal membrane potential", "renal potassium regulation", "DASH-pattern blood pressure reduction", "cerebrovascular risk pathway (indirect, unproven for potassium specifically)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_decline", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Community-dwelling older adults, mean age 74, Health ABC cohort", "dose_or_intake": "Dietary potassium intake as measured by food-frequency questionnaire (range reflecting typical US older-adult diets)", "duration": "6.9-year follow-up", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Dietary potassium intake alone was not associated with odds of clinically significant cognitive decline (3MS score) or with structural/microstructural brain MRI indices; only the sodium:potassium ratio showed a weak positive association with decline.", "effect_estimate": "Potassium alone: no significant association (exact OR not reported in abstract); sodium:potassium ratio OR 2.02 (95% CI 1.01-4.03) per unit increase [nowak2018]", "certainty_notes": "Single cohort, FFQ-based dietary assessment (substantial measurement error), no randomisation; downgraded for indirectness (ratio effect, not potassium alone) and imprecision (wide CI for the ratio finding, crossing close to 1 for related analyses).", "key_study_ids": ["nowak2018"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy young adults, students, knowledge workers", "dose_or_intake": "not studied", "duration": "not studied", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No trial of potassium supplementation or intake manipulation for cognitive outcomes in healthy young adults was located.", "effect_estimate": "not reported - no trials identified", "certainty_notes": "Absence of evidence; graded D pending future research.", "key_study_ids": []}], "acute_matrix": [], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "6.9 years", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "outcome": "cognitive decline (3MS) and brain MRI indices in relation to dietary sodium and potassium intake", "effect_estimate": "Potassium and sodium alone: no association; sodium:potassium ratio OR 2.02 (1.01-4.03) [nowak2018]", "evidence_quality": "low-moderate (single cohort, FFQ-based)", "limitations": "Food-frequency questionnaire measurement error, observational design, residual confounding, no randomisation.", "study_ids": ["nowak2018"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Dietary potassium from food is safe and generally under-consumed relative to recommended intakes in many populations; potassium supplements (especially potassium chloride in concentrated form) carry a real risk of hyperkalemia, particularly in people with impaired kidney function or those taking certain medications, which can cause dangerous cardiac arrhythmia. Severe hypokalemia (usually from non-dietary causes) likewise causes weakness, arrhythmia and in extreme cases confusion.", "adverse_effects": ["Hyperkalemia from excessive potassium supplementation, especially in renal impairment: muscle weakness, cardiac arrhythmia, potentially fatal", "Gastrointestinal irritation/ulceration from concentrated potassium chloride tablets", "Hypokalemia from non-dietary causes (diuretics, GI losses) causing weakness, arrhythmia, rarely confusion"], "contraindications": ["Chronic kidney disease or reduced renal function (impaired potassium excretion)", "Addison's disease or other causes of impaired potassium regulation", "Concurrent use of potassium-sparing diuretics, ACE inhibitors, ARBs, or NSAIDs without medical supervision"], "drug_interactions": ["ACE inhibitors, ARBs, and potassium-sparing diuretics (spironolactone, amiloride) can cause dangerous hyperkalemia when combined with potassium supplements", "NSAIDs can impair renal potassium excretion", "Digoxin toxicity risk is altered by potassium status (both high and low)"], "upper_limits": "No formal tolerable upper intake level (UL) has been set for potassium from food by EFSA or the US Institute of Medicine because toxicity from food sources in people with normal renal function is essentially unseen; supplement doses are typically capped (e.g., US OTC potassium supplements limited to 99 mg per dose due to historical GI injury reports) and prescription-strength potassium requires medical supervision.", "sleep_impact": "No established direct effect of dietary potassium on sleep architecture at nutritional intakes.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "High-potassium dietary patterns (DASH) have been studied over months to a few years for blood pressure and some cognitive outcomes; isolated long-term potassium supplementation for cognition has not been studied.", "daily_use_appropriate": "yes", "special_populations": "Patients with chronic kidney disease must strictly limit potassium intake under medical supervision; patients on ACE inhibitors/ARBs/potassium-sparing diuretics need monitoring if increasing potassium intake; healthy pregnant/lactating women and children can safely meet potassium needs through food.", "specialist_review_needed": ["Nephrology input for any potassium-fortified product given renal clearance is the dominant safety determinant", "Cardiology input if any concentrated potassium supplement format (versus food) is considered"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "Dietary/food-based potassium is safe and encouraged (pass); concentrated potassium supplementation requires conditional status pending confirmation of normal renal function and absence of interacting medications."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Potassium has an EU Nutrient Reference Value (NRV 2000 mg/day) and is permitted in food supplements under Directive 2002/46/EC with maximum levels sometimes restricted at member-state level due to hyperkalemia risk; no EFSA-authorised cognitive-performance claim exists for potassium (authorised claims relate to normal functioning of the nervous system and muscle function, and maintenance of normal blood pressure).", "us": "Potassium has an FDA Daily Value (4700 mg) and labeling requirements; FDA limits OTC potassium supplement tablets to 99 mg of elemental potassium per dose due to historical reports of small-bowel injury from concentrated potassium chloride tablets. No FDA-reviewed cognitive claim exists.", "morocco": "Potassium-containing foods and supplements fall under ONSSA's general food and supplement regulations; no specific cognitive claim framework exists; Morocco follows Codex Alimentarius reference values for labeling.", "permitted_claims_examples": ["EU: 'Potassium contributes to normal functioning of the nervous system' (authorised under Regulation (EC) 1924/2006)", "EU: 'Potassium contributes to normal muscle function' (authorised)", "EU: 'Potassium contributes to the maintenance of normal blood pressure' (authorised)"], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "Potassium salts used in food (chloride, citrate, etc.) are well established, not novel food/NDI issues.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Excellent as whole-food messaging (fruit, vegetables, legumes); potassium chloride is sometimes used as a sodium-reduction salt substitute in processed foods but has a metallic/bitter off-taste at higher substitution levels.", "formats": ["Whole-food based (fruit, vegetable, legume-forward products)", "Potassium chloride as partial salt substitute in formulated foods", "Potassium citrate in some beverage/supplement formats"], "stability": "Potassium salts are generally stable through food processing.", "taste": "Potassium chloride has a distinct bitter/metallic aftertaste that limits substitution levels in salt-reduction formulations (typically capped around 25-30% substitution before taste becomes objectionable).", "bioavailability_matrix": "Potassium from both food and supplement sources is well absorbed (~85-90%) in people with normal gut function; the limiting factor for formulation is taste and renal safety, not bioavailability.", "dose_feasibility": "An EU/US NRV-relevant potassium contribution (e.g., 300-600 mg per serving from fruit/vegetable concentrates) fits a food serving easily; no cognitive dose target exists since no cognitive benefit is established for potassium specifically.", "cost_availability": "Low cost; potassium-rich foods (bananas, legumes, potatoes) are widely available and affordable in Morocco, EU and US markets.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 2, "effect_magnitude": 0, "safety": 7, "reproducibility": 2, "food_suitability": 9, "cost_access": 9, "rationale": "evidence_quality low (grade C/D) because the only dedicated cognition study (Nowak 2018) was null for potassium itself and no RCT exists in healthy adults; effect_magnitude 0 because no enhancement effect is demonstrated; safety scored 7 because food-based potassium is very safe while concentrated supplementation carries a real renal/cardiac risk that must be managed; reproducibility low given a single study; food_suitability and cost_access high reflecting potassium's ubiquity in affordable whole foods."}, "research_gaps": ["No RCT has directly tested isolated potassium supplementation (versus a multi-nutrient DASH-type pattern) for cognitive outcomes in any population.", "The Nowak 2018 sodium:potassium ratio finding has not been replicated in an independent cohort and used FFQ-based intake estimation; a biomarker-based (e.g., 24-hour urinary potassium) replication would strengthen or refute it.", "No data exist on potassium intake and cognition specifically in healthy young adults, students or knowledge workers.", "The degree to which DASH-pattern cognitive benefits (if replicated) are attributable to potassium versus other co-varying nutrients/behaviours has not been isolated in any trial design (e.g., factorial manipulation)."], "_derived": {"composite_score": 4.0, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": null, "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": null, "best_grade_overall": null, "benefit_domains": {}, "n_outcome_rows": 2, "n_studies": 1, "n_verified": 1, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 1, "n_systematic_reviews": 0, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["insufficient", "no_benefit"]}}, {"id": "selenium", "name": "Selenium", "category": "C", "category_name": "Minerals and trace elements", "aliases": ["selenomethionine", "sodium selenite", "sodium selenate", "selenocysteine"], "food_sources": ["Brazil nuts (by far the richest and most variable source)", "fish and seafood", "organ meats (liver, kidney)", "eggs", "sunflower seeds", "cereals/bread (content depends heavily on soil selenium content of the growing region)"], "active_constituents": ["selenomethionine and selenocysteine, incorporated into selenoproteins including glutathione peroxidases (GPx), thioredoxin reductases and iodothyronine deiodinases"], "one_line_verdict": "Selenium is essential via antioxidant and thyroid-hormone-activating selenoproteins, but the largest randomized trial (PREADViSE, ~7500 older men) found selenium supplementation did not reduce dementia incidence, and there is no credible RCT evidence that it improves cognition in healthy, selenium-replete adults; its safety margin between adequate and toxic intake is unusually narrow for a mineral.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. Selenium is incorporated into selenoproteins, including glutathione peroxidases that protect cells from oxidative stress and type 2/3 iodothyronine deiodinases that activate/inactivate thyroid hormone; these biochemical roles are well established in humans. The hypothesis relevant to Brain Food is whether selenium supplementation or higher selenium status protects cognition, particularly in aging or mild cognitive impairment (MCI). The largest and most rigorous test is PREADViSE, an ancillary dementia-surveillance study nested in the SELECT prostate-cancer prevention trial: 7540 men aged 60+ randomized to selenium (200 microg/day as L-selenomethionine), vitamin E, both, or placebo for up to several years (trial converted to cohort follow-up after SELECT closed early for futility in 2009). Dementia incidence did not differ by arm: adjusted hazard ratio for selenium vs placebo was 0.83 (95% CI 0.60-1.13), i.e. no significant protective or harmful effect [kryscio2017]. This is a high-quality, adequately powered, long-duration RCT and is the single most important data point for this monograph; it argues directly against a cognitive-protective effect of selenium supplementation in older men, a population where benefit would most plausibly be expected if selenium mattered for cognition. Status-based (non-interventional) cohort data give a different, more positive signal: in the French EVA (Etude du Vieillissement Artériel) cohort, lower or declining plasma selenium over 4 and 9 years of follow-up was associated with greater cognitive decline in older adults, after adjustment for confounders [akbaraly2007]; this is an association between low selenium status and decline, not evidence that supplementing already-adequate people helps, and is subject to reverse causation (illness/poor diet lowering both selenium and cognition). A 2022 systematic review and meta-analysis of selenium supplementation specifically in patients with MCI or Alzheimer's disease found a signal of benefit on some cognitive measures but based on a small number of generally short, small, heterogeneous trials, graded as preliminary [pereira2022]. The 2018 Cochrane review of vitamin/mineral supplementation (including selenium-containing regimens) for maintaining cognitive function in cognitively healthy mid/late-life adults found no convincing evidence of benefit for any single micronutrient, including selenium, graded as generally low-to-moderate-certainty evidence of no effect [rutjes2018]. A small pilot RCT in older adults with MCI (n=31, 20 completers) found that one Brazil nut per day for 6 months (providing ~288.75 microg selenium) raised blood selenium and erythrocyte glutathione peroxidase activity and was associated with greater improvement than control on verbal fluency (p=0.007) and constructional praxis (p=0.031) on the CERAD battery [cardoso2016]. This is hypothesis-generating only: very small sample, food-based intervention confounded by other Brazil nut nutrients (healthy fats, other minerals), and conducted in an MCI population, not healthy adults. In whom, and how large, in the Brain Food target population (healthy young adults/students). No RCT has tested selenium supplementation on cognition in healthy, selenium-replete or even mildly-low young adults or students; the entire intervention evidence base is in older adults (PREADViSE, 60+) or MCI/AD patients (Brazil nut trial, Pereira review). Given that PREADViSE, the best-powered trial in the most plausible responder group (older men), was null, there is no basis to expect benefit in younger, healthy, better-nourished adults. What is unknown/not supported. There is no evidence for an EU-authorised cognitive health claim for selenium (unlike iodine and copper); EFSA-authorised selenium claims relate to normal thyroid function, normal immune system function, protection of cells from oxidative stress, normal spermatogenesis and normal hair/nails, not cognition. Reproducibility and bias. No major industry funding conflicts were identified in the key trials reviewed (PREADViSE/SELECT was NIH-funded with declared no conflicts; the Brazil nut trial was academic/Brazilian public funding); this is a case where the overall evidence quality, not funding bias, is the limiting factor. Selenium also has an unusually narrow range between the recommended intake (55 microg/day adults) and documented toxicity (selenosis) from supplements, which is a safety-relevant consideration independent of efficacy.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Classic frank selenium deficiency (as in endemic low-selenium-soil regions, e.g. parts of China with Keshan disease) is rare in adequately varied Western/Moroccan diets because even a single Brazil nut or modest fish intake supplies ample selenium; most people in Europe, the US and Morocco are not frankly selenium-deficient, though selenium intake varies widely by regional soil content and some European populations (parts of the UK, parts of continental Europe) have historically had borderline-low intake. The cohort evidence (EVA study) linking lower selenium status to faster cognitive decline [akbaraly2007] is consistent with a correction-of-marginal-deficiency hypothesis in older, possibly undernourished adults, but the single RCT powered to test this directly in a comparable population (PREADViSE) found no benefit of raising selenium intake on dementia incidence [kryscio2017]. There is no enhancement evidence at all: no trial shows that supplementing adequately selenium-replete healthy adults or students improves cognition above baseline, and given selenium's narrow safety margin, uncontrolled 'more is better' supplementation is actively discouraged rather than merely unsupported.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Marketing claims suggesting selenium or Brazil nuts 'protect the brain' or 'prevent cognitive decline' typically cite status-cohort associations (like EVA) or the small Brazil nut MCI pilot trial while omitting PREADViSE, the largest and most definitive RCT, which found no dementia-protective effect of selenium supplementation in older men (HR 0.83, 95% CI 0.60-1.13, not significant) [kryscio2017]. The EU has not authorised any cognition-related health claim for selenium under Regulation (EC) No 1924/2006/Commission Regulation (EU) No 432/2012; authorised selenium claims concern normal thyroid function, normal function of the immune system, protection of cells from oxidative stress, normal spermatogenesis, and maintenance of normal hair and nails - none of these is a cognitive claim, and this should be checked against the live EU claims register before any communication. Honest positioning: selenium is an essential antioxidant/thyroid-support mineral readily obtained from 1-2 Brazil nuts or modest seafood/meat intake per week; the 'Brazil nuts for brain health' narrative popular in wellness media overstates a preliminary, small, MCI-population pilot finding and ignores the null result from the far larger and more rigorous PREADViSE trial in the exact population (older adults) where benefit would be expected if it existed.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Dietary selenium (mainly as selenomethionine from plants/nuts or selenocysteine from animal foods) is absorbed in the small intestine and incorporated, via a dedicated co-translational insertion machinery (selenocysteine tRNA), into at least 25 human selenoproteins. The two families most relevant to the brain are glutathione peroxidases (antioxidant defense against reactive oxygen species, which the brain is particularly vulnerable to given its high oxygen consumption) and iodothyronine deiodinases (which convert T4 to the active T3 thyroid hormone, including locally in brain tissue). Selenoprotein P transports selenium to the brain and is itself important for neuronal selenium supply. The hypothesized cognitive-protective pathway is therefore indirect: adequate selenium -> adequate selenoprotein antioxidant capacity and local thyroid hormone activation -> protection against oxidative-stress-driven neurodegeneration. This is biologically plausible and demonstrated at the biomarker level (selenoprotein/GPx activity responds to intake), but the clinical translation to measurable cognitive protection is not demonstrated in the best-powered human RCT.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Dietary selenomethionine/selenocysteine -> intestinal absorption (generally efficient, >70-90%) -> plasma selenium pool", "detail": "Well-characterised human absorption kinetics; absorption efficiency is higher for organic (food) forms than some inorganic supplement forms.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Plasma selenium -> incorporation into selenoproteins via selenocysteine tRNA machinery, including glutathione peroxidases and iodothyronine deiodinases", "detail": "Demonstrated by dose-dependent changes in erythrocyte GPx activity after Brazil nut or supplement intake [cardoso2016]; selenoprotein synthesis is prioritised/maintained in brain tissue even under systemic deficiency, suggesting brain selenium is partly protected.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Selenoprotein (GPx) activity -> reduction of oxidative stress burden in neural tissue", "detail": "Mechanistically plausible antioxidant pathway; demonstrated biochemically but not directly linked to a measured cognitive outcome in the same trial.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Selenium-dependent deiodinase activity -> local brain thyroid hormone (T3) availability", "detail": "Established biochemical pathway; its downstream cognitive relevance overlaps with, but is distinct from, iodine/thyroid status discussed under the iodine monograph.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Lower selenium status -> faster cognitive decline in older observational cohorts", "detail": "EVA cohort found declining plasma selenium associated with greater cognitive decline over 4-9 years [akbaraly2007]; association, not causation, and subject to reverse-causation/illness confounding.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Selenium supplementation -> reduced dementia/cognitive decline incidence in older adults", "detail": "Directly tested in the largest RCT (PREADViSE, n=7540, up to ~13 years exposure/follow-up): selenium vs placebo hazard ratio for dementia 0.83 (95% CI 0.60-1.13), not significant [kryscio2017]. This is the strongest available causal test and it is null.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["glutathione peroxidase (GPx) antioxidant selenoproteins", "iodothyronine deiodinase (thyroid hormone activation)", "selenoprotein P (brain selenium transport)", "thioredoxin reductase (redox regulation, less brain-specific evidence)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "dementia_risk", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Asymptomatic men aged 60+ (SELECT/PREADViSE participants), largely selenium-replete US population", "dose_or_intake": "200 microg/day selenium (as L-selenomethionine), alone or with vitamin E", "duration": "chronic (trial plus cohort follow-up, up to ~13 years total exposure/observation)", "grade": "B", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Largest and best-powered RCT testing selenium for dementia prevention: no difference in dementia incidence between selenium and placebo arms.", "effect_estimate": "HR 0.83 (95% CI 0.60-1.13) for selenium vs placebo; HR 1.00 (0.75-1.35) for combination with vitamin E [kryscio2017]", "certainty_notes": "High-quality RCT with long exposure and large sample, but downgraded from A to B because the trial transitioned from randomized intervention to an observational cohort phase partway through (loss of some randomization integrity after SELECT closed early), and dementia ascertainment relied on telephone screening plus physician follow-up rather than uniform in-person neuropsychological testing.", "key_study_ids": ["kryscio2017"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_decline", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Community-dwelling older French adults, EVA cohort, selenium status assessed longitudinally", "dose_or_intake": "not applicable (observational; plasma selenium status, not supplementation)", "duration": "chronic (4-9 year follow-up)", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Lower or declining plasma selenium over time was associated with greater subsequent cognitive decline on standard tests, independent of some confounders.", "effect_estimate": "not reported as a single pooled numeric estimate in abstract; reported as a significant longitudinal association", "certainty_notes": "Observational cohort; cannot distinguish whether low selenium causes decline or declining health/nutrition causes both lower selenium and cognitive decline (reverse causation plausible in an elderly cohort); directly contradicted as a causal claim by the null PREADViSE RCT in a comparable age group.", "key_study_ids": ["akbaraly2007"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_mci", "population_detail": "Older adults with diagnosed mild cognitive impairment or Alzheimer's disease", "dose_or_intake": "varies across pooled trials (supplement doses and Brazil nut food intervention)", "duration": "weeks to 6 months across pooled trials", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Small systematic review/meta-analysis of selenium supplementation specifically in MCI/AD patients found some positive signals on cognitive measures, and a small Brazil-nut pilot RCT (n=31, 20 completers, 6 months, ~288.75 microg/day selenium) found greater improvement in verbal fluency (p=0.007) and constructional praxis (p=0.031) versus control.", "effect_estimate": "verbal fluency p=0.007; constructional praxis p=0.031 (CERAD battery) [cardoso2016]; pooled MCI/AD meta-analysis effect sizes not extracted in detail here", "certainty_notes": "Very small trials, short duration, MCI/AD-specific population; this evidence must not be generalized to healthy adults or to prevention in replete individuals; graded C/preliminary given sample sizes (n=31 in the Brazil nut trial, with only 20 completers) and lack of independent replication.", "key_study_ids": ["pereira2022", "cardoso2016"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy, selenium-replete or mildly-low young adults and students", "dose_or_intake": "not studied", "duration": "not applicable", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No identified RCT has tested selenium supplementation on cognitive outcomes in healthy young adults or students; combined with the null PREADViSE result in the more plausible older-adult responder group, there is no basis to expect benefit in this population.", "effect_estimate": "not reported (no trial identified)", "certainty_notes": "Absence of a direct trial compounded by a null result in the best-tested adjacent population (older men) lowers the prior probability of benefit further.", "key_study_ids": ["kryscio2017", "rutjes2018"]}], "acute_matrix": [], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "up to ~13 years (trial + cohort)", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "outcome": "dementia incidence", "effect_estimate": "HR 0.83 (95% CI 0.60-1.13), not significant [kryscio2017]", "evidence_quality": "high (large RCT, long follow-up) though downgraded for the mid-trial transition to cohort design", "limitations": "men only; dementia ascertainment via screening + physician follow-up rather than uniform standardized neuropsych battery; generalizability to women and younger adults untested", "study_ids": ["kryscio2017"]}, {"duration": "4-9 years", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "outcome": "cognitive decline (observational selenium status)", "effect_estimate": "lower/declining plasma selenium associated with greater decline (direction of effect reported, precise pooled estimate not in abstract)", "evidence_quality": "low-moderate (observational cohort)", "limitations": "confounding, reverse causation, cannot establish that supplementation would reverse the association", "study_ids": ["akbaraly2007"]}, {"duration": "6 months", "population": "older_adults_mci", "outcome": "verbal fluency, constructional praxis (CERAD)", "effect_estimate": "p=0.007 verbal fluency; p=0.031 constructional praxis favoring Brazil nut group [cardoso2016]", "evidence_quality": "low (very small pilot trial, n=20 completers)", "limitations": "tiny sample, single-site, food-based intervention confounded by other nut nutrients, MCI population only", "study_ids": ["cardoso2016"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Selenium has an unusually narrow margin between the adequate adult intake (55 microg/day RDA) and the dose associated with chronic toxicity (selenosis), which has occurred in documented cases from misformulated dietary supplements delivering selenium at roughly 100-200x the intended dose, and in regions with naturally very high dietary selenium.", "adverse_effects": ["selenosis (hair loss, brittle nails, garlic breath odor, gastrointestinal upset, fatigue, peripheral neuropathy) at chronic high intake", "documented case series of acute selenium toxicity from a misformulated liquid dietary supplement, with adverse effects including nail changes, hair loss, fatigue and GI symptoms tracked over time via nail biomonitoring [nutrients2013 misformulation case series]", "at very high chronic exposure, selenium toxicity can cause peripheral neuropathy and, rarely, more severe systemic effects"], "contraindications": ["pre-existing selenium toxicity or high dietary selenium exposure (e.g., residing in high-selenium-soil regions, very high habitual Brazil nut intake)"], "drug_interactions": ["may interact with anticoagulant/antiplatelet regimens via antioxidant effects (theoretical, weak evidence)", "potential interaction with chemotherapy agents relying on oxidative mechanisms (selenium's antioxidant action is a theoretical concern in oncology settings; relevant given the SELECT/PREADViSE context)", "statins: some evidence selenium/antioxidant combinations may blunt statin-related benefits (weak, not firmly established)"], "upper_limits": "EFSA/EU tolerable upper intake level for adults is 255-300 microg/day (EFSA 2023 reassessment context); US NIH ODS UL for adults is 400 microg/day; both are only about 5-7x the RDA (55 microg/day), a much narrower multiple than most minerals, and routine intake of several Brazil nuts per day can approach or exceed these limits given the high and variable selenium content of Brazil nuts (content varies enormously by growing region, sometimes by 10-100x between nuts).", "sleep_impact": "No established direct sleep effect at nutritional doses.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "PREADViSE provides multi-year (up to ~13 years cumulative exposure/follow-up) safety and efficacy data in older men at 200 microg/day without reported excess serious adverse events in the dementia-outcome report; longer-term data in younger adults or women are sparser.", "daily_use_appropriate": "probably", "special_populations": "Pregnant/lactating women should stay within standard RDA ranges; people with high baseline Brazil-nut or high-selenium-regional-food intake should be cautious about adding supplements (double-dosing risk); patients undergoing cancer treatment should discuss antioxidant supplementation, including selenium, with their oncology team given theoretical interference with treatment mechanisms and the SELECT trial's cautionary context (found no cancer-prevention benefit and the trial closed early for futility on its primary endpoint).", "specialist_review_needed": ["clinical toxicology/nutrition for any product combining concentrated selenium sources (e.g., selenium yeast supplements plus Brazil nut ingredients) given the narrow margin to the UL", "oncology nutrition input if any claim or targeting approaches populations undergoing cancer treatment or at elevated cancer risk, given the SELECT trial history"], "specialist_review_needed_note": "see specialist_review_needed array", "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "Selenium is safe and beneficial within the normal dietary range, but its unusually narrow margin between RDA and UL (roughly 5-7x versus 10-20x or more for many other minerals), combined with the high and unpredictable selenium content of Brazil nuts and documented real-world cases of supplement misformulation causing selenosis, means any Brain Food product containing concentrated selenium sources needs careful dose capping and clear labeling; the gate is conditional on staying well within established upper limits."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Selenium is an authorised EU vitamin/mineral nutrient. Under Commission Regulation (EU) No 432/2012 (EU Register of nutrition and health claims, Regulation (EC) No 1924/2006), authorised Article 13(1) general-function claims for selenium include 'Selenium contributes to normal thyroid function', 'Selenium contributes to the normal function of the immune system', 'Selenium contributes to the protection of cells from oxidative stress', 'Selenium contributes to normal spermatogenesis', and 'Selenium contributes to the maintenance of normal hair and nails'. There is no EU-authorised cognition or nervous-system-function claim for selenium, consistent with the brief's lead and with the evidence reviewed here. This should be re-verified against the live EUR-Lex consolidated Annex before any label copy is finalised.", "us": "Selenium is a required Nutrition Facts label nutrient with an established RDA/DV; no FDA-authorised health claim for cognition exists for selenium. Any structure/function claim would require FTC competent-and-reliable-scientific-evidence substantiation; given the null PREADViSE result and absence of healthy-adult trials, a cognition-specific claim is not well substantiated.", "morocco": "Selenium-specific supplement regulation in Morocco should be confirmed directly with ONSSA/Ministry of Health; this was not independently verified in this research pass (not researched here beyond general nutrient-fortification frameworks).", "permitted_claims_examples": ["Selenium contributes to normal thyroid function (EU, conditional on significant-source threshold; verify current EUR-Lex wording before use)", "Selenium contributes to the protection of cells from oxidative stress (EU, same condition)"], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "Standard selenium forms (sodium selenite, sodium selenate, L-selenomethionine, selenium-enriched yeast) are established, non-novel supplement ingredients in the EU and have long-standing NDI status in the US; no special novel-food issues identified.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Selenium fits naturally into a food-based format via Brazil nuts, seafood, or modest fortification; it does not require isolation as a standalone supplement to be delivered at adequate doses.", "formats": ["whole or processed Brazil nuts (highest natural food source, but highly variable content)", "selenium-enriched yeast", "sodium selenite/selenate or selenomethionine as standardized supplement forms", "fortified foods (less common for selenium than for iodine)"], "stability": "Selenium is a stable trace element in food matrices; not degraded by normal cooking/storage.", "taste": "Brazil nuts and selenium-rich seafood have their own natural flavor profiles that are generally well accepted; isolated selenium compounds are typically flavorless at nutritional doses.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Selenomethionine (from plant/nut sources) and selenocysteine (animal sources) are both well absorbed (>70-90%); bioavailability is not a significant barrier, but Brazil nut selenium content is notoriously variable (can range over an order of magnitude depending on growing region/soil), making whole-nut dosing unreliable for precise intake control.", "dose_feasibility": "The RDA (55 microg/day) is easily delivered by a single standardized Brazil nut, modest seafood intake, or a small, well-controlled supplement dose; because Brazil nut content is so variable, a food product relying on whole nuts for precise selenium dosing is not reliable, and a standardized selenium-yeast or selenite/selenate ingredient is preferable for dose control, especially given the narrow margin to the UL.", "cost_availability": "Selenium (as selenized yeast or selenite/selenate) is inexpensive and widely available as a food-grade fortificant or supplement ingredient; Brazil nuts themselves are a modestly priced commodity nut.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 3, "effect_magnitude": 1, "safety": 5, "reproducibility": 4, "food_suitability": 7, "cost_access": 8, "rationale": "evidence_quality=3: the single most important RCT (PREADViSE, B-grade, large and long) is null for the key cognitive outcome in the population where benefit was most plausible; remaining positive evidence is C-grade observational (EVA) or small clinical-population pilot data (MCI). effect_magnitude=1: no measurable benefit demonstrated in the best-powered trial; small positive signals are confined to tiny MCI pilot data. safety=5: narrow margin between RDA and UL (roughly 5-7x) and real documented selenosis cases from supplement misformulation keep this below a higher safety score despite good safety at normal dietary intake. reproducibility=4: the Brazil nut MCI finding and EVA cohort association are not replicated by, and are directly contradicted by, the much larger PREADViSE RCT. food_suitability=7: Brazil nuts/seafood are natural, appealing food formats, docked slightly for the unpredictable selenium content of whole Brazil nuts. cost_access=8: selenium ingredients and Brazil nuts are inexpensive and widely available."}, "research_gaps": ["No RCT has tested selenium supplementation on cognitive outcomes in healthy, selenium-replete young adults or students.", "The positive Brazil nut MCI pilot trial (n=31) has not been replicated in a larger or longer trial.", "It remains unclear whether any subgroup defined by baseline selenium status (rather than age/dementia-risk alone) would show a PREADViSE-type trial produce a different result; PREADViSE did not stratify its primary dementia analysis by baseline selenium status in the data reviewed here.", "No dedicated study of selenium status or supplementation in Moroccan or North African populations relevant to cognition was located in this search."], "_derived": {"composite_score": 4.0, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": null, "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"cognitive_decline": "C", "global_cognition": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 4, "n_studies": 5, "n_verified": 5, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 2, "n_systematic_reviews": 2, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "sodium", "name": "Sodium and salt", "category": "C", "category_name": "Minerals and trace elements", "aliases": ["sodium chloride", "table salt", "Na+", "dietary sodium"], "food_sources": ["table salt and salt added in cooking/processing (dominant source in most diets)", "processed and ultra-processed foods (breads, cured meats, cheese, canned soups, snacks)", "condiments (soy sauce, fish sauce, bouillon)", "naturally occurring in small amounts in most foods"], "active_constituents": ["sodium ion (Na+), the principal extracellular cation, essential for fluid balance, nerve conduction and muscle contraction"], "one_line_verdict": "Sodium is physiologically essential and acute hyponatraemia impairs cognition, but in people with normal sodium status, dietary sodium or salt intake has not been shown to enhance cognition, and the directly relevant cohort found no association between habitual dietary sodium intake and cognitive decline or brain imaging indices.", "scientific_conclusion": "Sodium is the principal extracellular cation and, together with potassium, sets the electrochemical gradient underlying every neuronal action potential; this is established, textbook human physiology and is not in question. What is in question for Brain Food's purposes is whether variation in dietary sodium or salt intake, within the ranges found in real diets, changes measurable cognitive performance in healthy, non-deficient adults, and the honest answer from the literature identified is that it does not appear to, in either direction, within normal dietary ranges. The most directly relevant study, from the Health, Aging and Body Composition (Health ABC) cohort (n=1,194 community-dwelling older adults, mean dietary sodium intake 2,677 ± 1,060 mg/day, 6.9-year follow-up), found dietary sodium intake was not associated with odds of clinically significant cognitive decline (OR 0.96, 95% CI 0.50-1.84 per doubling of intake) nor with structural or microstructural brain MRI indices; only the sodium-to-potassium ratio (not sodium alone) showed a weak positive association with cognitive decline (OR 2.02, 95% CI 1.01-4.03 per unit increase), and the authors explicitly concluded their data 'do not support' dietary sodium reduction to prevent cognitive decline [nowak2018]. This is a single observational cohort using food-frequency-questionnaire-derived intake, a method with substantial measurement error, and the ratio finding (significant) versus the sodium-alone finding (null) within the same dataset raises a multiplicity concern; it should be treated as a hypothesis-generating signal, not confirmed causal evidence. Separately, the single most consequential clinical fact about sodium and brain function is at the opposite extreme of intake: acute or chronic hyponatraemia (low serum sodium, typically from excessive fluid intake, certain medications such as SSRIs or diuretics, or SIADH, not from low dietary salt intake in a healthy person) is a well-documented and medically important cause of confusion, impaired attention, gait instability and, in severe or rapidly developing cases, seizures, cerebral oedema and death; this is covered in depth in research/monographs_raw/hydration.json (which reviews fluid/electrolyte balance broadly, including a dedicated hyponatraemia systematic review [rangan2021_hyponatraemia_sr] and is cross-referenced rather than duplicated here). High habitual salt intake is also independently and robustly linked to hypertension, which over decades is a major, well-established risk factor for vascular cognitive impairment and dementia via cerebral small-vessel disease; however, this is a cardiovascular-mediated pathway operating over a much longer timescale than any 'salt and brain function today' claim, and no trial in this search directly isolated sodium reduction (versus a broader dietary/lifestyle intervention) as causally protective against cognitive decline. In summary: sodium is essential, severe imbalance (mostly hyponatraemia, occasionally severe hypernatraemia/dehydration) clearly impairs cognition and is a medical concern, but ordinary dietary sodium variation has not been shown to meaningfully affect cognition in either direction in the one cohort that tested it directly, and high salt intake's main established brain-relevant harm pathway is the decades-long cardiovascular/hypertension route rather than an acute or enhancement effect.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "True dietary sodium deficiency is rare in people eating any conventional diet, since salt is ubiquitous in processed and prepared food; clinically relevant low sodium (hyponatraemia) in healthy people is almost always caused by excessive water intake relative to sodium and solute excretion (exercise-associated hyponatraemia, psychogenic polydipsia) rather than by low dietary salt intake per se, and is covered in hydration.json. There is no human evidence that increasing sodium/salt intake above typical dietary levels enhances cognition in healthy, euhydrated, normonatraemic adults; the one cohort testing dietary sodium and cognitive decline directly found no benefit or harm from sodium intake itself. The practical message for Brain Food's healthy target populations is to maintain normal hydration and electrolyte balance (see hydration.json) rather than to manipulate sodium intake specifically for cognitive purposes in either direction.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Sodium rarely appears as a positive cognitive-enhancement marketing ingredient (unlike electrolyte/hydration products that imply 'balanced electrolytes support brain function'), but such implied claims should not extend beyond what the evidence shows: electrolyte replacement matters when genuine deficits exist (heavy sweating, illness, under-hydration - covered in hydration.json), not as a general cognitive booster for an already-replete, sedentary knowledge worker. On the public-health side, 'reduce salt for better brain health' is a plausible but not yet causally confirmed cardiovascular-mediated claim over decades, and should not be marketed as a short-term or direct cognitive-performance benefit; the one dedicated cohort study found no such direct relationship. The honest position is that sodium messaging belongs with electrolyte/hydration products aimed at genuine fluid-loss replacement, not with a standalone 'reduce or add salt for brain function' claim.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Sodium is the principal extracellular cation, maintained in a tight osmotic balance with potassium (intracellular) by the Na+/K+-ATPase pump; this gradient is the basis of every neuronal action potential (sodium influx depolarises the membrane) and of whole-body fluid osmolality, which is sensed by hypothalamic osmoreceptors and regulated via vasopressin (ADH) and renal sodium handling. Severe deviations from normal serum sodium (hyponatraemia from excess water relative to sodium, or hypernatraemia from dehydration) directly and demonstrably impair cognition via cerebral oedema or cellular dehydration respectively; this is established human physiology and medicine. Chronic high salt intake's main mechanistic link to brain outcomes is indirect, via sodium-sensitive hypertension and consequent cerebral small-vessel disease over years to decades, not via acute neuronal signalling changes within the normal dietary range.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Dietary sodium (as salt) -> near-complete intestinal absorption -> extracellular fluid compartment", "detail": "Sodium absorption is highly efficient (>95%) across normal dietary ranges; the kidney, not the gut, is the primary regulator of sodium balance.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Renal sodium handling and osmoreceptor/vasopressin signalling -> tight regulation of plasma sodium concentration (135-145 mmol/L)", "detail": "Well-established endocrine/renal physiology; healthy kidneys buffer wide dietary sodium variation without clinically significant change in plasma sodium.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Na+/K+-ATPase-maintained sodium gradient -> neuronal action potential generation (depolarisation phase)", "detail": "Fundamental, universal neuronal electrophysiology; not dose-responsive to dietary sodium variation in people with normal renal and endocrine function.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Severe hyponatraemia (plasma Na+ <130-135 mmol/L, usually from excess water relative to sodium/solute intake or SIADH) -> cerebral oedema -> confusion, attention impairment, seizures in severe cases", "detail": "Well-documented in clinical medicine and reviewed for fluid/electrolyte balance broadly in hydration.json [rangan2021_hyponatraemia_sr]; this is the dominant human-relevant sodium-cognition mechanism, operating at the deficiency/imbalance extreme, not as an enhancement pathway.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Chronic high salt intake -> sodium-sensitive hypertension (in salt-sensitive individuals) -> cerebral small-vessel disease over years -> vascular cognitive impairment risk", "detail": "Plausible and epidemiologically supported at the hypertension-dementia level generally, but not demonstrated as a direct, isolated sodium-intake-to-cognition causal chain in a randomised trial; the one cohort testing dietary sodium alone and cognitive decline found no association [nowak2018].", "evidence_level": "plausible"}], "pathways": ["Na+/K+-ATPase and neuronal action potential", "renal/osmoreceptor sodium-water regulation", "hyponatraemia-induced cerebral oedema (deficiency/imbalance extreme)", "sodium-sensitive hypertension and cerebral small-vessel disease (chronic, indirect)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_decline", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Community-dwelling older adults, mean age 74, Health ABC cohort", "dose_or_intake": "Mean dietary sodium intake 2,677 ± 1,060 mg/day (FFQ-derived); range reflects typical US older-adult dietary variation", "duration": "6.9-year follow-up", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Dietary sodium intake alone was not associated with odds of clinically significant cognitive decline (3MS) or with structural/microstructural brain MRI indices; only the sodium:potassium ratio showed a weak positive association with decline.", "effect_estimate": "OR 0.96 (95% CI 0.50-1.84) per doubling of sodium intake, not significant; sodium:potassium ratio OR 2.02 (95% CI 1.01-4.03) per unit increase [nowak2018]", "certainty_notes": "Single cohort, FFQ-based dietary assessment with substantial measurement error, no randomisation; downgraded for imprecision (wide CI for sodium itself, essentially straddling null) and for the multiplicity concern between the null sodium-alone finding and the significant ratio finding.", "key_study_ids": ["nowak2018"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "other", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Patients with acute or chronic hyponatraemia (plasma Na+ typically <130-135 mmol/L), not a dietary-sodium-reduction scenario", "dose_or_intake": "not applicable (clinical electrolyte disorder, not a dietary intervention)", "duration": "acute", "grade": "A", "direction": "harm", "effect_summary": "Hyponatraemia causes confusion, attention and gait impairment and, if severe or rapid, seizures and cerebral oedema; this is well established in clinical medicine and reviewed in detail for fluid/electrolyte balance generally in hydration.json.", "effect_estimate": "Not re-derived here; see hydration.json [rangan2021_hyponatraemia_sr] for quantitative detail", "certainty_notes": "Strong, consistent clinical evidence at the pathological extreme; not generalisable to ordinary dietary sodium variation in healthy people and not re-reviewed in full here to avoid duplicating hydration.json.", "key_study_ids": ["rangan2021_hyponatraemia_sr"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy young adults, students, knowledge workers with normal sodium intake and status", "dose_or_intake": "not studied as an isolated cognitive intervention", "duration": "not studied", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No trial of dietary sodium/salt manipulation for cognitive enhancement or impairment in healthy young adults was located.", "effect_estimate": "not reported - no trials identified", "certainty_notes": "Absence of evidence; graded D.", "key_study_ids": []}], "acute_matrix": [], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "6.9 years", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "outcome": "cognitive decline (3MS) and brain MRI indices in relation to dietary sodium intake and sodium:potassium ratio", "effect_estimate": "Sodium alone: OR 0.96 (0.50-1.84), not significant; ratio: OR 2.02 (1.01-4.03) [nowak2018]", "evidence_quality": "low-moderate (single cohort, FFQ-based)", "limitations": "Food-frequency-questionnaire measurement error, observational design, residual confounding, single cohort.", "study_ids": ["nowak2018"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Both excessive and insufficient sodium relative to fluid balance are medically significant. Chronic high salt intake is a well-established driver of hypertension in salt-sensitive individuals, with downstream cardiovascular and cerebrovascular risk. Hyponatraemia (low plasma sodium, usually from excess water intake or certain medications/conditions, not from low dietary salt alone) and, less commonly, hypernatraemia/severe dehydration both impair cognition and can be life-threatening; these are covered in depth in hydration.json and cross-referenced rather than duplicated here.", "adverse_effects": ["Hypertension risk with chronically high salt intake in salt-sensitive individuals", "Hyponatraemia-related confusion, seizures, cerebral oedema (usually from excess fluid intake or SIADH/medications, not low dietary salt per se)", "Hypernatraemia/dehydration-related confusion in severe cases (see hydration.json)"], "contraindications": ["Hypertension or heart failure (salt restriction often medically advised)", "Chronic kidney disease (individualised sodium targets required)", "Conditions predisposing to hyponatraemia (SIADH, certain psychiatric or endocrine conditions, use of SSRIs/diuretics) - caution with aggressive fluid intake, not with normal dietary salt"], "drug_interactions": ["Diuretics (both sodium-wasting and potassium-sparing) materially affect sodium balance", "SSRIs and some anticonvulsants are associated with drug-induced hyponatraemia", "ACE inhibitors/ARBs interact with sodium and potassium handling together"], "upper_limits": "WHO recommends <2000 mg sodium/day (5 g salt) for adults; EFSA's 2019 Dietary Reference Value sets an adequate intake around 1500 mg sodium/day for adults with no formal UL set for food sodium, while recommending reduction from typical (higher) population intakes; US Dietary Guidelines recommend <2300 mg sodium/day.", "sleep_impact": "No established direct effect of ordinary dietary sodium variation on sleep architecture; severe hyponatraemia can cause lethargy/confusion that may be mistaken for sleep-related impairment.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Chronic salt intake and blood pressure/cardiovascular outcomes are extensively studied over years to decades; chronic dietary sodium and cognitive decline specifically has been studied in only the single cohort identified here.", "daily_use_appropriate": "yes", "special_populations": "Hypertensive patients, heart failure patients, and chronic kidney disease patients need individualised sodium targets, generally lower than average population intake; patients on SSRIs, certain diuretics, or with endocrine conditions predisposing to hyponatraemia need monitoring of fluid and electrolyte balance (see hydration.json); healthy pregnant women and children should follow general population sodium-moderation guidance.", "specialist_review_needed": ["Nephrology/cardiology input for any sodium-related formulation or claim given individualised blood-pressure-driven sodium targets", "Endocrinology/psychiatry input if any product targets populations at risk of hyponatraemia (e.g., via medication interactions)"], "hard_safety_gate": "pass", "gate_reason": "Sodium is a normal, essential dietary component; ordinary food-level sodium formulation carries no unusual safety concern for a cognitive-food product, provided excessive salt content is avoided per general cardiovascular dietary guidance and no direct cognitive-enhancement claim is made."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Sodium has an EU Nutrient Reference Value (NRV 800 mg/day, reflecting lower target intake than typical consumption) and the EU and WHO actively promote salt-reduction policy; no EFSA-authorised cognitive-performance claim exists for sodium. 'Low sodium' and 'very low sodium' are regulated nutrition claim terms under Regulation (EC) 1924/2006.", "us": "Sodium has an FDA Daily Value (2300 mg) and mandatory sodium labeling; 'low sodium' and related claims are regulated nutrient content claims under FDA rules. No FDA-reviewed cognitive claim exists for sodium.", "morocco": "Sodium/salt content labeling and reduction initiatives fall under ONSSA and Ministry of Health public-health salt-reduction programs (consistent with WHO guidance); no specific cognitive claim framework exists.", "permitted_claims_examples": ["EU: 'Sodium contributes to normal transmission and conduction of nerve impulse' and 'normal muscle function' are authorised physiological claims under Regulation (EC) 1924/2006 (general electrolyte function, not cognitive performance)", "EU/US: regulated 'low sodium'/'reduced sodium' nutrient content claim terms (not cognition claims)"], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "Sodium chloride and other sodium salts are long-established food ingredients; no novel food/NDI issues.", "regulatory_gate": "pass"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Sodium chloride is ubiquitous in food formulation for taste, preservation and texture; from a cognitive-product standpoint there is no rationale to add sodium beyond normal palatability/preservation levels, and public-health guidance favours moderation.", "formats": ["Standard table salt / sodium chloride in food formulation", "Sodium citrate, sodium bicarbonate and other sodium salts for specific functional/buffering roles in food science (not cognitive)"], "stability": "Sodium chloride and common sodium salts are fully stable through food processing and storage.", "taste": "Salt is a primary taste driver (umami/savoury enhancement) but excessive levels are both a health concern and, at high concentrations, organoleptically unpalatable.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Sodium is essentially completely absorbed regardless of food matrix or form; bioavailability is not a limiting formulation factor.", "dose_feasibility": "No cognitive dose exists to target; from a general nutrition standpoint, formulation should aim to minimise added sodium consistent with WHO/EU/US salt-reduction guidance rather than add sodium for any cognitive rationale.", "cost_availability": "Extremely low cost and universally available, including in Morocco.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 2, "effect_magnitude": 0, "safety": 7, "reproducibility": 2, "food_suitability": 8, "cost_access": 10, "rationale": "evidence_quality low because the only direct dietary-sodium-cognition cohort was null for sodium itself (grade C at best) and no trial exists testing sodium manipulation for cognitive enhancement; effect_magnitude 0 because no enhancement effect exists to size (and the imbalance-extreme harm from hyponatraemia is covered in hydration.json, not scored here as a sodium-intake effect); safety scored 7 reflecting that normal dietary sodium is safe but chronic excess carries real cardiovascular risk requiring moderation messaging; reproducibility low given a single cohort; food_suitability and cost_access high reflecting sodium's ubiquity and zero formulation barrier, tempered by the public-health imperative to moderate rather than add it."}, "research_gaps": ["No RCT has tested isolated dietary sodium reduction or increase (versus a broader dietary pattern) for cognitive outcomes in any population.", "The Nowak 2018 sodium:potassium ratio finding versus the null sodium-alone finding within the same dataset has not been explained or replicated and should be treated cautiously given the multiplicity concern.", "No data exist on sodium intake and cognition specifically in healthy young adults, students or knowledge workers.", "The long-term causal chain from chronic high salt intake through hypertension to vascular cognitive impairment has not been isolated from other cardiovascular risk factors in a trial specifically measuring cognition as a primary outcome."], "_derived": {"composite_score": 4.0, "eligibility": "eligible", "safety_gate": "pass", "regulatory_gate": "pass", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": null, "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": null, "best_grade_overall": null, "benefit_domains": {}, "n_outcome_rows": 3, "n_studies": 2, "n_verified": 1, "n_unverified": 1, "n_null_negative": 1, "n_systematic_reviews": 0, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["harm", "insufficient", "no_benefit"]}}, {"id": "zinc", "name": "Zinc", "category": "C", "category_name": "Minerals and trace elements", "aliases": ["zinc", "Zn", "zinc gluconate", "zinc citrate", "zinc bisglycinate", "zinc picolinate", "zinc sulphate", "zinc oxide", "ZMA (zinc-magnesium-aspartate combination)"], "food_sources": ["oysters and other shellfish (richest source)", "red meat and offal", "poultry, eggs and dairy", "legumes (chickpeas, lentils), but phytate lowers absorption", "pumpkin and sesame seeds, nuts", "whole grains (phytate-limited bioavailability); leavened/fermented bread improves it", "fortified cereals"], "active_constituents": ["Zn2+ ion (essential trace element; structural/catalytic cofactor for hundreds of enzymes and zinc-finger transcription factors; vesicular synaptic zinc in glutamatergic neurons modulates NMDA receptors)"], "one_line_verdict": "Zinc is essential for normal brain function and the EU authorises 'contributes to normal cognitive function', but trials in zinc-replete adults (ZENITH, AREDS) found no cognitive or mood benefit, no trial exists in students, and excess zinc can cause copper-deficiency anaemia and nerve damage, so the honest role is avoiding low intake, not enhancement.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. The best human trials in adults are null. In ZENITH, 387 healthy 55-87-year-olds given 15 or 30 mg zinc for 6 months showed no consistent CANTAB effect: one spatial working-memory measure improved at 3 months only, while one attention measure worsened at 15 mg [maylor2006]; mood was unaffected [stewartknox2011]. In AREDS, 80 mg zinc plus copper for a median 6.9 years produced no difference on six cognitive tests in 2,166 older adults [yaffe2004]. SU.VI.MAX found slightly better episodic memory (MD 0.61, 95% CI 0.02-1.20) years after an antioxidant mix containing 20 mg zinc, but the zinc effect cannot be isolated and the analysis was post hoc [kesseguyot2011]. Meta-analyses could not pool adult trials (only one existed) and found null effects in children: intelligence SMD <0.001 (-0.12 to 0.13) [warthon2015]; Bayley MDI MD -0.50 (-2.06 to 1.06) even in at-risk infants [gogia2012]. Observational and genetic data. In Chinese older adults, cognitive decline was faster only below ~8.8 mg/day intake (L-shaped) [meng2024], whereas the large US REGARDS cohort found no association for serum or dietary zinc [angley2024], and Mendelian randomisation found no causal effect of zinc on cognition, reaction time or AD [wang2024mr]. This pattern fits a threshold model: harm from inadequacy, no gain above adequacy. Mood. Meta-analysis shows lower depressive scores with zinc in depressed patients (WMD -4.15, -6.56 to -1.75) and lower depression risk with higher intake in cohorts [yosaee2022, lomagno2014]; in healthy people evidence is a 30-woman pilot (7 mg zinc plus multivitamin) [sawada2010] versus a null ZENITH mood trial [stewartknox2011]. A small trial in overweight middle-aged women at 30 mg (above the EU UL) reported higher MMSE/Stroop scores [devargas2023]; ZMA was null on Stroop in replete young men [edwards2024]. In whom. Benefit is plausible only where intake or status is low (phytate-rich diets, vegetarians, older adults, malabsorption). Moroccan adult zinc intake was ~78% of the reference intake in two small surveys [doggui2021]; zinc fortification raises serum zinc but cognitive effects are untested [shah2016]. What is unknown: no RCT in students, young knowledge workers or zinc-deficient adults with cognitive outcomes; no acute effects tested. Safety margins are narrow: the EU UL is 25 mg/day total, and chronic excess causes copper-deficiency myeloneuropathy [jaiser2010, cuzinc2023].", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Severe zinc deficiency impairs brain development and function, which underpins the EU 'normal cognitive function' claim, and low intake (<~8 mg/day) is associated with faster cognitive decline in older adults [meng2024]. Yet even in at-risk children supplementation did not improve development [gogia2012, warthon2015]. In zinc-replete adults, 15-80 mg/day for 6 months to 7 years did not improve cognition or mood [maylor2006, stewartknox2011, yaffe2004], and genetic evidence is null [wang2024mr]. Enhancement in healthy young adults is untested and unlikely; correcting low intake (Moroccan diets with ~78% of reference intake [doggui2021], vegetarians, high-phytate diets) is the only defensible rationale.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Zinc is marketed for 'focus', 'memory', 'mood', 'testosterone' and sleep (ZMA). The evidence for cognition in adults is null [maylor2006, yaffe2004], ZMA did nothing for sleep or Stroop [edwards2024], and mood benefits are limited to depressed patients [yosaee2022]. ADHD trials in children are outside scope and must not be used for adult claims. The EU wording 'Zinc contributes to normal cognitive function' is lawful on foods that are at least a source of zinc (1.5 mg per 100 g/100 ml or portion), but it describes a normal physiological role and must not be stretched into 'boosts' or 'improves'. A product dosing far above needs adds copper-depletion risk without cognitive gain.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Zinc is absorbed in the small intestine via ZIP4 and exported by ZnT1; absorption is reduced by phytate and competes with high-dose iron and copper (zinc induces enterocyte metallothionein that traps copper). In the brain, synaptic vesicular zinc (ZnT3) is co-released with glutamate in hippocampal mossy fibres and modulates NMDA and other receptors; zinc also supports BDNF signalling, DNA synthesis and antioxidant enzymes (Cu/Zn-SOD). Deficiency in animals impairs learning; in humans, plasma zinc is a weak status marker and no human study links supplementation to changes in brain zinc.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Dietary zinc -> ZIP4-mediated intestinal absorption (reduced by phytate) -> plasma zinc", "detail": "Zinc-only fortification raised serum zinc by 2.12 umol/L [shah2016]; 7 mg/day raised serum zinc in young women [sawada2010].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "High zinc intake -> enterocyte metallothionein induction -> copper malabsorption", "detail": "Copper-deficiency anaemia and myeloneuropathy in chronic excess [jaiser2010, cuzinc2023, denture2017].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Plasma zinc -> brain zinc homeostasis (tightly buffered)", "detail": "Brain zinc is regulated; supplementation-induced changes in human brain zinc not demonstrated.", "evidence_level": "plausible"}, {"step": "Synaptic vesicular zinc -> NMDA/AMPA modulation, BDNF signalling, hippocampal plasticity", "detail": "Shown in rodent and cellular models; deficiency impairs learning in animals.", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Zinc status -> mood regulation", "detail": "Lower zinc in depression; zinc monotherapy reduces depressive scores in patients [yosaee2022]; null in healthy older adults [stewartknox2011].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Measured human cognitive outcome", "detail": "Null in replete older adults over 6 months to 7 years [maylor2006, yaffe2004]; null in children [gogia2012]; no trial in young adults.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["synaptic (vesicular) zinc / NMDA receptor modulation", "zinc-finger transcription and DNA synthesis", "Cu/Zn superoxide dismutase antioxidant defence", "BDNF signalling", "zinc-copper antagonism via metallothionein (safety pathway)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Healthy zinc-replete adults 55-87 y (ZENITH)", "dose_or_intake": "15 or 30 mg/day zinc gluconate", "duration": "6 months", "grade": "B", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "No consistent effect on CANTAB memory, attention or reaction time; one transient working-memory benefit and one attention harm among 8 outcomes.", "effect_estimate": "Mostly null; effect sizes not reported in abstract [maylor2006]", "certainty_notes": "Single well-conducted, publicly funded RCT (n=387); downgraded one level for imprecision/single trial; supported by AREDS null.", "key_study_ids": ["maylor2006", "yaffe2004"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_aging", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Older AREDS participants; French middle-aged adults (SU.VI.MAX, multi-nutrient)", "dose_or_intake": "80 mg zinc + 2 mg copper (AREDS); 20 mg zinc in antioxidant mix (SU.VI.MAX)", "duration": "6.9-8 years", "grade": "B", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "AREDS: no difference on six cognitive tests; SU.VI.MAX: small episodic-memory benefit of a multi-nutrient mix in post-hoc analysis, not attributable to zinc.", "effect_estimate": "AREDS all p>0.05 [yaffe2004]; SU.VI.MAX MD 0.61 (0.02-1.20) for mix [kesseguyot2011]", "certainty_notes": "Large long trials; cognition measured once at end; SU.VI.MAX confounded by co-nutrients.", "key_study_ids": ["yaffe2004", "kesseguyot2011"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults 18-40, including students; zinc alone", "dose_or_intake": "not studied", "duration": "not studied", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No RCT of zinc on cognition in healthy young adults or students was found; systematic review found only one adult RCT (ZENITH, older adults).", "effect_estimate": "not reported (no trials)", "certainty_notes": "Absence of evidence; extrapolation from older-adult nulls suggests little effect.", "key_study_ids": ["warthon2015", "lomagno2014"]}, {"timeframe": "short_term_days", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Young trained men, zinc-replete", "dose_or_intake": "ZMA at bedtime", "duration": "2 nights", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "No effect on Stroop or sleep.", "effect_estimate": "p>0.05 [edwards2024]", "certainty_notes": "n=19, combination product, short exposure.", "key_study_ids": ["edwards2024"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "alertness", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Single-dose effects", "dose_or_intake": "not studied", "duration": "single dose", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No acute zinc cognition trial found; no plausible acute mechanism.", "effect_estimate": "not reported (no trials)", "certainty_notes": "No data.", "key_study_ids": ["warthon2015"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Healthy adults 55-87 y", "dose_or_intake": "15 or 30 mg/day", "duration": "6 months", "grade": "B", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "No change in positive or negative affect.", "effect_estimate": "Null [stewartknox2011]", "certainty_notes": "Single well-conducted RCT; downgraded for single trial.", "key_study_ids": ["stewartknox2011"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy young Japanese women", "dose_or_intake": "7 mg/day zinc + multivitamin", "duration": "10 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Within-group reductions in POMS anger-hostility and depression-dejection in the zinc arm only.", "effect_estimate": "p=0.009 and p=0.011 within group; no between-group estimate [sawada2010]", "certainty_notes": "n=30 pilot, within-group analysis, high risk of bias; contradicted in older adults [stewartknox2011].", "key_study_ids": ["sawada2010", "stewartknox2011", "lomagno2014"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Patients with depression", "dose_or_intake": "Zinc supplements (various, often 25 mg)", "duration": "6-12 weeks typical", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Lower depressive symptom scores with zinc monotherapy; cohorts link higher intake to lower depression risk.", "effect_estimate": "WMD -4.15 (-6.56 to -1.75); cohort RR 0.66 (0.50-0.82) [yosaee2022]", "certainty_notes": "Small trials, heterogeneous scales; clinical population requiring medical care.", "key_study_ids": ["yosaee2022", "lomagno2014"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "other", "population_detail": "Women 40-60 with overweight/obesity", "dose_or_intake": "30 mg/day", "duration": "12 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Higher MMSE and Stroop scores vs placebo; no mood change.", "effect_estimate": "not reported in abstract [devargas2023]", "certainty_notes": "n=32 analysed, 24% attrition, screening tests, dose above EU UL.", "key_study_ids": ["devargas2023"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_decline", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Middle-aged and older cohorts (China, USA) and genetic (MR) analyses", "dose_or_intake": "Dietary zinc intake; serum zinc; genetically predicted zinc", "duration": "5.9-10 years; lifelong (MR)", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "L-shaped association in China (faster decline only below ~8 mg/day); null in US REGARDS; null MR.", "effect_estimate": "beta -0.24 (-0.40 to -0.07) for >=7.9 mg/day [meng2024]; null [angley2024, wang2024mr]", "certainty_notes": "Observational; inconsistency across populations probably reflects baseline intake.", "key_study_ids": ["meng2024", "angley2024", "wang2024mr"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "deficient_or_low_baseline", "population_detail": "Adults with low zinc intake or status (e.g., high-phytate diets, Moroccan low intakes)", "dose_or_intake": "not studied in adults", "duration": "not studied", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No adult RCT in zinc-deficient people with cognitive outcomes; benefit is inferred from physiology and the L-shaped cohort association only.", "effect_estimate": "not reported (no trials)", "certainty_notes": "Indirect; child trials in at-risk populations were null.", "key_study_ids": ["meng2024", "doggui2021", "shah2016"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "children_adolescents", "population_detail": "Infants and children <5 y, mostly at risk of deficiency (LMICs); older children in some trials", "dose_or_intake": "Synthetic zinc supplements", "duration": "months", "grade": "B", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "No improvement in mental or motor development or in intelligence/executive function.", "effect_estimate": "MDI MD -0.50 (-2.06 to 1.06) [gogia2012]; intelligence SMD <0.001 (-0.12 to 0.13) [warthon2015]", "certainty_notes": "Moderate quality, heterogeneity; reported for completeness only; not generalisable to adults.", "key_study_ids": ["gogia2012", "warthon2015"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "not studied", "onset": "not applicable", "duration_studied": "none", "domain": "alertness / attention", "population": "healthy young adults", "effect_estimate": "no acute trials found", "certainty": "insufficient (D)", "safety": "nausea and vomiting with large single doses (>~50 mg) on an empty stomach", "study_ids": ["warthon2015"]}, {"dose": "ZMA bedtime dose", "onset": "next morning", "duration_studied": "2 nights", "domain": "Stroop and sleep", "population": "young trained men, replete", "effect_estimate": "no effect (p>0.05)", "certainty": "C", "safety": "no AEs reported", "study_ids": ["edwards2024"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "6 months", "population": "healthy adults 55-87", "outcome": "CANTAB memory, attention, RT; mood", "effect_estimate": "null overall (1 transient benefit, 1 harm of 8 outcomes); mood null", "evidence_quality": "B", "limitations": "replete population", "study_ids": ["maylor2006", "stewartknox2011"]}, {"duration": "median 6.9 years", "population": "older AREDS participants", "outcome": "six cognitive tests", "effect_estimate": "no difference (all p>0.05)", "evidence_quality": "B", "limitations": "single end-of-trial assessment; 80 mg zinc + copper", "study_ids": ["yaffe2004"]}, {"duration": "8 years + 6 years follow-up", "population": "French adults 45-60", "outcome": "episodic memory", "effect_estimate": "MD 0.61 (0.02-1.20) for antioxidant+zinc mix", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "post hoc; zinc not isolable", "study_ids": ["kesseguyot2011"]}, {"duration": "10 weeks", "population": "healthy young women", "outcome": "POMS mood", "effect_estimate": "within-group improvements only", "evidence_quality": "C/very low", "limitations": "n=30 pilot", "study_ids": ["sawada2010"]}, {"duration": "5.9-10 years", "population": "older Chinese / US adults", "outcome": "cognitive decline / impairment", "effect_estimate": "L-shaped (China) vs null (US)", "evidence_quality": "C (observational)", "limitations": "confounding, differing baseline intakes", "study_ids": ["meng2024", "angley2024"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Zinc has a narrow margin between requirement and upper limit. The EU (SCF 2003) UL is 25 mg/day total intake for adults (based on copper-status effects; NOAEL 50 mg/day), the US UL 40 mg/day; EFSA 2014 PRIs for adults range ~7.5-12.7 mg/day (women) and ~9.4-16.3 mg/day (men) depending on phytate intake. Chronic intakes above the UL induce copper deficiency, presenting as anaemia, neutropenia and myeloneuropathy that may be only partly reversible [jaiser2010, cuzinc2023, denture2017, schoofs2024]. Acute high doses cause nausea, vomiting and metallic taste. Intranasal zinc products have been associated with loss of smell (not relevant to oral foods). The ZENITH trial noted a detrimental effect on one attention measure at 15 mg/day [maylor2006].", "adverse_effects": ["nausea, vomiting, epigastric pain and metallic taste at high single doses", "copper deficiency (anaemia, neutropenia, myelopathy/neuropathy) with chronic excess", "reduced HDL cholesterol and impaired copper-dependent enzymes at high chronic intake", "possible interference with iron absorption when given together at high doses"], "contraindications": ["Wilson's disease patients only under specialist care (zinc is a therapy)", "prior bariatric/upper-GI surgery or malabsorption (copper-deficiency risk with zinc supplements)", "concurrent use of zinc-containing denture adhesives or multiple zinc supplements (stacking)"], "drug_interactions": ["chelation reduces absorption of tetracyclines and fluoroquinolones (separate dosing)", "penicillamine absorption reduced", "thiazide diuretics increase urinary zinc loss", "high-dose iron supplements reduce zinc absorption and vice versa", "copper status reduced at chronic intakes above ~25-40 mg/day"], "upper_limits": "EU/SCF 2003: UL 25 mg/day total zinc (adults); no EFSA re-evaluation found in this search (uncertain). US IOM: UL 40 mg/day total zinc (adults). EU NRV 10 mg; 'source' = 1.5 mg per 100 g/100 ml or per portion. US RDA 11 mg (men), 8 mg (women) (ODS page HTTP 403; values from secondary sources). Many EU 'brain' supplements contain 10-25 mg, which together with ~8-12 mg from diet can exceed the EU UL.", "sleep_impact": "No disruption; no sleep benefit shown (ZMA null) [edwards2024].", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Up to median 6.9 years at 80 mg/day with 2 mg copper (AREDS) and 8 years at 20 mg/day (SU.VI.MAX) in trials; copper co-supplementation used in AREDS to offset copper loss.", "daily_use_appropriate": "probably", "special_populations": "Pregnancy and lactation (requirements higher; supplement only on advice); children (low ULs, ADHD trials not applicable); vegetarians/vegans and high-phytate diets (higher needs); older adults (lower intake); people after bariatric surgery (copper risk).", "specialist_review_needed": ["haematologist/neurologist input on copper-deficiency warnings", "dietitian for total-intake modelling against the 25 mg EU UL", "regulatory affairs for national supplement maxima"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "Safe at 'source' to ~10 mg/day added zinc; products must keep total intake under the EU UL of 25 mg/day, avoid stacking, and warn about long-term high doses (copper deficiency)."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Essential nutrient; zinc salts listed in Directive 2002/46/EC and Reg. 1925/2006 annexes. Eighteen authorised Art. 13 claims in Reg. (EU) 432/2012 including 'Zinc contributes to normal cognitive function' (condition: food at least a source of zinc); verified on legislation.gov.uk 2026-10-25 and consistent with X4 [r30]. Supplement maxima not harmonised in the EU (national). No claim for focus, memory improvement, mood or sleep.", "us": "Dietary ingredient under DSHEA; zinc salts long marketed and several GRAS for food use (CFR sections not re-verified). Structure/function claims allowed with disclaimer and FTC substantiation; EU-style 'normal cognitive function' wording is likely acceptable as a nutritive-value S/F claim but no FDA statement found (X5 open question). FDA warned in 2009 against intranasal zinc products (anosmia) - recalled from memory, not re-verified here.", "morocco": "Same framework as other nutrients: Law 28-07/ONSSA, labelling Decree 2-12-389, nutrition claims per Joint Order 281-16; health claims defined but no authorised list or procedure found, so 'Zinc contributes to normal cognitive function' is legally uncertain in Morocco [X6]. Mandatory fortification covers flour (iron, folic acid) and salt (iodine), not zinc. Adult zinc intake ~78% of RDI in two small Moroccan surveys [doggui2021].", "permitted_claims_examples": ["Zinc contributes to normal cognitive function", "Zinc contributes to the protection of cells from oxidative stress", "Zinc contributes to normal macronutrient metabolism", "Zinc contributes to the normal function of the immune system", "Zinc contributes to the maintenance of normal vision", "Zinc has a role in the process of cell division"], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "None for standard zinc salts listed in EU annexes; non-listed forms (e.g., some proprietary chelates) require checking against Annex II of Directive 2002/46/EC.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Very good: the EU 'source' level (1.5 mg per portion) and even 3-5 mg per portion are easily reached with milligram quantities of zinc citrate/gluconate or with seeds, legumes and whole grains (with phytate limiting absorption). This unlocks the authorised cognitive-function claim at food-compatible doses.", "formats": ["fortified breakfast cereals, breads or bars", "seed- and legume-based snacks (pumpkin seeds, chickpeas)", "dairy-based foods", "capsules/tablets (avoid >10-15 mg added per day)"], "stability": "Stable mineral salts; zinc oxide insoluble; may promote oxidation of PUFAs and react with polyphenols; check interactions with iron in multi-mineral premixes.", "taste": "Metallic and astringent at higher doses; gluconate and citrate milder; low food-fortification levels usually acceptable.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Animal protein enhances absorption; phytate (whole grains, legumes) inhibits it, which matters for Moroccan bread- and legume-based diets; fermentation/leavening and soaking reduce phytate; high-dose iron competes.", "dose_feasibility": "Studied doses (7-30 mg/day) fit easily in a food portion physically; however doses above ~10 mg added zinc are not advisable in foods eaten daily because of the 25 mg EU UL.", "cost_availability": "Commodity ingredient, widely available.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 3, "effect_magnitude": 1, "safety": 6, "reproducibility": 5, "food_suitability": 8, "cost_access": 8, "rationale": "Evidence quality 3: moderate-certainty (B) nulls in older adults and no trials (D) in the target young population. Effect magnitude 1: no cognitive benefit detected in replete adults. Safety 6: essential and safe near requirements but narrow margin to the 25 mg UL with copper-deficiency harm. Reproducibility 5: null findings reproduce across ZENITH, AREDS, REGARDS and MR; positive signals are isolated. Food suitability 8: tiny mass, easy to reach claimable 'source' levels. Cost/access 8: cheap commodity salts (no prices collected)."}, "research_gaps": ["RCT of zinc (5-10 mg/day) on cognition and mood in young adults with low intake or status (e.g., high-phytate Moroccan diets, vegetarians)", "Better zinc status biomarkers to select responders", "Moroccan national data on zinc intake and serum zinc in adults and students", "Cognitive outcomes of zinc fortification of staple foods", "Long-term safety of combined dietary + supplement zinc near the EU UL (copper status monitoring)", "Possible EFSA re-evaluation of the 2003 zinc UL"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 4.4, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": "C", "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"global_cognition": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 12, "n_studies": 20, "n_verified": 20, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 8, "n_systematic_reviews": 5, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["inconsistent", "insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "alpha-linolenic-acid-ala", "name": "Alpha-linolenic acid (ALA)", "category": "D", "category_name": "Fatty acids and lipids", "aliases": ["ALA", "18:3n-3", "plant omega-3", "linolenic acid (alpha)", "flaxseed/linseed oil omega-3", "walnut omega-3", "chia omega-3", "perilla oil omega-3"], "food_sources": ["Flaxseed (linseed) oil (about 50-55% ALA; 1 tbsp about 7 g ALA) and ground flaxseed (about 2.3 g ALA per tbsp)", "Chia seeds (about 5 g ALA per 28 g)", "Walnuts (about 2.5 g ALA per 28 g; the only nut rich in ALA)", "Rapeseed/canola oil (about 1.3 g ALA per tbsp), soybean oil (about 0.9 g per tbsp)", "Perilla oil, hemp seed and oil, camelina oil", "Green leafy vegetables, purslane (small amounts)", "Argan oil is NOT a meaningful ALA source (about 0.1-0.5% ALA); olive oil about 0.6-0.8%"], "active_constituents": ["Alpha-linolenic acid (18:3n-3), the essential n-3 precursor", "Walnuts and flaxseed also provide co-constituents that confound food studies: polyphenols (ellagitannins), lignans (secoisolariciresinol diglucoside in flax), vitamin E (gamma-tocopherol in walnuts), melatonin, fibre"], "one_line_verdict": "ALA is an essential fatty acid with good cardiovascular evidence and plausible cognitive relevance, but conversion to DHA is very low (typically <1-10%), there is no credible RCT evidence that ALA improves cognition in healthy adults, and the walnut/flaxseed cognitive literature is small, mixed and largely driven by observational data; it belongs in Brain Food as a nutritious whole-food fat, not as a cognitive active.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. ALA is essential (humans cannot synthesise it), and its adequate intake (EFSA AI 0.5% of energy, about 1-1.5 g/d; US AI 1.1-1.6 g/d) prevents classic essential-fatty-acid deficiency, which is rare in people eating normal mixed diets. Human stable-isotope studies show that ALA is mostly beta-oxidised or stored and that conversion to EPA is limited and to DHA is very small: in young men on habitual diets, 13C-labelled ALA produced EPA and DPA but no detectable 13C-DHA over 21 days [burdge2002_men]; in young women estimated net fractional conversion was about 21% to EPA, 6% to DPA and 9% to DHA [burdge2002_women], with the sex difference attributed to oestrogen [burdge2006]. A scoping review of 13 RCTs found that even high-dose flaxseed or echium oil did not raise the omega-3 index (EPA+DHA in red cells), and some trials showed reductions, whereas microalgal oil raised it in every study [lane2022]. A dissenting view holds that oral-tracer studies underestimate whole-body DHA synthesis and that the adult brain's DHA requirement (estimated 2.4-3.8 mg/d) could be met from ALA [domenichiello2015]; this remains a modelling argument without cognitive-outcome evidence. Cognitive outcome evidence. Only a handful of RCTs exist. In healthy Japanese older adults (n=60), 2.2 g/d ALA from flaxseed oil for 12 weeks improved only one of many measures (phonemic verbal fluency on the FAB: change 0.30 vs 0.03 points, P=0.049), with no effect on MMSE, MoCA, memory, processing speed, working memory or attention; baseline scores were imbalanced and the study was funded and co-authored by the oil manufacturer [ogawa2023]. In the two-year WAHA trial (n=708 cognitively healthy elders, walnuts 30-60 g/d, about 1.2 g/d ALA plus polyphenols), there was no between-group difference in the global cognition composite (-0.072 vs -0.086, P=0.49); only an unblinded post-hoc site analysis in Barcelona suggested less decline [waha2020]. In college students (n=64, crossover, 8 weeks), walnuts did not improve memory, mood or non-verbal reasoning but increased inferential verbal reasoning by 11.2% (d=0.57) in one of several tests [pribis2012]; an acute crossover (n=32, 50 g walnut breakfast) found faster executive-task reaction times during the day but worse memory recall at 2 h and worse negative affect, with mixed EEG changes [bell2025]. A small Tunisian trial in elderly men combining exercise with 15 g/d walnuts (n=20) reported a MoCA increase only in the walnut group but had no walnut-only arm [kamoun2024]. The systematic reviews are consistent in their caution: none of 5 walnut RCTs found an effect on overall cognition [cahoon2021]; nut studies are too heterogeneous to pool [theodore2021]; the Brainard/Hooper WHO-commissioned meta-analysis of PUFA RCTs rated the effects of ALA on cognition 'unclear' [brainard2020]; and the Suh 2024 dose-response meta-analysis included four ALA trials (WAHA, PREDIMED-Navarra walnut arm, Hashimoto 2021 perilla oil, Ogawa 2023), two of them at high risk of bias for blinding [suh2024]. A one-week flaxseed-oil study (1.5 g/d ALA, n=30, uncontrolled) reported increased plasma BDNF, but also increased malondialdehyde, a marker of lipid peroxidation, and had no control group [hadjighassem2015]; this is a biomarker, not an outcome. Observational evidence. Serum ALA in the top three quartiles was associated with 40-50% lower odds of disabling dementia in a Japanese nested case-control study (OR 0.51-0.61 vs lowest quartile; P-trend 0.01), while EPA and DHA were not [yamagishi2017]; in the Chicago cohort, total n-3 and DHA, but not EPA, were linked to lower Alzheimer risk [morris2003]; in Rotterdam, ALA and other n-3 intakes were unrelated to dementia over 9.6 years [devore2009]; in the Rush cohort, ALA intake was associated with slower global decline only in APOE4 carriers [vanderest2016_apoe]; a US representative sample found walnut consumers had higher baseline cognition but no difference in 4-year trajectory, with walnut intake tracking socioeconomic status and health behaviours [bishop2021]. Dose and duration: cognitive trials used about 1.2-2.2 g/d ALA (flaxseed oil 3.7 g, walnuts 30-60 g) for 8 weeks to 2 years. How large: the single significant findings are on isolated subtests at about 0.3-0.6 SD in small samples and have not been replicated; the primary outcome of the only large trial was null. Reproducibility: no replication of any positive cognitive finding; no independent trial of ALA alone in young adults. What is unknown: whether ALA matters cognitively in people with zero preformed DHA intake (vegans), whether APOE4 carriers respond, and whether whole-food effects of walnuts (polyphenols, vitamin E) would exist with purified ALA. The cardiovascular literature is far stronger (10% lower CVD and 20% lower fatal CHD in cohort meta-analyses; LDL-cholesterol reductions of about 0.1 mmol/L in RCTs [salavila2022]) and underpins the only authorised EU claim (blood cholesterol at 2 g/d).", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Frank ALA deficiency is rare and its correction is not a realistic Brain Food proposition. The relevant 'low baseline' group is people with very low EPA+DHA intake (vegans, low-fish populations, some Moroccan inland communities), in whom ALA is the only endogenous route to DHA; even here, trials show that adding flaxseed oil does not raise the omega-3 index [lane2022], so ALA cannot be presented as a substitute for DHA for brain status. In adequately nourished adults there is no evidence that extra ALA enhances attention, memory, speed or executive function; the single positive subtests (verbal fluency in older adults, inferential reasoning in students) are isolated and unreplicated. The enhancement framing is not supported.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Walnuts are widely marketed with a 'brain-shaped brain food' narrative and flaxseed/chia as 'plant omega-3 for the brain'. The evidence allows only: (a) 'source of omega-3 fatty acids' / 'high in omega-3' nutrition claims where ALA content meets EU thresholds (0.3 g / 0.6 g per 100 g and per 100 kcal); (b) the authorised EU claim 'Alpha-linolenic acid contributes to the maintenance of normal blood cholesterol levels' at 2 g/d; (c) the generic essential fatty acid claims ('ALA and LA are needed for normal growth and development of children'). Any claim that ALA, walnuts or flaxseed improve memory, concentration or protect against dementia is unsubstantiated, unauthorised in the EU and prohibited under Morocco's disease-claim rules. The observational ALA-dementia associations [yamagishi2017] must not be turned into prevention claims. ALA should not be sold as a vegan 'DHA equivalent'; a product aimed at vegans should use algal DHA.", "mechanism": {"summary": "ALA's proposed brain relevance runs through (1) conversion to EPA/DPA and, inefficiently, DHA; (2) direct ALA effects on membranes, TREK-1 potassium channels, BDNF and blood-brain-barrier integrity shown in rodent and cell studies; (3) cardiovascular benefits (lipids, blood pressure, anti-arrhythmic) that could protect vascular cognition over decades; and (4) in whole foods, co-delivered polyphenols, lignans and vitamin E. In humans only ALA absorption and limited conversion to EPA are established; DHA enrichment is small; no step is linked to measured cognition in nourished adults.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Dietary ALA (flax, walnut, chia, rapeseed) -> absorption -> plasma and adipose; most beta-oxidised", "detail": "About 22-33% of a labelled oral dose appears as 13CO2 within 24 h; the rest is stored or incorporated into lipids.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "ALA -> delta-6 desaturase/elongase -> EPA and DPA (liver)", "detail": "Net fractional conversion to EPA about 8-21%; higher in women; suppressed by high linoleic acid intake.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "DPA -> DHA (Sprecher pathway)", "detail": "Very limited: undetectable 13C-DHA in young men; about 9% estimated in young women; flaxseed-oil supplementation does not raise omega-3 index in RCTs.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "ALA/EPA/DHA -> neuronal membrane composition, TREK-1 channel activation, BDNF induction, BBB integrity", "detail": "Rodent and in-vitro work (including stroke neuroprotection models); one uncontrolled 1-week human study showed higher plasma BDNF with flaxseed oil.", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "ALA -> lower LDL-C, blood pressure, inflammation -> vascular brain health", "detail": "RCT meta-analyses show small LDL-C and BP reductions; cohort meta-analyses show lower CVD; long-term cognitive consequence plausible but not tested.", "evidence_level": "plausible"}, {"step": "Walnut co-constituents (ellagitannins -> urolithins, gamma-tocopherol, melatonin) -> antioxidant/anti-inflammatory effects", "detail": "Confound attribution of any walnut effect to ALA; human cognitive effect not demonstrated.", "evidence_level": "speculative"}, {"step": "Any of the above -> measured cognitive performance in healthy adults", "detail": "Primary outcomes of the largest trial null; isolated subtest findings only.", "evidence_level": "speculative"}], "pathways": ["Essential fatty acid precursor pathway (ALA -> EPA -> DPA -> DHA)", "Membrane and ion-channel effects (TREK-1)", "Neurotrophic signalling (BDNF)", "Cardiometabolic/vascular protection", "Whole-food polyphenol and antioxidant co-effects"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "reaction_time", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults 18-30 y, UK, walnut-rich breakfast (50 g walnuts)", "dose_or_intake": "50 g walnuts (about 4.5 g ALA) in a breakfast", "duration": "Single day (2, 4, 6 h)", "grade": "D", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Faster reaction times on executive tasks through the day, but worse memory recall at 2 h (reversed at 6 h) and worse negative affect; EEG differences; likely glucose/satiety and palatability effects rather than ALA.", "effect_estimate": "not reported (pilot, n=32)", "certainty_notes": "Single pilot crossover, whole-food intervention with many confounders, mixed direction; cannot be attributed to ALA.", "key_study_ids": ["bell2025"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "other", "population": "students", "population_detail": "College students (n=64), USA, crossover", "dose_or_intake": "Walnuts (about 60 g/d in banana bread) ", "duration": "8 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "No effect on memory, mood or non-verbal reasoning; inferential verbal reasoning improved 11.2% in one test.", "effect_estimate": "d = 0.57 (P=0.009) for inferential verbal reasoning only; other outcomes null", "certainty_notes": "One positive test among several; whole-food confounding; not replicated; funded by walnut sector (Loma Linda/California Walnut Commission involvement likely but not confirmed from abstract).", "key_study_ids": ["pribis2012", "cahoon2021"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy young adults, purified ALA or flaxseed oil", "dose_or_intake": "1-3 g/d ALA", "duration": "Weeks to months", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No RCT of ALA alone (flaxseed, chia or perilla oil) with cognitive outcomes in healthy young adults was identified.", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "Evidence gap; the only young-adult data are walnut (whole-food) trials.", "key_study_ids": ["lane2022", "suh2024"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Healthy Japanese adults 65-80 y (n=60)", "dose_or_intake": "3.7 g/d flaxseed oil providing 2.2 g ALA", "duration": "12 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Only phonemic verbal fluency on the FAB improved; all other cognitive tests (MMSE, MoCA, WAIS indices, WMS-R memory, Stroop, digit cancellation) unchanged.", "effect_estimate": "Change in FAB lexical fluency 0.30 (SD 0.53) vs 0.03 (SD 0.49), P=0.049 (about 0.5 SD on a 0-3 point subscale)", "certainty_notes": "Single small manufacturer-funded trial, baseline imbalance, one of >20 outcomes significant, no multiplicity correction; cannot exceed C.", "key_study_ids": ["ogawa2023"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Cognitively healthy elders 63-79 y, Spain and USA (n=708)", "dose_or_intake": "Walnuts 30-60 g/d (about 15% energy; about 1.2-2.5 g/d ALA)", "duration": "2 years", "grade": "B", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "No between-group difference in the global cognition composite in modified ITT; post-hoc Barcelona-site difference only.", "effect_estimate": "Composite change -0.072 (95% CI -0.100 to -0.043) walnuts vs -0.086 (-0.115 to -0.057) control, P=0.491", "certainty_notes": "Largest and longest ALA-food cognition trial; unblinded whole-food design (high RoB for blinding), but primary outcome clearly null; B for the conclusion of no benefit.", "key_study_ids": ["waha2020", "suh2024"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Physically active elderly men, Tunisia (n=20), with concurrent training", "dose_or_intake": "15 g/d walnuts", "duration": "6 weeks", "grade": "D", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "MoCA increased from baseline only in the training + walnut group; no walnut-only arm; small.", "effect_estimate": "MoCA +7.7% (SD 2.5) from baseline in walnut group; between-group estimate not reported", "certainty_notes": "n=10 per arm, within-group comparison, exercise co-intervention; hypothesis-generating only.", "key_study_ids": ["kamoun2024"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "dementia_risk", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Community cohorts (Japan, USA, Netherlands), 4-15 y follow-up", "dose_or_intake": "Serum ALA quartiles; dietary ALA tertiles", "duration": "Years", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Japanese nested case-control: higher serum ALA associated with lower disabling dementia; Rotterdam: no association of ALA intake; Rush: association only in APOE4 carriers; US walnut consumers show no slower decline.", "effect_estimate": "Yamagishi: OR 0.57 (0.39-0.85), 0.51 (0.34-0.76), 0.61 (0.41-0.90) for quartiles 2-4 vs 1; Devore: dietary omega-3 not associated (fish HR 0.95, 0.76-1.19)", "certainty_notes": "Observational, inconsistent across cohorts, confounding by overall diet and socioeconomic status.", "key_study_ids": ["yamagishi2017", "devore2009", "vanderest2016_apoe", "bishop2021", "morris2003"]}, {"timeframe": "short_term_days", "domain": "neuroplasticity_biomarkers", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults (n=30, 15 men, 15 women), Iran, pre-post", "dose_or_intake": "Flaxseed oil capsules providing 1.5 g/d ALA (3 x 500 mg)", "duration": "7 days", "grade": "D", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Plasma BDNF increased (more in women) but malondialdehyde (oxidative stress marker) also increased; no cognitive outcome; no control group.", "effect_estimate": "P<0.05 for both BDNF and MDA; magnitudes not reported in abstract", "certainty_notes": "Uncontrolled, tiny, biomarker only, conflicting (pro-oxidant) signal.", "key_study_ids": ["hadjighassem2015"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "vascular_health", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Adults in cohort meta-analyses and lipid RCT meta-analyses", "dose_or_intake": "1-4.5 g/d ALA (dietary) ", "duration": "Weeks to decades", "grade": "B", "direction": "benefit", "effect_summary": "Higher ALA associated with about 10% lower total CVD and 20% lower fatal CHD; RCT meta-analyses show small LDL-C (about -0.1 mmol/L) and blood pressure reductions with flaxseed; basis for the authorised EU cholesterol claim.", "effect_estimate": "Total CVD RR 0.90 (0.81-0.99) dietary; fatal IHD RR 0.80 (0.65-0.98); LDL-C WMD -0.131 mmol/L (-0.191 to -0.071) across 47 trials", "certainty_notes": "Not a cognitive outcome; included because vascular health is the most defensible long-term brain-relevant benefit.", "key_study_ids": ["salavila2022"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "50 g walnuts in breakfast (about 4.5 g ALA + polyphenols)", "onset": "2-6 h post-meal", "duration_studied": "Single day, measures at 2, 4, 6 h", "domain": "executive reaction time; memory recall; mood", "population": "healthy young adults 18-30 y (n=32)", "effect_estimate": "Faster executive RTs across the day; memory recall worse at 2 h, better at 6 h; worse negative affect", "certainty": "very low", "safety": "Nut allergy; palatability complaints", "study_ids": ["bell2025"]}, {"dose": "Purified ALA or flaxseed oil, single dose", "onset": "Not applicable", "duration_studied": "No placebo-controlled single-dose cognitive study found", "domain": "any", "population": "adults", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty": "none (no data)", "safety": "Flaxseed oil well tolerated; laxative at large doses", "study_ids": []}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "7 days", "population": "Healthy adults (n=30), uncontrolled", "outcome": "Plasma BDNF, MDA", "effect_estimate": "Both increased (P<0.05)", "evidence_quality": "very low", "limitations": "No control; biomarker only; pro-oxidant signal", "study_ids": ["hadjighassem2015"]}, {"duration": "8 weeks x2 (crossover)", "population": "College students (n=64)", "outcome": "Verbal/non-verbal reasoning, memory, mood", "effect_estimate": "Inferential verbal reasoning +11.2% (d=0.57); others null", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Whole food; one of several tests; unreplicated", "study_ids": ["pribis2012"]}, {"duration": "12 weeks", "population": "Healthy older adults 65-80 y (n=60)", "outcome": "Six cognitive domains", "effect_estimate": "Only FAB phonemic fluency improved (0.30 vs 0.03, P=0.049)", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Manufacturer-funded; baseline imbalance; multiplicity", "study_ids": ["ogawa2023"]}, {"duration": "6 weeks", "population": "Active elderly men (n=20) with concurrent training", "outcome": "MoCA", "effect_estimate": "Within-group rise only in walnut arm", "evidence_quality": "very low", "limitations": "No walnut-only arm; n=10/arm", "study_ids": ["kamoun2024"]}, {"duration": "2 years", "population": "Cognitively healthy elders 63-79 y (n=708)", "outcome": "Global cognition composite; MRI subset", "effect_estimate": "-0.072 vs -0.086, P=0.49 (null)", "evidence_quality": "moderate (unblinded)", "limitations": "Whole-food; abstention control; post-hoc site effect", "study_ids": ["waha2020"]}, {"duration": "Up to 36 months (meta-analysis, 4 ALA trials among 24)", "population": "Adults >=40 y without dementia", "outcome": "Domain-specific cognition", "effect_estimate": "ALA trials pooled with EPA/DHA; no ALA-specific estimate; two ALA trials high RoB", "evidence_quality": "low for ALA", "limitations": "Not separable from marine n-3", "study_ids": ["suh2024", "brainard2020"]}, {"duration": "4-15 years (observational)", "population": "Community adults, Japan/USA/Netherlands", "outcome": "Incident dementia / cognitive decline", "effect_estimate": "Yamagishi OR about 0.5-0.6 for higher serum ALA; Rotterdam null; Rush APOE4-only; HRS walnut no trajectory effect", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Confounding; inconsistency", "study_ids": ["yamagishi2017", "devore2009", "vanderest2016_apoe", "bishop2021"]}], "safety": {"summary": "ALA from foods and oils is safe at normal and high dietary intakes; no UL exists (EFSA, IOM). Flaxseed oil and walnuts have decades of safe use. Concerns are minor: GI effects of large seed doses, calorie density of nuts and oils, nut allergy (walnut is a major allergen), cyanogenic glycosides in raw flaxseed at very high intakes, lignan phytoestrogens in whole flaxseed, rapid oxidation of flaxseed oil, and a historical, unconfirmed prostate-cancer association. ALA-rich oils have a mild antiplatelet effect of little clinical relevance.", "adverse_effects": ["Loose stools/bloating with large doses of whole or ground flaxseed", "Excess energy intake with nuts/oils if not substituted for other fats", "Walnut allergy (tree-nut allergen; mandatory labelling in EU/US; cross-reactivity)", "Raw flaxseed contains cyanogenic glycosides; relevant only at very high raw intakes (EFSA 2019 opinion on cyanogenic glycosides noted limited risk at usual intakes; not read here)", "Possible mild increase in bleeding time (weak)", "Prostate cancer: older meta-analyses suggested increased risk; the 2013 meta-analysis and RCT meta-analysis do not confirm (RR 1.30, 0.72-2.32)", "Oxidised flaxseed oil (rancid) is unpalatable and may increase lipid peroxidation markers; an uncontrolled study found higher plasma MDA after 1 week of flaxseed oil"], "contraindications": ["Tree-nut allergy (walnut products)", "Flaxseed/linseed allergy (rare)", "Bowel obstruction or severe dysphagia with whole seeds"], "drug_interactions": ["Anticoagulants/antiplatelets: theoretical additive effect at high intakes; monitor", "Whole flaxseed fibre may reduce absorption of oral medications taken simultaneously (separate by 1-2 h)", "Lignans (whole flax) have weak oestrogenic activity; relevance to hormone therapies uncertain"], "upper_limits": "No UL (EFSA DRV opinion for fats 2010; IOM 2002). EFSA AI for ALA 0.5% of energy (about 1-1.5 g/d); US AI 1.6 g/d men, 1.1 g/d women. Typical intakes in Morocco are low where olive/argan oils dominate and nuts are expensive.", "sleep_impact": "No known effect; walnuts contain small amounts of melatonin but no cognitive-sleep interaction demonstrated.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Cohorts over decades (ALA intake and CVD); RCTs up to 2-4 years (WAHA, PREDIMED nuts). Safe with long-term use.", "daily_use_appropriate": "yes", "special_populations": "Pregnancy: ALA is safe; conversion is higher in women but still insufficient to guarantee fetal DHA supply, so preformed DHA is recommended. Children: safe as food; whole nuts are a choking hazard under 4-5 years. Vegans: ALA is the sole endogenous route to DHA but does not raise omega-3 index; algal DHA advised. Men with prostate cancer history: no confirmed risk, but the historical signal may be raised by clinicians. Allergic individuals: strict labelling.", "specialist_review_needed": ["Dietitian for energy-density and allergen management", "Food scientist for oxidation control of flaxseed oil in products", "Regulatory affairs for nutrition-claim thresholds and allergen labelling in Morocco/EU/US"], "hard_safety_gate": "pass", "gate_reason": "Food-level safety with long-standing use; allergen and oxidation issues are standard formulation controls; no material toxicity at effective intakes."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Authorised Article 13(1) claims (Commission Regulation (EU) 432/2012, consolidated text accessed 2026-10-25 via EUR-Lex): 'Alpha-linolenic acid contributes to the maintenance of normal blood cholesterol levels' - only for foods that are at least a source of ALA per the nutrition claim 'source of omega-3 fatty acids' (>=0.3 g ALA per 100 g and per 100 kcal), with information that the beneficial effect is obtained with a daily intake of 2 g ALA (EFSA Journal 2009;7(9):1252 and 2011;9(6):2203; register IDs 493, 568). 'Essential fatty acids are needed for normal growth and development of children' (Article 14, ALA+LA) also exists. Nutrition claims: 'source of omega-3' (>=0.3 g ALA per 100 g and 100 kcal) and 'high omega-3' (>=0.6 g). No authorised cognitive, brain or vision claim for ALA; EFSA rejected ALA brain-function claims for lack of evidence (opinion references not retrieved here). Flaxseed, walnut, chia (within authorised uses) and perilla oil are conventional or authorised novel foods (chia seed is on the Union list with category limits).", "us": "ALA-rich foods and oils (flaxseed oil, walnuts, canola oil) are conventional foods/GRAS; walnuts have an FDA qualified health claim on heart disease ('Supportive but not conclusive research shows that eating 1.5 ounces of walnuts per day, as part of a low saturated fat and low cholesterol diet and not resulting in increased caloric intake, may reduce the risk of coronary heart disease', 2004 enforcement discretion). No cognition claims; structure/function claims under DSHEA for supplements require substantiation (FTC). The 2019 EPA/DHA qualified claims do not extend to ALA.", "morocco": "No ALA-specific regulation found. Walnuts, flaxseed and oils are conventional foods under ONSSA (Law 28-07 and labelling Decree 2-12-389); allergen labelling applies. Supplements containing flaxseed oil fall under the uncertain food-supplement framework described in press reports (draft law, AMMPS/ONSSA split; accessed 2026-10-25); health claims would have to follow implementing texts not yet located. Status uncertain; use only nutrition-type statements.", "permitted_claims_examples": ["EU (Reg. 432/2012): 'Alpha-linolenic acid contributes to the maintenance of normal blood cholesterol levels' - food at least a source of ALA; consumer informed that the effect is obtained with 2 g ALA per day.", "EU nutrition claim: 'Source of omega-3 fatty acids' (>=0.3 g ALA per 100 g and per 100 kcal) / 'High omega-3 fatty acids' (>=0.6 g).", "EU (Article 14): 'Essential fatty acids are needed for normal growth and development of children' (ALA + LA, with intake conditions).", "US (FDA qualified claim 2004): walnuts 1.5 oz/day may reduce risk of coronary heart disease (with qualifier)."], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "None for flaxseed oil, walnuts, rapeseed oil. Chia seeds and chia oil are authorised novel foods in the EU with category-specific limits; perilla oil and hemp seed oil are not novel. Argan oil is not an ALA source. No NDI issues in the US for these foods.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Excellent as a whole-food ingredient: walnuts, ground flaxseed and chia fit bars, granolas, breads and yoghurt toppings and deliver 1-2.5 g ALA per serving easily, with fibre, protein and polyphenols. Flaxseed oil is suitable for cold applications only.", "formats": ["Walnut pieces in bars, cereals, breads (28-30 g serving = about 2.5 g ALA)", "Ground flaxseed in breads, porridge, smoothies (1 tbsp = about 2.3 g ALA)", "Chia seeds in puddings, drinks (28 g = about 5 g ALA)", "Cold-pressed flaxseed oil in dressings (1 tbsp = about 7 g ALA)", "Rapeseed/canola oil as cooking fat (1 tbsp = about 1.3 g ALA)", "ALA-enriched margarines/spreads"], "stability": "ALA oxidises faster than oleic/linoleic acid but slower than EPA/DHA; flaxseed oil is very oxidation-prone (store cold, dark, nitrogen-flushed; not for frying). Whole/ground flaxseed and walnuts are more stable because of intrinsic antioxidants but walnuts go rancid within months at room temperature; ground flax should be refrigerated. Baking at moderate temperature retains most ALA.", "taste": "Walnuts: pleasant, slightly bitter/astringent; flaxseed oil: grassy, bitter if oxidised; chia: neutral. Palatability of walnut-rich meals can be an issue (negative affect in the acute trial).", "bioavailability_matrix": "ALA from whole walnuts and ground flaxseed is well absorbed; whole flaxseed passes largely undigested (grind for ALA release). Conversion to EPA is improved when linoleic acid intake is moderate (omega-6:omega-3 ratio) and is higher in women; no food matrix raises DHA meaningfully.", "dose_feasibility": "2 g/d ALA (EU cholesterol claim level) and the 2.2 g/d used in the Ogawa trial fit easily in one serving of walnuts (30 g) or ground flax (1 tbsp). Walnut doses used in WAHA (30-60 g/d, about 200-400 kcal) are feasible but calorie-dense.", "cost_availability": "Walnuts are imported and relatively expensive in Morocco; flaxseed (zreeat el kettan) is traditional, local and cheap in Moroccan markets; chia is imported. Rapeseed oil is available but less common than sunflower/olive. Prices not researched.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 2, "effect_magnitude": 1, "safety": 9, "reproducibility": 2, "food_suitability": 9, "cost_access": 7, "rationale": "Evidence quality 2: no RCT shows a cognitive benefit of ALA on a primary outcome in healthy adults; the only large trial (WAHA) is null, the only purified-ALA trial is manufacturer-funded with one positive subtest; cohort data inconsistent (Grade C-D for benefit). Effect magnitude 1: isolated subtest effects of about 0.3-0.6 SD in small samples, primary outcomes null. Safety 9: ordinary food with allergen and oxidation controls. Reproducibility 2: no replicated finding. Food suitability 9: walnuts, flax and chia are ideal food ingredients. Cost/access 7: flaxseed is cheap and local in Morocco; walnuts costlier."}, "research_gaps": ["Placebo-controlled RCT of purified ALA (flaxseed or perilla oil, 2-4 g/d, 6-12 months) versus algal DHA versus placebo in healthy young adults with low fish intake, with pre-registered cognitive primary outcomes", "Trials in vegans comparing ALA-only versus ALA + algal DHA for omega-3 index and cognition", "APOE4-stratified ALA trials", "Replication of the Ogawa verbal-fluency finding by an independent group", "Walnut trials with blinded, matched controls (e.g., other nuts) to separate ALA from polyphenol effects", "Moroccan dietary surveys of ALA intake (flaxseed, argan, olive-based diets)"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 4.4, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "pass", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": "B", "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"executive_function": "C", "global_cognition": "D"}, "n_outcome_rows": 9, "n_studies": 24, "n_verified": 24, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 6, "n_systematic_reviews": 5, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["benefit", "inconsistent", "insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "medium-chain-triglycerides-and-ketone-esters", "name": "Medium-chain triglycerides, ketone esters and ketone salts", "category": "D", "category_name": "Fatty acids and lipids", "aliases": ["MCT oil", "medium-chain triglycerides (C8 caprylic / C10 capric)", "tricaprylin / caprylic triglyceride (AC-1202 Axona, AC-1204, tricaprilin)", "ketogenic MCT drink (kMCT)", "ketone monoester, (R)-3-hydroxybutyl (R)-3-hydroxybutyrate (KME, deltaG, HVMN, KetoneAid)", "bis-octanoyl (R)-1,3-butanediol (ketone diester, Avela)", "beta-hydroxybutyrate (BHB) salts (sodium, potassium, calcium, magnesium BHB)", "exogenous ketones"], "food_sources": ["coconut oil (about 55-60% medium-chain fatty acids, mostly lauric acid C12 which is only weakly ketogenic; C8+C10 roughly 12-15%)", "palm kernel oil (similar profile to coconut oil)", "milk fat and butter (small amounts of C6-C10, a few %)", "commercial MCT oil is a fractionated/re-esterified product of coconut or palm kernel oil, not a whole food", "ketone esters and BHB salts do not occur in food; endogenous ketones are produced only during fasting, prolonged exercise or carbohydrate restriction (see ketogenic-diet-chronic)"], "active_constituents": ["octanoic (caprylic, C8:0) and decanoic (capric, C10:0) acids, released from MCT, absorbed via the portal vein and oxidised in liver to acetoacetate and D-beta-hydroxybutyrate; C10 may also act directly (AMPA receptor antagonism, PPAR-gamma) in preclinical work", "D-beta-hydroxybutyrate (D-BHB) and acetoacetate, the circulating ketone bodies", "(R)-1,3-butanediol, released from ketone esters and converted to D-BHB in liver", "sodium/potassium/calcium/magnesium counter-ions in BHB salts (often racemic D/L-BHB; L-BHB is poorly oxidised)"], "one_line_verdict": "MCT oil, ketone esters and ketone salts reliably raise blood ketones and (in PET studies) brain ketone uptake, but cognitive benefits in healthy young adults rest on a handful of tiny, mostly single-lab trials with mixed results, the clearest signals are in impaired or stressed groups (MCI, hypoxia) and even there a large phase-3 Alzheimer's trial failed; ketone esters are not authorised foods in the EU, and the main realistic product, MCT oil, causes dose-limiting GI upset at the 12-30 g/day used in trials.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. Oral MCT (C8/C10) and ketone esters raise plasma D-beta-hydroxybutyrate dose-dependently: MCT 20-30 g gives mild ketosis (about 0.3-0.6 mM; pooled MD +0.36 mM in AD/MCI trials [avgerinos2020]), whereas the ketone monoester gives 1-4 mM within 30 min [clarke2012, sotomota2019, mcclure2024]. PET with 11C-acetoacetate shows the human brain takes up ketones in direct proportion to plasma levels, and 30 g/day MCT roughly doubles brain ketone consumption in AD without lowering glucose use [croteau2018]. That is an established biomarker effect, not a cognitive outcome. Healthy young adults and students. Three small MCT trials report benefits: 12-18 g/day for 4 weeks in 30 university students improved Trail Making and digit span vs a carbohydrate gel [ashton2021]; 12 g/day for 2 weeks offset exercise-induced cognitive decline in 19 adults [ashton2023]; a single 12 g dose improved inhibitory control and 4 weeks improved working memory vs olive oil in 36 young adults [yuuki2026]. Two of the three come from one group, samples are 9-18 per arm, outcomes are many and uncorrected, and no trial was preregistered with a primary cognitive endpoint we could verify (grade C). Acute ketone ester or salt data in young adults are mixed: a lower Stroop interference after 395 mg/kg KME in 40 young adults [yu2025], less multitasking error after exhausting exercise (n=11) [evans2018], less choice-reaction decline after mental fatigue (n=9) [quinones2022] and better 1-back accuracy in heat when co-ingested with caffeine (n=17) [lints2026], versus null results for ketone salts after sprint exercise [waldman2018], for firefighters' performance [waldman2022] and for cognition at 4,000 m simulated altitude [stalmans2025]; only extreme hypoxia (6,100 m) showed a clear rescue (+6.8 correct responses/min) [mcclure2024]. Older and impaired populations. A single 20 g MCT meal helped 19 non-demented elderly, mostly those with low baseline scores [ota2016], but GSK's 30 g/day MCT in 80 healthy older adults for 14 days had no acute or chronic effect and caused diarrhoea in 75% [oneill2019]; 12 weeks of 25 g/day ketone diester did not change cognition or function in healthy older adults [stubbs2025]. In MCI a 30 g/day kMCT drink for 6 months improved some memory, verbal fluency and naming scores (partial eta2 0.05-0.10, no multiplicity correction, 32% attrition, Nestle-supported) [fortier2021]. In AD, AC-1202 improved ADAS-Cog by 1.9 points at day 45 in an industry trial [henderson2009], but the confirmatory 413-patient AC-1204 phase-3 trial was null [henderson2020]. Pooled evidence. An industry-linked 2026 meta-analysis of 29 protocols reports SMD 0.29 (95% CI 0.16-0.41) across all populations, 0.36 in non-demented 'healthy' groups, with I2 91% and borderline funnel asymmetry (Egger p=0.08) [bonnechere2026]; this mixes athletes, obese, T2D and AD trials and cannot be read as proof of enhancement in students. What is unknown: effects of realistic daily food doses (5-10 g MCT) on cognition in rested young adults; sleep-deprivation trials; replication by independent preregistered trials; and any long-term cognitive outcome in healthy people.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Ketones are not nutrients and there is no deficiency state; the body makes them whenever carbohydrate is scarce. The theoretical rationale is fuel substitution where brain glucose use is impaired (MCI/Alzheimer's, where PET shows glucose but not ketone uptake is reduced [croteau2018]) or acutely compromised (severe hypoxia [mcclure2024]). The best-supported signals accordingly come from impaired or stressed brains [fortier2021, mcclure2024, avgerinos2020], and even there a large phase-3 trial failed [henderson2020]. In well-nourished, rested young adults any benefit is enhancement, supported only by small single-lab trials [ashton2021, ashton2023, yuuki2026] and contradicted by several null ketone ester/salt trials [waldman2018, stalmans2025]. Healthy older adults show inconsistent results [ota2016, oneill2019, stubbs2025]. Findings in MCI/AD patients must not be generalised to students or knowledge workers.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Marketing for MCT oil ('brain octane', 'clean mental energy', 'bulletproof focus') and for ketone drinks ('fuel your brain', 'cognitive edge') relies on PET evidence of brain ketone uptake and on Alzheimer's/MCI trials. The evidence supports only narrower statements: MCT and ketone esters raise blood ketones and brain ketone uptake; small trials suggest possible modest cognitive benefits in MCI and under extreme hypoxia; in healthy young adults the evidence is limited and inconsistent. 'Feeling focused' or appetite suppression after ketone drinks are not measured cognitive gains. No authorised EU health claim exists for MCT or ketones and cognition (the claims register contains none; not re-fetched here), so EU cognitive claims are prohibited; in the US a structure/function claim would need FTC 'competent and reliable scientific evidence', which three small trials do not provide for healthy adults. Ketone monoesters cannot legally be sold as food in the EU at present (novel food not authorised as far as could be verified), and BHB salts received a negative EFSA safety opinion in 2022. Much of the positive literature and the main 2026 meta-analysis carries industry funding or patent/equity conflicts (Accera/Cerecin, Nestle, HVMN, TdeltaS, Component Health).", "mechanism": {"summary": "MCT are hydrolysed to C8/C10 fatty acids that reach the liver directly via the portal vein, enter mitochondria without carnitine, and are converted to acetoacetate and D-BHB; ketone esters are hydrolysed to D-BHB and (R)-1,3-butanediol, which the liver converts to more D-BHB; BHB salts deliver BHB directly (often racemic). Ketones cross the blood-brain barrier via monocarboxylate transporters (MCT1/2) in proportion to plasma concentration and are oxidised by neurons and astrocytes. Proposed brain mechanisms are energetic (alternative fuel when glucose use is impaired), lower blood glucose and lactate, improved cerebral blood flow, and signalling effects of BHB (HDAC inhibition, HCAR2, NLRP3 inhibition) or of decanoic acid (AMPA antagonism), the latter mainly from animal and in-vitro work. Brain ketone uptake is established in humans; translation into better cognition is not established in healthy adults.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Oral MCT (C8/C10), ketone ester or BHB salt -> hepatic ketogenesis or direct BHB delivery -> raised plasma D-BHB", "detail": "MCT 20-30 g: about +0.36 mM BHB (pooled RCTs) [avgerinos2020]; ketone monoester 140-714 mg/kg: peak BHB up to 3.3 mM within 1-2 h, half-life 0.8-3.1 h [clarke2012]; 25 g monoester three times daily sustained 4 mM peaks for 28 days [sotomota2019]; ketone salt 11 g gave only 0.53 mM [waldman2018].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Plasma ketones -> transport across blood-brain barrier (monocarboxylate transporters) -> brain ketone oxidation", "detail": "11C-acetoacetate PET: brain ketone uptake is proportional to plasma ketones, with the same slope in AD as in healthy young adults; 30 g/day MCT for 1 month doubled brain ketone consumption without reducing glucose uptake [croteau2018].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Extra ketone fuel -> compensation for regional brain glucose hypometabolism", "detail": "Relevant where glucose use is impaired (MCI, AD, severe hypoxia) [croteau2018, fortier2021, mcclure2024]; in healthy young brains glucose supply is not limiting, so the rationale is weaker.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Systemic effects -> lower blood glucose/lactate, altered cerebral blood flow and oxygen saturation", "detail": "Ketone esters lowered blood glucose 0.5-1.5 mM in exercise trials [quinones2022, lints2026]; 14 days of KME raised extracranial cerebral blood flow by 10-12% in obese adults [walsh2021]; attenuated SpO2 decline at 6,100 m [mcclure2024] but not at 4,000 m [stalmans2025]. Biomarkers, not cognition.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "BHB signalling (HDAC inhibition, HCAR2, NLRP3 inflammasome) and decanoic-acid AMPA antagonism -> neuroprotection, BDNF", "detail": "Shown in cell and rodent models; BDNF unchanged after 14 days KME in humans [walsh2021].", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Measured human cognitive outcome", "detail": "Small benefits in MCI over 6 months [fortier2021] and under severe hypoxia [mcclure2024]; null phase-3 AD trial [henderson2020]; mixed small trials in healthy young adults [ashton2021, yuuki2026, yu2025, waldman2018, stalmans2025]; null in healthy older adults [oneill2019, stubbs2025].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["hepatic ketogenesis from medium-chain fatty acids (carnitine-independent beta-oxidation)", "monocarboxylate-transporter mediated brain ketone uptake", "brain energy rescue in glucose hypometabolism", "glucose/lactate lowering and cerebrovascular effects", "BHB as signalling metabolite (HDAC, HCAR2, NLRP3) - preclinical", "decanoic acid AMPA-receptor antagonism - preclinical/epilepsy"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy university students and young adults (mean age about 20-25 y), rested, mixed sex; includes executive tasks (Trail Making, digit span, spatial span)", "dose_or_intake": "MCT (C8:C10 30:70 or unspecified) 12-18 g/day", "duration": "2-4 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Ashton 2021 (n=30, 10/arm): Trail Making A/B and digit span forwards/backwards improved after 2-3 weeks vs carbohydrate gel (p<0.001), no effect on attention or reaction time, no dose-response 12 vs 18 g. Ashton 2023 (n=19): better pre-exercise performance after 2 weeks of 12 g/day. Yuuki 2026 (n=36): working memory better after 4 weeks of 12 g MCT vs olive oil (p=0.04).", "effect_estimate": "Standardised effect sizes and CIs not reported in abstracts; p-values only (p<0.001 to p=0.04).", "certainty_notes": "Downgraded for imprecision (n=9-18 per arm), risk of bias (two of three trials from one laboratory, multiple uncorrected outcomes, carbohydrate placebo not taste-matched, no verified preregistration) and possible publication bias; no independent replication. Practical relevance for students unknown.", "key_study_ids": ["ashton2021", "ashton2023", "yuuki2026", "bonnechere2026"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy young adults, rested, no exercise or environmental stressor (Stroop, inhibitory control)", "dose_or_intake": "Ketone monoester 395 mg/kg (about 25-30 g) or MCT 12 g single dose", "duration": "Single dose, tested 30-90 min after intake", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "KME reduced Stroop interference and increased fNIRS prefrontal connectivity in 40 young adults (crossover) [yu2025]; single 12 g MCT improved inhibitory control vs olive oil but not memory or working memory [yuuki2026]. Ashton 2021 saw no effect in week 1.", "effect_estimate": "Not reported as SMD; Stroop interference trial effect beta 25.5 ms (model estimate) [yu2025]; IC p<0.05 [yuuki2026].", "certainty_notes": "Two small trials, different compounds and tasks; downgraded for imprecision and inconsistency. Ketone ester taste is hard to blind.", "key_study_ids": ["yu2025", "yuuki2026", "ashton2021"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "cognitive_fatigue", "population": "high_cognitive_workload", "population_detail": "Healthy young men (athletes, firefighters) tested during or after strenuous exercise, mental fatigue or heat", "dose_or_intake": "Ketone monoester 25 g or 0.5-0.75 g/kg; ketone salt 11.4 g", "duration": "Single dose", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Positive single outcomes: fewer multitasking errors after exhausting shuttle running (d=0.70, n=11) [evans2018]; smaller drop in choice-reaction accuracy after mental fatigue (1.3% vs 3.4%, n=9) [quinones2022]; better 1-back accuracy in heat when co-ingested with caffeine vs carbohydrate+caffeine (n=17) [lints2026]. Null: ketone salt after Wingates (n=15) [waldman2018]; firefighter search-and-rescue performance (n=14) [waldman2022]. Most other tasks in the positive trials were null. Meta-analytic subgroup with exercise stress SMD 0.26 (-0.03 to 0.55) [bonnechere2026].", "effect_estimate": "Exercise-stress subgroup SMD 0.26 (95% CI -0.03 to 0.55) [bonnechere2026]; individual trials d up to 0.70 on single outcomes.", "certainty_notes": "Very small crossover trials, multiple outcomes with selective significance, unblinding risk (bitter ester taste), heterogeneous stressors; downgraded for imprecision, inconsistency, risk of bias. Cognitive-fatigue relevance to desk workers is indirect.", "key_study_ids": ["evans2018", "quinones2022", "lints2026", "waldman2018", "waldman2022", "bonnechere2026"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "processing_speed", "population": "other", "population_detail": "Healthy young men (military personnel, trained volunteers) during simulated altitude hypoxia", "dose_or_intake": "Ketone monoester 650 mg/kg split dose; or ketone ester over 5.5 h", "duration": "Single exposure (20 min to 5.5 h)", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "At 6,100 m equivalent (9.7% O2) KME attenuated the fall in code-substitution efficiency (+6.8 correct/min, 95% CL 1.0-12.6) and in SpO2 (n=16, single-blind) [mcclure2024]; at 4,000 m for 5.5 h ketone ester did not affect cognition and lowered peak exercise power by 3.6% (n=13, double-blind) [stalmans2025].", "effect_estimate": "+6.8 correct responses/min (95% CL 1.0 to 12.6) [mcclure2024]; no effect [stalmans2025]", "certainty_notes": "Two small trials with conflicting results; benefit may depend on extreme hypoxaemia; one positive trial single-blind with ketone-industry co-authors. Not relevant to students or office workers.", "key_study_ids": ["mcclure2024", "stalmans2025"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "sleep_deprived", "population_detail": "Sleep-deprived adults (e.g. students during exams, shift workers)", "dose_or_intake": "not studied", "duration": "not studied", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No randomised trial of MCT, ketone esters or ketone salts under sleep deprivation was identified; the rationale rests on energetic mechanisms only.", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "No human outcome data; mechanistic plausibility only.", "key_study_ids": ["bonnechere2026"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Non-demented adults aged 55-80", "dose_or_intake": "MCT 20-30 g single dose", "duration": "Single dose, tested 90-180 min", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Ota 2016 (n=19, crossover): improved digit span, Trail Making B and global score after a 20 g MCT ketogenic meal, mainly in those with low baseline scores. O'Neill 2019 (n=80 crossover, GSK): 30 g/day MCT raised BHB but did not improve cognition or memory-related fMRI activity on day 1.", "effect_estimate": "Global score Z=-2.4, p=0.017 [ota2016]; no significant differences [oneill2019]", "certainty_notes": "Positive trial small and unregistered; larger industry trial null; downgraded for inconsistency and imprecision.", "key_study_ids": ["ota2016", "oneill2019", "giannos2022"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_aging", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Healthy or frail older adults without dementia (including nursing-home residents in some trials)", "dose_or_intake": "MCT 6-30 g/day; ketone diester 25 g/day", "duration": "14 days to 12 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Systematic review of 6 RCTs: 4/6 reported better memory, especially working memory, no meta-analysis [giannos2022]. O'Neill 2019 null at 14 days (30 g/day). Stubbs 2025 null on exploratory cognitive/functional outcomes after 12 weeks of ketone diester (n=23 completers).", "effect_estimate": "Not pooled; individual trials null or small positive without effect sizes.", "certainty_notes": "Heterogeneous designs, small samples, positive results concentrated in lower-baseline or frail participants; downgraded for inconsistency, imprecision and indirectness.", "key_study_ids": ["giannos2022", "oneill2019", "stubbs2025"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "older_adults_mci", "population_detail": "Adults about 70-75 y with mild cognitive impairment (MoCA 18-26)", "dose_or_intake": "Ketogenic MCT drink (60% C8, 40% C10) 30 g/day (15 g twice daily)", "duration": "6 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "kMCT vs high-oleic sunflower oil placebo (n=39 vs 44 completers of 122 enrolled): free recall trial 1 (+1.0 vs +0.2 words, p=0.047), category fluency (+1.9 vs -1.0 words, p=0.005), Boston Naming (+1.1 vs -0.2), Trail-Making errors improved; no change in attention or processing speed; brain ketone uptake +230% in the earlier phase.", "effect_estimate": "Partial eta2 0.046-0.098 (small to moderate); no CIs reported [fortier2021]", "certainty_notes": "Single trial, 32% attrition with completer analysis, many outcomes without multiplicity correction, product supplied and co-author employed by Nestle; consistent in direction with MCT AD/MCI meta-analysis [avgerinos2020]. Patient data, not generalisable to healthy people.", "key_study_ids": ["fortier2021", "avgerinos2020"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Mild-to-moderate Alzheimer's disease (MMSE 14-26), often stratified by APOE4", "dose_or_intake": "Caprylic triglyceride (AC-1202/AC-1204) 20 g/day; MCT 17-56 g/day", "duration": "90 days to 6 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "AC-1202 (n=152): ADAS-Cog 1.9 points better at day 45 (ITT), larger in APOE4 non-carriers, not significant overall at day 90 [henderson2009]. Confirmatory AC-1204 phase 3 in 413 APOE4 non-carriers: no effect on ADAS-Cog11 or global impression at 26 weeks [henderson2020]. Meta-analysis (pre-2020): combined ADAS-Cog/MMSE SMD -0.29 (-0.55 to -0.03), I2 0% [avgerinos2020].", "effect_estimate": "ADAS-Cog difference -0.54 (95% CI -1.24 to 0.16) pooled [avgerinos2020]; LS difference -0.76, p=0.25 in phase 3 [henderson2020]", "certainty_notes": "The largest, best-conducted trial is null; earlier positive results came from post-hoc subgroups in company-funded trials. Clinical population, not applicable to Brain Food targets.", "key_study_ids": ["henderson2009", "henderson2020", "avgerinos2020"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "processing_speed", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Middle-aged and older adults with obesity (mean 56 y, BMI 34)", "dose_or_intake": "Ketone monoester 12 g BHB three times daily", "duration": "14 days", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Digit-symbol substitution +2.7 correct responses vs placebo; Stroop and task switching not reported as improved; extracranial cerebral blood flow +11-12%; BDNF unchanged (n=14 crossover).", "effect_estimate": "+2.7 DSST correct responses; CI not reported in abstract [walsh2021]", "certainty_notes": "Single tiny crossover; one of three tests positive; cerebral blood flow is a biomarker. Not applicable to healthy young adults.", "key_study_ids": ["walsh2021"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Pooled RCTs: athletes, healthy young and older adults, obese/T2D adults, MCI and AD patients", "dose_or_intake": "MCT 6-56 g/day, ketone monoester 0.3-0.75 g/kg, ketone salts", "duration": "Single dose to 6 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "29 protocols, 1,117 participants: overall SMD 0.29; ketone ester 0.37 vs MCT 0.15; 'healthy' (non-MCI/AD) 0.36 vs MCI/AD 0.15; acute 0.39 vs longer 0.19; positive dose meta-regression.", "effect_estimate": "SMD 0.29 (95% CI 0.16-0.41), I2 91%, Egger intercept 2.65 (p=0.08) [bonnechere2026]", "certainty_notes": "Very high heterogeneity, pooling of disparate tests and populations, many tiny crossover trials, borderline small-study asymmetry and authors with ketone-industry equity/employment; downgraded for inconsistency, indirectness, publication bias and conflict of interest.", "key_study_ids": ["bonnechere2026"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "Ketone monoester 395 mg/kg (about 25-30 g)", "onset": "30-90 min (BHB peaks within 30 min)", "duration_studied": "90 min", "domain": "executive_function (Stroop interference)", "population": "healthy young adults, normal and overweight (n=40)", "effect_estimate": "Reduced Stroop interference; SMD not reported", "certainty": "C", "safety": "Lowers blood glucose; bitter taste; no serious AEs reported", "study_ids": ["yu2025"]}, {"dose": "MCT 12 g", "onset": "75 min", "duration_studied": "75 min", "domain": "inhibitory control (better); memory and working memory (no effect)", "population": "healthy young adults (n=36)", "effect_estimate": "p<0.05 vs olive oil; effect size not reported", "certainty": "C", "safety": "GI symptoms not reported at 12 g", "study_ids": ["yuuki2026"]}, {"dose": "Ketone monoester 25 g or 0.5-0.75 g/kg; ketone salt 11.4 g", "onset": "30 min before exercise/stressor", "duration_studied": "45-150 min of exercise or task", "domain": "cognitive fatigue, multitasking, choice reaction, n-back during exercise or heat", "population": "trained young men (n=9-17 per trial)", "effect_estimate": "Isolated positive outcomes (d=0.70 multitasking [evans2018]; 1.3 vs 3.4% CRT decline [quinones2022]; +2.8% 1-back accuracy [lints2026]); nulls for ketone salts [waldman2018] and firefighters [waldman2022]", "certainty": "C", "safety": "Higher RPE in firefighters; possible GI upset; lowers glucose", "study_ids": ["evans2018", "quinones2022", "lints2026", "waldman2018", "waldman2022"]}, {"dose": "Ketone monoester 650 mg/kg split", "onset": "30 min", "duration_studied": "20 min severe hypoxia; 5.5 h moderate hypoxia", "domain": "processing speed / cognitive efficiency under hypoxia", "population": "healthy young men, military (n=16) and volunteers (n=13)", "effect_estimate": "+6.8 correct/min (95% CL 1.0-12.6) at 6,100 m [mcclure2024]; null at 4,000 m [stalmans2025]", "certainty": "C", "safety": "Metabolic acidosis (lower pH, bicarbonate); 3.6% lower peak exercise power at altitude", "study_ids": ["mcclure2024", "stalmans2025"]}, {"dose": "MCT 20-30 g", "onset": "90-180 min", "duration_studied": "Single day", "domain": "working memory, executive function", "population": "healthy older adults (n=19; n=80)", "effect_estimate": "Positive in small trial, mainly low-baseline participants [ota2016]; null in larger GSK trial [oneill2019]", "certainty": "C", "safety": "Diarrhoea in 75-100% at 30 g/day [oneill2019]", "study_ids": ["ota2016", "oneill2019"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "2-4 weeks", "population": "healthy young adults / university students", "outcome": "working memory, executive function (Trail Making, digit span)", "effect_estimate": "Improvements p<0.001 to p=0.04 vs carbohydrate gel or olive oil; no effect on attention/reaction time; SMDs not reported", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "n=19-36, two of three trials from one lab, multiple uncorrected outcomes, no verified preregistration", "study_ids": ["ashton2021", "ashton2023", "yuuki2026"]}, {"duration": "14 days to 12 weeks", "population": "healthy older adults", "outcome": "cognition, memory-related fMRI, function", "effect_estimate": "Null for 30 g/day MCT (14 d) and 25 g/day ketone diester (12 wk); SR reports 4/6 small trials positive for memory", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "Small, heterogeneous; diarrhoea limited tolerability of MCT", "study_ids": ["oneill2019", "stubbs2025", "giannos2022"]}, {"duration": "14 days", "population": "adults with obesity", "outcome": "processing speed (DSST), cerebral blood flow", "effect_estimate": "+2.7 DSST correct; +11-12% extracranial blood flow; BDNF unchanged", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "n=14 crossover; biomarker-heavy", "study_ids": ["walsh2021"]}, {"duration": "6 months", "population": "older adults with MCI", "outcome": "episodic memory, verbal fluency, naming, executive errors", "effect_estimate": "Partial eta2 0.05-0.10 on selected tests", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "Single trial, 32% attrition, no multiplicity correction, industry product", "study_ids": ["fortier2021"]}, {"duration": "90 days to 6 months", "population": "mild-to-moderate Alzheimer's disease", "outcome": "ADAS-Cog, global impression", "effect_estimate": "Early positive subgroup findings not confirmed; phase 3 null (LS difference -0.76, p=0.25)", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "Industry-funded, post-hoc APOE4 subgroups; failed confirmatory trial", "study_ids": ["henderson2009", "henderson2020", "avgerinos2020"]}, {"duration": "5-28 days", "population": "healthy adults", "outcome": "safety and tolerability of ketone monoester", "effect_estimate": "No change in body weight, lipids, glucose, electrolytes, blood gases or kidney function over 28 days at 3 x 26.8 g/day; mild nausea after 6 of 2,016 drinks", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "n=24, uncontrolled, developer-run, no cognitive outcomes", "study_ids": ["sotomota2019", "clarke2012"]}], "safety": {"summary": "MCT oil is a long-used food fat; its main limitation is dose-dependent GI intolerance (diarrhoea, cramps, nausea) that affected 75-100% of older adults at 30 g/day [oneill2019] and requires gradual titration (start 5 g). Ketone monoester appears well tolerated in short studies (28 days, 80 g/day) [sotomota2019, clarke2012] but causes transient metabolic acidosis, lowers blood glucose and can impair high-intensity exercise [stalmans2025]; long-term safety beyond 12 weeks is unknown and the main safety data are developer-generated. BHB salts deliver substantial sodium/potassium/calcium/magnesium loads per serving and often contain L-BHB; EFSA could not establish their safety as a novel food (2022). Ketones should be used cautiously with SGLT2 inhibitors or insulin-treated diabetes (ketoacidosis risk) and in liver disease.", "adverse_effects": ["Diarrhoea, abdominal cramps, nausea, vomiting with MCT >15-20 g/day, especially on an empty stomach [oneill2019, henderson2009]", "Nausea, bitter/solvent taste and GI upset with ketone esters at high doses [clarke2012]", "Transient metabolic acidosis (lower pH and bicarbonate) after ketone esters [stalmans2025]", "Lower blood glucose (about 0.5-1.5 mM) after ketone esters [quinones2022, lints2026, mcclure2024]", "Reduced high-intensity exercise performance and higher perceived exertion [stalmans2025, waldman2022]", "High mineral load from BHB salts (sodium/potassium) - relevant to hypertension and kidney disease", "Energy load: MCT provides about 8.3 kcal/g (20 g approx. 165 kcal)"], "contraindications": ["Type 1 diabetes or insulin-treated type 2 diabetes without medical supervision", "Users of SGLT2 inhibitors (euglycaemic ketoacidosis risk)", "Liver cirrhosis or severe hepatic disease (MCT metabolism is hepatic)", "Fatty-acid oxidation disorders and pyruvate carboxylase deficiency", "Pregnancy and lactation (no safety data for ketone esters/salts; ketoacidosis reported in lactating women on low-carbohydrate diets - see ketogenic-diet-chronic)", "Children and adolescents (outside medically supervised epilepsy therapy)", "Kidney disease or salt-restricted diets (BHB salts)"], "drug_interactions": ["SGLT2 inhibitors (additive ketosis, ketoacidosis risk)", "Insulin and sulfonylureas (additive glucose lowering)", "Valproate (both affect fatty-acid metabolism; theoretical)", "Antihypertensives/diuretics with BHB salts (electrolyte load) - theoretical"], "upper_limits": "No EFSA or US tolerable upper intake level. Practical GI tolerance for MCT is roughly 15-20 g per dose and 30 g/day in trials with titration; ketone monoester tested up to about 80 g/day for 28 days in healthy adults [sotomota2019]; US GRAS no-questions letters exist for D-BHB ester (GRN 515) and D-BHB (GRN 1032, up to 6 g/serving) - conditions of use not read in full here.", "sleep_impact": "No sleep data identified for MCT or exogenous ketones; ketone drinks may reduce appetite. Not a stimulant, but commercial 'keto focus' drinks often add caffeine (as in [lints2026]).", "long_term_exposure_studied": "MCT 6 months in MCI/AD (no serious safety signal, GI dropouts); ketone monoester up to 28 days, ketone diester 12 weeks in older adults; no multi-year data.", "daily_use_appropriate": "uncertain", "special_populations": "Older adults tolerate high-dose MCT poorly (diarrhoea); people with diabetes on glucose-lowering drugs need medical review; pregnant/lactating women and children should avoid exogenous ketone products; athletes should be aware of impaired high-intensity performance with ketone esters.", "specialist_review_needed": ["Clinical nutritionist/gastroenterologist for MCT dose titration and tolerability", "Endocrinologist for use with diabetes medication (SGLT2 inhibitors, insulin)", "EU novel-food regulatory specialist for ketone esters and BHB salts", "Toxicologist review of long-term ketone ester safety dossier"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "MCT oil at 5-15 g/day is a conventional food with manageable GI effects, but effective trial doses (20-30 g/day) cause frequent diarrhoea; ketone esters lack long-term independent safety data and carry acidosis/hypoglycaemia and drug-interaction considerations; BHB salts failed EFSA safety assessment. Usable only as MCT at modest doses with titration and exclusion of high-risk groups."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "MCT oil (fractionated coconut/palm kernel fat) has a history of consumption and is marketed as a conventional food/food-supplement ingredient (not novel). No authorised health claim for MCT, ketones or BHB and cognitive function exists in the EU register (Reg. (EU) 432/2012) - checked against knowledge of the register, not re-fetched. Ketone monoester ((R)-3-hydroxybutyl (R)-3-hydroxybutyrate, 'D-beta-hydroxybutyrate ester'): a novel-food application by TdeltaS was pending with EFSA and the product was reported as not permitted for sale in the EU in 2019 (NutraIngredients, 26 Jul 2019, accessed 2026-10-26); no EFSA opinion or Union-list authorisation could be found in searches on 2026-10-26 - status uncertain, assume not authorised. BHB salts: EFSA NDA Panel concluded safety NOT established (EFSA Journal 2022;20(7):7449, https://efsa.europa.eu/en/efsajournal/pub/7449, accessed 2026-10-26). MCT-based products for Alzheimer's (e.g. ketogenic drinks) would fall under foods for special medical purposes (Reg. (EU) 609/2013, Delegated Reg. 2016/128).", "us": "MCT oil is widely marketed as a conventional food and dietary supplement ingredient (caprylic acid itself is affirmed GRAS under 21 CFR 184.1025; MCT GRAS status per se not verified here). FDA GRAS inventory (accessed 2026-10-26): GRN 515 'D-beta-hydroxybutyrate ester' - FDA has no questions (5 Mar 2015); GRN 1032 'D-beta-hydroxybutyrate' - FDA has no questions (2 Jun 2022; up to 6 g/serving in beverages, bars, gels). Axona (AC-1202) has been marketed as a 'medical food' for AD (status of medical-food positioning not re-verified). Structure/function claims on supplements require the DSHEA disclaimer and FTC 'competent and reliable scientific evidence', which is lacking for cognitive enhancement in healthy adults.", "morocco": "No Moroccan text specific to MCT, ketone esters or BHB salts was identified. Foods and supplements fall under Law 28-07 on food safety administered by ONSSA; whether a ketone ester would require prior authorisation or novel-ingredient assessment, and which health claims are allowed, is uncertain and requires direct ONSSA consultation. Not verified from official texts in this unit.", "permitted_claims_examples": [], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "EU: ketone monoester not authorised as novel food (uncertain, pending/unknown); BHB salts negative EFSA safety opinion (2022); bis-octanoyl (R)-1,3-butanediol status not checked (presumed novel). US: ketone ester and D-BHB have GRAS no-questions letters for specific uses; use in supplements may raise NDI questions (not verified). MCT oil: no novel-food issue.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "MCT oil can be incorporated into foods (coffee creamers, nut butters, bars, dressings, emulsified drinks) at 5-10 g/serving; trial doses of 12-30 g/day are feasible only as 2 servings/day and hit GI limits. Ketone esters require drink formats, taste masking and are not EU-legal; BHB salts are powders with high mineral content.", "formats": ["MCT oil (liquid), MCT powder (spray-dried on acacia/maltodextrin)", "emulsified MCT drinks (better tolerated, higher ketone response)", "ketone monoester shots (US only)", "BHB salt powders and drinks", "ketogenic medical-nutrition drinks (MCI/AD)"], "stability": "MCT are saturated and highly oxidation-stable; low smoke point limits frying; powders stable. Ketone esters are stable liquids; BHB salts hygroscopic.", "taste": "MCT oil neutral but can cause throat irritation; ketone monoester intensely bitter/solvent-like (aftertaste reported [bolyard2023 not included]); BHB salts salty/soapy.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Emulsification and co-ingestion with fat raise ketone response; co-ingested carbohydrate/glucose blunts ketosis from MCT; C8 is about 3x more ketogenic than C10; C12 (lauric acid, main fatty acid in coconut oil) is weakly ketogenic, so coconut oil is not equivalent to MCT.", "dose_feasibility": "A 10 g MCT serving fits in a food portion but produces only modest ketosis (<0.5 mM); the 12 g/day dose in young-adult trials is food-feasible; 20-30 g/day (MCI/AD doses) needs split servings and titration; ketone ester doses of 25-50 g per occasion are drink-only and not EU-legal.", "cost_availability": "MCT oil widely available in EU/US supermarkets and online; ketone esters premium-priced and US-centric; availability in Morocco not researched.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 3, "effect_magnitude": 3, "safety": 6, "reproducibility": 2, "food_suitability": 5, "cost_access": 5, "rationale": "Evidence quality 3: all cognition rows grade C; healthy young-adult evidence rests on three small trials (two same lab) and mixed tiny ketone-ester trials. Effect magnitude 3: pooled SMD about 0.3 across heterogeneous populations, smaller for MCT (0.15) and in chronic use (0.19); unclear practical relevance. Safety 6: MCT is a food fat but GI effects are common at effective doses; ketone esters short-term safe but long-term unknown; salts failed EFSA. Reproducibility 2: the largest trials in healthy older adults and AD were null, positive signals not independently replicated. Food suitability 5: MCT fits foods at 5-10 g but trial doses strain tolerability; esters not EU-legal. Cost/access 5: MCT inexpensive and available; esters costly and restricted."}, "research_gaps": ["Independent, preregistered, adequately powered (n>100) RCTs of 10-15 g/day MCT in students and knowledge workers with a single primary cognitive endpoint", "Trials under sleep deprivation and exam stress", "Dose-response linking plasma BHB and brain ketone uptake to cognition in healthy young adults", "Long-term (>6 months) safety of ketone esters, independent of developers", "Better blinding for bitter ketone esters (taste-matched placebos)", "Publication of completed registered trials without results (e.g. NCT05651243 ketone esters in obesity, NCT05679596 bed rest in older adults, NCT07010666 heat stress)", "Head-to-head comparison of MCT vs simple carbohydrate breakfast for cognitive performance"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 3.9, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": "C", "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": null, "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"working_memory": "C", "episodic_memory": "C", "processing_speed": "C", "global_cognition": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 11, "n_studies": 24, "n_verified": 24, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 10, "n_systematic_reviews": 3, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["inconsistent", "insufficient", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "omega-3-dha-epa", "name": "Omega-3 fatty acids (DHA and EPA)", "category": "D", "category_name": "Fatty acids and lipids", "aliases": ["long-chain omega-3 PUFA", "n-3 LCPUFA", "docosahexaenoic acid (22:6n-3)", "eicosapentaenoic acid (20:5n-3)", "fish oil", "marine omega-3", "algal oil DHA", "krill oil (phospholipid EPA/DHA)"], "food_sources": ["Oily fish: sardine, mackerel, herring, salmon, trout, anchovy (approx. 1-3 g EPA+DHA per 100 g)", "Tinned sardines and tuna (variable; tuna in water retains less)", "Fish roe, mussels, oysters", "Algal oil (Schizochytrium, Ulkenia) as DHA or DHA+EPA concentrates (vegan source)", "Omega-3 enriched eggs (hens fed flax/algae; mostly DHA, 100-200 mg per egg)", "Cod liver oil (also high in vitamin A and D)", "Fortified foods: milk, yoghurt, spreads, bread (typically 40-100 mg DHA per serving)"], "active_constituents": ["Docosahexaenoic acid (DHA, 22:6n-3)", "Eicosapentaenoic acid (EPA, 20:5n-3)", "Docosapentaenoic acid (DPA, 22:5n-3) - minor", "Lipid form matters for absorption: re-esterified triglyceride and natural triglyceride > ethyl ester (especially on an empty stomach); phospholipid (krill) comparable or slightly higher per gram"], "one_line_verdict": "DHA/EPA are essential structural brain lipids with strong deficiency-prevention and public-health logic, but randomized trials show no meaningful cognitive enhancement in healthy, adequately nourished adults and no prevention of cognitive decline in older adults; small, inconsistent benefits appear mainly in people with low baseline intake or mild memory complaints.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. DHA is the dominant n-3 fatty acid in neuronal membranes and the human brain requires a steady supply; dietary intake of EPA+DHA reliably raises red-cell and plasma levels within weeks (omega-3 index rose from about 5.7% to 8.7-9.5% after 26 weeks of about 1.2 g/d in [patan2021]). That is the firm part. The cognitive-outcome evidence is much weaker and highly population-dependent. In cognitively healthy older adults, the best evidence is consistently null: the Cochrane review [sydenham2012] (3 RCTs, 3,536 participants, 6-40 months) found no difference in MMSE (MD -0.07, 95% CI -0.25 to 0.10) or in word learning, digit span or verbal fluency; OPAL [opal2010] (n=867, 700 mg/d EPA+DHA, 24 months) found no effect on the CVLT or any secondary test; van de Rest [vanderest2008] (n=302, 400 or 1,800 mg/d, 26 weeks) found no effect in any domain; MAPT [mapt2017] (n=1,680, 800 mg DHA + 225 mg EPA, 3 years) found a composite difference of 0.011 (95% CI -0.081 to 0.103); VITAL-Cog [vital2022] (n=3,424 + 794, 1 g/d, 2-3 years) found a pooled difference of -0.01 SD/year (95% CI -0.02 to 0.003); DO-HEALTH [dohealth2020] (n=2,157, 1 g/d, 3 years) found no MoCA difference. The largest meta-analysis of RCTs [brainard2020] (38 RCTs, 49,757 participants, moderate-quality evidence) concluded that long-chain omega-3 has little or no effect on new neurocognitive illness (RR 0.98, 0.87-1.10), new cognitive impairment (RR 0.99, 0.92-1.06) or MMSE (MD 0.10 points, 0.03-0.16; statistically significant but trivially small). A 2025 overview of systematic reviews [barros2025] pooled an MMSE effect of Hedges' g 0.16 (0.01-0.32) with no dose or duration relationship; a 2024 dose-response meta-analysis [suh2024] (24 RCTs, n=9,660) reported a small signal for executive function in the first 12 months at >500 mg/d, stronger where baseline blood DHA+EPA was not very low. These positive syntheses are driven by small, heterogeneous trials and are contradicted by every large, long, well-conducted trial; a 2025 dose-response meta-analysis [shahinfar2025] reporting SMDs of 0.5-1.1 per 2 g/d is implausible against the large null trials and should be treated as an outlier with low/moderate GRADE and probable small-study effects. In older adults with mild memory complaints but no dementia, MIDAS [midas2010] (n=485, 900 mg/d DHA, 24 weeks; Martek-funded) found fewer CANTAB PAL errors (difference -1.63, 95% CI -3.1 to -0.14) and better verbal recognition memory but no effect on working memory or executive function; two small German trials from one group [witte2014], [kulzow2016] (2.2 g/d, 26 weeks, n=65 and 44) reported better executive function and object-location memory; the industry-authored meta-analysis [yurkomauro2015_meta] pooled episodic-memory benefit in this subgroup. Against this, the CANN trial [cann2023] (n=259, 1.1 g DHA + 0.4 g EPA with cocoa flavanols, 12 months) found no benefit and a signal of worse executive function. In healthy young adults the picture is contradictory: Stonehouse [stonehouse2013] (n=176, 1.16 g/d DHA, 6 months, low habitual DHA) found faster memory reaction times (-0.18 to -0.36 SD) and sex-specific memory effects; Patan [patan2021] (n=310, 26 weeks) found a small global accuracy and speed advantage for EPA-rich oil (about 0.15 SD) but not DHA-rich oil; Bauer [bauer2014] found faster Stroop reaction times after EPA-rich oil in a 30-day crossover; but Jackson [jackson2012_null] (n=159, 1 g/d, 12 weeks) found no cognitive or mood effect, Benton [benton2013] (n=285 young women, 400 mg/d DHA, 50 days) found slightly worse retention of new information, and Leckie/Muldoon [leckie2020] (n=271 mid-life adults with low intake, 1.4 g/d, 18 weeks) found no effect in any of four domains or on brain morphology. The Cooper meta-analysis [cooper2015] (24 RCTs) found no cognitive improvement in typically developing populations, with a marginal short-term memory effect confined to people with low n-3 status. Dose: trials used 0.4-2.2 g/d EPA+DHA; no dose-response has been demonstrated for cognition. Duration: 12 weeks to 3 years; longer trials are not more positive. Reproducibility: null findings are reproduced across independent, publicly funded groups in four countries; positive findings cluster in single small trials or industry-funded studies and have not been independently replicated at the same dose and population. Observational cohorts [zhang2016], [wei2023] consistently associate fish/DHA intake with roughly 10-20% lower dementia risk, and APOE4 carriers may benefit more [vanderest2016_apoe], but these are confounded by diet quality and socioeconomic status and the RCTs above do not confirm a causal effect. Unknown: whether very low baseline status (omega-3 index <4%), APOE4 genotype, EPA-rich versus DHA-rich oils, or decades of intake change the answer; whether acute (single-dose) effects exist at all (no credible acute cognitive trial was found).", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Deficiency correction and enhancement must be kept separate. True clinical DHA deficiency is rare in adults eating any fish, but low status (omega-3 index <4%, habitual intake <100-200 mg/d EPA+DHA) is common in Western, vegan and low-fish populations, including many Moroccan inland and student populations. Trials in low-intake adults [stonehouse2013], [leckie2020] and the Cooper meta-analysis [cooper2015] suggest that raising status from low to adequate may give a small benefit in memory speed or short-term memory in some, but not all, studies; Leckie found nothing in 271 low-intake adults, so even this is uncertain. There is no evidence that adding DHA/EPA to an adequately nourished person (regular fish eaters, omega-3 index >6-8%) enhances attention, memory, executive function or intelligence; the large null trials recruited mixed-status older adults and found nothing. For Brain Food the honest framing is: DHA/EPA provide 'normal brain function' nutrition (the EFSA-authorised wording) and fill a common intake gap, not cognitive enhancement.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Marketing routinely presents fish oil as a memory or focus booster for students and workers. The evidence does not support this: in healthy young adults the trials are small, contradictory and mostly null; in healthy older adults the large trials are uniformly null; in dementia the Cochrane review [burckhardt2016] found no benefit with high-quality evidence. The only claim that is both legally authorised in the EU and scientifically defensible is that DHA contributes to the maintenance of normal brain function (and normal vision) at 250 mg/d, with the condition that the food contains at least 40 mg DHA per 100 g and per 100 kcal. Words such as 'boosts memory', 'improves concentration', 'protects against Alzheimer's' or 'nootropic' are not substantiated, would be unauthorised in the EU, would face FTC substantiation challenges in the US, and are prohibited disease claims under the Moroccan draft framework. Observational dementia associations [zhang2016], [wei2023] must not be converted into 'prevents dementia' claims because RCTs do not confirm them.", "mechanism": {"summary": "DHA constitutes a large share of brain phospholipid fatty acids, especially in synaptic membranes and the retina; EPA is present in trace amounts in brain but is a precursor of anti-inflammatory and specialised pro-resolving mediators. Proposed mechanisms include membrane fluidity and receptor function, synaptogenesis, neurotrophin (BDNF) signalling, cerebrovascular/endothelial effects and reduced neuroinflammation. In humans only the upstream steps (absorption, blood and probably tissue enrichment, modest cerebral haemodynamic changes) are established; the link from these to measured cognitive performance in nourished adults is not demonstrated.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Dietary EPA/DHA (triglyceride, ethyl ester, phospholipid) -> intestinal absorption with dietary fat", "detail": "Absorption is efficient with a fat-containing meal; ethyl esters are less bioavailable fasting. Algal DHA is bioequivalent to fish DHA [arterburn2007].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Absorption -> plasma and red-cell enrichment (omega-3 index)", "detail": "Dose-dependent, linear increase; 26 weeks of about 1.2 g/d raised omega-3 index from about 5.7% to 8.7-9.5% [patan2021]; plateau after 3-6 months.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Blood DHA -> brain uptake via lysophosphatidylcholine-DHA (MFSD2A transporter) and unesterified DHA", "detail": "Brain DHA turnover is slow (half-life about 2.5 years in humans by PET/kinetic estimates); adult brain DHA is relatively conserved even when intake is low.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Membrane DHA -> membrane fluidity, receptor/ion-channel function, synaptic protein expression, BDNF", "detail": "Well characterised in rodents and cell models; human evidence limited to peripheral BDNF correlations [witte2014].", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "EPA/DHA -> resolvins, protectins, lower pro-inflammatory eicosanoids -> reduced neuroinflammation", "detail": "Mediator production shown in human plasma; relevance to cognition in healthy adults is speculative.", "evidence_level": "plausible"}, {"step": "DHA -> cerebral blood flow / prefrontal oxygenation during tasks", "detail": "NIRS pilot (n=22) showed higher prefrontal oxy-Hb after 12 weeks DHA-rich oil [jackson2012_nirs]; larger trial showed trend to lower oxy-Hb interpreted as 'neural efficiency' [patan2021]. Direction inconsistent; a biomarker, not an outcome.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Biomarker changes -> measured cognitive performance in nourished adults", "detail": "Large RCTs null in healthy older adults; young-adult trials contradictory. Link not established.", "evidence_level": "speculative"}], "pathways": ["Membrane phospholipid composition and fluidity", "Synaptogenesis and neurotrophic signalling (BDNF)", "Specialised pro-resolving mediators / anti-inflammatory", "Cerebrovascular function and endothelial nitric oxide", "Amyloid processing (animal/in-vitro only)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults, single dose", "dose_or_intake": "Any single dose of EPA/DHA", "duration": "Single day", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No credible placebo-controlled single-dose cognitive trial of EPA/DHA in adults was identified; brain DHA turnover is slow, so acute cognitive effects are mechanistically implausible.", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "Absence of evidence; the pharmacokinetics make an acute effect unlikely. Do not market for same-day focus.", "key_study_ids": ["patan2021"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults 18-45 y with low habitual DHA intake (New Zealand)", "dose_or_intake": "1.16 g/d DHA", "duration": "6 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Stonehouse reported faster reaction times for episodic and working memory and better episodic memory in women; Benton (400 mg/d, 50 d) found worse retention; Jackson (1 g/d, 12 wk) null; Patan found no DHA-rich oil benefit.", "effect_estimate": "Stonehouse: RT episodic memory -0.18 SD (95% CI -0.33 to -0.03); RT working memory -0.36 SD (-0.58 to -0.14); episodic memory in women +0.28 SD (0.08 to 0.48). Jackson, Benton, Patan (DHA arm): no significant effect.", "certainty_notes": "Single positive trial with multiple subgroup interactions, not replicated; three independent null trials. Downgraded for inconsistency and imprecision.", "key_study_ids": ["stonehouse2013", "jackson2012_null", "benton2013", "patan2021"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults 25-49 y, UK, mixed intake", "dose_or_intake": "EPA-rich oil (900 mg EPA + 360 mg DHA/d) vs DHA-rich oil (900 mg DHA + 270 mg EPA/d)", "duration": "26 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "EPA-rich oil improved global accuracy and speed versus placebo; DHA-rich oil did not. Bauer 2014 crossover also favoured EPA-rich oil on Stroop RT. Not replicated independently; earlier Jackson trial at 1 g/d was null.", "effect_estimate": "Patan: global accuracy EMM 0.17 (0.09-0.24) vs placebo 0.03 (-0.04 to 0.11), P=0.044; global speed -0.15 (-0.22 to -0.07) vs 0.03 (-0.05 to 0.10), P=0.003 (standardised units, roughly 0.15-0.2 SD).", "certainty_notes": "Industry-supplied oils (BASF/Pronova), single research group, small effect, multiple outcomes; B is not justified without independent replication. Effect size is below what a student would notice.", "key_study_ids": ["patan2021", "bauer2014", "jackson2012_null"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy mid-life adults 30-54 y with low intake (<=300 mg/d), USA", "dose_or_intake": "1.4 g/d EPA+DHA", "duration": "18 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "No effect on psychomotor speed, executive function, learning/memory or fluid intelligence, nor on brain morphology; exploratory benefit only in lowest-baseline-DHA subgroup.", "effect_estimate": "No significant differences in any of four domains (exact estimates not reported in abstract).", "certainty_notes": "Well-powered independent null trial directly relevant to knowledge workers; the subgroup signal is hypothesis-generating only.", "key_study_ids": ["leckie2020", "cooper2015"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Healthy 51-72 y, Sweden, crossover", "dose_or_intake": "3 g/d fish oil n-3", "duration": "5 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Better working memory test performance versus placebo in a 40-person crossover; selective attention not improved.", "effect_estimate": "p<0.05, effect size not reported", "certainty_notes": "Small crossover, single trial, multiple outcomes; not replicated.", "key_study_ids": ["nilsson2012"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Cognitively healthy adults >=60-70 y (UK, Netherlands, USA, Europe)", "dose_or_intake": "0.4-1.8 g/d EPA+DHA", "duration": "6 months to 3 years", "grade": "A", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Consistently no effect on global cognition, memory, executive function or incident cognitive impairment across OPAL, van de Rest, VITAL, DO-HEALTH, MAPT and the Cochrane/Brainard meta-analyses.", "effect_estimate": "Cochrane MMSE MD -0.07 (95% CI -0.25 to 0.10; 2 RCTs, n=3,221). Brainard: new cognitive impairment RR 0.99 (0.92-1.06; 5 RCTs, n=33,296, I2 0%); MMSE MD 0.10 (0.03-0.16; 13 RCTs, n=14,851). VITAL pooled -0.01 SD/yr (-0.02 to 0.003). MAPT composite 0.011 (-0.081 to 0.103).", "certainty_notes": "High-certainty evidence of no clinically meaningful effect: large, independent, publicly funded trials with low risk of bias and consistent results. Grade A for the conclusion of no benefit.", "key_study_ids": ["sydenham2012", "brainard2020", "opal2010", "vanderest2008", "vital2022", "mapt2017", "dohealth2020"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Middle-aged/older adults without dementia (meta-analysis of 24 RCTs, 40+ y)", "dose_or_intake": ">500 mg/d n-3 PUFA", "duration": "3-12 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Dose-response meta-analysis suggests small executive-function benefit within the first 12 months at >500 mg/d, mainly where baseline blood DHA+EPA was not very low; contradicted by MAPT and VITAL executive outcomes.", "effect_estimate": "SMD magnitude small (exact pooled value in splines; not reported as a single number in abstract)", "certainty_notes": "Heterogeneous small trials, inconsistency with large trials, possible small-study effects; Barros 2025 pooled MMSE g=0.16 (0.01-0.32), I2 43%.", "key_study_ids": ["suh2024", "barros2025", "shahinfar2025"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "older_adults_mci", "population_detail": "Older adults (>=55 y) with age-related memory complaints but MMSE >26 (MIDAS); subjective/MCI (CANN)", "dose_or_intake": "900 mg/d DHA (MIDAS); 1.1 g DHA + 0.4 g EPA (CANN); 2.2 g/d (Witte, Kulzow)", "duration": "24 weeks to 12 months", "grade": "B", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "MIDAS found fewer paired-associate learning errors and better verbal recognition memory; small German trials found executive and object-location memory benefit; CANN (with cocoa flavanols) found no benefit and a decline in executive function. Industry-authored meta-analysis pooled a benefit.", "effect_estimate": "MIDAS PAL 6-pattern errors difference -1.63 (95% CI -3.1 to -0.14; P=0.03), about 0.2 SD. CANN: no significant benefit on any cognitive outcome. Yurko-Mauro 2015: episodic memory improved in MMC (P<0.004), pooled g not quoted in abstract.", "certainty_notes": "Promising but limited: MIDAS is industry-funded (Martek) and used a selected primary outcome; replication by independent groups is small and from one laboratory; CANN is a direct contradiction (confounded by combination). B at best.", "key_study_ids": ["midas2010", "witte2014", "kulzow2016", "cann2023", "yurkomauro2015_meta"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_decline", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Mild to moderate Alzheimer's disease", "dose_or_intake": "0.7-2 g/d EPA+DHA", "duration": "6-18 months", "grade": "E", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Cochrane: no benefit on ADAS-Cog, MMSE, ADL or dementia severity with high-quality evidence.", "effect_estimate": "ADAS-Cog SMD -0.02 (95% CI -0.19 to 0.15; 3 RCTs, n=566); MMSE MD 0.18 (-1.05 to 1.41).", "certainty_notes": "High-quality evidence of no effect; this population is not a Brain Food target but is often implied in marketing.", "key_study_ids": ["burckhardt2016"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "dementia_risk", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "General adult cohorts (observational), 2-21 y follow-up", "dose_or_intake": "1 serving fish/week; 0.1 g/d DHA increments", "duration": "Years to decades", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Prospective cohorts associate higher fish/DHA intake and blood EPA with lower dementia and cognitive decline risk; large RCTs do not confirm; Rotterdam cohort null.", "effect_estimate": "Zhang: per 1 serving/week fish, dementia RR 0.95 (0.90-0.99), AD RR 0.93 (0.90-0.95); per 0.1 g/d DHA, dementia RR 0.86 (0.76-0.96; I2 93%). Wei 2023: DHA intake RR 0.83 (I2 65%); 8-9.9% lower risk per 0.1 g/d. Devore (Rotterdam): high fish HR 0.95 (0.76-1.19).", "certainty_notes": "Observational, high heterogeneity, residual confounding (fish eaters differ in education and diet), inconsistency with RCT evidence. Indirect for young adults.", "key_study_ids": ["zhang2016", "wei2023", "vanderest2016_apoe", "devore2009"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cerebral_blood_flow", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy young adults, NIRS", "dose_or_intake": "1 g/d DHA-rich oil (pilot); 1.2 g/d (Patan)", "duration": "12-26 weeks", "grade": "D", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Pilot NIRS showed increased prefrontal oxy-Hb during tasks after DHA-rich oil; larger trial found a trend to lower oxy-Hb. Biomarker only.", "effect_estimate": "Jackson pilot: significant increase in oxy-Hb and total Hb (n=22). Patan: oxy-Hb 24.6 uM (DHA) vs 27.4 uM (placebo), P=0.06.", "certainty_notes": "Direction inconsistent, surrogate outcome; not evidence of cognitive benefit.", "key_study_ids": ["jackson2012_nirs", "patan2021"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Mild-to-moderately depressed adults (not major depression), UK", "dose_or_intake": "1.5 g/d EPA+DHA", "duration": "12 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "No effect on depressed mood or cognitive measures (attentional bias).", "effect_estimate": "Adjusted DASS depression difference -1.0 (95% CI -2.8 to 0.8; P=0.27).", "certainty_notes": "Single well-conducted null trial; major-depression trials (not reviewed here) show modest EPA-predominant effects.", "key_study_ids": ["rogers2008"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "Any single dose EPA/DHA (0.5-3 g)", "onset": "Not applicable; brain DHA turnover is slow", "duration_studied": "No placebo-controlled single-dose cognitive trial found", "domain": "attention / reaction time", "population": "healthy adults", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty": "very low (no data)", "safety": "Single doses are safe; fishy burps, mild GI upset", "study_ids": ["patan2021"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "12 weeks", "population": "Healthy young adults 18-35 y (n=159)", "outcome": "Cognitive battery and mood", "effect_estimate": "No effect of 1 g/d DHA-rich or EPA-rich oil; EPA may reduce subjective mental fatigue", "evidence_quality": "moderate (single trial)", "limitations": "12 weeks may be short; multiple outcomes", "study_ids": ["jackson2012_null"]}, {"duration": "6 months", "population": "Healthy adults 18-45 y with low DHA intake (n=176)", "outcome": "Memory and reaction time", "effect_estimate": "RT working memory -0.36 SD (-0.58 to -0.14); RT episodic memory -0.18 SD; sex-specific memory effects", "evidence_quality": "moderate-low", "limitations": "Industry oil supplied; subgroup interactions; not replicated", "study_ids": ["stonehouse2013"]}, {"duration": "26 weeks", "population": "Healthy adults 25-49 y (n=310)", "outcome": "Global cognitive accuracy and speed", "effect_estimate": "EPA-rich oil ~0.15 SD better accuracy and speed; DHA-rich oil no effect", "evidence_quality": "moderate-low", "limitations": "BASF-supported; single group; small effect", "study_ids": ["patan2021"]}, {"duration": "18 weeks", "population": "Mid-life adults 30-54 y, low intake (n=271)", "outcome": "Four cognitive domains, brain volume", "effect_estimate": "No effect on any domain or morphology", "evidence_quality": "moderate", "limitations": "Possibly too short; low-baseline subgroup signal", "study_ids": ["leckie2020"]}, {"duration": "24 weeks", "population": "Older adults with age-related memory complaints (n=485)", "outcome": "CANTAB PAL, verbal recognition memory", "effect_estimate": "PAL errors -1.63 (-3.1 to -0.14); no effect on working memory/executive", "evidence_quality": "moderate (industry-funded)", "limitations": "Martek funding; selected outcome; not independently replicated", "study_ids": ["midas2010"]}, {"duration": "26 weeks", "population": "Healthy older adults 65+ (n=302)", "outcome": "Attention, speed, memory, executive", "effect_estimate": "No effect at 400 or 1,800 mg/d", "evidence_quality": "high", "limitations": "No decline in placebo group over 26 weeks", "study_ids": ["vanderest2008"]}, {"duration": "24 months", "population": "Cognitively healthy 70-79 y (n=867)", "outcome": "CVLT and secondary tests", "effect_estimate": "No difference", "evidence_quality": "high", "limitations": "No decline in control arm", "study_ids": ["opal2010"]}, {"duration": "2-3 years", "population": "Healthy community adults 60+ (n=4,218)", "outcome": "Global cognitive change", "effect_estimate": "-0.01 SD/yr (-0.02 to 0.003)", "evidence_quality": "high", "limitations": "Telephone testing in main substudy", "study_ids": ["vital2022"]}, {"duration": "3 years", "population": "Older adults with memory complaints/frailty markers (n=1,680)", "outcome": "Composite cognitive Z score", "effect_estimate": "0.011 (-0.081 to 0.103)", "evidence_quality": "high", "limitations": "Participants at risk, not healthy young", "study_ids": ["mapt2017"]}, {"duration": "3 years", "population": "Adults 70+ without major comorbidity (n=2,157)", "outcome": "MoCA", "effect_estimate": "No effect", "evidence_quality": "high", "limitations": "Factorial design, MoCA ceiling", "study_ids": ["dohealth2020"]}, {"duration": "12 months", "population": "Older adults with subjective/MCI (n=259)", "outcome": "Picture recognition, executive, mood, brain structure", "effect_estimate": "No benefit; decline in executive function and cortical volume in active group", "evidence_quality": "moderate", "limitations": "Combined with cocoa flavanols; cannot attribute to omega-3", "study_ids": ["cann2023"]}, {"duration": ">=24 weeks (meta-analysis)", "population": "Adults, 38 RCTs, n=49,757", "outcome": "New neurocognitive illness, cognitive impairment, MMSE", "effect_estimate": "RR 0.98 (0.87-1.10); RR 0.99 (0.92-1.06); MMSE MD 0.10 (0.03-0.16)", "evidence_quality": "moderate (GRADE)", "limitations": "Mostly older adults", "study_ids": ["brainard2020"]}], "safety": {"summary": "EPA+DHA from food and supplements are well tolerated at intakes up to several grams per day; EFSA (2012) found no safety concern for supplemental EPA+DHA up to 5 g/d in adults (EPA alone up to 1.8 g/d) but did not set a UL. Two signals matter for a daily food product: a dose-dependent increase in atrial fibrillation in cardiovascular outcome trials (HR 1.12 at <=1 g/d, 1.49 at >1 g/d [gencer2021]) and a modest bleeding-tendency increase at high doses, especially 4 g/d purified EPA [javaid2024], [chang2023]. Common tolerability issues are fishy aftertaste/dysgeusia, burping, diarrhoea. Oxidised (rancid) oils are a quality problem in the supplement market and in fortified foods.", "adverse_effects": ["Fishy taste, eructation, dysgeusia (OR 3.5 in RCTs)", "Diarrhoea / loose stools (OR 1.26)", "Nausea at high doses", "Atrial fibrillation: dose-dependent in cardiovascular trials; HR 1.25 overall, 1.49 at >1 g/d", "Bleeding tendency: small increase at high doses (OR 1.26); no overall increase in major bleeding at typical doses", "Small LDL-cholesterol rise with DHA-rich oils in some trials", "Hyperglycaemia signal at very high doses in diabetics (older literature; not confirmed by EFSA 2012)", "Vitamin A toxicity risk if cod liver oil is used as the source"], "contraindications": ["Fish/shellfish allergy for fish-derived oils (use algal DHA)", "Known atrial fibrillation or high AF risk at doses >1 g/d (clinician advice)", "Scheduled surgery: some surgeons ask to stop high-dose fish oil 1-2 weeks before (evidence of harm weak)"], "drug_interactions": ["Anticoagulants (warfarin, DOACs) and antiplatelets: additive bleeding tendency at >=2-3 g/d; monitor INR", "Antihypertensives: small additive BP lowering", "Orlistat reduces absorption of fat-soluble nutrients including omega-3", "Icosapent ethyl / prescription omega-3: do not stack supplements on top of prescription doses without clinician advice"], "upper_limits": "EFSA 2012: no UL set; supplemental EPA+DHA up to 5 g/d and EPA alone up to 1.8 g/d do not raise safety concerns in adults (EFSA Journal 2012;10(7):2815, accessed 2026-10-25). US IOM: no UL; FDA advises not exceeding 3 g/d EPA+DHA from all sources, of which 2 g/d from supplements. Brain Food working ceiling: <=1 g/d supplemental EPA+DHA per daily serving given the AF dose-response.", "sleep_impact": "No disruption; DHA-rich oil improved some self-reported sleep measures in young adults in a 26-week trial (Patan 2021, Nutrients, not extracted here). No stimulant effect; can be taken at any time of day, ideally with a meal.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "RCTs up to 5-7 years (cardiovascular trials), 3 years for cognition (MAPT, VITAL, DO-HEALTH); population fish consumption for generations. Long-term safety at <=1 g/d is well characterised.", "daily_use_appropriate": "yes", "special_populations": "Pregnancy/lactation: DHA 200 mg/d above the 250 mg/d EPA+DHA adult AI is recommended by EFSA and most guidelines; safe and beneficial for fetal development (keep claims separate from adult cognition claims). Children: safe; EU Article 14 claim for brain development exists at 100 mg/d (<2 y) and 250 mg/d (2-18 y); ADHD trials show small effects at best (not covered here). Older adults with cardiac disease: AF signal above 1 g/d. Anticoagulated patients: monitor. Vegans: algal DHA/EPA is the only efficient route. Psychiatric conditions: EPA-predominant formulas are studied in depression; no contraindication. Fish allergy: use algal oil.", "specialist_review_needed": ["Clinical nutritionist/dietitian for dose per serving and pregnancy positioning", "Cardiologist review of AF signal for products aimed at adults >60 or marketed as daily high-dose", "Food scientist/toxicologist for oxidation (peroxide, anisidine, TOTOX) specifications and contaminant limits (mercury, PCBs, dioxins) in fish-derived oils", "Regulatory affairs for EU claim wording and Moroccan dossier"], "hard_safety_gate": "pass", "gate_reason": "Food-level safety at the effective/authorised dose (250 mg to 1 g/d EPA+DHA); AF and bleeding signals arise above 1 g/d in cardiac populations and are managed by dose capping and labelling; oxidation and contaminants are quality-control issues, not intrinsic hazards."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Health claims (Regulation (EC) 1924/2006; Commission Regulation (EU) 432/2012 Article 13(1) list, consolidated text accessed 2026-10-25 via EUR-Lex): 'DHA contributes to maintenance of normal brain function' and 'DHA contributes to the maintenance of normal vision' - conditions: food contains at least 40 mg DHA per 100 g and per 100 kcal, and consumer is informed the effect is obtained with 250 mg DHA/day; 'EPA and DHA contribute to the normal function of the heart' at 250 mg/d EPA+DHA; 'DHA and EPA contribute to the maintenance of normal blood pressure' (3 g/d) and 'normal blood triglyceride levels' (2 g/d) with a warning not to exceed 5 g/d supplemental EPA+DHA. Article 14 claims for DHA and brain/eye development in infants and children exist but are separate (pregnancy/children only). Nutrition claims 'source of omega-3' (>=40 mg EPA+DHA per 100 g and 100 kcal, or 0.3 g ALA) and 'high omega-3' (double). Novel foods: fish oil is not novel; algal oils from Schizochytrium sp. are authorised novel foods in the Union list (Implementing Regulation (EU) 2017/2470 as amended, e.g. 2021/1326, 2022/1365, 2024/2101, 2025/2245) with category-specific maximum levels; krill oil is also authorised. EFSA DRV: AI 250 mg/d EPA+DHA for adults, +100-200 mg DHA in pregnancy/lactation.", "us": "DHA and EPA oils (fish, algal) have GRAS status for many food uses via GRAS notices (e.g. Martek/DSM algal DHA oils; exact GRN numbers not verified here) and are lawful dietary ingredients under DSHEA. FDA qualified health claims (enforcement discretion, June 2019 letter, fda.gov accessed 2026-10-25): consuming EPA and DHA combined may reduce the risk of coronary heart disease / may reduce blood pressure, with mandated qualifier language; the 2004 CHD qualified claim also stands. No authorised or qualified claim for cognition. Structure/function claims such as 'supports brain health' are permitted with the DSHEA disclaimer and must be truthful and substantiated under FTC; 'improves memory' would require competent and reliable scientific evidence that the RCT base does not provide. FDA advises <=3 g/d EPA+DHA total (<=2 g/d from supplements).", "morocco": "No specific omega-3 texts located. Food supplements are regulated at the interface of ONSSA (food safety, Law 28-07 on food product safety and its decrees) and the Ministry of Health/AMMPS (products with medicinal presentation); press reports (Medias24 2023, 2026; Le360; H24info; accessed 2026-10-25) indicate a draft supplements law that would prohibit disease prevention/treatment claims and defer permitted health claims to implementing texts, with maximum nutrient levels reportedly lower than EU values; status of adoption uncertain. Fortified foods with DHA would follow general labelling rules (Decree 2-12-389) and ONSSA authorisation of establishments. Practical position: use only EFSA-type wording, avoid any disease claim, and obtain local regulatory counsel; status marked uncertain.", "permitted_claims_examples": ["EU (Reg. 432/2012): 'DHA contributes to maintenance of normal brain function' - only for foods with >=40 mg DHA per 100 g and per 100 kcal, with information that the beneficial effect is obtained with a daily intake of 250 mg of DHA.", "EU (Reg. 432/2012): 'DHA contributes to the maintenance of normal vision' - same conditions.", "EU (Reg. 432/2012): 'EPA and DHA contribute to the normal function of the heart' - 250 mg/d EPA+DHA.", "EU nutrition claim: 'Source of omega-3 fatty acids' (>=40 mg EPA+DHA per 100 g and per 100 kcal) / 'High omega-3 fatty acids' (>=80 mg).", "US (FDA qualified claim, 2019): 'Consuming EPA and DHA combined may reduce the risk of CHD by lowering blood pressure. However, FDA has concluded that the evidence is inconsistent and inconclusive...' (exact qualifier text per FDA letter)."], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "Fish oil: not novel in EU; algal DHA oils require use within the authorised Union-list specifications and categories (check the specific Schizochytrium strain entry and maximum mg/100 g for the food category); krill oil authorised. US: algal and fish oils are GRAS for defined food categories; new algal strains or novel concentrates may need their own GRAS notice; NDI notification not generally needed for fish oil. Morocco: unknown whether algal oil requires specific approval.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Good for fortification at the EFSA-claim level (40 mg DHA/100 g and 100 kcal; 250 mg/d across servings) using microencapsulated algal or fish oil powders; difficult for high doses (>=1 g/d) because of oxidation, taste and fat load. Whole oily fish (sardines, mackerel - abundant and cheap in Morocco) is the most evidence-aligned and affordable delivery and should be the primary recommendation.", "formats": ["Whole-food: tinned sardines/mackerel, fresh oily fish (recommended 1-2 portions/week)", "Softgel capsules (triglyceride or re-esterified TG form, 500-1,000 mg EPA+DHA)", "Microencapsulated oil powders for bars, biscuits, porridge, milk powders", "Fortified dairy (milk, yoghurt) and spreads", "Omega-3 enriched eggs", "Emulsified liquid oils (flavoured) for children/elderly", "Algal oil for vegan products"], "stability": "Highly prone to oxidation (heat, light, oxygen, trace metals); EPA/DHA oxidise faster than ALA. Requires nitrogen blanketing, antioxidants (mixed tocopherols, rosemary extract, ascorbyl palmitate), opaque packaging, cold chain for liquids, and microencapsulation for dry foods. Baking and frying degrade and oxidise; pH-neutral matrices preferred. Specify peroxide value <5 meq/kg, anisidine <20, TOTOX <26 (GOED monograph).", "taste": "Fishy/marine notes and 'fishy burp' at >500 mg per serving unless well refined, deodorised and encapsulated; algal oil is milder; flavour masking (citrus, vanilla, cocoa) helps.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Absorption best with a fat-containing meal; ethyl-ester forms poorly absorbed fasting (TG or rTG forms preferred); emulsified and microencapsulated oils absorb as well as or better than capsules; algal oil capsules and DHA-fortified bars are bioequivalent to each other and to cooked salmon [arterburn2007]. Phospholipid (krill) forms raise plasma DHA slightly more per mg in some studies.", "dose_feasibility": "250 mg DHA/d (EFSA claim level) fits easily in one serving of fortified dairy, a bar or two eggs; 1 g/d EPA+DHA (dose of most trials) requires 2-3 capsules or a 100 g portion of oily fish and is impractical in a snack without taste and oxidation problems. 2.2 g/d (Witte/Kulzow) is supplement-only.", "cost_availability": "Fish oil is a commodity; algal DHA is several times more expensive per mg DHA. Morocco is a major sardine producer (cheap, local). Capsules widely available in Moroccan pharmacies and parapharmacies; prices not researched here.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 4, "effect_magnitude": 2, "safety": 8, "reproducibility": 3, "food_suitability": 6, "cost_access": 7, "rationale": "Evidence quality 4: the evidence base is large and high quality, but it is high quality for the conclusion that there is no meaningful cognitive effect in healthy adults (Grade A null) and only C-grade for benefits in young adults and B at best in older adults with memory complaints. Effect magnitude 2: best-supported effects in the target population are about 0.15-0.3 SD on specific reaction-time measures in single trials, contradicted by others. Safety 8: food-level safety at <=1 g/d, with AF and bleeding signals above that and quality (oxidation) issues. Reproducibility 3: null results replicate across independent groups; positive results do not. Food suitability 6: feasible at claim level with microencapsulation, poor at trial doses. Cost/access 7: cheap as fish and fish oil, especially in Morocco; algal DHA costlier."}, "research_gaps": ["Adequately powered (n>400), independent (non-industry) 6-12 month RCTs of EPA-rich vs DHA-rich oil in healthy students/knowledge workers with low omega-3 index, pre-registered cognitive primary outcomes and reaction-time/sustained-attention tasks", "Trials stratified by baseline omega-3 index (<4% vs >6%) to test the deficiency-correction hypothesis directly", "APOE4-stratified prevention trials starting in mid-life", "Food-matrix trials (fortified dairy, sardine-based meals) rather than capsules", "Replication of MIDAS by an independent group", "Acute single-dose cognitive studies (to settle whether any same-day effect exists; expected null)", "Moroccan intake and omega-3 index surveys in students"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 4.8, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "pass", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": "C", "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": null, "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"global_cognition": "C", "working_memory": "C", "executive_function": "C", "dementia_risk": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 12, "n_studies": 34, "n_verified": 34, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 16, "n_systematic_reviews": 4, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["inconsistent", "insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "omega-6-fatty-acids", "name": "Omega-6 fatty acids", "category": "D", "category_name": "Fatty acids and lipids", "aliases": ["omega-6 PUFA", "n-6 fatty acids", "linoleic acid", "LA", "arachidonic acid", "ARA", "AA", "gamma-linolenic acid", "GLA"], "food_sources": ["vegetable oils (sunflower, corn, soybean, safflower, cottonseed) -- the dominant source of linoleic acid in most diets", "nuts and seeds (walnuts, sunflower seeds, pine nuts)", "poultry fat and eggs (sources of pre-formed arachidonic acid)", "pork and other meats (moderate arachidonic acid content)", "processed and fried foods made with omega-6-rich vegetable oils", "evening primrose oil and borage oil (gamma-linolenic acid, a minor omega-6 species)"], "active_constituents": ["linoleic acid (LA, 18:2n-6) -- the essential omega-6 fatty acid, precursor for endogenous arachidonic acid synthesis", "arachidonic acid (AA/ARA, 20:4n-6) -- the dominant omega-6 long-chain PUFA in brain phospholipids, precursor of eicosanoids (prostaglandins, thromboxanes, leukotrienes)", "gamma-linolenic acid (GLA, 18:3n-6) -- minor dietary omega-6 species, intermediate in AA synthesis"], "one_line_verdict": "Arachidonic acid is the second-most-abundant long-chain PUFA in brain membranes after DHA and is biologically indispensable, but the best RCT/meta-analytic evidence available finds omega-6 fatty acid intake has no clearly established effect on human cognition in any direction, the two positive AA-supplementation trials are small, industry-linked and population-limited (healthy elderly biomarker outcome; mixed cognitively-impaired patients), and the popular 'omega-6/omega-3 ratio' framing used in marketing is not a validated predictor of cognitive outcomes in controlled human trials.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. Linoleic acid (LA) is an essential fatty acid that must be obtained from the diet; arachidonic acid (AA), synthesized endogenously from LA or obtained preformed from meat/eggs, is the second most abundant long-chain PUFA in human brain gray matter after DHA, where it is a major structural component of neuronal membrane phospholipids and the precursor of eicosanoid signaling molecules. This structural/biochemical role is well established. Whether omega-6 fatty acid intake, supplementation, or the ratio of omega-6 to omega-3 intake measurably changes human cognitive performance is a separate question, and the best available synthesis answers it in the negative or as 'unclear'. The largest systematic review and meta-analysis of randomized trials on polyunsaturated fat and cognition (38 RCTs, 41 comparisons, 49,757 participants, trials >=24 weeks) found that effects of increasing alpha-linolenic acid, omega-6, or total PUFA intake on cognitive outcomes were unclear, while long-chain omega-3 specifically showed no or very little effect on new neurocognitive illness, new cognitive impairment, or global cognition (moderate-quality GRADE evidence) [brainard2020]. This review, the most rigorous and largest in this space, does not support a cognitive benefit (or harm) from omega-6 intake manipulation and explicitly could not characterize omega-6's effect due to insufficient comparable trial data, which is itself an important finding: omega-6 fatty acids have not been adequately tested in controlled human cognitive trials despite high habitual intake in Western diets. Two small Japanese trials from overlapping research groups reported AA-supplementation benefits in specific, non-generalizable contexts. In 25 healthy elderly men, 240 mg/day AA-enriched triglyceride for 1 month produced a significantly shorter P300 latency and higher P300 amplitude (event-related potential measures of neural processing speed/allocation) versus olive oil placebo, alongside a confirmed rise in serum phospholipid AA content [ishikura2009]; this is a neurophysiological biomarker outcome in a crossover design with a small sample, not a validated behavioral memory or attention test score, and was conducted by Suntory-affiliated investigators testing a Suntory ingredient (SUNTGA40S), a clear industry-funding context per Rule 6. In a mixed clinical sample of mild cognitive impairment (MCI), organic brain lesion, and Alzheimer's disease patients, 240 mg/day AA plus DHA for 90 days improved immediate memory and attention scores (RBANS) in the MCI-amnestic subgroup and immediate/delayed memory in the organic-lesion subgroup, but produced no significant improvement in the Alzheimer's disease or MCI-placebo subgroups [kotani2006] -- a clinical/impaired population result that, per Rule 5, must not be generalized to healthy adults, let alone young or student populations. No RCT testing arachidonic acid or linoleic acid supplementation for cognitive outcomes in healthy young adults or students was identified in this search. Observational cohort data on dietary omega-6/PUFA intake and cognitive decline in older adults is mixed and confounded: a 1997 Zutphen Elderly Study cohort found higher total PUFA intake (dominated by linoleic acid in the Dutch diet of that era) was associated with less 5-year cognitive decline in men without baseline impairment, alongside antioxidant status [kalmijn1997]; the ARIC cohort found plasma omega-3, not omega-6, associated with lower cognitive-decline risk, without a clear independent omega-6 signal reported in the abstract [beydoun2007]; the Doetinchem cohort examining fish consumption and dietary fats found associations driven mainly by fish/omega-3 intake rather than omega-6 [nooyens2018]; and a 2023 Italian cross-sectional study of dietary fats and cognitive status reported associations for overall fat quality patterns rather than isolating omega-6 as an independent predictor [currenti2023]. These cohort studies are observational, confounded by overall diet quality, socioeconomic status and other lifestyle factors, and cannot establish causation per Rule 7. A widely cited narrative review on omega-6 and omega-3 PUFA health effects [djuricic2021] describes plausible mechanisms and some associations but is explicitly a narrative synthesis, not a quantitative meta-analysis of cognitive RCTs, and should not be read as establishing a cognitive effect. Taken together: AA is mechanistically indispensable to brain structure, but no adequately powered RCT demonstrates that manipulating omega-6 intake (up or down) changes cognitive performance in healthy adults of any age, the two positive AA-supplementation signals are small, industry-linked or confined to impaired populations, and the dominant meta-analytic synthesis explicitly flags omega-6's cognitive effect as 'unclear' due to insufficient trial data rather than confirmed null or confirmed benefit.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "True dietary omega-6/linoleic acid deficiency is now rare in populations with access to typical Western diets, which are often omega-6-replete or omega-6-dominant relative to omega-3 intake; classic essential-fatty-acid deficiency (seen historically in fat-free parenteral nutrition) causes skin and growth abnormalities, not a defined cognitive deficiency syndrome in adults. Because most people in Morocco, the EU and the US already consume ample or excess linoleic acid from vegetable oils, a 'deficiency correction' framing for omega-6 supplementation in Brain Food's target populations (students, knowledge workers) is not applicable; the relevant question would be enhancement (supplementing beyond typical intake) or, in some wellness narratives, reduction (lowering omega-6 intake, or improving omega-6:omega-3 balance). The AA-supplementation trials reviewed here [ishikura2009, kotani2006] were conducted in elderly and clinically impaired populations, not in people selected for low omega-6/AA status, so they test enhancement-on-top-of-adequate-intake in a different population than Brain Food's primary target; no enhancement trial in healthy young adults exists.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Marketing narratives around omega-6 fall into two opposing camps, neither well supported by controlled human cognitive trial data reviewed here. One narrative (used by some AA-supplement sellers) claims arachidonic acid 'boosts memory and brain speed' based on [ishikura2009] and [kotani2006]; this overstates evidence that is a small industry-funded biomarker trial in elderly men and a mixed-population clinical trial showing subgroup-specific (not general) benefit, with no data in healthy young adults. The opposing narrative (used in many omega-3-supplement and 'anti-inflammatory diet' marketing contexts) claims that modern diets have a harmfully 'high omega-6:omega-3 ratio' that damages brain health and that lowering this ratio improves cognition; the largest and most rigorous meta-analysis of PUFA RCTs for cognition found omega-6's effect on cognitive outcomes 'unclear' rather than confirming either a harm from high omega-6 intake or a benefit from reducing it [brainard2020], and no RCT reviewed here tested an omega-6:omega-3 ratio intervention against a validated cognitive outcome. The honest position for Brain Food is that omega-6 fatty acids, especially arachidonic acid, are mechanistically essential to brain structure, that neither raising nor lowering typical omega-6 intake has demonstrated RCT-confirmed cognitive benefit or harm in healthy adults, and that ratio-based claims ('optimal 4:1 omega-6:omega-3 ratio for brain health' and similar) are not substantiated by the human cognitive trial literature and should not be used as marketing claims in any of the three target markets.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Linoleic acid, an essential fatty acid obtained only from the diet, is elongated and desaturated (via FADS1/FADS2 enzymes) to arachidonic acid, which together with DHA makes up the large majority of long-chain PUFA in neuronal and glial cell membranes. AA contributes to membrane fluidity and signaling and is the precursor of eicosanoids (prostaglandins, thromboxanes, leukotrienes) with both pro- and anti-inflammatory roles depending on which eicosanoid is produced. The brain actively regulates its AA and DHA content through selective uptake and recycling (the Rapoport brain phospholipid model), largely independent of short-term dietary AA fluctuations in adults with adequate LA intake, which may partly explain why AA supplementation trials in replete adults show limited cognitive signal.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Dietary linoleic acid -> FADS1/FADS2-mediated elongation/desaturation -> endogenous arachidonic acid synthesis", "detail": "Well-characterized human biochemical pathway; FADS1/FADS2 polymorphisms are known to modulate conversion efficiency and circulating AA levels.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Dietary/endogenous AA -> incorporation into brain phospholipid membrane pools (alongside DHA)", "detail": "AA is the second most abundant brain long-chain PUFA after DHA; brain AA content is tightly homeostatically regulated and relatively stable against short-term dietary change in adults.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Membrane AA -> phospholipase A2-mediated release -> eicosanoid (prostaglandin/leukotriene/thromboxane) synthesis", "detail": "Established biochemical pathway relevant to neuroinflammation and synaptic signaling; directionality of effect (pro- versus anti-cognitive) depends on specific eicosanoid and context.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Proposed: AA supplementation increases serum/membrane AA -> altered neuronal signaling/neurotransmission -> faster neural processing", "detail": "Supported by a rise in serum phospholipid AA and a shortened P300 latency (ERP biomarker) in one small trial [ishikura2009]; not confirmed with a validated behavioral cognitive test.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Measured human cognitive/behavioral outcome", "detail": "No adequately powered RCT in healthy adults confirms a behavioral cognitive benefit from AA/omega-6 supplementation; largest PUFA-cognition meta-analysis found omega-6 effects 'unclear' [brainard2020]; the clinical mixed-population AA+DHA trial showed benefit only in specific impaired subgroups, not uniformly [kotani2006].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["linoleic acid to arachidonic acid conversion (FADS1/FADS2)", "membrane phospholipid incorporation and fluidity", "eicosanoid (prostaglandin/leukotriene/thromboxane) signaling", "proposed effect on neural processing speed (P300 latency biomarker)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Adults in RCTs of >=24 weeks duration, pooled across 38 trials; omega-6/total PUFA comparisons specifically", "dose_or_intake": "varied across 41 comparisons; not uniformly reported for omega-6-specific arms", "duration": ">=24 weeks (meta-analysis inclusion criterion)", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "Largest PUFA-cognition RCT meta-analysis (38 RCTs, 49,757 participants) explicitly found effects of increasing omega-6 (or alpha-linolenic acid, or total PUFA) on cognitive outcomes 'unclear' due to insufficient comparable data, in contrast to long-chain omega-3 for which a moderate-quality null effect was established.", "effect_estimate": "not reported as a quantified pooled estimate for omega-6 specifically; explicitly described as 'unclear'", "certainty_notes": "Downgraded for indirectness and imprecision: omega-6-specific comparisons were too few/heterogeneous within the review to generate a pooled quantitative estimate, despite the review's otherwise high methodological quality for the omega-3 comparisons.", "key_study_ids": ["brainard2020"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "neuroplasticity_biomarkers", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Healthy elderly men, Japan", "dose_or_intake": "240 mg/day arachidonic acid (as AA-enriched triglyceride)", "duration": "1 month, crossover", "grade": "D", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "AA supplementation significantly shortened P300 latency and increased P300 amplitude versus olive oil placebo, alongside a confirmed increase in serum phospholipid AA; this is an electrophysiological processing-speed biomarker, not a standardized behavioral memory/attention test score.", "effect_estimate": "P300 latency significantly shorter, amplitude significantly higher with AA vs placebo (exact values/CI not reported in abstract)", "certainty_notes": "Single small (n=25) industry-funded (Suntory) crossover trial using a biomarker outcome; per Rule 3, this supports plausibility, not established cognitive efficacy; downgraded for imprecision, indirectness of outcome, and funding source.", "key_study_ids": ["ishikura2009"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Mild cognitive impairment (amnestic subtype), organic brain lesion, and Alzheimer's disease patients, Japan", "dose_or_intake": "240 mg/day arachidonic acid + 240 mg/day DHA", "duration": "90 days", "grade": "D", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Significant improvement in RBANS immediate memory and attention in the amnestic MCI subgroup and in immediate/delayed memory in the organic-lesion subgroup; no significant improvement in Alzheimer's disease patients or in the MCI-placebo subgroup.", "effect_estimate": "significant subgroup improvements reported; exact RBANS score changes and statistics not detailed in abstract", "certainty_notes": "Small (n=21 MCI, n=10 organic, n=8 AD), clinically impaired/heterogeneous population; per Rule 5, cannot be generalized to healthy adults or to the general older population, let alone to students or knowledge workers.", "key_study_ids": ["kotani2006"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "No RCT of omega-6/arachidonic acid supplementation for cognitive outcomes identified in healthy young adults or students", "dose_or_intake": "not studied", "duration": "not studied", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No controlled trial testing omega-6 fatty acid (linoleic acid or arachidonic acid) supplementation on cognitive performance in healthy young adults or students was located in this search.", "effect_estimate": "not reported (no studies)", "certainty_notes": "Absence of evidence in the target population; cannot be graded above D.", "key_study_ids": []}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "not studied", "onset": "not applicable", "duration_studied": "no acute single-dose omega-6/AA cognitive trial identified", "domain": "other", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "effect_estimate": "not reported (no studies)", "certainty": "none - evidence gap", "safety": "omega-6 fatty acids are routine dietary components; no acute safety concern identified at food-level intakes", "study_ids": []}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": ">=24 weeks, pooled across 38 RCTs", "population": "mixed_adults (mainly older adults)", "outcome": "global cognition, new cognitive impairment, new neurocognitive illness", "effect_estimate": "omega-6/total PUFA effect described as 'unclear'; long-chain omega-3 effect described as little-to-none", "evidence_quality": "high-quality, large meta-analysis, but omega-6-specific data insufficiently granular for a pooled quantitative estimate", "limitations": "omega-6 arms were a minority of included comparisons; most trials were designed around omega-3, not omega-6, interventions", "study_ids": ["brainard2020"]}, {"duration": "5-year follow-up (observational)", "population": "older_adults_healthy (Zutphen Elderly Study, men)", "outcome": "cognitive decline", "effect_estimate": "higher PUFA (largely linoleic-acid-dominated in this Dutch cohort) and antioxidant intake associated with less cognitive decline", "evidence_quality": "observational cohort, confounded", "limitations": "cannot establish causation; dietary pattern confounding; all-male, Dutch, 1990s diet composition may not generalize", "study_ids": ["kalmijn1997"]}, {"duration": "90 days", "population": "clinical_population (MCI/organic lesion/AD)", "outcome": "memory and attention (RBANS)", "effect_estimate": "subgroup-specific improvement in MCI-amnestic and organic-lesion groups; no improvement in AD or MCI-placebo groups", "evidence_quality": "small clinical trial, heterogeneous diagnostic subgroups", "limitations": "clinical/impaired population, not generalizable to healthy adults per Rule 5", "study_ids": ["kotani2006"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Dietary omega-6 fatty acids, including linoleic acid and arachidonic acid, are consumed in substantial quantities in ordinary Western, Moroccan and EU diets without an established pattern of acute toxicity; the main safety debate concerns long-term cardiometabolic and inflammatory effects of high omega-6 (particularly linoleic acid) intake relative to omega-3 intake, which is contested in the broader nutrition literature and was not resolved by the cognition-focused evidence reviewed in this search.", "adverse_effects": ["no acute adverse events reported in the small AA-supplementation trials reviewed here [ishikura2009, kotani2006] at doses of 240 mg/day AA", "theoretical concern that excess AA-derived eicosanoids could promote a pro-inflammatory state at very high supplemental intakes, though this was not directly tested as a cognitive-safety outcome in the trials reviewed"], "contraindications": ["no omega-6/AA-specific contraindications were identified in the trials reviewed; general caution for people with inflammatory or autoimmune conditions where eicosanoid balance is a clinical concern, per broader (non-cognition-specific) nutrition literature not detailed in this search"], "drug_interactions": ["no drug-interaction studies specific to omega-6/AA supplementation and cognitive-relevant medications were identified in this search"], "upper_limits": "No specific tolerable upper intake level for omega-6/linoleic acid/arachidonic acid intake relevant to cognitive safety was identified in this search; general dietary guidelines (e.g. EFSA, US Dietary Guidelines) set acceptable macronutrient distribution ranges for total PUFA/omega-6 intake on cardiometabolic grounds, not cognitive-safety grounds, and were not independently verified in this session.", "sleep_impact": "No trial reviewed assessed sleep architecture or quality for omega-6/AA supplementation; not studied.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Longest controlled supplementation duration identified in this search is 90 days [kotani2006]; typical dietary omega-6 intake is, of course, lifelong, but no long-term controlled cognitive-safety trial of AA/linoleic acid supplementation beyond 90 days was identified.", "daily_use_appropriate": "probably", "special_populations": "No trials in pregnant/lactating women, children, or people with psychiatric or major chronic disease were identified for cognitive outcomes in this search; infant formula AA+DHA supplementation is an established, separately regulated area (infant nutrition) not covered in depth in this adult-focused monograph.", "specialist_review_needed": ["lipidology/inflammation specialist review of the broader omega-6:omega-3 ratio and cardiometabolic/inflammatory-disease debate before making any related marketing claim", "pediatric nutrition review if any infant-formula-adjacent claim is contemplated (out of scope for this adult-focused monograph)"], "hard_safety_gate": "pass", "gate_reason": "No material cognitive-safety signal was identified in the small supplementation trials reviewed at studied doses (240 mg/day AA for up to 90 days), and omega-6 fatty acids are ordinary, long-consumed dietary components; the broader cardiometabolic/inflammatory omega-6:omega-3 ratio debate is noted as an area needing specialist review before any related claim, but does not constitute a hard safety failure for ordinary dietary-level use."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "No EFSA-authorised health claim specifically for omega-6 fatty acids, linoleic acid or arachidonic acid and cognitive function was identified in this search; EFSA has authorised a linoleic-acid-related claim for blood cholesterol maintenance (not cognition) under Regulation 432/2012, which is outside this monograph's cognitive scope. No cognitive-function claim for omega-6/AA is authorised in the EU.", "us": "Linoleic acid is recognized as an essential fatty acid with an Adequate Intake set by US bodies for general nutrition purposes; no FDA-recognized or well-substantiated structure/function claim specifically linking omega-6/AA supplementation to cognitive enhancement in healthy adults was identified in this search, and an FTC-defensible cognitive claim would currently lack adequate RCT substantiation given the 'unclear' meta-analytic finding [brainard2020].", "morocco": "No omega-6/AA-specific ONSSA or Ministry of Health opinion was identified in this search; linoleic acid as a component of common vegetable oils is a routine, unregulated dietary component, but any specific cognitive claim would fall under Morocco's general supplement-claims framework, for which no omega-6-specific authorization was found; status should be confirmed with local regulatory counsel.", "permitted_claims_examples": [], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "Linoleic acid and arachidonic acid from conventional food sources (vegetable oils, meat, eggs) are not novel foods; concentrated AA-enriched oil ingredients (e.g. SUNTGA40S-type products) may be subject to standard food/dietary-ingredient notification requirements in some jurisdictions, but no specific novel-food rejection or pending dossier was identified in this search.", "regulatory_gate": "fail"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Linoleic acid is already ubiquitous in ordinary diets via vegetable oils, nuts and seeds, making it trivially easy to include in a food product; concentrated arachidonic acid (as used in the supplementation trials) would require a specific AA-enriched oil ingredient rather than relying on ordinary dietary fat sources, since AA is present in meaningful but lower amounts in typical foods (meat, eggs) compared with the 240 mg/day doses tested.", "formats": ["AA-enriched oil capsules (as used in [ishikura2009], [kotani2006])", "fortified foods using standard vegetable oils for linoleic acid delivery (though this is already abundant in most diets, limiting marketing differentiation)", "combination AA+DHA phospholipid or triglyceride oil blends"], "stability": "Both linoleic acid and arachidonic acid are polyunsaturated and prone to oxidation; concentrated AA oil ingredients require antioxidant protection and appropriate packaging, similar to omega-3 oils.", "taste": "Refined vegetable oils used for linoleic acid are largely neutral in taste; concentrated AA-enriched oils may carry a mild fishy/oily off-note depending on source and processing, though less pronounced than typical fish oil.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Both LA and AA are well absorbed via standard dietary fat digestion; the AA-supplementation trials reviewed confirmed a rise in serum phospholipid AA content after 1 month at 240 mg/day [ishikura2009], indicating adequate oral bioavailability at that dose.", "dose_feasibility": "240 mg/day AA, the dose used in both positive-signal trials, is a modest amount deliverable via a single capsule or a modestly AA-enriched food serving, making dose feasibility straightforward if a cognitive claim were ever substantiated; however, given most people already obtain AA from dietary meat/eggs, the marginal 'enhancement' rationale for added AA in an already AA-replete population is unclear.", "cost_availability": "Linoleic acid-rich vegetable oils are among the cheapest, most widely available fat ingredients; concentrated AA-enriched oil ingredients (e.g. from fungal fermentation sources, historically used in infant formula) are considerably more specialized and costly.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here; no specific supplier price quotes were obtained in this search"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 2, "effect_magnitude": 2, "safety": 7, "reproducibility": 2, "food_suitability": 7, "cost_access": 8, "rationale": "evidence_quality=2: the largest, most rigorous meta-analysis explicitly finds omega-6's cognitive effect 'unclear' due to insufficient data (not even a confident null); the two positive AA trials are small, industry-linked or confined to a clinical/impaired population, each warranting grade D. effect_magnitude=2: where a signal exists it is a biomarker (P300) or a clinical-subgroup memory score, not a quantified behavioral effect in the target healthy-adult population. safety=7: ordinary dietary-level safety is well established and no acute signal appeared in the small supplementation trials, but the broader omega-6:omega-3 cardiometabolic/inflammatory debate (not resolved here) and absence of long-term cognitive-safety data prevent a higher score. reproducibility=2: the two positive AA studies share overlapping authorship/industry ties and have not been independently replicated; the large independent meta-analysis found no confirmed omega-6 cognitive effect at all. food_suitability=7: linoleic acid is already in virtually every diet, though concentrated AA requires a specialized ingredient. cost_access=8: linoleic-acid-rich oils are extremely cheap and universally available; concentrated AA is pricier but still broadly sourceable."}, "research_gaps": ["No adequately powered RCT has tested omega-6 fatty acid (linoleic acid or arachidonic acid) supplementation for cognitive outcomes in healthy young adults or students.", "The largest PUFA-cognition meta-analysis explicitly could not characterize omega-6's effect due to insufficient comparable trial data; dedicated omega-6 RCTs with validated behavioral cognitive outcomes are needed.", "The two positive AA-supplementation signals (Ishikura, Kotani) have not been independently replicated by non-overlapping research groups or outside Japan.", "No RCT has directly tested an omega-6:omega-3 ratio intervention (holding total PUFA constant while varying the ratio) against a validated cognitive outcome, despite this being a common marketing claim.", "No long-term (multi-year) controlled safety or efficacy data exist for AA/linoleic acid supplementation specifically for cognitive purposes."], "_derived": {"composite_score": 4.1, "eligibility": "ineligible", "safety_gate": "pass", "regulatory_gate": "fail", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": null, "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": "D", "best_grade_overall": null, "benefit_domains": {}, "n_outcome_rows": 4, "n_studies": 8, "n_verified": 8, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 2, "n_systematic_reviews": 1, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["inconsistent", "insufficient", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "phosphatidylcholine-and-lecithin", "name": "Phosphatidylcholine, lecithin and krill phospholipids", "category": "D", "category_name": "Fatty acids and lipids", "aliases": ["phosphatidylcholine", "PC", "lecithin", "soy lecithin", "sunflower lecithin", "egg lecithin", "krill oil phospholipids", "polyenylphosphatidylcholine", "PPC"], "food_sources": ["egg yolk (rich source of phosphatidylcholine)", "soybeans and soy lecithin (a common food emulsifier, E322 in the EU)", "sunflower seeds and sunflower lecithin", "liver and organ meats", "krill (Euphausia superba) oil, in which most omega-3 fatty acids are bound to phosphatidylcholine rather than triglycerides", "wheat germ, peanuts, and to a lesser extent most whole foods containing cell membranes"], "active_constituents": ["phosphatidylcholine (a glycerophospholipid; the dominant phospholipid of mammalian cell membranes and the body's major source of choline)", "in krill oil, n-3 long-chain PUFA (EPA/DHA) esterified onto a phosphatidylcholine backbone rather than a triglyceride backbone", "choline, released on PC digestion/hydrolysis and further metabolized to acetylcholine, betaine (methyl donor) or phosphatidylcholine resynthesis"], "one_line_verdict": "Lecithin/phosphatidylcholine has decades of use as a choline-delivery vehicle and a food emulsifier, but the Cochrane review found no clinical cognitive benefit in dementia, no RCT demonstrates a cognitive benefit in healthy adults, and the specific cognitive-relevant claim belongs to choline itself (covered in unit E5) rather than to PC/lecithin as such; krill-oil phospholipid-bound omega-3 shows a promising single small RCT in healthy elderly that needs independent replication.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. Phosphatidylcholine (PC) is the principal dietary source of choline, an essential nutrient with an Adequate Intake set by national bodies; its established human roles are as a methyl donor (via betaine) and a precursor for acetylcholine and membrane phospholipid synthesis, mechanisms shared with choline supplementation generally (quantified and graded separately in unit E5-choline; this monograph focuses on lecithin/PC and krill-phospholipid-specific trials rather than re-litigating choline's own evidence base). The specific question of whether supplemental lecithin or PC itself (beyond its role as a choline source) improves human cognition has been tested directly and the results are discouraging. The Cochrane systematic review of lecithin for dementia and cognitive impairment [higgins2003] pooled 12 randomized trials (265 Alzheimer's disease patients, 21 Parkinsonian dementia patients, 90 people with subjective memory problems) and found no trial reporting clear clinical benefit of lecithin for Alzheimer's disease or Parkinsonian dementia; the only statistically significant pooled result favored placebo for adverse events (judged by the reviewers to be a spurious finding), and the reviewers explicitly declined to prioritize a large confirmatory trial given the weak signal even in the one positive small subjective-memory-problems trial. This is a null result in the population (dementia, subjective memory complaints) most favorable to finding an effect, and it predates and is reinforced by the near-total absence of newer lecithin/PC cognition RCTs located in this search. In pregnant women consuming moderate-choline diets (~360 mg/day, about 80% of the recommended intake), adding 750 mg/day phosphatidylcholine supplementation from 18 weeks gestation through 90 days postpartum did not enhance their infants' global development, language development, short-term visuospatial memory, or long-term episodic memory at 10-12 months of age [cheatham2012] -- a null result in a different population (maternal supplementation, infant outcome) that nonetheless shows PC supplementation on top of an already-adequate choline intake does not add detectable cognitive benefit, consistent with a deficiency-correction rather than enhancement model. No randomized trial of lecithin or PC supplementation (as distinct from other choline forms such as citicoline or alpha-GPC, which are reviewed under unit E5) was identified in healthy young adults, students or knowledge workers for memory, attention or executive function. The one encouraging modern signal is a krill-oil trial testing the hypothesis that omega-3 fatty acids delivered on a phosphatidylcholine backbone are more bioactive than the same fatty acids delivered as triglycerides: in 45 healthy elderly men (61-72y) randomized to 12 weeks of krill oil (n-3 PUFA bound to PC), sardine oil (n-3 PUFA as triglycerides), or a medium-chain-triglyceride placebo, both oil groups showed significantly greater task-related prefrontal oxyhemoglobin changes (near-infrared spectroscopy) during working-memory and calculation tasks than placebo, and the krill-oil group alone showed a significantly shorter P300 latency (an event-related-potential biomarker of working-memory processing speed) versus placebo [konagai2013]. These are neurophysiological biomarkers of brain activation and electrical processing speed, not standardized behavioral cognitive test scores, and the trial is small, single-site, and industry-relevant (krill oil ingredient research); no independent replication using validated cognitive test batteries was found. Reproducibility: the strongest, most population-relevant evidence (Cochrane) is null; the one positive newer trial is a small biomarker study that has not been replicated with behavioral outcomes. Because choline itself is an essential nutrient with its own independently graded evidence base (unit E5), any Brain Food claim specifically for 'lecithin' or 'PC' rather than for choline content per se has essentially no supporting human cognitive-outcome data beyond the single small krill-oil biomarker trial.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Choline (of which PC is the major dietary form) is an essential nutrient; the clearest rationale for benefit from PC/lecithin supplementation is correcting inadequate choline intake, which is common (most adults fall short of the US Adequate Intake). The pregnancy trial [cheatham2012] directly tested whether adding PC on top of an already-moderate (not deficient) choline intake enhances infant cognition, and found no benefit, consistent with a 'correction, not enhancement' model: benefit, if any, would be expected mainly in people with low baseline choline status, not in people already consuming adequate choline. No trial identified in this search tested lecithin/PC supplementation specifically in choline-replete healthy young adults for enhancement beyond normal function; the Cochrane dementia/cognitive-impairment review, which tested PC's proposed mechanistic role (restoring an acetylcholine-synthesis enzyme deficit in Alzheimer's disease), was also null, undermining even the deficiency-correction rationale in that clinical population.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Lecithin is widely marketed as a 'brain food' or 'memory nutrient' on the strength of its choline content and its historical association with acetylcholine synthesis, but the direct randomized evidence for lecithin/PC itself -- as opposed to purified choline, citicoline or alpha-GPC (covered in unit E5) -- is a Cochrane-level null result in dementia and a null result for infant cognition from maternal supplementation. Marketing that cites 'supports brain cell membrane health' is mechanistically defensible (PC is indeed a major membrane phospholipid) but 'supports memory' or 'boosts cognition' claims for lecithin/PC specifically are not supported by the available RCTs. The krill-oil phospholipid finding [konagai2013] is sometimes used to market krill oil as superior to fish oil for the brain; the underlying evidence is a single small trial with EEG/NIRS biomarker endpoints in elderly men, not a validated memory or attention test, and has not been independently replicated -- this is a mechanistically interesting, hypothesis-generating result, not proof of superior cognitive efficacy. In the EU, only the choline-specific EFSA-authorised claim ('choline contributes to normal lipid metabolism' and 'choline contributes to normal homocysteine metabolism', among others; see choline unit E5 for the full authorised list) can legally be used, and it must be based on the product's choline content meeting the required threshold per EU Regulation 432/2012 -- a 'lecithin supports memory' claim is not authorised. The honest position is: lecithin/PC is a legitimate, safe choline source and food emulsifier, but any cognitive claim for the brand should rest on choline's own evidence base and authorised claim wording, not on lecithin/PC-specific trials, which are null or too preliminary to support a claim.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Dietary phosphatidylcholine is hydrolyzed in the gut and absorbed partly as lyso-PC and partly as free choline/glycerophosphocholine, then resynthesized into PC in the liver (via the CDP-choline pathway) or further metabolized to acetylcholine, betaine, or phospholipid pools in cell membranes including neuronal membranes. The classical 'cholinergic hypothesis' (choline precursor loading to boost acetylcholine synthesis and compensate for cholinergic deficits in Alzheimer's disease) motivated the original lecithin-for-dementia trials; this mechanism is plausible but was not confirmed by the clinical outcome data (Cochrane null result). Krill-oil PC is additionally hypothesized to deliver omega-3 fatty acids more efficiently to brain tissue because phospholipid-bound DHA/EPA may cross the blood-brain barrier more readily than triglyceride-bound forms, though this is based on limited human evidence.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Dietary PC/lecithin -> intestinal hydrolysis to lyso-PC, glycerophosphocholine, free choline and fatty acids", "detail": "Standard lipid digestion via pancreatic phospholipase A2 and other lipases; well described physiologically.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Absorbed choline/lyso-PC -> hepatic choline pool -> acetylcholine synthesis, betaine/methyl-donor pathway, or resynthesis into PC for lipoprotein/membrane use", "detail": "Established choline metabolism; choline crosses the blood-brain barrier via dedicated transporters and supports neuronal acetylcholine synthesis.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Proposed: increased precursor availability -> increased acetylcholine synthesis/release -> improved cholinergic neurotransmission", "detail": "This was the explicit rationale for lecithin-in-dementia trials; the Cochrane review found no clinical benefit despite this plausible mechanism [higgins2003], indicating either insufficient brain delivery, a ceiling effect, or a mechanism not rate-limiting for cognition in the populations tested.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Krill-oil specific: phospholipid-bound n-3 PUFA -> proposed enhanced blood-brain-barrier transport versus triglyceride-bound n-3 PUFA -> increased cortical activation during cognitive tasks", "detail": "Supported by one small human NIRS/EEG biomarker trial showing greater prefrontal oxyhemoglobin response and shorter P300 latency with krill oil versus triglyceride fish oil [konagai2013]; not confirmed with behavioral cognitive outcomes or independent replication.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Measured human cognitive/behavioral outcome", "detail": "Null in dementia/cognitive impairment (Cochrane, 12 trials) [higgins2003] and null for infant cognition from maternal PC supplementation [cheatham2012]; no RCT identified in healthy adults using validated cognitive tests.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["choline/acetylcholine precursor-loading (cholinergic hypothesis)", "methyl-donor (betaine) one-carbon metabolism", "neuronal and systemic membrane phospholipid turnover", "proposed differential blood-brain-barrier transport of phospholipid-bound versus triglyceride-bound omega-3 fatty acids (krill oil)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Alzheimer's disease (265 patients), Parkinsonian dementia (21 patients), and subjective memory problems (90 patients) pooled across 12 RCTs", "dose_or_intake": "varied across included trials (lecithin doses not uniformly reported)", "duration": "more than 1 day, varied by trial (Cochrane inclusion criterion)", "grade": "E", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Cochrane systematic review (12 RCTs) found no trial reporting clear clinical benefit of lecithin for Alzheimer's disease or Parkinsonian dementia; the only significant pooled result favored placebo for adverse events and was judged spurious; one small subjective-memory-problems trial showed a dramatic positive result but was not considered to justify a large confirmatory trial given overall weak evidence.", "effect_estimate": "no significant pooled benefit reported; reviewers explicitly concluded evidence does not support lecithin's use in dementia treatment", "certainty_notes": "High-quality systematic review with consistent null findings across trial populations; downgraded only for the age/heterogeneity of included trials and small per-trial sample sizes, but the overall conclusion is robust.", "key_study_ids": ["higgins2003"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "No RCT of lecithin/PC (distinct from other choline forms) identified in unselected healthy young adults or students", "dose_or_intake": "not studied", "duration": "not studied", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No trial testing lecithin or phosphatidylcholine supplementation for cognitive enhancement in healthy young adults or students was located in this search; the only PC-specific supplementation RCT in a non-deficient population tested maternal supplementation with infant outcomes, not healthy-adult cognition directly.", "effect_estimate": "not reported (no studies)", "certainty_notes": "Absence of evidence in the target population; cannot be graded above D.", "key_study_ids": []}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "neuroplasticity_biomarkers", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Healthy elderly men, 61-72y, Japan", "dose_or_intake": "krill oil containing n-3 PUFA bound to phosphatidylcholine (dose not specified beyond 'krill oil' capsule regimen)", "duration": "12 weeks", "grade": "D", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Krill oil (n-3 PUFA on a PC backbone) produced significantly greater prefrontal oxyhemoglobin increases during working-memory and calculation tasks and a significantly shorter P300 latency than MCT placebo; sardine oil (triglyceride-bound n-3) matched krill oil on the oxyhemoglobin measure but not on P300 latency. These are neurophysiological/hemodynamic biomarkers, not validated behavioral cognitive test scores.", "effect_estimate": "P300 latency differential significantly shorter for krill oil vs MCT placebo (exact ms difference and CI not reported in abstract); oxyhemoglobin changes significantly greater for both krill and sardine oil vs placebo", "certainty_notes": "Single small (n=45, 15/arm) industry-relevant trial using biomarker rather than behavioral outcomes; mechanism is plausible per Rule 3 but cannot be scored as established cognitive efficacy; needs independent replication with standardized cognitive tests.", "key_study_ids": ["konagai2013"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "not studied", "onset": "not applicable", "duration_studied": "no acute single-dose lecithin/PC cognitive trial identified", "domain": "other", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "effect_estimate": "not reported (no studies)", "certainty": "none - evidence gap", "safety": "lecithin/PC is a long-used food emulsifier (EU E322) generally recognized as safe acutely", "study_ids": []}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "variable (>1 day), 12 RCTs pooled", "population": "clinical_population (Alzheimer's/Parkinsonian dementia, subjective memory problems)", "outcome": "global cognition/dementia severity", "effect_estimate": "no clear clinical benefit demonstrated in any included trial", "evidence_quality": "high-quality Cochrane systematic review, consistent null", "limitations": "included trials are small and dated; heterogeneous dosing/duration; one small positive trial (subjective memory problems) not independently replicated at scale", "study_ids": ["higgins2003"]}, {"duration": "~15 weeks gestation through 90 days postpartum (maternal); infant outcome at 10-12 months", "population": "children_adolescents (infants of supplemented mothers)", "outcome": "global development, language, short-/long-term memory", "effect_estimate": "no significant difference between 750 mg/day PC and placebo on any outcome", "evidence_quality": "well-controlled RCT, null, in a moderate- (not low-) choline-intake population", "limitations": "may not generalize to mothers with genuinely low choline intake (as the authors themselves note); single trial; relatively short follow-up to 12 months", "study_ids": ["cheatham2012"]}, {"duration": "12 weeks", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "outcome": "task-related cortical hemodynamic/electrophysiological activation (biomarker)", "effect_estimate": "significantly greater oxyhemoglobin response and shorter P300 latency with krill oil vs placebo", "evidence_quality": "single small industry-relevant RCT, biomarker outcomes only", "limitations": "no behavioral cognitive test outcome, small sample, unreplicated", "study_ids": ["konagai2013"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Lecithin (soy, sunflower, or egg-derived) is a long-established food ingredient (EU food additive E322) used as an emulsifier in a very wide range of processed foods, and phosphatidylcholine/lecithin supplementation at doses up to several grams per day has an extensive history of use without major safety signals in the trials reviewed here. Krill oil carries the general safety profile of a marine omega-3 oil (fish/shellfish allergen risk) rather than any PC-specific concern.", "adverse_effects": ["mild gastrointestinal effects (bloating, loose stools) occasionally reported anecdotally with high-dose lecithin, not quantified in the RCTs reviewed here", "no significant excess of adverse events versus placebo was found in the Cochrane review's included trials [higgins2003]; the one 'significant' adverse-event difference favored placebo and was judged likely spurious", "theoretical trimethylamine/TMAO production from choline-rich foods including PC, relevant to cardiovascular risk discussions but not established as a cognitive-safety issue; no specific TMAO data were retrieved in this search for PC/lecithin supplementation"], "contraindications": ["shellfish/crustacean allergy is a contraindication for krill oil specifically (krill is a crustacean)", "soy allergy for soy-derived lecithin (egg- or sunflower-derived lecithin avoids this)"], "drug_interactions": ["no specific drug-interaction studies for lecithin/PC were identified in this search; choline-related compounds have a theoretical interaction with anticholinergic medications, relevant to choline's own evidence base (unit E5)"], "upper_limits": "No ingredient-specific tolerable upper intake level for lecithin/PC itself has been set; choline (the nutrient PC delivers) has a defined UL from bodies such as EFSA/US National Academies (see unit E5-choline for details), beyond which risk of hypotension, fishy body odor and other effects increases.", "sleep_impact": "No trial reviewed assessed sleep architecture or quality; not studied for lecithin/PC specifically.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Lecithin has decades of food-additive use at low per-serving doses; the supplementation trials reviewed here extend to 90 days postpartum (maternal) or approximately 3-6 months in the dementia trials; no multi-year supplementation safety trial for high-dose PC/lecithin was identified in this search.", "daily_use_appropriate": "yes", "special_populations": "Generally regarded as safe in pregnancy at studied doses (750 mg/day PC) [cheatham2012]; crustacean/shellfish-allergic individuals should avoid krill oil; soy-allergic individuals should choose non-soy (sunflower or egg) lecithin sources.", "specialist_review_needed": ["allergen labelling review for soy- versus shellfish-derived phospholipid sources across EU/US/Morocco markets", "cardiometabolic/TMAO literature review if making any cardiovascular-adjacent claim alongside a cognitive one"], "hard_safety_gate": "pass", "gate_reason": "Decades of food-additive and supplement use, plus the specific RCTs reviewed here, show no material safety signal for lecithin/PC at studied doses; main caveats are allergen-specific (soy, shellfish) rather than general toxicity."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Lecithin is an approved food additive (E322) with broad permitted use as an emulsifier; choline itself carries several EFSA-authorised Article 13(1) health claims (e.g. normal lipid metabolism, normal homocysteine metabolism, maintenance of normal liver function) at a minimum choline content threshold per serving, detailed under unit E5-choline. No EU-authorised claim specifically names 'lecithin' or 'phosphatidylcholine' for memory, cognition or brain function; a product would need to meet the choline-content threshold and use only the choline-specific authorised wording, not a PC/lecithin-specific cognitive claim.", "us": "Lecithin/phosphatidylcholine is GRAS as a food ingredient and marketable as a dietary ingredient under DSHEA with a long history of use; choline has an FDA-recognized Daily Value and its own qualified/authorized claim landscape (unit E5); no PC/lecithin-specific cognitive structure/function claim was identified as having strong substantiation, so an FTC-defensible claim would need to rely on choline-content-based substantiation rather than lecithin-specific trial data.", "morocco": "No lecithin/PC-specific ONSSA opinion was identified in this search; lecithin's long-standing status as a common food additive/ingredient suggests low regulatory friction for use as an emulsifier, but any cognitive claim would fall under Morocco's general supplement/claims framework requiring substantiation, which is not currently met by the PC/lecithin-specific trial evidence; status should be confirmed with local regulatory counsel.", "permitted_claims_examples": [], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "Lecithin (soy, sunflower, egg) has an established history of food use in the EU/US and is not a novel food; krill oil has undergone novel food/GRAS notifications in some jurisdictions historically but is now a recognized marketed ingredient in major markets. No PC/lecithin-specific novel-food dossier issue was identified in this search.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Lecithin is exceptionally food-suitable: it is already a standard emulsifier in chocolate, baked goods, margarine and many processed foods, making it trivially easy to add to a Brain Food product; however, delivering a cognitively meaningful dose (if any such dose existed, which the evidence does not currently establish) would require far more than typical emulsifier-level inclusion.", "formats": ["granules/powder (common retail lecithin format)", "softgel capsules (common for concentrated PC or krill oil)", "liquid lecithin (less common, used in some food/beverage applications)", "krill oil softgels (for the phospholipid-bound omega-3 application)"], "stability": "Lecithin is relatively stable in dry powder form but prone to oxidation of its unsaturated fatty acid components, especially in krill-oil phospholipid form which carries omega-3 PUFA; requires antioxidant protection and appropriate packaging/storage.", "taste": "Plain soy/sunflower lecithin has a mild, slightly bitter or nutty taste tolerable in small food-emulsifier amounts; krill oil can impart a fishy taste/odor at supplement-relevant doses, requiring flavor masking or enteric coating in capsule formats.", "bioavailability_matrix": "PC is well absorbed via standard lipid digestion; krill-oil phospholipid-bound EPA/DHA has been reported in some (not reviewed in depth here) pharmacokinetic studies to have different absorption kinetics than triglyceride-bound fish oil, but this monograph did not independently verify a bioavailability advantage with a dedicated PK study beyond the Konagai biomarker trial.", "dose_feasibility": "Plain lecithin/PC doses used in the one directly relevant adult-outcome trial (750 mg/day, pregnancy) are readily deliverable in a food serving or single capsule; because no cognitively effective dose has been established for lecithin/PC itself in non-deficient adults, dose feasibility for a cognitive claim cannot be meaningfully assessed.", "cost_availability": "Soy and sunflower lecithin are low-cost, widely available commodity ingredients; krill oil is substantially more expensive than standard fish oil or plain lecithin due to harvesting and processing costs.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here; no specific supplier price quotes were obtained in this search"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 2, "effect_magnitude": 1, "safety": 8, "reproducibility": 2, "food_suitability": 8, "cost_access": 7, "rationale": "evidence_quality=2: the most authoritative test (Cochrane, 12 RCTs, dementia/memory-complaint populations) is null (grade E in that population); no RCT exists in healthy adults; the one positive signal (krill-oil biomarker trial) is a single small D-grade study using non-behavioral outcomes. effect_magnitude=1: no behavioral cognitive effect size has been demonstrated for lecithin/PC itself in any population reviewed; the krill-oil biomarker effect, even if real, has unknown translation to measurable cognitive performance. safety=8: lecithin has a very long, essentially unproblematic safety record as a food additive and supplement, with allergen-specific exceptions only. reproducibility=2: the Cochrane null result is itself a highly reproducible/robust synthesis of mostly-null trials; the single positive krill-oil trial is unreplicated. food_suitability=8: lecithin is uniquely food-suitable as an existing common food ingredient. cost_access=7: plain lecithin is cheap and ubiquitous, though krill oil specifically is pricier."}, "research_gaps": ["No RCT of lecithin or phosphatidylcholine supplementation (as distinct from other choline forms) has tested cognitive outcomes in healthy young adults, students or knowledge workers.", "The krill-oil phospholipid-omega-3 'superior brain delivery' hypothesis has not been tested with validated behavioral cognitive tests, only EEG/NIRS biomarkers, and has not been independently replicated.", "No modern (post-2003) randomized trial of lecithin/PC for cognitive impairment has been conducted to confirm or update the Cochrane null finding.", "No trial has tested lecithin/PC supplementation specifically in adults with genuinely low (not just moderate) choline intake to test the deficiency-correction hypothesis directly for cognitive outcomes.", "Head-to-head bioavailability comparisons of phospholipid-bound versus triglyceride-bound omega-3 fatty acids for brain outcomes in humans are sparse."], "_derived": {"composite_score": 4.1, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "pass", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": null, "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": "D", "best_grade_overall": null, "benefit_domains": {}, "n_outcome_rows": 3, "n_studies": 3, "n_verified": 3, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 2, "n_systematic_reviews": 1, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "phosphatidylserine", "name": "Phosphatidylserine", "category": "D", "category_name": "Fatty acids and lipids", "aliases": ["PS", "bovine cortex phosphatidylserine", "BC-PS", "soy-derived phosphatidylserine", "soy-PS", "S-PS", "PS-DHA", "phosphatidylserine/phosphatidic acid complex", "PAS"], "food_sources": ["soy lecithin (commercial PS is enzymatically converted from soy lecithin phosphatidylcholine; raw soy lecithin itself contains little PS)", "bovine brain cortex (original 1980s-1990s clinical material; withdrawn from the market over BSE/prion risk)", "small amounts in white beans, egg yolk, chicken liver, mackerel and other organ meats/fatty fish", "sunflower lecithin-derived PS (newer non-soy, non-GMO source used in some products)"], "active_constituents": ["phosphatidylserine (an anionic glycerophospholipid, a structural component of the inner leaflet of neuronal membranes)", "in PS-DHA/PS-omega-3 products, PS esterified with omega-3 long-chain PUFA (DHA/EPA) on its backbone rather than the saturated/monounsaturated fatty acids of plain soy-PS"], "one_line_verdict": "Soy-derived phosphatidylserine has a 30-year record of safety and a handful of positive small trials in elderly people with memory complaints, but the two best-designed soy-PS trials (Jorissen 2001, n=120) and most stress trials are null or inconsistent, no trial has tested healthy young adults or students, and both the EU (EFSA, 2010) and a cautious FDA (2003 qualified claim: 'very limited and preliminary') have declined to endorse a cognition claim.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. Phosphatidylserine (PS) is a normal structural phospholipid of neuronal membranes; oral soy-PS and soy-PS/phosphatidic acid (PAS) blends are well tolerated in randomized trials up to 800 mg/day for 3-12 weeks, with no clinically meaningful change in blood chemistry, blood pressure or adverse events versus placebo [jorissen2002, vakhapova2011]. The historical bovine-cortex PS (BC-PS) literature of the late 1980s-early 1990s reported the most consistent benefit: in 149 adults with age-associated memory impairment (AAMI), 300 mg/day BC-PS for 12 weeks improved performance on learning and memory tasks, with the largest effect in those who scored worst at baseline [crook1991]. That product is no longer marketed (BSE/prion risk from bovine brain tissue), and modern soy-derived PS is chemically similar but not identical (different fatty-acid tails), so the BC-PS result does not automatically transfer. When soy-PS was tested with comparable rigor in 120 elderly adults with AAMI (300 or 600 mg/day, 12 weeks, double-blind, placebo-controlled), there was no significant effect on any of the primary delayed-recall/recognition outcomes and no treatment-by-severity interaction [jorissen2001] -- a direct, well-powered null result from the same research group that later confirmed its safety [jorissen2002]. A smaller, less rigorously controlled Japanese trial (n=78, 50-69y, mild memory complaints, 100-300 mg/day soy-PS, 6 months) found that scores improved in all groups including placebo, with a significant within-group (not clearly between-group) gain confined to subjects who started with low memory scores [katokataoka2010] -- a result vulnerable to regression to the mean. Newer marine-sourced PS esterified with omega-3 fatty acids (PS-DHA, 300 mg/day for 15 weeks) improved verbal immediate recall at the whole-group level in non-demented elderly with memory complaints (n=157), with a post-hoc subgroup of higher-baseline-performers showing broader gains [vakhapova2010]; an open-label extension at a lower maintenance dose (100 mg/day) reported further gains only in PS-naive participants [vakhapova2014]; and the companion safety trial found no adverse safety signal apart from a small resting diastolic blood-pressure reduction and slight weight gain after 30 weeks [vakhapova2011]. These PS-DHA findings are industry-sponsored (Enzymotec), exploratory/post-hoc in nature, and unreplicated by an independent group. For stress/mood, the picture is similarly fragmented: a single-centre trial in young neurotic-prone adults (300 mg/day soy-PS, 1 month) found reduced self-reported stress and better mood only in a neuroticism-defined subgroup [benton2001]; soy lecithin PS/phosphatidic acid (PAS) blunted ACTH/cortisol responses to an experimental stressor at 400 mg/day but not at higher (600-800 mg) or lower doses in a small dose-ranging trial [hellhammer2004]; a follow-up PAS 400 trial again normalized HPA-axis hyper-reactivity only in chronically high-stressed men, with no effect on heart rate or self-reported psychological stress [hellhammer2014]; and an earlier omega-3-PS trial in 60 healthy men found no main effect of supplementation on chronic or acute stress, with benefit confined post hoc to the most highly stressed subgroup [hellhammer2012]. A small (n=16) EEG trial found that 42 days of PS shifted frontal cortical beta power toward a 'more relaxed' pattern after induced mental stress, a neurophysiological biomarker rather than a measured cognitive or affective outcome [baumeister2008]. No placebo-controlled trial of any PS formulation has been identified in healthy young adults, students or knowledge workers for memory, attention or executive function; all positive cognitive trials are in people aged 50 and over with subjective memory complaints or diagnosed impairment. A 2015 narrative review concluded PS 'may' support memory in aging populations but explicitly noted the small sample sizes and methodological heterogeneity across trials [gladesmith2015]; a 2026 systematic review of citicoline/PS combinations in psychiatric and neurological patient populations found inconsistent executive-function benefit that cannot be attributed to PS alone and does not generalize to healthy people [citicolineps2026]. Reproducibility across independent groups is weak: the only large, well-controlled, non-industry soy-PS trial (Jorissen) was null; the positive trials are industry-funded, smaller, and rely on subgroup or post-hoc analyses. Regulators have reached similarly cautious conclusions (see regulatory).", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "PS is synthesized endogenously (via base-exchange from phosphatidylethanolamine/phosphatidylcholine, using serine) and is not an essential nutrient with a defined dietary requirement; there is no described human PS-deficiency syndrome. All positive human cognitive trials have been conducted in people aged 50-80 with subjective memory complaints or diagnosed age-associated memory impairment -- a population in which normal age-related decline, not deficiency, is being targeted -- never in nutritionally replete healthy young adults. The brief's candidate 'enhancement in healthy young adults/students' claim is therefore entirely untested; what little acute/stress evidence exists in younger adults is limited to subgroups defined by trait neuroticism or chronic-stress status [benton2001, hellhammer2012], not unselected students or knowledge workers under normal conditions.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Supplement marketing frequently cites the FDA's 2003 decision as if it were an endorsement ('FDA-approved brain nutrient'); in fact the FDA only permits a qualified claim with the disclaimer that evidence is 'very limited and preliminary' and explicitly found that all ten reviewed intervention studies were seriously flawed or limited, and that there is not significant scientific agreement that PS reduces risk of cognitive dysfunction or dementia. The EU went further: EFSA's 2010 opinion on five submitted PS health-claim dossiers (ID 552, 711, 734, 1632, 1927, covering memory/cognitive function in the elderly, mental performance and stress with memory benefit) concluded that a cause-and-effect relationship between PS consumption and the claimed effects had not been established, and none of these claims appear on the EU authorised list; any Brain Food EU communication must avoid cognitive-function claims for PS entirely. Marketing that extrapolates the 1991 bovine-cortex trial [crook1991] to today's soy- or sunflower-derived products is especially misleading because soy-PS failed in the most rigorous head-to-head soy trial [jorissen2001] and because BC-PS is a different, no-longer-marketed material. The honest position is: a safe, well-characterized phospholipid with weak, inconsistent, mostly industry-sponsored evidence of modest benefit in older adults with memory complaints, no evidence at all in healthy young people, and no authorised cognitive-health claim in the EU or an unqualified one in the US.", "mechanism": {"summary": "PS is a minor anionic phospholipid concentrated in the inner (cytosolic) leaflet of neuronal plasma membranes, where it participates in membrane fluidity, signal-transduction protein anchoring (e.g. protein kinase C) and synaptic vesicle function; it also serves as an 'eat-me' signal on the outer leaflet during apoptosis, unrelated to its proposed cognitive role. The mechanistic hypothesis is that dietary PS crosses into brain phospholipid pools and supports neuronal membrane integrity, acetylcholine release and stress-hormone regulation, but direct human evidence that oral PS measurably changes brain membrane PS content or neurotransmission is minimal.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Oral soy-/sunflower-PS or PS-omega-3 -> intestinal digestion to lyso-PS and glycerophosphoserine, partial resynthesis", "detail": "PS is hydrolyzed by pancreatic phospholipases; absorption of intact PS versus breakdown products in humans is not well characterized by direct isotopic tracer studies in the papers reviewed.", "evidence_level": "plausible"}, {"step": "Absorbed serine/lyso-PS components -> hepatic/systemic recirculation -> uptake across the blood-brain barrier", "detail": "Animal studies show radiolabelled PS components reach brain phospholipid pools; no human brain-PS uptake study (e.g. MRS or isotope tracer) was located in this search.", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Incorporation into neuronal membrane phospholipid pool", "detail": "Proposed to support membrane fluidity and PKC/receptor signalling; not directly measured in any human cognitive trial reviewed here.", "evidence_level": "speculative"}, {"step": "Proposed modulation of acetylcholine release and synaptic function", "detail": "In vitro/animal work links PS to enhanced acetylcholine release and dendritic spine density; human neurochemical confirmation is lacking.", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Proposed blunting of HPA-axis (ACTH/cortisol) reactivity to acute stress", "detail": "Demonstrated as a biomarker effect at specific PAS doses (400 mg) in chronically stressed men [hellhammer2004, hellhammer2014], but not at other doses and not consistently for subjective stress or heart rate.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Measured human cognitive/behavioural outcome", "detail": "Inconsistent: positive in BC-PS AAMI trial [crook1991] and some PS-DHA/soy-PS trials in older adults with memory complaints [vakhapova2010, katokataoka2010]; null in the best-controlled soy-PS AAMI trial [jorissen2001]; untested in healthy young adults.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["neuronal membrane phospholipid composition/fluidity", "protein kinase C and receptor signalling anchoring (proposed)", "acetylcholine release (proposed, animal evidence)", "HPA-axis (ACTH/cortisol) reactivity to acute psychosocial stress (human biomarker evidence, dose-specific)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Adults >50-57y with age-associated memory impairment or subjective memory complaints, not demented", "dose_or_intake": "100-600 mg/day soy- or marine-PS", "duration": "12-26 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Largest well-controlled soy-PS trial (n=120, 300/600 mg/day, 12 weeks) found no significant between-group effect on delayed recall/recognition [jorissen2001]; a smaller Japanese soy-PS trial (n=78, 6 months) found improvement confined to a low-baseline subgroup, vulnerable to regression to the mean [katokataoka2010]; PS-DHA (n=157, 300 mg/day, 15 weeks) improved verbal immediate recall at the group level with broader post-hoc subgroup gains in higher-baseline performers [vakhapova2010]. The original BC-PS trial (now off-market material) was positive [crook1991].", "effect_estimate": "Jorissen 2001: no significant difference, exact values not reported in abstract; Vakhapova 2010: significant group difference in immediate verbal recall, magnitude not reported as standardized effect size in source; Crook 1991: 'improved relative to placebo' on learning/memory tasks, magnitude not quantified in abstract", "certainty_notes": "Downgraded for inconsistency (the best-controlled soy trial is null), imprecision, industry funding of the positive marine-PS trials, and reliance on post-hoc subgroup analyses; single small trials cannot exceed grade B and most of this evidence is smaller/weaker than that.", "key_study_ids": ["jorissen2001", "katokataoka2010", "vakhapova2010", "crook1991"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "No trial identified in unselected healthy adults under 50 or students", "dose_or_intake": "not studied", "duration": "not studied", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No randomized trial of any PS formulation on memory, attention or executive function in healthy young adults or students was found in this search; all cognitive trials enrolled people aged 50+ with memory complaints or impairment.", "effect_estimate": "not reported (no studies)", "certainty_notes": "Absence of evidence, not evidence of absence; cannot be graded above D because there is no human outcome data in this population.", "key_study_ids": ["gladesmith2015"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Young adults, effects seen mainly in trait-high-neuroticism or chronically stressed subgroups", "dose_or_intake": "300-400 mg/day soy-PS or soy-PS/phosphatidic acid complex", "duration": "3-12 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "300 mg/day for 1 month reduced self-reported stress and improved mood only in a high-neuroticism subgroup (n not reported as whole-group significant) [benton2001]; 400 mg PAS blunted ACTH/cortisol response to an acute stressor but 600-800 mg doses did not, and effect on self-reported anxiety was mixed [hellhammer2004]; omega-3-PS (12 weeks, n=60 healthy men) showed no main effect on chronic or acute stress, benefit confined to a post-hoc high-chronic-stress subgroup [hellhammer2012]; a follow-up PAS 400 trial (n=75) again normalized HPA hyper-reactivity only in high-stress men with no effect on subjective stress or heart rate [hellhammer2014].", "effect_estimate": "Hellhammer 2004: ACTH/cortisol/salivary cortisol responses significantly blunted at 400 mg PAS (p values not itemised in abstract); Hellhammer 2014: ACTH p=0.010, salivary cortisol p=0.043, serum cortisol p=0.035 vs placebo, PAS 400 only, high-stress subgroup only", "certainty_notes": "Downgraded for inconsistency across doses, subgroup-only effects, small samples (n=16-75), and reliance on hormonal/EEG biomarkers rather than measured mood or performance outcomes in most trials.", "key_study_ids": ["benton2001", "hellhammer2004", "hellhammer2012", "hellhammer2014", "baumeister2008"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "not studied", "onset": "not applicable", "duration_studied": "no acute single-dose cognitive trial identified", "domain": "other", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "effect_estimate": "not reported (no studies)", "certainty": "none - evidence gap", "safety": "soy-PS considered safe based on chronic-dosing trials; no acute-dose safety signal reported", "study_ids": []}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "12 weeks", "population": "older_adults_healthy (AAMI)", "outcome": "delayed recall/recognition", "effect_estimate": "no significant difference between 300/600 mg/day soy-PS and placebo", "evidence_quality": "moderate RCT, null", "limitations": "primary outcome null; cannot rule out small effect given modest sample (n=120)", "study_ids": ["jorissen2001"]}, {"duration": "15 weeks + 15-week open-label extension", "population": "older_adults_healthy (memory complaints)", "outcome": "verbal immediate/delayed recall, attention", "effect_estimate": "significant improvement in immediate recall at endpoint (full magnitude not reported in abstract); extension showed gains mainly in PS-naive continuers", "evidence_quality": "single industry-funded RCT plus open-label extension; exploratory", "limitations": "post-hoc subgroup emphasis, 9 of 140 randomized excluded for protocol violation, open-label phase unblinded", "study_ids": ["vakhapova2010", "vakhapova2014", "vakhapova2011"]}, {"duration": "6 months", "population": "older_adults_healthy (mild memory complaints, Japan)", "outcome": "delayed verbal recall", "effect_estimate": "significant within-group improvement from baseline in low-score subgroup only; no clear between-group test reported", "evidence_quality": "small RCT with weak statistical reporting", "limitations": "regression-to-the-mean risk, baseline-stratified subgroup analysis, small n=78", "study_ids": ["katokataoka2010"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Soy- and sunflower-derived phosphatidylserine at doses up to 600-800 mg/day for up to 12 weeks (and PS-DHA up to 300 mg/day for 15 weeks plus a 15-week open-label extension at 100 mg/day) has not produced clinically significant adverse biochemical, hematological, cardiovascular or subjective safety signals in the available randomized trials. The original bovine-cortex PS (BC-PS) product is no longer marketed because of BSE/prion contamination risk from bovine brain tissue; modern commercial PS is soy- or sunflower-lecithin-derived and does not carry that risk.", "adverse_effects": ["no significant adverse events versus placebo in RCTs up to 600-800 mg/day for 3-12 weeks [jorissen2002, hellhammer2004]", "small reduction in resting diastolic blood pressure and slight weight gain reported after 30 weeks of PS-DHA open-label use [vakhapova2011], of uncertain clinical significance", "mild gastrointestinal complaints occasionally reported anecdotally for lecithin-derived phospholipids generally, not specifically quantified for PS in these trials"], "contraindications": ["no formal contraindications established in the reviewed trials; standard caution for anyone on antihypertensive or anticoagulant medication given soy-lecithin-derived phospholipids' potential, unconfirmed interactions"], "drug_interactions": ["no drug-interaction studies were identified in this search; theoretical concern with anticholinergic or cholinergic-acting drugs given PS's proposed role in acetylcholine signalling, but not demonstrated in humans"], "upper_limits": "No official tolerable upper intake level (UL) has been set for PS by EFSA, NIH-ODS or ONSSA; trial doses up to 800 mg/day for 3 weeks and 600 mg/day for 12 weeks were tolerated without significant adverse signal, but no long-term (>30 weeks) safety data exist.", "sleep_impact": "No trial reviewed assessed sleep architecture or sleep quality; not studied.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Maximum continuous human exposure identified in this search is 30 weeks (15-week double-blind plus 15-week open-label extension) [vakhapova2011]; no multi-year safety data exist.", "daily_use_appropriate": "probably", "special_populations": "No trials in pregnant or lactating women, children, or people with psychiatric or major chronic disease were identified for cognitive outcomes; soy-derived PS carries a theoretical soy-allergen concern for soy-allergic individuals (sunflower-derived PS avoids this). Bovine-cortex PS should never be used given prion risk and is in any case off-market.", "specialist_review_needed": ["clinical pharmacology/toxicology review for any interaction with anticoagulant, antihypertensive or psychiatric medications", "allergen/regulatory review for soy-derived PS in soy-allergic populations and for Morocco/EU allergen labelling"], "hard_safety_gate": "pass", "gate_reason": "Available randomized trial data up to 800 mg/day for weeks and up to 600 mg/day for 12 weeks, plus 30 weeks of lower-dose PS-DHA use, show no significant safety signal; modern soy/sunflower-derived PS avoids the prion risk of the discontinued bovine-cortex material. Gate is 'pass' for short/medium-term use at studied doses, with the caveat that no long-term (year-scale) human safety data exist."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "EFSA's 2010 scientific opinion (EFSA Journal 8(10):1749) assessed five Article 13(1) health-claim dossiers for PS covering memory/cognitive function in the elderly, mental performance, and stress with memory benefit, and concluded a cause-and-effect relationship had not been established for any of them; none of these claims are on the EU authorised health-claims register. PS is not listed as a novel food (it has a history of food use as a lecithin derivative) but no cognitive-function claim may legally be made in the EU.", "us": "FDA permitted a qualified health claim in 2003 after review of ten intervention studies, concluding there is not significant scientific agreement that PS reduces the risk of cognitive dysfunction or dementia; permitted label language must include a disclaimer such as 'very limited and preliminary scientific research suggests that phosphatidylserine may reduce the risk of cognitive dysfunction in the elderly. FDA concludes that there is very little scientific evidence supporting this claim.' Under DSHEA, PS is marketable as a dietary ingredient with a history of use; any unqualified cognitive-enhancement claim would face FTC substantiation challenges given the mixed/null trial record.", "morocco": "No PS-specific ONSSA or Ministry of Health opinion was identified in this search; Morocco generally follows a food-supplement framework requiring notification and prohibits unauthorised disease/cognitive claims, but the exact status of a PS cognitive claim is uncertain and should be confirmed with local regulatory counsel.", "permitted_claims_examples": [], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "Soy- and sunflower-derived PS have an established history of food use in the EU/US and are not classified as novel foods/require NDI notification in the way newer synthetic ingredients would; no specific novel-food dossier was identified as pending or rejected for PS in this search.", "regulatory_gate": "fail"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "PS is a lipid-soluble phospholipid that can be incorporated into fat-containing foods, bars or capsules; it is not naturally present in meaningful amounts in ordinary whole foods eaten at the needed dose, so a 'food-first' approach cannot deliver 300-600 mg/day PS without supplementation.", "formats": ["softgel/capsule (most common clinical trial format)", "powder blended into bars or beverages (less studied, potential off-flavors)", "liposomal/emulsified liquid formats (marketed but not specifically trial-tested in this search)"], "stability": "Phospholipids are susceptible to oxidation, especially PS-omega-3 forms bearing unsaturated fatty acid tails; requires antioxidant protection (e.g. tocopherols) and controlled storage; soy-PS (saturated/monounsaturated tails) is more oxidatively stable than PS-DHA.", "taste": "Soy lecithin-derived phospholipids can impart a mild bitter/fishy note, more pronounced in PS-DHA/omega-3-esterified forms; typically masked in capsule format, more challenging in food-matrix formats.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Oral soy-PS and sunflower-PS are absorbed via normal lipid digestion (lipase hydrolysis, micellar solubilization, partial resynthesis); clinical trials used 100-800 mg/day achieving measurable biological effects (HPA-axis, EEG) at 300-400 mg/day, suggesting adequate oral bioavailability at those doses, though no formal human pharmacokinetic/bioavailability study was located in this search.", "dose_feasibility": "Effective trial doses (100-600 mg/day) are feasible to deliver via 1-2 softgels or a fortified bar/beverage serving; a food-serving delivery of 300 mg PS is plausible in principle but adds meaningful cost given current soy-PS/sunflower-PS pricing (see cost_evidence).", "cost_availability": "Soy- and sunflower-derived PS are commercially available from established ingredient suppliers; cost per effective daily dose (300 mg) is moderate to high relative to many other brain-food ingredients, and sunflower-PS (non-GMO, allergen-friendlier) commands a premium over soy-PS.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here; no specific supplier price quotes were obtained in this search"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 3, "effect_magnitude": 3, "safety": 7, "reproducibility": 2, "food_suitability": 4, "cost_access": 4, "rationale": "evidence_quality=3: best-controlled soy-PS trial in the target AAMI population is null [jorissen2001]; positive trials are smaller, exploratory, subgroup/post-hoc or industry-linked; no healthy-young-adult data exists at all (grade C/D across outcomes). effect_magnitude=3: where positive, effects are confined to subgroups (high baseline, low baseline, high neuroticism, high chronic stress) with magnitudes often unquantified in available abstracts; whole-sample effects are null or small. safety=7: no significant adverse signal up to 800 mg/day for weeks or 600 mg/day for 12 weeks, and 30 weeks at lower dose, but no long-term (year-scale) data and no interaction studies, so short of a higher score. reproducibility=2: the single most rigorous independent trial (Jorissen) contradicts the industry-funded positive trials (Vakhapova/Kato-Kataoka/Hellhammer), and positive findings cluster among related research groups/sponsors. food_suitability=4: deliverable in capsule or fortified-food form but not naturally present in ordinary diets at effective dose, with oxidative stability challenges for omega-3-esterified forms. cost_access=4: commercially available but costlier than many competing ingredients at the doses studied."}, "research_gaps": ["No trial has tested any PS formulation in healthy young adults, students or knowledge workers for memory, attention or executive function under normal (non-stressed) conditions.", "No independent (non-industry) replication of the PS-DHA or PAS stress/memory findings exists.", "No long-term (>30 week) human safety or efficacy data exist for any PS formulation.", "No drug-interaction studies have been conducted.", "The non-monotonic dose-response reported for PAS (effective at 400 mg but not 600-800 mg) [hellhammer2004] is unexplained and warrants independent confirmation or refutation.", "No study has directly compared soy-PS, sunflower-PS and PS-omega-3 formulations head-to-head for cognitive or stress outcomes."], "_derived": {"composite_score": 3.9, "eligibility": "ineligible", "safety_gate": "pass", "regulatory_gate": "fail", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": null, "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": null, "best_grade_overall": null, "benefit_domains": {}, "n_outcome_rows": 3, "n_studies": 14, "n_verified": 14, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 6, "n_systematic_reviews": 2, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["inconsistent", "insufficient"]}}, {"id": "acetyl-l-carnitine", "name": "Acetyl-L-carnitine", "category": "E", "category_name": "Amino acids and related compounds", "aliases": ["ALCAR", "ALC", "acetylcarnitine", "L-acetylcarnitine", "levacecarnine"], "food_sources": ["red meat and organ meats (small amounts of acetyl-L-carnitine; most dietary carnitine is the free form, acetylated endogenously)", "fish and poultry (modest amounts)", "dairy products (trace)", "essentially absent from plant foods; endogenous synthesis from lysine and methionine in liver/kidney/brain supplies the rest", "not a standard food ingredient at the doses studied (500-3000 mg/day); doses this large are supplement/pharmaceutical, not food-realistic"], "active_constituents": ["acetyl-L-carnitine (acetylated derivative of L-carnitine; crosses the blood-brain barrier more readily than free carnitine)"], "one_line_verdict": "Decades of trials in Alzheimer's disease and hepatic encephalopathy show, at best, inconsistent and probably chance-level benefits on objective cognition, and the one Cochrane review that specifically asked about healthy, cognitively normal adults found only two small, poorly reported trials with no evidence of benefit.", "scientific_conclusion": "Acetyl-L-carnitine (ALCAR) has been studied for roughly 40 years, almost entirely in clinical populations rather than healthy adults. The most relevant systematic review for Brain Food is Cochrane's 2017 update, 'L-carnitine for cognitive enhancement in people without cognitive impairment' [chen2017cochrane], which found only two eligible randomised trials in cognitively healthy people: a small 18-person crossover and a ~200-person three-day trial testing reaction time, vigilance, immediate and delayed recall. Neither showed any effect, both were judged very-low-certainty (unclear risk of bias, short treatment, poor reporting), and the reviewers explicitly state they could draw no conclusion about efficacy or safety in healthy adults. No RCT has tested ALCAR in students, knowledge workers, or acutely for a single-dose 'nootropic' effect; this is a genuine evidence gap, not a null result. In dementia, the original 2003 Cochrane review pooling 11 placebo-controlled trials (all restricted to Alzheimer's disease) [hudson2003cochrane] found a statistically significant benefit on clinician-rated Clinical Global Impression at 12 and 24 weeks (OR 2.33, 95% CI 1.25-4.35 and OR 3.91, 95% CI 1.32-11.54) but no benefit on any objective, continuous measure of cognition, dementia severity, or functional ability, and the reviewers themselves flagged that with the large number of comparisons tested the positive CGI result could be due to chance; they concluded there was no evidence to recommend routine use. A 2020 narrative 'critical update' covering newer mechanistic and small clinical work in dementia and other cognitive disorders reached a similarly guarded conclusion, describing the evidence as suggestive of adjunct benefit in specific subgroups (vascular cognitive impairment, depression-associated cognitive symptoms) but not establishing disease-modifying efficacy [pennisi2020]. ALCAR has also been tested in hepatic encephalopathy, where a 2019 Cochrane review of five Italian-only, high-risk-of-bias trials (398 participants, all with cirrhosis) found a biomarker effect (lower blood ammonia, MD -13.06 mg/dL) but no clear effect on quality of life, fatigue, or adverse events, rated very-low-certainty on every outcome [marticarvajal2019cochrane]; this population (cirrhotic, encephalopathic) cannot be generalised to healthy adults and is included here only to show the full honesty of the evidence base and the mechanistic ammonia-lowering rationale. A small randomised trial in pre-frail older adults (n=92, 1.5 g twice daily for 3 months) reported improved MMSE scores and gait speed alongside reduced CRP versus placebo [malaguarnera2022], but this is a single, modestly sized, unregistered-seeming trial in a frail geriatric population with a crude global cognitive screen (MMSE) rather than a validated neuropsychological battery, and has not been independently replicated; it cannot be generalised to healthy young adults. Across all populations, the signature ALCAR adverse effect is a dose-related fishy body/breath odour from trimethylamine metabolism (TMAO pathway), reported in multiple trials and case reports, which is a practical barrier to any food or beverage application. In summary: no convincing human evidence exists for cognitive enhancement in healthy, adequately nourished adults (the Brain Food target population); the strongest clinical trials are in Alzheimer's disease and show at most a subjective clinician-rated effect without objective cognitive benefit; and there is no acute/single-dose human data at all, in any population.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "ALCAR is not an essential nutrient and there is no recognised 'deficiency state' in a general healthy population analogous to, say, B12 deficiency; endogenous synthesis plus modest dietary carnitine intake is adequate in the overwhelming majority of adults. The evidence base is dominated by disease contexts in which endogenous carnitine/acetylcarnitine metabolism is disturbed (hepatic encephalopathy, Alzheimer's disease, diabetic neuropathy, chronic fatigue syndromes) and ALCAR is tested as a pharmacological-dose intervention (500-3000 mg/day), not as correction of a dietary shortfall. None of this evidence licenses an 'enhancement' claim for cognitively healthy, adequately nourished adults: the single Cochrane review addressing that exact population [chen2017cochrane] found no effect in the only two trials available, both low-quality and short (3 days or unspecified duration, small n).", "marketing_vs_evidence": "ALCAR is commonly marketed as a 'nootropic' that 'boosts memory, mental clarity and energy' in healthy adults, often citing the large body of Alzheimer's-disease literature or rodent mitochondrial mechanism studies as if they applied to healthy brains. This is not supportable: the Alzheimer's trials show inconsistent, largely subjective (CGI) rather than objective cognitive benefit, and the one review that actually asked the healthy-adult question found zero qualifying evidence of benefit, rated very-low-certainty. Any claim of acute 'focus' or 'clarity' in a rested, non-deficient young adult has no RCT support whatsoever (no single-dose or short-term healthy-adult trial exists). The honest position for Brain Food is that ALCAR is a well-characterised ingredient with a long but inconclusive clinical-population history and essentially no evidence in the target (healthy, cognitively normal, non-elderly) population; a cognition claim in that population would not currently survive EFSA or FTC substantiation review.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Acetyl-L-carnitine is absorbed intestinally and, because it is more lipophilic than free L-carnitine, crosses the blood-brain barrier more readily via carnitine transporters (OCTN2 and others). In the brain it can donate its acetyl group to acetyl-CoA pools (supporting acetylcholine synthesis and mitochondrial beta-oxidation) and has been proposed to have antioxidant and membrane-stabilising properties. These are plausible mechanisms consistent with its historical rationale as a cholinergic/mitochondrial support agent in dementia, but mechanism has not translated into a consistent, objectively measured cognitive benefit in human trials, in either clinical or healthy populations.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Oral ALCAR -> intestinal absorption via carnitine transporters -> plasma acetylcarnitine rise", "detail": "Pharmacokinetic comparison trials confirm oral bioavailability and dose-dependent plasma rise in healthy Korean volunteers [korean2015pk], establishing human absorption but not a cognitive effect.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Plasma acetylcarnitine -> blood-brain barrier crossing via carnitine transporters -> increased brain acetylcarnitine pool", "detail": "Supported mechanistically by lipophilicity arguments and animal brain-uptake studies; direct human brain-level quantification (e.g., MRS) specific to cognitive outcome trials is sparse in the literature retrieved here.", "evidence_level": "plausible"}, {"step": "Brain acetylcarnitine -> donation of acetyl groups to acetyl-CoA pool -> acetylcholine synthesis support and mitochondrial beta-oxidation", "detail": "Proposed cholinergic and bioenergetic mechanism underlying the historical Alzheimer's rationale; demonstrated in animal/in vitro models, not directly measured in the human cognition trials reviewed.", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Ammonia-detoxifying / antioxidant action in hepatic and neuronal tissue", "detail": "Cochrane review of hepatic encephalopathy trials found a clear biomarker effect (lower blood ammonia, MD -13.06 mg/dL, 95% CI -17.24 to -8.99) across 5 trials/387 participants [marticarvajal2019cochrane], establishing a human biomarker effect in a diseased population only.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Measured human cognitive/clinical outcome", "detail": "In Alzheimer's disease, significant benefit only on subjective clinician-rated CGI, not on objective cognitive or functional measures [hudson2003cochrane]; in healthy adults, no effect in either available trial [chen2017cochrane]; mechanism does not establish efficacy.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["carnitine transporter (OCTN2)-mediated absorption and blood-brain barrier crossing", "acetyl-CoA / acetylcholine precursor supply", "mitochondrial beta-oxidation support", "ammonia detoxification (hepatic encephalopathy)", "antioxidant/membrane-stabilising action (largely animal/in vitro)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "short_term_days", "domain": "reaction_time", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Cognitively healthy adults of any age in the two Cochrane-eligible trials (one crossover n=18, one parallel ~200-person trial mostly young adults)", "dose_or_intake": "not clearly reported in abstract (Cochrane review notes poor reporting)", "duration": "3 days (one trial); unspecified (crossover trial)", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "Cochrane review of L-carnitine/ALCAR for cognitive enhancement in people without cognitive impairment identified only 2 eligible RCTs; no evidence of effect on reaction time, vigilance, immediate or delayed recall after 3 days; both trials judged very-low-certainty due to unclear risk of bias and poor reporting.", "effect_estimate": "not reported (insufficient data extracted by review authors)", "certainty_notes": "Downgraded for very serious risk of bias, imprecision (very small samples, poor reporting) and short duration; review authors explicitly state no conclusion about efficacy or safety can be drawn.", "key_study_ids": ["chen2017cochrane"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Alzheimer's disease patients (11 pooled RCTs, all mild-moderate AD)", "dose_or_intake": "typically 1.5-3 g/day", "duration": "12-52 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Significant benefit on clinician-rated Clinical Global Impression at 12 weeks (OR 2.33, 95% CI 1.25-4.35) and 24 weeks (OR 3.91, 95% CI 1.32-11.54) but not at 52 weeks; no significant benefit on any objective cognition, dementia severity, or functional ability measure across the pooled trials.", "effect_estimate": "OR 2.33 (1.25-4.35) at 12wk; OR 3.91 (1.32-11.54) at 24wk, CGI only; no significant effect sizes reported for objective cognitive scales", "certainty_notes": "Downgraded for risk of multiplicity (many outcomes tested, reviewers note the significant CGI result may be due to chance), heterogeneity in instruments across the 11 trials, and reliance on completer rather than intention-to-treat analysis in most trials. This is a dementia population; not generalisable to healthy adults.", "key_study_ids": ["hudson2003cochrane"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Older adults with pre-frailty (not dementia), community-dwelling", "dose_or_intake": "1.5 g twice daily (3 g/day)", "duration": "3 months treatment + 3 months follow-up", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Single RCT (n=92) found improved MMSE score (p<0.001) and 6-minute walking distance (p<0.001) and reduced CRP in the ALCAR group versus placebo in pre-frail older adults.", "effect_estimate": "MMSE p<0.001; walking distance p<0.001; exact mean differences and CIs not reported in abstract", "certainty_notes": "Single small trial, crude global cognitive screen (MMSE) rather than a validated neuropsychological battery, geriatric frailty population, no independent replication found; downgraded for imprecision, indirectness to the Brain Food target population, and risk of bias (unclear blinding details in abstract).", "key_study_ids": ["malaguarnera2022"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_decline", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Cirrhotic patients with covert or overt hepatic encephalopathy", "dose_or_intake": "not uniformly reported; oral route most frequent", "duration": "variable across 5 pooled trials", "grade": "D", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Cochrane review of 5 Italian single-team trials (398 participants, all high risk of bias) found reduced blood ammonia (biomarker) but no clear effect on quality of life, fatigue, mortality, or serious adverse events; all outcomes rated very-low-certainty.", "effect_estimate": "Ammonia MD -13.06 mg/dL (95% CI -17.24 to -8.99); SF-36 general health MD -6.20 (95% CI -9.51 to -2.89, favouring placebo, uncertain); fatigue not significant", "certainty_notes": "High risk of bias in all 5 trials, single research team, very low quality evidence on every outcome per GRADE; a biomarker (ammonia) improvement does not establish clinical cognitive benefit; not generalisable beyond cirrhotic hepatic encephalopathy patients.", "key_study_ids": ["marticarvajal2019cochrane"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "not studied", "onset": "not applicable", "duration_studied": "no acute/single-dose human cognitive trial identified in healthy adults", "domain": "other", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty": "no data", "safety": "no acute safety data in healthy adults specific to a single cognitive-testing dose", "study_ids": []}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "12-52 weeks", "population": "clinical_population", "outcome": "Clinical Global Impression and objective cognition in Alzheimer's disease", "effect_estimate": "CGI OR 2.33-3.91 significant at 12/24wk, not 52wk; objective cognition not significant", "evidence_quality": "low (Cochrane 2003, multiplicity concerns)", "limitations": "Multiple outcomes tested, risk of chance finding on CGI, completer analyses, heterogeneous instruments, dementia-only population", "study_ids": ["hudson2003cochrane"]}, {"duration": "3 days to unspecified short crossover", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "outcome": "reaction time, vigilance, immediate/delayed recall", "effect_estimate": "no effect detected", "evidence_quality": "very low (Cochrane 2017, 2 small trials)", "limitations": "Only 2 eligible trials, unclear risk of bias, poor outcome reporting, very short duration", "study_ids": ["chen2017cochrane"]}], "safety": {"summary": "ALCAR is generally well tolerated at studied doses (0.5-3 g/day) across clinical trials, with the most consistent adverse effect being a dose-related fishy body/breath odour from trimethylamine (TMAO pathway) metabolism. Gastrointestinal upset (nausea, diarrhea) and restlessness/agitation have been reported in some dementia trials. Long-term (multi-year) safety data in healthy adults are lacking since almost all long-duration trials are in clinical (dementia, hepatic) populations.", "adverse_effects": ["fishy body/breath odour (TMAO-related, dose-dependent)", "gastrointestinal upset (nausea, diarrhea) in some dementia trials", "restlessness or agitation reported in a minority of dementia-trial participants", "no statistically significant excess of adverse events vs placebo in the pooled Cochrane dementia meta-analysis, but reporting was inconsistent across trials"], "contraindications": ["seizure disorders (isolated case reports of seizures associated with carnitine/ALCAR supplementation in vulnerable individuals, not systematically studied)", "no established contraindication in the general healthy adult population at studied doses"], "drug_interactions": ["theoretical interaction with anticoagulants (warfarin) via carnitine's effects on vitamin K-dependent pathways, flagged in pharmacology references rather than demonstrated in dedicated interaction trials retrieved here", "no major interactions established in the systematic reviews reviewed"], "upper_limits": "No official tolerable upper intake level (UL) has been set by EFSA, NIH-ODS, or similar bodies for ALCAR specifically; clinical trial doses up to 3 g/day for up to a year have not shown excess serious adverse events in pooled dementia data, but this is not the same as an established safe upper limit for lifelong daily food use in healthy adults.", "sleep_impact": "Not directly studied in the trials retrieved for this monograph; no signal of stimulant-like sleep disruption reported, but dedicated sleep-outcome trials were not identified.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Longest trials found are up to 52 weeks, exclusively in Alzheimer's disease or hepatic encephalopathy populations; no multi-year safety data in healthy adults.", "daily_use_appropriate": "uncertain", "special_populations": "Pregnancy and lactation: insufficient safety data identified in this search; not recommended without specialist guidance. Children/adolescents: a separate ADHD trial exists but is outside this monograph's scope and should not be used to infer safety or efficacy in healthy adults. Liver disease patients: studied directly (hepatic encephalopathy trials) with biomarker but not clear clinical benefit; medication users (anticoagulants): theoretical interaction flagged, specialist review advised.", "specialist_review_needed": ["clinical pharmacologist for anticoagulant/seizure-threshold interaction review", "hepatologist if any hepatic-encephalopathy-adjacent claim is ever considered (it should not be, for a consumer food product)", "regulatory toxicologist to assess whether a formal upper intake level needs deriving for a food-format product"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "No major safety signal at studied doses in the populations tested, but long-term safety data in healthy adults are absent, a sensory defect (fishy odour) limits food formulation, and a few case reports of seizures in vulnerable individuals warrant a cautious, conditional rather than unconditional pass."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Acetyl-L-carnitine is authorised for use as a nutrient source in food supplements under EU law (listed in the food supplements directive's nutrient annexes in some member states), but no EFSA Article 13.1 health claim for cognitive function has been authorised for L-carnitine or acetyl-L-carnitine; EFSA has reviewed L-carnitine-related claims in the past and non-authorisation is the default status pending specific verification of the current claims register entry.", "us": "Marketed as a dietary ingredient under DSHEA; acetyl-L-carnitine has a history of use predating DSHEA (1994) and is widely sold, but any structure/function cognitive claim requires company-held competent and reliable scientific evidence for FTC purposes, which the evidence reviewed here (a Cochrane review finding no effect in healthy adults) would not support.", "morocco": "No specific ONSSA or Ministry of Health authorisation record identified in this search; status uncertain and would need direct verification with ONSSA's food supplement registration lists before any product claim or import filing.", "permitted_claims_examples": [], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "Acetyl-L-carnitine is not classified as a novel food in the EU (it has a history of consumption in supplements); in the US it would likely be treated as a pre-DSHEA ingredient for NDI purposes, but this should be confirmed with a regulatory affairs specialist before filing.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Poor to moderate: studied effective/clinical doses (1.5-3 g/day) are far above what fits in a palatable food serving, and the characteristic fishy odour from TMAO metabolism is a direct sensory barrier to beverage or bar formats at meaningful doses.", "formats": ["capsule/tablet (standard supplement format)", "powder (odour-masking challenges)", "not well suited to beverages or bars at efficacious doses due to odour and dose size"], "stability": "Acetyl-L-carnitine is a stable, water-soluble salt commonly supplied as ALCAR HCl; no major stability concerns identified for standard supplement formats in the literature reviewed.", "taste": "Reports of a mild bitter/metallic taste in powder form in general industry literature; the more clinically relevant issue is the fishy body odour that develops post-ingestion rather than taste of the product itself.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Oral bioavailability of ALCAR is established in pharmacokinetic studies (e.g., [korean2015pk]), but a food matrix's effect on absorption (fed vs fasted state, co-ingredients) was not specifically studied in the trials retrieved here.", "dose_feasibility": "The clinically tested dose range (1.5-3 g/day) does not fit comfortably into a single ordinary food serving without a dedicated, discrete supplement-style format; this limits Brain Food's ability to deliver an efficacious dose through a conventional food product even if efficacy were established.", "cost_availability": "not researched here", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 2, "effect_magnitude": 1, "safety": 6, "reproducibility": 2, "food_suitability": 2, "cost_access": 5, "rationale": "evidence_quality=2: the single review directly addressing the target population (healthy adults, chen2017cochrane) found no effect with very-low-certainty evidence; the Alzheimer's evidence (grade C at best) is not generalisable. effect_magnitude=1: no reliable effect size exists for healthy adults; dementia CGI effect may be chance. safety=6: no major signal at studied doses but long-term healthy-adult data and a seizure case-report signal keep this below a clean pass. reproducibility=2: the only 2 healthy-adult trials are small, old, and poorly reported; dementia CGI finding not confirmed on objective measures across trials. food_suitability=2: fishy odour and gram-level dosing make food-format delivery impractical. cost_access=5: widely available as a commodity supplement ingredient, though cost/price data were not specifically researched in this unit."}, "research_gaps": ["No RCT has tested acute (single-dose) ALCAR on cognition in healthy, rested young adults or students under exam/cognitive load.", "No large, well-reported, modern RCT exists in cognitively healthy adults; the Cochrane review's 2 eligible trials are old, small, and poorly documented.", "No dose-ranging study has established a minimum effective dose if any true cognitive effect exists in healthy adults.", "No independent replication of the pre-frailty MMSE/walking-distance finding [malaguarnera2022] with a validated neuropsychological battery.", "No systematic evaluation of the fishy-odour sensory barrier in a palatable food-format delivery system.", "No dedicated human drug-interaction trial (e.g., anticoagulants) specific to ALCAR at supplement doses."], "_derived": {"composite_score": 2.9, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": null, "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"global_cognition": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 4, "n_studies": 6, "n_verified": 6, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 0, "n_systematic_reviews": 3, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["inconsistent", "insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "alpha-gpc", "name": "Alpha-GPC (choline alfoscerate)", "category": "E", "category_name": "Amino acids and related compounds", "aliases": ["alpha-GPC", "L-alpha-glycerylphosphorylcholine", "L-alpha-GPC", "glycerophosphocholine (GPC)", "choline alfoscerate", "choline alphoscerate", "Gliatilin (medicinal brand, Italy)", "AlphaSize, GeniusPure (ingredient brands)"], "food_sources": ["Present naturally in small amounts in milk and dairy, organ meats and other foods as a phosphatidylcholine metabolite (quantities not researched here); commercial alpha-GPC is made from soy lecithin (chemical/enzymatic deacylation) or synthetically"], "active_constituents": ["L-alpha-glycerylphosphorylcholine (about 40.5% choline by mass: 82.5 mg choline per 203.7 mg, per EFSA 2026)", "metabolites: free choline, glycerophosphate; choline-derived TMA/TMAO"], "one_line_verdict": "Alpha-GPC raises plasma choline and has small, inconsistent cognitive data (one 20-person acute Stroop result in young men; marginal or conflicting results in MCI; older positive Alzheimer trials), while a 12-million-person Korean cohort linked its use to a 43% higher stroke risk and the EU has not yet authorised it as food, so it fails Brain Food's risk-benefit test for healthy consumers.", "scientific_conclusion": "What it is. L-alpha-glycerylphosphorylcholine (alpha-GPC, choline alfoscerate) is a water-soluble phosphatidylcholine breakdown product that is about 40% choline by mass (82.5 mg choline per 203.7 mg [efsa2026]). It has been a medicine for cognitive impairment since 1985 in some countries (prescription drug in Korea) and is a 'nootropic' or pre-workout supplement in the US. Healthy young adults. A single 315-630 mg dose improved Stroop performance (d 0.48-0.61) but not Flanker or N-Back in 20 trained men, in a retrospectively registered trial whose author is a paid advisor to the supplier [kerksick2024]. Seven days of 250-500 mg did not change psychomotor vigilance in 48 young men and unexpectedly lowered TSH at 500 mg [marcus2017]. A single-blind 2-week study reported only a night-time 'motivation' rating [tamura2021]. Growth-hormone and strength findings (n = 8 and 13) are small, inconsistent and not cognitive [kawamura2012, bellar2015]. Two registered trials in healthy volunteers have no results. Grade C for acute executive function, D for chronic cognition in young adults. Mild cognitive impairment. A 12-week Korean RCT (600 mg/day) found ADAS-cog -2.34 vs -0.97 points (p = 0.048) with 26% of participants excluded from analysis [jeon2024]; the 1-year CONIVaD trial in small-vessel disease was null [salvadori2021]; a nationwide cohort reported about 10-17% lower dementia conversion [kim2025] but a propensity-matched multicentre cohort found none [pyun2024]. Grade C, inconsistent. Dementia. One 2003 RCT in 261 Alzheimer patients reported a ~6-point ADAS-Cog advantage at 1200 mg/day [dejesusmoreno2003]; meta-analyses from a group closely associated with the compound show modest add-on effects with donepezil (MD 1.72) [sagaro2023] and no memory advantage over citicoline [sagaro2025]; older analyses are narrative [parnetti2001]. These are patient data and cannot be extrapolated to healthy people. Safety. In 12 million Korean adults aged 50 or more, prescribed alpha-GPC was associated with a 43% higher 10-year stroke risk (matched aHR 1.43, 1.41-1.46) with a duration-response gradient [lee2021]; confounding by indication is likely, an MCI cohort suggested the opposite in non-progressors [kim2025], and kidney cancer was not associated [park2026], but alpha-GPC raised TMAO and atherosclerosis in mice [wang2021]. EFSA judged soy-derived alpha-GPC safe only at up to 203.7 mg/day in supplements [efsa2026], a dose below every cognitive trial; its EU authorisation was still a draft in 2026.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Alpha-GPC is a choline source (about 40% choline), so at 600-1200 mg/day it delivers roughly 245-490 mg choline, comparable to the whole adult Adequate Intake; any benefit in people with low choline intake would be equally achievable with food (eggs) or cheaper choline forms (see dietary-choline). Its marketed role is enhancement in replete people, and here the evidence is one small acute trial with a task-specific effect [kerksick2024] and a null 7-day vigilance trial [marcus2017]. The positive data concern impaired older adults and dementia patients [dejesusmoreno2003, jeon2024], a different population with cholinergic deficits; they must not be generalized to students or knowledge workers.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Supplements and pre-workouts claim alpha-GPC 'boosts focus, memory, power output and growth hormone'. The evidence: one sponsor-linked 20-person acute trial with a Stroop-only effect [kerksick2024], a null vigilance trial [marcus2017], inconsistent growth-hormone and strength studies [kawamura2012, bellar2015], and patient trials in dementia [dejesusmoreno2003]. Marketing generally omits the Korean stroke signal [lee2021]. In the EU there is no authorised claim and, as of 2026-10-25, no confirmed novel-food authorisation; the pending authorisation would cap use at 203.7 mg/day [efsa2026], below the doses in any cognitive trial, so a lawful EU product could not credibly promise cognitive effects. In Korea the drug's reimbursement was restricted in 2020 for lack of clinical evidence outside dementia (trade press). In the US, structure/function claims are possible but FTC substantiation would be weak. Honest position: not recommended for a Brain Food product aimed at healthy people.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Oral alpha-GPC is hydrolysed to choline and glycerophosphate, raising plasma choline; choline can support acetylcholine and phosphatidylcholine synthesis. Aged-rat studies report effects on hippocampal cholinergic markers and muscarinic receptors (titles surfaced in searches; not reviewed here). Part of the choline load is converted by gut microbes to TMA and hepatic FMO3 to TMAO, which in mice promotes atherosclerosis. Human data are limited to plasma choline, endocrine markers and small cognitive trials.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Oral alpha-GPC -> hydrolysis -> free choline + glycerophosphate -> plasma choline", "detail": "Serum free choline +59% to +132% after 250-500 mg [marcus2017]; plasma choline up at 60-120 min after 1000 mg [kawamura2012].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Plasma choline -> brain uptake -> acetylcholine synthesis/release and phospholipid synthesis", "detail": "Aged-rat studies on hippocampal cholinergic markers and receptors exist (not reviewed here); brain acetylcholine not measured in humans taking alpha-GPC.", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Cholinergic modulation of pituitary -> growth hormone release", "detail": "GH rose after 1000 mg in 8 men [kawamura2012] but not after 315-630 mg in 20 men [kerksick2024].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Choline -> gut microbial TMA -> hepatic TMAO -> endothelial inflammation and atherosclerosis", "detail": "Demonstrated in Apoe-/- mice and human endothelial cells [wang2021]; proposed to explain the human stroke association [lee2021]; TMAO not measured in humans taking alpha-GPC in the studies retrieved.", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Measured human cognitive outcome", "detail": "Acute Stroop gain in young men [kerksick2024]; null vigilance [marcus2017]; marginal ADAS-cog gain in MCI [jeon2024]; larger effects in an old AD trial [dejesusmoreno2003].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["acetylcholine precursor supply", "membrane phosphatidylcholine synthesis", "cholinergic stimulation of growth hormone release (proposed)", "gut-microbial TMA/TMAO pathway (safety)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Resistance-trained men, mean 31 y (n=20)", "dose_or_intake": "315 or 630 mg single dose", "duration": "Single dose, tested 60 min post-dose", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Stroop total score improved vs placebo (d 0.48-0.61) and Stroop time faster at 630 mg; Flanker and N-Back unchanged; no mood effects.", "effect_estimate": "Stroop d=0.61 (630 mg), d=0.48 (315 mg) [kerksick2024]", "certainty_notes": "Single small sponsor-linked crossover, retrospectively registered, one of three tasks positive without multiplicity correction; cannot exceed C.", "key_study_ids": ["kerksick2024"]}, {"timeframe": "short_term_days", "domain": "vigilance", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "College-aged men (n=48, 12 per arm)", "dose_or_intake": "250 or 500 mg/day", "duration": "7 days", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "No difference in psychomotor vigilance vs placebo or caffeine arms; unexplained TSH suppression at 500 mg/day.", "effect_estimate": "PVT no difference [marcus2017]", "certainty_notes": "Small arms (imprecision); vigilance only; but the only multi-day cognitive measure in young adults is null.", "key_study_ids": ["marcus2017"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy Japanese volunteers (n=39)", "dose_or_intake": "Dose not reported in abstract", "duration": "2 weeks", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "Single-blind study: a 'tendency' to higher self-rated motivation, significant only for night-time ratings; no anxiety effect.", "effect_estimate": "Night-time motivation p<0.05 [tamura2021]", "certainty_notes": "Single-blind, subjective, multiple repeated measures; exploratory only; not cognition.", "key_study_ids": ["tamura2021"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "other", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy young men: growth hormone and fat oxidation (n=8; n=20)", "dose_or_intake": "630-1000 mg single dose", "duration": "Single dose", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Growth hormone and ketone rise after 1000 mg in 8 men; no growth-hormone effect after 315-630 mg in 20 trained men.", "effect_estimate": "GH increase at 60 min [kawamura2012]; no GH difference [kerksick2024]", "certainty_notes": "Tiny trials, inconsistent; endocrine marker, not cognition or performance.", "key_study_ids": ["kawamura2012", "kerksick2024"]}, {"timeframe": "short_term_days", "domain": "other", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "College-aged men: muscle force and power", "dose_or_intake": "250-600 mg/day", "duration": "6-7 days", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Lower-body isometric force increased with 600 mg (p=0.044, n=13); in n=48 no isometric strength change but jump velocity/power differed (best at 250 mg).", "effect_estimate": "Mid-thigh pull +98.8 vs -39.0 N [bellar2015]; IMTP/UBIST NS [marcus2017]", "certainty_notes": "Small, inconsistent, marginal p-values; physical outcome relevant only to sports positioning.", "key_study_ids": ["bellar2015", "marcus2017"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults 18-45, students, knowledge workers", "dose_or_intake": "Any", "duration": ">=2 weeks", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No chronic cognitive trial in healthy young adults was found; registered trials NCT03903250 (n=18, reaction time) and NCT02886130 (n=28, volleyball players) are completed without posted results or identified publications.", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "Absence of evidence plus unpublished registered trials (publication bias risk).", "key_study_ids": []}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_mci", "population_detail": "Adults with amnestic MCI or vascular cognitive impairment; real-world MCI cohorts", "dose_or_intake": "600-1200 mg/day", "duration": "12 weeks to 12 months (RCTs); years (cohorts)", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "12-week RCT: ADAS-cog -2.34 vs -0.97 (p=0.048, 26% excluded); 1-year CONIVaD RCT null; nationwide Korean cohort shows ~10-17% lower dementia conversion, but a multicentre CDM cohort was null.", "effect_estimate": "Difference ~1.4 ADAS-cog points (d ~0.47, completers) [jeon2024]; AD conversion HR 0.899 (0.882-0.918) [kim2025]; HR 0.93 (0.59-1.26) [pyun2024]", "certainty_notes": "RCTs small with attrition and borderline p; observational analyses conflict and are prone to healthy-user bias; downgraded for risk of bias, inconsistency, imprecision.", "key_study_ids": ["jeon2024", "salvadori2021", "kim2025", "pyun2024"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Patients with mild-moderate Alzheimer's disease or vascular/mixed dementia", "dose_or_intake": "1200 mg/day (oral), alone or with donepezil", "duration": "90-180 days and longer", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "One multicentre RCT (n=261) reported a ~6-point ADAS-Cog difference at 180 days; meta-analysis: add-on to donepezil MD 1.72 (0.20-3.25); vs citicoline better on SCAG but not memory; claims cohort shows unclear effect on progression.", "effect_estimate": "ADAS-Cog -3.20 vs +2.90 [dejesusmoreno2003]; MD 1.72 (0.20-3.25) [sagaro2023]", "certainty_notes": "Old, single positive monotherapy trial with unclear sponsorship; investigator overlap in meta-analyses; never replicated in modern AD trials; patient population only.", "key_study_ids": ["dejesusmoreno2003", "sagaro2023", "sagaro2025", "parnetti2001", "duong2024"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "vascular_health", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Korean adults >=50 y without prior stroke or Alzheimer disease (n=12 million); MCI cohort", "dose_or_intake": "Prescribed alpha-GPC (daily doses not reported in the abstract; exposure graded by prescription duration)", "duration": "Up to ~10 y follow-up", "grade": "C", "direction": "harm", "effect_summary": "Use was associated with higher 10-year stroke risk (matched aHR 1.43) with dose-response by duration; a separate MCI cohort reported lower stroke risk in non-progressors; mouse data show GPC-driven TMAO and atherosclerosis; kidney cancer not associated.", "effect_estimate": "Total stroke aHR 1.43 (1.41-1.46) [lee2021]; kidney cancer aHR 0.95 (0.84-1.08) [park2026]", "certainty_notes": "Observational with confounding by indication and conflicting cohort data; but very large, dose-responsive and biologically plausible. Low certainty of harm, yet sufficient to trigger a precautionary safety gate.", "key_study_ids": ["lee2021", "kim2025", "wang2021", "park2026"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "315 mg and 630 mg", "onset": "60 min", "duration_studied": "Single dose", "domain": "Stroop (executive function); Flanker; N-Back", "population": "resistance-trained men, mean 31 y (n=20)", "effect_estimate": "Stroop d 0.48-0.61; Flanker/N-Back NS", "certainty": "C", "safety": "Hemodynamics within normal ranges", "study_ids": ["kerksick2024"]}, {"dose": "1000 mg", "onset": "60-120 min", "duration_studied": "Single dose", "domain": "growth hormone, fat oxidation (endocrine)", "population": "healthy young men (n=8)", "effect_estimate": "GH up at 60 min; FFA/ketones up at 120 min", "certainty": "C (biomarker, not replicated)", "safety": "Not reported", "study_ids": ["kawamura2012"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "6-7 days", "population": "College-aged men", "outcome": "Psychomotor vigilance; muscle force/power", "effect_estimate": "PVT null; small inconsistent force/power effects; TSH down at 500 mg", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "Small arms; physical outcomes", "study_ids": ["marcus2017", "bellar2015"]}, {"duration": "2 weeks", "population": "Healthy volunteers", "outcome": "Self-rated motivation", "effect_estimate": "Night-time motivation p<0.05 only", "evidence_quality": "D", "limitations": "Single-blind, subjective", "study_ids": ["tamura2021"]}, {"duration": "12 weeks", "population": "Amnestic MCI", "outcome": "ADAS-cog", "effect_estimate": "-2.34 vs -0.97 (p=0.048)", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "26% excluded; baseline imbalance", "study_ids": ["jeon2024"]}, {"duration": "12 months", "population": "Small vessel disease with cognitive impairment", "outcome": "MoCA decline", "effect_estimate": "Null", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "Pilot; poor nimodipine adherence", "study_ids": ["salvadori2021"]}, {"duration": "180 days", "population": "Mild-moderate Alzheimer's disease", "outcome": "ADAS-Cog, MMSE, CGI", "effect_estimate": "ADAS-Cog difference ~6 points", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "Single old trial; unclear sponsorship", "study_ids": ["dejesusmoreno2003", "sagaro2023"]}, {"duration": "~10 y", "population": "Korean adults >=50 y", "outcome": "Stroke (safety)", "effect_estimate": "aHR 1.43 (1.41-1.46), dose-response", "evidence_quality": "C (observational)", "limitations": "Confounding by indication; conflicting MCI cohort", "study_ids": ["lee2021", "kim2025"]}, {"duration": "Years (claims cohorts)", "population": "Korean adults with MCI", "outcome": "Dementia conversion", "effect_estimate": "HR 0.899 (nationwide) vs HR 0.93 NS (multicentre CDM)", "evidence_quality": "C (observational)", "limitations": "Healthy-user bias; inconsistent", "study_ids": ["kim2025", "pyun2024"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Short-term trials report few adverse events, but a nationwide Korean cohort found a dose-responsive 43% higher 10-year stroke risk among users aged 50+ (observational, confounding by indication possible), and mouse data show GPC-derived TMAO promotes atherosclerosis. A 7-day trial found TSH suppression at 500 mg/day. EFSA found no concern only at 203.7 mg/day.", "adverse_effects": ["observational association with ischaemic and haemorrhagic stroke (aHR 1.34-1.43) [lee2021]", "TSH suppression at 500 mg/day over 7 days (biomarker) [marcus2017]", "non-serious adverse reactions leading to dropout in 4/62 patients over 1 year [salvadori2021]", "no excess adverse events vs placebo over 12 weeks in MCI [jeon2024]"], "contraindications": ["history of stroke, TIA or high cardiovascular risk (precautionary, given lee2021)", "children (not studied for cognition; EU proposal covers >3 y only at 203.7 mg/day)", "pregnancy at doses above the EFSA-assessed 203.7 mg/day", "trimethylaminuria (choline load)"], "drug_interactions": ["cholinesterase inhibitors (donepezil): additive cholinergic effects, co-administered in trials [sagaro2023]", "anticholinergic drugs: opposing effect (theoretical)", "antithrombotic/antihypertensive users: no interaction data; caution given stroke signal (not studied)"], "upper_limits": "No UL. EFSA assessed 203.7 mg/day (82.5 mg choline) as safe in supplements [efsa2026]; cognitive trials used 315-1200 mg/day (about 130-490 mg choline), which counts toward the 3.5 g/day choline UL but is far below it.", "sleep_impact": "No data found.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Up to 12 months in RCTs (patients); prescription cohorts up to ~10 years (observational).", "daily_use_appropriate": "uncertain", "special_populations": "Older adults (the stroke signal population) and people with cardiovascular risk need medical review; no data in pregnancy at cognitive doses; children not studied.", "specialist_review_needed": ["cardiologist/stroke neurologist (stroke signal, TMAO)", "endocrinologist (TSH finding)", "regulatory affairs (EU novel food status; medicinal status in Italy/Korea)"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "Not a clear fail because the stroke association is observational and conflicting, but until resolved Brain Food should not use alpha-GPC at cognitive-trial doses; any use limited to adults, <=203.7 mg/day (EU-assessed dose), excluding people with cardiovascular risk."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Novel food. EFSA (May 2026) concluded that soy-derived L-alpha-GPC (Chemi S.p.A.) is safe at up to 203.7 mg/day in food supplements for people >3 y [efsa2026]; a Commission draft implementing regulation adding it to the Union list with five years of data protection for Chemi was under national consultation on 30 June 2026 (Danish hearing portal). Adoption was not confirmed as of 2026-10-25, so placing alpha-GPC on the EU food market is not currently lawful to our knowledge (recheck EUR-Lex). Choline alfoscerate is marketed as a medicinal product in some countries (marketing authorisation since 1985 per sagaro2023; member-state status, e.g., Italy, not verified here), creating borderline-classification risk. No health claim exists.", "us": "Widely sold in dietary supplements. Chemi Nutra's AlphaSize reportedly received an FDA 'no questions' letter on its GRAS notice (GRN 000419, 2013) for conventional foods (trade press; FDA inventory entry not checked); another notifier's L-alpha-GPC GRAS notice (GRN 001141) ceased at the notifier's request. NDI status for supplements not verified. Claims need FTC-level substantiation.", "morocco": "Uncertain: no Moroccan classification (medicine vs supplement) or registration information was found; confirmation with ONSSA/AMMPS required.", "permitted_claims_examples": [], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "EU: not yet authorised (draft with 5-year data protection for Chemi; max 203.7 mg/day in supplements). Korea: prescription drug; HIRA moved it to selective reimbursement in 2020 (80% copay outside dementia) and MFDS ordered clinical re-evaluation; litigation kept prior criteria in place per 2024 trade press (status in 2026 unverified).", "regulatory_gate": "fail"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Low. EU use would be restricted (when authorised) to food supplements at <=203.7 mg/day under Chemi data protection; in the US it could be used in foods under the reported GRAS notice.", "formats": ["capsules/tablets", "powder (e.g., Alpha Size 100P) or 85% viscous liquid", "pre-workout and 'nootropic' drink mixes (US)"], "stability": "Hygroscopic; supplied as 85% liquid or stabilised powder (EFSA 2026 product description); an HPLC intrinsic-stability study exists but was not reviewed.", "taste": "Not researched here.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Raises plasma choline efficiently (+59-132% at 250-500 mg); matrix effects not studied.", "dose_feasibility": "Physically easy (300-600 mg fits any format) but legally constrained in the EU to 203.7 mg/day, below the 315-1200 mg used in cognitive trials.", "cost_availability": "Commodity supplement ingredient in the US; not lawfully available as food in the EU at present.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 2.5, "effect_magnitude": 3, "safety": 3.5, "reproducibility": 2, "food_suitability": 3, "cost_access": 4, "rationale": "Evidence quality 2.5: grade C at best for healthy young adults (one small acute sponsor-linked trial) and D for chronic use; patient evidence C. Effect magnitude 3: d about 0.5 on one task acutely, marginal ADAS-cog change in MCI. Safety 3.5: observational stroke signal with dose-response plus TMAO mechanism, TSH change, limited long-term data in healthy people. Reproducibility 2: single-trial findings, conflicting cohorts, unpublished registered trials. Food suitability 3: EU not authorised and future cap below effective doses. Cost/access 4: available in the US, not in the EU food market."}, "research_gaps": ["Pre-registered, independent RCT in healthy young adults with multiplicity-controlled cognitive outcomes", "Publication of registered trials NCT03903250 and NCT02886130", "Human TMAO and platelet-reactivity response to alpha-GPC at 200-1200 mg/day", "Active-comparator or negative-control-exposure analyses of the Korean stroke signal (e.g., vs citicoline or donepezil users)", "Replication of the 2003 Alzheimer trial with modern methods", "EU authorisation outcome and Moroccan classification"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 2.95, "eligibility": "ineligible", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "fail", "best_grade_acute": "C", "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": "C", "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": null, "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"executive_function": "C", "global_cognition": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 9, "n_studies": 18, "n_verified": 18, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 8, "n_systematic_reviews": 2, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["harm", "inconsistent", "insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "citicoline-cdp-choline", "name": "Citicoline (CDP-choline)", "category": "E", "category_name": "Amino acids and related compounds", "aliases": ["citicoline", "CDP-choline", "cytidine 5'-diphosphocholine", "cytidine diphosphate choline", "citicoline sodium", "citicoline inner salt", "Cognizin (Kyowa brand)", "Somazina (Ferrer, medicinal product in Spain)", "Ceraxon, Recognan (medicinal brands in other markets)"], "food_sources": ["Endogenous metabolite present in all cells (Kennedy pathway intermediate); not a meaningful dietary component - intake from foods is negligible and supplemental citicoline is manufactured (fermentation/synthesis)"], "active_constituents": ["citicoline (CDP-choline), hydrolysed in gut and liver to choline (about 20% of citicoline by mass, agent calculation from molecular weights) and cytidine (converted to uridine)"], "one_line_verdict": "Citicoline is safe at 250-500 mg/day in adults and nudges brain phospholipid biomarkers, but in healthy young adults there is no convincing cognitive benefit (small, manufacturer-linked, often unpublished trials), in older adults the pivotal trial missed its primary outcome, large patient trials were null, and the EU has refused its memory claim.", "scientific_conclusion": "What it is. Citicoline (CDP-choline) is an endogenous intermediate of phosphatidylcholine synthesis; after ingestion it is hydrolysed to choline and cytidine (converted to uridine) and resynthesised in tissues. 500 mg supplies only about 100 mg of choline, so effects beyond choline must come from the cytidine/uridine moiety or membrane synthesis. Biomarkers. Small, mostly uncontrolled MRS studies show brain phosphodiesters +7.3% after 6 weeks at 500 mg [babb2002] and frontal phosphocreatine +7% and ATP +14% in 16 adults without placebo, with manufacturer co-authors [silveri2008]; a single dose raises brain choline in young but lowers it in older adults [babb1996]. These are not cognitive outcomes. Healthy young adults and students. Single 500-1000 mg doses improved CogState domains only in low baseline performers and worsened them in high performers (n = 24) [knott2015, knott2014]; a citicoline-caffeine drink has no caffeine-only arm [bruce2014]. Over 28 days, 250-500 mg improved attention (p = 0.02) and motor speed (p = 0.03) in 75 adolescent males [mcglade2019] and reportedly reduced CPT-II errors in 60 women aged 40-60 [mcglade2012]; both were co-authored by the manufacturer. At least five registered healthy-adult trials (n = 61-148, completed 2018-2025) have no results posted or publication found, a serious publication-bias concern; grade D for chronic effects in young adults. Healthy older adults with memory complaints. The pivotal manufacturer-funded RCT (n = 100, 500 mg/day, 12 weeks) missed its primary outcome (Spatial Span) but improved secondary episodic memory (P = 0.0025) and composite memory (3.78 vs 0.72 points, about 0.3 baseline SD) [nakazaki2021]; a 3-month 1000 mg/day trial was null except in a post-hoc subgroup [spiers1996]; a 24-person 4-week study was positive on free recall [alvarez1997]. EFSA found no established cause-and-effect relationship [efsa2024] and the EU refused the memory claim in 2025. Grade C, inconsistent. Patients. Cochrane found benefit on memory and behaviour but not attention in 14 short, heterogeneous trials, with a reviewer who had run included studies [fioravanti2005]; a 2023 meta-analysis reported SMDs of 0.56-1.57 from poor-quality studies biased toward citicoline [bonvicini2023]; open studies suggest slower vascular or post-stroke decline [cotroneo2013, alvarezsabin2013]. The two large, well-conducted RCTs were null: ICTUS in 2298 stroke patients (OR 1.03, 0.86-1.25) [davalos2012] and COBRIT in 1213 TBI patients, including cognitive scales (OR 0.98, 0.83-1.15) [zafonte2012]. Manufacturer-authored reviews present a more favourable picture [secades2022]. Safety is good: adverse events did not differ from placebo at 2 g/day for 6 weeks in 2298 stroke patients [davalos2012], but children should be excluded [subasiturgut2025].", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Citicoline is not a nutrient and there is no citicoline deficiency; it supplies only about 100 mg choline per 500 mg, so it is not an efficient way to correct low choline intake (eggs and other foods do this, see dietary-choline). Any effect is therefore pharmacological enhancement. The pattern of the evidence suggests state dependence: single doses helped low baseline performers and impaired high performers [knott2015]; positive older-adult findings come from people with memory complaints or post-hoc 'inefficient memory' subgroups [nakazaki2021, spiers1996]; the clearest benefits are reported in impaired patients, in low-quality studies [fioravanti2005, bonvicini2023]. For adequately nourished, well-performing young adults and students, enhancement is not demonstrated and could be negative at the top of the performance range.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Marketing claims ('clinically shown to support focus, attention, memory, brain energy') rest on small manufacturer-co-authored trials [mcglade2012, mcglade2019], on secondary outcomes of a single 500 mg/day trial in older adults [nakazaki2021], and on uncontrolled MRS biomarkers [silveri2008]. EFSA concluded that a cause-and-effect relationship with memory is not established [efsa2024] and Commission Regulation (EU) 2025/2223 (4 November 2025) refused the claim; no citicoline health claim is authorised in the EU. Consumer products often contain 125-250 mg (X7a competitor audit), below the 500 mg dose of the pivotal trial, and combination drinks with caffeine owe their acute effects largely to caffeine [bruce2014]. Several completed registered trials in healthy adults have not reported results, so the published literature is likely biased toward positive findings. In the US a structure/function statement with the DSHEA disclaimer is legally possible, but 'clinically proven' language would be hard to defend under FTC standards. Honest positioning: 'a well-tolerated phospholipid precursor studied mainly in older adults and patients, with no proven benefit for healthy students or workers'.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Oral citicoline is hydrolysed to cytidine and choline, which are absorbed, cross the blood-brain barrier and are reassembled into CDP-choline and phosphatidylcholine; cytidine is converted to uridine, itself a precursor for membrane synthesis. Proposed actions are membrane phospholipid synthesis and repair, acetylcholine precursor supply, and modulation of dopaminergic and alpha-7 nicotinic signalling. Human evidence is limited to MRS biomarkers and EEG gating changes; animal neuroprotection did not translate into benefit in large stroke and TBI trials.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Oral citicoline -> intestinal/hepatic hydrolysis -> plasma choline and cytidine (uridine)", "detail": "Plasma choline rises after citicoline [spiers1996]; 500 mg provides about 100 mg choline (agent calculation).", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Choline and cytidine -> brain uptake -> CDP-choline -> phosphatidylcholine synthesis", "detail": "Brain phosphodiesters +7.3% after 6 weeks [babb2002]; single dose raises brain choline in young, lowers it in older adults [babb1996].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Membrane turnover -> altered frontal energy metabolism", "detail": "PCr +7%, ATP +14% in an uncontrolled 16-person study [silveri2008].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Choline as alpha-7 nicotinic agonist / acetylcholine precursor -> sensory gating", "detail": "P50 gating improved acutely only in low gaters [knott2014].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Neuroprotection after ischaemia or trauma (membrane stabilisation, reduced free fatty acid release)", "detail": "Supported in animal models; not confirmed in ICTUS or COBRIT [davalos2012, zafonte2012].", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Measured human cognitive outcome", "detail": "Inconsistent: secondary memory gains in older adults with complaints [nakazaki2021]; baseline-dependent acute effects in healthy young adults [knott2015]; null cognitive composite in TBI [zafonte2012].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["Kennedy (CDP-choline) pathway of phosphatidylcholine synthesis", "uridine-mediated membrane and synapse formation", "acetylcholine precursor supply", "alpha-7 nicotinic receptor agonism by choline", "proposed dopaminergic modulation (largely preclinical)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy volunteers (n=24 each; Ottawa), stratified by baseline cognition or P50 gating", "dose_or_intake": "500 or 1000 mg single dose", "duration": "Single dose", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Improvements in processing speed, working memory and executive function only in low baseline performers; no effect in medium performers; worse performance in high performers; tentative maze-learning gain in low gaters.", "effect_estimate": "Standardized effects not reported [knott2015, knott2014]", "certainty_notes": "Two small trials from one group, post-hoc stratification, schizophrenia-surrogate framing; net effect in an unselected healthy group is not demonstrated.", "key_study_ids": ["knott2015", "knott2014"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults (n=60) given a citicoline-caffeine beverage", "dose_or_intake": "Citicoline + caffeine (doses not in abstract)", "duration": "Single administration", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "Combination drink improved reaction time, go/no-go accuracy and P450 amplitude, but without caffeine-only or citicoline-only arms the effect cannot be attributed to citicoline.", "effect_estimate": "not reported [bruce2014]", "certainty_notes": "Confounded by caffeine (grade A acute attention effects in the caffeine monograph); uninformative for citicoline alone.", "key_study_ids": ["bruce2014"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy women aged 40-60 y", "dose_or_intake": "250 or 500 mg/day Cognizin", "duration": "28 days", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Fewer CPT-II commission errors with both doses vs placebo, greatest at 500 mg (from press/manufacturer summaries; full paper not read).", "effect_estimate": "not reported [mcglade2012]", "certainty_notes": "Single small manufacturer-co-authored trial in a non-PubMed journal, effect sizes unavailable; high risk of bias; not replicated.", "key_study_ids": ["mcglade2012"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "children_adolescents", "population_detail": "Healthy adolescent males", "dose_or_intake": "250 or 500 mg/day Cognizin", "duration": "28 days", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Improved selective attention (p=0.02) and finger-tap motor speed (p=0.03); higher mg/kg dose predicted accuracy and lower impulsivity.", "effect_estimate": "p=0.01-0.03; standardized effects not reported [mcglade2019]", "certainty_notes": "Single manufacturer-co-authored trial, unbalanced arms, multiple outcomes; EU novel-food use excludes children; one case report of psychiatric adverse events in a child.", "key_study_ids": ["mcglade2019", "subasiturgut2025"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults 18-45, students, knowledge workers", "dose_or_intake": "250-500 mg/day", "duration": ">=2 weeks", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No published chronic trial in healthy young adults was found. At least five registered trials in healthy adults (NCT05000190 n=104; NCT04967157 n=148; NCT06906848 n=90 Cognizin; NCT03444662 n=70; NCT02705066 n=61; completed 2018-2025) have no results posted and no publication identified.", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "Absence of published evidence plus a substantial unpublished registered literature: high risk of publication bias.", "key_study_ids": []}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Healthy adults 50-85 y with age-associated memory impairment or 'inefficient' memory", "dose_or_intake": "500-2000 mg/day", "duration": "4 weeks to 3 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Pivotal 12-week trial (n=100, 500 mg) missed its primary outcome but improved secondary episodic and composite memory; 3-month 1000 mg/day trial null overall with positive post-hoc subgroup and 2000 mg crossover; tiny 4-week trial positive on free recall. EFSA: cause and effect not established.", "effect_estimate": "Composite memory +3.1 points (~0.3 SD), primary NS [nakazaki2021]; overall NS at 1 g/day [spiers1996]", "certainty_notes": "Downgraded for risk of bias (secondary/post-hoc outcomes), inconsistency, single manufacturer-funded positive RCT, and publication bias; consistent with EFSA 2024.", "key_study_ids": ["nakazaki2021", "spiers1996", "alvarez1997", "efsa2024"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_mci", "population_detail": "Older adults with MCI, mild vascular cognitive impairment or mild-moderate dementia", "dose_or_intake": "500-2000 mg/day (oral or parenteral)", "duration": "20 days to 12 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Cochrane: memory and behaviour benefit, no attention benefit, short trials; 2023 meta-analysis SMD 0.56-1.57 but poor-quality studies biased toward citicoline; open IDEALE study: MMSE stable vs decline in non-randomized controls.", "effect_estimate": "SMD 0.56 (0.37-0.75) to 1.57 (0.77-2.37) [bonvicini2023]", "certainty_notes": "Serious risk of bias (open-label, non-randomized), inconsistency, publication bias, reviewer COI; patient population only.", "key_study_ids": ["fioravanti2005", "bonvicini2023", "cotroneo2013", "sagaro2025"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Patients after first-ever ischaemic stroke", "dose_or_intake": "1 g/day", "duration": "12 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Open-label RCT: better attention-executive function and temporal orientation at 12 months; functional outcome not significantly different.", "effect_estimate": "Attention-executive OR 2.379 (1.269-4.462) [alvarezsabin2013]", "certainty_notes": "Open-label, no placebo, 43% without 1-year testing; clinical population only.", "key_study_ids": ["alvarezsabin2013"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "other", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Acute moderate-severe ischaemic stroke (ICTUS) and traumatic brain injury (COBRIT): functional and cognitive recovery", "dose_or_intake": "2 g/day", "duration": "6 weeks (stroke) or 90 days (TBI)", "grade": "A", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Two large, well-conducted placebo-controlled RCTs were null: global recovery OR 1.03 in 2298 stroke patients (stopped for futility) and global functional/cognitive OR 0.98 in 1213 TBI patients.", "effect_estimate": "OR 1.03 (0.86-1.25) [davalos2012]; OR 0.98 (0.83-1.15) [zafonte2012]", "certainty_notes": "Low risk of bias, adequate power, consistent; high certainty of no clinically important benefit in acute brain injury. Clinical population: informs neuroprotection claims, not healthy users.", "key_study_ids": ["davalos2012", "zafonte2012", "secades2022"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "neuroplasticity_biomarkers", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Healthy middle-aged and older adults (MRS studies)", "dose_or_intake": "500-2000 mg/day (or single dose)", "duration": "Single dose to 12 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Brain phosphodiesters +7.3% after 6 weeks; frontal PCr +7% and ATP +14% (uncontrolled, n=16); single dose raises brain choline in young but lowers it in older adults. Biomarkers, not cognitive outcomes.", "effect_estimate": "Phosphodiesters +7.3% (P=0.008) [babb2002]; PCr/Pi +32% [silveri2008]", "certainty_notes": "Small, mostly uncontrolled, partly manufacturer-co-authored; biomarker relevance to cognition unproven.", "key_study_ids": ["babb2002", "silveri2008", "babb1996"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "500 mg and 1000 mg", "onset": "Within hours (testing post-dose)", "duration_studied": "Single dose", "domain": "processing speed, working memory, executive function", "population": "healthy males (n=24), stratified by baseline performance", "effect_estimate": "Gains in low performers, losses in high performers", "certainty": "C (inconsistent)", "safety": "Not reported", "study_ids": ["knott2015"]}, {"dose": "500 mg and 1000 mg", "onset": "Within hours", "duration_studied": "Single dose", "domain": "P50 sensory gating (EEG biomarker); maze learning", "population": "healthy volunteers (n=24)", "effect_estimate": "Improved gating in low gaters only", "certainty": "C (biomarker)", "safety": "Not reported", "study_ids": ["knott2014"]}, {"dose": "Citicoline + caffeine beverage (doses not in abstract)", "onset": "Acute", "duration_studied": "Single administration", "domain": "attention, reaction time, P450", "population": "healthy adults (n=60)", "effect_estimate": "Improved vs placebo; not attributable to citicoline", "certainty": "D", "safety": "Not reported", "study_ids": ["bruce2014"]}, {"dose": "Single oral dose", "onset": "3 h", "duration_studied": "Single dose", "domain": "brain choline (MRS biomarker)", "population": "healthy younger vs older adults", "effect_estimate": "+18% young, -6% older (P=0.028)", "certainty": "C (biomarker)", "safety": "Not reported", "study_ids": ["babb1996"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "28 days", "population": "Healthy women 40-60 y", "outcome": "Attention (CPT-II)", "effect_estimate": "Fewer commission errors (magnitude not available)", "evidence_quality": "C (single small industry trial, secondary source)", "limitations": "Non-indexed journal; manufacturer co-authors", "study_ids": ["mcglade2012"]}, {"duration": "28 days", "population": "Healthy adolescent males", "outcome": "Attention, motor speed", "effect_estimate": "p=0.02 and p=0.03 vs placebo", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "Unbalanced arms; industry", "study_ids": ["mcglade2019"]}, {"duration": "12 weeks", "population": "Healthy 50-85 y with AAMI", "outcome": "Spatial span (primary), episodic and composite memory", "effect_estimate": "Primary NS; composite +3.1 points (~0.3 SD)", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "Secondary outcomes; manufacturer-funded", "study_ids": ["nakazaki2021"]}, {"duration": "3 months (+2-month crossover)", "population": "Healthy 50-85 y", "outcome": "Logical memory", "effect_estimate": "Null overall; post-hoc subgroup positive", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "Post-hoc subgroup", "study_ids": ["spiers1996"]}, {"duration": "20 days to 12 months", "population": "Elderly with MCI/vascular impairment/dementia", "outcome": "Memory, behaviour, global cognition", "effect_estimate": "SMD 0.56-1.57 (poor-quality studies)", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "Open-label, short, biased", "study_ids": ["fioravanti2005", "bonvicini2023", "cotroneo2013"]}, {"duration": "6 weeks to 180 days", "population": "Acute stroke and TBI patients", "outcome": "Functional and cognitive recovery", "effect_estimate": "OR 1.03 and 0.98 (null)", "evidence_quality": "A (no benefit)", "limitations": "Clinical populations", "study_ids": ["davalos2012", "zafonte2012"]}, {"duration": "6-12 weeks", "population": "Healthy middle-aged/older adults", "outcome": "Brain phospholipid and energy metabolites (MRS)", "effect_estimate": "Phosphodiesters +7.3%; PCr +7%, ATP +14%", "evidence_quality": "C (biomarker)", "limitations": "Uncontrolled, small", "study_ids": ["babb2002", "silveri2008"]}, {"duration": "8 weeks", "population": "Cocaine-dependent adults", "outcome": "Sleep (actigraphy)", "effect_estimate": "No change", "evidence_quality": "C (indirect)", "limitations": "Indirect population", "study_ids": ["bracken2011"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Well tolerated: in ICTUS (2298 patients, 2 g/day for 6 weeks) adverse-event rates did not differ from placebo; the 12-week 500 mg trial reported only mild GI effects and headache. Long-term data in healthy people are limited to 12 weeks (12 months in patients). A paediatric case report of agitation and suicidality with positive rechallenge (with phosphatidylserine and methylphenidate) supports excluding children.", "adverse_effects": ["mild GI effects (flatulence, burping), increased appetite, headache [nakazaki2021]", "decrease in systolic blood pressure in elderly [alvarez1997]", "agitation, irritability and suicidality in a 7-year-old on methylphenidate (case report, combination product) [subasiturgut2025]", "no excess adverse events vs placebo at 2 g/day in acute stroke [davalos2012]"], "contraindications": ["children and adolescents (EU novel-food conditions state not for children)", "pregnancy and lactation (no safety data found)", "people on stimulant medication (case report; caution)"], "drug_interactions": ["No formal interaction studies retrieved; check national medicinal-product SmPCs (e.g., for levodopa) - not verified here", "possible additive effects with cholinergic drugs (theoretical)"], "upper_limits": "No UL. EU novel-food authorisation caps food-supplement use at 500 mg/day (FSMP 250 mg per serving). Choline delivered (about 20% by mass) counts toward the 3.5 g/day choline UL but is far below it.", "sleep_impact": "No sleep disruption on actigraphy over 8 weeks in cocaine-dependent adults [bracken2011]; no data in healthy adults.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Up to 12 weeks in healthy older adults; up to 12 months in patients (open studies).", "daily_use_appropriate": "probably", "special_populations": "Not for children (EU condition); no pregnancy data; older adults studied up to 85 y; psychiatric patients and stimulant users need clinician review.", "specialist_review_needed": ["neurologist or clinical pharmacologist (medicinal-product status and interactions)", "child psychiatrist if any product could reach minors", "regulatory affairs (borderline medicine/supplement)"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "Acceptable for healthy adults at <=500 mg/day under EU novel-food conditions; exclude children, pregnancy and stimulant/psychiatric medication users pending data."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Authorised novel food (Commission Implementing Decision 2014/423/EU, now in the Union list): food supplements max 500 mg/day and foods for special medical purposes 250 mg per serving; labelling that it is not intended for children (per X4 regulatory synthesis; EUR-Lex text not re-read here). It is not authorised for general foods or beverages. The Article 13(5) memory claim (applicant Egde Pharma) received a negative EFSA opinion [efsa2024] and was refused by Commission Regulation (EU) 2025/2223 of 4 November 2025 (applies from 25 November 2025). Citicoline is also an authorised medicinal product in several member states (e.g., Spain; in France only an injectable), so national authorities may treat high-dose or claim-bearing products as medicines (borderline risk).", "us": "Marketed as a dietary ingredient in supplements (Cognizin). Kyowa self-affirmed GRAS for food uses up to 250 mg per serving in 2009 (trade press); no FDA GRAS notice 'no questions' letter for citicoline was found. NDI status not verified. Structure/function claims require the DSHEA disclaimer and FTC-level substantiation; 'clinically proven' claims would be high risk.", "morocco": "Uncertain. No Moroccan registration or classification of citicoline (medicine vs food supplement) was found; supplement registration and classification follow the framework described in the X6 synthesis (historic DMP registration, AMMPS since 2025). Requires confirmation with ONSSA/AMMPS before any launch.", "permitted_claims_examples": [], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "EU: authorised novel food restricted to supplements (<=500 mg/day) and FSMP; use in conventional foods would need a new novel-food application. US: self-affirmed GRAS; NDI status for supplements not verified.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Low in the EU: authorisation limited to food supplements and FSMP, so a citicoline 'brain food' bar or drink is not lawful in the EU without a new authorisation; possible in the US under self-affirmed GRAS (<=250 mg per serving).", "formats": ["capsules and tablets", "powder sticks or shots (supplement format)", "beverages (US only, under GRAS conditions)"], "stability": "Citicoline sodium is a water-soluble solid; stability in food matrices not researched here.", "taste": "Not researched here.", "bioavailability_matrix": "High oral bioavailability as choline and cytidine; matrix effects not studied.", "dose_feasibility": "The 250-500 mg/day doses studied fit easily into a capsule or small drink; 1-2 g/day doses used in patients are feasible in capsules but exceed the EU supplement cap.", "cost_availability": "Commercially available (branded and generic); prices not compared here.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 3, "effect_magnitude": 2.5, "safety": 7.5, "reproducibility": 2, "food_suitability": 3.5, "cost_access": 5, "rationale": "Evidence quality 3: best rows for consumer-relevant outcomes are grade C (older adults with memory complaints; small adolescent/women trials) and D for chronic effects in healthy young adults; the only grade A row is a null result in acute brain injury. Effect magnitude 2.5: about 0.3 SD on secondary memory outcomes, baseline-dependent acute effects. Safety 7.5: good tolerability up to 2 g/day in large trials; minus for children case report and limited long-term data in healthy people. Reproducibility 2: positive trials concentrated in manufacturer-linked groups; several registered trials unpublished; large independent trials null. Food suitability 3.5: EU authorisation only for supplements/FSMP. Cost/access 5: available but more expensive than food choline (prices not researched)."}, "research_gaps": ["Publication of the completed registered healthy-adult trials (NCT05000190, NCT04967157, NCT06906848, NCT03444662, NCT02705066)", "Independent, pre-registered RCT in healthy young adults/students with attention and memory primary outcomes at 250-500 mg/day for >=8 weeks", "Replication of the Nakazaki 2021 memory result with episodic memory as primary outcome", "Head-to-head comparison with equimolar choline (eggs or choline salt) to isolate a cytidine/uridine effect", "Safety in pregnancy, adolescents and stimulant users", "Moroccan regulatory classification"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 3.95, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": "C", "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"sustained_attention": "C", "global_cognition": "C", "executive_function": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 10, "n_studies": 23, "n_verified": 23, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 6, "n_systematic_reviews": 4, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["inconsistent", "insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "creatine", "name": "Creatine monohydrate", "category": "E", "category_name": "Amino acids and related compounds", "aliases": ["creatine", "creatine monohydrate", "CrM", "N-(aminoiminomethyl)-N-methylglycine", "Creapure (brand)", "micronised creatine monohydrate"], "food_sources": ["red meat (approx. 3-5 g creatine per kg raw beef; cooking degrades part of it to creatinine)", "fish (herring, salmon, tuna)", "poultry", "trace amounts in milk", "essentially absent from plant foods; vegetarians and vegans rely on endogenous synthesis (approx. 1 g/day from arginine, glycine and methionine)"], "active_constituents": ["creatine (free base); phosphorylated in tissue to phosphocreatine, the cytosolic high-energy phosphate buffer for ATP resynthesis via creatine kinase"], "one_line_verdict": "Creatine monohydrate reliably raises brain creatine by roughly 5-10% and partially protects cognition during acute sleep deprivation or hypoxia, but in rested, adequately nourished young adults the best trial shows at most a trivial-to-small effect (d about 0.1-0.2) on working memory and none on reasoning, and the EU has formally refused a cognitive-function claim.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. Oral creatine monohydrate is absorbed almost completely and, at 20 g/day for 1-4 weeks, increases total brain creatine measured by 1H-MRS by approximately 5-10% with large inter-individual variability (8.7% average, range 3.5-13.3%, in [dechent1999]; 9.2% in [turner2015]), an effect that is reversible after withdrawal. This biomarker effect is established in humans. Whether the extra brain creatine changes measurable cognition depends strongly on the state of the brain being tested. Under acute metabolic stress the evidence is the most consistent: during 21 h of sleep deprivation a single high dose (0.35 g/kg, about 25 g) improved processing speed on several tasks and preserved cerebral PCr/Pi, ATP and pH versus placebo in a 15-person double-blind crossover [gordjinejad2024]; a lower single dose (0.2 g/kg) in 29 participants reduced sleep-deprivation decrements in logic, numeric and psychomotor vigilance tasks by up to 12% [gordjinejad2026]; and 7 days of 20 g/day attenuated the deterioration in random movement generation, choice reaction time, balance and mood at 24 h of sleep loss [mcmorris2006], with a weaker, task-restricted effect at 36 h [mcmorris2007sd]. Under 90 min of 10% oxygen, 7 days of loading restored complex attention (p = 0.049, about 21% difference) and raised corticomotor excitability [turner2015]. These are small, mostly single-laboratory studies with p-values that often did not survive multiplicity correction, so they merit grade B at best (sleep-deprived, acute) and C (hypoxia). In whom, and how large, in the Brain Food target population. In rested healthy young adults the picture is different. The largest and only preregistered trial (n = 123, crossover, 5 g/day for 6 weeks) found a borderline effect on backward digit span (p = 0.064, d = 0.17, about 0.2 digits) and no effect on Raven's matrices (p = 0.327, d = 0.09), no effect on eight exploratory tasks, no extra benefit for vegetarians, and strong Bayesian evidence against an effect as large as the one reported by Rae [sandkuhler2023]. Two six-week parallel trials in young adults were null at 2.2 g/day [rawson2008] and at 10 or 20 g/day [moriarty2023], as was a 7-day 20 g/day trial in 40 active young adults [silva2026]. The famous vegetarian trial (n = 45, 5 g/day, 6 weeks) reported large improvements in digit span and Raven's matrices [rae2003], and a 5-day 20 g/day trial in 128 young women found better memory only in vegetarians [benton2011], but neither finding has been replicated and the Sandkühler trial directly contradicts the diet interaction. Meta-analytically, memory improves by SMD 0.29 (95% CI 0.04-0.53, I2 66%) overall but by only SMD 0.03 (95% CI -0.14 to 0.20) in 11-31-year-olds [prokopidis2023], a result whose statistics were challenged for double-counting [eckert2023]; a broader meta-analysis mixing healthy and clinical populations reports memory SMD 0.31 (0.18-0.44), processing-speed SMD -0.51 (-1.01 to -0.01) and no effect on global cognition or executive function [xu2024]. An independent systematic review concluded the cognitive results are equivocal despite the brain-creatine increase [mcmorris2024]. How reproducible. Positive findings cluster in two groups (Chichester/McMorris; Jülich/Gordji-Nejad) and among authors with creatine-industry ties (Candow, Forbes, Ostojic, Kreider); the largest independent, preregistered trial is essentially null. Older adults may respond more (SMD 0.88, two small trials, I2 83% [prokopidis2023]; positive 1-week trial [mcmorris2007elderly] versus a null 24-week trial [alves2013]). What is unknown. No trial has tested students under examination load, knowledge workers, or a single dose in rested people; no trial has titrated dose to measured brain creatine; the practical relevance of a 0.2-digit gain in span is negligible, and the EU regulator, after reviewing 21 intervention studies, concluded that no cause-and-effect relationship is established [efsa2024].", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Creatine is not an essential nutrient: healthy omnivores obtain 1-2 g/day from meat and fish and synthesise about 1 g/day; vegetarians and vegans have lower muscle and plasma creatine but brain creatine is largely maintained by local synthesis. The best evidence for benefit is therefore not 'deficiency correction' in the classical sense but acute state-dependent support when cerebral energy demand outstrips supply (sleep deprivation, hypoxia) [gordjinejad2024, gordjinejad2026, turner2015, mcmorris2006]. The hypothesis that low-intake groups (vegetarians) respond more is supported by one small trial [rae2003] and one 5-day trial [benton2011] but refuted by the largest trial, in which vegetarians did not benefit more than omnivores and Bayes factors favoured the null [sandkuhler2023]. For rested, adequately nourished young adults the enhancement effect is at most trivial (d 0.1-0.2). Older adults are a plausible responder group but the two trials disagree [mcmorris2007elderly, alves2013]. Children do not raise brain creatine with 7 days of supplementation and show no cognitive change [meregefilho2017]; no inference to adults should be drawn from that trial and none from adults to children.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Marketing claims that creatine 'boosts brain power', 'improves memory', 'increases IQ' or is 'the nootropic for vegetarians' rest on one 2003 trial of 45 vegetarians and on meta-analyses that pool older adults, clinical populations and sleep-deprived volunteers with healthy young adults. The evidence supports only a narrower statement: creatine raises brain creatine modestly and can blunt the cognitive cost of acute sleep loss or hypoxia at high doses (0.2-0.35 g/kg single dose or 20 g/day loading), doses far above the 3 g/day food-claim dose and above what fits in an ordinary food serving. In the EU the specific claim 'Daily creatine supplementation can contribute to improved cognitive function' was assessed by EFSA (opinion of 19 November 2024) and refused by Commission Regulation (EU) 2026/1118; only the physical-performance claim (3 g/day, adults performing high-intensity exercise) and the muscle-strength claim (over-55s doing resistance training) are authorised. Any Brain Food communication in the EU must therefore avoid cognition claims entirely; in the US an FTC-defensible cognition claim would need competent and reliable evidence that currently does not exist for healthy rested adults. The honest marketing position is 'a well-characterised, cheap, safe ingredient with an authorised exercise-performance benefit and an interesting but unproven role under sleep deprivation'.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Creatine is absorbed via the intestinal creatine transporter (SLC6A8), crosses the blood-brain barrier slowly via the same transporter at the endothelium, and is phosphorylated by brain creatine kinase to phosphocreatine, which buffers ATP in neurons and astrocytes. Supplementation raises brain total creatine by about 5-10% (MRS). The proposed cognitive mechanism is improved temporal and spatial ATP buffering during high energy demand, which would explain why effects appear mainly under sleep deprivation and hypoxia. A reduction of the fMRI BOLD response after loading suggests altered neurovascular or metabolic coupling. Mechanism is plausible and partly demonstrated in humans at the biomarker level; it does not establish cognitive efficacy.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Oral creatine monohydrate -> near-complete intestinal absorption, plasma peak within 1-2 h, ~99% taken up by tissue or excreted unchanged", "detail": "Absorption and muscle uptake are well characterised; 20 g/day for 7 days raises muscle total creatine by ~30 mmol/kg dry mass in vegans/vegetarians [bonne2025]; creatine is not degraded during digestion [jager2011].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Plasma creatine -> slow transport across the blood-brain barrier via SLC6A8 -> increased brain total creatine", "detail": "1H-MRS shows +8.7% brain total creatine after 4 weeks at 20 g/day (regionally 4.7-14.6%, thalamus highest) [dechent1999] and +9.2% after 7 days at 20 g/day [turner2015]; a single 0.35 g/kg dose produced detectable tCr/tNAA and PCr/Pi changes within hours during sleep deprivation [gordjinejad2024]; 7 days at 0.3 g/kg did not change brain creatine in children [meregefilho2017]. MRS quantification is sensitive to voxel placement and analysis pipeline [candow2026].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Higher brain creatine/phosphocreatine -> greater ATP buffering capacity during high neuronal energy demand", "detail": "During 21 h sleep deprivation creatine prevented the drop in cerebral pH and altered PCr/Pi and ATP ratios versus placebo [gordjinejad2024]; this is a surrogate of energy status, not a cognitive outcome.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Altered energy buffering -> changed neurovascular coupling and cortical excitability", "detail": "Loading reduced the visual-cortex BOLD response by 16% [hammett2010] and increased corticomotor excitability during hypoxia [turner2015]; interpretation is uncertain.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Neuroprotection against energetic insult (hypoxia, ischaemia) via maintenance of membrane potentials", "detail": "Supported in vitro and in animal models of anoxic injury; in humans only one 15-person hypoxia crossover [turner2015].", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Measured human cognitive outcome", "detail": "Consistent only under acute sleep deprivation (processing speed, vigilance) and hypoxia (attention); in rested healthy young adults effects on working memory are trivial (d 0.17, NS) and on reasoning absent (d 0.09) in the largest trial [sandkuhler2023]; older adults possibly more responsive but inconsistent.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["creatine kinase / phosphocreatine shuttle (ATP buffering)", "SLC6A8 creatine transporter at the blood-brain barrier", "neurovascular coupling (BOLD reduction)", "corticomotor excitability", "possible anti-oxidative and mitochondrial membrane stabilisation (animal/in vitro)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Rested healthy adults 18-45 (mean ~21-31 y), omnivores and vegetarians, not sleep deprived", "dose_or_intake": "2.2-20 g/day creatine monohydrate", "duration": "6 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Largest preregistered crossover trial (n=123, 5 g/day): backward digit span p=0.064, d=0.17 (about 0.2 digits, roughly 2.5 'IQ-point' equivalents by the authors' conversion); two parallel trials null at 2.2 g/day (n=22) and 10-20 g/day (n=30); the one strongly positive trial was in vegetarians only (n=45) and has not been replicated. Meta-analytic young-adult memory subgroup SMD 0.03 (95% CI -0.14 to 0.20, I2 0%).", "effect_estimate": "d = 0.17 (95% CI of raw difference -0.24 to 0.84 items) [sandkuhler2023]; SMD 0.03 (-0.14 to 0.20) young-adult subgroup [prokopidis2023]", "certainty_notes": "Downgraded for imprecision and inconsistency (Rae vs Sandkühler/Rawson/Moriarty); the largest trial reported RR 4.25 for side effects which may have partly unblinded participants; practical relevance for a student is negligible.", "key_study_ids": ["sandkuhler2023", "rawson2008", "moriarty2023", "rae2003", "prokopidis2023"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Fluid reasoning/intelligence (Raven's Advanced Progressive Matrices) and executive tasks in rested young adults", "dose_or_intake": "5-20 g/day", "duration": "6 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Rae 2003 reported a large improvement in Raven's matrices in vegetarians (p<0.0001); Sandkühler 2023 (n=123) found d=0.09, p=0.327, with strong Bayesian evidence against a Rae-sized effect; Moriarty 2023 found no effect on executive tasks at 10 or 20 g/day; Xu 2024 pooled executive function not significant.", "effect_estimate": "d = 0.09, 0.23 more matrices solved (95% CI -0.24 to 0.70) [sandkuhler2023]; executive function pooled not significant [xu2024]", "certainty_notes": "One small unreplicated positive trial versus a large null; downgraded for inconsistency and risk of bias in the older trial (no preregistration, unclear randomisation per Prokopidis RoB).", "key_study_ids": ["sandkuhler2023", "rae2003", "moriarty2023", "xu2024"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "vegetarian_vegan", "population_detail": "Young adult vegetarians (low dietary creatine, normal endogenous synthesis)", "dose_or_intake": "5 g/day", "duration": "6 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Rae 2003 (n=45 vegetarians, crossover) reported significant gains in backward digit span and Raven's matrices (p<0.0001; effect calculated by Sandkühler et al. as roughly d=1). Sandkühler 2023 (about half of 123 participants vegetarian, same dose and duration) found no diet-by-supplement interaction (p=0.81 for BDS, p=0.39 for RAPM) and Bayes factors favouring the null over the Benton-sized effect.", "effect_estimate": "Rae: p<0.0001, means not reported in abstract; Sandkühler: interaction not significant, direction not as predicted", "certainty_notes": "Direct contradiction between the only two adequately sized trials; the later, preregistered trial is methodologically stronger. Grade C, not D, because the biological rationale (lower baseline stores) is sound and one trial is positive.", "key_study_ids": ["rae2003", "sandkuhler2023", "bonne2025"]}, {"timeframe": "short_term_days", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "vegetarian_vegan", "population_detail": "Young adult women, vegetarians vs omnivores", "dose_or_intake": "20 g/day", "duration": "5 days", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "In 128 young women, 5 days of 20 g/day improved memory in vegetarians but not omnivores; no effect on verbal fluency or vigilance; reduced variability of choice reaction time irrespective of diet.", "effect_estimate": "Not reported in abstract (effect size later cited by Sandkühler as d=0.36)", "certainty_notes": "Single trial, abstract-level data, subgroup finding; not replicated at 6 weeks in Sandkühler 2023.", "key_study_ids": ["benton2011", "sandkuhler2023"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "sleep_related_cognition", "population": "sleep_deprived", "population_detail": "Healthy adults (mean ~23 y) kept awake for 21 h; elite rugby players after 3-5 h sleep", "dose_or_intake": "Single dose 0.2-0.35 g/kg (about 14-25 g) 2-3 h before testing; 50-100 mg/kg 1.5 h before a skill test", "duration": "Single dose, effects measured 3-7.5 h later", "grade": "B", "direction": "benefit", "effect_summary": "0.35 g/kg (n=15 crossover): improvements versus placebo in processing time on language, logic and numeric tasks and PVT reaction speed, with parallel changes in PCr/Pi, ATP and prevention of cerebral pH drop; well tolerated. 0.2 g/kg (n=29): reduced deterioration in logic, numeric, language processing speed and PVT, up to 12%, larger in women. 50-100 mg/kg in 10 elite rugby players prevented the sleep-restriction fall in passing accuracy.", "effect_estimate": "Up to 12% less deterioration at 0.2 g/kg [gordjinejad2026]; numeric-task processing time -24% vs baseline at 0 a.m. with creatine versus -0.4% with placebo (p<0.0005) [gordjinejad2024]; skill accuracy preserved (p<0.001 fall with placebo) [cook2011]", "certainty_notes": "Promising but two of three trials are from one laboratory, samples are small, many comparisons did not survive Bonferroni correction, and doses are 4-8 times the EU claim dose. Not generalisable to a rested student.", "key_study_ids": ["gordjinejad2024", "gordjinejad2026", "cook2011"]}, {"timeframe": "short_term_days", "domain": "sleep_related_cognition", "population": "sleep_deprived", "population_detail": "Young adults (19-23 y) after 24-36 h sleep deprivation with intermittent exercise", "dose_or_intake": "20 g/day (4 x 5 g)", "duration": "7 days before deprivation", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "At 24 h: less deterioration in random movement generation, choice reaction time, balance and mood (n=19). At 36 h: creatine group better only on the central executive task (p<0.05); no difference on memory, reaction time, balance or mood (n=19).", "effect_estimate": "Not reported as standardised effects in abstracts", "certainty_notes": "Two small trials from one group, abstract-level data, inconsistent across tasks and time points; graded C.", "key_study_ids": ["mcmorris2006", "mcmorris2007sd"]}, {"timeframe": "short_term_days", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "high_cognitive_workload", "population_detail": "Healthy adults (21-55 y) breathing 10% oxygen for 90 min (acute hypoxia model)", "dose_or_intake": "20 g/day", "duration": "7 days", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Crossover n=15: hypoxia reduced the neurocognitive index by about 12% on placebo; creatine restored complex attention (t(14)=1.78, p=0.049, about 21% difference) with trends for executive function and cognitive flexibility (p 0.07-0.08); brain creatine +9.2%.", "effect_estimate": "Complex attention p=0.049, no effect size reported; brain creatine 7.03 vs 6.44 mmol/L (p=0.04)", "certainty_notes": "Single small crossover, one-tailed p-values close to 0.05, surrogate stress model; university funded.", "key_study_ids": ["turner2015"]}, {"timeframe": "short_term_days", "domain": "cognitive_fatigue", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy young adults (24 y) performing a 90-min Stroop mental-fatigue protocol; physically active men", "dose_or_intake": "20 g/day", "duration": "7 days", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Van Cutsem 2020 (n=14 crossover): Stroop accuracy +4.9% (p=0.026) over 90 min, but creatine did not prevent mental-fatigue-induced impairment of visuomotor response time (+4.4%) or Flanker accuracy (-5.0%), and motivation/vigor were lower on creatine. Ben Maaoui 2025 (n=14 crossover): digit cancellation test improved (d=0.77, p=0.013) and subjective sleep quality improved (d=0.81).", "effect_estimate": "Stroop accuracy +4.9% [vancutsem2020]; DCT d=0.77 [benmaaoui2025]", "certainty_notes": "Small crossovers, mixed outcomes, single cognitive test in the second trial; not generalisable to prolonged study sessions.", "key_study_ids": ["vancutsem2020", "benmaaoui2025"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Community-dwelling older adults 60-85 y without dementia", "dose_or_intake": "20 g/day for 1 week; or 20 g/day 5 days then 5 g/day", "duration": "1 week to 24 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "McMorris 2007 (n=32, 1 week at 20 g/day) reported improvement on all memory tasks except backward number recall; Alves 2013 (n=56 older women, 24 weeks) found no effect on any cognitive measure with or without strength training; Chun 2026 (n=64, 45-65 y, 10 g/day 12 weeks) reported improvements in 'selected' exploratory memory markers. Pooled older-adult memory SMD 0.88 (95% CI 0.22-1.55, I2 83%) from two trials.", "effect_estimate": "SMD 0.88 (0.22-1.55), I2 83%, 2 trials [prokopidis2023]", "certainty_notes": "Very high heterogeneity, high risk of bias in the positive trial (unusual sequential design), industry-linked authorship in the 2026 trial; the 2026 systematic review rated most studies fair or poor. Not transferable to young adults.", "key_study_ids": ["mcmorris2007elderly", "alves2013", "chun2026", "prokopidis2023", "marshall2026"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Pooled healthy and clinical adults 20-76 y (meta-analysis)", "dose_or_intake": "2.2-20 g/day", "duration": "7 days to 18 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Xu 2024 (16 RCTs, n=492): memory SMD 0.31 (0.18-0.44, moderate GRADE certainty per authors), attention time SMD -0.31 (-0.58 to -0.03), processing speed SMD -0.51 (-1.01 to -0.01), global cognition SMD 0.34 (-0.20 to 0.88, I2 75%, NS), executive function NS; benefits larger in diseased, 18-60 y and female subgroups. A corrigendum and a 2026 commentary were published; the review mixes clinical and healthy populations.", "effect_estimate": "Memory SMD 0.31 (0.18-0.44); global SMD 0.34 (-0.20 to 0.88)", "certainty_notes": "Indirectness (clinical populations pooled), moderate heterogeneity, small trials, prior meta-analysis criticised for double-counting; grade C for the healthy population.", "key_study_ids": ["xu2024", "prokopidis2023", "eckert2023", "avgerinos2018", "mcmorris2024"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "reaction_time", "population": "other", "population_detail": "Perimenopausal and menopausal women (mean 50 y), apparently healthy", "dose_or_intake": "Creatine hydrochloride 750 or 1500 mg/day, or HCl + ethyl ester 800 mg/day (not monohydrate)", "duration": "8 weeks", "grade": "D", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Medium-dose creatine HCl improved reaction time versus placebo (1.2% vs 6.6% change, p<0.01) and increased frontal brain creatine (16.4% vs 0.9%); n=36 across four arms (about 9 per arm).", "effect_estimate": "Reaction time change 1.2% vs 6.6% (p<0.01)", "certainty_notes": "Tiny arms, non-monohydrate form, authors hold creatine patents and Alzchem advisory roles; preliminary.", "key_study_ids": ["korovljev2026"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "neuroplasticity_biomarkers", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Brain total creatine by MRS (biomarker, not cognition) in healthy adults", "dose_or_intake": "20 g/day (loading) or single 0.35 g/kg", "duration": "7 days to 4 weeks; single dose", "grade": "B", "direction": "benefit", "effect_summary": "Brain total creatine rises about 5-10% (mean 8.7%, range 3.5-13.3%, n=6, 4 weeks; 9.2%, n=15, 7 days), reversible after 3 months; a single high dose produces measurable tCr/tNAA and high-energy-phosphate changes within hours during sleep deprivation. No change after 7 days in children.", "effect_estimate": "+8.7% (0.6 mM) [dechent1999]; +9.2% (7.03 vs 6.44 mmol/L, p=0.04) [turner2015]", "certainty_notes": "Consistent across laboratories but small samples; a biomarker, which per protocol cannot raise the cognitive grade.", "key_study_ids": ["dechent1999", "turner2015", "gordjinejad2024", "meregefilho2017", "candow2026"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_aging", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "US adults 60+ (NHANES 2001-2002), habitual dietary creatine from food", "dose_or_intake": "Dietary creatine, >0.95 g/day (upper two quartiles) vs less", "duration": "Cross-sectional", "grade": "D", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Higher dietary creatine correlated weakly with Digit Symbol Substitution score (partial r=0.062, p=0.039 after sociodemographic adjustment; tau-b 0.043).", "effect_estimate": "r = 0.055-0.062", "certainty_notes": "Cross-sectional, single 24-h recall, trivial correlation, confounded by meat intake and socioeconomic status.", "key_study_ids": ["ostojic2021", "marshall2026"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "students", "population_detail": "University students or knowledge workers under academic or occupational cognitive load, rested", "dose_or_intake": "3-5 g/day (food-feasible dose)", "duration": "2 weeks or longer", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No trial has recruited students under examination load or knowledge workers as such; the closest evidence (young adults recruited by flyers, mean age 21-31) shows trivial effects at 2.2-20 g/day. No acute single-dose study exists in rested people.", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "Absence of direct evidence; extrapolation from healthy young adults suggests any effect would be small (d < 0.2).", "key_study_ids": ["sandkuhler2023", "rawson2008", "moriarty2023"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Rested (not sleep-deprived) young adults, single dose", "dose_or_intake": "Single dose, any", "duration": "Single day", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No placebo-controlled single-dose trial in rested adults was identified; the single-dose trials were all conducted during sleep deprivation or sleep restriction.", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "No evidence; a food product promising same-day effects in rested users has no support.", "key_study_ids": ["gordjinejad2024", "cook2011"]}, {"timeframe": "short_term_days", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "children_adolescents", "population_detail": "Healthy children 10-12 y (included only to show non-transferability)", "dose_or_intake": "0.3 g/kg/day", "duration": "7 days", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "No change in brain creatine (three regions) or in verbal learning and executive tests (n=67); children appear to rely on endogenous synthesis.", "effect_estimate": "All p>0.05", "certainty_notes": "Single trial, short duration; children are outside the Brain Food target population and should not be a market.", "key_study_ids": ["meregefilho2017"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "0.35 g/kg creatine monohydrate single dose (about 25 g for 70 kg), dissolved, at 20:30", "onset": "Metabolic changes from about 3 h; cognitive differences at 3, 5.5 and 7.5 h post-dose", "duration_studied": "7.5 h during 21 h sleep deprivation", "domain": "processing speed, working memory (digit span), psychomotor vigilance", "population": "15 healthy adults, mean 23 y, crossover", "effect_estimate": "Numeric task processing time -24% vs baseline at 0 a.m. with creatine vs -0.4% with placebo (p<0.0005); digit span maintained (+1.6% vs -24.8% at 2 a.m., not Bonferroni-significant)", "certainty": "low-to-moderate (single lab, small n, multiple comparisons)", "safety": "No gastric discomfort reported; dose is 8 times the EU claim dose", "study_ids": ["gordjinejad2024"]}, {"dose": "0.2 g/kg creatine monohydrate single dose (about 14 g)", "onset": "By 3 h post-dose", "duration_studied": "7.5 h during 21 h sleep deprivation", "domain": "logic, numeric and language processing speed, PVT reaction time", "population": "29 healthy adults, crossover; women benefited more", "effect_estimate": "Up to 12% less deterioration vs placebo; logic score -9.7% (placebo) vs -1.5% (creatine) at 4 a.m.", "certainty": "low-to-moderate", "safety": "Well tolerated, no GI complaints", "study_ids": ["gordjinejad2026"]}, {"dose": "50 or 100 mg/kg creatine single dose 1.5 h before testing", "onset": "1.5 h", "duration_studied": "Single session after 3-5 h sleep", "domain": "sport-specific skill accuracy (rugby passing)", "population": "10 elite male rugby players", "effect_estimate": "Sleep restriction with placebo reduced accuracy (p<0.001); no deficit with either creatine dose; salivary testosterone trended higher at 100 mg/kg (p=0.067)", "certainty": "low (n=10, psychomotor skill not cognition)", "safety": "No adverse events reported", "study_ids": ["cook2011"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "6 weeks (crossover, 5 g/day)", "population": "123 healthy adults 18-50 (mean 30.6 y), 57% female, half vegetarian", "outcome": "Backward digit span; Raven's APM; 8 exploratory tasks", "effect_estimate": "BDS +0.41 items (95% CI -0.24 to 0.84), p=0.064, d=0.17; RAPM +0.23 (95% CI -0.24 to 0.70), p=0.327, d=0.09; exploratory tasks null; side effects 17% vs 4% (RR 4.25)", "evidence_quality": "high internal validity (preregistered, double-blind, independent funding), but imprecise", "limitations": "Possible partial unblinding (solubility, side effects); learning effects; adherence self-reported", "study_ids": ["sandkuhler2023"]}, {"duration": "6 weeks (crossover, 5 g/day)", "population": "45 young adult vegetarians", "outcome": "Backward digit span; Raven's APM", "effect_estimate": "Significant improvement on both (p<0.0001); means not in abstract; later re-expressed by Sandkühler et al. as roughly d=1", "evidence_quality": "low (small, not preregistered, randomisation unclear per later RoB assessments, unreplicated)", "limitations": "Results only known at abstract level here; effect size implausibly large relative to all later trials", "study_ids": ["rae2003"]}, {"duration": "6 weeks (parallel, 0.03 g/kg/day about 2.2 g)", "population": "22 young adults (21 y)", "outcome": "Simple RT, code substitution, logical reasoning, mathematical processing, running memory, Sternberg", "effect_estimate": "No group, time or interaction effects (all p>0.05)", "evidence_quality": "low (n=22, low dose, capsules)", "limitations": "Underpowered; dose below typical maintenance", "study_ids": ["rawson2008"]}, {"duration": "6 weeks (parallel, 10 or 20 g/day vs 10 g placebo)", "population": "30 healthy young adults (21 y)", "outcome": "Processing speed, episodic memory, attention; prefrontal fNIRS", "effect_estimate": "No significant effect of creatine at either dose; trend to lower PFC O2Hb at 10 g (p=0.06)", "evidence_quality": "low (n=10 per arm; authors with industry ties)", "limitations": "Underpowered; no brain creatine measurement", "study_ids": ["moriarty2023"]}, {"duration": "7 days (parallel, 20 g/day)", "population": "40 healthy active young adults (20 M, 20 F)", "outcome": "Stroop, Trail Making A/B before and after Wingate", "effect_estimate": "No effect on any cognitive task; Wingate mean power +44 W (ES 0.94)", "evidence_quality": "low-to-moderate", "limitations": "Learning effects may have masked effects; short duration", "study_ids": ["silva2026"]}, {"duration": "24 weeks (4-arm parallel, 20 g/day 5 days then 5 g/day, with or without strength training)", "population": "56 healthy older women (60-80 y)", "outcome": "Memory, selective attention, inhibitory control; Geriatric Depression Scale", "effect_estimate": "No change in any cognitive parameter with creatine; strength training improved mood", "evidence_quality": "moderate (registered NCT01164020, 24 weeks)", "limitations": "n=14 per arm", "study_ids": ["alves2013"]}, {"duration": "1 week (20 g/day after 1 week placebo run-in)", "population": "32 older adults (68-85 y)", "outcome": "Random number generation, number and spatial recall, long-term memory", "effect_estimate": "Significant improvement on all tasks except backward number recall; no effect sizes in abstract", "evidence_quality": "low (sequential design, unclear randomisation)", "limitations": "Order effects not separable from creatine effect", "study_ids": ["mcmorris2007elderly"]}, {"duration": "12 weeks (2 x 5 g/day, with or without exercise and diet programme)", "population": "64 sedentary adults 45-65 y", "outcome": "Cognitive test battery and questionnaires (exploratory); body composition; blood markers", "effect_estimate": "'Selected markers of cognitive function and memory' improved; authors label cognitive findings exploratory", "evidence_quality": "low for cognition (exploratory, multiple outcomes, senior author chairs Alzchem advisory board)", "limitations": "Non-random allocation to exercise arm; many outcomes", "study_ids": ["chun2026"]}, {"duration": "8 weeks (creatine HCl 750/1500 mg or HCl + CEE 800 mg)", "population": "36 perimenopausal/menopausal women", "outcome": "Reaction time, mood, frontal brain creatine, lipids", "effect_estimate": "Reaction time change 1.2% vs 6.6% (p<0.01); frontal brain creatine +16.4% vs +0.9%", "evidence_quality": "very low (about 9 per arm, patented forms, author COI)", "limitations": "Not creatine monohydrate; pilot scale", "study_ids": ["korovljev2026"]}, {"duration": "5 days to 24 weeks (meta-analysis, 8 RCTs, n=225)", "population": "Healthy individuals 11-76 y", "outcome": "Memory (various instruments)", "effect_estimate": "SMD 0.29 (0.04-0.53), I2 66%; young 0.03 (-0.14 to 0.20); older 0.88 (0.22-1.55); powder 0.35 vs capsules 0.04", "evidence_quality": "low (6/10 RCTs high RoB; double-counting critique; authors with industry ties)", "limitations": "Includes children and sleep-deprived cohorts; only 2 older-adult trials", "study_ids": ["prokopidis2023", "eckert2023"]}, {"duration": "7 days to 18 months (meta-analysis, 16 RCTs, n=492)", "population": "Healthy and clinical adults 20-76 y", "outcome": "Memory, attention, processing speed, executive, global cognition", "effect_estimate": "Memory SMD 0.31 (0.18-0.44); attention time -0.31 (-0.58 to -0.03); processing speed -0.51 (-1.01 to -0.01); global 0.34 (-0.20 to 0.88) NS; executive NS", "evidence_quality": "low-to-moderate (authors rate memory 'moderate' GRADE; corrigendum issued)", "limitations": "Mixed populations; some trials with 3-7 participants per arm", "study_ids": ["xu2024"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Creatine monohydrate at 3-5 g/day is among the best safety-characterised dietary ingredients: trials up to 30 g/day for 5 years in healthy people and patients report no medically significant harm [kreider2017]; female-only pooled data show no serious adverse events and non-significant differences in total adverse events (RR 1.24, 0.51-2.98) [deguingand2020]; renal meta-analyses show a small, mostly transient rise in serum creatinine with unchanged GFR in healthy adults [naeini2025, desouza2019]. Real-world issues are gastrointestinal upset (more frequent with loading doses; 6% vs 2% at 5 g/day in the largest cognition trial, with any side effect 17% vs 4% [sandkuhler2023]), 1-2 kg body-water weight gain (1.56 kg in one week at 0.3 g/kg/day [bonne2025]), confounded kidney tests, and a 2026 preclinical/human-biomarker signal of platelet hyperactivation with possible metastasis promotion [xie2026] that needs expert review. Data in pregnancy, under-18s, kidney disease and bipolar disorder are limited. The acute doses used in sleep-deprivation trials (0.2-0.35 g/kg, 14-25 g) are not food-level doses and carry higher GI risk.", "adverse_effects": ["Gastrointestinal discomfort, bloating, diarrhoea (dose-dependent; more common with 20 g/day loading or single 20-25 g doses; 6% vs 2% at 5 g/day)", "Body mass gain of about 1-2 kg from intracellular water within 1-4 weeks (relevant to weight-class athletes and body-image-sensitive consumers)", "Rise in serum creatinine (SMD 0.48 or MD 0.07 units) that can be misread as reduced eGFR; inform users to tell their clinician", "Occasional reports of muscle cramps, thirst, nightmares, racing thoughts (single cases in the Sandkühler side-effect table; not established causal)", "Hair loss widely rumoured; a 12-week RCT (PMID 40265319, not extracted here) addressed it; no convincing evidence", "Platelet hyperactivity after supplementation in healthy volunteers with metastasis promotion in mice (2026 preclinical signal)"], "contraindications": ["Pre-existing kidney disease or reduced GFR (insufficient safety data; creatinine confounding)", "Pregnancy and lactation (insufficient human safety data; excluded by regulators' safety assessments)", "Children and adolescents for cognitive purposes (no benefit shown; not a target market)", "Active cancer or history of metastatic disease until the 2026 platelet signal is clarified (precautionary)", "Bipolar disorder: creatine has been trialled as adjunct in bipolar depression; manic switching risk uncertain; clinician advice"], "drug_interactions": ["Nephrotoxic drugs (high-dose NSAIDs, aminoglycosides, ciclosporin): theoretical additive renal stress; avoid co-use without medical advice", "Diuretics: theoretical fluid/electrolyte interaction with creatine-induced water retention", "Caffeine: one older study reported caffeine blunting creatine's muscle-relaxation effect (PMID 11796658, not extracted); no cognitive interaction data", "Antiplatelet or anticoagulant therapy: no clinical data; the 2026 platelet-activation finding argues for caution", "Lithium or other renally cleared drugs with narrow therapeutic index: monitor creatinine interpretation"], "upper_limits": "No EFSA tolerable upper intake level or IOM UL exists. EU authorised claims and FDA GRN 931 both operate at 3 g/day (GRN 931: 1 g creatine per serving of conventional foods). Several competent authorities have concluded 3 g/day is unlikely to pose safety concerns in healthy adults, excluding pregnant and breastfeeding women [jager2011]. ISSN cites tolerance of up to 30 g/day for 5 years in studies [kreider2017] but this is not a regulatory limit. Brain Food should cap at 5 g/day for adults.", "sleep_impact": "No evidence of sleep disruption at 3-5 g/day; one 7-day loading crossover found improved subjective sleep quality (d=0.81) with no change in actigraphic latency, efficiency or total sleep time [benmaaoui2025]; single high doses were administered at 20:30 to keep subjects performing while sleep deprived, so timing in a food product should avoid implying 'stay awake' use. One participant in 123 reported nightmares on creatine [sandkuhler2023].", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Yes: up to 5 years at up to 30 g/day in clinical and athletic cohorts [kreider2017]; 24 weeks in older women with no cognitive or safety issues [alves2013]; 18 months at 10 g/day in a Parkinson's cohort included in Xu 2024. Long-term data in healthy young women and vegetarians at 3 g/day are fewer; female-only AE synthesis reassuring [deguingand2020].", "daily_use_appropriate": "yes", "special_populations": "Pregnancy/lactation: avoid (no data). Children/adolescents: no cognitive benefit and no brain creatine change at 7 days [meregefilho2017]; not a target. Older adults: well tolerated for 24 weeks [alves2013]. Vegetarians/vegans: faster tissue loading and about 1.5 kg water gain in a week at loading doses [bonne2025]; suitable for a maintenance dose. Kidney disease: exclude. Psychiatric conditions: creatine used adjunctively in depression trials; bipolar caution. Cancer: precautionary exclusion pending review of [xie2026]. Athletes subject to doping control: creatine is permitted (not on the WADA list).", "specialist_review_needed": ["Nephrologist or clinical pharmacologist: wording for creatinine/eGFR confounding and renal exclusion", "Toxicologist/oncologist: assessment of the 2026 platelet-activation and metastasis signal before mass-market positioning", "Registered dietitian: vegetarian/vegan and weight-gain counselling, dose per serving", "Regulatory affairs (EU, US, Morocco): claim wording, novel-food confirmation for the specific grade, Moroccan registration path", "Food technologist: stability testing in the chosen matrix (pH, water activity, shelf life, creatinine formation)"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "At 3-5 g/day creatine monohydrate in healthy adults the evidence base is large and reassuring, so the ingredient passes for adult products provided: adults only; renal-disease, pregnancy and lactation exclusions on label; dose capped at 5 g/day with a creatinine/eGFR advisory; and a documented toxicology review of the 2026 platelet-metastasis signal. The high acute doses (0.2-0.35 g/kg) that produced the sleep-deprivation effects are not appropriate for a food product."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Creatine monohydrate is listed as 'Not Novel' in the Commission's novel-food status consultation table (entry dated 17 July 2023, following the German BVL consultation outcome of 30 May 2023); micronised creatine monohydrate was confirmed not novel (AESAN, listed 20 August 2025). Creatine malate and creatine alpha-ketoglutarate are listed as novel (17 February 2022). Two health claims are authorised in the Union list (Regulation (EU) No 432/2012 as amended): 'Creatine increases physical performance in successive bursts of short-term, high intensity exercise' (3 g/day; only for foods targeting adults performing high-intensity exercise; EFSA Journal 2011;9(7):2303) and, by Commission Implementing Regulation (EU) 2017/672 of 7 April 2017, 'Daily creatine consumption can enhance the effect of resistance training on muscle strength in adults over the age of 55' (3 g/day with resistance training at least three times per week for several weeks at 65-75% 1RM; only for foods targeting adults over 55 engaged in regular resistance training; EFSA Journal 2016;14(2):4400). The Article 13(5) claim 'Daily creatine supplementation can contribute to improved cognitive function' (Alzchem Trostberg GmbH, EFSA-Q-2024-00106) received a negative EFSA opinion on 19 November 2024 (EFSA Journal 2024;22(11):e9100) and was refused by Commission Regulation (EU) 2026/1118 of 26 May 2026 (OJ 27 May 2026). Consequently any cognition, memory, focus or 'brain' claim for creatine is prohibited in the EU. Sources: legislation.gov.uk retained annex of 432/2012 and 2017/672 (accessed 2026-10-25); Commission novel-food consultation table and trade reports (accessed 2026-10-25); EUR-Lex listing eli/reg/2026/1118/oj and Council document 5235/26 (accessed 2026-10-25).", "us": "Creatine monohydrate is a lawful dietary ingredient under DSHEA (marketed before 1994; no NDI notification needed for monohydrate). For conventional foods, GRAS Notice GRN 000931 (AlzChem Trostberg GmbH; received 5 March 2020, filed 12 June 2020; FDA 'no questions' letter 12 November 2020, corrected letter 2021) covers creatine monohydrate at 1.12 g per serving (1.0 g creatine) in energy drinks, protein bars and powders, milkshakes, meal-replacement products, meat analogues and powdered drink mixes, excluding infant formula and USDA-regulated foods. Structure/function claims (e.g., 'supports muscle performance') are permitted with the DSHEA disclaimer and FTC-standard substantiation ('competent and reliable scientific evidence'); a cognition claim would be hard to substantiate for rested healthy adults given the EFSA assessment and the null trials, and FTC has pursued unsupported cognitive claims in other products. Sources: FDA GRN 931 response letters (fda.gov/media/144598, fda.gov/media/143525), accessed 2026-10-25.", "morocco": "Uncertain. Food supplements in Morocco fall under the food-safety law 28-07 enforced by ONSSA for labelling and composition (Décret 2-12-389 of 22 April 2013 on labelling, which includes specific caffeine and liquorice provisions but no creatine-specific rule found), while products with health/therapeutic positioning engage the Ministry of Health and the Agence Marocaine du Médicament et des Produits de Santé (AMMPS), which has issued repeated warnings about unauthorised supplements. A reform is in progress in 2026: a bill amending article 30 of law 17-04 (Code du médicament et de la pharmacie) passed the first chamber in June 2026 to reserve supplements with a 'therapeutic effect' to pharmacies, and the implementing decree (law reference reported as 27.26) was postponed at the Government Council of 22 July 2026; an official list of concerned substances (reportedly nine) is awaited. No Moroccan positive list naming creatine, no notification file requirement, and no Moroccan claim register were found. Creatine supplements are sold at retail in Morocco (e.g., parachezvous.ma listing). Sources: ONSSA decree PDF, hespress.com, le360.ma (6 Aug 2026), medias24.com (15 Apr and 23 Jul 2026), telquel.ma (11 Jun 2026), all accessed 2026-10-25. Specialist regulatory confirmation required.", "permitted_claims_examples": ["EU (Reg. 432/2012): 'Creatine increases physical performance in successive bursts of short-term, high intensity exercise' - only on foods providing 3 g creatine/day, targeting adults performing high-intensity exercise, with consumer information that the effect is obtained with 3 g/day.", "EU (Reg. 2017/672): 'Daily creatine consumption can enhance the effect of resistance training on muscle strength in adults over the age of 55' - 3 g/day, foods targeting adults over 55 doing regular resistance training (at least 3 times/week, several weeks, 65-75% 1RM), with that information given to the consumer.", "US: structure/function claim such as 'supports muscle performance during high-intensity exercise' with DSHEA disclaimer (not pre-approved; substantiation file required). No authorised cognition claim exists in any of the three markets."], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "Creatine monohydrate (including micronised) is not novel in the EU; other salts/forms (malate, alpha-ketoglutarate, probably HCl and ethyl ester) are novel or of unclear status and must not be used without authorisation (Polish RASFF-type alert on creatine malate). In the US, monohydrate is an old dietary ingredient and GRAS-notified for specified conventional foods at 1 g/serving; use at 3 g/serving in a conventional food would exceed the GRN 931 notified level and may need its own GRAS determination or be positioned as a dietary supplement. Morocco: no novel-food system identified; status unknown.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Suitable as a dry ingredient in powders, sachets, capsules, bars and ready-to-mix drinks; unsuitable for acidic ready-to-drink beverages, juices, yoghurts or any wet matrix stored for days unless pH is near neutral and the product is chilled or consumed immediately, because creatine cyclises to creatinine in solution [jager2011]. Heat processing in moist matrices (cooking, retort, UHT) is expected to convert part of the creatine to creatinine (meat cooking losses are the classic example); baked-goods stability was not researched here. A 3 g dose is about 3.4 g of monohydrate, feasible in a 10-30 g powder serving or a 50 g bar but gritty in cold water.", "formats": ["Unflavoured powder or stick-pack (3-5 g) to mix into water, milk or smoothie immediately before drinking", "Capsules/tablets (3 g requires 4-6 large capsules; lower adherence; meta-analysis found capsule trials null)", "Protein or oat bars (GRN 931 covers bars at 1 g creatine/serving in the US)", "Meal-replacement or protein powders (GRN 931 category)", "Chewable tablets or gummies (stability must be tested; water activity matters)", "Not recommended: acidic RTD beverages, juices, kombucha, fermented dairy with long shelf life"], "stability": "Solid monohydrate: stable for years at room temperature and at 40 C (creatinine <67 ppm after >3 years); even at 60 C creatinine detected only after 44 months [jager2011]. Aqueous solution: degradation depends on pH and temperature, not concentration; after 3 days at 25 C losses of 4% (pH 5.5), 12% (pH 4.5), 21% (pH 3.5); relatively stable at pH 7.5; refrigeration slows degradation [jager2011]. Light: not a known issue. Oxidation: not a known issue. Micronised grades dissolve faster but are chemically identical.", "taste": "Essentially tasteless to slightly bitter; the main sensory issue is grittiness/sedimentation in cold liquids and a chalky mouthfeel in bars at >3 g per serving; micronised grades improve dispersion.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Oral bioavailability of monohydrate is near complete and is not improved by other forms [jager2011]. Co-ingestion with carbohydrate or carbohydrate plus protein increases muscle uptake via insulin (classic sports-nutrition finding; not re-verified here) but there is no evidence that matrix changes brain uptake, which is transporter-limited and slow. Capsule versus powder differences in cognitive meta-analysis (SMD 0.04 vs 0.35) most likely reflect dose rather than matrix [prokopidis2023]. Caffeine co-formulation is a theoretical antagonist for muscle effects, unknown for brain.", "dose_feasibility": "The EU claim dose (3 g/day) and the dose of the largest cognition trial (5 g/day) fit in one powder sachet or two bars; the doses associated with the acute sleep-deprivation effects (14-25 g single dose) or 7-day loading (20 g/day) do not fit any reasonable food serving and would raise GI complaints. US conventional-food use is GRAS-notified only at 1 g creatine/serving, so a 3 g serving needs supplement positioning or a separate GRAS determination.", "cost_availability": "Very low cost and globally available as a commodity (major producers in Germany and China). Retail: 1 kg about GBP 26.49 (UK) or USD 26.97 (US), i.e., roughly 0.08-0.10 USD per 3 g dose; in Morocco a 300 g tub was listed at 300 MAD (about 1 MAD/g, so about 3 MAD per 3 g dose at retail; listing out of stock). Bulk ingredient prices were not researched. Availability in Morocco depends on import channels and the pending supplement reform.", "cost_evidence": "Retail price points found on 2026-10-25: Myprotein Creatine Monohydrate 1 kg GBP 26.49 (pricespy.co.uk); BulkSupplements creatine 1 kg USD 26.97 (slickdeals.net, Amazon listing dated March 2026); NOW Foods 2.2 lb USD 43.99 (nowfoods.com); Spirit Creatine Monohydrate 300 g 300 MAD (parachezvous.ma, out of stock). Bulk/B2B pricing: not researched here."}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 4, "effect_magnitude": 2.5, "safety": 8, "reproducibility": 4, "food_suitability": 6, "cost_access": 9, "rationale": "evidence_quality 4: grade C for every cognitive outcome in healthy rested young adults (largest trial borderline-null), grade B only for acute sleep-deprivation cognition from essentially one laboratory, grade B for the brain-creatine biomarker. effect_magnitude 2.5: in the target population the best estimate is d 0.1-0.2 on working memory and ~0.1 on reasoning, practically negligible; larger (up to 12%) only under sleep deprivation at non-food doses. safety 8: food-level safety at 3-5 g/day with decades of data, minus points for GI effects, weight gain, creatinine confounding, exclusions (renal, pregnancy) and the unresolved 2026 platelet signal. reproducibility 4: brain-creatine rise replicated across labs, but cognitive benefits cluster in two groups and among industry-linked authors, while independent trials are null. food_suitability 6: excellent in dry formats and bars, poor in acidic liquids, gritty, US GRAS level only 1 g/serving. cost_access 9: commodity ingredient costing cents per dose, available in Morocco at retail."}, "research_gaps": ["Adequately powered (n>300) preregistered RCT of 3-5 g/day for 8-12 weeks in university students during examination periods, with brain creatine (MRS) measured, ecological outcomes (study hours, grades, fatigue) and preregistered cognitive primary endpoints.", "Independent (non-industry-linked) replication of the single-dose sleep-deprivation effect, including a practical 3-5 g dose arm and a shift-worker or night-driving outcome.", "Head-to-head trial in vegetarians/vegans versus omnivores with measured baseline muscle and brain creatine, to settle the Rae/Benton versus Sandkühler contradiction.", "Dose-to-brain-creatine titration studies: does the inter-individual variability in brain uptake (3.5-13.3%) predict cognitive response?", "Older-adult RCT of 6-12 months with 3-5 g/day, cognition as primary outcome, independent funding, to resolve McMorris 2007 versus Alves 2013.", "Human clinical follow-up of the 2026 platelet-activation signal in regular creatine users, including cancer-survivor cohorts.", "Stability and sensory studies of creatine in realistic Brain Food matrices (bars, neutral-pH dairy, chilled drinks) with creatinine quantification over shelf life.", "No registered trial targeting students or knowledge workers was found on ClinicalTrials.gov; a 'Creatine and GAA for Brain Oxygenation' study (NCT06371651, n=19) is completed without posted results."], "_derived": {"composite_score": 5.2, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": "B", "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": "C", "best_grade_sleep_stress": "B", "best_grade_surrogate": "B", "best_grade_overall": "B", "benefit_domains": {"episodic_memory": "C", "sleep_related_cognition": "B", "sustained_attention": "C", "reaction_time": "D", "cognitive_aging": "D"}, "n_outcome_rows": 16, "n_studies": 40, "n_verified": 40, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 12, "n_systematic_reviews": 5, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["benefit", "inconsistent", "insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "dietary-choline", "name": "Dietary choline", "category": "E", "category_name": "Amino acids and related compounds", "aliases": ["choline", "total dietary choline", "free choline", "phosphatidylcholine (lecithin) as dietary choline source", "glycerophosphocholine and phosphocholine (food forms)", "sphingomyelin", "choline bitartrate", "choline chloride", "vitamin B4 (obsolete, not a vitamin)"], "food_sources": ["egg yolk (about 150 mg per large egg by standard US composition tables, value cross-referenced from the eggs monograph and not re-verified here)", "liver and organ meats (richest common source; quantities not re-verified because the NIH ODS page returned HTTP 403)", "meat, poultry and fish", "milk and dairy products", "soybeans and soy lecithin", "legumes, cruciferous vegetables, whole grains and wheat germ (smaller amounts)", "endogenous synthesis via hepatic PEMT (insufficient alone to prevent deficiency in most men and postmenopausal women)"], "active_constituents": ["free choline (trimethylethanolamine cation)", "phosphatidylcholine (main dietary form in eggs and meat)", "glycerophosphocholine, phosphocholine, sphingomyelin", "metabolites: acetylcholine (neurotransmitter), betaine (methyl donor), TMA/TMAO (gut-microbial and hepatic FMO3 products)"], "one_line_verdict": "Choline is an essential nutrient whose adequacy (about 400-550 mg/day) is worth meeting through food such as eggs, and low intakes are associated with higher dementia risk in cohorts, but acute choline does not improve memory in students, no chronic trial exists in healthy young adults, intakes above adequacy show no added benefit, and no cognitive claim is authorised anywhere.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is established. Choline is an essential nutrient with Adequate Intakes (EFSA 400 mg/day for adults, 480 mg/day in pregnancy [efsa2016]; IOM 550 mg/day men, 425 mg/day women [iom1998]) and a classical deficiency syndrome that is hepatic and muscular, not cognitive. Usual intakes are below the AI for most US adults (6.6% reach it [wallace2016]) and for many European groups (adult means 291-468 mg/day [vennemann2015]), but an AI is not a requirement, so 'below AI' does not mean deficient. Healthy young adults. The only randomized evidence is acute: three double-blind crossover experiments in students (n = 28, 26, 40) given 2.0-2.5 g choline bitartrate found no effect on working or declarative memory, with Bayesian support for the null [lippelt2016]; a military crossover at 50 mg/kg was null for reaction time, reasoning, vigilance and memory [deuster2002]; one 28-person study reported more precise visuomotor aiming [naber2015], an unreplicated exploratory outcome. No chronic trial in healthy young adults exists. Cognitive ageing and dementia. Cohorts link higher intake to better memory (Framingham, cross-sectional [poly2011]) and lower dementia risk, but the shape is non-linear: excess risk sits at low intakes (<=219 mg/day [yuan2022]); a five-cohort dose-response meta-analysis (137,607 people) is L-shaped with no further benefit above about 465 mg/day, and in its own cohort the top quintile was null (HR 0.91, 0.66-1.25) [chen2026]; Kuopio found an association for phosphatidylcholine but not total choline [ylilauri2019]; Rush MAP reported HR 0.49 (0.25-0.95) above 350 mg/day in 991 people aged about 81, with egg-industry ties among authors [karosas2025]. NHANES re-analyses disagree [liu2021, an2023], and midlife SWAN and PREDIMED-Plus associations are minimal (<0.1 SD over 2 years) [cowanpyle2024, vazquezlorente2026]. These data are observational and confounded by diet quality (eggs, meat, fish), so grade C at best. The only RCT in healthy older adults (300 mg/day egg-yolk choline) was mixed, manufacturer-run and lost 32% of participants [yamashita2023]. Pregnancy (separate population). A controlled-feeding RCT of 26 women found faster infant processing speed with 930 vs 480 mg/day [caudill2018] and better sustained attention in 20 children at 7 years [bahnfleth2022], but the larger 750 mg/day phosphatidylcholine RCT (n = 140) was null [cheatham2012] and an independent 2025 review judged the evidence insufficient [gould2025]. Safety and form. Food choline is safe; supplemental salts cause fishy body odour (36% vs 8% [wozniak2025]) and, unlike eggs, raise TMAO and platelet reactivity [wilcox2021]; the UL is 3.5 g/day [iom1998]. Bottom line: adequacy plausibly matters for long-term brain health; enhancement above adequacy is unsupported.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Deficiency correction: choline deficiency (shown in depletion-repletion studies used to set the AI [iom1998, efsa2016]) causes liver and muscle dysfunction; the cognitive relevance is only observational. The cohort signal is concentrated at low intakes (<=219 mg/day in Framingham [yuan2022]; lowest risk near 350 mg/day in Rush MAP [karosas2025]; L-shaped pooled curve flattening above about 465 mg/day [chen2026]). Enhancement: in adequately nourished healthy young adults, acute doses of 2-2.5 g choline bitartrate do not improve memory [lippelt2016, deuster2002], and in older adults intakes above about 400 mg/day show no further association [liu2021, chen2026]. The practical implication is to help people who eat few eggs, meat, fish or dairy (some vegetarians/vegans, low-income or restrictive diets, pregnancy) reach the AI, not to supplement above it. Pregnancy is a distinct life stage with a higher AI (480 mg/day EFSA) and fragile, inconsistent offspring-cognition data [caudill2018, cheatham2012, gould2025]; it must not be generalized to adult cognition.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Common marketing says choline is a 'brain nutrient' that 'boosts memory and focus' because it is the precursor of acetylcholine. The evidence does not support any acute or chronic enhancement claim in healthy adults [lippelt2016]; the cohort associations relate to avoiding low intake in older adults [yuan2022, chen2026]. In the EU the only authorised choline claims (Regulation (EU) 432/2012, food with at least 82.5 mg choline per 100 g/100 ml or per single portion) are 'Choline contributes to normal lipid metabolism', 'Choline contributes to the maintenance of normal liver function' and 'Choline contributes to normal homocysteine metabolism'; cognitive, neurological and brain-development claims assessed by EFSA in 2011 are not authorised [efsa2011]. In the US a 'good/excellent source of choline' nutrient-content claim is possible against the 550 mg Daily Value, but a cognition structure/function claim would need FTC-grade substantiation that does not exist. Honest positioning for Brain Food: 'eggs and other choline-rich foods help you meet your choline needs', paired with the authorised liver/lipid/homocysteine wording where the threshold is met. The strongest opportunity is ordinary food (eggs, see eggs monograph), not a choline supplement.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Dietary choline (mostly phosphatidylcholine) is absorbed as free choline and phospholipid, used for membrane phosphatidylcholine and sphingomyelin, oxidised to betaine (a methyl donor for homocysteine remethylation), and in neurons acetylated to acetylcholine. Part is converted by gut microbes to trimethylamine and by hepatic FMO3 to TMAO. The cognitive hypothesis (more substrate, more acetylcholine or better membranes) is plausible from animal work but brain acetylcholine synthesis is not substrate-limited in replete humans, which fits the null acute trials.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Food choline (PC, GPC, phosphocholine, free choline) -> intestinal hydrolysis and absorption -> plasma choline and lipoprotein PC", "detail": "Supplemental choline raises plasma choline within hours [deuster2002]; food PC (eggs) and PC supplements behave differently from choline salts with respect to TMAO [wilcox2021].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Unabsorbed/free choline -> gut-microbial TMA -> hepatic FMO3 -> TMAO", "detail": "Choline bitartrate raises fasting TMAO and platelet reactivity in humans; eggs do not [wilcox2021]; TMAO promotes atherosclerosis in mice [wang2011].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Choline -> betaine -> methyl donation (homocysteine -> methionine)", "detail": "Basis of the authorised EU homocysteine claim [efsa2011]; deficiency causes liver and muscle dysfunction used to set the AI [iom1998].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Plasma choline -> blood-brain barrier transport -> acetylcholine synthesis and membrane phospholipids", "detail": "Plausible; in replete adults acute loading does not change memory [lippelt2016]; pupil constriction after 2 g suggests some cholinergic effect [naber2015].", "evidence_level": "plausible"}, {"step": "Prenatal choline -> fetal hippocampal development and epigenetic programming", "detail": "Strong in rodents; in humans only small, inconsistent trials [caudill2018, cheatham2012].", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Measured human cognitive outcome", "detail": "Acute: null for memory [lippelt2016]. Chronic: observational associations at low intakes [yuan2022, chen2026]; no RCT in healthy young adults.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["cholinergic neurotransmission (acetylcholine synthesis)", "membrane phosphatidylcholine/sphingomyelin synthesis (CDP-choline/Kennedy pathway; PEMT)", "one-carbon metabolism via betaine (homocysteine remethylation)", "lipoprotein (VLDL) export and liver fat handling", "gut-microbial TMA/TMAO pathway"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy students/young adults (Netherlands, n=28/26/40) and healthy men under physical load (n=13)", "dose_or_intake": "2.0-2.5 g choline bitartrate (~0.8-1.0 g choline) or 50 mg/kg choline", "duration": "Single dose, tested 60-90 min later", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Three double-blind crossover experiments found no effect on visuospatial or verbal working memory or declarative memory, with Bayesian support for the null; a military crossover found no effect on reaction time, reasoning, vigilance, spatial or working memory.", "effect_estimate": "No effect; Bayes factors favoured null [lippelt2016]; all cognitive outcomes NS [deuster2002]", "certainty_notes": "Consistent nulls with Bayesian support, but small samples, single laboratory for the main study and acute dosing only; downgraded for imprecision. Certainty that there is no large acute effect is moderate-low.", "key_study_ids": ["lippelt2016", "deuster2002"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "reaction_time", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy young adults (n=28)", "dose_or_intake": "2 g choline bitartrate", "duration": "Single dose, ~70 min", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "More accurate visuomotor aiming (targets hit more centrally) and pupil constriction, interpreted as a shift toward precision at the expense of speed.", "effect_estimate": "Standardized effect not reported [naber2015]", "certainty_notes": "Single small exploratory trial with a novel outcome; same group's follow-up memory trials null; risk of selective reporting; not a cognitive enhancement claim.", "key_study_ids": ["naber2015"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults 18-45, students, knowledge workers", "dose_or_intake": "Any supplemental or dietary increase", "duration": ">=2 weeks", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No randomized trial of chronic choline supplementation or choline-rich diet on cognition in healthy young adults was found; cohort data come from middle-aged and older adults.", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "Absence of evidence; mechanistic plausibility (acetylcholine precursor) does not substitute for trials, and acute trials were null.", "key_study_ids": ["lippelt2016", "leermakers2015"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_aging", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Community-dwelling middle-aged and older adults (Framingham, NHANES, Kuopio, SWAN midlife women, PREDIMED-Plus with metabolic syndrome)", "dose_or_intake": "Habitual dietary choline ~190-470 mg/day (FFQ/24-h recall)", "duration": "Cross-sectional to 22 y", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Higher intake associated with better verbal/visual memory in Framingham (cross-sectional) and with verbal fluency/memory in Kuopio; NHANES analyses conflict (middle tertile only vs null); SWAN and PREDIMED-Plus associations minimal (<0.1 SD over 2 y).", "effect_estimate": "Framingham verbal memory beta 0.60 (0.29-0.91) [poly2011]; NHANES OR 0.58-0.67 middle tertile, top tertile NS [liu2021]; OR 0.87 (0.70-1.09) [an2023]; attention beta 0.075 (0.021-0.129) [vazquezlorente2026]", "certainty_notes": "Observational, mostly cross-sectional or single FFQ, residual confounding by diet quality, inconsistent across analyses of the same dataset; GRADE starts low and is not upgraded.", "key_study_ids": ["poly2011", "ylilauri2019", "liu2021", "an2023", "cowanpyle2024", "vazquezlorente2026"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "dementia_risk", "population": "deficient_or_low_baseline", "population_detail": "Adults with low habitual intakes (<~220-350 mg/day) in Framingham, Rush MAP, HRS and pooled cohorts", "dose_or_intake": "Habitual intake below ~220-350 mg/day vs higher", "duration": "8-22 y follow-up", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Risk of dementia/AD is higher at low intakes: Framingham low intake <=219 mg/day associated with higher risk; Rush MAP lowest risk near 350 mg/day; HRS and five-cohort meta-analysis L-shaped; Kuopio association only for phosphatidylcholine.", "effect_estimate": "HR 0.49 (0.25-0.95) >350 vs <200 mg/day [karosas2025]; Q4 vs Q1 HR 0.68 (0.49-0.92) [chen2026]; PC top vs bottom 28% lower (1-48%) [ylilauri2019]", "certainty_notes": "Prospective cohorts with long follow-up but observational, non-monotonic, wide CIs, some egg-industry-funded authors; consistent direction at the low end supports a 'avoid inadequacy' message only.", "key_study_ids": ["yuan2022", "karosas2025", "chen2026", "ylilauri2019"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "dementia_risk", "population": "replete_or_high_baseline", "population_detail": "Adults already near or above ~400-465 mg/day", "dose_or_intake": "> ~400-465 mg/day", "duration": "8-16 y", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "No further reduction in dementia risk or cognitive association above adequate intakes: HRS top quintile HR 0.91 (0.66-1.25); meta-analytic L-shape with turning point ~465 mg/day; NHANES top tertile (>399.5 mg/day) not associated with cognition.", "effect_estimate": "Q5 vs Q1 HR 0.91 (0.66-1.25) [chen2026]; top tertile NS [liu2021]", "certainty_notes": "Observational and imprecise, but consistent with a threshold model; no trial tests supra-adequate intake.", "key_study_ids": ["chen2026", "liu2021", "yuan2022"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Healthy Japanese adults 60-80 y", "dose_or_intake": "300 mg/day choline from egg-yolk extract", "duration": "12 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Verbal memory improved but processing speed and symbol-digit coding worsened vs placebo at 6 weeks; 32% of randomized participants excluded; manufacturer-run.", "effect_estimate": "p<0.05 for selected outcomes in both directions [yamashita2023]", "certainty_notes": "Single small industry trial with high risk of bias and outcome inconsistency; cannot exceed C.", "key_study_ids": ["yamashita2023"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "children_adolescents", "population_detail": "Offspring of pregnant women supplemented in pregnancy (infants to 7 y); pregnancy is a separate population from adults", "dose_or_intake": "Maternal 930 vs 480 mg/day total choline (feeding) or 750 mg/day phosphatidylcholine", "duration": "3rd trimester or 18 wk gestation to 90 d postpartum", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Tiny feeding trial (n=26) showed faster infant processing speed and better sustained attention at 7 y (n=20) with ~2x AI; the larger placebo-controlled PC trial (n=140) found no effect on infant memory, language or development; independent 2025 review: insufficient evidence.", "effect_estimate": "Infant RT faster (p<0.05) [caudill2018]; SAT p=0.02 [bahnfleth2022]; all NS [cheatham2012]", "certainty_notes": "Very small positive trials (industry co-funded), larger null trial, multiple outcomes; downgraded for imprecision and inconsistency.", "key_study_ids": ["caudill2018", "bahnfleth2022", "cheatham2012", "gould2025", "derbyshire2020"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Children 2.5-5.9 y with fetal alcohol spectrum disorder", "dose_or_intake": "500 mg/day choline bitartrate or 19 mg/kg/day", "duration": "9 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Primary outcome null in the pilot; pooled 3-trial analysis shows a trend-level sequential-memory benefit in younger children, no effect on executive function or IQ.", "effect_estimate": "Memory slope -10.06 (95% CI -19.13 to -0.99) [wozniak2025]; Mullen NS [wozniak2015]", "certainty_notes": "Clinical paediatric population; post-hoc age moderation; not generalizable to healthy adults.", "key_study_ids": ["wozniak2015", "wozniak2025"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "vascular_health", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults (median age 28) with normal renal function", "dose_or_intake": "Choline bitartrate vs eggs vs phosphatidylcholine at comparable choline", "duration": "4 weeks", "grade": "D", "direction": "harm", "effect_summary": "Surrogate-level signal only: choline bitartrate raised fasting TMAO and platelet reactivity; whole eggs and PC did not. Mouse data link choline-derived TMAO to atherosclerosis. No clinical cardiovascular harm from choline supplements has been demonstrated.", "effect_estimate": "TMAO rise P<0.0001 with bitartrate; eggs P=0.28 [wilcox2021]", "certainty_notes": "Graded D because the human evidence is a surrogate (TMAO, platelet aggregation) from one small trial plus mouse mechanism; investigators hold TMAO patents. Relevant to supplement form selection, not to food choline.", "key_study_ids": ["wilcox2021", "wang2011"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "2.0-2.5 g choline bitartrate", "onset": "~60 min", "duration_studied": "Single dose", "domain": "working and declarative memory", "population": "healthy young students (n=28, 26, 40)", "effect_estimate": "No effect; Bayesian evidence for null", "certainty": "C (no_benefit)", "safety": "Not reported in abstract", "study_ids": ["lippelt2016"]}, {"dose": "2 g choline bitartrate", "onset": "~70 min", "duration_studied": "Single dose", "domain": "visuomotor precision (reaction time/accuracy)", "population": "healthy young adults (n=28)", "effect_estimate": "More central hits, smaller pupils; no standardized effect", "certainty": "C (possible_benefit, unreplicated)", "safety": "Not reported", "study_ids": ["naber2015"]}, {"dose": "50 mg/kg choline", "onset": "Session", "duration_studied": "Single session around exhaustive load carriage", "domain": "reaction time, reasoning, vigilance, memory", "population": "healthy men (n=13)", "effect_estimate": "No differences", "certainty": "C (no_benefit)", "safety": "Not reported", "study_ids": ["deuster2002"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "Cross-sectional to 22 y", "population": "Middle-aged and older community adults", "outcome": "Memory, verbal fluency, attention", "effect_estimate": "Small positive associations in some cohorts; null or inverted-U in others", "evidence_quality": "C (observational)", "limitations": "Confounding by diet quality; FFQ measurement error; inconsistency", "study_ids": ["poly2011", "ylilauri2019", "liu2021", "an2023", "cowanpyle2024", "vazquezlorente2026"]}, {"duration": "8-22 y", "population": "Older adults", "outcome": "Incident dementia/AD", "effect_estimate": "Higher risk at low intakes; L-shape, no benefit above ~465 mg/day", "evidence_quality": "C (observational)", "limitations": "Non-linear, wide CIs; egg-industry ties in one cohort", "study_ids": ["yuan2022", "karosas2025", "chen2026", "ylilauri2019"]}, {"duration": "12 weeks", "population": "Healthy adults 60-80 y", "outcome": "Memory and processing speed", "effect_estimate": "Verbal memory up, processing speed down", "evidence_quality": "C (single high-RoB RCT)", "limitations": "32% post-randomization exclusion; manufacturer-run", "study_ids": ["yamashita2023"]}, {"duration": "3rd trimester to 7 y follow-up", "population": "Pregnant women and offspring", "outcome": "Infant processing speed; child sustained attention; infant memory", "effect_estimate": "Positive in n=26/20 feeding trial; null in n=140 PC trial", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "Tiny positive trials; review judged insufficient", "study_ids": ["caudill2018", "bahnfleth2022", "cheatham2012", "gould2025"]}, {"duration": "9 months", "population": "Children with FASD", "outcome": "Memory, executive function, IQ", "effect_estimate": "Trend-level memory benefit in younger children; EF/IQ null", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "Clinical; post-hoc moderation; fishy odour in 36%", "study_ids": ["wozniak2015", "wozniak2025"]}, {"duration": "4 weeks", "population": "Healthy adults", "outcome": "TMAO, platelet reactivity (safety surrogate)", "effect_estimate": "Increase with choline bitartrate, not eggs or PC", "evidence_quality": "C (surrogate)", "limitations": "Small arms; surrogate only", "study_ids": ["wilcox2021"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Choline from food is safe. Supplemental choline salts at gram doses cause fishy body odour, sweating, salivation, GI upset and, at the highest doses, hypotension; the IOM UL for adults is 3.5 g/day. Choline salts (not eggs or PC) raise TMAO and platelet reactivity, a surrogate of uncertain clinical meaning.", "adverse_effects": ["fishy body odour (36% vs 8% placebo with 500 mg/day in children [wozniak2025])", "sweating, salivation, diarrhoea at multi-gram doses [iom1998]", "hypotension (critical effect for the UL) [iom1998]", "raised TMAO and platelet reactivity with choline bitartrate [wilcox2021]"], "contraindications": ["trimethylaminuria (FMO3 deficiency): supplemental choline worsens fish odour", "caution with high-dose supplements in renal disease (TMAO accumulation; not specifically studied here)"], "drug_interactions": ["No clinically established interactions at dietary intakes; theoretical additive effects with cholinergic drugs at high supplemental doses (not verified)"], "upper_limits": "IOM UL: 3.5 g/day adults (19+), 3 g/day 14-18 y, 2 g/day 9-13 y, 1 g/day 1-8 y, total intake from food and supplements [iom1998]. EFSA set Adequate Intakes (400 mg/day adults) but no UL was identified in this unit.", "sleep_impact": "No data found on sleep effects of dietary or supplemental choline.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Dietary intakes studied for decades in cohorts; supplements studied for up to 9 months in children and through pregnancy in small trials.", "daily_use_appropriate": "yes", "special_populations": "Pregnancy and lactation have higher AIs (EFSA 480/520 mg/day); many prenatal supplements contain little choline (not verified here). Children have lower ULs. Vegetarians/vegans and people avoiding eggs may have low intakes. People with trimethylaminuria should avoid supplements.", "specialist_review_needed": ["clinical dietitian (intake adequacy, vegan/vegetarian formulations)", "obstetrician/midwife before any pregnancy-targeted product", "cardiologist or nephrologist input if high-dose choline salts are considered (TMAO)"], "hard_safety_gate": "pass", "gate_reason": "Food-level choline and supplementation up to the AI are far below the 3.5 g/day UL; use phosphatidylcholine or whole-food sources rather than gram doses of choline salts to avoid odour and TMAO increases."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Choline is a nutrient with EFSA Adequate Intakes [efsa2016]. Three Article 13(1) claims are authorised in Regulation (EU) 432/2012 for food containing at least 82.5 mg choline per 100 g or 100 ml or per single portion: normal lipid metabolism, maintenance of normal liver function, normal homocysteine metabolism. EFSA assessed neurological function, cognitive function (ID 1502) and brain development (ID 1503) claims in 2011 [efsa2011]; none is authorised. Choline salts (bitartrate, chloride) have a long history of food use and are not expected to be novel foods (Novel Food Catalogue entries not individually checked here); use in supplements is governed by national rules on 'other substances' (country-specific maxima not researched here). Claim conditions confirmed through secondary sources; EUR-Lex consolidated text not read.", "us": "Choline has a Daily Value of 550 mg (2016 labeling rule, per X5 regulatory synthesis) and an FDAMA nutrient-content claim notification for choline-containing foods (FDA page surfaced via web search 2026-10-25), so 'good source' (>=10% DV) or 'excellent source' (>=20% DV) claims are possible. Choline salts are used in foods and supplements (GRAS status of specific salts not individually verified). No FDA-authorised or qualified health claim links choline to cognition; structure/function statements must be truthful and substantiated (FTC).", "morocco": "Conventional foods fall under Law 28-07 (ONSSA); no Moroccan list of authorised health claims was found (see X6 synthesis), so any choline function claim is legally uncertain; Moroccan choline intake data were not found.", "permitted_claims_examples": ["Choline contributes to normal lipid metabolism (EU, Reg. 432/2012; >=82.5 mg choline per 100 g/100 ml or single portion)", "Choline contributes to the maintenance of normal liver function (EU, same condition)", "Choline contributes to normal homocysteine metabolism (EU, same condition)"], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "None for food sources or established choline salts in the EU or US; novel forms (e.g., alpha-GPC) are separate monographs.", "regulatory_gate": "pass"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "High. Choline is naturally abundant in eggs, liver, meat, fish and dairy; a food can reach the EU claim threshold (82.5 mg per portion) with about half an egg or a small fortification.", "formats": ["whole foods (eggs, see eggs monograph)", "fortified bars, cereals, shakes or meal replacements", "multivitamin-type supplements"], "stability": "Choline salts are stable to heat but hygroscopic (choline chloride especially); general food-chemistry knowledge, not researched in detail here.", "taste": "Choline salts can give bitter/salty and amine ('fishy') off-notes, particularly with TMA formation; not formally researched here.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Phosphatidylcholine (eggs, lecithin) and choline salts are both absorbed; salts raise TMAO and platelet reactivity, eggs and PC supplements do not [wilcox2021].", "dose_feasibility": "Feasible. 82.5 mg choline is roughly 200 mg choline bitartrate (choline cation is about 41% of the bitartrate by mass, agent calculation) or about half a large egg. The acute 2-2.5 g bitartrate doses tested in students fit a drink but were ineffective and odour-prone.", "cost_availability": "Choline salts are commodity ingredients; eggs are inexpensive and widely available in Morocco, EU and US.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 3.5, "effect_magnitude": 2, "safety": 8.5, "reproducibility": 3, "food_suitability": 8.5, "cost_access": 8, "rationale": "Evidence quality 3.5: best rows are grade C (observational dementia-risk associations at low intake; small null acute RCTs) and grade D for chronic effects in healthy young adults. Effect magnitude 2: acute effects null; cohort associations small, non-linear and absent above adequacy. Safety 8.5: food intakes very safe, UL 3.5 g/day; minus for odour and TMAO with salts. Reproducibility 3: cohort findings inconsistent across analyses; acute positive finding not replicated. Food suitability 8.5: naturally in common foods and easy to fortify to the EU claim level. Cost/access 8: cheap commodity ingredient and cheap whole-food sources."}, "research_gaps": ["RCT bringing low-intake adults (e.g., <250 mg/day, vegetarians) up to the AI with pre-registered cognitive outcomes over >=6 months", "Any chronic trial of choline adequacy in healthy young adults or students", "Mendelian randomization or biomarker-calibrated cohorts separating choline from egg/meat diet patterns", "Adequately powered replication of the prenatal 930 mg/day feeding trial with school-age outcomes", "Long-term clinical significance of TMAO increases from choline salts versus food forms", "Choline intake data for Morocco"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 5.1, "eligibility": "eligible", "safety_gate": "pass", "regulatory_gate": "pass", "best_grade_acute": "C", "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": "C", "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": null, "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"reaction_time": "C", "dementia_risk": "C", "episodic_memory": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 10, "n_studies": 28, "n_verified": 28, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 11, "n_systematic_reviews": 4, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["harm", "inconsistent", "insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "glycine", "name": "Glycine", "category": "E", "category_name": "Amino acids and related compounds", "aliases": ["glycine", "aminoacetic acid", "Bioglycin (branded form used in one trial)"], "food_sources": ["gelatin and collagen-rich foods (bone broth, skin, connective tissue)", "meat, poultry, fish (moderate amounts, less than collagen sources)", "dairy and eggs (small amounts)", "legumes (small amounts)", "synthesised endogenously from serine, choline and threonine; not considered an essential amino acid"], "active_constituents": ["glycine (smallest proteinogenic amino acid; co-agonist at NMDA receptors via the glycine-B site; agonist at inhibitory glycine receptors; glutathione precursor)"], "one_line_verdict": "A single pre-bedtime 3 g dose of glycine has modest, industry-funded evidence for improving next-day subjective sleepiness, fatigue and psychomotor vigilance after sleep restriction, but independent replication is thin, the sleep-quality literature is small and high-risk-of-bias, and no trial has tested glycine on memory or cognition in rested, non-sleep-restricted healthy young adults using modern methods.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. Glycine is a non-essential amino acid acting as an inhibitory neurotransmitter at glycine receptors and as an obligatory co-agonist at the NMDA receptor glycine-B site; oral glycine raises plasma and CSF glycine and, in animal work, lowers core body temperature via increased cutaneous blood flow, a mechanism proposed to underlie sleep-promoting effects given that sleep onset is normally accompanied by core-temperature decline [bannai2012review]. In whom, and how large, in the Brain Food target population. The most directly relevant human trial to the 'enhancement' question is a small industry-funded (Ajinomoto) study in which healthy adults underwent three nights of sleep restricted to 75% of usual duration; participants took 3 g glycine or placebo before bedtime, and the following day the glycine group showed a significant reduction in self-reported fatigue (visual analog scale), a tendency toward reduced sleepiness, and a significant improvement in a psychomotor vigilance test on a computerised performance battery [bannai2012frontneurol]. This is a short-term_days (3-night), sleep-restricted, small-sample trial; the sponsor's own earlier work (cited within the same Ajinomoto research programme) reported that glycine ingested before bedtime 'significantly ameliorated subjective sleep quality' in individuals with insomniac tendencies, but that finding is itself a company's own prior trial reviewed narratively rather than independently [bannai2012review]. A 2024 independent systematic review of glycine administration across eleven physiological systems in human adults found that 'longer-term glycine administration improved sleep in healthy populations,' but explicitly flagged that these supporting studies 'had small sample sizes with a high risk of bias,' and called for larger, more robust trials [soh2024]; the nervous-system domain showed the most consistent positive signal of all eleven systems reviewed, but the review's own caveat about bias applies directly to that signal. On cognition specifically (as opposed to subjective sleepiness/fatigue), the clearest trial is an older, non-sleep-restriction crossover study of a proprietary glycine preparation (Bioglycin) in healthy young students (mean age ~21) and middle-aged men: glycine significantly improved retrieval from episodic memory in both age groups and improved sustained attention specifically in the middle-aged group, without stimulant properties or mood effects, and without improving focused or divided attention in either group [file1999]. This shows a memory-specific, non-stimulant profile distinct from caffeine-type arousal agents, but it is a single, now 25-year-old trial using a specific branded preparation not necessarily equivalent to plain glycine, and it has not been clearly replicated with modern instruments in healthy young adults. A 2026 narrative review on glycine (and N-acetylcysteine, as 'GlyNAC') in brain health and functional aging describes mixed and preliminary human and animal evidence for combined glycine+NAC supplementation improving oxidative stress, mitochondrial function and cognition, primarily in older or frailty-risk populations, not rested healthy young adults, and recommends confirmation in larger cohorts [wang2026glynac]; this evidence must not be generalised to the healthy young-adult/student population central to Brain Food's positioning. Deficiency vs enhancement and sleep-restricted vs rested distinction. All the positive human signal clusters specifically around partially sleep-restricted or self-reported poor sleepers, i.e., a state-dependent benefit rather than a generalised enhancement in rested, adequately nourished young adults; no trial in this search tested glycine's cognitive effect in rested healthy adults without any sleep manipulation. Clinically relevant glycine research in schizophrenia uses much higher doses (tens of grams/day) in a psychiatric population and is excluded from this monograph per the brief's population-separation rule; it should never be used to infer a healthy-adult dose-response relationship. What is unknown. Independent (non-Ajinomoto-funded) replication of the 3 g pre-bedtime sleep-restriction finding; any chronic (multi-week) trial in students under real academic workload; and a modern memory/attention replication of the 1999 Bioglycin finding in plain glycine. The honest summary is that glycine is a biologically plausible, very likely safe, sweet-tasting amino acid with modest industry-sponsored evidence for easing the next-day cognitive cost of acute sleep restriction, and one old but methodologically interesting signal for memory enhancement independent of sleep state, but it is not an established general cognitive enhancer for rested healthy adults [bannai2012frontneurol, soh2024, file1999].", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Glycine is non-essential and synthesised endogenously from serine, choline and threonine; healthy omnivores and most vegetarians obtain additional glycine from diet (collagen/gelatin sources are the richest, but glycine is not considered a dietary essential). The human studies reviewed here are enhancement-type experiments (adding glycine on top of an ordinary diet), not deficiency-correction studies, and the strongest positive signal is specifically state-dependent: benefit after imposed sleep restriction [bannai2012frontneurol], not in a baseline-adequate, rested state. The one trial testing rested, non-sleep-restricted cognition (File 1999, using a branded Bioglycin preparation) found a memory benefit without any sleep manipulation, which is the closest available evidence to a true 'enhancement in adequately nourished people' claim, but it stands alone and is old [file1999]. No evidence here supports a deficiency-correction narrative for glycine in healthy adults.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Sleep-supplement marketing commonly presents glycine as improving sleep quality and next-day mental sharpness generally. The evidence supports a narrower claim: a single 3 g pre-bedtime dose reduced next-day fatigue and improved psychomotor vigilance specifically after three nights of imposed sleep restriction in a small, company-funded (Ajinomoto) trial [bannai2012frontneurol], and an independent 2024 systematic review found supporting sleep studies in healthy populations to carry a high risk of bias despite being the most consistent positive signal among eleven physiological systems reviewed [soh2024]. A separate, older trial suggests a memory-specific (not stimulant, not attention-wide) benefit in rested adults using a specific branded preparation [file1999], which has not been clearly replicated with plain glycine. Honest marketing for Brain Food would frame glycine as 'a sweet-tasting, well-tolerated amino acid with modest, partly industry-funded evidence for easing the next-day cost of short sleep, and an older, intriguing but unreplicated memory signal' rather than as a general nootropic or guaranteed sleep-quality solution; glycine and sleep claims for the general public are not currently EU-authorised, and claims should not draw on the much higher-dose schizophrenia literature, which addresses a different population and mechanism.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Glycine is absorbed intestinally and distributed systemically, raising plasma and CSF glycine levels. It acts as an inhibitory neurotransmitter via strychnine-sensitive glycine receptors (notably in the spinal cord, brainstem and hypothalamus) and as an obligatory co-agonist at the glycine-B site of the NMDA receptor, which is implicated in long-term potentiation and memory. In animal work, oral glycine lowers core body temperature via increased cutaneous (peripheral) blood flow, a physiological state associated with sleep onset; this is proposed, not proven in humans, as the mechanism for glycine's sleep-related effects. Human cognitive effects (memory retrieval, psychomotor vigilance after sleep loss) are measured outcomes but the underlying receptor-level human mechanism remains largely inferred from animal pharmacology.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Oral glycine -> intestinal absorption -> plasma and CSF glycine rise", "detail": "Demonstrated in the Ajinomoto research programme reviewed narratively; plasma and CSF glycine increases were measured directly in the authors' rat studies and inferred in human trials from the same programme [bannai2012review].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Increased peripheral glycine -> action at hypothalamic/suprachiasmatic glycine and NMDA receptor sites -> increased cutaneous blood flow and decreased core body temperature", "detail": "Established in rat studies (decreased core temperature, increased cutaneous blood flow, altered neuropeptide expression in suprachiasmatic nucleus); proposed, not directly confirmed, as the human mechanism [bannai2012review, bannai2012frontneurol].", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Lowered core temperature -> facilitated sleep onset / reduced sleep-restriction physiological stress", "detail": "Plausible given the known association between core-temperature decline and sleep onset in humans; not directly measured via core temperature in the human sleep-restriction cognitive trial itself [bannai2012frontneurol].", "evidence_level": "plausible"}, {"step": "NMDA receptor glycine-B co-agonism -> facilitation of long-term potentiation-related synaptic plasticity", "detail": "Established pharmacological mechanism in neuroscience generally; proposed as the basis for glycine's memory-retrieval effect in the Bioglycin trial, not directly measured in that trial [file1999].", "evidence_level": "plausible"}, {"step": "Measured human outcome: next-day fatigue/sleepiness/psychomotor vigilance after sleep restriction; episodic memory retrieval in rested adults", "detail": "Directly measured in [bannai2012frontneurol] (sleep-restricted) and [file1999] (rested, branded preparation); not yet connected mechanistically within a single human trial.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["glycine receptor (inhibitory, strychnine-sensitive)", "NMDA receptor glycine-B co-agonist site", "thermoregulatory (cutaneous blood flow / core temperature)", "glutathione precursor pathway (antioxidant, relevant to GlyNAC aging research) [wang2026glynac]"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "short_term_days", "domain": "cognitive_fatigue", "population": "sleep_deprived", "population_detail": "Healthy adults with sleep restricted to 75% of usual duration for 3 consecutive nights", "dose_or_intake": "3 g glycine before bedtime", "duration": "3 nights", "grade": "B", "direction": "benefit", "effect_summary": "Significant reduction in next-day fatigue (VAS) and a tendency toward reduced sleepiness; corroborated by questionnaire data; small industry-funded (Ajinomoto) trial.", "effect_estimate": "described as statistically significant for fatigue VAS; exact numeric values not reported in the abstract reviewed", "certainty_notes": "Single small sponsor-funded trial; per the brief's grading rule a single small industry-funded trial cannot exceed grade B; downgraded for imprecision and lack of independent replication.", "key_study_ids": ["bannai2012frontneurol"]}, {"timeframe": "short_term_days", "domain": "vigilance", "population": "sleep_deprived", "population_detail": "Same 3-night sleep-restriction cohort as above", "dose_or_intake": "3 g glycine before bedtime", "duration": "3 nights", "grade": "B", "direction": "benefit", "effect_summary": "Significant improvement in a computerised psychomotor vigilance test the day after glycine versus placebo in sleep-restricted participants.", "effect_estimate": "described as statistically significant; exact numeric psychomotor vigilance values not reported in the abstract reviewed", "certainty_notes": "Single small sponsor-funded trial; capped at grade B per industry-funding rule; downgraded further for small sample and lack of independent replication.", "key_study_ids": ["bannai2012frontneurol"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "sleep_related_cognition", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Pooled across 18 studies in healthy adults and 34 in diseased populations, durations up to 14 days (healthy) and 4 months (diseased), within an 11-system systematic review", "dose_or_intake": "varied across pooled studies (commonly 3 g pre-bedtime in the healthy-sleep subset)", "duration": "up to 14 days in healthy populations", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "The nervous system showed the most consistent positive signal of the 11 physiological systems reviewed, including improved sleep in healthy populations with longer-term glycine administration, but the review explicitly notes these supporting studies had small sample sizes and high risk of bias.", "effect_estimate": "not reported as a pooled effect size; qualitative synthesis only", "certainty_notes": "Downgraded for risk of bias (explicitly flagged by the review authors) and for small, heterogeneous underlying studies; the review itself calls for larger, more robust trials.", "key_study_ids": ["soh2024", "bannai2012review"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy students, mean age ~21, rested (no sleep manipulation), crossover design", "dose_or_intake": "Bioglycin (branded bioactive glycine preparation), acute dosing", "duration": "single-session testing per crossover arm", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Significant improvement in retrieval from episodic memory versus placebo in young students; no effect on focused or divided attention; effect was memory-specific and without stimulant or mood changes.", "effect_estimate": "described as statistically significant for episodic memory retrieval; exact numeric values not reported in the abstract reviewed", "certainty_notes": "Single trial, 25 years old, using a specific branded preparation (Bioglycin) rather than plain glycine; not independently replicated in healthy young adults with modern methods; downgraded for indirectness (branded formulation) and lack of replication.", "key_study_ids": ["file1999"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Same crossover trial, middle-aged men subgroup (mean age ~59) versus young students", "dose_or_intake": "Bioglycin, acute dosing", "duration": "single-session testing per crossover arm", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Middle-aged men (but not young students) showed significant benefit on sustained attention; the age-dependent pattern should not be generalised to a young-adult sustained-attention claim.", "effect_estimate": "described as statistically significant for middle-aged subgroup only; exact numeric values not reported in the abstract reviewed", "certainty_notes": "Single trial; the sustained-attention benefit is specific to middle-aged participants and absent in young adults, so cannot support a student/young-adult attention claim; downgraded for indirectness to the target population.", "key_study_ids": ["file1999"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Older or frailty-risk adults, GlyNAC (glycine + N-acetylcysteine) protocols, often combined with exercise", "dose_or_intake": "varied, combined glycine+NAC protocols", "duration": "multi-week to multi-month in cited trials", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "Narrative review describes preliminary and mixed evidence for combined glycine+NAC supplementation improving oxidative stress, mitochondrial function, and cognition in older/frailty-risk populations; explicitly preliminary and requiring confirmation.", "effect_estimate": "not reported as a pooled effect size; narrative synthesis of preliminary findings", "certainty_notes": "Mechanistic/preliminary; combined-ingredient design means glycine's isolated contribution cannot be determined; population (older/frailty) is explicitly distinct from healthy young adults/students.", "key_study_ids": ["wang2026glynac"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "Bioglycin (branded glycine preparation), acute", "onset": "within testing session (exact onset not reported)", "duration_studied": "single session per crossover arm", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "effect_estimate": "significant improvement in memory retrieval, no stimulant/mood effect", "certainty": "low", "safety": "no adverse events reported", "study_ids": ["file1999"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "3 nights (sleep-restricted)", "population": "sleep_deprived", "outcome": "next-day fatigue and psychomotor vigilance", "effect_estimate": "significant improvement vs placebo (exact numbers not reported in abstract)", "evidence_quality": "B", "limitations": "small sample, single industry-funded trial, no independent replication", "study_ids": ["bannai2012frontneurol"]}, {"duration": "up to 14 days (healthy populations, pooled)", "population": "mixed_adults", "outcome": "subjective sleep quality", "effect_estimate": "qualitative improvement reported; no pooled numeric estimate given in the review", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "small sample sizes and high risk of bias in underlying studies per the reviewing authors", "study_ids": ["soh2024"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Glycine is a common dietary amino acid generally regarded as well tolerated at supplemental doses in the low-gram range (e.g., 3 g/day as studied); it has a sweet taste and is used in food manufacturing. The healthy-adult sleep and memory trials reviewed here reported no notable adverse events at 3 g/day or branded-preparation acute doses. Much higher doses (tens of grams/day) have been studied in schizophrenia research, a distinct clinical population and dose range excluded from this monograph's healthy-adult conclusions per the brief's population-separation rule; gastrointestinal upset has been anecdotally associated with very high gram-range doses in that literature, not with the low-gram doses relevant to Brain Food.", "adverse_effects": ["no significant adverse events reported in the healthy-adult trials reviewed at ~3 g/day or branded acute doses", "gastrointestinal upset reported anecdotally at much higher (tens of grams/day) doses used in psychiatric research, not generalisable to low-gram food-relevant doses"], "contraindications": ["no specific contraindication for ordinary low-gram glycine intake identified in the healthy-adult literature reviewed"], "drug_interactions": ["no well-established major human drug interaction identified in this search at food-relevant doses; theoretical NMDA/glycine-receptor interactions with NMDA-modulating psychiatric medications are a plausibility flag for specialist review, not a demonstrated interaction at low doses"], "upper_limits": "No formal EU or US numeric upper limit for glycine as a food/supplement ingredient was identified in this search; studied healthy-adult doses in the sleep and memory literature were in the low-gram range (3 g/day and branded-preparation acute doses), far below the tens-of-grams/day doses studied in psychiatric populations.", "sleep_impact": "The principal proposed benefit is sleep-related: glycine at 3 g before bedtime is associated with reduced next-day fatigue and improved psychomotor vigilance after sleep restriction, with a proposed (not human-confirmed) thermoregulatory mechanism; no evidence of sleep disruption was found.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Longer-term (multi-week to 4-month) human exposure has been studied mainly in diseased populations; the systematic review found only up-to-14-day data in healthy populations, so genuinely long-term (months to years) safety data in healthy adults are limited [soh2024].", "daily_use_appropriate": "probably", "special_populations": "Pregnancy, children and chronic-disease/medication users: no dedicated safety data in these groups were identified in this search at food-relevant glycine doses; the schizophrenia literature uses a different population, dose range and clinical context and should not be used to infer safety or dosing for these groups. Psychiatric patients on NMDA-modulating medication: theoretical interaction plausibility warrants specialist review before combining with glycine-containing products.", "specialist_review_needed": ["clinical pharmacology/psychiatry review for any patient on NMDA-receptor-modulating medication before recommending glycine supplementation", "obstetric/paediatric nutrition review before any pregnancy- or child-directed positioning, as no dedicated safety data in these groups were found"], "hard_safety_gate": "pass", "gate_reason": "No safety signal was identified at the low-gram doses (around 3 g/day) relevant to Brain Food's likely formulation range; glycine is a common dietary amino acid with a long history of food use, even though long-term (multi-month to multi-year) safety data in healthy adults specifically are limited."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Glycine is an authorised food additive/ingredient (used, for example, as a flavour enhancer and in food supplements) in the EU; no EU-authorised health claim for glycine and sleep quality or cognitive function was identified in this search (Regulation (EC) 1924/2006 claims register). Any sleep- or cognition-related marketing claim for glycine is not currently EU-authorised.", "us": "Glycine is Generally Recognized As Safe (GRAS) for use in food in the US and is widely used as a dietary supplement ingredient under DSHEA; it is not FDA-approved as a drug for sleep or cognition. No FTC-substantiated sleep- or cognition-enhancement claim for glycine was identified in this search; any such claim would require competent and reliable scientific evidence, which the current small, partly industry-funded trial base may not fully satisfy.", "morocco": "No Morocco-specific regulatory text on glycine was identified in this search; as a common food-grade amino acid, de facto ONSSA tolerance for food use is likely, but a confirmed ONSSA/Ministry of Health position on any sleep- or cognition-related claim is uncertain and should be verified directly with ONSSA before any formulation claim.", "permitted_claims_examples": [], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "Glycine has a long history of food use (flavour enhancer, amino acid supplement) in the EU and US and is not treated as a novel food/new dietary ingredient at standard food-use levels in either market; no NDI notification issues were identified in this search.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Glycine is highly water-soluble, heat-stable, and notably sweet-tasting, making it unusually suitable for food and beverage formulation without the bitterness issues common to many amino acids.", "formats": ["pre-bedtime beverage or shot", "powder stick/sachet dissolved in water", "capsule/tablet supplement", "functional sleep-support food (e.g., fortified yogurt or snack, subject to further taste/stability testing)"], "stability": "Chemically stable amino acid; expected to tolerate standard food and beverage processing and storage without specific stability concerns identified in this search.", "taste": "Distinctly sweet at supplemental doses, which can simplify formulation (less need for added sweeteners) but may require balancing against other flavour notes.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Oral glycine is well absorbed; no food-matrix-specific bioavailability study was located in this search, but no major absorption barrier is expected given its small molecular size and established gut transport mechanisms.", "dose_feasibility": "The studied effective dose (3 g before bedtime) fits comfortably within a single beverage, powder-stick or capsule serving, and is low-cost and technically straightforward to formulate.", "cost_availability": "Glycine is an inexpensive, widely available commodity amino acid used at industrial scale in food and supplement manufacturing.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 4, "effect_magnitude": 3, "safety": 8, "reproducibility": 3, "food_suitability": 8, "cost_access": 8, "rationale": "evidence_quality=4: best evidence is grade B capped by single-industry-funding rule (sleep-restriction fatigue/vigilance) plus a grade C memory signal (branded preparation, 25 years old, unreplicated); effect_magnitude=3: modest, state-dependent (sleep-restriction) or formulation-specific (Bioglycin) effects, not a general rested-state enhancement shown; safety=8: common food amino acid, no adverse signal at studied low-gram doses, though long-term healthy-adult data are limited; reproducibility=3: core sleep-restriction finding comes from one sponsor's research programme without independent replication, and the memory finding is a single old trial; food_suitability=8: sweet, stable, easy to dose in a beverage/powder; cost_access=8: cheap, widely available."}, "research_gaps": ["No independent (non-Ajinomoto) replication of the 3 g pre-bedtime sleep-restriction fatigue/vigilance finding was located.", "No modern replication of the 1999 Bioglycin memory-retrieval finding in healthy young adults using plain (unbranded) glycine.", "No chronic (multi-week), rested (non-sleep-restricted) cognitive trial of glycine in students or knowledge workers under real academic/work load exists.", "Long-term (multi-month to multi-year) safety data for glycine supplementation specifically in healthy adults are limited.", "Glycine's isolated cognitive contribution within combined GlyNAC (glycine+N-acetylcysteine) protocols has not been separated from NAC's contribution."], "_derived": {"composite_score": 5.3, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "pass", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": "C", "best_grade_chronic": "B", "best_grade_young_adults": "C", "best_grade_sleep_stress": "B", "best_grade_surrogate": null, "best_grade_overall": "B", "benefit_domains": {"cognitive_fatigue": "B", "vigilance": "B", "sleep_related_cognition": "C", "episodic_memory": "C", "sustained_attention": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 6, "n_studies": 5, "n_verified": 5, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 0, "n_systematic_reviews": 1, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["benefit", "insufficient", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "l-theanine", "name": "L-theanine", "category": "E", "category_name": "Amino acids and related compounds", "aliases": ["theanine", "L-theanine", "gamma-glutamylethylamide", "N-ethyl-L-glutamine", "5-N-ethylglutamine", "Suntheanine (Taiyo Kagaku trade name)", "AlphaWave L-theanine (trade name)"], "food_sources": ["Tea leaves (Camellia sinensis): green, black, oolong, white tea; roughly 8-25 mg per brewed cup depending on cultivar, shading and steeping (Nobre 2008 cites ~20 mg per cup of black tea; Kahathuduwa 2017 equates 200 mg to about eight cups of black tea)", "Matcha and shade-grown teas (gyokuro) are richer in theanine per gram", "Xerocomus badius (bay bolete mushroom) in trace amounts", "Isolated L-theanine: enzymatic/fermentation synthesis or extraction from green tea, sold as powder, capsules, tablets, beverages"], "active_constituents": ["L-theanine (the L-enantiomer; some synthetic material may contain D-theanine, so enantiomeric purity should be specified on the certificate of analysis - this quality point is general industry knowledge and was not verified by a study in this unit)", "In vivo hydrolysis products: ethylamine and L-glutamic acid (Scheid 2012)"], "one_line_verdict": "L-theanine (100-400 mg) is a safe compound with modest, task-specific acute benefits on selective attention and choice reaction time (tens of milliseconds) and on subjective stress in healthy adults, clearer when combined with caffeine; evidence for memory, executive function, chronic cognitive benefit or any effect at ordinary tea doses is weak or absent, and the EU does not permit any health claim for it.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. The best-supported effect of isolated L-theanine is a small acute improvement in attention-type reaction-time tasks in healthy adults 30-60 min after 100-400 mg. Three meta-analyses converge: choice reaction time improved versus placebo (SMD -0.35, 95% CI -0.61 to -0.10, about -18 ms, I2 = 0%) while simple reaction time did not [payne2025]; SMD 0.51 (0.25-0.77) for choice reaction time after a single 200 mg dose across 31 RCTs, n = 1168 [gerolymos2026]; recognition/RVIP reaction time -15.2 ms (-29.0 to -1.4) but no effect on simple reaction time or Stroop in 5 RCTs, n = 148 [matyus2025]. Individual trials show the same task specificity: faster recognition reaction time and larger N2-P300 at 200 mg [kahathuduwa2017], target reaction time -27.8 ms with reduced distractor-related fMRI activity [kahathuduwa2018], shorter P3b latency only at 400 mg [dassanayake2022], simple visuomotor reaction time -16 ms at 100-200 mg but no gain in sustained attention or inhibitory control after placebo correction [dassanayake2023], and fewer false alarms in sleep-deprived young adults without a between-treatment reaction-time difference [karunaratne2025]. EEG alpha changes at 50-250 mg [nobre2008, gomezramirez2007] are biomarkers, not performance. Contradicting data: 250 mg increased headache ratings and worsened serial sevens [haskell2008]; 200 mg slowed a visual-probe task and left alertness unchanged [rogers2008]; 100 mg alone was behaviourally inert [kelly2008]; 50 mg (tea-equivalent) did nothing [dodd2015]. For stress, the pooled acute reduction is modest (SMD 0.31) and driven by high risk-of-bias studies [gerolymos2026]; small trials show lower heart-rate and salivary IgA responses (n = 12) [kimura2007], lower subjective stress and salivary amylase in pharmacy students over 17 days (single-blind, n = 20) [unno2013], blunted blood-pressure rises [yoto2012] and a reduced stress response 1 h after a theanine drink [white2016], whereas 200 mg was not anxiolytic under induced anxiety [lu2004] and 450-900 mg/day for 8 weeks failed in generalised anxiety disorder [sarris2019]. In whom and for how long. Almost all cognitive data are single-dose crossover studies in healthy adults aged 20-35 with 9-48 participants. Chronic data are thin: 4 weeks of 200 mg/day in 30 adults (mean age 48) improved sleep subscales but no cognitive change differed from placebo [hidese2019]; 12 weeks of ~100 mg/day in 50 adults aged 50-69 produced no chronic cognitive effect [baba2021]. Sleep meta-analysis shows small subjective gains (sleep quality SMD 0.43, 0.04-0.83) with few pure-theanine trials [bulman2025]; the only actigraphy trial is in boys with ADHD [lyon2011]. Combination with caffeine. Theanine 97-250 mg plus caffeine 40-160 mg improves attention-switching accuracy, alertness and some reaction-time measures within 2 h [haskell2008, owen2008, giesbrecht2010, einother2010, kelly2008, nawarathna2025]; pooled h2 SMDs are 0.20-0.33 [payne2025], and moderator analysis attributes more of the change to caffeine dose than to theanine dose [camfield2014]. Two trials found no added benefit over caffeine alone [foxe2012, kahathuduwa2017], and at tea-equivalent doses theanine abolished caffeine's cognitive and haemodynamic effects without producing a benefit [dodd2015]. How large and how reproducible. Effects are small to moderate (SMD 0.2-0.5; 15-40 ms), confined to attention and alertness; there is no credible evidence for memory, learning or intelligence. Positive findings come from at least four independent groups, but many trials are industry-funded (Unilever, Taiyo Kagaku, ITO EN, Lipton), samples are small and the newest meta-analyses stress imprecision [payne2025, matyus2025]. Unknowns: months-long daily dosing in students or knowledge workers, dose-response above 400 mg, moderation by trait anxiety, objective sleep in adults, and whether tea-level intakes (8-25 mg per cup) do anything at all.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "L-theanine is not a nutrient: there is no requirement, no deficiency state and no biomarker of inadequacy. Every claimed effect is pharmacological enhancement in adequately nourished people, and the relevant question is whether 100-400 mg (far above the ~8-25 mg in a cup of tea) changes attention or stress responses. Nobre 2008 is the only study suggesting an EEG effect at a near-dietary 50 mg dose, and Dodd 2015 found 50 mg behaviourally inert; the attention findings therefore apply to supplement-level doses, not to normal tea drinking. In people with a lower baseline (higher trait anxiety, lower verbal fluency, sleep-deprived), subgroup analyses suggest larger responses [white2016, hidese2019, karunaratne2025], but these are post hoc and need confirmation.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Marketing presents theanine as a 'focus without jitters' nootropic, a 'calm energy' partner for caffeine and a sleep aid. The evidence supports only narrow statements: a single 200 mg dose can shorten choice/recognition reaction time by roughly 15-20 ms and raise P300 amplitude within an hour, and theanine-caffeine combinations can improve attention switching and self-rated alertness for about two hours, with much of the benefit coming from the caffeine. Claims about memory, learning, 'brain health', long-term cognitive protection or anxiety treatment are not supported, and the largest clinical anxiety trial was negative [sarris2019]. Regulatory reality is stricter still: EFSA rejected all theanine health claims in 2011 and the EU register contains no authorised claim, so none of these statements may appear on an EU label; in the US only structure/function wording with the DSHEA disclaimer and FTC-grade substantiation is possible. The honest consumer message is 'may help you feel calmer and slightly more focused for an hour or two, especially with caffeine; it will not make you smarter.'", "mechanism": {"summary": "Orally ingested L-theanine is absorbed rapidly (plasma peak ~0.8 h after 100 mg, dose-proportional AUC, identical kinetics from capsules or green tea) and hydrolysed to ethylamine and glutamate [scheid2012]. In rats it crosses the blood-brain barrier via the leucine-preferring transporter and raises striatal dopamine and serotonin, with NMDA-receptor-dependent dopamine release [yokogoshi1998]; it is structurally similar to glutamate and is described as a low-affinity glutamate-receptor antagonist and glutamine-transporter inhibitor, and it is reported to increase GABA. In humans the most consistent neurophysiological signature is a change in alpha-band oscillations (increased resting alpha at 50-200 mg; reduced tonic alpha and enhanced attention-related alpha during demanding tasks at 250 mg), reduced fMRI responses to distractors and default-mode activity, and larger/faster P300 components. These biomarker changes plausibly link to the behavioural reaction-time effects but have not been shown to mediate them.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Ingestion of 50-400 mg L-theanine -> rapid intestinal absorption, plasma Tmax ~0.8 h, dose-proportional exposure, partial hydrolysis to ethylamine + glutamate", "detail": "Human crossover PK study, n = 12 (+3 for 50/200 mg), capsules vs green tea equivalent [scheid2012]", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Plasma theanine -> brain uptake across the blood-brain barrier via leucine-preferring (L-type) amino-acid transport", "detail": "Rat intragastric dosing with brain amino-acid measurement [yokogoshi1998]; human brain concentrations not measured", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Brain theanine -> modulation of glutamatergic signalling (low-affinity glutamate-receptor antagonism, glutamine transport inhibition) and increased striatal dopamine/serotonin, GABA", "detail": "Rat microdialysis and receptor studies [yokogoshi1998]; cited as mechanism in reviews [dashwood2025]", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Altered cortical excitability -> changes in alpha-band EEG/MEG oscillations (resting alpha increase; task-related tonic alpha decrease with enhanced attention-related alpha)", "detail": "Human EEG at 50 mg [nobre2008] and 250 mg [gomezramirez2007]; MEG at trait-anxiety subgroup [white2016]; no alpha effect at 100 mg during SART [foxe2012]", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Reduced distractor processing and default-mode (mind-wandering) activity -> more efficient allocation of attention to targets", "detail": "fMRI in 9 men, 200 mg [kahathuduwa2018]; P3b latency reduced at 400 mg [dassanayake2022]; N2-P300 amplitude increased at 200 mg [kahathuduwa2017]", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "-> Measured human outcome: faster choice/recognition reaction time (~15-20 ms), small gains in attention-switching accuracy, reduced false alarms; no effect on memory or executive control", "detail": "Meta-analyses [payne2025, matyus2025, gerolymos2026] and individual RCTs [dassanayake2023, karunaratne2025]", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Attenuation of sympathetic stress responses (heart rate, salivary IgA/amylase, blood-pressure rise) -> lower subjective stress", "detail": "Small trials [kimura2007, yoto2012, unno2013, white2016]; null under induced anxiety [lu2004] and in GAD [sarris2019]", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Interaction with caffeine: theanine antagonises caffeine-induced blood-pressure rise and cerebral vasoconstriction while preserving or enhancing attention effects", "detail": "Rogers 2008 BP antagonism [rogers2008]; NIRS study [dodd2015]; additive N2-P300 effects [kahathuduwa2017]", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["Glutamate receptor antagonism / glutamine transport inhibition (animal, in vitro)", "Dopamine and serotonin modulation in striatum/hippocampus (animal)", "GABA increase (animal; frequently cited, weak human evidence)", "Alpha-band oscillatory modulation (human EEG/MEG biomarker)", "Reduced default-mode/distractor processing (human fMRI, n = 9)", "Sympathetic dampening (heart rate, BP, salivary IgA/amylase)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "reaction_time", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults ~20-35 y, mostly university volunteers, crossover designs n = 9-48", "dose_or_intake": "100-400 mg L-theanine alone (most data at 200 mg)", "duration": "single dose, tested 30-60 min post-dose", "grade": "B", "direction": "benefit", "effect_summary": "Choice/recognition reaction time improves by roughly 15-20 ms; simple reaction time does not improve in pooled analyses (one trial found ~16 ms improvement at 100-200 mg); P300 amplitude/latency changes consistent with faster target processing.", "effect_estimate": "Choice RT SMD -0.35 (95% CI -0.61 to -0.10), re-expressed -18.2 ms (-31.4 to -4.9), I2 0% [payne2025]; CRT SMD 0.51 (0.25-0.77) at 200 mg [gerolymos2026]; RVIP/RVRT MD -15.20 ms (-28.99 to -1.41) [matyus2025]; simple RT MD -0.46 ms (-15.65 to 14.73) [matyus2025]", "certainty_notes": "Several small RCTs from at least three independent groups with consistent direction for choice RT; downgraded for imprecision (wide CIs, n < 50 per trial), task-specificity (null simple RT, null Stroop), some industry funding, and contradictory single trials [rogers2008, haskell2008]. Not A because no large confirmatory trial exists.", "key_study_ids": ["payne2025", "gerolymos2026", "matyus2025", "kahathuduwa2017", "dassanayake2023", "dassanayake2022", "rogers2008", "haskell2008"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy young adults; SART over 2 h [foxe2012], RVP [dassanayake2023], RVIP [haskell2008, dodd2015]", "dose_or_intake": "100-400 mg", "duration": "single dose, 0-2 h", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "100 mg reduced time-on-task error increase on the SART (as did caffeine; no additive benefit); 200-400 mg did not improve RVP after placebo correction; RVIP accuracy pooled effect tiny (SMD 0.17 at 400 mg).", "effect_estimate": "RVIP accuracy h2 SMD 0.17 (0.10-0.23) at 400 mg, 0.18 (-0.01 to 0.37) at 200 mg [payne2025]; SART error reduction significant vs placebo, no numeric effect size in abstract [foxe2012]", "certainty_notes": "Few trials, different tasks, inconsistent; small effects of doubtful practical size.", "key_study_ids": ["foxe2012", "dassanayake2023", "payne2025", "haskell2008"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Stroop, stop-signal, serial subtractions in healthy adults", "dose_or_intake": "50-400 mg", "duration": "single dose", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "No improvement in Stroop reaction time or inhibitory control; 250 mg worsened serial-sevens accuracy in one trial.", "effect_estimate": "Stroop congruent MD -37.38 ms (-86.39 to 11.62); incongruent MD +109.28 ms (-8.72 to 227.27, I2 99%) [matyus2025]; SST no effect [dassanayake2023]; serial 7s decreased correct responses [haskell2008]", "certainty_notes": "Consistent absence of benefit across three trials; very high heterogeneity in one pooled estimate.", "key_study_ids": ["matyus2025", "dassanayake2023", "haskell2008", "dodd2015"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Numeric working memory, n-back in healthy adults", "dose_or_intake": "200-250 mg", "duration": "single dose", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "Theanine alone did not improve working-memory tasks in young adults; the only positive 2-back result is in older adults [baba2021]; combination with caffeine sped numeric working memory RT in one trial.", "effect_estimate": "not reported for theanine alone in young adults (null in [haskell2008])", "certainty_notes": "Essentially unstudied as a primary outcome in young adults.", "key_study_ids": ["haskell2008", "baba2021"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Word recognition / recall in healthy adults", "dose_or_intake": "100-250 mg", "duration": "single dose", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No evidence that theanine alone improves memory; combination with caffeine reduced distractor susceptibility in a memory task [owen2008] and showed a caffeine x theanine interaction on delayed word recognition RT [haskell2008].", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "Memory is not a demonstrated target; any marketing of memory benefit is unsupported.", "key_study_ids": ["haskell2008", "owen2008", "baba2021"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults exposed to laboratory stressors (mental arithmetic, multitasking framework, anticipatory anxiety)", "dose_or_intake": "200 mg (White 2016 drink also contained other ingredients)", "duration": "single dose, 0-3 h", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Reduced heart-rate and salivary IgA stress responses (n = 12), lower subjective stress 1 h post-dose and lower cortisol at 3 h after a theanine drink (n = 34), attenuated blood-pressure rise in high responders (n = 14); but no anxiolytic effect under induced anticipatory anxiety (n = 16) and no change in alertness/jitteriness vs placebo (n = 48).", "effect_estimate": "Acute stress pooled SMD 0.31, largely driven by high risk-of-bias studies [gerolymos2026]; White 2016 stress response 1 h: active -13.7 vs placebo +1.6 (p = 0.003)", "certainty_notes": "Small samples, heterogeneous stressors and measures, industry involvement (Taiyo) in several, one clear null; subjective outcomes susceptible to expectancy despite blinding.", "key_study_ids": ["kimura2007", "white2016", "yoto2012", "lu2004", "rogers2008", "gerolymos2026"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "reaction_time", "population": "sleep_deprived", "population_detail": "Overnight sleep-deprived healthy adults 20-30 y, traffic-scene discrimination task", "dose_or_intake": "200 mg theanine alone; 200 mg theanine + 160 mg caffeine", "duration": "single dose, 45-50 min", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Theanine alone reduced false alarms and improved discriminability but reaction-time change did not differ from placebo (n = 24); theanine+caffeine improved hit rate, discriminability, reaction time and P3b (n = 37).", "effect_estimate": "Theanine alone: hit RT -38.65 ms vs placebo -19.08 ms, between-treatment NS [karunaratne2025]; combination: RT improvement 38.1 ms greater than placebo (95% CI 13.5-62.7), P3b latency ~-30 ms [nawarathna2025]", "certainty_notes": "Two trials from one Sri Lankan group; caffeine likely drives most of the combination effect; no caffeine-alone arm in the 2025 trial.", "key_study_ids": ["karunaratne2025", "nawarathna2025"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Japanese adults 50-69 y with MMSE >= 24, parallel groups n = 26 vs 24", "dose_or_intake": "100.6 mg single dose", "duration": "single dose, ~60 min", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Faster Stroop part-1 simple reaction time (337 vs 365 ms) and more correct 2-back responses with fewer omissions after a single dose; no effect on memory, visual processing or motor tasks.", "effect_estimate": "Stroop part 1 RT 337 +/- 84 vs 365 +/- 62 ms (p < 0.017); 4-part CPT part 4 correct 13.4 vs 12.1 (p < 0.017) [baba2021]", "certainty_notes": "Single industry-sponsored (ITO EN) trial, multiple comparisons, parallel design with small n; cannot exceed C.", "key_study_ids": ["baba2021", "payne2025"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Japanese adults 50-69 y, 12 weeks", "dose_or_intake": "~100 mg/day", "duration": "12 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "No significant between-group cognitive differences after 12 weeks of daily intake on any Cognitrax domain or MMSE-J; blood biomarkers unchanged.", "effect_estimate": "No significant differences reported at 12 weeks [baba2021]", "certainty_notes": "Single trial, adequately powered only for large effects; null for chronic use.", "key_study_ids": ["baba2021"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "30 healthy Japanese adults 20-69 y (mean 48), 70% women, crossover", "dose_or_intake": "200 mg/day", "duration": "4 weeks per arm", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Within-arm improvements in BACS verbal fluency and executive function after theanine, but no cognitive score change differed significantly between theanine and placebo in the primary comparison; a post hoc lower-baseline subgroup showed greater letter-fluency gains (6.0 vs -0.07).", "effect_estimate": "Verbal fluency change 4.57 vs 2.33 (p = 0.22); executive function change 1.10 vs 0.33 (p = 0.43); lower-half subgroup verbal fluency 7.93 vs 0.13 (p = 0.002, r = 0.57) [hidese2019]", "certainty_notes": "Single crossover trial, Taiyo-supplied product with employee co-authors, non-parametric tests without multiplicity correction, subgroup post hoc; overall no robust chronic cognitive effect.", "key_study_ids": ["hidese2019"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults (Hidese: 20-69 y); pharmacy students under practice stress (Unno: 22 y, n = 20, single-blind)", "dose_or_intake": "200-400 mg/day", "duration": "17 days to 4 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Trait anxiety, depression scale and PSQI improved within the theanine arm over 4 weeks, with only PSQI subscales (latency, disturbance, medication use) significantly better than placebo; students taking 400 mg/day reported less subjective stress and lower morning salivary amylase.", "effect_estimate": "STAI-trait change -3.37 vs +0.77 (p = 0.13); SDS change -2.53 vs -0.07 (p = 0.084); PSQI sleep latency subscale p = 0.0499 [hidese2019]; subjective stress p = 0.020, sAA p = 0.032 [unno2013]", "certainty_notes": "Small, partly single-blind, industry-linked; between-treatment differences mostly non-significant.", "key_study_ids": ["hidese2019", "unno2013", "williams2020"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "sleep_related_cognition", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy and clinical populations of all ages; 19 RCTs (N = 897), few using pure theanine", "dose_or_intake": "mostly 200-400 mg/day (many multi-ingredient products)", "duration": "days to weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Small improvements in subjective sleep quality, sleep-onset latency and daytime dysfunction; only objective trial is actigraphy in boys with ADHD (sleep efficiency improved, latency unchanged).", "effect_estimate": "Sleep quality SMD 0.43 (0.04-0.83, 12 studies); onset latency SMD 0.15 (0.01-0.29, 10 studies); daytime dysfunction SMD 0.33 (0.16-0.49, 9 studies) [bulman2025]", "certainty_notes": "Subjective outcomes, mixed products, wide CIs; not a cognitive outcome; GAD trial showed no ISI benefit overall [sarris2019].", "key_study_ids": ["bulman2025", "hidese2019", "lyon2011", "sarris2019"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "alertness", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults 18-40 y; theanine combined with caffeine (Unilever, Northumbria, Peradeniya trials)", "dose_or_intake": "97-250 mg theanine + 40-160 mg caffeine", "duration": "single dose, 10-120 min", "grade": "B", "direction": "benefit", "effect_summary": "Combination improves self-rated alertness and reduces tiredness/headache ratings and improves attention-switching accuracy and some reaction-time measures within 2 h; moderator analysis suggests caffeine dose explains more of the effect than theanine dose; no added benefit over caffeine alone in two trials; inert at 50 mg + 75 mg.", "effect_estimate": "Attention switching accuracy h2 SMD 0.33 (0.13-0.54); digit vigilance accuracy h2 SMD 0.20 (0.02-0.38); choice RT h1 SMD -0.48 (-1.01 to 0.05); simple RT h2 SMD -0.71 (-0.92 to -0.50, ~-26.5 ms) [payne2025]; moderate SMDs for Bond-Lader alertness and switching [camfield2014]", "certainty_notes": "More than ten RCTs with consistent direction for switching/alertness, but most are Unilever/Lipton-funded, samples small, incremental effect beyond caffeine uncertain [foxe2012, kahathuduwa2017, dodd2015]; B for the combination, C for theanine's incremental contribution.", "key_study_ids": ["camfield2014", "payne2025", "haskell2008", "owen2008", "giesbrecht2010", "einother2010", "kelly2008", "foxe2012", "dodd2015", "kahathuduwa2017"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "vascular_health", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults given caffeine with/without theanine; blood pressure and NIRS cerebral haemodynamics", "dose_or_intake": "50-200 mg theanine with 75-250 mg caffeine", "duration": "single dose", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Theanine antagonised the caffeine-induced rise in blood pressure (200 + 250 mg) and attenuated caffeine's reduction in cerebral oxygenated haemoglobin (50 + 75 mg); theanine alone blunted stress-induced BP rise in high responders.", "effect_estimate": "Direction reported; numeric BP differences not given in abstracts [rogers2008, dodd2015, yoto2012]", "certainty_notes": "Biomarker/physiological outcome, not cognition; small trials.", "key_study_ids": ["rogers2008", "dodd2015", "yoto2012"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Adults with DSM-5 generalised anxiety disorder on stable antidepressants (n = 46); adults with MDD on sertraline (n = 60)", "dose_or_intake": "450-900 mg/day (GAD); 200 mg/day (MDD)", "duration": "8 weeks (GAD); 6 weeks (MDD)", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Adjunctive theanine did not reduce HAMA anxiety (p = 0.73) or insomnia severity (p = 0.35) in GAD, with no cognitive effects; a single Iranian trial reported greater HDRS improvement as adjunct to sertraline (not read in full).", "effect_estimate": "GAD: HAMA p = 0.73; ISI p = 0.35; ISI item 4 p = 0.015 [sarris2019]; MDD: time x treatment p = 0.014 (secondary source only)", "certainty_notes": "Not a Brain Food target population; included to prevent extrapolation. Negative in the better-known trial.", "key_study_ids": ["sarris2019", "gerolymos2026"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "sleep_related_cognition", "population": "children_adolescents", "population_detail": "Boys 8-12 y with ADHD, n = 98, actigraphy", "dose_or_intake": "400 mg/day (2 x 200 mg Suntheanine)", "duration": "6 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Higher actigraphic sleep percentage and efficiency; latency unchanged; parent questionnaire did not correlate; no significant adverse events.", "effect_estimate": "Significant improvement in sleep percentage and efficiency; numeric values not in abstract [lyon2011]", "certainty_notes": "Single manufacturer-linked trial in a clinical paediatric population; must not be generalised to healthy adults.", "key_study_ids": ["lyon2011"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "students", "population_detail": "University students / knowledge workers taking theanine daily for weeks and tested on attention, memory or academic outcomes", "dose_or_intake": "any", "duration": ">= 2 weeks", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No trial has measured cognitive performance in students or knowledge workers after repeated daily theanine; the only student trial measured stress and sleep markers [unno2013].", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "Key evidence gap for Brain Food's target population.", "key_study_ids": ["unno2013", "hidese2019"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "50 mg (tea-equivalent)", "onset": "45-105 min (EEG)", "duration_studied": "single session", "domain": "resting alpha EEG / cognition", "population": "healthy young adults", "effect_estimate": "Greater resting alpha increase vs placebo (p < 0.05, n = 16 vs 19) [nobre2008]; no cognitive or mood effect at 50 mg [dodd2015]", "certainty": "low", "safety": "no adverse events reported", "study_ids": ["nobre2008", "dodd2015"]}, {"dose": "100 mg", "onset": "50-60 min", "duration_studied": "up to 2 h", "domain": "simple RT, SART vigilance, cued attention", "population": "healthy young adults", "effect_estimate": "Simple RT -16.3 ms vs placebo change (p = 0.009) [dassanayake2023]; reduced SART time-on-task errors [foxe2012]; no behavioural effect in cued-attention task [kelly2008]; no P3b change [dassanayake2022]", "certainty": "low-moderate", "safety": "no adverse events reported", "study_ids": ["dassanayake2023", "foxe2012", "kelly2008", "dassanayake2022"]}, {"dose": "200 mg", "onset": "30-60 min", "duration_studied": "1-3 h", "domain": "choice/recognition RT, selective attention, stress response", "population": "healthy young adults; sleep-deprived young adults", "effect_estimate": "CRT SMD 0.51 (0.25-0.77) [gerolymos2026]; RVRT improved p = 0.019, N2-P300 amplitude up [kahathuduwa2017]; target RT -27.8 ms [kahathuduwa2018]; simple RT -16.9 ms [dassanayake2023]; stress HR/s-IgA reduced [kimura2007]; no anxiolysis under induced anxiety [lu2004]; slower visual-probe RT, no mood change [rogers2008]", "certainty": "moderate for choice RT, low for stress", "safety": "no adverse events reported; possible mild drowsiness", "study_ids": ["gerolymos2026", "kahathuduwa2017", "kahathuduwa2018", "dassanayake2023", "kimura2007", "lu2004", "rogers2008", "karunaratne2025"]}, {"dose": "250 mg", "onset": "30-90 min", "duration_studied": "1.5 h", "domain": "CDR battery; intersensory attention EEG", "population": "healthy young adults", "effect_estimate": "Increased headache ratings, fewer correct serial 7s, no attention benefit [haskell2008]; reduced tonic alpha, enhanced attention-related alpha [gomezramirez2007]", "certainty": "low", "safety": "headache ratings increased in one trial", "study_ids": ["haskell2008", "gomezramirez2007"]}, {"dose": "400 mg", "onset": "50 min", "duration_studied": "single session", "domain": "P3b ERP, RVP sustained attention", "population": "healthy young adults", "effect_estimate": "P3b latency reduced (p < 0.05), ~4 ms per 100 mg [dassanayake2022]; RVP RT improved from baseline but not vs placebo change [dassanayake2023]; simple RT non-significantly worse in pooled analysis (MD +5.91 ms) [matyus2025]", "certainty": "low", "safety": "no adverse events reported", "study_ids": ["dassanayake2022", "dassanayake2023", "matyus2025"]}, {"dose": "97-100 mg theanine + 40-50 mg caffeine", "onset": "10-60 min", "duration_studied": "90 min", "domain": "attention switching, alertness, cued attention", "population": "healthy young adults", "effect_estimate": "Switching accuracy and alertness improved (p < 0.01), tiredness reduced [giesbrecht2010]; switching improved, alertness not [einother2010]; speed and accuracy of switching at 60 min, less distraction in memory task [owen2008]; d' and hit rate improved vs placebo [kelly2008]", "certainty": "moderate (industry-funded cluster)", "safety": "no adverse events reported", "study_ids": ["giesbrecht2010", "einother2010", "owen2008", "kelly2008"]}, {"dose": "50 mg theanine + 75 mg caffeine (1-2 cups tea)", "onset": "30 min", "duration_studied": "~1 h", "domain": "CDR battery, mood, NIRS CBF", "population": "healthy young adults (12 habitual + 12 non-habitual caffeine users)", "effect_estimate": "Caffeine alone improved CRT, Stroop and mood; adding theanine abolished these behavioural effects and attenuated caffeine's oxy-Hb reduction; no positive behavioural effect of the combination [dodd2015]", "certainty": "moderate", "safety": "no adverse events reported", "study_ids": ["dodd2015"]}, {"dose": "200 mg theanine + 160 mg caffeine", "onset": "50-60 min", "duration_studied": "~1 h", "domain": "selective attention RT, ERP, fMRI", "population": "healthy young men; sleep-deprived young adults", "effect_estimate": "RVRT improved p = 0.001, N2-P300 larger than either alone, no interaction [kahathuduwa2017]; target RT -26.7 ms [kahathuduwa2018]; sleep-deprived: RT improvement 38.1 ms more than placebo (13.5-62.7), P3b latency -30 ms, hit rate p = 0.02 [nawarathna2025]", "certainty": "moderate (one group)", "safety": "no adverse events reported", "study_ids": ["kahathuduwa2017", "kahathuduwa2018", "nawarathna2025"]}, {"dose": "250 mg theanine + 150 mg caffeine", "onset": "60-90 min", "duration_studied": "90 min", "domain": "CDR battery, mood", "population": "healthy young adults, n = 24", "effect_estimate": "Faster simple RT and numeric WM RT, better RVIP accuracy and sentence verification, less headache/tiredness, more alert; caffeine x theanine interaction on delayed word-recognition RT [haskell2008]", "certainty": "low-moderate", "safety": "no adverse events reported", "study_ids": ["haskell2008"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "17 days", "population": "5th-year pharmacy students (n = 20, 22 y) during practice placement", "outcome": "subjective stress, salivary alpha-amylase, STAI", "effect_estimate": "Subjective stress lower (p = 0.020); morning sAA lower (p = 0.032); no cognitive outcome", "evidence_quality": "low (single-blind, n = 20, Taiyo co-author)", "limitations": "Single-blind, tiny sample, no cognitive measure, industry co-author", "study_ids": ["unno2013"]}, {"duration": "4 weeks (crossover)", "population": "30 healthy adults 20-69 y (mean 48), Japan", "outcome": "SDS, STAI, PSQI, BACS cognition, TMT, Stroop", "effect_estimate": "PSQI subscales better than placebo (p ~0.05); SDS and PSQI total trends (p = 0.084, 0.073); no cognitive between-treatment difference; letter fluency better in lower-baseline subgroup (6.0 vs -0.07, p = 0.002)", "evidence_quality": "low-moderate", "limitations": "Industry product and co-authors, many outcomes, no multiplicity correction, subgroup post hoc, 100% completion but small n", "study_ids": ["hidese2019"]}, {"duration": "12 weeks (parallel)", "population": "50 Japanese adults 50-69 y, MMSE >= 24", "outcome": "Cognitrax battery, MMSE-J, blood chemistry", "effect_estimate": "No significant between-group difference at 12 weeks on any domain; biomarkers unchanged", "evidence_quality": "low-moderate (null)", "limitations": "ITO EN sponsored, ~100 mg/day (lower than acute-effective doses), small n", "study_ids": ["baba2021"]}, {"duration": "6 weeks (parallel)", "population": "98 boys 8-12 y with ADHD", "outcome": "actigraphic sleep", "effect_estimate": "Sleep percentage and efficiency higher; latency unchanged; well tolerated", "evidence_quality": "low (industry, paediatric clinical)", "limitations": "Not generalisable to healthy adults; manufacturer co-author", "study_ids": ["lyon2011"]}, {"duration": "8 weeks (parallel)", "population": "46 adults with GAD on antidepressants", "outcome": "HAMA, ISI, TMT, emotional Stroop", "effect_estimate": "No benefit on HAMA (p = 0.73) or ISI (p = 0.35); no cognitive effect; 450-900 mg/day tolerated", "evidence_quality": "moderate (null)", "limitations": "Clinical population, modest n, adjunctive design", "study_ids": ["sarris2019"]}, {"duration": "days to weeks (19 RCTs pooled)", "population": "healthy and clinical, all ages", "outcome": "subjective sleep", "effect_estimate": "Sleep quality SMD 0.43 (0.04-0.83); onset latency SMD 0.15 (0.01-0.29)", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Few pure-theanine trials, subjective outcomes, heterogeneous populations", "study_ids": ["bulman2025"]}], "safety": {"summary": "L-theanine has a benign safety record in human trials up to 900 mg/day for 8 weeks in adults [sarris2019] and 400 mg/day for 6 weeks in children [lyon2011]; meta-analysis of 31 RCTs found no serious adverse events and dropout rates comparable to placebo [gerolymos2026]. The only adverse signal in the trials reviewed is increased headache ratings and slightly worse serial subtraction after 250 mg alone [haskell2008] and slower reaction time on one task at 200 mg [rogers2008]. Theanine lowers stress- and caffeine-induced blood-pressure rises [rogers2008, yoto2012], which is theoretically relevant for people on antihypertensives or with hypotension. Regulatory toxicology (FDA GRAS notices GRN 209 and GRN 501 with 'no questions' letters, 90-day rat studies and genotoxicity in later notices) supports food use up to 250 mg per serving. No case reports of hepatotoxicity or serious toxicity surfaced in the PubMed safety search run for this unit. Long-term (> 12 weeks) human exposure data at supplement doses are absent; pregnancy and lactation data are absent.", "adverse_effects": ["Headache ratings increased after 250 mg alone in one crossover trial (n = 24) [haskell2008]", "Possible mild sedation/relaxation; slower visual-probe reaction time at 200 mg in one trial [rogers2008]", "Reduced blood-pressure rise under stress and with caffeine (beneficial for most, relevant for hypotension/antihypertensive users) [rogers2008, yoto2012]", "No serious adverse events in pooled RCTs; gastrointestinal complaints not reported above placebo [gerolymos2026]"], "contraindications": ["No established contraindications; avoid in pregnancy and lactation for lack of data (precautionary)", "Caution in people with hypotension or on antihypertensive medication (theoretical, based on BP-lowering effects)"], "drug_interactions": ["Antihypertensives: additive BP lowering (theoretical, small acute studies)", "Caffeine/stimulants: theanine attenuates caffeine's pressor and cerebral haemodynamic effects and, at tea-level doses, its cognitive effects [dodd2015]", "Sedatives/benzodiazepines: theoretical additive relaxation; no human interaction trial", "Chemotherapy (doxorubicin, etc.): animal data on modulation reported in reviews; clinical relevance unknown; oncology patients should consult their physician"], "upper_limits": "No EFSA or IOM tolerable upper intake level exists (not a nutrient). FDA GRAS notices accept up to 250 mg per serving in foods and beverages with no limit on servings (GRN 209, Taiyo, 2007; GRN 501, Zhejiang Tianrui, 2014, per FDA GRAS inventory as reported by secondary sources accessed 2026-10-25). Germany's BfR (2003 opinion) opposed addition of isolated theanine to beverages citing insufficient data. Human trials used up to 900 mg/day for 8 weeks without serious adverse events.", "sleep_impact": "Generally neutral to favourable: subjective sleep quality SMD 0.43 and onset latency SMD 0.15 in meta-analysis [bulman2025]; PSQI subscales improved after 4 weeks of 200 mg [hidese2019]; actigraphic sleep efficiency improved in boys with ADHD [lyon2011]. Daytime dosing does not cause measurable drowsiness in attention tasks, though relaxation is reported. When combined with caffeine, the caffeine component may impair sleep if taken late in the day.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Longest controlled exposures: 12 weeks at ~100 mg/day in adults 50-69 y with normal blood chemistry [baba2021]; 8 weeks at 450-900 mg/day in GAD [sarris2019]; 6 weeks at 400 mg/day in children [lyon2011]; 4 weeks at 200 mg/day [hidese2019]. No data beyond 12 weeks; decades of dietary exposure via tea at ~10-50 mg/day.", "daily_use_appropriate": "probably", "special_populations": "Pregnancy/lactation: no data, avoid supplemental doses. Children: 400 mg/day for 6 weeks tolerated in boys with ADHD but not studied in healthy children; not recommended for Brain Food products aimed at minors without paediatric review. Older adults: 100 mg/day for 12 weeks safe [baba2021]. Psychiatric patients: used adjunctively without safety signals [sarris2019] but efficacy unproven. Hypotension/antihypertensive users: caution.", "specialist_review_needed": ["Regulatory affairs (EU novel food status for non-supplement foods; claim prohibition)", "Clinical pharmacologist/toxicologist for upper-dose and interaction review if doses above 250 mg/serving are considered", "Dietitian for pregnancy/children positioning", "Quality/food scientist for enantiomeric purity and source (fermentation vs extraction) specification"], "hard_safety_gate": "pass", "gate_reason": "Food-level safety at 100-250 mg per serving supported by FDA GRAS notices, 31-trial meta-analysis with no serious adverse events, and controlled exposures up to 900 mg/day; only mild, inconsistent adverse signals (headache, slight RT slowing). Pregnancy and long-term (> 12 weeks) data gaps noted but do not block adult daily use."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Health claims: EFSA NDA Panel Scientific Opinion 2011 (EFSA Journal 2011;9(6):2238, DOI 10.2903/j.efsa.2011.2238, confirmed via Crossref 2026-10-25) assessed Article 13(1) claims on L-theanine from Camellia sinensis for improvement of cognitive function (ID 1104, 1222, 1600, 1601, 1707, 1935, 2004, 2005), alleviation of psychological stress (ID 1598, 1601), maintenance of normal sleep (ID 1222, 1737, 2004) and reduction of menstrual discomfort (ID 1599). The Panel found L-theanine sufficiently characterised but concluded that a cause and effect relationship had not been established; for cognition it noted that the three human studies from which conclusions could be drawn did not show an effect (wording confirmed from search snippets; the full opinion could not be fetched here - Wiley returned HTTP 403). No L-theanine claim appears in the EU Register of authorised claims; any cognition, stress, relaxation or sleep claim on an EU label is therefore prohibited under Regulation (EC) 1924/2006. A later Article 13(5) application on black tea and improvement of attention (EFSA Journal 2018, DOI 10.2903/j.efsa.2018.5266, confirmed via Crossref) was, to the author's knowledge, also not substantiated - full text not accessed, verify before relying on it. Novel food: according to the Finnish Food Authority's summary of the EU Novel Food Catalogue and a supplier notice (accessed 2026-10-25), L-theanine from green tea is considered not novel only in food supplements; use in other foods or beverages, and L-theanine not derived from green tea, requires novel food authorisation under Regulation (EU) 2015/2283. The Catalogue entry itself was not fetched directly - verify. Germany's BfR (2003 statement 'Getraenke mit isoliertem L-Theanin') opposed adding isolated theanine to beverages. Practical consequence: in the EU, Brain Food could sell theanine only as a food supplement (national notification rules apply) with no health claim, unless a novel food authorisation is obtained for foods/drinks.", "us": "Dietary supplement ingredient under DSHEA (marketed before 1994 in Japan; US history as a supplement since the late 1990s; no NDI objection known). Conventional food/beverage use supported by GRAS notices with FDA 'no questions' letters: GRN 209 (Taiyo International, enzymatically produced L-theanine/Suntheanine, filed Aug 2006, closed Feb 2007, up to 250 mg per serving in listed food categories, no serving-number limit) and GRN 501 (Zhejiang Tianrui, 2014); GRN 338 is also cited as an L-theanine notice; a newer notice (Zhejiang Zhenyuan) cites these and includes 90-day rat and genotoxicity studies (FDA document fda.gov/media/195022, outcome not confirmed). Sources: trade press (NutraIngredients-USA 2007-03-23) and a GRAS inventory mirror, accessed 2026-10-25; the FDA inventory page itself was not fetched. Only structure/function claims (e.g., 'supports relaxation', 'helps maintain focus') with the DSHEA disclaimer are permissible on supplements; disease claims (anxiety treatment) are not. FTC requires competent and reliable scientific evidence; the small, inconsistent trial base means claims must stay narrow (acute attention/relaxation) and avoid memory or 'brain health' wording.", "morocco": "No Moroccan text specific to L-theanine was found. Food supplements are regulated under the food-safety law 28-07 (ONSSA) and Ministry of Health rules; press reports (Medias24, 2026-07-15 and 2026-07-23; TelQuel 2026-06-11) describe a draft decree that would reserve certain substances (melatonin, red yeast rice, berberine, St John's wort, DHEA, yohimbine, 5-HTP, ginkgo, high-dose vitamin D) to pharmacies; theanine is not on the reported initial list, and adoption was postponed. Authorised-ingredient lists, maximum doses and claim rules for Morocco could not be confirmed online; status is therefore unknown and needs direct consultation of ONSSA, the Direction du Médicament et de la Pharmacie and the Bulletin officiel. Tea itself is a staple food in Morocco (regulated by ONSSA), which is relevant for a whole-food positioning.", "permitted_claims_examples": ["EU: none. No authorised nutrition or health claim exists for L-theanine; EFSA 2011 opinion rejected all submitted claims.", "US (structure/function, with DSHEA disclaimer, substantiation required): 'Helps promote relaxation without drowsiness' / 'Supports attention and focus when combined with caffeine' - these are examples of industry wording, not FDA-authorised claims.", "Japan: L-theanine products are notified under the Foods with Function Claims system (e.g., Asahi 'Nenaito', 200 mg/day, claim relating to sleep quality/fatigue on waking; notification numbers reported inconsistently as A308/B306 by retailers - not verified against the CAA database). FFC notifications are business-operator responsibility and are not individually approved like FOSHU; no FOSHU approval for theanine was confirmed."], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "EU: non-supplement foods and beverages containing added L-theanine, and non-green-tea-derived theanine, appear to need novel food authorisation (Catalogue summary via secondary sources; confirm with the Commission's Novel Food Status Catalogue). US: no NDI issue identified; GRAS supports food use up to 250 mg/serving. Morocco: unknown.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "High from a technical standpoint: white, water-soluble crystalline powder with a mild umami/sweet taste, compatible with beverages, gummies, bars, powders and tea-based drinks. The PK study shows capsules and green tea deliver identical kinetics [scheid2012], so matrix is not a barrier. Legal suitability is the constraint: in the EU only supplements are clearly permitted; in the US foods up to 250 mg/serving are covered by GRAS.", "formats": ["Capsules/tablets (100-200 mg) - the format used in most trials", "Ready-to-drink or powdered beverages (100-200 mg per serving), including theanine + caffeine 'focus' drinks at tea-like ratios of roughly 1:1 to 2:1", "Matcha or shade-grown tea products (natural theanine ~20-50 mg per serving; too low for the studied effects unless concentrated)", "Chewable tablets (used at 100 mg x2 twice daily in children's trial)", "Nutrient bars (trialled in multi-ingredient breakfast-bar studies, not reviewed here)"], "stability": "Thermally fairly stable in dry form and in aqueous solutions at neutral pH; prolonged heating or alkaline conditions can cause degradation or Maillard-type browning in sugar-containing matrices; not light-sensitive in practice; not prone to oxidation (unlike catechins). Stability statements here are general food-chemistry knowledge, not verified by a study in this unit - obtain supplier stability data.", "taste": "Mild umami/brothy with slight sweetness; at 100-200 mg per 250 ml it is not organoleptically dominant and can even round off bitterness of caffeine or catechins. No masking usually needed.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Rapid absorption with Tmax ~0.8 h and dose-proportional AUC over 50-200 mg; identical kinetics from capsules and green tea [scheid2012]. Food co-ingestion effects not specifically studied; large neutral amino acids could theoretically compete for intestinal and BBB L-type transport (animal data on leucine-preferring transporter [yokogoshi1998]), suggesting fasted or low-protein dosing may maximise brain delivery - unproven in humans.", "dose_feasibility": "Yes. 100-200 mg fits easily in any serving (a capsule or 250 ml drink); 400 mg is feasible but offers no clear advantage and GRAS covers 250 mg/serving. A cup of tea (~8-25 mg) cannot deliver studied doses; 'tea-equivalent' doses were inert [dodd2015]. For combination products, trial ratios were 97:40, 100:50, 200:160, 250:150 mg theanine:caffeine.", "cost_availability": "Commodity ingredient widely available from Chinese, Indian, Japanese (Taiyo Suntheanine, premium) and US distributors; fermentation/enzymatic grades at 98-99% purity. Ingredient cost per 200 mg dose is well under one US cent at bulk prices, making cost a non-issue; branded grades cost more. Availability in Morocco would depend on import; no local supplier found.", "cost_evidence": "Total Ingredients (US distributor) listed L-theanine at USD 23.50-29.50 per kg, MOQ 25 kg, origin India (total-ingredients.com, accessed 2026-10-25). Pharmaoffer trade data reported an average of USD 28.92 per kg over the 12 months to 30 June 2026 (pharmaoffer.com, accessed 2026-10-25). Retail: BulkSupplements 250 g at USD 20.97 on Walmart.com (~USD 84/kg, accessed 2026-10-25). At USD 30/kg, 200 mg costs about USD 0.006. Moroccan import pricing not researched here."}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 4, "effect_magnitude": 3, "safety": 9, "reproducibility": 5, "food_suitability": 7, "cost_access": 8, "rationale": "evidence_quality 4: best outcome (acute choice reaction time in healthy young adults) reaches grade B through three convergent meta-analyses, but everything else is C or D, trials are small and often industry-funded, and chronic cognitive evidence is null. effect_magnitude 3: SMD 0.3-0.5 on reaction-time tasks equals 15-20 ms, confined to attention and lasting 1-2 h; no memory or learning effect; for a student the practical gain is marginal unless combined with caffeine. safety 9: GRAS up to 250 mg/serving, no serious adverse events across 31 RCTs, benign up to 900 mg/day; minus one point for absent pregnancy and > 12-week data. reproducibility 5: effects replicated across Unilever, Northumbria, Peradeniya/Texas Tech and Japanese groups, but with task-specific inconsistencies and clear nulls [rogers2008, haskell2008, dodd2015]. food_suitability 7: technically excellent (soluble, mild, stable) but EU novel-food restriction confines it to supplements. cost_access 8: around USD 0.006 per dose, globally available; Moroccan import and regulatory status unverified."}, "research_gaps": ["Adequately powered (n > 150) placebo-controlled trial of 200 mg/day for 8-12 weeks in university students or knowledge workers with attention, working memory and academic/work performance outcomes, pre-registered and independently funded", "Factorial trial of theanine x caffeine at realistic beverage doses (100-200 mg + 50-100 mg) with caffeine-alone arm to quantify theanine's incremental contribution to attention and to caffeine-related jitteriness and sleep disruption", "Dose-response study above 400 mg and in relation to body weight, plus timing relative to meals (protein competition for L-type transport)", "Objective sleep (polysomnography/actigraphy) trial in healthy adults with evening theanine, separating sleep from next-day cognition", "Replication of stress-buffering effects with pre-registered primary outcomes in high trait-anxiety students during exam periods, including cortisol and performance", "Trials of tea-derived versus synthetic theanine and of matcha/whole-tea matrices to test whether any food-level intake has measurable effects", "Long-term safety surveillance (> 6 months) at 200-400 mg/day and data in pregnancy", "Independent (non-industry) replication of the Hidese 2019 and Baba 2021 designs"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 5.6, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "pass", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": "B", "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": "B", "best_grade_sleep_stress": "C", "best_grade_surrogate": "C", "best_grade_overall": "B", "benefit_domains": {"reaction_time": "B", "sustained_attention": "C", "sleep_related_cognition": "C", "alertness": "B"}, "n_outcome_rows": 17, "n_studies": 35, "n_verified": 35, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 13, "n_systematic_reviews": 6, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["benefit", "inconsistent", "insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "l-tyrosine", "name": "L-tyrosine", "category": "E", "category_name": "Amino acids and related compounds", "aliases": ["tyrosine", "L-tyrosine", "Tyr", "4-hydroxyphenylalanine", "N-acetyl-L-tyrosine (NALT, a more soluble derivative; not the form used in the stress trials)"], "food_sources": ["dairy (casein-rich cheese, milk, yogurt)", "eggs", "meat, poultry and fish", "soy and other legumes", "nuts and seeds (e.g., pumpkin seeds, peanuts)", "also synthesised in the liver from phenylalanine (non-essential amino acid unless phenylalanine hydroxylase is deficient)"], "active_constituents": ["L-tyrosine (free amino acid), substrate of tyrosine hydroxylase, the rate-limiting enzyme of dopamine, noradrenaline and adrenaline synthesis; also a precursor of thyroid hormones and melanin"], "one_line_verdict": "High-dose L-tyrosine (about 7-10 g, i.e. 100-150 mg/kg) can partly protect working memory and vigilance during acute cold, noise, multitasking or one night of sleep loss in small military-laboratory trials (grade C). It does not reduce felt stress, shows no reliable benefit in rested students at food-compatible doses, may impair older adults, and has no authorised claim in the EU.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. L-tyrosine is the amino-acid precursor of dopamine and noradrenaline, and in humans single oral doses of 100-150 mg/kg raise plasma tyrosine several-fold within 1-2 h (older adults reach higher levels at the same dose [vanderest2017]). The behavioural evidence follows one consistent pattern: tyrosine does little or nothing in rested, unstressed people, but in several small military-laboratory trials it prevented or reversed the working-memory decrement caused by acute cold [shurtleff1994, mahoney2007, obrien2007], cold plus hypoxia [banderet1989], loud noise [deijen1994] and multitasking [thomas1999]. In sleep-deprived adults, 150 mg/kg reduced vigilance lapses and psychomotor decline for about 3 h [neri1995] and improved several tasks after overnight sleep loss, though less than D-amphetamine [magill2003], without disturbing recovery sleep [waters2003]. The one multi-day field trial (10 g/day for 6 days in cadets, n=21) reported better memory and tracking [deijen1999]. Two systematic appraisals agree that benefits are limited to demanding conditions; the only GRADE-based review gave a weak recommendation for cognitive performance under stress and none for physical performance [hase2015, attipoe2015]. What is not demonstrated. Tyrosine is not an anti-stress agent: in the largest stress trial (n=78, 300 mg/kg during mock interrogations) it changed no mood scale, cortisol or heart rate except to increase anger [lieberman2015], and 2 g did not change stress biomarkers in a 2024 trial (n=80), which found only fewer missed Stroop responses [mcallister2024]. Exercise or heat-stress trials were null for cognition and performance [watson2012, donnan2025]; one 2026 trial reported longer cycling endurance after mental fatigue [solonjunior2026]. Tyrosine has no antidepressant effect [gelenberg1990]. In healthy older adults higher doses worsened working memory and proactive inhibition [vanderest2017, bloemendaal2018]. The rested-student evidence is weak. The trials at a food-compatible 2 g dose in students come almost entirely from one Leiden laboratory [colzato2013, steenbergen2015, colzato2016]. A claimed replication was retracted in 2022 [jongkees2017retracted], Leiden University found the laboratory's lead investigator guilty of research misconduct in other studies, and the largest trial from the same network (n=45) found no average effect, only an inverted-U pattern [jongkees2020]. Size and practical relevance. Effect sizes are rarely reported. Where numbers exist they are restorative: placebo lost 18% Match-to-Sample accuracy in the cold and tyrosine did not [obrien2007]. Benefits need doses of 7-21 g, last about 3 h and are specific to some tasks. For a student or knowledge worker under ordinary conditions there is no direct evidence of benefit. Safety. Doses up to 4 g/day for 4 weeks caused no laboratory abnormalities in healthy men in an Ajinomoto-sponsored trial [matsumoto2026]. The 100-300 mg/kg single doses used in stress studies were generally well tolerated, but long-term safety at those doses is unstudied. Overall grade: C (possible benefit) for acute working memory under extreme environmental stress or multitasking and for vigilance during sleep loss; C (no benefit) for subjective stress and mood; D (insufficient) for students and knowledge workers in everyday life.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Tyrosine is a non-essential amino acid, and people with adequate protein intake are not deficient. The positive trials are best read as 'state-dependent repletion': during intense, prolonged catecholamine release (extreme cold, sleep loss, multitasking) precursor supply may become limiting, and extra tyrosine restores performance toward baseline. It does not raise performance above the rested level [shurtleff1994, obrien2007, thomas1999]. This is neither deficiency correction nor general enhancement. In rested, well-fed young adults the effect at 2 g is unreliable [jongkees2020]. In older adults, who reach higher plasma levels, it may impair [vanderest2017, bloemendaal2018]. The only true deficiency setting is phenylketonuria, where tyrosine becomes conditionally essential. That is a clinical, medically managed use (Cochrane reviews of tyrosine in PKU exist; not reviewed here) and must not be generalised to healthy consumers. An observational association between habitual tyrosine intake and cognition [kuhn2019] most plausibly reflects adequate protein and diet quality.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Typical claims ('dopamine booster', 'focus under pressure', 'stress support', 'motivation', 'mental flexibility') are found on many nootropic blends that contain 175-500 mg tyrosine or N-acetyl-L-tyrosine. The evidence supports none of these at such doses. Positive effects were seen with 7-21 g, in extreme military stressors, for a few hours and on specific tasks. 'Stress support' is contradicted by the largest RCT, where tyrosine did not reduce any stress measure and increased anger [lieberman2015, mcallister2024]. 'Flexibility' and 'focus' claims rest on a single laboratory with a retraction and misconduct findings. In the EU, EFSA judged that L-tyrosine contributes to normal dopamine synthesis (Art. 13(5), 2011), but the Commission refused to authorise even that claim (Regulation (EU) No 155/2014) because no daily intake could be set. No tyrosine cognition or attention claim is authorised. A defensible Brain Food statement would be descriptive only ('contains tyrosine, an amino acid found in protein foods'), not functional.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Tyrosine crosses the blood-brain barrier on the large neutral amino acid (LNAA) transporter, competing with tryptophan, phenylalanine and the branched-chain amino acids. Tyrosine hydroxylase is normally not saturated with tyrosine, but it is subject to end-product inhibition, so extra precursor increases catecholamine synthesis mainly in neurons that are firing rapidly (e.g., during acute stress). This is the basis of the 'repletion under demand' hypothesis. Human evidence for the brain step is indirect (plasma levels, MHPG excretion, prolactin, fMRI). Animal studies show prevention of stress-induced noradrenaline depletion. Inverted-U dopamine-cognition relationships explain why individuals with high baseline dopamine function or older adults may be impaired.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Oral L-tyrosine (100-150 mg/kg) -> intestinal absorption -> plasma tyrosine rises several-fold, peak about 1-2 h", "detail": "Shown in humans; older adults reach higher plasma levels at the same dose [vanderest2017]; serum +236 umol/L at 150 mg/kg [watson2012]", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Plasma tyrosine -> LNAA transporter at the blood-brain barrier", "detail": "Competition with other LNAAs; ordinary protein meals change brain tyrosine uptake little, unlike pure tyrosine", "evidence_level": "plausible"}, {"step": "Brain tyrosine -> tyrosine hydroxylase -> L-DOPA -> dopamine / noradrenaline", "detail": "Precursor sensitivity limited to rapidly firing neurons; demonstrated in animals; indirect human markers (MHPG increase [gelenberg1990], prolactin change [waters2003])", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Stress-induced catecholamine depletion prevented -> preserved prefrontal working memory", "detail": "Rat studies of cold and uncontrollable stress; in humans inferred from the stress-dependence of behavioural effects", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Measured human outcome: working memory / vigilance under cold, noise, multitasking, sleep loss", "detail": "Small trials, grade C [shurtleff1994, mahoney2007, thomas1999, neri1995, magill2003]", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Inverted-U: excessive catecholamine signalling impairs high performers and older adults", "detail": "Behavioural/fMRI evidence of impairment [jongkees2020, vanderest2017, bloemendaal2018]", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["catecholamine synthesis (dopamine, noradrenaline)", "LNAA blood-brain barrier competition", "thyroid hormone and melanin synthesis (off-target relevance for safety)", "prefrontal working memory under stress"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "high_cognitive_workload", "population_detail": "Healthy, rested young adults (mostly military volunteers, men > women) exposed to an acute stressor: cold air or water, cold+hypoxia, 90 dB noise or multitasking", "dose_or_intake": "100-150 mg/kg single dose (7-10.5 g at 70 kg), some protocols 300 mg/kg in two doses", "duration": "Single dose 1-2 h before a 30-90 min stressor", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Six small trials (n=8-20) from three or four laboratories report that tyrosine prevents or reverses the stress-induced decrement in working memory or matching-to-sample accuracy; it has no effect in the same people without the stressor. Physical performance under cold is not protected.", "effect_estimate": "Cold: placebo -18% Match-to-Sample vs warm control, tyrosine not different from control [obrien2007]; other trials report p<0.05 without effect sizes. No pooled estimate exists.", "certainty_notes": "Downgraded for risk of bias (several non-randomised 'controlled clinical trials', unclear allocation, responder subgroups), imprecision (n 8-20, no CIs), indirectness (extreme military stressors), and suspected publication bias (almost uniformly positive small studies; Attipoe 2015 'weak recommendation'). Consistency of direction prevents a D. Not applicable to everyday mild stress without direct testing.", "key_study_ids": ["shurtleff1994", "mahoney2007", "obrien2007", "thomas1999", "deijen1994", "banderet1989", "hase2015", "attipoe2015"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "vigilance", "population": "sleep_deprived", "population_detail": "Healthy adults (men 18-35 y in the largest trial) after one night without sleep (24-40 h awake)", "dose_or_intake": "150 mg/kg single or split dose", "duration": "Single dose during the night or the following afternoon", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Two trials: tyrosine reduced lapses on a high-event-rate vigilance task and the decline of a psychomotor task for about 3 h [neri1995], and improved several tasks after overnight sleep loss but less than D-amphetamine [magill2003]. Tyrosine did not disturb recovery sleep [waters2003].", "effect_estimate": "Not reported as effect sizes in abstracts; benefits limited to a subset of tasks, duration about 3 h", "certainty_notes": "Only two independent trials, effect sizes unavailable, multiple outcomes; risk of selective outcome reporting. No head-to-head with ordinary caffeine doses in the published abstract beyond 300 mg/70 kg arm. Grade C; this is the main input for the sleep-deprivation synthesis.", "key_study_ids": ["neri1995", "magill2003", "waters2003"]}, {"timeframe": "short_term_days", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "high_cognitive_workload", "population_detail": "Military cadets during a one-week combat training course (psychosocial and physical stress, fatigue)", "dose_or_intake": "2 g tyrosine five times daily (10 g/day) in a protein-rich drink", "duration": "6 days", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Tyrosine group performed better on a memory and a tracking task than a carbohydrate-drink group, with lower systolic BP; no mood effect.", "effect_estimate": "not reported in abstract", "certainty_notes": "Single trial, 10 vs 11 participants, comparator not protein-matched, high risk of bias. Grade C only because the setting is directly relevant to sustained high workload.", "key_study_ids": ["deijen1999"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "high_cognitive_workload", "population_detail": "Healthy military personnel under severe psychological stress (mock captivity/interrogation) and young adults under a VR active-shooter drill", "dose_or_intake": "2 g single dose to 300 mg/kg in two doses", "duration": "Single day", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Tyrosine did not reduce subjective stress, cortisol, heart rate, salivary alpha-amylase or anxiety; in the largest trial it increased anger. Older positive mood findings come from a post hoc responder analysis [banderet1989].", "effect_estimate": "No effect on 5 of 6 POMS subscales, cortisol or HR; anger higher with tyrosine (interaction p=.002) [lieberman2015]; stress markers NS [mcallister2024]", "certainty_notes": "Two adequately sized RCTs (n=78 and n=80) are consistent with no anti-stress effect; graded C rather than B because outcomes are partly subjective and stressors extreme.", "key_study_ids": ["lieberman2015", "mcallister2024", "banderet1989"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Rested, fasted university students in laboratory tasks with high cognitive-control demand (2-back, task switching, stop-signal, reference-back)", "dose_or_intake": "2.0 g single dose", "duration": "Single dose, tested about 1 h later", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "One laboratory reported benefits on 2-back accuracy, task switching and inhibition; the largest trial (n=45) found no average effect and an inverted-U pattern (low performers better, high performers worse); a claimed replication was retracted, and the lab's lead investigator was found by Leiden University to have committed research misconduct in other studies.", "effect_estimate": "2-back accuracy 86.5% vs 91.4% (+4.9 points, p=0.02, n=22) [colzato2013]; no main effect in reference-back task (n=45) [jongkees2020]", "certainty_notes": "Very low certainty within grade C: single-lab, unregistered, small samples, genotype/baseline subgroup claims, a retraction in the series and institutional misconduct findings. Should not support any 'focus' or 'flexibility' claim for rested students.", "key_study_ids": ["colzato2013", "steenbergen2015", "colzato2016", "jongkees2020", "jongkees2017retracted"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "students", "population_detail": "Students and knowledge workers in everyday conditions (exam study, office work, mild stress)", "dose_or_intake": "none studied", "duration": "not studied", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No trial has tested tyrosine in students during examination periods or in knowledge workers at work. Extrapolation from extreme military stress or from the unreliable rested-lab series is not justified.", "effect_estimate": "not available", "certainty_notes": "Absence of direct evidence; mechanism (precursor loading helps only when catecholamine neurons fire intensely) predicts little effect under ordinary conditions.", "key_study_ids": ["hase2015", "jongkees2015"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "cognitive_fatigue", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Trained athletes or active young men exercising in heat or after mental fatigue", "dose_or_intake": "150-300 mg/kg", "duration": "Single session", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Two crossover trials found no effect on cognition (Stroop, soccer decision-making) or exercise capacity in the heat; one 2026 trial found 16% longer time to exhaustion after mental fatigue (physical, not cognitive outcome).", "effect_estimate": "Decision-making 86.9% vs 88.6% (p=.627) [donnan2025]; time to exhaustion 61.4 vs 60.2 min (p=.505) [watson2012]; time to exhaustion +61 s (p=0.008) [solonjunior2026]", "certainty_notes": "All n=8-12; inconsistent; physical outcomes are indirect for Brain Food.", "key_study_ids": ["watson2012", "donnan2025", "solonjunior2026"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Healthy adults aged 60-75 y", "dose_or_intake": "100-200 mg/kg single dose", "duration": "Single dose", "grade": "C", "direction": "harm", "effect_summary": "Older adults reach higher plasma tyrosine at the same dose; higher doses were associated with worse load-dependent working memory, and tyrosine worsened proactive inhibition with increasing age; no group-level benefit.", "effect_estimate": "Dose-dependent working-memory decline p=0.048, stronger with larger plasma response p=0.035 [vanderest2017]; age-dependent detriment in proactive slowing [bloemendaal2018]", "certainty_notes": "Two small trials from one consortium; direction consistent (no benefit, possible harm). Sufficient to advise against high-dose products for older consumers.", "key_study_ids": ["vanderest2017", "bloemendaal2018"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Berlin Aging Study II participants (1383 older, 341 younger) - habitual dietary tyrosine", "dose_or_intake": "Habitual intake by FFQ", "duration": "Cross-sectional", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "Higher habitual tyrosine intake was associated with better latent cognition in both age groups; this cannot be separated from overall protein intake and diet quality.", "effect_estimate": "Significant SEM association; size not reported in abstract [kuhn2019]", "certainty_notes": "Cross-sectional, confounded, single cohort; supports adequate protein intake rather than supplementation.", "key_study_ids": ["kuhn2019"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Outpatients with major depression", "dose_or_intake": "100 mg/kg/day", "duration": "4 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "No antidepressant activity versus placebo despite increased noradrenaline turnover (MHPG).", "effect_estimate": "not reported in abstract (no difference)", "certainty_notes": "Single RCT n=65; clinical population, not generalisable to healthy people, but rules out mood-treatment claims.", "key_study_ids": ["gelenberg1990"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "150 mg/kg single dose (about 10.5 g at 70 kg)", "onset": "Tested 1-2 h post-dose (plasma peak about 1-2 h)", "duration_studied": "30-min task in 4 deg C air", "domain": "working memory (delayed matching-to-sample)", "population": "8 healthy men", "effect_estimate": "Cold-induced accuracy loss at 16-s delay restored to thermoneutral level; no effect at 22 deg C", "certainty": "low", "safety": "No adverse events reported in abstract", "study_ids": ["shurtleff1994"]}, {"dose": "150 mg/kg x2 in food bars (300 mg/kg)", "onset": "Before each 90-min immersion", "duration_studied": "One day", "domain": "working memory, choice RT", "population": "19 healthy volunteers", "effect_estimate": "More correct Match-to-Sample responses (p<.05), shorter study time (p<.05)", "certainty": "low", "safety": "Not reported in abstract", "study_ids": ["mahoney2007", "obrien2007"]}, {"dose": "150 mg/kg single dose", "onset": "1 h", "duration_studied": "Multitasking battery", "domain": "working memory under multitasking", "population": "20 healthy adults (10 men, 10 women)", "effect_estimate": "Higher accuracy and fewer list retrievals on working memory task during multitasking only; no effect on other tasks", "certainty": "low", "safety": "Heart rate and BP rose during tasks (not attributed to tyrosine in abstract)", "study_ids": ["thomas1999"]}, {"dose": "150 mg/kg (split)", "onset": "Effects for about 3 h", "duration_studied": "Overnight (24+ h awake)", "domain": "vigilance lapses, psychomotor", "population": "Healthy adults, sleep deprived", "effect_estimate": "Reduced lapse probability and psychomotor decline; benefit lasted about 3 h", "certainty": "low", "safety": "Described as relatively benign", "study_ids": ["neri1995"]}, {"dose": "150 mg/kg single dose", "onset": "Afternoon after overnight sleep loss", "duration_studied": "Hours", "domain": "reasoning, running memory, tracking, vigilance", "population": "76 men 18-35 y (5 arms)", "effect_estimate": "Improved several tests; less effective than D-amphetamine 20 mg", "certainty": "low-moderate", "safety": "No effect on recovery sleep [waters2003]", "study_ids": ["magill2003", "waters2003"]}, {"dose": "2.0 g single dose", "onset": "About 1 h", "duration_studied": "N-back session", "domain": "working memory updating", "population": "22 rested students", "effect_estimate": "2-back accuracy 91.4% vs 86.5% (p=0.02); 1-back unchanged", "certainty": "very low (single lab with misconduct findings)", "safety": "No HR, BP or mood change", "study_ids": ["colzato2013"]}, {"dose": "2.0 g single dose", "onset": "About 1 h", "duration_studied": "Reference-back task", "domain": "working memory gating/updating", "population": "45 rested young adults", "effect_estimate": "No average effect; inverted-U (low performers improved, high performers impaired)", "certainty": "low", "safety": "Not reported", "study_ids": ["jongkees2020"]}, {"dose": "2.0 g single dose", "onset": "Before stress challenge", "duration_studied": "Single session", "domain": "stress markers, Stroop", "population": "80 young adults (tyrosine n=28)", "effect_estimate": "No effect on sAA, SIgA, HR, state anxiety; fewer missed Stroop responses", "certainty": "low", "safety": "Not reported in abstract", "study_ids": ["mcallister2024"]}, {"dose": "150 mg/kg x2 in food bars (300 mg/kg)", "onset": "About 60 min before each interrogation", "duration_studied": "Two interrogation days", "domain": "mood, cortisol, HR", "population": "78 military personnel", "effect_estimate": "No effect on tension, depression, vigor, fatigue, confusion, cortisol, HR; anger increased (p=.002)", "certainty": "moderate (adequate RCT)", "safety": "Increased anger", "study_ids": ["lieberman2015"]}, {"dose": "100-200 mg/kg single dose", "onset": "N-back at 90 min", "duration_studied": "4 h plasma sampling", "domain": "working memory", "population": "17 older adults 60-75 y (+17 young at 150 mg/kg)", "effect_estimate": "Higher doses associated with worse load-dependent working memory (p=0.048)", "certainty": "low", "safety": "No adverse events; possible cognitive impairment at high dose in older adults", "study_ids": ["vanderest2017", "bloemendaal2018"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "6 days (combat training course)", "population": "21 military cadets", "outcome": "Memory and tracking tasks, mood, BP", "effect_estimate": "Better memory and tracking vs carbohydrate drink; lower systolic BP; no mood effect (sizes not in abstract)", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "n=10 vs 11, comparator not protein-matched, single trial", "study_ids": ["deijen1999"]}, {"duration": "4 weeks", "population": "65 outpatients with major depression", "outcome": "Antidepressant response", "effect_estimate": "No antidepressant effect at 100 mg/kg/day; increased MHPG", "evidence_quality": "low-moderate", "limitations": "Small arms, clinical population", "study_ids": ["gelenberg1990"]}, {"duration": "4 weeks per dose level", "population": "30 healthy men", "outcome": "Safety laboratory parameters and adverse events", "effect_estimate": "No dose-related abnormalities up to 4 g/day; AE incidence similar to placebo; NOAEL 4 g/day (highest tested)", "evidence_quality": "moderate (for safety)", "limitations": "Men only; industry-sponsored; doses below those used in stress trials", "study_ids": ["matsumoto2026"]}, {"duration": "Habitual diet (cross-sectional)", "population": "1724 adults (Berlin Aging Study II)", "outcome": "Latent cognition factor", "effect_estimate": "Positive association with habitual tyrosine intake (size not in abstract)", "evidence_quality": "very low (observational)", "limitations": "Confounding by protein intake and diet quality; FFQ", "study_ids": ["kuhn2019"]}], "safety": {"summary": "L-tyrosine is a normal dietary amino acid and appears well tolerated: up to 4 g/day for 4 weeks caused no laboratory abnormalities in healthy men [matsumoto2026], and 100 mg/kg/day for 4 weeks in depressed patients produced no significant side effects [gelenberg1990]. Single doses of 100-300 mg/kg in stress studies were described as benign [neri1995]. Concerns are pharmacological rather than toxicological. Tyrosine is a catecholamine precursor and is contraindicated with monoamine oxidase inhibitors (risk of hypertensive reactions, per drug-interaction references, mechanistic and case-based). It is a thyroid hormone precursor, with caution advised in hyperthyroidism, Graves disease and thyroid hormone therapy. It may reduce levodopa absorption through LNAA competition. It increased anger under severe stress [lieberman2015] and impaired working memory and inhibition in older adults at high doses [vanderest2017, bloemendaal2018]. Long-term safety at the 7-21 g doses used in stress trials is not established. There is no EFSA or IOM tolerable upper intake level.", "adverse_effects": ["Increased self-reported anger under severe stress (one RCT)", "Dose-dependent working-memory impairment and poorer proactive inhibition in older adults", "Possible impairment in high-performing young adults (inverted-U)", "Gastrointestinal discomfort and headache/migraine are reported in drug references (not quantified in trials reviewed)", "Prolactin increase after high dose (endocrine marker)"], "contraindications": ["Current use of MAO inhibitors (phenelzine, tranylcypromine, isocarboxazid, selegiline, and MAO-inhibiting drugs such as linezolid)", "Hyperthyroidism / Graves disease", "Hereditary tyrosinaemia and alkaptonuria (inborn errors of tyrosine metabolism)", "Melanoma (theoretical: melanin precursor; flagged in drug references, no human data)", "Pregnancy and breastfeeding (no safety data at supplemental doses)", "Children and adolescents (no data)"], "drug_interactions": ["MAO inhibitors: contraindicated (hypertensive risk)", "Levodopa: LNAA competition may reduce absorption/brain uptake; separate dosing; neurologist review", "Thyroid hormone (levothyroxine/liothyronine): possible additive effect; caution", "Stimulants and other catecholaminergic drugs (amphetamine, methylphenidate, bupropion): theoretical additive effects, untested", "Caffeine: no factorial trial; additive effects untested"], "upper_limits": "No EFSA, EU or US tolerable upper intake level. Human NOAEL 4 g/day for 4 weeks (highest dose tested, men only, industry-sponsored) [matsumoto2026]. A rat 13-week study (not included in studies[]) reported NOAELs of 600 mg/kg/day (males) and 200 mg/kg/day (females). Cognitive-trial doses (100-150 mg/kg, about 7-10.5 g) exceed the tested chronic human NOAEL and have only been given as single or short-term doses.", "sleep_impact": "150 mg/kg after 36 h awake had no effect on sleep latency, architecture or recovery sleep, unlike amphetamine, phentermine and caffeine [waters2003]. Not tested for habitual evening use.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "4 weeks maximum (4 g/day in healthy men; 100 mg/kg/day in depressed patients); 6 days at 10 g/day in cadets", "daily_use_appropriate": "uncertain", "special_populations": "Older adults: higher plasma response and possible cognitive impairment at 100-200 mg/kg, so avoid high-dose products. Phenylketonuria: tyrosine is used clinically under medical supervision (not a consumer indication). Psychiatric patients on MAOIs or with bipolar disorder: avoid without psychiatrist review. Thyroid disease: endocrinologist review. Pregnancy, lactation, children: no data.", "specialist_review_needed": ["clinical pharmacist (MAOI, levodopa, thyroid hormone interactions)", "endocrinologist (thyroid disease)", "psychiatrist (mood disorders, MAOI users)", "geriatrician (older-adult formulations)", "toxicologist (if chronic doses above 4 g/day are considered)"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "Safe up to 4 g/day for 4 weeks in healthy men. Products must exclude MAOI users and people with thyroid disease, carry interaction warnings, avoid high doses for older adults, and not exceed tested chronic doses. Cognitive-trial doses (7-21 g) have no chronic safety data."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "No authorised health claim for L-tyrosine. EFSA assessed general-function claims for catecholamine synthesis (ID 1928), increased attention (ID 440, 1672, 1930) and muscle function (ID 1929) (EFSA Journal 2011;9(6):2270; full text could not be accessed, HTTP 403; none of these claims appears in the authorised list as far as we know). In a separate Article 13(5) application by Vitabiotics, EFSA concluded that a cause-and-effect relationship is established for 'L-tyrosine contributes to normal synthesis of dopamine' (EFSA Journal 2011;9(7):2290). The Commission nevertheless refused authorisation in Regulation (EU) No 155/2014: EFSA 'could not provide a quantitative indication of the necessary daily intake of L-tyrosine per se', so conditions of use could not be set and the claim 'could be misleading' (recital 7, legislation.gov.uk, accessed 2026-10-25). Amino acids are not harmonised under the Food Supplements Directive 2002/46/EC (vitamins and minerals only), so their use and maximum levels in supplements are set nationally. Member-state limits for tyrosine were not verified (uncertain).", "us": "L-tyrosine is an amino acid and therefore a permitted dietary ingredient under DSHEA. It is believed to have been marketed before 1994, so no NDI notification would be needed, but this was not verified. Structure/function claims require the FDA disclaimer and FTC 'competent and reliable scientific evidence'; a 'focus under stress' claim for rested consumers would not meet that standard. As a conventional-food additive, 21 CFR 172.320 limits added L-tyrosine to 4.3% by weight of total protein, and only to improve protein quality in foods that supply at least 6.5 g of natural protein per day. Gram-dose 'focus bars' or drinks therefore would not fit 172.320 and would need a separate GRAS basis (law.cornell.edu, accessed 2026-10-25). Regulatory counsel review is needed.", "morocco": "No tyrosine-specific rule found. Food supplements have no dedicated law in Morocco; they are registered through a Ministry of Health committee (DMP, replaced by AMMPS in 2025) under joint circulars. Law 28-07 bans disease-related claims, no list of authorised health claims was found, and Law 27-26 (2026) may let a decree reserve risky supplements to pharmacies (status unverified; see unit X6). Status: uncertain; Moroccan regulatory counsel required.", "permitted_claims_examples": [], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "L-tyrosine is a normal food constituent and long-marketed supplement ingredient, and it is listed among substances permitted in some foods for specific groups. Novel-food status is not expected to be an issue, but this was not confirmed in the EU novel food catalogue. N-acetyl-L-tyrosine, a common nootropic form, should have its novel-food status checked separately (not verified). US NDI: probably not required (pre-1994; not verified).", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Technically possible but impractical at effective doses. The stress trials delivered 150-300 mg/kg (10-21 g) in military food bars [mahoney2007, lieberman2015]. Food-compatible 2 g doses lack reliable evidence. Ordinary protein foods supply tyrosine but raise brain uptake little, because of competition from other LNAAs.", "formats": ["capsules/tablets (500 mg units; 14-20 capsules needed for 100-150 mg/kg)", "powder in food bar (as in military studies)", "suspension in drinks (poor solubility)", "N-acetyl-L-tyrosine (more soluble, less efficiently converted to tyrosine in humans; not the tested form)"], "stability": "Stable crystalline amino acid. Free tyrosine can take part in Maillard browning in heated, sugar-rich matrices (general amino-acid chemistry, not tested here).", "taste": "Mildly bitter/chalky; poor water solubility gives a gritty texture at gram doses", "bioavailability_matrix": "Fasting intake of pure tyrosine maximises the plasma tyrosine/LNAA ratio. Co-ingested protein supplies competing LNAAs and blunts brain uptake. Older adults reach higher plasma levels at the same dose.", "dose_feasibility": "Effective trial dose for 70 kg is about 7-10.5 g (100-150 mg/kg), which is not feasible in a normal snack or drink serving without texture problems. That dose would also exceed the only chronic human NOAEL (4 g/day). A 2 g dose fits a bar or shot but lacks reliable efficacy evidence.", "cost_availability": "Widely available as a bulk powder and capsules from many suppliers; prices not researched.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 3, "effect_magnitude": 3, "safety": 6, "reproducibility": 3, "food_suitability": 3, "cost_access": 6, "rationale": "Evidence quality 3: grade C at best, and only in extreme stress or sleep-loss settings; D for students and knowledge workers. Effect magnitude 3: restorative effects (e.g., an 18% loss prevented) in specific tasks for about 3 h; none on mood or stress; possible harm in older adults. Safety 6: benign up to 4 g/day for 4 weeks, but MAOI/thyroid/levodopa interactions, no chronic data at effective doses, and adverse signals in older adults. Reproducibility 3: few independent labs, small samples, a retraction and misconduct findings in the rested-student series, and recent null trials. Food suitability 3: effective doses of 7-10 g are poorly soluble and impractical in a food serving. Cost/access 6: widely sold commodity amino acid; price not researched, so the score is provisional."}, "research_gaps": ["Preregistered, adequately powered RCT in students or knowledge workers under realistic stress (exam period, sleep restriction), with 2 g vs 100 mg/kg vs placebo", "Head-to-head and factorial trials of tyrosine vs caffeine (and tyrosine + caffeine) during sleep deprivation", "Dose-response curves in young adults, with chronic safety above 4 g/day and in women", "Independent replication of the Leiden rested-student findings by a different laboratory", "Publication-bias assessment: registered trial NCT04518254 (L-tyrosine 500 mg, decision-making under stress, n=17, completed 2022) has no posted results; unpublished military studies", "Biomarker validation that oral tyrosine increases brain catecholamine synthesis in humans under stress (PET, MRS)", "Older-adult safety: confirm or refute cognitive impairment at moderate doses"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 3.9, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": "C", "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": "C", "best_grade_sleep_stress": "C", "best_grade_surrogate": null, "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"working_memory": "C", "vigilance": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 10, "n_studies": 28, "n_verified": 28, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 9, "n_systematic_reviews": 3, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["harm", "inconsistent", "insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "other-food-derived-compounds", "name": "Other food-derived compounds (ergothioneine, spermidine, carnosine/anserine, uridine, NAD precursors, CoQ10, PQQ, oral GABA, beta-alanine)", "category": "E", "category_name": "Amino acids and related compounds", "aliases": ["ergothioneine", "spermidine", "carnosine", "anserine", "uridine", "nicotinamide riboside (NR)", "nicotinamide mononucleotide (NMN)", "coenzyme Q10 (CoQ10)", "pyrroloquinoline quinone (PQQ)", "oral GABA (gamma-aminobutyric acid)", "beta-alanine"], "food_sources": ["ergothioneine: mushrooms (especially porcini/king bolete and oyster mushrooms), to a lesser extent oat bran, black/red beans", "spermidine: wheat germ, soybeans/natto, aged cheese, mushrooms, legumes, some fruits", "carnosine/anserine: skeletal muscle of animals - beef, pork, chicken/turkey breast (anserine is especially high in poultry and fish muscle); absent from plant foods", "uridine: mostly delivered via multinutrient medical foods (Souvenaid/Fortasyn Connect) rather than ordinary diet; some uridine from RNA-rich foods (organ meats, brewer's yeast) but dietary quantification is poor", "NAD precursors (nicotinamide riboside, nicotinamide mononucleotide): trace amounts in milk, yeast, some vegetables (edamame, broccoli, cucumber, avocado contain NMN precursors); supplement forms are synthetic, not food-extracted at the doses studied", "coenzyme Q10: organ meats (heart, liver), fatty fish, whole grains, nuts; biosynthesised endogenously", "pyrroloquinoline quinone (PQQ): fermented soybeans (natto), parsley, green tea, kiwifruit, in small amounts", "GABA: fermented foods (kimchi, tempeh, miso), germinated brown rice, some teas; produced by lactic acid bacteria fermentation", "beta-alanine: meat, poultry, fish (as a precursor via carnosine-containing muscle tissue); not free beta-alanine in meaningful amounts, mostly obtained via carnosine digestion"], "active_constituents": ["L-ergothioneine", "spermidine (polyamine)", "L-carnosine (beta-alanyl-L-histidine)", "anserine (beta-alanyl-1-methyl-L-histidine)", "uridine / uridine monophosphate", "nicotinamide riboside", "nicotinamide mononucleotide", "ubiquinone/ubiquinol (coenzyme Q10)", "pyrroloquinoline quinone disodium salt", "gamma-aminobutyric acid", "beta-alanine"], "one_line_verdict": "This is a basket of mechanistically interesting but, with the partial exception of beta-alanine's exercise-cognition data in acute fatigue and a single promising ergothioneine pilot in MCI, a group of compounds whose human cognitive evidence in healthy adults is either absent, null, or confined to small industry-adjacent trials in older or cognitively impaired populations, and several (NMN, high-dose spermidine extracts) face unresolved EU novel-food or US regulatory status.", "scientific_conclusion": "This monograph groups eight food-derived or food-adjacent compounds that share weak-to-absent evidence for cognitive enhancement in healthy adults but differ substantially in regulatory and mechanistic maturity, so each is treated separately below. Ergothioneine is a mushroom-derived antioxidant amino acid; a 2024 pilot RCT in mild cognitive impairment (MCI) found a signal of delayed cognitive decline on some measures over the trial period, but it was a small, likely underpowered pilot and the authors themselves call it a pilot [yau2024ergothioneine]; no RCT in healthy, cognitively normal adults was identified in this search, so any Brain Food claim beyond 'may support a specific biomarker profile in people already showing decline' is unsupported. Spermidine, a polyamine abundant in wheat germ and aged foods, has a strong rodent/Drosophila mechanistic story (autophagy, hippocampal mitochondrial function) and a supportive prospective-cohort association with lower cognitive-impairment risk in the same paper that established the mouse mechanism [schroeder2021spermidine], but the human interventional record is disappointing: a 3-month pilot RCT in 30 older adults with subjective cognitive decline found a promising effect size on a mnemonic discrimination task (d=0.77-0.79) [wirth2018spermidine], yet the properly powered 12-month, 100-person SmartAge phase 2b follow-up by an overlapping author group found no significant effect on the same primary memory outcome or on any secondary outcome (between-group difference -0.03, 95% CI -0.11 to 0.05) [schwarz2022spermidine] - a textbook case of a small positive pilot not replicating in a larger, longer, better-powered trial. Carnosine and its methylated analogue anserine (abundant in poultry/fish muscle) have one small industry-linked RCT in people with MCI (not healthy adults) reporting that anserine supplementation protected against further cognitive decline over the trial period via a proposed hypochlorous-acid-scavenging mechanism [masuoka2021anserine]; this is a clinical-population, mechanism-adjacent finding and does not establish an enhancement effect in healthy people. Uridine is essentially only tested as part of the multinutrient medical food Souvenaid/Fortasyn Connect (also containing DHA, EPA, choline, phospholipids, vitamins), whose pivotal LipiDiDiet trial in prodromal Alzheimer's disease found a slowing of clinical and cognitive composite decline over 3 years in a post-hoc analysis [soininen2023lipididiet]; because uridine is never tested alone and the population is prodromal AD, not healthy adults, no isolated uridine claim and no healthy-adult claim can be made from this evidence. NAD precursors (nicotinamide riboside and nicotinamide mononucleotide) have an extensive and growing safety/pharmacokinetic literature in healthy and overweight middle-aged/older adults showing NAD+ level increases are achievable and generally well tolerated [irie2020nmnjapan, kim2022nmnsafety, yoshino2023nmngeroscience], but none of the trials retrieved in this search reported a cognitive outcome; the rationale for a brain-cognition claim is currently purely mechanistic (cellular energy metabolism), and a 2026 systematic review context (not read in full here) continues to frame NAD+ supplementation primarily around metabolic/anti-aging biomarkers, not cognition. Coenzyme Q10 has essentially no positive human cognition signal: a 2021 RCT in schizophrenia/schizoaffective disorder found no effect on cognitive function [testerman2021coq10schizophrenia if applicable - see study record], and a 2026 RCT in older adults found CoQ10 raised plasma levels without improving mitochondrial function [schmucker2026coq10], consistent with the view that CoQ10 is a biomarker-active but functionally inert supplement in this context; no cognitive benefit in healthy adults has been demonstrated. Pyrroloquinoline quinone (PQQ) has one industry-sponsored RCT in healthy volunteers reporting improved performance on some cognitive tests after dietary PQQ disodium salt [shiojima2022pqqhealthy], but this is a single trial from a group with a commercial interest in the ingredient and has not been independently replicated; a separate 2024 RCT in MCI patients examined mitochondrial biomarkers and brain metabolism alongside cognition [baltic2024pqqmci], again a clinical, not healthy-adult, population. Oral GABA's systematic review of 14 placebo-controlled human trials found 'limited evidence for stress' benefits and 'very limited evidence for sleep' benefits, explicitly noting mixed results and that the mechanism by which an orally ingested amino acid that poorly crosses the blood-brain barrier could exert central effects remains unresolved [hepsomali2020gaba]; no claim of memory or cognitive enhancement is supported, only a tentative stress/anxiety signal. Beta-alanine, best known as a muscle-carnosine-boosting ergogenic aid, has the most relevant evidence for a specific, narrow context: a narrative review of studies (many military) found beta-alanine supplementation improved measures of cognitive function and resiliency specifically under conditions of physical/cognitive fatigue (e.g., sustained military operations), not in rested baseline testing [ostfeld2023betaalanine]; this is consistent with a fatigue-mitigation rather than general-enhancement mechanism and has not been shown in ordinary knowledge-worker contexts. Across all eight compounds, the Brain Food-relevant conclusion is consistent: promising mechanisms and biomarker changes abound, but controlled human cognitive-outcome evidence in healthy, adequately nourished adults is sparse, inconsistent, or simply absent, and the strongest positive findings cluster in clinically impaired (MCI, prodromal AD) or acutely fatigued/stressed populations rather than the rested healthy-adult target market.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "None of these eight compounds has an established deficiency syndrome in the general population analogous to a vitamin deficiency; they are better framed as dietary components whose higher habitual intake (ergothioneine, spermidine, carnosine/anserine) is associated with healthy-ageing outcomes in observational data, versus pharmacological-dose interventions (NAD precursors, PQQ, concentrated spermidine extracts, GABA, beta-alanine) tested at doses well above ordinary dietary intake. The clearest 'enhancement in a deficient-adjacent state' pattern is beta-alanine under physical/cognitive fatigue (where muscle carnosine depletion plausibly limits buffering capacity) and, more speculatively, ergothioneine and anserine in people already showing cognitive decline (MCI), where endogenous antioxidant/scavenging capacity may be reduced. For rested, adequately nourished healthy adults, no compound in this group has demonstrated a reliable enhancement effect beyond what a varied diet (mushrooms, legumes, poultry, fermented foods) already supplies.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "This group of ingredients is heavily marketed under 'longevity' and 'cellular energy' narratives (NAD boosters, spermidine, PQQ, CoQ10) that blur a biomarker change (raised NAD+, raised plasma CoQ10, raised brain creatine-adjacent energy metabolites) with a cognitive outcome; the evidence reviewed here shows this distinction matters: NMN/NR trials raise NAD+ reliably but report no cognitive outcome at all, and CoQ10 raises plasma levels without improving mitochondrial function, let alone cognition. Spermidine marketing frequently cites the promising 2018 pilot trial while omitting the larger, better-powered 2022 SmartAge null result from an overlapping author group - a textbook pilot-versus-confirmatory-trial problem that any honest communication must disclose. Beta-alanine and GABA have the most defensible narrow claims (fatigue-mitigation performance support; a tentative stress-reduction signal, respectively) but even these should not be extended to general 'memory' or 'focus' claims. The honest Brain Food position is that most of this basket belongs in a 'promising research area, not yet actionable for a healthy-adult cognition claim' category, with ordinary dietary sources (mushrooms, legumes, poultry, fermented foods) representing the evidence-consistent recommendation rather than concentrated extracts.", "mechanism": {"summary": "These compounds act through at least five distinct, largely independent mechanistic routes: (1) antioxidant/radical-scavenging action (ergothioneine's unique thiourea structure, carnosine/anserine's histidine-dipeptide scavenging of reactive carbonyls and hypochlorous acid); (2) autophagy and mitochondrial quality control (spermidine, acting via eIF5A hypusination and Atg7/Parkin/Pink1-dependent mitophagy in animal models); (3) NAD+ salvage pathway replenishment supporting sirtuin activity and cellular energy metabolism (nicotinamide riboside, nicotinamide mononucleotide); (4) mitochondrial electron transport chain support (coenzyme Q10, PQQ, the latter also a redox cofactor and reported neurotrophic-factor inducer in animal models); (5) inhibitory GABAergic neurotransmission modulation, of uncertain relevance when GABA is taken orally given limited blood-brain barrier permeability, and intramuscular pH buffering via carnosine synthesis (beta-alanine). None of these mechanisms has been directly linked to a validated, reproducible human cognitive-outcome change in healthy adults in the evidence reviewed; the closest to a demonstrated human-relevant pathway is beta-alanine's muscle carnosine loading (well established) feeding into a fatigue-resistance mechanism under physical/cognitive stress.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Mushroom/legume ergothioneine or carnosine/anserine from poultry/fish -> intestinal absorption via dedicated transporters (OCTN1 for ergothioneine; PEPT2/beta-alanine-histidine dipeptide transporters for carnosine/anserine)", "detail": "Ergothioneine uses the OCTN1 transporter and accumulates in tissues; anserine/carnosine are absorbed as dipeptides or hydrolysed then resynthesised; absorption is established in human pharmacokinetic literature cited in review sources.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Absorbed compound -> tissue antioxidant/scavenging action (reactive oxygen/nitrogen species, hypochlorous acid, reactive carbonyls)", "detail": "Proposed mechanism for both ergothioneine and anserine protecting against oxidative/carbonyl stress implicated in cognitive decline; demonstrated in vitro and in the mechanistic rationale of [masuoka2021anserine], limited direct human brain-level confirmation.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Dietary spermidine -> blood-brain barrier crossing -> hippocampal eIF5A hypusination and Atg7/Parkin/Pink1-dependent mitophagy -> improved mitochondrial respiratory competence", "detail": "Fully demonstrated in mouse and Drosophila models with genetic pathway dissection [schroeder2021spermidine]; human brain-level confirmation of this pathway has not been performed.", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Oral NAD precursor (NR/NMN) -> absorption and conversion -> increased blood/tissue NAD+ -> sirtuin and mitochondrial pathway activation", "detail": "Human NAD+ elevation after oral NMN/NR is repeatedly demonstrated and considered an established human pharmacodynamic effect [irie2020nmnjapan, kim2022nmnsafety]; translation to any measured cognitive outcome has not been tested in the trials retrieved.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Beta-alanine -> muscle carnosine synthesis (combining with histidine) -> intramuscular/possibly central pH buffering under metabolic stress", "detail": "Muscle carnosine loading with beta-alanine supplementation is one of the best-established human pharmacodynamic effects in sports nutrition; the cognitive-fatigue-mitigation link is inferred from performance studies under physical/cognitive load [ostfeld2023betaalanine], not from direct brain carnosine measurement.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Oral GABA -> limited blood-brain barrier crossing -> proposed peripheral/enteric nervous system or vagal signalling route to central stress/sleep circuits", "detail": "Mechanism by which peripherally administered GABA could affect central stress/sleep outcomes remains unresolved and is explicitly flagged as uncertain by the systematic review authors [hepsomali2020gaba].", "evidence_level": "speculative"}, {"step": "Measured human cognitive/clinical outcome", "detail": "Positive signals confined to small pilot or clinical-population trials (ergothioneine/anserine in MCI, spermidine pilot in subjective cognitive decline that failed to replicate, PQQ industry trial in healthy adults unreplicated, beta-alanine under fatigue); no consistent effect demonstrated in rested healthy adults for any of the eight compounds.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["OCTN1-mediated ergothioneine transport and antioxidant action", "polyamine/autophagy-mitophagy axis (spermidine)", "histidine-dipeptide (carnosine/anserine) carbonyl and hypochlorous-acid scavenging", "NAD+ salvage pathway and sirtuin activation (NR/NMN)", "mitochondrial electron transport chain support (CoQ10, PQQ)", "GABAergic neurotransmission (uncertain peripheral-to-central translation for oral GABA)", "muscle carnosine synthesis and pH buffering (beta-alanine)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "older_adults_mci", "population_detail": "Older adults with subjective cognitive decline (not formally diagnosed MCI) at risk for dementia", "dose_or_intake": "spermidine-rich plant extract supplement, 12-month trial 0.9 mg spermidine/day", "duration": "3 months (pilot) and 12 months (confirmatory)", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "3-month pilot RCT (n=30) found moderate-to-large effect on mnemonic discrimination (d=0.77-0.79); the properly powered 12-month, 100-person confirmatory SmartAge trial from an overlapping group found no significant effect on the same primary memory outcome or any secondary outcome.", "effect_estimate": "Pilot: Cohen's d=0.77 (95% CI 0, 1.53) for overall memory, d=0.79 (95% CI 0.01, 1.55) for mnemonic discrimination [wirth2018spermidine]; confirmatory: between-group difference -0.03 (95% CI -0.11 to 0.05), not significant [schwarz2022spermidine]", "certainty_notes": "Classic pilot-to-confirmatory-trial non-replication; downgraded heavily for inconsistency; the larger, longer, better-powered trial is the more reliable estimate and it is null. Neither trial is in healthy adults without subjective cognitive concerns.", "key_study_ids": ["wirth2018spermidine", "schwarz2022spermidine"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "resistance_to_decline", "population": "older_adults_mci", "population_detail": "Patients with mild cognitive impairment", "dose_or_intake": "ergothioneine, dose not detailed in abstract (pilot study)", "duration": "pilot trial duration not detailed in abstract (described as a pilot)", "grade": "D", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Small pilot RCT reported a signal of delayed cognitive decline on some measures in MCI subjects given ergothioneine versus placebo; authors describe it explicitly as a pilot study, implying it was not powered to confirm efficacy.", "effect_estimate": "not reported in sufficient detail in abstract; described qualitatively as delaying decline", "certainty_notes": "Single small pilot, clinical (MCI) population, no independent replication found; cannot be generalised to healthy adults or treated as more than preliminary/exploratory (grade D, mechanistic-adjacent human pilot).", "key_study_ids": ["yau2024ergothioneine"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "resistance_to_decline", "population": "older_adults_mci", "population_detail": "Patients with mild cognitive impairment", "dose_or_intake": "anserine supplementation, dose not detailed in abstract", "duration": "trial duration not detailed in abstract", "grade": "D", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Single RCT reported anserine (a hypochlorous-acid scavenger) protected against further cognitive decline in MCI patients relative to placebo over the study period.", "effect_estimate": "not reported in sufficient quantitative detail in abstract", "certainty_notes": "Single trial, clinical population (MCI), some authors have industry/commercial links to anserine-containing products (Katakura/poultry-extract industry ties noted in the brief's leads, though not directly confirmed for this specific author list in the record retrieved); downgraded for indirectness to healthy adults and lack of independent replication.", "key_study_ids": ["masuoka2021anserine"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Patients with prodromal Alzheimer's disease (multinutrient medical food containing uridine plus DHA, EPA, choline, phospholipids, vitamins - not uridine alone)", "dose_or_intake": "Souvenaid/Fortasyn Connect multinutrient drink, once daily", "duration": "3 years (post-hoc combined analysis of LipiDiDiet)", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Post-hoc analysis of the LipiDiDiet RCT found a slowing of decline on a composite clinical/cognitive measure over 3 years in prodromal Alzheimer's disease patients receiving the multinutrient product versus control.", "effect_estimate": "composite score slowing reported in post-hoc analysis; exact effect size not extracted from abstract-level record", "certainty_notes": "Multinutrient formulation (not isolated uridine), post-hoc (not pre-specified primary) analysis, prodromal Alzheimer's disease population; cannot support any isolated-uridine claim or any claim in healthy adults.", "key_study_ids": ["soininen2023lipididiet"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "other", "population": "replete_or_high_baseline", "population_detail": "Healthy middle-aged and older adults (NAD+ biomarker/safety trials, no cognitive outcome measured)", "dose_or_intake": "nicotinamide mononucleotide, various doses up to 500 mg+/day across trials", "duration": "weeks to a few months across trials", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "Multiple RCTs confirm NMN/NR supplementation reliably raises blood NAD+ levels and is generally well tolerated in healthy and overweight/obese middle-aged and older adults, but none of the trials identified in this search measured a cognitive outcome.", "effect_estimate": "NAD+ level increases reported in each trial (biomarker only); no cognitive effect size exists because none was measured", "certainty_notes": "This is a biomarker-only evidence base; mechanism is not efficacy, and no inference about cognition can be drawn, grade D reflects mechanistic/biomarker status not a tested-and-failed cognitive hypothesis.", "key_study_ids": ["irie2020nmnjapan", "kim2022nmnsafety"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "other", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Patients with schizophrenia/schizoaffective disorder (CoQ10) and older adults (mitochondrial function, not cognition specifically, in the 2026 trial)", "dose_or_intake": "coenzyme Q10, various doses across trials", "duration": "weeks to months", "grade": "E", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "RCT evidence found no effect of CoQ10 on cognitive function in a psychiatric clinical population, and a 2026 RCT in older adults found CoQ10 raised plasma CoQ10 levels without improving mitochondrial function, undercutting the proposed bioenergetic mechanism translating to any functional (including cognitive) benefit.", "effect_estimate": "no significant effect reported in either trial", "certainty_notes": "Small number of trials, clinical/geriatric populations rather than healthy adults, but the consistent null/negative direction and the mechanistic undercutting (biomarker rises without functional improvement) support a grade E (not supported) characterisation for any cognition claim.", "key_study_ids": ["schmucker2026coq10"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adult volunteers (industry-sponsored RCT)", "dose_or_intake": "pyrroloquinoline quinone disodium salt, dose per abstract (dietary PQQ)", "duration": "trial duration not detailed in abstract excerpt", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Single RCT in healthy volunteers reported improved performance on some cognitive function measures with dietary PQQ disodium salt versus placebo.", "effect_estimate": "not reported in sufficient quantitative detail in abstract excerpt", "certainty_notes": "Single trial with apparent industry sponsorship/commercial interest in the ingredient (PQQ disodium salt), no independent replication identified; downgraded for risk of bias and lack of replication despite being in the correct target population.", "key_study_ids": ["shiojima2022pqqhealthy"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Adults across 14 pooled placebo-controlled trials of oral GABA for stress and sleep", "dose_or_intake": "varied across the 14 pooled trials", "duration": "varied across the 14 pooled trials", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Systematic review of 14 placebo-controlled human trials found limited evidence for a stress-reduction benefit and very limited evidence for a sleep benefit of oral GABA; results across trials were mixed, and the biological plausibility of a peripherally administered amino acid producing central effects remains unresolved.", "effect_estimate": "not pooled quantitatively; narrative synthesis only, described as 'limited' and 'very limited' evidence", "certainty_notes": "Narrative systematic review without meta-analytic pooling, heterogeneous trial designs and GABA sources (natural vs biosynthetic), mechanism of action for an orally administered, poorly blood-brain-barrier-permeable amino acid remains uncertain; this is mood/stress, not memory or executive cognition.", "key_study_ids": ["hepsomali2020gaba"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "cognitive_fatigue", "population": "high_cognitive_workload", "population_detail": "Soldiers and other populations under sustained physical/cognitive fatigue (military operational studies)", "dose_or_intake": "beta-alanine, typical loading protocols (several weeks pre-loading then maintenance), doses per individual trials not pooled in abstract", "duration": "acute performance testing after chronic loading protocols", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Narrative review of beta-alanine supplementation studies, largely in military/soldier populations, found improvements in measures of cognitive function and resiliency specifically under conditions of physical and cognitive fatigue, consistent with a fatigue-mitigation rather than general cognitive-enhancement mechanism.", "effect_estimate": "not pooled quantitatively in the narrative review; described qualitatively as improved performance and resiliency under fatigue", "certainty_notes": "Narrative (non-systematic, non-meta-analytic) review, military/operational populations under extreme fatigue rather than ordinary knowledge workers, heterogeneous outcome measures across the underlying studies; downgraded for indirectness to a rested or moderately fatigued Brain Food target consumer.", "key_study_ids": ["ostfeld2023betaalanine"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "not systematically studied for acute single-dose cognitive testing across this group except beta-alanine's fatigue-context performance studies", "onset": "not applicable for most compounds in this group (chronic loading protocols typical)", "duration_studied": "beta-alanine requires weeks of chronic loading before any acute fatigue-context performance effect is observed; the 'acute' effect is tested after chronic loading, not after a single dose", "domain": "cognitive_fatigue", "population": "high_cognitive_workload", "effect_estimate": "qualitative improvement under fatigue reported in military studies [ostfeld2023betaalanine]; no quantitative effect size extracted", "certainty": "low (narrative review, operational populations)", "safety": "beta-alanine's main acute effect is paraesthesia (tingling), well characterised and benign in sports-nutrition literature", "study_ids": ["ostfeld2023betaalanine"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "3 months vs 12 months", "population": "older_adults_mci", "outcome": "memory (mnemonic discrimination) with spermidine", "effect_estimate": "pilot d=0.77-0.79 (positive); confirmatory trial between-group difference -0.03 (95% CI -0.11 to 0.05, null)", "evidence_quality": "low (classic pilot-vs-confirmatory non-replication)", "limitations": "Small pilot sample (n=30) likely overestimated effect size; confirmatory trial (n=100) is the more reliable null estimate; neither trial in healthy adults.", "study_ids": ["wirth2018spermidine", "schwarz2022spermidine"]}, {"duration": "weeks to months", "population": "replete_or_high_baseline", "outcome": "NAD+ biomarker elevation with NMN/NR, no cognitive outcome measured", "effect_estimate": "NAD+ levels reliably increased; cognitive effect unknown (not measured)", "evidence_quality": "biomarker only, not applicable to cognitive grading", "limitations": "No trial in this search measured a cognitive endpoint; cannot infer cognitive benefit from a metabolic biomarker change.", "study_ids": ["irie2020nmnjapan", "kim2022nmnsafety"]}, {"duration": "3 years (post-hoc)", "population": "clinical_population", "outcome": "composite clinical/cognitive decline in prodromal Alzheimer's disease with a uridine-containing multinutrient", "effect_estimate": "slowing of decline reported in post-hoc composite analysis", "evidence_quality": "low-moderate (post-hoc analysis, multinutrient not isolated uridine, prodromal AD population)", "limitations": "Cannot isolate uridine's contribution from the other active ingredients (DHA, EPA, choline, phospholipids, vitamins); not generalisable to healthy adults.", "study_ids": ["soininen2023lipididiet"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Safety profiles vary substantially across this heterogeneous group. Ergothioneine, spermidine, carnosine/anserine, and beta-alanine (all with long dietary-exposure history) have generally reassuring short/medium-term tolerability in the trials reviewed. NAD precursors (NR/NMN) have a growing, reassuring safety/tolerability literature in healthy and overweight adults at studied doses. CoQ10 is widely considered safe but functionally may not do what is claimed. Oral GABA and PQQ have smaller, less consistent safety databases at the doses used in cognitive-claim-oriented trials. Beta-alanine's main acute effect (paraesthesia/tingling) is well characterised and benign. No compound in this group showed a major safety signal in the trials reviewed, but several (NMN especially) still lack long-term (multi-year) human safety data, and regulatory status is unsettled for some (see regulatory section).", "adverse_effects": ["beta-alanine: paraesthesia/tingling (dose-related, benign)", "spermidine: adverse events balanced between groups in the largest trial, no specific signal reported", "NMN/NR: generally well tolerated in short/medium-term trials, no major signal reported in the safety trials reviewed", "GABA: adverse event data not systematically summarised across the 14 pooled trials in the review", "ergothioneine, anserine, PQQ, CoQ10: no major adverse event signal identified in the records reviewed, but detailed safety reporting was often sparse in the abstract-level metadata available for this compact-tier search"], "contraindications": ["no well-established contraindications identified for any of the eight compounds at typical studied doses in the general healthy adult population, based on the records reviewed", "GABA: theoretical caution in people taking GABAergic medications (e.g., benzodiazepines, baclofen) given shared neurotransmitter pathway, though not directly demonstrated in the trials reviewed"], "drug_interactions": ["GABA: theoretical interaction with GABAergic/sedative medications, not directly tested in the trials reviewed", "CoQ10: known theoretical interaction with warfarin (reduces anticoagulant effect) reported in broader pharmacology literature, not from a trial in this search specifically", "no other specific drug interactions identified in the trials reviewed for this unit"], "upper_limits": "No official tolerable upper intake levels (UL) have been set by EFSA or NIH-ODS for most of these compounds (ergothioneine, spermidine, anserine, PQQ, oral GABA) at the time of this search; NMN/NR have been assessed for safety up to specific trial doses (e.g., 500mg+/day in some RCTs) without a formal UL being established in the records reviewed; beta-alanine sports-nutrition literature supports loading doses around 3.2-6.4 g/day as generally well tolerated aside from paraesthesia.", "sleep_impact": "Oral GABA is specifically marketed for sleep, but the Hepsomali 2020 systematic review found only 'very limited evidence' for a sleep benefit; no other compound in this group has dedicated sleep-outcome data in the records reviewed.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Long-term (multi-year) human exposure data are essentially absent for ergothioneine, spermidine (longest trial 12 months), carnosine/anserine, PQQ, and oral GABA at supplement doses; NMN/NR have slightly more medium-term (months) safety data; CoQ10 has a longer history of general supplement use but not specifically for a cognitive claim duration.", "daily_use_appropriate": "uncertain", "special_populations": "Pregnancy/lactation: no dedicated safety data identified for any of these eight compounds at supplement doses; not recommended without specialist guidance. Psychiatric medication users: GABA's theoretical interaction with GABAergic/sedative drugs warrants specialist review. Chronic disease: NMN/NR trials in diabetic and overweight/obese older adults suggest reasonable tolerability in some chronic-disease contexts, but this should not be extrapolated to other conditions without specialist review.", "specialist_review_needed": ["clinical pharmacologist to review GABA/sedative medication interaction risk before any stress/sleep claim", "regulatory toxicologist to assess whether tolerable upper intake levels should be formally derived for ergothioneine, spermidine, anserine, PQQ, and oral GABA given the current absence of official ULs", "food safety/novel-food specialist for concentrated spermidine extracts and for NMN given unresolved EU/US regulatory status (see regulatory section)"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "No major safety signal was identified for any of the eight compounds in the trials reviewed, but the group as a whole lacks long-term human exposure data, lacks formally established upper intake levels for most members, and includes at least one ingredient (NMN) with contested regulatory/dietary-ingredient status that itself reflects unresolved safety-framework questions; a conditional rather than unconditional pass is warranted pending ingredient-by-ingredient specialist review."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Status varies by compound and requires individual verification before any claim: ergothioneine, carnosine/anserine, and beta-alanine have general food/supplement history without known novel-food barriers; concentrated spermidine extracts have faced novel-food classification questions in some EU assessments (per the brief's lead, to be confirmed against the current EU novel food catalogue); nicotinamide mononucleotide (NMN) is understood from the brief's leads to not be authorised as a novel food in the EU at the time of this search, pending direct verification against the EU Novel Food Catalogue; no EFSA Article 13.1 cognitive-function health claim has been authorised for any of the eight compounds based on the records reviewed in this unit.", "us": "All eight compounds are generally marketed as dietary ingredients under DSHEA; NMN's status as a lawful dietary ingredient has been publicly contested by FDA in connection with drug-investigation exclusion rules (per the brief's lead; this specific regulatory determination was not independently re-verified via a dedicated FDA document search in this unit and should be confirmed with a regulatory specialist before any NMN-containing product decision); structure/function cognitive claims for any of these ingredients would require company-held competent and reliable scientific evidence, which the evidence reviewed here is generally too weak (in healthy adults) to support.", "morocco": "No specific ONSSA or Ministry of Health authorisation records were identified in this search for any of the eight compounds; status uncertain for all and would need direct verification with ONSSA's food supplement registration lists before any product claim or import filing.", "permitted_claims_examples": [], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "Concentrated spermidine extracts and NMN are the two ingredients in this group with genuine, documented regulatory uncertainty (novel food status in the EU for spermidine extracts depending on concentration/source; contested dietary-ingredient status for NMN in the US); both require direct regulatory-affairs verification before any formulation decision. Ergothioneine, carnosine/anserine, PQQ, CoQ10, oral GABA, and beta-alanine were not identified as having similarly unresolved novel-food/NDI status in this search, but this was not independently verified against the EU Novel Food Catalogue or FDA NDI database for every item and should be confirmed by a regulatory specialist.", "regulatory_gate": "unknown"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Mixed: ergothioneine (mushroom extract), spermidine (wheat germ extract), and carnosine/anserine (poultry/fish-derived) have reasonably good food-matrix precedent since they occur naturally in recognisable foods; beta-alanine and oral GABA are small, stable amino-acid-like molecules compatible with beverage/powder formats; NMN, PQQ, and CoQ10 are typically delivered as standardised supplement-grade ingredients rather than whole-food formats at the doses studied.", "formats": ["capsule/tablet (most common across all eight compounds)", "powder/sachet (beta-alanine, GABA, some spermidine extracts)", "functional beverage (GABA, beta-alanine feasible; others less so due to dose or stability)", "fortified food matrix leveraging natural sources (mushroom-derived ergothioneine, wheat-germ spermidine, poultry-derived carnosine/anserine) is plausible but not demonstrated at scale in the trials reviewed"], "stability": "Beta-alanine and GABA are small, stable amino acids with good shelf stability; ergothioneine is notably heat- and oxidation-stable (part of its proposed biological role as an antioxidant); CoQ10 and PQQ have known formulation/bioavailability challenges (lipophilicity for CoQ10) addressed in industry formulations (e.g., ubiquinol vs ubiquinone) not detailed in the trials reviewed here; spermidine extract stability in food matrices was not specifically assessed in this search.", "taste": "Beta-alanine produces a characteristic skin tingling (paraesthesia) unrelated to taste but a notable sensory experience; GABA, ergothioneine, and carnosine/anserine are not reported to have major taste barriers in the records reviewed; detailed taste/palatability data were not the focus of the clinical trials reviewed and would need dedicated food-science testing.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Oral bioavailability is established for NMN, NR, beta-alanine, and GABA (serum/tissue level changes documented in the safety/pharmacokinetic trials); bioavailability data specific to a food (versus isolated supplement) matrix were not available for any of the eight compounds in this search.", "dose_feasibility": "Varies widely: the SmartAge spermidine dose (0.9 mg/day) is food-plausible (achievable via wheat germ/legume-rich diet) but was the dose that produced a null result, raising the question of whether a higher, less food-feasible dose would be needed for any effect; beta-alanine's typical loading doses (3.2-6.4 g/day) are large for a food serving but achievable via a dedicated powder/beverage; NMN, PQQ, and CoQ10 doses used in trials are generally supplement-scale rather than ordinary-food-serving-scale.", "cost_availability": "not researched here", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 2, "effect_magnitude": 2, "safety": 6, "reproducibility": 2, "food_suitability": 4, "cost_access": 4, "rationale": "evidence_quality=2: across all eight compounds, the only healthy-adult interventional cognitive trial with reasonable methodology (PQQ) carries a likely industry-sponsorship risk of bias, and the single best-powered trial available for any compound (spermidine SmartAge) is null; most other signals are small clinical-population pilots. effect_magnitude=2: where any signal exists (ergothioneine pilot, anserine, PQQ, beta-alanine under fatigue) it is small, unreplicated, or context-restricted (fatigue only for beta-alanine). safety=6: no major signal found but long-term data and formal upper limits are largely absent, and two ingredients (spermidine extract, NMN) carry open regulatory status reflecting unresolved safety-framework questions. reproducibility=2: the spermidine pilot-to-confirmatory non-replication is the clearest reproducibility failure in the whole file, and most other compounds have only a single trial each. food_suitability=4: several compounds (ergothioneine, spermidine, carnosine/anserine, beta-alanine, GABA) have genuine whole-food precedent even though efficacious doses may exceed ordinary serving sizes; others (NMN, PQQ, CoQ10) are supplement-format ingredients. cost_access=4: broadly available as commodity or semi-specialty supplement ingredients, though cost/price data were not specifically researched in this unit."}, "research_gaps": ["No trial of any of these eight compounds has tested cognitive outcomes in rested, healthy young adults or students, with the partial exception of the single, likely industry-linked PQQ trial.", "No independent (non-industry-affiliated) replication exists for the PQQ healthy-volunteer cognitive finding.", "No dose-ranging study has tested whether a higher, still food-feasible spermidine dose might succeed where the SmartAge 0.9 mg/day dose failed.", "No cognitive-outcome trial of NMN or NR has been conducted in any population despite an extensive NAD+/safety literature; this is the single largest and most actionable research gap in the group.", "No large, well-powered RCT exists for ergothioneine or anserine beyond small MCI pilots; healthy-adult trials are entirely absent.", "No trial has directly tested beta-alanine's cognitive-fatigue effect in civilian knowledge workers (e.g., exam stress, shift work) rather than military operational contexts.", "Industry/commercial conflicts of interest (spermidine: Longevity Labs; PQQ: Bagchi-affiliated authors; GABA review: Unilever-affiliated lead author) were identified for several key studies and should be weighted accordingly in any evidence synthesis used for marketing claims."], "_derived": {"composite_score": 3.2, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "unknown", "best_grade_acute": "C", "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": "C", "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": null, "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"resistance_to_decline": "D", "global_cognition": "C", "executive_function": "C", "cognitive_fatigue": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 9, "n_studies": 13, "n_verified": 13, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 2, "n_systematic_reviews": 2, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["inconsistent", "insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "taurine", "name": "Taurine", "category": "E", "category_name": "Amino acids and related compounds", "aliases": ["taurine", "2-aminoethanesulfonic acid", "L-taurine (non-chiral, often marketed this way)"], "food_sources": ["meat (beef, pork, lamb)", "poultry", "fish and shellfish (scallops, mussels, octopus especially high)", "dairy (small amounts)", "absent or negligible in plant foods; human milk contains taurine, infant formula is fortified", "energy drinks and some sports/functional beverages contain added taurine (commonly ~1000 mg per 250 ml)"], "active_constituents": ["taurine (free amino sulfonic acid, not incorporated into protein; abundant intracellularly in heart, retina, muscle and brain)"], "one_line_verdict": "Taurine is safe up to at least 6 g/day in adults and is biologically plausible as a neuromodulator, but no human trial has isolated a taurine-alone cognitive benefit in healthy adults; in the energy-drink literature taurine adds little or nothing to caffeine's effect and sometimes blunts it.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. Taurine is an abundant intracellular amino sulfonic acid synthesised endogenously from cysteine and obtained from meat, fish and dairy; it is not incorporated into protein and has no established dietary requirement in healthy humans. In animals it modulates GABA-A and glycine receptors and has antioxidant and osmoregulatory roles; these mechanisms are reviewed narratively but are supported mainly by animal and in-vitro work [chen2019taurine]. Direct human cognitive data come almost entirely from energy-drink research, where taurine is co-administered with caffeine and other ingredients, making isolated attribution difficult. In whom, and how large, in the Brain Food target population. The most interpretable study (n=15, 4-way crossover, students, acute) separated the three main Red Bull-type ingredients and found that caffeine alone improved executive control, working memory and reaction time, while taurine alone slowed choice reaction time, modestly sped a working-memory-task reaction time, and reversed caffeine's negative effect on vigour and withdrawal-type symptoms; taurine did not add measurable cognitive benefit beyond caffeine [giles2012]. A second factorial study (n=36, acute) found caffeine alone sped reaction time on a stimulus-degradation task, while adding taurine tended to attenuate (not augment) caffeine's benefit, with no effect on an oddball discrimination task [peacock2013]. An earlier, smaller, non-factorial trial in 10 graduate students found that the full three-ingredient energy-drink mixture (caffeine, taurine, glucuronolactone) versus placebo prevented the late-night rise in P300 latency and reaction time and preserved mood scores; because taurine was never tested alone, no taurine-specific effect can be inferred [seidl2000]. A comprehensive narrative review of 'energy blend' ingredients (32 studies reviewed) concluded there is an 'overwhelming lack of evidence' that taurine or other non-caffeine constituents of energy drinks have measurable performance or cognitive effects, and that caffeine accounts for essentially all of the benefit [mclellan2012]. A 2025 Bayesian network meta-analysis of caffeine and taurine across 12 mostly sport-performance studies reported a credible synergistic effect of caffeine+taurine on reaction time (g=0.75, 95% CrI 0.29-1.18) and anaerobic capacity (g=0.46, 95% CrI 0.19-0.71) [deng2025], but this pooled result conflicts directly with the two factorial trials above that isolated taurine's own contribution and found it neutral-to-negative for cognition; the discrepancy likely reflects pooling of heterogeneous sport/cognitive outcomes and publication bias toward positive energy-drink findings, and the factorial trials are methodologically stronger for isolating taurine's own effect. No trial has tested taurine alone, without caffeine, on cognition in healthy young adults; no chronic (multi-week) human cognitive trial of taurine exists in this population. Safety and concerning signals. Chronic intake up to 6 g/day has not shown adverse effects in adult safety trials reviewed by EFSA; higher-dose energy-drink patterns raise concern mainly because of co-ingested caffeine and sugar rather than taurine itself, although taurine's neuroendocrine (cortisol, vasopressin) and cardiac electrophysiological interactions are incompletely characterised in humans [caine2016]. Adolescent and developing-brain risk is a distinct, separately studied question and should not be generalised from or to adults [curran2017]. What is unknown. Whether taurine has any independent, non-caffeine cognitive effect in rested healthy adults; whether chronic supplementation changes any cognitive domain; and whether any benefit seen in energy-drink mixtures would survive removal of caffeine and sugar. The honest summary is that taurine is a well-tolerated, cheap, naturally occurring amino acid with a biologically plausible but human-cognition-unproven role; its presence in energy drinks is best understood as a marketing-differentiator ingredient riding on caffeine's real effect [giles2012, peacock2013, mclellan2012].", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Taurine is not an essential nutrient for healthy adult humans: it is synthesised endogenously from cysteine and abundant in an omnivorous diet; strict vegans have lower plasma taurine but no established clinical cognitive deficit has been demonstrated in healthy adult vegans. All the human cognitive data reviewed here are enhancement-type experiments in adequately nourished, non-deficient students and young adults, not deficiency-correction studies, and none isolates a clear cognitive benefit of adding taurine [giles2012, peacock2013]. There is no human evidence that taurine supplementation corrects a measurable taurine inadequacy to the benefit of cognition in the target population.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Energy-drink marketing presents taurine as a co-active 'brain fuel' alongside caffeine, implying it meaningfully adds to alertness or cognitive performance. The best available factorial human trials show the opposite: taurine alone does not improve, and sometimes mildly impairs, reaction time and attenuates part of caffeine's benefit [giles2012, peacock2013]; a comprehensive review of energy-drink constituents found an 'overwhelming lack of evidence' for taurine's independent contribution [mclellan2012]. The one meta-analysis suggesting a caffeine-taurine cognitive synergy pools heterogeneous sport-performance and cognitive outcomes and conflicts with the direct factorial evidence [deng2025]. No EU health claim for taurine and cognition is authorised, and EFSA's 2009 safety opinion addressed safety, not efficacy. An honest Brain Food position is that taurine can be included as a traditionally used, well-characterised, inexpensive and safe-at-studied-doses ingredient, but it cannot be marketed as improving mental performance beyond what caffeine itself provides.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Taurine is absorbed intact via intestinal transporters (TAUT/SLC6A6), distributed intracellularly in high concentration in excitable tissues including brain, and modulates GABA-A and glycine receptor chloride channels, acts as an osmolyte, and has antioxidant and mitochondrial-stabilising properties in animal and in-vitro systems. A cognitive mechanism (altered inhibitory tone, reduced oxidative stress in neurons) is plausible but has not been shown to translate into a measurable human cognitive effect independent of co-administered caffeine.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Dietary/supplemental taurine -> intestinal absorption via TAUT transporter -> plasma taurine rise", "detail": "Well characterised pharmacokinetically; oral taurine raises plasma levels within 1-2 h.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Plasma taurine -> uptake into excitable tissue (including brain) via taurine transporters", "detail": "Taurine crosses the blood-brain barrier via specific transporters; brain taurine content and turnover are studied mainly in animals.", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Intracellular taurine -> modulation of GABA-A and glycine receptor chloride channels and osmoregulation", "detail": "Receptor-modulatory and osmoregulatory actions are established in animal electrophysiology; human receptor-level confirmation is lacking [chen2019taurine].", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Receptor/osmotic modulation -> proposed neuroprotective/antioxidant action under metabolic stress", "detail": "Reviewed mechanistic hypothesis (mitochondrial stabilisation, reduced oxidative stress) based on animal models of injury and aging; not demonstrated in healthy human brain function [chen2019taurine].", "evidence_level": "plausible"}, {"step": "Measured human cognitive/behavioural outcome", "detail": "In factorial human trials taurine alone did not improve, and sometimes slowed, reaction time, and attenuated part of caffeine's benefit; taurine modestly improved mood/vigour measures in one trial [giles2012, peacock2013].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["GABA-A receptor modulation", "glycine receptor modulation", "cell volume/osmoregulation", "antioxidant/mitochondrial stabilisation (animal)", "possible neuroendocrine (cortisol/vasopressin) interaction [caine2016]"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "reaction_time", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "University students, acute single-dose, rested (not sleep-deprived)", "dose_or_intake": "~1.6-2 g taurine (dose equivalent to a standard energy-drink serving), isolated from caffeine and glucose in a 4-way factorial design", "duration": "single dose, tested over following hours", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Taurine alone slowed choice reaction time but modestly sped a working-memory-task reaction time; it reversed some of caffeine's negative effect on vigour/withdrawal symptoms but added no net cognitive benefit over caffeine alone [giles2012]. A second factorial trial found taurine attenuated rather than augmented caffeine's reaction-time benefit, with no effect on an oddball task [peacock2013].", "effect_estimate": "Direction and magnitude of individual contrasts not reported as single pooled numbers in the abstracts; effects described as directionally mixed and small; not a clean benefit or harm", "certainty_notes": "Only two factorial human trials isolate taurine from caffeine/glucose; small samples (n=15, n=36), single-dose, single-laboratory; downgraded for imprecision and indirectness (mixed, non-replicated directions).", "key_study_ids": ["giles2012", "peacock2013"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "alertness", "population": "students", "population_detail": "Graduate students, nighttime testing, combined caffeine+taurine+glucuronolactone (not taurine alone)", "dose_or_intake": "one commercial energy-drink serving (caffeine, taurine, glucuronolactone combined)", "duration": "single dose, measured over several hours", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "The combined drink prevented the late-night rise in P300 latency/reaction time and preserved well-being scores versus placebo in 10 students, but taurine was never isolated, so no taurine-specific inference is possible [seidl2000].", "effect_estimate": "Not reported as taurine-specific; whole-mixture effect only", "certainty_notes": "Confounded design; cannot attribute effect to taurine; small n=10; downgraded for indirectness.", "key_study_ids": ["seidl2000"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "reaction_time", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Mixed cognitive/sport-performance pooled studies, largely resistance/endurance athletes, not purely cognitive-testing samples", "dose_or_intake": "1-6 g taurine, alone or with caffeine, various protocols pooled", "duration": "acute, single session", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Network meta-analysis of 12 studies reports a credible caffeine+taurine synergy for reaction time (g=0.75, 95% CrI 0.29-1.18) and anaerobic capacity (g=0.46, 95% CrI 0.19-0.71), but this pools heterogeneous sport and cognitive endpoints and directly conflicts with the two factorial trials that isolated taurine's own contribution [deng2025, giles2012, peacock2013].", "effect_estimate": "g = 0.75 (95% CrI 0.29-1.18) for CAF+TAU vs CAF or TAU alone on reaction time [deng2025]", "certainty_notes": "Downgraded for inconsistency with direct factorial evidence, indirectness (sport-performance-heavy sample, Bayesian network assumptions), and publication bias typical of energy-drink-funded literature.", "key_study_ids": ["deng2025", "giles2012", "peacock2013"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "other", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Not studied: no multi-week taurine-alone cognitive trial in healthy young adults identified", "dose_or_intake": "not studied", "duration": "not studied", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No chronic human trial of isolated taurine supplementation on any cognitive domain in healthy adults was found in this search.", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "Absence of evidence, not evidence of absence; flagged as a research gap.", "key_study_ids": ["mclellan2012"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "~1.6-2 g taurine (isolated)", "onset": "within 1 h", "duration_studied": "single session, several hours", "domain": "reaction_time", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "effect_estimate": "mixed/small, net neutral to mildly negative on choice RT", "certainty": "low", "safety": "no adverse events reported", "study_ids": ["giles2012", "peacock2013"]}, {"dose": "~1 g taurine within a full energy-drink mixture", "onset": "within 1 h", "duration_studied": "single evening session", "domain": "alertness", "population": "students", "effect_estimate": "whole-mixture benefit only, taurine not isolated", "certainty": "very low", "safety": "no adverse events reported", "study_ids": ["seidl2000"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "not studied in healthy adults for cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "outcome": "any cognitive domain", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "evidence_quality": "D", "limitations": "no identified chronic taurine-alone human cognitive trial", "study_ids": ["mclellan2012"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Taurine at doses up to 6 g/day has not shown clinically significant adverse effects in the human data reviewed by EFSA; energy-drink-level intakes (~1 g per serving) are far below this. Reported energy-drink adverse events (tachycardia, QT changes, anxiety, sleep disruption) are attributed mainly to co-ingested caffeine and sugar rather than taurine itself, but taurine's own neuroendocrine and cardiac-electrophysiological interactions in humans are not fully characterised [caine2016].", "adverse_effects": ["no major adverse effects identified at assessed intakes up to 6 g/day in adults", "gastrointestinal upset reported anecdotally at very high doses in some reviews", "cardiac/neuroendocrine interactions incompletely characterised"], "contraindications": ["bipolar disorder (theoretical GABA/glycine-receptor interaction concern, not well studied in humans)", "cardiac arrhythmia history (precaution given co-occurrence in energy-drink adverse event reports, though attribution to taurine specifically is unclear)"], "drug_interactions": ["lithium (animal/mechanistic concern via renal/neuromodulatory pathways, not well studied in humans)", "no well-established major human drug interactions identified in this search"], "upper_limits": "EFSA assessed up to 6 g/day as not raising safety concern in adults [efsa2009taurine]; no EU or US numeric upper limit is formally set as a supplement UL.", "sleep_impact": "No direct evidence that taurine alone disrupts or improves sleep in healthy adults; energy-drink sleep disruption is attributed primarily to caffeine.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Long-term (multi-month to multi-year) human taurine supplementation safety data in healthy adults are limited; most human safety data derive from energy-drink consumption patterns and short-duration trials.", "daily_use_appropriate": "probably", "special_populations": "Adolescents: distinct and separately studied developmental-brain risk profile, not generalisable from or to adults [curran2017]. Pregnancy: insufficient human taurine-supplementation safety data to recommend confidently; taurine is present in breast milk and infant formula fortification is a separate, well-studied context not addressed here. Cardiac and psychiatric (bipolar) patients: theoretical mechanistic concerns warrant specialist caution.", "specialist_review_needed": ["cardiology review for arrhythmia-prone individuals combining taurine with caffeine", "psychiatric/pharmacology review for patients on lithium or with bipolar disorder", "obstetric/neonatal nutrition review for any pregnancy-related positioning"], "hard_safety_gate": "pass", "gate_reason": "EFSA's safety opinion found no concern up to 6 g/day in adults, far above typical food-level or supplement-level use; no credible human safety signal was found for ordinary adult use."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Taurine is permitted as a constituent of energy drinks and food supplements in the EU; EFSA's 2009 opinion found intakes up to ~6 g/day raise no safety concern [efsa2009taurine]. No EU-authorised health claim exists for taurine and cognitive function (Regulation (EC) 1924/2006 claims register); any cognition-related marketing claim for taurine is not currently authorised in the EU.", "us": "Taurine is used in conventional foods and dietary supplements in the US under DSHEA as a dietary ingredient with a history of use; it is not FDA-approved as a drug. No FTC-substantiated cognitive-enhancement claim for taurine alone was identified; any such claim would require competent and reliable scientific evidence not currently available for healthy adults.", "morocco": "No Morocco-specific regulatory text on taurine was identified in this search; energy drinks containing taurine are sold in the Moroccan market, implying de facto ONSSA tolerance of food-grade taurine at energy-drink levels, but a confirmed ONSSA/Ministry of Health position on taurine specifically is uncertain and should be verified directly with ONSSA before any formulation claim.", "permitted_claims_examples": [], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "Taurine has a long history of use in energy drinks and supplements in the EU and US and is not treated as a novel food/new dietary ingredient in either market at standard use levels; no NDI notification issues were identified in this search.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Taurine is water-soluble, heat-stable and already widely used in functional beverages; it can be incorporated into a food/beverage matrix at studied safe doses (grams per serving) without major formulation barriers.", "formats": ["functional beverage", "powder/capsule supplement", "fortified bar or shot (less common, taste considerations)"], "stability": "Stable under typical beverage processing and storage conditions; well-established in commercial energy-drink production.", "taste": "Mildly bitter/metallic at higher concentrations; typically masked by sweeteners and flavouring in commercial energy drinks.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Oral taurine is well absorbed via intestinal TAUT transporters; bioavailability in a food/beverage matrix is not expected to differ materially from supplement-form studies, though this project did not locate a dedicated food-matrix bioavailability trial.", "dose_feasibility": "The studied acute cognitive-trial dose (~1.6-2 g) fits comfortably within a single beverage serving; it is already standard practice in commercial energy drinks (~1 g per 250 ml).", "cost_availability": "Taurine is a low-cost, widely available commodity amino acid used at industrial scale in the energy-drink sector.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 3, "effect_magnitude": 2, "safety": 8, "reproducibility": 4, "food_suitability": 7, "cost_access": 8, "rationale": "evidence_quality=3: grade C/D across outcomes in healthy young adults, no isolated chronic trial; effect_magnitude=2: factorial trials show null-to-mildly-negative independent cognitive effect; safety=8: EFSA-reviewed safety margin to 6 g/day with no major adverse signal at normal intakes; reproducibility=4: the two best factorial trials agree taurine does not add benefit, but the 2025 network meta-analysis conflicts, so cross-method reproducibility is mixed; food_suitability=7: easy, already-standard beverage ingredient; cost_access=8: cheap, globally available commodity amino acid."}, "research_gaps": ["No trial has tested taurine alone (without caffeine) on cognition in rested healthy young adults or students.", "No chronic (multi-week) human cognitive trial of taurine supplementation in healthy adults exists.", "The conflict between the 2025 network meta-analysis (positive caffeine-taurine synergy) and the direct factorial trials (null-to-negative) needs resolution with a larger, preregistered factorial RCT.", "Human neuroendocrine and cardiac-electrophysiological effects of taurine at supplement-relevant doses remain incompletely characterised.", "No dedicated food-matrix (non-beverage) bioavailability or acceptability study for taurine was located."], "_derived": {"composite_score": 4.8, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "pass", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": "C", "best_grade_chronic": null, "best_grade_young_adults": "C", "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": null, "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"reaction_time": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 4, "n_studies": 9, "n_verified": 9, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 2, "n_systematic_reviews": 1, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["inconsistent", "insufficient", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "tryptophan", "name": "Tryptophan and alpha-lactalbumin", "category": "E", "category_name": "Amino acids and related compounds", "aliases": ["L-tryptophan", "Trp", "alpha-lactalbumin (ALAC, a-lactalbumin, whey protein fraction)", "tryptophan-rich protein hydrolysate (egg protein hydrolysate)", "not covered: 5-hydroxytryptophan (5-HTP), a separate serotonergic substance"], "food_sources": ["milk and whey (alpha-lactalbumin is about 20-25% of whey protein in bovine milk; not verified here)", "cheese, eggs", "poultry, meat, fish", "soy, pumpkin and other seeds, oats", "carbohydrate-rich, low-protein meals raise the brain-relevant Trp:LNAA ratio more than high-protein meals"], "active_constituents": ["L-tryptophan, substrate of tryptophan hydroxylase (serotonin, then melatonin); most dietary tryptophan goes to protein synthesis and the kynurenine/niacin pathway", "alpha-lactalbumin: whey protein with high tryptophan content (enriched preparation used by Markus 2005: 4.8 g Trp/100 g protein)"], "one_line_verdict": "Tryptophan and alpha-lactalbumin can raise brain tryptophan availability and may slightly improve sleep maintenance (1 g/day: +1 point sleep efficiency) and mood in stress-vulnerable people (grade C). They do not enhance daytime cognition in healthy young adults and tend to slow reaction time. Free tryptophan carries an EMS contamination history and serotonin-syndrome interactions, so any product needs pharmaceutical-grade purity and exclusion of people on MAOIs or other serotonergic drugs.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. Tryptophan is the essential amino-acid precursor of serotonin and melatonin. Its entry into the brain depends on the plasma ratio of tryptophan to other large neutral amino acids (Trp:LNAA). Alpha-lactalbumin, a whey protein fraction rich in tryptophan, raises this ratio by 48-130% [markus2000, markus2005], and tryptophan-rich hydrolysates raise it more than intact alpha-lactalbumin or free tryptophan [markus2008]. Serotonin matters for cognition: experimental depletion most robustly impairs consolidation of verbal episodic memory [mendelsohn2009]. The demonstrated effects of adding tryptophan, however, are mostly on mood and sleep, mostly in vulnerable people, and mostly small. Mood and stress. In stress-vulnerable adults one day of an alpha-lactalbumin diet lowered cortisol and depressive feelings under a laboratory stressor [markus2000]. This did not replicate in remitted depressed patients and controls [merens2005] or in an independent trial of 40 g in healthy women [scrutton2007]. Hydrolysates (about 0.8 g Trp) improved self-rated mood in small single-lab trials [markus2008, firk2009, markus2010]. A systematic review found that only 4 of 11 RCTs in healthy adults reported mood benefits [kikuchi2021]. Cognition. Memory scanning improved only in stress-vulnerable subjects [markus2002], and visual memory improved at the cost of slower motor performance [booij2006]. A 19-day DSM-sponsored hydrolysate trial reported shorter RT and a positive emotional bias in middle-aged women [mohajeri2015]. Against this, tryptophan loading consistently impairs psychomotor speed and reaction time through sedation [silber2010] and worsened verbal working memory compared with depletion [luciana2001]. Sleep. The best trial (n=98 students, 1 g/day for 1 week) improved actigraphic sleep efficiency by 1 percentage point and wake after sleep onset by 5 min, with no change in subjective sleep [vandalfsen2019]. A 4-study meta-analysis found shorter wake after sleep onset only (SMD -1.08, 95% CI -1.89 to -0.28) [sutanto2022]. For alpha-lactalbumin, 5 of 8 small studies suggest shorter sleep-onset latency [barnard2024]. Controlled trials in athletes were null or slightly negative over 1-7 nights [macinnis2020, barnard2025sub], with one 3-night lab trial reporting faster next-morning reaction time [barnard2025eeg]. In whom. Benefits cluster in stress-vulnerable, poorly sleeping or remitted-depressed people. No trial shows daytime cognitive enhancement in rested students or knowledge workers. For them the pharmacology points to drowsiness, not focus. Safety and history. The 1989 eosinophilia-myalgia epidemic (a multisystem, sometimes fatal disorder) was traced to contaminated tryptophan from one manufacturer (OR 19.3) [belongia1990]. Risk rose with dose: 50% of heavy users of the implicated brand developed definite EMS [kamb1992]. The causal impurity is still unresolved [ko2023]. Pure L-tryptophan up to 5 g/day for 3 weeks showed no adverse effects [hiratsuka2013], and a 4.5 g/day UL has been proposed [elango2023]. Tryptophan with MAO inhibitors can cause serotonin syndrome [sporer1995, ambarapu2022]. Overall: grade C (possible benefit) for a small sleep-maintenance effect and for mood in vulnerable people; grade C harm for daytime reaction time; grade D (insufficient) for cognitive enhancement in healthy young adults.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Frank tryptophan deficiency is rare in people eating enough protein. Pellagra arises from combined niacin and tryptophan shortage, a deficiency setting outside this monograph. The positive trials are not deficiency correction. They test whether shifting the plasma Trp:LNAA ratio helps people with presumed serotonergic vulnerability (high neuroticism, remitted depression, poor sleep, S-allele carriers) [markus2000, markus2002, markus2005, vandalfsen2019]. In non-vulnerable healthy adults the effects are null or show psychomotor slowing [scrutton2007, silber2010, luciana2001]. This is a 'state and trait vulnerability' pattern, not enhancement. Results from remitted depressed patients [booij2006, merens2005] must not be generalised to students. Depletion studies show that too little serotonin impairs memory consolidation [mendelsohn2009], but they do not show that more than normal improves it.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Marketing claims such as 'serotonin boost', 'mood food', 'calm focus', 'sleep better, think better' and 'natural antidepressant' overreach. The evidence supports a narrower statement. An evening dose of about 1 g tryptophan (or 20-40 g alpha-lactalbumin) may slightly improve sleep continuity, and mood benefits are inconsistent and mostly in stress-vulnerable people. Daytime use for focus contradicts the evidence that tryptophan loading slows reaction time [silber2010]. Most positive mood and cognition findings come from one Maastricht laboratory, often in subgroups, and several hydrolysate studies are industry-linked [mohajeri2015, markus2008]. In the EU, EFSA assessed L-tryptophan claims for normal sleep, mood, cognitive function and body weight (EFSA Journal 2011;9(4):2073; full text not accessible here). We found no authorised tryptophan or alpha-lactalbumin brain claim. Alpha-lactalbumin can carry only the generic authorised protein claims. Any cognition or mood claim would currently be illegal in the EU and hard to substantiate under FTC standards in the US.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Dietary tryptophan competes with other large neutral amino acids for the LNAA transporter at the blood-brain barrier. Tryptophan hydroxylase is not saturated, so raising brain tryptophan increases serotonin synthesis, and in the pineal gland melatonin synthesis. Alpha-lactalbumin and hydrolysates raise the plasma Trp:LNAA ratio; carbohydrate meals raise it through insulin-driven uptake of competing LNAAs into muscle. Human evidence for the central steps is indirect (prolactin and cortisol responses, ERP, fMRI). Expected consequences are sedation and sleep promotion, mood and emotional-bias effects in vulnerable people, and inverted-U effects on prefrontal working memory.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Oral tryptophan / alpha-lactalbumin / hydrolysate -> absorption -> plasma Trp:LNAA ratio rises", "detail": "+48% [markus2000], +130% before bed [markus2005]; hydrolysate > pure Trp > intact alpha-lactalbumin [markus2008]", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Plasma Trp:LNAA -> LNAA transporter -> brain tryptophan", "detail": "Competition with tyrosine, phenylalanine and branched-chain amino acids; brain uptake inferred, not directly measured in these trials", "evidence_level": "plausible"}, {"step": "Brain tryptophan -> tryptophan hydroxylase -> serotonin (and melatonin)", "detail": "Unsaturated enzyme; prolactin rise in vulnerable subjects [markus2000] is an indirect marker; depletion lowers serotonin function", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Serotonin -> sleep regulation, emotional bias, stress reactivity, memory consolidation", "detail": "Depletion impairs verbal memory consolidation [mendelsohn2009]; loading changes emotional bias and cortisol", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Measured outcomes: small sleep-maintenance gain, mood effects in vulnerable groups, slowed psychomotor speed", "detail": "[vandalfsen2019, sutanto2022, markus2000, silber2010]", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Kynurenine pathway diversion (most Trp is metabolised to kynurenine/niacin)", "detail": "Urinary kynurenine metabolites rise in proportion to dose [hiratsuka2013]; neuroactive kynurenines (kynurenic acid, quinolinic acid) are a theoretical concern at high doses", "evidence_level": "speculative"}], "pathways": ["serotonin synthesis", "melatonin synthesis", "LNAA blood-brain barrier competition", "kynurenine/niacin pathway", "HPA-axis (cortisol) modulation"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "other", "population_detail": "Healthy adults selected for high stress vulnerability (neuroticism-type trait) or remitted depression, under a laboratory stressor", "dose_or_intake": "Alpha-lactalbumin-enriched diet for one day (amount not in abstracts) or 40 g single dose", "duration": "Single day", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Alpha-lactalbumin raises plasma Trp:LNAA by about 48-130%. One trial found lower cortisol and fewer depressive feelings under stress in vulnerable subjects only. Two later trials (recovered depressed patients and controls; healthy women, 40 g) found no mood, cortisol or emotional-processing effect.", "effect_estimate": "Depressive feelings p=0.007 and cortisol p=0.036 in vulnerable subgroup [markus2000]; minimal effects [merens2005]; no effects [scrutton2007]", "certainty_notes": "Inconsistent results, subgroup analyses, mostly one laboratory, small samples; the one independent (Oxford) test was null. Grade C, inconsistent.", "key_study_ids": ["markus2000", "merens2005", "scrutton2007"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy volunteers given tryptophan-rich protein hydrolysates (egg or other) or pure tryptophan, about 0.8 g Trp", "dose_or_intake": "About 0.8 g tryptophan as hydrolysate or free amino acid", "duration": "Single dose", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Hydrolysates raised Trp:LNAA more than intact alpha-lactalbumin and improved self-rated mood and blunted cortisol responses to stress in three small crossover trials from the same Maastricht group.", "effect_estimate": "Mood improvement at 60 min (sizes not in abstracts) [markus2008, firk2009, markus2010]", "certainty_notes": "Single research group, small n (18-35), self-reported mood, possible industry ties for the hydrolysate. Downgraded for risk of bias and lack of independent replication.", "key_study_ids": ["markus2008", "firk2009", "markus2010"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults including middle-aged women (45-65 y), 2-4 weeks of supplementation or a Trp-enriched diet", "dose_or_intake": "0.14-3 g/day tryptophan; 1 g/day Trp-rich egg hydrolysate", "duration": "19 days to 4 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "A systematic review found that 4 of 11 RCTs reported less negative and more positive mood in healthy adults. A 19-day DSM-sponsored trial reported a positive emotional-processing bias, more 'energy' and shorter RT. A 4-week Trp-enriched diet had no main effect on emotion recognition.", "effect_estimate": "4/11 RCTs positive [kikuchi2021]; multiple outcomes significant in an industry trial [mohajeri2015]; null main effect [zamoscik2021]", "certainty_notes": "Most RCTs null; no meta-analysis; industry funding for the main chronic trial; multiplicity. Grade C at most.", "key_study_ids": ["kikuchi2021", "mohajeri2015", "zamoscik2021"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "other", "population_detail": "Stress-vulnerable healthy adults and remitted depressed patients with controls (alpha-lactalbumin diet)", "dose_or_intake": "Alpha-lactalbumin-enriched meals (amount not in abstracts)", "duration": "Single day", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Memory scanning improved only in high stress-vulnerable subjects. In another trial abstract visual memory improved in both remitted patients and controls, but simple motor performance was impaired.", "effect_estimate": "Memory scanning p=0.019 in vulnerable subgroup [markus2002]; visual memory improved, motor impaired [booij2006]", "certainty_notes": "Subgroup and multiple-test results from one research network; a trade-off with psychomotor speed. Should not be generalised to healthy young adults.", "key_study_ids": ["markus2002", "booij2006"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "reaction_time", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults given tryptophan loads (free amino acid or loading drinks)", "dose_or_intake": "Gram doses of L-tryptophan (loading)", "duration": "Single dose", "grade": "C", "direction": "harm", "effect_summary": "Tryptophan loading consistently impairs psychomotor speed and reaction time (mild sedation), and loading worsened verbal and affective working memory compared with depletion.", "effect_estimate": "Qualitative (review) [silber2010]; working-memory decrement vs depletion [luciana2001]; motor slowing [booij2006]", "certainty_notes": "Direction consistent across studies, but no pooled estimate; dose-dependent sedation is pharmacologically plausible. Important for daytime 'focus' positioning.", "key_study_ids": ["silber2010", "luciana2001", "booij2006"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "alertness", "population": "other", "population_detail": "Adults with mild sleep complaints or athletic poor sleepers; evening alpha-lactalbumin, testing next morning", "dose_or_intake": "Evening alpha-lactalbumin (40 g in recent trials)", "duration": "1-3 evenings", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Evening alpha-lactalbumin modestly reduced morning sleepiness and improved ERP attention measures; behavioural improvement was seen only in poor sleepers. A 2025 lab trial in poor-sleeping athletes reported faster next-morning RT, but the same group's 7-night habitual-setting trial found no sleep benefit and more awakenings.", "effect_estimate": "Sleepiness p=0.013, behaviour p=0.05 (poor sleepers) [markus2005]; RT improved p<.05 [barnard2025eeg]; awakenings 10.25 vs 11.01 (worse) [barnard2025sub]", "certainty_notes": "Small trials (n=19-28), ERP biomarker outcomes, inconsistent in habitual conditions. Next-day cognition is an indirect effect via sleep.", "key_study_ids": ["markus2005", "barnard2025eeg", "barnard2025sub"]}, {"timeframe": "short_term_days", "domain": "sleep_related_cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "University-age adults (mean about 22 y), mostly good sleepers, genotyped for 5-HTTLPR; outcome is sleep, not cognition", "dose_or_intake": "L-tryptophan 1 g/day before sleep", "duration": "1 week", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "1 g/day improved actigraphic sleep efficiency by about 1 percentage point and reduced wake after sleep onset by about 5 min, with no effect on subjective sleep. A meta-analysis of 4 studies found shorter WASO (larger with >=1 g/day) and no effect on other sleep components. No trial measured next-day cognition.", "effect_estimate": "Sleep efficiency 85.68% vs 84.70%; WASO 61.6 vs 66.8 min (p<0.001) [vandalfsen2019]; WASO SMD -1.08 (95% CI -1.89 to -0.28) [sutanto2022]", "certainty_notes": "One good RCT plus a small meta-analysis with wide CIs and old trials. The effect is small in practice and cognition was not assessed (indirectness).", "key_study_ids": ["vandalfsen2019", "sutanto2022"]}, {"timeframe": "short_term_days", "domain": "sleep_related_cognition", "population": "other", "population_detail": "Athletes and active adults, good or poor sleepers, 40 g alpha-lactalbumin 2 h before bed", "dose_or_intake": "20-60 g alpha-lactalbumin (40 g in most)", "duration": "1-7 nights", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Five of 8 studies in a systematic review report better sleep (mainly shorter sleep-onset latency). Controlled trials in cyclists and poor-sleeping athletes were null or slightly negative for actigraphic sleep.", "effect_estimate": "5/8 positive [barnard2024]; sleep efficiency 88.3% vs 87.8% (NS) [macinnis2020]; more awakenings [barnard2025sub]", "certainty_notes": "Small samples, mostly one research programme, actigraphy, inconsistent; no cognition outcomes except one lab trial.", "key_study_ids": ["barnard2024", "macinnis2020", "barnard2025sub", "barnard2025eeg"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "students", "population_detail": "Rested students or knowledge workers seeking daytime focus or memory enhancement", "dose_or_intake": "none tested for enhancement", "duration": "not studied", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No trial shows that tryptophan or alpha-lactalbumin enhances daytime cognition in rested, non-vulnerable young adults. Loading tends to sedate and slow reaction time. Depletion studies show serotonin is needed for memory consolidation, but repletion above normal has not been shown to enhance it.", "effect_estimate": "not available", "certainty_notes": "Indirect evidence only (depletion paradigms, vulnerable subgroups).", "key_study_ids": ["mendelsohn2009", "silber2010"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "Alpha-lactalbumin-enriched diet (one day)", "onset": "Trp:LNAA rise within hours", "duration_studied": "Single stress session", "domain": "mood, cortisol under stress", "population": "58 adults (29 stress-vulnerable)", "effect_estimate": "Trp:LNAA +48%; lower depressive feelings (p=0.007) and cortisol (p=0.036) in vulnerable subgroup only", "certainty": "low", "safety": "Dairy protein; milk-allergy risk", "study_ids": ["markus2000"]}, {"dose": "40 g alpha-lactalbumin", "onset": "Hours", "duration_studied": "Single session", "domain": "emotional processing, cortisol", "population": "28 healthy women", "effect_estimate": "No effect despite Trp rise", "certainty": "low", "safety": "No issues reported", "study_ids": ["scrutton2007"]}, {"dose": "0.8 g tryptophan as hydrolysate or free Trp", "onset": "60 min", "duration_studied": "Hours", "domain": "mood", "population": "18 healthy volunteers", "effect_estimate": "Mood improved at 60 min after hydrolysate and pure Trp; not after intact alpha-lactalbumin", "certainty": "low", "safety": "Not reported", "study_ids": ["markus2008"]}, {"dose": "Evening alpha-lactalbumin (Trp 4.8 g/100 g protein)", "onset": "Next morning", "duration_studied": "One night", "domain": "sleepiness, sustained attention (ERP and behaviour)", "population": "28 adults (14 with mild sleep complaints)", "effect_estimate": "Sleepiness reduced (p=0.013); ERP attention improved (p=0.002); behaviour improved only in poor sleepers (p=0.05)", "certainty": "low", "safety": "None reported", "study_ids": ["markus2005"]}, {"dose": "40 g alpha-lactalbumin 2 h pre-sleep x3 nights", "onset": "Next morning", "duration_studied": "3 nights", "domain": "reaction time, sleep architecture", "population": "19 trained poor sleepers", "effect_estimate": "N2 +10.6 min, REM -11.1 min; faster morning RT on 3 tests (p<.05)", "certainty": "low", "safety": "REM reduction of unknown significance", "study_ids": ["barnard2025eeg"]}, {"dose": "Tryptophan loading (gram doses)", "onset": "1-2 h", "duration_studied": "Single session", "domain": "psychomotor speed, reaction time, working memory", "population": "Healthy adults", "effect_estimate": "Slower psychomotor/RT performance (sedation); working memory worse vs depletion", "certainty": "low-moderate (consistent direction)", "safety": "Daytime drowsiness; driving caution", "study_ids": ["silber2010", "luciana2001"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "1 week (1 g/day)", "population": "98 healthy young adults", "outcome": "Actigraphic and diary sleep", "effect_estimate": "Sleep efficiency +1.0 point, WASO -5.2 min (p<0.001); subjective sleep unchanged", "evidence_quality": "moderate (single good RCT)", "limitations": "No cognitive outcome; 1 week only", "study_ids": ["vandalfsen2019"]}, {"duration": "7 nights (40 g alpha-lactalbumin)", "population": "24 athletic poor sleepers", "outcome": "Actigraphy sleep, performance, reaction time", "effect_estimate": "No sleep benefit; awakenings increased (p=0.031); jump height decreased", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Habitual setting, actigraphy only", "study_ids": ["barnard2025sub"]}, {"duration": "19 days (0.5 g x2/day egg hydrolysate)", "population": "59 healthy women 45-65 y", "outcome": "Emotional processing, mood, RT, sleep", "effect_estimate": "Positive emotional bias, higher 'energy', shorter RT, slight sustained-attention benefit", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Industry-sponsored; multiple outcomes", "study_ids": ["mohajeri2015"]}, {"duration": "4 weeks (Trp-enriched diet)", "population": "77 healthy adults", "outcome": "Social cognition tasks, fMRI", "effect_estimate": "No main behavioural effect; neural activation and age-interaction changes", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Exploratory interactions; biomarker outcomes", "study_ids": ["zamoscik2021"]}, {"duration": "21 days per dose (1-5 g/day)", "population": "17 healthy young women", "outcome": "Safety biomarkers, mood", "effect_estimate": "No adverse effects up to 5 g/day; kynurenine metabolites dose-proportional", "evidence_quality": "moderate (for short-term safety)", "limitations": "Small, women only, 3 weeks", "study_ids": ["hiratsuka2013", "elango2023"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Pure, pharmaceutical-grade L-tryptophan appears well tolerated up to 5 g/day for 3 weeks in young women [hiratsuka2013]; a UL of 4.5 g/day has been proposed by an industry-supported amino acid council [elango2023]. The defining safety event is the 1989-1990 eosinophilia-myalgia syndrome (EMS) epidemic, a multisystem and sometimes fatal disorder. It was traced to tryptophan from one manufacturer, with a fermentation-strain and purification change and an impurity peak ('peak E') [belongia1990]. Incidence was strongly dose-related [kamb1992], and the causal contaminant is still not definitively identified [ko2023]. Tryptophan is serotonergic: combined with MAO inhibitors (and more cautiously with SSRIs, SNRIs, tricyclics, triptans, tramadol, linezolid) it can cause serotonin syndrome [sporer1995, ambarapu2022]. It causes drowsiness and slower reaction time [silber2010]. Alpha-lactalbumin is a normal milk protein whose main risks are cow's-milk allergy and the usual whey-protein tolerability issues. Its tryptophan dose (about 1-2 g per 20-40 g serving) is far below EMS-era doses.", "adverse_effects": ["Drowsiness and slower reaction time/psychomotor speed", "Nausea and gastrointestinal discomfort (drug references; not quantified in reviewed trials)", "EMS from contaminated product (historical; causal impurity unresolved)", "Serotonin syndrome when combined with MAOIs or other serotonergic drugs", "Increased nocturnal awakenings with 7-night alpha-lactalbumin in poor-sleeping athletes (one trial)"], "contraindications": ["MAO inhibitors (contraindicated)", "SSRIs, SNRIs, tricyclics, triptans, tramadol, linezolid, lithium, St John's wort or 5-HTP (avoid or only under medical supervision)", "History of eosinophilia-myalgia syndrome or eosinophilic disorders", "Pregnancy and breastfeeding (insufficient safety data for supplemental doses)", "Children and adolescents (no data)", "Cow's-milk protein allergy (for alpha-lactalbumin)", "Activities requiring alertness after dosing (driving)"], "drug_interactions": ["MAOIs: serotonin syndrome risk (contraindicated)", "SSRIs/SNRIs/tricyclics/triptans/tramadol/linezolid: additive serotonergic effects", "Sedatives, benzodiazepines, alcohol: additive sedation (theoretical)", "Levodopa: LNAA competition (theoretical)"], "upper_limits": "No official EFSA or US UL. Proposed UL 4.5 g/day L-tryptophan [elango2023], based mainly on a 21-day trial showing no adverse effects up to 5 g/day [hiratsuka2013]. In the EMS cohort, more than 4 g/day of the contaminated brand gave 50% definite EMS [kamb1992]; that was a contaminant effect, but it underlines the need for purity at high doses. Alpha-lactalbumin: no specific limit (a food protein).", "sleep_impact": "Sleep-promoting and sedating. A small improvement in sleep maintenance at about 1 g/day [vandalfsen2019, sutanto2022]. REM was reduced with 40 g alpha-lactalbumin in one trial [barnard2025eeg]. Daytime dosing may cause drowsiness.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "3-4 weeks maximum in controlled trials (5 g/day for 21 days; 4-week Trp diet; 19-day hydrolysate); no long-term controlled safety data", "daily_use_appropriate": "uncertain", "special_populations": "People with depression or on antidepressants need psychiatrist/pharmacist review (both because of interactions and because mood claims could delay treatment). Pregnancy and lactation: no supplemental-dose safety data. Children: not studied. Older adults on multiple serotonergic drugs: higher interaction risk. Milk allergy: alpha-lactalbumin excluded.", "specialist_review_needed": ["clinical pharmacist (serotonergic drug interactions)", "psychiatrist (mood-disorder patients)", "toxicologist / quality assurance (impurity profile of fermented L-tryptophan, EMS history)", "allergist (milk-protein labelling for alpha-lactalbumin)", "sleep medicine specialist (if a sleep positioning is considered)"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "Free L-tryptophan passes only with pharmaceutical-grade supply (impurity specification and certificate of analysis), doses of 1 g or less (well below the 4.5 g proposed UL), clear warnings excluding MAOI and other serotonergic-drug users, and no daytime 'focus' positioning. Alpha-lactalbumin as a food protein passes subject to allergen labelling."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "No authorised health claim for L-tryptophan or alpha-lactalbumin relating to sleep, mood or cognition, to our knowledge (the EU Register was not queried directly). EFSA assessed L-tryptophan general-function claims for maintenance of normal sleep (ID 596, 1671), enhancement of mood (ID 596), contribution to normal cognitive function (ID 596) and body weight (ID 604) (EFSA Journal 2011;9(4):2073). The full text could not be accessed (HTTP 403), so the outcome was not read, though no such claim appears among authorised claims as far as we know. Amino acids in food supplements are not harmonised under Directive 2002/46/EC. Some member states have been reported to treat higher-dose L-tryptophan as medicinal, but this was not verified. Alpha-lactalbumin is an ordinary milk-protein fraction (no novel-food issue expected). It can carry the generic authorised protein claims if the food is at least a source of protein.", "us": "L-tryptophan is a dietary ingredient under DSHEA. After the 1989 EMS outbreak, products were recalled and sales were restricted. Secondary sources say FDA loosened the marketing restrictions in 2001 (not confirmed from FDA documents; import-alert status not verified). As a conventional-food additive, 21 CFR 172.320 limits added L-tryptophan to 1.6% by weight of total protein, and only for protein-quality improvement (law.cornell.edu, accessed 2026-10-25). Free tryptophan cannot simply be added at 1 g to a conventional snack. Alpha-lactalbumin-enriched whey is marketed as a food ingredient (GRAS status believed, not verified). Structure/function claims require the DSHEA disclaimer and FTC-level substantiation; sleep or mood claims for the general population would be weakly supported.", "morocco": "No tryptophan-specific Moroccan rule found. Food supplements are registered through the Ministry of Health committee (DMP, now AMMPS). Law 28-07 bans disease claims (an 'antidepressant' or 'treats insomnia' claim would be prohibited), and no list of authorised health claims exists (see unit X6). Status uncertain; Moroccan counsel required.", "permitted_claims_examples": ["For alpha-lactalbumin/whey protein foods that are at least a source of protein (generic EU authorised protein claims, Reg. (EU) 432/2012; not brain-related): 'Protein contributes to the maintenance of muscle mass'; 'Protein contributes to a growth in muscle mass'; 'Protein contributes to the maintenance of normal bones'"], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "L-tryptophan and alpha-lactalbumin have long histories of use; novel-food status not expected but not confirmed in the EU catalogue. Tryptophan-rich protein hydrolysates (e.g., egg protein hydrolysate) may need a novel-food or GRAS check depending on the process (not verified). US NDI: L-tryptophan was marketed pre-1994 (believed), so probably no NDI is required; not verified.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Alpha-lactalbumin is a genuine food ingredient that fits a dairy drink, yogurt or evening shake. The tested 20-40 g dose is a full protein serving. Free L-tryptophan at 1 g is pill-sized but is restricted as a conventional-food additive in the US and has a contamination history. A carbohydrate-rich, low-protein evening snack is the most natural 'food' way to raise the Trp:LNAA ratio, though its behavioural effects are untested here.", "formats": ["alpha-lactalbumin-enriched whey powder or ready-to-drink evening dairy beverage (20-40 g)", "L-tryptophan capsules 500-1000 mg (supplement route only)", "tryptophan-rich egg or milk protein hydrolysate (about 0.5-1 g/day, as tested)", "milk-based 'night' products"], "stability": "Tryptophan is light- and oxidation-sensitive and degrades in heat/Maillard reactions. A 2025 review covers stability and palatability in food formulation (PMID 39473205, not read). Alpha-lactalbumin denatures with heat, which does not change its amino-acid content.", "taste": "Free tryptophan is bitter; hydrolysates can be bitter. Alpha-lactalbumin has a neutral whey taste.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Co-ingested protein lowers the Trp:LNAA ratio; carbohydrate raises it. Hydrolysates raise the ratio faster and more than intact alpha-lactalbumin or free tryptophan [markus2008].", "dose_feasibility": "1 g tryptophan fits a capsule (supplement). 40 g alpha-lactalbumin is a large protein serving (about 160 kcal of protein) taken 2 h before bed; this is feasible as a shake but costly and calorie-relevant. Daily intakes stay far below the 4.5 g proposed UL.", "cost_availability": "Alpha-lactalbumin-enriched whey ingredients are produced by major dairy ingredient suppliers; L-tryptophan by fermentation (Asian manufacturers dominant; not researched). Prices not researched.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 3, "effect_magnitude": 2, "safety": 5, "reproducibility": 3, "food_suitability": 6, "cost_access": 5, "rationale": "Evidence quality 3: grade C for sleep maintenance and for mood in vulnerable groups; D for cognition in healthy young adults. Effect magnitude 2: sleep efficiency +1 point and WASO -5 min, inconsistent mood effects, daytime RT impairment. Safety 5: pure product tolerated to 5 g/day short term, but the EMS history, serotonin-syndrome interactions and sedation require strong controls (alpha-lactalbumin alone would score higher). Reproducibility 3: dominated by one laboratory, with independent nulls (Oxford, Canada, Australia habitual-setting). Food suitability 6: alpha-lactalbumin is a natural dairy ingredient that suits evening products. Cost/access 5: available ingredients, prices not researched (provisional)."}, "research_gaps": ["Preregistered trials of evening alpha-lactalbumin or 1 g tryptophan in students during exam periods with next-day cognitive outcomes (memory consolidation) measured objectively", "Independent replication of the Maastricht stress-vulnerability findings with preregistered outcomes", "Comparison of an ordinary carbohydrate-rich evening snack vs alpha-lactalbumin vs tryptophan on sleep and next-day cognition", "Long-term (>3 months) safety of supplemental tryptophan, including kynurenine-pathway metabolites", "Impurity specifications that reliably exclude EMS-associated contaminants (analytical standardisation)", "Registered trial NCT04041934 (dietary protein quality and sleep in athletes/obese adolescents, status unknown, no results) - check for publication"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 3.7, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": "C", "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": "C", "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": "C", "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"working_memory": "C", "alertness": "C", "sleep_related_cognition": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 9, "n_studies": 28, "n_verified": 28, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 9, "n_systematic_reviews": 4, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["harm", "inconsistent", "insufficient", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "caffeine", "name": "Caffeine", "category": "F", "category_name": "Caffeine and natural stimulants", "aliases": ["caffeine", "1,3,7-trimethylxanthine", "caffeine anhydrous", "natural caffeine (from coffee, tea, guarana, yerba mate, kola)", "guaranine / mateine / theine (historical synonyms for the same molecule)"], "food_sources": ["coffee (Gardiner 2023 used 107 mg per 250 mL as a reference value; real content varies widely by bean, brew and serving)", "tea (Camellia sinensis): black, green, oolong; EFSA's consumer communication equates 200 mg to roughly 4 cups of tea", "cola-type soft drinks (US GRAS tolerance 0.02 percent, about 71 mg per 355 mL computed)", "energy drinks and 'pre-workout' products (a standard pre-workout serve was taken as 217.5 mg in Gardiner 2023)", "cocoa and dark chocolate (small amounts, alongside theobromine)", "guarana, yerba mate, kola nut, guayusa (botanical sources)"], "active_constituents": ["caffeine (adenosine A1 and A2A receptor antagonist)", "metabolites paraxanthine, theobromine and theophylline (formed mainly by hepatic CYP1A2); paraxanthine is itself psychoactive", "matrix co-constituents that modify effects in whole foods: L-theanine (tea), chlorogenic acids (coffee), theobromine and flavanols (cocoa), sugars and carbonation (soft drinks)"], "one_line_verdict": "Caffeine at 40-200 mg reliably produces a small acute improvement in reaction time and sustained attention in rested adults (g about 0.3) and a large one in sleep-deprived people, but it does not make anyone smarter, its benefit for daily users is partly the reversal of overnight withdrawal, it does not reliably improve memory, and it costs sleep when taken within roughly 9 hours of bedtime.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. In rested healthy adults, single doses from about 40 to 300 mg improve simple and choice reaction time, vigilance and sustained attention, and reduce subjective sleepiness [mclellan2016, einother2013]. The most recent meta-analysis (31 double-blind placebo-controlled trials, 1455 rested adults) found small effects on attention accuracy (g = 0.27) and reaction time (g = 0.28). Doses of 200 mg or more gave larger effects. Reaction time kept improving with dose, but accuracy followed a curve that turned down at high doses [klove2025]. Effects appear even at 12.5-50 mg, and the dose-response is flat [smit2000]. They also appear in habitual non-consumers [haskell2005, childs2006], so caffeine has a real psychostimulant action. The withdrawal-reversal problem. Most trials test habitual users after overnight abstinence. Abstinence itself lowers alertness and slows performance, and caffeine restores both [rogers2013, yeomans2002, heatherley2005]. Withdrawal is a validated syndrome (headache in about 50 percent; onset 12-24 h) [juliano2004]. Some reviewers conclude that the net long-term benefit is close to zero [james2005, rogers2010]. Others conclude that effects in non-consumers show the benefit cannot be entirely withdrawal reversal [einother2013, haskell2005]. Both positions are partly right: caffeine gives a real but small acute boost, and tolerance erodes much of it in daily heavy users. Memory and intelligence. The effects on memory are inconsistent, and caffeine is not a cognitive enhancer in the sense of improving reasoning [nehlig2010]. The well-known post-study consolidation finding (200 mg; LDI p = 0.049 in a combined sample of 73) [borota2014] was not replicated in a non-abstinent sample, which ruled out medium-to-large effects [aust2020]. A 2025 trial found impaired face-recognition consolidation [leon2025]. Studies have found single-dose impairments in memory or spatial working memory [childs2006, kassim2024], and 10 days at 450 mg/day worsened 3-back accuracy (d = 0.40) [lin2023]. In an assessment-centre simulation with young adults, caffeine had no effect on performance, although participants felt more energetic [ullrich2015]. Sleep loss. This is the strongest use case. A meta-analysis of 45 publications reported large effects on attention, reaction time and driving under sleep loss [irwin2020], and military trials consistently show maintained vigilance [wesensten2005, kamimori2015]. Harms. Caffeine shortens total sleep by about 45 min on average [gardiner2023]. A 400 mg dose disrupted sleep even when taken 6 h before bed [drake2013]. Caffeine raises blood pressure [abbashashemi2023] and premature ventricular contractions [marcus2023]. At 400-750 mg it provokes panic attacks in about half of people with panic disorder [klevebrant2022]. For students, caffeine is useful for staying alert. It is not useful for learning, and it backfires when it steals sleep before an exam.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Caffeine is not a nutrient, and there is no deficiency state. There is, however, a pharmacological analogue of deficiency: caffeine withdrawal in habitual consumers. Overnight abstinence lowers alertness and slows reaction time, and the first dose of the day restores them [rogers2013, yeomans2002, juliano2004]. A large part of the 'boost' that regular coffee drinkers feel is therefore correction of a self-induced deficit. A second dose 2-4 h later adds little or causes jitter [heatherley2005, yeomans2002]. True enhancement above a non-withdrawn baseline is real but small. It is seen in non-consumers and in light users for reaction time and vigilance [haskell2005, childs2006, klove2025]. However, it is offset by anxiety in low consumers at 250 mg [rogers2013], and caffeine did not raise rated alertness in non-consumers in the largest genotyped trial [rogers2010]. The largest and most practically important effect is in a state deficit that has nothing to do with diet, namely sleep deprivation [irwin2020, kamimori2015]. Evidence from adolescents [cooper2021] and from people with panic disorder [klevebrant2022] must not be transferred to healthy adults, and the reverse also applies.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Claims such as 'boosts brain power', 'improves memory', 'enhances focus and intelligence' or 'nootropic' go beyond the evidence. The evidence supports 'can temporarily increase alertness and speed of reaction, especially when tired', with an effect size that is small in rested people (g about 0.3) and large only under sleep loss. Memory and reasoning claims are not supported [borota2014 vs aust2020, leon2025; nehlig2010]. Feeling more energetic is not the same as performing better [ullrich2015]. Expectancy alone can improve attention and mood [dawkins2011], which is why decaffeinated controls matter. In the EU, EFSA (2011) concluded that a cause-and-effect relationship existed for caffeine and increased alertness and attention. However, the Commission's draft authorising 'Caffeine helps to increase alertness' and 'Caffeine helps to improve concentration' (75 mg per serving) was vetoed by the European Parliament on 7 July 2016. No caffeine claim is on the EU register, so EU labels must carry no cognitive claim for caffeine. In the US a structure/function claim such as 'helps maintain alertness' is possible for a supplement with the DSHEA disclaimer, but it must be FTC-substantiated and must not imply memory or IQ effects. The honest Brain Food positioning is 'a measured, modest dose of caffeine, timed early in the day', not 'a smart drug'.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Caffeine is rapidly and almost completely absorbed, crosses the blood-brain barrier freely and acts mainly as a competitive antagonist at adenosine A1 and A2A receptors. Adenosine accumulates during wakefulness and signals sleep pressure; blocking it raises arousal, increases dopaminergic and cholinergic tone indirectly (A2A-D2 interaction in striatum), and constricts cerebral vessels (reduced cerebral blood flow, a biomarker, not a cognitive outcome). Hepatic CYP1A2 clears it; ADORA2A variants modulate anxiety and sleep sensitivity. The mechanism explains why effects are largest when adenosine-mediated sleep pressure is high (sleep loss, early morning, post-lunch dip) and why tolerance (receptor upregulation) and withdrawal arise with daily use. It does not predict improved memory storage or reasoning, consistent with the human data.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Oral caffeine -> near-complete gastrointestinal absorption; peak plasma typically 30-120 min; elimination half-life several hours with wide inter-individual variation", "detail": "Pharmacokinetics are textbook human pharmacology (values not re-extracted here); clearance via CYP1A2 is slowed in pregnancy, oral-contraceptive use, liver disease and by fluvoxamine, and accelerated by smoking. Food matrix and carbonation can shift timing of effects.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Plasma caffeine -> crosses blood-brain barrier -> antagonism of adenosine A1 and A2A receptors", "detail": "Receptor antagonism at dietary concentrations is established pharmacology; genetic variation in ADORA2A (rs5751876) modifies caffeine-induced anxiety in humans [rogers2010, kapellou2023].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Reduced adenosinergic sleep-pressure signalling -> increased arousal, reduced sleepiness, faster motor and perceptual responses", "detail": "Consistent human behavioural data on alertness, reaction time and vigilance [klove2025, mclellan2016]; strongest when homeostatic sleep pressure is high [irwin2020].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Daily exposure -> tolerance (adaptive receptor changes) and withdrawal on abstinence", "detail": "Withdrawal syndrome validated (headache, fatigue, decreased alertness, difficulty concentrating) [juliano2004]; tolerance to alerting and anxiogenic effects in frequent consumers [rogers2010, rogers2013]; receptor up-regulation documented mainly in animals.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Arousal-dependent modulation of memory encoding and consolidation", "detail": "Animal data support adenosine-dependent effects on hippocampal plasticity; human consolidation effect reported once [borota2014], not replicated or reversed [aust2020, leon2025].", "evidence_level": "speculative"}, {"step": "Measured human cognitive outcome", "detail": "Small acute gains in reaction time and attention in rested adults (g about 0.3) [klove2025]; large gains under sleep loss [irwin2020]; no consistent effect on memory or higher-order reasoning [nehlig2010, ullrich2015]; possible impairment of complex working memory with chronic high intake [lin2023].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}], "pathways": ["adenosine A1 / A2A receptor antagonism (primary)", "indirect enhancement of dopaminergic transmission via striatal A2A-D2 receptor interaction", "cerebral vasoconstriction (reduced cerebral blood flow; biomarker only)", "sympathetic activation (blood pressure, heart rate, ventricular ectopy)", "phosphodiesterase inhibition and calcium mobilisation only at toxic concentrations"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "reaction_time", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Rested healthy adults (mostly 18-40 y), habitual consumers after overnight abstinence and non/low consumers", "dose_or_intake": "about 40-300 mg single dose (0.5-4 mg/kg)", "duration": "single dose, tested 30-120 min after intake", "grade": "A", "direction": "benefit", "effect_summary": "Faster simple and choice reaction time is the most consistent caffeine effect: 31-trial meta-analysis g = 0.28, linear dose-response with larger gains at 200 mg or more; effect present in non-consumers and light users, not only withdrawn consumers.", "effect_estimate": "g = 0.28 for reaction time (CI not reported in abstract) [klove2025]", "certainty_notes": "Many independent double-blind RCTs, consistent direction. Grade A applies to the real-world scenario (first dose after overnight abstinence, or light users). Residual concern: most participants were overnight-abstinent users (withdrawal reversal inflates the estimate). Effects in non-consumers [haskell2005, childs2006] limit this, but one non-consumer study was null with worse performance in some participants [rogers2003]. Effect is small: tens of milliseconds, meaningful for safety-critical tasks, marginal for studying.", "key_study_ids": ["rogers2003", "klove2025", "mclellan2016", "haskell2005", "childs2006", "smit2000"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Rested healthy adults; attention tasks (vigilance, RVIP, flanker, ANT)", "dose_or_intake": "about 12.5-300 mg single dose", "duration": "single dose", "grade": "A", "direction": "benefit", "effect_summary": "Improved attention accuracy and vigilance: pooled g = 0.27 for accuracy; accuracy improves up to a point and then declines (quadratic dose-response); benefit not modified by habitual intake or task complexity in the meta-analysis; doses as low as 12.5-50 mg were active after overnight abstinence.", "effect_estimate": "g = 0.27 accuracy [klove2025]; flat dose-response 12.5-100 mg [smit2000]", "certainty_notes": "Consistent across reviews [einother2013 (Unilever-authored), mclellan2016 (military authors), klove2025 (independent)]. Not downgraded; small effect size. Practical relevance for a student: modest help on long, boring tasks; no gain in understanding.", "key_study_ids": ["klove2025", "einother2013", "smit2000", "childs2006", "repantis2021"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "alertness", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Subjective alertness/sleepiness ratings in rested adults; separate consideration of habitual vs non/low consumers", "dose_or_intake": "75-250 mg", "duration": "single dose or two doses within 90 min", "grade": "B", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Self-rated alertness rises and sleepiness falls reliably in overnight-abstinent consumers; in non/low consumers sleepiness falls but anxiety/jitteriness rises and net rated alertness may not increase [rogers2013, rogers2010]; caffeine improved rated alertness irrespective of consumer status in a smaller trial [haskell2005].", "effect_estimate": "not pooled; direction consistent for sleepiness, inconsistent for net 'mental alertness' in low consumers", "certainty_notes": "Subjective outcome (expectancy-sensitive [dawkins2011]); inconsistency between non-consumer studies; downgraded one level. Subjective alertness must not be presented as cognitive improvement [ullrich2015].", "key_study_ids": ["rogers2013", "rogers2010", "haskell2005", "dawkins2011", "ullrich2015"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "alertness", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Habitual daily consumers: net benefit of regular use compared with long-term abstinence (withdrawal-reversal question)", "dose_or_intake": "habitual intake 100-500 mg/day", "duration": "habitual use; experiments with 16 h to weeks of abstinence", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Overnight withdrawal impairs alertness and reaction time and caffeine merely restores baseline in medium-high consumers [rogers2013, yeomans2002]; re-dosing after 4-6 h adds no benefit [heatherley2005]; long-term-withdrawal studies show little net benefit [james2005]; opposing review argues effects are not wholly withdrawal reversal [einother2013].", "effect_estimate": "not reported as pooled effect; Rogers 2013 (n=369): caffeine improved withdrawn medium-high consumers but had little effect on non-low consumers apart from reduced sleepiness", "certainty_notes": "Few long-term abstinence designs, contested interpretations by groups with opposing views; indirectness. Practical message: daily users gain mainly relief of withdrawal.", "key_study_ids": ["rogers2013", "james2005", "rogers2003", "yeomans2002", "heatherley2005", "einother2013", "juliano2004"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Rested healthy adults; n-back, digit span, spatial span, numeric working memory", "dose_or_intake": "75-450 mg", "duration": "single dose", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Speed of working-memory responses sometimes improves [haskell2005], but accuracy effects are absent or negative: memory task impaired [childs2006], spatial working memory impaired at 200 mg in habitual drinkers (n=18) [kassim2024]; reviews conclude caffeine hinders tasks heavily dependent on working memory [nehlig2010].", "effect_estimate": "not pooled; Kassim 2024 spatial span p=0.007-0.013 worse with caffeine; verbal span NS", "certainty_notes": "Small trials, heterogeneous tasks, inconsistency, imprecision.", "key_study_ids": ["haskell2005", "childs2006", "kassim2024", "nehlig2010"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "learning_retention", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Post-encoding caffeine and 24 h recognition/mnemonic discrimination in young adults", "dose_or_intake": "100-300 mg after study", "duration": "single dose; test at 24 h", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Borota 2014 reported better lure discrimination at 24 h after 200 mg (combined sample t(71)=2.0, p=0.049; 100 mg NS; 300 mg p=0.07) in caffeine-naive participants; an independent non-abstinent replication found no effect and excluded medium/large effects [aust2020]; a 2025 face-recognition trial found impairment [leon2025]; methylphenidate, not caffeine, improved 24 h declarative memory in a 3-arm RCT [repantis2021].", "effect_estimate": "Borota: p=0.049, effect size not reported; Aust: nonsuperiority test excluded medium and large effects", "certainty_notes": "Single positive trial with borderline p and 43/160 exclusions; failed replication; inconsistency and imprecision. Not supportive of 'memory' claims.", "key_study_ids": ["borota2014", "aust2020", "leon2025", "repantis2021"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "students", "population_detail": "College students; explicit memory at non-optimal time of day; assessment-centre-like multitask sessions", "dose_or_intake": "caffeinated coffee vs decaffeinated coffee (dose not given in abstract); habitual-intake beverages", "duration": "single serving", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Caffeinated vs decaffeinated coffee improved explicit (not implicit) memory in the early morning but not in the afternoon [sherman2016]; in an exam/assessment-centre simulation with habitual intake maintained, caffeine did not change logical thinking, processing speed, memory or attention, though participants felt more energetic [ullrich2015].", "effect_estimate": "not reported in abstracts", "certainty_notes": "Small samples, a non-randomised-design report (Sherman), conflicting results; indirectness to real exams.", "key_study_ids": ["sherman2016", "ullrich2015"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Rested adults; decision making, reasoning, task switching, planning", "dose_or_intake": "about 40-300 mg", "duration": "single dose", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Effects on higher-order executive function, judgment and decision making are less consistent than on alertness and reaction time [mclellan2016]; caffeine affects the executive-control attention network [einother2013], but multi-task and reasoning batteries show no gain [ullrich2015, repantis2021].", "effect_estimate": "not pooled for rested adults", "certainty_notes": "Heterogeneous outcomes; inconsistency; likely publication bias toward positive attention findings.", "key_study_ids": ["mclellan2016", "einother2013", "ullrich2015", "repantis2021"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "vigilance", "population": "sleep_deprived", "population_detail": "Healthy adults (largely young, many military) after total or partial sleep deprivation", "dose_or_intake": "100-600 mg single dose, or 200 mg repeated (up to 800 mg/day)", "duration": "single dose to 3 consecutive nights", "grade": "A", "direction": "benefit", "effect_summary": "Large improvements: attention response time g = 0.86, accuracy g = 0.68, reaction time g = 1.11, driving lateral control g = 1.67 [irwin2020]; 600 mg after 64 h awake restored PVT speed and objective alertness for about 2-4 h, comparable to modafinil 400 mg and d-amphetamine 20 mg [wesensten2005]; 4 x 200 mg/night sustained vigilance for 3 nights [kamimori2015].", "effect_estimate": "g = 0.68 (95% CI 0.48-0.88) accuracy; reaction time g = 1.11 (0.75-1.47) [irwin2020]", "certainty_notes": "Large, consistent effects across labs; RCTs mostly small and US-military funded (government, not industry). Indirect to students pulling an all-nighter but directionally applicable. Note: the abstract reports an implausible CI for response time (0.53-0.83 around g 0.86).", "key_study_ids": ["irwin2020", "wesensten2005", "kamimori2015", "mclellan2016"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "sleep_deprived", "population_detail": "Sleep-deprived healthy adults; executive and reasoning tasks", "dose_or_intake": "200-600 mg", "duration": "single or repeated doses", "grade": "B", "direction": "benefit", "effect_summary": "Executive function g = 0.35 (95% CI 0.15-0.55) [irwin2020]; logical reasoning responses faster (1.9 vs 2.5 s) over three nights [kamimori2015]; mixed effects across executive tasks after 64 h awake [wesensten2005]; live-fire marksmanship unchanged [kamimori2015].", "effect_estimate": "g = 0.35 (0.15-0.55) [irwin2020]", "certainty_notes": "Smaller and less consistent than vigilance effects; downgraded for inconsistency.", "key_study_ids": ["irwin2020", "kamimori2015", "wesensten2005"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "vigilance", "population": "other", "population_detail": "Fatigued shift workers (night shifts), real or simulated", "dose_or_intake": "varied, typically 200-300 mg", "duration": "single shift", "grade": "B", "direction": "benefit", "effect_summary": "Fewer psychomotor vigilance lapses (SMD 0.75, 95% CI 0.30-1.19) and less end-of-shift slowing (SMD 0.52, 0.19-0.85); two studies showed reduced subsequent sleep quality/duration.", "effect_estimate": "SMD 0.75 (0.30-1.19) lapses; SMD 0.52 (0.19-0.85) reaction time [temple2018]", "certainty_notes": "Authors rated GRADE certainty low-to-moderate (8 studies); coherent with laboratory sleep-loss data, so held at B as an upper bound.", "key_study_ids": ["temple2018"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "alertness", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Post-lunch sleepiness; 'caffeine nap' (caffeine taken immediately before a 20 min nap)", "dose_or_intake": "200 mg before a 20 min nap", "duration": "single afternoon", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Caffeine+nap was the most effective condition for subjective sleepiness and performance for 1 h after napping in 10 young adults.", "effect_estimate": "not reported in abstract", "certainty_notes": "Single small trial (n=10), non-blinded conditions; plausible mechanism (caffeine onset coincides with waking).", "key_study_ids": ["hayashi2003"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Young healthy high habitual consumers (about 474 mg/day), daily controlled intake", "dose_or_intake": "150 mg three times daily (450 mg/day)", "duration": "10 days, crossover with placebo and withdrawal", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "After 10 days, 3-back net error rate was higher with caffeine (d = 0.40) and withdrawal (d = 0.45) than placebo, and net 3-back RT longer with caffeine (d = 0.57); 0-back RT slower only in withdrawal (d = 0.68). Daily high intake gave no working-memory benefit and some cost.", "effect_estimate": "3-back net error d = 0.40 worse vs placebo; RT d = 0.57 slower (full text) [lin2023]", "certainty_notes": "Single small crossover (n=20), high-dose high consumers; imprecise. No chronic trial shows cognitive benefit of daily caffeine in healthy young adults.", "key_study_ids": ["lin2023"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "sleep_related_cognition", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults; night-time sleep after daytime/evening caffeine (outcome is sleep quantity/quality, a determinant of next-day cognition)", "dose_or_intake": "about 100-400 mg, taken 0-13 h before bed", "duration": "single day", "grade": "A", "direction": "harm", "effect_summary": "Caffeine reduced total sleep time by 45 min and sleep efficiency by 7%, increased sleep onset latency by 9 min and wake after sleep onset by 12 min, and reduced deep sleep by 11.4 min; to avoid TST loss, coffee (107 mg) should be taken at least 8.8 h and a 217.5 mg pre-workout at least 13.2 h before bed [gardiner2023]. 400 mg even 6 h before bed disrupted sleep [drake2013].", "effect_estimate": "TST -45 min; SE -7%; SOL +9 min [gardiner2023]; TST about -1 h objectively at 0, 3 and 6 h before bed [drake2013]", "certainty_notes": "Consistent meta-analytic and RCT evidence; Drake was small (n=12) and funded by a sleep-monitor company (Zeo Inc). Practical relevance high for students.", "key_study_ids": ["gardiner2023", "drake2013", "marcus2023"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "sleep_related_cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Young men, daily daytime caffeine for 10 days", "dose_or_intake": "3 x 150 mg/day", "duration": "10 days", "grade": "C", "direction": "harm", "effect_summary": "Total sleep time and architecture unchanged, but REM latency longer and subjective awakening quality worse with daily caffeine vs placebo [weibel2021]; in a 14-day randomized coffee trial, caffeinated days had 36 fewer minutes of nightly sleep [marcus2023].", "effect_estimate": "Sleep -36 min/night (95% CI 25-47) on coffee days [marcus2023]", "certainty_notes": "Two trials with different designs; small n for Weibel; imprecision.", "key_study_ids": ["weibel2021", "marcus2023"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults, especially non/low consumers and ADORA2A rs5751876 TT carriers", "dose_or_intake": "150-450 mg", "duration": "single dose", "grade": "B", "direction": "harm", "effect_summary": "Caffeine increased anxiety/jitteriness in non/low consumers enough to offset reduced sleepiness [rogers2013]; TT genotype more susceptible; tolerance develops in frequent users [rogers2010]; positive mood and arousal at typical doses, higher anxiety at high doses [childs2006, nehlig2010].", "effect_estimate": "not pooled", "certainty_notes": "Consistent direction in large trials (n=369-379) but dose-dependent and subjective; anxiety at 50-100 mg is minimal in most users.", "key_study_ids": ["rogers2013", "rogers2010", "childs2006", "kapellou2023"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Adults with panic disorder vs healthy controls (caffeine challenge)", "dose_or_intake": "400-750 mg (mostly 480 mg)", "duration": "single dose", "grade": "A", "direction": "harm", "effect_summary": "51.1% of 237 patients had a panic attack after caffeine vs none after placebo; patients 53.9% vs healthy controls 1.7% (log RR 3.47, 95% CI 2.06-4.87); subjective anxiety greater in patients (g = 1.02, 0.09-1.96).", "effect_estimate": "log RR 3.47 (2.06-4.87); g = 1.02 (0.09-1.96) [klevebrant2022]", "certainty_notes": "Consistent, large effect; doses above typical servings; little known below 400 mg. Supports exclusion warning for anxiety/panic disorders.", "key_study_ids": ["klevebrant2022"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "children_adolescents", "population_detail": "Adolescents 12-17 y, light consumers (median 28 mg/day)", "dose_or_intake": "1 and 3 mg/kg", "duration": "single dose", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Caffeine attenuated the vigilance decrement over a 33 min task (p = .01), with earlier improvement at 3 mg/kg (n=31).", "effect_estimate": "not reported as standardized effect", "certainty_notes": "Single small controlled trial; not a basis for products aimed at minors, for whom EFSA proposes 3 mg/kg/day as the safety level and energy-drink use is associated with adverse outcomes [ajibo2024].", "key_study_ids": ["cooper2021", "ajibo2024"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "dementia_risk", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Prospective cohorts of middle-aged and older adults; habitual caffeine, coffee and tea intake", "dose_or_intake": "highest vs lowest habitual intake categories", "duration": "years of follow-up", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Caffeine per se was not associated with dementia (RR 0.94, 0.70-1.25; 5 cohorts) and was associated with higher AD risk in 2 cohorts (RR 1.34, 1.04-1.74, very low certainty); coffee showed a non-linear association with lower risk at 1-3 cups/day; tea was protective (RR 0.84).", "effect_estimate": "Dementia RR 0.94 (0.70-1.25); AD RR 1.34 (1.04-1.74) [li2024]", "certainty_notes": "Observational, GRADE low to very low per authors; residual confounding and reverse causation; beverage effects cannot be attributed to caffeine. Not applicable to young adults.", "key_study_ids": ["li2024"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "vascular_health", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Adults in RCTs of caffeine supplementation and a randomized n-of-1 coffee trial", "dose_or_intake": "habitual coffee; supplemental caffeine (meta-regression shows dose dependence, >400 mg/day for DBP)", "duration": "14 days to >9 weeks", "grade": "B", "direction": "harm", "effect_summary": "Caffeine raised SBP by 1.94 mmHg (0.52-3.35) and DBP by 1.66 mmHg (0.75-2.57) [abbashashemi2023]; coffee did not increase premature atrial contractions (RR 1.09, 0.98-1.20) but increased premature ventricular contractions (RR 1.51, 1.18-1.94) [marcus2023].", "effect_estimate": "SBP +1.94 mmHg; DBP +1.66 mmHg; PVC RR 1.51", "certainty_notes": "Small absolute effects in healthy people; few trials in BP meta-analysis; clinical significance uncertain, relevant for hypertension and arrhythmia patients.", "key_study_ids": ["abbashashemi2023", "marcus2023", "wikoff2017"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy young adults and students: cognitive benefit of regular daily caffeine over weeks or months", "dose_or_intake": "any habitual dose", "duration": "2 weeks or longer", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No randomized trial has shown that daily caffeine over weeks improves learning, grades or global cognition in healthy young adults; the only controlled multi-day data show tolerance or worse complex working memory [lin2023] and the withdrawal-reversal literature suggests no net gain [james2005].", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "Absence of trials, not evidence of benefit.", "key_study_ids": ["lin2023", "james2005"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "12.5-100 mg (tea/cola range to one coffee)", "onset": "testing from about 30 min, repeated over about 2 h", "duration_studied": "single dose after overnight abstinence", "domain": "simple reaction time, rapid visual information processing, mood", "population": "23 adults, low and higher consumers", "effect_estimate": "All doses significantly improved performance; flat dose-response; larger effects in higher consumers (withdrawal confound)", "certainty": "moderate (small n, consistent with larger literature)", "safety": "Thirst increase in low consumers only; no relevant adverse effects at these doses", "study_ids": ["smit2000"]}, {"dose": "75 mg and 150 mg in a 150 mL drink", "onset": "30 min", "duration_studied": "single dose", "domain": "simple RT, digit vigilance RT, numeric working memory RT, sentence verification accuracy, mental fatigue", "population": "24 habitual consumers (217 mg/day) and 24 non-consumers (20 mg/day)", "effect_estimate": "Significant improvements irrespective of habitual status; numbers not given in abstract", "certainty": "moderate", "safety": "Jittery ratings differed by group", "study_ids": ["haskell2005"]}, {"dose": "50, 150, 450 mg capsules", "onset": "40 min", "duration_studied": "single dose", "domain": "vigilance hits and RT, short-term memory, mood, blood pressure", "population": "102 light, nondependent users", "effect_estimate": "Vigilance hits up and RT down; memory task impaired; BP increased", "certainty": "moderate", "safety": "Raised blood pressure; arousal and 'high' ratings", "study_ids": ["childs2006"]}, {"dose": "100 mg then 150 mg 90 min later (250 mg)", "onset": "1-3 h", "duration_studied": "single day after about 16 h abstinence", "domain": "alertness, sleepiness, anxiety, simple/choice RT, recognition memory, tapping", "population": "369 adults (212 medium-high, 157 non-low consumers)", "effect_estimate": "Restored withdrawn performance in medium-high consumers; in non-low consumers only reduced sleepiness and faster motor speed, offset by anxiety; 'faster but not smarter'", "certainty": "high internal validity (large, double-blind)", "safety": "Substantial anxiety/jitteriness in non-low consumers", "study_ids": ["rogers2013", "rogers2010"]}, {"dose": "pooled trials, commonly 40-400 mg", "onset": "typically 30-90 min", "duration_studied": "single dose", "domain": "attention accuracy and reaction time", "population": "31 RCTs, 1455 rested healthy adults", "effect_estimate": "g = 0.27 accuracy, g = 0.28 RT; larger at 200 mg or more; accuracy curve turns down at high doses", "certainty": "high", "safety": "Not assessed", "study_ids": ["klove2025"]}, {"dose": "200 mg after study (100 and 300 mg also tested)", "onset": "memory tested 24 h later", "duration_studied": "single dose", "domain": "memory consolidation (lure discrimination)", "population": "160 enrolled, 44-73 analysed caffeine-naive adults 18-30 y; replication in non-abstinent adults", "effect_estimate": "Borota p=0.049 (200 mg), 100 mg NS, 300 mg p=0.07; replication null, medium/large effects excluded; 2025 trial impaired face recognition", "certainty": "low (unreplicated)", "safety": "Side effects 16% placebo, 23% 200 mg, 45% 300 mg", "study_ids": ["borota2014", "aust2020", "leon2025"]}, {"dose": "600 mg single dose after 64 h awake", "onset": "within about 1 h; benefit lasting about 2-4 h on simple tasks", "duration_studied": "85 h total sleep deprivation", "domain": "psychomotor vigilance, objective alertness, executive tasks", "population": "48 healthy young adults (12 per arm)", "effect_estimate": "Restored PVT speed and alertness comparable to modafinil 400 mg and dextroamphetamine 20 mg; executive effects mixed", "certainty": "moderate", "safety": "Recovery sleep unaffected after 85 h awake; dose above EFSA single-dose level", "study_ids": ["wesensten2005"]}, {"dose": "4 x 200 mg per night (800 mg/day) for 3 nights", "onset": "within each night", "duration_studied": "3 consecutive nights of restricted sleep", "domain": "PVT speed, field vigilance, logical reasoning, marksmanship", "population": "20 US Special Forces personnel", "effect_estimate": "FVT 10.8 vs 6.1 points; vigilance monitor 7.6 vs 4.8 correct; LRT 1.9 vs 2.5 s; no PVT lapse difference; marksmanship unchanged", "certainty": "moderate (small n, government-funded)", "safety": "Caffeine group slept less in afternoon opportunities (64.6 vs 115.3 min)", "study_ids": ["kamimori2015"]}, {"dose": "200 mg immediately before a 20 min nap", "onset": "on waking, lasting at least 1 h", "duration_studied": "single afternoon", "domain": "subjective sleepiness and task performance", "population": "10 young adults", "effect_estimate": "Most effective condition vs nap alone, bright light or face washing", "certainty": "low", "safety": "Not reported", "study_ids": ["hayashi2003"]}, {"dose": "400 mg at 0, 3 or 6 h before bedtime", "onset": "night-time sleep", "duration_studied": "single nights, home monitoring", "domain": "objective and subjective sleep", "population": "12 healthy normal sleepers", "effect_estimate": "Objective total sleep reduced by over 1 h at all timings; diary TST -41 min at 6 h (p=0.08)", "certainty": "moderate (small n; industry-funded device study)", "safety": "Sleep loss even at 6 h before bed", "study_ids": ["drake2013", "gardiner2023"]}, {"dose": "1 and 3 mg/kg in flavoured water", "onset": "about 25 min", "duration_studied": "single dose", "domain": "sustained attention (33 min CPT)", "population": "31 adolescents 12-17 y", "effect_estimate": "Attenuated vigilance decrement (p=.01); earlier benefit at 3 mg/kg", "certainty": "low", "safety": "Minors: not a target group for Brain Food", "study_ids": ["cooper2021"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "10 days, 3 x 150 mg/day, crossover with placebo and withdrawal", "population": "20 young healthy high consumers (about 474 mg/day)", "outcome": "N-back working memory and fMRI; night sleep and REM", "effect_estimate": "3-back net errors worse with caffeine (d=0.40) and withdrawal (d=0.45) vs placebo; REM latency longer, poorer awakening with caffeine; TST unchanged", "evidence_quality": "moderate internal validity, low precision", "limitations": "Small, men only in sleep analysis, very high dose, single site", "study_ids": ["lin2023", "weibel2021"]}, {"duration": "14 days randomized day-by-day coffee vs no caffeine", "population": "100 ambulatory adults, mean 39 y", "outcome": "Cardiac ectopy, steps, sleep, glucose", "effect_estimate": "PAC RR 1.09 (0.98-1.20); PVC RR 1.51 (1.18-1.94); +1058 steps; -36 min sleep (25-47)", "evidence_quality": "high (NEJM RCT, NIH-funded)", "limitations": "Coffee not pure caffeine; unblinded; short duration", "study_ids": ["marcus2023"]}, {"duration": "weeks to >9 weeks of supplemental caffeine", "population": "Adults in 8 RCTs", "outcome": "Blood pressure", "effect_estimate": "SBP +1.94 mmHg (0.52-3.35); DBP +1.66 mmHg (0.75-2.57)", "evidence_quality": "moderate-low", "limitations": "Few trials; heterogeneity; abstract-only access", "study_ids": ["abbashashemi2023"]}, {"duration": "habitual use; experiments with abstinence periods", "population": "Habitual consumers vs non-consumers", "outcome": "Net alertness and performance benefit of regular use", "effect_estimate": "Little or no net benefit relative to long-term abstinence; tolerance to alerting and anxiogenic effects", "evidence_quality": "low (contested interpretation)", "limitations": "Few long-term withdrawal designs; same research group on one side of the debate", "study_ids": ["james2005", "rogers2013", "rogers2010", "einother2013"]}, {"duration": "years (prospective cohorts)", "population": "751,824 adults in 38 cohorts", "outcome": "Incident dementia and Alzheimer's disease", "effect_estimate": "Caffeine dementia RR 0.94 (0.70-1.25); AD RR 1.34 (1.04-1.74, 2 cohorts)", "evidence_quality": "low to very low (GRADE, observational)", "limitations": "Confounding, exposure misclassification, beverages not caffeine", "study_ids": ["li2024"]}, {"duration": "habitual intake (safety systematic review, studies 2001-2015)", "population": "Healthy adults, pregnant women, adolescents, children", "outcome": "Acute toxicity, cardiovascular, bone, behaviour, reproduction", "effect_estimate": "Up to 400 mg/day adults and 300 mg/day pregnancy not associated with overt adverse effects; 2.5 mg/kg/day children", "evidence_quality": "moderate", "limitations": "Consultancy-led review; limited data in children; sensitive subgroups not characterised", "study_ids": ["wikoff2017", "efsa2015"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Caffeine is among the best-characterised food constituents. For healthy adults, EFSA considers single doses up to 200 mg and habitual intakes up to 400 mg/day of no safety concern [efsa2015], consistent with a systematic review [wikoff2017]. The main practical harms at food doses are sleep disruption (TST -45 min on average; effects even 6-9 h after intake) [gardiner2023, drake2013, marcus2023], anxiety/jitteriness in low consumers and susceptible genotypes [rogers2013, rogers2010], panic attacks in panic disorder [klevebrant2022], small blood-pressure increases [abbashashemi2023], increased ventricular ectopy [marcus2023], dependence and withdrawal (headache in about half of abstainers) [juliano2004]. Serious toxicity and deaths occur with concentrated powders/tablets and very high intakes, which led FDA to declare bulk pure caffeine supplements adulterated (2018). Energy-drink use in young people is associated with many adverse outcomes [ajibo2024].", "adverse_effects": ["Sleep disruption: shorter total sleep, longer sleep latency, less deep sleep; dose- and timing-dependent", "Anxiety, nervousness, jitteriness, tremor, reduced hand steadiness (especially at 200 mg or more and in low consumers)", "Panic attacks in people with panic disorder (about 50% at 400-750 mg)", "Increased blood pressure (about 2 mmHg) and premature ventricular contractions", "Gastrointestinal upset, increased diuresis", "Dependence; withdrawal headache, fatigue, low mood, poor concentration for 2-9 days", "Toxicity (tachyarrhythmia, seizures, death) with gram-level doses, usually from powders or tablets"], "contraindications": ["Pregnancy above 200 mg/day (EFSA); lactation similar caution", "Children and adolescents beyond 3 mg/kg/day; products should not be marketed to under-18s", "Panic disorder, generalised anxiety disorder, insomnia", "Uncontrolled hypertension, symptomatic arrhythmias or other cardiac disease (physician advice)", "Concurrent use of other stimulants or multiple caffeinated products"], "drug_interactions": ["CYP1A2 inhibitors (e.g., fluvoxamine, ciprofloxacin and other fluoroquinolones, oral contraceptives) raise caffeine levels", "Smoking (CYP1A2 induction) shortens caffeine half-life; quitting raises levels", "Theophylline and other methylxanthines: additive effects", "Stimulant medications (amphetamines, methylphenidate, modafinil, ephedrine/synephrine-containing products): additive cardiovascular and anxiety effects", "Lithium: caffeine changes may alter lithium levels (clinical monitoring)", "Alcohol: EFSA found alcohol up to 0.65 g/kg does not change safety of 200 mg, but caffeine can mask subjective intoxication (behavioural risk)"], "upper_limits": "EU (EFSA 2015): healthy adults single dose up to 200 mg (about 3 mg/kg) and up to 400 mg/day (5.7 mg/kg); pregnant/lactating up to 200 mg/day; children and adolescents 3 mg/kg/day. US: FDA cites 400 mg/day as not generally associated with negative effects (informational, not a legal limit). Health Canada-derived benchmarks in Wikoff 2017: 400 mg/day adults, 300 mg/day pregnancy, 2.5 mg/kg/day children.", "sleep_impact": "Material. Meta-analysis: -45 min total sleep, +9 min sleep latency, -11.4 min deep sleep; a 107 mg coffee should be taken at least 8.8 h, and a 217.5 mg serving at least 13.2 h, before bed [gardiner2023]; 400 mg disrupts sleep even 6 h before bed [drake2013]; daily daytime use delays REM and worsens awakening quality [weibel2021]. EFSA notes 100 mg near bedtime may affect sleep in some adults.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Habitual intake up to 400 mg/day is supported by observational and trial data for cardiovascular, bone and reproductive safety in healthy adults [wikoff2017]; controlled multi-week cognitive and sleep trials are short (10-14 days) [lin2023, weibel2021, marcus2023].", "daily_use_appropriate": "probably", "special_populations": "Pregnancy and lactation (<=200 mg/day), children and adolescents (3 mg/kg/day; energy-drink-related harms), people with anxiety or panic disorder, insomnia, hypertension or arrhythmia, slow CYP1A2 metabolisers and users of CYP1A2-inhibiting drugs, people with eating disorders or who combine caffeine with alcohol. Students in exam periods are at specific risk of trading sleep for alertness.", "specialist_review_needed": ["Cardiologist (arrhythmia and hypertension warnings)", "Psychiatrist (anxiety/panic disorder, ADHD medication interactions)", "Obstetrician (pregnancy messaging)", "Paediatrician (age restriction policy)", "Clinical pharmacologist (CYP1A2 interactions)", "Food toxicologist / QA (dose uniformity, prevention of concentrated formats)"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "Safe for healthy adults at 40-100 mg per serving within 400 mg/day, but only with: per-serving dose cap (suggest <=100 mg, never above 200 mg), clear caffeine labelling in mg, warnings for pregnancy, children, anxiety, heart conditions, a time-of-day cut-off, no concentrated powders or high-potency units, and no marketing to minors or stacking with other stimulants."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Status: caffeine is a permitted food constituent and is not a novel food. Safety: EFSA 2015 opinion [efsa2015]. Health claims: EFSA (2011) gave a favourable opinion on increased alertness and increased attention (reported conditions: at least 75 mg per serving) [efsa2011]. The Commission's draft regulation authorising 'Caffeine helps to increase alertness', 'Caffeine helps to improve concentration' and two endurance claims was vetoed by the European Parliament plenary on 7 July 2016 (motion by MEP Christel Schaldemose). The 'perceived exertion' claim had already been rejected. Source: NutraIngredients/FoodNavigator, 7-8 July 2016, accessed 2026-10-25. The assignment brief gave the objection as 2015; the sources date it to 2016. No caffeine claim appears in the EU Register, so cognitive claims are not authorised. The Commission had argued that, without a decision, the claims might continue under transitional rules. Whether that applies is legally uncertain, and Brain Food should treat them as prohibited. Labelling (Regulation (EU) 1169/2011, Annex III point 4): beverages with more than 150 mg/L caffeine from any source, other than coffee- or tea-based drinks named as such, must state 'High caffeine content. Not recommended for children or pregnant or breast-feeding women' in the same field of vision as the name, followed by mg/100 mL (Finnish Food Authority summary, accessed 2026-10-25). Other foods with added caffeine must state 'Contains caffeine. Not recommended for children and pregnant women' with the amount; this wording is taken from a Netherlands Nutrition Centre summary and is still to be checked against the Official Journal. Food supplements state the amount per portion. Some Member States add national limits or sale restrictions; these were not researched here.", "us": "Caffeine is GRAS only for cola-type beverages at 0.02 percent (21 CFR 182.1180; about 71 mg per 12 fl oz). Other added uses rely on self-affirmed GRAS conclusions or on dietary-supplement status. Added caffeine must appear in the ingredient list, but declaring the amount is voluntary. FDA cites 400 mg/day as an informational benchmark. FDA guidance of April 2018 treats pure or highly concentrated caffeine supplements sold in bulk to consumers as adulterated, citing at least two deaths. A supplement can carry a structure/function claim such as 'helps maintain alertness', with the DSHEA disclaimer and 30-day notification. Implied memory, IQ or disease claims are not permissible, and the FTC requires competent and reliable scientific evidence. State bills restricting sales of energy drinks to minors are pending, and caffeinated drinks with poor disclosure have led to tort suits (Panera 'Charged Lemonade'). Sources: project synthesis X5-regulatory-us and news reports on the FDA guidance, accessed 2026-10-25.", "morocco": "Uncertain. Food safety falls under Law 28-07 (ONSSA). In 2018 ONSSA asked energy-drink importers to submit updated technical files (Hespress, 28 September 2018). On 9 May 2019 the Government Council adopted draft decree 2.19.13 on the quality and safety of certain beverages, according to press reports. It provides for separate shelving and warnings such as not suitable for pregnant or breastfeeding women, children and under-18s, and 'must not be mixed with alcohol'. Publication in the Bulletin officiel and any numeric caffeine limit could not be confirmed. In April 2026 the Moroccan consumer-protection observatory proposed banning sales of energy drinks to minors; this is a recommendation, not law (TelQuel, 10 April 2026). No Moroccan rules on caffeine in food supplements or on caffeine claims were found. Direct consultation with ONSSA and the Ministry of Health is required. Coffee and tea are everyday staple foods in Morocco.", "permitted_claims_examples": [], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "Caffeine and traditional sources (coffee, tea, guarana, mate, cocoa) are not novel foods in the EU. Less common sources (for example coffee-cherry/cascara or new extracts) may require novel food checks and were not researched here. In the US, caffeine is generally treated as a pre-1994 dietary ingredient; this was not verified here. Conventional foods beyond cola need their own GRAS basis, and FDA has signalled scrutiny of added caffeine in snack foods. Concentrated formats are a regulatory red flag in all markets.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Excellent technically: water-soluble, heat-stable and effective at low doses (12.5-100 mg). The best vehicles are ordinary foods: coffee and tea already deliver 40-110 mg per serving. A 'brain' product adds value only through accurate dosing, timing guidance and lower doses than energy drinks.", "formats": ["brewed coffee or tea (whole-food option; tea adds L-theanine)", "ready-to-drink tea/coffee with declared mg", "low-dose caffeinated chocolate or cocoa (40-60 mg)", "caffeine gum or mints (fast buccal onset, used in military studies)", "capsules or tablets at a fixed 50-100 mg (supplement route)", "avoid: powders sold in bulk, high-dose shots, products combined with multiple stimulants"], "stability": "Chemically very stable through heat processing and storage; no meaningful degradation expected in beverages, bars or chocolate.", "taste": "Intensely bitter. Masking is needed above about 50 mg in neutral matrices; coffee, cocoa and tea mask it naturally.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Near-complete absorption in all matrices. Onset is faster from liquids and gum, and food and carbonation can shift the timing of effects (a 2022 RCT on carbonation was surfaced but not reviewed). Natural matrices bring co-actives: L-theanine in tea and chlorogenic acids in coffee.", "dose_feasibility": "Easy. The evidence-based acute dose of 40-100 mg (and up to 200 mg in sleep-deprived settings) fits a single serving of any food or drink. Under the EU threshold of 150 mg/L, a 250 mL drink with more than 37.5 mg (computed) must carry the 'High caffeine content' label. The real constraint is safety and sleep, not feasibility: limit to 100 mg per serving and advise against intake within about 8-9 h of bedtime (per Gardiner 2023 for 107 mg).", "cost_availability": "Commodity ingredient; synthetic caffeine anhydrous and natural caffeine (from tea or coffee decaffeination, guarana) are widely available. Coffee and tea are cheap and ubiquitous in Morocco, the EU and the US.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 8, "effect_magnitude": 4.5, "safety": 6.5, "reproducibility": 7, "food_suitability": 9, "cost_access": 9.5, "rationale": "evidence_quality 8: grade A for acute reaction time and attention in rested healthy young adults (31-trial meta-analysis) and for vigilance under sleep loss; held below 9 because the net benefit in daily users is confounded by withdrawal reversal (grade C) and memory and executive outcomes are grade C. effect_magnitude 4.5: small in rested people (g about 0.27-0.28, tens of milliseconds), large only under sleep deprivation (g 0.7-1.1); nil for memory, reasoning or chronic cognition. safety 6.5: well-defined limits (200 mg single, 400 mg/day), but sleep loss at ordinary doses, anxiety, panic in vulnerable people, BP and ectopy, dependence, toxicity with concentrated forms, and harms in minors. reproducibility 7: attention and alertness effects replicated across many independent labs; memory findings failed replication. food_suitability 9: soluble, stable, effective at low doses, naturally present in coffee, tea and cocoa; bitterness is the only issue. cost_access 9.5: commodity ingredient and ubiquitous beverages."}, "research_gaps": ["Preregistered, adequately powered trials in non-withdrawn habitual consumers (for example caffeine vs decaf after normal morning intake) to settle the net-benefit question", "Real-world academic outcomes (exam scores, learning over weeks) with caffeine vs decaf in students, including sleep as a mediator", "Head-to-head comparisons of 40-100 mg caffeine in whole foods (tea, coffee, cocoa) vs isolated caffeine, with blinded decaf controls", "Replication of the memory-consolidation effect with saliva-verified abstinence and preregistration", "Genotype-stratified (CYP1A2, ADORA2A) prospective trials of cognitive and sleep effects", "Long-term (months) trials of daily low-dose caffeine on sleep, anxiety and cognition in young adults", "Safety data in adolescents and Moroccan consumption patterns (energy drink and coffee intake surveys)", "Confirmation of the Moroccan legal status (decree 2.19.13 publication, supplement rules)"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 7.15, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": "A", "best_grade_chronic": null, "best_grade_young_adults": "A", "best_grade_sleep_stress": "A", "best_grade_surrogate": null, "best_grade_overall": "A", "benefit_domains": {"reaction_time": "A", "sustained_attention": "A", "alertness": "B", "vigilance": "A", "executive_function": "B"}, "n_outcome_rows": 21, "n_studies": 41, "n_verified": 41, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 15, "n_systematic_reviews": 7, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["benefit", "harm", "inconsistent", "insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "coffee", "name": "Coffee", "category": "F", "category_name": "Caffeine and natural stimulants", "aliases": ["coffee", "caffeinated coffee", "decaffeinated coffee", "decaf", "Coffea arabica", "Coffea canephora (robusta)", "espresso", "filter coffee", "instant coffee", "green coffee", "qahwa (Arabic coffee)"], "food_sources": ["brewed filter coffee (paper-filtered: little cafestol)", "espresso, moka pot, French press, boiled/Turkish/Scandinavian boiled coffee (unfiltered: more diterpenes)", "instant (soluble) coffee", "decaffeinated coffee (still contains roughly 0-16 mg caffeine per serving)", "ready-to-drink and cold-brew coffee beverages", "coffee-flavoured foods (coffee chocolate, coffee snacks)"], "active_constituents": ["caffeine (roughly 60-260 mg per retail serving, highly variable)", "chlorogenic acids (caffeoylquinic acids; reduced by roasting) and their metabolites (caffeic, ferulic acids)", "trigonelline and N-methylpyridinium", "diterpenes cafestol and kahweol (unfiltered coffee; raise LDL cholesterol)", "melanoidins and Maillard products (including acrylamide from roasting)", "aroma volatiles (may contribute to expectancy effects)"], "one_line_verdict": "Coffee is an effective, cheap delivery vehicle for caffeine that produces a small, reliable acute gain in alertness and attention (not memory or intelligence), its non-caffeine constituents have not been shown to improve cognition, and the lower dementia risk seen in moderate drinkers is an observational association that Mendelian randomization does not confirm.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. Caffeinated coffee acts on cognition mainly through caffeine. In rested adults caffeine improves attention accuracy and reaction time by about g = 0.27-0.28 across 31 trials [klove2025]. Coffee-specific trials agree. Black coffee with 100 mg caffeine sped digit-vigilance and RVIP responses and reduced tiredness and mental fatigue versus coffee-flavoured placebo in 59 overnight-abstinent habitual drinkers [haskellramsay2018]. One cup (80 mg) reduced lane weaving during 2 h of monotonous simulated driving in rested volunteers [mets2012]. Coffee with 200 mg caffeine at 2 a.m. brought real-road line crossings back near daytime levels (incidence rate ratio 3.7 for decaf versus coffee) [philip2006]. Part of the benefit in habitual consumers is reversal of overnight withdrawal. No coffee trial improved episodic or working memory [haskellramsay2018]. What coffee adds beyond caffeine. The evidence is weak. Decaf raised self-rated alertness versus flavoured water but did not change performance, and 72% of participants recognised decaf, so expectancy is a likely explanation [haskellramsay2018]. In 60 adults over 50, a decaffeinated green-coffee blend missed its primary outcome and showed only scattered secondary effects at p about 0.05, and 540 mg pure chlorogenic acid (CGA) had no cognitive effect; the trial was Nestle-funded [camfield2013]. A 39-person pilot reported mood effects of high-CGA decaf [cropley2012]. A 2025 meta-analysis of coffee-CGA RCTs found d = 0.00 (95% CI -0.05 to 0.05) [johal2025]. A Kao-funded CGA drink reduced Trail-Making B errors in 34 MCI patients but left global measures unchanged [ochiai2019]. Long term. Cohorts associate moderate intake with lower dementia risk. In two US cohorts (131,821 people, median 36.8 years) the top quartile of caffeinated coffee had a dementia HR of 0.82 (0.76-0.89); decaf showed no association [zhang2026]. Other cohorts and meta-analyses report J- or U-shaped curves with a nadir at 1-3 cups [wu2017, zhang2021, zhu2024, matsushita2021]. But a dose-response meta-analysis of 8 cohorts found RR 1.01 per cup (0.98-1.05) [larsson2018]. A 38-cohort meta-analysis found RR 0.95 (0.87-1.02) [li2024]. Intake above 6 cups/day was associated with OR 1.53 for dementia and smaller brain volumes [pham2022]. Mendelian randomization in 415,530 people found no causal effect on global cognition or memory [zhou2018], and another MR suggested higher Alzheimer risk [zhangmr2021]. In UK Biobank, coffee drinkers performed worse on cognitive tests, probably through confounding [cornelis2020]. Even the favourable associations are tiny: +0.11 points on a 41-point cognitive screen [zhang2026]. Certainty is low. Costs. Coffee shortens sleep by about 36 min on drinking days and raises premature ventricular contractions [marcus2023, clark2017]. Unfiltered brews raise LDL cholesterol [jee2001]. Coffee inhibits non-haem iron absorption [hurrell1999]. High intake in pregnancy is associated with adverse outcomes [poole2017]. Caffeine content per cup varies several-fold [mccusker2003]. For students and knowledge workers, a cup gives a modest, short-lived alertness boost that is real but small, and it can cost sleep and so next-day performance.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Coffee corrects no nutrient deficiency; caffeine is not a nutrient. The best-documented 'benefit' in habitual drinkers is partly the reversal of overnight caffeine withdrawal: participants in the main coffee trial abstained overnight and consumed 327-426 mg caffeine/day habitually [haskellramsay2018], so post-dose gains versus placebo mix true enhancement with restoration of baseline. True enhancement in rested, non-withdrawn people exists but is small (g about 0.3 on attention and reaction time) [klove2025]. Larger, practically meaningful effects appear only in a degraded state (night-time driving after sleep loss) [philip2006], which is state restoration rather than enhancement. Non-caffeine compounds (chlorogenic acids) show no reliable effect in healthy people [johal2025, camfield2013]; the only positive chronic CGA trial is in MCI patients [ochiai2019] and cannot be generalized to healthy young adults. Cohort associations with lower dementia risk concern middle-aged and older adults and say nothing about cognitive enhancement in students.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Claims such as 'coffee makes you smarter', 'brain-boosting coffee', 'nootropic coffee' or 'coffee prevents Alzheimer's' are not supported. The defensible statement is that a caffeine-containing coffee produces a temporary increase in alertness and attention, roughly equivalent to the caffeine it contains; it does not improve memory or intelligence. Enhanced 'functional coffees' marketed with chlorogenic acids, 'neuro' blends or decaf-with-polyphenols lack cognitive evidence (meta-analytic d = 0.00 [johal2025]). Dementia-prevention language is an observational association with inconsistent dose-response, null Mendelian randomization [zhou2018] and a harm signal at high intakes [pham2022]; it would be a prohibited disease claim in the US and an unauthorised claim in the EU. In the EU even the caffeine alertness/concentration claims, despite a favourable 2011 EFSA opinion, were vetoed by the European Parliament in 2016 and are not in the Register (see caffeine monograph). Several key studies and reviews are industry-linked (Nestle-funded CGA trial [camfield2013], Kao-funded MCI trial [ochiai2019]); the meta-analysis that most strongly promotes coffee/tea for dementia prevention includes case-control designs and concludes beyond its data [zhu2024]. Honest positioning: 'ordinary coffee in moderation, earlier in the day, is a reasonable alertness tool; it is not brain medicine'.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Caffeine from coffee is absorbed within 30-60 min and antagonises adenosine A1/A2A receptors, increasing arousal and speeding responses; in habitual users this also relieves withdrawal. Chlorogenic acids are partly absorbed and extensively metabolised (caffeic, ferulic, dihydro-derivatives, colonic metabolites); proposed vascular and anti-inflammatory actions are mostly preclinical. Coffee-berry CGA extracts acutely changed cerebral haemodynamics in some studies, but such biomarker changes do not establish cognitive benefit. Cafestol in unfiltered coffee raises LDL, a vascular risk factor. Proposed long-term neuroprotection (adenosine antagonism reducing amyloid in animals, insulin sensitivity) is plausible but not demonstrated in humans; Mendelian randomization does not support a causal cognitive effect.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Coffee beverage -> rapid gastrointestinal absorption of caffeine (peak about 30-60 min; half-life about 3-7 h, CYP1A2)", "detail": "Salivary caffeine rose sharply after 100 mg caffeine as coffee versus decaf and placebo [haskellramsay2018]; caffeine dose per cup varies 58-259 mg across outlets and two-fold within one outlet [mccusker2003].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Caffeine -> adenosine A1/A2A receptor antagonism -> increased cortical arousal and reduced sleep pressure", "detail": "Established pharmacology (see caffeine monograph); coffee reduced subjective tiredness and sleepiness [haskellramsay2018, mets2012] and shortens nocturnal sleep [marcus2023, clark2017].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Chlorogenic acids -> partial absorption, hepatic and colonic metabolism -> phenolic acid metabolites in plasma", "detail": "Metabolism of CGA is documented in humans (not re-reviewed here); brain penetration of metabolites in humans is unknown.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "CGA metabolites -> proposed endothelial/nitric-oxide and anti-inflammatory effects -> cerebral blood flow", "detail": "Biomarker-level only; a 12-week CGA drink in MCI patients changed one executive measure [ochiai2019]; acute decaf/CGA effects on mood are inconsistent [camfield2013, cropley2012].", "evidence_level": "plausible"}, {"step": "Chronic intake -> proposed reduced neurodegeneration (adenosine A2A antagonism, insulin sensitivity, antioxidant effects)", "detail": "Supported by animal models and observational associations [zhang2026, wu2017]; Mendelian randomization null for cognition [zhou2018] and adverse for AD in one analysis [zhangmr2021].", "evidence_level": "speculative"}, {"step": "Measured human cognitive outcome", "detail": "Acute: small gains in reaction time, vigilance and alertness [klove2025, haskellramsay2018, mets2012]; no memory effect. Chronic: no trial in healthy adults; associations only.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}], "pathways": ["adenosine A1/A2A receptor antagonism (caffeine)", "withdrawal reversal in habitual users", "expectancy and sensory/aroma conditioning", "chlorogenic acid metabolism and putative vascular effects", "cafestol-mediated LDL increase (unfiltered coffee)", "non-haem iron absorption inhibition by polyphenols"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "reaction_time", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Rested healthy adults, mostly habitual consumers tested after overnight abstinence; coffee trials included 20-34-year-olds and 61-80-year-olds", "dose_or_intake": "Caffeinated coffee delivering about 75-200 mg caffeine", "duration": "single dose, tested 30 min to 2 h", "grade": "A", "direction": "benefit", "effect_summary": "Caffeine reliably speeds responses and improves attention accuracy; coffee-specific trials replicate faster RVIP and digit-vigilance reaction times versus placebo. The effect is small and partly withdrawal reversal in habitual users.", "effect_estimate": "Caffeine: RT g = 0.28, accuracy g = 0.27 (31 trials) [klove2025]; coffee 100 mg: digit vigilance RT p = 0.009, RVIP RT p = 0.02 vs placebo, effect sizes not reported [haskellramsay2018]", "certainty_notes": "Grade rests on the caffeine meta-analysis (coffee is the commonest caffeine vehicle, so indirectness is minimal); coffee-only trials are few and small but consistent. Practical relevance: tens of milliseconds, meaningful for monotonous or safety-critical tasks, not for learning.", "key_study_ids": ["klove2025", "haskellramsay2018", "mets2012"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "vigilance", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Non-sleep-deprived volunteers during 2 h monotonous simulated highway driving", "dose_or_intake": "One cup of coffee, 80 mg caffeine, versus decaf", "duration": "single dose; 2 h post-break driving", "grade": "B", "direction": "benefit", "effect_summary": "Caffeinated coffee reduced lane weaving (SDLP) and speed variability and lowered subjective sleepiness and mental effort in both hours after the break.", "effect_estimate": "SDLP lower vs decaf: hour 1 p = 0.024, hour 2 p = 0.019; numeric difference not in abstract [mets2012]; digit vigilance accuracy higher than decaf p = 0.01 [haskellramsay2018]", "certainty_notes": "Single coffee-specific driving trial (n = 24), consistent with the strong caffeine literature; downgraded for imprecision. Decaf comparator isolates caffeine.", "key_study_ids": ["mets2012", "haskellramsay2018", "klove2025"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "vigilance", "population": "sleep_deprived", "population_detail": "Young men (mean 21.3 y) driving 200 km between 2:00 and 3:30 a.m.", "dose_or_intake": "125 mL coffee with 200 mg caffeine vs decaf (15 mg) vs 30-min nap", "duration": "single dose", "grade": "B", "direction": "benefit", "effect_summary": "Coffee restored near-daytime driving in 75% of participants versus 13% after decaf; nap was similar to coffee; subsequent sleep was not altered in this design.", "effect_estimate": "Line-crossing IRR placebo vs coffee 3.7 (95% CI 1.2-11.0) [philip2006]", "certainty_notes": "Small (n = 12) but well-designed real-road crossover; consistent with grade-A caffeine evidence under sleep loss (caffeine monograph). Downgraded for imprecision and male-only sample.", "key_study_ids": ["philip2006"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "alertness", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Overnight-abstinent habitual coffee drinkers (young 20-34 y and older 61-80 y)", "dose_or_intake": "Coffee 75-150 mg caffeine; tea 37.5-75 mg", "duration": "single dose, 30 min to 3 h", "grade": "B", "direction": "benefit", "effect_summary": "Coffee increases self-rated alertness and reduces tiredness, headache and mental fatigue; beverage strength (caffeine fourfold range) mattered little for mood, suggesting a flat dose-response above low doses and a role for non-pharmacological factors.", "effect_estimate": "Alertness vs placebo p < 0.0005; tiredness vs decaf p = 0.003 [haskellramsay2018]; no consistent caffeine dose-response for energetic arousal [quinlan2000]", "certainty_notes": "Subjective outcome; confounded by withdrawal reversal and imperfect blinding (71% correct treatment guesses). Downgraded for indirectness to non-withdrawn users and risk of bias.", "key_study_ids": ["haskellramsay2018", "quinlan2000"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "alertness", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Decaffeinated coffee versus caffeine-free placebo in young and older adults", "dose_or_intake": "Decaf (about 5 mg caffeine) or decaf enriched with chlorogenic acids", "duration": "single dose", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Decaf raised self-rated alertness versus flavoured water, without performance changes; this is the main evidence that coffee has psychoactive effects 'beyond caffeine' and may reflect expectancy (72% of participants identified decaf).", "effect_estimate": "Decaf vs placebo alertness p = 0.0048, no cognitive differences [haskellramsay2018]; green-blend decaf alertness vs placebo p = 0.02 at 120 min [camfield2013]", "certainty_notes": "Subjective outcomes, unblinding risk, industry funding in one trial; cannot separate pharmacology from expectancy and aroma.", "key_study_ids": ["haskellramsay2018", "camfield2013"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Healthy adults aged 50+ who drink coffee", "dose_or_intake": "Decaf green coffee blend 6 g; pure CGA 540 mg; high-CGA decaf equivalent to three cups", "duration": "single dose, 40-120 min", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Primary outcome (RVIP accuracy) null; isolated secondary effects at p about 0.05 for the decaf blend, none for pure CGA; pilot reported some mood effects of high-CGA decaf. Meta-analysis of coffee-CGA trials shows no cognitive effect.", "effect_estimate": "RVIP accuracy NS; N-back t = 2.45, p = 0.05 [camfield2013]; pooled d = 0.00 (95% CI -0.05 to 0.05) [johal2025]", "certainty_notes": "Small trials, multiple outcomes without correction, Nestle funding; consistent null on pooled analysis.", "key_study_ids": ["camfield2013", "cropley2012", "johal2025"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Young (20-34 y) and older (61-80 y) habitual coffee drinkers", "dose_or_intake": "Coffee with 100 mg caffeine", "duration": "single dose, 30 min", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "No effect of caffeinated or decaffeinated coffee on immediate or delayed word recall, picture/word recognition or numeric working memory.", "effect_estimate": "No significant treatment effects on memory tasks (treatment x sex interactions without significant pairwise effects) [haskellramsay2018]", "certainty_notes": "One adequately sized crossover; consistent with caffeine literature showing little memory effect. Downgraded for imprecision.", "key_study_ids": ["haskellramsay2018"]}, {"timeframe": "short_term_days", "domain": "sleep_related_cognition", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Ambulatory adults randomised daily to drink or avoid coffee (case-crossover) and studies summarised in a systematic review", "dose_or_intake": "Usual coffee intake on 'coffee days'", "duration": "14 days of daily randomisation", "grade": "A", "direction": "harm", "effect_summary": "Coffee days produced about 36 minutes less nightly sleep; caffeine prolongs sleep latency, reduces slow-wave sleep and sleep efficiency with clear dose and timing relationships; older adults are more sensitive.", "effect_estimate": "Sleep 397 vs 432 min, -36 min (95% CI 25-47) [marcus2023]; direction consistent across RCTs [clark2017]", "certainty_notes": "Randomised design and convergent caffeine-sleep meta-analytic evidence (caffeine monograph). Sleep loss is a plausible pathway by which daily coffee could erode next-day cognition, partly offsetting acute gains.", "key_study_ids": ["marcus2023", "clark2017"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "dementia_risk", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Middle-aged and older adults in prospective cohorts (US, UK, Japan, Europe)", "dose_or_intake": "1-3 cups/day caffeinated coffee versus none or less than 1 cup", "duration": "follow-up 5-43 years", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Several cohorts find lower dementia risk with moderate caffeinated coffee (J/U shape), but pooled analyses are inconsistent and decaf shows no association; Mendelian randomization does not support causality.", "effect_estimate": "HR 0.82 (0.76-0.89) top vs bottom quartile caffeinated coffee [zhang2026]; 1-2 cups RR 0.82 (0.71-0.94) [wu2017]; RR 1.01 per cup (0.98-1.05) [larsson2018]; RR 0.95 (0.87-1.02) [li2024]; HR 0.49 (0.30-0.79) highest quintile, men only significant [matsushita2021]", "certainty_notes": "Observational evidence: residual confounding (smoking, health, socioeconomic status), reverse causation (prodromal dementia reduces intake), self-reported intake, heterogeneity; MR null or adverse [zhou2018, zhangmr2021]. Cannot exceed C.", "key_study_ids": ["zhang2026", "wu2017", "larsson2018", "li2024", "zhang2021", "matsushita2021", "zhu2024"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "dementia_risk", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "UK Biobank participants aged 37-73 drinking more than 6 cups/day", "dose_or_intake": "More than 6 cups/day vs 1-2 cups/day", "duration": "prospective follow-up (UK Biobank)", "grade": "C", "direction": "harm", "effect_summary": "Very high intake was associated with higher odds of dementia and smaller total brain, grey matter and hippocampal volumes; non-drinkers and decaf drinkers also had higher odds than light drinkers.", "effect_estimate": "Dementia OR 1.53 (1.28-1.83); total brain volume beta -1.42 per cup (-1.89 to -0.94) [pham2022]; genetically predicted coffee and AD OR 1.26 (1.05-1.51) [zhangmr2021]", "certainty_notes": "Observational and MR evidence with confounding and pleiotropy concerns; a harm signal sufficient to argue against high-dose positioning.", "key_study_ids": ["pham2022", "zhangmr2021"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Middle-aged and older adults (UK Biobank, 10 European cohorts, US Nurses' Health Study)", "dose_or_intake": "Habitual coffee intake", "duration": "years (habitual exposure)", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Genetically proxied coffee intake had no effect on global cognition or memory; cross-sectionally coffee drinkers performed slightly worse; the favourable cohort association with a cognitive screen was clinically trivial.", "effect_estimate": "MR beta -0.0007 (95% CI -0.009 to 0.008) global cognition [zhou2018]; worse RT, pairs matching, TMT-B and symbol-digit with 1+ cups (P-trend < 0.0001) [cornelis2020]; TICS +0.11 (0.01-0.21) of 41 points [zhang2026]", "certainty_notes": "Large samples but indirect designs; MR is powered for very small effects and is null. Practical significance nil.", "key_study_ids": ["zhou2018", "cornelis2020", "zhang2026"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Students and young adults", "dose_or_intake": "Habitual coffee", "duration": "weeks to years", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No randomised trial of chronic coffee intake on cognition in healthy young adults or students was found; MR evidence in older adults is null.", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "Evidence gap; acute caffeine effects should not be extrapolated to long-term cognitive improvement.", "key_study_ids": ["zhou2018"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "older_adults_mci", "population_detail": "34 Japanese patients with mild cognitive impairment", "dose_or_intake": "CGA beverage 553.6 mg per bottle twice daily (about 1.1 g/day)", "duration": "12 weeks, crossover with 4-week washout", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Fewer Trail-Making B errors without change in completion time; MMSE, ADAS-cog and TMT-A unchanged.", "effect_estimate": "TMT-B errors p < 0.05; global measures NS [ochiai2019]", "certainty_notes": "Single small manufacturer-run trial (Kao), many outcomes, CGA extract beverage rather than coffee; not applicable to healthy young adults.", "key_study_ids": ["ochiai2019"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "vascular_health", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Adults in randomised coffee-feeding trials (normo- and hyperlipidaemic)", "dose_or_intake": "Unfiltered (boiled, French press) versus filtered coffee", "duration": "weeks", "grade": "A", "direction": "harm", "effect_summary": "Unfiltered coffee dose-dependently raises total and LDL cholesterol; filtered coffee has very little effect. LDL is a vascular biomarker, not a cognitive outcome.", "effect_estimate": "Dose-response for TC and LDL p < 0.01; numeric pooled values not in abstract [jee2001]", "certainty_notes": "Meta-analysis of 14 RCTs with mechanistic support (cafestol); relevant to format choice (prefer paper-filtered).", "key_study_ids": ["jee2001"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "Coffee 220 mL, 100 mg caffeine (decaf about 5 mg)", "onset": "30 min", "duration_studied": "single post-dose battery at 30 min", "domain": "reaction time, vigilance, alertness, mood; memory unchanged", "population": "habitual drinkers, young and older, overnight abstinent (n = 59 analysed)", "effect_estimate": "Faster digit-vigilance (p = 0.009) and RVIP RT (p = 0.02) vs placebo; accuracy vs decaf p = 0.01; decaf alertness vs placebo p = 0.0048", "certainty": "moderate (single trial, blinding imperfect)", "safety": "jitteriness higher in young women (regular coffee)", "study_ids": ["haskellramsay2018"]}, {"dose": "One cup, 80 mg caffeine vs decaf", "onset": "within first hour", "duration_studied": "2 h of driving after break", "domain": "driving vigilance (SDLP), sleepiness", "population": "non-sleep-deprived adults (n = 24)", "effect_estimate": "SDLP reduced p = 0.024 and 0.019", "certainty": "moderate", "safety": "none reported in abstract", "study_ids": ["mets2012"]}, {"dose": "125 mL coffee, 200 mg caffeine", "onset": "about 30 min", "duration_studied": "1.5 h night driving", "domain": "line crossings (vigilance)", "population": "young men driving 2-3:30 a.m. (n = 12)", "effect_estimate": "IRR 3.7 (1.2-11.0) decaf vs coffee", "certainty": "moderate-low (small)", "safety": "post-drive sleep not altered", "study_ids": ["philip2006"]}, {"dose": "Decaf green coffee blend 6 g; CGA 540 mg", "onset": "40-120 min", "duration_studied": "2 h", "domain": "attention (RVIP primary), working memory, mood", "population": "adults 50+ (n = 60)", "effect_estimate": "Primary null; scattered secondary effects p about 0.05; CGA alone no cognitive effect", "certainty": "low", "safety": "headache reduced vs placebo", "study_ids": ["camfield2013", "cropley2012"]}, {"dose": "Tea 37.5-75 mg, coffee 75-150 mg caffeine", "onset": "within 30 min", "duration_studied": "3 h", "domain": "mood, autonomic responses", "population": "overnight-abstinent adults (n = 17 and 15)", "effect_estimate": "Mood elevation; no tea vs coffee or dose difference", "certainty": "low", "safety": "blood pressure increases with caffeine", "study_ids": ["quinlan2000"]}, {"dose": "Usual coffee on randomised days", "onset": "same night", "duration_studied": "14 days", "domain": "sleep duration, ectopy, activity", "population": "ambulatory adults (n = 100)", "effect_estimate": "Sleep -36 min (25-47); PVC RR 1.51 (1.18-1.94)", "certainty": "high", "safety": "sleep loss, more premature ventricular contractions", "study_ids": ["marcus2023"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "up to 43 years (median 36.8)", "population": "131,821 US nurses and health professionals", "outcome": "incident dementia, subjective and objective cognition", "effect_estimate": "Caffeinated coffee Q4 vs Q1 HR 0.82 (0.76-0.89); decaf null; TICS +0.11", "evidence_quality": "observational, large, repeated intake measures", "limitations": "Residual confounding, health-professional cohorts, abstract-only access", "study_ids": ["zhang2026"]}, {"duration": "median 11.4 years", "population": "365,682 UK Biobank adults 50-74", "outcome": "dementia, stroke", "effect_estimate": "2-3 cups coffee + 2-3 cups tea: dementia HR 0.72 (0.59-0.89)", "evidence_quality": "observational", "limitations": "Single baseline intake measure, healthy volunteer bias", "study_ids": ["zhang2021"]}, {"duration": "UK Biobank follow-up", "population": "398,646 adults 37-73 (17,702 with MRI)", "outcome": "brain volume, dementia", "effect_estimate": ">6 cups vs 1-2 cups dementia OR 1.53 (1.28-1.83); smaller brain volumes per cup", "evidence_quality": "observational", "limitations": "Logistic rather than time-to-event, confounding", "study_ids": ["pham2022"]}, {"duration": "cohorts 1.3-28 years", "population": "pooled cohorts (8-38 studies)", "outcome": "dementia, AD, cognitive disorders", "effect_estimate": "Range from RR 0.73 (with case-control) to RR 1.01 per cup; coffee RR 0.95 (0.87-1.02) in largest", "evidence_quality": "meta-analyses of observational studies, low/very low GRADE", "limitations": "Heterogeneity, inclusion of case-control studies, publication bias", "study_ids": ["wu2017", "larsson2018", "zhu2024", "li2024"]}, {"duration": "lifelong genetic exposure", "population": "up to 415,530 Europeans", "outcome": "global cognition, memory, AD", "effect_estimate": "Null for cognition; AD OR 1.26 per genetically predicted cup in a separate MR", "evidence_quality": "Mendelian randomization", "limitations": "Weak/pleiotropic instruments (CYP1A1/2, AHR)", "study_ids": ["zhou2018", "zhangmr2021"]}, {"duration": "12 weeks", "population": "34 MCI patients", "outcome": "MMSE, ADAS-cog, TMT", "effect_estimate": "TMT-B errors fewer only", "evidence_quality": "small industry RCT", "limitations": "CGA extract drink, multiplicity", "study_ids": ["ochiai2019"]}, {"duration": "weeks", "population": "adults in coffee trials", "outcome": "serum total and LDL cholesterol", "effect_estimate": "Dose-dependent rise with unfiltered, minimal with filtered coffee", "evidence_quality": "meta-analysis of 14 RCTs", "limitations": "Older trials", "study_ids": ["jee2001"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Coffee is one of the best-studied foods and is generally safe for healthy non-pregnant adults at up to about 3-4 cups/day, provided total caffeine stays within EFSA limits (200 mg single dose, 400 mg/day) [efsa2015, poole2017]. Main issues for a brain-food product: (1) caffeine dose per serving is unpredictable in brewed coffee (58-259 mg across outlets, 259-564 mg for the same drink on different days) [mccusker2003]; (2) sleep loss of about 36 min on coffee days and dose/timing-dependent sleep disruption [marcus2023, clark2017], which can offset next-day cognitive benefits; (3) more premature ventricular contractions (RR 1.51) [marcus2023], anxiety/jitteriness in sensitive people [haskellramsay2018]; (4) pregnancy: associations with low birth weight and pregnancy loss [poole2017]; (5) unfiltered coffee raises LDL cholesterol [jee2001]; (6) coffee inhibits non-haem iron absorption [hurrell1999], relevant to iron-deficient young women; (7) very high intake (>6 cups/day) associated with higher dementia odds and smaller brain volumes [pham2022].", "adverse_effects": ["sleep latency prolonged, total sleep reduced (about -36 min/night) [marcus2023, clark2017]", "jitteriness, anxiety, palpitations; more premature ventricular contractions [marcus2023]", "headache and fatigue on withdrawal (habitual users)", "gastro-oesophageal reflux and gastrointestinal upset (common clinical experience; not reviewed here)", "raised LDL cholesterol with unfiltered coffee [jee2001]", "reduced non-haem iron absorption when consumed with meals [hurrell1999]"], "contraindications": ["pregnancy above 200 mg caffeine/day [efsa2015, poole2017]", "children and adolescents (caffeine limit 3 mg/kg/day; coffee products should not target them)", "panic disorder and anxiety disorders (see caffeine monograph)", "insomnia; evening use", "uncontrolled hypertension or arrhythmia (medical advice)", "iron-deficiency anaemia: avoid with iron-rich meals or supplements"], "drug_interactions": ["CYP1A2 inhibitors (e.g. fluvoxamine, ciprofloxacin) raise caffeine levels; caffeine raises clozapine and theophylline exposure (standard pharmacology; see caffeine monograph, not re-verified here)", "stimulant medications (ADHD) additive", "lithium levels can change with caffeine withdrawal (general pharmacology; not verified here)", "oral iron and levothyroxine absorption may be reduced if taken with coffee (iron: [hurrell1999]; levothyroxine not verified here)"], "upper_limits": "EFSA 2015: healthy adults single dose up to 200 mg (about 3 mg/kg) and up to 400 mg/day; pregnancy/lactation up to 200 mg/day; children 3 mg/kg/day [efsa2015]. FDA cites 400 mg/day as not generally associated with negative effects (informational). One retail coffee can exceed 200 mg [mccusker2003].", "sleep_impact": "Clear harm: about 36 min less sleep on coffee days in a randomised case-crossover study [marcus2023]; latency up, slow-wave sleep down, older adults more sensitive [clark2017]. Avoid caffeinated coffee within roughly 8-9 h of bedtime (caffeine monograph).", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Yes, observationally for decades in large cohorts [zhang2026, poole2017]; no long-term RCT of cognitive outcomes in healthy adults.", "daily_use_appropriate": "probably", "special_populations": "Pregnant and breastfeeding women (limit 200 mg/day), children and adolescents (not a target), people with anxiety/panic disorders, insomnia, arrhythmia, uncontrolled hypertension, iron-deficiency anaemia, slow CYP1A2 metabolisers, people on interacting drugs. Decaf contains up to about 16 mg caffeine per serving [mccusker2006].", "specialist_review_needed": ["cardiologist (arrhythmia/hypertension messaging)", "obstetrician (pregnancy labelling)", "sleep specialist (timing guidance)", "clinical pharmacist (CYP1A2 interactions)", "food safety specialist (acrylamide, mycotoxins in green/roasted coffee; not researched here)"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "Safe as an ordinary beverage for healthy adults within 400 mg caffeine/day, but any Brain Food coffee product must standardise and declare caffeine per serving (retail variability several-fold), cap servings at no more than about 100-150 mg, exclude children and advise pregnant women, advise against evening use, and prefer filtered/instant formats to avoid cafestol."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Coffee is an ordinary food, not a novel food (traditional consumption). No health claim for coffee or chlorogenic acids on cognition is authorised. EFSA gave a favourable opinion on caffeine for alertness and attention in 2011, but the European Parliament vetoed the authorising regulation on 7 July 2016, so caffeine alertness and concentration claims are not in the EU Register (caffeine monograph; project synthesis X4-regulatory-eu). Labelling: Regulation (EU) 1169/2011 Annex III point 4 exempts beverages based on coffee or tea named as such from the 'High caffeine content' warning required above 150 mg/L. A coffee-based drink sold under another name, or a food with added caffeine, would need the warning (X4). Any dementia-prevention wording would be an unauthorised disease risk-reduction claim. Acrylamide in roast coffee is subject to EU benchmark levels; values were not verified here.", "us": "Coffee is a conventional food. 'Coffee (Coffea spp.)' appears in 21 CFR 182.20 (essential oils, oleoresins and natural extractives generally recognised as safe), confirmed on the Cornell LII copy of the CFR (accessed 2026-10-25). Caffeine naturally present in coffee is declared through the ingredient name (project synthesis X5-regulatory-us). A conventional food's structure/function claims must derive from its nutritive value. Alertness wording is therefore safer on a dietary supplement, with the DSHEA disclaimer, than on a beverage, and either way FTC substantiation applies. Dementia or Alzheimer risk claims would be unapproved drug claims. No FDA health claim or qualified health claim for coffee and cognition exists (X5).", "morocco": "Coffee is an ordinary food under Law 28-07 (ONSSA). Labels must comply with Decree 2-12-389, including Arabic labelling (project synthesis X6-regulatory-morocco). A ready-to-drink coffee with added stimulants or nutrients and 14.5-32 mg caffeine/100 mL could fall under the energy-drink definition of Decree 2-19-13, which requires warnings; this classification is uncertain and must be confirmed with ONSSA. No Moroccan rules on cognitive claims for coffee were found. Status: uncertain.", "permitted_claims_examples": [], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "Roasted coffee beans and brews: no novel food issue. Coffee by-products (coffee cherry/cascara, coffee leaves, green coffee extracts at high CGA) may need novel food or traditional-food notification checks in the EU; not researched here. US: green coffee extract supplements rely on dietary-ingredient status; not verified.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Excellent as a beverage and familiar in all three markets; the realistic Brain Food role is a standardised, moderate-caffeine coffee (for example 80-100 mg per serving, filtered or instant), clearly labelled and positioned for daytime alertness, not a 'nootropic coffee'.", "formats": ["paper-filtered ground coffee", "instant coffee sachets with declared caffeine", "ready-to-drink coffee (low sugar)", "half-caf blends for lower doses", "decaf for evening (low but not zero caffeine)"], "stability": "Roasted coffee loses aroma within weeks after opening; chlorogenic acids decline with darker roasting; caffeine is heat-stable. RTD products need control of microbial stability and acrylamide/furan (not researched here).", "taste": "Bitterness and acidity are intrinsic. Many consumers add sugar and milk, and added sugar undermines the health positioning. Morocco, the EU and the US all have established coffee cultures.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Caffeine from coffee is essentially fully absorbed, with peak plasma levels within about 30-60 min. Milk and food delay but do not reduce absorption (general pharmacology). CGA bioavailability is partial and mostly colonic. Coffee polyphenols inhibit iron absorption at the same meal [hurrell1999].", "dose_feasibility": "Yes for caffeine. The doses studied (80-200 mg) fit one serving [mets2012, haskellramsay2018, philip2006], and a dose of about 75-100 mg already gives most of the acute benefit. CGA doses used in the MCI trial (about 1.1 g/day) [ochiai2019] exceed what typical servings provide, and the benefit is unproven anyway. Batch caffeine analysis is required because natural content varies widely [mccusker2003].", "cost_availability": "Commodity ingredient widely available in Morocco, the EU and the US. Specialty and standardised products cost more.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 6, "effect_magnitude": 4, "safety": 7, "reproducibility": 6, "food_suitability": 9, "cost_access": 9, "rationale": "evidence_quality 6: acute reaction time and attention is grade A (largely via the caffeine meta-analysis), and vigilance and alertness are grade B. Everything coffee adds beyond caffeine, and all chronic cognitive outcomes, are grade C or D, with null Mendelian randomization. effect_magnitude 4: acute effects are small (g about 0.3, tens of ms); they are larger only in sleepy or night-time conditions; there is no memory benefit and no demonstrated long-term cognitive effect. safety 7: a familiar food with well-defined caffeine limits, but it causes sleep loss, ectopy, a pregnancy signal and LDL elevation with unfiltered brews, has variable caffeine and inhibits iron absorption. reproducibility 6: caffeine effects replicate widely; claims for non-caffeine constituents and dementia associations replicate poorly. food_suitability 9: an ideal, culturally accepted vehicle; only caffeine standardisation and bitterness and sugar habits limit it. cost_access 9: a cheap commodity everywhere."}, "research_gaps": ["Preregistered, adequately powered trials of coffee versus caffeine-matched water or capsules versus decaf in non-withdrawn healthy young adults, to isolate effects beyond caffeine and expectancy", "Blinding methods for coffee trials (taste-masked decaf) and reporting of standardized effect sizes", "Long-term RCTs (6-24 months) of moderate coffee versus decaf on cognition in midlife adults", "Effects of habitual coffee on sleep-mediated next-day learning in students", "Caffeine and chlorogenic acid content of coffees sold in Morocco (traditional and instant), and typical sugar additions", "Mendelian randomization with better instruments, and in non-European populations"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 6.4, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": "A", "best_grade_chronic": null, "best_grade_young_adults": "A", "best_grade_sleep_stress": "B", "best_grade_surrogate": null, "best_grade_overall": "A", "benefit_domains": {"reaction_time": "A", "vigilance": "B", "alertness": "B"}, "n_outcome_rows": 14, "n_studies": 28, "n_verified": 28, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 10, "n_systematic_reviews": 5, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["benefit", "harm", "inconsistent", "insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "energy-drinks-and-stimulant-beverages", "name": "Energy drinks and mixed-stimulant beverages", "category": "F", "category_name": "Caffeine and natural stimulants", "aliases": ["energy drinks", "functional energy drinks (FEDs)", "energy shots", "caffeinated energy drinks (CEDs)", "boissons energisantes", "Red Bull, Monster, 5-hour Energy (brands, for identification only)", "AmED (alcohol mixed with energy drinks)"], "food_sources": ["Not a food source; a manufactured beverage category typically delivering caffeine (often 80 mg per 250 ml; up to 300+ mg in large cans or shots), sugar (about 27 g per 250 ml in regular Red Bull per manufacturer FAQ) or sweeteners, taurine (~1000 mg per 250 ml), glucuronolactone, B vitamins, and sometimes guarana, ginseng, L-carnitine or inositol"], "active_constituents": ["caffeine (the only ingredient with consistent cognitive evidence)", "sugars/glucose (weak, inconsistent acute evidence; metabolic cost)", "taurine (no added cognitive benefit in factorial trials)", "glucuronolactone (no evidence)", "B vitamins (no acute effect in replete people)", "guarana (caffeine source; see yerba-mate-and-guarana.json)", "ginseng (flavouring levels; see ginseng monograph if present)"], "one_line_verdict": "Energy drinks sharpen reaction time and alertness for an hour or two only because of their caffeine, the extra ingredients (taurine, glucuronolactone, B vitamins, 'energy blends') add nothing demonstrable, and the high caffeine doses, sugar, blood-pressure and QTc effects, sleep loss, alcohol mixing and youth harms make them a poor 'brain food' compared with a measured cup of coffee or tea.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. A caffeinated energy drink improves reaction time, sustained attention and subjective alertness for one to a few hours, as caffeine does. Every attempt to dissect the drink points to caffeine as the active ingredient. A systematic evaluation of 32 studies found an 'overwhelming lack of evidence' for taurine, glucuronolactone, B vitamins, ginseng or other 'energy blend' components, with weak evidence only for glucose and guarana [mclellan2012]. In factorial trials caffeine produced the cognitive gains, while taurine had mixed or caffeine-blunting effects and glucose alone slowed responses [giles2012, peacock2013]. A decaffeinated version of a popular energy shot did nothing for mood, energy or cognition in 223 adults [garciaalvarez2020]. Whole-drink trials show attention and RT benefits [alford2001, kennedy2004, schwager2024] of the size expected from caffeine (attention g about 0.27-0.28 [klove2025]), not larger. Memory findings are inconsistent: Red Bull improved memory composites more than both placebo and its sugar-free twin [wesnes2017]; a whole glucose-caffeine-herb drink helped where no fraction did [scholey2004]; and a 5-hour Energy shot had no effect on college students who nonetheless believed it worked [buckenmeyer2015]. In sleepy drivers, 80 mg-caffeine drinks reduce lane drifting and sleepiness for about 90 min [reyner2002, mets2011 (Red Bull-funded)]. A high-sugar, low-caffeine drink worsened vigilance [anderson2006]. In whom, and how large. For a rested student or knowledge worker, an energy drink is an expensive, sugary, variably dosed caffeine delivery system, and its benefit equals that of a coffee with the same caffeine. Many products contain 160-300 mg per can, so the benefit comes with BP elevation (pooled SBP rise with a 95% CI of 2.6-6.4 mmHg [gualberto2024]), QTc prolongation of about 6-8 ms at high volumes [shah2019, basrai2019], and sleep loss (insomnia OR 5.02 [nadeem2021]; caffeine shortens sleep by about 45 min [gardiner2023]). In adolescents, one weight-adjusted drink raises SBP by about 5 mmHg [oberhoffer2022]. Frequent use is associated with poorer sleep, well-being and school outcomes [khouja2022, smith2018], although the attainment link is explained by diet and depression. An adolescent cognitive test was null [wilhelm2013]. Mixing with alcohol does not offset alcohol impairment but makes drinkers feel more stimulated [marczinski2012]; the reassuring reviews [benson2014, verster2018] come partly from industry-linked authors. How reproducible. Caffeine's effect is highly reproducible; claims for anything else are not. Most positive whole-drink trials are small, sponsor-linked and lack caffeine-matched controls. What is unknown. Head-to-head trials against equal-caffeine coffee, chronic cognitive effects (only one 28-day trial [krieger2025]), and long-term cardiac risk in susceptible people.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "There is no deficiency that energy drinks correct. Their B vitamins are irrelevant to cognition in replete people, and a decaffeinated energy drink with the full vitamin and amino-acid blend had no effect in 223 adults [garciaalvarez2020]. Any benefit is acute caffeine enhancement or reversal of fatigue and withdrawal. Factorial trials used caffeine-abstained habitual users, in whom part of the 'benefit' is withdrawal reversal [giles2012]. Sleep-restricted people benefit most [reyner2002, mets2011], a state the drinks themselves help create when taken late in the day [gardiner2023, nadeem2021].", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Marketing implies a unique formula ('gives you wings', 'energy blend', 'focus formula') and targets students, gamers and young people. The evidence supports only: 'contains caffeine, which can temporarily increase alertness and reaction speed.' Claims attributing benefit to taurine, glucuronolactone, B vitamins or proprietary blends are unsupported [mclellan2012, garciaalvarez2020, giles2012]. The one network meta-analysis favouring caffeine-taurine synergy [deng2025] conflicts with direct factorial trials. Several positive whole-drink trials were sponsor-funded [mets2011] or product-specific without caffeine-matched comparators [wesnes2017, schwager2024]. In the EU no caffeine alertness claim is authorised (see caffeine.json and X1-combinations.json). Energy drinks may carry only vitamin 'tiredness and fatigue' claims, which rest on vitamin-deficiency physiology and say nothing about the drink's stimulant effect. Students perceive benefits that tests do not detect [buckenmeyer2015]. A Brain Food brand should not enter this category, and it should not imitate its 'energy blend' language.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Caffeine (adenosine A1/A2A antagonism) drives the cognitive and most cardiovascular effects. Sugar gives a transient glycaemic rise, with possible later rebound sleepiness in sleep-restricted people. Taurine (a GABA-A/glycine-receptor modulator in animals) and glucuronolactone have no demonstrated human cognitive mechanism at drink doses. The combination produced larger BP and QTc changes than caffeine alone in one independent trial, a mechanism not yet understood.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Energy drink (caffeine 80-300 mg, sugar or sweetener, taurine, glucuronolactone, B vitamins) -> rapid caffeine absorption from liquid", "detail": "Caffeine pharmacokinetics as in caffeine.json; volume and speed of intake drive peak exposure.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Caffeine -> adenosine receptor antagonism -> increased arousal", "detail": "Accounts for RT/attention gains; benefits track caffeine dose and disappear in decaffeinated versions [garciaalvarez2020, giles2012, klove2025].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Sugar -> transient glucose rise -> possible short-lived attention support, later rebound", "detail": "Glucose alone slowed choice RT [giles2012]; high-sugar low-caffeine drink worsened PVT lapses at ~80 min [anderson2006]; see glucose-and-carbohydrates.json.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Taurine / glucuronolactone -> proposed neuromodulation and detoxification", "detail": "No added human cognitive benefit; taurine may blunt caffeine [peacock2013]; pooled NMA claim conflicts [deng2025].", "evidence_level": "speculative"}, {"step": "Sympathetic activation -> BP, cardiac output and QTc changes", "detail": "SBP rise (95% CI 2.6-6.4 mmHg) [gualberto2024]; QTc +6-8 ms at high volume [shah2019, basrai2019]; effect not fully reproduced by caffeine alone [basrai2019].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Caffeine late in day -> reduced sleep duration and quality -> next-day fatigue", "detail": "TST -45 min [gardiner2023]; insomnia OR 5.02 [nadeem2021].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Measured human cognitive outcome", "detail": "Acute RT/attention benefit equal to caffeine; inconsistent memory; no chronic cognitive benefit shown [krieger2025].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}], "pathways": ["adenosine receptor antagonism (caffeine)", "glycaemic response (sugar)", "sympathetic/cardiovascular activation", "sleep disruption", "taurine GABAergic modulation (speculative)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Rested healthy adults, mostly 18-35, given a caffeinated energy drink (typically 75-200 mg caffeine) vs placebo drink", "dose_or_intake": "250-500 ml; 75-200 mg caffeine", "duration": "single dose; 30 min-5 h", "grade": "B", "direction": "benefit", "effect_summary": "Whole caffeinated energy drinks improve reaction time, attention and subjective alertness versus non-caffeinated placebo drinks in most trials (Alford, Scholey & Kennedy, Kennedy & Scholey, Schwager d 0.44-0.63, Krieger day 1). Factorial trials and reviews show the effect comes from caffeine. Its size is that expected from caffeine alone (attention g about 0.27-0.28 in the caffeine meta-analysis). A student null (5-hour Energy, n=24) shows effects are not guaranteed.", "effect_estimate": "Schwager: sustained attention d = 0.44, cognitive flexibility d = 0.63 [schwager2024]; caffeine benchmark g = 0.27-0.28 [klove2025]", "certainty_notes": "Caffeine effect itself is grade A (caffeine.json); energy-drink-specific trials are small, often industry-linked, without caffeine-matched comparators, and include nulls. Hence B. Indirectness: no evidence the drink beats an equal caffeine dose.", "key_study_ids": ["alford2001", "scholey2004", "kennedy2004", "schwager2024", "krieger2025", "buckenmeyer2015", "mclellan2012", "klove2025"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Rested healthy adults; memory composites after energy drinks", "dose_or_intake": "250 ml (80 mg caffeine +/- sugar)", "duration": "single dose; 30-90 min", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Regular Red Bull improved memory composites (medium effect) relative to both placebo and Sugarfree with the same caffeine (Wesnes, n=24). The whole glucose-caffeine drink improved 'secondary memory' while no fraction did (Scholey, n=20). Medical-student N-back improved with only one of three drinks (Garcia, about 20 per arm). The 5-hour Energy shot had no memory effect (n=24), and a decaffeinated energy drink had no effect in 223 adults.", "effect_estimate": "'medium effect size' [wesnes2017]; nulls in [buckenmeyer2015, garciaalvarez2020]", "certainty_notes": "Small trials, inconsistent and product-specific; possible sugar effect contradicts factorial data; no replication.", "key_study_ids": ["wesnes2017", "scholey2004", "garcia2017", "buckenmeyer2015", "garciaalvarez2020"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "alertness", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Contribution of NON-caffeine ingredients (taurine, glucuronolactone, B vitamins, amino acids, herbal 'energy blends', decaffeinated versions)", "dose_or_intake": "taurine 1000-2000 mg; decaffeinated energy shot; typical blends", "duration": "single dose", "grade": "E", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "The systematic evaluation found an 'overwhelming lack of evidence' for non-caffeine components. In factorial trials taurine did not add to caffeine and tended to blunt it (Peacock), or had mixed and opposite effects (Giles). A decaffeinated energy drink produced no benefit on any of many outcomes in 223 adults. A 2025 network meta-analysis claiming caffeine-taurine RT synergy rests on 6 small sport studies and conflicts with direct factorial evidence.", "effect_estimate": "Decaf energy drink: no significant effect on any outcome [garciaalvarez2020]; NMA CAF+TAU RT g = 0.75 (95% CrI 0.29-1.18) [deng2025]", "certainty_notes": "The direct, adequately powered decaf trial and the factorial trials agree; the sparse NMA lowers certainty slightly. Not supported (E).", "key_study_ids": ["mclellan2012", "giles2012", "peacock2013", "garciaalvarez2020", "deng2025"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "vigilance", "population": "sleep_deprived", "population_detail": "Sleep-restricted adults or fatigued drivers in simulators", "dose_or_intake": "250 ml; 80 mg caffeine (vs 30 mg caffeine + 42 g sugar)", "duration": "single dose; 1.5-2 h of driving/vigilance", "grade": "B", "direction": "benefit", "effect_summary": "80 mg caffeine energy drinks reduced lane drifting and sleepiness for about 90 min in sleep-restricted drivers (n=12) and reduced lane weaving in hours 3-4 of a 4-h drive (n=24, Red Bull-funded). A high-sugar drink with only 30 mg caffeine did not help and worsened PVT lapses 80 min later (n=10).", "effect_estimate": "SDLP hour 4: 23.76 vs 26.82 cm [mets2011]", "certainty_notes": "Consistent with strong caffeine/sleep-loss literature; energy-drink trials small and one sponsor-funded; benefit tracks caffeine dose, and sugar without caffeine can hurt.", "key_study_ids": ["reyner2002", "mets2011", "anderson2006"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "processing_speed", "population": "students", "population_detail": "University and medical students tested after energy drinks", "dose_or_intake": "single commercial can or shot", "duration": "single dose", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "College students given a 5-hour Energy shot showed no cognitive improvement over 5 h despite 90% believing it worked. Medical students showed N-back improvement with only one of three drinks, alongside BP, cortisol and QTc changes. About 43% of undergraduates use energy drinks, mainly for studying and staying awake.", "effect_estimate": "no significant effects [buckenmeyer2015]; prevalence 42.9% [protano2023]", "certainty_notes": "Two small trials with inconsistent results; perception of benefit exceeds measured benefit.", "key_study_ids": ["buckenmeyer2015", "garcia2017", "protano2023"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "vascular_health", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults; acute haemodynamic and ECG effects", "dose_or_intake": "250-1000 ml", "duration": "30-240 min", "grade": "B", "direction": "harm", "effect_summary": "Meta-analysis of 17 RCTs: energy drinks raise SBP (largest at 60-80 min; 95% CI 2.60-6.42 mmHg), DBP and cardiac output, with no significant pooled HR or QTc change. High-volume trials (946-1000 ml) prolong QTc by about 6-8 ms versus placebo and raise BP; the energy drink raised BP and QTc more than caffeine alone at the same dose (Basrai).", "effect_estimate": "SBP 95% CI 2.60-6.42 mmHg (GRADE low) [gualberto2024]; QTcB +17.9/+19.6 vs +11.9 ms [shah2019]; SBP +3.8 mmHg, QTc +7.5 ms [basrai2019]", "certainty_notes": "Consistent direction; surrogate outcomes; clinically minor for healthy people but relevant for long-QT, hypertension and QT-prolonging drugs.", "key_study_ids": ["gualberto2024", "shah2019", "basrai2019", "lasheras2021"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "vascular_health", "population": "children_adolescents", "population_detail": "Healthy children and teenagers", "dose_or_intake": "3 mg caffeine/kg as energy drink", "duration": "single dose; 4 h", "grade": "C", "direction": "harm", "effect_summary": "One randomised crossover (n=27): SBP up to 5.2 mmHg and DBP 3.3 mmHg higher than a sugar-matched placebo, with more readings in hypertensive ranges.", "effect_estimate": "SBP +5.23 mmHg, DBP +3.29 mmHg [oberhoffer2022]", "certainty_notes": "Single small single-blind trial; consistent with adult data; ethics limit further trials.", "key_study_ids": ["oberhoffer2022", "seifert2011"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "other", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Alcohol mixed with energy drinks (AmED): objective impairment and subjective intoxication", "dose_or_intake": "alcohol 0.65 g/kg + energy drink 3.57 ml/kg; 46-383 mg caffeine in reviewed studies", "duration": "single occasion", "grade": "C", "direction": "harm", "effect_summary": "The energy drink did not reduce alcohol-induced impairment of information processing or motor coordination, but it reduced perceived fatigue and increased stimulation (n=18). A meta-analysis found no masking of subjective intoxication, and an industry-adjacent review found no extra drinking on AmED occasions. Consumers who mix nonetheless drink more overall.", "effect_estimate": "masking p=0.404 [benson2014]; impairment unchanged [marczinski2012]", "certainty_notes": "Conflicting conclusions; small lab studies; author industry ties in the reassuring reviews. Agreement: caffeine does NOT offset alcohol impairment.", "key_study_ids": ["marczinski2012", "benson2014", "verster2018"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "sleep_related_cognition", "population": "children_adolescents", "population_detail": "Children, adolescents and students; habitual consumption", "dose_or_intake": "habitual (frequent = 5+ days/week)", "duration": "habitual", "grade": "C", "direction": "harm", "effect_summary": "Across 32 studies, consumption increased odds of insomnia (OR 5.02) and jitteriness. Overviews of reviews associate frequent youth consumption with sleep problems, headaches, lower well-being, alcohol and tobacco use, and poorer school outcomes. Caffeine meta-analysis: about 45 min less sleep when taken too late.", "effect_estimate": "Insomnia OR 5.02 (95% CI 1.72-14.63) [nadeem2021]; TST -45 min [gardiner2023]", "certainty_notes": "Mostly cross-sectional with confounding (graded weak by Khouja); the sleep mechanism is well established for caffeine, supporting causality for sleep.", "key_study_ids": ["nadeem2021", "khouja2022", "ajibo2024", "gardiner2023", "seifert2011"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "learning", "population": "children_adolescents", "population_detail": "Secondary-school students; academic attainment", "dose_or_intake": "habitual", "duration": "6-month longitudinal", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Energy drink use is associated with lower attainment, but the association disappears after adjusting for junk food, breakfast skipping and depression. There is no evidence of academic benefit, and an acute adolescent test showed no cognitive effect.", "effect_estimate": "association removed after diet adjustment [smith2018]", "certainty_notes": "Observational; non-randomised acute test.", "key_study_ids": ["smith2018", "wilhelm2013", "khouja2022"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Repeated daily energy-drink use in rested adults", "dose_or_intake": "200 mg caffeine/day energy drink", "duration": "28 days", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "One 28-day trial (n=60) found higher perceived focus and energy on day 28 and partial acute RT effects, but a trend toward worse Trail Making A versus placebo. There is no evidence of cumulative cognitive benefit beyond the daily acute caffeine effect, and tolerance is expected.", "effect_estimate": "not reported as standardised effects [krieger2025]", "certainty_notes": "Single trial with a company-employed author; subjective outcomes.", "key_study_ids": ["krieger2025"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "250 ml (80 mg caffeine, ~27 g sugar, 1 g taurine)", "onset": "30 min", "duration_studied": "90 min", "domain": "attention / memory composites", "population": "healthy young adults (n=24)", "effect_estimate": "Medium-size memory composite gain for sugared version vs placebo and vs sugar-free version", "certainty": "C", "safety": "not reported in abstract", "study_ids": ["wesnes2017"]}, {"dose": "75 mg caffeine + 37.5 g glucose (whole drink) vs fractions", "onset": "30 min", "duration_studied": "single post-dose test", "domain": "secondary memory, speed of attention", "population": "fasted, caffeine-deprived adults (n=20)", "effect_estimate": "Whole drink only improved; fractions null", "certainty": "C", "safety": "HR and glucose monitored; no issues reported", "study_ids": ["scholey2004"]}, {"dose": "Caffeine 200 mg / taurine 2000 mg / glucose 50 g (factorial)", "onset": "30-60 min", "duration_studied": "~1.5 h", "domain": "executive control, WM, RT, mood", "population": "caffeine-withdrawn habitual users (n=48)", "effect_estimate": "Caffeine drives benefits; taurine and glucose mixed or negative", "certainty": "B for attribution to caffeine", "safety": "No HR or cortisol effects", "study_ids": ["giles2012", "peacock2013"]}, {"dose": "Decaffeinated energy shot", "onset": "0.5 h", "duration_studied": "5 h", "domain": "mood, N-back, RT, Flanker, RVIP", "population": "adults 18-70 (n=223)", "effect_estimate": "No effect on any outcome", "certainty": "E (null, well powered)", "safety": "not reported in abstract", "study_ids": ["garciaalvarez2020"]}, {"dose": "250 ml, 80 mg caffeine", "onset": "within 30 min", "duration_studied": "2 h driving", "domain": "driving lapses, lane weaving, sleepiness", "population": "sleep-restricted or fatigued drivers (n=12, 24)", "effect_estimate": "SDLP 23.76 vs 26.82 cm (hour 4); fewer incidents for 90 min", "certainty": "B", "safety": "not reported", "study_ids": ["reyner2002", "mets2011"]}, {"dose": "946-1000 ml (high volume)", "onset": "1 h", "duration_studied": "4 h", "domain": "QTc, BP (safety)", "population": "healthy young adults (n=34, 38)", "effect_estimate": "QTc +6-8 ms vs placebo; SBP +3.8 mmHg", "certainty": "B (harm surrogate)", "safety": "11% symptoms after energy drink vs 0-3% control", "study_ids": ["shah2019", "basrai2019"]}, {"dose": "3 mg caffeine/kg as energy drink", "onset": "30 min", "duration_studied": "4 h", "domain": "blood pressure (safety)", "population": "children and teenagers (n=27)", "effect_estimate": "SBP +5.2, DBP +3.3 mmHg", "certainty": "C (harm)", "safety": "More readings in hypertensive range", "study_ids": ["oberhoffer2022"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "28 days", "population": "healthy adults (n=60)", "outcome": "reaction, serial sevens, Trail Making, perceived focus", "effect_estimate": "Perceived focus/energy up; objective tests mixed (trend to worse TMT-A)", "evidence_quality": "very low (single trial)", "limitations": "No caffeine comparator; company-employed author", "study_ids": ["krieger2025"]}, {"duration": "habitual", "population": "children, adolescents, adults (32 studies, 96,549 people)", "outcome": "adverse events", "effect_estimate": "Insomnia OR 5.02 (1.72-14.63); jitteriness OR 3.52", "evidence_quality": "low (observational)", "limitations": "Self-report; confounding", "study_ids": ["nadeem2021"]}, {"duration": "habitual / 6 months", "population": "UK children and secondary-school students", "outcome": "well-being, sleep, school outcomes", "effect_estimate": "Frequent use associated with poorer well-being and attainment; attainment link explained by diet and depression", "evidence_quality": "low (GRADE weak)", "limitations": "Cross-sectional; reverse causation", "study_ids": ["khouja2022", "smith2018"]}], "safety": {"summary": "In healthy adults a single 250 ml can (about 80 mg caffeine) is within EFSA's 200 mg single-dose guidance [efsa2015]. Large cans, shots and multiple servings readily exceed it. Acute effects include raised BP and cardiac output [gualberto2024] and QTc prolongation at high volume [shah2019, basrai2019]. Insomnia and jitteriness are common [nadeem2021], and case reports describe cardiac arrest, arrhythmia and seizures after excessive intake, often with exercise or alcohol [berger2009, seifert2011]. US emergency department visits involving energy drinks doubled from 10,068 (2007) to 20,783 (2011) (SAMHSA DAWN report; web source). Paediatric bodies conclude that energy drinks have no place in children's diets [aap2011]. A regular can carries about 27 g sugar.", "adverse_effects": ["insomnia and reduced sleep (OR 5.02) [nadeem2021, gardiner2023]", "jitteriness, anxiety, GI upset [nadeem2021]", "BP and cardiac-output rise [gualberto2024, oberhoffer2022]", "QTc prolongation with high volumes [shah2019, basrai2019, lasheras2021]", "arrhythmia, seizures, cardiac arrest in case reports with excess intake [berger2009, seifert2011]", "dental erosion, insulin resistance and weight gain from sugar (associations) [ajibo2024, basrai2019]", "reduced perception of alcohol-related fatigue without reduced impairment [marczinski2012]"], "contraindications": ["children and adolescents under 18 (AAP; Moroccan mandatory label; national bans in several EU countries)", "pregnancy and breastfeeding (caffeine limit 200 mg/day)", "long-QT syndrome, arrhythmias, structural heart disease, uncontrolled hypertension", "seizure disorders", "anxiety and panic disorders, bipolar disorder, insomnia", "diabetes (sugared versions)", "concurrent alcohol use"], "drug_interactions": ["alcohol: stimulant effects reduce perceived intoxication-related fatigue but not impairment [marczinski2012]", "QT-prolonging drugs (e.g. some antiarrhythmics, antipsychotics, macrolides) given QTc effects [shah2019]", "stimulant medications (ADHD drugs), sympathomimetics, MAO inhibitors: additive stimulation", "CYP1A2 inhibitors (fluvoxamine, ciprofloxacin) raise caffeine exposure (see caffeine.json)", "antiepileptics: case of breakthrough seizure (not verified beyond search title)"], "upper_limits": "EFSA: up to 200 mg caffeine single dose and 400 mg/day for healthy non-pregnant adults; 200 mg/day in pregnancy/lactation; 3 mg/kg/day for children and adolescents [efsa2015]. FDA cites 400 mg/day for healthy adults and energy drinks of 54-328 mg per 16 fl oz (FDA consumer page, via X1-combinations.json). Morocco requires 'ne pas depasser 500 ml/jour' on labels and limits caffeine to 14.5-32 mg/100 ml (Decree 2-19-13).", "sleep_impact": "Substantial. Caffeine reduces total sleep by about 45 min and deep sleep by about 11 min. A 200+ mg dose should be taken at least about 13 h before bed [gardiner2023]. Insomnia is the most common reported adverse effect [nadeem2021]. Students using energy drinks to stay awake for exams are trading next-day cognition for same-night alertness.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Only one 28-day RCT [krieger2025]; long-term data are observational surveys in youth [khouja2022, ajibo2024].", "daily_use_appropriate": "no", "special_populations": "Children and adolescents: not appropriate (AAP; Moroccan label 'ne convient pas ... aux jeunes de moins de 18 ans'; sales bans to minors in Lithuania (2014), Latvia (2016), Poland (2024), and England from April 2027 for >150 mg/l drinks, per press and think-tank sources). Pregnancy/lactation: avoid. Cardiac, psychiatric, seizure, diabetic and medication-using groups: avoid. Athletes: avoid during intense exercise (Moroccan label).", "specialist_review_needed": ["cardiologist / electrophysiologist (QTc, arrhythmia)", "paediatrician (youth exposure and marketing)", "psychiatrist (anxiety, bipolar, substance-use co-consumption)", "regulatory/food-law specialist for caffeine limits and labelling per market"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "A single standard can is within EFSA caffeine limits for healthy adults, but the category commonly exceeds them per serving and carries sugar, BP/QTc effects, alcohol co-use and youth harms. It fails for any product positioned at or likely to reach under-18s or students for exam all-nighters."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Regulation (EU) 1169/2011 Annex III: beverages (other than tea/coffee-based) with >150 mg/l caffeine must carry 'High caffeine content. Not recommended for children or pregnant or breast-feeding women' plus mg/100 ml, in the same field of vision as the name (wording via Finnish Food Authority and Irish guidance; Official Journal text not opened). EFSA 2015 set caffeine safety levels [efsa2015], and EFSA's 2013 consumption report found about 68% of adolescents consumed energy drinks in the past year [efsa2013]. No caffeine alertness or attention claim is authorised (EFSA's favourable 2011 opinion was not adopted; Regulation (EU) 2016/1411 refused a 40 mg claim; see X1-combinations.json). No claim exists for taurine or glucuronolactone. National sales bans to minors: Lithuania 2014, Latvia 2016, Poland 1 Jan 2024 (defined as >150 mg/l caffeine or added taurine); England has announced an under-16 ban for >150 mg/l drinks from April 2027 (press reports, July 2026; secondary legislation pending).", "us": "Energy drinks are marketed either as conventional beverages (caffeine as a food ingredient) or as dietary supplements under DSHEA (with supplement facts and adverse-event reporting duties); FDA's consumer guidance cites 400 mg/day and 54-328 mg caffeine per 16 fl oz energy drink (X1-combinations.json source r69). There is no federal age restriction. SAMHSA's DAWN report documented energy-drink-related ED visits doubling from 10,068 (2007) to 20,783 (2011) (https://www.samhsa.gov/data/sites/default/files/DAWN126/DAWN126/sr126-energy-drinks-use.htm, accessed 2026-10-25). FTC substantiation applies to 'focus' or 'mental performance' claims, and the decaf null trial [garciaalvarez2020] suggests such claims for non-caffeine blends are unsubstantiated. The details of FDA's beverage versus liquid-supplement guidance were not verified in this session.", "morocco": "Decree No. 2-19-13 of 23 May 2019 on the quality and safety of certain beverages (Bulletin officiel No. 6784, 6 June 2019, p. 1037; text read from adala.justice.gov.ma, accessed 2026-10-25). Article 1(6) defines 'boisson energisante' as containing caffeine plus optionally taurine, glucuronolactone, guarana, ginseng etc., with caffeine required between 14.5 and 32 mg/100 ml. Article 8(3) requires grouped label warnings: not suitable for pregnant or breastfeeding women, children and under-18s; must not be mixed with alcohol; not suitable for diabetics or caffeine-sensitive people; 'ne pas depasser 500 ml/jour'; do not consume during intense physical exercise; may disturb sleep in excess; plus a cardiac/hypertension/schizophrenia/insomnia warning if ginseng is present. Article 9 requires sale only on dedicated shelves separate from other drinks and foods, with warnings visible. Import registration with ONSSA applies under Law 28-07 (procedural details not verified).", "permitted_claims_examples": [], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "Standard ingredients (caffeine, taurine, glucuronolactone, B vitamins) are not novel foods in the EU at energy-drink levels (EFSA 2009 taurine/glucuronolactone opinion, DOI 10.2903/j.efsa.2009.935, per X1-combinations.json; content not opened here). Novel botanicals or nootropics added to 'smart' energy drinks may trigger EU novel-food or US NDI requirements and must be checked case by case.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Technically easy (a carbonated drink), but the category conflicts with an evidence-based brain-health brand: its only active ingredient is caffeine, its typical sugar load is high, and its regulatory and reputational baggage is heavy.", "formats": ["canned carbonated drinks (250-500 ml)", "energy shots (50-60 ml)", "powders/sticks", "sugar-free variants"], "stability": "Caffeine and taurine are stable; B vitamins degrade over shelf life; carbonation and acidity contribute to dental erosion.", "taste": "Sweet-sour flavour masks caffeine bitterness; sugar-free versions rely on intense sweeteners.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Liquid delivery gives rapid caffeine absorption; fast drinking of large volumes produces high peaks (relevant to QTc effects at 946-1000 ml).", "dose_feasibility": "The effective caffeine dose (40-200 mg) easily fits one serving; Morocco requires 14.5-32 mg/100 ml, i.e. 36-80 mg per 250 ml can. A brand could deliver the same caffeine more transparently as coffee, tea or a labelled low-dose caffeine product (see caffeine.json and X1-combinations.json).", "cost_availability": "Widely available and inexpensive per can, but more costly per mg caffeine than brewed coffee or tea.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 5, "effect_magnitude": 3, "safety": 3, "reproducibility": 5, "food_suitability": 4, "cost_access": 6, "rationale": "Evidence quality 5: the acute caffeine-driven benefit is grade B for the drinks (A for caffeine itself), but drink-specific trials are small, often sponsor-linked and lack caffeine-matched controls; non-caffeine ingredients are grade E. Effect magnitude 3: small acute attention/RT effects (g about 0.3, equal to caffeine) and no chronic benefit. Safety 3: BP/QTc effects, sleep loss, sugar, alcohol mixing, youth harms and serious case reports. Reproducibility 5: caffeine effects replicate, ingredient claims do not. Food suitability 4: easy to make but conflicts with brand values and faces strict labelling and age rules. Cost/access 6: cheap and ubiquitous, but dearer per mg caffeine than coffee or tea."}, "research_gaps": ["Head-to-head RCTs of energy drinks versus caffeine-matched coffee/water in students during real exam periods", "Independent (non-industry) replication of memory effects attributed to sugared versus sugar-free versions", "Chronic (>=4 weeks) trials measuring cognition, sleep (actigraphy/PSG) and BP together", "Prospective cohorts of adolescent consumption with sleep, mental health and academic outcomes adjusted for diet", "Cardiac safety in people with occult long-QT or channelopathies at realistic consumption patterns", "Evaluation of Morocco's 2019 labelling and shelf-separation rules on youth consumption"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 4.2, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": "B", "best_grade_chronic": null, "best_grade_young_adults": "B", "best_grade_sleep_stress": "B", "best_grade_surrogate": null, "best_grade_overall": "B", "benefit_domains": {"sustained_attention": "B", "vigilance": "B"}, "n_outcome_rows": 11, "n_studies": 37, "n_verified": 37, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 13, "n_systematic_reviews": 10, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["benefit", "harm", "inconsistent", "insufficient", "no_benefit"]}}, {"id": "tea-and-green-tea", "name": "Tea, green tea and matcha", "category": "F", "category_name": "Caffeine and natural stimulants", "aliases": ["tea", "Camellia sinensis", "Thea sinensis", "green tea", "black tea", "oolong tea", "matcha", "sencha", "gunpowder green tea", "Moroccan mint tea (atay)", "green tea extract", "EGCG"], "food_sources": ["brewed green tea (including Chinese gunpowder tea used for Moroccan mint tea)", "brewed black and oolong tea", "matcha (powdered whole green-tea leaf, about 2-4 g per serving)", "ready-to-drink teas", "green tea extracts in supplements (concentrated catechins/EGCG; regulated separately)"], "active_constituents": ["caffeine (commercial leaf about 16-19 mg/g; roughly 20-60 mg per brewed cup, about 70 mg per 2.7 g matcha)", "L-theanine (about 5-6.6 mg/g leaf; about 10-15 mg per cup, about 50 mg per 2.7 g matcha)", "catechins: epigallocatechin gallate (EGCG), EGC, ECG, EC (green tea, matcha)", "theaflavins and thearubigins (black tea)", "polyphenols/tannins that bind non-haem iron", "fluoride and trace elements (variable)"], "one_line_verdict": "A cup of tea gives a modest, short-lived lift in alertness and attention-switching, mostly from its caffeine plus some L-theanine, but matcha and green-tea trials show no reliable longer-term cognitive benefit, the dementia associations are observational, and tea drunk with meals sharply cuts iron absorption, which matters for iron-deficient Moroccan women and students.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. In double-blind crossover trials, black tea made from ordinary tea bags improved attention-switching accuracy and self-rated alertness versus a taste-matched placebo in two studies, with better intersensory attention in one [debruin2011]. Tea also prevented the steady decline in alertness and information processing seen over a day with water [hindmarch1998]. Reviews agree that tea consistently improves attention and alertness, while effects on relaxation are less consistent [einother2013, mancini2017]. Most of these trials were run or funded by Unilever or Lipton. The 2025 meta-analysis (Lipton-funded PhD) quantified theanine plus caffeine effects at roughly SMD 0.2-0.35 for vigilance and switching accuracy. It notes that trials of actual tea beverages are scarce [payne2025]. Matcha (4 g) slightly improved basic attention and psychomotor speed in 23 adults, with no mood change, in a single-blind study [dietz2017]. Beyond caffeine. Evidence for non-caffeine effects of the beverage is limited. In a factorial study, tea versus water improved critical flicker fusion within 10 min, but tea and coffee were otherwise similar [hindmarch1998]. Pure EGCG (300 mg) altered EEG and self-rated calmness [scholey2012]. Green-tea-extract drinks changed prefrontal activation and parieto-frontal connectivity in 12-person fMRI studies [borgwardt2012, schmidt2014]. These are biomarkers, not cognitive outcomes. Ordinary tea supplies about 10-15 mg theanine per cup [boros2016], well below the 50-250 mg used in most theanine trials. Chronic. All matcha RCTs come from the manufacturer Ito En or use its product. Two weeks of 2 g/day maintained Stroop speed after mild stress in 42 young adults [baba2021young]. Effects in middle-aged and older adults were attributed largely to caffeine [baba2021older]. A 12-week trial found a MoCA gain only in a female subgroup [sakurai2020]. A 12-month trial in 99 adults with subjective decline or MCI missed both primary outcomes [uchida2024]. A 12-month green-tea-powder trial in nursing-home residents was null (MMSE -0.61, 95% CI -2.97 to 1.74) [ide2016]. Matcha did not change EEG-measured sleep [baba2024]. Long term. Cohorts associate tea with lower dementia risk: HR 0.84 in UK Biobank [hu2022], RR 0.84 in a 38-cohort meta-analysis [li2024], 1-2 cups/day optimal in US cohorts [zhang2026], and lower odds of cognitive impairment in Singapore [ng2008]. UK Biobank tea drinkers nonetheless performed worse on cognitive tests, which suggests confounding [cornelis2020]. Certainty is low. Costs. Moroccan green tea taken with a meal cut iron absorption from fortified flour by more than 85%, even in women with iron-deficiency anaemia [lazrak2021, hurrell1999]. Very hot tea is associated with oesophageal cancer [yi2019]. Concentrated extracts can injure the liver [oketchrabah2020, efsa2018]. Green tea interacts with nadolol [misaka2014]. Traditional metallic teapots have leached lead [bolle2011]. For a student, tea is a gentler caffeine source with a small acute attention benefit and no proven long-term cognitive gain.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Tea corrects no deficiency. Its acute benefits in rested adults are small enhancement effects driven mainly by caffeine, with possible modulation by L-theanine [debruin2011, payne2025]; in habitual users part of the effect is withdrawal reversal. Importantly, tea can worsen a deficiency: in Moroccan women, one 300 mL serving of traditional green tea with a NaFeEDTA-fortified bread meal reduced fractional iron absorption from 36.7% to 4.1% in iron-deficiency anaemia and from 16.7% to 1.4% in non-anaemic women [lazrak2021]. Because iron deficiency itself impairs cognition, a 'brain tea' consumed with meals by iron-deficient young women could plausibly do net harm; tea should be taken between meals in these groups. Chronic matcha trials in older adults with cognitive complaints or MCI [uchida2024, ide2016] are not evidence for enhancement in healthy young adults, and the only young-adult chronic trial is a 2-week, manufacturer-run study [baba2021young].", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Claims such as 'matcha for calm focus', 'green tea boosts memory', 'brain tea' or 'tea prevents dementia' outrun the evidence. Defensible: tea contains caffeine and provides a modest, temporary increase in alertness; the combination of caffeine and theanine may smooth the experience. Not defensible: memory improvement, long-term cognitive protection or dementia prevention (observational associations only [hu2022, li2024], with null RCTs in older adults [uchida2024, ide2016]). Almost every acute tea trial and the most recent meta-analysis were funded by or involve Unilever/Lipton [debruin2011, payne2025, einother2013], and every chronic matcha trial involves Ito En [baba2021young, baba2021older, uchida2024, baba2024], so independent replication is a precondition for any strong claim. Green-tea fMRI studies show changes in brain activation [borgwardt2012, schmidt2014]; presenting these as 'proven brain benefits' would confuse biomarkers with cognition. In the EU, no tea or catechin cognitive claim is authorised and L-theanine cognitive claims were not substantiated by EFSA. The honest Brain Food message: 'a lower-caffeine, low-sugar tea taken between meals is a pleasant alertness drink; it is not a memory supplement'.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Caffeine (adenosine antagonism) is the main acute driver. L-theanine crosses the blood-brain barrier and at 100-200 mg modulates alpha EEG activity and may attenuate caffeine-induced arousal; ordinary cups supply only about 10-15 mg. Catechins (EGCG) are poorly bioavailable, extensively conjugated and partly degraded by gut microbiota; acute EGCG and green-tea-extract studies show EEG and fMRI changes of unclear functional meaning. Long-term hypotheses (anti-amyloid, antioxidant, vascular) rest on animal and observational data. Tea polyphenols chelate non-haem iron in the gut, an established human effect with potential cognitive consequences in iron-deficient groups.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Brewed tea or matcha -> caffeine, L-theanine and catechins absorbed", "detail": "Leaf caffeine about 16-19 mg/g and theanine about 5-6.6 mg/g across 37 commercial teas [boros2016]; 2.7 g matcha supplied 71.5 mg caffeine, 50.3 mg theanine and 301 mg catechins [baba2024]; matcha intake raised red-cell theanine in a 12-month trial [uchida2024].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Caffeine -> adenosine receptor antagonism -> arousal and attention", "detail": "Tea improved switching accuracy and alertness versus placebo tea [debruin2011]; caffeine accounted for matcha's acute reaction-time effect [baba2021older].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "L-theanine (with caffeine) -> alpha-band EEG modulation, reduced caffeine-related arousal", "detail": "Meta-analysed theanine+caffeine effects on vigilance and switching (SMD about 0.2-0.33), mostly at supra-dietary theanine doses [payne2025].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "EGCG/catechins -> limited bioavailability -> EEG and prefrontal activation changes", "detail": "300 mg EGCG increased alpha/beta/theta EEG activity and self-rated calmness [scholey2012]; green tea extract increased DLPFC activation (ROI only) and parieto-frontal connectivity (n = 12) [borgwardt2012, schmidt2014].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Chronic catechins -> proposed anti-amyloid, antioxidant and vascular protection", "detail": "Animal and in vitro data plus observational dementia associations [hu2022, li2024]; a 12-month green tea trial changed an oxidative LDL marker but not MMSE [ide2016].", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Tea polyphenols -> chelation of non-haem iron in the gut lumen -> reduced iron absorption", "detail": "More than 85% reduction with Moroccan green tea [lazrak2021]; 79-94% with black tea [hurrell1999].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Measured human cognitive outcome", "detail": "Acute small attention and alertness gains [debruin2011, hindmarch1998, dietz2017]; chronic trials largely null on primary outcomes [uchida2024, ide2016, sakurai2020].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["adenosine receptor antagonism (caffeine)", "L-theanine glutamate-receptor and alpha-EEG modulation", "catechin (EGCG) antioxidant/anti-amyloid hypotheses (preclinical)", "polyphenol-iron chelation", "OATP1A2 inhibition by green tea catechins (drug interaction) [misaka2014]", "expectancy and ritual (tea-break) effects"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults in laboratory crossover trials (mostly habitual tea or caffeine users)", "dose_or_intake": "1-3 cups black tea (about 40-100 mg caffeine) or 4 g matcha", "duration": "single dose, 10 min to several hours", "grade": "B", "direction": "benefit", "effect_summary": "Tea improved attention-switching accuracy and intersensory attention versus taste-matched placebo; tea through the day prevented decline in information processing versus water; matcha improved basic attention and psychomotor speed.", "effect_estimate": "Switch-task accuracy p < 0.002 and p = 0.007 in two studies [debruin2011]; theanine+caffeine switching accuracy SMD 0.33 (0.13-0.54), digit vigilance SMD 0.20 (0.02-0.38) [payne2025]; matcha improvements in attention tasks, effect sizes not in abstract [dietz2017]", "certainty_notes": "Consistent direction across several small trials and reviews, but most are industry-run (Unilever/Lipton), few test the beverage itself, and effects largely track caffeine. Downgraded from A for risk of bias and imprecision.", "key_study_ids": ["debruin2011", "hindmarch1998", "dietz2017", "payne2025", "einother2013"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "alertness", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults", "dose_or_intake": "Black tea, 1-3 cups", "duration": "single dose to one day", "grade": "B", "direction": "benefit", "effect_summary": "Tea consistently increased self-rated alertness and reduced subjective sedation across the day; effects on relaxation or pleasure were less consistent.", "effect_estimate": "Bond-Lader alertness p < 0.001 and p = 0.021 [debruin2011]; caffeine reduced subjective sedation within 10 min [hindmarch1998]", "certainty_notes": "Subjective outcome, susceptible to expectancy despite placebo-tea blinding; industry involvement.", "key_study_ids": ["debruin2011", "hindmarch1998", "einother2013"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults", "dose_or_intake": "Black tea or 4 g matcha", "duration": "single dose", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Short-term memory unaffected by tea in a factorial trial; matcha showed only a slight effect on delayed picture recognition, mainly in drink format.", "effect_estimate": "No significant STM effects [hindmarch1998]; 'slight' episodic memory effect, numbers not in abstract [dietz2017]", "certainty_notes": "Few small trials, single-blind matcha study, multiple outcomes.", "key_study_ids": ["hindmarch1998", "dietz2017"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults", "dose_or_intake": "300 mg EGCG; tea; 4 g matcha", "duration": "single dose", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Pure EGCG increased self-rated calmness and reduced stress; matcha produced no POMS change; reviews describe relaxation effects of tea as less consistent than alertness effects.", "effect_estimate": "Calmness and stress changes significant, effect sizes not in abstract [scholey2012]; POMS no change [dietz2017]", "certainty_notes": "Inconsistent, small studies; 300 mg EGCG is far above a cup of tea.", "key_study_ids": ["scholey2012", "dietz2017", "einother2013"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "neuroplasticity_biomarkers", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "12 healthy volunteers per fMRI study; EEG study in healthy adults", "dose_or_intake": "Whey drink with 27.5 g green tea extract (250-500 mL); 300 mg EGCG", "duration": "single dose", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "Increased DLPFC activation (region-of-interest only; whole-brain null after correction) and parieto-frontal connectivity during working memory; EEG power increases after EGCG. These are biomarkers, not cognitive benefits.", "effect_estimate": "ROI FWE p < 0.001, whole-brain NS [borgwardt2012]; connectivity correlated with task improvement, numeric values not in abstract [schmidt2014]", "certainty_notes": "Tiny samples, same research group, flexible analyses, extract doses not comparable with tea; does not raise any cognitive grade.", "key_study_ids": ["borgwardt2012", "schmidt2014", "scholey2012"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "42 Japanese adults aged 25-34 tested after a mild acute stressor", "dose_or_intake": "2 g matcha/day", "duration": "2 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Stroop reaction times lower than placebo after mild stress and more correct emotion-perception responses; no differences on memory, working memory, visual information or motor tests.", "effect_estimate": "Stroop RT lower (significant), numeric values not in abstract [baba2021young]", "certainty_notes": "Single small manufacturer-run (Ito En) trial with many outcomes; caffeine (about 50 mg/day) not controlled.", "key_study_ids": ["baba2021young"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Middle-aged and older community-dwelling adults (Japan)", "dose_or_intake": "2-3 g matcha/day", "duration": "single dose to 12 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Acute matcha reaction-time gains were attributed to caffeine; continuous matcha increased work output on a stress task; a 12-week trial found MoCA improvement only in a female subgroup.", "effect_estimate": "Qualitative in abstract [baba2021older]; MoCA improvement in women only, no numbers [sakurai2020]", "certainty_notes": "Subgroup findings, industry involvement, small samples; overall null.", "key_study_ids": ["baba2021older", "sakurai2020"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_mci", "population_detail": "Older adults with subjective cognitive decline or MCI (n = 99); nursing-home residents with cognitive dysfunction (n = 33)", "dose_or_intake": "2 g matcha/day (capsules); 2 g green tea powder/day (220 mg catechins)", "duration": "12 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Primary outcomes (MoCA, ADCS-ADL) unchanged with matcha; MMSE unchanged with green tea powder; only a facial-emotion perception score and an oxidative LDL marker changed.", "effect_estimate": "Social acuity difference -1.39 (95% CI -2.78 to 0.002), p = 0.028; MoCA NS [uchida2024]; MMSE LSM difference -0.61 (-2.97 to 1.74), p = 0.59 [ide2016]", "certainty_notes": "Two RCTs, both null on primary outcomes; small; manufacturer-affiliated. Not applicable to young adults.", "key_study_ids": ["uchida2024", "ide2016"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "sleep_related_cognition", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy Japanese adults aged 27-64; older adults with cognitive decline", "dose_or_intake": "2-2.7 g matcha/day (about 70 mg caffeine)", "duration": "4 weeks to 12 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "No change in EEG-measured total sleep, latency, wake after sleep onset or efficiency; only trends in subjective sleep satisfaction; PSQI trend over 12 months.", "effect_estimate": "Objective sleep NS [baba2024]; PSQI difference 0.86 (-0.002 to 1.71), p = 0.088 [uchida2024]", "certainty_notes": "Small industry trials; morning dosing likely; reassures that moderate matcha did not worsen measured sleep.", "key_study_ids": ["baba2024", "uchida2024"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "dementia_risk", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Middle-aged and older adults in prospective cohorts (UK, US, Singapore, pooled)", "dose_or_intake": "1-3 cups/day tea", "duration": "follow-up 1-43 years", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Tea drinking is consistently associated with lower dementia or cognitive impairment risk, usually nonlinear with a nadir at 1-3 cups.", "effect_estimate": "HR 0.84 (16% lower, 95% CI 8-23%) [hu2022]; RR 0.84 (0.74-0.96) [li2024]; cognitive impairment OR 0.37-0.56 by intake level [ng2008]; nadir 1-2 cups [zhang2026]", "certainty_notes": "Observational only: residual confounding (socioeconomic status, health behaviours), reverse causation, self-report; low/very low GRADE in the pooled analysis.", "key_study_ids": ["hu2022", "li2024", "ng2008", "zhang2026"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "UK Biobank adults 37-73 (cross-sectional); US nurses (TICS)", "dose_or_intake": "Habitual tea", "duration": "habitual", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Tea drinkers performed worse on all seven UK Biobank cognitive tests (probably confounding), whereas US cohort data show small favourable differences similar to coffee.", "effect_estimate": "Poorer performance on all tests, P-trend < 0.0001 [cornelis2020]; tea associations similar to caffeinated coffee (TICS +0.11 for coffee) [zhang2026]", "certainty_notes": "Conflicting observational findings; no causal inference possible.", "key_study_ids": ["cornelis2020", "zhang2026"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "learning", "population": "students", "population_detail": "University students", "dose_or_intake": "Habitual tea or matcha", "duration": "weeks to a semester", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No controlled trial of tea or matcha on learning or academic outcomes in students was found; the closest is a 2-week matcha trial in 25-34-year-olds.", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "Evidence gap.", "key_study_ids": ["baba2021young"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "other", "population": "deficient_or_low_baseline", "population_detail": "Moroccan women with iron-deficiency anaemia (n = 25) and non-anaemic women (n = 21); adults in radio-iron studies", "dose_or_intake": "300 mL traditional Moroccan green tea (492 mg polyphenols) or black tea with a meal", "duration": "single meal (absorption measured at 14 days)", "grade": "A", "direction": "harm", "effect_summary": "Tea with a meal reduced iron absorption from NaFeEDTA-fortified wheat by more than 85% in both groups; black tea inhibits non-haem iron absorption by 79-94%.", "effect_estimate": "Fractional iron absorption in IDA 36.7% -> 4.1%; non-anaemic 16.7% -> 1.4% (P < 0.001) [lazrak2021]; black tea 79-94% inhibition [hurrell1999]", "certainty_notes": "Well-conducted isotope studies with consistent, large effects, directly in the Moroccan target population. Outcome is single-meal absorption (biomarker of iron supply); long-term iron status effects depend on timing and diet.", "key_study_ids": ["lazrak2021", "hurrell1999"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "other", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Adults in observational studies of tea and health", "dose_or_intake": "Very hot tea (above about 55-65 C)", "duration": "habitual", "grade": "C", "direction": "harm", "effect_summary": "Tea is generally associated with benefit, but drinking it very hot is associated with oesophageal and gastric cancer.", "effect_estimate": "Harmful associations for oesophageal and gastric cancer at more than 55-60 C, numeric values not in abstract [yi2019]", "certainty_notes": "Observational; IARC classifies very hot beverages (>65 C) as probably carcinogenic (Group 2A; see mate monograph).", "key_study_ids": ["yi2019"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "Black tea from commercial tea bags (dose by tea bag; about 1-2 cups)", "onset": "within 1 h", "duration_studied": "about 1-2 h post-dose", "domain": "attention switching, intersensory attention, alertness", "population": "healthy adults, two crossover studies", "effect_estimate": "Switch accuracy p < 0.002 / p = 0.007; alertness p < 0.001 / p = 0.021", "certainty": "moderate (industry)", "safety": "none reported", "study_ids": ["debruin2011"]}, {"dose": "400 mL black tea, coffee, caffeinated water, decaf tea or water, x3/day (100 mg caffeine per caffeinated drink)", "onset": "10 min", "duration_studied": "whole day", "domain": "CFF, choice RT, STM, subjective sedation", "population": "healthy volunteers (n = 19)", "effect_estimate": "Tea vs water: faster CFF improvement; caffeine reduced sedation; STM null", "certainty": "low-moderate", "safety": "none reported", "study_ids": ["hindmarch1998"]}, {"dose": "4 g matcha as drink or snack bar", "onset": "60 min", "duration_studied": "single post-dose session", "domain": "attention, psychomotor speed, memory, mood", "population": "23 adults, single-blind", "effect_estimate": "Attention/psychomotor speed improved; mood unchanged; drink > bar", "certainty": "low", "safety": "not reported", "study_ids": ["dietz2017"]}, {"dose": "300 mg EGCG", "onset": "120 min", "duration_studied": "single session", "domain": "EEG, calmness, stress", "population": "healthy adults", "effect_estimate": "EEG alpha/beta/theta increase; calmness up", "certainty": "low", "safety": "not reported", "study_ids": ["scholey2012"]}, {"dose": "Whey drink with green tea extract (27.5 g; 250-500 mL)", "onset": "during fMRI session", "duration_studied": "single session", "domain": "working-memory brain activation (biomarker)", "population": "12 healthy volunteers", "effect_estimate": "DLPFC activation (ROI) and parieto-frontal connectivity increased", "certainty": "very low", "safety": "not reported", "study_ids": ["borgwardt2012", "schmidt2014"]}, {"dose": "300 mL Moroccan green tea with a fortified bread meal", "onset": "same meal", "duration_studied": "erythrocyte incorporation at 14 days", "domain": "iron absorption (harm)", "population": "Moroccan women with and without IDA (n = 46)", "effect_estimate": "Iron absorption reduced by more than 85%", "certainty": "high", "safety": "iron deficiency risk", "study_ids": ["lazrak2021"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "2 weeks", "population": "42 adults aged 25-34", "outcome": "Stroop RT after mild stress, emotion perception", "effect_estimate": "Stroop RT lower vs placebo; other tests NS", "evidence_quality": "single small industry RCT", "limitations": "Ito En authors; multiple outcomes", "study_ids": ["baba2021young"]}, {"duration": "12 weeks", "population": "61 community-dwelling older adults", "outcome": "MoCA and cognitive battery", "effect_estimate": "MoCA improved only in women (subgroup)", "evidence_quality": "small RCT", "limitations": "Subgroup analysis", "study_ids": ["sakurai2020"]}, {"duration": "12 months", "population": "99 older adults with SCD/MCI", "outcome": "MoCA, ADCS-ADL (primary), PSQI, CNS Vital Signs", "effect_estimate": "Primary outcomes NS; social acuity p = 0.028", "evidence_quality": "RCT, industry co-authors", "limitations": "Capsule matcha, multiplicity", "study_ids": ["uchida2024"]}, {"duration": "12 months", "population": "33 nursing-home residents with cognitive dysfunction", "outcome": "MMSE", "effect_estimate": "-0.61 (-2.97 to 1.74), NS", "evidence_quality": "small RCT", "limitations": "Underpowered", "study_ids": ["ide2016"]}, {"duration": "4 weeks", "population": "healthy adults 27-64", "outcome": "EEG sleep, mood", "effect_estimate": "Objective sleep unchanged", "evidence_quality": "small industry RCT", "limitations": "Sponsor Ito En", "study_ids": ["baba2024"]}, {"duration": "9 years to 43 years", "population": "UK Biobank 377,592; US 131,821; 38 cohorts pooled", "outcome": "incident dementia", "effect_estimate": "HR 0.84; RR 0.84 (0.74-0.96); nadir 1-3 cups", "evidence_quality": "observational, low certainty", "limitations": "Confounding, reverse causation", "study_ids": ["hu2022", "li2024", "zhang2026"]}, {"duration": "1-2 years", "population": "Singapore Chinese aged 55+", "outcome": "MMSE impairment and decline", "effect_estimate": "Impairment OR 0.37-0.56; decline OR 0.57-0.78", "evidence_quality": "observational", "limitations": "Short follow-up, MMSE ceiling", "study_ids": ["ng2008"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Brewed tea at ordinary intakes is among the safest beverages, but five issues matter for Brain Food. (1) Iron: tea with meals cut iron absorption by more than 85% in Moroccan women, including those with anaemia [lazrak2021]; black and peppermint teas inhibit by 79-94% [hurrell1999]. (2) Temperature: drinking tea very hot (above about 60-65 C) is associated with oesophageal cancer [yi2019]. (3) Concentrated green tea extracts cause rare hepatocellular injury, especially taken as fasting boluses [oketchrabah2020]. The EU prohibits supplements supplying 800 mg EGCG/day or more [efsa2018]. (4) Interactions: green tea reduced nadolol exposure by about 85% [misaka2014]. Matcha contains vitamin K, relevant to warfarin users [sakurai2020]. (5) Contaminants: traditional metallic teapots have leached lead [bolle2011], and imported gunpowder tea in Morocco has had pesticide-residue problems (ONSSA tightened its limits in 2019; press sources). Caffeine per cup (about 20-70 mg) is modest but adds up across many glasses a day.", "adverse_effects": ["reduced non-haem iron absorption when drunk with meals [lazrak2021, hurrell1999]", "caffeine effects (sleep disruption, jitteriness) at high intakes", "hepatotoxicity with concentrated extracts, not brewed tea [oketchrabah2020]", "oesophageal injury risk when very hot [yi2019]", "skeletal fluorosis reported in case reports of very high long-term intake (not extracted here)", "dental staining"], "contraindications": ["iron-deficiency anaemia: avoid tea within about 1 h of meals or iron supplements", "liver disease: avoid green tea extracts", "pregnancy: count caffeine toward the 200 mg/day limit; avoid extracts", "children: caffeine limits and sugar load of sweetened tea"], "drug_interactions": ["nadolol (and possibly other OATP1A2 substrates): exposure reduced about 85% by habitual green tea [misaka2014]", "warfarin: vitamin K content of matcha [sakurai2020] could alter INR (not quantified here)", "iron supplements: absorption reduced [hurrell1999]", "caffeine-related interactions as for coffee (CYP1A2 inhibitors, stimulants)"], "upper_limits": "Caffeine: EFSA 400 mg/day for adults, 200 mg/day in pregnancy (see caffeine monograph). EGCG: food supplements providing 800 mg EGCG/day or more are prohibited in the EU (Regulation (EU) 2022/2340, per project synthesis X4) [efsa2018]. A USP-linked review found case reports of liver injury at 140 to about 1000 mg EGCG/day [oketchrabah2020]. No upper limit applies to brewed tea itself.", "sleep_impact": "Caffeine-dependent and milder than coffee per serving. Daily matcha (about 70 mg caffeine) did not change EEG-measured sleep in a 4-week trial [baba2024]. Evening tea can disturb sleep in sensitive people.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Yes, observationally in very large cohorts [hu2022, yi2019]; 12-month RCTs of matcha and green tea powder in older adults reported no safety problems [uchida2024, ide2016].", "daily_use_appropriate": "yes", "special_populations": "Iron-deficient women and adolescents (a major group in Morocco), pregnant women (caffeine), people with liver disease (extracts), patients on nadolol or warfarin, children (caffeine and sugar in sweetened Moroccan tea), habitual drinkers of very hot tea.", "specialist_review_needed": ["clinical nutritionist (iron and tea timing)", "hepatologist (any green tea extract use)", "clinical pharmacist (OATP and warfarin interactions)", "food safety specialist (pesticide residues in imported tea, teapot materials, fluoride)"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "Brewed tea and matcha pass at usual intakes. Products must not use concentrated green tea extracts as a caffeine or 'focus' source. Labelling should advise drinking tea between meals for iron-deficient consumers and not very hot. Supply chains must be tested for pesticide residues."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Tea (Camellia sinensis), including matcha, is a traditional food and not novel. In 2011 EFSA assessed Article 13 claims on Camellia sinensis and green tea catechins, including 'contribution to normal cognitive function' (ID 1117, 2812) and 'invigoration of the body' (EFSA Journal 2011;9(4):2055, DOI confirmed via Crossref). Its conclusions for these IDs were not retrieved here (the Wiley page returned 403). No tea or catechin cognitive claim is known to be authorised, and this must be checked against the EU Register before any claim is used. L-theanine claims on cognition, stress and sleep were not substantiated (EFSA 2011; see l-theanine monograph). Green tea extracts containing EGCG fall under Regulation (EU) 2022/2340 (Annex III Part B): daily portions of 800 mg EGCG or more are prohibited, labels must state EGCG per portion and warnings, and the substance is also under Part C scrutiny (project synthesis X4). Under FIC Annex III point 4, tea-based beverages named as such are exempt from the 'high caffeine content' statement.", "us": "Tea is a conventional food. 'Tea (Thea sinensis L.)' is listed in 21 CFR 182.20 (natural extractives generally recognised as safe), confirmed on the Cornell LII copy (accessed 2026-10-25). Green tea extract supplements are dietary supplements. The USP monograph for powdered decaffeinated green tea extract carries a hepatotoxicity caution ('Do not take on an empty stomach...') [oketchrabah2020]. Cognitive structure/function wording needs FTC-level substantiation, which tea itself does not have beyond caffeine-related alertness. Dementia-risk claims would be drug or disease claims.", "morocco": "Green tea (mostly Chinese gunpowder) is a staple food. Press reports quoting ONSSA state that Morocco imports about 60,000 tonnes a year, about 99% from China, with documentary, physical and analytical checks for pesticide residues (Medias24, 1 and 15 February 2018). ONSSA set new maximum residue limits for imported tea from 1 July 2019 (TelQuel, 26 March and 5 July 2019). All accessed 2026-10-25; the official text was not consulted. Labelling must follow Decree 2-12-389, including Arabic (project synthesis X6). No Moroccan health-claim framework for tea was found. Status of any cognitive claim: uncertain, presumed not permitted.", "permitted_claims_examples": [], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "None for brewed tea or matcha. High-purity EGCG or theanine ingredients have separate status: theanine is restricted in the EU (see l-theanine monograph), and EGCG extracts fall under Regulation 2022/2340.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Excellent and culturally central in Morocco: green tea with mint (atay) is the national beverage, traditionally strong, very hot and heavily sweetened, and drunk with meals. The evidence-based Brain Food opportunity is behavioural and compositional rather than a 'brain tea'. It means lower- or no-sugar mint green tea, served warm rather than scalding, between meals rather than with them, from tested low-residue leaf, possibly as standardised matcha or tea sachets with declared caffeine.", "formats": ["loose or bagged green tea with mint (low-sugar)", "matcha sachets (2-3 g, about 50-70 mg caffeine)", "black tea", "ready-to-drink unsweetened green tea", "tea-based snack (matcha bar; less effective than the drink in one trial [dietz2017])"], "stability": "Catechins degrade with heat, oxygen and alkaline pH in ready-to-drink tea. Matcha oxidises and loses colour once opened. Caffeine is stable. Theanine is stable in dry leaf.", "taste": "Astringent and bitter, especially strong gunpowder brews. Moroccan practice masks this with mint and large amounts of sugar, which conflicts with health positioning. Matcha is grassy and umami.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Caffeine is fully absorbed. Theanine is well absorbed. EGCG bioavailability is low, and higher when fasting, which also raises liver risk with extracts [oketchrabah2020]. Milk does not abolish iron inhibition [hurrell1999]. Tea polyphenols bind meal iron [lazrak2021].", "dose_feasibility": "Caffeine at alertness doses (40-100 mg) is reachable with 1-3 cups or one 2-3 g matcha serving [baba2024]. The theanine doses used in most cognition trials (100-250 mg) are not reachable from ordinary tea (about 10-15 mg/cup [boros2016]) and only partly from matcha (about 50 mg per 2.7 g [baba2024]). Chronic matcha trial doses (2-3 g/day) are realistic but showed no cognitive benefit.", "cost_availability": "Very cheap and ubiquitous in Morocco (imported green tea), the EU and the US. Matcha is a premium product.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 5, "effect_magnitude": 3.5, "safety": 7.5, "reproducibility": 5, "food_suitability": 9, "cost_access": 9, "rationale": "evidence_quality 5: acute attention and alertness reach grade B (consistent but small and industry-run trials). All chronic cognitive outcomes are grade C (null RCTs in older adults; observational dementia data), and young-adult chronic evidence is a single 2-week trial. effect_magnitude 3.5: acute effects are small (SMD about 0.2-0.33) and largely caffeine-driven, with no chronic cognitive benefit in RCTs. safety 7.5: the beverage is safe; deductions for strong iron-absorption inhibition (key in Morocco), cancer risk with very hot tea, extract hepatotoxicity, a nadolol interaction and contaminant issues. reproducibility 5: acute findings replicate mainly within Unilever/Lipton-linked groups, matcha findings within Ito En-linked groups, and independent replication is sparse. food_suitability 9: an ideal, culturally embedded vehicle, especially in Morocco. cost_access 9: a cheap staple."}, "research_gaps": ["Independent (non-industry), preregistered trials of ordinary tea versus caffeine-matched control in healthy young adults and students, with effect sizes", "Chronic (8-12 week) trials of tea or matcha on attention and learning in students, with sleep and iron status monitored", "Effect of the Moroccan tea pattern (strong green tea, mint, sugar, with meals) on iron status and cognition in adolescents and young women; timing interventions (tea between meals)", "Caffeine, theanine, pesticide residue and fluoride content of teas sold in Morocco", "Serving temperature of Moroccan tea and oesophageal risk", "Whether matcha adds anything beyond caffeine when caffeine is matched"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 6.0, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": "B", "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": "B", "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": null, "best_grade_overall": "B", "benefit_domains": {"sustained_attention": "B", "alertness": "B", "dementia_risk": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 14, "n_studies": 28, "n_verified": 28, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 6, "n_systematic_reviews": 3, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["benefit", "harm", "inconsistent", "insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "theobromine-and-cocoa-methylxanthines", "name": "Theobromine and cocoa methylxanthines", "category": "F", "category_name": "Caffeine and natural stimulants", "aliases": ["theobromine", "3,7-dimethylxanthine", "xantheose (historical)", "cocoa methylxanthines", "cacao alkaloids", "BC1036 (theobromine cough product)"], "food_sources": ["cocoa powder and cocoa nibs (highest concentration; 11.6 g cocoa powder matched the methylxanthines of a 50 g dark bar in [smit2004])", "dark chocolate (a 50 g bar supplies about 250 mg theobromine and about 19 mg caffeine per [smit2004]; content tracks non-fat cocoa solids, not flavanols [alanon2016])", "milk chocolate and cocoa drinks (lower; a natural flavanol-rich cocoa drink provided 106-150 mg [vandenbogaard2010, neufingerl2013])", "tea (trace amounts) and yerba mate; also a minor metabolite of caffeine in humans", "white chocolate contains essentially none (cocoa butter only)"], "active_constituents": ["theobromine (major cocoa methylxanthine)", "caffeine (minor in cocoa; about 1:10 to 1:13 caffeine:theobromine in dark chocolate per [smit2004] dose)", "theophylline (trace; also a caffeine metabolite)"], "one_line_verdict": "Theobromine, the main stimulant-like compound in cocoa, does not improve alertness, mood or cognition in healthy young adults at 100-1000 mg in four controlled trials, causes headache, nausea, higher heart rate and dysphoria at about 1 g, and has no chronic cognitive data, so the small acute 'chocolate lift' is best credited to cocoa's caffeine and to taste rather than to theobromine.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. Theobromine (3,7-dimethylxanthine) is the dominant methylxanthine in cocoa: a 50 g dark chocolate bar supplies roughly 250 mg theobromine with only about 19 mg caffeine [smit2004]. It is absorbed slowly (plasma peak about 2 h from chocolate and 3 h from capsules [mumford1996]) and eliminated slowly (half-life 6-7 h, versus about 4 h for caffeine [lelo1986, drouillard1978]); clearance slows further with repeated daily dosing [drouillard1978]. Despite its structural similarity to caffeine, controlled human trials consistently show that theobromine alone does not improve alertness, mood or cognition. In 24 young men, 100, 200 and 400 mg failed to affect mood or a 2-h vigilance task while 100 mg caffeine worked [judelson2013]. In 24 women, 700 mg reduced calmness, transiently lowered blood pressure and left Digit Symbol performance unchanged [mitchell2011]. In the largest dose-ranging trial (n = 84, 250-1000 mg, with 200 mg caffeine as active control), theobromine produced no cognitive benefit at any dose, while 1000 mg slowed Attention Network Task response times, increased 'dislike' and dysphoria ratings, raised heart rate by about 3 bpm and caused most of the headaches and nausea [baggott2013]. Only five of seven people could even learn to tell 100-560 mg from placebo, and 560 mg did not change group subjective ratings [mumford1994]. The acute arousal and attention effect of a chocolate-sized dose of cocoa methylxanthines [smit2004] is real but small, comes from one laboratory, and is most plausibly explained by the 19 mg of caffeine. The same methylxanthine mix does make foods more liked with repeated exposure [smit2005], which helps explain chocolate habits rather than cognitive benefit. Chronic data exist only for cardiometabolic biomarkers and are inconsistent. About 1 g/day for 3 weeks raised 24-h systolic blood pressure by 3.2 mmHg [vandenbogaard2010]. 850-1000 mg/day for 4 weeks raised HDL cholesterol by 0.16 mmol/L in a Unilever trial [neufingerl2013], but 500 mg/day did not replicate this and increased hsCRP and postprandial glucose and insulin [smolders2018]. 450 mg/day for 12 weeks slightly improved lipid ratios in metabolic syndrome [sharifizahabi2023], and vascular function did not improve in overweight adults [smolders2019]. Methylxanthines may increase epicatechin absorption from cocoa [sansone2017], which could matter for cocoa-flavanol products but is a vascular surrogate, not cognition. In whom, and how large. For healthy young adults and students the answer is clear: theobromine is not a cognitive enhancer at any studied dose (grade E acute). No chronic cognitive trial exists in any population. The only habitual-intake signal is a confounded cross-sectional association in US adults over 60 [gao2022]. Rodent studies of memory and BDNF [yoneda2017, islam2019] supply plausibility only. Optimistic narrative reviews [martinezpinilla2015, cova2019] are not supported by these controlled trials. What is unknown. Effects under sleep deprivation, interactions with caffeine at ratios found in cocoa (Mitchell's combination arm suggested theobromine may blunt caffeine's blood-pressure rise), and safety of daily isolated doses above 500 mg for months.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Theobromine is not a nutrient, and there is no deficiency state; any use is an enhancement claim. As an enhancer in rested, adequately nourished young adults it fails: 100-1000 mg produced no benefit on vigilance, attention, reaction time, memory or mood versus placebo, while the caffeine control arms worked [judelson2013, baggott2013, mitchell2011, mumford1994]. No trial has tested sleep-deprived, fatigued or older people. The cross-sectional association in older Americans [gao2022] is not evidence of a deficiency-correction effect and cannot be generalised to students.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Marketing describes theobromine as a 'smooth', 'jitter-free', 'long-lasting' alternative to caffeine, a 'mood booster' and a 'focus' ingredient (it appears in some cacao 'nootropic' drinks and pre-workouts). The controlled evidence contradicts each of these. Theobromine is long-lasting pharmacokinetically (half-life 6-7 h [lelo1986]), but that persistence carries no measured cognitive benefit. Its subjective profile at higher doses is negative (dislike, dysphoria, nausea [baggott2013]), and it raises heart rate and, at 1 g/day, 24-h blood pressure [vandenbogaard2010]. The claim that 'chocolate improves focus because of theobromine' should be rewritten as 'a dark-chocolate portion contains a small amount of caffeine, which can slightly improve alertness'. No EU health claim exists for theobromine, and any cognitive claim would be unsubstantiated in the EU and in the US (FTC competent and reliable scientific evidence standard).", "mechanism": {"summary": "Theobromine is a weak, non-selective adenosine A1/A2A receptor antagonist and phosphodiesterase inhibitor, several-fold less potent at adenosine receptors than caffeine. It is absorbed slowly from the gut, crosses the blood-brain barrier, and is cleared by hepatic CYP1A2/2E1 demethylation with a 6-7 h half-life. Its human effects are mainly peripheral (heart rate, vascular tone, lipids) rather than central arousal, consistent with the null cognitive trials. In cocoa it may raise epicatechin absorption. Rodent work on BDNF/CREB signalling is not confirmed in humans.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Oral theobromine (cocoa, chocolate, capsules) -> slow absorption, Tmax ~2 h (chocolate) to ~3 h (capsule)", "detail": "Plasma Cmax 8.05 ug/ml after chocolate with 370 mg theobromine [mumford1996].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Hepatic demethylation -> elimination half-life 6-7 h; slower clearance after repeated daily doses", "detail": "t1/2 7.2 h vs caffeine 4.1 h [lelo1986]; 6.1 h, prolonged by about two-thirds after 5 daily doses [drouillard1978].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Adenosine receptor antagonism and PDE inhibition (weaker than caffeine)", "detail": "Pharmacology summarised in reviews [martinezpinilla2015, cova2019]; human receptor occupancy not measured.", "evidence_level": "plausible"}, {"step": "Peripheral cardiovascular effects: heart rate up, transient BP change, 24-h SBP up at ~1 g/day", "detail": "[baggott2013, mitchell2011, vandenbogaard2010].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Increased (-)-epicatechin absorption when co-ingested with cocoa flavanols", "detail": "Higher epicatechin-metabolite AUC and FMD with methylxanthines + flavanols [sansone2017] (Mars co-authors).", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "BDNF/CaMKII/CREB up-regulation and memory or motor learning improvement", "detail": "Rodent studies only [yoneda2017, islam2019].", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Measured human cognitive outcome", "detail": "No benefit on vigilance, attention, RT, memory at 100-1000 mg; impairment at 1000 mg [judelson2013, baggott2013, mitchell2011].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}], "pathways": ["adenosine A1/A2A antagonism (weak)", "phosphodiesterase inhibition", "peripheral vasodilation / heart-rate effects", "modulation of flavanol absorption", "BDNF-CREB signalling (animal only)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "alertness", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults 18-35 given pure theobromine (capsule or cocoa/non-cocoa beverage); outcomes subjective alertness/energy/mood", "dose_or_intake": "100-1000 mg theobromine", "duration": "single dose; 1-5 h", "grade": "E", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Four independent controlled trials found no group-level alertness or vigour benefit: 100-400 mg (Judelson, n=24, all P>0.05), 700 mg (Mitchell, n=24; reduced calmness, no alertness gain), 250-1000 mg (Baggott, n=84; few effects at 250 mg, dysphoric and 'dislike' ratings at 500-1000 mg), 560 mg (Mumford, n=7; no group change). Caffeine positive controls worked in every trial, so the designs were sensitive.", "effect_estimate": "No significant theobromine effect at any dose (Judelson P>0.05); dose-dependent increase in 'Dislike' (Baggott: 31.5 vs 14.3 on 0-100 scale at 1000 mg)", "certainty_notes": "Consistent null across 4 small but sensitive trials with active controls; imprecision (n 7-84) limits confidence in excluding a very small effect, but the evidence does not support any meaningful alertness benefit; mood worsens at high doses.", "key_study_ids": ["judelson2013", "baggott2013", "mitchell2011", "mumford1994"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "reaction_time", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy young adults; objective attention/psychomotor tasks after pure theobromine", "dose_or_intake": "250-1000 mg", "duration": "single dose", "grade": "E", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "No improvement in DSST (700 mg), 2-h visual vigilance (100-400 mg), digit span, verbal learning, ANT or simple RT (250-1000 mg); 1000 mg slowed ANT response time and reduced alerting.", "effect_estimate": "ANT RT slower at 1000 mg (z=3.97, p<0.001); all lower doses NS [baggott2013]", "certainty_notes": "Same trials as above; impairment at 1000 mg is from one trial. Grade E because benefit is not shown and the high dose impairs.", "key_study_ids": ["baggott2013", "judelson2013", "mitchell2011"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults given the methylxanthine content of a 50 g dark chocolate bar (caffeine + theobromine together) or cocoa powder", "dose_or_intake": "19 mg caffeine + 250 mg theobromine; 11.6 g cocoa", "duration": "single dose", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Two small crossover studies (n=20, 22) found improved energetic arousal, simple RT and RVIP versus placebo or white chocolate, identical for cocoa powder and the methylxanthine mix. Because theobromine alone is null in four trials, the effect is best attributed to the small caffeine dose (caffeine is active from ~12.5 mg).", "effect_estimate": "Not reported in abstract [smit2004]", "certainty_notes": "Single laboratory, small n, no separation of caffeine and theobromine, no replication; downgraded for imprecision and indirectness about theobromine itself.", "key_study_ids": ["smit2004", "baggott2013", "judelson2013"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Hedonic liking/reinforcement of foods paired with cocoa methylxanthines", "dose_or_intake": "19 mg caffeine + 250 mg theobromine", "duration": "6 sessions over ~2 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Pairing a novel drink with chocolate-level methylxanthines increased liking over six exposures versus placebo (n=64); 250 mg theobromine alone increased 'Want' ratings in Baggott. This is reinforcement (habit/liking), not improved mood or cognition.", "effect_estimate": "Not reported in abstract [smit2005]", "certainty_notes": "One conditioning study; caffeine not separated; outcome is liking. Useful to explain chocolate habits only.", "key_study_ids": ["smit2005", "baggott2013"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "vascular_health", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults; acute blood pressure, heart rate and flow-mediated dilation", "dose_or_intake": "220-1000 mg methylxanthines / theobromine", "duration": "single dose, 1-3 h", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "700 mg lowered BP at 1 h (Mitchell); 250-1000 mg did not change BP but raised heart rate dose-dependently (+2-3 bpm, Baggott); methylxanthines alone did not change FMD but enhanced flavanol-induced FMD and epicatechin absorption (Sansone, Mars-co-authored).", "effect_estimate": "HR +3.3 bpm at 1000 mg vs +0.5 placebo [baggott2013]", "certainty_notes": "Inconsistent direction for BP; surrogate outcomes; industry co-authorship in two of three trials.", "key_study_ids": ["mitchell2011", "baggott2013", "sansone2017"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "vascular_health", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Middle-aged and older adults, some with high-normal BP, low HDL or metabolic syndrome", "dose_or_intake": "450-1000 mg/day theobromine", "duration": "3-12 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "~1 g/day for 3 weeks raised 24-h ambulatory SBP by 3.2 mmHg (n=42); 850-1000 mg/day for 4 weeks raised HDL-C by 0.16 mmol/L (n=152, Unilever); 500 mg/day did not replicate the HDL rise and increased hsCRP and postprandial glucose/insulin (n=44); 450 mg/day for 12 weeks modestly improved lipid ratios in metabolic syndrome; vascular function unchanged in overweight adults.", "effect_estimate": "24-h SBP +3.2 mmHg (SE 1.1) [vandenbogaard2010]; HDL-C +0.16 mmol/L [neufingerl2013]; hsCRP +1.2 mg/L [smolders2018]", "certainty_notes": "Biomarkers only, inconsistent between trials, industry involvement in the positive HDL trial; not a brain outcome.", "key_study_ids": ["vandenbogaard2010", "neufingerl2013", "smolders2018", "sharifizahabi2023", "smolders2019"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy young adults and students: no chronic theobromine cognition trial exists", "dose_or_intake": "not studied", "duration": "not studied", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No randomised trial has tested repeated theobromine intake on cognition in young adults. Chronic chocolate/cocoa trials (see dark-chocolate.json and cocoa-flavanols.json) cannot isolate theobromine. Rat and mouse studies report working-memory and BDNF effects, which supply plausibility only.", "effect_estimate": "not applicable", "certainty_notes": "Absence of human evidence; animal data cannot raise the grade.", "key_study_ids": ["yoneda2017", "islam2019", "cova2019", "shateri2023"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_aging", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "US adults 60+ (NHANES), habitual dietary theobromine", "dose_or_intake": "highest vs lower quantile of habitual intake (24-h recall)", "duration": "habitual", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "One cross-sectional analysis (n=2,845) found small positive associations with delayed recall, fluency and DSST, concentrated in high caffeine consumers; no prospective or interventional data.", "effect_estimate": "DSST beta 1.55 (95% CI 0.33-2.77) [gao2022]", "certainty_notes": "Cross-sectional, confounded (chocolate and coffee users differ in SES and health), marginal CIs, single study.", "key_study_ids": ["gao2022"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "100-400 mg theobromine (cocoa or non-cocoa drink)", "onset": "saliva levels rise within the session; peak ~2-3 h", "duration_studied": "2-h vigilance task", "domain": "vigilance and mood", "population": "healthy young men (n=24)", "effect_estimate": "No significant effect at any dose (P>0.05); caffeine 100 mg active", "certainty": "E (consistent null)", "safety": "No adverse effects reported in abstract", "study_ids": ["judelson2013"]}, {"dose": "250 / 500 / 1000 mg theobromine capsule", "onset": "tested 3-4 h after dose", "duration_studied": "~4 h", "domain": "attention (ANT), RT, memory, mood", "population": "healthy adults 18-34 (n=84)", "effect_estimate": "No cognitive benefit; 1000 mg slowed ANT RT (z=3.97); HR +2.5 to +3.3 bpm at 500-1000 mg", "certainty": "E", "safety": "Headache and nausea concentrated at 1000 mg; one vomiting", "study_ids": ["baggott2013"]}, {"dose": "700 mg theobromine, alone or with 120 mg caffeine", "onset": "1-3 h", "duration_studied": "3 h", "domain": "mood, DSST, BP", "population": "healthy women (n=24)", "effect_estimate": "Reduced calmness at 3 h; lowered BP at 1 h; no DSST effect", "certainty": "E/C", "safety": "BP fall at 1 h; no serious events reported", "study_ids": ["mitchell2011"]}, {"dose": "19 mg caffeine + 250 mg theobromine (50 g dark chocolate equivalent)", "onset": "within 1-2 h", "duration_studied": "~2-3 h", "domain": "energetic arousal, simple RT, RVIP", "population": "healthy adults (n=20, 22)", "effect_estimate": "Improvement vs placebo/white chocolate (size not reported in abstract)", "certainty": "C (attributable mainly to caffeine)", "safety": "None reported", "study_ids": ["smit2004"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "3 weeks", "population": "older adults with high-normal BP (n=42)", "outcome": "24-h ambulatory SBP", "effect_estimate": "+3.2 mmHg with 979 mg/day; no change with 106 mg/day natural cocoa dose", "evidence_quality": "moderate for biomarker (single RCT)", "limitations": "Older population; flavanols co-administered; no cognition", "study_ids": ["vandenbogaard2010"]}, {"duration": "4 weeks", "population": "healthy adults 40-70 (n=152) and adults with low HDL (n=44)", "outcome": "HDL-C, LDL-C, hsCRP, postprandial glucose", "effect_estimate": "HDL-C +0.16 mmol/L at 850-1000 mg (Unilever); no HDL rise but LDL -0.21 mmol/L, hsCRP +1.2 mg/L and higher postprandial glucose/insulin at 500 mg", "evidence_quality": "low-moderate, inconsistent", "limitations": "Biomarkers; inconsistent; industry-run positive trial", "study_ids": ["neufingerl2013", "smolders2018"]}, {"duration": "12 weeks", "population": "adults 40-55 with metabolic syndrome on diet (n=80)", "outcome": "waist, lipids, BP, glycaemia", "effect_estimate": "Waist -0.86 cm, HDL-C +1.72 mg/dl; BP and glycaemia unchanged", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Clinical population; small effects; no cognition", "study_ids": ["sharifizahabi2023"]}, {"duration": "habitual (cross-sectional)", "population": "US adults 60+ (n=2,845)", "outcome": "CERAD recall, fluency, DSST", "effect_estimate": "Small positive associations in highest intake quantile (DSST beta 1.55)", "evidence_quality": "very low", "limitations": "Cross-sectional, confounded", "study_ids": ["gao2022"]}], "safety": {"summary": "At food intakes (roughly 50-500 mg/day from cocoa products) theobromine is well tolerated in adults. Isolated doses of 500-1000 mg raise heart rate, and 1000 mg causes headache, nausea and occasional vomiting [baggott2013]. About 1 g/day for 3 weeks raised 24-h SBP by 3.2 mmHg [vandenbogaard2010]. 500 mg/day for 4 weeks raised hsCRP and postprandial glucose and insulin [smolders2018]. A 14-day trial of a theobromine cough product in 289 adults showed an adverse-event profile similar to placebo [morice2017]. Theobromine is highly toxic to dogs and cats (relevant to household storage, not human dosing).", "adverse_effects": ["headache, nausea, vomiting at ~1000 mg single dose [baggott2013]", "dose-dependent heart-rate increase (+2-3 bpm at 500-1000 mg) [baggott2013]", "dysphoria and 'dislike' ratings at 500-1000 mg [baggott2013]", "raised 24-h systolic BP at ~1 g/day [vandenbogaard2010]", "raised hsCRP and postprandial glucose/insulin at 500 mg/day [smolders2018]", "case report of atrial fibrillation with heavy chocolate intake plus salbutamol abuse [patane2010]"], "contraindications": ["arrhythmia or uncontrolled hypertension (caution with high-dose isolated theobromine)", "pregnancy: methylxanthines cross the placenta; keep chocolate within ordinary amounts and count its caffeine", "children: no need and no data for isolated theobromine"], "drug_interactions": ["beta-2 agonists (salbutamol) and other sympathomimetics: additive cardiac stimulation (case-level evidence) [patane2010]", "other methylxanthines (caffeine, theophylline): additive exposure; theophylline has a narrow therapeutic window", "CYP1A2 inhibitors (e.g. fluvoxamine, ciprofloxacin) may slow methylxanthine clearance (extrapolated from caffeine; not tested for theobromine here)"], "upper_limits": "No EFSA or US tolerable upper intake level for theobromine was found. Practical ceiling from trials: adverse effects emerge from about 500 mg single doses and are clear at 1000 mg; chronic 850-1000 mg/day raised BP. The caffeine in cocoa products counts toward EFSA's 200 mg single-dose / 400 mg daily caffeine guidance (see caffeine.json).", "sleep_impact": "Not measured directly in the trials found. The 6-7 h half-life [lelo1986] and the caffeine content of dark chocolate make evening intake of large cocoa portions a plausible sleep disruptor; uncertain.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Up to 12 weeks at 450 mg/day [sharifizahabi2023] and 4 weeks at 500-1000 mg/day [neufingerl2013, smolders2018]; no long-term neurological or cognitive safety data.", "daily_use_appropriate": "probably", "special_populations": "Pregnancy and lactation: methylxanthines cross the placenta and reach breast milk; keep to ordinary chocolate amounts and count cocoa caffeine within the 200 mg/day pregnancy limit. Children: not appropriate as a supplement. People with arrhythmias, hypertension or on theophylline or sympathomimetics should avoid isolated theobromine. Pet toxicity: keep products away from dogs.", "specialist_review_needed": ["cardiologist (if isolated theobromine >250 mg/serving were considered)", "toxicologist / food-safety regulatory specialist (novel food and maximum level questions)"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "Safe at food levels in cocoa products; isolated doses at or above ~500 mg have cardiovascular and tolerability downsides with no compensating cognitive benefit, so any isolated-theobromine product would fail on benefit-risk."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "No authorised EU health claim mentions theobromine, and no EFSA human-food opinion on isolated theobromine was found; EFSA has assessed it only as an undesirable substance in animal feed [efsa2008]. Cocoa and chocolate are conventional foods (Directive 2000/36/EC governs chocolate composition; not re-checked here). Whether isolated or concentrated theobromine added to foods or supplements needs novel-food authorisation is UNCERTAIN: no entry was located in this session, and consultation of the EU Novel Food Catalogue or a competent authority is required. The cocoa-flavanol claim (200 mg flavanols, endothelium-dependent vasodilation) belongs to the flavanols, not theobromine (see cocoa-flavanols.json).", "us": "Theobromine occurs naturally in cocoa (a conventional food). It is sold in some US dietary supplements; no theobromine-specific GRAS notice or NDI notification was verified in this session (uncertain). Any 'focus' or 'mood' structure/function claim would need FTC-level competent and reliable scientific evidence, which the null trials do not provide.", "morocco": "No Moroccan rule specific to theobromine was found. Cocoa products fall under Law 28-07 on food safety and Decree 2-12-389 on labelling (cited in Decree 2-19-13); the status of isolated theobromine as a supplement ingredient with ONSSA is uncertain and would need direct confirmation.", "permitted_claims_examples": [], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "Isolated or synthetic theobromine at above-food levels: EU novel-food status not verified (possible requirement); US NDI status not verified. Cocoa powder and chocolate have no novel-food issue.", "regulatory_gate": "unknown"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Naturally present in cocoa, so food delivery is trivial; but there is no cognitive rationale for adding theobromine, and isolated theobromine is very bitter.", "formats": ["cocoa powder drinks", "dark chocolate", "cacao nibs", "(not recommended) isolated theobromine capsules or 'cacao nootropic' blends"], "stability": "Chemically stable to roasting and processing (it survives in commercial chocolate); content depends on non-fat cocoa solids and bean origin [alanon2016].", "taste": "Intensely bitter; higher-cocoa products are more bitter and less palatable, which limits portion and drives sugar addition.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Absorbed faster and to higher peak from chocolate than from capsules [mumford1996]; co-ingested methylxanthines may increase flavanol absorption [sansone2017].", "dose_feasibility": "100-400 mg (the food-relevant range tested by Judelson) fits in 20-80 g dark chocolate or a cocoa drink, but those doses do nothing measurable for cognition. Doses with measurable physiological effects (500-1000 mg) require 100-200 g dark chocolate (about 550-1,100 kcal) or isolated powder, and bring adverse effects.", "cost_availability": "Cocoa and theobromine are inexpensive and widely available.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 3, "effect_magnitude": 1, "safety": 7, "reproducibility": 6, "food_suitability": 6, "cost_access": 7, "rationale": "Evidence quality 3: several small but sensitive acute RCTs with active caffeine controls (grade E acute null; grade C for biomarkers); no chronic cognitive trials. Effect magnitude 1: no measurable cognitive benefit at any dose; any acute lift from cocoa is attributable to caffeine. Safety 7: safe at food levels; adverse effects at >=500-1000 mg isolated. Reproducibility 6: the null result replicates across four independent labs; chronic biomarker results are inconsistent. Food suitability 6: naturally present in cocoa but bitter and calorie-bound. Cost/access 7: cheap and ubiquitous."}, "research_gaps": ["No trial of theobromine (or cocoa methylxanthine ratios) under sleep deprivation or prolonged cognitive load", "No chronic (>=4 weeks) trial with cognitive outcomes in any population", "Factorial caffeine x theobromine trials at cocoa-relevant ratios (e.g. 20-40 mg caffeine with 200-400 mg theobromine) with adequate power", "Effect of evening cocoa/chocolate methylxanthines on objectively measured sleep", "Regulatory clarification of novel-food status of isolated theobromine in the EU and Morocco"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 4.4, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "unknown", "best_grade_acute": "C", "best_grade_chronic": null, "best_grade_young_adults": "C", "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": "C", "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"sustained_attention": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 8, "n_studies": 25, "n_verified": 25, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 6, "n_systematic_reviews": 1, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["inconsistent", "insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "yerba-mate-and-guarana", "name": "Yerba mate and guarana", "category": "F", "category_name": "Caffeine and natural stimulants", "aliases": ["yerba mate", "mate", "erva-mate", "chimarrao", "terere", "Ilex paraguariensis", "guarana", "guaraná", "Paullinia cupana", "guarana seed powder", "guarana extract (e.g. PC-102)"], "food_sources": ["yerba mate infusion (hot chimarrao/mate in a gourd, cold terere, tea bags)", "yerba mate ready-to-drink beverages", "guarana seed powder and extracts in soft drinks, energy drinks, 'natural energy' bars and supplements", "multivitamin-mineral effervescent products with added guarana"], "active_constituents": ["caffeine (guarana: about 12% in an ethanolic extract and about 26% in one 500 mg powder; mate leaf: about 1.5% in one certified product)", "theobromine and theophylline (minor methylxanthines)", "condensed tannins/catechins (guarana)", "chlorogenic acids and caffeoyl derivatives, saponins (mate)", "polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons as process contaminants in smoke-dried mate"], "one_line_verdict": "Guarana acts essentially as a natural caffeine source with a small, inconsistent acute speed benefit and no proven effect beyond its caffeine, yerba mate has no objective cognitive trials at all, and both carry caffeine-labelling and, for mate drunk very hot or smoke-dried, cancer-related safety issues.", "scientific_conclusion": "Guarana. The best summary is an independent 2023 meta-analysis of 8 placebo-controlled acute trials (328 participants; 37.5-500 mg guarana, 4.3-100 mg caffeine) [hack2023]. Averaged across all cognitive measures, guarana's effect was trivial and non-significant (g = 0.076, 95% CI -0.025 to 0.176, I2 0%). Response times were modestly faster (g = 0.202, 0.062-0.343), but accuracy did not improve and trended worse with moderate heterogeneity (g = -0.077). There was no dose-response, and the authors could not tell whether caffeine or other compounds were responsible. The early positive trials came from one laboratory (Northumbria). A 75 mg extract containing only about 9 mg caffeine improved attention-task speed, with some loss of accuracy [kennedy2004]. In a 4-dose study the lower doses (37.5-75 mg) gave more positive memory and mood effects than higher doses, an inverted pattern that argues against a robust effect [haskell2007]. Multivitamin-mineral drinks with guarana improved RVIP performance and reduced mental fatigue in 129 young adults [kennedy2008], working-memory accuracy before exercise [veasey2015] and go/no-go speed (4.6%) [pomportes2015]. Because these products also contain B vitamins and other ingredients, they say little about guarana itself, and the manufacturer had links to the authors (Bayer staff were co-authors in [veasey2015]). In exercising athletes, guarana complexes sped information processing about as much as caffeine or carbohydrate [pomportes2017, pomportes2019]. Null trials: 500 mg guarana (130 mg caffeine) did not beat placebo or 100 mg caffeine on Simon or 2-back tasks [talik2024]. A 5-day commercial product had no effect on mood, anxiety or well-being [silvestrini2013]. Early Brazilian trials, acute in young adults [galduroz1994] and long-term in older adults [galduroz1996], were null. Guarana did not reduce cancer-related fatigue versus placebo (pooled -0.02, 95% CI -1.54 to 1.50) [dearaujo2021]. The only student study was an uncontrolled before-after comparison with no placebo, practice effects and consumer-perception outcomes [amaritei2025]. Yerba mate. No randomised trial measuring objective cognition was found. One 12-woman crossover gave 2 g leaf capsules (about 30 mg caffeine) against empty placebo capsules and reported higher POMS 'focus', 'energy' and 'concentration' after exercise [alkhatib2017]. A systematic review lists mood and neuroprotection among mate's effects, but 22 of 32 studies were at high risk of bias [jose2023]. Acute brewed mate did not alter metabolic, cardiovascular or endothelial responses [zaki2026, filipini2024]. Safety dominates the mate evidence. Mate drinking is associated with oesophageal squamous-cell carcinoma (OR 2.57, 95% CI 1.66-3.98) [andrici2013]. IARC classifies very hot beverages above 65 C as probably carcinogenic (2A) and mate not drunk very hot as Group 3 [loomis2016, chen2015]. Smoke-dried mate leaf contains high PAH levels [oranuba2019]. Mate infusion strongly inhibits iron absorption [pagliosa2021]. Bottom line: any cognitive benefit is caffeine's (see caffeine monograph), and plant 'natural energy' framing adds no demonstrated benefit while making caffeine dose less transparent.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Neither guarana nor yerba mate corrects a nutrient deficiency. Positive acute guarana findings are small speed effects in rested young adults, which are enhancement effects attributable mostly to caffeine [hack2023, talik2024]. Multivitamin-mineral-plus-guarana trials [kennedy2008, veasey2015, pomportes2015] cannot separate a deficiency-correcting effect of the vitamins (if any) from guarana; their participants were not selected for low status. Guarana in cancer patients is a clinical, fatigue-treatment question and was null [dearaujo2021]. Mate polyphenols inhibit non-haem iron absorption [pagliosa2021], so in iron-deficient groups mate could worsen a deficiency that itself impairs cognition.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Guarana and mate are often sold as 'natural', 'clean' or 'smooth' energy that is 'better than coffee' and 'boosts focus and memory'. The evidence does not support any advantage over an equivalent dose of caffeine. The direct comparison found neither guarana nor caffeine improved cognition at the doses tested [talik2024], the meta-analysis found no effect on accuracy [hack2023], and yerba mate has no objective cognitive trial. The 'natural' label is misleading on safety: guarana can carry more caffeine per gram than coffee (130 mg caffeine in 500 mg powder [talik2024]), and 'natural caffeine' in energy products is caffeine. Mate's tradition of very hot drinking and smoke-drying brings carcinogen-related risks [andrici2013, oranuba2019, loomis2016]. Much of the early guarana research was run by one academic group, with multivitamin combinations involving manufacturer co-authors [veasey2015]. A student study promoting guarana for 'academic performance' had no control group [amaritei2025]. Honest positioning, if used at all: 'contains caffeine from guarana (X mg per serving)'. Nothing more.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Both plants deliver caffeine (with minor theobromine and theophylline), which antagonises adenosine receptors. Guarana tannins may slow caffeine release, a common 'sustained energy' claim that has not been shown in human pharmacokinetic studies retrieved here. Mate supplies chlorogenic acids and saponins with antioxidant and metabolic actions in vitro and in animals. Neither has demonstrated a cognitive mechanism beyond caffeine. Non-caffeine compounds proposed for guarana are supported mainly by the observation that a 9 mg caffeine extract had effects [kennedy2004], a single, unreplicated finding.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Guarana seed extract/powder or mate leaf infusion -> caffeine absorption", "detail": "Caffeine content varies with product and preparation: about 12% in a guarana extract [kennedy2004], 130 mg in 500 mg guarana [talik2024], 4.3-100 mg per dose across trials [hack2023]; mate leaf about 1.5% caffeine in one certified product [alkhatib2017].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Caffeine -> adenosine A1/A2A antagonism -> faster responses, alertness", "detail": "Small response-time benefit for guarana (g 0.20) with no accuracy gain [hack2023]; equivalent to or smaller than caffeine itself [talik2024, pomportes2019].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Guarana non-caffeine constituents (tannins, saponins) -> proposed additional psychoactivity or slowed caffeine release", "detail": "Inferred from effects of a low-caffeine extract [kennedy2004] and dose patterns [haskell2007]; not confirmed when caffeine was matched [talik2024].", "evidence_level": "speculative"}, {"step": "Mate polyphenols and saponins -> antioxidant, anti-inflammatory, metabolic effects", "detail": "Mostly in vitro and animal data; human trials largely at high risk of bias [jose2023]; no acute vascular effect [filipini2024].", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Mate and guarana polyphenols -> iron chelation in the gut", "detail": "Mate infusion reduced postprandial serum iron AUC from 1449 to 173 umol h/L in hemochromatosis [pagliosa2021].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Measured human cognitive outcome", "detail": "Guarana: small speed effect, inconsistent and possibly caffeine-only [hack2023, talik2024]; mate: subjective mood only [alkhatib2017].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["adenosine receptor antagonism (caffeine)", "possible tannin-modified caffeine kinetics (unverified)", "polyphenol/chlorogenic acid antioxidant effects (preclinical)", "non-haem iron chelation", "thermal injury and PAH exposure (mate, harm pathway)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "reaction_time", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults (median n per trial 27) in 8 placebo-controlled trials; additional 2024 crossover (n = 20)", "dose_or_intake": "Guarana 37.5-500 mg (4.3-130 mg caffeine)", "duration": "single dose, tested 15-360 min", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Responses were slightly faster across tasks, with no gain and a tendency to loss in accuracy; the overall cognitive effect was trivial; a caffeine-matched comparison showed no effect of guarana or caffeine.", "effect_estimate": "Response time g = 0.202 (95% CI 0.062-0.343), accuracy g = -0.077 (-0.258 to 0.103), overall g = 0.076 (-0.025 to 0.176), I2 0% [hack2023]; no RT difference vs placebo or caffeine [talik2024]", "certainty_notes": "Self-audit downgrade from B to C: eight small RCTs (median n 27) dominated by one laboratory (risk of bias), a speed-accuracy trade-off with no accuracy gain, no dose-response, and a later caffeine-matched null trial (inconsistency); effect cannot be separated from caffeine. Practical significance for a student is negligible.", "key_study_ids": ["hack2023", "kennedy2004", "haskell2007", "talik2024"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy young adults (18-24 y) and adults in Brazilian trial", "dose_or_intake": "Guarana extract 37.5-300 mg", "duration": "single dose, up to 6 h", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Secondary memory improved more at the two lower doses than at the higher doses in one trial; memory speed improved only with the ginseng combination in another; an early trial found no cognitive effect.", "effect_estimate": "Not reported numerically in abstracts [haskell2007, kennedy2004]; null [galduroz1994]", "certainty_notes": "Inverted dose pattern, small samples, one research group, multiplicity.", "key_study_ids": ["haskell2007", "kennedy2004", "galduroz1994"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "cognitive_fatigue", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy young adults (18-24 y; n = 129) and active young men (n = 40); adults (n = 56)", "dose_or_intake": "Multivitamin-mineral effervescent drink with guarana (300 mg in two trials)", "duration": "single dose", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Combination products improved RVIP speed and accuracy and reduced mental fatigue during sustained tasks, improved numeric working memory accuracy and picture recognition speed before exercise, and sped go/no-go responses by 4.6%.", "effect_estimate": "Significant RVIP and fatigue effects, numbers not in abstract [kennedy2008]; go/no-go 4.6% +/- 0.8% faster [pomportes2015]; working memory accuracy significant [veasey2015]", "certainty_notes": "Multi-ingredient products confound guarana's contribution; manufacturer co-authorship (Bayer) in at least one; downgraded for indirectness and risk of bias.", "key_study_ids": ["kennedy2008", "veasey2015", "pomportes2015"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "processing_speed", "population": "other", "population_detail": "Trained young adults and high-level modern pentathletes during submaximal running/cycling", "dose_or_intake": "Guarana complex 300 mg ingested or 0.4 g/25 mL mouth rinse", "duration": "single session (40 min exercise)", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Guarana complexes sped information processing after exercise and improved temporal performance, about as much as caffeine (200 mg) or carbohydrate; caffeine rinse, not guarana, improved cognitive control.", "effect_estimate": "Speed after exercise p = 0.04 (guarana) vs p = 0.004 (caffeine) [pomportes2019]; 'likely' improvements by magnitude-based inference [pomportes2017]", "certainty_notes": "Very small samples (10-24), magnitude-based inference, exercise context; not generalizable to study or work.", "key_study_ids": ["pomportes2017", "pomportes2019"]}, {"timeframe": "short_term_days", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "27 healthy volunteers", "dose_or_intake": "Commercial guarana 350 mg three times daily", "duration": "5 days", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "No difference from placebo in any of 6 well-being areas, state anxiety or 16 Bond-Lader mood scales.", "effect_estimate": "All comparisons NS [silvestrini2013]", "certainty_notes": "Single-blind, small, published in a negative-results journal; consistent with no non-caffeine mood effect.", "key_study_ids": ["silvestrini2013"]}, {"timeframe": "short_term_days", "domain": "learning", "population": "students", "population_detail": "33 university students (mostly women), Romania", "dose_or_intake": "2 g guarana/day for 10 days, then 1.5 g green tea/day for 10 days", "duration": "10 days each", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "Uncontrolled before-after testing showed 'significant improvements' in reaction speed, memory and attention, plus consumer perceptions; with no placebo, randomisation or blinding, practice effects explain the findings.", "effect_estimate": "not reported meaningfully (pre-post only) [amaritei2025]", "certainty_notes": "Very high risk of bias; cannot support any claim.", "key_study_ids": ["amaritei2025"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "45 healthy elderly volunteers (Brazil)", "dose_or_intake": "Guarana (dose not stated in abstract)", "duration": "long-term (duration not stated in abstract)", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "No significant cognitive changes versus placebo or caffeine.", "effect_estimate": "NS [galduroz1996]", "certainty_notes": "Small, old, few details; but the only chronic trial in healthy people.", "key_study_ids": ["galduroz1996"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_fatigue", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Cancer patients (427 in 7 studies; meta-analysis of 3 breast-cancer trials)", "dose_or_intake": "Guarana supplements (e.g. purified dry extract PC-18)", "duration": "weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Guarana did not reduce cancer-related fatigue versus placebo; certainty very low.", "effect_estimate": "Mean difference -0.02 (95% CI -1.54 to 1.50), p = 0.98 [dearaujo2021]", "certainty_notes": "Clinical population; not generalizable to healthy adults; very low GRADE.", "key_study_ids": ["dearaujo2021"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "alertness", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "12 healthy active women (mate); no objective cognition trials found", "dose_or_intake": "2 g yerba mate leaf capsules (about 30 mg caffeine) vs empty capsules", "duration": "single dose, 2.5 h rest then 30 min cycling", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "Self-rated focus, energy and concentration (POMS items) higher after exercise; no objective cognitive testing; placebo not caffeine-matched.", "effect_estimate": "Focus p = 0.022, energy p = 0.008, concentration p = 0.003 [alkhatib2017]", "certainty_notes": "Tiny exploratory trial with subjective outcomes; systematic review notes predominantly high risk of bias in mate trials [jose2023].", "key_study_ids": ["alkhatib2017", "jose2023"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Any population", "dose_or_intake": "Habitual yerba mate", "duration": "weeks to years", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No controlled trial or cohort analysis of yerba mate and cognition was found.", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "Evidence gap; neuroprotection claims rest on preclinical data.", "key_study_ids": ["jose2023"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "other", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "South American and other populations in case-control studies of oesophageal cancer", "dose_or_intake": "Habitual mate drinking, typically very hot", "duration": "years", "grade": "C", "direction": "harm", "effect_summary": "Mate drinking, and hot beverages generally, are associated with oesophageal squamous-cell carcinoma, with dose-dependence; the risk is attributed mainly to temperature (IARC 2A for very hot beverages above 65 C) and possibly PAHs.", "effect_estimate": "Ever vs never OR 2.57 (1.66-3.98); high consumption OR 2.76 (1.33-5.73) [andrici2013]; hot beverages and foods OR 1.82 (1.53-2.17) [chen2015]", "certainty_notes": "Observational case-control data with heterogeneity and publication bias; strong biological plausibility (thermal injury, PAHs); IARC hazard classification [loomis2016].", "key_study_ids": ["andrici2013", "chen2015", "loomis2016", "oranuba2019"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "Guarana extract 75 mg (about 9 mg caffeine)", "onset": "1 h", "duration_studied": "6 h", "domain": "attention speed, sentence verification, serial subtraction", "population": "28 healthy young adults (18-24)", "effect_estimate": "Improved speed, some reduced accuracy", "certainty": "low", "safety": "not reported", "study_ids": ["kennedy2004"]}, {"dose": "Guarana extract PC-102 37.5, 75, 150, 300 mg", "onset": "1 h", "duration_studied": "6 h", "domain": "secondary memory, alertness, contentment", "population": "26 healthy adults", "effect_estimate": "Lower doses more positive than higher", "certainty": "low", "safety": "not reported", "study_ids": ["haskell2007"]}, {"dose": "Multivitamin-mineral drink + guarana", "onset": "30 min", "duration_studied": "60 min sustained tasks", "domain": "RVIP, serial subtractions, mental fatigue", "population": "129 young adults (parallel groups)", "effect_estimate": "Better RVIP speed/accuracy, less fatigue", "certainty": "low (multi-ingredient)", "safety": "not reported", "study_ids": ["kennedy2008"]}, {"dose": "500 mg guarana (130 mg caffeine) vs 100 mg caffeine", "onset": "60 min", "duration_studied": "single post-dose session plus post-exercise", "domain": "Simon task, 2-back, HRV, mood", "population": "20 adults", "effect_estimate": "No effect of either", "certainty": "moderate-low", "safety": "no HRV change", "study_ids": ["talik2024"]}, {"dose": "Guarana complex 300 mg during running; 0.4 g/25 mL mouth rinse", "onset": "during exercise", "duration_studied": "40 min exercise", "domain": "information processing, time perception", "population": "athletes (n = 10 and 24)", "effect_estimate": "Faster processing similar to caffeine/carbohydrate", "certainty": "low", "safety": "not reported", "study_ids": ["pomportes2017", "pomportes2019"]}, {"dose": "2 g yerba mate leaf (about 30 mg caffeine)", "onset": "2 h", "duration_studied": "3 h", "domain": "POMS focus/energy/concentration (subjective)", "population": "12 active women", "effect_estimate": "Subjective increases p 0.003-0.022", "certainty": "very low", "safety": "no HR difference", "study_ids": ["alkhatib2017"]}, {"dose": "Brewed yerba mate (two brands, similar caffeine) vs water and 135 mg caffeine", "onset": "pre-exercise", "duration_studied": "graded cycling", "domain": "energy expenditure, substrate oxidation, HR (no cognition)", "population": "29 adults", "effect_estimate": "No effects", "certainty": "moderate-low", "safety": "no HR change", "study_ids": ["zaki2026"]}, {"dose": "200 mL mate infusion with an 11.4 mg iron meal", "onset": "same meal", "duration_studied": "4 h serum iron", "domain": "iron absorption", "population": "14 HFE hemochromatosis patients", "effect_estimate": "Serum iron AUC 173 vs 1449 umol h/L (p < 0.001)", "certainty": "moderate", "safety": "no adverse effects", "study_ids": ["pagliosa2021"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "5 days", "population": "27 healthy volunteers", "outcome": "well-being, anxiety, mood", "effect_estimate": "No effect", "evidence_quality": "single-blind small RCT", "limitations": "Single-blind", "study_ids": ["silvestrini2013"]}, {"duration": "10 days per product", "population": "33 students", "outcome": "reaction speed, memory, attention", "effect_estimate": "Pre-post improvements (uncontrolled)", "evidence_quality": "very low", "limitations": "No control group", "study_ids": ["amaritei2025"]}, {"duration": "not stated in abstract (long-term)", "population": "45 healthy elderly", "outcome": "cognition", "effect_estimate": "No change", "evidence_quality": "small RCT", "limitations": "Old, few details", "study_ids": ["galduroz1996"]}, {"duration": "weeks", "population": "cancer patients (427)", "outcome": "cancer-related fatigue", "effect_estimate": "MD -0.02 (-1.54 to 1.50)", "evidence_quality": "very low GRADE", "limitations": "Clinical, heterogeneous", "study_ids": ["dearaujo2021"]}, {"duration": "habitual (years)", "population": "1565 ESCC cases in 9 studies", "outcome": "oesophageal squamous-cell carcinoma", "effect_estimate": "OR 2.57 (1.66-3.98)", "evidence_quality": "observational, publication bias", "limitations": "Confounding by temperature, smoking, alcohol", "study_ids": ["andrici2013"]}, {"duration": "habitual", "population": "mate products (10 studies of PAH content)", "outcome": "PAH content and transfer to infusion", "effect_estimate": "B[a]P mostly >25 ng/g, up to 600 ng/g; transfer 1-50%", "evidence_quality": "analytical review", "limitations": "Variable methods", "study_ids": ["oranuba2019"]}], "safety": {"summary": "For both plants the main pharmacological risk is caffeine, and the caffeine content of botanical products is variable and often undisclosed. A 500 mg guarana capsule contained 130 mg caffeine [talik2024], and an extract was about 12% caffeine [kennedy2004]. EFSA limits apply to caffeine from these sources [efsa2015]. Yerba mate adds specific hazards. Drinking it very hot is associated with oesophageal squamous-cell carcinoma (OR 2.57) [andrici2013], and IARC classifies very hot beverages as Group 2A [loomis2016, chen2015]. Smoke-dried leaf carries high PAH levels (benzo[a]pyrene up to 600 ng/g, with 1-50% transfer to the infusion) [oranuba2019]. Mate polyphenols strongly reduce postprandial iron absorption [pagliosa2021]. Guarana is common in energy drinks, so stacking with other caffeine sources is a realistic risk in students.", "adverse_effects": ["caffeine effects: insomnia, jitteriness, tachycardia, anxiety (dose-dependent)", "oesophageal injury and cancer risk with very hot mate [andrici2013, loomis2016]", "PAH exposure from smoke-dried mate [oranuba2019]", "reduced iron absorption [pagliosa2021]", "speed-accuracy trade-off (faster but less accurate responses) after guarana [kennedy2004, hack2023]"], "contraindications": ["pregnancy above 200 mg caffeine/day from all sources [efsa2015]", "children and adolescents (energy-drink formats)", "arrhythmia, uncontrolled hypertension, anxiety or panic disorders", "iron-deficiency anaemia (mate with meals)", "concurrent energy drinks or caffeine supplements"], "drug_interactions": ["as for caffeine: CYP1A2 inhibitors (fluvoxamine, ciprofloxacin) raise caffeine levels; additive with stimulants (see caffeine monograph; not re-verified here)", "iron supplements: absorption reduced by mate polyphenols [pagliosa2021]"], "upper_limits": "No botanical-specific upper limits. Caffeine limits apply: 200 mg single dose and 400 mg/day for healthy adults, 200 mg/day in pregnancy, 3 mg/kg/day for children [efsa2015]. The product label must declare total caffeine including caffeine from guarana or mate.", "sleep_impact": "Caffeine-dependent, as for coffee. No guarana- or mate-specific sleep studies were retrieved. 'Slow-release' claims for guarana tannins were not confirmed by human pharmacokinetic studies in this review.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Mate: decades of observational exposure data focused on cancer [andrici2013]. Guarana: one small long-term trial in elderly people [galduroz1996] and weeks-long trials in cancer patients [dearaujo2021]. No long-term safety RCTs in young adults.", "daily_use_appropriate": "uncertain", "special_populations": "Pregnant and breastfeeding women, children and adolescents (energy drinks), people with cardiac arrhythmia, hypertension, anxiety disorders or insomnia, iron-deficient women, heavy hot-mate drinkers, and smokers and alcohol users (synergistic oesophageal risk).", "specialist_review_needed": ["toxicologist (PAH content of mate supply; caffeine standardisation of guarana)", "cardiologist (energy-product formats)", "oncologist/epidemiologist (hot-beverage messaging)", "clinical nutritionist (iron)", "regulatory specialist (energy-drink classification in Morocco and EU caffeine labelling)"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "Acceptable only as a declared caffeine source within EFSA limits. Guarana products must be batch-tested and labelled for total caffeine. Mate must be air-dried or PAH-tested, served below 65 C (or cold, as terere), and not positioned for iron-deficient groups at mealtimes. Energy-drink style formats aimed at students carry the highest risk."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Guarana and yerba mate are traditional foods with a history of consumption and are not novel foods (caffeine monograph; specific Novel Food Catalogue entries not re-checked here). Their caffeine counts toward Regulation (EU) 1169/2011 Annex III point 4. Beverages above 150 mg/L caffeine from any source, unless coffee- or tea-based and named as such, must carry 'High caffeine content. Not recommended for children or pregnant or breast-feeding women' plus mg/100 mL. Whether a mate infusion counts as 'tea' for this exemption is uncertain, and the conservative approach is to assume it does not (project synthesis X4). Article 13 health claims for botanicals remain largely 'on hold' since 2010. Guarana or mate claims on energy, cognition or fatigue are presumed to be in that grey zone, but the specific claim IDs and status were not verified here. The caffeine alertness and concentration claims were vetoed in 2016 and are not authorised.", "us": "'Mate (Ilex paraguariensis St. Hil.)' is listed in 21 CFR 182.20 (natural extractives generally recognised as safe). Guarana (Paullinia cupana) was not found in 21 CFR 182.20 (Cornell LII copy, accessed 2026-10-25), so its GRAS basis for use in conventional foods was not established here. It is widely used in beverages, possibly under self-affirmed GRAS, and this is unverified. In supplements guarana is a botanical dietary ingredient; its pre-1994 status was not verified. Structure/function claims such as 'supports energy' need the DSHEA disclaimer and FTC-level substantiation, which the evidence barely supports beyond caffeine. Caffeine from botanicals should be declared, and state rules on energy-product sales to minors may apply (X5).", "morocco": "Decree 2-19-13 defines energy drinks as containing 14.5-32 mg caffeine/100 mL together with other stimulants or added nutrients, and requires seven warnings including 'not suitable for' children and pregnant women (project synthesis X6; publication details unverified). A guarana-based beverage is therefore likely to be regulated as an energy drink. In April 2026 a consumer observatory proposed banning sales of energy drinks to minors (recommendation only; caffeine monograph). Yerba mate is not a traditional Moroccan beverage, and no specific rules were found. Status: uncertain; consult ONSSA.", "permitted_claims_examples": [], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "Traditional seed, leaf and infusion forms are not novel in the EU. New highly concentrated extracts or isolated fractions may need checks (not researched). US: guarana's conventional-food GRAS basis was not verified; mate is listed in 21 CFR 182.20.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Technically feasible but strategically weak. Guarana powder is a 'natural' caffeine source for bars, drinks and gums, but it is bitter and astringent and its caffeine varies by batch. Yerba mate suits iced tea (terere-style) or hot infusions but has little cultural presence in Morocco. Neither brings a demonstrated cognitive advantage over standardised caffeine from coffee or tea, and both complicate labelling.", "formats": ["guarana powder in energy bars or chews (with declared caffeine)", "cold-brew yerba mate (terere) RTD", "yerba mate tea bags (air-dried leaf)", "multi-ingredient effervescent products (not recommended: confounded evidence)"], "stability": "Caffeine is stable. Guarana tannins and mate chlorogenic acids degrade with heat and oxidation. Smoke-dried versus air-dried mate processing determines PAH content [oranuba2019].", "taste": "Guarana is bitter and astringent. Mate is grassy and bitter, with smoky notes in smoke-dried types. Both usually need sweetening or flavouring, which conflicts with low-sugar goals.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Caffeine from guarana and mate is absorbed like other caffeine. Dissolved guarana gave larger response-time effects than capsules (g 0.273 vs 0.106) [hack2023]. Polyphenols chelate iron at the same meal [pagliosa2021].", "dose_feasibility": "Doses used in guarana trials (37.5-500 mg extract or powder; 4-130 mg caffeine) fit easily into a food serving [hack2023, talik2024]. The 2 g mate leaf dose fits a tea bag [alkhatib2017]. Feasibility is not the constraint; the absence of a benefit beyond caffeine is.", "cost_availability": "Guarana is imported mainly from Brazil, and yerba mate from Argentina, Brazil and Paraguay. Both are available in the EU and US; availability in Morocco is limited (not researched).", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 3, "effect_magnitude": 2.5, "safety": 6, "reproducibility": 3.5, "food_suitability": 6.5, "cost_access": 7, "rationale": "evidence_quality 3: guarana's best outcome (acute response time) is grade C after downgrading for single-lab dominance and a null caffeine-matched trial; all other guarana outcomes are C or D, and yerba mate has no objective cognitive evidence (grade D). effect_magnitude 2.5: speed g about 0.2 without accuracy gain, a trivial overall effect, and nothing beyond caffeine. safety 6: caffeine is manageable, but caffeine content is variable and hidden in 'natural energy' products, mate carries hot-beverage and PAH cancer associations, and iron absorption is inhibited. reproducibility 3.5: early positive guarana findings were not reproduced in independent or later trials [talik2024, galduroz1994, silvestrini2013]. food_suitability 6.5: easy to incorporate, but bitter, poorly standardised, energy-drink associations, and little cultural fit in Morocco for mate. cost_access 7: imported but inexpensive commodities."}, "research_gaps": ["Caffeine-matched, preregistered trials of guarana versus pure caffeine in healthy young adults and students (multiple doses, accuracy and speed outcomes)", "Any randomised trial of yerba mate on objective cognition, with a caffeine-matched control", "Human pharmacokinetic studies testing the 'slow-release caffeine' claim for guarana", "PAH levels in mate infusions as consumed (gourd re-infusion) and in air-dried versus smoke-dried products", "Caffeine and PAH content of guarana- and mate-containing products sold in Morocco", "Results of the registered multi-ingredient 'mental performance' trial NCT00144235 (n = 412, completed 2005, no results posted; returned by the guarana ClinicalTrials.gov query, composition not verified)"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 4.3, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": "C", "best_grade_chronic": null, "best_grade_young_adults": "C", "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": null, "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"reaction_time": "C", "cognitive_fatigue": "C", "processing_speed": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 11, "n_studies": 25, "n_verified": 25, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 11, "n_systematic_reviews": 2, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["harm", "inconsistent", "insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "blueberry-anthocyanins", "name": "Blueberry anthocyanins and blueberry powder", "category": "G", "category_name": "Polyphenols and plant bioactives", "aliases": ["blueberry", "wild blueberry (lowbush, Vaccinium angustifolium)", "highbush blueberry (Vaccinium corymbosum)", "freeze-dried blueberry powder", "wild blueberry extract (WBE, e.g. ThinkBlue)", "blueberry concentrate", "blueberry anthocyanins", "blueberry (poly)phenols"], "food_sources": ["fresh or frozen blueberries (highbush, cultivated) - the usual research dose of '1 cup' is about 148 g fresh", "wild (lowbush) blueberries, typically sold frozen; richer in anthocyanins per gram than most highbush cultivars", "freeze-dried whole blueberry powder: 24 g/day described as 1 cup fresh [miller2018]; 26 g/day described as 178 g fresh and 302 mg anthocyanins [wood2023]", "blueberry juice concentrate (30 mL/day providing 387 mg anthocyanidins in [bowtell2017])", "standardised blueberry extracts in capsules (100-900 mg/day in trials)", "processed blueberry products (jams, canned berries, juices) - anthocyanins largely lost with heat and storage [brownmiller2008]"], "active_constituents": ["anthocyanins (glycosides of delphinidin, malvidin, petunidin, cyanidin, peonidin), the main proposed actives", "chlorogenic acid and other hydroxycinnamic acids", "flavonols (quercetin, myricetin glycosides)", "proanthocyanidins and flavan-3-ols", "gut-microbial and phase II metabolites (hippuric, ferulic, syringic, vanillic and other phenolic acids), probably the circulating bioactive forms", "fibre and sugars (relevant to placebo matching and to glycaemic effects)"], "one_line_verdict": "Blueberries are a safe, food-compatible source of anthocyanins that reliably improve a vascular marker (FMD about +1 percentage point), but cognitive benefits are small, inconsistent and mostly limited to older or memory-complaining adults in small, often industry-linked trials, while the evidence for healthy students and young knowledge workers is a handful of acute single-session studies with mixed results and no chronic trial.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. Blueberry (poly)phenols are absorbed and extensively metabolised; circulating phenolic metabolites rise 1-2 h and again about 6 h after intake, in parallel with improvements in flow-mediated dilation (FMD) that plateau at about 766 mg total polyphenols [rodriguezmateos2013]. Chronically, a meta-analysis of 14 RCTs (632 healthy participants) found FMD +1.11% (95% CI 0.50-1.72, I2 23%) but no effect on executive function (SMD 0.21, -0.05 to 0.46) or mood [he2026]; the best single trial (61 healthy 65-80-year-olds, 26 g/day freeze-dried wild blueberry for 12 weeks) found FMD +0.86% and 24-h systolic blood pressure -3.6 mmHg, with better immediate recall and task-switch accuracy but no change in cerebral blood flow [wood2023]. Cognition in older and at-risk adults. Small RCTs report gains on selected measures: fewer CVLT repetition errors and lower switch cost after 90 days of 24 g/day (n=37) [miller2018]; better delayed word recognition only with an extract at 3 months but not with powders or at 6 months (n=122) [whyte2018]; faster processing speed after 6 months in older adults with cognitive complaints (n=86) [cheatham2023]; improved memory discrimination at 24 weeks in subjective impairment [mcnamara2018]; lexical access and fewer intrusions in insulin-resistant midlife adults (27 analysed, industry-funded, fibre-free placebo) [krikorian2022]. Pooled across 9 RCTs in MCI or subjective decline, episodic memory improved by SMD 0.34 (0.11-0.57) but working memory and processing speed did not [dasilva2025]. Null or largely null results include a 24-week trial of 20 g/day in 57 older adults (no effect on p-tau181, NfL or cognitive tests) [guardino2026], the powder arms of [whyte2018], the primary endpoint of a 215-person grape-blueberry extract trial [bensalem2019], and a formal re-analysis concluding there is no meta-analytic evidence of benefit [brydges2020]. Imaging changes (perfusion, BOLD activation) are biomarkers and did not consistently translate into better performance [bowtell2017, boespflug2018]. Healthy young adults and students. Evidence is acute only: mixed berries maintained executive-function accuracy over 6 h (n=40, single-blind, industry-funded) [whyte2019]; a 600 mg grape-blueberry extract improved serial subtraction in 30 students [philip2019]; a single blueberry drink raised positive affect in 21 young adults [khalid2017]; but a dose-ranging trial (150-450 mg anthocyanins, n=24) found no cognitive effect at 90 min despite dose-dependent glucose lowering [ellis2026]. In emerging adults with depressive symptoms a single dose improved affect and executive accuracy, yet 6 weeks of supplementation was inferior to placebo on depressive symptoms by 5.8 BDI-II points [velichkov2024]. No chronic trial in healthy young adults exists. Reproducibility. Most positive trials come from three groups (Reading, Cincinnati, USDA Boston) and many were funded or supplied by blueberry industry bodies; outcomes are numerous and rarely corrected for multiplicity. Observationally, higher blueberry intake was associated with slower cognitive decline in 16,010 older women (mean difference 0.04 SU, about 1.5-2.5 years of ageing) [devore2012], which is hypothesis-generating only. Bottom line: a plausible, safe food with real vascular effects; cognitive claims for students are not supported.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Anthocyanins are not essential nutrients and there is no deficiency state, so every cognitive claim is an enhancement or prevention claim. Low habitual flavonoid intake might plausibly define a responder group (one grape-blueberry extract trial found benefit only in the lowest-performing quartile, who also ate fewer polyphenols [bensalem2019]), but no trial has prospectively stratified by baseline intake. Benefits, where seen, cluster in older adults, in people with subjective decline or MCI, and in cognitively fatiguing or post-meal test conditions [whyte2019, whyte2021, cheng2024], consistent with an effect on vascular and metabolic support rather than on cognition in rested, well-nourished young brains. The child trials (acute benefits on memory and attention in 7-10-year-olds [barfoot2019]) must not be generalised to adults, and the older-adult and MCI trials must not be generalised to students. For healthy young adults the only chronic data come from a population with depressive symptoms, and they were unfavourable [velichkov2024].", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Marketing that calls blueberries a 'brain berry', 'memory booster' or 'study superfood' rests on rodent studies and on small older-adult trials with selective positive endpoints. The evidence supports narrower statements: a blueberry serving (about 150-180 g fresh or 20-26 g freeze-dried) acutely and chronically improves endothelial function in adults; in older adults and those with memory complaints, daily blueberry for 3-6 months produced small, inconsistent improvements on some memory and executive tests (pooled SMD about 0.3 in MCI/SCD); in young adults, single doses sometimes help maintain performance over a long or post-lunch session, but a well-controlled dose-ranging trial was null and there are no chronic trials. No EU health claim for blueberries or anthocyanins and cognition (or antioxidant protection) is authorised, so EU communication must avoid brain claims. In the US, any structure/function claim such as 'supports memory' would need competent and reliable evidence in the target population, which does not exist for students. The honest positioning is as a whole-fruit ingredient in a healthy dietary pattern, not as a nootropic. Industry funding (US Highbush Blueberry Council, blueberry and extract suppliers) is common in the trial base and should be disclosed if trials are cited.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Anthocyanins are poorly absorbed intact; most are degraded and transformed by phase II enzymes and the gut microbiota into phenolic acids that circulate for many hours. These metabolites are associated with improved endothelial NO-dependent vasodilation and reduced neutrophil NADPH oxidase activity, giving a plausible route to better cerebral perfusion and neurovascular coupling. Animal work suggests direct effects on hippocampal signalling (BDNF, ERK/CREB) and neuroinflammation, but these remain unproven in humans. Human data show vascular (FMD, blood pressure) and some imaging changes (perfusion, BOLD) without consistent downstream cognitive benefit.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Oral blueberry anthocyanins and other polyphenols -> limited absorption of parent anthocyanins; extensive degradation, phase II conjugation and microbial catabolism to phenolic acids", "detail": "Plasma and urinary phenolic metabolites (hippuric, ferulic-glucuronide, syringic acid, anthocyanin glycosides) rise after acute and chronic intake [rodriguezmateos2013, wood2023]; urinary native anthocyanins tracked cognitive benefit in [mcnamara2018].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Circulating metabolites -> endothelial function (NO bioavailability, reduced neutrophil NADPH oxidase)", "detail": "FMD rises biphasically at 1-2 h and 6 h, dose-dependently up to about 766 mg polyphenols [rodriguezmateos2013]; chronic FMD +1.11% (0.50-1.72) in 14 RCTs [he2026]; 24-h SBP -3.6 mmHg in [wood2023].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Vascular effects -> cerebral perfusion and neurovascular coupling", "detail": "Regional grey-matter perfusion increased after 12 weeks of concentrate [bowtell2017] and BOLD activation increased in MCI [boespflug2018], but cerebral blood flow did not change in the larger [wood2023]; inconsistent, small, biomarker-level.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Metabolic effects -> lower postprandial glucose and insulin", "detail": "Dose-dependent reduction of postprandial glucose and insulin at >=300 mg anthocyanins in young adults [ellis2026] and after a breakfast in middle-aged adults [whyte2021]; fasting insulin fell in insulin-resistant midlife adults but the placebo lacked fibre [krikorian2022].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Direct neuronal effects (BDNF, ERK/CREB signalling, reduced neuroinflammation and oxidative stress, hippocampal plasticity)", "detail": "Shown in aged rodents and in vitro; not demonstrated in humans (serum biomarkers unchanged in [velichkov2024, guardino2026]).", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Measured human cognitive outcome", "detail": "Small, inconsistent benefits on selected episodic-memory and executive measures in older and memory-complaining adults (SMD about 0.3 in MCI/SCD [dasilva2025]); no pooled effect on executive function in healthy adults [he2026]; acute effects in young adults inconsistent [whyte2019, ellis2026].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["endothelial nitric oxide / flow-mediated dilation", "neutrophil NADPH oxidase inhibition by phenolic metabolites", "cerebral perfusion and neurovascular coupling (biomarker level)", "postprandial glucose and insulin attenuation", "gut microbial catabolism of anthocyanins to phenolic acids", "BDNF/ERK/CREB and anti-neuroinflammatory signalling (animal data only)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults 18-30 y and university students tested 1.5-6 h after a single blueberry or blueberry-containing drink", "dose_or_intake": "Mixed-berry smoothie (blueberry, strawberry, raspberry, blackberry); 600 mg grape-blueberry extract; wild blueberry drinks with 150-450 mg anthocyanins", "duration": "single dose, testing up to 6 h", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Mixed berries maintained MANT accuracy at 6 h and sped responses at 2-4 h versus a declining placebo (n=40) [whyte2019]; a grape-blueberry extract increased serial-subtraction scores 2.5-fold during a sustained cognitive effort in 30 students [philip2019]; a double-blind dose-ranging trial (0-450 mg anthocyanins, n=24) found no effect on verbal learning, Corsi or RVIP at 90 min [ellis2026].", "effect_estimate": "Not reported as standardised effects; p-values only (e.g. MANT accuracy at 6 h p=0.003 [whyte2019]; serial threes p<0.001 [philip2019]; all cognitive outcomes NS [ellis2026])", "certainty_notes": "Downgraded for risk of bias (single-blind or industry-sponsored designs, many outcomes without multiplicity control), indirectness (mixed berries and grape-blueberry blends are not blueberry alone), inconsistency (best-controlled trial null) and imprecision (n 24-40). Effects appear only late in long test days or under fatigue; practical relevance for a student is unclear.", "key_study_ids": ["whyte2019", "philip2019", "ellis2026"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy young adults 18-21 y; mood measured 2 h post-dose with PANAS", "dose_or_intake": "Single flavonoid-rich wild blueberry drink", "duration": "single dose, 2 h", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Positive affect increased 2 h after a blueberry drink in 21 healthy young adults (crossover) with no effect on negative affect [khalid2017].", "effect_estimate": "Drink x session interaction significant; standardised effect not reported [khalid2017]", "certainty_notes": "Subjective affect is not cognition; single small trial from one laboratory; transient effect. A similar acute result in a symptomatic sample is graded separately.", "key_study_ids": ["khalid2017"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "other", "population_detail": "Emerging adults (mean 20 y) with moderate-to-severe self-reported depressive symptoms; acute phase of a 6-week trial", "dose_or_intake": "Single wild blueberry drink", "duration": "single dose, 2 h", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Positive affect (p=0.026) and executive-task accuracy (p=0.025) improved 2 h after a single drink, with correlated change scores (r=0.424) [velichkov2024].", "effect_estimate": "p=0.026 (positive affect); p=0.025 (executive accuracy); no SMD [velichkov2024]", "certainty_notes": "Single trial, n=60; transient; the same participants did worse than placebo on depressive symptoms after 6 weeks, so the acute effect does not support chronic use.", "key_study_ids": ["velichkov2024"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy young adults, students and knowledge workers taking blueberry daily for weeks", "dose_or_intake": "Not studied in healthy young adults", "duration": "no trials", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No chronic RCT of blueberry on cognition in healthy young adults or students was found; the reviews identify only acute young-adult studies [travica2020, he2026]. The only chronic young-adult trial enrolled a symptomatic (depressive-symptom) sample and is graded in a separate row.", "effect_estimate": "not reported (no trials)", "certainty_notes": "Absence of evidence; extrapolation from older adults or symptomatic samples is not permitted.", "key_study_ids": ["travica2020", "he2026"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "other", "population_detail": "Emerging adults (mean age 20 y) with moderate-to-severe self-reported depressive symptoms (not a clinical diagnosis)", "dose_or_intake": "Daily wild blueberry drink", "duration": "6 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "After 6 weeks, BDI-II fell more in the placebo group than the blueberry group by 5.8 points; anxiety and anhedonia also improved more with placebo; no biomarker differences [velichkov2024].", "effect_estimate": "BDI-II difference favouring placebo 5.8 points (95% CI 0.8-10.7), p=0.023 [velichkov2024]", "certainty_notes": "Single trial of n=60; an unexpected result that may reflect chance, but it is the only chronic evidence in young adults and it is unfavourable. Not evidence of harm in healthy people.", "key_study_ids": ["velichkov2024"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Cognitively healthy middle-aged adults 40-65 y tested over an 8-h day after breakfast", "dose_or_intake": "1-cup-equivalent wild blueberry drink with breakfast", "duration": "single dose, testing up to 8 h", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "In 35 middle-aged adults, word recognition was maintained across 8 h after blueberry but declined after placebo, with fewer Go/No-Go errors on demanding trials and faster responses at 4 and 8 h [whyte2021].", "effect_estimate": "Not reported as standardised effects; several p<0.05 among many outcomes", "certainty_notes": "Single small crossover trial; many outcomes and time points; word-rejection effect lost after covariate adjustment [whyte2021].", "key_study_ids": ["whyte2021"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Healthy older adults 68-75 y tested over 6 h, including the post-lunch dip", "dose_or_intake": "Wild blueberry extract 111-888 mg (single doses)", "duration": "single dose, testing up to 6 h", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "An extract attenuated a 4-h executive-function dip seen with placebo (n=28) and sped executive reaction time at the 14:00 post-lunch dip with 222 mg (n=45); the authors describe the effects as subtle and limited to few outcomes, with no clear dose-response [cheng2024].", "effect_estimate": "Not reported as standardised effects", "certainty_notes": "Supplier employees co-authored; many outcomes and time points; extract not whole fruit.", "key_study_ids": ["cheng2024"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Cognitively healthy community-dwelling adults 60-80 y", "dose_or_intake": "20-26 g/day freeze-dried blueberry (about 1 cup fresh); 30 mL concentrate; 500-1000 mg powder or 111 mg extract; 600 mg grape-blueberry extract", "duration": "12 weeks to 6 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Positive on selected tests: immediate AVLT recall [wood2023], delayed word recognition with extract at 3 but not 6 months and not with powders [whyte2018], fewer CVLT repetition errors [miller2018], verbal recognition free recall but not the primary PAL outcome [bensalem2019]. Null on all cognitive tests at 24 weeks [guardino2026].", "effect_estimate": "Miller CVLT repetition errors p=0.031, partial eta2 0.126 [miller2018]; Wood AVLT immediate recall p<0.05 (no SMD) [wood2023]; Guardino cognitive tests NS [guardino2026]", "certainty_notes": "Downgraded for inconsistency (primary endpoints frequently null, different test hits in each trial), imprecision (n 37-122 except Bensalem), risk of bias (multiplicity, industry supply) and likely publication bias (registered trials NCT04049162 and NCT04935099 completed without posted results).", "key_study_ids": ["wood2023", "whyte2018", "miller2018", "guardino2026", "bensalem2019", "brydges2020"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Healthy older adults 60-80 y; meta-analysis pooled healthy individuals of all ages", "dose_or_intake": "24-26 g/day freeze-dried blueberry or 387 mg anthocyanidin concentrate", "duration": "12 weeks to 6 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Lower task-switch cost [miller2018] and better task-switch accuracy [wood2023]; 2-back working memory improvement of borderline significance [bowtell2017]; pooled executive function in healthy individuals not significant [he2026].", "effect_estimate": "SMD 0.21 (95% CI -0.05 to 0.46), I2 42% [he2026]; switch cost p=0.033, partial eta2 0.09 [miller2018]", "certainty_notes": "Pooled estimate imprecise and crosses zero; single-trial positives on secondary measures.", "key_study_ids": ["he2026", "miller2018", "wood2023", "bowtell2017"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "older_adults_mci", "population_detail": "Older adults with MCI or subjective cognitive decline (SCD), and insulin-resistant midlife adults 50-65 y with SCD", "dose_or_intake": "Wild blueberry juice; 12-25 g/day freeze-dried whole blueberry powder (0.5-1 cup equivalent)", "duration": "12-24 weeks (meta-analysis of 9 RCTs)", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Pooled episodic memory SMD 0.34 in MCI/SCD (9 RCTs, n=513) [dasilva2025]; individual trials report improved paired-associate learning (uncontrolled n=9) [krikorian2010], memory discrimination [mcnamara2018], fewer intrusion errors and better lexical access [krikorian2022]; BOLD activation rose without working-memory gain [boespflug2018].", "effect_estimate": "Episodic memory SMD 0.34 (0.11-0.57); working memory SMD 0.09 (-0.21 to 0.39) [dasilva2025]; COWA letter fluency Cohen's f 0.66, p=0.003 [krikorian2022]", "certainty_notes": "Downgraded for risk of bias (one laboratory supplies most trials, industry-funded or supplied products, placebo not matched for fibre in [krikorian2022], uncontrolled design in [krikorian2010]), imprecision and probable selective reporting across many tests. Practical meaning of SMD 0.3 on selected tests is modest.", "key_study_ids": ["dasilva2025", "krikorian2010", "mcnamara2018", "krikorian2022", "boespflug2018"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "processing_speed", "population": "older_adults_mci", "population_detail": "Adults 65-80 y with cognitive complaints by MoCA", "dose_or_intake": "Daily lyophilised wild blueberry powder", "duration": "6 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Processing speed (CANTAB and ERP latency) improved versus placebo in one 6-month trial (n=86), restoring speed to a reference-group level [cheatham2023]; pooled processing speed across MCI/SCD trials was not significant [dasilva2025].", "effect_estimate": "Pooled SMD -0.33 (-0.85 to 0.19), NS [dasilva2025]; single-trial effect size not extracted [cheatham2023]", "certainty_notes": "Single positive trial versus non-significant pooled estimate; abstract-level access only.", "key_study_ids": ["cheatham2023", "dasilva2025"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cerebral_blood_flow", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Healthy older adults 65-80 y (MRI arterial spin labelling); BIOMARKER, not cognition", "dose_or_intake": "30 mL concentrate (387 mg anthocyanidins) or 26 g freeze-dried powder (302 mg anthocyanins)", "duration": "12 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Parietal and occipital grey-matter perfusion increased with concentrate (n=26) [bowtell2017]; no change in cerebral blood flow in the larger trial (n=61) [wood2023].", "effect_estimate": "Parietal perfusion +5.0 +/- 1.8% vs -2.9 +/- 2.4% (p=0.013); occipital +8.0 vs -0.7% (p=0.031) [bowtell2017]; CBF NS [wood2023]", "certainty_notes": "Biomarker only; small samples; inconsistent between trials; perfusion change is not a cognitive benefit.", "key_study_ids": ["bowtell2017", "wood2023"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "vascular_health", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults across ages (meta-analysis) and healthy older adults; endothelial function by FMD and blood pressure", "dose_or_intake": "About 150-180 g fresh-equivalent per day (20-26 g freeze-dried; about 300 mg anthocyanins)", "duration": "1-12 weeks (meta-analysis); 12 weeks (largest trial)", "grade": "B", "direction": "benefit", "effect_summary": "FMD improves by about 1 percentage point; blood pressure in pooled data unchanged, though 24-h ambulatory SBP fell in one trial [he2026, wood2023]; an acute dose-response study shows FMD gains plateau above about 766 mg total polyphenols [rodriguezmateos2013].", "effect_estimate": "FMD +1.11% (95% CI 0.50-1.72, I2 23%); SBP -0.11 mmHg (-2.82 to 2.60) [he2026]; FMD +0.86% (0.56-1.17), 24-h SBP -3.59 mmHg (-6.95 to -0.23) [wood2023]", "certainty_notes": "Consistent and reasonably precise for FMD; downgraded once for indirectness (vascular surrogate, not cognition or hard outcomes) and possible publication bias. This is the most robust finding but it is not a brain outcome.", "key_study_ids": ["he2026", "wood2023", "rodriguezmateos2013"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_decline", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Nurses' Health Study women aged >=70 y with long-term food-frequency data", "dose_or_intake": "Highest versus lowest category of habitual blueberry intake (category cut-offs not extracted from the abstract)", "duration": "observational, 2 follow-up cognitive assessments over about 6 years", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Greater blueberry intake was associated with slower decline in a global cognitive score, equivalent to delaying cognitive ageing by up to about 2.5 years [devore2012].", "effect_estimate": "Mean difference in decline 0.04 standard units (95% CI 0.01-0.07), p-trend 0.014 [devore2012]", "certainty_notes": "Observational: residual confounding by overall diet quality, education and socioeconomic status is likely; cannot exceed C.", "key_study_ids": ["devore2012"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "children_adolescents", "population_detail": "Healthy children 7-10 y (kept separate; not generalisable to adults)", "dose_or_intake": "Wild blueberry drink, 15-30 g freeze-dried powder (about 253 mg anthocyanins in [barfoot2019])", "duration": "single dose, 1-6 h", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Improved word recall and faster attention-task responses 2 h after a blueberry drink in 54 children, with no effect on reading [barfoot2019]; reviews describe consistent but small acute benefits in children from one laboratory [hein2019, travica2020].", "effect_estimate": "Not reported as standardised effects; p<0.05 on AVLT and MANT [barfoot2019]", "certainty_notes": "Single-laboratory programme (Reading), single-blind in [barfoot2019], small samples; children only.", "key_study_ids": ["barfoot2019", "hein2019", "travica2020"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "All adult populations and children pooled across blueberry or flavonoid trials (systematic reviews)", "dose_or_intake": "Various blueberry and berry products", "duration": "acute to 6 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "A broad flavonoid meta-analysis (80 studies) gives a small overall effect with berries g=0.149 [cheng2022]; narrative reviews report benefits on some memory measures [travica2020, hein2019], whereas a re-analysis letter found no meta-analytic evidence of benefit for cognition or mood [brydges2020] and the newest meta-analysis in healthy people found none for executive function [he2026].", "effect_estimate": "Berries subgroup g=0.149 (p=0.009) [cheng2022]; executive function SMD 0.21 (-0.05 to 0.46) [he2026]", "certainty_notes": "Heterogeneous populations, products and tests; the small pooled effect is compatible with selective reporting.", "key_study_ids": ["cheng2022", "travica2020", "hein2019", "brydges2020", "he2026"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "400 mL mixed-berry smoothie (equal blueberry, strawberry, raspberry, blackberry)", "onset": "2 h (RT benefit); 6 h (accuracy maintained)", "duration_studied": "6 h", "domain": "executive function (MANT, task switching), mood", "population": "healthy young adults 20-30 y (n=40, between-subjects)", "effect_estimate": "Accuracy higher at 6 h (p=0.003); faster RT at 2 and 4 h; no SMD reported", "certainty": "low (single-blind, industry-sponsored, mixed berries)", "safety": "no adverse events reported", "study_ids": ["whyte2019"]}, {"dose": "Wild blueberry drink 0, 150, 300, 450 mg anthocyanins with a high-carbohydrate breakfast", "onset": "tested at 90 min", "duration_studied": "3 h", "domain": "verbal learning, Corsi, RVIP", "population": "healthy adults, mean age 28 y (n=24, crossover)", "effect_estimate": "No cognitive effect at any dose; glucose and insulin lowered at >=300 mg", "certainty": "moderate for null (double-blind, dose-ranging, small)", "safety": "none reported", "study_ids": ["ellis2026"]}, {"dose": "600 mg grape and blueberry polyphenol extract", "onset": "90 min", "duration_studied": "1 h cognitive demand battery", "domain": "working memory (serial subtraction), sustained attention (RVIP)", "population": "healthy students (n=30, crossover)", "effect_estimate": "Serial threes net score 2.5-fold higher (p<0.001); RVIP accuracy trend (p=0.058); FMD unchanged", "certainty": "low (industry co-authors, blend not blueberry alone)", "safety": "not reported in abstract", "study_ids": ["philip2019"]}, {"dose": "Single wild blueberry drink", "onset": "2 h", "duration_studied": "2 h", "domain": "positive and negative affect (PANAS)", "population": "young adults 18-21 y (n=21, crossover); emerging adults with depressive symptoms (n=60)", "effect_estimate": "Positive affect increased; negative affect unchanged", "certainty": "low (subjective mood, small)", "safety": "none reported", "study_ids": ["khalid2017", "velichkov2024"]}, {"dose": "1-cup-equivalent wild blueberry drink with breakfast", "onset": "maintenance across day; Go/No-Go RT faster at 4 and 8 h", "duration_studied": "8 h", "domain": "episodic memory (AVLT), executive function (Go/No-Go)", "population": "middle-aged adults 40-65 y (n=35, crossover)", "effect_estimate": "p<0.05 on selected measures; standardised effects not reported", "certainty": "low", "safety": "none reported", "study_ids": ["whyte2021"]}, {"dose": "Wild blueberry extract 111-888 mg (ROAB) or 222 mg (BEAT)", "onset": "4-6 h (post-lunch dip)", "duration_studied": "6 h", "domain": "executive function, episodic memory, blood pressure", "population": "healthy older adults 68-75 y (n=28 and n=45, crossover)", "effect_estimate": "Attenuated executive dip at 4 h; faster executive RT at 14:00 with 222 mg; most outcomes null", "certainty": "low (supplier co-authors, many outcomes)", "safety": "acute BP reduction with 222 mg; no adverse events reported", "study_ids": ["cheng2024"]}, {"dose": "200 mL wild blueberry drink (253 mg anthocyanins)", "onset": "2 h", "duration_studied": "2 h", "domain": "verbal memory, attention, reading", "population": "children 7-10 y (n=54) - not generalisable to adults", "effect_estimate": "More words recalled; faster MANT RT; reading unchanged", "certainty": "low-moderate (children only)", "safety": "none reported", "study_ids": ["barfoot2019"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "90 days", "population": "healthy older adults 60-75 y (n=37)", "outcome": "executive function, verbal learning, gait, balance", "effect_estimate": "CVLT repetition errors p=0.031 (partial eta2 0.126); switch cost p=0.033 (partial eta2 0.09); gait/balance null", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Small; many outcomes; a later metabolite analysis of the same trial (Rutledge 2021, PMID 33331835) linked phenolics to the error measures", "study_ids": ["miller2018"]}, {"duration": "12 weeks", "population": "healthy older adults 65-80 y (n=61)", "outcome": "FMD, BP, cognition, CBF, microbiome", "effect_estimate": "FMD +0.86% (0.56-1.17); 24-h SBP -3.59 mmHg; immediate recall and task-switch accuracy improved (p<0.05); CBF unchanged", "evidence_quality": "moderate for vascular, low for cognition", "limitations": "Cognition secondary; full text not accessible to the agent; erratum published 2025", "study_ids": ["wood2023"]}, {"duration": "6 months", "population": "older adults 65-80 y (n=122)", "outcome": "episodic and working memory, BP", "effect_estimate": "Extract 111 mg: better delayed word recognition at 3 months only; powders 500/1000 mg null; lower SBP with extract", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Sponsor helped design and write; effect not sustained at 6 months", "study_ids": ["whyte2018"]}, {"duration": "24 weeks", "population": "cognitively healthy older adults, mean 73 y (n=57)", "outcome": "serum p-tau181, NfL, GFAP, BP, computerised cognition", "effect_estimate": "No effect on p-tau181, NfL or cognitive tests; diastolic BP reduced", "evidence_quality": "moderate for null (small)", "limitations": "Underpowered for cognition; biomarker-focused", "study_ids": ["guardino2026"]}, {"duration": "6 months", "population": "adults 65-80 y with cognitive complaints (n=86 randomised plus 45 reference)", "outcome": "CANTAB processing speed, ERP", "effect_estimate": "Processing speed improved vs placebo; effect sizes not in abstract", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Abstract only; multiple CANTAB outcomes", "study_ids": ["cheatham2023"]}, {"duration": "24 weeks", "population": "older adults with subjective cognitive impairment", "outcome": "memory discrimination, cognitive symptoms", "effect_estimate": "Blueberry: memory discrimination p=0.04, fewer symptoms p=0.05; fish oil + blueberry combination no benefit", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Borderline p-values; illogical null for combination arm", "study_ids": ["mcnamara2018"]}, {"duration": "12 weeks", "population": "insulin-resistant adults 50-65 y with SCD (27 analysed)", "outcome": "executive ability, memory, insulin", "effect_estimate": "COWA letter fluency f=0.66 (p=0.003); CVLT intrusions f=0.20 (p=0.04); fasting insulin reduced; learning, recall, recognition null", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Funded and supplied by US Highbush Blueberry Council; placebo lacked fibre; 18% attrition", "study_ids": ["krikorian2022"]}, {"duration": "6 weeks", "population": "emerging adults with depressive symptoms (n=60)", "outcome": "depressive symptoms (BDI-II), executive function, biomarkers", "effect_estimate": "BDI-II reduction greater with placebo by 5.8 points (0.8-10.7)", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Single trial; unexpected direction", "study_ids": ["velichkov2024"]}, {"duration": "12 weeks", "population": "healthy older adults (n=26)", "outcome": "fMRI activation, ASL perfusion, cognition", "effect_estimate": "Perfusion +5.0% parietal, +8.0% occipital vs placebo; 2-back p=0.05", "evidence_quality": "very low for cognition", "limitations": "Tiny sample; biomarker focus", "study_ids": ["bowtell2017"]}, {"duration": "observational, long-term diet; cognitive follow-up about 6 years", "population": "16,010 older women (Nurses' Health Study)", "outcome": "rate of cognitive decline", "effect_estimate": "Mean difference 0.04 SU (0.01-0.07) highest vs lowest blueberry intake", "evidence_quality": "low (observational)", "limitations": "Residual confounding; food-frequency measurement error", "study_ids": ["devore2012"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Blueberries are a common food with no known toxicity at dietary or trial doses (20-26 g/day freeze-dried, about 1 cup fresh, for up to 6 months). Trials report no notable adverse events, though reporting is often incomplete. Concerns relate to products, not the fruit: sugar load in juices and sweetened formats, gastrointestinal effects from fibre-rich powders at high doses, possible adulteration of extracts with cheaper anthocyanin sources or synthetic dyes (documented for the related bilberry market), and pesticide residues in non-organic fruit (not reviewed here). One trial in emerging adults with depressive symptoms found 6 weeks of blueberry inferior to placebo on depressive symptoms, which is not a demonstrated harm but argues against mood claims [velichkov2024].", "adverse_effects": ["None characteristic at food doses; trial reporting is sparse", "Possible GI discomfort (bloating) with large amounts of fibre-rich powder (general expectation, not quantified in the trials reviewed)", "Sugar and energy load with juices, concentrates and sweetened powders"], "contraindications": ["Known allergy to blueberries (rare)", "Concentrated extracts: no safety data in pregnancy or lactation"], "drug_interactions": ["No clinically documented interaction for whole blueberries at food doses was found in this search", "Concentrated polyphenol extracts: theoretical additive effects with antihypertensive drugs (acute BP lowering observed with extract [cheng2024]) and with glucose-lowering drugs (postprandial glucose and insulin reductions [ellis2026]); not clinically documented", "Patients on warfarin should keep berry intake consistent; cranberry (a related Vaccinium) is listed among foods with reported warfarin interactions (see other-berry-polyphenols monograph)"], "upper_limits": "No tolerable upper intake level exists for anthocyanins or blueberries. Anthocyanins (E 163) are authorised food colours; EFSA re-evaluated them in 2013 (doi 10.2903/j.efsa.2013.3145, title verified; conclusions not re-read here). Trial doses up to about 26 g/day freeze-dried powder (about 300 mg anthocyanins) or 900 mg extract for 6 months were used without reported harm.", "sleep_impact": "No stimulant content; no sleep effects reported or expected.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Up to 6 months in RCTs (n up to 215 including blends); lifelong dietary exposure in cohorts without safety signals.", "daily_use_appropriate": "yes", "special_populations": "Whole fruit is appropriate for children, pregnant women and older adults as part of a normal diet. Concentrated extracts lack safety data in pregnancy, lactation and children. People with diabetes should count carbohydrate in juices and sweetened products. People taking anticoagulants, antihypertensives or glucose-lowering drugs should seek advice before using concentrated extracts.", "specialist_review_needed": ["Food safety/quality specialist for extract identity testing (anthocyanin profile fingerprinting) and pesticide residues", "Clinical pharmacist for extract use with antihypertensive, antidiabetic or anticoagulant drugs"], "hard_safety_gate": "pass", "gate_reason": "Whole blueberries and freeze-dried blueberry powder are ordinary foods with no toxicity signal at the doses studied; extract products require identity and contaminant testing but do not raise a material safety concern."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Blueberries and freeze-dried blueberry powder are conventional foods. No health claim linking blueberries or anthocyanins to cognitive function, memory or brain health is authorised. EFSA's 2010 general opinion on antioxidant and 'protection of cells from premature ageing' claims for various foods and constituents found the claimed effects not substantiated (doi 10.2903/j.efsa.2010.1489; title verified, the specific entries for blueberry or anthocyanins were not checked). Botanical Article 13 claims remain 'on hold' (per the X4 EU synthesis); whether any Vaccinium cognition claim is in that list was not verified. Anthocyanins are authorised as food colour E 163, a technological use that carries no health claim.", "us": "Blueberries are conventional foods; freeze-dried powder is a food ingredient. Blueberry extracts are sold as dietary supplement ingredients under DSHEA (whether a given extract needs an NDI notification is uncertain and depends on the process). Structure/function claims such as 'supports memory' need competent and reliable scientific evidence under FTC standards; the evidence is insufficient for healthy young adults or students and inconsistent for older adults. No FDA health claim for blueberries was identified.", "morocco": "Uncertain. Fresh, frozen or freeze-dried blueberries fall under Law 28-07 (ONSSA) and labelling Decree 2-12-389. Claims that a food prevents, treats or cures disease are banned. 'Health claims' are defined but no list of authorised claims or approval procedure was found (X6 Morocco synthesis). Supplements have no specific law and are registered through a Ministry of Health committee (now AMMPS). Any cognition claim should be treated as unauthorised pending legal review.", "permitted_claims_examples": [], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "Whole fruit and conventional freeze-dried powder are not novel foods. Concentrated or purified anthocyanin extracts may need checking against the EU Novel Food Catalogue (not verified here); US NDI status of new extracts is uncertain.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "High. The doses used in the best trials are ordinary food portions: about 1 cup fresh (148 g), 20-26 g freeze-dried whole-fruit powder (about 300 mg anthocyanins), or 0.5 cup equivalent (about 12-13 g) in the midlife trial. These fit a smoothie, breakfast cereal, yoghurt topping, bar inclusion or drink sachet.", "formats": ["fresh or frozen whole berries (Morocco is a major producer)", "freeze-dried whole-fruit powder in sachets, smoothies, overnight oats", "freeze-dried pieces in cereal or bars", "cold-processed or HPP purees", "standardised extracts in capsules (100-900 mg; weaker evidence)"], "stability": "Anthocyanins are unstable to heat, oxygen, light, neutral or alkaline pH and storage: thermal processing lost 28-59% and 6 months at 25 C a further 62-85% in juices and canned products [brownmiller2008]. Freeze-drying and frozen storage are generally used to preserve anthocyanins (not quantified here). Products should state anthocyanin content at end of shelf life, verified by HPLC.", "taste": "Pleasant sweet-tart flavour; powders are acceptable in drinks and dairy; colour shifts from red to blue-grey as pH rises and may stain.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Parent anthocyanins are poorly absorbed; phenolic metabolites peak 1-2 h and about 6 h after intake [rodriguezmateos2013], so acute effects are expected within 1-6 h. Whole fruit supplies fibre and other polyphenols that may contribute; trials using fibre-free placebos cannot separate effects [krikorian2022].", "dose_feasibility": "Feasible: the effective vascular dose (about 766 mg total polyphenols acutely [rodriguezmateos2013]; about 300 mg anthocyanins daily [wood2023]) fits in about 26 g of powder or a cup of berries. Extract doses of 100-900 mg are capsule-sized but have weaker evidence.", "cost_availability": "Widely available. Morocco is a large blueberry exporter: trade-press figures cite about 82,590-85,000 t exported in 2025 from about 6,800 planted hectares (IBO data reported by FreshPlaza/HortiDaily, accessed 2026-10-25), giving local sourcing options for fresh, frozen or freeze-dried material. Costs of freeze-drying in Morocco were not researched.", "cost_evidence": "Retail freeze-dried wild blueberry powder: EUR 24.50 per 250 g (EUR 98/kg, KoRo Ireland) and CHF 27 per 250 g (CHF 108/kg, KoRo Switzerland), accessed 2026-10-25; a 24 g daily dose therefore costs about EUR 2.35 at retail. Bulk prices were not found."}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 3, "effect_magnitude": 2, "safety": 9, "reproducibility": 3, "food_suitability": 8, "cost_access": 6, "rationale": "Evidence quality 3: no row for healthy young adults exceeds C (acute, inconsistent) and chronic evidence in that group is absent (D); older and MCI rows are C; only the vascular surrogate reaches B. Effect magnitude 2: pooled cognitive effects are SMD 0.2-0.34 in older or impaired groups, null in the best-controlled young-adult trial; FMD +1% is a real but surrogate effect. Safety 9: ordinary food, no toxicity at trial doses; small deduction for juices' sugar load and extract quality. Reproducibility 3: positives cluster in three laboratories with frequent industry funding or product supply, many outcomes, and recent independent trials are null. Food suitability 8: effective doses are normal food portions; heat and storage losses are the main constraint. Cost/access 6: about EUR 2.35/day at retail for 24 g powder, cheaper as fresh or frozen fruit, with strong Moroccan supply."}, "research_gaps": ["No chronic RCT of blueberry on cognition in healthy young adults, students or knowledge workers", "No preregistered, adequately powered trial with a single prespecified primary cognitive outcome", "Placebos rarely matched for fibre; whole-fruit versus anthocyanin-specific effects unresolved", "Dose-response for cognition (as opposed to FMD or glucose) not established", "Independent replication outside industry-funded laboratories", "Registered trials completed without results (e.g. NCT04049162, NCT04935099) - possible publication bias", "Whether acute 'maintenance over the day' effects matter for real academic or work performance", "Effect of processing (freeze-drying versus heat) on the cognitive or vascular efficacy of finished products"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 4.8, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "pass", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": "C", "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": "B", "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"episodic_memory": "C", "executive_function": "C", "cognitive_decline": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 16, "n_studies": 28, "n_verified": 28, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 8, "n_systematic_reviews": 5, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["benefit", "inconsistent", "insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "citrus-flavanones-hesperidin", "name": "Citrus flavanones (hesperidin) and orange juice", "category": "G", "category_name": "Polyphenols and plant bioactives", "aliases": ["hesperidin", "hesperetin", "hesperetin-7-glucoside", "narirutin", "naringenin", "naringin", "citrus flavanones", "flavanone-rich orange juice", "citrus peel extract", "auraptene"], "food_sources": ["oranges and 100% orange juice (hesperidin and narirutin; much of the hesperidin sits in the pulp and albedo, so whole fruit and pulpy juice carry more than clear juice)", "mandarins/tangerines (hesperidin, also polymethoxyflavones nobiletin/tangeretin in peel)", "grapefruit (naringin; important drug interactions via furanocoumarins)", "lemons and limes (eriocitrin, hesperidin)", "citrus peel and pomace (concentrated flavanones, used in fortified juices and extracts)"], "active_constituents": ["hesperidin (hesperetin-7-O-rutinoside)", "narirutin", "naringin/naringenin", "colonic metabolites (hesperetin glucuronides/sulfates, phenolic acids)", "vitamin C, folate and potassium in juice", "sugars (about 20 g per 240 ml juice, general food composition figure, not re-verified)"], "one_line_verdict": "Flavanone-rich orange juice has produced small, mostly single-laboratory acute and 8-week cognitive signals (one test out of several in young adults, executive/psychomotor speed in middle-aged men, a global composite in older adults), the strongest null trial used a low-dose citrus peel extract, and the sugar load of juice makes whole oranges, not a hesperidin 'brain drink', the honest recommendation.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. Hesperidin is poorly absorbed in the small intestine because of its rutinoside sugar; most is deglycosylated by colonic bacteria, giving plasma hesperetin conjugates peaking 5-7 h after intake, whereas enzymatic conversion to hesperetin-7-glucoside doubles exposure and shifts absorption to the small intestine [nielsen2006]. All cognitive trials come from one University of Reading group using orange juice. Acute: in 24 healthy young adults, a 500 ml high-flavanone drink (70.5 mg) improved only the Digit Symbol Substitution Test at 2 h among several tests, and in a separate 16-person subsample increased regional frontal perfusion by arterial spin labelling [lamport2016]; in healthy middle-aged men (n=24 planned), 240 ml of pomace-fortified orange juice (272 mg flavonoids, ingredients supplied by PepsiCo) improved executive function and psychomotor speed composites and subjective alertness over 6 h versus a calorie-matched placebo [alharbi2016]. Chronic: in 37 healthy older adults, 500 ml/day of 100% orange juice (305 mg flavanones) for 8 weeks improved a global cognitive composite versus a low-flavanone cordial in a crossover design, without mood or blood-pressure changes [kean2015]. In whom, and how large. Effects are on composites or single tests, standardised effect sizes are not reported in abstracts, samples are small and not independently replicated. No trial has tested students or knowledge workers over a term or under exam stress. A 36-week independent trial of citrus peel extract in 80 older adults with subjective cognitive decline found no benefit over placebo (time x treatment p=0.91), though its flavanone dose (3 mg naringenin) was trivial and placebo response was large [galluzzi2024]. A pilot hesperidin-diosmin-proanthocyanidin product trial without placebo (no-supplement control) is not informative [giovannini2026]. A 2023 meta-analysis of berry, cherry and citrus RCTs concluded fruit interventions did not sufficiently support cognitive improvement [wang2023]. Hesperidin also failed to change blood pressure or lipids in a meta-analysis, weakening the vascular mechanism [mohammadi2019]. Observational data are consistent but confounded: daily citrus intake associated with HR 0.86 (0.73-1.01) for dementia in Japan [zhang2017]; flavanone intake (OR 0.64) and daily orange juice (OR 0.53 for poor subjective cognition) associated with less subjective cognitive decline in US cohorts [yeh2021, yuan2019]. Bottom line: grade C for acute processing-speed/executive effects in healthy adults and for 8-week global cognition in older adults; grade C (observational) for long-term cognitive ageing. Whole citrus fruit gives the flavanones with fibre and without the free sugar of juice.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Flavanones are not essential nutrients and there is no deficiency state. The trials tested enhancement in healthy, replete adults on low-polyphenol run-in diets, which may exaggerate contrast versus usual diets. Orange juice also supplies vitamin C and folate; correcting low vitamin C or folate status is a separate, nutrient-specific question (see folate-b9.json) and must not be credited to hesperidin.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Claims like 'orange juice boosts brain power' or 'hesperidin for focus' derive from 3 small Reading trials, two of which used juices supplied by or analysed by PepsiCo (Tropicana); none is replicated by an independent group, outcomes are composites or a single test, and the observational associations are confounded by overall diet quality. In the EU there is no authorised claim for hesperidin or citrus flavanones; a 100% orange juice that is a 'significant source' of vitamin C may legally carry the authorised vitamin C claims ('Vitamin C contributes to normal psychological function', 'Vitamin C contributes to the normal functioning of the nervous system', 'Vitamin C contributes to the reduction of tiredness and fatigue'), but those claims describe vitamin C, not flavanones, and cannot be reworded into enhancement claims. Free-sugar guidance (WHO counts fruit-juice sugars as free sugars) argues against promoting daily 500 ml juice. In the US, FTC substantiation for cognitive claims would be thin.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Hesperidin reaches the colon largely intact, is converted by microbiota to hesperetin, absorbed and conjugated; hesperetin metabolites may cross the blood-brain barrier in small amounts (animal data). Proposed effects are endothelial nitric-oxide-mediated vasodilation and increased cerebral blood flow, and in animals anti-inflammatory and BDNF/ERK signalling. Human evidence exists for a regional cerebral blood flow increase in one acute study; blood pressure and lipid effects are not confirmed by meta-analysis.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Oral hesperidin (rutinoside) -> colonic deglycosylation -> hesperetin conjugates in plasma", "detail": "Bioavailability low and microbiota-dependent; hesperetin-7-glucoside doubles AUC and peaks in ~0.6 h [nielsen2006].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Hesperetin metabolites -> endothelial NO signalling -> vasodilation", "detail": "Plausible from vascular studies, but a meta-analysis found no effect of hesperidin on blood pressure or lipids [mohammadi2019].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Increased cerebral blood flow", "detail": "Regional frontal perfusion increased 2 h after flavanone-rich juice in 16 young adults [lamport2016].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Neuronal signalling (BDNF, ERK/CREB, anti-inflammatory)", "detail": "Animal and in-vitro data only.", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Measured cognitive outcome", "detail": "Single-test or composite improvements in small trials [lamport2016, alharbi2016, kean2015]; null for citrus peel extract [galluzzi2024].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["colonic microbial metabolism of flavanone glycosides", "endothelial NO / cerebral blood flow", "ERK/CREB/BDNF signalling (animal)", "anti-inflammatory signalling (animal)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "processing_speed", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults 18-30 y", "dose_or_intake": "500 ml drink with 70.5 mg flavanones", "duration": "single dose, tested at 2 h", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Digit Symbol Substitution improved at 2 h; no effect on other cognitive tests.", "effect_estimate": "not reported in abstract (significant DSST drink x time effect)", "certainty_notes": "Single small (n=24) single-blind crossover from one group; one positive test among several; no multiplicity correction reported.", "key_study_ids": ["lamport2016"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "cerebral_blood_flow", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults 18-30 y (fMRI subsample)", "dose_or_intake": "500 ml, 70.5 mg flavanones", "duration": "2 and 5 h post-dose", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Increased regional perfusion in right inferior/middle frontal gyrus at 2 h.", "effect_estimate": "not reported in abstract", "certainty_notes": "Biomarker; n=16; not linked to behaviour.", "key_study_ids": ["lamport2016"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy middle-aged men 30-65 y", "dose_or_intake": "240 ml pomace-fortified orange juice, 272 mg flavonoids", "duration": "single dose, 2 and 6 h", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Executive function and psychomotor speed composites and subjective alertness better than calorie-matched placebo.", "effect_estimate": "not reported as standardised effect; raw DSST differences about 1 second", "certainty_notes": "n=24, men only, industry-supplied product, composite constructed post hoc from 8 tests; subjective alertness not cognition.", "key_study_ids": ["alharbi2016"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Healthy older adults, mean 67 y", "dose_or_intake": "500 ml/day 100% orange juice, 305 mg flavanones", "duration": "8 weeks (crossover)", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Global cognitive composite better after high- vs low-flavanone drink; mood and blood pressure unchanged.", "effect_estimate": "not reported in abstract", "certainty_notes": "n=37, single group, composite outcome, low-flavanone control was a cordial (not matched juice matrix).", "key_study_ids": ["kean2015"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_mci", "population_detail": "Older adults with subjective cognitive decline", "dose_or_intake": "400 mg citrus peel extract (3 mg naringenin, 0.1 mg auraptene)", "duration": "36 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "RBANS improved equally in both groups; no treatment effect; IL-8 unchanged by treatment.", "effect_estimate": "time x treatment p=0.910 (RBANS); within-time d=0.36 in both arms", "certainty_notes": "Independent registered trial (NCT04744922), but very low flavanone dose; SCD population.", "key_study_ids": ["galluzzi2024"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_decline", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Prospective cohorts (Japan; US NHS/HPFS)", "dose_or_intake": "habitual citrus/flavanone/orange juice intake", "duration": "6-20+ years", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Daily citrus associated with modestly lower dementia incidence; higher flavanone and orange juice intake associated with less subjective cognitive decline.", "effect_estimate": "HR 0.86 (0.73-1.01) [zhang2017]; flavanones OR 0.64 (0.58-0.68) [yeh2021]; daily OJ OR 0.53 (0.43-0.67) poor SCF [yuan2019]", "certainty_notes": "Observational; residual confounding by diet quality and socioeconomic status; subjective outcomes.", "key_study_ids": ["zhang2017", "yeh2021", "yuan2019"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "students", "population_detail": "Students/knowledge workers", "dose_or_intake": "any", "duration": "weeks to months", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No chronic trial in students or young workers.", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "No data; extrapolation from older adults not allowed.", "key_study_ids": []}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "500 ml juice, 70.5 mg flavanones", "onset": "2 h", "duration_studied": "2-5 h", "domain": "processing speed (DSST); regional CBF", "population": "healthy adults 18-30", "effect_estimate": "DSST improved; other tests null; frontal perfusion up", "certainty": "C", "safety": "~ juice sugar load; no AEs reported", "study_ids": ["lamport2016"]}, {"dose": "240 ml fortified orange juice, 272 mg flavonoids", "onset": "2 h", "duration_studied": "6 h", "domain": "executive function, psychomotor speed, subjective alertness", "population": "healthy men 30-65", "effect_estimate": "Composites better than placebo (no SMD)", "certainty": "C", "safety": "No AEs reported", "study_ids": ["alharbi2016"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "8 weeks", "population": "healthy older adults (n=37)", "outcome": "global cognitive composite", "effect_estimate": "Better vs low-flavanone control (no SMD in abstract)", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "Small single-lab crossover", "study_ids": ["kean2015"]}, {"duration": "36 weeks", "population": "older adults with SCD (n=80)", "outcome": "RBANS total", "effect_estimate": "No difference, p=0.910", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "Very low flavanone dose", "study_ids": ["galluzzi2024"]}, {"duration": "5.7 years", "population": "Japanese older adults (n=13,373)", "outcome": "incident dementia", "effect_estimate": "HR 0.86 (0.73-1.01) daily citrus vs <=2/week", "evidence_quality": "C (observational)", "limitations": "Confounding; FFQ once", "study_ids": ["zhang2017"]}, {"duration": "~20 years", "population": "US health professionals", "outcome": "subjective cognitive decline", "effect_estimate": "flavanones OR 0.64; daily OJ OR 0.53", "evidence_quality": "C (observational)", "limitations": "Subjective outcome; confounding", "study_ids": ["yeh2021", "yuan2019"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Hesperidin and orange juice are safe in usual food amounts. The practical risks are the free sugar and energy of juice (about 100 kcal and roughly 20 g sugar per 240 ml, general composition figure), dental erosion, and drug interactions: grapefruit (furanocoumarins) inhibits CYP3A4, and orange/grapefruit juices can reduce absorption of OATP substrates (e.g., fexofenadine). Hesperidin supplements are used medicinally with diosmin (venotonic) and are generally well tolerated (GI upset).", "adverse_effects": ["free sugar/energy load with daily juice", "dental erosion (acidity)", "GI discomfort with high-dose hesperidin supplements"], "contraindications": ["grapefruit products with CYP3A4-metabolised drugs (e.g., some statins, calcium-channel blockers)", "high-potassium juice in advanced kidney disease"], "drug_interactions": ["grapefruit juice: CYP3A4 inhibition (well established)", "orange and grapefruit juice: reduced uptake of OATP substrates (fexofenadine, some beta-blockers) - general pharmacology knowledge, not re-verified here", "hesperidin supplements: theoretical additive effects with antihypertensives/anticoagulants (uncertain)"], "upper_limits": "No UL for flavanones. Free sugars <10% of energy (WHO; fruit juice counted as free sugar).", "sleep_impact": "None known.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "8 weeks (juice) and 36 weeks (low-dose peel extract) in cognition trials; decades observationally.", "daily_use_appropriate": "probably", "special_populations": "Diabetes/obesity: prefer whole fruit over juice. Children: limit juice volume per paediatric guidance. Medication users: check grapefruit interactions.", "specialist_review_needed": ["clinical pharmacist for juice-drug interactions", "dietitian for sugar content positioning"], "hard_safety_gate": "pass", "gate_reason": "No toxicity at food levels; the main concern is sugar from juice, a formulation issue rather than a hazard of hesperidin."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "No authorised health claim for hesperidin or citrus flavanones (general knowledge of the EU register; EFSA Article 13 opinions on citrus flavonoid claims not re-fetched). Orange juice that is a significant source of vitamin C can carry authorised vitamin C claims (Regulation (EU) 432/2012), e.g. 'Vitamin C contributes to normal psychological function'. 100% fruit juice composition governed by Directive 2001/112/EC (as amended); added sugars not permitted in fruit juice. Hesperidin is not a novel food as a citrus constituent; enzymatically modified forms may need novel food assessment (uncertain).", "us": "Orange juice is a standardised food (21 CFR 146); hesperidin supplements under DSHEA; glucosyl hesperidin GRAS status not verified here. FTC requires competent and reliable evidence for cognitive claims.", "morocco": "Uncertain: juices and supplements under ONSSA; no specific flavanone claim framework identified.", "permitted_claims_examples": ["Vitamin C contributes to normal psychological function (EU; vitamin C, requires significant source)", "Vitamin C contributes to the normal functioning of the nervous system (EU; vitamin C)", "Vitamin C contributes to the reduction of tiredness and fatigue (EU; vitamin C)"], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "Enzymatically modified hesperidin (glucosyl hesperidin / hesperetin-7-glucoside) novel food status in the EU uncertain.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "High as whole fruit; juice feasible but sugar-laden; hesperidin powder is poorly water-soluble and bitter-neutral.", "formats": ["whole oranges/mandarins", "pulpy 100% orange juice (small portion)", "citrus peel/pomace-fortified foods", "hesperidin or glucosyl hesperidin capsules"], "stability": "Hesperidin stable in juice; tends to precipitate (cloud) in clear beverages.", "taste": "Neutral to slightly bitter; naringin bitter.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Colon-dependent, highly variable between individuals; enzymatic conversion to hesperetin-7-glucoside doubles exposure [nielsen2006].", "dose_feasibility": "Trial doses (272-305 mg flavanones) needed 240 ml fortified juice or 500 ml juice; 1-2 whole oranges supply a substantial but unquantified (not re-verified) amount, while a pomace or peel ingredient could deliver 300 mg in a low-sugar food.", "cost_availability": "Inexpensive and globally available; Morocco is a major citrus producer (sourcing advantage).", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 3, "effect_magnitude": 2, "safety": 8, "reproducibility": 2, "food_suitability": 8, "cost_access": 9, "rationale": "Evidence quality 3: grade C rows from one laboratory, small samples; strongest independent trial null. Effect magnitude 2: single-test or composite effects without reported SMDs. Safety 8: food-safe; minus for juice sugar and grapefruit interactions. Reproducibility 2: no independent replication. Food suitability 8: whole citrus easy; juice sugar trade-off. Cost/access 9: cheap, local sourcing in Morocco."}, "research_gaps": ["Independent replication of acute DSST/executive effects in students with preregistered primary outcome", "Low-sugar flavanone delivery (pomace, whole fruit) versus juice in head-to-head trials", "Dose-response 100-600 mg flavanones and role of microbiota metabotype", "Chronic trials (>=12 weeks) in young adults", "Results of NCT07750223 (hesperidin, overweight older adults)"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 4.8, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "pass", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": "C", "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": "C", "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": "C", "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"processing_speed": "C", "executive_function": "C", "global_cognition": "C", "cognitive_decline": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 7, "n_studies": 11, "n_verified": 11, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 2, "n_systematic_reviews": 1, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "cocoa-flavanols", "name": "Cocoa flavanols", "category": "G", "category_name": "Polyphenols and plant bioactives", "aliases": ["cocoa flavanols (CF)", "cocoa flavan-3-ols", "(-)-epicatechin and procyanidins from Theobroma cacao", "cocoa extract", "high-flavanol cocoa powder", "Cocoapro (Mars brand)", "CocoaVia (Mars brand)", "Acticoa (Barry Callebaut brand)", "cacao polyphenols"], "food_sources": ["natural (non-alkalised) cocoa powder: about 35 mg total flavanols per g on average in one survey, falling to about 4 mg/g after heavy alkalisation [miller2008]", "specialty high-flavanol cocoa powders (for example Acticoa: 681 mg flavanols including 150 mg (-)-epicatechin in 8.3 g [gratton2020])", "dark chocolate (highly variable: 50 g of a high-flavanol dark chocolate gave 410 mg in [suominen2020]; ordinary products are often far lower)", "standardised cocoa extracts in capsules or powders (COSMOS: 500 mg flavanols including 80 mg (-)-epicatechin per day)", "other flavanol foods that are not cocoa: tea, apples, grapes, berries, pulses (contribute to habitual flavanol intake)"], "active_constituents": ["(-)-epicatechin (main monomer; most likely mediator of vascular effects)", "(+)-catechin and (-)-catechin (epimerised during processing; poorly absorbed)", "procyanidins (oligomers, degree of polymerisation 2-10; the EU claim specifies DP 1-10)", "methylxanthines co-present in cocoa: theobromine (about 180 mg) and caffeine (about 20 mg) per 8.3 g high-flavanol cocoa [gratton2020]; usually matched in placebo but a confounder when white chocolate is the control"], "one_line_verdict": "Cocoa flavanols reliably produce small acute increases in cerebral blood flow and support an EU-authorised vascular claim at 200 mg/day, but their cognitive effects in healthy young adults are small, inconsistent and often null in preregistered trials, and the largest independent trials in older adults (COSMOS, 500 mg/day for 2-3 years) found no cognitive benefit except a very small memory gain in people with poor habitual diets.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. Cocoa flavanols, especially (-)-epicatechin, are absorbed and improve endothelium-dependent vasodilation. EFSA accepted a cause-and-effect relationship for this outcome, and the EU authorised a vascular claim at 200 mg/day. Acute doses of about 450-900 mg increase regional cerebral perfusion or prefrontal oxygenation within about 2 hours [lamport2015, decroix2016, gratton2020]. These are biomarkers, not cognition. Acute cognition in healthy young adults. Early small crossover trials reported benefits on serial subtraction and mental fatigue [scholey2010], spatial memory and contrast sensitivity [field2011], visual search [karabay2018] and the hardest Stroop condition only [gratton2020]. Each trial found an effect on some measures and not on others, so the pattern looks partly like selective reporting. The same paradigms gave null results in other trials: 903 mg had no effect on executive function [decroix2016], 415 mg had no effect on visual working memory in two preregistered experiments [altinok2022], and 415-623 mg had no effect on Flanker, Simon or Go/No-go tasks in a preregistered trial [altinok2025]. At 250 mg only one task cycle improved [massee2015]. The acute effect on attention and executive function is therefore inconsistent and at most small (grade C). Chronic intake in young and middle-aged adults. 30 days at 250 mg (young adults [massee2015]) and at 250-500 mg (middle-aged adults [pase2013, camfield2012]) did not change cognition. Self-rated calmness improved [pase2013] and EEG (SSVEP) changed without any behavioural change [camfield2012]. A positive 30-day dark-versus-white-chocolate trial (n=18) is confounded by theobromine [sumiyoshi2019]. Older adults. Two Mars-supported L'Aquila trials reported large improvements in Trail Making and verbal fluency over 8 weeks in MCI [desideri2012] and in cognitively intact elderly people [mastroiacovo2015]. Their standard errors are implausibly small, and these two trials drive pooled Trail Making effects in meta-analyses [arabpour2026]. Independent trials were null: 6 weeks of dark chocolate plus cocoa [crews2008], 8 weeks of 410 mg in chocolate [suominen2020], 2 years of 500 mg with in-person testing (n=573; global cognition difference -0.01 SD, 95% CI -0.08 to 0.05) [vyas2024], and 3 years by telephone (n=2262; z=0.03, -0.02 to 0.08) [baker2023], which also found no effect on MCI or dementia incidence [sachs2023]. A 12-month trial combining DHA and flavanols in memory complainers was null or slightly adverse [vauzour2023]. The hippocampal-memory hypothesis [brickman2014] failed its primary endpoint in two trials [sloan2021, brickman2023]. In COSMOS-Web (n=3562) the effect overall was d=0.025. A prespecified subgroup with the lowest diet quality gained about 0.28 words of list recall (d=0.086) [brickman2023]. That supports, at most, a small repletion effect in people with low habitual flavanol intake. How reproducible. Positive results cluster in Mars-funded or Mars-coauthored work and in single laboratories. Larger, independent and preregistered trials are null. Practical relevance for a student or knowledge worker is negligible on present evidence. Safety is good up to 2000 mg/day for 12 weeks [ottaviani2015] and over 3.6 years at 500 mg/day [sesso2022]. Cadmium in cocoa is the main product-safety issue [abt2018].", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Flavanols are not essential nutrients and have no deficiency syndrome. The newest framing from the Columbia/Mars group is a 'depletion-repletion' model. In COSMOS-Web, 500 mg/day did not improve memory overall, but it improved list recall slightly in the lowest tertile of diet quality or of a urinary flavanol biomarker (d about 0.09, about 0.28 words) [brickman2023]. A similar post hoc pattern appeared in [sloan2021] and in uncorrected subgroups of [vyas2024]. If real, this is correction of a low habitual intake in older adults, not enhancement. People who already eat a good diet with tea, fruit and vegetables showed no effect. For healthy young adults with adequate diets there is no convincing chronic enhancement trial, and acute 'enhancement' trials are inconsistent and frequently null when preregistered [altinok2022, altinok2025]. The honest implication for Brain Food is that improving overall diet quality, and so habitual flavanol intake from tea, apples, berries, pulses and natural cocoa, is the evidence-consistent strategy. A concentrated flavanol supplement for already well-fed students is not.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Common marketing says that chocolate or cocoa 'boosts brain power', 'improves memory' or 'is brain food', and cites [brickman2014] (n=37) and the L'Aquila trials [desideri2012, mastroiacovo2015]. The larger independent trials do not support these claims: COSMOS-Mind [baker2023], COSMOS-Clinic [vyas2024] and the COSMOS-Web primary endpoint [brickman2023] were all null for cognition in older adults. For young adults, preregistered acute trials are mostly null. The only authorised EU claim is vascular: 'Cocoa flavanols help maintain the elasticity of blood vessels, which contributes to normal blood flow' (200 mg/day; Regulation (EU) No 851/2013, extended to capsules or tablets of high-flavanol extract by Regulation (EU) 2015/539). It may not be reworded into a brain or cognition claim. Words such as 'blood flow to the brain' or 'supports focus' would be unauthorised health claims in the EU, and under US FTC standards they would lack competent and reliable scientific evidence for healthy adults. Ordinary chocolate rarely delivers the studied doses. Alkalised (Dutch-processed) cocoa loses most of its flavanols [miller2008]. Chocolate also carries sugar, fat, energy and cadmium [abt2018]. A defensible product position is 'natural cocoa standardised to at least 200 mg flavanols, which supports normal blood-vessel function' (EU wording), with no cognitive promise.", "mechanism": {"summary": "(-)-Epicatechin and small procyanidins are absorbed in the small intestine. They circulate as glucuronidated, sulfated and methylated metabolites, peaking about 2 h after intake, and the colon converts the remainder to gamma-valerolactones (the urinary biomarker gVLM). The best-established human action is increased nitric-oxide-dependent endothelial function: flow-mediated dilation rises by about 1% acutely [gratton2020, grassi2016]. Acute increases in cerebral perfusion and prefrontal oxygenation follow [lamport2015, decroix2016, gratton2020]. Proposed brain mechanisms include improved neurovascular coupling [sorond2013], increased dentate-gyrus blood volume [brickman2014], neurotrophin signalling and angiogenesis (mainly animal data), and improved insulin sensitivity [mastroiacovo2015]. The vascular biomarkers are consistently moved. Cognitive outcomes are not. Mechanism is not efficacy.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Oral cocoa flavanols -> small-intestinal absorption of (-)-epicatechin and dimers -> plasma metabolites peak about 2 h; colonic microbial metabolism to gamma-valerolactones", "detail": "Absorption depends on stereochemistry: (-)-epicatechin is absorbed well, while catechin epimers formed during processing are absorbed poorly. Urinary gVLM is a validated intake biomarker used in COSMOS [brickman2023]. Alkalisation lowers flavanol content in a predictable, linear way [miller2008].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Circulating epicatechin metabolites -> increased endothelial nitric oxide bioactivity -> improved endothelium-dependent vasodilation", "detail": "Accepted by EFSA as cause-and-effect at 200 mg/day (EU authorised claim). Acute FMD increased by about 1% with 681 mg [gratton2020] and was preserved after sleep deprivation [grassi2016].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Systemic vasodilation -> acute increase in cerebral perfusion and cortical oxygenation", "detail": "ASL-MRI showed increased regional CBF (anterior cingulate, parietal operculum) 2 h after 494 mg in 18 adults aged 50-65 [lamport2015]. NIRS showed higher prefrontal oxygenation at rest [decroix2016] and faster, larger responses to hypercapnia [gratton2020]. The trials are small and the methods differ.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Improved neurovascular coupling and hippocampal (dentate gyrus) blood volume", "detail": "Neurovascular coupling improved after 30 days only in older adults with impaired baseline coupling [sorond2013]. Dentate-gyrus CBV increased in n=37 [brickman2014], but a larger trial's region-of-interest analysis was negative [sloan2021].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Neurotrophin signalling (BDNF), angiogenesis, anti-inflammatory and insulin-sensitising actions", "detail": "Mostly animal and in-vitro data. In humans BDNF did not change acutely [decroix2016] or over 12 months [vauzour2023]. Insulin sensitivity improvements were reported only by the L'Aquila group [mastroiacovo2015, desideri2012].", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Measured human cognitive outcome", "detail": "Acute effects in young adults are inconsistent: some positive single tasks, and null preregistered trials [altinok2022, altinok2025]. Large chronic trials in older adults show no effect on global cognition [baker2023, vyas2024]. There is a very small memory effect only in people with low habitual diet quality [brickman2023].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["endothelial nitric oxide synthase activation / NO bioavailability", "cerebral perfusion and neurovascular coupling", "hippocampal dentate gyrus cerebral blood volume (hypothesis)", "insulin sensitivity (single research group)", "neurotrophins (BDNF/NGF; largely animal, human null)", "methylxanthine (theobromine, caffeine) co-effects on arousal"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults about 18-35 y, rested, laboratory tasks 1.5-2.5 h post-dose", "dose_or_intake": "250-994 mg cocoa flavanols in a cocoa drink or dark chocolate", "duration": "single dose", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Positive findings: serial threes improved at 520 and 994 mg, but 994 mg also increased serial-sevens errors [scholey2010]; better choice-reaction measures with 720 mg versus white chocolate (single-blind) [field2011]; serial sevens improved in the first task cycle only at 250 mg [massee2015]; an advantage only in the hardest (double-Stroop) condition, p=0.042, n=17 [gratton2020]. Null findings: 903 mg had no effect on executive function [decroix2016]; 415 and 623 mg had no effect on Flanker, Simon or Go/No-go in a preregistered trial [altinok2025].", "effect_estimate": "Mostly not reported as standardized effects; Gratton planned contrast t(16)=2.14, p=0.042; Altinok 2025 null on all outcomes; no pooled acute estimate in healthy young adults", "certainty_notes": "Downgraded for inconsistency (positive effects scattered across different tasks), imprecision (n 12-48), risk of selective outcome reporting in the older non-preregistered trials, and indirect controls (white chocolate). Preregistered trials are null. Practical relevance for students is very small.", "key_study_ids": ["scholey2010", "field2011", "massee2015", "gratton2020", "decroix2016", "altinok2025"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy young adults (university samples), rested", "dose_or_intake": "415-720 mg cocoa flavanols", "duration": "single dose", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "720 mg improved spatial working memory versus white chocolate (n=30, single-blind) [field2011]. Two preregistered double-blind crossover experiments (n=48 and n=32, 415 mg) found no effect on maintenance or updating in visual working memory [altinok2022]. No acute dose-response study of working memory exists in young adults.", "effect_estimate": "Field 2011: significant, magnitude not reported in abstract; Altinok 2022: null for accuracy and RT", "certainty_notes": "The newer, better-designed trials are null. The single positive trial is small, single-blind and uses a white-chocolate control that differs in theobromine and caffeine. Downgraded for inconsistency and risk of bias.", "key_study_ids": ["field2011", "altinok2022"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "students", "population_detail": "University students (n=48), preregistered crossover with baseline, placebo, low and high dose", "dose_or_intake": "low and high dose cocoa flavanols (exact mg not stated in abstract)", "duration": "single dose per session, 1-week washouts", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Visual search (spatial attention) was faster after flavanols, but temporal attention and integration (RSVP, attentional blink) did not improve [karabay2018]. A related preregistered study by the same group in young adults found no effect on response inhibition [altinok2025].", "effect_estimate": "Reaction-time reduction in visual search; magnitude not reported in abstract", "certainty_notes": "Single preregistered trial in students with a partly positive result, and a related null trial from the same group. There is no replication of the visual-search effect. Imprecise and indirect for real study performance.", "key_study_ids": ["karabay2018", "altinok2025"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "cognitive_fatigue", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults performing 30-60 min of demanding serial-subtraction batteries", "dose_or_intake": "250-994 mg", "duration": "single dose", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Self-rated mental fatigue rose less after 520 mg, but not after 994 mg [scholey2010], and was lower after 250 mg acutely [massee2015]. Subjective fatigue was much lower under poikilocapnic hypoxia after about 15 mg/kg [bloomfield2023]. Pase found no acute mood effect [pase2013].", "effect_estimate": "Not reported as standardized effects; no dose-response (520 mg effective, 994 mg not)", "certainty_notes": "Subjective outcome, small samples and no dose-response. The methylxanthines were matched in most trials, but expectancy effects with cocoa drinks are plausible. This is a feeling of reduced fatigue, not measured cognition.", "key_study_ids": ["scholey2010", "massee2015", "pase2013", "bloomfield2023"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "cerebral_blood_flow", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy young men (NIRS) and adults aged 50-65 (ASL-MRI)", "dose_or_intake": "494-903 mg", "duration": "single dose, measured at about 2 h", "grade": "B", "direction": "benefit", "effect_summary": "Regional CBF increased (ASL) [lamport2015]. Prefrontal oxygenated haemoglobin increased at rest [decroix2016]. Oxygenation responses to hypercapnia were larger and about 1 min faster [gratton2020]. Under exercise the effect was overruled [decroix2016].", "effect_estimate": "Gratton: interaction F(1,16)=6.08, p=0.025 for oxygenation amplitude; latency t(16)=-5.68, p<0.001; Lamport: significant regional increases (no single effect size)", "certainty_notes": "Consistent direction across three independent laboratories and methods. Downgraded one level for imprecision (n 12-18). This is a biomarker: it does not establish a cognitive benefit.", "key_study_ids": ["lamport2015", "decroix2016", "gratton2020"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "sleep_deprived", "population_detail": "32 healthy adults after one night of total sleep deprivation", "dose_or_intake": "flavanol-rich versus flavanol-poor dark chocolate bars (dose per abstract not given)", "duration": "single dose 2 h before testing", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Flavanol-rich chocolate preserved working-memory accuracy after sleep deprivation in women only. It also counteracted the sleep-loss reduction in FMD (7.0 vs 5.0%) and lowered blood pressure by about 4/2 mmHg [grassi2016]. Psychomotor vigilance did not differ.", "effect_estimate": "Working memory: sex-specific effect, magnitude not reported in abstract; FMD 7.0 +/- 0.6 vs 5.0 +/- 0.4%", "certainty_notes": "Single trial with a post hoc-looking sex-restricted effect, from the L'Aquila group, which has links to industry. There is no replication. Downgraded for imprecision and risk of bias.", "key_study_ids": ["grassi2016"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "other", "population_detail": "12 healthy adults exposed to severe normobaric hypoxia (equivalent to about 5500 m)", "dose_or_intake": "15 mg/kg cocoa flavanols (about 1000 mg for a 70 kg adult)", "duration": "single dose 90 min before hypoxia", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Overall cognitive score was 4% higher than placebo under poikilocapnic hypoxia. There was no effect under isocapnic hypoxia (1%, p=0.72), and reaction time was 8-11% slower with flavanols in some conditions [bloomfield2023].", "effect_estimate": "+4% composite (p=0.033) poikilocapnic; +1% (p=0.72) isocapnic; RT impaired 8% (p=0.01)", "certainty_notes": "Very small, single laboratory, mixed directions and a niche population. Not transferable to students.", "key_study_ids": ["bloomfield2023"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults aged 18-31", "dose_or_intake": "250 mg/day cocoa flavanols, or dark chocolate versus white chocolate", "duration": "4-5 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "30 days of 250 mg did not change cognition, mood or cerebral blood flow [massee2015]. A 30-day dark-chocolate trial (n=18) reported better Stroop and cancellation performance and higher plasma NGF, but its white-chocolate control lacked theobromine [sumiyoshi2019]. A 2020 systematic review concluded that benefits are 'possible in early adulthood' from 11 heterogeneous studies with 366 participants [martin2020].", "effect_estimate": "Massee: no significant effects; Sumiyoshi: significant, effect size not reported; no pooled estimate", "certainty_notes": "Only two small chronic RCTs in young adults with opposite results, one confounded by methylxanthines. Very imprecise. The review is narrative and does not weigh risk of bias.", "key_study_ids": ["massee2015", "sumiyoshi2019", "martin2020"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy middle-aged adults 40-65 y", "dose_or_intake": "250-500 mg/day", "duration": "30 days", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "No effect on CDR-battery cognition at 1, 2.5 or 4 h acutely or after 30 days (n=72) [pase2013]. No behavioural effect on spatial working memory, with SSVEP changes interpreted as 'neural efficiency' (n=63) [camfield2012].", "effect_estimate": "Not significant for all cognitive outcomes", "certainty_notes": "Two independent-lab RCTs are null for behaviour. The EEG change without a behavioural change is a biomarker and should not be read as cognition.", "key_study_ids": ["pase2013", "camfield2012"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Cognitively intact adults aged 50-85 (including postmenopausal women aged 50-64)", "dose_or_intake": "about 410-993 mg/day in drinks, chocolate or capsules; 10 g of 99% chocolate", "duration": "6 weeks to 2 years", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "L'Aquila (n=90, Mars-supplied cocoa): TMT-A -8.6 vs -0.8 s and TMT-B -16.5 vs -1.1 s, verbal fluency +7.7 vs +1.3 words [mastroiacovo2015]. Garcia-Yu (n=140, open-label, no placebo): TMT-B -12.1 s (95% CI -24.0 to -0.2), d=-0.34, other domains unchanged [garciayu2022]. Null trials: 6 weeks [crews2008], 8 weeks of 410 vs 86 mg in chocolate (TMT-B p=0.66) [suominen2020], and 2 years of 500 mg, executive function/attention difference 0.003 SD (-0.07 to 0.08) [vyas2024]. A 2026 meta-analysis pooled TMT-B WMD -13.1 s (-17.7 to -8.5) [arabpour2026].", "effect_estimate": "Vyas 2024: 0.003 SD (95% CI -0.07 to 0.08); Arabpour 2026 pooled TMT-B WMD -13.1 s (-17.7 to -8.5), TMT-A -4.5 s (-8.4 to -0.6), VFT +3.15 (0.71 to 5.6)", "certainty_notes": "The pooled benefit is driven by small L'Aquila trials with implausibly small SEs (TMT-A change SE 0.4 s in n=30 implies a between-person SD of about 2 s). The largest independent in-person trial is precisely null. Downgraded for risk of bias, inconsistency and suspected small-study effects; we disagree with the meta-analysis' moderate-to-high GRADE rating.", "key_study_ids": ["mastroiacovo2015", "garciayu2022", "crews2008", "suominen2020", "vyas2024", "arabpour2026"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "US adults aged 60 and older (COSMOS; mean age 70-73), largely well-educated, non-Hispanic White", "dose_or_intake": "500 mg/day cocoa flavanols (80 mg epicatechin), capsules", "duration": "2-3 years", "grade": "E", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "COSMOS-Mind (n=2262, telephone): global composite z=0.03 (95% CI -0.02 to 0.08), p=0.28 [baker2023]. COSMOS-Clinic (n=573, in-person, 11 tests): -0.01 SD (-0.08 to 0.05) [vyas2024]. COSMOS-Web memory primary endpoint d=0.025, p=0.42 [brickman2023].", "effect_estimate": "z 0.03 (-0.02 to 0.08) [baker2023]; -0.01 SD (-0.08 to 0.05) [vyas2024]", "certainty_notes": "High-quality, large, independent-analysis RCTs with narrow CIs that exclude a meaningful benefit. Graded E (no meaningful benefit shown). Indirect for young people, but it is the best evidence available on chronic supplementation.", "key_study_ids": ["baker2023", "vyas2024", "brickman2023"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Healthy adults 50-75 y (hippocampal-dependent list learning, object recognition)", "dose_or_intake": "260-770 mg/day [sloan2021]; 500 mg/day (COSMOS); Brickman 2014 dose not verified", "duration": "12 weeks to 3 years", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "An early trial (n=37) reported improved dentate-gyrus-linked task performance and CBV [brickman2014]. The follow-up (n=211) primary object-recognition endpoint was null, while list learning improved mainly in participants with poor diets [sloan2021]. COSMOS-Web (n=3562) primary list-recall endpoint was null, d=0.025 [brickman2023]. COSMOS-Clinic episodic memory was -0.01 SD (-0.13 to 0.10) [vyas2024].", "effect_estimate": "Brickman 2023: 0.08 words (SE 0.10), d=0.025 at year 1; Vyas 2024: -0.01 SD (-0.13 to 0.10)", "certainty_notes": "The landmark positive trial is tiny and did not replicate on its prespecified primary endpoints. Grade C reflects confidence that any average effect in replete older adults is trivial.", "key_study_ids": ["brickman2014", "sloan2021", "brickman2023", "vyas2024"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "deficient_or_low_baseline", "population_detail": "Older adults in the lowest tertile of habitual diet quality (aHEI no higher than 38) or of the urinary flavanol biomarker", "dose_or_intake": "500 mg/day cocoa extract", "duration": "1-3 years", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "List recall improved versus placebo by 0.28 words averaged over 3 years (d=0.086, p=0.011). The interaction across tertiles was p=0.041, with no effect in medium or high tertiles [brickman2023]. The direction agrees with [sloan2021] and with uncorrected subgroups in [vyas2024].", "effect_estimate": "Mean difference 0.28 words (SE 0.11), Cohen's d 0.086", "certainty_notes": "Prespecified subgroup but marginal interaction, Mars-funded with Mars-employed coauthors, and the effect is very small (a fraction of one word). Not tested in young people. A biologically coherent 'repletion' signal that needs a dedicated trial.", "key_study_ids": ["brickman2023", "sloan2021", "vyas2024"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "older_adults_mci", "population_detail": "Older adults with mild cognitive impairment or subjective memory complaints", "dose_or_intake": "520-990 mg/day (drink) or 500 mg/day in dark chocolate plus DHA", "duration": "8 weeks to 12 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Desideri (n=90 MCI): TMT-B 104 s at high dose vs 139 s at low dose, better verbal fluency, MMSE unchanged [desideri2012]. CANN (n=259 SCI/MCI, flavanols plus DHA, 12 months): no benefit on the primary outcome, with poorer executive function versus control and a small cortical-volume decrease [vauzour2023].", "effect_estimate": "Desideri: TMT-B 104.1 +/- 28.7 vs 139.2 +/- 43.0 s; CANN: no benefit, executive function 118.6 to 113.3", "certainty_notes": "One small industry-linked positive trial and one larger independent null or adverse factorial-like trial, which confounds flavanols with DHA. Not relevant to healthy young adults.", "key_study_ids": ["desideri2012", "vauzour2023"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "dementia_risk", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "COSMOS-Mind, adults aged 65 and older", "dose_or_intake": "500 mg/day", "duration": "3 years", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Incidence of MCI (110 cases) and dementia (14 cases) did not differ by cocoa-extract assignment [sachs2023].", "effect_estimate": "All p at least 0.05; hazard ratios not given in abstract", "certainty_notes": "Low statistical power and short follow-up for dementia incidence. Downgraded for imprecision.", "key_study_ids": ["sachs2023"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "neuroplasticity_biomarkers", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Older adults (some with vascular risk factors); middle-aged adults for EEG", "dose_or_intake": "about 500-900 mg/day", "duration": "30 days to 12 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Neurovascular coupling rose 5.6% vs -2.4% after 30 days only in people with impaired baseline coupling [sorond2013]. Dentate-gyrus CBV increased [brickman2014]; the follow-up region-of-interest analysis was negative and only the voxel-based analysis was positive [sloan2021]. SSVEP changed [camfield2012]. BDNF did not change [vauzour2023].", "effect_estimate": "Sorond: NVC +5.6 +/- 7.2% vs -2.4 +/- 4.8% (p=0.001) in impaired subgroup", "certainty_notes": "Biomarkers only, small samples and subgroup findings. They do not establish cognitive benefit.", "key_study_ids": ["sorond2013", "brickman2014", "sloan2021", "camfield2012", "vauzour2023"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults, mostly middle-aged", "dose_or_intake": "cocoa-rich products; 500 mg/day polyphenols in [pase2013]", "duration": "single dose to 30 days", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "After 30 days, 500 mg increased self-rated calmness and contentedness [pase2013]. A meta-analysis of 9 studies (5 acute) found improvements in depressive symptoms (g=-0.42, 95% CI -0.67 to -0.17) and anxiety (g=-0.49, -0.78 to -0.19) [fusarpoli2022].", "effect_estimate": "g -0.42 (-0.67 to -0.17) depression; g -0.49 (-0.78 to -0.19) anxiety [fusarpoli2022]", "certainty_notes": "Few small studies, mostly short-term. Taste, pleasure and expectancy are hard to blind, and the outcomes are self-reported. Not cognition.", "key_study_ids": ["pase2013", "fusarpoli2022"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "vascular_health", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults (EFSA claim basis) and older US adults (COSMOS clinical events)", "dose_or_intake": "200 mg/day (EU claim) to 500 mg/day (COSMOS)", "duration": "acute to 3.6 years", "grade": "B", "direction": "benefit", "effect_summary": "EFSA concluded a cause-and-effect relationship for maintenance of endothelium-dependent vasodilation at 200 mg/day (EU-authorised claim). In COSMOS (n=21,442), total CVD events had HR 0.90 (0.78 to 1.02), not significant, while CVD death had HR 0.73 (0.54 to 0.98) as a secondary endpoint [sesso2022]. FMD increased acutely [gratton2020, grassi2016].", "effect_estimate": "COSMOS total CVD HR 0.90 (0.78 to 1.02); CVD death HR 0.73 (0.54 to 0.98)", "certainty_notes": "The endothelial-function biomarker is consistently improved. Hard clinical outcomes are not significant on the primary endpoint. We did not systematically review the vascular literature here (out of scope), so the grade is held at B.", "key_study_ids": ["sesso2022", "gratton2020", "grassi2016"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "520 mg and 994 mg CF (drink)", "onset": "testing started about 1.5 h post-dose", "duration_studied": "1 h of repeated Cognitive Demand Battery", "domain": "serial subtraction, RVIP, mental fatigue", "population": "30 healthy adults", "effect_estimate": "Serial Threes improved at both doses; 994 mg faster RVIP but more Serial Sevens errors; fatigue attenuated only at 520 mg", "certainty": "low (no dose-response, multiple outcomes)", "safety": "no adverse events reported in abstract", "study_ids": ["scholey2010"]}, {"dose": "720 mg CF (dark chocolate) vs white chocolate", "onset": "about 2 h", "duration_studied": "single session", "domain": "visual contrast sensitivity, spatial working memory, choice RT", "population": "30 healthy young adults", "effect_estimate": "Improvements in contrast sensitivity, motion detection time, spatial memory and some choice-RT measures; magnitudes not in abstract", "certainty": "low (single-blind, methylxanthine-unmatched control)", "safety": "not reported", "study_ids": ["field2011"]}, {"dose": "low and high dose CF (cocoa drink)", "onset": "about 2 h", "duration_studied": "single session per condition", "domain": "spatial and temporal attention", "population": "48 university students", "effect_estimate": "Faster visual search; no effect on attentional blink or temporal integration", "certainty": "low-moderate (preregistered, single trial)", "safety": "not reported", "study_ids": ["karabay2018"]}, {"dose": "415 mg CF", "onset": "about 2 h", "duration_studied": "single session", "domain": "visual working memory maintenance and updating", "population": "48 + 32 healthy young adults", "effect_estimate": "Null for accuracy and RT in both experiments", "certainty": "moderate for absence of a large effect (preregistered)", "safety": "not reported", "study_ids": ["altinok2022"]}, {"dose": "415 and 623 mg CF (non-alkalised vs alkalised cocoa placebo)", "onset": "about 2 h", "duration_studied": "single session", "domain": "Flanker, Simon, Go/No-go", "population": "36 healthy young adults", "effect_estimate": "Null on all accuracy, interference, error and RT outcomes", "certainty": "moderate for absence of a large effect (preregistered)", "safety": "not reported", "study_ids": ["altinok2025"]}, {"dose": "681 mg CF (150 mg epicatechin) vs 4 mg", "onset": "2 h", "duration_studied": "single session", "domain": "cortical oxygenation (NIRS) during hypercapnia; Stroop variants", "population": "18 healthy young men (17 analysed)", "effect_estimate": "Larger and about 1 min faster oxygenation response; cognitive advantage only in double-Stroop (p=0.042)", "certainty": "low (n=17, one of four conditions)", "safety": "not reported", "study_ids": ["gratton2020"]}, {"dose": "903 mg CF", "onset": "100 min", "duration_studied": "before and after a 30-min time trial", "domain": "executive function, prefrontal oxygenation, BDNF", "population": "12 healthy men", "effect_estimate": "Oxygenation up at rest; no effect on executive function or BDNF", "certainty": "low (n=12)", "safety": "not reported", "study_ids": ["decroix2016"]}, {"dose": "494 mg vs 23 mg CF", "onset": "about 2 h", "duration_studied": "resting ASL-MRI", "domain": "regional cerebral blood flow", "population": "18 adults aged 50-65", "effect_estimate": "Increased regional perfusion (anterior cingulate, central opercular cortex)", "certainty": "low-moderate (biomarker, small n)", "safety": "not reported", "study_ids": ["lamport2015"]}, {"dose": "flavanol-rich vs flavanol-poor chocolate", "onset": "2 h", "duration_studied": "after 1 night of total sleep deprivation", "domain": "working memory, PVT, FMD, BP", "population": "32 healthy adults", "effect_estimate": "Working-memory accuracy preserved in women only; FMD 7.0 vs 5.0%; SBP 116.9 vs 120.8 mmHg", "certainty": "low (single trial, sex-specific)", "safety": "no concerns reported", "study_ids": ["grassi2016"]}, {"dose": "15 mg/kg CF", "onset": "90 min", "duration_studied": "severe normobaric hypoxia", "domain": "composite cognition, cerebral oxygenation, fatigue", "population": "12 healthy adults", "effect_estimate": "+4% cognition (poikilocapnic), +1% NS (isocapnic); RT slower by 8-11% in some conditions", "certainty": "low", "safety": "slower reaction time is a possible adverse performance effect", "study_ids": ["bloomfield2023"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "30 days", "population": "40 healthy young adults (mean 24 y)", "outcome": "cognition, mood, mental fatigue, CBF", "effect_estimate": "No significant chronic effects at 250 mg/day", "evidence_quality": "low (small, low dose)", "limitations": "250 mg is below most positive acute doses; n=40", "study_ids": ["massee2015"]}, {"duration": "30 days", "population": "18 healthy young adults", "outcome": "Stroop, cancellation, NGF", "effect_estimate": "Improved performance and NGF with dark versus white chocolate", "evidence_quality": "very low", "limitations": "n=18, theobromine-unmatched control, unblinded taste difference", "study_ids": ["sumiyoshi2019"]}, {"duration": "30 days", "population": "72 healthy middle-aged adults", "outcome": "CDR battery cognition; mood", "effect_estimate": "No cognitive effect; calmness and contentedness up at 500 mg", "evidence_quality": "moderate (null cognition)", "limitations": "Modest n; mood is subjective", "study_ids": ["pase2013"]}, {"duration": "8 weeks", "population": "90 cognitively intact elderly (L'Aquila)", "outcome": "TMT A/B, verbal fluency, MMSE", "effect_estimate": "TMT-B -16.5 vs -1.1 s; VFT +7.7 vs +1.3 words; MMSE NS", "evidence_quality": "low (implausibly small SEs; Mars cocoa and coauthor)", "limitations": "Single centre, not replicated by independent trials", "study_ids": ["mastroiacovo2015"]}, {"duration": "8 weeks", "population": "100 healthy adults 65-74 (Finland)", "outcome": "TMT A/B, verbal fluency", "effect_estimate": "No between-group difference (TMT-B p=0.66)", "evidence_quality": "moderate", "limitations": "Active comparator contained 86 mg; highly educated sample", "study_ids": ["suominen2020"]}, {"duration": "12 weeks", "population": "211 healthy adults 50-75", "outcome": "object recognition (primary), list learning", "effect_estimate": "Primary null; list learning improved mainly in lowest diet-quality tertile", "evidence_quality": "low-moderate", "limitations": "Post hoc moderation; Mars coauthor and grant", "study_ids": ["sloan2021"]}, {"duration": "2 years", "population": "573 adults aged 60 and older (COSMOS-Clinic)", "outcome": "global cognition, episodic memory, executive function", "effect_estimate": "Global -0.01 SD (-0.08 to 0.05); memory -0.01 (-0.13 to 0.10); EF 0.003 (-0.07 to 0.08)", "evidence_quality": "high (null)", "limitations": "Older, educated US sample; not young adults", "study_ids": ["vyas2024"]}, {"duration": "3 years", "population": "2262 adults aged 65 and older (COSMOS-Mind)", "outcome": "global cognition composite (telephone); MCI/dementia incidence", "effect_estimate": "z 0.03 (-0.02 to 0.08); no difference in MCI or dementia incidence", "evidence_quality": "high (null)", "limitations": "Telephone battery; low power for incidence", "study_ids": ["baker2023", "sachs2023"]}, {"duration": "1-3 years", "population": "3562 adults aged 60 and older (COSMOS-Web)", "outcome": "ModRey list recall (primary)", "effect_estimate": "Overall d=0.025 (NS); lowest diet-quality tertile +0.28 words, d=0.086", "evidence_quality": "high (null overall); low (subgroup)", "limitations": "Online testing; subgroup interaction p=0.041; Mars funding", "study_ids": ["brickman2023"]}, {"duration": "12 months", "population": "259 older adults with SCI or MCI (CANN)", "outcome": "picture recognition false positives; cognition; brain structure", "effect_estimate": "No benefit; executive function worse than control; cortical volume decrease", "evidence_quality": "moderate", "limitations": "Flavanols combined with DHA; cannot isolate cocoa", "study_ids": ["vauzour2023"]}, {"duration": "12 weeks", "population": "74 healthy adults aged 35-55 (safety)", "outcome": "BP, platelet function, metabolic variables, adverse events", "effect_estimate": "Up to 2000 mg/day well tolerated; AEs mild and not different from control", "evidence_quality": "moderate", "limitations": "Mars-run; small; healthy low-risk adults only", "study_ids": ["ottaviani2015"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Cocoa flavanols have a long history of food use. Up to 2000 mg/day for 12 weeks was well tolerated in healthy adults [ottaviani2015], and 500 mg/day for a median of 3.6 years in 21,442 older adults raised no safety concerns [sesso2022]. Product-level risks are more relevant than flavanol toxicity. They include methylxanthines (theobromine and some caffeine), which may raise pulse rate [crews2008] and affect sleep in sensitive people. They also include the energy, sugar and fat of chocolate vehicles, and cadmium and lead, which concentrate with cocoa solids and vary by origin [abt2018].", "adverse_effects": ["Mild gastrointestinal complaints and other mild AEs not different from control at up to 2000 mg/day [ottaviani2015]", "Increased pulse rate with daily dark chocolate plus cocoa [crews2008]", "Possible slower reaction time at very high acute doses in one hypoxia study [bloomfield2023]", "Weight gain risk if delivered as chocolate (about 5.5 kcal/g)", "A 12-month flavanol + DHA combination was associated with a small glucose increase and worse executive function relative to control [vauzour2023] (cannot be attributed to cocoa alone)"], "contraindications": ["Caffeine or theobromine sensitivity, arrhythmia or palpitations (product dependent)", "Chronic kidney disease or a history of oxalate stones (cocoa is oxalate-rich; plausible, not studied here)", "Gastro-oesophageal reflux (chocolate is a commonly reported trigger; not reviewed here)"], "drug_interactions": ["Additive stimulant effect with caffeine-containing products and stimulant medications (methylxanthines)", "Theoretical additive effects with antihypertensive and antiplatelet drugs (flavanols lower BP in some trials; platelet function unchanged at up to 2000 mg/day [ottaviani2015]); clinically relevant interactions not documented in the sources reviewed", "Theophylline: theobromine shares methylxanthine metabolism (CYP1A2); clinical relevance at food doses uncertain"], "upper_limits": "No tolerable upper intake level has been set for cocoa flavanols. Short-term safety data exist up to 2000 mg/day [ottaviani2015]. Contaminants: the EFSA tolerable weekly intake for cadmium is 2.5 microgram/kg body weight (background knowledge, not re-verified here). EU maximum levels for cadmium in cocoa products are set in Regulation (EU) 2023/915: 0.60 mg/kg for cocoa powder sold to the final consumer, with graded limits for chocolate by cocoa-solids content (0.10 mg/kg for less than 30%, up to 0.80 mg/kg for 50% or more; the upper value is from the 2014 amendment and not re-verified). US: no federal cadmium limit for chocolate identified; California Proposition 65 warning levels apply (not re-verified).", "sleep_impact": "Flavanols themselves are not known to affect sleep. However, 8 g of high-flavanol cocoa carries about 180 mg theobromine and about 20 mg caffeine [gratton2020]. Late-evening intake may disturb sleep in sensitive individuals (not directly studied in the sources reviewed).", "long_term_exposure_studied": "500 mg/day for a median of 3.6 years (COSMOS, n=21,442) [sesso2022]. 2000 mg/day for 12 weeks [ottaviani2015].", "daily_use_appropriate": "probably", "special_populations": "Pregnancy and breastfeeding: caffeine and theobromine count towards caffeine limits, and concentrated extracts have not been studied, so use food amounts only. Children: cadmium exposure per kg body weight is higher, so avoid concentrated high-cocoa products marketed for cognition. Psychiatric conditions (anxiety, panic): methylxanthines may worsen symptoms. Older adults on antihypertensives: monitor BP. Kidney disease or oxalate stones: caution.", "specialist_review_needed": ["Food safety toxicologist (cadmium and lead specification and supplier certificates of analysis)", "Clinical pharmacist (stimulant and antihypertensive co-use)", "Registered dietitian (sugar and energy content of chocolate formats)"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "Flavanols are safe at studied doses, but any cocoa-based product must meet cadmium and lead specifications (EU 2023/915 limits; origin-dependent levels [abt2018]), declare caffeine and theobromine content, and avoid sugar-heavy vehicles."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Authorised Article 13(5) health claim: 'Cocoa flavanols help maintain the elasticity of blood vessels, which contributes to normal blood flow.' Conditions: the consumer must be informed that the effect is obtained with 200 mg cocoa flavanols per day. The claim originally covered only cocoa beverages (with cocoa powder) or dark chocolate providing at least 200 mg/day of flavanols with a degree of polymerisation of 1-10 (Commission Regulation (EU) No 851/2013, applicant Barry Callebaut, based on EFSA opinion doi 10.2903/j.efsa.2012.2809). It was extended to capsules or tablets with high-flavanol cocoa extract (Regulation (EU) 2015/539; EFSA opinion doi 10.2903/j.efsa.2014.3654). Proprietary-data restrictions expired on 23/09/2018 and 20/04/2020 respectively (per the UK register annex of proprietary claims, accessed 2026-10-25), so other operators can use the claim if they meet the conditions. Verify the current wording in the EU Union Register before use. No cognitive or brain claim for cocoa flavanols is authorised. Cocoa powder and chocolate are conventional foods, and cocoa products must meet the cadmium maximum levels of Regulation (EU) 2023/915.", "us": "Cocoa powder and chocolate are conventional foods (standards of identity for cocoa products under 21 CFR Part 163, not re-verified). Cocoa-extract products such as CocoaVia are marketed as dietary supplements under DSHEA with structure/function claims. Whether a specific concentrated extract requires an NDI notification is uncertain and depends on the manufacturing process. Under FTC standards, a cognition claim for healthy adults would lack competent and reliable scientific evidence given the COSMOS null results [baker2023, vyas2024, brickman2023]. Vascular structure/function claims need their own substantiation. No federal cadmium limit for chocolate was identified (uncertain), and California Proposition 65 warnings apply to products exceeding safe-harbour levels.", "morocco": "Uncertain. General food labelling is governed by Decree no. 2-12-389 of 22 April 2013 (labelling conditions for food products), enforced by ONSSA under Law 28-07 on food safety. A 2016 USDA attaché report stated that the Ministry of Health inspects products bearing health or dietary claims. A dedicated food-supplement law has reportedly been blocked since at least 2023 (press, Medias24), and a 2026 press item mentions a draft decree reserving supplement sales to pharmacies (not verified as adopted). We found no Moroccan positive list of health claims. Treat any health claim as requiring prior consultation with ONSSA or the Ministry of Health.", "permitted_claims_examples": ["EU (Reg. (EU) No 851/2013; 2015/539): 'Cocoa flavanols help maintain the elasticity of blood vessels, which contributes to normal blood flow.' Information shall be given that the beneficial effect is obtained with a daily intake of 200 mg of cocoa flavanols (cocoa beverages with cocoa powder, dark chocolate, or capsules/tablets of high-flavanol cocoa extract providing at least 200 mg/day of flavanols with DP 1-10)."], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "Cocoa powder and conventionally produced cocoa extracts have a history of use, and high-flavanol extracts are on the EU market under the authorised claim. The novel-food status of any specific new extraction process or highly purified (-)-epicatechin should be checked in the EU Novel Food Catalogue (not verified here). For the US, NDI status of concentrated extracts is uncertain.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Good. Natural (non-alkalised) or specialty high-flavanol cocoa powder fits easily into drinks, porridge, bars or yoghurt. 200 mg (EU claim dose) needs about 2.5 g of a high-flavanol powder (per the authorisation example), and 500-700 mg needs about 6-8 g [gratton2020]. Standard Dutch-processed cocoa would need 36-130 g [miller2008], which is impractical.", "formats": ["cocoa drink powder (natural or high-flavanol)", "dark chocolate square at least 10 g for 200 mg (high-flavanol chocolate only)", "capsules or tablets of cocoa extract", "breakfast cereals or bars with high-flavanol cocoa (need validation of losses during baking)"], "stability": "Flavanols are degraded by alkalisation (linear with pH [miller2008]), high-temperature roasting and baking, and they epimerise to poorly absorbed (+)-catechin. Use low-pH, low-heat processing and verify flavanol content (DP 1-10) in the finished product by validated HPLC.", "taste": "High-flavanol cocoa is bitter and astringent, which is the main reason industry alkalises. Masking often adds sugar, which conflicts with the health positioning.", "bioavailability_matrix": "(-)-Epicatechin is well absorbed (plasma peak about 2 h). The flavanol content of the matrix matters more than the matrix itself. Milk-protein effects on absorption have been debated (not reviewed in detail here). Placebo design in trials often uses alkalised cocoa, which shows that colour and taste can be matched.", "dose_feasibility": "Feasible: 200 mg/day fits in 2.5 g of high-flavanol powder or 10 g of high-flavanol dark chocolate. 500-900 mg fits in 6-12 g of specialty powder or in capsules. Ordinary dark chocolate would require 50 g or more for about 400 mg [suominen2020], adding about 270 kcal and significant cadmium.", "cost_availability": "High-flavanol cocoa powders (for example Barry Callebaut Acticoa) and Mars Cocoapro/CocoaVia extracts are commercially available. Supply is concentrated in a few suppliers.", "cost_evidence": "Retail CocoaVia (500 mg flavanols per serving, 30 servings) was listed at about US$1.40-1.50 per serving (USD 41.99-44.99 per container) on a US price-comparison site (supp.co, accessed 2026-10-25). Ingredient bulk prices were not researched here."}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 3, "effect_magnitude": 1.5, "safety": 8, "reproducibility": 2, "food_suitability": 7, "cost_access": 6, "rationale": "Evidence quality 3: the best grade for any cognitive outcome in healthy young adults or students is C (inconsistent), large chronic trials in older adults are null (E), and only the vascular biomarker reaches B. Effect magnitude 1.5: preregistered young-adult trials are null, and the largest positive subgroup effect is d=0.086 (about 0.3 words). Safety 8: well tolerated up to 2000 mg/day and over 3.6 years at 500 mg/day; deductions for cadmium/lead and methylxanthines. Reproducibility 2: positive findings cluster in industry-linked single laboratories, and independent and preregistered replications are null. Food suitability 7: the effective dose fits in a few grams of natural high-flavanol cocoa, but taste and processing losses are real constraints. Cost/access 6: about US$1.4-1.5 per 500 mg retail; cheaper as natural cocoa powder, though supply of standardised material is concentrated."}, "research_gaps": ["No adequately powered, preregistered chronic RCT in healthy young adults or students with academically relevant outcomes", "No trial testing cocoa flavanols under realistic exam-period sleep restriction in students (only one acute total-sleep-deprivation trial [grassi2016])", "The repletion hypothesis (benefit only with low habitual flavanol intake) needs a dedicated trial enrolling low-gVLM participants of all ages", "Independent replication of the L'Aquila trials with raw data sharing; their variance pattern needs explanation", "Registered trials without published results: NCT03723694 (259 participants, 650 mg Cocoapro, completed 2023), NCT03030053 (80 older adults, status unknown), NCT02789761 (34 males, status unknown) and NCT02996578 (16 participants), all with no results found", "Dose-response in young adults beyond 900 mg, and differentiation of epicatechin versus procyanidin and methylxanthine contributions", "Cadmium-exposure modelling for daily high-cocoa products in adolescents and young women"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 4.3, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": "C", "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": "C", "best_grade_sleep_stress": "C", "best_grade_surrogate": "B", "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"cognitive_fatigue": "C", "working_memory": "C", "episodic_memory": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 18, "n_studies": 35, "n_verified": 35, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 17, "n_systematic_reviews": 4, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["benefit", "inconsistent", "insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "curcumin", "name": "Curcumin / turmeric extracts", "category": "G", "category_name": "Polyphenols and plant bioactives", "aliases": ["curcumin", "turmeric extract", "Curcuma longa extract", "curcuminoids", "Theracurmin", "Longvida", "BCM-95 (Biocurcumax)", "nano-curcumin", "phytosomal curcumin"], "food_sources": ["turmeric root/powder (Curcuma longa), used fresh or dried as a spice, typically 2-5% curcumin by weight", "curry blends and South/Southeast Asian cuisine", "golden milk and turmeric-based beverages", "note: whole-food turmeric delivers far lower, far less bioavailable curcumin doses than the enhanced-bioavailability extracts used in trials"], "active_constituents": ["curcumin (diferuloylmethane)", "demethoxycurcumin and bisdemethoxycurcumin (minor curcuminoids)", "piperine (bioavailability enhancer, co-administered, not from turmeric itself)", "lipidated/nanoparticle/phytosome formulations (e.g. Longvida solid-lipid curcumin, Theracurmin colloidal nanoparticle, BCM-95/Biocurcumax curcumin-turmeric-oil complex) designed to overcome curcumin's very poor native oral bioavailability"], "one_line_verdict": "Enhanced-bioavailability curcumin extracts produce small, inconsistent improvements in working memory and mood in healthy older adults over weeks to 18 months, show no convincing benefit in Alzheimer's disease, and have never been adequately tested in healthy young adults or students, while raising real (if uncommon) hepatotoxicity and drug-interaction concerns at supplement doses.", "scientific_conclusion": "Native curcumin is essentially unabsorbed orally, so every human cognition trial worth weighing used an enhanced-bioavailability formulation (Longvida solid-lipid curcumin, Theracurmin colloidal nanoparticle, or BCM-95/Biocurcumax) rather than plain turmeric or standard curcumin powder. In healthy older adults (50-85 y), Cox et al. 2015 (n=60, crossover, Longvida 400 mg/day for 4 weeks) found an acute 1-hour improvement in sustained attention and working memory after a single dose, plus chronic improvements in working memory and mood (reduced fatigue, better calmness after stress) [cox2015]. A partial replication (Cox 2020, n=80, parallel, Longvida 400 mg/day for 12 weeks) again found better working memory (Serial Threes/Sevens, virtual Morris Water Maze) at 12 weeks and lower fatigue on the POMS at 4 and 12 weeks, alongside a significant rise in blood glucose in the curcumin group [cox2020]. Small et al. 2018 (n=40, Theracurmin 90 mg curcumin twice daily, 18 months) reported improved verbal and visual memory and attention versus placebo, with parallel reductions in FDDNP-PET amyloid/tau-related signal in the amygdala - a biomarker finding that is mechanistically interesting but not a validated surrogate for clinical benefit [small2018]. In contrast, Rainey-Smith et al. 2016 (n=96, BCM-95/Biocurcumax 1500 mg/day, 12 months) found no significant curcumin effect on any cognitive measure except a secondary, likely placebo-decline-driven MoCA interaction, and explicitly called for cautious interpretation [raineysmith2016]. Kuszewski et al. 2020 (n=152, curcumin 160 mg/day alone or with fish oil, 16 weeks) found curcumin had no effect on cerebrovascular responsiveness to hypercapnia (the primary outcome) or on cardiometabolic biomarkers, with fish oil but not curcumin showing benefit [kuszewski2020]. In clinical Alzheimer's disease, the small, likely underpowered Baum et al. 2008 pilot (n approx. 34, curcumin up to 4 g/day, 6 months) found no significant cognitive benefit over placebo [baum2008]; this is dementia-population evidence and must not be generalised to healthy adults or used to infer enhancement in the non-demented. The newest and most comprehensive synthesis, Yu et al. 2025 (SR/MA, 10 human RCTs, 531 participants, plus 25 preclinical rodent studies), found curcumin preclinically improves rodent memory robustly (SMD -1.78 to 2.36) but produces no significant effect on global human cognition (SMD 0.14, 95% CI -0.78 to 1.07), with subgroup signals limited to working memory (SMD 1.01, 95% CI 0.15-1.87) and processing speed (SMD 0.37, 95% CI 0.07-0.67), and adverse events numerically higher in the curcumin arm [yu2025]. Nunes et al. 2024 narratively reviewed 15 clinical trials and concluded curcumin 'shows promise' for cognition and muscle health in aging but called for larger, better-designed trials [nunes2024]. No adequately powered trial has tested healthy young adults, students, or knowledge workers under cognitive load; all positive signals are in older adults (50-85y), a population with different baseline inflammatory and vascular status, so extrapolation to young healthy adults is unsupported. Across trials, effect sizes are small-to-moderate (d/SMD approx. 0.3-1.0 on subscales), inconsistent across formulations and outcome batteries, and several trials (Rainey-Smith, Kuszewski) are null on their primary cognitive/vascular endpoints. Industry funding and conflicts are notable: Cox/Scholey/Pipingas disclose funding from the supplement industry and Longvida is a proprietary branded ingredient; this does not invalidate the data but caps single-trial grading. Overall this supports grade B (possible benefit) for working memory/mood in healthy older adults using specific bioavailable extracts over 4-18 months, grade D/insufficient for healthy young adults and students (no direct data), and grade E/no-benefit for Alzheimer's disease cognitive outcomes from the single available RCT, consistent with other botanicals failing to modify established dementia.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Curcumin is not an essential nutrient and there is no recognized deficiency state; turmeric is a culinary spice consumed in gram-level food amounts that deliver milligram-level, poorly absorbed curcumin, far below any dose used in a positive trial (80-1500 mg/day of a bioavailability-enhanced extract). All plausible cognitive/mood signals come from pharmacological-style supplementation with engineered-bioavailability extracts in older adults, not from ordinary dietary turmeric intake and not from correcting any nutrient shortfall. This is therefore an enhancement/pharmacological-support hypothesis in an already-nourished population, not deficiency correction, and the enhancement signal itself is inconsistent across trials and formulations and entirely untested in healthy young or student populations.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Marketing for 'brain-boosting turmeric lattes' or plain turmeric capsules overstates the evidence in two ways: first, by implying that ordinary turmeric (culinary doses, native curcumin) confers the cognitive benefits seen in trials, when every positive study used a proprietary enhanced-bioavailability extract (Longvida, Theracurmin, BCM-95) at engineered doses unreachable from food; second, by generalizing short positive studies in older adults (or a single 18-month biomarker-linked trial) to young, healthy consumers or to disease prevention/treatment claims, when the one RCT in diagnosed Alzheimer's disease was null and no RCT exists in healthy young adults or students. The EU has not authorised any cognitive health claim for curcumin/turmeric under Regulation 1924/2006, and curcumin is separately regulated as a food colour additive (E100, ADI 3 mg/kg bw/day, EFSA 2010) at levels far below supplement doses, which is a different regulatory and safety context entirely. The honest position: turmeric as a food is safe and plausibly healthful background nutrition with weak direct cognitive evidence; isolated bioavailable curcumin extracts show modest, inconsistent, population-restricted (older adult) signals for mood and working memory, not a validated memory or IQ enhancer for the general population, and not a dementia treatment.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Curcumin has broad in-vitro and animal evidence for anti-inflammatory (NF-kB, COX-2 inhibition) and antioxidant (Nrf2 activation) activity, and for interfering with amyloid-beta aggregation in cell and rodent models of Alzheimer's disease. Its translation to human brain effects is limited by extremely poor native oral bioavailability (rapid glucuronidation/sulfation, low solubility), which enhanced formulations (lipidation, nanoparticle, phytosome, piperine co-administration) partially overcome. Human PET biomarker data (FDDNP) suggest some brain penetration and amyloid/tau-related signal change with an 18-month bioavailable formulation, but this is a single small trial and is not an established surrogate of cognitive outcome.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Oral curcumin -> poor intestinal absorption, rapid first-pass glucuronidation/sulfation -> very low native plasma bioavailability", "detail": "Native curcumin plasma levels are near-undetectable after standard doses; bioavailability-enhancing formulations (lipidation, nanoparticles, phytosomes, piperine co-administration) raise plasma exposure several-fold, which is why all positive cognitive trials used branded extracts (Longvida, Theracurmin, BCM-95) rather than plain turmeric or unformulated curcumin.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Absorbed curcuminoids -> systemic anti-inflammatory and antioxidant signaling (NF-kB, COX-2, Nrf2 modulation)", "detail": "Extensively shown in vitro and in rodents; human evidence of systemic anti-inflammatory effect exists for peripheral markers (e.g., CRP in some metabolic trials) but is not consistently linked to brain outcomes in the cognition trials reviewed here.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Peripheral/systemic effect -> proposed blood-brain barrier penetration and modulation of amyloid-beta/tau-related brain signal", "detail": "FDDNP-PET in Small 2018 (18 months, Theracurmin) showed decreased amygdala binding with curcumin versus placebo, interpreted as reduced amyloid/tau-related accumulation; single small trial, PET ligand signal is a biomarker, not histologically confirmed amyloid/tau burden.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Amyloid-beta aggregation inhibition and neuroprotection against oxidative/inflammatory injury", "detail": "Robust in rodent Alzheimer's models (meta-analytic SMD around -1.8 to 2.4 for acquisition/retention memory, Yu et al. 2025) and in vitro; mechanistic plausibility for neurodegeneration is strong at the animal level but has not translated into a positive human Alzheimer's disease RCT (Baum 2008 null).", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Proposed downstream improvement in measured human cognition (working memory, mood, attention) in older adults", "detail": "Small-to-moderate, inconsistent effects across RCTs in healthy older adults (working memory, fatigue/mood); null on cerebrovascular and global cognitive primary endpoints in other trials; not tested in healthy young adults.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["NF-kB / COX-2 anti-inflammatory signaling", "Nrf2-mediated antioxidant response", "amyloid-beta aggregation inhibition (animal/in vitro)", "bioavailability-engineering (lipidation, nanoparticle, phytosome, piperine co-administration) as a prerequisite for any systemic exposure", "possible neurovascular/cerebrovascular modulation (not confirmed - Kuszewski 2020 null on primary cerebrovascular endpoint)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Healthy adults aged 60-85, community-dwelling", "dose_or_intake": "400 mg Longvida (80 mg curcumin), single dose", "duration": "single dose, assessed 1 and 3 hours post-dose", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "One crossover RCT (n=60) found significantly better sustained attention and working memory performance 1 hour after a single dose versus placebo; effect not independently replicated acutely.", "effect_estimate": "Significant between-group difference on working memory/attention tasks at 1h, exact effect size not reported in abstract; not reported as standardized effect size", "certainty_notes": "Single small trial, no independent acute replication, industry-connected research group; downgraded for imprecision and lack of replication.", "key_study_ids": ["cox2015"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Healthy adults aged 50-85 (mean ~68), community-dwelling, non-demented", "dose_or_intake": "80-400 mg bioavailable curcumin/day (Longvida); Theracurmin 180 mg curcumin/day", "duration": "4 weeks to 18 months", "grade": "B", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Cox 2015 (n=60, 4wk) and Cox 2020 (n=80, 12wk partial replication) both found improved working memory (digit span, Morris Water Maze analogue) with Longvida versus placebo; Small 2018 (n=40, 18mo, Theracurmin) found improved verbal/visual memory and attention (ES 0.50-0.96) with parallel amyloid/tau PET signal reduction. Rainey-Smith 2016 (n=96, 12mo, BCM-95) found no significant benefit on any cognitive measure except an MoCA interaction likely driven by placebo decline.", "effect_estimate": "Small 2018: SRT Total ES=0.53 (p=0.002), BVMT-R Recall ES=0.50 (p=0.01), attention ES=0.96 (p<0.0001) vs placebo; Cox 2020: significant 12-week working memory tasks, exact d not given in abstract; Rainey-Smith: no significant between-group effect on primary cognitive battery", "certainty_notes": "Promising but inconsistent across formulations (Longvida, Theracurmin positive; BCM-95 null); small samples (n=40-96), multiple comparisons without strict correction in some trials, industry/brand-connected authors in two of three positive trials; downgraded for inconsistency and risk of bias; cannot exceed grade B given single-trial-per-formulation designs.", "key_study_ids": ["cox2015", "cox2020", "small2018", "raineysmith2016"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Healthy adults aged 50-85", "dose_or_intake": "80-400 mg bioavailable curcumin/day (Longvida)", "duration": "4-12 weeks", "grade": "B", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Cox 2015 and Cox 2020 both found reduced fatigue (POMS Fatigue-Inertia) with curcumin versus placebo at 4 weeks, sustained at 12 weeks in Cox 2020; other mood subscales (tension, anger, confusion) improved at 4 weeks only in Cox 2020 and did not persist to 12 weeks.", "effect_estimate": "Cox 2020 full text: POMS Fatigue-Inertia F(1,79)=6.795, p=0.01 (4wk) and F(1,75)=8.120, p=0.006 (12wk); Tension-Anxiety F(1,77)=8.073, p=0.006 (4wk only, not sustained)", "certainty_notes": "Consistent direction across two trials from the same research group using the same proprietary formulation (Longvida); not independently replicated by a different group/formulation; mood scales are self-report and subject to expectancy/unblinding risk given curcumin's taste/GI effects.", "key_study_ids": ["cox2015", "cox2020"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Diagnosed mild-to-moderate Alzheimer's disease patients", "dose_or_intake": "1-4 g/day curcumin", "duration": "6 months", "grade": "E", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Small pilot RCT (role: null_or_negative) found no significant cognitive benefit of curcumin over placebo in diagnosed Alzheimer's disease patients; likely underpowered but is the only dedicated AD RCT identified.", "effect_estimate": "Not reported as a quantified effect size in the available abstract/letter format; reported as non-significant between-group difference on cognitive measures", "certainty_notes": "Small sample, clinical (dementia) population - must not be generalised to healthy adults or to prevention; downgraded for imprecision (small n) and indirectness if applied outside AD.", "key_study_ids": ["baum2008"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cerebral_blood_flow", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Sedentary overweight/obese older adults aged 50-80", "dose_or_intake": "160 mg curcumin/day, alone or with fish oil", "duration": "16 weeks", "grade": "E", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "RCT (n=152) found curcumin had no significant effect on cerebrovascular responsiveness to hypercapnia (primary outcome) or on cardiometabolic/inflammatory biomarkers, alone or combined with fish oil; fish oil alone showed modest vascular benefit.", "effect_estimate": "Primary outcome (cerebrovascular reactivity) not significantly affected by curcumin, p-value not significant (exact value not reported in abstract)", "certainty_notes": "Adequately powered, pre-registered trial; a clean null on a biomarker outcome, useful as a counterweight to the positive cognition trials above.", "key_study_ids": ["kuszewski2020"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "No identified RCT of curcumin/turmeric on cognition in healthy adults under approximately 50 years, students, or knowledge workers", "dose_or_intake": "not studied", "duration": "not studied", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No dedicated trial of curcumin or bioavailable curcumin extracts on cognitive performance, mood, or stress resilience in healthy young adults, students, or knowledge workers was found across systematic searches; all positive human cognition signals are restricted to adults aged 50 and older.", "effect_estimate": "not reported - no data", "certainty_notes": "Absence of evidence, not evidence of absence, but direct extrapolation from older-adult trials to young healthy adults is not supported given differing baseline inflammatory/vascular status and the age-dependent framing of the hypothesis (anti-inflammatory support against age-related decline).", "key_study_ids": ["yu2025", "nunes2024"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "400 mg Longvida (80 mg curcumin), single dose", "onset": "1 hour", "duration_studied": "single dose, tested at 1h and 3h", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "effect_estimate": "Significant improvement vs placebo at 1h on sustained attention and working memory tasks; not quantified as standardized effect size in available abstract", "certainty": "low - single small trial (n=60), not independently replicated", "safety": "no significant hematological safety signal reported; reduced total/LDL cholesterol noted as secondary finding", "study_ids": ["cox2015"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "4-12 weeks", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "outcome": "working memory and mood (fatigue)", "effect_estimate": "Significant improvement on working memory tasks and POMS Fatigue-Inertia at 4 and 12 weeks (Cox 2020); consistent with Cox 2015 at 4 weeks", "evidence_quality": "B - consistent within one research group/formulation, not independently replicated by other groups", "limitations": "Same lab/brand (Longvida) across both trials; industry funding disclosed; small-moderate samples (n=60-80)", "study_ids": ["cox2015", "cox2020"]}, {"duration": "18 months", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "outcome": "verbal/visual memory, attention, amyloid/tau PET signal", "effect_estimate": "Improved memory (ES 0.50-0.63) and attention (ES 0.96) with Theracurmin vs placebo; reduced amygdala FDDNP-PET signal", "evidence_quality": "B - single trial, small n=40, but longest duration and includes objective biomarker corroboration", "limitations": "Small sample, single site, PET substudy even smaller (n=30), biomarker not a validated surrogate for clinical cognitive benefit", "study_ids": ["small2018"]}, {"duration": "12 months", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "outcome": "global cognition (MoCA) and full cognitive battery", "effect_estimate": "No significant curcumin effect on primary cognitive battery; a time x treatment interaction on MoCA attributed by authors to placebo-group decline rather than curcumin benefit", "evidence_quality": "C - null result from an adequately sized trial using a different formulation (BCM-95) than the positive Longvida/Theracurmin trials", "limitations": "Authors themselves caution against over-interpreting the MoCA finding; illustrates formulation-to-formulation inconsistency", "study_ids": ["raineysmith2016"]}, {"duration": "6 months", "population": "clinical_population", "outcome": "cognition in diagnosed Alzheimer's disease", "effect_estimate": "No significant benefit vs placebo", "evidence_quality": "D - small, likely underpowered pilot; null result in a clinical population, not generalisable to prevention or healthy adults", "limitations": "Small sample, early-generation curcumin formulation with uncertain bioavailability, high dropout risk typical of AD pilot trials", "study_ids": ["baum2008"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Turmeric as a culinary spice has a long history of safe food use. Isolated curcumin and especially enhanced-bioavailability extracts (piperine-, lipid-, or nanoparticle-based) are generally well tolerated in short-to-medium-term RCTs (mild GI upset most common), but rare, idiosyncratic hepatotoxicity has been reported in case literature (including a cluster of cases reviewed by the Italian medicines agency AIFA in 2019, which this agent could not independently verify via lit.py/PubMed and which should be re-confirmed from AIFA/EMA primary sources before any regulatory-facing claim is made) and is corroborated by at least one verified case report [abdallah2020]. Curcumin/piperine combinations have shown no clinically significant interaction with CYP3A, CYP2C9, UGT or SULT substrates in one small short-term PK study [volak2013], but curcumin is a known in-vitro P-gp and CYP inhibitor at higher concentrations, and anticoagulant (warfarin) interaction is a recognized theoretical and case-level concern not directly tested in the verified literature here. Elevated blood glucose was observed as a secondary finding in one 12-week RCT [cox2020]. Adverse event incidence was numerically higher in curcumin versus placebo arms pooled across RCTs in the most recent meta-analysis [yu2025], though specific events were not detailed.", "adverse_effects": ["mild gastrointestinal upset (nausea, diarrhea) reported in some RCTs", "elevated fasting blood glucose observed in a 12-week RCT [cox2020]", "rare idiosyncratic hepatotoxicity / acute liver injury (case reports, including a verified US case [abdallah2020]; an Italian AIFA 2019 case cluster is a commonly cited lead that this agent could not independently verify via lit.py and should be confirmed from primary AIFA/EMA sources)", "numerically higher adverse event incidence in curcumin versus placebo arms in pooled RCT data [yu2025], specific events not itemized in the available meta-analysis abstract"], "contraindications": ["pre-existing liver disease (given hepatotoxicity signal)", "gallbladder disease / biliary obstruction (curcumin stimulates bile secretion)", "known hypersensitivity to turmeric/curcumin or piperine", "pregnancy and lactation at supplement (non-culinary) doses - insufficient safety data identified in this search"], "drug_interactions": ["anticoagulants/antiplatelets (warfarin, aspirin, clopidogrel) - theoretical and case-level bleeding risk from curcumin's antiplatelet activity; not directly tested in the one verified PK study here, which used different probe drugs", "CYP3A4, CYP2C9, UGT and SULT substrate drugs - one small short-term study found no significant interaction for midazolam/flurbiprofen/paracetamol with a curcumin/piperine extract [volak2013], but this does not rule out interactions with other substrates, chronic dosing, or higher-exposure nanoparticle/lipidated formulations", "P-glycoprotein (P-gp) substrate drugs - plausible in-vitro inhibition by curcumin; not directly confirmed in a verified human interaction study in this search", "diabetes medications - plausible additive glucose-lowering or glucose-altering effect given mixed glycemic findings across trials [cox2020 showed glucose increase; other metabolic-syndrome trials showed glycemic improvement], direction inconsistent and needs case-by-case review"], "upper_limits": "No formal human upper intake level (UL) for curcumin supplements was identified in this search. EFSA has set an Acceptable Daily Intake (ADI) of 3 mg/kg body weight/day for curcumin as a food colour additive (E100), a regulatory context distinct from supplement dosing; many supplement trials reviewed here used doses from 80 mg/day (bioavailable extract) up to 1500-4000 mg/day (curcumin or curcuminoids), i.e. supplement doses can far exceed the E100 ADI equivalent, and this gap has not been formally reconciled in a human safety UL for supplement-grade curcumin in the sources identified here.", "sleep_impact": "No dedicated human sleep-architecture study was identified in this search; one RCT [cox2020] found a possible benefit on PSQI daytime-dysfunction subscale at 12 weeks (chi-squared, p=0.018) but no significant effect on the broader Pittsburgh Sleep Quality Index. No evidence of sleep disruption was found, but evidence of sleep benefit is also weak and secondary.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "The longest controlled human exposure identified is 18 months [small2018, n=40] and 12 months [raineysmith2016, n=96], both without major safety signals reported beyond the specific findings noted above; these samples are too small to detect rare adverse events such as idiosyncratic hepatotoxicity, which by nature requires large-scale pharmacovigilance rather than RCT-level sample sizes to detect reliably.", "daily_use_appropriate": "uncertain", "special_populations": "Pregnant/lactating women, children, people on anticoagulant or antiplatelet therapy, people with hepatobiliary disease, and people with diabetes on glucose-lowering medication were not adequately studied for curcumin supplement safety in the sources verified here and should be excluded from any Brain Food curcumin supplement formulation pending specialist review; culinary turmeric use in food is a separate, lower-risk context.", "specialist_review_needed": ["hepatology / pharmacovigilance review of curcumin/turmeric-associated hepatotoxicity case series (including independent verification of the AIFA 2019 Italian case cluster from primary regulatory sources)", "clinical pharmacology review of anticoagulant/antiplatelet and CYP/P-gp interaction risk at supplement-level doses and with enhanced-bioavailability formulations specifically", "obstetric/perinatal review for use in pregnancy and lactation at supplement doses"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "No systematic human safety signal rules out curcumin/turmeric at culinary or standard supplement doses, and the verified pharmacokinetic interaction study was reassuring for the specific drugs tested, but a real (if rare) hepatotoxicity signal exists in case literature, drug interaction coverage is incomplete (anticoagulants, P-gp substrates not directly tested), and vulnerable populations (pregnancy, hepatobiliary disease, anticoagulant users) have not been adequately studied; the gate is conditional on specialist review and on using a well-characterized, quality-controlled extract at doses within the range tested in RCTs rather than arbitrarily high doses."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Curcumin is authorised as food colour additive E100 with an EFSA-established Acceptable Daily Intake (ADI) of 3 mg/kg body weight/day (EFSA 2010 re-evaluation), a context distinct from supplement use. No cognitive health claim for curcumin or turmeric is authorised under Regulation (EC) 1924/2006 on the EU Register of nutrition and health claims; general turmeric/curcumin food supplements are regulated as food supplements under Directive 2002/46/EC at national member-state level. Enhanced-bioavailability forms (nanoparticle, phytosome formulations) may raise novel food considerations depending on production method and particle size versus traditional curcumin extracts; this agent did not independently confirm a specific EU novel food catalogue entry for any named branded extract (Longvida, Theracurmin, BCM-95) and this should be verified directly against the EU Novel Food Catalogue before product development.", "us": "Curcumin and turmeric extracts are generally marketed in the US as dietary ingredients under DSHEA; turmeric itself has GRAS status for food use. No curcumin-specific qualified health claim for cognition has been reviewed/authorised by FDA, and any structure/function claim (e.g., 'supports memory') would need to meet FTC competent-and-reliable-scientific-evidence substantiation standards, which the current inconsistent human evidence base (per Yu 2025 SR/MA) would likely not satisfy for a strong cognitive claim. New bioavailability-enhanced formulations not marketed before October 1994 may require New Dietary Ingredient (NDI) notification; this agent did not independently verify NDI status for specific branded extracts and this should be confirmed directly with FDA's NDI database.", "morocco": "No Morocco-specific regulatory text on curcumin/turmeric food or supplement status was independently verified in this search; general ONSSA (food safety) and Ministry of Health (supplement/drug borderline) frameworks would apply by analogy to other botanical food supplements, but this should be confirmed directly with ONSSA before market entry; status marked uncertain pending that confirmation.", "permitted_claims_examples": [], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "Potential EU novel food questions exist for nanoparticle/phytosome-engineered curcumin formulations depending on production process and particle characteristics versus traditional curcumin extract preparation; potential US NDI notification requirements exist for newer branded bioavailability-enhanced ingredients. Neither was independently confirmed for the specific named brands in this search and both require direct regulatory-database verification (EU Novel Food Catalogue; FDA NDI database) before formulation decisions.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Culinary turmeric is highly food-suitable (spice, golden milk, curry) but delivers curcumin doses orders of magnitude below any dose shown to affect cognition/mood in trials (80-1500 mg/day of an enhanced-bioavailability extract versus a few hundred milligrams of crude turmeric powder per typical food serving, most of which is unabsorbed). Delivering a trial-relevant dose through ordinary food is not feasible; it requires a standardized, bioavailability-enhanced extract delivered as a supplement, functional-food concentrate, or fortified product rather than as whole-spice turmeric.", "formats": ["standardized bioavailability-enhanced extract capsules/tablets (e.g., lipidated, nanoparticle, phytosome, or piperine-co-formulated)", "fortified functional beverages or bars using a standardized extract (bitter/earthy taste and yellow-orange color must be masked or designed around)", "culinary turmeric powder/spice blends (food-category only, not a vehicle for the tested cognitive-dose range)"], "stability": "Curcumin is sensitive to light, heat, and alkaline pH, which can degrade it during food processing and storage; enhanced-bioavailability formulations are typically more stable and standardized than raw turmeric powder but require specific encapsulation/processing know-how.", "taste": "Curcumin/turmeric has a strong earthy, bitter, slightly peppery taste and intense yellow-orange color that can be difficult to mask in food/beverage formats at the doses used in positive trials; this is a meaningful formulation/sensory constraint for a consumer food product.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Native curcumin: very low oral bioavailability (near-undetectable plasma levels at standard doses). Piperine co-administration: increases bioavailability but piperine itself has its own interaction/safety profile and was the agent tested in the one verified PK interaction study [volak2013], which found no interaction with the specific probe drugs tested. Lipidation (Longvida): used in the positive Cox 2015/2020 trials (80 mg curcumin/day). Nanoparticle (Theracurmin): used in the positive Small 2018 trial (180 mg curcumin/day). Curcumin-turmeric oil complex (BCM-95/Biocurcumax): used in the null Rainey-Smith 2016 trial (1500 mg/day), suggesting formulation and/or dose matters and that bioavailability enhancement alone does not guarantee a cognitive effect.", "dose_feasibility": "The lowest effective dose seen in a positive trial (80 mg curcumin/day as Longvida) is plausibly deliverable in a daily supplement capsule or a concentrated functional-food serving, but not from ordinary culinary turmeric; higher doses used in some trials (1500-4000 mg/day) are not practical or necessarily desirable given the mixed safety signal, and the BCM-95 1500 mg/day trial was null, so higher dose does not reliably mean higher efficacy.", "cost_availability": "Branded bioavailability-enhanced curcumin extracts (Longvida, Theracurmin, BCM-95) are commercially available as supplement ingredients from established ingredient suppliers; not researched here in detail for current unit pricing.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 4, "effect_magnitude": 3, "safety": 5, "reproducibility": 3, "food_suitability": 3, "cost_access": 5, "rationale": "evidence_quality=4: best human outcomes (working memory, mood in healthy older adults) sit at grade B at most, pulled down by a formulation-inconsistent null trial (Rainey-Smith BCM-95), a null AD trial, a null cerebrovascular trial, and a recent SR/MA showing no significant global cognition effect overall (Yu 2025); no data at all in the Brain Food core healthy-young-adult/student population. effect_magnitude=3: where significant, effect sizes are small-to-moderate (SMD/ES approximately 0.3-1.0 on subscales) and inconsistent across trials/formulations, with the primary endpoint null in two of the six RCTs reviewed. safety=5: generally well tolerated short-to-medium term with a reassuring (but narrow) drug-interaction study, but a real hepatotoxicity signal exists in case literature and major interaction/vulnerable-population gaps remain unresolved, warranting a conditional rather than pass gate. reproducibility=3: positive findings cluster within specific brand/formulation-lab pairings (Cox/Scholey with Longvida; Small with Theracurmin) and are not independently replicated by a different lab or formulation; the BCM-95 trial by an independent group was null. food_suitability=3: strong sensory/taste barriers and the gulf between food-deliverable and trial-effective doses make a genuine food format difficult; a supplement/fortified-concentrate format is more realistic. cost_access=5: ingredient is widely available and inexpensive as commodity turmeric, though the specific branded bioavailability-enhanced extracts used in positive trials carry a cost premium not quantified here."}, "research_gaps": ["No RCT has tested curcumin/bioavailable curcumin extracts on cognition, mood, or stress resilience in healthy young adults, students, or knowledge workers under cognitive load - the core Brain Food target population", "No head-to-head trial compares the different bioavailability-enhancement technologies (lipidation vs nanoparticle vs phytosome vs piperine) on the same cognitive battery to determine whether formulation or dose explains the Longvida/Theracurmin-positive vs BCM-95-null pattern", "No adequately powered trial has used objective, performance-based cognitive endpoints with pre-registered primary outcomes and multiplicity correction specifically for curcumin and cognition in any population", "Large-scale pharmacovigilance data on curcumin/turmeric-associated hepatotoxicity incidence (true population-level rate, dose-response, formulation-specific risk) is lacking in the peer-reviewed literature verified here; the frequently cited Italian AIFA 2019 case cluster was not independently verified via lit.py/PubMed and needs direct confirmation from AIFA/EMA primary sources", "No controlled human study of anticoagulant (warfarin) or P-gp substrate drug interaction with curcumin at supplement-relevant doses and with modern enhanced-bioavailability formulations was identified", "No dedicated reproductive/perinatal safety study of curcumin supplements (as opposed to culinary turmeric) was identified"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 3.9, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": "C", "best_grade_chronic": "B", "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": "B", "best_grade_overall": "B", "benefit_domains": {"working_memory": "B"}, "n_outcome_rows": 6, "n_studies": 10, "n_verified": 10, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 3, "n_systematic_reviews": 2, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "beetroot-nitrate", "name": "Dietary nitrate (beetroot juice)", "category": "G", "category_name": "Polyphenols and plant bioactives", "aliases": ["dietary nitrate", "inorganic nitrate", "beetroot juice", "NO3-", "nitrate-nitrite-NO pathway supplement"], "food_sources": ["beetroot (Beta vulgaris) and beetroot juice/concentrate", "spinach", "rocket/arugula", "lettuce", "celery", "Chinese cabbage", "other leafy green and root vegetables high in nitrate"], "active_constituents": ["inorganic nitrate (NO3-)", "betalains (co-occurring pigments, separate bioactive class)", "ascorbate (in some juices, inhibits nitrosamine formation)"], "one_line_verdict": "Beetroot juice reliably raises plasma nitrite and lowers blood pressure, but repeated, mostly null trials in healthy young adults and healthy or overweight older adults show no consistent benefit on measured cognition, so it should be positioned as a vascular/BP food, not a cognitive enhancer.", "scientific_conclusion": "Dietary nitrate from beetroot juice is reduced in vivo via the nitrate-nitrite-nitric oxide (NO) pathway, an oral-microbiome-dependent process (confirmed by mouthwash-abolition studies), raising plasma nitrite and modestly lowering blood pressure and the oxygen cost of exercise. This vascular/hemodynamic effect is well established in humans [KELLY2013; BONDONNO2014]. The translation of this mechanism into measured cognitive performance is far weaker and inconsistent. In healthy young adults, a single parallel-group dose (~5.5 mmol nitrate, 450 mL juice) modulated prefrontal cerebral blood flow (CBF) and improved one of several cognitive tasks (serial subtraction) [WIGHTMAN2015], but a separate crossover acute study in young healthy adults combining beetroot with ginseng/sage and phenolic extracts found robust mood/CBF effects attributable mainly to the phenolic (apple, coffee berry) components, not nitrate per se, and was industry (PepsiCo) funded with a co-author employee [JACKSON2020]. In healthy older adults (60-70y), 3 days of concentrated beetroot juice (~9.6 mmol/day) lowered blood pressure and sped VO2 kinetics but did not alter cognitive function or brain metabolites [KELLY2013]. A longer, dose-ranging 13-week pilot RCT in overweight/obese older adults (60-75y) found no effect of nitrate (low/medium/high dose vs placebo) on cognition or CBF at any dose [BABATEEN2022]. In type 2 diabetes, 2 weeks of nitrate-rich juice significantly sped simple reaction time (13.9 ms faster, 95% CI 3.8-24.0, p=0.009) but no other cognitive measure changed [GILCHRIST2014] - a single small, non-industry-funded trial in a diseased population, not generalizable to healthy adults. During prolonged intermittent sprint exercise, 7 days of nitrate attenuated reaction-time slowing in the second half of exercise without improving accuracy [THOMPSON2015], while a separate incremental cycling-exercise study found nitrate improved physical tolerance but did not prevent exercise-induced mental fatigue or improve cognitive performance [THOMPSON2014]. Across these studies the pattern is: consistent vascular/hemodynamic signal, inconsistent and mostly null cognitive signal, with the few positive findings being single outcomes among several tested (multiplicity risk), small samples (n=12-40), and short durations. Narrative reviews from the most active research group in this area (Newcastle/Northumbria/Exeter) explicitly conclude that recent (2021-2022) nitrate-cognition studies are \"typically null\" and that evidence is \"insufficient to recommend increased dietary nitrate intake for the purpose of improving brain health\" [SHANNON2022; SIERVO2022]. A 2025 perspective review on Alzheimer's disease notes preclinical plausibility (antioxidant, anti-inflammatory, cholinergic-pathway effects of beetroot's combined nitrate-betalain-polyphenol profile) but states human AD-specific trial data are essentially absent and existing cognitive trial data are inconsistent [KIMBLE2025]. A 2025 systematic review of beetroot and healthy aging similarly concludes evidence for cognitive benefit is \"limited and inconsistent\" even though ergogenic/physical-performance evidence is stronger [NOWAK2025]. No systematic review or meta-analysis pools a cognition-specific effect size for dietary nitrate with a usable summary CI; the field remains at the level of individual small trials. Safety: beetroot juice itself is a food with a long history of consumption; its principal flagged risks are not nitrate toxicity in typical dietary amounts but (a) oxalate load relevant to kidney-stone formers, (b) harmless beeturia/red urine or stool, (c) blood-pressure-lowering that can interact with antihypertensives and PDE5 inhibitors (risk of hypotension), and (d) a broader public-health debate about nitrate/nitrite intake and endogenous N-nitroso compound formation and cancer risk, which a large French cohort found associated specifically with food-additive nitrate/nitrite (processed meat), not natural vegetable sources [CHAZELAS2022; ZAMANI2021]. Overall: Brain Food should not market beetroot/nitrate as a cognitive enhancer for healthy young adults or students; the honest claim is vascular support (blood pressure, exercise oxygen economy), with cognitive benefit remaining unproven and plausible mainly in reaction-time-under-fatigue or diseased/compromised-perfusion subgroups that require further dedicated trials.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "There is no recognized 'nitrate deficiency' state in adequately nourished people; dietary nitrate intake varies widely with vegetable consumption and is not a classified essential nutrient. The relevant question is therefore purely one of enhancement/supplementation above habitual dietary levels, not correction of a deficit. Trials in deficient or compromised populations (T2DM in GILCHRIST2014, overweight/obese older adults in BABATEEN2022) cannot be generalized to replete healthy adults, and the healthy-adult and healthy-older-adult trials included here (WIGHTMAN2015, KELLY2013) show the enhancement signal on cognition is weak to absent even though vascular/BP effects are reproducible.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "The supplement/sports-nutrition market markets beetroot/nitrate heavily as a 'brain and body' performance booster, extrapolating from robust exercise-performance and blood-pressure data to an unproven cognitive-enhancement claim. The evidence actually supports a reliable vascular effect (nitrite rise, modest BP reduction, improved exercise O2 economy) and, at most, a possible reaction-time benefit during physical/mental fatigue or in vascularly compromised groups (T2DM, team-sport athletes under fatigue) - not a general memory, attention or 'brain power' benefit in healthy resting people. Marketing copy that implies 'boosts brainpower' or 'improves memory' for beetroot juice in healthy consumers goes beyond what the published RCTs and the field's own narrative reviews (which call recent findings 'typically null') support.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Dietary inorganic nitrate undergoes enterosalivary circulation: salivary glands concentrate nitrate from plasma, oral commensal bacteria reduce it to nitrite (the step blocked by antibacterial mouthwash), swallowed nitrite is further reduced non-enzymatically and enzymatically to nitric oxide (NO) and other reactive nitrogen species, especially under hypoxic/acidic conditions. NO is a vasodilator and signaling molecule; the proposed brain pathway is NO-mediated cerebral vasodilation and improved regional cerebral blood flow (CBF), which is hypothesized (not established) to support cognitive performance, particularly under conditions of high metabolic demand (exercise, fatigue) or compromised perfusion (aging, T2DM, obesity).", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Ingestion of nitrate-rich food/juice", "detail": "Beetroot, spinach and other nitrate-rich vegetables deliver inorganic nitrate (NO3-), typically 5-13 mmol per studied dose", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Enterosalivary circulation and oral bacterial reduction to nitrite", "detail": "Salivary glands actively concentrate plasma nitrate; oral commensal bacteria reduce NO3- to NO2-; effect is abolished by antibacterial mouthwash in controlled studies", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Systemic reduction of nitrite to nitric oxide and reactive nitrogen species", "detail": "Swallowed nitrite is reduced to NO non-enzymatically (gastric acid) and enzymatically (xanthine oxidase, deoxygenated hemoglobin/myoglobin), favored under hypoxia/acidosis", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Vascular and hemodynamic effects", "detail": "NO-mediated vasodilation lowers blood pressure and reduces O2 cost of submaximal exercise; plasma nitrite rise and BP reduction are consistently reproduced", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Cerebral blood flow modulation", "detail": "Near-infrared spectroscopy studies show nitrate alters frontal cortex hemodynamic responses to cognitive task performance", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Proposed downstream cognitive benefit", "detail": "Hypothesized improvement in attention, reaction time or executive function via improved cerebral perfusion and mitochondrial efficiency", "evidence_level": "plausible"}, {"step": "Animal/preclinical neuroprotective pathways (antioxidant, anti-inflammatory, cholinergic)", "detail": "Preclinical work on beetroot's combined nitrate-betalain-polyphenol profile suggests antioxidant defence, mitochondrial bioenergetics and cholinergic pathway modulation relevant to Alzheimer's pathology", "evidence_level": "animal"}], "pathways": ["nitrate-nitrite-nitric oxide pathway", "oral microbiome-dependent nitrate reduction", "NO-mediated vasodilation / cerebral blood flow", "mitochondrial efficiency (reduced O2 cost)", "antioxidant/anti-inflammatory (betalains, polyphenols, preclinical)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "40 healthy adults, parallel-group acute dose study", "dose_or_intake": "~5.5 mmol nitrate (450 mL beetroot juice)", "duration": "single dose, 90-min absorption + 54-min cognitive battery", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Improved only one of several frontal-cortex cognitive tasks (serial 3s subtraction); other tasks unaffected; CBF hemodynamic pattern altered", "effect_estimate": "Significant improvement reported on serial-subtraction task only; exact effect size/CI not reported in abstract (not reported)", "certainty_notes": "Single small trial, multiple cognitive outcomes tested with one positive (multiplicity/selective-outcome risk), parallel design limits power; downgraded for imprecision and risk of selective reporting", "key_study_ids": ["WIGHTMAN2015"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "cerebral_blood_flow", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "32 healthy adults 18-49y, crossover, beetroot+ginseng+sage combined with phenolic extracts", "dose_or_intake": "beetroot extract combined with other botanicals/phenolics (nitrate dose not isolated)", "duration": "acute, measured to 360 min post-dose", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "CBF/mood effects were attributed mainly to phenolic (apple, coffee berry) components rather than the nitrate-containing beetroot/ginseng/sage blend; nitrate's isolated contribution not determined", "effect_estimate": "Not reported for nitrate alone (confounded multi-ingredient design)", "certainty_notes": "Cannot isolate nitrate effect; industry (PepsiCo)-funded with employee co-author; downgraded for indirectness", "key_study_ids": ["JACKSON2020"]}, {"timeframe": "short_term_days", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "12 healthy older adults aged 60-70y, double-blind crossover", "dose_or_intake": "~9.6 mmol/day nitrate (2x70 mL concentrated beetroot juice/day)", "duration": "3 days", "grade": "E", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Blood pressure fell and VO2 kinetics improved, but cognitive function, functional capacity (6-min walk) and brain metabolite concentrations were not altered", "effect_estimate": "No significant cognitive change reported (not reported numerically)", "certainty_notes": "Small n=12 crossover; clear null for cognition despite positive vascular effect in the same trial; well-conducted but underpowered for cognitive outcomes", "key_study_ids": ["KELLY2013"]}, {"timeframe": "short_term_days", "domain": "reaction_time", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "27 patients with type 2 diabetes mellitus, mean age 67.2y, double-blind crossover", "dose_or_intake": "7.5 mmol nitrate per 250 mL juice (dose/day not fully specified in abstract)", "duration": "2 weeks per arm", "grade": "B", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Simple reaction time significantly faster with nitrate (327±40 ms) vs placebo (341.8±52.7 ms); mean difference 13.9±25.6 ms; no other of 5 cognitive measures differed", "effect_estimate": "Mean difference 13.9 ms (95% CI 3.8-24.0), p=0.009, single outcome of several tested", "certainty_notes": "Single trial, T2DM population only, not generalizable to healthy adults or students; one positive outcome among 5 tested (multiplicity risk); non-industry funded, well-designed placebo", "key_study_ids": ["GILCHRIST2014"]}, {"timeframe": "short_term_days", "domain": "reaction_time", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "16 male team-sport players, double-blind crossover, intermittent sprint exercise protocol", "dose_or_intake": "12.8 mmol/day nitrate (140 mL/day beetroot juice)", "duration": "7 days", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Reaction time during cognitive tasks performed simultaneously with exercise was better maintained in the second half of a fatiguing intermittent sprint test with nitrate vs placebo; no difference in accuracy", "effect_estimate": "Second-half reaction time: BR 817±86 ms vs PL 847±118 ms (p<0.05); first-half not different", "certainty_notes": "Small n=16, male-only, athletic population performing exercise-embedded cognition, not resting cognitive testing; context-specific (fatigue-attenuation), not a general cognitive enhancement", "key_study_ids": ["THOMPSON2015"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "cognitive_fatigue", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "16 subjects, double-blind crossover, incremental cycling exercise to exhaustion", "dose_or_intake": "beetroot juice (dose not fully specified in abstract) vs nitrate-depleted placebo, acute/short-term", "duration": "single exercise session (nitrate loading protocol not detailed in abstract)", "grade": "E", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Nitrate improved exercise tolerance at high intensity but did not prevent exercise-induced mental fatigue or improve cognitive performance post-fatigue", "effect_estimate": "Cognitive performance and perceived energy levels similar between conditions; mental fatigue onset at 70% VO2peak in both arms", "certainty_notes": "Clear null for cognition in a well-controlled crossover; consistent with other null findings in exercise-cognition designs", "key_study_ids": ["THOMPSON2014"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "62 overweight/obese older adults aged 60-75y, single-blind, parallel, 4-arm dose-ranging pilot", "dose_or_intake": "high (2x70 mL/day), medium (70 mL/day), low (70 mL on alternate days) vs placebo", "duration": "13 weeks", "grade": "E", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "No dose of nitrate-rich beetroot juice affected cognitive function (COMPASS battery) or cerebral blood flow (NIRS) versus placebo over 13 weeks", "effect_estimate": "No significant between-group differences reported at any dose (not reported numerically in abstract)", "certainty_notes": "Largest and longest trial in this population to date but still a pilot (not powered for definitive conclusions); authors call for confirmation with MRI in larger samples; clear null result, well-conducted, no industry funding declared", "key_study_ids": ["BABATEEN2022"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "students and knowledge workers specifically", "population_detail_note": "no dedicated chronic RCT in students/knowledge workers identified", "dose_or_intake": "not studied", "duration": "not studied", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No chronic RCT of dietary nitrate on cognition in healthy students or knowledge workers was identified in this search; evidence base is acute/short-term and skews toward older/clinical/athletic populations", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "Absence of direct evidence in the target population; grade reflects lack of applicable human data rather than a negative finding", "key_study_ids": []}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "~5.5 mmol nitrate (450 mL beetroot juice)", "onset": "~90 min post-dose", "duration_studied": "54 min cognitive battery", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "effect_estimate": "1 of several tasks improved (serial subtraction); others null", "certainty": "low", "safety": "no adverse events reported", "study_ids": ["WIGHTMAN2015"]}, {"dose": "beetroot+ginseng+sage blend (nitrate dose not isolated)", "onset": "measured to 360 min", "duration_studied": "360 min", "domain": "cerebral_blood_flow", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "effect_estimate": "CBF/mood effect attributed to co-ingredients, not isolable nitrate effect", "certainty": "very_low", "safety": "no adverse events reported", "study_ids": ["JACKSON2020"]}, {"dose": "beetroot juice (acute, pre-exercise)", "onset": "during incremental cycling", "duration_studied": "single exercise session to exhaustion", "domain": "cognitive_fatigue", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "effect_estimate": "no cognitive benefit; exercise tolerance improved only", "certainty": "moderate", "safety": "no adverse events reported", "study_ids": ["THOMPSON2014"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "3 days", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "outcome": "global cognition, functional capacity", "effect_estimate": "no change despite BP reduction", "evidence_quality": "low (n=12)", "limitations": "small crossover, short duration, underpowered for cognition", "study_ids": ["KELLY2013"]}, {"duration": "2 weeks", "population": "clinical_population (T2DM)", "outcome": "reaction time, broader cognitive battery", "effect_estimate": "reaction time improved 13.9 ms (95% CI 3.8-24.0); other 4 measures null", "evidence_quality": "moderate (n=27, single trial)", "limitations": "disease population, not generalizable, single positive outcome among several tested", "study_ids": ["GILCHRIST2014"]}, {"duration": "7 days", "population": "healthy_young_adults (male athletes)", "outcome": "reaction time under exercise fatigue", "effect_estimate": "second-half reaction time improved (817 vs 847 ms, p<0.05); accuracy unchanged", "evidence_quality": "low (n=16)", "limitations": "male-only, exercise-embedded cognition, not resting cognitive testing", "study_ids": ["THOMPSON2015"]}, {"duration": "13 weeks", "population": "older_adults_healthy (overweight/obese)", "outcome": "global cognition, cerebral blood flow", "effect_estimate": "no significant effect at any of 3 doses vs placebo", "evidence_quality": "moderate (n=62, pilot, dose-ranging)", "limitations": "pilot, not powered for definitive conclusions, single-blind not double-blind", "study_ids": ["BABATEEN2022"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Beetroot and beetroot juice have a long history of use as food and are generally well tolerated at typical dietary and studied supplemental doses (5-13 mmol nitrate/day, roughly 70-300 mL concentrate or 200-300 g beetroot). The main safety-relevant mechanism is the same one responsible for its claimed benefits: NO-mediated vasodilation and blood-pressure lowering, which can be additive with antihypertensive medication and is contraindicated with PDE5 inhibitors (sildenafil and related drugs) due to risk of severe hypotension. Harmless but alarming cosmetic effects (beeturia - red/pink urine, and red stool) occur in a genetically susceptible minority. Oxalate content is relevant to recurrent calcium-oxalate kidney-stone formers. A broader public-health debate concerns nitrate/nitrite intake, endogenous N-nitroso compound (nitrosamine) formation, and cancer risk; the best available large cohort evidence (CHAZELAS2022) links this risk specifically to food-additive nitrate/nitrite (processed meat preservatives), with no association found for natural vegetable-derived nitrate, but the field acknowledges (ZAMANI2021) that risk-side research on vegetable/juice sources specifically is comparatively sparse and total intake above EFSA's acceptable daily intake (ADI, 3.7 mg/kg body weight/day for nitrate) from concentrated juice plus other dietary sources is plausible with regular high-dose beetroot juice use.", "adverse_effects": ["beeturia (red/pink urine) and red stool - harmless, occurs in a genetically susceptible subset", "mild GI upset occasionally reported with large juice volumes", "symptomatic hypotension/dizziness, especially combined with antihypertensive medication", "rare case reports of nitrate-related anaphylaxis (very rare)"], "contraindications": ["concurrent use of PDE5 inhibitors (sildenafil, tadalafil) due to severe hypotension risk", "history of recurrent calcium-oxalate kidney stones (oxalate content)", "known nitrate/sulfite hypersensitivity"], "drug_interactions": ["antihypertensive medications (additive blood-pressure-lowering effect)", "PDE5 inhibitors (severe hypotension risk)", "theoretical interaction with organic nitrate medications (e.g., nitroglycerin) via overlapping NO pathway"], "upper_limits": "No nitrate-specific UL for food sources is set by EFSA for healthy adults; EFSA's acceptable daily intake (ADI) for nitrate is 3.7 mg/kg body weight/day, which can be approached or exceeded by regular high-dose concentrated beetroot juice (70-140 mL/day) combined with other dietary nitrate sources; infants and young children are a specifically protected group due to methemoglobinemia risk from nitrate/nitrite, which is why nitrate limits exist for infant foods and water.", "sleep_impact": "Not specifically studied; no evidence of stimulant-like sleep disruption given the mechanism is vasodilatory rather than CNS-stimulating.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Longest controlled trial identified here is 13 weeks (BABATEEN2022); no RCT evidence beyond approximately 3 months for either cognitive outcomes or long-term safety; longer-term safety data rely on epidemiological nitrate/nitrite intake studies (e.g., CHAZELAS2022) rather than dedicated beetroot-juice supplementation trials.", "daily_use_appropriate": "probably", "special_populations": "Pregnancy: no dedicated safety trials identified in this search; beetroot as a food is standard in pregnancy diets, but concentrated high-dose juice supplementation has not been specifically safety-tested in pregnancy and should default to food-form moderation. Children: beetroot as food is fine; nitrate exposure limits for infants/young children are stricter due to methemoglobinemia risk - concentrated juice supplementation is not advised. Chronic kidney disease/kidney stone formers: oxalate load is a specific concern requiring dietitian input. Cardiovascular disease on antihypertensive or PDE5-inhibitor therapy: medical supervision needed due to hypotension risk.", "specialist_review_needed": ["cardiology/clinical pharmacology for antihypertensive and PDE5-inhibitor interaction specifics", "nephrology/urology for oxalate and kidney-stone risk in susceptible individuals", "toxicology/food-safety for cumulative nitrate ADI exposure modeling across a realistic daily diet including other vegetables and water"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "Beetroot/beetroot juice as a food is safe for the general healthy population at ordinary dietary and studied intakes, but a product positioned for regular/concentrated nitrate dosing requires explicit label warnings for antihypertensive and PDE5-inhibitor users, oxalate-sensitive individuals, and should avoid encouraging intake levels that approach the EFSA ADI when combined with the rest of a normal vegetable-containing diet."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Beetroot and beetroot juice are conventional foods under EU law, not a novel food. No EFSA-authorised health claim exists for 'dietary nitrate' or 'beetroot juice' improving cognitive function, memory or brain health (checked against the EU Register of nutrition and health claims); an EFSA opinion on exercise/oxygen-uptake-related nitrate claims from beetroot has been considered but EFSA's assessments of nitrate-related claims have generally not resulted in authorisation for cognitive outcomes. EFSA has also set a nitrate ADI of 3.7 mg/kg bw/day as part of its food-additive/contaminant risk assessments, relevant to overall exposure management rather than claims. Status of any specific cognitive claim should be treated as unauthorised/uncertain pending direct verification of the current EU claims register entry.", "us": "Beetroot and beetroot juice are conventional foods (GRAS by common food use) and fall outside DSHEA's dietary-supplement NDI framework when sold as food/juice; if marketed as a dietary supplement (e.g., beetroot extract capsules/powders), DSHEA structure/function claim rules apply and any cognitive claim would need adequate substantiation under FTC standards, which the current evidence (mostly null in healthy adults) would not clearly support. No FDA-authorized health claim for nitrate/beetroot and cognition exists.", "morocco": "No Brain-Food-specific regulatory determination found in this search; beetroot/beetroot juice as a conventional food would fall under ONSSA's general food-safety framework (Morocco has no identified nitrate-specific food-claims regime analogous to EFSA's). Any supplement-form product would require ONSSA/Ministry of Health registration as a food supplement; specific claim rules should be confirmed directly with ONSSA before marketing - marked uncertain here rather than assumed.", "permitted_claims_examples": [], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "Beetroot and beetroot juice are not novel foods in the EU (long history of consumption before 1997) and are conventional foods with GRAS status in the US; no NDI notification is required for beetroot juice itself. A concentrated/isolated inorganic nitrate ingredient added at supraphysiological doses could, in principle, raise novel-food or NDI questions depending on concentration and form, but this was not specifically identified in this search and should be verified against current EU Novel Food Catalogue and FDA NDI databases before formulation decisions.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "High - beetroot is itself a whole food and beetroot juice/concentrate is a widely available conventional beverage; it fits naturally into a 'brain food' product line as a food-first vascular-support item rather than requiring supplement-form isolation.", "formats": ["fresh or juiced beetroot", "commercial beetroot juice (unconcentrated)", "concentrated beetroot juice shots (e.g., 70 mL 'shot' format common in sports nutrition)", "beetroot powder/crystals for reconstitution", "beetroot capsules/extract (less food-aligned, more supplement-like)"], "stability": "Nitrate content is generally stable in refrigerated or shelf-stable pasteurized juice but can degrade or convert to nitrite during prolonged improper storage or microbial contamination; commercial concentrate products standardize nitrate content via supplier specification.", "taste": "Distinctive earthy/sweet beetroot flavor is a known barrier to habitual consumption for some consumers; commercial products often blend with apple or other fruit juices to improve palatability, which can dilute nitrate concentration per serving if not accounted for.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Nitrate bioavailability from beetroot juice is high and well characterized (near-complete absorption, enterosalivary recirculation); bioavailability is reduced by antibacterial mouthwash use or antiseptic mouth rinses, which block the oral bacterial reduction step - a notable real-world confound for any product claim.", "dose_feasibility": "The studied effective dose range (5-13 mmol nitrate/day, roughly equivalent to 70-300 mL commercial beetroot juice concentrate or about 200-300 g whole beetroot) is readily achievable in a normal food serving or a single juice shot, making this one of the more formulation-realistic ingredients in this category, even though the cognitive benefit itself remains unproven.", "cost_availability": "Beetroot and beetroot juice are inexpensive and widely available in Morocco, the EU and the US as conventional grocery items; concentrated 'nitrate shot' commercial products are a well-established, moderately priced category in sports nutrition (not researched here for Morocco-specific retail pricing).", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 3, "effect_magnitude": 2, "safety": 7, "reproducibility": 3, "food_suitability": 9, "cost_access": 8, "rationale": "evidence_quality=3: repeated small RCTs in healthy young and older adults are mostly null for cognition (KELLY2013, BABATEEN2022, THOMPSON2014), with only a single small positive finding in a diseased population (GILCHRIST2014) and narrative reviews from the field's leading group explicitly calling recent findings 'typically null' - this is C-grade territory at best for any cognitive outcome, dragging the overall score down despite a robust vascular/mechanistic literature. effect_magnitude=2: where any cognitive signal exists it is a single secondary outcome (reaction time) with a modest absolute change (~14-30 ms), not a general cognition improvement. safety=7: well-tolerated as food with known, manageable interaction/contraindication profile (antihypertensives, PDE5 inhibitors, oxalate, ADI considerations); not higher because of the unresolved concentrated-dose ADI/nitrosamine exposure question and lack of long-term (>13 week) safety trials. reproducibility=3: the acute CBF-modulation and BP/nitrite findings replicate well, but the cognitive outcome findings do not replicate across independent labs/populations - most attempts to show cognition benefit failed to replicate the few positive findings. food_suitability=9: beetroot is a genuine whole food, inexpensive, familiar, and the effective dose fits an ordinary juice serving. cost_access=8: cheap and widely available across all three target markets as a conventional grocery item."}, "research_gaps": ["No adequately powered, long-duration (>3 months) RCT of dietary nitrate on cognition specifically in healthy young adults or students exists", "No pooled meta-analysis with a quantitative summary effect size and CI for nitrate and any single cognitive domain was found", "The oral-microbiome-dependent mechanism (mouthwash abolition) is well established for the nitrate-nitrite pathway but its downstream link to cognitive outcomes specifically has not been tested in an interventional design manipulating oral microbiome status directly alongside cognitive testing", "No dedicated trial in sleep-deprived or high-cognitive-workload healthy adults (the population most relevant to a 'knowledge worker' brand positioning) was identified", "Dose-response relationship for any cognitive outcome (as opposed to BP/VO2 outcomes, which are dose-responsive) is unestablished", "Long-term safety data specific to regular concentrated beetroot-juice supplementation (beyond 13 weeks) are lacking", "Registered trials without published results (e.g., several ClinicalTrials.gov entries on nitrate and cerebrovascular/cognitive aging) should be tracked for publication to rule out publication bias"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 4.7, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": "B", "best_grade_young_adults": "C", "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": null, "best_grade_overall": "B", "benefit_domains": {"reaction_time": "B"}, "n_outcome_rows": 8, "n_studies": 14, "n_verified": 14, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 6, "n_systematic_reviews": 5, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["inconsistent", "insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "grape-polyphenols", "name": "Grape polyphenols and grape juice", "category": "G", "category_name": "Polyphenols and plant bioactives", "aliases": ["Concord grape juice", "purple grape juice", "grape seed extract", "grape seed polyphenol extract (GSPE)", "grape and blueberry extract (Memophenol)", "anthocyanins and flavan-3-ols from Vitis vinifera/labrusca"], "food_sources": ["Concord (Vitis labrusca) and other dark grape juices", "whole dark/purple grapes and raisins", "grape seed extract supplements", "proprietary grape+blueberry polyphenol extracts (e.g. Memophenol)", "red wine (alcohol confounds brain effects; not covered here)"], "active_constituents": ["anthocyanins (malvidin, delphinidin glycosides)", "flavan-3-ols / proanthocyanidins (catechin, epicatechin oligomers, concentrated in grape seed extract)", "flavonols (quercetin glycosides)", "hydroxycinnamic acids", "resveratrol (minor contributor in grape products; handled in depth in unit G6)"], "one_line_verdict": "Concord grape juice has repeatedly, but only in small industry-linked trials, improved memory in older adults with mild cognitive impairment over 12-16 weeks, while the one acute trial in healthy young adults found an attention/mood effect but no memory effect, and a 12-week grape seed extract trial in healthy young adults was essentially null -- so grape polyphenols look like a plausible but still grade-C, population-dependent and under-replicated candidate, not a demonstrated cognitive enhancer for healthy adults.", "scientific_conclusion": "Older adults with memory decline/MCI. Three independent-group Krikorian-lineage trials in older adults with subjective memory decline or MCI found benefits of Concord grape juice: a 12-week pilot (n=12) improved verbal learning (p<0.05) with non-significant trends for verbal/spatial recall [krikorian2010]; a 16-week follow-up (n not separately reported beyond the pilot cohort) reduced semantic interference on memory tasks and showed greater right-hemisphere fMRI activation [krikorian2012]. These are small (n=12-20), single-group, Welch Foods Inc.-linked (author affiliation at Welch Foods appears on the related Lamport trial) studies without independent replication by unrelated labs, so despite consistent direction the evidence is grade C in this population, not B, because of small samples, single research group, and funding ties. Middle-aged adults under cognitive/stress load. A 12-week randomized crossover in 25 healthy working mothers (355 mL/day Concord grape juice, 777 mg total polyphenols, registered NCT01411631, Welch Foods co-author) found improved immediate spatial memory and driving-simulator performance versus placebo, with a carry-over effect into the washout arm [lamport2016]. This extends the chronic finding beyond MCI to a non-deficient midlife population under everyday stress, but remains one trial from one collaborating group. Healthy young adults, acute. In the only acute trial in healthy young adults (n=20, crossover, 230 mL purple grape juice), reaction time on a composite attention measure improved (p=0.047) and calm ratings rose (p=0.046) after a single dose, but no significant effect appeared on any memory measure; the authors explicitly note memory in young healthy adults may be less responsive to acute polyphenol intake than in older or impaired groups [haskellramsay2017]. This is the only evidence for students/young adults and shows an attention/mood signal, not a memory or executive-function signal, after a single dose. Grape seed extract, chronic, healthy young adults. A well-controlled acute-on-chronic RCT (n=60, 18-30y, 400 mg grape seed polyphenol extract, 12 weeks) found no consistent pattern of cognitive benefit versus placebo either acutely or chronically across a full computerized battery; isolated improvements in reaction time (acute) and psychomotor skill (chronic) were offset by placebo also improving on some measures [bell2022]. This is the most methodologically rigorous trial in exactly our target population (healthy young adults/students) and it is essentially a null result, directly tempering extrapolation from the grape-juice-in-older-adults literature. Grape+blueberry extract (Memophenol), older adults with MCI. A 6-month RCT (n=143, 60-80y with MCI, 150 mg twice daily, industry-funded by Activ'Inside which supplied the extract and employed three co-authors) found improved processing speed (p=0.020), visuospatial learning (p=0.012) and a global executive-function questionnaire (p=0.046), but no significant difference on most other cognitive tests or self-report measures [lopresti2023]. A companion acute young-adult trial referenced in the brief (Philip 2019) could not be verified in PubMed/Europe PMC searches under that description and is dropped per the no-invented-citation rule; this is noted as a research gap. A 2021 critical review of epidemiological and RCT literature on grapes/wine and cognition concluded that grape juice (200-500 mL/day) and light-to-moderate wine were generally associated with improved cognitive performance, cautioned that results for other alcoholic beverages were inconsistent, and flagged resveratrol's low bioavailability as a limitation even though resveratrol likely explains only part of any effect [restani2021]. Null/registered evidence and funding. A phase 2 trial of a different grape seed extract (Meganatural-Az) as an anti-amyloid-oligomerization agent in Alzheimer's disease (NCT02033941, n target not reported, TERMINATED after enrolling only ~10) never reported results, consistent with publication-bias risk in this literature. Across nearly every positive trial reviewed here, some author or funding tie exists to Welch Foods, Activ'Inside, or wine/grape industry bodies (Restani review authors include wine-industry affiliates), which does not invalidate findings but caps single small industry trials at grade B at most per GRADE convention, and most of these trials are additionally too small or too narrow to reach B. Overall: direction of effect is consistently positive in older/impaired or midlife-stressed populations over chronic dosing, essentially null in healthy young adults on a rigorous 12-week trial, and limited to attention/mood (not memory) acutely in young adults.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Grape polyphenols do not correct any recognized nutrient deficiency; there is no dietary requirement for anthocyanins or proanthocyanidins. All positive trials are framed as protecting or restoring function in populations already showing decline (MCI, age-related memory complaints) or under chronic stress load (working mothers), i.e. compensating for an existing deficit in performance, not enhancing an already-optimal baseline. The single rigorous enhancement test in healthy, non-deficient young adults (grape seed extract, 12 weeks) was null [bell2022], and the only acute young-adult trial showed an attention/mood effect, not memory enhancement [haskellramsay2017]. For Brain Food's stated healthy-young-adult/student target population, the honest framing is 'unproven for enhancement, promising for protection against age- or stress-related decline', and any claim of memory enhancement in students would outrun the data.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Marketing of grape/Concord juice or grape seed extract as a 'memory booster' for students or knowledge workers is not supported: the only chronic trial in a comparable non-impaired population (healthy young adults, grape seed extract) found no consistent cognitive benefit [bell2022], and the positive juice trials were conducted in older adults with memory decline or midlife adults under stress, not healthy young adults. Claims implying disease prevention ('prevents dementia', 'protects against Alzheimer's') are not supported by controlled human trial evidence and would be prohibited or require EFSA/FDA substantiation that does not currently exist for grape polyphenols specifically (resveratrol-specific claims are handled separately in unit G6). No EU Article 13 health claim or US FDA-authorised claim currently covers grape/grape-seed polyphenols and cognition; any 'antioxidant' or 'supports healthy ageing' wording used by supplement brands is structure/function marketing language, not a regulator-reviewed efficacy claim. The repeated industry funding (Welch Foods, Activ'Inside) behind the positive trials is a legitimate reason for caution, not dismissal, and should be disclosed in any Brain Food communication citing this literature.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Grape anthocyanins and proanthocyanidins are absorbed in small, variably bioavailable amounts, act mainly as modulators of vascular/endothelial function and as mild anti-inflammatory and antioxidant agents, with animal data additionally suggesting effects on hippocampal signalling and neurogenesis; human cognitive benefit, where seen, is best explained by improved cerebral perfusion and reduced oxidative/inflammatory burden in populations with baseline vascular or age-related impairment, rather than a direct neurotransmitter or receptor action established in humans.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Ingestion of grape juice/extract -> gut absorption of anthocyanins and flavan-3-ol metabolites (glucuronides, methylated and microbial-derived phenolic acids)", "detail": "Bioavailability of intact anthocyanins is low (<1-2%); most circulating bioactivity comes from microbial and hepatic metabolites, which varies between individuals and was not measured in most of the cited trials.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Circulating phenolic metabolites -> endothelial nitric-oxide pathway and reduced systemic oxidative stress/inflammation", "detail": "Supported by grape-juice cardiovascular/endothelial-function literature outside cognition; not directly measured alongside cognition in the cited trials.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Improved vascular function -> cerebral blood flow and perfusion in regions supporting memory", "detail": "fMRI showed greater right-hemisphere activation with grape juice in MCI [krikorian2012], consistent with a perfusion/activation mechanism but not a direct causal chain to behavioural outcome.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Polyphenol exposure -> modulation of hippocampal BDNF/synaptic signalling and reduced neuroinflammation", "detail": "Established in rodent models of aging and grape/berry polyphenol feeding; human biomarker confirmation (e.g. serum BDNF) is largely absent from the cited trials.", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Possible monoamine oxidase inhibition and acute glucoregulatory effects -> transient changes in alertness/attention", "detail": "Proposed by Haskell-Ramsay et al. to explain the acute attention/mood effect in young adults; mechanism not directly tested in that trial.", "evidence_level": "speculative"}], "pathways": ["vascular/endothelial function and cerebral perfusion", "antioxidant/anti-inflammatory modulation", "putative hippocampal neurotrophic signalling (animal)", "acute glucoregulation/monoamine modulation (speculative)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "older_adults_mci", "population_detail": "older adults with subjective memory decline or diagnosed MCI, not dementia", "dose_or_intake": "Concord grape juice, approx. 2 cups/day equivalent to published Welch's trials", "duration": "12-16 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Improved verbal learning/semantic interference and increased task-related brain activation across two small, related trials from one research group.", "effect_estimate": "krikorian2010: significant improvement in verbal learning measure (exact statistic not reported beyond p<0.05 in abstract); krikorian2012: reduced semantic interference, qualitative fMRI activation difference, no quantitative effect size reported", "certainty_notes": "Downgraded for very small samples (n=12-20), lack of independent replication outside the Krikorian/Welch collaboration, and industry (Welch Foods) ties; single small trials cannot exceed grade B and these have further limitations.", "key_study_ids": ["krikorian2010", "krikorian2012"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "healthy working mothers aged 40-50y with high cognitive/driving demands, not deficient", "dose_or_intake": "355 mL/day Concord grape juice, 777 mg total polyphenols", "duration": "12 weeks (crossover with 4-week washout)", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Improved immediate spatial memory and simulated driving performance versus placebo in a randomized crossover trial; effect persisted into placebo arm (order effect).", "effect_estimate": "Significant group difference reported for spatial memory and driving metrics; exact effect sizes/CIs not given in abstract", "certainty_notes": "Single trial, n=25 (driving subsample n=17), registered (NCT01411631) but funded with Welch Foods co-authorship; carry-over effect complicates crossover interpretation.", "key_study_ids": ["lamport2016"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "healthy adults, mean age not specified beyond 'young adults', n=20", "dose_or_intake": "230 mL purple grape juice, single dose", "duration": "single dose, measured over ~20-120 min post-ingestion", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Improved reaction time on a composite attention measure and increased calm mood ratings after a single dose; no significant effect on memory measures.", "effect_estimate": "Reaction time p=0.047; calm ratings p=0.046; memory measures not significant (exact effect sizes not reported in abstract)", "certainty_notes": "Very small sample (n=20), single trial, multiple comparisons across a battery raise risk of a false positive on the attention measure; authors themselves note memory appears less responsive acutely in young adults.", "key_study_ids": ["haskellramsay2017"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "healthy adults aged 18-30y, non-deficient, n=60", "dose_or_intake": "400 mg grape seed polyphenol extract (GSPE)/day", "duration": "12 weeks, with acute assessments at 2/4/6h on day 1", "grade": "D", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "No consistent pattern of cognitive benefit versus placebo acutely or chronically across a full computerized test battery; isolated reaction-time/psychomotor findings were matched by placebo improvements on other measures.", "effect_estimate": "No significant overall group effect reported; isolated nominal findings not robust to the overall pattern", "certainty_notes": "This is the most rigorous trial in the exact target population (healthy young adults) and is essentially null; downgraded to D (effectively no convincing benefit) despite being a well-conducted RCT, because the null is clear and consistent with the authors' own conclusion that <400 mg doses may be insufficient in this population.", "key_study_ids": ["bell2022"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "processing_speed", "population": "older_adults_mci", "population_detail": "adults aged 60-80y with diagnosed MCI, n=143", "dose_or_intake": "150 mg grape+blueberry polyphenol extract (Memophenol) twice daily (300 mg/day)", "duration": "6 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Improved processing speed, visuospatial learning, and an executive-function questionnaire versus placebo; most other cognitive and self-report outcomes not significantly different.", "effect_estimate": "Processing speed p=0.020; visuospatial learning p=0.012; BRIEF-A global score p=0.046; other measures not significant (exact effect sizes/CIs not reported in abstract)", "certainty_notes": "Single trial in a patented commercial extract, sponsor (Activ'Inside) supplied product and employed 3 of 7 authors; multiple outcomes tested with only a few reaching significance raises multiplicity concern; cannot exceed grade B per single-industry-trial rule and further limited by partial outcome pattern.", "key_study_ids": ["lopresti2023"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "healthy students/young adults specifically", "dose_or_intake": "not applicable - no chronic trial identified", "duration": "not applicable", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No chronic grape-juice (as opposed to grape seed extract) trial exists in healthy young adults or students; evidence is extrapolated from older/impaired or midlife-stressed populations.", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "Evidence gap; direction of effect in the one directly relevant chronic trial (grape seed extract, healthy young adults) was null [bell2022].", "key_study_ids": ["bell2022"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "230 mL purple grape juice (single dose)", "onset": "within 20 minutes", "duration_studied": "up to ~2 hours post-dose", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "effect_estimate": "composite reaction time improved, p=0.047; memory unaffected", "certainty": "low (single small trial, n=20)", "safety": "no adverse events reported; food-level exposure", "study_ids": ["haskellramsay2017"]}, {"dose": "400 mg grape seed polyphenol extract (single dose, day 1 of 12-week trial)", "onset": "assessed at 2, 4, 6h post-dose", "duration_studied": "6 hours acute window", "domain": "reaction_time", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "effect_estimate": "isolated reaction-time improvement not forming a consistent pattern; overall acute effect null", "certainty": "moderate (well-controlled RCT, n=60) that the acute effect is null/negligible", "safety": "well tolerated, no adverse events reported", "study_ids": ["bell2022"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "12-16 weeks", "population": "older_adults_mci", "outcome": "verbal/episodic memory", "effect_estimate": "improved verbal learning and reduced semantic interference versus placebo in two small trials", "evidence_quality": "C - low, small single-group trials", "limitations": "n=12-20, same research group/funder across both trials, no independent replication", "study_ids": ["krikorian2010", "krikorian2012"]}, {"duration": "12 weeks", "population": "mixed_adults", "outcome": "spatial memory and driving performance", "effect_estimate": "improved versus placebo in working mothers; carry-over into washout", "evidence_quality": "C - single RCT with crossover carry-over confound", "limitations": "n=25 (driving subsample n=17), industry co-authorship, crossover washout contamination", "study_ids": ["lamport2016"]}, {"duration": "12 weeks", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "outcome": "global cognitive battery", "effect_estimate": "no consistent benefit versus placebo", "evidence_quality": "B-level RCT methodology reaching a null result", "limitations": "single dose level tested (400 mg); may be below effective threshold for this population", "study_ids": ["bell2022"]}, {"duration": "6 months", "population": "older_adults_mci", "outcome": "processing speed, visuospatial learning, executive function (questionnaire)", "effect_estimate": "improved on 3 of multiple measures versus placebo", "evidence_quality": "C - single sponsor-funded RCT with partial outcome pattern", "limitations": "sponsor supplied extract and employed 3/7 authors; most outcomes not significant", "study_ids": ["lopresti2023"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Grape juice and grape seed/skin polyphenol extracts at the doses studied (juice 230-355 mL/day; extracts 300-400 mg/day) were well tolerated across the cited trials, with the main caution being sugar/calorie load from juice and theoretical interactions with anticoagulant/antiplatelet medication via vitamin K and polyphenol effects on platelet function, plus caffeine-like stimulant absence (grape products are not stimulants).", "adverse_effects": ["no significant adverse events reported in any cited RCT", "small increase in fasting insulin observed in one 12-week Concord grape juice trial [krikorian2010], relevant to glycaemic control in metabolically at-risk users", "grape juice sugar/caloric load (355 mL/day Concord juice contains substantial added and intrinsic sugars) is a practical concern for daily chronic use, independent of polyphenol safety"], "contraindications": ["caution in diabetes or insulin resistance given the observed fasting-insulin signal and inherent sugar content of juice-based interventions", "caution with grape seed extract in people on anticoagulant/antiplatelet therapy given plausible antiplatelet polyphenol activity (not directly tested adverse-event data in these trials, but a known class caution)"], "drug_interactions": ["grape/fruit juices as a class have documented food-drug interaction potential (CYP and transporter-mediated) distinct from grapefruit juice, per a dedicated review identified in searches but not itself a cognition study (PMID 29703387, not included in studies[] as it is off-topic for cognition but flagged here for the safety specialist)", "possible additive effect with anticoagulants/antiplatelets (theoretical, class-level)"], "upper_limits": "No formal upper intake level (UL) is established for grape anthocyanins/proanthocyanidins; doses studied (up to 400 mg/day extract, up to 355 mL/day juice) were within ranges normally consumed as food and were well tolerated over 6 months in the longest trial [lopresti2023].", "sleep_impact": "Not studied directly in the cited trials; grape products contain no caffeine or known stimulant/sedative compounds at relevant doses, so sleep disruption is not an expected concern.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Longest controlled exposure identified is 6 months (Memophenol trial, MCI population) [lopresti2023]; no multi-year RCT safety data exist for isolated grape polyphenol extracts at supplement doses.", "daily_use_appropriate": "probably", "special_populations": "Pregnant/breastfeeding, children, and psychiatric-medication users were not studied in any cited trial; diabetic or insulin-resistant individuals should be cautious with juice-based interventions given sugar load and the signal of increased fasting insulin in one trial; people on anticoagulant/antiplatelet medication should seek pharmacist/physician advice before using concentrated grape seed extracts.", "specialist_review_needed": ["clinical pharmacologist on grape polyphenol-anticoagulant/antiplatelet interaction risk", "registered dietitian on sugar/caloric load of juice-based delivery versus whole-fruit or extract alternatives for Brain Food formulation"], "hard_safety_gate": "pass", "gate_reason": "No serious adverse events reported across any cited human RCT at food-relevant or supplement doses up to 6 months; residual caution relates to sugar content of juice vehicles and a theoretical anticoagulant interaction, not to the polyphenols' inherent toxicity."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "No EFSA Article 13 authorised health claim exists specifically for grape, grape seed, or grape+blueberry polyphenols and cognitive function; several submitted claims on grape-derived antioxidants for general health have previously received non-favourable EFSA opinions due to insufficient characterisation of the active substance or insufficient human evidence. Grape juice itself is an ordinary food, not novel.", "us": "No FDA-authorised or qualified health claim exists for grape polyphenols and cognition. Grape seed extract and proprietary extracts (e.g. Memophenol) are marketed as dietary supplements under DSHEA with structure/function claims (e.g. 'supports memory') requiring FTC-level substantiation but not FDA pre-approval; such claims must carry the standard disclaimer and cannot imply disease prevention/treatment.", "morocco": "Grape juice and grape products are regulated as ordinary foods/beverages under ONSSA food safety rules; no specific Moroccan health claim framework exists for grape polyphenols, and any supplement extract would need ONSSA food-supplement registration without authorised cognitive claims.", "permitted_claims_examples": [], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "Whole grape juice and grape seed extract used as food ingredients are not novel foods in the EU; standardized proprietary extracts (e.g. Memophenol) manufactured by extraction processes may require novel food or NDI (US) safety dossiers depending on exact processing and prior-market history, which should be confirmed with the specific supplier before formulation.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Grape juice and whole dark grapes are easily incorporated into a food/beverage brand; concentrated grape seed or grape+blueberry extracts are supplement-format ingredients (capsules/powders) rather than naturally food-integrable at the studied doses without substantial reformulation.", "formats": ["100% Concord/dark grape juice or juice blend", "whole or dried dark grapes/raisins as a snack format", "standardized grape seed polyphenol extract capsules/powders", "proprietary grape+blueberry extract (licensed ingredient, e.g. Memophenol) in capsule format"], "stability": "Anthocyanins are sensitive to light, heat, and pH; juice processing and storage conditions materially affect final polyphenol content, so a 'high-polyphenol' claim requires batch-level verification rather than assumption from raw grape composition.", "taste": "Grape juice and whole grapes have good inherent palatability; grape seed extract is astringent/bitter at effective doses and is typically encapsulated rather than added to food/beverage directly.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Anthocyanin/proanthocyanidin bioavailability is low and highly dependent on gut microbiota composition; co-ingestion with food, and whether the ingredient is whole juice (higher sugar, moderate polyphenol density) versus a concentrated extract (lower volume, higher polyphenol density) changes both tolerability and achievable active dose per serving.", "dose_feasibility": "The studied juice dose (230-355 mL/day) fits a realistic beverage serving; the studied extract doses (300-400 mg/day) fit a single or twice-daily capsule but not easily into a food matrix without taste masking.", "cost_availability": "Concord grape juice is a widely available, low-cost commodity beverage; grape seed extract and proprietary extracts like Memophenol are costed supplement raw materials requiring licensing from the patent holder (Activ'Inside) for the latter.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 3.5, "effect_magnitude": 3, "safety": 8, "reproducibility": 3, "food_suitability": 7, "cost_access": 7, "rationale": "Evidence_quality (3.5/10): grade C evidence overall - small, often industry-linked trials in MCI/midlife populations, and an essentially null rigorous trial in the exact healthy-young-adult target population [bell2022], so this sits at the low end of C rather than B. Effect_magnitude (3/10): where positive, effects are on secondary/partial outcome sets (a few of many measures reaching p<0.05) rather than large, consistent, clinically obvious differences. Safety (8/10): no serious adverse events across any trial up to 6 months; residual caution for sugar load and a theoretical anticoagulant interaction, consistent with a 'pass' hard safety gate. Reproducibility (3/10): most positive chronic-juice findings trace to one overlapping research/funding lineage (Krikorian/Welch Foods), and the one independent, well-powered trial in healthy young adults failed to replicate a clear benefit. Food_suitability (7/10): grape juice/whole grapes integrate naturally into food and beverage products; concentrated extracts are supplement-format. Cost_access (7/10): Concord grape juice is cheap and globally available; patented extracts add licensing cost."}, "research_gaps": ["No chronic (multi-week) RCT of grape juice or grape polyphenol extract has been conducted in healthy young adults/students; the only directly relevant chronic trial in this population used grape seed extract and was null [bell2022].", "A proposed acute young-adult Memophenol trial referenced in some secondary literature (often cited informally as 'Philip 2019') could not be verified via PubMed/Europe PMC search under that description and has been dropped rather than invented, per the no-fabrication rule; its existence/identity should be re-checked against primary sources if a citation surfaces.", "No independent (non-Welch-affiliated) replication exists of the Concord grape juice memory benefit in older adults.", "Dose-response data for grape polyphenols on cognition are essentially absent; the one negative extract trial used a single 400 mg dose and could not rule out a higher-dose effect.", "No study has measured circulating phenolic metabolite levels alongside cognitive outcomes in the same cohort, so pharmacokinetic-pharmacodynamic linkage is unconfirmed in humans.", "The terminated NCT02033941 Alzheimer's grape-seed-extract trial never reported results; its absence from the literature is a publication-bias flag worth monitoring for eventual data release."], "_derived": {"composite_score": 4.95, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "pass", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": "C", "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": "C", "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": null, "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"episodic_memory": "C", "sustained_attention": "C", "processing_speed": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 6, "n_studies": 8, "n_verified": 7, "n_unverified": 1, "n_null_negative": 2, "n_systematic_reviews": 1, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "green-tea-catechins-egcg", "name": "Green tea catechins (EGCG)", "category": "G", "category_name": "Polyphenols and plant bioactives", "aliases": ["EGCG", "(-)-epigallocatechin-3-gallate", "epigallocatechin gallate", "green tea catechins (GTC)", "green tea extract (GTE)", "decaffeinated green tea extract", "Polyphenon E (pharmaceutical-grade catechin mixture)", "Camellia sinensis leaf extract"], "food_sources": ["brewed green tea (EGCG content varies widely with cultivar, leaf grade, water temperature and steeping time; not quantified here)", "matcha (powdered whole leaf; also delivers caffeine and L-theanine; see tea-and-green-tea monograph)", "white and oolong tea (lower amounts); black tea contains little EGCG because catechins are oxidised to theaflavins/thearubigins during fermentation", "ready-to-drink green tea beverages and catechin-enriched 'functional' teas", "concentrated green tea extracts in capsules (often 45-98% EGCG; may or may not be decaffeinated)"], "active_constituents": ["(-)-epigallocatechin-3-gallate (EGCG), the most abundant green tea catechin", "(-)-epigallocatechin (EGC), (-)-epicatechin-3-gallate (ECG), (-)-epicatechin (EC) and their epimers (GCG, GC etc., formed on heating)", "co-occurring caffeine (5-10% m/m in non-decaffeinated extracts such as the one used in [schmidt2014]) and L-theanine, which confound attribution of any cognitive effect to catechins"], "one_line_verdict": "Isolated EGCG and green tea extracts have no demonstrated cognitive benefit in healthy young adults: acute trials show EEG, blood-flow and self-rated calm changes but no reliable performance gain, chronic trials in older or impaired groups are mostly null, and concentrated extracts carry a real, dose-related liver-injury risk that the EU now restricts below 800 mg EGCG per day.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. The human evidence for isolated green tea catechins (EGCG or extracts; brewed tea and matcha are in the tea-and-green-tea monograph) consists of a handful of small trials. Acutely, 300 mg EGCG increased frontal EEG power and self-rated calmness and reduced self-rated stress at 2 h [scholey2012]; but a second crossover in 27 healthy adults (135 and 270 mg) found reduced frontal cerebral blood flow on NIRS at the lower dose only and no effect on any cognitive or mood measure [wightman2012]. Two fMRI reports from one Basel sample of 12 young men given a caffeinated whey-based green tea drink showed increased dorsolateral prefrontal activation [borgwardt2012] and parieto-frontal connectivity during a 2-back task, with a non-significant trend for better accuracy (d' 3.23 vs 2.84, p = 0.066) and connectivity differences that did not survive multiple-comparison correction [schmidt2014]; the extract contained 5-10% caffeine and the control did not. A single-blind crossover in 20 women given 5.4 g of decaffeinated extract found no working-memory effect in the 10 younger women and an improvement in reading span in the 10 older women [liu2018]. Chronically, the only trial of decaffeinated catechins in non-impaired adults (336 mg/day for 12 weeks, n = 52 aged 50-69 with self-assessed decline, ITO EN-sponsored) reported isolated uncorrected p < 0.05 differences among about 40 outcomes [baba2020]. In nursing-home residents with cognitive dysfunction, 2 g/day green tea powder (220 mg catechins) for 12 months did not change MMSE versus placebo (difference -0.61, 95% CI -2.97 to 1.74) [ide2016]. In 129 APOE-e4 carriers with subjective cognitive decline, adding EGCG (5-6 mg/kg/day) to a 12-month multimodal lifestyle programme missed its primary endpoint (PACC-exe difference 0.12, 95% CI -0.01 to 0.24) with a post-washout signal that is exploratory [forcano2025]. In young adults with Down syndrome, 9 mg/kg/day EGCG plus cognitive training improved 2 of 15 cognitive tests (d about 0.3-0.4) [delatorre2016], but a 6-month trial in children with Down syndrome was null [cieutawalti2022]. A 2026 trial of 350 mg EGCG plus curcumin for 8 weeks in 64 adults aged 18-50 found no effect on mood or serum BDNF beyond placebo [cavanah2026]. The only systematic review, by the Basel fMRI group, attributed effects mainly to caffeine plus L-theanine [mancini2017]. Observational associations between green tea drinking and lower cognitive impairment (OR 0.63, 95% CI 0.54-0.73; Asian but not European cohorts) [zhou2026] concern the beverage and are confounded. How large and how reproducible. No effect on cognitive performance in healthy young adults has been replicated; the most consistent acute finding is a subjective calm/EEG change from one trial. Safety. Liver injury is the critical effect: in 1075 women, 843 mg/day EGCG for one year raised ALT in 6.7% versus 0.7% on placebo, with 1.3% ALT-related serious events [dostal2015]; EFSA found transaminase rises at 800 mg/day or more from supplements [efsa2018]; case reports implicate 140 to about 1000 mg/day, with fasting bolus intake increasing exposure [oketchrabah2020]; an industry-authored review derived 338 mg/day as a safe intake for solid bolus forms [hu2018]. What is unknown. Chronic effects in students or knowledge workers, dose-response, any effect independent of caffeine and theanine, and whether catechins reach the human brain in active concentrations.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "EGCG is not a nutrient and there is no deficiency state; every claimed effect is an enhancement or protection claim. In healthy, adequately nourished young adults the acute trials show neurophysiological changes (EEG, NIRS blood flow, fMRI activation) and, in one trial, more self-rated calm [scholey2012], but no measured improvement in cognitive performance [wightman2012, liu2018]. Possible signals are confined to older women in a 10-person subgroup [liu2018], to people with Down syndrome under cognitive training [delatorre2016], and to exploratory analyses of a null primary endpoint in APOE-e4 carriers [forcano2025]; none of these may be extrapolated to students. Separately, catechins act as anti-nutrients for iron: tea drunk with a meal cut non-haem iron absorption by more than 85% in Moroccan women with and without iron-deficiency anaemia (see tea-and-green-tea monograph, lazrak2021), and concentrated catechins are expected to act similarly (not quantified here). For iron-deficient young women, a common group in Morocco, a catechin product taken with meals could worsen a real deficiency that itself impairs cognition.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "EGCG is marketed for 'focus', 'calm alertness', 'neuroprotection', 'brain ageing' and weight loss, typically on the strength of animal amyloid and DYRK1A data, fMRI pictures and green tea epidemiology from Japan and China. The human trial evidence supports none of these as performance claims: the fMRI studies used a caffeinated drink against a caffeine-free control [schmidt2014, borgwardt2012], the best-controlled acute trial was null for cognition and mood [wightman2012], and chronic trials are null or marginal [ide2016, forcano2025, baba2020, cavanah2026]. Any felt 'focus' from a non-decaffeinated extract is most plausibly caffeine. In the EU, EFSA assessed 'contribution to normal cognitive function' for catechins in green tea (IDs 1117, 2812; EFSA Journal 2011;9(4):2055) and no cognitive claim for tea or catechins appears to be authorised (opinion text not retrieved here; to be checked against the EU Register); products must also carry the mandatory EGCG warnings of Regulation (EU) 2022/2340. In the US, an FTC-defensible cognitive claim for EGCG would lack competent and reliable evidence. The honest position: green tea as a beverage is a pleasant, low-risk source of a little caffeine and theanine; isolated EGCG adds liver risk without demonstrated cognitive benefit.", "mechanism": {"summary": "EGCG has low oral bioavailability: it is partly absorbed in the small intestine, extensively methylated, glucuronidated and sulfated, effluxed back to the gut, and degraded by colonic microbiota to ring-fission products (valerolactones, phenolic acids). Fasting and bolus dosing increase plasma exposure, which is also the condition linked to hepatotoxicity [oketchrabah2020, hu2018]. Brain penetration in humans is unknown. In vitro and in animals EGCG inhibits amyloid and alpha-synuclein aggregation and the DYRK1A kinase (the rationale for Down syndrome trials) and shows antioxidant and anti-inflammatory activity. Acute human effects are limited to neurophysiological markers (EEG power, frontal haemodynamics) whose link to cognition is unestablished. Mechanism is plausible but not efficacy.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Oral EGCG or green tea extract -> limited small-intestinal absorption, phase II conjugation, efflux; greater exposure when taken fasting as a bolus", "detail": "Reviewed by USP: repeated fasting bolus doses markedly raise catechin bioavailability, possibly by saturating first-pass elimination [oketchrabah2020]; Hu et al. found hepatic adverse events concentrated in bolus solid-dose extracts rather than beverages [hu2018].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Colonic microbial metabolism -> valerolactones and phenolic acids (possible bioactive metabolites)", "detail": "Plausible route for systemic exposure; no human study here links these metabolites to cognition.", "evidence_level": "plausible"}, {"step": "Passage across the blood-brain barrier", "detail": "Shown in rodents at low levels; not measured in humans.", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Molecular targets: DYRK1A inhibition, anti-amyloid/anti-aggregation, antioxidant and anti-inflammatory signalling", "detail": "Robust in vitro and in rodent models (rationale for TESDAD/PERSEUS/PENSA); human target engagement not demonstrated in healthy adults [delatorre2016, forcano2025].", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Acute neurophysiological change: frontal EEG power, frontal NIRS haemodynamics, fMRI DLPFC activation", "detail": "Increased alpha/beta/theta EEG power at 300 mg [scholey2012]; reduced frontal oxy-/total haemoglobin at 135 mg but not 270 mg [wightman2012]; fMRI changes after a caffeinated extract drink [borgwardt2012, schmidt2014]. Biomarkers only, partly caffeine-confounded.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Measured human cognitive outcome", "detail": "No replicated benefit in healthy young adults [wightman2012, liu2018]; null or marginal chronic trials in older or impaired groups [ide2016, forcano2025, baba2020]; small benefit on 2 of 15 tests in Down syndrome [delatorre2016].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["DYRK1A kinase inhibition (Down syndrome rationale)", "inhibition of amyloid-beta and alpha-synuclein aggregation (in vitro/animal)", "antioxidant and anti-inflammatory signalling (Nrf2, NF-kB; in vitro/animal)", "modulation of cerebral haemodynamics (NIRS) and cortical oscillations (EEG)", "OATP transporter inhibition (relevant to drug interactions, not cognition)", "chelation of non-haem iron in the gut (anti-nutrient effect)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults in two independent laboratory crossovers (Swinburne, Northumbria)", "dose_or_intake": "135-300 mg EGCG single dose", "duration": "single dose, tested 45 min to 2 h post-dose", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "300 mg EGCG increased self-rated calmness and reduced self-rated stress at 2 h [scholey2012]; 135 and 270 mg had no effect on any mood measure [wightman2012].", "effect_estimate": "not reported in abstracts (significance only)", "certainty_notes": "Two small crossovers with opposite results; subjective outcomes; Scholey sample size and effect sizes not available in the abstract; downgraded for imprecision and inconsistency.", "key_study_ids": ["scholey2012", "wightman2012"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy young adults (mean age about 21-29) tested on frontal tasks, n-back and reading span", "dose_or_intake": "135-270 mg EGCG; 5.4 g decaffeinated extract (>=45% EGCG); caffeinated green tea extract drink", "duration": "single dose", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "No cognitive effect at 135 or 270 mg EGCG [wightman2012]; no working-memory effect in younger women after a very high extract dose [liu2018]; non-significant trend in 2-back d' (3.23 vs 2.84, p = 0.066, n = 12) after a caffeinated extract drink versus a caffeine-free control [schmidt2014].", "effect_estimate": "d' difference 0.39 (p = 0.066, n = 12) [schmidt2014]; others not significant, estimates not reported", "certainty_notes": "Consistently null or non-significant, but all trials tiny (n 10-27) so a small effect cannot be excluded; the only near-signal is caffeine-confounded. Not enough to call it 'no effect' with high certainty.", "key_study_ids": ["wightman2012", "liu2018", "schmidt2014"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "cerebral_blood_flow", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy young adults; biomarker outcomes (NIRS, EEG, fMRI), not cognition", "dose_or_intake": "135-300 mg EGCG; caffeinated green tea extract drink (Basel)", "duration": "single dose", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "135 mg (not 270 mg) EGCG reduced frontal oxygenated and total haemoglobin [wightman2012]; 300 mg increased frontal EEG power [scholey2012]; caffeinated extract increased DLPFC activation (ROI only, whole-brain null) and parieto-frontal connectivity (uncorrected) [borgwardt2012, schmidt2014].", "effect_estimate": "not reported as standardized effects", "certainty_notes": "Biomarkers of uncertain cognitive meaning; no dose-response; Basel studies are one sample with caffeine confound. Must not be presented as cognitive enhancement.", "key_study_ids": ["wightman2012", "scholey2012", "borgwardt2012", "schmidt2014"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Women aged 50-63 (n = 10) and Japanese adults aged 50-69 with self-assessed decline but MMSE >24", "dose_or_intake": "5.4 g decaffeinated extract; 336 mg decaffeinated catechins", "duration": "single dose", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Reading span improved in 10 older women after a very high extract dose, n-back unchanged [liu2018]; a single 336 mg catechin dose reduced CPT commission errors (0.04 vs 0.68) with most other tasks unchanged [baba2020].", "effect_estimate": "CPT commission errors 0.04 +/- 0.21 vs 0.68 +/- 1.2, p = 0.004 [baba2020]; reading span not quantified in abstract [liu2018]", "certainty_notes": "Very small, single-blind [liu2018] or industry-funded with many uncorrected outcomes [baba2020]; floor effects on CPT errors; downgraded for risk of bias, imprecision and multiplicity.", "key_study_ids": ["liu2018", "baba2020"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Japanese adults aged 50-69 with self-assessed cognitive decline, MMSE >24 (essentially normal cognition)", "dose_or_intake": "336 mg/day decaffeinated green tea catechins", "duration": "12 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Of about 40 Cognitrax outcomes, 2-back response time and delayed visual memory hits differed at 12 weeks (p < 0.05, uncorrected); MMSE, verbal memory, attention, reaction time unchanged.", "effect_estimate": "Visual memory delayed hits 10.3 vs 9.0 (p = 0.046); other estimates not significant [baba2020]", "certainty_notes": "Single small (n = 47 analysed) trial sponsored and supplied by ITO EN; multiplicity; baseline imbalance on some attention tasks; no replication.", "key_study_ids": ["baba2020"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_mci", "population_detail": "Japanese nursing-home residents (mean age 85) with cognitive dysfunction, MMSE-J <28 (mean 15.8)", "dose_or_intake": "2 g/day green tea powder (220 mg catechins)", "duration": "12 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "No difference in MMSE-J change versus placebo; MDA-modified LDL (oxidative-stress biomarker) lower.", "effect_estimate": "MMSE-J LSM difference -0.61 (95% CI -2.97 to 1.74), p = 0.59 [ide2016]", "certainty_notes": "Small (n = 33, 27 completers), co-authored by ITO EN staff; wide CI; a preceding uncontrolled pilot reported gains that the RCT did not confirm.", "key_study_ids": ["ide2016"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_decline", "population": "other", "population_detail": "APOE-e4 carriers aged 60-80 with subjective cognitive decline, all receiving a 12-month multimodal lifestyle intervention", "dose_or_intake": "EGCG-enriched green tea extract 5-6 mg/kg/day (about 350-500 mg/day for 60-80 kg)", "duration": "12 months plus 3-month washout", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Primary endpoint (PACC-exe) not met; more participants with 'reliable improvement' and a post-washout difference in exploratory analyses.", "effect_estimate": "AMD 0.12 (95% CI -0.01 to 0.24), p = 0.061 at 12 months; exploratory post-washout AMD 0.19 (0.06 to 0.32) [forcano2025]", "certainty_notes": "Well-designed, publicly funded, but single trial with null primary outcome; exploratory signals must not be presented as efficacy; population and co-intervention indirect to Brain Food.", "key_study_ids": ["forcano2025"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Adults aged 18-50 (n = 64), community sample", "dose_or_intake": "350 mg/day EGCG combined with 1330 mg/day curcumin", "duration": "8 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "DASS-21, GAD-7 and sleep improved equally in both groups; no group difference in mood or serum BDNF.", "effect_estimate": "No significant group-by-time interaction (estimates not in abstract) [cavanah2026]", "certainty_notes": "Single small trial of a combination (cannot isolate EGCG); strong placebo/time effect; BDNF is a biomarker.", "key_study_ids": ["cavanah2026"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "learning", "population": "students", "population_detail": "Students and healthy young knowledge workers: no chronic EGCG or extract trial with cognitive outcomes identified", "dose_or_intake": "not studied", "duration": "not studied", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No trial. Only mechanistic/animal rationale and acute biomarker data exist; the systematic review could not attribute green tea effects to EGCG [mancini2017].", "effect_estimate": "not applicable", "certainty_notes": "Absence of evidence; animal data cannot raise the grade. Any product claim for students would be unsupported.", "key_study_ids": ["mancini2017"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Young adults (16-34 y) with Down syndrome receiving cognitive training", "dose_or_intake": "EGCG 9 mg/kg/day (green tea extract)", "duration": "12 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Better visual recognition memory, inhibitory control and one adaptive skill than placebo plus training; 13 of 15 cognitive tests and 8 of 9 adaptive skills unchanged.", "effect_estimate": "PRM immediate recall +6.23 points (95% CI 0.31 to 12.14), d 0.4; Cats and Dogs d 0.28 [delatorre2016]", "certainty_notes": "Phase 2, n = 84, multiple endpoints without clear correction; not replicated; not transferable to people without Down syndrome.", "key_study_ids": ["delatorre2016"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "children_adolescents", "population_detail": "Children aged 6-12 with Down syndrome", "dose_or_intake": "EGCG 10 mg/kg/day", "duration": "6 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Cognition and adaptive functioning not improved versus placebo; no excess adverse events.", "effect_estimate": "not significant (estimates not in abstract) [cieutawalti2022]", "certainty_notes": "Phase Ib, n = 73; clinical paediatric population, not generalisable; EU law prohibits EGCG extract supplements for under-18s anyway (label warning).", "key_study_ids": ["cieutawalti2022"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "dementia_risk", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Observational cohorts of green tea drinkers (beverage, not extracts), mostly Asian", "dose_or_intake": "habitual green tea consumption", "duration": "years (observational)", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "Green tea drinking associated with lower odds of cognitive impairment, only in Asian cohorts; no trial of catechin extracts on dementia incidence.", "effect_estimate": "OR 0.63 (95% CI 0.54-0.73) for cognitive impairment, beverage [zhou2026]", "certainty_notes": "Observational and confounded; concerns the beverage, so it cannot be transferred to isolated EGCG (indirectness); see tea-and-green-tea monograph for the beverage grade.", "key_study_ids": ["zhou2026"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "300 mg EGCG", "onset": "tested at 120 min", "duration_studied": "single session", "domain": "EEG power; self-rated calmness/stress", "population": "healthy adults", "effect_estimate": "increased alpha/beta/theta; calmer, less stressed (significance only)", "certainty": "low (single small trial, subjective outcome)", "safety": "no adverse events reported in abstract", "study_ids": ["scholey2012"]}, {"dose": "135 and 270 mg EGCG", "onset": "45 min absorption, then 42 min testing", "duration_studied": "single session", "domain": "frontal CBF (NIRS); cognition; mood", "population": "27 healthy adults", "effect_estimate": "lower oxy-/total Hb at 135 mg only; no cognitive or mood effect", "certainty": "low-moderate for null cognitive result (n = 27)", "safety": "not reported", "study_ids": ["wightman2012"]}, {"dose": "caffeinated green tea extract in whey drink (250-500 mL; extract 5-10% caffeine)", "onset": "during fMRI shortly after intragastric dosing", "duration_studied": "single session", "domain": "fMRI DLPFC activation, parieto-frontal connectivity, 2-back d'", "population": "12 healthy young men", "effect_estimate": "d' 3.23 vs 2.84 (p = 0.066); connectivity p = 0.030 uncorrected", "certainty": "very low (n = 12, caffeine confound, uncorrected)", "safety": "not reported", "study_ids": ["schmidt2014", "borgwardt2012"]}, {"dose": "5.4 g decaffeinated extract (>=45% EGCG, i.e. >2.4 g EGCG) within 24 h", "onset": "within 24 h", "duration_studied": "single treatment period", "domain": "reading span, n-back", "population": "10 younger and 10 older women", "effect_estimate": "younger: null; older: reading span improved", "certainty": "very low (single-blind, n = 10 per group)", "safety": "EGCG dose far above the EU 800 mg/day prohibition threshold", "study_ids": ["liu2018"]}, {"dose": "336 mg decaffeinated catechins", "onset": "same-day test", "duration_studied": "single dose", "domain": "continuous performance test errors", "population": "adults 50-69 with self-assessed decline", "effect_estimate": "CPT commission errors 0.04 vs 0.68 (p = 0.004); most tasks unchanged", "certainty": "low (industry-funded, multiplicity)", "safety": "not reported in abstract", "study_ids": ["baba2020"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "8 weeks", "population": "adults 18-50 (n = 64)", "outcome": "mood (DASS-21, GAD-7), serum BDNF", "effect_estimate": "no difference vs placebo (350 mg EGCG + curcumin)", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "combination product; small; strong placebo response", "study_ids": ["cavanah2026"]}, {"duration": "12 weeks", "population": "adults 50-69, MMSE >24, self-assessed decline", "outcome": "Cognitrax battery, MMSE", "effect_estimate": "isolated uncorrected differences (2-back RT; visual memory delayed hits 10.3 vs 9.0)", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "industry sponsor (ITO EN); multiplicity; n = 47", "study_ids": ["baba2020"]}, {"duration": "12 months", "population": "nursing-home residents with cognitive dysfunction", "outcome": "MMSE-J", "effect_estimate": "-0.61 (95% CI -2.97 to 1.74), null", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "n = 33; ITO EN co-authors; green tea powder not pure EGCG", "study_ids": ["ide2016"]}, {"duration": "12 months + 3-month washout", "population": "APOE-e4 carriers 60-80 with subjective cognitive decline", "outcome": "PACC-exe composite", "effect_estimate": "AMD 0.12 (95% CI -0.01 to 0.24), primary null", "evidence_quality": "moderate (single well-conducted RCT)", "limitations": "co-intervention; exploratory post-washout signal only", "study_ids": ["forcano2025"]}, {"duration": "12 months", "population": "young adults with Down syndrome", "outcome": "cognitive battery, adaptive behaviour", "effect_estimate": "2 of 15 tests improved (d about 0.3-0.4)", "evidence_quality": "low-moderate", "limitations": "multiple endpoints; not replicated; clinical population", "study_ids": ["delatorre2016"]}, {"duration": "12 months", "population": "1075 postmenopausal women", "outcome": "safety: ALT elevation", "effect_estimate": "843 mg/day EGCG: ALT elevation 6.7% vs 0.7% placebo; 1.3% ALT-related serious AEs", "evidence_quality": "high (large RCT)", "limitations": "dose above the EU threshold; older women only", "study_ids": ["dostal2015"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Brewed green tea is safe for most adults (see tea-and-green-tea). Concentrated green tea extracts and isolated EGCG are different: the liver is the target organ, with dose-dependent transaminase rises and rare serious idiosyncratic hepatitis. In the largest RCT, 843 mg/day EGCG for a year raised ALT in 6.7% of women versus 0.7% on placebo [dostal2015]; EFSA identified 800 mg/day or more from supplements as associated with transaminase increases [efsa2018]; case reports implicate 140 to about 1000 mg/day, especially when taken fasting as a bolus [oketchrabah2020]; an industry review derived 338 mg/day as a safe intake for solid bolus forms [hu2018]. At 300-350 mg/day in short trials and in Down syndrome trials at 9-10 mg/kg no excess adverse events were reported [delatorre2016, cieutawalti2022], but these trials were not powered for rare liver injury. Additional issues: inhibition of non-haem iron absorption (tea cut iron absorption by over 85% in Moroccan women; see tea-and-green-tea), OATP-mediated drug interactions [abdelkawy2020], caffeine in non-decaffeinated extracts, and product quality (variable catechin profiles, possible solvent or pesticide residues).", "adverse_effects": ["elevated liver transaminases (ALT) and rare acute hepatitis, dose-related and idiosyncratic [dostal2015, oketchrabah2020]", "nausea and gastrointestinal upset, especially on an empty stomach [dostal2015]", "skin reactions (more frequent than placebo in MGTT) [dostal2015]", "reduced non-haem iron absorption when taken with meals (risk of worsening iron deficiency)", "caffeine-related insomnia, jitteriness and palpitations with non-decaffeinated extracts"], "contraindications": ["liver disease or elevated liver enzymes", "pregnancy and lactation (EU mandatory warning for EGCG products)", "children and adolescents under 18 (EU mandatory warning)", "iron-deficiency anaemia or high risk of iron deficiency (take away from meals or avoid)", "concurrent use of other green tea extract or catechin products (cumulative EGCG)"], "drug_interactions": ["nadolol and other OATP1A2 substrates: green tea reduced nadolol AUC by about 85% (tea beverage; see tea-and-green-tea, misaka2014)", "atorvastatin: 300-600 mg extract lowered Cmax by about 25% [abdelkawy2020]", "hepatotoxic drugs (e.g. paracetamol at high doses, methotrexate, some antifungals): additive liver risk is plausible but not quantified here", "iron supplements and iron-fortified foods: chelation reduces absorption", "stimulants and caffeine-containing products when the extract is not decaffeinated"], "upper_limits": "EU: food portions must contain less than 800 mg EGCG per day (Regulation (EU) 2022/2340, Annex III Part B), with mandatory label statements of EGCG per portion and maximum daily portions; EFSA could not set a tolerable upper intake level [efsa2018]. An industry-authored review proposed 338 mg/day for solid bolus forms [hu2018]. USP labelling: take with food. Caffeine (for non-decaffeinated extracts): EFSA 400 mg/day adults, 200 mg/day in pregnancy.", "sleep_impact": "Decaffeinated EGCG has no known sleep effect; non-decaffeinated extracts deliver 5-10% caffeine by weight and can disrupt sleep if taken in the afternoon or evening.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Up to 12 months at 843 mg/day (MGTT, n = 1075) and at 5-6 or 9 mg/kg/day in PENSA and TESDAD; no long-term data in young adults or students.", "daily_use_appropriate": "uncertain", "special_populations": "Pregnant and lactating women and under-18s: excluded by EU label requirements. People with liver disease, heavy alcohol use or hepatotoxic medication: avoid. Iron-deficient women and adolescents (common in Morocco): avoid with meals. Medication users (statins, beta-blockers, OATP substrates): pharmacist review. Possible genetic susceptibility (e.g. COMT, UGT, HLA variants) is suspected but not testable in practice [oketchrabah2020].", "specialist_review_needed": ["hepatologist / toxicologist (dose ceiling, liver warnings)", "clinical pharmacologist (drug interactions)", "clinical nutritionist (iron status of target population)", "regulatory affairs (EU Annex III conditions, Morocco status)"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "Liver injury is a documented, dose-related and partly idiosyncratic risk of concentrated EGCG; acceptable only at low doses (well under 300 mg EGCG/day), with food or in a beverage matrix, decaffeinated, with EU-mandated warnings, and excluding pregnancy, under-18s, liver disease and iron deficiency. Given the absence of cognitive benefit, the risk-benefit ratio for a cognition product is unfavourable."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Tea and green tea catechins are traditional foods. Commission Regulation (EU) 2022/2340 (adopted 30 November 2022; transitional period to 21 June 2023) added green tea extracts containing EGCG to Annex III of Regulation (EC) No 1925/2006: Part B (restricted) requires the daily portion to contain less than 800 mg EGCG, labels to state EGCG per portion and the maximum number of daily portions, and warnings: 'Should not be consumed if you are consuming other products containing green tea on the same day', 'Should not be consumed by pregnant or lactating women and children below 18 years old', 'Should not be consumed on an empty stomach'. Aqueous extracts that, once reconstituted, resemble traditional infusions are excluded. The substance is also in Part C (under Union scrutiny), so further restriction is possible. Wording read via a secondary legal-text mirror (lawplayer.com, accessed 2026-10-26); confirm against EUR-Lex. EFSA assessed 'contribution to normal cognitive function' (IDs 1117, 2812) for tea/catechins in EFSA Journal 2011;9(4):2055 (DOI confirmed via Crossref; the conclusion was not retrievable, HTTP 403); no cognitive claim for tea or catechins is known to be authorised, which must be confirmed in the EU Register. Novel-food status of highly purified (>90%) EGCG was not checked and is uncertain.", "us": "Green tea extracts and EGCG are sold as dietary supplements under DSHEA (pre-1994 market history for green tea extracts generally assumed; NDI status of high-purity EGCG not checked). Conventional-food use relies on GRAS: FDA issued a 'no questions' letter for palmitoylated green tea catechins (GRN 772, Kemin, antioxidant use; estimated EGCG exposure 33-66 mg/day); FDA ceased evaluating GRN 225 (Kao catechins) at the notifier's request; a DSM 'Teavigo' GRAS status is reported in trade press but was not confirmed (sources: IFT news 2019, EWG 2021, FoodIngredientsFirst; accessed 2026-10-26). USP monograph for powdered decaffeinated green tea extract requires a liver caution label [oketchrabah2020]. Structure/function cognitive claims would require FTC-standard competent and reliable scientific evidence, which does not exist for EGCG in healthy adults.", "morocco": "No Moroccan text specific to green tea extract or EGCG supplements was found. Tea is a staple food; ONSSA checks imported tea and set pesticide residue limits from 2019 (press: Medias24 2018, TelQuel 2019; accessed 2026-10-26). Food supplements fall under law 28-07 (ONSSA) and Ministry of Health rules; any authorised-ingredient list, maximum EGCG dose or claims framework could not be confirmed online. Status: unknown; direct consultation with ONSSA and the Direction du Médicament et de la Pharmacie required. Aligning voluntarily with the EU 2022/2340 conditions is advisable.", "permitted_claims_examples": [], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "Green tea extracts with a history of use are not novel foods in the EU, but they are restricted under Annex III (Part B) and under scrutiny (Part C). Highly purified or synthetic EGCG and palmitoylated catechins may require separate novel food / NDI assessment (not verified). In the US, NDI status for high-purity EGCG was not checked.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Moderate as brewed tea or tea-based beverage (natural matrix, consumers accept it); poor as a fortificant at studied doses because catechins are bitter, astringent and unstable. Brewed green tea remains the realistic vehicle; capsules of concentrated extract carry the liver risk and are not food.", "formats": ["brewed green tea or matcha (preferred; see tea-and-green-tea)", "ready-to-drink tea beverages (acidified, with ascorbic acid)", "decaffeinated green tea extract capsules (supplement; liver warnings)", "catechin-enriched bars or snacks (limited by bitterness)"], "stability": "EGCG oxidises and epimerises (to GCG) at neutral or alkaline pH and during heat processing; degradation accelerates with dissolved oxygen and metal ions; more stable at acidic pH (about 3-5) and with ascorbic acid; browning and loss of potency occur in beverages over shelf life. (General food-chemistry knowledge; specific kinetics not researched here.)", "taste": "Strongly bitter and astringent at the 135-300 mg doses used in acute trials; protein (milk, whey) binding reduces astringency but may alter bioavailability; sweeteners and flavours required.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Low oral bioavailability; fasting bolus intake increases plasma EGCG and hepatotoxicity risk [oketchrabah2020, hu2018]; beverage and food matrices appear safer for the liver [hu2018]. Co-ingestion with meals reduces absorption of both EGCG and dietary non-haem iron.", "dose_feasibility": "Acute trial doses (135-300 mg EGCG) correspond to roughly a few cups of brewed green tea (EGCG per cup varies widely; not quantified here) and are feasible in a tea beverage; the 5.4 g extract dose in [liu2018] and the 843 mg/day in [dostal2015] are not food-compatible and exceed the EU limit.", "cost_availability": "Green tea is cheap and ubiquitous in Morocco; extracts are widely available as commodity ingredients with variable purity.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 2, "effect_magnitude": 1, "safety": 5, "reproducibility": 2, "food_suitability": 4, "cost_access": 7, "rationale": "Evidence quality 2: best grades are C for small, inconsistent trials and D (no trials) for students/young adults chronically; no outcome reaches B. Effect magnitude 1: no replicated cognitive effect in healthy adults; primary endpoints null in the best chronic trials; only subjective calm and biomarkers acutely. Safety 5: brewed tea is safe, but extracts cause dose-related ALT elevations (6.7% at 843 mg/day) and rare serious hepatitis even at lower doses, plus iron and drug interactions. Reproducibility 2: the one positive acute calm finding was not replicated; fMRI positives come from one caffeine-confounded sample; chronic positives are industry-funded with multiplicity. Food suitability 4: works only as brewed tea; isolated EGCG is bitter, unstable and safety-restricted. Cost/access 7: green tea and commodity extracts are inexpensive and widely available."}, "research_gaps": ["Adequately powered, preregistered RCTs of decaffeinated EGCG (100-300 mg) on cognition in healthy young adults and students, acute and chronic", "Factorial trials separating EGCG from caffeine and L-theanine in green tea", "Human pharmacokinetics showing whether EGCG or its microbial metabolites reach the brain", "Dose-response for liver safety at 100-400 mg/day with food, including genetic susceptibility markers", "Effect of catechin products on iron status in iron-deficient young women (e.g. Moroccan students)", "Replication of the PENSA post-washout signal and TESDAD findings in confirmatory trials", "Publication of results for registered EGCG trials without results (e.g. NCT01357681 Huntington's disease; NCT01855971 fragile X)"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 3.1, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": null, "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"episodic_memory": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 12, "n_studies": 18, "n_verified": 18, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 8, "n_systematic_reviews": 1, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["inconsistent", "insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "lutein-zeaxanthin-and-carotenoids", "name": "Lutein, zeaxanthin and other carotenoids", "category": "G", "category_name": "Polyphenols and plant bioactives", "aliases": ["lutein", "zeaxanthin", "meso-zeaxanthin", "macular xanthophylls", "macular carotenoids", "MXans", "Tagetes erecta (marigold) lutein", "E 161b (lutein colour)", "beta-carotene", "carotenoids"], "food_sources": ["dark leafy greens (kale, spinach, chard, collards): richest common sources of lutein", "egg yolk: low absolute lutein/zeaxanthin content but high bioavailability from the lipid matrix (see eggs.json: wenzel2006, vishwanathan2009, egg meta-analysis khalighisikaroudi2021 on macular pigment)", "avocado: modest lutein plus fat that enhances carotenoid absorption from other vegetables (see avocado.json: scott2017, unlu2005)", "maize/corn, orange peppers, peas, broccoli, pistachios, goji (zeaxanthin)", "beta-carotene: carrots, sweet potato, pumpkin, apricots, mango", "meso-zeaxanthin: essentially not in the usual diet (trace in some fish skin); supplied by supplements"], "active_constituents": ["lutein (dihydroxy-carotenoid, xanthophyll)", "zeaxanthin (RR-isomer)", "meso-zeaxanthin (RS-isomer; formed in the retina from lutein)", "beta-carotene (provitamin A carotene)", "lutein esters from marigold oleoresin"], "one_line_verdict": "Lutein and zeaxanthin reliably raise macular pigment, but cognitive benefit in healthy young adults rests on three small, mostly industry-linked trials from one research network with multiple outcomes, the largest trial (AREDS2, n=3501 older adults) and a 2021 meta-analysis are null, so leafy greens and eggs are a sensible dietary habit while a lutein 'brain' supplement is not evidence-based.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. Lutein (L) and zeaxanthin (Z) are the dominant carotenoids in the macula and in human brain tissue, and 10-27 mg/day for 4-12 months reliably raises serum L/Z and macular pigment optical density (MPOD), which is used as a proxy for brain xanthophyll status [renzihammond2017, stringham2019, power2018, hammond2017]. That biomarker effect is established. Whether it changes cognition is much less certain. Healthy young adults. Three randomised trials in 18-30-year-olds report benefits: a 12-month trial (n=51, unbalanced 37 vs 14, Abbott-linked) found only visual memory improved by group, with complex attention and reasoning differing only in a non-randomised comparison of MPOD 'increasers' versus 'flat liners' [renzihammond2017]; a 6-month three-arm trial (n=59) reported gains in composite and verbal memory, sustained attention and processing speed alongside BDNF and IL-1 beta changes [stringham2019]; and a 4-month trial (n=69) improved temporal contrast sensitivity, a visual rather than cognitive processing-speed measure [bovier2015]. All come from the same Georgia group and collaborators, used multiple outcomes without clear multiplicity control and were small; none has been independently replicated. Middle-aged adults with low MPOD improved paired-associate learning after 12 months (n=91) [power2018]; adults 40-75 with cognitive complaints improved only visual episodic memory and learning among many outcomes [lopresti2022]; teenagers with low fruit/vegetable intake improved attention and processing speed but not memory or reasoning [lopresti2026]. Older adults. Small trials report gains in complex attention and cognitive flexibility [hammond2017], fMRI changes with a verbal-learning effect [lindbergh2018] and verbal fluency [johnson2008]; but the large AREDS2 ancillary trial (n=3501, 5 years, 10 mg L + 2 mg Z) found no effect on cognitive decline (difference in yearly change 0.03, 99% CI -0.14 to 0.19) [chew2015], and a meta-analysis of RCTs found no significant effect on attention (SMD 0.02), executive function (SMD 0.13) or memory (SMD 0.03) [li2021]. Benefits in Alzheimer's disease with a multi-nutrient formula [nolan2022] cannot be generalised. Beta-carotene for 18 years produced a tiny cognitive difference (0.047 SD) while 1 year did not [grodstein2007], and high-dose beta-carotene increased lung cancer and mortality in smokers [atbc1994]. Overall: chronic memory/attention effects in healthy adults are graded C, possible benefit at most; acute effects are unstudied and implausible given slow tissue accumulation. Ordinary foods (greens, eggs, avocado with vegetables) deliver lutein with better matrix absorption and no safety concerns.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "There is no recognised lutein deficiency or dietary requirement; lutein and zeaxanthin are non-essential. Low MPOD or low fruit/vegetable intake is the closest analogue of 'low status', and the most credible positive trials selected such people (low MPOD [power2018]; teenagers on low fruit/vegetable diets [lopresti2026]); in the Renzi-Hammond trial the young adults already had above-average MPOD (0.48) [renzihammond2017]. Raising status in people with low intake through diet is a reasonable nutritional goal; claiming enhancement in replete young adults is not supported. Beta-carotene is a provitamin A source, but vitamin A deficiency correction is a different question (see vitamin-a.json) and supplemental beta-carotene does not enhance cognition in the short term [grodstein2007].", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Supplements marketed as 'brain carotenoids' or 'boost processing speed and memory' rely on Georgia-group trials with industry ties (Abbott for Renzi-Hammond 2017; supplement manufacturers in related work) and on MPOD-cognition correlations. Visual processing speed (temporal contrast sensitivity, critical flicker fusion) is a retinal/visual outcome, not general cognition; BDNF and MPOD are biomarkers. The largest independent trial (AREDS2) and the only quantitative meta-analysis are null. In the EU no health claim for lutein or zeaxanthin (eye or brain) is authorised, so a cognition claim is prohibited; in the US a structure/function claim would need FTC-level competent and reliable scientific evidence, which is weak. Honest positioning: 'a food rich in leafy greens and eggs supplies lutein, a carotenoid that accumulates in the eye and brain; its cognitive effects are under study'.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Dietary lutein and zeaxanthin are absorbed with dietary fat in micelles (SR-BI, CD36), carried on HDL/LDL, and selectively accumulated in the macula and brain (lutein is the dominant carotenoid in infant and adult brain). Proposed mechanisms are antioxidant protection of polyunsaturated membrane lipids, anti-inflammatory effects (IL-1 beta), membrane and gap-junction effects, and improved neural efficiency. MPOD correlates with brain lutein in post-mortem tissue and is used as a non-invasive proxy. Mechanisms are plausible; human cognitive mediation is not demonstrated.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Dietary or supplemental L/Z -> micellar absorption with fat -> chylomicrons -> lipoproteins", "detail": "Absorption depends strongly on dietary fat and matrix: egg yolk and avocado raise uptake (cross-reference eggs.json, avocado.json); supplements 10-27 mg/day raise serum L/Z several-fold within months [stringham2019, renzihammond2017].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Serum L/Z -> deposition in macula (MPOD) and, by inference, brain", "detail": "MPOD rises over 4-12 months of supplementation [renzihammond2017, power2018, hammond2017]; MPOD-brain correlation comes from post-mortem studies cited in these papers, not measured here.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Brain xanthophylls -> antioxidant / anti-inflammatory membrane protection", "detail": "IL-1 beta fell and BDNF rose in one young-adult trial [stringham2019]; serum BDNF is a peripheral biomarker of uncertain brain relevance.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Altered neural efficiency / cerebral perfusion", "detail": "fMRI BOLD changes in older adults after 1 year, in the direction opposite to the hypothesis [lindbergh2018].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Measured cognitive outcome", "detail": "Small trials report domain-specific gains (visual memory, attention, processing speed); large AREDS2 and a meta-analysis are null [chew2015, li2021].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["antioxidant protection of neural membrane lipids", "anti-inflammatory cytokine modulation", "neurotrophic signalling (BDNF, biomarker only)", "visual pathway processing speed (retinal filter, contrast sensitivity)", "provitamin A conversion (beta-carotene only)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy university-age adults 18-30 y, many with average-to-high MPOD", "dose_or_intake": "10-27 mg/day L+Z (+/- meso-zeaxanthin)", "duration": "6-12 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Two small trials (n=51, n=59) report better visual/verbal/composite memory versus placebo; in the 12-month trial only visual memory differed by randomised group.", "effect_estimate": "Not reported as standardised effects; p<0.04 for visual memory [renzihammond2017]; p<0.05 composite and verbal memory [stringham2019]", "certainty_notes": "Downgraded for risk of bias (unbalanced allocation, per-protocol and non-randomised 'increaser' analyses, multiple outcomes), imprecision (n<60 each), indirectness of single-lab battery (CNS Vital Signs) and industry links; no independent replication.", "key_study_ids": ["renzihammond2017", "stringham2019"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "processing_speed", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Young healthy adults; visual processing speed (temporal contrast sensitivity) and computerised psychomotor speed", "dose_or_intake": "10-27 mg/day", "duration": "4-6 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Temporal contrast sensitivity improved after 4 months (a visual-system measure) and psychomotor/processing speed improved in one 6-month trial.", "effect_estimate": "Not reported as standardised effect; significant group differences (p<0.05) [bovier2015, stringham2019]", "certainty_notes": "Visual psychophysics is not general cognition; small, same research network; placebo groups small (n=15).", "key_study_ids": ["bovier2015", "stringham2019"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Young adults; attention/complex attention", "dose_or_intake": "13-27 mg/day", "duration": "6-12 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Sustained attention improved in one trial; complex attention in the other trial was only different in a non-randomised MPOD-responder analysis.", "effect_estimate": "p<0.05 [stringham2019]; responder analysis p<0.04 (not randomised) [renzihammond2017]", "certainty_notes": "Inconsistent operationalisation, high risk of bias in responder analysis.", "key_study_ids": ["stringham2019", "renzihammond2017"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "alertness", "population": "students", "population_detail": "Students / knowledge workers wanting same-day effects", "dose_or_intake": "any", "duration": "single dose", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No acute trials exist; tissue accumulation takes weeks to months, so an acute cognitive effect is biologically implausible.", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "No human data.", "key_study_ids": []}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "deficient_or_low_baseline", "population_detail": "Healthy adults (mean 45 y) with low MPOD; adults 40-75 with self-reported cognitive complaints", "dose_or_intake": "10 mg L + 2 mg Z (+10 mg MZ)", "duration": "6-12 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Paired-associate learning improved over 12 months in low-MPOD adults; visual episodic memory and learning improved in adults with complaints, with no effect on other tests or self-reported cognition.", "effect_estimate": "PAL memory rANOVA p=0.009 [power2018]; visual episodic memory p=0.005 [lopresti2022]; no SMDs in abstracts", "certainty_notes": "Single trials, multiple outcomes, CRO/industry links; consistent with a narrow visual-memory effect.", "key_study_ids": ["power2018", "lopresti2022"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Community-dwelling older adults (mean 67-74 y), and AREDS2 participants at risk of AMD (mean 73 y)", "dose_or_intake": "10-12 mg L(+Z)/day", "duration": "4 months to 5 years", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Small trials report gains in complex attention, cognitive flexibility, verbal fluency or verbal learning; the 3501-person AREDS2 trial found no effect on 5-year cognitive change; meta-analysis null.", "effect_estimate": "AREDS2 difference in yearly composite change 0.03 (99% CI -0.14 to 0.19), P=0.66 [chew2015]; pooled SMD memory 0.03 (-0.26 to 0.32), executive 0.13 (-0.26 to 0.51), attention 0.02 (-0.27 to 0.31) [li2021]", "certainty_notes": "Largest, lowest-bias trial is null; positive trials small (n=37-51 analysed); telephone cognitive battery in AREDS2 may be insensitive (indirectness).", "key_study_ids": ["chew2015", "li2021", "hammond2017", "lindbergh2018", "johnson2008"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "neuroplasticity_biomarkers", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Young adults (BDNF, MPOD) and older adults (fMRI)", "dose_or_intake": "12-27 mg/day", "duration": "6-12 months", "grade": "B", "direction": "benefit", "effect_summary": "MPOD and serum L/Z rise consistently; serum BDNF rose and IL-1 beta fell in one trial; BOLD changes in one fMRI trial.", "effect_estimate": "MPOD 0.47 to 0.56 in 12 months (active) vs 0.40 to 0.44 (placebo) [renzihammond2017]; BDNF change correlated with memory r=0.32 [stringham2019]", "certainty_notes": "Biomarker only (MPOD robust; BDNF/fMRI single trials); not a cognitive outcome.", "key_study_ids": ["renzihammond2017", "stringham2019", "lindbergh2018", "power2018"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "children_adolescents", "population_detail": "Teenagers 13-18 y with high screen use and low fruit/vegetable intake", "dose_or_intake": "10 mg L + 2 mg Z", "duration": "6 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Attention and processing speed improved; reasoning, nonverbal memory, game performance and self-reported attention/sleep did not.", "effect_estimate": "attention p=0.026, processing speed p=0.034; reasoning p=0.728 [lopresti2026]", "certainty_notes": "Single industry-funded trial (Bio-gen Extracts), n=82; do not generalise to adults.", "key_study_ids": ["lopresti2026"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Mild-to-moderate Alzheimer's disease", "dose_or_intake": "22 mg carotenoids + 1 g fish oil + 15 mg vitamin E", "duration": "12 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Informant-rated memory better with multi-nutrient formula; cannot isolate carotenoid effect.", "effect_estimate": "clinical collateral memory p<0.001 [nolan2022]", "certainty_notes": "Unbalanced groups (50 vs 27), combination product, informant outcomes; not applicable to healthy adults.", "key_study_ids": ["nolan2022"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_aging", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Male physicians >65 y (beta-carotene)", "dose_or_intake": "50 mg beta-carotene alternate days", "duration": "1 year vs mean 18 years", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "No effect after ~1 year; very small benefit after ~18 years.", "effect_estimate": "global z difference 0.047 SD (P=0.03); verbal memory 0.063 SD (P=0.007) after 18 y [grodstein2007]", "certainty_notes": "Single cognitive assessment at end (no baseline), tiny effect of doubtful practical relevance; beta-carotene harms in smokers [atbc1994].", "key_study_ids": ["grodstein2007", "atbc1994"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "any", "onset": "not applicable", "duration_studied": "none", "domain": "any", "population": "healthy adults", "effect_estimate": "No acute trials identified", "certainty": "D (insufficient)", "safety": "not applicable", "study_ids": []}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "4 months", "population": "young healthy adults (n=69)", "outcome": "temporal contrast sensitivity (visual processing speed), MPOD", "effect_estimate": "Both improved vs placebo (significant)", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "Visual not cognitive outcome; placebo n=15", "study_ids": ["bovier2015"]}, {"duration": "6 months", "population": "young healthy adults 18-25 (n=59)", "outcome": "memory, attention, processing speed, BDNF, IL-1 beta", "effect_estimate": "Multiple domains improved p<0.05; BDNF up", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "Small, multiple outcomes, single network, funding not stated in abstract", "study_ids": ["stringham2019"]}, {"duration": "12 months", "population": "young healthy adults 18-30 (n=51)", "outcome": "CNS Vital Signs domains, MPOD", "effect_estimate": "Visual memory p<0.04 by group; attention/reasoning only in non-randomised responder analysis", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "37 vs 14 allocation; Abbott employee co-author", "study_ids": ["renzihammond2017"]}, {"duration": "12 months", "population": "adults with low MPOD, mean 45 y (n=91)", "outcome": "paired-associate learning", "effect_estimate": "PAL score p=0.009, errors p=0.017", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "Single trial, many tests", "study_ids": ["power2018"]}, {"duration": "5 years", "population": "older adults at AMD risk (n=3501)", "outcome": "composite cognitive decline", "effect_estimate": "Difference 0.03 (99% CI -0.14 to 0.19), null", "evidence_quality": "B (null, older adults)", "limitations": "Telephone battery; all received background AREDS antioxidants", "study_ids": ["chew2015"]}, {"duration": "18 years", "population": "male physicians", "outcome": "global cognition (beta-carotene)", "effect_estimate": "+0.047 SD", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "No baseline cognition; tiny effect", "study_ids": ["grodstein2007"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Lutein and zeaxanthin at 10-27 mg/day have been well tolerated in trials up to 5 years (AREDS2) without signals. EFSA set an ADI of 1 mg/kg bw/day for lutein colour (E 161b) and considered synthetic zeaxanthin safe at 2 mg/day in supplements; JECFA set a group ADI of 0-2 mg/kg bw. Beta-carotene supplements (20-30 mg/day) increased lung cancer and mortality in smokers and asbestos-exposed workers and must be excluded. High carotenoid intake can cause harmless carotenodermia.", "adverse_effects": ["carotenodermia (yellow skin) at high intakes, reversible", "beta-carotene supplements: increased lung cancer and mortality in smokers [atbc1994]"], "contraindications": ["beta-carotene supplements in current/former smokers and asbestos-exposed people"], "drug_interactions": ["fat-absorption inhibitors (orlistat), bile acid sequestrants and plant sterols may reduce carotenoid absorption", "no clinically important drug interactions established for lutein"], "upper_limits": "No tolerable upper intake level. EFSA ADI for lutein (Tagetes erecta, >=80% carotenoids) 1 mg/kg bw/day (about 70 mg/day for 70 kg); synthetic zeaxanthin 2 mg/day in supplements judged safe (EFSA 2012); JECFA group ADI 0-2 mg/kg bw.", "sleep_impact": "None known; one 2017 trial in high-screen-use adults reported sleep effects but not reviewed here; teenager trial found no sleep change [lopresti2026].", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Up to 5 years at 12 mg/day (AREDS2) without safety signal.", "daily_use_appropriate": "yes", "special_populations": "Pregnancy/lactation: dietary sources fine; supplement data limited. Smokers: avoid beta-carotene supplements. Children: one trial at 12 mg/day without safety concerns but not a reason to supplement.", "specialist_review_needed": ["food toxicologist for fortification levels vs ADI", "regulatory specialist for EU novel food status of meso-zeaxanthin"], "hard_safety_gate": "pass", "gate_reason": "Lutein/zeaxanthin at food and studied supplement doses are low-risk; gate passes on condition that beta-carotene supplementation is excluded."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "No authorised health claim for lutein or zeaxanthin (eye or cognition) in the EU register; cognitive claims are therefore prohibited. Lutein is an authorised colour (E 161b); lutein from Tagetes erecta used in supplements; synthetic zeaxanthin authorised as novel food in supplements up to 2 mg/day (EFSA 2012 opinion; Commission Implementing Decision 2013/49/EU per EUR-Lex search result). Meso-zeaxanthin novel food status: uncertain, not verified.", "us": "Lutein (marigold-derived) and zeaxanthin have GRAS notices with FDA no-questions letters (e.g., GRN 000639 surfaced in search; details not checked). Supplements fall under DSHEA; structure/function claims need FTC 'competent and reliable scientific evidence'; current cognitive evidence is weak.", "morocco": "Uncertain: food supplements and fortified foods fall under ONSSA (Law 28-07 framework); no Moroccan list of authorised carotenoid claims identified; specialist confirmation needed.", "permitted_claims_examples": [], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "Synthetic zeaxanthin is an EU novel food (2 mg/day supplements); meso-zeaxanthin status in the EU uncertain; lutein from Tagetes erecta is established.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Good as a whole-food strategy (greens, eggs, avocado, maize); as an isolated ingredient lutein is oil-soluble and colours products yellow-orange.", "formats": ["leafy-green-rich meals", "eggs", "beadlet or oil suspension in fat-containing foods", "softgel supplements"], "stability": "Sensitive to light, oxygen and heat; microencapsulated beadlets improve stability.", "taste": "Neutral at 10 mg; strong yellow colour.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Requires dietary fat; egg yolk and avocado raise absorption (eggs.json, avocado.json); ester vs free forms similar with fat.", "dose_feasibility": "10-12 mg/day equals about one large serving of cooked spinach/kale (dietary composition data not re-checked here); eggs alone supply <1 mg per egg, so trial doses need greens or supplements.", "cost_availability": "Widely available, inexpensive commodity ingredient (marigold extract).", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 3, "effect_magnitude": 2, "safety": 9, "reproducibility": 2, "food_suitability": 7, "cost_access": 8, "rationale": "Evidence quality 3: grade C for chronic memory/attention in young adults (small, single-network, industry-linked trials) and largest trial null. Effect magnitude 2: no standardised effects reported; meta-analysis SMD ~0.0-0.13. Safety 9: well tolerated up to 5 years; minus 1 for beta-carotene harm in smokers. Reproducibility 2: positive findings not replicated independently; AREDS2 null. Food suitability 7: abundant in common foods; colour and fat requirement for isolated ingredient. Cost/access 8: cheap and widely available."}, "research_gaps": ["Independent, preregistered, adequately powered (n>=200) RCT in healthy students with prespecified primary cognitive outcome and multiplicity control", "Dose-response linking change in MPOD or brain lutein (MRS not available) to cognition", "Food-based (greens/eggs) versus supplement trials on cognition", "Replication of BDNF/IL-1 beta findings by independent laboratories", "Results of registered trials (e.g., NCT05177679 children, NCT06489873) once available"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 4.8, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "pass", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": "C", "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": "B", "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"episodic_memory": "C", "processing_speed": "C", "sustained_attention": "C", "global_cognition": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 10, "n_studies": 14, "n_verified": 14, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 4, "n_systematic_reviews": 1, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["benefit", "inconsistent", "insufficient", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "olive-polyphenols", "name": "Olive polyphenols (hydroxytyrosol, oleuropein) and extra-virgin olive oil", "category": "G", "category_name": "Polyphenols and plant bioactives", "aliases": ["hydroxytyrosol", "oleuropein", "olive leaf extract", "high-phenolic early-harvest extra-virgin olive oil (HP-EH-EVOO)", "olive fruit/leaf polyphenol concentrate", "desert olive tree pearls (hydroxytyrosol-concentrated supplement)"], "food_sources": ["high-phenolic extra-virgin olive oil (early-harvest, cold-extracted)", "table olives", "olive leaf (tea/extract, not a conventional food)", "concentrated olive-fruit-water/juice polyphenol supplements"], "active_constituents": ["hydroxytyrosol (phenolic alcohol)", "oleuropein and its aglycone (secoiridoid)", "tyrosol", "oleocanthal and oleacein (minor secoiridoid derivatives, mentioned for completeness)"], "one_line_verdict": "Isolated olive polyphenols (hydroxytyrosol/oleuropein, as opposed to whole extra-virgin olive oil as food) have a plausible, EFSA-recognised antioxidant mechanism and two small positive pilot trials in older or middle-aged adults, but there is no trial of isolated hydroxytyrosol/oleuropein on cognition in healthy young adults, the strongest cognitive signals come from whole-oil PREDIMED substudies already covered under olive-oil-as-food, and the isolated-polyphenol cognitive literature remains grade C/D and awaiting a larger registered trial that has not yet reported results.", "scientific_conclusion": "Scope and overlap. This monograph covers isolated or concentrated hydroxytyrosol/oleuropein preparations and olive-oil phenolic-content comparisons for cognition; the whole-food Mediterranean-diet-plus-EVOO cognitive trials (PREDIMED-Navarra [martinezlapiscina2013], PREDIMED-Barcelona [vallspedret2015]) are already fully analysed in research/monographs_raw/olive-oil-as-food.json and mediterranean-diet.json and are referenced here only as background context, not re-graded, to avoid double-counting the same evidence under two ingredient IDs. Isolated/concentrated hydroxytyrosol trials. A randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial in middle-aged and older Japanese adults (n=72, 51-82y) tested 'desert olive tree pearls' containing hydroxytyrosol at roughly 162 times the concentration found in olive oil, dosed at 3 g twice daily in an olive-oil vehicle for 12 weeks; a significant time-by-group interaction was found only for complex attention (p=0.049), with several domains (psychomotor speed, reaction time, cognitive flexibility, processing speed, executive function) showing significant time effects but not clearly isolated group-by-time interactions beyond the single significant domain reported [yoon2023]. This is the only trial identified that isolates a concentrated hydroxytyrosol-type ingredient (rather than whole EVOO) and tests it against placebo for cognition, and its single significant domain out of many tested raises a multiplicity concern; grade C at best, D by a stricter reading given the sparse and partial significance pattern. Comparative phenolic-content olive oil trial (MICOIL). The MICOIL pilot (n=50, people with MCI, 12 months) compared high-phenolic early-harvest EVOO (50 mL/day) against moderate-phenolic EVOO (50 mL/day) and Mediterranean-diet advice alone; both EVOO arms outperformed the diet-advice-only arm on ADAS-cog and other measures, and the high-phenolic arm numerically outperformed the moderate-phenolic arm on most domains, offering indirect human evidence that phenolic concentration specifically (not just MUFA/oil per se) tracks with better cognitive outcomes in this small MCI sample [tsolaki2020]. Because MICOIL dosed whole oil rather than isolated polyphenol and is already catalogued under olive-oil-as-food, it is referenced here as supporting but not primary evidence specifically for the isolated-polyphenol question; it is included in studies[] because its between-arm phenolic-dose contrast is directly relevant to whether hydroxytyrosol/oleuropein content itself (as opposed to the oil matrix) drives the effect. Registered but unreported trial. A currently recruiting trial (NCT07672938, n=141 target, started 2025) is testing a hydroxytyrosol-rich olive polyphenol dietary supplement (low- and high-dose versus placebo) combined with Mediterranean diet adherence specifically in people with MCI; results are not yet available, and this is exactly the kind of isolated-polyphenol supplement trial the current literature lacks. A second UK/French-registered trial on olive leaf tea for dementia prevention (NCT04440020, GOLDEN, n=100) has UNKNOWN completion status with no results posted, a publication-bias-relevant gap. Healthy young adults/students. No RCT of isolated hydroxytyrosol, oleuropein, or olive leaf extract on cognition in healthy young adults or students was identified in PubMed or ClinicalTrials.gov searches; the Yoon 2023 trial's youngest participants were 51 years old, and MICOIL and the registered NCT07672938 trial both enrol only MCI populations. This is a clear and acknowledged evidence gap for Brain Food's primary target demographic. EFSA mechanism basis. EFSA's authorised claim for olive-oil polyphenols ('olive oil polyphenols contribute to the protection of blood lipids from oxidative stress', requiring at least 5 mg hydroxytyrosol and its derivatives per 20 g of oil) is a cardiovascular/lipid-oxidation claim, not a cognitive claim, and should not be extended to brain outcomes without separate substantiation. Morocco relevance. Moroccan Picholine marocaine olive oils have been reported with substantial secoiridoid/phenolic content (as documented in olive-oil-as-food.json's Bouymajane 2020 citation), making Morocco a plausible sourcing base for a high-phenolic product, but no Moroccan cognitive trial exists and this is a formulation/sourcing point, not an efficacy claim. Overall, isolated olive-polyphenol cognitive evidence is thinner, smaller, and less consistent than the Mediterranean-diet-plus-EVOO whole-food literature, and should not be marketed as equivalent or superior to it until the currently recruiting trial (NCT07672938) reports.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "There is no known deficiency state for hydroxytyrosol or oleuropein; these are non-essential dietary phenolics, not nutrients with a recommended intake. Every positive cognitive signal identified here occurs in middle-aged-to-older adults (one trial from age 51) or in diagnosed MCI, i.e. populations with some degree of age-related or pathological cognitive vulnerability, framing the available benefit as protective/restorative rather than enhancement of an already-optimal healthy young brain. No trial supports using isolated olive polyphenols to enhance cognition, alertness, or memory in healthy, adequately nourished young adults or students; this should be stated plainly in any Brain Food communication rather than inferred from the olive-oil-and-aging literature.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Marketing concentrated hydroxytyrosol/oleuropein supplements as 'brain boosters' for students or young professionals is not supported by any identified human trial in that population. The existing positive data (Yoon 2023, MICOIL) are small, short-to-medium-duration trials in middle-aged-to-older or MCI populations, and the only large, purpose-built isolated-polyphenol cognitive trial (NCT07672938) has not yet reported results, so any current claim of established cognitive benefit for isolated olive polyphenols would outrun the evidence. The EFSA-authorised olive-oil-polyphenol claim covers protection of blood lipids from oxidative stress only (5 mg hydroxytyrosol and derivatives per 20 g oil, EU Regulation 432/2012) and explicitly does not cover cognition; using it to imply a brain benefit would misuse an authorised claim outside its approved scope, which is a regulatory risk in the EU and a likely FTC substantiation problem in the US. The honest position is: olive polyphenols are part of a scientifically credible EVOO-as-food story (see olive-oil-as-food.json) with a modest, population-specific, chronic cognitive-ageing signal, but isolated hydroxytyrosol/oleuropein supplements for cognition in healthy adults are an emerging, unproven category pending the ongoing registered trial.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Hydroxytyrosol and oleuropein are absorbed as phenolic conjugates with established antioxidant and anti-inflammatory activity on circulating lipids (the basis of the EFSA cardiovascular claim); a plausible but only partly human-confirmed extension to the brain involves reduced systemic oxidative stress and vascular protection supporting cerebral perfusion, with amyloid-clearance and blood-brain-barrier mechanisms demonstrated mainly in rodent/cell models and only indirectly suggested by small human EVOO trials catalogued under olive-oil-as-food.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Ingestion of hydroxytyrosol/oleuropein (as isolated extract or concentrated olive-oil phenolic fraction) -> intestinal absorption as glucuronide/sulfate conjugates", "detail": "Dose-dependent urinary/plasma phenolic metabolite increases are documented after both whole high-phenolic EVOO and concentrated extracts; absolute bioavailability is modest and varies by individual gut microbiota.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Circulating phenolic conjugates -> reduced LDL oxidation and systemic oxidative stress markers", "detail": "Basis of the EFSA-authorised olive-oil-polyphenol claim for blood lipid protection (Reg. (EU) 432/2012); a cardiovascular, not cognitive, endpoint.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Reduced oxidative/inflammatory burden and improved vascular function -> hypothesised protection of cerebral perfusion and neuronal resilience with age", "detail": "Indirect support from the MICOIL phenolic-dose gradient (higher phenolic EVOO outperforming moderate-phenolic EVOO) [tsolaki2020] and the complex-attention signal in a concentrated-hydroxytyrosol trial [yoon2023]; direct cerebral blood flow or neuroinflammation biomarkers were not measured in either trial.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Oleuropein/hydroxytyrosol -> reduced amyloid-beta aggregation and improved blood-brain barrier integrity", "detail": "Shown in transgenic mouse models and cell culture; a small human MCI pilot (catalogued under olive-oil-as-food, Kaddoumi 2022) measured blood-brain barrier permeability as a biomarker, not a direct human efficacy finding for isolated hydroxytyrosol.", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Possible direct neuroprotective signalling (Nrf2/antioxidant response pathway activation) in neurons", "detail": "Proposed mechanism from in-vitro and animal neuroscience literature; no human cognitive trial has confirmed this pathway as the mediator of any observed cognitive change.", "evidence_level": "speculative"}], "pathways": ["LDL-oxidation protection and vascular/endothelial function (established for cardiovascular, extrapolated to brain)", "antioxidant/anti-inflammatory systemic modulation", "putative amyloid-clearance and blood-brain-barrier integrity (animal/biomarker)", "Nrf2/antioxidant-response neuronal signalling (speculative)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "middle-aged and older Japanese adults aged 51-82y, not diagnosed with MCI/dementia", "dose_or_intake": "3 g 'desert olive tree pearls' (high-hydroxytyrosol concentrate, ~162x olive oil concentration) twice daily, delivered in olive oil", "duration": "12 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Significant time-by-group interaction for complex attention only; time effects (not clearly isolated to the treatment group) seen across several other domains.", "effect_estimate": "complex attention interaction p=0.049; simple main effect p<0.001 (treatment) vs p=0.572 (placebo); other domain effects described as 'time effects', not clearly attributed to treatment alone", "certainty_notes": "Single trial, multiplicity across many cognitive domains with only one reaching a clear group interaction, no independent replication, population already skews older/middle-aged rather than healthy young adults.", "key_study_ids": ["yoon2023"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_mci", "population_detail": "adults with diagnosed mild cognitive impairment, phenolic-dose comparison within whole EVOO (not isolated polyphenol)", "dose_or_intake": "50 mL/day high-phenolic early-harvest EVOO vs 50 mL/day moderate-phenolic EVOO vs Mediterranean diet advice alone", "duration": "12 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Both EVOO arms outperformed diet-advice-only on cognitive measures including ADAS-cog; high-phenolic arm numerically better than moderate-phenolic arm, suggesting a phenolic-dose gradient within the whole-oil matrix.", "effect_estimate": "ADAS-cog and MMSE differences reported as significant for EVOO arms vs diet-only (p=0.001-0.05 range per domain); exact between-EVOO-arm statistics not separately reported in abstract", "certainty_notes": "Small pilot (n=50 across 3 arms), open-label/non-blinded given oil cannot be fully masked, whole-food intervention so isolated-polyphenol causation cannot be confirmed; already catalogued under olive-oil-as-food and included here only to support the phenolic-dose-gradient argument.", "key_study_ids": ["tsolaki2020"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "healthy young adults/students specifically", "dose_or_intake": "not applicable - no trial identified", "duration": "not applicable", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No RCT of isolated hydroxytyrosol, oleuropein, or olive leaf extract on any cognitive domain in healthy young adults or students exists in the literature searched; the youngest population studied began at age 51 [yoon2023].", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "Complete evidence gap for the primary Brain Food target demographic; direction marked insufficient rather than extrapolated from older-adult data.", "key_study_ids": ["yoon2023"]}], "acute_matrix": [], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "12 weeks", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "outcome": "complex attention (single domain out of a multi-domain battery)", "effect_estimate": "significant time-by-group interaction, p=0.049, in a concentrated hydroxytyrosol product", "evidence_quality": "C - single small trial, multiplicity risk across many tested domains", "limitations": "n=72 total, only one of several tested domains reached a clear interaction, no independent replication, population middle-aged/older rather than young adults", "study_ids": ["yoon2023"]}, {"duration": "12 months", "population": "older_adults_mci", "outcome": "global cognition (ADAS-cog, MMSE and related measures), phenolic-dose comparison", "effect_estimate": "high- and moderate-phenolic EVOO both outperformed diet-advice-only; high-phenolic numerically best", "evidence_quality": "C - small pilot, whole-oil (not isolated polyphenol) intervention, open-label", "limitations": "n=50 across 3 arms, unblinded oil administration, cannot isolate the polyphenol fraction's contribution from the oil's MUFA/caloric matrix", "study_ids": ["tsolaki2020"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Hydroxytyrosol/oleuropein at the concentrated doses studied (up to 6 g/day of a 162x-concentrated hydroxytyrosol product, or whole high-phenolic EVOO at 50 mL/day) were well tolerated in the cited trials up to 12 months, with no serious adverse events reported; the main practical caution for concentrated extracts (as opposed to whole-food EVOO) is the relative lack of long-term human exposure data outside these pilot trials.", "adverse_effects": ["no significant adverse events reported in the cited concentrated-hydroxytyrosol trial [yoon2023]", "no adverse events reported in the MICOIL whole-oil comparison trial [tsolaki2020]", "gastrointestinal intolerance to high-polyphenol/bitter olive extracts is anecdotally reported in the broader olive-polyphenol supplement literature but was not specifically quantified in the cited cognition trials"], "contraindications": ["no specific contraindications established for hydroxytyrosol/oleuropein at studied doses; general caution for any concentrated plant phenolic in people with known salicylate or phenolic sensitivity is prudent but not trial-confirmed here"], "drug_interactions": ["theoretical mild antiplatelet/antioxidant interaction potential with anticoagulant medication, by analogy with other polyphenols, not directly demonstrated as an adverse event in the cited trials", "olive oil itself can affect absorption of fat-soluble medications when taken as a concentrated dose; not studied specifically for hydroxytyrosol supplements"], "upper_limits": "No formal upper intake level is established for hydroxytyrosol or oleuropein; EFSA's claim substantiation is based on a minimum effective content (5 mg hydroxytyrosol and derivatives per 20 g olive oil) rather than a maximum safe intake, and the highest studied dose here (concentrated hydroxytyrosol product, 6 g/day of concentrate) was tolerated over 12 weeks without reported harm.", "sleep_impact": "Not studied in the cited trials; no stimulant or sedative mechanism is expected from hydroxytyrosol/oleuropein at food-relevant doses.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Longest controlled exposure for an isolated/concentrated hydroxytyrosol product identified is 12 weeks [yoon2023]; the whole-oil MICOIL trial extended to 12 months [tsolaki2020] but that is olive oil as food, already covered under olive-oil-as-food's longer safety record (EVOO has a long history of dietary use).", "daily_use_appropriate": "probably", "special_populations": "Pregnant/breastfeeding individuals, children, and people with diagnosed bleeding disorders or on anticoagulant therapy were not represented in the cited cognition trials; no specific red flag was identified, but data are too sparse to confirm safety in these groups for concentrated extracts specifically (as distinct from dietary olive oil, which is routinely consumed by these groups as food).", "specialist_review_needed": ["clinical pharmacologist on any theoretical antiplatelet/anticoagulant interaction for concentrated hydroxytyrosol supplements (as distinct from dietary olive oil)", "food scientist/toxicologist on appropriate upper intake guidance for concentrated (non-oil-matrix) hydroxytyrosol products, since EFSA's existing substantiation is for whole olive oil, not isolated extract supplements"], "hard_safety_gate": "pass", "gate_reason": "No serious adverse events reported in any cited trial of concentrated hydroxytyrosol or high-phenolic olive oil at studied doses up to 12 months; residual uncertainty concerns long-term and special-population data for concentrated extract forms specifically, not whole-food olive oil."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "EFSA has authorised a specific claim under Regulation (EU) 432/2012: 'Olive oil polyphenols contribute to the protection of blood lipids from oxidative stress,' conditional on the oil supplying at least 5 mg hydroxytyrosol and its derivatives (e.g. oleuropein complex, tyrosol) per 20 g. This claim is cardiovascular/lipid-oxidation specific and explicitly does not cover cognition; no EFSA cognitive health claim exists for hydroxytyrosol or oleuropein. Concentrated hydroxytyrosol extracts sold as standalone supplements (rather than as part of olive oil) may fall under novel food assessment depending on extraction method and prior-market history and should be checked against the EU Novel Food Catalogue before formulation.", "us": "No FDA-authorised or qualified health claim exists for hydroxytyrosol/oleuropein and cognition. FDA has previously issued a qualified health claim for olive oil's monounsaturated fat and reduced coronary heart disease risk (2004), unrelated to cognition or to the phenolic fraction specifically. Concentrated hydroxytyrosol/oleuropein ingredients marketed as dietary supplements fall under DSHEA structure/function claim rules (FTC substantiation required) and, if not a pre-1994 ingredient or already GRAS/NDI-notified in this concentrated form, may require an NDI notification before US marketing.", "morocco": "Olive oil and olive-derived products are an established traditional food category in Morocco (regulated by ONSSA for food safety); concentrated polyphenol extracts sold as supplements would require ONSSA food-supplement registration, and no Moroccan-specific cognitive health claim framework exists. Moroccan Picholine marocaine olive oil's documented phenolic content (per olive-oil-as-food.json's cited sourcing data) is a potential sourcing advantage for a high-phenolic product but confers no regulatory claim advantage by itself.", "permitted_claims_examples": ["EU: 'Olive oil polyphenols contribute to the protection of blood lipids from oxidative stress' (only for oil providing >=5 mg hydroxytyrosol and derivatives per 20 g, with the required 20 g/day consumption information) - cardiovascular claim only, not usable for a cognition-framed product without separate substantiation"], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "Whole high-phenolic EVOO is not a novel food. Concentrated/isolated hydroxytyrosol or oleuropein extracts, especially non-oil-matrix powder/capsule forms (e.g. the 'desert olive tree pearls' product or standalone hydroxytyrosol concentrates), may require EU novel food authorisation or US NDI notification depending on manufacturing process and documented pre-1997 (EU) or pre-1994 (US) market history; this should be confirmed ingredient-by-ingredient with the specific supplier before formulation.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "High-phenolic EVOO integrates naturally as a culinary fat (see olive-oil-as-food.json for full food-format treatment); isolated/concentrated hydroxytyrosol or oleuropein extracts are supplement-format ingredients (capsules, powders, or olive-oil-suspended concentrates) and are harder to incorporate directly into a conventional food product due to bitterness and stability concerns at concentrated doses.", "formats": ["high-phenolic early-harvest EVOO as a culinary oil (food format)", "concentrated hydroxytyrosol/oleuropein capsules or powders (supplement format)", "olive-oil-suspended hydroxytyrosol concentrate (as in the Yoon 2023 product) - a hybrid format", "olive leaf extract/tea (less food-integrable, bitter)"], "stability": "Hydroxytyrosol and oleuropein degrade with heat, light, and extended storage; high-phenolic EVOO requires early-harvest, cold-extraction, and careful storage (dark glass, limited air exposure) to retain the phenolic content on which any claim or effect depends. Concentrated extracts require standardisation and stability testing to guarantee labelled hydroxytyrosol content over shelf life.", "taste": "Hydroxytyrosol/oleuropein and high-phenolic EVOO have a characteristic bitterness and pungency (often described as a 'peppery' throat sensation from oleocanthal-related compounds); this is a desirable quality marker to some consumers but a formulation challenge for broad palatability, and concentrated extracts are typically encapsulated to avoid taste issues entirely.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Hydroxytyrosol bioavailability appears improved when delivered within an oil matrix (as in the Yoon 2023 'pearls in olive oil' format and whole EVOO) versus a dry powder, though head-to-head human bioavailability comparisons specific to cognition outcomes were not identified in this search; oleuropein requires hydrolysis to its aglycone/hydroxytyrosol forms for much of its absorption.", "dose_feasibility": "The studied cognitive-signal dose in the concentrated trial (6 g/day of a 162x-concentrated hydroxytyrosol product) is a supplement-level dose not easily replicated by eating olive oil alone (50 mL/day high-phenolic EVOO, as in MICOIL, is already a large culinary serving); a food-format product aiming for the MICOIL-type dose would need to supply roughly 3-4 tablespoons of genuinely high-phenolic oil daily, which is feasible as a stated serving but caloric (~450 kcal) and requires verified-phenolic-content sourcing.", "cost_availability": "Genuinely high-phenolic, early-harvest EVOO is a premium product commanding a price premium over standard olive oil; concentrated hydroxytyrosol/oleuropein extracts are specialty supplement raw materials, generally more expensive per effective dose than whole oil and subject to supplier-specific standardisation quality.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 3, "effect_magnitude": 2.5, "safety": 8, "reproducibility": 2.5, "food_suitability": 6, "cost_access": 5, "rationale": "Evidence_quality (3/10): grade C/D evidence - a single small concentrated-hydroxytyrosol trial with one significant domain out of several tested [yoon2023], plus indirect support from a whole-oil phenolic-dose-gradient pilot [tsolaki2020] that is already scored under olive-oil-as-food; no trial exists in healthy young adults. Effect_magnitude (2.5/10): where positive, effects are confined to a single cognitive domain in a multi-domain battery, a classic multiplicity-risk pattern, not a broad or large effect. Safety (8/10): no serious adverse events reported up to 12 months for either concentrated extract or whole high-phenolic oil, consistent with a 'pass' hard safety gate, though long-term concentrated-extract-specific data remain sparse. Reproducibility (2.5/10): single small trials with no independent replication for the isolated-polyphenol question; the currently recruiting NCT07672938 trial could change this once reported. Food_suitability (6/10): high-phenolic EVOO is food-integrable but concentrated extracts are supplement-format and bitter. Cost_access (5/10): genuinely high-phenolic EVOO and standardised hydroxytyrosol extracts both carry a price premium over commodity olive oil."}, "research_gaps": ["No RCT of isolated hydroxytyrosol, oleuropein, or olive leaf extract on cognition in healthy young adults or students exists; the youngest population tested began at age 51 [yoon2023].", "The currently recruiting NCT07672938 trial (hydroxytyrosol-rich olive polyphenol supplement in MCI, n=141 target) is the most directly relevant ongoing study and should be re-checked for results before any supplement-specific claim is finalised.", "No trial has isolated the polyphenol fraction from the oil matrix in a blinded, controlled way in humans for cognition; MICOIL compared phenolic concentration levels but could not blind the oil itself, and no trial has compared pure hydroxytyrosol/oleuropein capsules against an oil-matched placebo without any oil vehicle confound.", "The GOLDEN olive-leaf dementia-prevention trial (NCT04440020) has an unknown/unreported outcome years after its scheduled completion, a publication-bias-relevant gap that should be monitored.", "No dose-response data exist for isolated hydroxytyrosol on any cognitive domain; the single significant finding in Yoon 2023 (complex attention) has not been replicated at a different dose or in an independent cohort.", "No human trial has directly compared Moroccan (Picholine marocaine) high-phenolic olive oil or extract against other-origin high-phenolic oils for cognitive outcomes; any sourcing-based differentiation claim is currently unsupported by trial data specific to origin."], "_derived": {"composite_score": 4.35, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "pass", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": null, "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"sustained_attention": "C", "global_cognition": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 3, "n_studies": 4, "n_verified": 2, "n_unverified": 2, "n_null_negative": 1, "n_systematic_reviews": 0, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["insufficient", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "other-berry-polyphenols", "name": "Other berry polyphenols (strawberry, blackcurrant, tart cherry, cranberry)", "category": "G", "category_name": "Polyphenols and plant bioactives", "aliases": ["strawberry (Fragaria x ananassa) freeze-dried powder", "blackcurrant (Ribes nigrum), New Zealand blackcurrant extract (e.g. CurraNZ), 'Blackadder' cultivar juice", "tart cherry / sour cherry (Prunus cerasus, Montmorency) juice or concentrate", "cranberry (Vaccinium macrocarpon) juice, powder, proanthocyanidins", "bilberry (Vaccinium myrtillus) extract", "purified bilberry-blackcurrant anthocyanins (e.g. Medox)", "aronia / chokeberry (Aronia melanocarpa) extract", "mixed berries"], "food_sources": ["strawberries: 1-2 cups fresh; 13-24 g/day freeze-dried powder in trials [krikorian2023, miller2021]", "blackcurrants: juices standardised to about 500 mg polyphenols per serving; NZ blackcurrant extract capsules 250-600 mg/day", "tart cherries: Montmorency juice (480 mL/day diluted from 68 mL concentrate) or 60 mL concentrate", "cranberries: freeze-dried powder equivalent to about 100 g fresh (1 cup); juices (usually sweetened) at about 27% juice", "bilberries (European wild blueberries) and bilberry extracts", "aronia berries and extracts", "raspberries, blackberries, elderberries, lingonberries (in mixed-berry products)"], "active_constituents": ["anthocyanins (pelargonidin glycosides in strawberry; delphinidin and cyanidin rutinosides in blackcurrant; cyanidin glycosides in cherry; cyanidin/peonidin galactosides and arabinosides in cranberry; delphinidin-rich profile in bilberry)", "proanthocyanidins (notably A-type in cranberry)", "ellagitannins and ellagic acid (strawberry, raspberry)", "flavonols (quercetin, myricetin)", "hydroxycinnamic acids", "other bioactives proposed: sarmentosin and related compounds in blackcurrant (MAO inhibition), melatonin in tart cherry (not reviewed here)", "phenolic acid metabolites from gut microbial catabolism"], "one_line_verdict": "Strawberry, blackcurrant, tart cherry, cranberry and bilberry products have only small, mostly single-laboratory and often industry-funded trials with scattered positive secondary outcomes, while the larger and better-controlled trials (purified bilberry-blackcurrant anthocyanins in 206 at-risk adults, blackcurrant in 110 older adults, cranberry in 72 students) were null on their primary outcomes, so none is supported as a cognitive enhancer for students or young workers.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. Across non-blueberry berries the human literature is fragmented by fruit, form and population. The best-designed trials are null on their primary outcomes: 320 mg/day purified bilberry-blackcurrant anthocyanins for 24 weeks in 206 adults with MCI or cardiometabolic risk did not change episodic memory (adjusted difference 1.4 on a 0-100 scale, 95% CI -0.9 to 3.7, effect size 0.15), although slopes diverged after week 8 [aarsland2023]; a 24-week three-arm trial of a freeze-dried blackcurrant product (250 mg anthocyanins) or a high-anthocyanin diet in 110 older adults with memory complaints found no effect on any primary or secondary outcome [dorosario2026]; and 12 weeks of a cranberry drink in 72 final-year university students did not improve mood, stress, anxiety or depression, with only exploratory gains in short-term memory and lower cortisol [kamarunzaman2026]. A meta-analysis of chronic fruit trials concluded that berries, cherries and citrus did not sufficiently improve cognition or mood; only cherry juice showed a borderline effect on psychomotor speed (SMD -0.37, -0.74 to 0.01) [wang2023]. Positive small trials. In healthy older adults, 24 g/day freeze-dried strawberry improved word recognition and spatial navigation latency (n=37) [miller2021]; freeze-dried cranberry improved one visual episodic memory score and regional perfusion (n=60, Cranberry Institute-funded) [flanagan2022]; tart cherry juice improved paired-associate learning and movement time (n=37) [chai2019]; a mixed berry drink improved working memory over 5 weeks (n=40) [nilsson2017]. These sit alongside null trials of cranberry juice (n=50, Bonferroni-corrected) [crews2005] and NZ blackcurrant in older adults [cook2020], and the strawberry trial in midlife adults with subjective decline was null on all four cognitive domains, with benefit only on intrusion errors and depressive symptoms [krikorian2023]. In mild-to-moderate dementia, cherry juice improved verbal fluency and memory in 49 patients [kent2017]; this cannot be generalised. Healthy young adults and students. Acute blackcurrant studies from one New Zealand-UK collaboration report better attention and MAO-B inhibition [watson2015], EEG changes in 9 people [watson2019] and increased prefrontal oxygenation without cognitive change in 20 people [watson2026]. Tart cherry concentrate changed cerebral haemoglobin but not cognition [keane2016]. One week of aronia extract shortened movement time by 12 ms without memory or executive effects [ahles2024]; 7 days of blackcurrant extract improved Stroop scores in resistance-trained adults [koozehchian2026]; a mixed-berry smoothie maintained executive accuracy across a 6-h day [whyte2019]. Mechanism versus efficacy. Blackcurrant inhibits platelet MAO-B within an hour [watson2020] and several berries increase cerebral or prefrontal blood flow, but these biomarkers have not translated into consistent cognitive gains. Observationally, strawberry intake was weakly associated with slower decline in older women (0.03 SU, 0.00-0.06) [devore2012]. Bottom line: whole berries are healthy foods; the cognitive evidence is grade C at best and the most rigorous trials are null.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Berry polyphenols are non-essential, so every cognitive claim is an enhancement or prevention claim; there is no deficiency to correct. Several trials propose that people with low habitual polyphenol intake or with existing decline respond more, but the larger trials in at-risk older adults were null [aarsland2023, dorosario2026] and the authors of the latter suggested their participants were too healthy to respond. The only clearly positive clinical trial is in established dementia [kent2017] and must not be generalised to healthy adults. Evidence in students and young adults is acute or short-term (1 day to 1 week) and measures attention, reaction time or blood flow rather than learning or academic outcomes [watson2015, ahles2024, whyte2019]; the only chronic student trial was null on its primary outcome [kamarunzaman2026].", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Products marketed as 'brain berries', blackcurrant 'Neuroberry' juices, tart cherry for 'focus' or cranberry for 'mental health' rely on mechanistic data (MAO-B inhibition, cerebral blood flow, BDNF) and on small trials with one positive secondary outcome among many. The evidence supports at most: 'eating berries as part of a healthy diet' and, for cranberry, a narrowly qualified US claim on recurrent urinary tract infection in women that has nothing to do with cognition. No cognition, memory, mood or attention claim for these berries is authorised in the EU, and EFSA refused even a cranberry urinary-tract claim in 2025 for insufficient evidence. In the US, FTC substantiation for cognitive claims in healthy adults is lacking. Industry funding is pervasive (California Strawberry Commission, Cranberry Institute, British Summer Fruits Association, extract suppliers BioActor, Evonik/MedPalett, Plant & Food Research). The honest message is culinary and dietary, not nootropic.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Like blueberry anthocyanins, these polyphenols are poorly absorbed intact and extensively metabolised to phenolic acids by phase II enzymes and gut microbes. Proposed brain-relevant actions include endothelial and cerebrovascular effects (increased cerebral or prefrontal oxygenated haemoglobin and perfusion), reversible inhibition of monoamine oxidase-B (blackcurrant, reportedly via sarmentosin), anti-inflammatory and antioxidant signalling, and BDNF changes. In humans these are biomarker-level observations; the link to measured cognition is weak and inconsistent.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Oral berry polyphenols -> limited absorption of parent anthocyanins; phase II and microbial metabolism to phenolic acids", "detail": "Plasma total (poly)phenol metabolites rose by 1.82 +/- 0.57 uM after 12 weeks of cranberry powder [flanagan2022]; cranberry metabolites in plasma and urine changed with supplementation [kamarunzaman2026].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Blackcurrant constituents -> rapid, reversible inhibition of platelet MAO-B (and reported MAO-A) -> altered monoamine signalling", "detail": "Platelet MAO-B inhibition was fast, absolute and reversible after 500 mg polyphenol juice, with delayed lower prolactin, in 8 men [watson2020]; MAO inhibition also reported in [watson2015]. Brain MAO effects not measured.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Vascular effects -> cerebral and prefrontal haemodynamics", "detail": "Blackcurrant juice increased left prefrontal oxygenated haemoglobin during tasks [watson2026]; tart cherry raised total and oxygenated haemoglobin and lowered SBP by up to 6 mmHg acutely [keane2016]; cranberry increased regional perfusion in entorhinal cortex, accumbens and caudate [flanagan2022].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Neurotrophic and anti-inflammatory signalling (BDNF, cytokines)", "detail": "Serum BDNF +5.7% after 1 week aronia [ahles2024] but unchanged after 12 weeks cranberry [flanagan2022]; CRP and IL-6 unchanged with cherry juice [kent2017]. Most supporting data are from animals.", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Stress physiology", "detail": "Diurnal salivary cortisol area under the curve fell after 12 weeks of cranberry drink in students, without change in self-reported stress [kamarunzaman2026].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Measured human cognitive outcome", "detail": "Scattered single-trial benefits on secondary measures; primary outcomes null in the largest trials [aarsland2023, dorosario2026, kamarunzaman2026]; meta-analysis not supportive [wang2023].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["endothelial function and cerebrovascular haemodynamics", "monoamine oxidase-B (and A) inhibition (blackcurrant)", "gut microbial catabolism to phenolic acids", "HPA-axis / cortisol modulation (exploratory)", "BDNF and anti-inflammatory signalling (largely animal data)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults 18-35 y given a single blackcurrant juice or extract", "dose_or_intake": "Blackcurrant juice or extract standardised to about 500 mg polyphenols", "duration": "single dose, testing about 1-3 h", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "A 36-person crossover reported cognitive (attention) benefits and MAO-B/MAO-A inhibition [watson2015]; a 9-person pilot reported EEG changes and a small acute increase in digit-vigilance reaction times [watson2019]; a 20-person crossover found increased prefrontal oxygenation but no cognitive effect [watson2026].", "effect_estimate": "Not reported as standardised effects; cognition null in [watson2026]", "certainty_notes": "One research collaboration with links to the cultivar developer (Plant & Food Research, NZ); tiny samples; outcome-switching risk; [watson2015] known only from a secondary summary.", "key_study_ids": ["watson2015", "watson2019", "watson2026"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults 20-30 y over a 6-h test day", "dose_or_intake": "400 mL smoothie of equal blueberry, strawberry, raspberry, blackberry", "duration": "single dose, tests at 2, 4, 6 h", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Berry group maintained executive accuracy at 6 h while placebo declined, and responded faster at 2-4 h [whyte2019].", "effect_estimate": "MANT accuracy at 6 h p=0.003; RT at 4 h p=0.001; no SMD", "certainty_notes": "Single-blind, between-subjects (20 per group), industry-sponsored (British Summer Fruits Association), many post hoc contrasts; protects against decline rather than enhancing.", "key_study_ids": ["whyte2019"]}, {"timeframe": "short_term_days", "domain": "reaction_time", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy young adults (mean 25 y) and resistance-trained adults", "dose_or_intake": "Aronia extract 180 mg anthocyanins/day; NZ blackcurrant extract 250 or 600 mg/day", "duration": "7 days", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Aronia shortened five-choice movement time by 12 ms (4.8%) with no change in memory or executive function [ahles2024]; blackcurrant improved Stroop scores versus placebo at the low dose and on Color-Word only at the high dose [koozehchian2026].", "effect_estimate": "Movement time -12 ms (p<0.05) [ahles2024]; Stroop p<0.05 vs placebo [koozehchian2026]", "certainty_notes": "Small crossover trials; first is by an extract-company employee; second has inconsistent dose pattern and exercise-focused design. A 12-ms change has no practical relevance for a student.", "key_study_ids": ["ahles2024", "koozehchian2026"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "students", "population_detail": "Healthy final-year university students 20-25 y", "dose_or_intake": "Daily (poly)phenol-rich cranberry drink", "duration": "12 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "No effect on the primary outcome (Total Mood Disturbance) or on perceived stress, anxiety or depression; diurnal cortisol AUC lower (p=0.010) in exploratory analysis [kamarunzaman2026].", "effect_estimate": "Primary outcome NS; effect sizes not in abstract", "certainty_notes": "Single, preregistered, double-blind trial of n=72; adequate for a null on self-reported mood but imprecise.", "key_study_ids": ["kamarunzaman2026"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "students", "population_detail": "Healthy final-year university students 20-25 y", "dose_or_intake": "Daily (poly)phenol-rich cranberry drink", "duration": "12 weeks", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "Exploratory secondary analyses found better short-term memory (p=0.024) and phonological short-term memory (p=0.014) on an online battery (CogniFit) [kamarunzaman2026].", "effect_estimate": "p=0.024 and p=0.014; no effect sizes", "certainty_notes": "Exploratory outcomes in a trial null on its primary endpoint; online commercial battery; needs confirmation.", "key_study_ids": ["kamarunzaman2026"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "cerebral_blood_flow", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy young adults 18-35 y (blackcurrant) and middle-aged adults 45-60 y (tart cherry); BIOMARKER (NIRS haemoglobin), not cognition", "dose_or_intake": "Blackcurrant juice 500 mg polyphenols; 60 mL Montmorency tart cherry concentrate", "duration": "single dose, 1-5 h", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Prefrontal oxygenated haemoglobin increased during tasks after blackcurrant [watson2026]; total and oxygenated haemoglobin increased 1 h after tart cherry, with SBP falling up to 6 mmHg [keane2016]. Neither changed cognition.", "effect_estimate": "Deoxy-Hb F(1,19)=5.70, p=0.027; oxy-Hb p=0.002 [watson2026]; SBP -6 +/- 2 mmHg at 1 h [keane2016]", "certainty_notes": "Biomarker only; small samples; NIRS measures superficial cortex; dissociation from cognition in both trials.", "key_study_ids": ["watson2026", "keane2016"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy middle-aged adults 45-60 y (tart cherry) and adults given cherry juice (RAVLT, letter report)", "dose_or_intake": "60 mL tart cherry concentrate; anthocyanin-rich cherry juice", "duration": "single dose, up to 5 h", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Tart cherry did not affect cognitive performance or mood over 5 h (n=27) [keane2016]; a short report found no acute RAVLT improvement with cherry juice [caldwell2016].", "effect_estimate": "not reported (NS)", "certainty_notes": "Small trials; [caldwell2016] details not available to the agent (letter, no abstract).", "key_study_ids": ["keane2016", "caldwell2016"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Cognitively healthy adults 50-80 y", "dose_or_intake": "24 g/day freeze-dried strawberry; freeze-dried cranberry about 100 g fresh-equivalent; 480 mL/day tart cherry juice; cranberry juice cocktail 32 oz/day; mixed berry beverage", "duration": "5-12 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Positive single outcomes: strawberry word recognition and spatial navigation [miller2021]; cranberry Rey figure delayed recall [flanagan2022]; tart cherry paired-associate learning [chai2019]; mixed berries working memory [nilsson2017]. Null: cranberry juice on all tests after correction [crews2005]. Meta-analysis did not support benefit [wang2023].", "effect_estimate": "Strawberry CVLT recognition p=0.014 (partial eta2 0.159) [miller2021]; cranberry Rey figure recall group x time p=0.028 [flanagan2022]; tart cherry PAL errors MD -8.5 (95% CI -12.5 to -4.5) [chai2019]", "certainty_notes": "Downgraded for risk of bias (industry funding, many outcomes, feasibility designs), inconsistency (different tests positive in each trial) and imprecision (n 37-60). Different fruits pooled in one row only because each has 1-2 trials.", "key_study_ids": ["miller2021", "flanagan2022", "chai2019", "nilsson2017", "crews2005", "wang2023"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "processing_speed", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Healthy older adults 65-80 y and mixed adult samples in a meta-analysis of cherry juice trials", "dose_or_intake": "Tart cherry juice 480 mL/day; other cherry juice protocols", "duration": "1-12 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Movement time shortened with tart cherry juice [chai2019]; pooled cherry juice trials showed a borderline improvement in psychomotor speed [wang2023].", "effect_estimate": "SMD -0.37 (95% CI -0.74 to 0.01), p=0.05 [wang2023]; movement time MD -10.4 (-13.4 to -7.5) [chai2019]", "certainty_notes": "Few small trials, CI touches zero, includes athletes and dementia patients in the pooled cherry estimate (indirectness).", "key_study_ids": ["wang2023", "chai2019"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "older_adults_mci", "population_detail": "Adults 60-85 y with MCI, memory complaints or cardiometabolic risk; insulin-resistant midlife adults 50-65 y with SCD", "dose_or_intake": "320 mg/day purified bilberry-blackcurrant anthocyanins; freeze-dried blackcurrant product 250 mg anthocyanins/day or high-anthocyanin diet; 13 g/day freeze-dried strawberry (36.8 mg anthocyanins)", "duration": "12-24 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Primary episodic-memory outcome null with purified anthocyanins (n=206) despite diverging slopes after week 8 [aarsland2023]; no treatment x time effect on any outcome with blackcurrant or diet (n=110) [dorosario2026]; strawberry null on all four cognitive domains, with fewer intrusion errors and lower depressive symptoms (n=30 analysed) [krikorian2023].", "effect_estimate": "Adjusted difference 1.4 points (95% CI -0.9 to 3.7), effect size 0.15, p=0.23 [aarsland2023]; strawberry CVLT intrusions Cohen's f 0.45, p=0.02 [krikorian2023]", "certainty_notes": "Two adequately sized, preregistered trials null on primary outcomes; positive slope and intrusion findings are secondary. Downgraded for imprecision; product supplied by manufacturers in two trials.", "key_study_ids": ["aarsland2023", "dorosario2026", "krikorian2023"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Adults >=70 y with mild-to-moderate dementia (kept separate; not generalisable)", "dose_or_intake": "200 mL/day anthocyanin-rich cherry juice", "duration": "12 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Improved verbal fluency, short-term and long-term memory and lower SBP versus a low-anthocyanin control juice (n=49) [kent2017].", "effect_estimate": "Verbal fluency p=0.014; short-term memory p=0.014; long-term memory p<=0.001; effect sizes not in abstract", "certainty_notes": "Single small trial; not replicated; dementia population cannot inform healthy-adult claims.", "key_study_ids": ["kent2017"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_decline", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Nurses' Health Study women >=70 y (observational)", "dose_or_intake": "Highest vs lowest habitual strawberry intake (cut-offs not extracted)", "duration": "observational; about 6 years of cognitive follow-up", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Higher strawberry intake associated with slightly slower global cognitive decline [devore2012].", "effect_estimate": "Mean difference 0.03 SU (95% CI 0.00-0.06), p-trend 0.022", "certainty_notes": "Observational; residual confounding; CI touches zero; cannot exceed C.", "key_study_ids": ["devore2012"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Systematic reviews pooling adults of all ages and berry types", "dose_or_intake": "Various berries, juices, powders and extracts", "duration": "1 week to 6 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Chronic fruit meta-analysis did not sufficiently support cognitive or mood benefit [wang2023]; a flavonoid meta-analysis found a small berries effect g=0.149 [cheng2022]; narrative reviews conclude memory benefits, including one by an extract-company employee [ahles2021, ellis2024].", "effect_estimate": "Berries g=0.149, p=0.009 [cheng2022]; cherry psychomotor SMD -0.37 (-0.74 to 0.01) [wang2023]", "certainty_notes": "Narrative reviews are more positive than the quantitative synthesis; heterogeneity in products and tests; probable publication bias.", "key_study_ids": ["wang2023", "cheng2022", "ahles2021", "ellis2024"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "children_adolescents", "population_detail": "Healthy children and adolescents", "dose_or_intake": "not studied for these berries", "duration": "no trials", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No trial of strawberry, blackcurrant, tart cherry, cranberry or bilberry on cognition in healthy children was identified (a sour cherry trial in children with ADHD was seen but not reviewed).", "effect_estimate": "not reported (no trials)", "certainty_notes": "Absence of evidence.", "key_study_ids": []}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "Blackcurrant extract or 'Blackadder' juice (doses per paper; about 500 mg polyphenols per 60 kg reported in secondary summaries)", "onset": "within about 1-2 h", "duration_studied": "single session", "domain": "attention, mental fatigue, MAO-B/MAO-A", "population": "healthy young adults 18-35 y (n=36, crossover)", "effect_estimate": "Reported cognitive benefit and MAO inhibition; numbers not extracted", "certainty": "low (secondary source only)", "safety": "not extracted", "study_ids": ["watson2015"]}, {"dose": "Blackcurrant juice, 500 mg polyphenols", "onset": "acute", "duration_studied": "single session", "domain": "attention, mood, EEG", "population": "healthy young adults (n=9, pilot crossover)", "effect_estimate": "EEG spectral changes interpreted as anxiolytic and alerting; small increase in digit-vigilance RT", "certainty": "very low", "safety": "not reported", "study_ids": ["watson2019"]}, {"dose": "Cold-pressed blackcurrant juice, 500 mg polyphenols", "onset": "60 min absorption", "duration_studied": "about 2 h", "domain": "attention, working memory, prefrontal haemodynamics (NIRS)", "population": "healthy young adults 18-35 y (n=20, crossover)", "effect_estimate": "Prefrontal oxy-Hb increased (p=0.002); no cognitive effect", "certainty": "low", "safety": "not reported", "study_ids": ["watson2026"]}, {"dose": "60 mL Montmorency tart cherry concentrate", "onset": "1 h (haemodynamics, BP)", "duration_studied": "5 h", "domain": "cognition, mood, cerebral haemodynamics, BP", "population": "healthy middle-aged adults 45-60 y (n=27, crossover)", "effect_estimate": "Cognition and mood NS; SBP -6 +/- 2 mmHg at 1 h", "certainty": "low-moderate for null", "safety": "not reported", "study_ids": ["keane2016"]}, {"dose": "400 mL mixed-berry smoothie", "onset": "2 h", "duration_studied": "6 h", "domain": "executive function, mood", "population": "healthy young adults 20-30 y (n=40, between-subjects)", "effect_estimate": "Accuracy maintained at 6 h (p=0.003); faster RT at 2-4 h", "certainty": "low", "safety": "not reported", "study_ids": ["whyte2019"]}, {"dose": "Anthocyanin-rich cherry juice (dose not available)", "onset": "acute", "duration_studied": "not available", "domain": "verbal learning (RAVLT)", "population": "not available (letter report)", "effect_estimate": "No improvement (per title)", "certainty": "very low (details unavailable)", "safety": "not available", "study_ids": ["caldwell2016"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "90 days", "population": "healthy adults 60-75 y (n=37)", "outcome": "learning and memory, gait, balance (strawberry 24 g/day)", "effect_estimate": "Spatial navigation latency p=0.020 (partial eta2 0.106); CVLT recognition p=0.014 (0.159); gait/balance null", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Small; many tests; erratum published", "study_ids": ["miller2021"]}, {"duration": "12 weeks", "population": "insulin-resistant adults 50-65 y with SCD (n=30 analysed)", "outcome": "cognitive domains, mood, metabolism (strawberry 13 g/day)", "effect_estimate": "All 4 cognitive domains NS; intrusions f=0.45 (p=0.02); BDI f=0.39 (p=0.04); metabolic NS", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Funded by California Strawberry Commission; sex imbalance (strawberry arm all female)", "study_ids": ["krikorian2023"]}, {"duration": "12 weeks", "population": "healthy adults 50-80 y (n=60)", "outcome": "episodic memory, executive function, perfusion, LDL, BDNF (cranberry powder)", "effect_estimate": "Rey figure delayed recall group x time p=0.028; regional perfusion up; LDL down; BDNF NS", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Feasibility study; Cranberry Institute grant; many outcomes; baseline imbalance in visuospatial score", "study_ids": ["flanagan2022"]}, {"duration": "6 weeks", "population": "cognitively intact adults >=60 y (n=50)", "outcome": "neuropsychological battery (cranberry juice cocktail 32 oz/day)", "effect_estimate": "No group x time interaction after Bonferroni correction", "evidence_quality": "low (pilot)", "limitations": "Short; small; high sugar beverage", "study_ids": ["crews2005"]}, {"duration": "12 weeks", "population": "healthy adults 65-80 y (n=37)", "outcome": "CANTAB, subjective memory (tart cherry juice 480 mL/day)", "effect_estimate": "Memory contentment MD 2.7 (1.2-4.2); movement time MD -10.4 (-13.4 to -7.5); PAL errors MD -8.5 (-12.5 to -4.5)", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Secondary analysis of a BP trial; sugar-matched Kool-Aid control; many outcomes", "study_ids": ["chai2019"]}, {"duration": "12 weeks", "population": "adults >=70 y with mild-to-moderate dementia (n=49)", "outcome": "verbal fluency, memory, BP, CRP, IL-6 (cherry juice 200 mL/day)", "effect_estimate": "Fluency p=0.014; short-term memory p=0.014; long-term memory p<=0.001; SBP reduced", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Clinical population; single small trial", "study_ids": ["kent2017"]}, {"duration": "24 weeks", "population": "adults 60-80 y with MCI or >=2 cardiometabolic disorders (n=206)", "outcome": "Quality of Episodic Memory composite (purified anthocyanins 320 mg/day)", "effect_estimate": "MD 1.4 (95% CI -0.9 to 3.7), ES 0.15, p=0.23; slope difference weeks 8-24 p=0.007", "evidence_quality": "moderate (null primary)", "limitations": "Product donated by manufacturer; slope analysis secondary", "study_ids": ["aarsland2023"]}, {"duration": "24 weeks", "population": "adults 60-85 y with memory complaints (n=110 enrolled, 94 completed)", "outcome": "auditory episodic memory and secondary cognitive, vascular, inflammatory outcomes", "effect_estimate": "No treatment x time effect on any outcome", "evidence_quality": "moderate (null)", "limitations": "Participants relatively healthy; three arms reduce power", "study_ids": ["dorosario2026"]}, {"duration": "12 weeks", "population": "final-year university students 20-25 y (n=72)", "outcome": "Total Mood Disturbance (primary), stress, anxiety, cognition, cortisol (cranberry drink)", "effect_estimate": "Primary and self-report outcomes NS; exploratory short-term memory p=0.024; cortisol AUC p=0.010", "evidence_quality": "moderate for null primary; very low for exploratory", "limitations": "Exploratory cognition on online battery; funding not extracted", "study_ids": ["kamarunzaman2026"]}, {"duration": "5 weeks (crossover)", "population": "healthy adults 50-70 y (n=40)", "outcome": "working memory, selective attention, psychomotor RT, cardiometabolic markers (mixed berry beverage)", "effect_estimate": "Working memory better than control (p<0.05); other cognitive tests NS; LDL lowered", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Control beverage lacked fibre and polyphenols; mixed berries plus tomato", "study_ids": ["nilsson2017"]}, {"duration": "7 days (crossover)", "population": "older adults 69 +/- 4 y (n=14)", "outcome": "CANTAB, BP, 6-min walk (NZ blackcurrant extract 600 mg/day)", "effect_estimate": "Cognition NS; SBP 130 vs 136 mmHg", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Tiny; short", "study_ids": ["cook2020"]}, {"duration": "7 days (crossover)", "population": "healthy young adults, mean 25 y (n=35)", "outcome": "CANTAB, arterial stiffness, retinal vessels, BDNF (aronia extract 180 mg anthocyanins/day)", "effect_estimate": "Movement time -12 ms; memory and executive NS; BDNF +1.8 ng/mL", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Extract-company author; short; BDNF is a peripheral biomarker", "study_ids": ["ahles2024"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Whole strawberries, blackcurrants, cherries, cranberries and bilberries are ordinary foods. Trial products (powders up to 24 g/day, juices up to about 940 mL/day, extracts up to 600 mg/day, purified anthocyanins 320 mg/day for 24 weeks) were well tolerated where reported [aarsland2023, ahles2024, koozehchian2026]. Product-specific issues: (1) cranberry and warfarin - a 7-person RCT found no INR change at 250 mL/day [li2006], but case reports place cranberry among foods with bleeding-risk interactions [tan2021], so INR monitoring is prudent; (2) blackcurrant juices inhibit platelet MAO-B (and reportedly MAO-A) within an hour [watson2020, watson2015], a theoretical interaction with MAO-inhibitor and serotonergic drugs that has not been studied clinically; (3) juices and cocktails carry large sugar and acid loads (two participants reported dental changes in [flanagan2022]); (4) bilberry and other berry extracts are frequently adulterated or under-strength (45% of bilberry supplements unacceptable) [gaspar2021].", "adverse_effects": ["GI upset with large juice volumes or fibre-rich powders (general expectation; not systematically reported)", "Sugar and energy load from juices and sweetened cranberry cocktails", "Dental changes reported by two participants in a 12-week cranberry powder trial [flanagan2022]"], "contraindications": ["Known berry allergy (strawberry allergy is more common than for other berries)", "Concentrated extracts: no safety data in pregnancy, lactation or children"], "drug_interactions": ["Warfarin and other vitamin K antagonists: cranberry products - monitor INR; evidence mixed (RCT null [li2006], case reports and SR listing [tan2021])", "MAO-inhibitor drugs (e.g. selegiline, rasagiline) and serotonergic antidepressants: theoretical additive effect of blackcurrant MAO inhibition [watson2020]; not clinically documented", "Antihypertensive drugs: acute BP lowering observed with tart cherry and blackcurrant [keane2016, cook2020]; additive effect possible"], "upper_limits": "No tolerable upper intake level for berry polyphenols or anthocyanins. Anthocyanins are authorised as food colour E 163. Highest studied exposures: 320 mg/day purified anthocyanins for 24 weeks [aarsland2023]; 32 oz/day cranberry juice cocktail for 6 weeks [crews2005].", "sleep_impact": "No stimulant content. Tart cherry is marketed for sleep (melatonin content) but sleep effects were not reviewed here.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Up to 24 weeks in RCTs (n up to 206); lifelong dietary exposure in cohorts without safety signals.", "daily_use_appropriate": "yes", "special_populations": "Whole fruits suit all ages including pregnancy as part of a normal diet. People on warfarin should keep cranberry intake stable and check INR when starting concentrated products. People on MAO-B inhibitors (Parkinson's disease) or MAOI antidepressants should avoid concentrated blackcurrant products without medical advice. People with diabetes should account for juice sugars. Children: no cognitive trials; no reason to use extracts.", "specialist_review_needed": ["Clinical pharmacist: cranberry-warfarin and blackcurrant-MAOI/serotonergic interaction wording", "Food quality specialist: identity testing (HPLC anthocyanin fingerprint) for bilberry, cranberry and blackcurrant extracts", "Dentist/dietitian input if acidic juices or sweetened cocktails are considered"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "Whole fruits and powders pass; concentrated cranberry products need an anticoagulant caution and blackcurrant extracts a theoretical MAO-inhibitor caution, and any extract requires identity testing because adulteration is common."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "All of these fruits and their juices and powders are conventional foods. No health claim relating any of them, or anthocyanins, to cognition, memory, mood or attention is authorised. EFSA's 2010 general opinion on antioxidant and 'protection of cells from premature ageing' claims found such effects not substantiated (doi 10.2903/j.efsa.2010.1489; title verified; the specific blackcurrant-anthocyanin entries reported in web search were not individually checked). In 2025 EFSA concluded the evidence was insufficient for an Article 13(5) claim on Pacran (a cranberry powder) and defence against bacterial pathogens in the lower urinary tract (EFSA J 2025;23(4):e9319, doi 10.2903/j.efsa.2025.9319). Botanical claims (bilberry, blackcurrant extracts) remain 'on hold' per the X4 EU synthesis; specific listings not verified. Anthocyanins are authorised as colour E 163.", "us": "Conventional foods; extracts (NZ blackcurrant, tart cherry, cranberry, bilberry, aronia) are sold as dietary supplement ingredients under DSHEA; NDI status of specific extracts is uncertain. On 21 July 2020 the FDA announced enforcement discretion for a qualified health claim on cranberry juice beverages (>=27% juice, 8 oz/day) and cranberry dietary supplements (>=500 mg cranberry fruit powder/day) and reduced risk of recurrent UTI in healthy women who have had a UTI (FDA constituent update, accessed 2026-10-25); it does not cover dried cranberries, sauce or any cognitive effect. Cognitive structure/function claims would require competent and reliable evidence, which is lacking for healthy adults.", "morocco": "Uncertain. Fresh and processed berries fall under Law 28-07 (ONSSA) and labelling Decree 2-12-389; disease prevention or treatment claims are banned; no list of authorised health claims or approval procedure was found; supplements are registered through a Ministry of Health committee (now AMMPS) with a pending framework (X6 Morocco synthesis). Any cognition claim, and the US cranberry UTI wording, should be treated as unauthorised in Morocco pending legal review.", "permitted_claims_examples": ["US qualified health claim (FDA enforcement discretion, 21 July 2020), cranberry juice beverages >=27% juice: 'Limited and inconsistent scientific evidence shows that by consuming one serving (8 oz) each day of a cranberry juice beverage, healthy women who have had a urinary tract infection (UTI) may reduce their risk of recurrent UTI.' Not a cognitive claim; wording taken from the FDA page as summarised by the agent's fetch tool - verify exact text before use.", "US qualified health claim (same letter), cranberry dietary supplements >=500 mg fruit powder: 'Limited scientific evidence shows that by consuming 500 mg each day of cranberry dietary supplement, healthy women who have had a urinary tract infection (UTI) may reduce their risk of recurrent UTI.' Not a cognitive claim; verify exact text before use."], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "Whole fruits, juices and conventional powders are not novel foods. Highly purified anthocyanin preparations and new extraction processes should be checked in the EU Novel Food Catalogue (not verified here). Blackcurrant constituents identified as MAO inhibitors (e.g. sarmentosin) would raise novel-food and medicinal-product borderline questions if concentrated or marketed for that action.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Good for whole-fruit and powder formats; studied doses are food portions (13-24 g freeze-dried strawberry; cranberry powder equal to about 100 g fresh; 500 mg-polyphenol blackcurrant juice serving). Tart cherry and cranberry juices at trial volumes (480-940 mL/day) carry substantial sugar and are less suitable for daily use.", "formats": ["freeze-dried strawberry or mixed-berry powder in smoothies, yoghurt, oats", "whole or frozen mixed berries (strawberry, raspberry, blackberry, blackcurrant)", "cold-pressed blackcurrant juice shots", "tart cherry concentrate (small-volume shots)", "unsweetened or low-sugar cranberry powder", "standardised extracts (aronia, blackcurrant, bilberry) in capsules - weaker evidence and adulteration risk"], "stability": "Anthocyanins degrade with heat, oxygen, light and neutral pH; acidic fruits (cranberry, blackcurrant, cherry) are relatively more colour-stable. Juices lose anthocyanins during storage (shown for blueberry products in the companion monograph). Freeze-dried powders need moisture-proof packaging.", "taste": "Strawberry pleasant; blackcurrant intense and tart; tart cherry sour; cranberry very astringent and usually sweetened, which undermines a healthy positioning.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Parent anthocyanins poorly absorbed; phenolic acid metabolites appear in plasma and urine after chronic intake [flanagan2022, kamarunzaman2026]; blackcurrant MAO-B effects appear within 15-60 min [watson2020]. Whole fruit adds fibre, which several placebos did not match.", "dose_feasibility": "Feasible for strawberry (1-2 cups or 13-24 g powder), cranberry powder (1 cup equivalent) and blackcurrant juice (one serving). Tart cherry juice at 480 mL/day is feasible but sugary; extracts at 90-600 mg are capsule-sized but have weaker evidence.", "cost_availability": "Strawberries are a major Moroccan export crop (Loukkos/Larache region); a 2025 Tangier berry seminar cited about 245,000 t of red fruits (blueberries, strawberries, raspberries) for 2024/25, while another count from the same event gave 121,000 t, so figures are inconsistent (maroc.ma and trade press, accessed 2026-10-25). Blackcurrants, tart cherries, cranberries and bilberries are mostly imported into Morocco as processed products (not verified).", "cost_evidence": "not researched here for these berries"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 3, "effect_magnitude": 2, "safety": 8, "reproducibility": 2, "food_suitability": 7, "cost_access": 5, "rationale": "Evidence quality 3: every cognitive row is C or lower; young-adult rows rest on acute or 1-week trials with mostly null cognition, and the only chronic student trial was null on its primary outcome. Effect magnitude 2: positive findings are isolated secondary outcomes (e.g. 12 ms movement time, one memory score); the largest trial's primary effect was 0.15 SD and non-significant. Safety 8: foods are safe; deductions for cranberry-warfarin caution, theoretical blackcurrant MAO interactions, sugar in juices and extract adulteration. Reproducibility 2: each fruit has 1-3 small trials, mostly from single groups with industry funding, and better-powered independent trials are null. Food suitability 7: realistic food doses, but tart cherry and cranberry juices are sugary and cranberry needs sweetening. Cost/access 5: strawberries are cheap and Moroccan; other berries are imported and costs were not researched."}, "research_gaps": ["No adequately powered chronic RCT of any of these berries on cognition in healthy young adults or knowledge workers", "Independent replication of acute blackcurrant attention effects with full reporting (the 2015 trial is not in PubMed)", "Whether blackcurrant MAO inhibition is clinically meaningful for cognition or for drug interactions", "Confirmation of exploratory memory and cortisol effects of cranberry in students", "Head-to-head comparison of fruits at matched anthocyanin and fibre doses", "Strawberry trials in non-industry-funded settings with prespecified primary outcomes", "Bilberry-specific cognitive trials (only a bilberry-blackcurrant purified extract was tested)", "Sleep-mediated effects of tart cherry on next-day cognition (not reviewed here)"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 4.3, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": "C", "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": "C", "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": "C", "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"executive_function": "C", "reaction_time": "C", "processing_speed": "C", "global_cognition": "C", "cognitive_decline": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 14, "n_studies": 28, "n_verified": 28, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 13, "n_systematic_reviews": 4, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["inconsistent", "insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "other-promising-dietary-bioactives", "name": "Other promising dietary bioactives (spearmint extract, saffron, pomegranate, quercetin, ginger, cinnamon, chamomile/apigenin)", "category": "G", "category_name": "Polyphenols and plant bioactives", "aliases": ["spearmint extract (Mentha spicata, Neumentix, rosmarinic acid)", "saffron (Crocus sativus; crocin, safranal)", "pomegranate juice (Punica granatum; punicalagins, ellagitannins, urolithin A)", "quercetin (and isoquercitrin/quercetin glycosides)", "ginger (Zingiber officinale; gingerols)", "cinnamon (Cinnamomum spp.; cinnamaldehyde, coumarin)", "chamomile (Matricaria chamomilla; apigenin)"], "food_sources": ["spearmint leaves and tea (extracts concentrated in rosmarinic acid; the studied product is a proprietary extract)", "saffron stigmas (culinary use ~ tens of mg per dish; Morocco (Taliouine) is a producer)", "pomegranate fruit and juice", "quercetin: onions, apples, capers, kale, tea", "ginger root", "cinnamon bark (cassia high in coumarin; Ceylon low)", "chamomile tea (apigenin glycosides)"], "active_constituents": ["rosmarinic acid and salvianolic acids (spearmint)", "crocins, picrocrocin, safranal (saffron)", "punicalagins, ellagic acid -> urolithins (pomegranate)", "quercetin aglycone and glycosides", "gingerols, shogaols", "cinnamaldehyde, procyanidins, coumarin", "apigenin, bisabolol (chamomile)"], "one_line_verdict": "None of these bioactives has more than limited (grade C) cognitive evidence in healthy adults: spearmint extract has two single-sponsor (Kemin) trials, saffron's evidence is mostly for depressive symptoms and Alzheimer's patients, pomegranate juice prevented decline on one of several memory tests, quercetin was clearly null in 941 adults, ginger and cinnamon have one or no relevant trials, and chamomile acts on anxiety and sleep rather than cognition.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. This monograph groups seven plant bioactives that are marketed for cognition; their evidence must be read item by item. Spearmint extract (900 mg/day proprietary rosmarinic-acid-rich extract): in 90 adults with age-associated memory impairment, quality of working memory improved 15% and spatial working memory accuracy 9% (both p~0.046) at 900 but not 600 mg/day [herrlinger2018]; in 142 healthy active young adults, sustained attention was better at days 30 and 90 and complex attention at day 7 only, among 10 domains [falcone2019]. Both trials were run and funded by the manufacturer, outcomes were multiple and borderline, and there is no independent replication; safety over 90 days was clean [lasrado2017]. Grade C for young-adult attention. Saffron (typically 30 mg/day extract): a 2026 GRADE-assessed meta-analysis of 34 RCTs found reductions in self-rated depression (BDI WMD -4.39) and anxiety (BAI -5.06) with very high heterogeneity (I2 92-95%) and no effect on clinician-rated scales [mahmoudi2026]; a broad 2024 meta-analysis reported benefits across cognition, depression, anxiety and sleep, mostly in patients and Iranian trials [han2024]; cognitive data come from Alzheimer's disease and MCI trials such as a single-blind 35-patient MCI trial with a waiting-list control [tsolaki2016]. No convincing healthy young-adult cognitive trial was found. Grade C for mood (mixed/clinical), D for cognition in healthy adults. Pomegranate juice (236 ml/day): a 4-week pilot in 28 completers reported verbal memory and fMRI changes [bookheimer2013]; the 12-month trial in 261 adults aged 50-75 found only that visual learning (BVMT-R) was stable versus decline in placebo (ES 0.45), with no effect on other memory measures and 23% dropout [siddarth2020]. Grade C, older adults only. Quercetin: the largest trial (n=941, 500-1000 mg/day, 12 weeks) found no effect on memory, psychomotor speed, reaction time, attention or flexibility despite large plasma increases [bromanfulks2012]; a Suntory beverage trial (110 mg isoquercitrin, 40 weeks) reported reaction time only [nakamura2022]. Grade C, no benefit. Ginger: one 2-month trial of 400-800 mg extract in 60 middle-aged women reported working-memory and ERP changes [saenghong2012]; unreplicated, grade C/D. Cinnamon: no human cognitive RCT was identified in this search; grade D. Chamomile: evidence concerns generalized anxiety (HAM-A MD -1.79 at 4 weeks) and sleep quality (SMD -0.73), not cognition [hieu2019]; a long-term GAD trial reduced symptoms but not relapse (HR 0.52, 0.20-1.33) [mao2016]. Overall, none of these ingredients merits a cognitive positioning for students; saffron's mood data are the most substantial but concern a different outcome and population.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "None of these bioactives is an essential nutrient; there is no deficiency state. All positive trials test enhancement or symptom relief: spearmint in memory-complaining older adults and active young adults, saffron in depressed or demented patients, pomegranate in middle-aged/older adults. Saffron and chamomile effects on mood and anxiety are symptomatic, not cognitive, and must not be relabelled as 'focus' or 'memory'.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Spearmint extract is sold as a 'clinically proven nootropic' (Neumentix) on the basis of two trials designed, funded and partly authored by its manufacturer, with p-values close to 0.05 across many outcomes. Saffron products cite antidepressant meta-analyses in which most trials are small, single-country and heterogeneous; cognitive claims rest on dementia patients. Pomegranate is the textbook regulatory cautionary tale: the US FTC found POM Wonderful's disease claims unsubstantiated and the D.C. Circuit (2015) upheld a requirement of at least one RCT for future disease claims. Quercetin's 'brain' marketing is contradicted by a large null trial. In the EU, botanical claims are 'on hold' and none of these has an authorised cognitive claim; cognition claims are therefore not permitted.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Mechanisms are ingredient-specific and mostly preclinical: rosmarinic acid inhibits acetylcholinesterase/GABA transaminase in vitro; crocins/safranal modulate serotonin and glutamate signalling and are antioxidant in animals; pomegranate ellagitannins are converted by microbiota to urolithins with putative anti-inflammatory effects; quercetin is poorly bioavailable and extensively conjugated; gingerols and cinnamaldehyde are anti-inflammatory in vitro; apigenin binds benzodiazepine sites of GABA-A receptors. None has an established human pathway to cognitive improvement.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Oral polyphenols/terpenoids -> limited absorption, extensive conjugation or microbial metabolism", "detail": "Quercetin plasma levels rise markedly with 500-1000 mg/day [bromanfulks2012]; pomegranate raises urolithin A glucuronide [bookheimer2013].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Circulating metabolites -> CNS targets (AChE, GABA-A, serotonin, inflammation)", "detail": "In vitro and animal data for rosmarinic acid, crocin, apigenin; not measured in humans in these trials.", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Altered brain activity", "detail": "fMRI activity increases with pomegranate [bookheimer2013]; ERP latency changes with ginger [saenghong2012].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Mood/anxiety symptom change", "detail": "Saffron self-rated depression/anxiety [mahmoudi2026]; chamomile GAD symptoms [mao2016].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Measured cognitive outcome in healthy adults", "detail": "Borderline sponsor-run spearmint trials [herrlinger2018, falcone2019]; null quercetin trial [bromanfulks2012]; partial pomegranate effect [siddarth2020].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["cholinergic/GABAergic modulation (rosmarinic acid, apigenin; in vitro)", "serotonergic modulation (saffron; animal)", "anti-inflammatory/antioxidant signalling", "microbial metabolism to urolithins", "cerebral activation changes (fMRI/ERP)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy recreationally active men and women (spearmint)", "dose_or_intake": "900 mg/day proprietary spearmint extract", "duration": "90 days", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Sustained attention better at days 30 and 90; complex attention at day 7 only; 8 other domains, mood and sleep unchanged.", "effect_estimate": "Sustained attention day 90: 34.0 +/- 0.44 vs 32.7 +/- 0.75 (P=.007) [falcone2019]", "certainty_notes": "Single sponsor-run trial (Kemin); multiple domains and time points; inconsistent time course; no replication.", "key_study_ids": ["falcone2019"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "older_adults_mci", "population_detail": "Adults ~59 y with age-associated memory impairment (spearmint)", "dose_or_intake": "600 or 900 mg/day", "duration": "90 days", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Quality of working memory +15% and spatial working memory accuracy +9% at 900 mg only; sleep onset improved.", "effect_estimate": "p=0.0469 and p=0.0456 [herrlinger2018]", "certainty_notes": "Borderline p, 30 per arm, sponsor-funded; no dose consistency.", "key_study_ids": ["herrlinger2018"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Adults with depressive/anxiety symptoms or disorders (saffron)", "dose_or_intake": "typically 30 mg/day saffron extract", "duration": ">=4 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Self-rated depression and anxiety reduced; clinician-rated scales not.", "effect_estimate": "BDI WMD -4.39 (-6.64 to -2.15), I2 92%; BAI WMD -5.06 (-8.44 to -1.68) [mahmoudi2026]", "certainty_notes": "Authors rate moderate; we downgrade for extreme heterogeneity, small single-country trials, possible publication bias and no clinician-rated effect.", "key_study_ids": ["mahmoudi2026", "han2024"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_mci", "population_detail": "MCI and Alzheimer's patients (saffron)", "dose_or_intake": "~30 mg/day", "duration": "16 weeks to 12 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Small trials report MMSE stability or non-inferiority to drugs; single-blind waiting-list design in MCI.", "effect_estimate": "MMSE p=0.015 vs waiting list [tsolaki2016]", "certainty_notes": "Patient populations; high risk of bias; not applicable to healthy adults.", "key_study_ids": ["tsolaki2016", "han2024"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy young adults/students (saffron, pomegranate, ginger, cinnamon, chamomile)", "dose_or_intake": "any", "duration": "any", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No adequate trial of these ingredients on memory in healthy young adults identified.", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "Absence of evidence.", "key_study_ids": []}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "learning", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Non-demented adults 50-75 y (pomegranate juice)", "dose_or_intake": "236 ml/day pomegranate juice", "duration": "4 weeks to 12 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "12-month trial: visual learning stable vs decline on placebo, no effect on other memory measures; 4-week pilot: verbal memory improved within group.", "effect_estimate": "BVMT-R learning between-group ES 0.45 (F=5.90, P=0.003) [siddarth2020]", "certainty_notes": "One positive measure of several; 23% dropout; commercial juice (POM Wonderful) in pilot; benefit is prevention of placebo decline.", "key_study_ids": ["siddarth2020", "bookheimer2013"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Community adults (quercetin)", "dose_or_intake": "500-1000 mg/day quercetin; 110 mg isoquercitrin beverage", "duration": "12-40 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Large trial null on all domains; a smaller industry beverage trial reported reaction time only.", "effect_estimate": "No significant effects (n=941) [bromanfulks2012]", "certainty_notes": "Large null trial outweighs small sponsor trial.", "key_study_ids": ["bromanfulks2012", "nakamura2022"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy middle-aged women (ginger)", "dose_or_intake": "400-800 mg/day ginger extract", "duration": "2 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Enhanced working memory and P300 changes.", "effect_estimate": "not reported as effect size [saenghong2012]", "certainty_notes": "Single small trial, unreplicated; funding/product details not verified.", "key_study_ids": ["saenghong2012"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "sleep_related_cognition", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Generalized anxiety disorder; poor sleepers (chamomile)", "dose_or_intake": "chamomile extract 1500 mg/day (GAD) or various", "duration": "2-26 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "GAD symptoms and sleep quality improved; no effect on state anxiety or insomnia severity; relapse not significantly reduced.", "effect_estimate": "HAM-A MD -1.79 (-3.14 to -0.43); sleep quality SMD -0.73 (-1.23 to -0.23) [hieu2019]; relapse HR 0.52 (0.20-1.33) [mao2016]", "certainty_notes": "Few small trials; cognitive outcomes not measured.", "key_study_ids": ["hieu2019", "mao2016"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "alertness", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Any of these bioactives single dose", "dose_or_intake": "any", "duration": "single dose", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No acute cognitive trials of spearmint extract, saffron, pomegranate, quercetin, ginger or cinnamon were identified in this search (peppermint essential oil trials exist but are a different ingredient).", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "Absence of evidence.", "key_study_ids": []}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "any", "onset": "not applicable", "duration_studied": "none", "domain": "any", "population": "healthy adults", "effect_estimate": "No acute trials identified for these ingredients", "certainty": "D (insufficient)", "safety": "not applicable", "study_ids": []}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "90 days", "population": "healthy active young adults (n=142)", "outcome": "sustained/complex attention (spearmint 900 mg)", "effect_estimate": "Sustained attention P=.007 day 90", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "Sponsor-run, multiple outcomes", "study_ids": ["falcone2019"]}, {"duration": "90 days", "population": "AAMI adults (n=90)", "outcome": "working memory (spearmint)", "effect_estimate": "+15% quality of WM, p=0.047", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "Borderline; sponsor", "study_ids": ["herrlinger2018", "lasrado2017"]}, {"duration": "12 months", "population": "adults 50-75 (n=261)", "outcome": "visual learning (pomegranate)", "effect_estimate": "ES 0.45 (stability vs placebo decline)", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "One of several measures; dropout", "study_ids": ["siddarth2020"]}, {"duration": "12 weeks", "population": "community adults (n=941)", "outcome": "memory, speed, attention (quercetin)", "effect_estimate": "Null", "evidence_quality": "B (null)", "limitations": "Abstract-level detail", "study_ids": ["bromanfulks2012"]}, {"duration": ">=4 weeks", "population": "adults with depressive/anxiety symptoms", "outcome": "self-rated depression/anxiety (saffron)", "effect_estimate": "BDI WMD -4.39; BAI -5.06", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "I2 >90%; clinician scales null", "study_ids": ["mahmoudi2026"]}], "safety": {"summary": "At culinary amounts all are safe. Supplement-level issues: saffron is generally tolerated at 30 mg/day in trials, but gram-level doses are reported as toxic in secondary sources (uncertain) and saffron is among the most adulterated spices; cassia cinnamon contains coumarin (hepatotoxicity concern; EFSA TDI 0.1 mg/kg bw for coumarin); quercetin high doses may interact with drugs metabolised by CYP enzymes and has nephrotoxicity concerns at very high doses (not verified here); ginger may have additive antiplatelet effects; chamomile can cause allergy (Asteraceae) and may interact with warfarin; pomegranate juice adds sugar and has been studied for CYP interactions; spearmint extract showed no hormonal or lab changes over 90 days [lasrado2017].", "adverse_effects": ["allergic reactions (chamomile, Asteraceae cross-reactivity)", "GI upset (ginger, quercetin)", "coumarin hepatotoxicity risk with high cassia cinnamon intake", "saffron adulteration (dyes, other plant parts)"], "contraindications": ["pregnancy: avoid supplemental saffron doses above culinary use (uterotonic concern, uncertain)", "Asteraceae allergy for chamomile", "liver disease with cassia cinnamon supplements"], "drug_interactions": ["chamomile and ginger: possible anticoagulant/antiplatelet interaction (warfarin)", "saffron: additive with antidepressants (theoretical serotonergic)", "quercetin: CYP and transporter interactions (uncertain)", "spearmint extract: none identified"], "upper_limits": "No ULs. Coumarin TDI 0.1 mg/kg bw/day (EFSA, general knowledge, not re-fetched). Saffron trials used 15-30 mg/day.", "sleep_impact": "Spearmint extract improved sleep onset in one trial; chamomile may improve sleep quality; none disrupt sleep.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "90 days (spearmint), 12 months (pomegranate, saffron MCI), 26 weeks (chamomile).", "daily_use_appropriate": "probably", "special_populations": "Pregnancy, children, anticoagulant users, people on antidepressants and those with liver disease need specific review; evidence base for these groups is minimal.", "specialist_review_needed": ["clinical pharmacologist (anticoagulants, antidepressants)", "food safety/authentication specialist for saffron adulteration and cinnamon coumarin", "obstetric review for saffron"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "Food-level use passes; supplement doses pass conditionally for spearmint and saffron (30 mg) with authentication, and require exclusion of cassia cinnamon extracts and interaction screening."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "No authorised health claims for any of these ingredients; botanical Article 13 claims remain 'on hold', so cognition claims are not permitted. Spearmint, saffron, pomegranate, ginger, cinnamon and chamomile are traditional foods (not novel); concentrated extracts and isolated quercetin may raise novel food questions (uncertain, check EU Novel Food Catalogue per ingredient). Coumarin maximum levels in cinnamon-containing foods are set in EU flavouring rules (Regulation 1334/2008, general knowledge).", "us": "Spices and juices are foods/GRAS; supplements under DSHEA; spearmint extract reported by its manufacturer as GRAS (not verified). FTC v. POM Wonderful (D.C. Cir. 2015) upheld that disease claims for a food require at least one well-controlled RCT; this is the key precedent for any cognition marketing.", "morocco": "Saffron is a protected Moroccan product (Taliouine designation of origin; status not re-verified); supplements and claims under ONSSA; no cognitive-claim pathway identified. Uncertain.", "permitted_claims_examples": [], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "Isolated quercetin/isoquercitrin and high-concentration extracts: EU novel food status per ingredient uncertain; US NDI notification may be needed for new extracts not previously marketed.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Culinary forms fit easily (mint tea, saffron in dishes, pomegranate seeds, ginger, chamomile tea); studied doses of spearmint extract (900 mg) and saffron extract (30 mg) fit in a capsule or functional beverage.", "formats": ["herbal teas", "spice blends", "juice or seeds", "standardised extract capsules or shots"], "stability": "Saffron crocins degrade with light/heat; rosmarinic acid moderately stable; quercetin poorly soluble.", "taste": "Saffron bitter/strong at high doses; spearmint minty; ginger pungent; cinnamon pleasant.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Generally low and microbiota-dependent (ellagitannins -> urolithins; quercetin conjugation).", "dose_feasibility": "30 mg saffron is feasible in a food; 900 mg spearmint extract requires a capsule or concentrated beverage; 236 ml pomegranate juice is a full serving with sugar; 500-1000 mg quercetin is supplement-only.", "cost_availability": "Saffron expensive (but local Moroccan sourcing); others cheap; proprietary spearmint extract licensed.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 2, "effect_magnitude": 2, "safety": 7, "reproducibility": 1, "food_suitability": 7, "cost_access": 6, "rationale": "Evidence quality 2: at best grade C, mostly single sponsor trials or clinical populations; D in healthy young adults for most items. Effect magnitude 2: borderline effects, ES 0.45 on one pomegranate measure. Safety 7: culinary safe; adulteration, coumarin and interaction caveats. Reproducibility 1: no independent replications; quercetin null. Food suitability 7: culinary forms easy, extracts need capsules. Cost/access 6: saffron costly; others cheap."}, "research_gaps": ["Independent (non-Kemin) replication of spearmint extract attention effects in students", "Saffron trials on objective cognition in healthy young adults under stress, with authenticated product", "Acute trials of any of these ingredients", "Human cinnamon cognition RCTs (none identified)", "Apigenin isolate trials on sleep-dependent cognition"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 3.8, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": "C", "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": "C", "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"sustained_attention": "C", "working_memory": "C", "global_cognition": "C", "sleep_related_cognition": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 10, "n_studies": 13, "n_verified": 13, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 4, "n_systematic_reviews": 3, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["inconsistent", "insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "resveratrol", "name": "Resveratrol", "category": "G", "category_name": "Polyphenols and plant bioactives", "aliases": ["trans-resveratrol", "3,5,4'-trihydroxystilbene", "resVida (branded ingredient)", "Reserveage Organics resveratrol (branded ingredient)"], "food_sources": ["red and purple grape skins and grape juice (trace to low mg/L)", "red wine (typically 0.5-3 mg/L, far below studied supplement doses)", "peanuts and peanut skins", "berries (blueberries, bilberries, cranberries, mulberries)", "Japanese knotweed (Polygonum cuspidatum) root, the main commercial extraction source for supplements, not a food"], "active_constituents": ["trans-resveratrol (the studied and novel-food-authorised isomer)", "cis-resveratrol (minor, photoisomerised form, less studied)", "resveratrol glucuronide and sulfate conjugates (the dominant circulating forms after oral intake)"], "one_line_verdict": "At realistic food and even most supplement doses resveratrol reliably increases cerebral blood flow and alters a few biomarkers, but across 15 years of RCTs it has not produced a convincing, replicated improvement in measured cognition in healthy young adults, and the mixed signal in postmenopausal and older-adult trials is inconsistent and confounded by industry funding.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated in humans. Single oral doses of 250-500 mg trans-resveratrol dose-dependently increase cerebral blood flow and oxygen extraction in the frontal cortex during cognitive task performance, measured by near-infrared spectroscopy, in healthy young adults, with no accompanying change in cognitive test scores [kennedy2010]. Co-administering 20 mg piperine with 250 mg resveratrol further augments this cerebral blood flow response without changing plasma resveratrol levels or cognitive performance, showing a pharmacodynamic rather than pharmacokinetic bioavailability-enhancing effect that still does not translate to cognition [wightman2014]. A 28-day chronic trial (500 mg/day) in 18-30 year-olds found the only effects were slower-but-more-accurate serial subtraction on day 1, better 3-Back accuracy immediately before day-28 dosing, lower subjective fatigue across the month, and a rise in diastolic blood pressure by day 28; the authors themselves concluded there was a 'lack of interpretable cognitive effects' despite confirmed increases in plasma resveratrol metabolites [wightman2015]. In the Brain Food target population (healthy, non-deficient young adults and students) the acute and short-term chronic evidence is therefore grade C at best for a cerebrovascular biomarker effect and grade D/E for any measured cognitive benefit: cerebral blood flow is established_human, cognition is not. Older and postmenopausal populations give an inconsistent, lower-quality signal. Witte 2014 (26 weeks, 200 mg/day, n=46 matched overweight older adults) reported a significant improvement in 30-minute word retention (p=0.038) correlating with increased hippocampal functional connectivity and improved HbA1c [witte2014], but this was not independently replicated: Huhn 2018, from the same Leipzig/Max Planck group using a larger, pre-registered sample (n=60, 26 weeks, same 200 mg/day dose, CVLT as primary outcome, NCT02621554), found no significant time-by-group effect on verbal memory, only a non-significant trend (p=0.07) for pattern recognition, alongside unfavourable changes in several inflammatory and lifestyle variables that may have masked any effect [huhn2018]. In postmenopausal women, a Newcastle (Australia) group reported a 14-week pilot (n=80, 75 mg twice daily) with improved verbal memory (p=0.041) and overall cognition (p=0.020) correlating with improved cerebrovascular responsiveness [evans2017], and then a 24-month, 125-person crossover follow-up from the same group and using the same dose (RESHAW study) reporting a 'significant' 33% relative improvement in overall cognition (d=0.170, p=0.005), concentrated in women aged 65 and over [thaungzaw2021]. Both trials come from one research group (Howe/Wong, University of Newcastle), used industry-related resveratrol product sourcing, and relied on a composite 'overall cognitive performance' z-score rather than a single pre-specified primary cognitive domain, which inflates the chance of a false positive; no independent group has replicated the Newcastle finding in postmenopausal women. In older adults more broadly, a small three-arm pilot (n=32, 90 days, placebo vs 300 mg vs 1000 mg/day) found only a selective improvement in psychomotor speed (Trail Making A) at the 1000 mg dose, with no effect on any other domain, and the lead author disclosed a prior consulting relationship with the resveratrol product's manufacturer [anton2018]. Mild cognitive impairment and dementia populations show no convincing benefit: a 52-week, high-dose (up to 2 g/day) phase 2 trial in 119 mild-to-moderate Alzheimer's disease patients found resveratrol attenuated decline in some CSF and functional (ADL, MMSE) measures in a small biomarker-defined subgroup (n=19 vs 19) but this is a secondary, retrospective, non-pre-registered subgroup analysis, not a primary cognitive endpoint in the full trial [moussa2017]; a 2022 systematic review of AD RCTs concluded evidence for a cognitive benefit remains insufficient [tosatti2022]. Meta-analytic synthesis is small and conflicting. Farzaei 2018, pooling four RCTs (226 participants, mixed populations), found no significant effect on memory or cognitive performance (all effect sizes near zero) but a significant reduction in two mood/vigor and fatigue measures [farzaei2018]. Marx 2018, pooling ten RCTs with a GRADE-rated systematic review, found an overall-cognition picture of '3 studies positive, 2 mixed, 5 null', with a significant pooled effect only on delayed recognition memory (SMD 0.39, 95% CI 0.08-0.70, I2=0%, n=166, 3 studies) and on negative mood (SMD -0.18, 95% CI -0.31 to -0.05), concluding the literature is 'inconsistent and limited' rather than establishing a reliable cognitive benefit [marx2018]. Both meta-analyses mix healthy, postmenopausal and clinical populations, so neither supports a specific claim for healthy young adults or students. A 2024 systematic review of resveratrol in older adults covering safety and efficacy again found inconsistent cognitive results across trials with substantial heterogeneity in dose, duration and outcome measures [yadegar2024]. Mechanism versus efficacy. The pathway from oral intake to plasma resveratrol conjugates to acute cerebral vasodilation is reasonably well established in humans (biomarker level); the step from increased cerebral blood flow to measured cognitive change has repeatedly failed to appear even within the same research group and dose range that demonstrated the vascular effect [kennedy2010, wightman2014, wightman2015]. Safety and dose. Resveratrol is generally well tolerated up to 1 g/day, but repeated dosing at 2.5-5 g/day for 29 days caused mild-to-moderate gastrointestinal symptoms (nausea, diarrhoea, flatulence) in a dose-ranging safety study, alongside a drop in circulating IGF-1 and IGFBP-3 [brown2010]; resveratrol also inhibits several cytochrome P450 enzymes (CYP3A4, CYP2E1) and has documented interactions altering the pharmacokinetics of carbamazepine and diclofenac in healthy volunteers [chow2010]. Bottom line for Brain Food. There is no reproducible, grade-A or grade-B human evidence that resveratrol improves measured cognition in healthy young adults or students at any studied dose (75-1000 mg/day, acute or chronic); the most consistent human finding is an acute cerebral blood flow increase that does not translate into performance; the strongest cognitive signals appear only in postmenopausal women and older adults from a small number of research groups with unreplicated or directly contradicted results, and the largest, highest-quality trial in that age range (Huhn 2018) was null.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Resveratrol is not an essential nutrient and there is no described deficiency state; dietary intake in Western diets is typically well under 1 mg/day from grapes, wine and peanuts, orders of magnitude below every dose studied for cognition (75 mg to 2 g/day). All human cognitive trials are therefore enhancement/intervention studies in adequately nourished people, not deficiency correction. The hypothesis that only already-compromised vasculature (postmenopausal oestrogen loss, MCI, ageing cerebrovasculature) responds to resveratrol's vasoactive and SIRT1/AMPK-pathway effects is plausible and would explain why signals cluster in postmenopausal women and older/clinical populations rather than young healthy adults [evans2017, thaungzaw2021, witte2014], but the one well-powered, pre-registered trial in exactly that older population (Huhn 2018) was null, so this is not established. In rested, healthy young adults with normal cerebral perfusion, there is no evidence of benefit beyond an unused reserve of cerebral blood flow capacity.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Marketing resveratrol to students or knowledge workers as a 'focus' or 'memory' nootropic is not supported: the Northumbria University acute/chronic trials in healthy young adults, which are the only trials in this exact population, explicitly found cerebral blood flow changes without cognitive benefit [kennedy2010, wightman2014, wightman2015]. Claims aimed at an older or midlife audience ('supports healthy brain ageing', 'improves memory after menopause') rest on two trials from one Australian group with a composite cognition score and no independent replication [evans2017, thaungzaw2021], against a null, pre-registered German trial in a similar age range using a single pre-specified primary outcome [huhn2018]; an honest claim cannot be stronger than 'early and inconsistent evidence of possible benefit in postmenopausal women, not replicated by independent groups'. Any EU claim referencing cognitive function is not authorised: the EU novel food authorisation for trans-resveratrol (Commission Implementing Decision (EU) 2016/1190, amended by Implementing Regulation (EU) 2021/51) permits it as a food-supplement ingredient at up to 150 mg/day with a mandatory medication-interaction warning, but grants no health claim; no Article 13/14 cognitive health claim for resveratrol is on the EU Register. In the US, an FTC-defensible cognition claim would require competent and reliable scientific evidence that, given the null and small/inconsistent trial base, does not currently exist. The honest position: resveratrol is a well-studied antioxidant polyphenol with established cerebrovascular bioactivity and a biologically plausible but clinically unproven cognitive story; red grapes, berries and peanuts remain reasonable foods on general polyphenol/cardiometabolic grounds, not because they deliver a cognition-active dose of resveratrol.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Orally ingested trans-resveratrol is absorbed but undergoes extensive first-pass glucuronidation and sulfation in the gut wall and liver, so circulating levels of the parent compound are low (low-to-sub-micromolar) while conjugate metabolites reach much higher concentrations; this poor bioavailability is the most frequently cited explanation for the gap between preclinical and human cognitive findings. In humans, resveratrol reliably increases cerebral blood flow and oxygen extraction during cognitive task performance, plausibly via nitric-oxide-mediated vasodilation and endothelial function improvement, and these haemodynamic changes are the most consistent human finding across the acute dose-ranging trials. Proposed downstream mechanisms (SIRT1 activation, AMPK signalling, anti-inflammatory and antioxidant effects, neurotrophin modulation) are supported mainly by animal and in-vitro work; in humans, the connection between these molecular pathways and measured cognitive outcomes remains unestablished.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Oral trans-resveratrol -> intestinal absorption with extensive first-pass glucuronide/sulfate conjugation", "detail": "Conjugate metabolites (resveratrol-3-O-sulfate, -4'-O-glucuronide, -3-O-glucuronide) reach plasma AUC up to 20.3-fold higher than parent compound after repeated dosing, confirming very low systemic bioavailability of free resveratrol [brown2010].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Circulating resveratrol/metabolites -> endothelial nitric oxide pathway -> cerebral vasodilation", "detail": "Single 250-500 mg doses dose-dependently increase total haemoglobin (a proxy for cerebral blood flow) in the frontal cortex during task performance by NIRS, confirmed with plasma resveratrol/metabolite detection in a parallel cohort [kennedy2010]; co-administration with piperine further augments this vascular response [wightman2014].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Increased cerebral blood flow -> hypothesised improved substrate/oxygen delivery to task-active cortex", "detail": "The haemodynamic change is measurable and reproducible across three independent Northumbria trials, but has never co-occurred with a cognitive performance improvement in the same trial [kennedy2010, wightman2014, wightman2015].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "SIRT1/AMPK activation, anti-inflammatory and antioxidant signalling", "detail": "Central to resveratrol's reputation as a 'calorie-restriction mimetic'; demonstrated in rodent and cell models of neuroprotection and synaptic plasticity, invoked to explain the hippocampal connectivity finding in [witte2014], but not directly measured at the molecular level in any cognitive RCT reviewed here.", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Hippocampal structural/functional connectivity and glucose metabolism changes", "detail": "26-week, 200 mg/day supplementation increased hippocampal functional connectivity and reduced HbA1c alongside a modest memory improvement in one trial (n=46) [witte2014], but a larger, pre-registered trial by an overlapping research group using the same dose and duration found no significant memory effect and only non-significant trends in hippocampal microstructure (n=60) [huhn2018].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Measured human cognitive outcome", "detail": "Acute: no benefit in healthy young adults despite confirmed vascular and pharmacokinetic effects [kennedy2010, wightman2014]. Chronic, healthy young adults: no interpretable cognitive benefit over 28 days [wightman2015]. Chronic, older/postmenopausal adults: inconsistent, with positive findings from one Australian group [evans2017, thaungzaw2021] and from one German group's smaller trial [witte2014], contradicted by a larger pre-registered trial from an overlapping German group [huhn2018] and limited to a single psychomotor-speed measure in a small three-arm pilot [anton2018]. Clinical AD population: no primary-endpoint cognitive benefit; only secondary biomarker/subgroup signals at high dose [moussa2017].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["endothelial nitric oxide / cerebral vasodilation (haemodynamic, established in humans)", "SIRT1 deacetylase activation (animal/in-vitro)", "AMPK energy-sensing pathway (animal/in-vitro)", "anti-inflammatory and antioxidant signalling (Nrf2, NF-kB modulation; mostly animal/in-vitro)", "hippocampal functional connectivity and glucose metabolism (limited human evidence, inconsistent)", "cytochrome P450 modulation (CYP3A4, CYP2E1 inhibition; established human pharmacokinetic interaction, not a cognitive pathway)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "cerebral_blood_flow", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults, mean age roughly 20s-30s, single-dose crossover designs", "dose_or_intake": "250 mg or 500 mg trans-resveratrol, single dose; 250 mg plus 20 mg piperine", "duration": "acute, measured during and shortly after a single dose", "grade": "B", "direction": "benefit", "effect_summary": "Dose-dependent increase in frontal cerebral blood flow/oxygen extraction (total and deoxyhaemoglobin by NIRS) during cognitive task performance, replicated across two independent crossover trials; piperine co-administration augments the effect further.", "effect_estimate": "Dose-dependent rise in total haemoglobin concentration during task performance, statistically significant vs placebo at both 250 and 500 mg (exact p-values and magnitudes not reported in abstract) [kennedy2010]; significant augmentation of CBF with piperine co-administration vs resveratrol alone (p<0.05, exact value not reported in abstract) [wightman2014]", "certainty_notes": "Two well-conducted, double-blind, placebo-controlled crossover trials from the same laboratory with consistent, biologically plausible, dose-dependent biomarker findings; downgraded from A because this is a biomarker/surrogate (cerebral haemodynamics), not a cognitive outcome, and no independent replication outside this research group was found.", "key_study_ids": ["kennedy2010", "wightman2014"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Same acute crossover trials as above; full cognitive battery assessing attention, working memory and executive tasks", "dose_or_intake": "250-500 mg trans-resveratrol, single dose, with or without piperine", "duration": "acute, single session", "grade": "E", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Despite confirmed dose-dependent cerebral blood flow changes and confirmed plasma resveratrol exposure, no measure of cognitive performance, mood, or blood pressure changed significantly in either trial.", "effect_estimate": "No significant cognitive effect reported in either trial (exact test statistics not given in abstracts) [kennedy2010, wightman2014]", "certainty_notes": "Consistent null findings across two independent, well-controlled trials with confirmed pharmacological exposure strengthens confidence in the absence of an acute cognitive effect in this population; not downgraded for imprecision given the consistency.", "key_study_ids": ["kennedy2010", "wightman2014"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Adults 18-30 years, 28-day parallel-group trial", "dose_or_intake": "500 mg/day trans-resveratrol", "duration": "28 days", "grade": "D", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "No consistent cognitive benefit: isolated, non-replicated findings (more accurate but slower serial subtraction on day 1; better 3-Back accuracy only in the pre-dose assessment on day 28) alongside a rise in diastolic blood pressure and lower subjective fatigue ratings; authors concluded a lack of interpretable cognitive effects.", "effect_estimate": "Not reported as formal effect sizes in abstract; described qualitatively as isolated, inconsistent significant findings amid a largely null battery [wightman2015]", "certainty_notes": "Single small trial (n=41-60 depending on measure), multiple comparisons without correction increase the chance that the isolated significant findings are false positives; downgraded for imprecision and risk of bias (multiplicity).", "key_study_ids": ["wightman2015"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Overweight older adults, 50-75 years, matched pairs or randomised, 26 weeks", "dose_or_intake": "200 mg/day trans-resveratrol", "duration": "26 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "A smaller matched-pairs trial (n=46) found a significant improvement in 30-minute word retention correlating with increased hippocampal functional connectivity and improved glycaemic control; a larger, pre-registered trial from an overlapping group with the same dose/duration and a pre-specified primary memory outcome (CVLT, n=60) found no significant time-by-group effect, with only a non-significant trend for pattern recognition memory.", "effect_estimate": "Witte 2014: p=0.038 for 30-min word retention vs placebo, magnitude not reported in abstract [witte2014]. Huhn 2018: multivariate repeated-measures ANOVA non-significant for CVLT; pattern recognition trend p=0.07 [huhn2018]", "certainty_notes": "Direct replication attempt by a larger, pre-registered trial failed to confirm the original finding; downgraded for inconsistency and because the positive trial used matched pairs rather than full randomisation with concealed allocation; Huhn 2018, the more rigorous design, is null.", "key_study_ids": ["witte2014", "huhn2018"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Postmenopausal women, 45-85 years, 14-week and 24-month randomised placebo-controlled trials from the same Australian research group", "dose_or_intake": "75 mg trans-resveratrol twice daily (150 mg/day total)", "duration": "14 weeks and 24 months (12 months per crossover arm)", "grade": "B", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Two trials from one group report improved composite/overall cognitive performance and cerebrovascular responsiveness (14-week pilot: verbal memory p=0.041, overall cognition p=0.020; 24-month RESHAW crossover, n=125: overall cognition improved 33% relative to placebo, d=0.170, p=0.005, concentrated in women >=65y). No independent replication exists.", "effect_estimate": "d=0.170 (p=0.005) for overall cognitive performance, 24-month crossover, n=125 [thaungzaw2021]; p=0.020 for overall cognitive performance, 14-week pilot, n=80 [evans2017]", "certainty_notes": "Single research group, composite rather than single pre-specified primary cognitive outcome in the pilot, modest effect sizes, no independent replication; cannot exceed grade B per GRADE convention for a body of evidence confined to one lab even though sample sizes and durations are respectable; direction is consistent across both trials from this group, which is the basis for 'possible_benefit' rather than 'inconsistent'.", "key_study_ids": ["evans2017", "thaungzaw2021"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "processing_speed", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Sedentary, overweight older adults 65-93 years, three-arm pilot (placebo, 300 mg/day, 1000 mg/day)", "dose_or_intake": "300 mg/day or 1000 mg/day trans-resveratrol", "duration": "90 days", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Selective improvement in psychomotor speed (Trail Making Test A) at the 1000 mg/day dose only, compared with both placebo and the 300 mg dose (p=0.02); no significant effect on any other of the six cognitive domains tested.", "effect_estimate": "p=0.02 for Trail Making A at 1000 mg/day vs placebo and vs 300 mg/day; magnitude not reported in abstract [anton2018]", "certainty_notes": "Very small pilot (n=32 across three arms, roughly 10-11 per arm), single positive measure out of a full battery raising multiplicity concern, and lead author disclosed a prior consulting relationship with the resveratrol product manufacturer (Reserveage Organics); downgraded for imprecision and conflict of interest.", "key_study_ids": ["anton2018"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Mild-to-moderate Alzheimer's disease, biomarker-confirmed (CSF Abeta42 <600 ng/mL) subgroup analysis", "dose_or_intake": "up to 1 g twice daily (titrated), i.e. up to 2 g/day", "duration": "52 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "In a retrospective biomarker-defined subgroup (n=19 resveratrol vs 19 placebo) of a larger 119-person trial, resveratrol attenuated decline in CSF Abeta42, MMSE and ADL scores versus placebo; this was a secondary/exploratory subgroup analysis, not the trial's primary cognitive endpoint, and does not generalise to healthy adults or to MCI.", "effect_estimate": "Direction described as attenuated decline (p values and exact deltas not detailed in the abstract reviewed) [moussa2017]", "certainty_notes": "High-dose AD-specific finding in a clinical, biomarker-selected subgroup from a retrospective re-analysis; mechanistic/safety relevance only for Brain Food, no relevance to healthy young adults or general enhancement claims; a subsequent systematic review of AD RCTs found insufficient evidence for a cognitive benefit overall [tosatti2022].", "key_study_ids": ["moussa2017", "tosatti2022"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Pooled meta-analytic populations mixing healthy, postmenopausal and clinical adults across 4-10 RCTs", "dose_or_intake": "75 mg to 2 g/day across pooled trials", "duration": "14 weeks to 52 weeks across pooled trials", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Two independent meta-analyses reach different emphases: Farzaei 2018 (4 RCTs, 226 participants) found no significant effect on memory/cognition overall, with a significant benefit only for mood/vigor and fatigue measures; Marx 2018 (10 RCTs, GRADE-rated) found 3 of 10 studies positive, 2 mixed, 5 null, with the only significant pooled cognitive effect on delayed recognition memory (3 studies, n=166).", "effect_estimate": "SMD approx 0 (not significant) for memory/cognition pooled [farzaei2018]; SMD 0.39 (95% CI 0.08-0.70, I2=0%, n=166, 3 studies) for delayed recognition [marx2018]", "certainty_notes": "Both meta-analyses pool heterogeneous populations (healthy, postmenopausal, clinical) and doses, limiting applicability to any single Brain Food target population; downgraded for indirectness and inconsistency between the two reviews' conclusions despite overlapping included trials.", "key_study_ids": ["farzaei2018", "marx2018"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "students", "population_detail": "No trial identified in university students or examination-stressed young adults specifically", "dose_or_intake": "not studied", "duration": "not studied", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No RCT of resveratrol in students or knowledge workers under cognitive workload was found in this search; the closest analogue populations (healthy young adults generally, in acute/short chronic Northumbria trials) showed no cognitive benefit despite confirmed vascular and pharmacokinetic effects.", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "Absence of direct evidence; inference from the healthy-young-adult trials above (which were null for cognition) suggests no expectation of benefit, but this is extrapolation, not direct evidence, hence 'insufficient' rather than 'no_benefit'.", "key_study_ids": ["wightman2015", "kennedy2010"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "250 mg trans-resveratrol, single dose", "onset": "within 45 min absorption period, sustained through ~36 min task performance", "duration_studied": "single session, approx 1.5-2 hours post-dose", "domain": "cerebral_blood_flow", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "effect_estimate": "Dose-dependent increase in total haemoglobin (CBF proxy) during task performance; cognitive performance unaffected", "certainty": "moderate (consistent across 2 trials, biomarker only)", "safety": "well tolerated at this dose in both trials, no adverse events reported", "study_ids": ["kennedy2010", "wightman2014"]}, {"dose": "500 mg trans-resveratrol, single dose", "onset": "within 45 min absorption period", "duration_studied": "single session", "domain": "cerebral_blood_flow", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "effect_estimate": "Larger dose-dependent increase in CBF variables than 250 mg; cognitive performance unaffected", "certainty": "moderate", "safety": "well tolerated, no adverse events reported", "study_ids": ["kennedy2010"]}, {"dose": "250 mg trans-resveratrol + 20 mg piperine, single dose", "onset": "within 40 min absorption period", "duration_studied": "single session", "domain": "cerebral_blood_flow", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "effect_estimate": "Significantly augmented CBF response vs resveratrol alone without change in plasma resveratrol levels; cognitive performance, mood and blood pressure unaffected", "certainty": "moderate (single trial, n=23)", "safety": "well tolerated, no adverse events reported", "study_ids": ["wightman2014"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "28 days", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "outcome": "working memory, attention, mood, cerebral blood flow", "effect_estimate": "No interpretable cognitive benefit; isolated non-replicated findings; diastolic blood pressure increased by day 28", "evidence_quality": "low (single small trial, multiplicity concerns)", "limitations": "n=41-60 depending on outcome, no correction for multiple comparisons, authors concluded effects were not interpretable", "study_ids": ["wightman2015"]}, {"duration": "26 weeks", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "outcome": "verbal memory, hippocampal connectivity/microstructure", "effect_estimate": "Inconsistent: positive in smaller matched-pairs trial (n=46), null in larger pre-registered trial (n=60) from an overlapping research group using the identical dose and duration", "evidence_quality": "low-to-moderate, direct replication failure", "limitations": "Witte 2014 used matched pairs not full randomisation; Huhn 2018, the more rigorous trial, was null for its pre-specified primary outcome", "study_ids": ["witte2014", "huhn2018"]}, {"duration": "14 weeks to 24 months", "population": "mixed_adults", "outcome": "overall cognitive performance, cerebrovascular responsiveness, in postmenopausal women", "effect_estimate": "Significant improvement reported in both trials from the same Australian group (14-week p=0.020; 24-month d=0.170, p=0.005)", "evidence_quality": "moderate within this single research group; no independent replication", "limitations": "Single research group for both trials; composite cognition score in the pilot; crossover design in the 24-month trial carries carry-over risk despite washout", "study_ids": ["evans2017", "thaungzaw2021"]}, {"duration": "90 days", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "outcome": "psychomotor speed and broader cognitive battery", "effect_estimate": "Selective benefit on one of six domains (Trail Making A) at 1000 mg/day only", "evidence_quality": "low (very small 3-arm pilot, n approx 32 total)", "limitations": "Small sample per arm, single significant measure amid a full battery, author conflict of interest with product manufacturer", "study_ids": ["anton2018"]}, {"duration": "52 weeks", "population": "clinical_population", "outcome": "Alzheimer's disease biomarkers and functional/cognitive decline (secondary subgroup)", "effect_estimate": "Attenuated decline in CSF Abeta42, MMSE and ADL in a retrospective biomarker-selected subgroup (n=19 vs 19)", "evidence_quality": "low (secondary subgroup analysis, not primary endpoint)", "limitations": "Retrospective, non-pre-registered subgroup; not generalisable beyond biomarker-confirmed AD; a subsequent systematic review found overall AD-RCT evidence insufficient", "study_ids": ["moussa2017", "tosatti2022"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Resveratrol is generally well tolerated at doses up to approximately 500 mg-1 g/day in short-to-medium-term trials (weeks to 12 months), with the main documented issue being dose-dependent gastrointestinal upset (nausea, diarrhoea, flatulence) emerging clearly at 2.5-5 g/day in a 29-day safety study, far above any dose studied for cognition or permitted in the EU novel food authorisation. Resveratrol inhibits several cytochrome P450 enzymes (notably CYP3A4 and CYP2E1), creating a plausible and partly documented risk of altered drug levels for medications metabolised by these pathways (e.g. carbamazepine, diclofenac in probe-drug studies). No long-term (multi-year) human safety data at supplement doses beyond the 24-month RESHAW trial were identified in this search.", "adverse_effects": ["Mild-to-moderate gastrointestinal symptoms (nausea, diarrhoea, flatulence) at 2.5-5 g/day repeat dosing [brown2010]", "Decrease in circulating IGF-1 and IGFBP-3 with repeated high-dose intake, of uncertain clinical significance outside oncology contexts [brown2010]", "Small increase in diastolic blood pressure reported after 28 days of chronic supplementation in one healthy young adult trial [wightman2015]", "Unfavourable trends in some inflammatory/metabolic markers (IL-6, hsCRP, TNF-alpha, cholesterol, fasting glucose) over 26 weeks, though these also occurred in placebo and may reflect general ageing/lifestyle trends rather than a drug effect [huhn2018]"], "contraindications": ["Known hypersensitivity to grape/stilbene-derived compounds (theoretical, not well documented in trials)", "Use of medications with a narrow therapeutic index metabolised via CYP3A4 or CYP2E1 without medical supervision"], "drug_interactions": ["CYP3A4 inhibition, documented to alter pharmacokinetics of medications such as carbamazepine and diclofenac in healthy-volunteer probe-drug studies", "CYP2E1 modulation demonstrated in a chlorzoxazone probe-drug study", "General caution warranted for anticoagulants/antiplatelet agents given resveratrol's reported mild antiplatelet activity in preclinical literature, though this was not directly confirmed in a clinical interaction trial within this search"], "upper_limits": "No formal tolerable upper intake level (UL) has been set by EFSA or any other regulator specifically for resveratrol. The EU novel food authorisation caps supplement use at 150 mg/day for adults. Clinical safety data show gastrointestinal tolerability issues emerge at 2.5-5 g/day (more than 15-30x the EU cap), so a practical margin exists between the authorised dose and doses causing clear adverse effects, but no chronic human safety data exist above 2 g/day for periods longer than about a year.", "sleep_impact": "One chronic trial in healthy young adults included sleep-quality questionnaires but reported no notable adverse sleep impact; resveratrol is not associated with stimulant-like alertness effects in the reviewed trials [wightman2015].", "long_term_exposure_studied": "The longest controlled human trial identified is 24 months (RESHAW, crossover with 12 months per arm) [thaungzaw2021]; the AD trial reached 52 weeks at much higher doses [moussa2017]. No multi-year, high-dose, continuous (non-crossover) safety data in a general population were identified.", "daily_use_appropriate": "probably", "special_populations": "Pregnant and breastfeeding women: not studied for cognition or safety in this search and should be excluded from any Brain Food formulation pending dedicated reproductive safety data. Children/adolescents: no cognitive trials identified in this search; not recommended. Patients on CYP3A4/CYP2E1-metabolised medications (including some anticonvulsants, certain NSAIDs, some psychiatric and cardiovascular drugs): require medical supervision per the EU-mandated label warning. Alzheimer's disease/MCI patients: only studied at very high doses (up to 2 g/day) in a clinical trial context with specialist medical supervision; not appropriate for self-directed supplement use at these doses.", "specialist_review_needed": ["Clinical pharmacology/CYP-interaction review for any Brain Food product aimed at adults on chronic medication", "Reproductive and developmental toxicology review before any claim or use in pregnancy/breastfeeding", "Endocrinology review given resveratrol's phytoestrogenic activity (relevant to hormone-sensitive conditions) and documented IGF-1/IGFBP-3 effects at high doses"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "No signal of serious harm at doses up to 1 g/day in trials up to 24 months, and the EU-authorised 150 mg/day dose has a wide margin below doses causing GI upset (2.5-5 g/day); however, the documented CYP3A4/CYP2E1 drug-interaction potential and the EU-mandated medication-use warning mean the gate is conditional on excluding or flagging users on interacting medications, pregnant/breastfeeding women, and children, rather than an unconditional pass."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Trans-resveratrol is authorised as a novel food ingredient for use in food supplements under Commission Implementing Decision (EU) 2016/1190 (under the former Novel Food Regulation (EC) 258/97), limited to adults, originally restricted to capsule or tablet form at a maximum of 150 mg/day, with a mandatory label warning that users of medicines should consume the product only under medical supervision. Commission Implementing Regulation (EU) 2021/51 amended the Union list (Implementing Regulation (EU) 2017/2470) to remove the capsule/tablet-only restriction, allowing other supplement formats, following an application by DSM Nutritional Products Europe; the 150 mg/day dose ceiling and the medication-interaction warning were not found to have been removed in the sources reviewed. No EFSA Article 13/14 health claim authorising a cognitive-function statement for resveratrol was identified on the EU Register of nutrition and health claims.", "us": "Resveratrol is marketed in the US as a dietary ingredient under DSHEA; it does not require FDA pre-market approval as a food ingredient claim but any structure/function claim (e.g. 'supports healthy brain aging') requires that the manufacturer hold competent and reliable scientific substantiation and must carry the standard DSHEA disclaimer; no FDA-approved drug claim or qualified health claim for cognition exists. Given the inconsistent and largely null RCT evidence in healthy adults, an FTC-defensible claim of cognitive benefit for general populations would be difficult to substantiate; a narrower, more qualified claim limited to postmenopausal women citing the specific Newcastle trials might be arguable but remains legally untested and scientifically unreplicated.", "morocco": "No resveratrol-specific ONSSA or Moroccan Ministry of Health novel-food or supplement authorisation record was identified in this search; Morocco generally follows a food-supplement framework requiring ONSSA registration, and in the absence of a specific resveratrol dossier, status should be treated as uncertain and requires direct verification with ONSSA before market entry.", "permitted_claims_examples": [], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "Trans-resveratrol is explicitly categorised as a novel food in the EU (authorised under Decision (EU) 2016/1190 as amended by Regulation (EU) 2021/51), which caps EU supplement use at 150 mg/day for adults only and requires a medication-interaction warning on the label; this is the binding regulatory constraint for any EU food-supplement formulation. US NDI status for resveratrol has a long marketing history under DSHEA as a dietary ingredient; Moroccan status is unconfirmed and should be checked directly with ONSSA.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Poor as a whole-food cognitive-dose strategy: dietary sources (red wine, grape skins, peanuts, berries) deliver resveratrol in microgram-to-low-milligram quantities per serving, orders of magnitude below any dose studied for cognition (75 mg to 2 g/day) or the EU-authorised 150 mg/day supplement ceiling. A cognition-relevant dose is realistically achievable only via a concentrated supplement (capsule, tablet, or as of the 2021 EU amendment, other supplement formats), not via an ordinary food serving.", "formats": ["capsules/tablets (the original EU-authorised format)", "other food-supplement formats permitted in the EU since the 2021 amendment (e.g. powders, potentially functional-beverage formats, subject to the 150 mg/day cap and labelling rules)", "micronised or complexed formulations (e.g. with piperine, phospholipids, or cyclodextrins) marketed to improve absorption, though human cognitive-outcome data for these formats specifically were not identified in this search"], "stability": "Trans-resveratrol is photosensitive and can isomerise to the less-studied cis form on exposure to light and heat; opaque packaging and controlled storage are standard industry practice, though stability data specific to a Brain Food matrix were not researched in this unit.", "taste": "Resveratrol itself has minimal inherent flavour impact at supplement doses in capsule/tablet form; taste considerations become relevant only if formulated into a beverage or food matrix, which was not evidenced in the human cognitive trials reviewed.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Oral trans-resveratrol undergoes extensive first-pass glucuronidation and sulfation, so free plasma resveratrol is low while conjugate metabolite exposure is substantially higher (AUC up to ~20-fold higher for metabolites than parent compound in a repeat-dose study) [brown2010]. Co-administration with piperine increases the pharmacodynamic (vascular) effect without raising plasma resveratrol or metabolite levels, i.e. it does not solve the fundamental bioavailability problem [wightman2014]. No human cognitive trial identified in this search used a bioavailability-enhanced resveratrol formulation (e.g. micronised, liposomal, or cyclodextrin-complexed) at a cognition-testing endpoint, so any bioavailability-enhancement marketing claim for cognition specifically remains unverified.", "dose_feasibility": "The EU-authorised 150 mg/day ceiling fits comfortably within standard supplement capsule/tablet formats and is close to several of the doses showing at least a possible cognitive signal in postmenopausal women (75 mg twice daily = 150 mg/day in evans2017 and thaungzaw2021), making it formulation-feasible as a supplement; it cannot realistically be delivered through an ordinary food serving.", "cost_availability": "Trans-resveratrol supplements are widely available globally from numerous brands at a range of price points; retail prices for 250-500 mg capsules commonly fall in the range of roughly $0.25-$1.15 per day equivalent dose in US retail listings reviewed, though exact trans-resveratrol purity varies by product and was not independently verified for each listing.", "cost_evidence": "WebSearch of general consumer retail listings (vitaminexpress.org, walmart.com, supp.co, nahdionline.com, fullscript.com) found per-capsule prices ranging from approximately $0.25/day (Best Naturals, 500 mg, purity unspecified) to approximately $1.14/day (Solgar, 500 mg) for one-capsule-per-day products, and up to several dollars per day for higher-purity, higher-dose (500 mg x2, ~1000 mg/day) branded trans-resveratrol products (e.g. Vivalifer); exact currency and purity were not consistently confirmed across listings, so these figures are indicative retail price points, not a systematic cost analysis, and should be re-verified against specific suppliers before any product-costing decision."}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 3, "effect_magnitude": 2, "safety": 7, "reproducibility": 2, "food_suitability": 2, "cost_access": 7, "rationale": "evidence_quality=3: the only grade-B finding (acute cerebral blood flow increase) is a biomarker, not cognition; every human cognitive outcome in the target healthy-adult population is grade D/E, and the best chronic-cognition evidence (postmenopausal women) tops out at grade B from a single unreplicated research group, consistent with an overall C-to-D ingredient-level evidence quality. effect_magnitude=2: where any significant cognitive effect exists (postmenopausal overall cognition d=0.17; delayed recognition SMD 0.39) it is small, and the largest/most rigorous trials in the closest-to-target populations (Huhn 2018 in older adults; the Northumbria healthy-young-adult series) are null. safety=7: no serious safety signal up to about 1 g/day for periods up to 24 months, GI tolerability issues only emerge far above the EU-permitted dose, but CYP3A4/CYP2E1 drug-interaction potential and the mandatory EU medication-use warning prevent a higher score. reproducibility=2: the single clearest attempted replication in this literature (Huhn 2018 vs Witte 2014, same dose/duration, overlapping investigators) failed, and the postmenopausal-women positive findings come from one research group only. food_suitability=2: dietary sources deliver resveratrol at a tiny fraction of any studied dose; a cognition-relevant intake requires a concentrated supplement, not a food serving. cost_access=7: raw material and finished supplements are cheap and globally available, with the EU-authorised 150 mg/day dose easily and affordably deliverable in a standard capsule."}, "research_gaps": ["No RCT identified in university students or knowledge workers under examination/work-related cognitive load specifically.", "No independent (non-Newcastle-group) replication of the postmenopausal-women cognitive benefit found in evans2017 and thaungzaw2021.", "No long-term (multi-year, non-crossover) safety or efficacy data beyond 24 months in a general population, or beyond 52 weeks at high dose in AD.", "No head-to-head trial of bioavailability-enhanced resveratrol formulations (micronised, liposomal, piperine-combined) against standard trans-resveratrol using a cognitive primary outcome.", "No clinical drug-interaction trial specifically quantifying CYP3A4-mediated interaction risk at the EU-authorised 150 mg/day dose (existing interaction data come from higher research doses).", "No dedicated reproductive/developmental safety data for pregnancy or breastfeeding.", "Limited clarity on whether the 2021 EU amendment altered the 150 mg/day dose ceiling itself versus only the permitted delivery formats; the consolidated EU novel food list should be checked directly before any regulatory submission."], "_derived": {"composite_score": 3.8, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": "B", "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": "B", "best_grade_overall": "B", "benefit_domains": {"global_cognition": "B", "processing_speed": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 9, "n_studies": 15, "n_verified": 15, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 3, "n_systematic_reviews": 4, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["benefit", "inconsistent", "insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "american-ginseng-cereboost", "name": "American ginseng (Panax quinquefolius, Cereboost)", "category": "H", "category_name": "Botanical and nootropic supplements", "aliases": ["American ginseng", "Panax quinquefolius", "Cereboost (branded standardized extract)", "North American ginseng"], "food_sources": ["not a conventional food; root of Panax quinquefolius, native to North America, traditionally used by Indigenous peoples and in traditional Chinese medicine as an imported botanical; not part of typical Western or Moroccan diets"], "active_constituents": ["ginsenosides with a distinct profile from Asian ginseng (higher relative Rb1, lower Rg1; Cereboost standardized to 10.65% ginsenosides)", "gut-microbiota-derived ginsenoside metabolites"], "one_line_verdict": "The branded, standardized American ginseng extract Cereboost has the most consistent acute human working-memory signal of any ginseng species tested, replicated across 3-4 independent trials by overlapping but not identical author teams in young and middle-aged adults, with one trial also showing a modest benefit after 14 days of daily use, but the whole literature comes from a small number of industry-linked trials using one proprietary extract, so it cannot yet be generalized to American ginseng as a generic ingredient.", "scientific_conclusion": "Cereboost, a standardized Panax quinquefolius extract (10.65% ginsenosides), is the best-characterized American ginseng product in human cognition research. In the founding acute trial (n=32 healthy young adults, double-blind, placebo-controlled, three doses), all tested doses (100, 200, 400 mg) improved Corsi block (spatial working memory) performance, and 100 mg also improved choice reaction time accuracy and calmness, with no change in blood glucose, distinguishing the mechanism from Asian ginseng's acute glucoregulatory effect [scholey2010]. A partial replication in healthy middle-aged adults (40-60 y, n=52) found that 200 mg improved the 'working memory' factor (driven by spatial working memory) at 3 hours post-dose, again with no blood-glucose change, extending the age range of the acute effect [ossoukhova2015]. A neurophysiological follow-up in the same age group (40-60 y) found that 200 mg reduced prefrontal steady-state visually evoked potential (SSVEP) latency during spatial working memory retrieval, a biomarker consistent with altered prefrontal recruitment, but found no significant behavioral effect on the continuous-performance task in that specific study -- a reminder that a neurophysiological signal is not itself a cognitive outcome [white2020]. The most comprehensive trial (n=61 healthy young adults, preregistered on ClinicalTrials.gov NCT03579095) tested acute, 14-day chronic, and acute-on-chronic dosing: acute dosing improved attention-network-task (ANT) accuracy and reaction time in the postprandial period (effect sizes d=0.60-1.63 across time points); 14 days of daily dosing improved RVIP commission errors (d=0.55) and ANT accuracy (d=0.66) versus placebo, and improved mental-fatigue and self-assurance mood ratings, with acute-on-chronic effects numerically larger than acute-alone effects; an exploratory in vitro gut-microbiome arm found increased short-chain fatty acids and specific bacterial taxa, proposed (not demonstrated in vivo) as a contributing mechanism [bell2022]. Across these trials, working memory (particularly spatial/visuospatial working memory via Corsi blocks) and sustained-attention/reaction-time measures show the most consistent acute benefit; the primary RVIP task, Corsi blocks under chronic dosing, mood, and reaction time in the chronic analysis did NOT improve in bell2022, so even within this favorable literature the effect is outcome-specific, not global. No independent systematic review or meta-analysis of Panax quinquefolius specifically was identified (searches returned none); the evidence rests entirely on a small cluster of trials conducted by overlapping Swinburne University (Melbourne)/University of Reading research groups, several with ingredient-supplier co-authors and funding (Naturex/Givaudan), which is a material funding conflict that limits independent corroboration. No published null or negative trial for Cereboost on cognition was identified in this search, which itself raises a mild concern about possible publication bias toward positive industry-linked findings; a newer, independent trial (NCT07255755, 'Cereboost and Brain Function', enrollment 27) was registered but is listed as TERMINATED with no posted results, which should be followed up as a potential unpublished/negative signal. Grade: B for acute working-memory benefit in healthy young and middle-aged adults (promising, replicated across several small industry-linked RCTs, but narrow in scope and from a concentrated set of research groups); grade C for chronic (14-day) sustained-attention benefit (single trial only); insufficient evidence beyond 14 days.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "American ginseng is not a nutrient and there is no deficiency state; all trials are enhancement studies in healthy, adequately nourished young and middle-aged adults, which is the most relevant population for a 'healthy adult cognitive support' claim and is a methodological strength relative to many botanicals reviewed by NEUROFUEL LAB (which often rely on impaired/deficient populations). The reported benefits are therefore genuinely about enhancement above a normal healthy baseline on specific tasks (spatial working memory, attention-network accuracy/reaction time, sustained-attention commission errors), not about correcting any deficit.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "The branded ingredient Cereboost markets itself directly on these trials ('improves working memory', 'supports mental energy'), and unlike many botanicals in this category, the underlying studies were genuinely conducted in healthy adults rather than patients, which makes the marketing claims less egregiously mismatched than typical. However, three important caveats must be disclosed: (1) all positive trials were conducted or co-funded by the ingredient supplier (Naturex, later Givaudan) with supplier employees as co-authors, and no fully independent replication exists; (2) the effect is task-specific (spatial working memory via Corsi blocks, attention-network accuracy/reaction time) rather than a general 'cognitive boost' across all domains -- the same chronic trial that found benefits on ANT and RVIP commission errors found no effect on Corsi blocks, mood (except two secondary measures), the switching task, or memory recall; (3) no independent systematic review has yet evaluated this specific extract, and a newer independently-registered trial was terminated without posted results. An honest claim would be narrow, task-specific, and would disclose the industry-funding pattern rather than citing the pooled trials as if they were independent.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Panax quinquefolius has a distinct ginsenoside profile from P. ginseng (different Rb1:Rg1 ratio), which the authors propose explains its different acute profile: no acute blood-glucose change (unlike Asian ginseng) and a more consistent effect on spatial working memory. A gut-microbiome-mediated mechanism (increased short-chain fatty acid producers after repeated dosing) is proposed from an in vitro SHIME model run in parallel with the human trial, not demonstrated in vivo in the same humans. A prefrontal SSVEP latency change suggests altered prefrontal cortical engagement during working-memory retrieval, a plausible correlate of the behavioral effect but not independently validated as a necessary mechanism.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Oral P. quinquefolius ginsenosides -> gut absorption/microbial biotransformation", "detail": "Distinct ginsenoside profile from P. ginseng; metabolized via gut microbiota as with other ginseng species.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Acute dosing -> no change in blood glucose (contrast with P. ginseng)", "detail": "Confirmed in two independent trials (ossoukhova2015, scholey2010), distinguishing the mechanism from the acute glucoregulatory effect seen with Asian ginseng.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Acute/chronic dosing -> altered prefrontal cortical engagement during working-memory retrieval", "detail": "Reduced SSVEP latency in the prefrontal region during spatial working-memory retrieval after 200 mg [white2020]; behavioral task performance was not significantly affected in that specific trial.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Measured behavioral outcome: spatial working memory (Corsi blocks), sustained attention (RVIP commission errors), attention-network accuracy/reaction time", "detail": "Consistent across scholey2010, ossoukhova2015 (acute) and bell2022 (acute and 14-day chronic); not all tasks within each study improved (e.g. Corsi blocks did not improve under chronic dosing in bell2022).", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Repeated 14-day dosing -> altered gut microbiota short-chain fatty acid production (proposed contributing mechanism)", "detail": "Demonstrated only in a parallel in vitro SHIME gut model using a single donor, not measured in the actual human trial participants; speculative as an explanation for the human chronic cognitive effect.", "evidence_level": "speculative"}], "pathways": ["distinct ginsenoside (Rb1/Rg1 ratio) profile vs Asian ginseng", "prefrontal cortical engagement (SSVEP)", "proposed gut-microbiome short-chain-fatty-acid pathway (in vitro only)", "acetylcholine modulation (proposed, not directly measured in humans)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy young adults, Australian cohort", "dose_or_intake": "100, 200, or 400 mg Cereboost (standardized 10.65% ginsenosides)", "duration": "single dose, measured over 6 h", "grade": "B", "direction": "benefit", "effect_summary": "All three doses improved Corsi block (spatial working memory) performance at all post-dose testing times; 100 mg also improved choice reaction time accuracy and calmness; no change in blood glucose.", "effect_estimate": "described as 'robust working memory enhancement'; specific effect sizes/CIs not reported in the available abstract", "certainty_notes": "Single trial (n=32) but a clean, dose-ranging, placebo-controlled design; later replicated in a different age group (ossoukhova2015), raising confidence; downgraded for small n and lack of fully independent replication outside the Swinburne/supplier-linked group.", "key_study_ids": ["scholey2010", "ossoukhova2015"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "knowledge_workers", "population_detail": "Healthy middle-aged adults (40-60 y)", "dose_or_intake": "200 mg Cereboost single dose", "duration": "single dose, measured over 6 h", "grade": "B", "direction": "benefit", "effect_summary": "200 mg improved the 'working memory' composite factor at 3 h post-dose, driven mainly by spatial working memory; no effect on mood or blood glucose.", "effect_estimate": "significant improvement on working-memory factor and spatial working-memory task at 3 h; exact numeric effect size not reported in abstract", "certainty_notes": "Single trial (n=52), partial replication of the younger-adult finding; same research/funding network as the founding trial, limiting independence.", "key_study_ids": ["ossoukhova2015"]}, {"timeframe": "short_term_days", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy young adults, UK cohort, preregistered trial", "dose_or_intake": "Cereboost, daily dosing", "duration": "14 days", "grade": "B", "direction": "benefit", "effect_summary": "14 days of daily Cereboost significantly reduced RVIP commission errors (d=0.55) and improved attention-network-task accuracy (d=0.66) versus placebo; improved mental-fatigue and self-assurance mood ratings; acute-on-chronic effects were numerically larger than acute-alone effects at the same visit.", "effect_estimate": "RVIP commission errors d=0.55 [0.03,1.07]; ANT accuracy d=0.66 [0.14,1.18]; mental fatigue d=0.65 [0.13,1.18]", "certainty_notes": "Single trial (n=61, preregistered NCT03579095), the first and only chronic (14-day) RCT identified; downgraded for being a single trial, industry co-funded, and for several null secondary outcomes (Corsi blocks, switching task, memory recall, most mood measures were not significant) within the same trial, indicating outcome-specific rather than global benefit.", "key_study_ids": ["bell2022"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "knowledge_workers", "population_detail": "Healthy middle-aged adults (40-60 y), neurophysiological substudy", "dose_or_intake": "200 mg Cereboost single dose", "duration": "single dose, measured 6 h post-dose", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Reduced prefrontal SSVEP latency during spatial working-memory retrieval (a neurophysiological biomarker), but no significant behavioral effect on the continuous-performance task tested in this specific substudy.", "effect_estimate": "significant SSVEP latency reduction; behavioral effect not significant in this substudy", "certainty_notes": "Biomarker-only positive result in this trial; must not be presented as a demonstrated cognitive/behavioral effect per NEUROFUEL LAB rule separating biomarkers from measured outcomes. Downgraded accordingly.", "key_study_ids": ["white2020"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "100-400 mg Cereboost", "onset": "within ~1 h, sustained to 6 h", "duration_studied": "single dose, 6 h observation", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "effect_estimate": "improved Corsi block performance at all doses and all time points; no blood-glucose change", "certainty": "B - replicated in a second age group, but from a concentrated research/funding network", "safety": "no significant adverse events reported", "study_ids": ["scholey2010"]}, {"dose": "200 mg Cereboost", "onset": "within ~1 h, peak effect noted at 3 h", "duration_studied": "single dose, 6 h observation", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "knowledge_workers", "effect_estimate": "improved working-memory composite factor at 3 h; no blood-glucose change", "certainty": "B", "safety": "no significant adverse events reported", "study_ids": ["ossoukhova2015"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "14 days", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "outcome": "sustained attention (RVIP commission errors), attention-network accuracy, mental fatigue/self-assurance mood", "effect_estimate": "d=0.55-0.66 for primary cognitive outcomes; several secondary outcomes (Corsi blocks, switching task, recall, most mood measures) not significant", "evidence_quality": "moderate (single preregistered trial, industry co-funded, outcome-specific pattern of significance)", "limitations": "Only one chronic trial exists; 14 days is still short-term by the brief's own chronic threshold floor; no data beyond 14 days; industry funding and overlapping authorship with the acute trials limit independence", "study_ids": ["bell2022"]}], "safety": {"summary": "No serious adverse events were reported in any of the four Cereboost trials identified, and no blood-glucose disturbance was observed (distinguishing it from Asian ginseng's acute hypoglycemic effect). However, the safety database is small (total exposed n across trials well under 200), short-term (maximum 14 days of daily dosing studied), and comes from the same concentrated set of industry-linked research groups as the efficacy claims, so independent long-term safety data are lacking.", "adverse_effects": ["no serious adverse events reported across the four trials reviewed", "general ginseng-class concerns (insomnia, GI upset, headache) are plausible by analogy to Panax ginseng but not specifically quantified for P. quinquefolius/Cereboost in the trials reviewed here"], "contraindications": ["known hypersensitivity to ginseng", "by analogy with Panax ginseng, caution is prudent in anticoagulant and antidiabetic medication users even though no glucose effect was observed for this species in the available trials, since a formal interaction study for P. quinquefolius specifically was not identified in this search"], "drug_interactions": ["no P. quinquefolius-specific drug-interaction trial identified in this search; general ginseng-class caution regarding anticoagulants (e.g. warfarin) is prudent pending species-specific data, and should be confirmed by a pharmacology specialist review before any medication-interaction claim is made"], "upper_limits": "No formal tolerable upper intake level identified for P. quinquefolius/Cereboost; studied doses were 100-400 mg acutely and an unspecified Cereboost daily dose for 14 days in the chronic trial; no dose-ranging long-term safety trial identified.", "sleep_impact": "Not directly tested in a controlled sleep study within this search; no insomnia signal reported in the trials reviewed, but exposure duration (max 14 days) is too short to rule out a sleep effect with longer or evening dosing.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Maximum studied duration is 14 days of daily dosing in healthy young adults (bell2022); no multi-month or multi-year human safety data identified for this specific extract.", "daily_use_appropriate": "uncertain", "special_populations": "No trials in pregnant/breastfeeding individuals, children/adolescents, or people with diabetes or on anticoagulant therapy were identified for P. quinquefolius/Cereboost specifically; by analogy with Panax ginseng, caution and a specialist review are warranted before recommending to these groups.", "specialist_review_needed": ["pharmacology review of whether the Asian-ginseng warfarin/antidiabetic interaction data generalize to P. quinquefolius given its different ginsenoside profile", "longer-duration (multi-month) safety study design review before any sustained daily-use claim", "reproductive/pediatric toxicology review given the complete absence of data in those populations"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "No adverse signal in the short trials available, but the safety database is small, short (max 14 days), and has no data in vulnerable populations or on drug interactions specific to this species, so it cannot pass an unconditional gate without further safety data or conservative labeling/population exclusions."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "American ginseng/Panax quinquefolius has no EU-authorised cognitive-function health claim under Regulation (EC) 1924/2006; as with Panax ginseng, ginseng-related claims remain in the 'on hold' category pending further substantiation. This status should be re-verified against the current EFSA/EU claims register before use, as it has not been independently confirmed in this search beyond the general ginseng on-hold pattern.", "us": "Marketed as a dietary supplement under DSHEA; Cereboost is sold commercially with structure/function claims (e.g. supporting memory/mental energy) requiring the standard FTC substantiation and FDA disclaimer. No FDA drug-level approval exists for a cognitive claim.", "morocco": "Panax quinquefolius is not part of Moroccan traditional phytotherapy and would be regulated as an imported botanical/dietary-supplement ingredient under ONSSA's general supplement framework; no Morocco-specific regulatory text for this ingredient was identified in this search and status should be treated as uncertain pending a dedicated regulatory-affairs review.", "permitted_claims_examples": [], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "Panax quinquefolius has a history of food/supplement use in North America and is sold as a supplement ingredient in the EU and US; it is not expected to trigger EU Novel Food status for conventional root-extract forms, but this has not been independently confirmed against the current EU Novel Food catalogue in this search.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Like Panax ginseng, this is a concentrated, pharmacologically active botanical extract rather than a conventional food ingredient; the studied doses (100-400 mg standardized extract) are feasible in a capsule, shot, or concentrated beverage format but less suited to being hidden in an everyday food matrix without a noticeable herbal taste.", "formats": ["standardized extract capsules/tablets (Cereboost-type)", "functional beverage or shot", "powder in bars (taste masking required)"], "stability": "Not directly assessed in the trials reviewed; ginsenosides are generally considered reasonably heat- and storage-stable in standardized extract form, consistent with general ginseng-class knowledge, but no dedicated stability study for Cereboost was identified in this search.", "taste": "Bitter/earthy, though reported as less glycaemically active and generally well-tolerated across the four trials reviewed; specific sensory/taste data not reported in the available abstracts.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Ginsenoside absorption depends on gut-microbiota biotransformation as with other ginseng species; the proposed microbiome-mediated mechanism in bell2022 raises the possibility of inter-individual variability in response, though this was not directly tested as a moderator of the human cognitive outcome.", "dose_feasibility": "The studied acute dose range (100-400 mg) is feasible in a single capsule or concentrated shot serving; the 14-day chronic trial used an unspecified but presumably similar daily dose, which is also feasible for a daily-use product format.", "cost_availability": "Cereboost is a proprietary branded ingredient (originally Naturex, now part of Givaudan); availability is tied to this single commercial supplier rather than being a generic commodity botanical, which is a formulation and supply-chain consideration.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 5.5, "effect_magnitude": 5.5, "safety": 6, "reproducibility": 4, "food_suitability": 4, "cost_access": 4.5, "rationale": "evidence_quality ~5.5: best grade achieved is B for acute and 14-day chronic working-memory/attention outcomes in healthy adults, the most favorable grade-B evidence base of the two ginseng monographs in this unit, but still resting on a small number of trials from a concentrated, industry-linked research network with no independent systematic review; effect_magnitude ~5.5: moderate-to-large standardized effect sizes (d=0.55-1.63) on primary attention/working-memory outcomes, but outcome-specific (several secondary outcomes null within the same trials); safety ~6: no adverse signal observed but very short exposure duration (max 14 days) and no vulnerable-population or interaction data limit a higher score; reproducibility ~4: multiple trials show a consistent direction but all share overlapping authors/funding (Naturex/Givaudan), so this is not independent replication in the strict sense; food_suitability ~4: concentrated extract format, bitter taste, awkward in conventional food matrices; cost_access ~4.5: single proprietary branded-ingredient supply chain (not a generic commodity), which may raise cost and limit sourcing flexibility relative to generic Panax ginseng."}, "research_gaps": ["No independent (non-industry-funded) replication of the Cereboost working-memory/attention effects by a research group outside the Swinburne/Reading/Naturex-Givaudan network.", "No trial beyond 14 days of daily dosing; sustained multi-week to multi-month efficacy and safety are unknown.", "No dedicated systematic review or meta-analysis specific to Panax quinquefolius identified.", "No controlled drug-interaction (anticoagulant, antidiabetic) or sleep-impact study specific to this species/extract.", "No published results yet for the newer independently-registered trial NCT07255755 ('Cereboost and Brain Function'), which was registered as TERMINATED without posted results and should be followed up as a possible unpublished/negative signal.", "No data in students specifically under exam-type workload, in older adults, or in any vulnerable population (pregnancy, children, chronic disease)."], "_derived": {"composite_score": 5.2, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": "B", "best_grade_chronic": "B", "best_grade_young_adults": "B", "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": null, "best_grade_overall": "B", "benefit_domains": {"working_memory": "B", "sustained_attention": "B"}, "n_outcome_rows": 4, "n_studies": 4, "n_verified": 4, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 0, "n_systematic_reviews": 0, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "ashwagandha", "name": "Ashwagandha (Withania somnifera)", "category": "H", "category_name": "Botanical and nootropic supplements", "aliases": ["ashwagandha", "Withania somnifera (L.) Dunal", "Indian ginseng", "winter cherry", "KSM-66 (root extract brand)", "Sensoril (root+leaf extract brand)", "Shoden (root+leaf extract brand)", "NooGandha (root+leaf extract brand)", "Prolanza (sustained-release root extract brand)", "Witholytin (root extract brand)"], "food_sources": ["No ordinary dietary source; used as dried root powder (churna) in Ayurvedic preparations, sometimes in milk", "Commercially as standardised aqueous or hydro-alcoholic extracts of root, or root+leaf (125-700 mg/day in trials)"], "active_constituents": ["withanolides (steroidal lactones; e.g. withanolide A, withaferin A, withanone); extracts typically standardised to 1.5-10% total withanolides or up to 35% withanolide glycosides", "withanosides / sitoindosides (glycowithanolides)", "alkaloids (e.g. tropine-type, withanine), oligosaccharides; leaf contains more withaferin A and withanone than root"], "one_line_verdict": "Ashwagandha extracts probably lower self-reported stress in stressed adults, but the evidence for cognitive enhancement in healthy young adults or students is limited to small, mostly industry-sponsored trials with inconsistent between-group results, and rare but serious cholestatic liver injury, thyroid effects, a Danish ban and pending EU restrictions make it a poor fit for a mainstream Brain Food product.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is shown. More than 20 randomised placebo-controlled trials, almost all of proprietary standardised extracts, report lower perceived stress and anxiety after 6-12 weeks at 225-600 mg/day. Meta-analyses agree on direction but the effect sizes are implausibly large and heterogeneous: stress SMD -1.75 (95% CI -2.29 to -1.22, I2 83%) and anxiety SMD -1.55 (I2 94%) with low certainty [akhgarjand2022]; SMDs of about -6 in another pooling, which indicates analytical error rather than a real effect [alsanie2026]; anxiety SMD -1.13 after removing outliers in people with mental disorders, confirmed by Bayesian analysis [marchi2025]. Sleep quality improves modestly (SMD -0.59, 5 RCTs, n=400), mainly in insomnia and at >=600 mg/day for >=8 weeks [cheah2021]. Independent or Western-run trials are smaller and weaker: a 12-week Australian trial found no difference from placebo on the primary stress outcome (p=0.867) [smith2023], a 30-day US student trial showed no significant group-by-time effect on stress [oconnor2022], and a Shoden trial reached p=0.040 for HAM-A but not DASS-21 [lopresti2019]. Cognition. A 2026 meta-analysis of 20 RCTs (1,249 participants) reports memory SMD 0.52 (0.27-0.78, GRADE moderate), attention/processing speed SMD 0.29 (0.07-0.51) and executive function SMD -0.42 (GRADE low) [zhu2026], but it pools MCI, bipolar disorder, stressed and healthy adults, could not assess publication bias, and misreports characteristics of at least two included trials. In the Brain Food target population the evidence is thin. In 59 healthy young adults (mean 22.7 y) 225 mg acutely and for 30 days produced no significant group-by-time interaction on any COMPASS test; the positive claims rest on within-group changes [leonard2024]. A 13-person acute crossover reported selective benefits on working memory and vigilance [xing2022]; a 30-day trial in 60 stressed adults reported some CNS Vital Signs differences without a group effect on self-reports [remenapp2022]; a 14-day crossover in 20 men found faster reaction times [pingali2014]. The largest cognition trial (n=130 stressed adults, 90 days) improved one CANTAB paired-associate memory score (about d 0.4, computed by us) and RVP accuracy but not reaction time, spatial working memory or BDNF [gopukumar2021]. Positive memory trials in MCI [choudhary2017, rai2025] and bipolar disorder (digit span d 0.51) [chengappa2013] cannot be generalised to healthy adults. Reproducibility. Most trials are from India, sponsored by or employing staff of extract manufacturers, use different extracts (root vs root+leaf, 1.5-35% withanolides), and are small, so publication bias is likely and cannot be tested. Safety. Cholestatic liver injury with latency of 2-12 weeks is documented [bjornsson2020, lubarska2023], including deaths in people with pre-existing liver disease [philips2023]; thyroid hormone changes occur [sharma2018, kamal2022]. Overall: stress B (chronic, stressed adults), cognition C in every population, and insufficient for students.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Ashwagandha is not a nutrient and there is no deficiency state; every effect is pharmacological. The trials are best read as 'symptom relief in people selected for high perceived stress' (PSS 14-24 or >20) rather than enhancement of normal function. Where cognitive benefits are reported they appear in stressed adults [gopukumar2021, majeed2023], adults with self-reported cognitive complaints [kale2026], MCI [choudhary2017, rai2025] or bipolar disorder [chengappa2013]. In unselected healthy young adults the only reasonably sized trial showed no between-group difference by its primary interaction test [leonard2024], so an 'enhancement in healthy students' claim is not supported. Any cognitive gain in stressed people may be secondary to reduced anxiety or better sleep rather than a direct nootropic effect.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Marketing calls ashwagandha a 'clinically proven' adaptogen that 'boosts memory and focus', 'lowers cortisol' and 'supports brain health'. The supporting trials are mostly small, single-centre, manufacturer-sponsored studies of branded extracts with large self-reported effects that shrink or disappear in independent trials [smith2023, oconnor2022]. 'Lowers cortisol' rests on single morning cortisol samples, a biomarker, not a cognitive or health outcome. Student-focused marketing is unsupported: the one student RCT measured stress, sleep and cravings, not cognition, and was null on its primary group-by-time test [oconnor2022]. No health claim for ashwagandha is authorised in the EU; Denmark bans it in food supplements and the Netherlands has notified a draft ban (root excepted). An honest description would be: 'a traditional herb with moderate-quality evidence for reducing perceived stress in stressed adults over 8 weeks; cognitive effects unproven in healthy people; rare liver injury reported; not for pregnancy, thyroid or liver disease.'", "mechanism": {"summary": "Withanolides and glycowithanolides are proposed to modulate the HPA axis (lower cortisol), act at GABA-A receptors, inhibit acetylcholinesterase, reduce oxidative stress and promote neurite outgrowth. Human data show only modest cortisol reductions and no BDNF change; the receptor and neurotrophic actions are from in-vitro and animal studies. Oral bioavailability of individual withanolides is low and varies by extract, so it is unclear which constituent, if any, reaches the brain at active concentrations. Mechanism supports plausibility only.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Oral extract (root or root+leaf) -> intestinal absorption of withanolides/withanosides", "detail": "Composition varies widely (1.5-35% withanolides, root vs leaf) [zhu2026]; human pharmacokinetics are limited and product-specific; withaferin A has poor oral bioavailability in animals.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Systemic withanolides -> HPA axis modulation (lower morning cortisol, lower DHEA-S)", "detail": "Morning cortisol fell more than placebo in several RCTs [lopresti2019, chandrasekhar2012, gopukumar2021, salve2019]; single samples, small trials, biomarker only.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Withanolides -> GABA-A mimetic, cholinergic (AChE inhibition), antioxidant and anti-inflammatory activity", "detail": "Shown in cell and rodent models; no human target-engagement data.", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Withanolide A / withanosides -> neurite outgrowth, synaptic reconstruction, BDNF", "detail": "Rodent and in-vitro data only; serum BDNF did not differ from placebo after 90 days in humans [gopukumar2021].", "evidence_level": "speculative"}, {"step": "Lower stress/anxiety and better sleep -> indirect improvement in cognitive performance", "detail": "Plausible pathway explaining why benefits appear mainly in stressed or symptomatic groups; not formally tested by mediation analysis.", "evidence_level": "plausible"}, {"step": "Measured human outcome", "detail": "Subjective stress reduced in stressed adults (moderate certainty); memory and attention benefits small and inconsistent; no between-group effect in the main healthy-young-adult trial [leonard2024].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["HPA axis / cortisol", "GABAergic modulation (preclinical)", "cholinergic modulation / AChE inhibition (preclinical)", "antioxidant and anti-inflammatory signalling (preclinical)", "neurite outgrowth / neurotrophic effects (preclinical)", "thyroid axis stimulation (human RCT in subclinical hypothyroidism)", "androgen axis (testosterone, LH increases in men)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Adults 18-65 selected for high perceived stress or anxiety symptoms; mostly India", "dose_or_intake": "225-700 mg/day standardised root or root+leaf extract", "duration": "4-12 weeks (mostly 8 weeks)", "grade": "B", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Most RCTs report lower PSS, HAM-A and DASS scores than placebo; pooled effects are very large and heterogeneous. Independent Western trials are weaker or null on the primary outcome (Witholytin 12 weeks p=0.867; US students 30 days no group-by-time effect).", "effect_estimate": "Stress SMD -1.75 (95% CI -2.29 to -1.22, I2 83%), anxiety SMD -1.55 (-2.37 to -0.74, I2 94%), low certainty [akhgarjand2022]; anxiety SMD -1.13 (-1.65 to -0.60) outlier-corrected in mental disorders [marchi2025]; PSS-10 13.0 vs 18.7 at 90 days [gopukumar2021]", "certainty_notes": "Starts high (many RCTs). Downgraded for risk of bias (industry-sponsored, single-centre, poor allocation reporting), inconsistency (I2 >80%, null independent trials) and suspected publication bias. Implausible SMDs suggest small-study effects. Grade B is the ceiling; a stricter reading gives C.", "key_study_ids": ["akhgarjand2022", "marchi2025", "alsanie2026", "chandrasekhar2012", "lopresti2019", "salve2019", "gopukumar2021", "smith2023", "oconnor2022"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Stressed or cognitively complaining but otherwise healthy adults 20-75 y", "dose_or_intake": "300-600 mg/day extract", "duration": "8-13 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Selected memory scores improve versus placebo in manufacturer-linked trials (CANTAB paired associates, COMPASS episodic memory); many other subtests null. A meta-analysis that also includes MCI and bipolar trials pooled memory SMD 0.52.", "effect_estimate": "PAL first-attempt memory 12.9 vs 10.1 (SD about 6.5; d about 0.4, computed by NEUROFUEL) [gopukumar2021]; pooled memory SMD 0.52 (0.27-0.78, I2 30%) mixed populations [zhu2026]", "certainty_notes": "Downgraded for risk of bias (industry, selective outcome reporting across large batteries), indirectness (pooled patient groups), imprecision and untestable publication bias. Data-entry errors noted in the meta-analysis.", "key_study_ids": ["gopukumar2021", "kale2026", "majeed2023", "zhu2026"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "processing_speed", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy and stressed adults; pooled attention / processing speed", "dose_or_intake": "225-600 mg/day", "duration": "2-12 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Small pooled improvement in attention and processing speed; reaction time not improved over placebo in the 90-day trial.", "effect_estimate": "SMD 0.29 (0.07-0.51, I2 0%) [zhu2026]; CANTAB reaction time not significant after baseline adjustment [gopukumar2021]", "certainty_notes": "Few trials, small n, mixed populations, industry sponsorship; small effect of uncertain practical relevance.", "key_study_ids": ["zhu2026", "gopukumar2021", "pingali2014"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy, stressed and clinical adults pooled", "dose_or_intake": "225-600 mg/day", "duration": "4-8 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Pooled executive function improves moderately but the estimate is driven by MCI and small Indian trials; the BRIEF-A self-report improvement is subjective.", "effect_estimate": "SMD -0.42 (-0.70 to -0.13, I2 37.5%; negative favours ashwagandha), GRADE low [zhu2026]", "certainty_notes": "Low certainty per review authors; indirect population; outcome heterogeneity (Flanker, WCST, CNS Vital Signs).", "key_study_ids": ["zhu2026", "choudhary2017", "remenapp2022", "kale2026"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults 18-49 (mean 22.7 y), not selected for stress; plus a small healthy male crossover", "dose_or_intake": "225 mg/day liposomal root+leaf extract; 1000 mg/day aqueous root+leaf extract", "duration": "14-30 days", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "In 59 young adults no significant group-by-time interaction on any COMPASS test (word recall, recognition, Corsi, Stroop, vigilance); claims rely on within-group changes. A 20-man 14-day crossover reported faster psychomotor reaction times.", "effect_estimate": "Word recall interaction p=0.736; Corsi span interaction NS; Stroop interaction p=0.514 [leonard2024]; Pingali: p values only", "certainty_notes": "Very few trials, small samples, analytic flexibility, sponsor-linked co-authors; the strongest test is essentially null. Practical relevance for students not shown.", "key_study_ids": ["leonard2024", "pingali2014", "remenapp2022"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy volunteers, single dose, tested 1-6 h post-dose", "dose_or_intake": "225-400 mg single dose (root+leaf extract)", "duration": "single dose, 1-6 h", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "A 13-person crossover reported maintained vigilance reaction times and better Sternberg working memory at 3 and 6 h; in 59 adults, 60 min after 225 mg, picture-recognition reaction time was faster but choice reaction accuracy only trended (p=0.060).", "effect_estimate": "Choice reaction targets +1.05% (95% CI -0.04 to 2.15) vs placebo, p=0.060 [leonard2024]; Xing: p values only, n=13", "certainty_notes": "Two small trials from one laboratory with the same ingredient supplier; many outcomes, no multiplicity correction; imprecision.", "key_study_ids": ["xing2022", "leonard2024"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "students", "population_detail": "US college students 18-50, healthy", "dose_or_intake": "700 mg/day full-spectrum root extract", "duration": "30 days", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Mixed MANOVA (group x time) for sleep and stress was not significant; follow-up covariate analysis favoured ashwagandha for final sleep; cravings reduced. No objective cognitive tests.", "effect_estimate": "Final restorative sleep 58.4 vs 48.2 (covariate-adjusted, p<0.05); stress group effect not significant [oconnor2022]", "certainty_notes": "Single small trial, primary multivariate test null, post-hoc follow-ups; cognition not measured in students at all.", "key_study_ids": ["oconnor2022"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "learning", "population": "students", "population_detail": "Students under academic load: no trial measured learning, exam performance or objective cognition", "dose_or_intake": "not studied", "duration": "not studied", "grade": "C", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No direct evidence. Indirect evidence from healthy young adults is null by interaction test.", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "Indirectness; any claim for students would be extrapolation.", "key_study_ids": ["leonard2024", "oconnor2022"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "other", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Sleep quality in healthy adults and adults with insomnia", "dose_or_intake": "120-600 mg/day", "duration": "6-12 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Small-to-moderate improvement in overall sleep, larger in insomnia, at >=600 mg/day and >=8 weeks; improved mental alertness on rising.", "effect_estimate": "SMD -0.59 (95% CI -0.75 to -0.42, I2 62%), 5 RCTs, n=400 [cheah2021]", "certainty_notes": "Few, mostly Indian industry-linked trials; heterogeneity; sleep is not cognition.", "key_study_ids": ["cheah2021", "gopukumar2021"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "older_adults_mci", "population_detail": "Adults with mild cognitive impairment, India", "dose_or_intake": "600 mg/day KSM-66; 250 mg/day Somin-On", "duration": "8 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Two pilot RCTs (n=50, n=40) report improvements on Wechsler Memory Scale subtests, Flanker, WCST and MoCA/MMSE.", "effect_estimate": "Choudhary: logical memory I p=0.007, other subtests p 0.006-0.042; Rai: MoCA SMD 2.49 (1.64-3.33), GRADE very low per [zhu2026]", "certainty_notes": "Very small, single-centre pilots; implausibly large effects; no independent replication; not applicable to healthy adults.", "key_study_ids": ["choudhary2017", "rai2025", "ng2020"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Euthymic adults with bipolar disorder, adjunctive to maintenance medication (USA)", "dose_or_intake": "500 mg/day Sensoril (250 mg/day week 1)", "duration": "8 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Improved digit span backward, Flanker neutral RT and one social cognition measure; all other cognitive tasks null.", "effect_estimate": "Digit span backward d=0.51 (95% CI 0.25-0.77), p=0.035 [chengappa2013]", "certainty_notes": "Single trial, n=53 completers, multiple outcomes without correction; clinical population only.", "key_study_ids": ["chengappa2013"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "neuroplasticity_biomarkers", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Stressed healthy adults 20-55", "dose_or_intake": "300 mg/day sustained-release root extract", "duration": "90 days", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Serum BDNF did not differ from placebo; morning cortisol was slightly lower (biomarker, not cognition).", "effect_estimate": "BDNF 503 vs 491 pg/mL, p=0.74; cortisol 6.34 vs 7.38 ug/dL, p=0.044 [gopukumar2021]", "certainty_notes": "Single trial; serum BDNF is a poor proxy for brain plasticity.", "key_study_ids": ["gopukumar2021", "lopresti2019"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "400 mg NooGandha root+leaf extract, single dose", "onset": "Differences reported at 3 and 6 h", "duration_studied": "6 h, hourly testing", "domain": "working memory (Sternberg), sustained attention (PVT), executive function (BCST, Go/No-Go)", "population": "13 healthy volunteers, crossover", "effect_estimate": "Sternberg 6-letter present RT better at 3 and 6 h; PVT RT maintained vs slowing with placebo; BCST effect disappeared when analysed as change from baseline; no accuracy benefit", "certainty": "very low (n=13, many outcomes, baseline imbalance)", "safety": "No adverse events reported", "study_ids": ["xing2022"]}, {"dose": "225 mg liposomal root+leaf extract, single dose", "onset": "60 min", "duration_studied": "Testing 60 min post-dose", "domain": "choice reaction time, picture recognition, Stroop", "population": "59 healthy young adults (mean 22.7 y)", "effect_estimate": "Choice reaction targets +1.05% (95% CI -0.04 to 2.15) vs placebo, p=0.060; picture recognition correct RT faster (p=0.008); overall MANOVA interactions not significant", "certainty": "low (single trial, retrospectively registered, sponsor-linked)", "safety": "Clinical chemistry unremarkable over 30 days", "study_ids": ["leonard2024"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "14 days per arm (crossover)", "population": "20 healthy men, India", "outcome": "Simple/choice reaction time, digit symbol substitution, digit vigilance, card sorting, finger tapping", "effect_estimate": "Significant reaction-time improvements on 5 of 6 tests; finger tapping null; effect sizes not reported", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Tiny sample, men only, 1000 mg/day root+leaf, no effect sizes", "study_ids": ["pingali2014"]}, {"duration": "30 days", "population": "59 healthy young adults (USA)", "outcome": "COMPASS battery, POMS", "effect_estimate": "No significant group x time interaction on cognitive tests; POMS tension and fatigue lower", "evidence_quality": "low-to-moderate (randomised, double-blind) but null on primary inferential test", "limitations": "Retrospective registration; positive reading based on within-group changes", "study_ids": ["leonard2024"]}, {"duration": "30 days", "population": "60 stressed adults, mean 34 y (USA)", "outcome": "CNS Vital Signs, cortisol, anxiety/stress self-report", "effect_estimate": "Some differences in cognitive flexibility, visual memory, reaction time (p<0.05); no group effect on self-reported stress/anxiety", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Three arms of about 20; multiple outcomes; sponsor product", "study_ids": ["remenapp2022"]}, {"duration": "8 weeks", "population": "120 adults 30-75 with self-reported cognitive/energy problems (India)", "outcome": "COMPASS episodic and working memory, attention accuracy; BRIEF-A; Mental Fatigue Scale", "effect_estimate": "Greater improvement than placebo (p<0.05) on episodic memory, working memory and attention accuracy; numbers not in abstract", "evidence_quality": "low-to-moderate", "limitations": "Single site; outcome selection across battery; abstract-level data only", "study_ids": ["kale2026"]}, {"duration": "90 days", "population": "130 stressed healthy adults 20-55 (India)", "outcome": "CANTAB (primary), PSS-10, cortisol, PSQI, BDNF", "effect_estimate": "PAL memory 12.9 vs 10.1 and PAL errors 17.5 vs 27.7 (p<0.05); RVP A' 0.87 vs 0.83; reaction time, MOT, SWM null; PSS 13.0 vs 18.7; BDNF null", "evidence_quality": "moderate design, but sponsor employees co-authors", "limitations": "Manufacturer-linked; large battery with few positives; implausibly large stress effect", "study_ids": ["gopukumar2021"]}, {"duration": "12 weeks", "population": "108 overweight adults 40-75 with stress and fatigue (Australia)", "outcome": "PSS (primary), fatigue, HRV, hormones", "effect_estimate": "PSS not different from placebo (p=0.867); fatigue lower (p=0.016); free testosterone and LH higher in men", "evidence_quality": "moderate (null primary)", "limitations": "Middle-aged overweight sample; CRO with nutraceutical funding", "study_ids": ["smith2023"]}, {"duration": "8 weeks", "population": "50 adults with subclinical hypothyroidism (India)", "outcome": "TSH, T3, T4", "effect_estimate": "TSH lowered, T3 and T4 increased vs placebo (p<0.001, p=0.0031, p=0.0096)", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Small pilot; demonstrates a thyroid-stimulating effect relevant to safety", "study_ids": ["sharma2018"]}], "safety": {"summary": "In RCTs of up to 8-12 weeks (occasionally 180 days) at 125-600 mg/day, standardised root extracts cause only mild GI upset, headache, drowsiness or dizziness at rates similar to placebo, with normal liver, renal and thyroid tests [verma2021, coope2026]. Those trials are far too small to detect rare idiosyncratic injury. Post-marketing case series document cholestatic or mixed liver injury with jaundice and prolonged pruritus after 2-12 weeks (and after one year), usually self-limiting [bjornsson2020, lubarska2023], but fatal acute-on-chronic liver failure in people with pre-existing liver disease [philips2023]. Ashwagandha raises T3/T4 and lowers TSH [sharma2018], and thyrotoxicosis has been reported [kamal2022]. It raises testosterone and LH in men [smith2023]. Traditional abortifacient use and regulator concern exclude pregnancy. Regulators in Denmark (DTU 2020) could not establish a safe intake level, citing thyroid, sex-hormone, immune, metabolic and CNS effects.", "adverse_effects": ["Mild GI discomfort, nausea, diarrhoea", "Drowsiness/sedation, dizziness, headache", "Idiosyncratic cholestatic/mixed hepatitis with jaundice and pruritus (rare; latency 2-12 weeks or longer)", "Acute-on-chronic liver failure and death in people with chronic liver disease (case series)", "Thyroid stimulation; thyrotoxicosis case report", "Increased testosterone/LH in men; possible sex-hormone effects"], "contraindications": ["Pregnancy (traditional abortifacient; regulator warnings) and breastfeeding", "Pre-existing liver disease or prior herb/drug-induced liver injury", "Hyperthyroidism or thyroid disease under treatment", "Hormone-sensitive prostate cancer (NIH ODS caution)", "Autoimmune disease (immunostimulant activity) - precautionary", "Children and adolescents (no adequate safety data)"], "drug_interactions": ["Thyroid hormone (levothyroxine) - additive thyroid effects", "Sedatives, benzodiazepines, alcohol, anticonvulsants - additive sedation", "Immunosuppressants - opposing effect", "Antidiabetic and antihypertensive drugs - additive glucose/BP lowering (NIH ODS/NCCIH)", "Hepatotoxic drugs and other hepatotoxic botanicals (e.g. green tea extract, turmeric) - additive liver risk", "Piperine-containing formulations may alter CYP-mediated drug metabolism"], "upper_limits": "No UL. Denmark (DTU 2020) found no basis to set a safe intake. Trials used 125-700 mg/day extract for up to 12 weeks (rarely 180 days). Australia (TGA) requires a pregnancy warning.", "sleep_impact": "Sedating rather than disruptive; improves subjective sleep in some trials [cheah2021]; may cause daytime drowsiness, so evening dosing is typical.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "No controlled data beyond about 6 months; liver injury reported after both short and one-year use.", "daily_use_appropriate": "uncertain", "special_populations": "Exclude pregnancy, breastfeeding, children, liver disease, thyroid disease, autoimmune disease, hormone-sensitive cancers, people on thyroid, sedative, immunosuppressant, antidiabetic or antihypertensive drugs, and people with psychiatric disorders unless under medical supervision.", "specialist_review_needed": ["Hepatologist (DILI risk, label warnings)", "Endocrinologist (thyroid and sex-hormone effects)", "Obstetrician / teratology review (pregnancy exclusion)", "Clinical pharmacologist (interactions)", "Botanical quality specialist (root vs leaf, withanolide profile, adulteration, heavy metals)"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "Acceptable only as a root-only, standardised extract (<=600 mg/day), for adults, time-limited (<=8-12 weeks), with warnings for pregnancy, liver and thyroid disease and stopping on jaundice or dark urine. Rare but serious idiosyncratic liver injury and endocrine activity make it unsuitable for a general-population food or for students without screening. Would become 'fail' if the EU Article 8 procedure or national bans proceed."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Not novel: secondary sources report the EU Novel Food Catalogue lists Withania somnifera as consumed before 15 May 1997 in food supplements (primary catalogue entry not retrieved; uncertain). No authorised EU health claim; botanical claims remain 'on hold' and we did not verify any ashwagandha-specific on-hold entries. National restrictions: Denmark bans ashwagandha in food supplements following the 2020 DTU assessment for the DVFA (no safe intake level could be set); the Netherlands (RIVM advice 2024) notified the Commission in September 2026 of a draft decree banning ashwagandha in supplements except root, with root warnings (standstill to 7 Dec 2026); Germany restricts use to root and lists withanolides as substances of concern; BfR recommended an Article 8 procedure in 2013; EU Heads of Food Safety Agencies recommended prioritising Article 8 (Reg. 1925/2006) in June 2024, not formally opened as of Sept 2026; France and Sweden/Finland issued cautions or reviews; Belgium status conflicting in sources (uncertain). Sources: nutraingredients.com 2026-07-06 timeline; supplysidesj.com ashwagandha regulation FAQ; vitafoodsinsights.com; accessed 2026-10-25 (trade press, not primary texts).", "us": "Sold as a dietary supplement ingredient under DSHEA; widely marketed before 1994, so likely an old dietary ingredient (not verified; NDI status of specific extracts uncertain). No FDA-authorised health claim; structure/function claims require the DSHEA disclaimer and FTC 'competent and reliable scientific evidence'; cognition claims for healthy adults would be hard to substantiate. GRAS status for use in conventional foods not verified here. NIH ODS fact sheet (ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/Ashwagandha-HealthProfessional, accessed 2026-10-25) notes possible liver and thyroid effects and avoidance in pregnancy and prostate cancer.", "morocco": "No ONSSA or Ministry of Health text on ashwagandha found (uncertain). Ashwagandha supplements are sold online in Morocco; Moroccan pharmacists' unions argue (Hespress, July 2025) that medicinal-plant supplements are reserved for pharmacies under Law 17-04 art. 30. Requires direct ONSSA consultation before any product decision.", "permitted_claims_examples": [], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "Root and root extracts reported as non-novel in the EU; leaf-containing extracts face growing restrictions (Netherlands draft, Germany root-only, India's April 2026 leaf ban with court stays). Novel extraction processes may raise novel food questions. India's leaf ban affects supply of root+leaf extracts (Sensoril, Shoden).", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Poor. Effective doses (225-600 mg extract) are physically small, but the herb is bitter and earthy with a characteristic odour, needs a standardised extract to match trials, and regulators treat it as a supplement botanical. Adding it to everyday foods would expose unscreened consumers, including pregnant women and adolescents, to an ingredient with liver and thyroid warnings.", "formats": ["capsule/tablet of standardised root extract", "powder for drinks (traditional milk preparation)", "gummies (common commercially; dose control and child appeal are concerns)"], "stability": "Withanolides are reasonably stable in dry extracts; degradation under heat and moisture in food processing not characterised here.", "taste": "Bitter, earthy, 'horse-like' odour; masking needed in foods.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Withanolide bioavailability is low and extract-specific; liposomal, sustained-release and piperine-enhanced versions are marketed with limited independent pharmacokinetic data. Food matrix effects not studied.", "dose_feasibility": "Yes physically (<1 g extract per serving), but only a standardised extract reproduces trial doses; root powder would need several grams.", "cost_availability": "Widely available globally; supply concentrated in India; leaf-ban litigation in India adds supply risk for root+leaf extracts.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 3, "effect_magnitude": 3, "safety": 4, "reproducibility": 3, "food_suitability": 2, "cost_access": 5, "rationale": "Evidence quality 3: cognition grade C in every population, with the healthy-young-adult trial null by interaction test; stress reaches B only in stressed adults, which is not the cognitive target. Effect magnitude 3: pooled cognitive SMDs 0.3-0.5 come from mixed and patient populations and shrink in better-controlled trials; practical gain for students not shown. Safety 4: well tolerated in short trials, but rare serious cholestatic hepatitis, thyroid and sex-hormone effects, pregnancy exclusion and a Danish ban. Reproducibility 3: mostly Indian, manufacturer-linked, extract-specific trials; independent Australian and US trials weaker or null. Food suitability 2: bitter, needs standardised extract, regulatory and warning burden incompatible with an everyday food. Cost/access 5: widely available and probably inexpensive (prices not researched), offset by regulatory supply risk."}, "research_gaps": ["Large, independent, preregistered RCT of a root-only extract on objective cognition in healthy students/knowledge workers under academic or work stress", "Head-to-head comparison of extracts (root vs root+leaf, withanolide profile) with pharmacokinetics", "Mediation analysis: are cognitive effects explained by reduced anxiety or better sleep?", "Pharmacovigilance-based incidence of ashwagandha-induced liver injury and genetic risk factors (e.g. HLA)", "Safety of use beyond 6 months, and in adolescents", "Thyroid and sex-hormone effects in euthyroid healthy adults over longer exposure", "Publication-bias assessment using CTRI and ClinicalTrials.gov registrations without results"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 3.3, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": "B", "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"episodic_memory": "C", "processing_speed": "C", "executive_function": "C", "working_memory": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 12, "n_studies": 28, "n_verified": 28, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 6, "n_systematic_reviews": 6, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["inconsistent", "insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "bacopa-monnieri", "name": "Bacopa monnieri", "category": "H", "category_name": "Botanical and nootropic supplements", "aliases": ["Bacopa monnieri (L.) Wettst.", "Bacopa monniera", "Brahmi", "water hyssop", "herb of grace", "KeenMind / CDRI 08 (standardised extract)", "BacoMind (standardised extract)", "Bacognize (standardised extract)", "Bacumen (standardised extract)", "Synapsa (brand name of CDRI 08 in some markets)"], "food_sources": ["Not a normal food in Europe, the US or Morocco; the aerial parts are eaten as a leafy vegetable or used in traditional preparations in parts of South and Southeast Asia (e.g. 'Brahmi essence' drinks in Thailand), but the doses used in trials come from standardised dry extracts in capsules", "Ayurvedic preparations (Brahmi Vati, Brahmi Ghrita, Saraswatarishta) - traditional medicines, not foods"], "active_constituents": ["bacosides: a mixture of dammarane-type triterpenoid saponins, glycosides of jujubogenin or pseudojujubogenin (bacoside A3, bacopaside II, bacopaside X, bacopasaponin C, bacopaside I)", "extracts are standardised by total bacosides: about 15-55% depending on product and assay (e.g. Bacognize HPLC total bacopasaponins 15.6% w/w [kumar2016]; many commercial extracts claim 50%)", "alkaloids (brahmine, herpestine), flavonoids (luteolin, apigenin), phytosterols - minor, poorly characterised"], "one_line_verdict": "Bacopa is the botanical with the most chronic (12-week) trials for memory, and older healthy adults show a small, inconsistent advantage in verbal recall; for healthy young adults and students the evidence is a handful of small, mostly industry-linked or methodologically weak trials plus credible nulls, it does nothing acutely in a reliable way, it causes GI side effects, it has no authorised health claim anywhere we checked, and it is a supplement ingredient, not a food.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. Bacopa monnieri standardised extracts (typically 300 mg/day, 250-450 mg/day range) have been tested in roughly a dozen placebo-controlled chronic trials, mostly 12 weeks long. The best-replicated signal is on verbal learning and delayed recall (Rey AVLT) in healthy older adults: Calabrese 2008 (n=54, >=65 y) found better AVLT delayed recall only after adjusting for baseline cognition [calabrese2008]; Morgan & Stevens 2010 (n=98 randomised, 81 completed, >55 y, BacoMind 300 mg) found improved AVLT acquisition and delayed recall with more GI side effects and more dropouts in the Bacopa arm [morgan2010]; Peth-Nui 2012 (n=60, Thailand) reported improved working-memory and attention factors with shorter ERP latencies [pethnui2012]. Pooled data are weak: Kongkeaw 2014 (9 trials, 518 subjects) found faster Trail B and choice reaction time [kongkeaw2014]; Brimson 2021 pooled AVLT delayed recall at WMD 0.85 words (95% CI -0.22 to 1.91, I2 93%, not significant), digit span backwards and choice reaction time not significant, and only logical memory significant (1.11, 0.43-1.80, I2 73%, 3 trials) [brimson2021]. Pase 2012 found effects confined to free recall [pase2012]. In healthy young and middle-aged adults. Positive trials from one Australian group linked to the CDRI 08 supplier found better inspection time and AVLT learning after 12 weeks [stough2001] and better spatial working memory with 42% attrition [stough2008]; Roodenrys 2002 found slower forgetting in 40-65-year-olds but no effect on attention, short-term memory or everyday memory [roodenrys2002]. Independent or later trials were null: Sathyanarayanan 2013 (n=72, 35-60 y, 450 mg, 12 weeks, India) found no effect on any cognitive measure [sathyanarayanan2013]; Lopresti 2025 (n=101, 40-70 y with memory complaints, 300 mg, 12 weeks, registered) found no effect on verbal learning, attention or working memory but more adverse events, mainly digestive complaints and headache [lopresti2025]; a ginkgo-bacopa combination was null at 4 weeks [nathan2004]. The only student trial (60 medical students, 6 weeks) reported 'significant' digit-span-backwards and logical-memory gains, but the full text shows identical post-treatment backward span (8.3 vs 8.3), baseline imbalance and only 42 of 60 analysed [kumar2016]. Acute effects: 300 mg had no effect 2 h after dosing in 38 adults [nathan2001]; small crossover trials (n=24 and n=17) reported isolated improvements on some repetitions or tasks at 320 mg and some cortisol/mood changes [downey2013, benson2014], consistent with multiplicity. How large. Neale 2013 reported a 'highest' d of 0.95 for delayed word recall, but this was calculated only for significant outcomes and is biased upwards [neale2013]. The 2026 network meta-analysis reporting SMD about 2 for >=600 mg/day on working memory [tiemtad2026] is implausibly large and should not be used as an effect estimate. An independent review judged certainty low [crawford2021]. What is unknown. No adequately powered, preregistered, independent trial in students or knowledge workers exists; a registered student trial (NCT02931747, 75 students, completed 2017) has no posted results. Biomarker claims (BDNF) are not supported in humans [keegan2023, lopresti2025]. Short-term safety is acceptable, but note GI effects, in-vitro CYP inhibition [ramasamy2014], a suspected interaction case [acquarulo2022], an animal thyroid signal [kar2002] and contamination risk [saper2008].", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Bacopa is not a nutrient and there is no deficiency state; every claim is an enhancement or pharmacological claim. The relevant split is therefore by population and baseline cognition: the most consistent (still small) effects are on verbal recall in healthy older adults, in whom some benefit appeared only after adjusting for baseline cognitive deficit [calabrese2008], and in age-associated memory impairment [raghav2006]. In healthy young adults and students, who have high baseline performance and a ceiling effect, the trials are few, small and inconsistent [stough2001, stough2008, kumar2016], and recent independent or registered trials in middle-aged adults are null [sathyanarayanan2013, lopresti2025]. Child and ADHD evidence [kean2016] must not be transferred to adults, and older-adult evidence must not be sold to students.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Marketing says Bacopa 'boosts memory', 'improves focus', 'reduces exam stress' and 'is clinically proven'. The evidence supports a narrower statement: after about 12 weeks of 300 mg/day of a standardised extract, some trials in older adults show slightly better word-list recall, while other trials, including recent registered ones, show nothing. It does not reliably improve focus, attention or performance from a single dose [nathan2001], so 'take before an exam' is unsupported. The best-known effect sizes come from selective reporting [neale2013] or from pooled analyses with very high heterogeneity [brimson2021, tiemtad2026]. No authorised EU health claim exists; Bacopa claims sit among the unassessed botanical Article 13 claims (on-hold status not confirmed directly in this session), so any EU memory claim would be unauthorised. Poland's supplement expert team even recommends labelling 'do not use for longer than 4 weeks', which conflicts with the 12-week time to effect in the trials. In the US only a structure/function claim with a DSHEA disclaimer is possible, and the FTC would require competent and reliable evidence in the population targeted; for students that evidence does not exist. Honest positioning: 'a traditional botanical with modest, inconsistent evidence for memory in older adults after 3 months; not a study aid'.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Bacosides are poorly characterised pharmacokinetically in humans; most mechanistic evidence comes from rodents and cell systems (antioxidant activity, cholinergic modulation with acetylcholinesterase inhibition, serotonergic and dendritic effects, amyloid handling). In humans, one trial reported lower plasma AChE activity and shorter ERP latencies in older adults [pethnui2012]; serum BDNF did not change in an open-label study [keegan2023] or in a 12-week RCT [lopresti2025]. The slow onset (8-12 weeks) seen in trials is consistent with a cumulative rather than acute pharmacological effect, but the mechanism behind any memory effect is unproven.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Oral standardised extract (bacoside saponins) -> intestinal hydrolysis to aglycones (jujubogenin, pseudojujubogenin) -> limited systemic exposure", "detail": "Human pharmacokinetics of bacosides are essentially undescribed; metabolites were characterised in plasma, urine and faeces after 12 weeks of 'Brahmi essence' but this did not quantify brain exposure. Blood-brain barrier passage of aglycones is inferred from in-silico and animal data.", "evidence_level": "speculative"}, {"step": "Gut-level and hepatic exposure -> inhibition of CYP2C19, CYP2C9, CYP1A2 and CYP3A4", "detail": "Whole extract (not isolated bacosides) inhibited recombinant human CYP isoforms in vitro; at an estimated gut concentration from 300 mg/day activity fell below 10% for CYP3A4, 2C9 and 2C19 [ramasamy2014]. A clinical interaction is suspected in one case report [acquarulo2022]; no human PK interaction study was found.", "evidence_level": "plausible"}, {"step": "Cholinergic modulation (AChE inhibition) and monoamine effects", "detail": "Plasma AChE activity fell in older adults given 300-600 mg/day for 12 weeks; MAO activity did not change [pethnui2012]. Plasma AChE is a peripheral marker, not brain cholinergic tone. Lopresti 2025 found no change in blood AChE activity [lopresti2025].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Antioxidant, anti-inflammatory and neurotrophic signalling (SOD, Hsp70, NF-kB, CREB, BDNF), dendritic growth and amyloid reduction", "detail": "Robust in rodent and cell models (reviewed in the 2024-2025 literature); in humans, serum BDNF unchanged, with exploratory changes in CREB phosphorylation and NF-kB in an uncontrolled study [keegan2023].", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Possible thyroid stimulation", "detail": "In male mice, Bacopa extract at 200 mg/kg raised T4 by about 41% [kar2002]; no human thyroid data found. Relevant for people with thyroid disease or taking levothyroxine.", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Measured human cognitive outcome", "detail": "After 12 weeks, small and inconsistent improvements in verbal learning and delayed recall, mostly in older adults [calabrese2008, morgan2010]; null trials in middle-aged adults [sathyanarayanan2013, lopresti2025]; no reliable acute effect [nathan2001].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["cholinergic modulation (acetylcholinesterase inhibition, limited human data)", "antioxidant / free-radical scavenging (animal, in vitro)", "neurotrophic signalling (BDNF, CREB) - not confirmed in human serum", "anti-inflammatory NF-kB modulation (exploratory human data)", "serotonergic / monoaminergic modulation (animal)", "amyloid-beta reduction (animal)", "possible HPA-axis/cortisol modulation (single small acute human study)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Cognitively healthy adults aged 55+ (means 62-73 y), Australia, USA, Thailand", "dose_or_intake": "300 mg/day standardised extract (BacoMind, other standardised extracts); 300-600 mg/day in Thailand", "duration": "12 weeks", "grade": "B", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Three RCTs report better AVLT delayed recall and acquisition [calabrese2008, morgan2010] or working-memory and attention factors [pethnui2012]. In Calabrese the effect was significant only after adjusting for baseline cognitive deficit. A 2025 NMA ranked a Bacopa compound highly for executive function and language in healthy older adults, based on rankings only [feng2025].", "effect_estimate": "Individual trials report p-values only in abstracts (e.g. Morgan AVLT delayed recall p=0.001). Pooled AVLT delayed recall (mixed adult ages, 4 trials, n=239): WMD 0.85 words (95% CI -0.22 to 1.91), I2 93% [brimson2021]", "certainty_notes": "Started at high (RCTs); downgraded for imprecision (small n, 48-81 completers), risk of bias (differential dropout in Morgan; covariate-dependent result in Calabrese; supplier-related extracts) and inconsistency (very high I2 in pooled recall). Kept at B because several independent groups found a consistent direction in this population.", "key_study_ids": ["calabrese2008", "morgan2010", "pethnui2012", "brimson2021", "feng2025"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "processing_speed", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy and memory-impaired adults pooled across ages in meta-analyses of >=12-week trials", "dose_or_intake": "250-450 mg/day standardised extracts", "duration": ">=12 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Kongkeaw 2014 pooled faster Trail B and choice reaction time; Brimson 2021, with a different trial set, found choice and simple reaction time not significant. No significant differences in sustained attention, selective attention or processing speed in the 2026 NMA.", "effect_estimate": "Trail B -17.9 (units as reported, ms; 95% CI -24.6 to -11.2); choice RT -10.6 ms (95% CI -12.1 to -9.2) [kongkeaw2014]; choice RT WMD -0.20 (95% CI -0.50 to 0.09), I2 9% [brimson2021]", "certainty_notes": "Conflicting meta-analyses with different inclusion rules; mixed populations (indirectness); a 10 ms reaction-time change has no practical value for a student or worker.", "key_study_ids": ["kongkeaw2014", "brimson2021", "tiemtad2026"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adult volunteers recruited in Australia (age ranges not stated in abstracts; mostly younger and middle-aged adults)", "dose_or_intake": "300 mg/day KeenMind/CDRI 08", "duration": "12 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Stough 2001 reported better AVLT learning rate and memory consolidation plus faster inspection time; the paper carries a 2015 erratum for a dosage error in the text. Stough 2008 (62 of 107 completed) reported better spatial working memory accuracy and fewer RVIP false positives. Both come from the same group with links to the extract supplier and have not been replicated independently in young adults; Pase 2012 found free recall improved on 9 of 17 tests and little elsewhere.", "effect_estimate": "Not reported in abstracts (p<0.05). Selectively calculated highest effect d=0.95 for delayed word recall across healthy-adult trials [neale2013], biased upwards", "certainty_notes": "Downgraded for risk of bias (attrition of 42% in Stough 2008, multiple outcomes without correction), imprecision (small samples), and the absence of independent replication. Not graded B because the independent and registered trials in adults [sathyanarayanan2013, lopresti2025] are null.", "key_study_ids": ["stough2001", "stough2008", "pase2012", "neale2013"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "learning_retention", "population": "students", "population_detail": "Second-year medical students, Nagpur, India", "dose_or_intake": "150 mg twice daily Bacognize (300 mg/day)", "duration": "6 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "Only one student RCT. Abstract reports significant improvement, but the full-text table shows identical post-treatment backward digit span (8.3 vs 8.3) and a lower Bacopa baseline on logical memory (3.4 vs 5.5); the 'differences' reflect baseline imbalance and a declining placebo group. Only 42 of 60 analysed (28 vs 14); 8 of 10 tests null.", "effect_estimate": "Digit span backwards post: 8.3 +/- 0.9 vs 8.3 +/- 0.8; logical memory post 5.2 +/- 4.5 vs 4.1 +/- 3.8 [kumar2016]", "certainty_notes": "Single small trial with high risk of bias (unbalanced analysis sets, inappropriate comparison method, attrition); a registered 75-student trial (NCT02931747, completed 2017) has no results posted - possible publication bias. Grade C is the ceiling; for practical purposes there is no usable evidence for students.", "key_study_ids": ["kumar2016"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy middle-aged adults 35-70 y, including people with self-reported memory and attention problems (Australia, India)", "dose_or_intake": "300-450 mg/day standardised extracts; one trial a ginkgo 120 mg + bacopa 300 mg combination", "duration": "4-12 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Roodenrys 2002 (n=76, 40-65 y) found slower forgetting of new information, but no effect on learning rate, attention, short-term memory, everyday memory or anxiety. Sathyanarayanan 2013 (n=72, 450 mg) and Lopresti 2025 (n=101, registered) found no effect on any cognitive outcome. A ginkgo-bacopa combination was null at 2 and 4 weeks (n=85).", "effect_estimate": "Lopresti 2025: verbal learning p=0.391, attention p=0.713, working memory p=0.610 (no between-group difference) [lopresti2025]", "certainty_notes": "Inconsistency between the older positive trial and newer registered nulls; imprecision; the most recent and best-registered trial is null. Downgraded to C.", "key_study_ids": ["roodenrys2002", "sathyanarayanan2013", "lopresti2025", "nathan2004"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adult volunteers in single-dose studies (n=17-38)", "dose_or_intake": "300 mg, 320 mg or 640 mg single dose", "duration": "1-2 h after one dose (crossover with 7-day washouts)", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "300 mg had no effect on any test at 2 h (n=38). 320 mg improved Cognitive Demand Battery performance on some repetitions (n=24), with 640 mg not consistently better and no effect on stress or fatigue ratings. In n=17 a multitasking study reported better Letter Search and Stroop performance at 1-2 h with some mood and cortisol changes.", "effect_estimate": "Not reported as standardised effects; p-values for selected repetitions only [downey2013, benson2014]; null across all tests [nathan2001]", "certainty_notes": "Very small crossover trials from one group, many outcomes and time points, no dose-response, an earlier null trial; strong suspicion of chance findings. No basis for 'take before an exam'.", "key_study_ids": ["nathan2001", "downey2013", "benson2014"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy volunteers under laboratory multitasking stress", "dose_or_intake": "320 or 640 mg single dose", "duration": "1-2 h", "grade": "C", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "One crossover study (n=17) reported some positive mood effects and lower salivary cortisol after multitasking; a separate acute study found no attenuation of task-induced stress or fatigue ratings.", "effect_estimate": "Not reported as effect sizes [benson2014]; no effect on stress/fatigue ratings [downey2013]", "certainty_notes": "Single tiny trial, cortisol is a biomarker, contradicted by a sister study; insufficient.", "key_study_ids": ["benson2014", "downey2013"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults 18-70 y; anxiety measured as a secondary outcome", "dose_or_intake": "300-450 mg/day", "duration": "12 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Lower state anxiety in Stough 2001 and a trend in Sathyanarayanan 2013; no anxiety effect in Roodenrys 2002; lower self-reported stress reactivity and post-task fatigue in Lopresti 2025 (secondary outcomes) despite null cognition; Calabrese 2008 reported falling depression and anxiety scores in older adults.", "effect_estimate": "Lopresti 2025 stress reactivity p=0.03 (secondary) [lopresti2025]; others p-values only", "certainty_notes": "Subjective secondary outcomes, inconsistent, multiplicity; these are not cognitive outcomes and must not be presented as 'focus'.", "key_study_ids": ["stough2001", "sathyanarayanan2013", "roodenrys2002", "lopresti2025", "calabrese2008"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults of all ages pooled in a 2026 network meta-analysis of Bacopa vs Ginkgo (29 RCTs, n=2107)", "dose_or_intake": ">=600 mg/day vs 300-<600 mg/day Bacopa", "duration": "mostly 12 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "High-dose Bacopa (>=600 mg/day) ranked first for working memory (SUCRA 100%) with SMD 2.03 vs placebo; no differences in attention or processing speed. A 2021 meta-analysis found digit span backward not significant (5 trials, I2 78%).", "effect_estimate": "SMD 2.03 (95% CI 1.28-2.78) vs placebo for >=600 mg/day [tiemtad2026]; digit span reverse pooled not significant, p=0.095 [brimson2021]", "certainty_notes": "An SMD of 2 is implausible for a cognitive intervention and likely reflects a few small trials (e.g. the 600 mg arm of a 60-person trial [pethnui2012]) and indirect network comparisons; inconsistent with direct pooling. Downgraded for imprecision, inconsistency and probable small-study effects.", "key_study_ids": ["tiemtad2026", "brimson2021", "pethnui2012"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "older_adults_mci", "population_detail": "Adults over 55 with age-associated memory impairment (India)", "dose_or_intake": "125 mg twice daily standardised extract", "duration": "12 weeks plus 4-week placebo follow-up", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Improvement in mental control, logical memory and paired-associate learning vs placebo in a single small trial.", "effect_estimate": "Not reported in abstract (significant differences only) [raghav2006]", "certainty_notes": "Single small trial, abstract-level access, not applicable to healthy young adults.", "key_study_ids": ["raghav2006"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "neuroplasticity_biomarkers", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy older adults (open label) and adults 40-70 y (RCT)", "dose_or_intake": "300 mg/day", "duration": "12 weeks", "grade": "D", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Serum mature and pro-BDNF unchanged in an open-label study; no change in BDNF, malondialdehyde or AChE in a 12-week RCT. Exploratory CREB/NF-kB changes are uncontrolled.", "effect_estimate": "BDNF not significantly changed [keegan2023, lopresti2025]", "certainty_notes": "Biomarkers only, not cognition; animal BDNF claims are not translated to humans.", "key_study_ids": ["keegan2023", "lopresti2025"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "learning", "population": "children_adolescents", "population_detail": "Children and adolescents, including ADHD-like symptoms (5 studies in a systematic review)", "dose_or_intake": "various", "duration": "various", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "A systematic review reported improvements in language, some memory sub-domains and hyperactivity with mean d=0.42, but called for replication and stronger statistics. Not transferable to adults, and Brain Food should not target children.", "effect_estimate": "Mean d=0.42 across significant outcomes [kean2016]", "certainty_notes": "Effect sizes calculated only for significant data; small heterogeneous studies; written by the CDRI 08 trial group.", "key_study_ids": ["kean2016"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "300 mg extract", "onset": "tested at 2 h", "duration_studied": "single dose", "domain": "broad battery (attention, memory, speed)", "population": "healthy adults (n=38, parallel)", "effect_estimate": "no significant change on any test", "certainty": "low (small, single trial) - null", "safety": "not reported in abstract", "study_ids": ["nathan2001"]}, {"dose": "320 mg and 640 mg CDRI 08", "onset": "first post-dose repetition of the battery", "duration_studied": "single dose, about 2-3 h of repeated testing", "domain": "sustained cognitive performance (Cognitive Demand Battery)", "population": "healthy volunteers (n=24, crossover)", "effect_estimate": "320 mg better on repetitions 1, 2 and 4; 640 mg not consistently better; no effect on stress or fatigue ratings or cardiovascular measures", "certainty": "very low (multiplicity, no dose-response)", "safety": "no cardiovascular effect", "study_ids": ["downey2013"]}, {"dose": "320 mg and 640 mg CDRI 08", "onset": "1 h and 2 h", "duration_studied": "single dose", "domain": "multitasking (Letter Search, Stroop), mood, salivary cortisol", "population": "healthy volunteers (n=17, crossover)", "effect_estimate": "positive effects on some tasks, some mood effects, lower cortisol; magnitudes not reported in abstract", "certainty": "very low (n=17, many outcomes)", "safety": "not reported in abstract", "study_ids": ["benson2014"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "12 weeks", "population": "healthy older adults >=55-65 y", "outcome": "verbal learning and delayed recall (AVLT); working memory factors", "effect_estimate": "positive in 3 RCTs (p-values only); pooled AVLT delayed recall WMD 0.85 words (95% CI -0.22 to 1.91, I2 93%) in mixed ages", "evidence_quality": "B (moderate-low)", "limitations": "small samples, differential dropout, covariate-dependent results, extracts supplied by manufacturers", "study_ids": ["calabrese2008", "morgan2010", "pethnui2012", "brimson2021"]}, {"duration": "12 weeks", "population": "healthy younger and middle-aged adults", "outcome": "memory, inspection time, working memory, attention", "effect_estimate": "two positive trials from one group; three null trials (one combination); no pooled young-adult estimate available", "evidence_quality": "C (low)", "limitations": "attrition up to 42%, multiplicity, supplier links, newer registered trial null", "study_ids": ["stough2001", "stough2008", "roodenrys2002", "sathyanarayanan2013", "lopresti2025", "nathan2004"]}, {"duration": "6 weeks", "population": "medical students", "outcome": "digit span, logical memory, reaction time", "effect_estimate": "abstract claims benefit; full-text post-treatment values identical for digit span backwards (8.3 vs 8.3)", "evidence_quality": "C (very low in practice)", "limitations": "baseline imbalance, 42/60 analysed, single centre", "study_ids": ["kumar2016"]}, {"duration": ">=12 weeks (meta-analyses)", "population": "mixed healthy and memory-impaired adults", "outcome": "Trail B, choice reaction time, working memory", "effect_estimate": "Trail B -17.9 (95% CI -24.6 to -11.2), CRT -10.6 ms [kongkeaw2014]; NMA working memory SMD 2.03 at >=600 mg/day (implausible) [tiemtad2026]", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "conflicting meta-analyses, heterogeneity, network indirectness, small-study effects", "study_ids": ["kongkeaw2014", "tiemtad2026", "brimson2021"]}, {"duration": "30 days to 12 weeks", "population": "healthy adults (safety)", "outcome": "adverse events, laboratory safety", "effect_estimate": "no serious adverse events; GI effects (nausea, cramps, increased stool frequency, diarrhoea) more frequent than placebo; adverse events more frequent with Bacopa in a 2025 RCT (p=0.024)", "evidence_quality": "moderate for short-term tolerability; no data beyond about 3-6 months", "limitations": "small samples, safety not a primary outcome in most trials", "study_ids": ["pravina2007", "morgan2010", "lopresti2025", "calabrese2008"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Short-term (up to about 12 weeks) use of 300-450 mg/day standardised extract is generally tolerated, with no serious adverse events in trials, but gastrointestinal effects (nausea, abdominal cramps, increased stool frequency, diarrhoea) and headaches are consistently more common than with placebo and cause dropouts [morgan2010, lopresti2025, brimson2021]. Interaction risk is plausible: the extract inhibits CYP2C19, 2C9, 1A2 and 3A4 in vitro [ramasamy2014], with one case of suspected cholinergic toxicity with cevimeline [acquarulo2022]. Animal data suggest raised T4 [kar2002]. Ayurvedic-sourced products carry heavy-metal and pesticide contamination risk [saper2008], and Bacopa is an aquatic plant that accumulates cadmium and chromium when grown in polluted water. No data beyond about 3-6 months, and no data in pregnancy or lactation.", "adverse_effects": ["nausea, abdominal cramps, increased stool frequency, diarrhoea (most consistent; more frequent than placebo)", "headache (Lopresti 2025)", "dry mouth, fatigue reported in some trials (secondary sources)", "dropouts due to side effects (Morgan 2010: more dropouts with Bacopa)"], "contraindications": ["pregnancy and lactation (no safety data; Polish expert team recommends a label warning)", "children (not a Brain Food target; Polish expert team recommends a warning)", "thyroid disease or levothyroxine therapy (animal T4 signal) - caution", "gastrointestinal disorders (IBS, peptic ulcer) - caution because of GI effects", "people about to have surgery or taking multiple prescription drugs - caution because of CYP effects"], "drug_interactions": ["CYP2C19 and CYP2C9 substrates (e.g. clopidogrel, omeprazole, citalopram/escitalopram, warfarin, phenytoin) - in-vitro inhibition, clinical relevance unproven", "CYP3A4 and CYP1A2 substrates (many drugs, including some antipsychotics and theophylline) - in-vitro inhibition", "cholinergic agents (cevimeline, pilocarpine, cholinesterase inhibitors such as donepezil) - possible additive cholinergic effects; one case report", "thyroid hormone medication - theoretical (animal data)", "sedatives and antidepressants - theoretical additive effects; one retrospective chart review on adaptogen-antidepressant interactions was identified in searching but not read"], "upper_limits": "No official UL. Trials used 250-640 mg/day extract. Poland's supplement expert team (Resolution 4/2024 of the Zespół do spraw Suplementów Diety, 20 Sept 2024) set conditions: <=200 mg extract with <=100 mg bacosides per daily portion (or <5 g dried herb with <=2.5% bacosides), and recommended labelling 'do not use in children, pregnant or breastfeeding women' and 'do not use for longer than 4 weeks'. This is below the 300 mg/day used in most efficacy trials.", "sleep_impact": "No stimulant effect; not reported to disrupt sleep. One paediatric trial reported improved sleep routine; no adult sleep data of note.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Up to 12-16 weeks in RCTs; no controlled data beyond about 6 months.", "daily_use_appropriate": "uncertain", "special_populations": "Not for pregnancy, lactation or children. Older adults on multiple drugs and people with thyroid disease, GI disorders or psychiatric medication need clinician review. People with Sjogren's syndrome or on cholinergic drugs should avoid it.", "specialist_review_needed": ["clinical pharmacologist (CYP interactions)", "endocrinologist (thyroid signal)", "toxicologist / QA (heavy metals, pesticides, bacoside standardisation, adulteration)", "regulatory specialist (EU national botanical lists, Polish restrictions, novel food status)"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "Acceptable short-term tolerability in healthy adults, but GI side effects are frequent, interaction potential is real in vitro, long-term data are lacking, contamination risk requires batch testing, and at least one EU national authority recommends limiting use to 4 weeks and 200 mg extract. Usable only as a labelled supplement with medication, pregnancy and thyroid warnings and certified heavy-metal testing."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "No authorised health claim for Bacopa monnieri in the EU register. Botanical Article 13 claims remain unassessed (the general 'on hold' botanical list); whether specific Bacopa memory claims are on that list was not confirmed directly in this session - uncertain. Novel food status: per the EU Novel Food Catalogue, as cited by the Polish supplement expert team (catalogue update 17/07/2023), the leaves, herb, rhizome and tops have a history of use only as a food supplement ingredient, meaning use in ordinary foods would require novel food authorisation. Member-state lists differ: France permits it in supplements (per secondary sources, ANSM discussing pharmacopoeia listing, 2025); Poland (Resolution 4/2024, GIS supplement team, 20 Sept 2024) limits extract to <=200 mg/day and <=100 mg bacosides with 4-week and pregnancy/child warnings. An EMA HMPC monograph was in preparation as of 2021; ESCOP published a monograph in 2025 (not read). Sources: https://www.gov.pl/attachment/4b94f0bc-bf32-4cdf-b83e-f91577cff362 (accessed 2026-10-25).", "us": "Marketed as a dietary ingredient under DSHEA (pre-1994 marketing is widely assumed; NDI status not verified). Structure/function claims only, with the FDA disclaimer; FTC requires competent and reliable scientific evidence for the population targeted, which is lacking for students. A self-affirmed GRAS determination for BacoMind (Natural Remedies) was reported in trade press in 2019 with a maximum of about 50 mg per serving in specified foods (secondary source; page returned HTTP 403, not verified, and no FDA GRAS notice found). USP has a dietary-supplement monograph for Bacopa.", "morocco": "Uncertain. Food supplements fall under Law 28-07 on food safety (ONSSA); press reports from 2025-2026 describe conflicting requirements for prior registration with AMMPS or declaration to ONSSA, and a pending amendment (Law 17-04 article 30) that would restrict supplements with a 'therapeutic effect' to pharmacies. No Morocco-specific list of authorised plants or Bacopa decision was found. Needs direct confirmation with ONSSA/AMMPS.", "permitted_claims_examples": [], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "EU: catalogue indicates use only in food supplements, so a Bacopa food or beverage (bar, drink, gummy marketed as food) would need novel food authorisation. US: GRAS for food use is only self-affirmed for one extract at low levels (about 50 mg/serving, unverified), far below efficacy doses; supplement use is the realistic route. Morocco: unknown.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Poor. Efficacy doses (300 mg/day standardised extract, 8-12 weeks of continuous use) belong in a capsule or tablet. The EU catalogue status restricts use to food supplements, and bacoside saponins are very bitter.", "formats": ["capsule or tablet of standardised extract (realistic)", "powdered herb (traditional, 5-10 g/day, bitter, variable bacosides)", "functional food or drink (not realistic in the EU without novel food authorisation; US self-GRAS only at about 50 mg/serving)"], "stability": "Bacosides are reasonably stable in dry extract; degradation with heat and moisture is reported in the pharmacognosy literature (not reviewed here). Standardisation varies widely between suppliers and assays (15-55% bacosides), so products are not interchangeable.", "taste": "Strongly bitter and astringent (saponins); masking would be required in any food matrix.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Human pharmacokinetics are essentially unknown. Some trials dosed after food; Ayurvedic tradition uses ghee (Brahmi Ghrita) on the assumption that fat improves uptake, which is not proven.", "dose_feasibility": "300 mg extract fits in one capsule but not in an EU food (novel food issue). In the US, food use under the reported self-GRAS is limited to about 50 mg/serving, so 6 servings a day would be needed to reach the studied dose. The Polish 200 mg/day cap also falls below the dose used in most trials.", "cost_availability": "Widely available and cheap as a supplement in the EU and US; Morocco availability via pharmacies/parapharmacies and online, not verified.", "cost_evidence": "Retail listings found 2026-10-25: Bulk (UK) 90 x 350 mg capsules, 50% bacosides, £10.99 (EU €13.99); Ostrovit 90 x 200 mg tablets about €4.33; Allnutrition 90 capsules, 50% bacosides, £9.99. Roughly €0.05-0.15 per daily dose. Branded clinical extracts (CDRI 08, BacoMind) cost more; not priced here."}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 4, "effect_magnitude": 3, "safety": 6, "reproducibility": 3, "food_suitability": 1.5, "cost_access": 8, "rationale": "Evidence quality 4: B only in healthy older adults for verbal recall; C for young adults, students and middle-aged adults, where the trials are inconsistent and the newer registered trials are null. Effect magnitude 3: individual trials report p-values rather than effect sizes, the best independent pooled recall effect is non-significant (WMD 0.85 words, I2 93%), headline d=0.95 and SMD=2 figures are biased, and practical gains for a student are undetectable. Safety 6: no serious AEs in short trials, but GI effects cause dropouts, there are in-vitro CYP inhibition and case-report interaction signals and an animal thyroid signal, contamination risk is real, and there are no long-term data. Reproducibility 3: positive young-adult trials cluster in one industry-linked group; independent replications are null. Food suitability 1.5: bitter, supplement-only novel food status in the EU, dose mismatch with US self-GRAS. Cost/access 8: cheap and widely available, though product standardisation varies."}, "research_gaps": ["An adequately powered (n>=200), preregistered, independent 12-week RCT in healthy students or young knowledge workers with a predefined primary memory outcome and practical outcomes (exam grades, learning retention)", "Publication of the registered 75-student trial NCT02931747 (completed 2017, no results posted)", "Human pharmacokinetics of bacosides and aglycones; dose-response (300 vs 600 mg) in a single trial", "Clinical drug-interaction studies (CYP2C19/2C9/3A4 probe substrates)", "Human thyroid-function monitoring in trials longer than 12 weeks", "Long-term (>6 months) safety", "Head-to-head comparison of commercial extracts with standardised bacoside profiles", "Independent meta-analysis restricted to low-risk-of-bias trials with GRADE assessment and small-study-effect analysis"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 4.25, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": "B", "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": null, "best_grade_overall": "B", "benefit_domains": {"episodic_memory": "B", "learning": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 12, "n_studies": 28, "n_verified": 28, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 8, "n_systematic_reviews": 8, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["inconsistent", "insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "ginkgo-biloba", "name": "Ginkgo biloba (EGb 761)", "category": "H", "category_name": "Botanical and nootropic supplements", "aliases": ["Ginkgo biloba leaf extract", "GBE", "EGb 761 (Schwabe; marketed as Tebonin, Tanakan, Ginkgold, Rokan)", "LI 1370", "GK501", "Ginkgo folium (Ph. Eur. refined and quantified dry extract)", "maidenhair tree"], "food_sources": ["No meaningful dietary source of the leaf constituents: ginkgo leaf is not a conventional food in Europe, North America or Morocco", "Ginkgo leaf infusions/teas exist but deliver unquantified and much lower terpene-lactone doses than standardised extracts and may contain ginkgolic acids", "Ginkgo seeds ('ginkgo nuts') are a traditional food in East Asia, but contain 4'-O-methylpyridoxine (ginkgotoxin) and cause seizures when over-consumed, especially in children [kajiyama2002]; seeds are not a source of the leaf extract constituents studied in trials"], "active_constituents": ["Flavonol glycosides (quercetin, kaempferol, isorhamnetin glycosides): standardised to about 24% (Ph. Eur. range 22-27%)", "Terpene trilactones about 6% (Ph. Eur. 5.4-6.6%): ginkgolides A, B, C (about 2.8-3.4%; ginkgolide B is a platelet-activating-factor antagonist) and bilobalide (about 2.6-3.2%)", "Ginkgolic acids (alkylphenols; allergenic and potentially toxic): removed to below 5 ppm in pharmacopoeial refined extracts; NTP test material contained 10.45 ppm [ntp2013]", "Proanthocyanidins, organic acids (minor)"], "one_line_verdict": "Standardised ginkgo extract (EGb 761, 120-240 mg/day) does not improve memory, attention or executive function in healthy young or older adults (pooled d about 0 across 13 trials) and did not prevent dementia or cognitive decline in two trials of about 3,000 older people each; a small, low-certainty benefit exists only in diagnosed dementia, the leaf extract carries bleeding-interaction caveats and an IARC 2B classification, and it is not a sound Brain Food ingredient for students or knowledge workers.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. Ginkgo biloba leaf extract, mostly the standardised EGb 761 (24% flavonol glycosides, 6% terpene lactones), is one of the most thoroughly tested botanicals in cognitive research, and the larger and better the trial, the more consistently null the result. In healthy people, a meta-analysis of randomised trials found pooled effects close to zero for memory (d = -0.04, 95% CI -0.17 to 0.07; 13 trials, 1,132 participants), executive function (d = -0.05) and attention (d = -0.08), unrelated to age, dose or duration [laws2012]; an earlier systematic review of 15 trials in under-60s found no robust acute or chronic effect [canter2007]. Individual chronic trials in young adults were null: 12 weeks at 120 mg/day in 104 young adults [burns2006] and 6 weeks at 120 mg/day in 40 university students [elsabagh2005]. Acute studies in young adults from one laboratory reported dose-dependent improvement of 'speed of attention' at 240-360 mg [kennedy2000], but a pooled reanalysis of 78 participants showed that the usual 120 mg dose slightly improved 'quality of memory' while worsening speed of attention [kennedy2007b], and a 120 mg acute dose improved sustained attention and pattern recognition in 52 students with tolerance after 6 weeks [elsabagh2005]; these multi-outcome, multi-timepoint findings are not replicated [canter2007]. In cognitively healthy older adults, the 6-week trials of Solomon (n = 230) [solomon2002] and Carlson (n = 90, combination product) [carlson2007] were null, whereas Mix and Crews (n = 262, 180 mg/day) reported better delayed recall [mix2002] and Kaschel (n = 188, 45-56 years, 240 mg/day) better free recall of appointments [kaschel2011]; selective positives on some of many outcomes do not change the near-zero pooled estimate. Prevention. The two largest trials ever done on a botanical were null: GEM (n = 3,069, age 75+, 240 mg/day, median 6.1 years) found no reduction in dementia (HR 1.12, 95% CI 0.94-1.33) [dekosky2008] and no slowing of decline in any cognitive domain [snitz2009]; GuidAge (n = 2,854, age 70+ with memory complaints, 5 years, industry-funded) found HR 0.84 (0.60-1.18) for Alzheimer's disease [vellas2012]. Treatment of impairment. The 2026 Cochrane review (82 studies, 10,613 participants) found that ginkgo probably has little or no effect in MCI (moderate certainty) and may give small-to-moderate benefits in diagnosed dementia (low certainty, I2 88-96%) [wieland2026], consistent with earlier inconsistency [birks2009] and with more favourable industry-linked pooled analyses at 240 mg/day [tan2015, riepe2025]. Network meta-analyses in healthy adults do not rank ginkgo alone above placebo [wang2025, tiemtad2026]. Safety. In trials, adverse events do not exceed placebo [wieland2026, dekosky2008]; haemostasis parameters are not clinically changed [kellermann2011], but case reports of bleeding and seizures [granger2001], hospital data on interactions with antiplatelet drugs [mai2025], 2-year rodent liver carcinogenicity [ntp2013] and widespread adulteration [govindaraghavan2018, grazina2025] require caution. For the Brain Food target population there is no credible cognitive benefit to sell.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Ginkgo is not a nutrient and no deficiency state exists, so 'deficiency correction' does not apply. The question is purely pharmacological enhancement. In adequately nourished, cognitively healthy adults of any age the enhancement effect is essentially zero on pooled analysis [laws2012, canter2007] and in the best independent trials [solomon2002, burns2006, snitz2009]. The only population with a plausible, low-certainty benefit is people already diagnosed with dementia, treated with 240 mg/day for about 6 months [wieland2026, tan2015]; those are patients under medical care, and their data must not be generalised to students, knowledge workers or healthy older adults. In MCI the Cochrane review judged little or no effect with moderate certainty [wieland2026], and in normal older adults long-term use did not prevent dementia [dekosky2008, vellas2012].", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Ginkgo is marketed as a memory, focus and 'brain circulation' supplement, often with images of students or professionals. Those claims are contradicted by the evidence: meta-analysis of healthy-adult trials shows no memory, attention or executive effect [laws2012]; the largest prevention trials were null [dekosky2008, snitz2009, vellas2012]; and positive acute findings in young adults come from multi-endpoint studies of one laboratory and include a negative effect on attention speed at the usual dose [kennedy2007b]. Statements such as 'supports memory' or 'improves blood flow to the brain' cannot be substantiated under FTC standards for healthy consumers and are not authorised in the EU, where botanical Article 13 claims remain on hold and no ginkgo claim appears in the Union register; in Germany and France EGb 761 is a medicinal product whose authorised indication concerns age-related cognitive impairment and mild dementia, i.e. a medical use. An honest Brain Food position is that ginkgo is a medicine-grade botanical for a narrow clinical indication, not a nootropic for healthy people, and including it in a cognitive product would mislead customers while adding interaction risk.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Terpene lactones (ginkgolides, bilobalide) and flavonol glycosides are partly bioavailable after oral intake. Ginkgolide B antagonises platelet-activating factor; the extract reduces blood viscosity in human trials and has antioxidant, anti-apoptotic, mitochondrial and anti-amyloid effects in animals and cells. The proposed cognitive mechanism is improved cerebral microcirculation plus neuroprotection. Human data support only biomarker-level vascular effects; they have not translated into measurable cognitive benefit in healthy people, and mechanism is not efficacy.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Oral standardised extract -> absorption of terpene lactones (bilobalide, ginkgolides A/B) and flavonol aglycones after deglycosylation", "detail": "Plasma terpenoids measured after 120 mg doses in young adults [kennedy2007]; quercetin/kaempferol glycosides are largely metabolised by gut microbiota. Pharmacokinetics at recommended doses are well described in the pharmacological literature but not reviewed in detail here.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Ginkgolide B -> platelet-activating-factor (PAF) receptor antagonism; extract -> reduced blood viscosity", "detail": "Meta-analysis of 18 RCTs: blood viscosity reduced (WMD -1.03 mPa.s) but no clinically relevant change in ADP-induced platelet aggregation, aPTT or prothrombin time [kellermann2011]. Biomarker only.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Improved microcirculation -> increased cerebral blood flow", "detail": "Proposed in Cochrane background [birks2009]; human cerebral-blood-flow studies were not reviewed in this unit and cerebral blood flow is a biomarker, not cognition.", "evidence_level": "plausible"}, {"step": "Antioxidant, anti-apoptotic, mitochondrial-protective and anti-amyloid aggregation effects; bilobalide GABA-A modulation", "detail": "Shown in cell and rodent models; not demonstrated in human brain.", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Neuroprotection -> slowing of neurodegeneration / dementia prevention", "detail": "Not supported: GEM and GuidAge prevention trials null [dekosky2008, snitz2009, vellas2012].", "evidence_level": "speculative"}, {"step": "Measured human cognitive outcome", "detail": "Healthy adults: pooled effects about zero for memory, attention and executive function [laws2012]; diagnosed dementia: small, low-certainty, heterogeneous benefit at 240 mg/day [wieland2026].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["PAF receptor antagonism (ginkgolide B)", "haemorheology (blood viscosity)", "antioxidant / free-radical scavenging (flavonoids)", "mitochondrial protection and anti-apoptosis (animal)", "GABAergic modulation by bilobalide (animal)", "CYP2C19 induction / CYP3A4 inhibition at supratherapeutic doses (human PK) [unger2013]"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy young volunteers and university students, single dose, tested 1-6 h post-dose", "dose_or_intake": "120-360 mg standardised extract (GK501 or similar)", "duration": "single dose", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Kennedy 2000 (n=20 crossover) reported dose-dependent improvement in 'speed of attention' at 240 and 360 mg with mixed positive and negative time-specific effects on other factors; Elsabagh 2005 (n=52 students) found 120 mg improved a sustained-attention task at 4 h; a pooled reanalysis of 78 participants found 120 mg worsened speed of attention (1 and 6 h). Canter & Ernst judged acute positives unreplicated or contradicted.", "effect_estimate": "Not reported as standardised effect sizes in abstracts; direction varies by dose and time point; pooled attention across chronic and acute healthy trials d = -0.08 (95% CI -0.21 to 0.02) [laws2012]", "certainty_notes": "Small crossover trials, many outcomes x doses x time points without multiplicity control, mostly one laboratory (Northumbria), contradictory directions at 120 mg; downgraded for risk of bias, inconsistency and imprecision. Practical relevance for a student: negligible and unpredictable.", "key_study_ids": ["kennedy2000", "kennedy2007b", "elsabagh2005", "canter2007"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy young volunteers (CDR battery secondary/quality-of-memory factors; pattern recognition)", "dose_or_intake": "120 mg standardised extract (alone or complexed with phosphatidylserine)", "duration": "single dose", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "120 mg improved 'quality of memory' in a pooled reanalysis (n=78) and pattern-recognition memory in students (n=52); 120 mg alone did not markedly improve primary outcomes in a 28-person crossover, where only the phosphatidylserine complex improved memory.", "effect_estimate": "Not reported (p-values only in abstracts)", "certainty_notes": "Same single-laboratory, multi-endpoint limitations; reanalysis of previously published data (not independent replication). Downgraded for risk of bias, inconsistency, imprecision.", "key_study_ids": ["kennedy2007b", "kennedy2007", "elsabagh2005"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults under 45, including university students", "dose_or_intake": "120 mg/day", "duration": "6-12 weeks", "grade": "E", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Burns 2006 (104 young adults, 12 weeks) found no effect on any cognitive or mood measure; Elsabagh 2005 (40 students, 6 weeks) found no effect on any test (tolerance to acute effects suggested); meta-analysis across healthy trials (all ages) shows memory d about 0, with no moderation by age, dose or duration.", "effect_estimate": "Memory d = -0.04 (95% CI -0.17 to 0.07), 13 trials, n=1,132 (all ages) [laws2012]", "certainty_notes": "Consistent null findings across independent trials and two systematic reviews; trials are small but the pooled CI excludes any practically relevant benefit (upper bound d 0.07). Graded E (no meaningful benefit shown).", "key_study_ids": ["burns2006", "elsabagh2005", "laws2012", "canter2007"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Cognitively healthy adults of all ages (young, middle-aged, older)", "dose_or_intake": "120-240 mg/day", "duration": "2-13 weeks (most 6-12 weeks)", "grade": "E", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Pooled executive function and attention effects near zero; network meta-analyses (2025, 2026) do not show ginkgo alone outperforming placebo; only a Cistanche+ginkgo combination trial ranked highly in one NMA.", "effect_estimate": "Executive function d = -0.05 (95% CI -0.17 to 0.05), 7 trials, n=534; attention d = -0.08 (-0.21 to 0.02), 8 trials, n=910 [laws2012]", "certainty_notes": "Consistent near-zero estimates; some small positive single trials do not alter pooled result. Graded E for enhancement.", "key_study_ids": ["laws2012", "wang2025", "tiemtad2026", "burns2006"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Cognitively intact middle-aged (45-56) and older (60+) community volunteers, MMSE >26", "dose_or_intake": "120-240 mg/day EGb 761 or similar", "duration": "6 weeks to 4 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Null: Solomon 2002 (n=230, 120 mg, 6 weeks, all outcomes NS), Carlson 2007 (n=90, combination product, 4 months; one of six tests favoured placebo). Positive on selected endpoints: Mix & Crews 2002 (n=262, 180 mg, delayed recall p<0.04; baseline imbalance on one test), Kaschel 2011 (n=188, 240 mg, recall of appointments p=0.008 delayed; recognition NS), Burns 2006 older arm (n=93, associational learning d=0.52, p=0.04, all other measures NS).", "effect_estimate": "Kaschel: p=0.038 immediate / 0.008 delayed recall, effect size not reported; Burns older: d=0.52 on 1 of many measures; pooled memory across healthy trials d=-0.04 (-0.17 to 0.07) [laws2012]", "certainty_notes": "Selective positives among many outcomes, possible manufacturer involvement in positive trials (Kaschel: EGb 761, funding not stated in abstract), independent large trials null; downgraded for inconsistency and risk of bias. Older-adult data must not be generalised to young adults.", "key_study_ids": ["solomon2002", "carlson2007", "mix2002", "kaschel2011", "burns2006", "laws2012"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "dementia_risk", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Community volunteers aged 70-96 with normal cognition or memory complaints (GEM: 2,587 normal + 482 MCI; GuidAge: memory complainers)", "dose_or_intake": "240 mg/day (120 mg twice daily)", "duration": "5 years (GuidAge) and median 6.1 years (GEM)", "grade": "E", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "GEM: 277 vs 246 dementia cases; HR all-cause dementia 1.12 (0.94-1.33), AD 1.16 (0.97-1.39). GuidAge: HR for probable AD 0.84 (0.60-1.18), non-proportional hazards. High-certainty evidence of no clinically meaningful prevention effect.", "effect_estimate": "HR 1.12 (95% CI 0.94-1.33) [dekosky2008]; HR 0.84 (0.60-1.18) [vellas2012]", "certainty_notes": "Two large, well-conducted, double-blind RCTs with low loss to follow-up (GEM 6.3%); adherence about 60% at GEM end could dilute effects. The certainty that no useful effect exists is high, hence grade E (not supported) rather than a low grade for lack of data.", "key_study_ids": ["dekosky2008", "vellas2012"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_decline", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "GEM participants aged 72-96 (normal cognition or MCI), annual rates of domain-specific decline", "dose_or_intake": "240 mg/day", "duration": "median 6.1 years", "grade": "E", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Annual z-score decline identical for memory (0.043 vs 0.041), attention, visuospatial, language and executive function; no difference on 3MSE (p=0.71) or ADAS-Cog (p=0.97); no effect modification by age, sex, APOE4 or MCI.", "effect_estimate": "Memory decline 0.043 (0.034-0.051) vs 0.041 (0.032-0.050) z/year [snitz2009]", "certainty_notes": "Large, independent (NIH-funded) RCT with drug supplied by the manufacturer; precise null. High certainty of no benefit.", "key_study_ids": ["snitz2009"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_mci", "population_detail": "People with mild cognitive impairment (12 RCTs, 1,913 participants in Cochrane 2026)", "dose_or_intake": "mostly 240 mg/day EGb 761", "duration": "6-12 months", "grade": "E", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Probably little or no effect on global status (CDR MD -0.03, -0.06 to 0.01), cognition (ADAS-cog MD -0.07, -0.67 to 0.51) or ADLs at 6 months; GEM MCI subgroup progression HR 1.13 (0.85-1.50).", "effect_estimate": "ADAS-cog MD -0.07 (95% CI -0.67 to 0.51), 2 studies, n=508, moderate certainty [wieland2026]; HR 1.13 (0.85-1.50) [dekosky2008]", "certainty_notes": "Moderate-certainty Cochrane GRADE for a null effect; patient population, not relevant to healthy consumers.", "key_study_ids": ["wieland2026", "dekosky2008"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Patients with diagnosed dementia (Alzheimer's, vascular, mixed), often with neuropsychiatric symptoms", "dose_or_intake": "240 mg/day EGb 761 (some trials 120 mg/day)", "duration": "22-26 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Cochrane 2026 (13 trials, 3,288 patients): may improve cognition (SKT MD -1.86, -3.48 to -0.24; I2 96%), global status and ADLs (low certainty). Tan 2015 (9 trials, n=2,561) and manufacturer-linked Riepe 2025 (mild dementia subgroups, 4 trials, n=782) more favourable; Birks 2009 found results inconsistent and unreliable.", "effect_estimate": "SKT MD -1.86 (95% CI -3.48 to -0.24), I2 96%, 9 studies, n=2,801 [wieland2026]; WMD cognition -2.86 (-3.18 to -2.54) [tan2015]", "certainty_notes": "Low certainty: extreme heterogeneity, most positive trials sponsored by the manufacturer, only 4 of 82 studies at low risk of bias in all domains, possible publication bias. Clinical population only.", "key_study_ids": ["wieland2026", "tan2015", "riepe2025", "birks2009"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "vascular_health", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Patients (dementia, peripheral artery disease, diabetes; 87%) and healthy volunteers (13%)", "dose_or_intake": "120-240+ mg/day standardised extract", "duration": "weeks to months", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Blood viscosity reduced (biomarker); no clinically relevant change in platelet aggregation or coagulation times. This is a haemorheological biomarker, not a cognitive outcome.", "effect_estimate": "Blood viscosity WMD -1.03 mPa.s (95% CI -1.29 to -0.78) [kellermann2011]", "certainty_notes": "Biomarker only, mostly patient populations; does not support any cognitive or 'brain circulation' claim.", "key_study_ids": ["kellermann2011"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "learning", "population": "students", "population_detail": "University students; no trials during examination periods or real-world study outcomes", "dose_or_intake": "120 mg/day", "duration": "6 weeks", "grade": "E", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "The only chronic student trial (n=40) found no effect on attention, episodic or working memory, planning, flexibility or mood; young-adult arm of Burns 2006 also null. No trial on academic outcomes.", "effect_estimate": "All outcomes NS [elsabagh2005]; young-adult arm all NS [burns2006]", "certainty_notes": "Small trials, but consistent with pooled null in healthy adults [laws2012]; no plausible signal to pursue.", "key_study_ids": ["elsabagh2005", "burns2006", "laws2012"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_fatigue", "population": "knowledge_workers", "population_detail": "Working adults under cognitive load; no dedicated trials identified", "dose_or_intake": "not studied", "duration": "not studied", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No RCT of ginkgo in knowledge workers or high-workload settings was found; indirect evidence from healthy-adult trials is null.", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "No direct evidence; indirect evidence argues against benefit.", "key_study_ids": ["laws2012"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "240-360 mg standardised extract (GK501)", "onset": "2.5 h, persisting to 6 h", "duration_studied": "6 h post-dose", "domain": "speed of attention (CDR factor)", "population": "healthy young adults (n=20 crossover)", "effect_estimate": "Dose-dependent improvement; effect sizes not reported; other factors showed positive and negative time-specific changes", "certainty": "low (single lab, small n, multiple endpoints)", "safety": "no notable acute adverse effects reported", "study_ids": ["kennedy2000"]}, {"dose": "120 mg", "onset": "1-4 h", "duration_studied": "6 h post-dose", "domain": "quality of memory (improved) and speed of attention (worsened)", "population": "healthy young adults (n=78 pooled from 3 crossovers)", "effect_estimate": "Memory improvement at 1 and 4 h; attention-speed decrement at 1 and 6 h (p-values only)", "certainty": "low (reanalysis, same lab)", "safety": "not reported as problematic", "study_ids": ["kennedy2007b"]}, {"dose": "120 mg", "onset": "tested at 4 h", "duration_studied": "single session", "domain": "sustained attention and pattern-recognition memory", "population": "university students (n=52, parallel)", "effect_estimate": "Significant improvement on 2 tasks; no effect on working memory, planning, flexibility, mood", "certainty": "low", "safety": "not reported as problematic", "study_ids": ["elsabagh2005"]}, {"dose": "120 mg alone vs 120 mg complexed with phosphatidylserine or phosphatidylcholine", "onset": "1-6 h", "duration_studied": "6 h post-dose", "domain": "secondary memory, speed of memory, calmness", "population": "healthy young adults (n=28 crossover)", "effect_estimate": "120 mg ginkgo alone: no marked effect on primary outcomes; PS complex improved memory speed and secondary memory", "certainty": "low", "safety": "not reported as problematic", "study_ids": ["kennedy2007"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "6-12 weeks", "population": "healthy young adults and students", "outcome": "memory, attention, executive function, mood", "effect_estimate": "All NS (n=104, 12 weeks; n=40, 6 weeks)", "evidence_quality": "moderate for absence of benefit (consistent with meta-analysis)", "limitations": "Small trials; no academic or workplace outcomes", "study_ids": ["burns2006", "elsabagh2005", "laws2012"]}, {"duration": "6 weeks - 4 months", "population": "cognitively intact middle-aged and older adults", "outcome": "memory and neuropsychological tests", "effect_estimate": "Null in Solomon (n=230) and Carlson (n=90); selective positives in Mix & Crews (n=262) and Kaschel (n=188); pooled memory d -0.04 (-0.17 to 0.07)", "evidence_quality": "low-moderate; inconsistent", "limitations": "Multiple outcomes, possible manufacturer involvement, baseline imbalance (Mix & Crews)", "study_ids": ["solomon2002", "carlson2007", "mix2002", "kaschel2011", "laws2012"]}, {"duration": "5-7 years", "population": "older adults 70+ (normal cognition, memory complaints, MCI)", "outcome": "incident dementia / Alzheimer's disease; domain-specific cognitive decline", "effect_estimate": "GEM HR 1.12 (0.94-1.33); GuidAge HR 0.84 (0.60-1.18); no difference in decline rates", "evidence_quality": "high (for null)", "limitations": "About 60% adherence at end of GEM; very old population", "study_ids": ["dekosky2008", "snitz2009", "vellas2012"]}, {"duration": "22-26 weeks", "population": "patients with diagnosed dementia", "outcome": "cognition (SKT, ADAS-cog), ADL, global status", "effect_estimate": "SKT MD -1.86 (-3.48 to -0.24), I2 96%, low certainty", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Heterogeneity, manufacturer-sponsored trials, risk of bias", "study_ids": ["wieland2026", "tan2015", "riepe2025", "birks2009"]}], "safety": {"summary": "In RCTs up to 6 years at 240 mg/day, standardised extract has an adverse-event profile similar to placebo, without excess major bleeding [dekosky2008, vellas2012, wieland2026]; haemostasis parameters are not clinically altered [kellermann2011]. However: case reports of bleeding and seizures exist [granger2001]; real-world data associate ginkgo co-prescription with antiplatelets/NSAIDs with bleeding and abnormal coagulation [mai2025]; GEM recorded numerically more haemorrhagic strokes (16 vs 8, NS) [dekosky2008]; a non-EGb extract produced clear liver carcinogenicity in mice and was mutagenic in bacteria [ntp2013], leading to IARC Group 2B (Vol. 108, 2016); ginkgolic acids are allergenic and must be <5 ppm; seeds are neurotoxic [kajiyama2002]; and adulteration with quercetin/rutin is documented [govindaraghavan2018, grazina2025].", "adverse_effects": ["Mild gastrointestinal upset, headache, dizziness, allergic skin reactions (generally similar to placebo in trials)", "Possible bleeding, especially combined with antithrombotic drugs (case reports; observational signal)", "Seizure precipitation in people with epilepsy (case reports)", "Contact dermatitis/allergy from ginkgolic acids in poorly refined products", "Seed ingestion: ginkgotoxin poisoning with seizures (children)"], "contraindications": ["Pregnancy and breastfeeding (no adequate safety data; mouse reproductive study exists but human data lacking - EMA monograph wording not verified here)", "Children and adolescents under 18 (no efficacy or safety data in this review)", "Epilepsy or seizure history", "Bleeding disorders; scheduled surgery (discontinue beforehand; exact interval to be set by clinical pharmacologist)", "Hypersensitivity to ginkgo"], "drug_interactions": ["Anticoagulants (warfarin, DOACs) and antiplatelets (aspirin, clopidogrel), NSAIDs: possible additive bleeding risk [mai2025]; RCT haemostasis data reassuring [kellermann2011]", "CYP2C19 substrates (e.g., omeprazole) and CYP3A4 substrates (e.g., midazolam): weak induction / inhibition above 240 mg/day [unger2013]", "P-gp/OATP substrates (e.g., talinolol) with high-dose poorly characterised extracts [unger2013]", "Antiepileptics: theoretical lowering of seizure threshold [granger2001]"], "upper_limits": "No tolerable upper intake level (not a nutrient). Medicinal posology 240 mg/day refined extract (EMA HMPC well-established use); trials up to 240 mg/day for 5-6 years. No safety data support higher doses.", "sleep_impact": "No consistent sleep disruption reported in trials; not stimulant. Not systematically studied.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Yes in older adults: GEM median 6.1 years (n=3,069) and GuidAge 5 years (n=2,854) at 240 mg/day without excess serious AEs. No long-term data in young adults; rodent 2-year carcinogenicity positive (non-EGb extract).", "daily_use_appropriate": "uncertain", "special_populations": "Pregnancy, lactation and under-18s: avoid. Older people on antithrombotics or with prior haemorrhagic stroke: medical supervision. Epilepsy: avoid. Psychiatric medication users (e.g., SSRIs with antiplatelet effects, antipsychotics): pharmacist review. Pre-surgery: discontinue.", "specialist_review_needed": ["clinical pharmacologist (antithrombotic and CYP interactions)", "toxicologist (NTP/IARC 2B interpretation and ginkgolic acid specifications)", "neurologist (seizure risk)", "quality/analytical chemist (anti-adulteration testing)"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "Acceptable tolerability in long trials of pharmacopoeial extract, but bleeding interactions, seizure signal, IARC 2B classification, and high adulteration rates mean it could only be used with pharmaceutical-grade specifications, strict exclusions (antithrombotics, epilepsy, pregnancy, under-18s, pre-surgery) and labelling; combined with no efficacy in the target population this risk is not justified."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "EGb 761 and other refined/quantified ginkgo extracts are authorised medicinal products in Germany (e.g., Tebonin) and France (Tanakan, non-reimbursed, free price per French public medicines database accessed 2026-10-25). EMA HMPC adopted an EU herbal monograph (opinion 28 Jan 2015, EMA/HMPC/61601/2015) with well-established use for improvement of age-related cognitive impairment and quality of life in mild dementia, 240 mg/day, with recorded dissent from Spanish and French members (EMA pages accessed 2026-10-25). Ginkgo leaf extracts are also sold as food supplements in many Member States, subject to national botanical lists that vary (not verified per country). Article 13 health claims for botanicals, including ginkgo, are 'on hold' since 2010 and none is authorised in the EU Register; borderline food/medicine classification at 240 mg/day is a real risk in Germany/France.", "us": "Marketed as a dietary supplement under DSHEA (pre-1994 ingredient, no NDI needed for traditional leaf extract). Only structure/function claims with FDA notification and disclaimer; FTC requires competent and reliable scientific evidence, which the null healthy-adult trials [laws2012, solomon2002] undermine for memory/focus claims. NTP 2013 'clear evidence' in mice and IARC 2B could trigger Proposition 65 considerations in California (status not verified here).", "morocco": "Uncertain. Ginkgo extract medicines (e.g., Tanakan-type products) are believed to be sold in Moroccan pharmacies, and food supplements fall under ONSSA notification; no Moroccan price or registration record was found in this search (searches 2026-10-25). Requires local regulatory confirmation.", "permitted_claims_examples": [], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "Leaf extract in food supplements has history of use before 1997, so not novel in that category; use in conventional foods (drinks, bars) is not established and national rules and possible novel-food status would need checking. Ginkgo seeds are a traditional Asian food but toxic. US: no NDI issue for traditional extract.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Poor. Effective-dose claims rely on 120-240 mg of a pharmacopoeial refined extract with specified flavonoid, terpene lactone and ginkgolic acid limits; that is a medicinal-type ingredient, not a food. Bitter/astringent taste, borderline medicinal status in the EU at 240 mg/day, and interaction labelling are incompatible with a mainstream food product.", "formats": ["tablets or capsules (120 mg, 240 mg)", "oral solution (40 mg/mL medicinal products)", "teas (unstandardised; not equivalent)"], "stability": "Terpene lactones and flavonol glycosides reasonably stable in dry dosage forms; stability in beverages or heat-processed foods not established in this review.", "taste": "Bitter, astringent (terpene lactones, flavonoids); would require masking in food.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Studied as dry extract tablets on fasted or unspecified conditions; food-matrix effects not reviewed here.", "dose_feasibility": "Physically feasible (240 mg fits a capsule or a functional drink), but feasibility is irrelevant because no dose shows benefit in healthy people; 240 mg/day equals the medicinal dose in the EU.", "cost_availability": "Widely available worldwide as supplements and OTC medicines; quality highly variable.", "cost_evidence": "US: Nature's Way Ginkgold Max 120 mg x 60 tablets listed at USD 32.90 (Vitacost) to USD 45.99 (Pine Street Clinic), accessed 2026-10-25 via web search; EU/Morocco retail prices not found."}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 1, "effect_magnitude": 1, "safety": 5, "reproducibility": 2, "food_suitability": 2, "cost_access": 5, "rationale": "Evidence quality 1: for the target population (healthy young adults, students, knowledge workers) the grade is E (no benefit) and acute findings are C-inconsistent; the large body of evidence is informative mainly in showing absence of effect. Effect magnitude 1: pooled d about 0 (upper CI 0.07) in healthy adults; small low-certainty effect only in dementia. Safety 5: well tolerated in long trials, but antithrombotic interactions, seizure reports, IARC 2B/NTP and adulteration. Reproducibility 2: acute positives from one lab not replicated; independent large trials null. Food suitability 2: pharmaceutical-grade extract, bitter, medicinal status in DE/FR. Cost/access 5: inexpensive and widely available, but quality inconsistent."}, "research_gaps": ["No adequately powered, preregistered RCT of standardised ginkgo in healthy young adults or students with real-world academic or work outcomes", "No trials under high cognitive workload or sleep deprivation", "Human relevance of NTP mouse liver tumours for EGb 761 specifically (different composition) not resolved; no long-term safety data in young people", "Clinical bleeding risk with DOACs and dual antiplatelet therapy poorly quantified (only observational data)", "Registered trials without results (e.g., NCT02181972 in middle-aged cognitively intact adults, n=120; NCT02386852 ginkgo/ginseng cognition) suggest possible publication bias", "Market surveillance of adulteration in Moroccan and EU supplements"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 2.4, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": "C", "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"global_cognition": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 12, "n_studies": 28, "n_verified": 28, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 9, "n_systematic_reviews": 8, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["inconsistent", "insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "gotu-kola-and-other-adaptogens", "name": "Gotu kola, schisandra, eleuthero, holy basil, maca and other adaptogens", "category": "H", "category_name": "Botanical and nootropic supplements", "aliases": ["Centella asiatica (gotu kola)", "Schisandra chinensis (five-flavor berry)", "Eleutherococcus senticosus (Siberian ginseng, eleuthero)", "Ocimum sanctum / Ocimum tenuiflorum (holy basil, tulsi)", "Lepidium meyenii (maca)"], "food_sources": ["gotu kola: eaten as a leafy vegetable/salad herb in parts of South and Southeast Asia; not a traditional Moroccan food", "schisandra berries: eaten/used as tea in East Asia/Russia; not traditional in Morocco", "eleuthero: root used as tea/tonic in Russia and China; not a food in the ordinary sense", "holy basil (tulsi): leaves used as tea/culinary herb and in traditional Ayurvedic preparations in South Asia; not traditional in Morocco", "maca: root consumed as a food/flour in Peru (roasted, boiled, or as a powder in baked goods and drinks); not traditional in Morocco"], "active_constituents": ["gotu kola: triterpenoid saponins (asiaticoside, madecassoside, asiatic acid, madecassic acid)", "schisandra: dibenzocyclooctadiene lignans (schisandrin, gomisin compounds)", "eleuthero: eleutherosides (lignan and phenylpropanoid glycosides), not true ginsenosides despite the 'Siberian ginseng' misnomer", "holy basil: eugenol, ursolic acid, rosmarinic acid, other phenolics", "maca: glucosinolates, macamides, macaenes; no classical 'nootropic' active constituent identified"], "one_line_verdict": "This is a heterogeneous group of traditional 'adaptogen' botanicals united mainly by marketing category rather than evidence: gotu kola has a 2017 systematic review/meta-analysis finding no significant cognitive benefit despite a mood effect; eleuthero has a negative, well-conducted trial adding it to stress management; holy basil has one small positive trial needing replication; and maca has essentially no human cognitive trial evidence at all.", "scientific_conclusion": "Adaptogen is a traditional-medicine and marketing category, not an evidence-based pharmacological class with a shared demonstrated human mechanism; each botanical here must be judged on its own human trial evidence. Gotu kola (Centella asiatica): the best evidence is a 2017 systematic review and meta-analysis pooling 5 RCTs of C. asiatica alone plus 6 RCTs of C. asiatica-containing combination products; the pooled analysis found no significant difference versus placebo in any individual cognitive domain tested, but did find a significant improvement in a subjective 'alert' mood score (SMD 0.71, 95% CI 0.01-1.41, I2=30.5%) and a reduction in anger scores at 1 hour post-dose (SMD -0.81, 95% CI -1.51 to -0.09) [puttarak2017]. This is an important distinction: the demonstrated effect is on self-reported mood/alertness, not on any measured cognitive-performance domain, and the review authors explicitly concluded there is 'not strong evidence to support the use of C. asiatica for cognitive function improvement.' No adverse effects were reported across the pooled studies, and dose/preparation heterogeneity across trials was noted as a limitation. Eleuthero (Eleutherococcus senticosus): a well-conducted, registered (German Clinical Trials Register DRKS00000692), 144-person, 8-week randomized trial in people with chronic-stress-related fatigue and reduced working capacity found that adding 120 mg/day eleuthero root extract to professional stress-management training produced no additional benefit over stress-management training alone on cognitive performance, fatigue, mood, sleep or cortisol awakening response; the authors concluded any effect of adding eleuthero was 'negligible' [schaffler2013null]. This is a genuine, well-designed null trial in a population with impaired concentration (not even requiring extrapolation from a healthy population), and it is one of the better-quality trials in this entire ingredient cluster precisely because it found nothing. A broader review of plant adaptogens (covering Schisandra, Eleutherococcus, Rhodiola and others) summarises mostly mechanistic/animal and older, lower-quality human stress-fatigue literature without establishing a specific cognitive-performance effect in healthy adults [panossian2021history, ref also seifritz2012review-type sources]. No dedicated cognition RCT for Schisandra chinensis alone in healthy adults, separate from combination-product or fatigue-focused trials, was identified in this search. Holy basil (Ocimum sanctum/tulsi): one small placebo-controlled trial (30-day, dose not clearly separated by group size in the abstract, ethanolic leaf extract 300 mg/day) in healthy adult volunteers found improvement versus placebo on several components of the Sternberg memory-scanning task and Stroop interference task, plus a reduction in P300 auditory event-related-potential latency (a neurophysiological biomarker of processing speed) and improvement on a stress/anxiety inventory, but the placebo group also improved on some measures before stabilising, and the trial is small, single-site, and has not been independently replicated in this search [sampath2015holybasil]. This is a genuine, if preliminary, positive signal in the correct target population (healthy adults) but needs replication before any claim. Maca (Lepidium meyenii): despite maca's popularity as a 'superfood' with claimed energy and mood benefits, a dedicated PubMed search for maca and cognitive/memory outcomes returned zero relevant human trials; maca's human RCT literature instead concerns sexual function, menopausal symptoms, and mood, not measured cognition. The honest statement is that there is no cognitive-function human evidence for maca at all, positive or negative; any cognitive claim for maca would be entirely unsupported. Overall, this cluster illustrates why the 'adaptogen' category should not be treated as a unit: gotu kola and eleuthero have genuine (if modest-to-null) human data in a cognition-adjacent context and the honest answer for both is 'no demonstrated cognitive-performance benefit,' holy basil has a single unreplicated positive signal, and maca has no cognitive data to evaluate.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "None of these botanicals corrects a known nutrient deficiency; they are proposed as general 'stress resilience' or enhancement agents in already-nourished people, so any human evidence found here speaks only to the enhancement question, not deficiency correction. Gotu kola's one demonstrated effect (improved alertness/reduced anger, SMD 0.71 and -0.81 respectively [puttarak2017]) is a subjective mood effect, not a cognitive-performance enhancement, and should not be reframed as a cognition claim. Eleuthero was tested specifically in people already reporting impaired concentration/fatigue (a population that should, if anything, be more likely to show a correctable effect) and still showed no incremental benefit over behavioural stress management [schaffler2013null], which is a stronger null finding than a trial in already-healthy, non-fatigued adults would be. Holy basil's positive trial was in healthy adult volunteers without baseline impairment, so if replicated it would represent a genuine enhancement effect rather than deficiency correction [sampath2015holybasil]. Maca has no cognitive evidence in either framework.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "The 'adaptogen' marketing category (gotu kola, schisandra, eleuthero, holy basil, maca, often sold together or interchangeably as 'stress and brain support') substantially outruns the evidence for cognitive claims specifically. Gotu kola is widely marketed as a 'memory herb,' but the best systematic review found no cognitive-domain benefit and recommends caution; the defensible claim, if any, is limited to subjective alertness/mood, not memory or cognitive performance. Eleuthero is marketed for 'mental clarity under stress,' but the most rigorous trial identified found adding it to stress management training produced no incremental benefit on cognition, fatigue, or physiological stress markers; the honest statement is that the best available trial is a null result. Holy basil has a single small positive signal that is scientifically interesting and worth tracking but does not meet this project's bar for a cognition claim without replication. Maca has essentially no cognitive evidence and should not be marketed for cognition at all; its genuine (separate) evidence base concerns sexual/menopausal symptoms, which is a different, non-cognitive product story. A brand built on 'science first' should either drop cognitive claims for this entire cluster or, at most, offer a narrowly worded 'may support a subjective feeling of alertness' statement for gotu kola, explicitly distinguished from a memory or cognitive-performance claim, and should flag holy basil as 'promising but preliminary, pending replication.'", "mechanism": {"summary": "These botanicals are grouped under the traditional 'adaptogen' concept (non-specific resistance to stress), but their proposed mechanisms are diverse and mostly supported only by animal/in vitro data: gotu kola triterpenoids (asiaticoside, madecassoside) show antioxidant and neurotrophic (BDNF-related) activity in rodent models; eleutherosides are proposed to modulate the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis and cortisol response; holy basil phenolics (eugenol, ursolic acid) show antioxidant and anxiolytic activity in animal models and some in vitro cholinergic/anti-inflammatory activity; maca's macamides/macaenes have unclear CNS mechanisms, with most mechanistic work focused on hormonal/sexual-function pathways rather than cognition. In each case, demonstrated human cognitive-performance outcomes are weak to absent, so the mechanistic plausibility exceeds the human efficacy evidence by a wide margin.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Gotu kola triterpenoids -> antioxidant and putative BDNF/neurotrophic signalling (animal models)", "detail": "Shown in rodent hippocampal/Alzheimer's-model studies; not demonstrated as a human cognitive mechanism.", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Gotu kola oral intake -> acute change in subjective alertness and anger/mood ratings in humans", "detail": "Meta-analytic SMD 0.71 (alertness) and -0.81 (anger) across pooled RCTs [puttarak2017]; no corresponding change in objective cognitive-performance domains.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Eleutheroside compounds -> proposed HPA-axis/cortisol modulation (animal and some human endocrine studies)", "detail": "Plausible mechanism for a 'stress adaptogen' effect; the one well-designed human trial found no attenuation of cortisol awakening response beyond behavioural stress management alone [schaffler2013null].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Eleuthero -> cognitive performance or concentration in fatigued/stressed adults", "detail": "Not demonstrated; null result in the best-available trial.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Holy basil phenolics -> antioxidant/anxiolytic activity and reduced P300 latency (neurophysiological processing-speed biomarker) in one human trial", "detail": "P300 latency improvement and Sternberg/Stroop task gains reported in [sampath2015holybasil]; single small trial, unreplicated.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Maca compounds -> proposed CNS/mood/energy effects", "detail": "No human cognitive trial identified; mechanistic literature focuses on hormonal pathways relevant to sexual function, not cognition.", "evidence_level": "speculative"}], "pathways": ["antioxidant/neurotrophic signalling (gotu kola, animal)", "HPA-axis/cortisol modulation (eleuthero, holy basil)", "GABAergic/anxiolytic activity (holy basil, animal)", "hormonal/endocrine pathways (maca, unrelated to cognition)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "pooled across 5 RCTs of Centella asiatica alone and 6 RCTs of C. asiatica combination products, healthy and some stressed adult samples", "dose_or_intake": "variable extract preparations across pooled trials", "duration": "mostly acute, up to 1 hour post-dose for the significant mood findings", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Meta-analysis found significant improvement in 'alert' mood score (SMD 0.71, 95% CI 0.01-1.41) and reduced anger score at 1 h (SMD -0.81, 95% CI -1.51 to -0.09); these are mood/subjective-state outcomes, not cognitive-performance measures.", "effect_estimate": "SMD 0.71 (0.01 to 1.41) for alertness; SMD -0.81 (-1.51 to -0.09) for anger reduction [puttarak2017]", "certainty_notes": "Downgraded for preparation/dose heterogeneity across pooled trials (I2 30.5% for alertness) and because this is a mood outcome being distinguished here from any cognition claim.", "key_study_ids": ["puttarak2017"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "pooled across 5 RCTs of C. asiatica alone and 6 RCTs of combination products", "dose_or_intake": "variable extract preparations", "duration": "variable across pooled trials, mostly weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Meta-analysis found no significant difference versus placebo in any cognitive-function domain tested (memory, attention, executive function pooled across available domains).", "effect_estimate": "No significant SMD reported for any cognitive domain [puttarak2017]", "certainty_notes": "Downgraded for inconsistency in preparations/doses across pooled trials and for the review authors' own conclusion that evidence does not support a cognitive-function claim.", "key_study_ids": ["puttarak2017"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_fatigue", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "144 adults with chronic-stress-related asthenia, reduced working capacity and impaired concentration (a deficient/low-baseline-function population, not healthy young adults)", "dose_or_intake": "120 mg/day Eleutherococcus senticosus root extract", "duration": "8 weeks", "grade": "B", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Registered RCT found no incremental benefit of adding eleuthero to professional stress-management training on cognitive performance, fatigue, mood, sleep, physical complaints, or cortisol awakening response at 8 weeks; differences that did appear (mental fatigue, restlessness) favoured the combination with behavioural training, not eleuthero itself.", "effect_estimate": "No significant group difference at week 8 for eleuthero vs stress-management-training-alone comparisons (exact test statistics not extracted in this compact pass)", "certainty_notes": "This is a comparatively high-quality, registered, adequately powered trial; the null result is therefore reasonably trustworthy (grade B, not downgraded to C, reflecting good trial conduct despite the negative finding). Population is stress-fatigued adults with impaired concentration, not healthy young adults or students; do not generalize this null result as proof eleuthero fails in genuinely healthy, unstressed adults, though it is the most relevant available human data.", "key_study_ids": ["schaffler2013null"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "single small placebo-controlled trial, healthy adult volunteers, India", "dose_or_intake": "300 mg/day ethanolic Ocimum sanctum (holy basil) leaf extract", "duration": "30 days", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Holy basil group showed significantly better reaction time and error rate on Sternberg memory-scanning and Stroop interference tasks versus placebo, plus reduced P300 latency (a neurophysiological processing-speed biomarker) and improved anxiety inventory scores; placebo group also improved on some measures before stabilising after day 15, complicating interpretation.", "effect_estimate": "Significant intergroup differences on specified Sternberg/Stroop reaction-time and error-rate measures; P300 latency improvement significant only within the holy basil group over time [sampath2015holybasil]", "certainty_notes": "Single small trial, not independently replicated, unclear blinding/allocation detail in the available abstract, placebo-group improvement over time muddies interpretation of the 'true' holy basil-specific effect size; downgraded for imprecision and lack of replication despite being in the correct target population.", "key_study_ids": ["sampath2015holybasil"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "other", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "no cognitive trial of maca identified in any population", "dose_or_intake": "not studied for cognition", "duration": "not studied for cognition", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "A dedicated PubMed search for maca (Lepidium meyenii) and cognitive/memory outcomes returned zero relevant human trials; maca's existing RCT literature addresses sexual function, menopause-related symptoms and mood, not measured cognition.", "effect_estimate": "not reported (no trials exist)", "certainty_notes": "Complete absence of evidence; any cognitive claim for maca is entirely unsupported and should not be made.", "key_study_ids": []}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "variable Centella asiatica extract preparations (pooled)", "onset": "within 1 h for mood effects", "duration_studied": "1 h (mood outcomes)", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "effect_estimate": "SMD 0.71 (alertness), SMD -0.81 (anger reduction)", "certainty": "low-moderate (meta-analysis of heterogeneous small trials)", "safety": "no adverse effects reported across pooled studies", "study_ids": ["puttarak2017"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "weeks (pooled trial durations)", "population": "mixed_adults", "outcome": "cognitive function (all domains pooled)", "effect_estimate": "no significant difference vs placebo", "evidence_quality": "low-moderate (heterogeneous preparations/doses)", "limitations": "Combines C. asiatica alone and combination-product trials; dose/preparation heterogeneity; no domain reached significance.", "study_ids": ["puttarak2017"]}, {"duration": "8 weeks", "population": "clinical_population", "outcome": "cognitive performance, fatigue, cortisol awakening response (eleuthero added to stress management training)", "effect_estimate": "no incremental benefit of eleuthero over stress-management training alone", "evidence_quality": "moderate-good (registered, adequately powered trial)", "limitations": "Population is stress-fatigued, not healthy young adults; does not directly test eleuthero vs no intervention.", "study_ids": ["schaffler2013null"]}, {"duration": "30 days", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "outcome": "memory-scanning/interference task performance, P300 latency, anxiety (holy basil)", "effect_estimate": "significant improvement vs placebo on several measures", "evidence_quality": "low (single small unreplicated trial)", "limitations": "Small sample, single site, placebo-group improvement over time complicates interpretation, no independent replication found.", "study_ids": ["sampath2015holybasil"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Gotu kola and holy basil have long traditional food/herbal-medicine use with generally mild reported side effects (GI upset, rare allergic skin reaction for gotu kola; generally well tolerated for holy basil) in the trials reviewed. Eleuthero was well tolerated in the one high-quality trial reviewed. Maca has a long food-use history in Peru as a root vegetable/flour and is generally regarded as safe in food amounts, though its concentrated-extract supplement safety for non-cognitive uses (hormonal effects) is a separate consideration not covered in depth here since no cognitive claim applies. None of these botanicals showed a significant safety signal in the trials reviewed for this monograph, but none has long-term (multi-month/year) safety data at supplement doses in healthy adults either.", "adverse_effects": ["gotu kola: mild GI upset, rare allergic skin reactions, possible hepatotoxicity reported in isolated case reports with long-term high-dose extract use (not seen in the short pooled trials reviewed here)", "eleuthero: well tolerated in the reviewed trial; rare reports of insomnia or irritability at high doses in other literature", "holy basil: generally well tolerated; mild sedation or GI upset possible; theoretical blood-glucose-lowering effect relevant to diabetic patients on medication", "maca: generally well tolerated as a food; limited data on concentrated extract safety"], "contraindications": ["gotu kola: known hepatic disease (given isolated hepatotoxicity case reports with prolonged high-dose use), pregnancy (traditional caution, limited human safety data)", "eleuthero: hypertension (traditional caution regarding stimulant-like effects), pregnancy (insufficient data)", "holy basil: diabetes on glucose-lowering medication (theoretical additive hypoglycemia risk), pregnancy (traditional caution, insufficient human safety data), bleeding disorders/anticoagulant use (theoretical antiplatelet activity)", "maca: hormone-sensitive conditions (theoretical, given maca's studied effects on sexual/hormonal function, though this is unrelated to any cognitive use)"], "drug_interactions": ["gotu kola: theoretical additive sedation with CNS depressants; caution with hepatotoxic drugs given isolated case reports", "eleuthero: theoretical interaction with digoxin and antidiabetic/antihypertensive medications reported in older literature, not confirmed in the trial reviewed here", "holy basil: theoretical additive effect with antidiabetic and anticoagulant medications", "maca: no major interactions well characterised; theoretical hormonal-pathway interactions not relevant to cognition use"], "upper_limits": "No formal upper limit (UL/ADI) established for any of these botanicals by EFSA, FDA, or ONSSA as far as this search could establish; trial doses reviewed here (120 mg/day eleuthero extract; 300 mg/day holy basil extract; variable gotu kola doses) were well tolerated short-term but long-term upper-limit data are lacking for all four.", "sleep_impact": "Not systematically assessed for cognition-relevant doses in the trials reviewed; eleuthero's traditional 'stimulant adaptogen' reputation suggests caution with evening dosing, though the reviewed trial found no significant sleep-quality differences.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Longest trial reviewed was 8 weeks (eleuthero); no multi-month or multi-year safety data exist for any of these four botanicals at supplement doses for cognitive use in healthy adults.", "daily_use_appropriate": "uncertain", "special_populations": "Pregnancy, breastfeeding, and people on hepatotoxic, antidiabetic, anticoagulant or antihypertensive medications should avoid concentrated extracts of any of these botanicals without medical supervision, given theoretical interactions and limited human safety data; children's safety data are essentially absent for all four.", "specialist_review_needed": ["hepatology input on gotu kola's isolated hepatotoxicity case reports before any chronic-use product", "herbal medicine/pharmacognosy review of eleuthero and holy basil drug-interaction potential", "endocrinology input if maca is ever considered for a hormonal (non-cognitive) product line"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "Short-term use of standard doses of gotu kola, eleuthero and holy basil showed no material safety signal in the trials reviewed, but long-term safety data are lacking for all, and isolated hepatotoxicity case reports exist for high-dose chronic gotu kola extract use; the gate is conditional on using only modest, trial-equivalent-or-lower doses and excluding pregnant/breastfeeding people and those on relevant interacting medications pending specialist review."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "None of these botanicals has an EFSA-authorised cognitive-function health claim in the EU Register of nutrition and health claims as far as this search could establish. Gotu kola, eleuthero and holy basil have some history of use as traditional herbal/food products in various EU member states but specific novel food or herbal medicinal product status varies by country and should be confirmed in the EU Novel Food status catalogue before use. Maca is recognised in some EU jurisdictions as a traditional food (root vegetable/flour) with a pre-1997 consumption history in its country of origin, but EU-wide novel food status should be independently confirmed.", "us": "Gotu kola, eleuthero, holy basil (tulsi) and maca are all commonly sold as dietary supplement ingredients under DSHEA in the US with no identified FDA objection in this search; no FDA-approved or FTC-vetted cognitive-enhancement claim exists for any of them, so any such claim would require independent substantiation.", "morocco": "None of these botanicals is a traditional Moroccan food or herbal ingredient; ONSSA-specific guidance was not identified in this search, and each would likely require a specific import/novel-ingredient dossier before legal sale in Morocco. This should be confirmed directly with ONSSA before any product development targeting the Moroccan market.", "permitted_claims_examples": [], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "Likely novel-food considerations for the Moroccan and possibly some EU markets given the lack of a traditional local food-use history for any of these four botanicals outside their regions of origin; US market entry as a dietary supplement ingredient appears more straightforward given existing DSHEA-based sale, but a specific cognitive-function claim would still require new substantiation under FTC standards in all markets.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Poor-to-moderate for the Moroccan/EU/US food-brand context: none of these botanicals is a traditional food ingredient in the target markets (unlike the sage/rosemary/mint cluster), so any product would need to introduce an unfamiliar ingredient with import/sourcing and consumer-acceptance challenges, on top of a weak-to-null cognitive evidence base for three of the four (gotu kola, eleuthero, maca) and a single unreplicated positive trial for the fourth (holy basil).", "formats": ["standardized extract capsule/tablet (as used in the trials reviewed)", "tea/infusion (traditional format for eleuthero, holy basil, schisandra)", "powder (maca, as traditionally used in Peru)"], "stability": "Dried extracts/powders are generally shelf-stable; taste and bioactive-compound stability in a food matrix (e.g., baked into a bar or blended into a drink) were not studied in any of the trials reviewed here.", "taste": "Gotu kola has a somewhat bitter/grassy taste uncommon in Moroccan cuisine; maca has an earthy, malty taste used in Peruvian food products but unfamiliar locally; eleuthero and holy basil are typically consumed as encapsulated extracts or teas rather than culinary ingredients, limiting food-format taste data.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Not systematically compared between extract and food-matrix delivery for any of these four botanicals in the trials reviewed; this is an additional evidence gap on top of the efficacy gaps already noted.", "dose_feasibility": "Trial doses (120 mg/day eleuthero extract; 300 mg/day holy basil extract; variable gotu kola doses) are plausibly deliverable via a standardized extract capsule but have not been tested as realistic food-serving amounts for any of the four; maca's traditional food-use amounts (grams of root powder) are larger and more food-compatible but have no cognitive dose-response data to anchor a formulation decision.", "cost_availability": "not researched here", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 2.5, "effect_magnitude": 1.5, "safety": 5.5, "reproducibility": 2, "food_suitability": 2.5, "cost_access": 5, "rationale": "evidence_quality low (2.5/10): the best-quality trial in the cluster (eleuthero, grade B for trial conduct) is a null result; gotu kola's meta-analysis found no cognitive-domain benefit; holy basil has only one small unreplicated positive trial; maca has zero cognitive trials. effect_magnitude very low (1.5/10): the only demonstrated effects are a modest mood/alertness signal for gotu kola (not cognition) and one small unreplicated holy basil trial; eleuthero showed no effect and maca has no data. safety moderate (5.5/10): no major signal in short trials reviewed, but long-term data are absent for all four and isolated gotu kola hepatotoxicity case reports exist, hence 'conditional' safety gate rather than a clean pass. reproducibility low (2/10): essentially no cross-study replication exists for any single ingredient in this cluster (one meta-analysis, one single null trial, one single positive trial, zero trials). food_suitability low (2.5/10): none of these are traditional foods in the Moroccan/EU/US target markets, unlike the culinary-herb cluster, adding sourcing/acceptance/regulatory friction on top of the weak evidence. cost_access moderate (5/10): available globally as supplement-industry ingredients but not as familiar foods in the target markets."}, "research_gaps": ["No independent replication of the gotu kola meta-analysis's alertness/anger findings with a standardized single preparation and dose.", "No trial of eleuthero in genuinely healthy, non-fatigued young adults or students (the one high-quality trial was in stress-fatigued adults).", "Holy basil's single positive trial has not been replicated; sample size and full methodological detail (randomisation, blinding, allocation concealment) are not available at abstract level and should be checked in full text.", "No human cognitive trial of maca exists in any population; this is a complete evidence gap, not a negative finding.", "No trial of Schisandra chinensis alone (separate from combination products) on cognition in healthy adults was identified.", "No trial has tested any of these four botanicals in students or knowledge workers specifically, or under acute cognitive/exam stress."], "_derived": {"composite_score": 3.1, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": "C", "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": "C", "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"executive_function": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 5, "n_studies": 3, "n_verified": 3, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 1, "n_systematic_reviews": 1, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "huperzine-a-and-vinpocetine", "name": "Huperzine A and vinpocetine (drug-like constituents sold as supplements)", "category": "H", "category_name": "Botanical and nootropic supplements", "aliases": ["huperzine A", "Hup A", "Huperzia serrata extract", "club moss extract", "vinpocetine", "ethyl apovincaminate", "Cavinton (brand)", "Kavinton (brand)"], "food_sources": ["none; huperzine A is a purified alkaloid extracted/synthesised from the club moss Huperzia serrata, not a food", "none; vinpocetine is a semisynthetic ester derived from vincamine, an alkaloid of the periwinkle plant Vinca minor, not a food"], "active_constituents": ["huperzine A: a sesquiterpene alkaloid, reversible and selective inhibitor of acetylcholinesterase (AChE), also an NMDA receptor antagonist and antioxidant in vitro", "vinpocetine: a semisynthetic vinca alkaloid; weak phosphodiesterase-1 (PDE1) inhibitor, voltage-gated sodium-channel blocker, and IKK/NF-kB inhibitor at higher concentrations"], "one_line_verdict": "Both are isolated, drug-like alkaloids with no food role and no healthy-adult human efficacy evidence; huperzine A has only low-quality dementia trial data and one 1999 Chinese student trial that cannot be verified as replicated, while vinpocetine's Cochrane review found its dementia evidence inconclusive and the FDA has tentatively ruled it is not a lawful dietary ingredient, with a specific pregnancy/miscarriage safety signal.", "scientific_conclusion": "Huperzine A and vinpocetine are both single, defined chemical entities acting on classical drug targets (acetylcholinesterase; phosphodiesterase-1/ion channels), not whole foods or nutrients, and both sit in a regulatory grey zone precisely because of that drug-like character. Huperzine A: the strongest human evidence is in Alzheimer's disease (AD). The 2008 Cochrane review (6 trials, 454 patients) found benefits on MMSE, ADAS-Cog, CDR and ADL versus placebo but rated most trials as low methodological quality and concluded evidence was inadequate to recommend use [li2008cochrane]. A larger 2013 systematic review/meta-analysis (20 RCTs, 1823 AD patients, mostly Chinese-language, high risk of bias) reported similar cognitive gains but explicitly flagged poor trial quality [yang2013]. A separate Cochrane review in mild cognitive impairment (MCI) found insufficient evidence [yue2012mci], and one in vascular dementia likewise found inadequate evidence [hao2009vascular]. None of this generalizes to healthy adults: AD and MCI populations have cholinergic deficits that a cholinesterase inhibitor is mechanistically suited to partially correct, which is a deficiency/disease-correction model, not enhancement. The only report in a non-clinical population is a 1999 Chinese trial of 34 matched pairs of adolescent students reporting a higher memory quotient with huperzine-A capsules (115+/-6 vs 104+/-9, p<0.01) [sun1999students]; this is a single, small, 1990s trial in adolescents (not adults), published only in a Chinese pharmacology journal, with no independent replication identified in this search, no registration, and insufficient methodological detail to assess risk of bias - it cannot support a healthy-adult claim and should not be generalized to adults or Western populations. No RCT of huperzine A in healthy young adults, students, or knowledge workers under modern reporting standards was found. A review of 'botanicals with nootropic effects' catalogued huperzine A among AChE-inhibitor nootropics but did not identify new healthy-adult efficacy trials [roe2021]. Vinpocetine: the Cochrane review (Szatmari & Whitehouse 2003) pooled 3 trials, 583 people with various 1970s-80s dementia diagnoses, found a benefit of 30-60 mg/day on cognitive scales but concluded the evidence was 'inconclusive and does not support clinical use' given outdated diagnostic criteria, short trials, and unclear safety reporting; the sole reviewer with pharmaceutical-industry consulting ties is disclosed [szatmari2003cochrane]. No trial of vinpocetine in healthy adults, students, or knowledge workers for cognition was identified; most recent vinpocetine-cognition literature concerns epilepsy, stroke and Parkinson's models, not healthy enhancement [reviewed by roe2021]. What is unknown/not studied: acute or chronic effects of either compound in rested healthy young adults or students using contemporary cognitive batteries; dose-response; comparative effectiveness versus caffeine or sleep; long-term safety of chronic non-medical use. Both compounds fail the project's basic suitability test for a food company regardless of efficacy debates, because they are isolated pharmacologically active alkaloids dosed at drug-like levels, not nutrients or food constituents, and because (for vinpocetine) a regulator has an active safety concern in a vulnerable population (pregnancy) [fda2016notice].", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Neither compound corrects a nutrient deficiency; there is no dietary requirement for huperzine A or vinpocetine and no food contains them in meaningful amounts. The human evidence that exists is a disease-correction model (partial restoration of cholinergic/vascular function in AD, vascular dementia, or MCI, i.e. clinical populations with pre-existing pathology) [li2008cochrane, yang2013, yue2012mci, hao2009vascular, szatmari2003cochrane]. This is categorically different from cognitive enhancement in adequately nourished, healthy young adults, for whom no adequate trial exists for either compound (the single 1999 adolescent-student huperzine A trial is too small, old, unreplicated and population-mismatched to establish an enhancement effect in adults) [sun1999students]. Brain Food's target consumer (healthy adults, students, knowledge workers) is therefore an untested population for both ingredients; any marketing implying 'students take huperzine A to boost memory' would misapply dementia-population evidence (or a single uncontrolled-quality adolescent trial) to a different population and claim.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Both ingredients are marketed online as 'natural nootropics' ('clears brain fog', 'boosts memory', 'enhances focus') largely by extrapolating from AD trials (huperzine A) or from 1970s-80s dementia trials of uncertain diagnostic validity (vinpocetine), neither of which licenses a healthy-adult cognitive-enhancement claim. For huperzine A the honest evidence summary is: plausible AChE-inhibitor mechanism, low-quality but repeatedly positive signal in diagnosed AD, essentially no credible data in healthy adults. For vinpocetine the honest summary is sharper: Cochrane rated the dementia evidence inconclusive, no healthy-adult cognition RCTs were found, the compound is pharmacologically closer to a drug (weak PDE1 inhibitor developed and marketed in Europe as the prescription drug Cavinton) than a dietary ingredient, the US FDA tentatively concluded in 2016 that vinpocetine is not a lawful dietary ingredient (because it was first investigated as a new drug) and in 2019 specifically warned against its use by women of childbearing age/pregnant women based on a National Toxicology Program report suggesting decreased fetal weight and increased pregnancy loss in animals [fda2016notice]. A company built on 'science first, product second' should not market either ingredient for cognitive enhancement in its core demographic, and must not market vinpocetine to women of reproductive age under any claim.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Huperzine A reversibly inhibits acetylcholinesterase, raising synaptic acetylcholine, and also antagonises NMDA receptors and shows antioxidant/anti-apoptotic activity in vitro and in rodent models; this is the same drug class logic as prescription AChE inhibitors (donepezil, galantamine) used in AD. Vinpocetine is a weak, non-selective PDE1 inhibitor and sodium-channel blocker that increases cerebral blood flow and neuronal excitability in animal and some human PET/SPECT studies, and inhibits NF-kB-dependent inflammatory signalling independent of PDE activity in vitro. Both mechanisms are plausible routes to cognitive effects in a diseased or hypoperfused brain; neither has demonstrated a cognition-relevant mechanism operating in healthy, normally perfused, cholinergically intact brains.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Oral huperzine A -> rapid absorption, crosses blood-brain barrier -> reversible AChE inhibition", "detail": "Established pharmacokinetics from AD trials; no human PK data identified specifically in healthy young adults at supplement doses.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Increased synaptic acetylcholine -> partial correction of cholinergic deficit in AD/MCI", "detail": "Basis for MMSE/ADAS-Cog improvements in Cochrane/meta-analytic AD trials [li2008cochrane, yang2013].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "In a cholinergically intact healthy brain -> unclear whether extra AChE inhibition improves memory/attention or merely risks cholinergic side effects", "detail": "No adequately controlled modern trial in healthy adults; single small 1999 adolescent trial only [sun1999students].", "evidence_level": "speculative"}, {"step": "NMDA antagonism and antioxidant activity -> neuroprotection against glutamate excitotoxicity and oxidative stress", "detail": "Shown in vitro and in rodent ischemia/Abeta models; not demonstrated in human cognition.", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Oral vinpocetine -> hepatic metabolism to apovincaminic acid -> weak PDE1 inhibition, Na+-channel blockade, increased cerebral blood flow (animal/PET studies)", "detail": "Mechanistic basis for proposed vascular cognitive benefit; human cerebral blood flow changes shown in small studies, not linked convincingly to cognitive outcome [szatmari2003cochrane].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Proposed cognitive outcome in diagnosed dementia/vascular cognitive impairment", "detail": "Cochrane review rated the cognitive-outcome evidence inconclusive across 3 old trials [szatmari2003cochrane]; no mechanism-to-outcome chain demonstrated in healthy adults.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["acetylcholinesterase inhibition (huperzine A)", "NMDA receptor antagonism (huperzine A, animal)", "PDE1 inhibition / cerebral blood flow (vinpocetine)", "NF-kB inhibition (vinpocetine, in vitro)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Diagnosed Alzheimer's disease patients, mixed severity, mostly Chinese trial populations", "dose_or_intake": "0.1-0.2 mg twice daily huperzine A", "duration": "6-16 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Cochrane 2008 (6 trials, n=454): MMSE WMD 2.81 (95% CI 1.87-3.76); 2013 meta-analysis (20 trials, n=1823) confirms direction but both reviews flag high risk of bias and mostly unregistered single-country trials.", "effect_estimate": "MMSE WMD 2.81 (1.87 to 3.76) [li2008cochrane]; consistent direction, magnitude not independently reproducible, in [yang2013]", "certainty_notes": "Downgraded for high risk of bias, publication in a single-country literature with limited independent replication, and absence of quality-of-life outcomes; not applicable to healthy adults.", "key_study_ids": ["li2008cochrane", "yang2013"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_mci", "population_detail": "Diagnosed mild cognitive impairment", "dose_or_intake": "not uniformly reported", "duration": "variable, up to 24 weeks", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "Cochrane MCI review found insufficient trial evidence to judge efficacy or safety.", "effect_estimate": "not reported (insufficient data for pooling) [yue2012mci]", "certainty_notes": "Very low certainty; few/small trials.", "key_study_ids": ["yue2012mci"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_decline", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Vascular dementia patients", "dose_or_intake": "not uniformly reported", "duration": "variable", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "Cochrane vascular dementia review concluded evidence was inadequate to support use.", "effect_estimate": "not reported [hao2009vascular]", "certainty_notes": "Few trials, high risk of bias.", "key_study_ids": ["hao2009vascular"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "learning_retention", "population": "children_adolescents", "population_detail": "34 pairs of matched Chinese junior-middle-school students reporting memory complaints, 1999 trial", "dose_or_intake": "100 micrograms huperzine A twice daily", "duration": "4 weeks", "grade": "D", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Memory quotient higher in huperzine-A group (115+/-6) than placebo (104+/-9, p<0.01); language-class scores also improved. Single small trial, no independent replication found, population is adolescents not adults, and reporting is minimal (short communication format).", "effect_estimate": "MQ 115 vs 104 (p<0.01), no CI reported [sun1999students]", "certainty_notes": "Single trial, unclear randomisation/blinding detail, adolescent population, 1999 publication in a Chinese pharmacology journal with no abstract methodological detail on allocation concealment; cannot be generalised to healthy adults or replicated elsewhere in this search.", "key_study_ids": ["sun1999students"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "other", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "No RCT identified in healthy adults for either compound in this search", "dose_or_intake": "not studied", "duration": "not studied", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No adequately controlled trial of huperzine A or vinpocetine on acute cognition in rested healthy adults was found in PubMed searches to 2026; absence of evidence, not evidence of no effect, but the gap is decision-relevant for a healthy-adult food product.", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "No human data in the target population for either acute or chronic use.", "key_study_ids": ["roe2021"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Various dementia diagnoses (vascular, Alzheimer's, mixed), trials conducted before 1990", "dose_or_intake": "30 or 60 mg/day vinpocetine", "duration": "up to 1 year", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Cochrane review of 3 trials (n=583) found apparent cognitive benefit versus placebo but rated the overall evidence 'inconclusive' given outdated diagnostic criteria, small long-duration subsamples, and inconsistent adverse-event reporting; review author discloses pharmaceutical industry consulting relationships.", "effect_estimate": "Direction favours vinpocetine on unspecified cognitive scales; no pooled effect size reported as reliable [szatmari2003cochrane]", "certainty_notes": "Downgraded for outdated diagnostic criteria, risk of bias, and imprecision; not applicable to healthy adults.", "key_study_ids": ["szatmari2003cochrane"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "not established", "onset": "not studied", "duration_studied": "not studied", "domain": "other", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "effect_estimate": "no trial identified", "certainty": "none (no data)", "safety": "unknown in this population/use-case", "study_ids": ["roe2021"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "6-16 weeks", "population": "clinical_population", "outcome": "global cognition (MMSE/ADAS-Cog) in diagnosed AD", "effect_estimate": "MMSE WMD 2.81 (1.87-3.76) [li2008cochrane]", "evidence_quality": "low (high risk of bias, mostly single-country trials)", "limitations": "Most trials pre-2010, Chinese-language sources, unclear allocation concealment, no quality-of-life data.", "study_ids": ["li2008cochrane", "yang2013"]}, {"duration": "up to 1 year", "population": "clinical_population", "outcome": "global cognition in dementia (vinpocetine)", "effect_estimate": "inconclusive per Cochrane review [szatmari2003cochrane]", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Pre-1990 trials, outdated diagnostic criteria, inconsistent adverse-event reporting.", "study_ids": ["szatmari2003cochrane"]}, {"duration": "healthy adults: none found", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "outcome": "any cognitive domain", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "evidence_quality": "no trials identified", "limitations": "Complete evidence gap for the target population.", "study_ids": ["roe2021"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Huperzine A carries classic cholinergic side effects (nausea, dizziness, sweating, blurred vision, bradycardia) at AD-treatment doses and risks additive toxicity with other cholinergic drugs or in people with bradycardia/seizure disorders (NMDA antagonism). Vinpocetine has a specific reproductive safety signal: a National Toxicology Program report prompted an FDA public health warning (2019) against use by pregnant women or women who could become pregnant, citing decreased fetal weight and increased pregnancy loss in animal studies at exposures achievable from marketed supplements. Neither ingredient has adequate long-term (months-years) safety data in healthy adults using over-the-counter supplement doses outside a clinical trial setting.", "adverse_effects": ["huperzine A: nausea, vomiting, dizziness, sweating, blurred vision, bradycardia, muscle twitching (cholinergic excess)", "vinpocetine: headache, dizziness, nausea, flushing, dry mouth, insomnia; animal reproductive toxicity (reduced fetal weight, pregnancy loss) at exposures comparable to marketed supplement doses"], "contraindications": ["huperzine A: bradycardia, seizure disorders, peptic ulcer disease, asthma/COPD, concurrent cholinergic or anticholinergic medication", "vinpocetine: pregnancy and women who may become pregnant (FDA public health warning); bleeding disorders or anticoagulant use (potential antiplatelet effect)"], "drug_interactions": ["huperzine A: other acetylcholinesterase inhibitors (donepezil, galantamine, rivastigmine), anticholinergic drugs, beta-blockers (additive bradycardia)", "vinpocetine: anticoagulants/antiplatelets (theoretical additive bleeding risk), CNS depressants"], "upper_limits": "No ADI/UL set by any major regulator for either compound as a food ingredient; AD trial doses for huperzine A were typically 0.2-0.4 mg/day and for vinpocetine 15-60 mg/day, both well above any plausible 'food' serving.", "sleep_impact": "Not systematically studied for either ingredient in healthy adults; cholinergic and vascular activity make sleep effects plausible but unquantified.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "AD trials extended up to roughly 16 weeks (huperzine A) or 1 year (vinpocetine, one trial only) in diseased populations; no long-term healthy-adult exposure data exist for either compound.", "daily_use_appropriate": "no", "special_populations": "Pregnant women and women who may become pregnant should not use vinpocetine (FDA warning); children/adolescents should not use either compound outside clinical supervision; people with cardiac conduction abnormalities, seizure disorders, or on cholinergic/anticholinergic medications should avoid huperzine A; people on anticoagulants should avoid vinpocetine without medical supervision.", "specialist_review_needed": ["clinical pharmacology/toxicology review of chronic low-dose exposure in healthy adults", "reproductive toxicology review of vinpocetine", "cardiology input on huperzine A cholinergic/bradycardia risk at supplement doses"], "hard_safety_gate": "fail", "gate_reason": "Vinpocetine has an active regulatory safety warning against use in pregnant women/women of childbearing age and FDA has tentatively concluded it is not a lawful dietary ingredient in the US; huperzine A is a drug-like AChE inhibitor with cholinergic adverse-effect potential and no healthy-adult safety data at chronic supplement doses. Neither ingredient is appropriate for an evidence-first food/supplement brand targeting the general healthy-adult population."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Neither ingredient is on the EU/Commission Novel Food status catalogue with confirmed non-novel status in the records checked by this agent; huperzine A and vinpocetine are not on the authorised EU health claims register for any cognitive claim. Status should be independently re-verified against the Commission's Novel Food status catalogue (food.ec.europa.eu) and national lists, as this agent's web search did not return a confirmed catalogue entry either way; treat as uncertain/likely restricted pending direct verification.", "us": "Huperzine A is sold as a dietary supplement ingredient (sourced from Huperzia serrata) under DSHEA, with no FDA objection identified in this search. Vinpocetine: FDA issued a 2016 Federal Register notice tentatively concluding vinpocetine is not a lawful dietary ingredient (because it was first subject to substantial clinical investigation as a new drug), and in 2019 issued a public health warning advising women who are pregnant or who could become pregnant to avoid vinpocetine-containing supplements, citing a National Toxicology Program developmental/reproductive toxicity study. FDA's 2016 tentative conclusion does not appear to have been finalized into a formal rule as of this search; industry continues to sell vinpocetine supplements in the US despite the agency's stated position.", "morocco": "No ONSSA-specific guidance on either ingredient was identified in this search; Morocco generally follows a precautionary, case-by-case authorisation model for novel supplement ingredients and would likely require a specific dossier. Status is uncertain and should be confirmed directly with ONSSA before any product development.", "permitted_claims_examples": [], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "Vinpocetine: FDA has tentatively ruled it is not a legal NDI/dietary ingredient in the US, creating direct marketability risk; several historical NDI notifications were filed but FDA's later position supersedes non-objection to those filings. Huperzine A: sold as a botanical-derived supplement ingredient in the US without an equivalent FDA objection; EU novel food status for both ingredients requires direct, authoritative confirmation beyond what this search could establish.", "regulatory_gate": "fail"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Very poor. Both are isolated, potent alkaloids dosed in milligram or microgram-per-day amounts with drug-like pharmacology; neither can be meaningfully incorporated into an ordinary food serving without functioning as a de facto pharmaceutical-style supplement, which conflicts with Brain Food's food-first philosophy.", "formats": ["capsule/tablet (huperzine A, where legally marketable)", "not recommended as a food-matrix ingredient for either compound"], "stability": "Both are stable crystalline alkaloids in capsule form; stability in a food matrix (e.g., baked goods, beverages) not studied and irrelevant given the recommendation against use.", "taste": "Not applicable at supplement doses; not intended for food incorporation.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Not applicable; no food-matrix bioavailability data exist or are needed given the recommendation against use.", "dose_feasibility": "No: effective/studied doses (0.1-0.4 mg/day huperzine A; 15-60 mg/day vinpocetine) are pharmaceutical-style doses that cannot be delivered via a normal food serving without essentially becoming a drug-dosed capsule product, and for vinpocetine carry an active regulatory/safety red flag.", "cost_availability": "not researched here", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 1.5, "effect_magnitude": 1, "safety": 2, "reproducibility": 2, "food_suitability": 0.5, "cost_access": 3, "rationale": "evidence_quality low (1.5/10): the only human evidence is low-quality dementia trials (grade C at best, clinical population only) plus a single unreplicated 1999 adolescent trial; no healthy-adult human data for either compound. effect_magnitude low (1/10): where any significant effect exists it is in diseased populations and does not establish an enhancement effect in healthy adults. safety low (2/10): vinpocetine carries an active FDA reproductive-safety warning and uncertain US legal-ingredient status; huperzine A has real cholinergic adverse-effect potential and no chronic healthy-adult safety data. reproducibility low (2/10): huperzine A AD trials cluster in a single national literature with high risk of bias; vinpocetine's only systematic review calls its own evidence inconclusive. food_suitability essentially zero (0.5/10): both are pharmaceutical-style isolated alkaloids, not food constituents, dosed well outside any food-serving-feasible range. cost_access moderate (3/10): widely sold online as capsules, but access is not evidence of appropriateness."}, "research_gaps": ["No RCT of huperzine A in healthy young adults, students, or knowledge workers under modern reporting standards (CONSORT, registration) exists.", "No RCT of vinpocetine for cognition in healthy adults exists at all.", "The 1999 adolescent huperzine A trial has never been replicated in adolescents or extended to adults.", "Chronic safety of either compound at typical supplement doses (months to years) in healthy adults is unstudied.", "Vinpocetine's reproductive toxicity signal needs clarification of human relevance and dose-exposure margins.", "Independent (non-Chinese-institution) replication of huperzine A's AD trial findings is limited."], "_derived": {"composite_score": 1.6, "eligibility": "ineligible", "safety_gate": "fail", "regulatory_gate": "fail", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": null, "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"global_cognition": "C", "learning_retention": "D"}, "n_outcome_rows": 6, "n_studies": 7, "n_verified": 7, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 0, "n_systematic_reviews": 5, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["inconsistent", "insufficient", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "lions-mane-mushroom", "name": "Lion's mane mushroom (Hericium erinaceus)", "category": "H", "category_name": "Botanical and nootropic supplements", "aliases": ["Hericium erinaceus", "Yamabushitake", "monkey head mushroom", "bearded tooth mushroom", "erinacine A-enriched mycelia (EAHE)"], "food_sources": ["Hericium erinaceus fruiting body as a culinary mushroom (fresh or dried, stir-fried/sauteed); mycelium-based supplements grown on substrate; not a meaningful dietary source outside people who specifically buy/forage it"], "active_constituents": ["hericenones (fruiting-body-specific, proposed NGF-stimulating)", "erinacines (mycelium-specific diterpenoids, cross blood-brain barrier more readily, proposed NGF/BDNF induction; erinacine A most studied)", "beta-glucan polysaccharides (immune-modulating, not specifically neuroactive)", "ergothioneine (shared with other fungi, antioxidant)"], "one_line_verdict": "In Japanese older adults with diagnosed mild cognitive impairment, 16 weeks of Hericium erinaceus produced a modest, reversible cognitive-scale benefit in one small 30-person trial, but in healthy young adults the only comparable-quality trial (n=41) found just a faster Stroop reaction time after a single dose and a borderline stress trend at 28 days, so a 'boosts your brain' claim for students is not supported by current human evidence.", "scientific_conclusion": "Lion's mane mushroom (Hericium erinaceus) is marketed heavily as a nootropic, but the human evidence is thin, population-specific and mostly from small Japanese or UK pilot trials using different preparations (fruiting-body powder vs mycelium vs erinacine-A-enriched mycelium), which matters because hericenones (fruiting body) and erinacines (mycelium) are chemically distinct and not interchangeable. The foundational trial [mori2009] randomized 30 Japanese adults aged 50-80 with clinically diagnosed mild cognitive impairment to 2 g/day dried fruiting-body powder or placebo for 16 weeks; the Hericium group's scores on a cognitive scale based on the Revised Hasegawa Dementia Scale rose progressively and significantly from week 8, but fell again within 4 weeks of stopping, implying no durable structural change and a need for continuous use. This is grade B evidence restricted to older adults with diagnosed MCI, not generalizable to healthy adults or students. A second small Japanese trial in a broader older/mixed adult sample [saitsu2019] (12 weeks, fruiting body) found a significant MMSE improvement but null results on the Benton visual retention test and a verbal paired-associate learning test, illustrating the common pattern of one positive global screening tool among several null specific-domain tests; this inconsistency and the absence of blinding detail in the abstract downgrade certainty. In cognitively impaired patients with a confirmed early Alzheimer's diagnosis, a 49-week erinacine-A-enriched mycelium trial (n=whole-sample not reported in abstract, pilot, double-blind, placebo-controlled, NCT04065061) [li2020] found the placebo group deteriorated on the Cognitive Abilities Screening Instrument while the treated group improved on MMSE and IADL, plus some biomarker and diffusion-tensor-imaging differences; four dropouts had GI or skin adverse events. This is a mild-AD clinical population and must not be extrapolated to healthy brains; it does support that higher-erinacine mycelium extracts, not fruiting-body powder, are the pharmacologically active form for central effects. For the Brain Food target population (healthy young adults, students, knowledge workers) the only directly relevant trial is a UK pilot [docherty2023]: 41 healthy adults 18-45, randomized, double-blind, parallel-group, testing both an acute single 1.8 g dose and 28-day chronic supplementation. The single positive finding was a faster Stroop reaction time 60 minutes after the acute dose (p=0.005, no effect size reported) and a borderline (p=0.051) trend toward lower subjective stress after 28 days; the authors explicitly flag null and limited negative findings elsewhere in their own battery and warn that the small sample requires cautious interpretation. An industry-funded acute crossover in healthy adults [lamonica2023] directly compared 1 g Nordic lion's mane extract against guayusa tea and placebo: lion's mane improved N-back reaction time and some Serial-7s and Go/No-go metrics and subjective happiness ratings, but the comparator guayusa (caffeine-containing) outperformed it on most objective cognitive measures, and the funder (Center for Applied Health Sciences; author receives industry funding/honoraria) raises a conflict-of-interest concern that caps this at grade B even with positive direction. A small trial reframed as mood rather than cognition [nagano2010] gave H. erinaceus cookies to 30 women with menopausal-range complaints for 4 weeks and found reduced CES-D depression and indefinite-complaints scores versus placebo; this is a mood/menopause biomarker-and-questionnaire study in a specific population, not a cognition trial in healthy young adults, and must be kept in its own evidence row. No systematic review or meta-analysis of Hericium RCTs in healthy adults was found; narrative reviews [contato2025, li2018mech, cha2024] summarize the same small primary trials and mechanistic/animal data without adding independent human evidence. Mechanistically, erinacines and hericenones stimulate NGF synthesis in vitro and in rodents, and erinacine-A mycelium reduces amyloid pathology in APP/PS1 mice; these are animal/in-vitro findings that support biological plausibility but do not raise the human evidence grade. Overall: grade B (small, mostly single-trial, some industry funding) for cognitively impaired older adults on fruiting-body or mycelium extracts over chronic timeframes; grade C (single small pilot, modest acute effect only, inconsistent battery) for healthy young adults/students in both acute and short-chronic timeframes; grade D for any claim of durable memory or IQ enhancement in healthy people, since no trial has shown that.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "There is no dietary deficiency state for Hericium erinaceus constituents; it is a culinary/medicinal mushroom, not an essential nutrient, so every human trial is testing enhancement/pharmacological supplementation rather than correcting a deficit. The two populations that show any signal are (a) older adults with diagnosed mild cognitive impairment [mori2009] or confirmed early Alzheimer's disease [li2020], where cognitive decline is already present and a disease-modifying or symptomatic framework applies, and (b) a single small healthy-adult pilot showing a faster reaction time after one dose [docherty2023]. These are not the same claim: reversing or slowing decline in an already-impaired brain is mechanistically and clinically distinct from enhancing an already well-functioning healthy brain, and the MCI benefit in Mori 2009 reversed within 4 weeks of stopping, consistent with symptomatic support rather than disease modification.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Lion's mane is widely marketed to healthy consumers as a 'focus and memory' nootropic, often citing NGF stimulation as if it were a demonstrated human cognitive effect; NGF induction is an animal/in-vitro finding, not a measured human cognitive outcome. The strongest human signal exists in older adults with diagnosed MCI or mild AD using specific standardized extracts (fruiting-body powder in Mori 2009; erinacine-A-enriched mycelium in Li 2020), not in healthy young consumers. The one healthy-adult trial [docherty2023] found a single significant result (faster Stroop reaction time, acute) among a larger battery of null outcomes, and the authors themselves call the findings 'tentative.' A direct industry-funded head-to-head [lamonica2023] found a caffeine-containing comparator (guayusa) outperformed lion's mane on most objective measures. Honest marketing for Brain Food should avoid implying lion's mane 'boosts memory or IQ' in healthy adults and, if used at all for this population, should frame it as an exploratory ingredient with a single small positive acute-reaction-time signal and plausible but unproven stress/mood effects, while being explicit that the MCI/AD evidence does not transfer to healthy consumers.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Hericium erinaceus produces two distinct classes of bioactive secondary metabolites: hericenones (fruiting body) and erinacines (mycelium), both proposed to stimulate nerve growth factor (NGF) synthesis and, for erinacines, to cross the blood-brain barrier more readily. In rodent and cell models this leads to increased NGF/BDNF expression, hippocampal neurogenesis, and reduced amyloid-beta pathology in transgenic Alzheimer mice. Human evidence for the proposed pathway is limited to indirect biomarker changes (e.g., slowed decline in BDNF and amyloid-beta 1-40 in the placebo arm versus stability in the treated arm of a small AD trial) and clinical scale changes; no human study has directly measured central NGF/BDNF increase after oral Hericium intake.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Oral fruiting-body powder or mycelium extract -> gut absorption of hericenones/erinacines/beta-glucans", "detail": "Pharmacokinetics in humans not well characterized; most trials use whole-powder or mycelial-extract capsules rather than purified compounds.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Erinacines (mycelium) -> proposed blood-brain barrier penetration -> central NGF/BDNF induction", "detail": "Shown in rodent brain tissue after oral erinacine-A-enriched mycelium; not directly measured in human CSF or brain imaging in any identified trial.", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "NGF/BDNF induction -> hippocampal neurogenesis and synaptic plasticity", "detail": "Demonstrated in rodent hippocampus; amyloid plaque reduction shown in APP/PS1 transgenic mice given erinacine-A mycelium [li2018mech-animal].", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Proposed neurotrophic support -> slowed cognitive decline in impaired brains", "detail": "Consistent with the reversible cognitive-scale gains in MCI [mori2009] and the MMSE/IADL/imaging differences in mild AD [li2020], but no human study has measured NGF or BDNF directly in these trials.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Proposed acute central effect -> faster processing speed in healthy adults", "detail": "A single significant Stroop reaction-time effect 60 minutes post-dose [docherty2023] is too fast for NGF-mediated neurogenesis and more plausibly reflects an acute pharmacological or placebo-adjacent effect; mechanism for any acute effect is speculative.", "evidence_level": "speculative"}, {"step": "Beta-glucan immune modulation -> indirect inflammation-mediated effects on mood/cognition", "detail": "Beta-glucans from Hericium and other fungi modulate gut microbiota and immune markers in RCTs [vlassopoulou2021], a plausible but unproven indirect route to CNS effects.", "evidence_level": "plausible"}], "pathways": ["NGF/BDNF induction via erinacines", "hippocampal neurogenesis (animal)", "amyloid-beta pathology reduction (animal)", "beta-glucan immune/gut-microbiota modulation", "unknown acute mechanism for reaction-time effect"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_mci", "population_detail": "Japanese men and women aged 50-80 with clinically diagnosed mild cognitive impairment", "dose_or_intake": "2 g/day dried Yamabushitake (Hericium erinaceus) fruiting-body powder (96% purity, as 4x250mg tablets x3/day)", "duration": "16 weeks, with 4-week washout", "grade": "B", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "n=30, double-blind, placebo-controlled, parallel-group. Cognitive function scale based on Revised Hasegawa Dementia Scale rose significantly from week 8 through week 16 vs placebo, then fell significantly within 4 weeks of discontinuation, suggesting symptomatic rather than disease-modifying benefit.", "effect_estimate": "Not reported as a standardized effect size in abstract; described as significantly increased scores at weeks 8, 12, 16 vs placebo [mori2009]", "certainty_notes": "Single small trial (n=15/group), no long-term replication found, unclear blinding/allocation detail beyond 'double-blind'; downgraded for imprecision and lack of replication. Reversibility on cessation argues against disease modification.", "key_study_ids": ["mori2009"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Older/mixed adult Japanese participants, cognitive status at baseline not detailed in abstract", "dose_or_intake": "H. erinaceus fruiting-body supplement, dose not specified in abstract", "duration": "12 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled parallel trial. MMSE score significantly improved/prevented deterioration, but two other cognitive instruments (Benton visual retention test, standard verbal paired-associate learning test) were null.", "effect_estimate": "MMSE: 'significantly improved' (no numeric values in abstract); Benton and S-PA: not significant [saitsu2019]", "certainty_notes": "One positive screening-level test among three outcomes in one small trial; multiplicity not addressed; downgraded for inconsistency and imprecision.", "key_study_ids": ["saitsu2019"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Patients with confirmed mild Alzheimer's disease (clinical trial NCT04065061)", "dose_or_intake": "Three 350 mg capsules/day of erinacine-A-enriched H. erinaceus mycelia (5 mg/g erinacine A)", "duration": "49 weeks", "grade": "B", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Pilot double-blind placebo-controlled trial. Placebo group significantly declined on CASI; treated group significantly improved on MMSE and had better IADL and contrast sensitivity than placebo; some biomarker (BDNF, amyloid-beta 1-40) and DTI differences favored treatment. Four dropouts from GI/skin adverse events.", "effect_estimate": "Direction and significance reported; absolute score changes and CIs not given in abstract [li2020]", "certainty_notes": "Diagnosed mild-AD population; must not be generalized to healthy adults or even to MCI. Small pilot, multiple secondary outcomes, no mention of formal adjustment for multiplicity; grade B capped by trial size and population specificity.", "key_study_ids": ["li2020"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "reaction_time", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults aged 18-45, UK", "dose_or_intake": "1.8 g Hericium erinaceus (single dose)", "duration": "60 minutes post-dose", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled, parallel-groups pilot (n=41). Stroop task performed significantly faster at 60 min post-dose (p=0.005); other acute cognitive measures in the battery were null; authors call findings tentative given small sample.", "effect_estimate": "p=0.005 for Stroop reaction time; no standardized effect size (d) or CI reported [docherty2023]", "certainty_notes": "Single small pilot trial, one of several outcomes tested was significant without correction; downgraded for imprecision, risk of selective emphasis, and absence of replication.", "key_study_ids": ["docherty2023"]}, {"timeframe": "short_term_days", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Same UK healthy-adult cohort as above", "dose_or_intake": "1.8 g/day", "duration": "28 days", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Trend toward reduced subjective stress after 28 days (p=0.051, borderline non-significant); other chronic outcomes not significant per authors' own caveat about null/limited negative findings.", "effect_estimate": "p=0.051 (borderline), magnitude not reported [docherty2023]", "certainty_notes": "Borderline p-value in a small single trial; this is a subjective questionnaire outcome, not an objective cognitive measure; should not be reported as a cognition effect.", "key_study_ids": ["docherty2023"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy US adults, crossover, industry-funded", "dose_or_intake": "1 g Nordic-grown Lion's Mane extract (single dose)", "duration": "up to 2 hours post-dose", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "n not given precisely in retrieved abstract excerpt but described as a 3-arm crossover; lion's mane improved N-back reaction time, Serial-7s attempts, and Go-stimulus reaction time versus placebo, but underperformed the caffeine-containing comparator (guayusa) on most objective measures.", "effect_estimate": "Significance at p<=0.05 for specific sub-measures; no composite effect size reported [lamonica2023]", "certainty_notes": "Single trial, industry funding and author with declared industry financial ties (honoraria, consulting) cap this at grade B-C; multiple comparisons without correction; comparator outperformed the test ingredient on most measures, which tempers the practical significance.", "key_study_ids": ["lamonica2023"]}, {"timeframe": "short_term_days", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "other", "population_detail": "Women aged ~40s-50s with menopausal-range complaints (Kupperman Menopausal Index cohort), Japan", "dose_or_intake": "H. erinaceus cookies, dose not quantified in abstract", "duration": "4 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "n=30, randomized to HE or placebo cookies. CES-D depression score and Indefinite Complaints Index fell significantly within the HE group; 'insentive' and 'palpitatio' subscale scores lower than placebo; 'concentration' showed only a non-significant trend.", "effect_estimate": "Significant within-group pre/post changes reported; between-group stats for most subscales not clearly separated in abstract [nagano2010]", "certainty_notes": "Small trial, specific population (menopausal-complaint women), mood/psychological outcome not cognitive performance; should not be used as evidence for cognition in healthy young adults or students.", "key_study_ids": ["nagano2010"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "other", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy young adults, general cognitive enhancement claim as marketed", "dose_or_intake": "typical marketed doses (0.5-3 g/day extract)", "duration": "chronic (weeks to months)", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No adequately powered chronic RCT in healthy young adults/students for memory, executive function, or learning outcomes was identified; existing chronic human data come only from MCI/AD populations.", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "Absence of evidence, not evidence of absence; downgraded to D (preliminary/insufficient) pending dedicated trials in this population (several are registered but not yet reporting results, e.g. NCT07405632, NCT07405957).", "key_study_ids": ["mori2009", "docherty2023"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "1.8 g single dose", "onset": "60 min", "duration_studied": "single session", "domain": "reaction_time", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "effect_estimate": "Faster Stroop RT, p=0.005, magnitude not reported", "certainty": "low (single small pilot)", "safety": "no adverse events reported", "study_ids": ["docherty2023"]}, {"dose": "1 g single dose (Nordic extract)", "onset": "1-2 h", "duration_studied": "single session (2 h)", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "effect_estimate": "Improved N-back RT and some Serial-7s/Go-No-go measures vs placebo; inferior to caffeine comparator on most measures", "certainty": "low (single industry-funded trial)", "safety": "no major adverse events reported", "study_ids": ["lamonica2023"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "16 weeks", "population": "older_adults_mci", "outcome": "global_cognition (HDS-R-based scale)", "effect_estimate": "Significant improvement vs placebo from week 8, reversed within 4 weeks of stopping", "evidence_quality": "B (single small RCT)", "limitations": "n=30, no long-term follow-up, mechanism of reversibility unclear", "study_ids": ["mori2009"]}, {"duration": "12 weeks", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "outcome": "global_cognition (mixed battery)", "effect_estimate": "MMSE improved; two other instruments null", "evidence_quality": "C (inconsistent single RCT)", "limitations": "multiplicity not addressed, dose not specified in abstract", "study_ids": ["saitsu2019"]}, {"duration": "49 weeks", "population": "clinical_population (mild AD)", "outcome": "global_cognition, function, biomarkers", "effect_estimate": "Treated group stable/improved on MMSE/IADL vs placebo decline on CASI", "evidence_quality": "B (single pilot RCT)", "limitations": "small pilot, population not generalizable, 4 dropouts for adverse events", "study_ids": ["li2020"]}, {"duration": "28 days", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "outcome": "subjective stress", "effect_estimate": "Trend toward reduction, p=0.051 (borderline NS)", "evidence_quality": "C (single small pilot)", "limitations": "underpowered, borderline result, subjective outcome", "study_ids": ["docherty2023"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Hericium erinaceus has a long history of culinary use as an edible mushroom and short (12-49 week) supplement trials report it as generally well tolerated, with mild gastrointestinal discomfort, nausea, or skin rash as the main reported adverse events (4/number-not-specified dropouts in the mild-AD trial [li2020]). No PubMed-indexed case reports of IgE-mediated allergy, anaphylaxis or occupational contact dermatitis specific to Hericium erinaceus were found despite a targeted search; this absence should be reported as 'not found in this search' rather than as proof of absence, since fungal/mold allergens are a recognized general category of risk and case reports for other medicinal mushrooms (e.g., chaga) do exist.", "adverse_effects": ["mild gastrointestinal discomfort", "nausea", "skin rash (reported in a subset of mild-AD trial participants who discontinued)", "no other adverse events reported across the small trials identified"], "contraindications": ["known mushroom/fungal allergy (precautionary, no Hericium-specific case report identified)", "immune-modulating conditions where beta-glucan immune stimulation could theoretically be relevant (not studied specifically for Hericium)"], "drug_interactions": ["no dedicated human drug-interaction studies identified; antiplatelet/anticoagulant interaction is theoretically discussed for some medicinal mushrooms generally but not demonstrated for Hericium specifically"], "upper_limits": "No established tolerable upper intake level; studied doses range from 1-2 g/day (fruiting body, cognition/mood trials) up to 1.05 g/day erinacine A-enriched mycelium (three 350 mg capsules); no dose-ranging toxicology in humans identified", "sleep_impact": "Not specifically studied; one trial measured sleep quality (PSQI) as a secondary outcome in a menopause cohort [nagano2010] without clearly reported between-group PSQI results in the retrieved abstract", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Longest controlled human exposure identified is 49 weeks in a mild-AD pilot trial [li2020]; no multi-year safety data in healthy adults", "daily_use_appropriate": "probably", "special_populations": "No trials in pregnant/breastfeeding women, children, or psychiatric/medication-using populations were identified; the mild-AD and MCI trials used supervised clinical settings, which does not establish general-population safety for those taking concurrent medications", "specialist_review_needed": ["allergist/immunologist review of fungal-allergen risk given the absence of identified Hericium-specific case reports but known cross-reactivity risk with other fungi", "clinical pharmacist review of theoretical interactions with antiplatelet/anticoagulant or immunosuppressant medications"], "hard_safety_gate": "pass", "gate_reason": "Short-duration human trials (up to 49 weeks) report only mild, self-limited adverse events (GI upset, rash) with no serious adverse events, no identified allergy/anaphylaxis case reports in PubMed, and long culinary history as a food; gate passes but with caveats about the limited duration of trial safety data and absence of dedicated interaction/allergy studies."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Hericium erinaceus fruiting body consumed as a culinary mushroom has a history of food use and is not listed on the EU Novel Food Catalogue as novel in that form; mycelium-based extracts and concentrated erinacine-A-enriched preparations may fall under Novel Food Regulation (EU) 2015/2283 scrutiny depending on processing and concentration, and this status should be confirmed per specific product/extract before EU marketing. No EFSA health claim (cognitive function, memory, mood) for Hericium erinaceus has been authorised under Regulation (EC) 1924/2006; no specific EFSA opinion on a Hericium cognitive claim was identified in this search.", "us": "Hericium erinaceus and its mycelium/fruiting-body extracts are marketed as dietary supplements under DSHEA; as a long-marketed mushroom ingredient many products rely on grandfathered/GRAS-adjacent status for the fruiting body, but concentrated or novel extract forms (e.g., specific erinacine-A-standardized mycelium) may require an NDI notification depending on prior marketing history, which should be confirmed per specific supplier extract. No FTC-vetted substantiation for a specific cognitive-enhancement claim was identified; any 'improves memory/focus' marketing claim in the US would need competent-and-reliable-evidence substantiation that current trial data (small, inconsistent, population-restricted) likely cannot support for healthy adults.", "morocco": "No Hericium erinaceus-specific ONSSA or Ministry of Health regulatory text was identified in this search; status as a food (dried culinary mushroom) versus a supplement/novel ingredient is uncertain and should be confirmed directly with ONSSA before any Moroccan market entry.", "permitted_claims_examples": [], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "Fruiting-body culinary use likely pre-dates novel food/NDI cutoffs in both EU and US; mycelium extracts and erinacine-A-standardized preparations are more likely to trigger novel food (EU) or NDI (US) review depending on processing method and prior market history - uncertain, requires per-product regulatory confirmation.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "The fruiting body is a genuine culinary mushroom (can be sauteed, used in soups, or dried/powdered) and is food-suitable in principle; however, the two trials showing any cognitive/mood signal used standardized 1.8-2 g/day powder or 1.05 g/day erinacine-A-enriched mycelium capsules, doses that are plausible to reach via a concentrated powder/extract format but difficult to guarantee via an ordinary culinary mushroom serving because erinacine/hericenone content varies by strain, growing substrate and processing.", "formats": ["dried fruiting-body powder/capsules", "mycelium extract capsules (standardized to erinacine A content)", "whole dried or fresh culinary mushroom", "cookie/food-matrix delivery (used in one trial)"], "stability": "Not specifically studied for hericenone/erinacine degradation under heat, storage or food processing in the identified literature; general mushroom bioactive stability concerns (heat-lability of some polyphenolic/terpenoid compounds) apply but are not quantified here.", "taste": "Fresh/dried fruiting body has a mild, seafood-like (crab/lobster) flavor commonly described as palatable in culinary use; concentrated extract powders are typically encapsulated or added to food matrices (e.g., cookies) to mask bitterness.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Not characterized in human pharmacokinetic studies for hericenones or erinacines; no data identified comparing bioavailability from whole fruiting body versus standardized extract versus food-matrix delivery.", "dose_feasibility": "The MCI and healthy-adult doses studied (1.8-2 g/day standardized powder) could plausibly fit into a fortified food serving (e.g., a bar or drink mix) as a powder/extract, but the mild-AD erinacine-A dose (1.05 g/day of a 5 mg/g erinacine-A extract, i.e., ~5.25 mg erinacine A/day) requires a standardized concentrated extract rather than whole mushroom, which is harder to deliver reliably in an ordinary food format.", "cost_availability": "Lion's mane fruiting-body powder and mycelium extract supplements are widely available globally from supplement brands and mushroom growers; not researched here in detail for Morocco-specific or EU-specific pricing.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 3, "effect_magnitude": 2, "safety": 7, "reproducibility": 2, "food_suitability": 5, "cost_access": 5, "rationale": "evidence_quality=3: best human evidence is grade B but confined to small, single trials in MCI/mild-AD populations; the only healthy-young-adult trial is a small pilot with one significant result among a null battery (grade C), so overall quality for the Brain Food target population is low. effect_magnitude=2: no standardized effect sizes reported in any trial; the one significant acute effect (faster Stroop RT) lacks a quantified magnitude and the chronic healthy-adult stress trend was borderline non-significant. safety=7: short trials (up to 49 weeks) show only mild self-limited adverse events and long culinary history, but duration of safety data is limited and allergy/interaction data are absent, preventing a higher score. reproducibility=2: each positive finding (MCI, mild AD, healthy-adult acute RT, menopause mood) comes from a single small trial with no independent replication, and the two trials in overlapping healthy-adult territory used different extracts, doses, and outcome batteries. food_suitability=5: the fruiting body is a genuine edible food, but the doses with any human signal require standardized powder/extract delivery rather than ordinary culinary servings, and extract standardization (erinacine content) is inconsistent across products. cost_access=5: widely available as a mainstream supplement/culinary ingredient globally, but not specifically costed for Morocco/EU/US here."}, "research_gaps": ["No adequately powered (n>100), preregistered RCT of Hericium erinaceus for cognitive performance in healthy young adults or students exists; the only direct trial (Docherty 2023) had n=41 and tentative findings.", "No systematic review or meta-analysis pooling Hericium erinaceus human cognitive RCTs was identified; one is warranted now that several small trials exist.", "No study has directly compared fruiting-body (hericenone) versus mycelium (erinacine) extracts head-to-head for cognitive outcomes in the same population.", "No human pharmacokinetic or blood-brain-barrier penetration data for hericenones or erinacines exist; the central mechanism remains inferred from rodent studies.", "No dedicated allergy/safety surveillance or interaction studies (anticoagulants, immunosuppressants) have been conducted for Hericium erinaceus specifically.", "Several newly completed/recruiting trials (NCT06870136, NCT07405632, NCT07405957, NCT04939961, NCT07665840) had not reported results as of this search and should be rechecked for updates."], "_derived": {"composite_score": 3.9, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "pass", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": "C", "best_grade_chronic": "B", "best_grade_young_adults": "C", "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": "C", "best_grade_overall": "B", "benefit_domains": {"global_cognition": "B", "reaction_time": "C", "working_memory": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 8, "n_studies": 10, "n_verified": 10, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 3, "n_systematic_reviews": 0, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["inconsistent", "insufficient", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "multi-ingredient-nootropic-blends", "name": "Multi-ingredient nootropic blends and proprietary formulas", "category": "H", "category_name": "Botanical and nootropic supplements", "aliases": ["nootropic stack", "brain supplement", "cognitive enhancement supplement", "proprietary blend", "multi-ingredient nootropic formulation", "focus formula / memory formula", "multinutrient supplement", "Alpha BRAIN (Onnit)", "Mind Lab Pro (Opti-Nutra / Performance Lab)", "Neuriva (Reckitt)", "Prevagen (Quincy Bioscience; single-ingredient apoaequorin, discussed as category exemplar)", "Focus Factor", "Qualia Mind (Neurohacker Collective)", "Thesis", "Magic Mind", "Berocca / Blackmores Executive B (high-dose B-complex formulas)", "Souvenaid / Fortasyn Connect (medical-food multinutrient, contrast case)"], "food_sources": ["Not a food: these are formulated products (capsules, powders, shots, gums) combining 3-30+ ingredients", "Several components have food analogues: caffeine and L-theanine (tea, coffee), cocoa flavanols (cocoa), tyrosine and choline (protein foods, eggs), B vitamins (whole grains, meat, legumes), coffee-fruit polyphenols (coffee cherry)", "The only blend-like 'food' with a consistent acute cognitive effect is a caffeinated beverage (tea or coffee), whose effect is explained by caffeine"], "active_constituents": ["Highly variable by product. Common: caffeine (anhydrous, guarana, coffee fruit, green tea), L-theanine, citicoline (CDP-choline), alpha-GPC, phosphatidylserine, Bacopa monnieri, Ginkgo biloba, Panax ginseng, Rhodiola rosea, lion's mane (Hericium erinaceus), L-tyrosine / N-acetyl-L-tyrosine, taurine, B vitamins (B6, B9, B12), vitamin C, magnesium, cocoa flavanols, whole coffee cherry extract", "Drug-like or regulatory-problem ingredients found in some products: huperzine A (cholinesterase inhibitor), vinpocetine (FDA 2019 pregnancy warning), DMAE, and, as adulterants or declared unapproved drugs, piracetam, omberacetam (noopept), aniracetam, phenibut, picamilon, centrophenoxine", "Proprietary blends frequently disclose only the total blend weight, not per-ingredient doses"], "one_line_verdict": "No multi-ingredient nootropic blend has shown a replicated, preregistered, independently funded cognitive benefit in healthy young adults; the acute effects that do appear are explained by caffeine they contain, chronic finished-product trials are small, industry-run and multiply tested (and partly null), and the category carries real adulteration and stacking risks, so blends earn grade C at best for any outcome in Brain Food's target population.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. Evidence for blends as a category is single finished-product trials, almost all funded by or co-authored with the brand owner, with no replication of any formula by an independent group. In healthy young adults, Alpha BRAIN improved delayed verbal recall and one executive task versus placebo after 6 weeks in 63 participants aged 18-35, but not other memory, attention or speed measures (Onnit co-author; tested formula contained vinpocetine, since removed) [solomon2016]. Mind Lab Pro's memory trial was pseudo-randomised (36 vs 13 completers, no effect sizes) [abbottimboden2023]; its reaction-time study was published outside PubMed with internal inconsistencies [utley2023]; and the third, EEG trial found no improvement in choice accuracy or reaction time after 60 days [oreilly2025]. Braini improved two of six CNS Vital Signs domains in 12 versus 19 young adults in a trial stopped early [lanou2023]. In middle-aged adults with memory complaints, Neuriva (100 mg coffee cherry extract plus 100 mg phosphatidylserine) showed scattered COMPASS task effects (p 0.02-0.04) while its co-primary BDNF outcome, the Everyday Memory Questionnaire and Go/No-Go were null [doma2023]. An independent-lab bacopa-ginseng-coffee-fruit blend improved N-back acutely (n = 40) [best2021] and delayed recall after 28 days (n = 52) [best2024]. A 141-person, 12-week trial of a B-vitamin plus bacopa plus ginkgo formula, the best-designed chronic blend trial, was null on its primary memory and attention outcomes [young2022]; a sage-rosemary-melissa blend was null overall [perry2018]. Acute effects. Where a blend contains caffeine (guarana, coffee, anhydrous), its acute effects track the caffeine: a multivitamin with guarana improved serial subtraction and RVIP, while the same multivitamin without guarana did not change performance [scholey2013, kennedy2008]; a caffeinated gaming nootropic improved reaction times (ES 0.4-0.6) [medrano2022]. The most informative trial is a 2026 crossover (n = 26) in which a caffeine-free 10-ingredient formulation of about 3.9 g matched 150 mg caffeine on flanker and Stroop reaction time but not on sustained attention (RVIP), with no effect on 3-back working memory; it was retrospectively registered [hearris2026]. Multivitamins. Ordinary multivitamin-minerals are the best-studied blend: a meta-analysis found immediate recall SMD 0.32 (0.09-0.56) but no effect on delayed recall [grima2012]; in COSMOS (older adults), MVM gave global cognition +0.07 SD (0.03-0.11) over 2-3 years [vyas2024]. Young-adult MVM trials are null or limited to subjective stress and fatigue [pipingas2014, kennedy2010, haskell2010, stough2011]. Prevagen's whole-sample result was null per the FTC record; claims rested on post hoc subgroups [moran2016]. A systematic review found insufficient evidence for any OTC supplement, multi-ingredient products included, to prevent cognitive decline [butler2018]. Souvenaid missed its 24-month primary endpoint in prodromal AD [soininen2017]; 36-month benefits came with heavy attrition [soininen2021]. Safety. Products sold as cognitive enhancers have contained unapproved drugs at pharmaceutical doses, and 75% of declared quantities were wrong [cohen2021, crawford2020]; energy and weight-loss products drive most supplement-related cardiac emergency visits in young adults [geller2015]. Unknown: whether any blend beats its caffeine content in students; no preregistered, powered, independent trial exists.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Most chronic 'blend' benefits that survive scrutiny are nutrient-repletion effects of the vitamin and mineral components: multivitamins raise B6, B12 and folate and lower homocysteine [pipingas2014, young2022], and the small COSMOS benefit in older adults [vyas2024] is compatible with correction of marginal status in ageing. In replete young adults, B-vitamin and multivitamin formulas do not improve objective cognition [pipingas2014] and mainly shift self-rated stress, vigour and mental fatigue [kennedy2010, stough2011, haskell2010]. Young 2022 hypothesised that people with sub-optimal diets would benefit more and found the opposite, which does not support a simple deficiency story [young2022]. The botanical and nootropic components (bacopa, ginkgo, citicoline, PS, lion's mane, rhodiola) are not nutrients, so their claimed effects are enhancement claims; for those, finished-product evidence in healthy young adults is limited to small, single, mostly industry trials with inconsistent results [solomon2016, abbottimboden2023, oreilly2025, lanou2023]. Souvenaid targets a clinical population (prodromal Alzheimer's disease) and cannot be generalised to healthy people [soininen2017, soininen2021]. For a student or knowledge worker who eats adequately, the evidence supports neither a deficiency nor an enhancement rationale for a nootropic stack; a food-first diet with adequate iodine, iron and B12 is the defensible deficiency route, and caffeine from tea or coffee is the only reliable acute enhancer.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "The category's main marketing device is substitution: brands cite trials of individual ingredients, often at higher doses, longer durations and in older or impaired populations, as if they were evidence for the finished product (Qualia, Neuriva, Magic Mind, Thesis per X7a). Where finished-product trials exist, claims such as 'clinically proven' rest on single industry-funded trials with many outcomes tested, no multiplicity correction, retrospective or absent registration and per-protocol reporting [solomon2016, abbottimboden2023, doma2023, lanou2023]. Qualia Mind's registered 60-person crossover (NCT04389723, completed 2020) has no posted or published results, and Alpha BRAIN's trial (NCT02739139) was registered after completion, both signs of possible publication bias. Biomarker framing ('boosts BDNF up to 143%') rests on a 10-person single-dose study [reyesizquierdo2013] and was null in the brand's own 138-person trial [doma2023]. Legal outcomes follow the evidence gaps. A US jury found no competent and reliable evidence for Prevagen's eight memory claims, leading to a 2024 nationwide injunction. Neuriva swapped 'clinically proven' for 'clinically tested' in a class-action settlement that was later vacated. Focus Factor's makers paid FTC redress in 2004 (all via X7a). In the EU no nootropic, focus or memory claim is authorised for any botanical or stack; only deficiency-type nutrient claims (iodine, iron, zinc for normal cognitive function; B vitamins for normal psychological function; DHA for normal brain function) are available, and the ASA treats 'nootropic' itself as a health claim. Honest positioning would be: 'a dose-transparent caffeine plus theanine product with a known, modest acute alertness effect', not 'a brain-boosting stack'.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Blends rest on the 'stacking' hypothesis: several low-dose actives acting on different pathways (cholinergic precursors, catecholamine precursors, adenosine antagonism, cerebral blood flow, neurotrophic signalling, B-vitamin one-carbon metabolism) will add up or act synergistically. In humans, only the adenosine-antagonist pathway (caffeine) has a consistently measurable acute behavioural effect. Other pathways rely on single-ingredient trials, biomarker changes (BDNF, fNIRS, EEG 'synergy', fMRI activation) or animal data. Synergy has been asserted but almost never tested factorially; the one direct factorial test in a related combination (ginkgo plus ginseng) found the combination no better than either extract (X1).", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Oral multi-ingredient product -> each ingredient absorbed according to its own pharmacokinetics; per-ingredient dose usually lower than in single-ingredient trials and often undisclosed (proprietary blend)", "detail": "Example: Mind Lab Pro bacopa 150 mg and lion's mane 500 mg versus about 300 mg and 1.8 g in single-ingredient trials (X7a); Hearris 2026 needed six capsules (~3.9 g) to reach trial-level doses of ten ingredients [hearris2026]. Label accuracy is not guaranteed [cohen2021, crawford2020].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Caffeine component (guarana, coffee fruit, green tea, anhydrous) -> adenosine A1/A2A receptor antagonism -> increased arousal", "detail": "Behavioural gains after a multivitamin with guarana but not without it [scholey2013]; caffeine-containing blends show acute RT and attention effects [kennedy2008, medrano2022]; caffeine itself is grade A for acute RT/attention in the caffeine monograph.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "B vitamins / minerals -> correction of marginal status -> lower homocysteine", "detail": "Multivitamins raise plasma B6, B12 and folate and lower homocysteine in young and middle-aged adults [pipingas2014, young2022, haskell2010]; this biomarker change does not translate into objective cognition in replete young adults.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Choline donors (citicoline, alpha-GPC), tyrosine, botanicals (bacopa, ginkgo, ginseng) -> proposed cholinergic, catecholaminergic, vascular and antioxidant actions", "detail": "Supported for individual ingredients by small human trials and animal work; in blends the individual contributions cannot be separated because no trial has used factorial or dismantling designs.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Polyphenol extracts (coffee cherry) -> claimed increase in circulating BDNF", "detail": "A 143% plasma BDNF rise in 10 fasted people after a single 100 mg dose [reyesizquierdo2013] was not reproduced as a 42-day effect in 138 adults [doma2023]; peripheral BDNF is a biomarker, not cognition.", "evidence_level": "speculative"}, {"step": "Altered brain signals (fNIRS PFC oxygenation, EEG information-theoretic synergy, fMRI activation)", "detail": "Reported after blends [best2021, best2024, oreilly2025, scholey2013]; direction varies (reduced PFC activation interpreted as efficiency in one study, increased activation interpreted as benefit in another) and EEG changes occurred without behavioural change [oreilly2025].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Measured human cognitive outcome", "detail": "Acute selective-attention RT gains comparable to 150 mg caffeine for one caffeine-free 10-ingredient formula [hearris2026]; chronic memory gains in single, small, industry trials [solomon2016, abbottimboden2023, doma2023] not supported by a null 12-week blend trial [young2022] or a null EEG trial [oreilly2025].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["adenosine receptor antagonism (caffeine sources)", "cholinergic precursor supply (citicoline, alpha-GPC, phosphatidylserine)", "catecholamine precursor supply under stress (tyrosine)", "one-carbon metabolism / homocysteine (B vitamins)", "cerebral blood flow and neurovascular effects (ginkgo, cocoa flavanols)", "proposed neurotrophic signalling (peripheral BDNF; coffee fruit)", "cholinesterase inhibition (huperzine A; drug-like)", "putative synergy between ingredients (untested factorially)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults 18-35 given caffeine-containing blends (multivitamin plus guarana; gaming nootropic with caffeine); rested, not sleep deprived", "dose_or_intake": "Single serving; caffeine dose often from guarana or anhydrous caffeine and not always disclosed", "duration": "Single dose, testing 30-90 min post-dose", "grade": "B", "direction": "benefit", "effect_summary": "Caffeine-containing blends improve RVIP speed and accuracy, serial subtraction and reaction times, and reduce task-induced mental fatigue. Where the same blend was tested with and without guarana, the behavioural benefit appeared only with guarana, so the effect is attributable to caffeine, not to the vitamins or other ingredients.", "effect_estimate": "Medrano 2022: ES 0.4-0.6 on several RT/accuracy outcomes; Kennedy 2008: significant RVIP speed and accuracy vs placebo (n = 129), effect sizes not in abstract; Scholey 2013: Serial Threes improved only with guarana", "certainty_notes": "Grade B reflects the caffeine effect (grade A in the caffeine monograph) delivered via blends; there is no evidence of added value from the other components. Small samples and industry links (industry-affiliated co-authors in the multivitamin-guarana trials, HSN product) limit the blend-specific inference to D.", "key_study_ids": ["kennedy2008", "scholey2013", "medrano2022"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults (Hearris 2026 n = 26, crossover; Best 2021 n = 40, 18-60 y, between-group) given caffeine-free botanical/amino-acid blends", "dose_or_intake": "Hearris: ginseng 400 mg, ginkgo 360 mg, sage 300 mg, citicoline 500 mg, cocoa flavanols 500 mg, bacopa 320 mg, lion's mane 500 mg, taurine 250 mg, tyrosine 500 mg, L-theanine 250 mg (6 capsules); Best: bacopa + American ginseng + whole coffee fruit", "duration": "Single dose; testing 45-300 min", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "A caffeine-free formula matched 150 mg caffeine on flanker and Stroop RT (selective attention / inhibition) without raising blood pressure, but did not match caffeine on sustained attention (RVIP accuracy) and had no effect on 3-back working memory. A bacopa-ginseng-coffee-fruit blend improved N-back accuracy and RT 45 min post-dose. Whole coffee fruit contains small amounts of caffeine, which was not quantified.", "effect_estimate": "Hearris: flanker RT P = 0.012 and Stroop RT P = 0.039 vs placebo; 3-back NS (P = 0.43-0.73); RVIP accuracy only caffeine > placebo (P < 0.001). Best 2021: N-back accuracy p < .01, RT p < .05; effect sizes not reported in abstracts", "certainty_notes": "Two small trials, different formulas, many outcomes and time points with no clear multiplicity correction; Hearris retrospectively registered. Imprecise and not replicated. No trial isolates which ingredient drives the effect.", "key_study_ids": ["hearris2026", "best2021"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults 18-35 (Alpha BRAIN), 20-68 mean 31 (Mind Lab Pro), 18-30 subgroup (Braini)", "dose_or_intake": "Proprietary or disclosed stacks (bacopa, alpha-GPC, huperzine A, PS, theanine, tyrosine, citicoline, lion's mane, B vitamins)", "duration": "28 days to 6 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Alpha BRAIN improved delayed verbal recall and one executive task but not other memory, attention or processing-speed measures; Mind Lab Pro improved immediate and delayed recall between groups in a pseudo-randomised 36 vs 13 trial; Braini improved 2 of 6 domains in 12 vs 19 young adults. A 60-day Mind Lab Pro trial found no behavioural effect (choice accuracy, RT). No product has been tested twice by independent investigators.", "effect_estimate": "Alpha BRAIN: significant group x time interaction for delayed verbal recall (ConsumerLab reports ~12% gain), effect size not reported; Mind Lab Pro: immediate recall p = 0.005, delayed recall p = 0.034, no effect sizes; Braini: cognitive flexibility p = 0.048, executive function p = 0.025; O'Reilly: no behavioural effect", "certainty_notes": "Downgraded for risk of bias (industry co-authorship/funding, pseudo-randomisation, unbalanced arms, retrospective registration, multiple outcomes without correction), imprecision (n 31-63), inconsistency (null EEG trial) and indirectness (tested formulas differ from marketed ones). Would be very low certainty under GRADE; C is the ceiling.", "key_study_ids": ["solomon2016", "abbottimboden2023", "lanou2023", "oreilly2025", "utley2023"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Middle-aged adults (35-65 y), some with self-reported memory problems, not cognitively impaired", "dose_or_intake": "Neuriva 100 mg coffee cherry extract + 100 mg PS; bacopa 300 mg + American ginseng 100 mg + coffee fruit 100 mg; B-vitamins + bacopa + ginkgo (Young 2022)", "duration": "28-84 days", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Neuriva: scattered COMPASS improvements (numeric working memory accuracy and RT, picture recognition) with null BDNF, null subjective memory (EMQ) and null Go/No-Go. Best 2024: delayed recall and positive affect improved (n = 52). Young 2022, the largest (n = 141, 12 weeks): no effect on primary memory (p = 0.47) or attention (p = 0.17); benefits only in subgroup interactions.", "effect_estimate": "Doma: p <= 0.024 (accuracy), p <= 0.031 (RT), picture recognition p = 0.035; Best 2024: fNIRS d = 0.6 (biomarker), behavioural ES not in abstract; Young: memory F = 0.53, p = 0.470; attention F = 1.89, p = 0.171", "certainty_notes": "The largest and most rigorous trial is null on its primaries; positive trials are small or industry-run (Reckitt employees co-authored Doma; Young funded by H&H Group but reported a null). Inconsistency plus risk of bias.", "key_study_ids": ["doma2023", "best2024", "young2022"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Community-dwelling adults 40-91 y with self-reported memory concerns (Prevagen, Madison Memory Study); healthy adults mean 61 y (sage-rosemary-melissa)", "dose_or_intake": "Apoaequorin 10 mg/day (single-ingredient brand, category exemplar); sage-rosemary-melissa extract", "duration": "2 weeks to 90 days", "grade": "E", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Madison Memory Study: published abstract reports improved verbal learning, but per the FTC/NY AG litigation record there was no treatment effect in the whole sample, and positives came from more than 30 post hoc subgroup analyses; a 2024 jury found no competent and reliable evidence for the memory claims. Sage-rosemary-melissa: no overall effect; a subgroup under 63 y showed d = 0.92 (small cells: 10 vs 16).", "effect_estimate": "Moran: whole-sample effect null (per FTC complaint, secondary source); Perry: overall NS, subgroup d = 0.92", "certainty_notes": "Primary analyses null; subgroup-driven claims are classic multiplicity artefacts. Grade E: no meaningful benefit shown.", "key_study_ids": ["moran2016", "perry2018"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "US adults >= 60 y in COSMOS (ordinary multivitamin-mineral, not a nootropic stack; contrast case)", "dose_or_intake": "Daily commercial multivitamin-mineral", "duration": "2-3 years", "grade": "B", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Meta-analysis of three COSMOS substudies: small benefit on global cognition and episodic memory; in-clinic subcohort benefit significant for episodic memory only. Earlier meta-analysis found immediate recall SMD 0.32 but no effect on delayed recall or verbal fluency.", "effect_estimate": "Global cognition +0.07 SD (95% CI 0.03 to 0.11); episodic memory +0.06 SD (0.03 to 0.10) [vyas2024]; immediate recall SMD 0.32 (0.09 to 0.56), delayed recall SMD -0.14 (-0.43 to 0.14) [grima2012]", "certainty_notes": "Large, independent, registered trials, but one parent trial, a modest effect (about 0.07 SD), partly telephone/web cognition and an older population; indirect for young adults.", "key_study_ids": ["vyas2024", "grima2012"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Adults 20-50 y (Pipingas 2014, multivitamin + minerals + herbs)", "dose_or_intake": "Daily multivitamin/mineral/herbal tablet (Swisse-type formula)", "duration": "16 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "No cognitive benefit in women; in men a trend on incongruent Stroop that did not survive multiplicity correction, despite large increases in B6, B12 and folate and lower homocysteine.", "effect_estimate": "Stroop (men) p = 0.01 unadjusted, NS after correction; no other effects", "certainty_notes": "Single trial, moderate size (n = 116 completers); biomarker change without cognitive change. Consistent with the absence of enhancement in replete young adults.", "key_study_ids": ["pipingas2014"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "knowledge_workers", "population_detail": "Employed adults: men 30-55 y (Berocca, n = 215), adults with work stress (Blackmores-type B-complex, n = 60), women 25-50 (Supradyn, n = 216)", "dose_or_intake": "High-dose B-complex with vitamin C and minerals, or full multivitamin-mineral", "duration": "33-90 days", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Improvements in self-rated stress (PSS), GHQ-12, vigour, personal strain and task-induced mental fatigue; objective performance gains limited to single tasks (Serial 3s; multitasking accuracy, Stroop).", "effect_estimate": "Kennedy 2010: PSS, GHQ-12, POMS vigour and Serial 3s significant (effect sizes not in abstract); Stough 2011: lower personal strain, confusion and dejection after covariate adjustment; Haskell 2010: attenuated task fatigue, better multitasking accuracy", "certainty_notes": "Mostly subjective outcomes; likely industry-linked (industry-affiliated co-author in Kennedy/Haskell, not confirmed from full text; Stough funding not stated in abstract); Stough effect only after statistical adjustment. Subjective feeling is not cognition.", "key_study_ids": ["kennedy2010", "stough2011", "haskell2010"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_decline", "population": "older_adults_mci", "population_detail": "Prodromal Alzheimer's disease (IWG-1), LipiDiDiet; medical-food multinutrient Fortasyn Connect (Souvenaid); clinical contrast case", "dose_or_intake": "125 mL/day drink (DHA, EPA, uridine, choline, phospholipids, B vitamins, vitamins C and E, selenium)", "duration": "24-36 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Primary NTB endpoint null at 24 months; at 36 months, reduced decline on NTB composite and CDR-SB in completers, with heavy attrition (81 of 311 in the 36-month efficacy analysis). Not generalisable to healthy adults.", "effect_estimate": "24 m: NTB difference 0.098 (95% CI -0.041 to 0.237), p = 0.166; 36 m: 0.212 (0.044 to 0.380), p = 0.014, d 0.25-0.31", "certainty_notes": "Missed primary endpoint; later benefits subject to attrition bias; extension funded by Danone Nutricia. Clinical population only.", "key_study_ids": ["soininen2017", "soininen2021"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "dementia_risk", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Adults with normal cognition or MCI; OTC supplements including multi-ingredient products, trials >= 6 months", "dose_or_intake": "Various", "duration": ">= 6 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "AHRQ-funded systematic review of 38 trials: evidence for multivitamins or multi-ingredient supplements is insufficient or low-strength and suggests no reduction in cognitive decline; a 10-year review of long-term nutraceutical trials in the elderly reached the same conclusion.", "effect_estimate": "Not pooled for multi-ingredient products (insufficient/low strength of evidence)", "certainty_notes": "High attrition, variable outcomes; independent review. Indirect for young adults.", "key_study_ids": ["butler2018"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "neuroplasticity_biomarkers", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Fasted healthy adults (single-dose BDNF study) and middle-aged adults (42-day Neuriva trial)", "dose_or_intake": "100 mg whole coffee fruit concentrate; Neuriva 100 mg coffee cherry + 100 mg PS", "duration": "Single dose (120 min); 42 days", "grade": "D", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Plasma BDNF rose 143% from baseline 60 min after a single dose in about 10 people per arm; the brand's 138-person trial found no BDNF difference versus placebo at day 42. Peripheral BDNF is a biomarker of uncertain relation to brain BDNF or cognition.", "effect_estimate": "+143% vs baseline (n = 10) [reyesizquierdo2013]; no significant BDNF difference [doma2023]", "certainty_notes": "Tiny exploratory study by the ingredient developer versus a larger null; biomarker only. Mechanism is not efficacy.", "key_study_ids": ["reyesizquierdo2013", "doma2023"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "learning_retention", "population": "students", "population_detail": "University students under real academic load (exams, coursework)", "dose_or_intake": "Any marketed nootropic stack", "duration": "Not studied", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No finished-product trial of a multi-ingredient nootropic measured learning, exam performance or retention in students. Young-adult trials used laboratory tasks over 4-8 weeks.", "effect_estimate": "not reported (no trials)", "certainty_notes": "Absence of evidence; extrapolation from laboratory tasks in small industry trials is not justified.", "key_study_ids": ["solomon2016", "abbottimboden2023"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "other", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Consumers of online 'cognitive enhancement' supplements labelled with racetams, centrophenoxine, huperzine A or similar drug-like ingredients", "dose_or_intake": "Label doses; actual content often inaccurate (up to 4-fold pharmaceutical doses)", "duration": "Unknown (no controlled exposure studies)", "grade": "E", "direction": "harm", "effect_summary": "Product analyses found undeclared or unapproved drugs (omberacetam, aniracetam, phenibut, vinpocetine, picamilon, piracetam) and stimulants never tested in humans; 67-83% of products had label mismatches. Health effects of these untested combinations are unknown, and some (phenibut) carry dependence and withdrawal risk.", "effect_estimate": "Cohen 2021: 75% (9/12) declared quantities inaccurate; up to 40.6 mg omberacetam per serving; Crawford 2020: 8/12 products missing a labelled ingredient, 10/12 containing unlabelled compounds", "certainty_notes": "Analytical surveys, not clinical outcome studies; they establish a material safety concern for this market segment rather than an effect size.", "key_study_ids": ["cohen2021", "crawford2020"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "Multivitamin/mineral + guarana drink (caffeine from guarana; amount not in abstract)", "onset": "30 min", "duration_studied": "60 min of repeated Cognitive Demand Battery", "domain": "sustained attention (RVIP), mental fatigue", "population": "129 healthy young adults", "effect_estimate": "Improved RVIP speed and accuracy, attenuated mental fatigue vs placebo; ES not reported in abstract", "certainty": "moderate for a caffeine effect; low for blend-specific value", "safety": "Caffeine-related; not reported in abstract", "study_ids": ["kennedy2008"]}, {"dose": "Multivitamin with vs without guarana (crossover)", "onset": "Post-dose testing (timing per paper)", "duration_studied": "10 min Cognitive Demand Battery; fMRI in 5 participants", "domain": "serial subtraction, mood", "population": "20 healthy adults, mean 29 y", "effect_estimate": "Only the guarana version improved Serial Threes and contentment; both increased fMRI activation (biomarker)", "certainty": "low (n = 20; fMRI n = 5)", "safety": "not reported", "study_ids": ["scholey2013"]}, {"dose": "10 g Evo-Gamers powder (tyrosine, acetyl-L-carnitine, citicoline, alpha-GPC, taurine, caffeine, theanine, mango leaf, huperzia)", "onset": "30 min", "duration_studied": "single test session", "domain": "processing speed, inhibitory control, spatial working memory, cognitive flexibility", "population": "26 healthy young adults (24.9 y)", "effect_estimate": "ES 0.4-0.6 on several RT/accuracy outcomes; verbal fluency, motivation and anxiety NS", "certainty": "low (single small crossover; caffeine confound; caffeine dose in supplementary table not read)", "safety": "HR and HRV unchanged; huperzine A present (cholinesterase inhibitor)", "study_ids": ["medrano2022"]}, {"dose": "Caffeine-free 10-ingredient formula, about 3.9 g in 6 capsules, vs 150 mg caffeine vs placebo", "onset": "60 min", "duration_studied": "60, 180 and 300 min", "domain": "selective attention / inhibition (flanker, Stroop); sustained attention (RVIP); working memory (3-back)", "population": "26 healthy young adults", "effect_estimate": "Flanker RT P = 0.012 and Stroop RT P = 0.039 vs placebo (comparable to caffeine); RVIP accuracy improved only with caffeine; 3-back NS", "certainty": "low (n = 26, retrospectively registered, many outcomes)", "safety": "No BP rise or negative-mood increase with the blend (caffeine raised BP and negative PANAS)", "study_ids": ["hearris2026"]}, {"dose": "Bacopa + American ginseng + whole coffee fruit (single serving)", "onset": "45 min", "duration_studied": "single post-dose session", "domain": "working memory (N-back), PFC haemodynamics", "population": "40 healthy adults 18-60 y", "effect_estimate": "N-back accuracy p < .01, RT p < .05; reduced PFC activation (biomarker)", "certainty": "low (between-group, n = 40; coffee fruit may contribute caffeine)", "safety": "not reported", "study_ids": ["best2021"]}, {"dose": "100 mg whole coffee fruit concentrate, single dose", "onset": "30-60 min", "duration_studied": "120 min", "domain": "plasma BDNF (biomarker only)", "population": "fasted healthy adults, about 10 per arm", "effect_estimate": "+143% BDNF vs baseline; no cognitive outcome", "certainty": "very low", "safety": "not reported", "study_ids": ["reyesizquierdo2013"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "6 weeks (after 2-week placebo run-in)", "population": "63 healthy adults 18-35", "outcome": "Delayed verbal recall, executive function, other memory/attention/speed tests", "effect_estimate": "Delayed verbal recall and one executive task improved; other measures NS; effect sizes not reported", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Onnit co-author; registered after completion (NCT02739139); tested formula included vinpocetine, later removed; many outcomes", "study_ids": ["solomon2016"]}, {"duration": "30 days", "population": "49 adults 20-68 (mean 31)", "outcome": "WMS-IV memory subtests", "effect_estimate": "Between-group immediate recall p = 0.005, delayed recall p = 0.034; no effect sizes", "evidence_quality": "very low", "limitations": "Pseudo-randomised; 36 vs 13; baseline diet differed; funded by Opti Nutra", "study_ids": ["abbottimboden2023"]}, {"duration": "60 days", "population": "Healthy adults (n = 37 enrolled per registry; 32 analysed per X7a)", "outcome": "Perceptual decision accuracy and RT; EEG network measures", "effect_estimate": "No behavioural improvement; EEG 'synergy' increases (biomarker)", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Retrospective registration; pseudo-randomised by recruitment order; funded by Performance Lab Group", "study_ids": ["oreilly2025"]}, {"duration": "28 days", "population": "31 young (18-30) and 29 older (55-80) healthy adults", "outcome": "CNS Vital Signs domains", "effect_estimate": "Young: cognitive flexibility p = 0.048, executive function p = 0.025 (12 vs 19); SAT-RT improved in both age groups", "evidence_quality": "very low", "limitations": "Stopped early (pandemic); unbalanced tiny arms; multiple domains", "study_ids": ["lanou2023"]}, {"duration": "42 days", "population": "138 adults 40-65 with self-reported memory problems", "outcome": "COMPASS tasks, BDNF, EMQ, Go/No-Go", "effect_estimate": "Some COMPASS accuracy/RT p 0.024-0.035; BDNF, EMQ and Go/No-Go null", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Reckitt co-authors; per-protocol presentation; many outcomes", "study_ids": ["doma2023"]}, {"duration": "28 days + 14-day washout", "population": "52 healthy adults 35-65", "outcome": "Delayed recall, affect, fNIRS, BDNF", "effect_estimate": "Delayed recall and positive affect improved; PFC activation d = 0.6 (biomarker)", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Small; single lab; multiple outcomes", "study_ids": ["best2024"]}, {"duration": "12 weeks", "population": "141 middle-aged adults (mean 52.8 y), optimal and sub-optimal diets", "outcome": "Primary memory and attention composites", "effect_estimate": "Memory p = 0.470, attention p = 0.171 (null); subgroup benefit in optimal-diet group only", "evidence_quality": "moderate (for a null)", "limitations": "Funded by H&H Group (Swisse); subgroup findings exploratory", "study_ids": ["young2022"]}, {"duration": "90 days", "population": "218 adults 40-91 with memory concerns", "outcome": "CogState ISL and ISL-DR", "effect_estimate": "Whole-sample null per FTC record; positive post hoc subgroups", "evidence_quality": "very low", "limitations": "Company-run; >30 post hoc subgroup analyses; claims enjoined 2024", "study_ids": ["moran2016"]}, {"duration": "16 weeks", "population": "116 adults 20-50", "outcome": "Computerised cognitive battery; B-vitamin status", "effect_estimate": "No cognitive benefit after multiplicity correction; B vitamins up, homocysteine down", "evidence_quality": "moderate (for a null)", "limitations": "Single trial; sex-specific trend only", "study_ids": ["pipingas2014"]}, {"duration": "33 days", "population": "215 employed men 30-55", "outcome": "PSS, GHQ-12, POMS, Cognitive Demand Battery", "effect_estimate": "Better stress, mental health and vigour ratings; Serial 3s improved", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Probable industry-affiliated co-author (not confirmed); mostly subjective outcomes", "study_ids": ["kennedy2010"]}, {"duration": "9 weeks", "population": "216 women 25-50", "outcome": "Multitasking performance, task fatigue", "effect_estimate": "Better multitasking accuracy, Stroop and maths speed/accuracy; less task fatigue", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Probable industry-affiliated co-author (not confirmed); single trial", "study_ids": ["haskell2010"]}, {"duration": "90 days", "population": "60 adults with work stress", "outcome": "Mood, anxiety, personal strain", "effect_estimate": "Lower personal strain, confusion and dejection only after adjusting for personality and work demands", "evidence_quality": "very low", "limitations": "Small; covariate-dependent; no cognitive tests; funding not in abstract", "study_ids": ["stough2011"]}, {"duration": "2-3 years", "population": "Adults >= 60 (COSMOS; 573 clinic + 2158 Mind + 2472 Web)", "outcome": "Global cognition, episodic memory", "effect_estimate": "+0.07 SD (0.03 to 0.11) global; +0.06 SD (0.03 to 0.10) episodic memory", "evidence_quality": "moderate", "limitations": "One parent trial; small effect; older population only", "study_ids": ["vyas2024"]}, {"duration": "24-36 months", "population": "311 people with prodromal AD", "outcome": "NTB composite, CDR-SB", "effect_estimate": "24 m NS (0.098, -0.041 to 0.237); 36 m 0.212 (0.044 to 0.380)", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Missed primary; attrition (81 in 36-m efficacy set); clinical population", "study_ids": ["soininen2017", "soininen2021"]}, {"duration": "2 weeks", "population": "44 healthy adults mean 61", "outcome": "Immediate and delayed word recall", "effect_estimate": "Overall NS; subgroup < 63 y d = 0.92", "evidence_quality": "very low", "limitations": "Pilot; tiny subgroup cells; post hoc age split", "study_ids": ["perry2018"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Safety is formula-specific. A dose-disclosed blend of food-grade ingredients (theanine, tyrosine, citicoline, cocoa flavanols, B vitamins at or below EU ULs) used by healthy adults appears well tolerated in short trials, but no blend has long-term safety data. Category-level risks are material: (1) adulteration and label inaccuracy, including unapproved drugs at pharmaceutical doses [cohen2021, crawford2020]; (2) drug-like ingredients (huperzine A, a cholinesterase inhibitor; vinpocetine, with an FDA 2019 warning for women of child-bearing age; phenibut, with dependence and withdrawal); (3) hidden or cumulative caffeine from guarana, coffee fruit, green tea and anhydrous caffeine, stacked with coffee and energy drinks, with sleep disruption and cardiovascular events in young adults [geller2015]; (4) B6 accumulation across products (EU UL 12 mg/day; neuropathy, per the vitamin B6 monograph); (5) interactions of botanicals (ginkgo with anticoagulants or antiplatelets; ginseng with warfarin and hypoglycaemics; 5-HTP with serotonergic drugs).", "adverse_effects": ["Caffeine-related: insomnia, jitteriness, anxiety, raised blood pressure (caffeine raised BP vs a caffeine-free blend [hearris2026])", "Palpitations, chest pain or tachycardia from energy-type stacks (ED surveillance [geller2015])", "Gastrointestinal upset (bacopa, high-dose botanical loads)", "Cholinergic effects with huperzine A (nausea, bradycardia risk)", "Peripheral neuropathy with chronic high-dose B6 from stacking several products", "Dependence/withdrawal with phenibut found in some 'nootropic' products [cohen2021]"], "contraindications": ["Pregnancy and breastfeeding (vinpocetine FDA warning; untested botanicals)", "Children and adolescents (caffeine; no data)", "Cardiac arrhythmia, uncontrolled hypertension (caffeine stacks)", "Anxiety and sleep disorders (stimulant components)", "Bipolar disorder or other psychiatric conditions (tyrosine, 5-HTP, stimulants; specialist review)", "Scheduled surgery (ginkgo; bleeding risk)"], "drug_interactions": ["Ginkgo with anticoagulants/antiplatelets (bleeding risk)", "Huperzine A with cholinesterase inhibitors, anticholinergics and beta-blockers", "Ginseng with warfarin and hypoglycaemic agents", "Caffeine with fluvoxamine, ciprofloxacin and other CYP1A2 inhibitors; stimulants for ADHD", "5-HTP / serotonergic botanicals with SSRIs, SNRIs, MAOIs (serotonin syndrome risk)", "Tyrosine with MAOIs and levothyroxine", "Undeclared racetams or phenibut with CNS depressants (unpredictable)"], "upper_limits": "No UL for blends as such; component limits apply: caffeine 200 mg single dose and 400 mg/day for healthy adults (EFSA 2015; caffeine monograph), 200 mg/day in pregnancy; vitamin B6 12 mg/day (EFSA 2023); folic acid 1 mg/day; niacin (nicotinic acid) 10 mg/day; zinc 25 mg/day; ginkgo and huperzine A have no food UL and are medicinal in some EU member states.", "sleep_impact": "Caffeine-containing blends disrupt sleep if taken within about 6-9 h of bedtime; caffeine content is often undisclosed (guarana, coffee fruit, green tea); caffeine-free blends have no demonstrated sleep effect (Alpha BRAIN measured sleep but results were not detailed in the abstract [solomon2016]).", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Finished nootropic stacks: at most 6-12 weeks [solomon2016, young2022]. Multivitamins: 2-3 years in older adults (COSMOS) [vyas2024]. Souvenaid: up to 36 months in prodromal AD [soininen2021]. No long-term data for botanical stacks in young adults.", "daily_use_appropriate": "uncertain", "special_populations": "Pregnancy, children and adolescents, people with psychiatric disorders, cardiovascular disease, epilepsy, or those on anticoagulants, antidepressants, ADHD stimulants or thyroid medication should not use multi-ingredient nootropics without medical review. Students are at particular risk of caffeine stacking (gum, shots, coffee, energy drinks) during exams.", "specialist_review_needed": ["Clinical pharmacist (interaction screen for every ingredient; huperzine A, ginkgo, ginseng, 5-HTP, tyrosine)", "Toxicologist (cumulative caffeine and B6; contaminant and adulterant testing plan)", "Psychiatrist (stimulant and serotonergic components in vulnerable users)", "Obstetrician (pregnancy exclusion wording)", "Quality assurance / analytical chemist (identity, potency, third-party testing)"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "Pass only for a fully dose-disclosed formula (no proprietary blend) of food-legal ingredients at studied doses, with no drug-like ingredients (huperzine A, vinpocetine, racetams, phenibut, DMAE, centrophenoxine), total caffeine <= 200 mg per serving and declared, B6 well below the UL, batch-level third-party testing for identity and adulterants, and clear warnings for pregnancy, under-18s and medication users. Products not meeting these criteria fail the gate."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "No authorised health claim exists for any nootropic blend or for most stack botanicals (bacopa, ginkgo, rhodiola, lion's mane, citicoline, PS); botanical claims remain 'on hold' and EFSA rejected a citicoline memory claim (2024, per X7a). Authorised cognition-relevant claims are nutrient claims only (Regulation (EU) 432/2012): iodine, iron and zinc 'contribute to normal cognitive function'; B6, B12, folate, niacin, thiamin, biotin, vitamin C and magnesium for 'normal psychological function' and/or 'reduction of tiredness and fatigue'; DHA 'maintenance of normal brain function' at 250 mg/day. Caffeine alertness/attention claims were vetoed by the European Parliament in 2016, so their status is legally uncertain (X4). Ginkgo, ginseng and some others are herbal medicinal products in several member states (EMA monographs), so their food-supplement status varies by country. Huperzine A and vinpocetine are not acceptable food-supplement ingredients in many member states (uncertain; country-specific). Citicoline is believed to be an authorised novel food in the EU (confirm entry and conditions of use in the Union list). Under Art. 1(3) of Regulation 1924/2006 a brand name such as 'Brain Food' or 'nootropic' can itself be treated as a health claim; the UK ASA treats 'nootropic' as a specific health claim (X7a).", "us": "Sold as dietary supplements under DSHEA; structure/function claims ('supports focus') need the 21 CFR 101.93 disclaimer and FDA notification. Ingredients not marketed before 1994 need an NDI notification. Vinpocetine carries an FDA 2019 safety statement, and racetams, phenibut and centrophenoxine are not lawful dietary ingredients. The FTC Health Products Compliance Guidance (Dec 2022) requires competent and reliable scientific evidence, generally RCTs of the product or an essentially equivalent formulation, for cognitive claims. Ingredient studies do not substantiate a blend. Enforcement precedents: Prevagen (2024 nationwide injunction on eight memory claims), Focus Factor (2004 FTC redress), Geniux (2019), Neuriva class actions, Alpha BRAIN class action (2024, dismissed 2025) (X5/X7a).", "morocco": "Food supplements are regulated by ONSSA under Law 28-07 on food safety and require prior authorisation or registration before sale; there is no specific Moroccan positive list of cognitive claims, and Moroccan practice largely follows Codex/EU principles (X6; details uncertain). Imported pharmacy-style 'memory' products are sold online with exam-period claims (X7a). Products containing drug-like ingredients (huperzine A, vinpocetine, racetams) would likely fall under the Ministry of Health medicines framework. Caffeinated drinks above 14.5 mg/100 mL fall under Decree 2-19-13 energy-drink labelling and warning rules. Specific status of each botanical: uncertain; confirm with ONSSA.", "permitted_claims_examples": ["EU (Reg. 432/2012), zinc: 'Zinc contributes to normal cognitive function' (only if the product contains a significant amount)", "EU (Reg. 432/2012), iodine: 'Iodine contributes to normal cognitive function'", "EU (Reg. 432/2012), iron: 'Iron contributes to normal cognitive function'", "EU (Reg. 432/2012), vitamin B12: 'Vitamin B12 contributes to normal psychological function'", "EU (Reg. 432/2012), DHA: 'DHA contributes to maintenance of normal brain function' (250 mg DHA/day)"], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "Each ingredient must be checked individually: some extracts and synthetic compounds (e.g., certain citicoline, PS sources, lion's mane extracts, magnesium L-threonate) may need EU novel food authorisation or US NDI notification; huperzine A and vinpocetine are drug-like and problematic in both markets; racetams, phenibut and centrophenoxine are unapproved drugs, not supplement ingredients.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Poor for a full stack. Trial-level doses of 8-10 botanicals and amino acids reach 3-4 g of actives [hearris2026], which needs 4-6 capsules or a powder sachet; many extracts (bacopa, ginseng, ginkgo) are bitter and astringent. A minimal two-ingredient product (caffeine 75-100 mg plus L-theanine 100-200 mg) fits easily in a drink, shot or tea-based food, and that pairing is the most evidence-aligned choice.", "formats": ["capsules/tablets (most stacks)", "powder sachets / drink mixes (gaming nootropics)", "liquid shots (Magic Mind-type)", "gums and mints (caffeine plus theanine)", "ready-to-drink beverages", "fortified foods (only feasible for vitamins, minerals, caffeine, theanine, cocoa flavanols)"], "stability": "Ingredient-dependent: B vitamins (B1, B12, folate) and flavanols degrade with heat, light and moisture; botanical marker compounds vary by batch; interactions in multi-component powders (hygroscopic citicoline, Maillard reactions with amino acids) complicate shelf-life claims. Each active needs a stability-indicating assay.", "taste": "Bitter (caffeine, bacopa, ginseng, ginkgo, citicoline), astringent (flavanols), sulphurous/umami (amino acids); masking requires sweeteners and flavours, which increases sugar or additive load in food formats.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Largely uncharacterised for blends; ingredient interactions (e.g., theanine blunting caffeine's pressor effect; food delaying caffeine absorption) are documented only for a few pairs. Bacopa is fat-soluble and is better absorbed with food.", "dose_feasibility": "Studied single-ingredient doses (bacopa 300 mg, ginkgo 120-240 mg, citicoline 250-500 mg, lion's mane 1-3 g, PS 100-300 mg) cannot all fit a single food serving at acceptable taste; commercial stacks under-dose to fit two capsules (e.g., bacopa 150 mg, lion's mane 500 mg in Mind Lab Pro, per X7a). Caffeine plus theanine at studied doses is fully feasible in a beverage.", "cost_availability": "Retail stacks are premium-priced; ingredients are widely available from B2B suppliers but quality and adulteration control add cost.", "cost_evidence": "Prices found in X7a (third-party and brand pages): Mind Lab Pro about $69 per 30 servings; Thesis 'starting at $59/mo'; Prevagen $39.95-89.95; Magic Mind about $2.38-5.95 per shot; Moroccan online memory product 210 DH (was 319 DH). Ingredient costs not researched here."}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 2.5, "effect_magnitude": 2, "safety": 4.5, "reproducibility": 1.5, "food_suitability": 3, "cost_access": 3, "rationale": "Evidence quality 2.5: best grades in target populations are C (chronic stack memory, acute caffeine-free blend), and the only B rows reflect caffeine or ordinary multivitamins in older adults, not nootropic stacks. Effect magnitude 2: isolated p 0.01-0.05 effects without reported effect sizes; acute RT gains of tens of ms; COSMOS 0.07 SD. Safety 4.5: well-designed transparent blends of food ingredients are likely safe short term, but the category carries adulteration, drug-like ingredients, hidden caffeine and interaction risks. Reproducibility 1.5: no formula has been independently replicated; null trials (Young 2022, O'Reilly 2025, Perry 2018) and an unpublished registered trial (Qualia, NCT04389723) suggest publication bias. Food suitability 3: full stacks need 3-4 g of bitter actives, though a caffeine-theanine food is feasible. Cost/access 3: premium retail pricing for unproven benefit."}, "research_gaps": ["No preregistered, independently funded, adequately powered (n > 150) trial of any marketed nootropic stack in healthy young adults or students", "No factorial or dismantling trials testing whether a blend outperforms its best single ingredient or its caffeine content (the key 'synergy' claim)", "No trials with real-world outcomes (exam performance, learning retention, work productivity) rather than laboratory tasks", "Unpublished registered trials (Qualia Mind NCT04389723, completed 2020; NCT02857829 stack vs caffeine, completed 2017) need results disclosure", "Long-term (> 12 weeks) safety of botanical stacks in young adults, including cumulative caffeine and B6 exposure", "Market surveillance of adulteration in EU and Moroccan nootropic products (most analytical data are US-based)", "Standardised effect-size reporting and multiplicity correction in blend trials", "Interaction data for common stack pairs (caffeine with tyrosine, citicoline with huperzine A, ginkgo with ginseng plus medications)"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 2.8, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": "B", "best_grade_chronic": "B", "best_grade_young_adults": "B", "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": "C", "best_grade_overall": "B", "benefit_domains": {"sustained_attention": "B", "executive_function": "C", "global_cognition": "B"}, "n_outcome_rows": 13, "n_studies": 28, "n_verified": 28, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 14, "n_systematic_reviews": 3, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["benefit", "harm", "inconsistent", "insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "other-functional-mushrooms", "name": "Other functional mushrooms (cordyceps, reishi, chaga, shiitake ergothioneine)", "category": "H", "category_name": "Botanical and nootropic supplements", "aliases": ["Cordyceps militaris", "Ophiocordyceps sinensis (Cordyceps sinensis)", "Ganoderma lucidum (reishi, Lingzhi)", "Inonotus obliquus (chaga)", "Lentinula edodes (shiitake)", "ergothioneine"], "food_sources": ["Shiitake, oyster, king oyster and other cultivated edible mushrooms are the richest common dietary source of ergothioneine; cordyceps, reishi and chaga are not conventional culinary foods and are consumed almost exclusively as dried fungus, tea, or extract supplements"], "active_constituents": ["cordycepin and polysaccharides (cordyceps)", "triterpenes (ganoderic acids) and beta-glucans (reishi)", "melanin-like pigments, polyphenols, betulinic-acid derivatives and high oxalate content (chaga)", "ergothioneine, a sulfur-containing amino acid/antioxidant concentrated in many edible fungi including shiitake"], "one_line_verdict": "Cordyceps, reishi and chaga have essentially no credible human evidence for cognitive enhancement (the only identified healthy-adult cognitive RCT combining cordyceps and reishi was null), while ergothioneine from shiitake and other edible mushrooms has a plausible nutritional/antioxidant story but no demonstrated cognitive-outcome trial in humans, so none of these four ingredients currently supports a brain-health claim.", "scientific_conclusion": "This monograph groups four mushroom-derived ingredients that are frequently marketed alongside lion's mane as 'functional mushroom' cognitive stacks, despite having a much weaker (cordyceps, chaga) or entirely absent (reishi) human cognition evidence base, plus one dietary micronutrient (ergothioneine) with a plausible but unproven neuro-antioxidant story. Cordyceps (Ophiocordyceps sinensis / Cordyceps militaris) is best studied for exercise performance and fatigue, not cognition. The single identified RCT testing a cordyceps-containing supplement on cognitive function in healthy young adults combined Ophiocordyceps sinensis with Ganoderma lucidum in a 3-arm, 30-day, parallel-group trial in 96 physical-education students; it found no significant difference between high-dose, low-dose, and placebo groups on any of eight cognitive domains tested (global cognition, memory, executive function, attention, processing speed, visuospatial ability, verbal function, motor skills) [tsuk2017]. This is a clear, directly relevant null result in exactly the population Brain Food targets (healthy young adults) and should be weighted accordingly; grade E (not supported) for cognitive enhancement from this cordyceps/reishi combination at the tested doses. No other RCT isolating cordyceps alone for cognition in healthy adults was identified; cordyceps human trials that do exist concentrate on exercise oxygen uptake and reaction time in athletic contexts [cordyceps2026-acute], which is a performance/physiological outcome, not a cognitive-domain outcome, and should not be reported as cognitive evidence. Reishi (Ganoderma lucidum) has a reasonably large human evidence base, but it is concentrated almost entirely in oncology supportive care, where a Cochrane systematic review of RCTs found no convincing evidence that reishi improves tumor response when added to chemo/radiotherapy, while it may enhance some immune parameters (increased NK-cell activity, lymphocyte counts); the review explicitly did not identify cognitive outcome data and recommended further rigorous trials for cancer endpoints only [reishi-cochrane2016]. A broader systematic review of medicinal mushroom supplements in cancer patients reached a similarly cautious conclusion about limited, heterogeneous evidence across mushroom species, again with no cognition-specific findings [narayanan2023]. No dedicated reishi-for-cognition RCT in any population (healthy or clinical) was identified in this search; the cognitive-enhancement marketing claim for reishi is therefore grade E (not supported by any human cognition trial identified) and rests entirely on mechanistic/animal extrapolation from its antioxidant and anti-inflammatory properties. Chaga (Inonotus obliquus) likewise has no identified human cognition RCT; its human literature located in this search is dominated by two case reports of oxalate nephropathy from heavy, prolonged ingestion of chaga powder (10-15 g/day for 3 months, causing acute kidney injury with nephrotic-range proteinuria, requiring hemodialysis and steroids in one case [chaga-case2022]; and progression to end-stage renal disease after long-term ingestion in another case [chaga-case2020]), reflecting chaga's unusually high oxalate content. This is a real, documented safety signal, not a cognition finding, but it is directly relevant to any Brain Food decision about including chaga in a consumer product. Shiitake and other cultivated mushrooms are the major dietary source of ergothioneine, a sulfur-containing antioxidant amino acid of interest for healthy ageing; a 2023 review frames ergothioneine as an 'underrecognised dietary micronutrient' with plausible roles in oxidative-stress mitigation relevant to ageing and neurodegeneration risk, but it explicitly describes this as an emerging hypothesis built on observational and mechanistic data, not demonstrated cognitive-outcome RCTs in humans [tian2023]. A 2025 review similarly describes ergothioneine's antioxidative/neuroprotective properties mechanistically without citing a human cognitive RCT [harasym2025]. Across all four ingredients, a broader cross-species mushroom-and-cognition review [cha2024] (shared with the lion's mane monograph) confirms that Hericium has markedly more (still limited) human cognitive trial data than cordyceps, reishi, or chaga, which is consistent with what this unit's own searches found: essentially no positive, replicated human cognitive-outcome evidence for cordyceps, reishi, or chaga, and no cognitive-outcome human trial at all for ergothioneine despite its biological plausibility as a dietary antioxidant.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Ergothioneine is the one ingredient here with a plausible nutritional-adequacy framing: dietary intake varies widely by mushroom consumption, and low ergothioneine status has been associated observationally with markers of ageing/frailty risk, which is a different claim from 'enhancing' cognition in an already well-nourished young adult [tian2023]. Cordyceps, reishi and chaga are not essential nutrients and have no deficiency-correction framework; every claim made for them is an enhancement/pharmacological claim, and the strongest directly relevant human trial for this population (cordyceps+reishi in healthy students) was null [tsuk2017]. No population-specific enhancement signal (healthy young adults, students, older adults) was identified for cordyceps, reishi, or chaga cognition in this search.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Functional-mushroom blends marketed for 'focus,' 'energy,' and 'immune-brain support' routinely include cordyceps, reishi, and chaga alongside lion's mane, implying a shared evidence base; the evidence does not support this. Cordyceps is reasonably studied for exercise/fatigue outcomes but not cognition, and the one cognition RCT combining it with reishi in healthy young adults was null [tsuk2017]. Reishi's best human evidence (Cochrane) concerns cancer supportive care, not brain health, and even there shows no convincing tumor-response benefit [reishi-cochrane2016]. Chaga's most substantial human case literature is a kidney-injury safety signal from oxalate [chaga-case2022, chaga-case2020], the opposite of a health claim. Ergothioneine (from shiitake and other mushrooms) has a genuinely interesting antioxidant/healthy-ageing hypothesis worth monitoring, but no human cognitive-outcome trial currently exists to support a direct brain-health claim. The honest position for Brain Food is that shiitake/mushroom-derived ergothioneine may belong in a 'general antioxidant/healthy ageing' food story once trial data mature, while cordyceps, reishi, and chaga currently have no credible cognitive-health rationale for inclusion, and chaga carries a documented kidney-safety concern at high intakes.", "mechanism": {"summary": "These four ingredients have distinct, mostly non-overlapping proposed mechanisms, none demonstrated to translate into a measured human cognitive outcome. Cordyceps's cordycepin and polysaccharides are proposed to improve cellular ATP/oxygen utilization (exercise physiology focus). Reishi's triterpenes and beta-glucans are immune-modulating (NK-cell activity, lymphocyte function), studied mainly in oncology. Chaga's polyphenols/melanin complexes are antioxidant in vitro, but its high oxalate content is a documented nephrotoxic risk at high intake, an adverse mechanism rather than a beneficial one. Ergothioneine is a thiourea-based antioxidant amino acid transported into cells via the OCTN1 transporter and concentrated in mitochondria, proposed to reduce oxidative stress relevant to neurodegeneration risk, but this is an extrapolation from general oxidative-stress biology, not a brain-specific demonstrated pathway in humans.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Oral cordyceps polysaccharides/cordycepin -> absorption -> proposed improved cellular energy metabolism and oxygen utilization", "detail": "Supported mainly for exercise/VO2 outcomes in athletic trials, not cognitive outcomes; acute reaction-time changes reported alongside oxygen-uptake changes in one exercise-physiology trial [cordyceps2026-acute].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Reishi triterpenes/beta-glucans -> immune modulation (NK-cell activity, lymphocyte counts)", "detail": "Demonstrated in oncology RCTs reviewed by Cochrane; no link to a cognitive pathway established [reishi-cochrane2016].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Reishi immune modulation -> hypothesized neuroinflammation reduction -> cognition", "detail": "Entirely speculative extrapolation; no human cognitive outcome data identified linking reishi's immune effects to brain function.", "evidence_level": "speculative"}, {"step": "Chaga polyphenols -> antioxidant activity in vitro", "detail": "Supported in vitro/animal antioxidant assays; not demonstrated as a human cognitive mechanism.", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Chaga high oxalate content -> absorption -> renal calcium-oxalate crystal deposition at high/prolonged intake", "detail": "Directly documented in two human case reports (acute kidney injury, nephrotic syndrome; progression to end-stage renal disease) [chaga-case2022, chaga-case2020] - an established adverse human pathway, not a benefit.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Dietary ergothioneine -> OCTN1-mediated cellular/mitochondrial uptake -> antioxidant buffering", "detail": "Transport and tissue accumulation reasonably well characterized in humans; proposed relevance to healthy ageing and neurodegeneration risk is based on observational associations and mechanistic plausibility, not an interventional cognitive-outcome trial [tian2023, harasym2025].", "evidence_level": "plausible"}], "pathways": ["cordyceps energy-metabolism/exercise-performance pathway (not cognition)", "reishi immune modulation (oncology-focused)", "chaga oxalate-nephropathy adverse pathway", "ergothioneine antioxidant/healthy-ageing hypothesis"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "short_term_days", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Physical education students, mean age ~26 years, Israel, mixed sex (n=96)", "dose_or_intake": "commercial supplement combining Ophiocordyceps sinensis and Ganoderma lucidum, high-dose and low-dose arms", "duration": "30 days", "grade": "E", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "3-arm RCT (high-dose, low-dose, placebo), blinded administration. No significant between-group differences on global cognitive score, memory, executive function, attention, processing speed, visuospatial ability, verbal function, or motor skills after 30 days.", "effect_estimate": "No significant differences reported on any of 8 cognitive domains (exact statistics not given in abstract) [tsuk2017]", "certainty_notes": "This is the single most directly relevant trial to the Brain Food population and is a clear null across a comprehensive cognitive battery; downgraded only for being a single trial, but direction is clearly 'no benefit' rather than 'insufficient.'", "key_study_ids": ["tsuk2017"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "other", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Cancer patients receiving reishi alongside conventional oncology treatment (Cochrane review population)", "dose_or_intake": "varies across included RCTs", "duration": "varies across included RCTs", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Cochrane systematic review (updated 2016) of RCTs found no convincing evidence reishi improves tumor response as an adjunct to chemo/radiotherapy; some immune parameters (NK-cell activity, lymphocyte counts) were enhanced. No cognitive outcome was assessed.", "effect_estimate": "Not applicable to cognition; reported for immune/tumor-response outcomes only [reishi-cochrane2016]", "certainty_notes": "Included for completeness because reishi is marketed for brain health; this is an oncology supportive-care evidence base, not cognitive evidence, and should not be used to support a brain-health claim.", "key_study_ids": ["reishi-cochrane2016", "narayanan2023"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "other", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "General healthy-adult cognitive-enhancement marketing claim for cordyceps, reishi, or chaga", "dose_or_intake": "typical marketed doses", "duration": "chronic (weeks to months)", "grade": "E", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No RCT isolating cordyceps alone, reishi alone, or chaga alone for a cognitive outcome in healthy adults was identified in this search; the only combination trial in this population was null [tsuk2017].", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "Graded E rather than D because the one directly relevant trial was an actual null result in the target population, not merely an absence of trials; evidence is both absent and, where present, negative.", "key_study_ids": ["tsuk2017"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "other", "population": "replete_or_high_baseline", "population_detail": "General adult population with varying dietary ergothioneine intake (mushroom consumers vs non-consumers)", "dose_or_intake": "dietary ergothioneine intake, amount not standardized across literature", "duration": "chronic/habitual dietary exposure", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "Reviews describe ergothioneine as a plausible healthy-ageing/antioxidant micronutrient based on observational associations between low ergothioneine status and frailty/ageing biomarkers; no interventional human cognitive-outcome RCT was identified.", "effect_estimate": "not reported (no RCT identified)", "certainty_notes": "Mechanistic/observational plausibility only; downgraded to D because no interventional cognitive trial exists, despite a biologically reasonable hypothesis.", "key_study_ids": ["tian2023", "harasym2025"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "not applicable to cognition - acute cordyceps trial measured exercise oxygen uptake and reaction time in a sport-physiology context", "onset": "not applicable", "duration_studied": "single session", "domain": "reaction_time", "population": "mixed_adults", "effect_estimate": "Faster reaction time and elevated resting oxygen uptake reported in an exercise-physiology trial, not a cognitive-task trial [cordyceps2026-acute]", "certainty": "very low (single trial, physiological not cognitive focus)", "safety": "not reported as a concern", "study_ids": ["cordyceps2026-acute"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "30 days", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "outcome": "global_cognition and 7 other cognitive domains", "effect_estimate": "No significant difference vs placebo on any domain (cordyceps+reishi combination)", "evidence_quality": "C (single RCT, clear null)", "limitations": "combination product, cannot isolate cordyceps vs reishi contribution; single trial", "study_ids": ["tsuk2017"]}, {"duration": "varies (oncology RCTs)", "population": "clinical_population", "outcome": "tumor response, immune markers (not cognition)", "effect_estimate": "No convincing tumor-response benefit; some immune parameters improved", "evidence_quality": "B (Cochrane systematic review)", "limitations": "oncology population and endpoints, not applicable to a cognition claim", "study_ids": ["reishi-cochrane2016"]}, {"duration": "3 months (case reports, not an RCT)", "population": "other", "outcome": "renal function (adverse)", "effect_estimate": "Acute kidney injury/nephrotic syndrome and progression to end-stage renal disease after heavy chaga intake", "evidence_quality": "case report level (not RCT evidence; safety signal)", "limitations": "two case reports only, high/prolonged intake context (10-15 g/day for 3 months in one case)", "study_ids": ["chaga-case2022", "chaga-case2020"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Cordyceps and reishi have reasonably long supplement-market histories without major identified safety signals in the trials reviewed here (Tsuk 2017 reported no notable adverse events; the reishi Cochrane review did not flag major safety concerns beyond general oncology-context monitoring). Chaga carries a documented, serious safety signal: at least two independent case reports describe oxalate nephropathy progressing to acute kidney injury or end-stage renal disease after high (10-15 g/day) or prolonged chaga ingestion, caused by chaga's unusually high oxalate content, especially risky in combination with vitamin C or in people with pre-existing kidney vulnerability. Ergothioneine from dietary mushroom sources (shiitake etc.) has no identified safety signal distinct from normal mushroom consumption at food-level intakes; concentrated ergothioneine supplement safety was not specifically assessed in this search.", "adverse_effects": ["no notable adverse events reported in the cordyceps+reishi cognition RCT [tsuk2017]", "chaga: acute kidney injury, nephrotic syndrome, and progression to end-stage renal disease reported in two case reports of high/prolonged intake [chaga-case2022, chaga-case2020]", "no ergothioneine-specific adverse events identified at dietary intake levels"], "contraindications": ["chaga should be avoided or used with strict dose/duration limits in people with kidney disease, kidney stone history, or those taking vitamin C supplements, given the documented oxalate-nephropathy signal", "reishi: oncology-context immune modulation warrants caution in people on immunosuppressive therapy or anticoagulants (general mushroom caution, not specifically demonstrated as a documented interaction in this search)"], "drug_interactions": ["no dedicated human drug-interaction RCTs identified for cordyceps, reishi, chaga, or ergothioneine in this search; theoretical anticoagulant/antiplatelet and immunosuppressant interaction concerns are commonly raised for reishi in the broader mushroom-supplement literature but not confirmed by a specific trial here"], "upper_limits": "No established tolerable upper intake level for any of the four ingredients; the chaga case reports indicate that 10-15 g/day for 3 months is sufficient to cause serious renal injury in at least one documented case, which should inform a conservative upper-use limit or exclusion for chaga in a consumer product", "sleep_impact": "Not specifically studied for any of these four ingredients in the identified literature", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Longest controlled human exposure identified is 30 days (cordyceps+reishi cognition RCT) and variable durations in reishi oncology RCTs (per Cochrane review); chaga's longest documented 'exposure' data come from case reports of months-long heavy use leading to serious harm, not from controlled chronic safety trials", "daily_use_appropriate": "uncertain", "special_populations": "Chaga: documented harm in at least one older adult with no stated prior kidney disease; caution strongly warranted for anyone with reduced kidney function, kidney stone history, or concurrent vitamin C supplementation. No data identified for pregnancy, children, or psychiatric/medication-using populations for any of the four ingredients.", "specialist_review_needed": ["nephrology review of chaga's oxalate content and safe upper intake before any product inclusion", "oncology/immunology review of reishi if marketed near any clinical population, given its evidence base is oncology-context", "clinical pharmacist review of theoretical reishi/cordyceps interactions with anticoagulants and immunosuppressants"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "Cordyceps and reishi show no major safety signal in the limited human trials identified, supporting a conditional pass for those two; chaga has a documented, serious, dose-related renal safety signal (two independent case reports of acute kidney injury/end-stage renal disease) that would fail a hard safety gate at supplement-level doses unless strict dose limits and contraindications are applied, so the combined ingredient group is marked conditional rather than pass. Ergothioneine at ordinary dietary mushroom intake levels shows no safety signal."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Cordyceps (as fruiting-body/mycelium extract), reishi fruiting body/extract, and chaga are all present in various EU supplement markets; chaga in particular has drawn attention for oxalate content but no specific EU restriction was identified in this search. No EFSA-authorised cognitive-function health claim exists for any of the four ingredients under Regulation (EC) 1924/2006. Shiitake and other culinary mushrooms (ergothioneine's dietary source) are ordinary foods with no novel food issue; isolated/concentrated ergothioneine as a supplement ingredient may require novel food assessment depending on production method - uncertain, requires confirmation.", "us": "All four are marketed as dietary supplements under DSHEA; cordyceps, reishi and chaga extracts commonly rely on prior-market history for GRAS-adjacent/NDI status, though concentrated or novel extraction methods may require NDI notification - uncertain per specific supplier product. No FTC-vetted cognitive-enhancement substantiation was identified for any of the four ingredients; the null cordyceps+reishi cognition trial [tsuk2017] would actively undermine any such claim if challenged.", "morocco": "No ONSSA or Moroccan Ministry of Health-specific regulatory text for cordyceps, reishi, chaga, or ergothioneine was identified in this search; status as food versus supplement/novel ingredient is uncertain and should be confirmed directly with ONSSA before market entry.", "permitted_claims_examples": [], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "Shiitake and other culinary mushrooms are ordinary foods; cordyceps, reishi and chaga extracts and isolated/concentrated ergothioneine may each have distinct novel food (EU) or NDI (US) considerations depending on processing method and prior marketing history - uncertain, requires per-product/per-market confirmation.", "regulatory_gate": "unknown"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Shiitake and other culinary mushrooms are genuinely food-suitable and are the practical vehicle for ergothioneine; cordyceps and reishi are not conventional culinary foods and are typically delivered as tea, powder, or extract capsules; chaga is consumed almost exclusively as a brewed tea or powder extract, and its safety profile argues against food-matrix inclusion at meaningful doses given the oxalate risk.", "formats": ["dried fungus powder/capsules (cordyceps, reishi, chaga)", "brewed tea (chaga, reishi)", "whole culinary mushroom (shiitake, for ergothioneine)", "isolated ergothioneine supplement (emerging format)"], "stability": "Not specifically assessed in the identified literature for any of the four ingredients in a food-matrix context.", "taste": "Shiitake has a well-established savory/umami culinary profile suitable for food inclusion. Cordyceps, reishi, and chaga have earthy/bitter profiles typically masked via encapsulation or tea preparation rather than direct food incorporation.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Not characterized in human pharmacokinetic studies for cordycepin, ganoderic acids, or chaga polyphenols in this search; ergothioneine absorption via OCTN1 transporter is reasonably characterized in the broader nutrition literature but not re-verified here with a dedicated trial.", "dose_feasibility": "Given the null cognitive result for cordyceps+reishi [tsuk2017] and the absence of any positive reishi or chaga cognitive trial, there is currently no evidence-based 'target dose' to formulate toward for a cognitive claim; for chaga, any inclusion would need to stay well below doses implicated in the oxalate-nephropathy case reports (10-15 g/day sustained use caused harm), likely limiting it to flavoring-level rather than therapeutic-level amounts if included at all.", "cost_availability": "All four are widely available commercially (cordyceps, reishi and chaga as extracts/teas; shiitake as a common culinary mushroom); not specifically costed for Morocco/EU/US here.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 1, "effect_magnitude": 0, "safety": 4, "reproducibility": 1, "food_suitability": 4, "cost_access": 5, "rationale": "evidence_quality=1: the only directly relevant human cognitive trial (cordyceps+reishi, healthy young adults) was null [tsuk2017]; reishi's best evidence is oncology-focused and does not address cognition; chaga has no cognition trial and a safety case-report literature instead; ergothioneine has no interventional cognitive trial. effect_magnitude=0: no positive, quantifiable cognitive effect size exists for any of the four ingredients in any population. safety=4: chaga's documented renal safety signal pulls the combined score down substantially despite cordyceps/reishi/ergothioneine appearing safe at studied/dietary intakes; reflects a conditional rather than clean safety gate. reproducibility=1: the one cognition trial was a single null result; there is nothing positive to reproduce. food_suitability=4: shiitake (ergothioneine source) is genuinely food-suitable, but cordyceps/reishi/chaga are non-culinary extract/tea formats with chaga specifically unsuited to liberal food inclusion given its oxalate risk. cost_access=5: all four widely available commercially, not specifically costed here."}, "research_gaps": ["No RCT has isolated cordyceps alone (without reishi) for a cognitive outcome in any human population.", "No dedicated reishi-for-cognition RCT exists in healthy adults or any non-oncology population; this should be explicitly tested before any brain-health claim is considered.", "No chaga human RCT for any outcome (cognitive or otherwise) was identified; chaga's human literature is limited to case reports of harm, and a formal safe-upper-limit study is needed given the oxalate-nephropathy signal.", "No interventional human RCT has tested isolated/concentrated ergothioneine supplementation (as opposed to whole-mushroom dietary intake) for a cognitive outcome.", "No study has assessed whether habitual shiitake/mushroom consumption (as a route to higher ergothioneine status) is associated with better long-term cognitive outcomes in a prospective human cohort; this prospective-cohort gap should be filled before any dietary pattern claim."], "_derived": {"composite_score": 2.1, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "unknown", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": null, "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": null, "best_grade_overall": null, "benefit_domains": {}, "n_outcome_rows": 4, "n_studies": 8, "n_verified": 8, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 1, "n_systematic_reviews": 2, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["insufficient", "no_benefit"]}}, {"id": "panax-ginseng", "name": "Panax ginseng (Asian/Korean ginseng, standardized extract G115)", "category": "H", "category_name": "Botanical and nootropic supplements", "aliases": ["Panax ginseng", "Asian ginseng", "Korean ginseng", "Korean red ginseng", "G115 (standardized extract, Pharmaton)", "ginsenosides"], "food_sources": ["not a conventional food; root of Panax ginseng C.A. Meyer consumed as tea, tonic, or heat-processed (red) ginseng in East Asian cuisine; not part of a typical Western or Moroccan diet"], "active_constituents": ["ginsenosides (triterpene saponins: Rb1, Rg1, Rg3, Re and others; standardized extract G115 defined to a fixed ginsenoside content)", "gut-microbiota-derived ginsenoside metabolites (e.g. compound K)", "polysaccharides (minor, mostly studied for immune effects, not cognition)"], "one_line_verdict": "Acute single doses of standardized Panax ginseng (G115, 200-400 mg) produce small, inconsistently replicated improvements in mental-arithmetic speed and self-rated mental fatigue during sustained cognitively demanding tasks in healthy young adults, mostly from one UK research group, with no added benefit and no sign of tolerance after 8 days of repeated dosing, and no high-quality evidence of benefit in chronic use or in dementia.", "scientific_conclusion": "The most internally consistent body of acute human data comes from three related double-blind, placebo-controlled crossover studies by Kennedy, Reay and Scholey using the standardized extract G115. Single doses of 200 mg improved Serial Sevens subtraction performance and reduced subjective mental fatigue during a sustained 'cognitive demand' battery, with a parallel fall in blood glucose, suggesting a glucoregulatory mechanism [reay2005]; a follow-up using glucose co-administration confirmed the glucose-lowering effect but found no synergy between ginseng and exogenous glucose on cognition, and the cognitive benefit itself was smaller and less consistent than in the first study [reay2006]. A repeated-dosing crossover (200 mg and 400 mg for 8 days) found no evidence that benefits grew or that tolerance developed with sub-chronic use; 400 mg improved mental arithmetic and calmness on both day 1 and day 8, but 200 mg slowed mental-arithmetic responding, an inconsistency the authors could not fully explain [reay2010]. An earlier dose-ranging study of a ginkgo-ginseng combination (not ginseng alone) found a non-monotonic, dose-dependent pattern across six cognitive factors, with the highest dose improving 'quality of memory' (secondary memory) but a mid-range dose worsening speed of attention [kennedy2001], illustrating that acute Panax ginseng effects are not simply dose-proportional and that most \"ginseng\" cognitive trials in this literature actually test a ginkgo-ginseng combination, not ginseng alone, which confounds attribution. The 2010 Cochrane systematic review of nine placebo-controlled RCTs (eight in healthy adults, one in age-associated memory impairment) concluded there is 'a lack of convincing evidence' for a cognitive-enhancing effect of P. ginseng in healthy people and no high-quality evidence in dementia, while noting no serious adverse events [geng2010]. A more recent meta-analysis (15 RCTs, n=671, mixed healthy/schizophrenia/hospitalized/Alzheimer populations, PROSPERO CRD42024514231) found a small but statistically significant pooled effect on memory (SMD 0.19, 95% CI 0.02-0.36; SMD 0.33, 0.04-0.61 at higher doses) but no effect on overall cognition, attention or executive function [zeng2024]; because the pooled population mixes healthy adults with clinical groups, this cannot be read as a healthy-adult effect size. In Alzheimer's disease, three open-label or single-blind Korean trials (not double-blind, no healthy comparator) reported improved MMSE/ADAS-Cog scores with red or heat-processed ginseng that reversed on discontinuation [lee2008; heo2008; heo2012]; these findings are specific to a cognitively impaired, culturally distinct population on background dementia treatment and must not be generalized to healthy young adults or students. No adequately powered, preregistered trial has tested Panax ginseng alone (without ginkgo) over weeks to months in healthy young adults or students under exam-type workload; the existing acute-effect literature is dominated by one UK laboratory group using the same G115 extract and overlapping cohorts, which limits independent replication. Overall: for healthy young adults, acute mental-fatigue and arithmetic-speed effects are plausible but small, inconsistent across doses, and not independently replicated outside the originating group (grade C); chronic enhancement in healthy adults is not established (grade D, insufficient data); the AD cognitive-improvement signal is real within that specific clinical, impaired population but is a different question entirely (grade B for that population only) and gives no license to infer benefit in healthy consumers.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Panax ginseng is not a nutrient and there is no deficiency state to correct; it is a botanical tonic used either acutely (mental-fatigue relief during sustained tasks) or chronically as an 'adaptogen'. The only population with a replicated, clinically meaningful cognitive signal is patients with diagnosed Alzheimer's disease on a background of impaired cholinergic function [lee2008; heo2008; heo2012] -- a cognitively impaired, not deficient, group, and this improvement is not evidence that ginseng enhances cognition in neurologically healthy people. In healthy young adults the acute data describe a modest counteracting of fatigue/glucose dips during demanding tasks, not enhancement above a normal baseline; there is no evidence it raises cognition above an already well-functioning baseline (ceiling effect plausible but untested).", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Marketing language commonly claims Panax ginseng 'boosts brain power', 'improves memory' or 'fights brain fog' for general consumers. The actual human evidence supports, at best, a narrow and modest claim: single doses of 200 mg standardized G115 extract may reduce subjective mental fatigue and modestly improve mental-arithmetic speed during sustained demanding cognitive tasks in healthy young adults, an effect documented mainly by one research group and not replicated by an independent, adequately powered trial. The Cochrane review explicitly found insufficient evidence for a cognitive-enhancing effect in healthy people [geng2010], and the one EU-relevant systematic appraisal of ginseng health claims under Regulation (EC) 1924/2006 resulted in claims being placed 'on hold' pending further substantiation (non-authorised, not refused, status uncertain -- see regulatory section). The Alzheimer's disease findings, while real within that population, are frequently and incorrectly cited by supplement marketers as general 'brain health' evidence for healthy consumers; this is a textbook deficiency/impairment-to-healthy-population generalization error that NEUROFUEL LAB explicitly flags as unsupported. Any Brain Food communication should restrict claims to the acute, task-specific, modest effect in healthy adults and should not cite the AD trials.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Ginsenosides are absorbed poorly as parent compounds; gut bacteria deglycosylate them to smaller, more bioavailable metabolites (e.g. compound K) that cross into circulation and are implicated in central effects. Proposed CNS mechanisms include modest acute glucoregulation (lowering blood glucose, which may explain the fatigue/arithmetic effects during sustained cognitive load), cholinergic and antioxidant activity shown in vitro/animal models, and anti-inflammatory/anti-amyloid actions proposed to underlie the AD cognitive findings. None of the cellular or animal mechanisms has been directly linked to the acute human cognitive effect via a demonstrated dose-response or blockade experiment in humans.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Oral ginsenosides -> limited direct intestinal absorption", "detail": "Parent ginsenosides (Rb1, Rg1, etc.) have low oral bioavailability; gut microbiota convert them via deglycosylation to secondary metabolites (e.g. compound K) that are better absorbed [mancuso2017].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Acute ingestion -> reduction in blood glucose", "detail": "Both 200 mg and 400 mg G115 significantly lowered blood glucose at all post-dose measurements in a fasted crossover trial [reay2005]; confirmed in a second trial, where ginseng alone (without added glucose) also reduced glucose [reay2006].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Lower/stabilized glucose during sustained task -> reduced subjective mental fatigue and modestly faster mental arithmetic", "detail": "Associated temporally with the glucose change in two trials from the same group [reay2005; reay2006]; causality (glucoregulation as the mechanism) is inferred, not proven by blockade.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Ginsenosides/metabolites -> cholinergic and antioxidant modulation (proposed)", "detail": "Shown in animal and cell models; proposed basis for cognitive effects in Alzheimer's disease trials but not directly measured in the human cognition trials reviewed here.", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Chronic use in Alzheimer's disease -> measured cognitive improvement (MMSE/ADAS-Cog)", "detail": "Open-label/non-blinded trials in AD patients showed improvement that reversed after ginseng discontinuation, consistent with an active pharmacological effect in an impaired cholinergic system, but design limitations (no blinding) prevent strong causal inference [lee2008; heo2008; heo2012].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["gut-microbiota ginsenoside biotransformation", "acute glucoregulation", "cholinergic modulation (proposed)", "antioxidant/anti-inflammatory activity (animal)", "amyloid-related pathways in AD (animal/mechanistic)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "cognitive_fatigue", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy young adults (mean ~23-30 y), fasted, UK cohorts, single-centre", "dose_or_intake": "200 mg standardized G115 extract, single dose", "duration": "single dose, measured over ~60-90 min of sustained task performance", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "200 mg reduced subjective mental fatigue across most post-dose time points during a sustained mental-arithmetic/RVIP battery and improved Serial Sevens accuracy; confirmed direction (smaller magnitude) in a follow-up with/without glucose.", "effect_estimate": "p<0.05 at most but not all post-dose time points; exact effect sizes/CIs not reported in abstracts [reay2005, reay2006]", "certainty_notes": "Downgraded for small samples (n=27-30), single research group, inconsistent dose-response (200 mg beneficial, 400 mg not tested in this exact paradigm, 400 mg showed different profile in the sub-chronic study), and lack of independent replication.", "key_study_ids": ["reay2005", "reay2006", "reay2010"]}, {"timeframe": "short_term_days", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy young adults (mean 22.9 y), 8-day repeated dosing crossover", "dose_or_intake": "200 mg or 400 mg G115 daily", "duration": "8 days", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "No evidence of growing benefit or tolerance over 8 days; 400 mg improved mental arithmetic and calmness on day 1 and day 8, but 200 mg slowed mental-arithmetic responding on both days -- an unexplained dose inconsistency.", "effect_estimate": "p<0.05 for dose-related effects; specific means not reported in abstract [reay2010]", "certainty_notes": "Single small trial (n=30), contradictory direction between doses, downgraded for inconsistency and imprecision.", "key_study_ids": ["reay2010"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Pooled trials mixing healthy adults with other groups", "dose_or_intake": "varies across pooled trials", "duration": "weeks (pooled across heterogeneous trials)", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "2010 Cochrane review (9 RCTs, 8 in healthy adults) found a lack of convincing evidence for cognitive enhancement in healthy participants; a 2024 meta-analysis found a small pooled memory SMD of 0.19-0.33 but no effect on global cognition, attention or executive function, and the pooled sample mixed healthy adults with schizophrenia, hospitalized and Alzheimer's patients, making the healthy-adult-specific estimate unextractable.", "effect_estimate": "memory SMD 0.19 (95% CI 0.02-0.36), high-dose subgroup SMD 0.33 (0.04-0.61); overall cognition SMD 0.06 (-0.64 to 0.77, ns); attention SMD 0.06 (-0.12 to 0.23, ns); executive function SMD -0.03 (-0.28 to 0.21, ns) [zeng2024]; Cochrane: 'lack of convincing evidence' [geng2010]", "certainty_notes": "Downgraded for indirectness (mixed clinical/healthy populations in pooled estimate), heterogeneity in dose/duration/outcome measures preventing Cochrane meta-analysis entirely, and small trial sizes.", "key_study_ids": ["geng2010", "zeng2024"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Diagnosed Alzheimer's disease patients, Korean cohorts, on background standard treatment", "dose_or_intake": "4.5 g/day ginseng powder, or standardized red/heat-processed ginseng extract", "duration": "12 weeks", "grade": "B", "direction": "benefit", "effect_summary": "Open-label and single-blind Korean trials found improved MMSE and ADAS-Cog scores during 12 weeks of ginseng treatment that reversed after discontinuation, in AD patients specifically; not blinded in two of three trials.", "effect_estimate": "p=0.029 (ADAS-Cog) and p=0.009 (MMSE) vs baseline [lee2008]; similar direction in heat-processed ginseng trial [heo2012] and adjuvant open-label trial [heo2008]", "certainty_notes": "Downgraded from A to B for lack of double-blinding in two of three trials and small samples; this grade and population must never be extrapolated to healthy adults, students or non-demented older adults.", "key_study_ids": ["lee2008", "heo2008", "heo2012"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "200 mg G115", "onset": "within 60 min", "duration_studied": "up to ~2 h of sustained task performance", "domain": "cognitive_fatigue", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "effect_estimate": "reduced subjective mental fatigue (p<0.05 at most time points); improved Serial Sevens accuracy", "certainty": "C - single-group literature, not independently replicated", "safety": "no serious adverse events reported; mild blood-glucose lowering noted (relevant for hypoglycemia-prone individuals)", "study_ids": ["reay2005", "reay2006"]}, {"dose": "400 mg G115", "onset": "within 60 min, persisting to day 8 of repeated dosing", "duration_studied": "single dose and 8-day repeated dosing", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "effect_estimate": "improved mental arithmetic speed and calmness (p<0.05); no synergy/attenuation after repeated dosing", "certainty": "C - single small trial", "safety": "no serious adverse events reported", "study_ids": ["reay2010"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "weeks (pooled, heterogeneous)", "population": "mixed_adults", "outcome": "memory, global cognition, attention, executive function", "effect_estimate": "memory SMD 0.19-0.33 (small, significant); no effect on global cognition/attention/executive function", "evidence_quality": "low (GRADE down for indirectness and heterogeneity)", "limitations": "Pools healthy adults with schizophrenia, hospitalized and AD patients; cannot isolate a healthy-adult chronic effect", "study_ids": ["zeng2024", "geng2010"]}, {"duration": "12 weeks", "population": "clinical_population", "outcome": "MMSE/ADAS-Cog in Alzheimer's disease", "effect_estimate": "significant improvement vs baseline, reversible on discontinuation", "evidence_quality": "moderate (open-label/non-blinded limits certainty)", "limitations": "No healthy comparator; cannot generalize beyond diagnosed AD", "study_ids": ["lee2008", "heo2008", "heo2012"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Panax ginseng has a generally favorable short-term safety record in the trials reviewed (no serious adverse events reported in the Cochrane review or in the acute UK trials), but it has pharmacologically meaningful interactions (notably with warfarin, lowering INR/reducing anticoagulant effect) and known acute glucose-lowering activity that is relevant for people on glucose-lowering medication. Long-term (multi-month to multi-year) safety in healthy adults is not well studied in controlled trials; most data come from short acute or sub-chronic (up to 12 weeks, mostly in AD patients) exposures.", "adverse_effects": ["insomnia/overstimulation reported anecdotally and in case literature (not quantified in the RCTs reviewed here)", "headache, GI upset reported in some ginseng safety literature", "acute blood-glucose lowering (demonstrated in reay2005/reay2006, a risk for hypoglycemia-prone individuals)", "no serious adverse events reported across the trials and the Cochrane review examined here"], "contraindications": ["uncontrolled diabetes or use of glucose-lowering medication (due to additive hypoglycemia risk)", "anticoagulant therapy (warfarin interaction)", "known hypersensitivity to ginseng"], "drug_interactions": ["warfarin: ginseng has been reported to reduce INR/anticoagulant effect, a clinically important interaction documented in case reports and pharmacology reviews, requiring caution and monitoring in anticoagulated patients", "antidiabetic medications (insulin, sulfonylureas): additive glucose-lowering risk given ginseng's own acute hypoglycemic effect shown in reay2005/reay2006", "potential MAOI interactions reported in older case literature (phenelzine) -- not directly verified in a controlled trial, flagged as a case-report-level concern"], "upper_limits": "No formal tolerable upper intake level (UL) set by EFSA or NIH-ODS for Panax ginseng; typical studied doses in cognition trials were 200-400 mg of standardized G115 extract acutely and up to 4.5 g/day of raw powder in the AD trials for 12 weeks; no formal long-term dose-ranging safety trial identified in this search.", "sleep_impact": "Not directly tested in a controlled sleep study within this search; ginseng is sometimes marketed as 'energizing' and anecdotal/traditional-use reports describe insomnia with evening or high-dose use. Specialist review of sleep pharmacology literature is recommended before any evening-use product claim.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Only the 12-week AD trials provide multi-week exposure data (in a clinical, not healthy, population); no identified RCT follows healthy adults for months to years on continuous ginseng intake.", "daily_use_appropriate": "uncertain", "special_populations": "Pregnancy and breastfeeding: insufficient controlled human safety data identified in this search; traditionally cautioned against in many herbal-safety references. Children/adolescents: no cognition RCTs identified; not recommended without pediatric-specific safety data. Diabetes/anticoagulant users: specific interaction risk as above. Psychiatric conditions: possible MAOI interaction (case-report level) and anecdotal overstimulation/insomnia reports warrant caution in anxiety-prone or insomnia-prone individuals.", "specialist_review_needed": ["clinical pharmacology review of the warfarin/ginseng interaction mechanism and monitoring recommendations", "endocrinology review of the hypoglycemia interaction risk with antidiabetic medication", "obstetric/reproductive toxicology review for pregnancy/breastfeeding safety", "sleep medicine review of stimulant/insomnia potential"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "No signal of serious harm in the trials reviewed, but a real, clinically important warfarin interaction and an acute glucose-lowering effect with implications for antidiabetic-medication users mean the ingredient cannot pass an unconditional safety gate for a general food product without clear labeling and exclusion of anticoagulated/diabetic consumers from unrestricted use claims."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Panax ginseng is a traditional botanical used in food supplements across the EU; no cognitive-function health claim for ginseng has been authorised under Regulation (EC) 1924/2006 -- ginseng-related claims remain in the 'on hold' category pending further scientific substantiation (the long-running EFSA on-hold list for botanicals), so no EU-authorised cognitive claim currently exists. Status should be treated as uncertain/on-hold rather than refused or approved; this must be re-verified against the current EU claims register before any product claim is made.", "us": "Marketed as a dietary supplement under DSHEA; ginseng root and standardized extracts are widely sold with structure/function claims (e.g. 'supports mental alertness') that require a disclaimer and must be truthful and non-misleading and substantiated under FTC standards. No ginseng cognitive-function claim has FDA-reviewed drug-level approval. An FTC-defensible structure/function claim would need to be anchored to the specific acute, modest effect shown in the UK trials, not to a general 'boosts brainpower' claim.", "morocco": "Ginseng is sold in Morocco as an imported herbal/dietary supplement; it is not part of traditional Moroccan phytotherapy (it is an East Asian botanical). ONSSA and Ministry of Health regulation of imported botanical supplements applies generically; no ginseng-specific Moroccan regulatory text was identified in this search and status should be treated as uncertain pending a dedicated regulatory-affairs review of ONSSA's imported-supplement framework.", "permitted_claims_examples": [], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "Panax ginseng root/extract has a long history of food and supplement use in the EU and US and is not expected to trigger EU Novel Food status or require a new US NDI notification for conventional extract forms, but this has not been independently confirmed against the EU Novel Food catalogue in this search and should be verified before formulation.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Ginseng extract is a bitter, pharmacologically active botanical rather than a conventional food ingredient; it can be incorporated into capsules, teas, or flavored functional beverages/bars but its bitterness and dose-dependent glucose-lowering activity make it less suited to an unobtrusive 'everyday food' format than to a clearly labeled functional product.", "formats": ["standardized extract capsules/tablets (e.g. G115-type)", "tea/infusion", "functional beverage or shot", "powder in bars (bitterness is a formulation challenge)"], "stability": "Ginsenosides are relatively heat-stable short-term (heat-processing to produce 'red ginseng' is a traditional practice that changes, rather than destroys, the ginsenoside profile); long-term stability in a packaged food matrix was not directly assessed in this search.", "taste": "Bitter, earthy root taste; typically masked with flavoring in commercial products.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Oral bioavailability of parent ginsenosides is low and depends on gut microbiota conversion to active metabolites (e.g. compound K); inter-individual variability in gut microbiota composition could plausibly affect response, though this was not directly tested as a moderator in the cognition trials reviewed.", "dose_feasibility": "The studied acute cognitive dose (200-400 mg standardized extract) is feasible to deliver in a capsule or concentrated shot but difficult to deliver inconspicuously in an everyday food serving (e.g. a bar or drink) without a noticeable bitter taste or a conspicuous supplement-style format.", "cost_availability": "Standardized G115-type Panax ginseng extract is a widely available commodity botanical ingredient sold by multiple global ingredient suppliers; red/Korean ginseng extracts are also broadly available.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 3.5, "effect_magnitude": 3, "safety": 6, "reproducibility": 2.5, "food_suitability": 4, "cost_access": 6, "rationale": "evidence_quality ~3.5: best healthy-adult evidence is grade C (acute, inconsistent, single-group), with a grade-B AD-specific signal that cannot be generalized; effect_magnitude ~3: effects are small (SMD ~0.2 pooled memory signal; acute effects not reported as standardized effect sizes but described as modest in the primary trials) and inconsistent across doses; safety ~6: no serious AEs reported but a real warfarin interaction and glucose-lowering effect warrant conditional labeling, so not a clean high score; reproducibility ~2.5: acute cognition literature dominated by one UK group with overlapping cohorts, no independent large replication identified; food_suitability ~4: bitter taste and dose size make it awkward in conventional food formats; cost_access ~6: widely available commodity extract, reasonably low cost (exact pricing not researched here)."}, "research_gaps": ["No adequately powered, preregistered RCT of Panax ginseng alone (without ginkgo) over multi-week chronic use in healthy young adults or students under exam-type cognitive load.", "No independent replication outside the originating UK research group for the acute fatigue/arithmetic effect.", "No direct comparison of G115 extract vs. red/heat-processed ginseng vs. raw root powder on the same cognitive outcomes in healthy adults.", "No controlled human sleep study assessing insomnia/stimulant potential.", "No dedicated interaction study quantifying the magnitude of the warfarin-ginseng interaction in a modern controlled pharmacokinetic trial (most evidence is case-report level).", "No confirmed, up-to-date check of current EU claims-register and EU Novel Food catalogue status specific to Panax ginseng extracts."], "_derived": {"composite_score": 4.1, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": "C", "best_grade_chronic": "B", "best_grade_young_adults": "C", "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": null, "best_grade_overall": "B", "benefit_domains": {"cognitive_fatigue": "C", "global_cognition": "B"}, "n_outcome_rows": 4, "n_studies": 10, "n_verified": 10, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 2, "n_systematic_reviews": 2, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["benefit", "inconsistent", "insufficient", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "rhodiola-rosea", "name": "Rhodiola rosea", "category": "H", "category_name": "Botanical and nootropic supplements", "aliases": ["Rhodiola rosea L.", "golden root", "arctic root", "roseroot", "SHR-5 (standardized extract)", "Vitano (brand)", "Arctic Root (brand)", "ADAPT-232 (combination with Schisandra chinensis and Eleutherococcus senticosus)"], "food_sources": ["not a food; Rhodiola rosea is a traditional herbal root/rhizome preparation, not consumed as food in Morocco, the EU or the US", "no meaningful dietary intake outside supplement/herbal-medicine use"], "active_constituents": ["rosavins (rosavin, rosin, rosarin) - marker compounds specific to R. rosea, standardized to 3%", "salidroside (rhodioloside) - also present in other Rhodiola species, standardized to 1%", "tyrosol, p-tyrosol glycosides", "proanthocyanidins, flavonoids (rhodiolin, rhodionin)", "organic acids (gallic, chlorogenic)"], "one_line_verdict": "Small, old, high-risk-of-bias trials suggest SHR-5 extract may blunt subjective and some objective fatigue under acute occupational stress (night duty, exams, cadet training), but the one rigorous modern RCT in healthy students found it worsened fatigue, no trial has isolated memory or executive function in rested healthy adults, and both systematic reviews call the evidence contradictory.", "scientific_conclusion": "Rhodiola rosea's human evidence base is small, old and methodologically weak. Two systematic reviews of the same core literature reached cautious, near-identical conclusions: Hung 2011 [hung2011] (11 RCTs) found the methodological quality of most trials moderate-to-good but noted a lack of independent replication, while Ishaque 2012 [ishaque2012] (11 RCTs/CCTs, mostly overlapping) judged every included study to carry high or unclear risk of bias and called the efficacy evidence 'contradictory'. The most commonly cited positive trials all used the SHR-5 extract (170-660 mg/day) from a single manufacturer-linked research group (Panossian, Wikman and colleagues) in acute occupational-stress settings: Darbinyan 2000 [darbinyan2000] (56 physicians, repeated 2-week night-duty cross-over) found a significant improvement in a composite 'fatigue index' (associative thinking, short-term memory, calculation, audio-visual perception speed) only during the first 2-week period, not reproduced in the second arm after washout, a pattern consistent with order effects or a time-limited response; Spasov 2000 [spasov2000] (40 foreign students, 20-day exam-period pilot, 100 mg/day) reported improved general well-being and mental fatigue (p<0.01) but no significant change on neuro-motor tapping or proofreading tests, and the authors themselves called the dose probably suboptimal; Shevtsov 2003 [shevtsov2003] (161 military cadets, single-dose, parallel-group) found a significantly better 'antifatigue index' at two doses versus placebo (p<0.001) but used a composite, non-standard outcome measure with no independent cognitive domain breakdown reported in the abstract. A single-dose combination product (ADAPT-232: Rhodiola + Schisandra + Eleutherococcus) improved attention speed/accuracy on the d2 test 2 hours after dosing in 40 stressed women [aslanyan2010] - the only trial with a clean acute-timeframe design, but it is a multi-herb combination, not Rhodiola alone, so it cannot be attributed to Rhodiola. Against this, two null/negative findings stand out and were likely underweighted by industry-linked reviews: Olsson 2009 [olsson2009] (60 adults diagnosed with burnout/fatigue syndrome, 28 days, 576 mg/day) found the extract improved burnout scale scores and some CCPT-II attention indices versus placebo, but is a clinical (fatigue-syndrome) population, not healthy adults, and both groups improved markedly (large placebo response); and critically, Punja 2014 [punja2014], the only trial run by an independent academic group (not Panossian/Wikman-affiliated) with a pre-registered protocol (NCT01278992) in a population close to Brain Food's target (nursing students on shift work, n=48, 42 days, 364 mg/day), found Rhodiola significantly WORSENED fatigue relative to placebo on both primary (Vitality subscale, -17.3 points, 95% CI -30.6 to -3.9, p=0.011) and secondary (VAS-F, p=0.015) outcomes. This is the largest and most rigorous null/negative signal in the literature and directly contradicts the brand-friendly narrative. Cropley 2015 [cropley2015] (80 mildly anxious adults, 14 days, 400 mg/day, non-placebo controlled) found improved self-reported mood/anxiety/stress but explicitly 'no relevant differences in cognitive performance' - a biomarker/mood-only result, not a cognition result, and without a placebo arm it cannot rule out expectancy effects. No trial has tested standardized working memory, executive function or episodic memory in rested healthy young adults or knowledge workers using validated cognitive batteries with adequate power; evidence_by_outcome below is graded accordingly as insufficient for cognition in healthy, non-stressed adults, and C (inconsistent) for fatigue/subjective-stress under acute occupational stress. A 2022 narrative review [ivanovastojcheva2022] describes the evidence as 'encouraging', but its authors are employed by a Rhodiola-extract manufacturer (Natac Biotech), a conflict of interest not disclosed as such in the paper despite a boilerplate 'no conflict of interest' statement; this review should not be treated as independent. Overall: plausible, modest, short-term anti-fatigue effect under acute occupational/exam stress in some but not all trials (grade C), no credible evidence of cognitive enhancement in rested healthy adults (grade D/insufficient), and at least one rigorous trial showing harm (worsened fatigue) in a population resembling students.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "There is no dietary deficiency state for Rhodiola rosea constituents; it is not a nutrient and has no RDA or established biomarker of inadequacy. All studied use cases are 'enhancement' or symptom-relief claims under acute stress, fatigue or burnout, not correction of a nutritional deficit. The EMA/HMPC traditional-use monograph [ema2012] frames it explicitly as 'temporary relief of symptoms of stress, such as fatigue and sensation of weakness' based on traditional use plus plausibility, not demonstrated efficacy, and that framing should not be confused with evidence of enhancing cognition in already well-rested, non-stressed healthy adults, where no positive trial exists.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Marketing claims such as 'boosts brain power', 'beats brain fog', 'adaptogen for focus' or 'nootropic for exam stress' are not supported by the evidence reviewed here. The strongest defensible claim-adjacent statement is the EU traditional-use wording: 'traditionally used for the temporary relief of symptoms of stress, such as fatigue and sensation of weakness', which exists only as a traditional-use registration (not an EFSA-authorised health claim) and cannot mention cognition, memory, concentration or mental performance. EFSA explicitly rejected a mental-fatigue/intellectual-function claim in 2012 because the two human studies submitted used an extract standardized only for salidroside, not matching the rosavin-standardized products marketed, meaning 'conclusions could not be drawn for the claim' [efsa2012]. The brand-relevant honesty point: the single most methodologically independent and rigorous trial in a population resembling Brain Food's target customer (students/shift workers) found Rhodiola made fatigue worse, not better [punja2014]. Any claim implying reliable cognitive or energy benefit for healthy young adults or students would not survive EFSA, FTC or an informed physician's scrutiny.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Rhodiola rosea is proposed to act as an 'adaptogen': rosavins and salidroside are hypothesized to modulate the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis, monoamine (serotonin, dopamine) signalling, and heat-shock protein / stress-response pathways, blunting cortisol release and perceived fatigue under acute stress. Human mechanistic evidence is limited to one small trial showing attenuated salivary cortisol response to awakening after chronic dosing in a burnout population [olsson2009]; the rest of the mechanistic literature (HSP70 induction, monoamine oxidase inhibition, antioxidant effects) is animal or in-vitro and does not establish human cognitive efficacy.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Oral Rhodiola rosea extract (rosavin/salidroside-standardized) -> gastrointestinal absorption", "detail": "Human pharmacokinetic data on rosavin/salidroside bioavailability are sparse; absorption and first-pass metabolism are inferred mainly from animal studies.", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Salidroside/tyrosol -> proposed modulation of HPA axis and cortisol response to acute stress", "detail": "One 28-day RCT in burnout/fatigue-syndrome patients found a significant reduction in salivary cortisol response to awakening stress versus placebo [olsson2009]; not replicated in healthy, non-clinical adults.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Proposed monoamine (serotonin/dopamine) and HSP70 stress-response modulation -> reduced perceived fatigue", "detail": "Supported mainly by rodent forced-swim/chronic-stress models and cell assays; no human neuroimaging or monoamine turnover data located in this search.", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Proposed central nervous system effect -> measured subjective fatigue / anti-fatigue indices in acute occupational stress", "detail": "Several small RCTs (physicians on night duty, cadets, exam-period students) show improved composite fatigue/antifatigue indices versus placebo [darbinyan2000, shevtsov2003, spasov2000], but outcomes are non-standardized composites, not validated cognitive domain tests, and one larger independent trial found the opposite direction [punja2014].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Proposed CNS effect -> measured standardized cognitive domains (working memory, executive function, episodic memory) in rested healthy adults", "detail": "No adequately powered trial using validated cognitive batteries in rested, non-stressed healthy adults was located; this step is speculative for the Brain Food target population.", "evidence_level": "speculative"}], "pathways": ["HPA-axis / cortisol modulation (limited human evidence, clinical fatigue population only)", "monoamine (serotonin/dopamine) modulation (animal only)", "heat-shock protein / cellular stress-response induction (animal/in-vitro only)", "antioxidant effects (animal/in-vitro only)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "40 stressed adult women, acute cognitive stress task (d2 Test of Attention)", "dose_or_intake": "single 270 mg tablet of ADAPT-232 (Rhodiola rosea + Schisandra chinensis + Eleutherococcus senticosus combination, not Rhodiola alone)", "duration": "single dose, tested 2 hours post-dose", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "Significant improvement in attention speed/accuracy (d2 test, TN-E score) versus placebo, 2 hours after a single dose, in self-reported chronically stressed women.", "effect_estimate": "p<0.05 on d2 TN-E score; exact effect size not reported in abstract", "certainty_notes": "Single small trial (n=40), multi-herb combination product (cannot isolate Rhodiola's contribution), no independent replication; downgraded for indirectness (combination product) and imprecision.", "key_study_ids": ["aslanyan2010"]}, {"timeframe": "short_term_days", "domain": "cognitive_fatigue", "population": "high_cognitive_workload", "population_detail": "healthy physicians on repeated night duty (occupational acute-stress model)", "dose_or_intake": "170 mg/day SHR-5 extract", "duration": "2 weeks (within a 6-week cross-over design)", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Composite 'fatigue index' (associative thinking, short-term memory, calculation, audio-visual perception speed) improved significantly during the first 2-week treatment period only; effect not clearly reproduced in the second arm post-washout.", "effect_estimate": "statistically significant improvement in fatigue index, first period only (exact numeric effect size not reported in available abstract/summary)", "certainty_notes": "High/unclear risk of bias per both systematic reviews [hung2011, ishaque2012]; composite non-standard outcome; cross-over period-effect confound; single research group with product-manufacturer ties (Swedish Herbal Institute/Wikman).", "key_study_ids": ["darbinyan2000"]}, {"timeframe": "short_term_days", "domain": "cognitive_fatigue", "population": "students", "population_detail": "40 foreign (Indian) male students, exam-period stress", "dose_or_intake": "100 mg/day SHR-5 extract", "duration": "20 days", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Significant improvement in general well-being (p<0.05) and mental fatigue (p<0.01) ratings; no significant change on a proofreading (correction text) test or neuro-muscular tapping test; authors stated the dose was probably suboptimal.", "effect_estimate": "subjective well-being and fatigue ratings improved (p<0.05, p<0.01); no significant change on objective proofreading/tapping tests", "certainty_notes": "Small pilot (n=40), subjective outcomes dominant, objective cognitive/motor tests null; high/unclear risk of bias.", "key_study_ids": ["spasov2000"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "cognitive_fatigue", "population": "high_cognitive_workload", "population_detail": "161 military cadets, single-dose, parallel-group plus untreated control arm", "dose_or_intake": "185-277.5 mg single dose (2 or 3 capsules)", "duration": "single dose", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Significantly better composite 'antifatigue index' (AFI) in both verum dose groups versus placebo (p<0.001); no significant dose-response difference between the two active doses; outcome is a non-standard composite index, not a validated cognitive domain score.", "effect_estimate": "AFI means ~1.04 and 1.02 (verum, 2 and 3 capsules) vs 0.90 (placebo), p<0.001", "certainty_notes": "Large by field standards (n=161) but single dose, non-standard composite outcome, same research-group/manufacturer lineage as other SHR-5 trials; unclear risk of bias.", "key_study_ids": ["shevtsov2003"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "60 adults (20-55y) diagnosed with stress-related fatigue/burnout syndrome per Swedish diagnostic criteria -- NOT healthy adults", "dose_or_intake": "576 mg/day SHR-5 extract", "duration": "28 days", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Significant improvement versus placebo on Pines' burnout scale and several CCPT-II attention indices (omissions, commissions, Hit RT variability); both groups improved on multiple measures (large placebo response); cortisol response to awakening stress decreased more in the treatment group.", "effect_estimate": "significant between-group differences on burnout scale and select CCPT-II indices (exact numeric effect sizes not reported in abstract); not reported for most secondary scales", "certainty_notes": "This is a clinical fatigue-syndrome/burnout population and must not be generalized to healthy young adults or students (brief rule 5); substantial placebo response in both arms reduces confidence in attributable effect.", "key_study_ids": ["olsson2009"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_fatigue", "population": "students", "population_detail": "48 nursing students (18-55y) on clinical shift-work rotations -- the closest population match to Brain Food's target among all Rhodiola trials, and the only independently (non-manufacturer) run, pre-registered trial", "dose_or_intake": "364 mg/day (up to 2 capsules)", "duration": "42 days", "grade": "C", "direction": "harm", "effect_summary": "Rhodiola rosea significantly WORSENED fatigue relative to placebo on both the pre-registered primary outcome (RAND-36 Vitality subscale) and the secondary visual analogue fatigue scale; total adverse events did not differ between groups.", "effect_estimate": "Vitality subscale change: -17.3 points favoring placebo (95% CI -30.6 to -3.9, p=0.011); VAS-F: 1.9 points favoring placebo (95% CI 0.4 to 3.5, p=0.015)", "certainty_notes": "Pre-registered (NCT01278992), independent academic group with no extract-manufacturer ties, moderate size for this literature; authors urge caution interpreting a single trial, but this is the most methodologically credible single RCT in the dataset and directly contradicts the positive SHR-5 literature.", "key_study_ids": ["punja2014"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "80 mildly anxious adults, non-placebo-controlled (no-treatment control arm)", "dose_or_intake": "400 mg/day (2x200mg Vitano)", "duration": "14 days", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Significant reduction in self-reported anxiety, stress, anger, confusion and depression, and improved total mood versus no-treatment control; explicitly 'no relevant differences in cognitive performance between the groups' were observed.", "effect_estimate": "significant improvements on mood/anxiety self-report scales (exact effect sizes not reported in abstract); cognitive test battery: no significant between-group difference", "certainty_notes": "No placebo arm (only no-treatment control) means expectancy/demand effects cannot be excluded; mood/subjective outcome only -- must not be conflated with a cognitive effect (brief rule 4).", "key_study_ids": ["cropley2015"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "healthy, rested, non-stressed young adults / knowledge workers / students outside acute occupational or exam stress", "dose_or_intake": "not studied at a defined dose in this population", "duration": "not applicable", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No RCT using validated, standardized cognitive domain tests (working memory, executive function, episodic memory, processing speed) in rested healthy young adults without an acute stressor was located; all positive trials occurred under an acute occupational, exam or clinical-fatigue stressor.", "effect_estimate": "not reported / no qualifying trial found", "certainty_notes": "Absence of evidence, not evidence of absence, but given both systematic reviews span all available RCTs and found none testing this population/outcome combination, the grade reflects genuine evidentiary gap rather than a borderline call.", "key_study_ids": ["hung2011", "ishaque2012"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "270 mg single dose (ADAPT-232 combination, not Rhodiola alone)", "onset": "effect measured at 2 hours", "duration_studied": "single dose", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "mixed_adults", "effect_estimate": "significant improvement on d2 Test of Attention TN-E score vs placebo (p<0.05); exact effect size not reported", "certainty": "low (single small trial, multi-herb product, no replication)", "safety": "minor adverse events (sleepiness, cold extremities) in both treatment and placebo groups; none serious", "study_ids": ["aslanyan2010"]}, {"dose": "185-277.5 mg single dose SHR-5 extract", "onset": "same-day (post-night-duty/post-exercise testing)", "duration_studied": "single dose", "domain": "cognitive_fatigue", "population": "high_cognitive_workload", "effect_estimate": "composite antifatigue index improved vs placebo, p<0.001; not a validated cognitive-domain score", "certainty": "low-moderate (larger n=161 but non-standard outcome, unclear risk of bias)", "safety": "no significant adverse events reported in abstract", "study_ids": ["shevtsov2003"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "14 days", "population": "mixed_adults", "outcome": "self-reported anxiety/stress/mood (not cognition)", "effect_estimate": "significant improvement in mood/anxiety self-report vs no-treatment control; no cognitive test difference", "evidence_quality": "low (no placebo arm)", "limitations": "no placebo control; subjective outcomes only; cannot exclude expectancy effects", "study_ids": ["cropley2015"]}, {"duration": "20-28 days", "population": "clinical_population", "outcome": "burnout/fatigue index and select attention indices in diagnosed stress-fatigue/burnout patients", "effect_estimate": "significant improvement vs placebo on burnout scale and some CCPT-II indices; large placebo response in both arms", "evidence_quality": "low-moderate; clinical population, not generalizable to healthy adults", "limitations": "diagnosed clinical fatigue syndrome population; cannot be extrapolated to healthy young adults or students", "study_ids": ["olsson2009"]}, {"duration": "42 days", "population": "students", "outcome": "fatigue (Vitality subscale, VAS-F) in nursing students on shift work", "effect_estimate": "Rhodiola significantly worsened fatigue vs placebo (Vitality: -17.3, 95% CI -30.6 to -3.9, p=0.011; VAS-F: 1.9, 95% CI 0.4 to 3.5, p=0.015)", "evidence_quality": "moderate (pre-registered, independent, closest population match to target market) -- the single most credible trial and it shows harm, not benefit", "limitations": "single trial; authors urge cautious interpretation; moderate sample size (n=48)", "study_ids": ["punja2014"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Short-term use (days to a few weeks) of standardized Rhodiola rosea extracts has a generally mild adverse-event profile across available RCTs, with no serious adverse events attributed to the extract and no significant difference in total adverse events versus placebo in the best-conducted trial [punja2014]. The main documented safety concern is a modest but statistically significant inhibition of CYP2C9 metabolic activity [thu2016], which could raise plasma levels of narrow-therapeutic-index CYP2C9 substrates such as warfarin and phenytoin -- consistent with the caution the EMA's 2023 draft monograph revision added. Long-term (multi-month to multi-year) safety has not been studied in any located RCT; all trials reviewed here ran 42 days or less.", "adverse_effects": ["mild, non-serious events reported in some trials: sleepiness, cold extremities (seen in both active and placebo arms in one trial) [aslanyan2010]", "no significant difference in total adverse event counts between Rhodiola and placebo in the best-conducted trial [punja2014]", "case-report-level concern: Rhodiola named as a possible co-culprit (alongside other botanicals) in at least one herbal-supplement-associated liver injury case series cross-referenced from the X2 synthesis; not independently verified as causally attributable to Rhodiola alone in this search", "theoretical concern, raised in secondary/clinical literature but not confirmed by a controlled human trial in this search: possible activation/destabilization risk in bipolar disorder given its stimulant-like adaptogenic profile"], "contraindications": ["pregnancy and lactation (EMA/HMPC 2012 monograph explicitly states safety not established and use is not recommended)", "bipolar disorder (theoretical caution per clinical/secondary sources; not tested in a controlled trial located here)", "patients on CYP2C9-metabolized narrow-therapeutic-index drugs (warfarin, phenytoin) without medical supervision"], "drug_interactions": ["CYP2C9 substrates (warfarin, phenytoin, and other CYP2C9-metabolized drugs): a controlled human PK study found a significant 21% reduction in CYP2C9 metabolic activity after 14 days of a commercial Rhodiola product [thu2016]", "no significant effect found on CYP1A2, CYP2C19, CYP2D6 or CYP3A4 in the same study [thu2016]", "theoretical interaction with MAOIs and other psychotropic/stimulant medications given its proposed monoamine-modulating mechanism; not tested in a controlled human trial located here"], "upper_limits": "No tolerable upper intake level has been established by EFSA, FDA or ONSSA. Studied doses in RCTs ranged from 100 mg/day to 660 mg/day (chronic) and up to 576 mg/day in a clinical fatigue-syndrome population; no dose-escalation or long-term toxicology trial in humans was located in this search.", "sleep_impact": "Not directly studied as a primary outcome in any located RCT; given its proposed stimulant-adaptogenic mechanism, evening dosing could plausibly disrupt sleep, but no controlled human sleep-architecture data were found.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Not studied. The longest trial located was 42 days [punja2014]; no RCT beyond 6 weeks exists in this literature, so chronic (multi-month or multi-year) safety in any population, including healthy young adults, is unknown.", "daily_use_appropriate": "uncertain", "special_populations": "Pregnant and lactating women: EMA explicitly advises against use due to lack of safety data. Patients on warfarin, phenytoin or other CYP2C9 substrates: caution warranted given a demonstrated human PK interaction [thu2016]. Patients with bipolar disorder: theoretical caution exists in secondary clinical literature but was not tested in any RCT reviewed here. Children and adolescents: no pediatric RCT was located; no data to support or refute use. People taking MAOIs or other psychoactive/stimulant medications: theoretical interaction risk, not empirically tested.", "specialist_review_needed": ["clinical pharmacology (CYP2C9 drug interaction risk assessment for warfarin/phenytoin co-users)", "psychiatry (bipolar disorder caution; theoretical mania/hypomania activation risk not empirically tested in this search)", "obstetrics/reproductive toxicology (pregnancy/lactation safety data gap)", "botanical authentication/quality control (given documented 1-in-5 mislabeling/adulteration rate with R. crenulata) [booker2016adulteration]"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "Short-term use in the trials reviewed shows no serious adverse events and no excess adverse-event rate versus placebo, so an outright fail is not warranted. However, the documented CYP2C9 interaction [thu2016], the complete absence of long-term (beyond 6 weeks) human safety data, the explicit EMA pregnancy/lactation caution, and well-documented market-wide species adulteration [booker2016adulteration] mean the gate is conditional: safe use would require sourcing from an authenticated, CYP-interaction-labeled, short-duration-use product, with exclusion of pregnant/lactating users and medical screening for interacting medications."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "A traditional-use Community herbal monograph was adopted by the EMA/HMPC on 27 March 2012, permitting the indication 'traditional herbal medicinal product used for the temporary relief of symptoms of stress, such as fatigue and sensation of weakness,' based on at least 30 years of traditional use (15 within the EU) and plausibility, explicitly not on demonstrated clinical efficacy; the HMPC noted several shortcomings in the small placebo-controlled trials it reviewed. A 2023 draft revision (EMA/HMPC/24177/2023) proposed updated wording ('fatigue and exhaustion'), a posology of 144-400 mg/day, and an added CYP2C9 interaction warning; as of this search, Ireland formally objected to the revised indication at the March 2024 HMPC meeting and the final adopted status of the revision was not confirmed in available sources. Separately, under the EU nutrition and health claims regulation, EFSA (2012, EFSA Journal opinion, application by Nutrilinks Sarl under Article 13(5)) rejected a proposed 'stimulates intellectual functions under conditions of stress' claim, finding that the two human studies submitted used an extract standardized only for salidroside, not matching the rosavin-standardized products marketed, so no cause-and-effect conclusion could be drawn; this claim remains unauthorised. Rhodiola rosea root/rhizome is not listed as requiring novel food authorisation in the sources checked, consistent with its long history of use as a traditional herbal medicinal product rather than a food, but this was not exhaustively verified against the full EU Novel Food catalogue in this search.", "us": "Under DSHEA, Rhodiola rosea extract can be marketed as a dietary supplement with structure/function claims (e.g. 'supports the body's response to occasional stress') provided the claim is substantiated and carries the standard FDA disclaimer; FDA has not evaluated it for cognitive-enhancement claims and no NDI (New Dietary Ingredient) concern applies given its long marketing history pre-1994 in the US supplement market. Any claim implying cognitive enhancement, memory improvement or reliable anti-fatigue benefit in healthy, non-stressed adults would need FTC-level competent-and-reliable-evidence substantiation, which the current literature (two systematic reviews calling results 'contradictory' and 'lacking replication', plus an independent null/negative trial in a near-target population) would not support.", "morocco": "Not verified in this search. No Moroccan-specific (ONSSA or Ministry of Health) regulatory text on Rhodiola rosea was located; status (whether registered as a food supplement, traditional remedy or unregulated import) is uncertain and should be confirmed directly with ONSSA before any Moroccan market claim or sale.", "permitted_claims_examples": ["EU traditional-use wording only (not an authorised health claim, and only on a registered traditional herbal medicinal product, not a conventional food): 'Traditional herbal medicinal product used for the temporary relief of symptoms of stress, such as fatigue and sensation of weakness, exclusively based upon long-standing use as a traditional remedy.'"], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "No novel food issue identified for the root/rhizome itself in sources checked (long history of traditional use pre-dates the EU Novel Food framework), but this was not exhaustively confirmed against the current EU Novel Food catalogue. In the US, no NDI notification issue identified given pre-1994 marketing history, but this was not independently confirmed with FDA's NDI database in this search.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Poor fit for a conventional food matrix. Rhodiola rosea is used exclusively as a standardized root/rhizome extract in capsule or tablet form across all RCTs reviewed; no trial tested it in a beverage, bar or conventional food format, and its bitter, astringent taste profile (driven by tannins/phenolics) is not amenable to palatable food incorporation at studied doses.", "formats": ["standardized dry extract capsule/tablet (3% rosavin / 1% salidroside, the EMA/HMPC reference preparation, DER 1.5-5:1, 67-70% ethanol extraction)", "no food, beverage or bar format has been clinically tested"], "stability": "Not assessed in this search; standardized extracts are generally stable as dry powders in capsule form, but no stability data in a food matrix (heat, moisture, pH) were located.", "taste": "Bitter and astringent; unsuitable for direct food incorporation without significant masking, which has not been tested in any trial reviewed here.", "bioavailability_matrix": "No human bioavailability data comparing extract-in-capsule versus extract-in-food matrix were located in this search; all PK/interaction data (e.g. the CYP2C9 study) used capsule-form commercial products.", "dose_feasibility": "The studied dose range (100-660 mg/day of standardized extract) could technically fit within a capsule or a small functional-food serving by mass, but given (a) the inconsistent and partly negative efficacy evidence, (b) no trial using a food vehicle, and (c) documented market-wide adulteration requiring verified sourcing, a food-format product is not currently justifiable on the evidence; a supplement-format product would also require the CYP2C9 interaction disclosure and pregnancy/lactation contraindication labeling.", "cost_availability": "Rhodiola rosea extract is a widely available commodity botanical ingredient sold by multiple global suppliers (e.g. Nektium/Rhodiolife, Natac Biotech, and generic Chinese/Eastern European suppliers), with wide price variation tied to verified rosavin/salidroside content and CITES compliance documentation (Rhodiola spp. were added to CITES Appendix II in November 2022, effective requiring permits for international trade in wild-collected and some cultivated material, exempting finished retail-packaged products).", "cost_evidence": "not researched here; no specific supplier price quotes were obtained in this search"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 3, "effect_magnitude": 2, "safety": 6, "reproducibility": 2, "food_suitability": 2, "cost_access": 6, "rationale": "evidence_quality=3: grade C evidence at best for fatigue under acute occupational stress, D/insufficient for cognition in healthy rested adults; both systematic reviews explicitly call the evidence contradictory or lacking independent replication, and the single most independent, pre-registered trial in a near-target population found a null/negative (harmful) result [punja2014]. effect_magnitude=2: where positive, effects are on non-standardized composite fatigue indices or subjective mood scales, not validated cognitive domains, and standardized effect sizes with CIs are mostly unreported in available abstracts; the one trial with full numeric reporting [punja2014] shows a reversed (negative) effect. safety=6: short-term use appears generally well tolerated with no excess adverse events vs placebo in the best trial, but a documented CYP2C9 interaction, no long-term human safety data beyond 6 weeks, an EMA pregnancy/lactation caution, and market-wide adulteration risk prevent a higher score. reproducibility=2: positive findings cluster almost entirely within one research-group/manufacturer lineage (Panossian, Wikman and SHR-5/Swedish Herbal Institute-linked trials); the one trial from an independent group with a pre-registered protocol found the opposite result, which is the single strongest reproducibility red flag in this monograph. food_suitability=2: only ever tested as a capsule/tablet extract, bitter/astringent taste, no food-matrix trial exists. cost_access=6: commodity-grade botanical extract widely available from multiple global suppliers, though CITES Appendix II (2022) permit requirements for wild/semi-wild material and the need for verified (non-adulterated) sourcing add cost and supply-chain complexity."}, "research_gaps": ["No adequately powered RCT using validated, standardized cognitive domain batteries (working memory, executive function, episodic memory) has tested Rhodiola rosea in rested, healthy, non-stressed young adults or knowledge workers.", "No trial has replicated the Punja 2014 null/negative finding in students/shift workers, despite it being the most methodologically rigorous and population-relevant trial available; an independent replication is the single highest-priority gap.", "No long-term (beyond 6 weeks) human safety or efficacy trial exists for any population.", "No trial has isolated Rhodiola rosea's contribution within the ADAPT-232 combination product tested by Aslanyan 2010.", "No controlled human trial has tested the theoretical bipolar-activation caution or confirmed/refuted it empirically.", "No dose-ranging or dose-response trial with a validated cognitive battery exists; studied doses (100-660 mg/day) were chosen historically, not pharmacokinetically optimized.", "Morocco-specific regulatory status (ONSSA registration category) was not confirmed in this search and needs direct verification before any Moroccan market activity.", "No trial has directly tested whether verified-authentic, rosavin-standardized extract performs differently from the adulterated products documented in the commercial supply (most trials predate the 2016 adulteration survey and may themselves have used variably authentic material)."], "_derived": {"composite_score": 3.5, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": "C", "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": "C", "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": "C", "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"cognitive_fatigue": "C", "sustained_attention": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 8, "n_studies": 12, "n_verified": 12, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 2, "n_systematic_reviews": 2, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["harm", "inconsistent", "insufficient", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "sage-rosemary-lemon-balm", "name": "Sage, rosemary, lemon balm, peppermint and other culinary herbs/aromas", "category": "H", "category_name": "Botanical and nootropic supplements", "aliases": ["Salvia officinalis (common sage)", "Salvia lavandulaefolia (Spanish sage)", "Rosmarinus officinalis / Salvia rosmarinus (rosemary)", "Melissa officinalis (lemon balm)", "Mentha piperita (peppermint)", "1,8-cineole", "rosmarinic acid", "thujone"], "food_sources": ["sage, rosemary, lemon balm, peppermint and spearmint as fresh or dried culinary herbs, teas, and herbal infusions", "rosemary and sage are everyday seasonings in Moroccan and Mediterranean cuisine (tagines, roasted meats, breads, rosemary/sage-infused oils)", "mint tea (atay/peppermint tea) is a daily dietary staple in Morocco", "essential oils of these herbs (used for aroma exposure, not ingestion, in most cited trials)"], "active_constituents": ["sage: 1,8-cineole, camphor, borneol, alpha/beta-thujone, rosmarinic acid, carnosic acid (AChE-inhibiting and antioxidant terpenoids/phenolics)", "rosemary: 1,8-cineole, camphor, alpha-pinene, rosmarinic acid, carnosic acid", "lemon balm: rosmarinic acid, citral, citronellal (GABA-transaminase inhibition, nicotinic/muscarinic receptor binding in vitro)", "peppermint: menthol, menthone (AChE inhibition and GABA-A/nicotinic receptor binding in vitro, per pre-trial screening)"], "one_line_verdict": "Acute aroma or oral doses of sage, rosemary, lemon balm and peppermint produce small, statistically detectable, mostly single-session changes in attention/memory speed or accuracy in healthy adults in a cluster of mostly UK-based trials, which is a real but modest and short-lived (acute) signal, not evidence of lasting cognitive enhancement, and the culinary relevance to Moroccan cuisine is a genuine strength even though no trial has tested amounts achievable by eating a normal dish.", "scientific_conclusion": "This is a cluster of related Lamiaceae culinary herbs studied almost exclusively for acute (single-dose or single-exposure) effects on cognition and mood in healthy adults, using the Cognitive Drug Research (CDR) computerised battery developed at Northumbria University; the same two research groups (Kennedy/Scholey/Tildesley and Moss) generated most of the primary data, which limits independent replication. Sage: a standardised Salvia lavandulaefolia essential oil, given orally as drops (not as culinary sage), improved immediate word recall at a 50 microlitre dose in two small crossover trials (n=20 and n=24) using a dose-response design [tildesley2003]; a related trial with dried sage leaf tablets similarly improved delayed word recall and mood in healthy adults [scholey2008, cited via roe2021 review]. These are acute, single-dose pharmacological-extract trials, not culinary-amount food exposures, and sage contains thujone, a compound with dose-dependent neurotoxic/convulsant potential, which caps how much extract can be safely used. Rosemary: aroma exposure (inhaled essential oil, no ingestion) enhanced overall memory quality and secondary memory in one 144-person three-arm trial versus a no-odour control, but also slowed speed of memory, and the comparison condition (lavender) showed impairment, illustrating that these aroma effects are inconsistent across cognitive subdomains within the same study [moss2003]. A separate trial of ingested 'rosemary water' (250 mL, an extract/hydrolat, not whole herb) in 80 healthy adults found small but statistically significant beneficial effects on several cognitive tasks alongside a novel finding of increased deoxygenated haemoglobin (by near-infrared spectroscopy) during testing, interpreted as greater oxygen extraction, a biomarker rather than a cognitive outcome in itself [moss2018rosemarywater]. Lemon balm: multiple Kennedy-group dose-ranging acute trials (not independently verified with full text in this compact-tier pass) report dose-dependent mood and calmness effects and mixed cognitive-speed effects, summarised in a 2024 narrative review as showing 'clinical efficacy and tolerability... in psychological well-being' with cognition as a secondary, less consistent outcome [mathews2024lemonbalmreview]; an ethnopharmacology review situates lemon balm's main traditional and pharmacological evidence base around anxiolytic/sedative and GABA-transaminase-inhibiting activity rather than cognitive enhancement per se [shakeri2016]. Peppermint: aroma exposure increased alertness and improved memory in one 144-person trial relative to a no-odour and a ylang-ylang control [moss2008peppermint], and an orally administered peppermint essential oil capsule (50 or 100 microlitres), selected via in-vitro receptor-binding pre-screening, improved performance on a demanding sustained-attention task (RVIP) and reduced mental fatigue in a 24-person crossover trial [kennedy2018peppermint]. In whom, and how large. All of the above trials enrolled healthy young/mixed adults (the target population for Brain Food), which is a genuine strength of this evidence base compared with most botanicals in this project, which lean on dementia populations. However every positive finding is acute (single dose or single exposure session), effect sizes are small (task-specific, often one or two subtests out of a multi-domain battery reaching significance), and no trial used doses equivalent to a normal culinary serving (a pinch of dried herb in a tagine, a cup of mint tea) rather than concentrated extracts, essential oils, or essential-oil aromas. No chronic (multi-week) trial of culinary-dose sage, rosemary, lemon balm or peppermint on cognition in healthy adults was identified in this search. What is unknown. Dose-response at food-relevant intakes; durability beyond a few hours; whether habitual Moroccan-style daily rosemary/sage/mint consumption produces any cumulative cognitive effect; and whether aroma-only effects (which plausibly reflect arousal/expectation as much as pharmacology) generalize beyond a laboratory testing context. The honest overall grade for healthy-adult cognition is C (acute, single-domain, small-sample, same-lab-cluster evidence) for sage, rosemary and peppermint, and C-to-D for lemon balm on cognition specifically (versus a somewhat stronger case for anxiolytic/mood effects, which is a different outcome domain).", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "None of these herbs corrects a nutrient deficiency; the proposed mechanism in every trial here is acute pharmacological/aromatic modulation of cholinergic, GABAergic or olfactory-arousal pathways in already well-nourished healthy adults, i.e. enhancement, not correction. This makes the within-population generalisation question simpler than for most ingredients in this project (no cross-population leakage from AD or deficient groups was found), but it also means any claimed benefit is additive stimulation/modulation on top of normal function, not restoration of something missing, and durability/safety margins have not been established for anything beyond acute lab exposure.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "The genuinely defensible marketing position is narrow but real: 'the aroma or a concentrated extract of rosemary, sage, lemon balm or peppermint has been shown, in small acute trials, to modestly and selectively improve some aspects of memory, attention or alertness in healthy adults, and to improve mood/calmness (lemon balm).' It is not defensible to claim that eating rosemary-seasoned food, drinking mint tea, or a daily sage supplement produces lasting cognitive enhancement, improves IQ, or 'boosts brainpower' in a general sense, because no trial tested culinary-equivalent doses or chronic use, and because within-battery effects are selective (some subtests improve, others are unaffected or impaired, as with rosemary's mixed effect on memory quality versus speed). The strongest, most honest brand angle for Morocco specifically is cultural/culinary: these are herbs Moroccan cuisine already uses daily (rosemary, sage, mint tea), so 'eat your tagine with rosemary and sage, drink your mint tea' is defensible everyday dietary advice with a plausible-but-modest acute cognitive upside, which is a more credible, less exaggerated story than most isolated-extract nootropic claims and should be framed as 'ordinary food you already enjoy, with an interesting but modest bonus' rather than as a supplement claim.", "mechanism": {"summary": "These Lamiaceae herbs contain volatile monoterpenes (1,8-cineole, menthol, menthone, camphor) and phenolic acids (rosmarinic acid, carnosic acid) with in vitro acetylcholinesterase-inhibiting, GABA-A/nicotinic receptor-binding, and antioxidant activity. Proposed human pathways are (a) direct pharmacological absorption after oral/extract dosing reaching the brain in amounts sufficient to modulate cholinergic/GABAergic tone, and (b) olfactory/trigeminal stimulation producing arousal and mood changes independent of systemic absorption, which may itself alter attention and memory performance via non-specific arousal rather than a drug-like mechanism. Both pathways are demonstrated in vitro; the degree to which culinary-amount exposure (versus concentrated extract or essential-oil aroma) reaches pharmacologically active levels in the human brain is not established.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "In vitro: monoterpenes and phenolics from sage/rosemary/peppermint inhibit acetylcholinesterase and bind GABA-A/nicotinic receptors", "detail": "Pre-trial screening (receptor binding, AChE inhibition assays) used to select active essential oil fractions in [kennedy2018peppermint]; broader in vitro basis reviewed in [shakeri2016].", "evidence_level": "plausible"}, {"step": "Oral ingestion of concentrated extract/essential oil -> systemic absorption -> putative CNS cholinergic/GABAergic modulation", "detail": "Demonstrated as plausible by acute cognitive changes after oral dosing of Salvia lavandulaefolia oil [tildesley2003] and rosemary water [moss2018rosemarywater]; absolute brain exposure not measured in humans.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Inhalation of essential oil aroma -> olfactory/trigeminal nerve stimulation -> arousal and mood change independent of systemic absorption", "detail": "Rosemary and peppermint aroma-only exposure (no ingestion) changed CDR battery performance and subjective alertness in 144-person trials [moss2003, moss2008peppermint].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Acute arousal/cholinergic modulation -> selective, task-specific changes in memory speed/accuracy or sustained attention", "detail": "Effects are inconsistent across subtests within the same study (e.g., rosemary improved memory quality but slowed speed of memory [moss2003]), suggesting a modest, non-uniform cognitive effect rather than global enhancement.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Culinary-amount exposure (normal food serving or tea) -> cognitive effect", "detail": "Not directly tested in any identified trial; inferred only by extrapolation from concentrated-extract/aroma studies.", "evidence_level": "speculative"}], "pathways": ["acetylcholinesterase inhibition (terpenoids)", "GABA-A/nicotinic receptor modulation", "olfactory/trigeminal arousal pathway", "antioxidant/anti-inflammatory activity (rosmarinic acid, carnosic acid, animal/in vitro)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "UK adults, two crossover trials (n=20, n=24)", "dose_or_intake": "50 microlitres standardised Salvia lavandulaefolia essential oil (oral drops)", "duration": "single dose, tested over 6 h", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "50 microlitre dose significantly improved immediate word recall in both trials; other doses (25, 100, 150 microlitres) less consistent, suggesting a non-linear dose-response.", "effect_estimate": "Significant improvement in immediate word recall at 50 microlitres vs placebo; exact statistics not extracted in this compact-tier pass (abstract-level data only)", "certainty_notes": "Small samples, same research group, acute-only design, concentrated essential-oil extract rather than culinary-dose sage; downgraded for imprecision and indirectness to culinary use.", "key_study_ids": ["tildesley2003"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "144 UK adults, 3-arm design (rosemary aroma, lavender aroma, no-odour control)", "dose_or_intake": "ambient rosemary essential oil aroma (no ingestion)", "duration": "single exposure during testing session", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Rosemary aroma improved overall memory quality and secondary memory factors but slowed speed of memory versus control; lavender aroma impaired working memory and reaction time. Effects are subtest-specific, not a uniform enhancement.", "effect_estimate": "Significant improvement on memory-quality/secondary-memory factors, significant slowing on speed-of-memory factor (exact F/p values not extracted in this pass)", "certainty_notes": "Single trial, aroma-only exposure, deception design to control expectancy; downgraded for inconsistency across subdomains and single-study status.", "key_study_ids": ["moss2003"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "80 UK adults", "dose_or_intake": "250 mL rosemary water (extract/hydrolat) ingested", "duration": "single dose, tested acutely post-ingestion", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Several statistically significant small beneficial effects on cognitive tasks versus plain water; novel NIRS finding of higher deoxygenated haemoglobin during testing in the rosemary condition, interpreted as a cerebrovascular/metabolic biomarker, not itself a cognitive outcome.", "effect_estimate": "Small, statistically significant effects described as 'small' by the authors; no single standardised effect size reported in abstract", "certainty_notes": "Single trial, biomarker (NIRS) result should not be conflated with the separately reported cognitive-task results; downgraded for being a single trial and for extract (not culinary) dosing.", "key_study_ids": ["moss2018rosemarywater"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "24 UK adults, mean age ~25", "dose_or_intake": "100 microlitre peppermint essential oil capsule (oral)", "duration": "single dose, tested at 1, 3, 6 h", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Highest dose improved Rapid Visual Information Processing (RVIP, a sustained-attention task) performance at 1 h and 3 h, and both doses reduced fatigue and improved a serial subtraction task at 3 h.", "effect_estimate": "Statistically significant improvement on RVIP at 1 h and 3 h post-dose at 100 microlitre dose (exact accuracy/RT deltas not extracted in this compact-tier pass)", "certainty_notes": "Small single trial, in-vitro-guided essential-oil dose far more concentrated than culinary peppermint/mint tea use; downgraded for imprecision and indirectness.", "key_study_ids": ["kennedy2018peppermint"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "alertness", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "144 UK adults, 3-arm (peppermint aroma, ylang-ylang aroma, no-odour control)", "dose_or_intake": "ambient peppermint essential oil aroma (no ingestion)", "duration": "single exposure", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Peppermint aroma enhanced a memory factor and increased subjective alertness versus control; ylang-ylang aroma impaired memory and lengthened processing speed, showing aroma effects are substance-specific, not a generic 'any pleasant smell helps' effect.", "effect_estimate": "Significant memory enhancement and increased alertness ratings for peppermint vs control (exact statistics not extracted in this pass)", "certainty_notes": "Single trial, aroma-only; downgraded for single-study status and for being a surrogate exposure route versus culinary ingestion.", "key_study_ids": ["moss2008peppermint"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "mixed healthy adult samples across multiple Kennedy-group dose-ranging trials, synthesised in a 2024 review", "dose_or_intake": "various standardised lemon balm extract doses", "duration": "acute, single-dose designs in underlying trials", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "2024 narrative review concludes lemon balm has reasonably consistent acute calming/anxiolytic effects in healthy adults with mixed, less consistent effects specifically on cognitive-speed measures; it is a secondary-outcome literature for cognition.", "effect_estimate": "Not a single pooled effect size; narrative synthesis of multiple small acute dose-ranging trials", "certainty_notes": "Downgraded for indirectness (review-level synthesis in this compact pass rather than full-text extraction of each underlying trial) and because cognition is a secondary, less consistent outcome relative to mood/calmness.", "key_study_ids": ["mathews2024lemonbalmreview", "shakeri2016"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "other", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "no chronic culinary-dose trial identified for any herb in this cluster", "dose_or_intake": "normal culinary serving (e.g., herb in cooked food, mint tea) sustained over weeks", "duration": "not studied", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No trial tested chronic, food-relevant (non-extract, non-aroma) intake of sage, rosemary, lemon balm or peppermint on any cognitive domain in healthy adults.", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "Complete evidence gap for the realistic daily-food-use scenario most relevant to a food brand; all existing positive findings are acute and use concentrated extracts or aromas, not culinary amounts.", "key_study_ids": []}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "50 microlitres Salvia lavandulaefolia oil (oral)", "onset": "within 1 h", "duration_studied": "up to 6 h", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "effect_estimate": "significant improvement in immediate word recall vs placebo", "certainty": "low-moderate (small n, same lab, dose-dependent, non-monotonic)", "safety": "no adverse events reported in trial; thujone content of sage caps safe extract concentration", "study_ids": ["tildesley2003"]}, {"dose": "ambient rosemary essential oil aroma", "onset": "during exposure", "duration_studied": "single session", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "effect_estimate": "improved memory quality, slowed speed of memory", "certainty": "low (single trial, subtest-specific, inconsistent direction across factors)", "safety": "aroma exposure only; no ingestion-related safety concern", "study_ids": ["moss2003"]}, {"dose": "250 mL rosemary water (extract/hydrolat)", "onset": "acute post-ingestion", "duration_studied": "single session", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "effect_estimate": "small significant cognitive benefits; NIRS biomarker change (deoxyhaemoglobin) noted separately", "certainty": "low (single trial)", "safety": "no adverse events reported; commercially available product", "study_ids": ["moss2018rosemarywater"]}, {"dose": "50-100 microlitres peppermint essential oil (oral capsule)", "onset": "1-3 h", "duration_studied": "up to 6 h", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "effect_estimate": "improved RVIP accuracy/speed at 1-3 h, reduced fatigue", "certainty": "low-moderate (small n, in-vitro-guided dose selection, single trial)", "safety": "no adverse events reported at these doses", "study_ids": ["kennedy2018peppermint"]}, {"dose": "ambient peppermint essential oil aroma", "onset": "during exposure", "duration_studied": "single session", "domain": "alertness", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "effect_estimate": "increased alertness, improved memory factor vs control", "certainty": "low (single trial)", "safety": "aroma exposure only", "study_ids": ["moss2008peppermint"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "none studied", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "outcome": "any cognitive domain under chronic culinary-amount intake", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "evidence_quality": "no trials identified", "limitations": "Entire evidence base for this herb cluster is acute/single-exposure; no multi-week trial exists for culinary-relevant doses in cognition.", "study_ids": []}], "safety": {"summary": "Culinary use of sage, rosemary, lemon balm and peppermint in normal food amounts (as used in Moroccan and Mediterranean cooking, or as herbal tea) is widely regarded as safe and has a long history of food use (GRAS-type status in the US, traditional food-use history in the EU/Morocco). Concentrated essential oils and extracts used in the cited trials carry different, dose-dependent risks: sage and some rosemary chemotypes contain thujone, a compound with neurotoxic/convulsant potential at high doses; essential oils are not meant to be ingested undiluted and can cause mucosal irritation, allergic reaction, or (rarely) seizures in susceptible individuals at concentrated doses; peppermint oil can worsen gastro-oesophageal reflux and is contraindicated in infants/young children due to laryngospasm risk with undiluted oil.", "adverse_effects": ["sage/rosemary essential oil or concentrated extract: potential for thujone-related CNS stimulation/seizure risk at high doses; mucosal irritation", "peppermint essential oil: can worsen GERD/heartburn; mucosal/skin irritation if applied undiluted", "lemon balm: generally well tolerated; mild sedation possible, theoretical additive effect with sedatives", "culinary-amount use of all four herbs: no significant adverse effects expected based on long food-use history"], "contraindications": ["sage (concentrated/thujone-containing extracts): pregnancy, epilepsy/seizure disorders, breastfeeding (thujone may reduce milk supply and cross into milk)", "peppermint oil: infants and young children (undiluted oil risk of laryngospasm); GERD/hiatal hernia (may worsen reflux)", "lemon balm: caution with sedative/hypnotic medication (additive sedation); hypothyroidism (theoretical TSH-suppressing activity reported in some sources, not confirmed in the trials reviewed here)"], "drug_interactions": ["sage/rosemary concentrated extracts: theoretical interaction with anticonvulsants (opposing effect) and with cholinergic/anticholinergic medications", "lemon balm: additive sedation with benzodiazepines, other sedatives, alcohol", "peppermint oil: may alter absorption of drugs sensitive to gastric pH/motility changes"], "upper_limits": "No formal upper limit set for culinary use of any of the four herbs. Essential oils: various national pharmacopeia/aromatherapy guidance caps thujone intake (e.g., WHO/Council of Europe limits on thujone in food/beverages, typically low parts-per-million in flavoured foods); exact regulatory thujone limits should be confirmed against EU Regulation 1334/2008 (flavourings) before any product claim.", "sleep_impact": "Lemon balm's calming/sedative properties could plausibly aid sleep onset (consistent with traditional use) but were not a primary cognitive outcome in the trials reviewed here; peppermint's alerting aroma effect could theoretically disrupt sleep if used in the evening. Neither was directly tested for sleep architecture in the cited trials.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Long-term (weeks-months) exposure at culinary or concentrated-extract doses was not studied for cognition in any trial identified here; long food-use history supports chronic culinary-amount safety, but not chronic extract/essential-oil safety for cognitive enhancement purposes.", "daily_use_appropriate": "yes", "special_populations": "Culinary-amount use is appropriate for the general population including pregnancy (standard dietary use), but concentrated sage/rosemary extracts or essential oils should be avoided in pregnancy, epilepsy, and young children; lemon balm extract should be used cautiously with sedative medications; peppermint oil should be avoided in infants and those with significant reflux disease.", "specialist_review_needed": ["food toxicology review of thujone limits if any sage/rosemary extract (beyond culinary amounts) is considered", "herbal medicine/pharmacognosy review of essential oil versus culinary-herb distinction for any product formulation"], "hard_safety_gate": "pass", "gate_reason": "At culinary/food-use amounts (the realistic product scenario for Brain Food), all four herbs have a long history of safe food use and no material safety signal in the cited human trials; the gate would become 'conditional' only if the product used concentrated essential oils or high-thujone extracts rather than ordinary culinary herb amounts."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Sage, rosemary, lemon balm and peppermint are established food/flavouring herbs with long traditional use; thujone content in flavoured foods/beverages is regulated under EU flavouring legislation (Regulation (EC) No 1334/2008 sets maximum thujone levels in certain food categories) rather than banned outright. No EFSA-authorised cognitive-function health claim exists for any of these herbs in the EU Register of nutrition and health claims as far as this search could establish; any cognition claim would require new EFSA-reviewed substantiation.", "us": "All four are GRAS (Generally Recognized As Safe) food/flavouring substances with long culinary use; no FDA-approved or FTC-vetted cognitive-enhancement claim exists for any of them as a food ingredient. Essential oil products are regulated as cosmetics/aromatherapy products or dietary supplements depending on labelling and intended use, with different (lower) substantiation expectations than drug claims but still subject to FTC 'competent and reliable scientific evidence' standards for any cognition claim.", "morocco": "All four herbs are long-standing traditional foods/seasonings in Moroccan cuisine (rosemary and sage in tagines/roasted dishes, mint in atay/mint tea) and would not require novel-food-type authorisation for culinary use; ONSSA-specific guidance on any cognitive health claim was not identified in this search and should be confirmed directly before any such claim is made in the Moroccan market.", "permitted_claims_examples": [], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "None for culinary-amount use (long history of food consumption predates any novel food cutoff in all three markets). Concentrated extracts or essential-oil-based cognitive-enhancement products would need to independently establish both safety (thujone, other constituents) and any claim substantiation; this has not been done for a cognition claim in any of the three markets as far as this search could establish.", "regulatory_gate": "pass"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Excellent for culinary-amount use: these are already everyday Moroccan/Mediterranean food ingredients (herb seasoning, tea) with established taste, availability, and consumer acceptance; poor-to-moderate for an extract/essential-oil 'nootropic' product, where dosing, taste masking, and thujone/safety limits become constraining factors.", "formats": ["dried/fresh culinary herb (sage, rosemary) in cooked dishes", "herbal tea/infusion (mint tea, lemon balm tea)", "standardised extract capsule or essential-oil product (as used in the cited trials, higher regulatory/safety burden)"], "stability": "Dried herbs and teas are shelf-stable with standard food storage; volatile monoterpenes (cineole, menthol) degrade with heat/light/time, so fresh or recently dried product and proper storage matter for both flavour and any residual bioactivity.", "taste": "Well established and liked in the target markets (rosemary/sage in savoury dishes, mint tea as a daily beverage); no taste-masking challenge for culinary-format products, unlike for isolated extracts.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Bioavailability of the key monoterpenes/phenolics (1,8-cineole, rosmarinic acid, menthol) from a culinary food matrix (e.g., rosemary cooked in oil, tea infusion) versus the concentrated extracts/essential oils used in trials has not been directly compared in the studies reviewed here; this is a genuine evidence gap for translating acute-extract findings into a realistic food product claim.", "dose_feasibility": "Uncertain/likely not feasible at trial-equivalent doses: the positive trials used concentrated essential oils (50-100 microlitres) or extract products (rosemary water, standardised capsules), not amounts achievable by eating a normal seasoned dish or drinking a cup of tea; whether a culinary serving delivers a pharmacologically comparable dose of 1,8-cineole/rosmarinic acid/menthol has not been tested.", "cost_availability": "not researched here", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 3.5, "effect_magnitude": 3, "safety": 8, "reproducibility": 3, "food_suitability": 8, "cost_access": 8, "rationale": "evidence_quality moderate-low (3.5/10): all positive findings are acute, single-session, small-sample trials in healthy adults (a genuine strength versus most botanicals reviewed in this project, which rely on clinical populations) but clustered in essentially two UK research groups with limited independent replication; grade C across outcomes. effect_magnitude small (3/10): effects are subtest-specific and modest, often appearing on one or two of several tested cognitive factors, with some inconsistency in direction (e.g., rosemary improving memory quality but slowing speed of memory). safety high (8/10) for culinary-amount use given long food-use history; would be markedly lower for concentrated essential-oil/thujone-containing extract products. reproducibility low (3/10): most trials come from the same two labs (Moss; Kennedy/Scholey/Tildesley) using the same CDR battery, and no independent multi-site replication was identified. food_suitability high (8/10): these are already everyday culinary ingredients in the target Moroccan/Mediterranean market with established taste and availability, a distinctive strength of this ingredient cluster. cost_access high (8/10): widely available, inexpensive, no supply chain novelty."}, "research_gaps": ["No trial has tested culinary-dose (as-eaten or as-drunk) sage, rosemary, lemon balm or peppermint against placebo on cognition in healthy adults; all positive trials used concentrated extracts, essential oils, or aroma exposure.", "No chronic (multi-week or longer) trial of any of these herbs on cognition in healthy adults was identified.", "Independent replication outside the Moss and Kennedy/Scholey/Tildesley research groups is lacking.", "Bioavailability of key constituents from a real food matrix (cooked herb, tea infusion) versus concentrated extract has not been compared.", "Dose-response relationship for sage was non-monotonic in the one dose-ranging trial identified, and has not been explored further.", "No trial has tested these herbs specifically in students or knowledge workers under cognitive/exam load, despite that being a natural target population."], "_derived": {"composite_score": 5.15, "eligibility": "eligible", "safety_gate": "pass", "regulatory_gate": "pass", "best_grade_acute": "C", "best_grade_chronic": null, "best_grade_young_adults": "C", "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": "C", "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"episodic_memory": "C", "sustained_attention": "C", "alertness": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 7, "n_studies": 7, "n_verified": 7, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 1, "n_systematic_reviews": 1, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["inconsistent", "insufficient", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "fermented-foods", "name": "Fermented foods", "category": "I", "category_name": "Gut-brain axis", "aliases": ["fermented vegetables (kimchi, sauerkraut)", "fermented soy (natto, miso, tempeh, fermented soybean powder)", "kefir", "kombucha", "fermented dairy (cross-ref yogurt-and-fermented-dairy)", "traditional Moroccan fermented foods (lben, raib, jben, fermented olives)", "psychobiotic diet (whole dietary pattern high in fermented and prebiotic foods)"], "food_sources": ["fermented vegetables: kimchi, sauerkraut, pickled vegetables", "fermented soy: natto, miso, tempeh, doenjang, cheonggukjang", "fermented milk/dairy: kefir, lben, raib (not duplicated here; see yogurt-and-fermented-dairy.json for yogurt/dairy-specific trials)", "fermented beverages: kombucha, traditional lactic-acid-fermented drinks (e.g., Mexican aguamiel-based beverages)", "fermented fish/olive products: Moroccan table olives, fish sauces (safety-relevant, not separately trialled for cognition)"], "active_constituents": ["live and dead lactic acid bacteria and yeasts (Lactobacillus, Lactococcus, Bifidobacterium species and others)", "bacterial and fungal fermentation metabolites (short-chain fatty acids, bioactive peptides, aglycone isoflavones in fermented soy, gamma-aminobutyric acid in some ferments)", "biogenic amines (histamine, tyramine) as fermentation by-products relevant to safety, not efficacy"], "one_line_verdict": "Fermented foods as a dietary category plausibly support gut microbial diversity and may modestly improve a general psychobiotic dietary pattern's effect on perceived stress, but the direct evidence that any specific fermented food improves cognition in healthy young adults is weak, the largest pooled cognition meta-analysis is null, and the clearest positive cognitive signals come from fermented soy and probiotic-fermented-dairy products tested in older or cognitively vulnerable adults, not students.", "scientific_conclusion": "Fermented foods are foods made through desired microbial growth and enzymatic conversion of food components, as formally defined by the ISAPP 2021 consensus statement, which also distinguishes them from probiotics: most fermented foods do not contain a defined, viable, sufficiently dosed strain with a demonstrated health benefit, so 'contains live cultures' does not automatically equal 'psychobiotic' [marco2021]. The human evidence for a direct fermented-food-to-brain effect is heterogeneous and generally weaker than for defined probiotic strains. A cross-sectional study of 710 young adults found that higher self-reported fermented-food consumption was associated with fewer social anxiety symptoms specifically in people with high trait neuroticism, an interaction that cannot establish causality and has not been tested prospectively [hilimire2015]. A rigorous 17-week RCT comparing a high-fiber diet against a high-fermented-food diet in 36 healthy adults (Stanford, n=18/arm) found the fermented-food diet steadily increased microbiota diversity and decreased several inflammatory markers, a notable and well-controlled finding, but the trial measured immune and microbiome outcomes, not cognition or mood, so it supports a physiological rationale, not a direct brain-outcome claim [wastyk2021]. The Berding 2023 'psychobiotic diet' trial (45 healthy adults, 4 weeks, cross-referenced from the prebiotics-and-synbiotics monograph because the diet combined fermented and prebiotic foods) found a numerically larger drop in perceived stress in the diet group (32%) than control (17%), but the between-group difference itself was not statistically significant, and biological stress markers did not change; only within-group dietary adherence correlated with stress reduction [berding2023]. On cognition specifically, the most authoritative pooled estimate (Marx et al. 2020, 6 fermented-food RCTs, n=472) found no significant effect on global cognition (g=0.164, 95% CI -0.017 to 0.345, p=0.076), a result at the border of significance but not crossing it, and no domain-specific effect reached significance either [marx2020]. A 2025 systematic review focused specifically on Lactobacillus/Bifidobacterium-fermented foods and cognition (21 studies, 8 interventional/13 observational) concluded that, while most studies reported some benefit (particularly episodic memory, executive function, global cognition), the evidence following EFSA's own standard is 'neither convincing nor sufficient' to establish causality, citing inadequate controls, small samples, short durations and poor food characterisation as the main limitations [harsa2025]. The most positive single trials come from fermented soy, tested almost exclusively in older or cognitively impaired adults: a 12-week RCT of a non-probiotic fermented soy powder in 61 cognitively healthy older adults (61-mean age, 47 completers) found a significant memory improvement overall (p=0.041) and a larger global-cognition and memory benefit in a post-hoc subgroup of women aged 70+ [west2025]; and a 12-week RCT of Lactobacillus plantarum C29-fermented soybean (DW2009) in 100 adults with mild cognitive impairment found significantly greater improvement in combined cognitive function, particularly attention, versus placebo, associated with increased serum BDNF [hwang2019] - a clinically impaired, not healthy, population, so this cannot be generalised to students. A large Japanese prospective cohort (41,447 participants, 1995-2016) found total soy product intake was not associated with incident disabling dementia, but natto intake specifically showed a marginal inverse association in women, clearer in those under 60 [murai2022] - an observational, hypothesis-generating finding subject to confounding by overall dietary pattern and health behaviours. For mental-health outcomes in healthy adults specifically, a comparative study of medical students found that higher fermented-food consumption was associated with more, not fewer, depressive and anxiety symptoms in psychiatrically healthy students under stress, while the opposite (fewer depressive symptoms with higher fermented-food intake) held only in the small subgroup of students with a diagnosed psychiatric illness [karbownik2022] - directly cautioning against assuming fermented foods benefit mental health in a healthy population, and suggesting any benefit (if real) may be concentrated in people who already have a mental-health vulnerability. A small RCT of a traditional Mexican lactic-acid-fermented beverage reduced academic stress scores in 27 medical students versus 18 controls (p=0.001 within fermented group, no significant change in controls) over 8 weeks [marquez-morales2021] - promising for a 'functional traditional beverage' positioning, but a small, single-site, non-blinded (beverage) trial. A more recent, better-designed RCT of kefir in 65 healthy young adults found non-significant improvements in sleep quality and mental-health scores alongside significant reductions in GI symptoms and several biochemical markers, explicitly noting its small sample likely limited its power to detect the expected mental-health benefit [bakirhan2026] - a fair, if underpowered, test. Overall: fermented foods, as a category, have a plausible mechanistic rationale (microbiota diversity, SCFA production, specific bioactive peptides in fermented soy) and some encouraging observational and small-trial signals, but no adequately powered trial has shown a fermented food improves cognition or mood in healthy young adults or students; the strongest cognitive signals are in older adults (fermented soy) or cognitively impaired populations (fermented soybean DW2009), and the largest cognition meta-analysis across fermented-food trials is non-significant.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "There is no fermented-food deficiency state in a nutritional sense; fermented foods are a traditional, culturally valued food category (lben, raib, jben and fermented olives in Morocco; yogurt and kefir across the EU/US) rather than a nutrient with a defined requirement. The research question is entirely about enhancement - whether adding or increasing fermented-food consumption on top of an already adequate diet improves brain-relevant outcomes - not correction of a shortfall. This is a different question from the broader nutritional case for fermented dairy, legumes and vegetables as nutrient-dense, culturally appropriate foods (covered in yogurt-and-fermented-dairy.json and legumes.json), which stands on its own merits independent of any gut-brain claim.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Marketing claims that 'fermented foods boost your gut-brain connection', 'improve mood' or 'sharpen focus' typically cite the general gut-brain-axis literature and isolated positive trials (fermented soy in older women, DW2009 in MCI patients) without disclosing that: (1) the largest cognition meta-analysis across fermented-food RCTs is not statistically significant (Marx 2020, p=0.076); (2) a dedicated recent systematic review explicitly concluded the evidence is 'neither convincing nor sufficient' by EFSA's own substantiation standard (Harsa 2025); (3) in healthy, non-clinical young adults, higher fermented-food intake was associated with more, not fewer, depressive/anxiety symptoms in at least one well-conducted comparative study (Karbownik 2022); and (4) the clearest positive cognitive findings are in older adults or people with mild cognitive impairment, not the healthy young/student population Brain Food targets. No EU health claim exists for any cognitive, mood or brain outcome for fermented foods generically or for kimchi, kefir, kombucha, natto, miso or tempeh specifically; the only EU-authorised claim touching this category concerns live yoghurt cultures and lactose digestion (shared with the dairy monograph) and iodine/calcium/B12 nutrient claims that describe the nutrient, not the fermentation process. The honest position for Brain Food is that fermented foods, including Moroccan traditional ferments (lben, raib, jben, olives), are nutritious, culturally resonant, generally safe foods worth including in a whole-diet strategy, and that a whole psychobiotic dietary pattern combining fermented and prebiotic foods has a promising but unconfirmed signal for perceived stress - but a specific, reliable cognitive-enhancement claim for any single fermented food in healthy young adults is not currently supported by the evidence.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Fermented foods are defined by desired microbial growth and enzymatic conversion of food components (ISAPP 2021); the resulting live/dead microorganisms, their metabolites (SCFAs, bioactive peptides, GABA, modified isoflavones) and altered nutrient matrices are the proposed active principles. Unlike a defined probiotic strain at a known colony-forming-unit dose, most fermented foods deliver an undefined, variable mixture of microbes and metabolites that differs by product, batch and fermentation method, which is a key reason the human evidence is harder to pool and interpret than for single-strain probiotic trials (cross-reference probiotics-and-psychobiotics.json). Proposed brain-relevant pathways include: increased microbiota diversity and SCFA production feeding into the same gut-brain signalling pathways reviewed in prebiotics-and-synbiotics.json; direct delivery of bioactive peptides and GABA generated during fermentation (particularly relevant to fermented soy and some fermented dairy); and reduced intake of competing less-healthy foods when fermented foods replace them in the diet (a dietary-displacement mechanism distinct from any direct microbial effect).", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Fermented food consumption -> delivery of live/dead microbes, SCFAs, peptides and modified nutrients (e.g., aglycone isoflavones in fermented soy, GABA in some ferments) to the gut", "detail": "Well characterised compositionally for specific products (kimchi, natto, kefir, miso); but composition varies substantially by product and fermentation method, unlike a standardised probiotic strain.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Microbial/metabolite exposure -> increased gut microbiota diversity (confirmed in a rigorous 17-week controlled feeding trial)", "detail": "High-fermented-food diet steadily increased microbiota diversity and decreased inflammatory markers in 18 healthy adults over 17 weeks [wastyk2021].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Increased diversity/reduced inflammation -> plausible downstream signalling via SCFA and immune-mediated gut-brain pathways", "detail": "Mechanistically shared with the SCFA-cortisol pathway documented in prebiotics-and-synbiotics.json (Dalile 2020); not independently re-demonstrated for a whole fermented food in a brain-relevant human endpoint in this search.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Fermentation-derived bioactive peptides (fermented soy, specific fermented dairy) -> proposed neuroprotective/BDNF-related signalling", "detail": "Associated with increased serum BDNF alongside cognitive improvement in a 12-week MCI trial of fermented soybean DW2009 [hwang2019]; association, not proven causal mediation.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Measured human cognitive/mood outcome", "detail": "Positive in some trials (fermented soy in older women, DW2009 in MCI, a Mexican fermented beverage for academic stress), null in others (kefir in healthy young adults, pooled meta-analysis at the border of significance); directionally mixed and population-dependent.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Rodent/cell studies showing fermented soybean, kimchi and kefir components reduce oxidative stress, amyloid-beta toxicity and neuroinflammation", "detail": "Numerous preclinical studies (not individually tabulated as key studies here) support plausibility for fermented soy and kimchi bioactives in neurodegeneration models; these are mechanistic/animal evidence only and do not establish human cognitive efficacy.", "evidence_level": "animal"}], "pathways": ["gut microbiota diversity and SCFA production", "fermentation-derived bioactive peptides and modified isoflavones (fermented soy)", "GABA and other neuroactive fermentation metabolites", "immune/inflammatory modulation", "dietary displacement of less-healthy foods"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Pooled across fermented-food RCTs, predominantly adult samples of varied health status", "dose_or_intake": "varied fermented-food interventions", "duration": "varied, pooled across 6 trials", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Meta-analysis of 6 fermented-food RCTs (n=472) found a borderline, non-significant effect on global cognition; a separate 2025 systematic review of 21 Lactobacillus/Bifidobacterium fermented-food studies found most reported some cognitive benefit but concluded the evidence is 'neither convincing nor sufficient' by EFSA standards.", "effect_estimate": "g=0.164, 95% CI -0.017 to 0.345, p=0.076 [marx2020]", "certainty_notes": "Downgraded for imprecision (CI crosses null), inconsistency across individual studies, and risk of bias/poor food characterisation noted by the harsa2025 review.", "key_study_ids": ["marx2020", "harsa2025"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy, psychiatrically well medical students under academic stress (cross-sectional/comparative design)", "dose_or_intake": "self-reported habitual fermented-food consumption", "duration": "not applicable (cross-sectional/observational)", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "In psychiatrically healthy medical students under stress, higher fermented-food consumption was associated with MORE depressive and anxiety symptoms, the opposite of the hypothesised benefit; a significant interaction with psychiatric diagnosis showed the expected inverse (benefit) association only in the small subgroup with a diagnosed psychiatric illness.", "effect_estimate": "Healthy students: direction opposite to hypothesis (not quantified as a single effect size in abstract); psychiatrically ill subgroup (n=25): adjusted beta=-0.52, 95% CI -0.85 to -0.19, p=0.0042 for depressive symptoms", "certainty_notes": "Single cross-sectional/comparative study; cannot establish causality; directly cautions against assuming a uniform fermented-food mental-health benefit in healthy populations.", "key_study_ids": ["karbownik2022"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Cross-sectional association, not an intervention; young adults (N=710) stratified by trait neuroticism", "dose_or_intake": "self-reported fermented-food consumption frequency", "duration": "not applicable (cross-sectional)", "grade": "D", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Higher fermented-food consumption was associated with fewer social anxiety symptoms specifically in people with high trait neuroticism; no main effect in the full sample independent of the interaction.", "effect_estimate": "Significant interaction term (exact statistics not fully quantified in abstract); cross-sectional, not causal", "certainty_notes": "Cross-sectional design cannot establish causation or direction; single study; graded D because it is correlational/hypothesis-generating rather than a controlled trial.", "key_study_ids": ["hilimire2015"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Cognitively healthy older adults >=65y, with a larger subgroup benefit in women >=70y", "dose_or_intake": "non-probiotic fermented soy powder added to usual diet", "duration": "12 weeks", "grade": "B", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Fermented soy group showed a significant overall memory improvement (p=0.041); post-hoc analysis in women 70y+ found significant improvement in both global cognition (p=0.028) and memory (p=0.049), with the placebo group showing minimal or no change.", "effect_estimate": "Memory: fermented soy +1.81% (95% CI -2.10, 5.72) vs placebo +1.16% (95% CI -3.64, 5.97), overall p=0.041; women 70+ subgroup memory mean change +8.47% (95% CI 5.05, 11.89) vs placebo +2.05% (95% CI -1.75, 5.84)", "certainty_notes": "Single RCT, modest sample (47 completers of 61 randomised), subgroup finding in women 70+ is post-hoc and exploratory; industry-funded (BESO Biological Research) though authors report no role in design/analysis/interpretation.", "key_study_ids": ["west2025"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_decline", "population": "older_adults_mci", "population_detail": "Adults with diagnosed mild cognitive impairment (clinical population, not healthy)", "dose_or_intake": "Lactobacillus plantarum C29-fermented soybean (DW2009), 800 mg/day", "duration": "12 weeks", "grade": "B", "direction": "benefit", "effect_summary": "DW2009 produced significantly greater improvement in combined cognitive function (memory and attention) versus placebo in a 100-person multi-center RCT, with the attention domain most affected; cognitive improvement correlated with increased serum BDNF.", "effect_estimate": "z=2.36, p (interaction)=0.02 for combined cognitive function; z=2.34, p=0.02 for attention domain", "certainty_notes": "This is a clinical (MCI) population and must not be generalised to healthy young adults or students; single trial, moderate sample size, industry-sponsor-affiliated authors (Dong-wha Pharmaceutical).", "key_study_ids": ["hwang2019"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "dementia_risk", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Large Japanese prospective cohort, adults 45-74y at baseline, followed for incident disabling dementia", "dose_or_intake": "habitual dietary intake of soy products and isoflavones via validated FFQ", "duration": "11-21 years of follow-up (1995/1998 baseline to 2006-2016 outcome ascertainment)", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Total soy product intake was not associated with disabling dementia risk in men or women; natto intake specifically showed a marginal inverse association in women (clearer under age 60), but this was not significant in men or in women 60+.", "effect_estimate": "Natto, highest vs lowest quintile, women <60y: HR=0.78, 95% CI 0.59-1.04, trend p=0.020; women >=60y: HR=0.90, 95% CI 0.77-1.05, trend p=0.23", "certainty_notes": "Large well-conducted cohort but observational, subject to residual confounding by overall dietary pattern and health-conscious behaviour; natto-specific effect is a secondary finding among many soy products tested, raising multiple-comparison concerns.", "key_study_ids": ["murai2022"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "students", "population_detail": "Medical students experiencing academic stress", "dose_or_intake": "100 mL/day traditional Mexican lactic-acid-fermented beverage (aguamiel-based, L. plantarum/paracasei/brevis)", "duration": "8 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "benefit", "effect_summary": "Academic stress scores (SISCO Inventory) decreased significantly in the fermented-beverage group (n=27) but not in controls (n=18); significant changes in environmental-demand and physical/psychological-factor subscales.", "effect_estimate": "Within-group p=0.001 (fermented beverage); control group p=0.607 (not significant)", "certainty_notes": "Small, single-site trial; beverage arm not placebo-matched/blinded (a fermented beverage is hard to blind against); downgraded for risk of bias and small sample.", "key_study_ids": ["marquez-morales2021"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy young adults, no specified baseline stress/mood impairment", "dose_or_intake": "250 mL/day lactose-free kefir", "duration": "6 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Kefir produced significant reductions in GI symptoms and several biochemical markers (cholesterol, creatinine, uric acid) but only non-significant trends toward improved sleep quality and mental-health scores; authors explicitly attribute the lack of significance partly to small sample size.", "effect_estimate": "Mental health/sleep quality scores improved numerically but p>0.05", "certainty_notes": "Likely underpowered for the mental-health/sleep outcomes specifically (adequately powered for the GI/biochemical primary outcomes that did reach significance); single trial.", "key_study_ids": ["bakirhan2026"]}], "acute_matrix": [], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "17 weeks", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "outcome": "gut microbiota diversity and inflammatory markers (not cognition/mood directly)", "effect_estimate": "Steady increase in microbiota diversity and decreased inflammatory markers in the fermented-food arm vs a high-fiber arm (primary cytokine-response outcome unchanged overall, but distinct immunological trajectories noted)", "evidence_quality": "B (for the microbiome/immune outcome; not a direct brain/cognition measure)", "limitations": "Small per-arm sample (n=18); outcome is immune/microbiome, not a cognitive or mood endpoint; cannot directly support a brain claim without an intermediate inferential step.", "study_ids": ["wastyk2021"]}, {"duration": "4 weeks", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "outcome": "mood_stress (perceived stress, as part of a combined fermented+prebiotic 'psychobiotic diet')", "effect_estimate": "32% stress reduction in diet group vs 17% in control; between-group difference not statistically significant; adherence correlated with stress reduction (adj. R2=0.45, p=0.007)", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "Whole dietary pattern, not isolated fermented food; between-group comparison null despite a promising within-group trend; single trial; cross-referenced from prebiotics-and-synbiotics.json.", "study_ids": ["berding2023"]}, {"duration": "12 weeks", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "outcome": "episodic_memory, global_cognition", "effect_estimate": "Significant overall memory improvement (p=0.041); larger subgroup benefit in women 70+", "evidence_quality": "B", "limitations": "Industry-funded; modest completer sample (n=47); subgroup finding is post-hoc.", "study_ids": ["west2025"]}, {"duration": "12 weeks", "population": "older_adults_mci", "outcome": "cognitive_decline (combined cognition, attention)", "effect_estimate": "Significantly greater improvement vs placebo (p=0.02 for combined cognition and attention)", "evidence_quality": "B", "limitations": "Clinical (MCI) population, not generalisable to healthy adults; industry-sponsor-affiliated.", "study_ids": ["hwang2019"]}, {"duration": "11-21 years", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "outcome": "dementia_risk", "effect_estimate": "No association for total soy; marginal inverse association for natto in women <60y (HR=0.78, 95% CI 0.59-1.04)", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "Observational; residual confounding; multiple-comparisons across several soy products.", "study_ids": ["murai2022"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Fermented foods as a category are generally safe, traditional, widely consumed foods with a long history of safe use (lben, raib, jben and fermented olives in Morocco; kefir, sauerkraut, kimchi and fermented dairy across the EU/US). The main safety considerations are: (1) biogenic amines (histamine, tyramine) produced during fermentation, which can cause intolerance-type symptoms in sensitive individuals and a specific, serious hypertensive-crisis risk in people taking monoamine oxidase inhibitor (MAOI) medications; (2) high sodium content in many traditional salt-fermented products (kimchi, some pickled vegetables, salted fermented fish, and some preparations of Moroccan preserved olives), associated observationally with increased gastric cancer risk at high habitual intake, a salt/nitrosamine-driven risk rather than a fermentation-specific one; (3) microbiological safety depends heavily on controlled fermentation conditions - home-made or poorly controlled artisanal ferments (including informally made lben/raib outside ONSSA-authorised facilities) carry a higher risk of pathogen contamination (Listeria, Brucella in unpasteurised dairy ferments) or excessive biogenic amine accumulation than commercially standardised products; (4) alcohol content in some ferments (kombucha, traditional fermented beverages) can be non-trivial and relevant for pregnant women, children, people avoiding alcohol for religious/personal reasons (directly relevant to the Moroccan market), or those on medications that interact with alcohol; (5) people with histamine intolerance, mast cell disorders, or taking MAOIs should limit or avoid high-histamine fermented foods and consult a specialist.", "adverse_effects": ["biogenic-amine-related intolerance symptoms (headache, flushing, GI upset, blood-pressure changes) in sensitive individuals", "tyramine-induced hypertensive crisis risk in MAOI users", "GI symptoms (bloating, gas) especially on initial introduction of high-fiber/high-microbial-load fermented vegetables", "microbiological contamination risk from poorly controlled artisanal fermentation (Listeria, Brucella in unpasteurised dairy ferments)"], "contraindications": ["monoamine oxidase inhibitor (MAOI) medication use, due to tyramine content in some aged/fermented products", "histamine intolerance or mast cell activation disorders", "immunocompromised status, for unpasteurised or minimally processed artisanal ferments specifically"], "drug_interactions": ["MAOI drugs interact with tyramine in aged/fermented foods (classic, well-established interaction, most relevant to aged fermented dairy and some fermented soy products rather than fresh kimchi/kefir, but caution is standard advice across the category)"], "upper_limits": "No formal nutrient-based Tolerable Upper Intake Level applies to 'fermented foods' as a category; practical limits are product-specific (e.g., sodium content of a kimchi serving, alcohol content of a kombucha serving) rather than a single fermentation-related ceiling.", "sleep_impact": "No consistent evidence of either sleep benefit or harm from fermented foods generically in this search; the kefir trial found a non-significant trend toward improved sleep quality [bakirhan2026]; fermented beverages with meaningful alcohol content (some kombucha batches) could plausibly disrupt sleep architecture via the alcohol itself, though this was not directly studied in the trials reviewed here.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Long-term cohort data exist for fermented dairy/soy and chronic disease outcomes (natto and dementia risk over up to 21 years; pickled vegetable and gastric cancer risk over long-term follow-up), but no long-term human safety data specific to a defined Brain Food fermented-food product were identified; the category's safety is primarily supported by its extensive traditional history of food use rather than by long-duration RCT safety data.", "daily_use_appropriate": "yes", "special_populations": "Pregnant women should avoid unpasteurised fermented dairy ferments and limit alcohol-containing fermented beverages (kombucha, some traditional fermented drinks); people on MAOIs need specialist dietetic guidance on tyramine-containing aged ferments; immunocompromised individuals should favour commercially controlled over home-made/artisanal ferments; children were not specifically studied for fermented-food cognitive outcomes in this search and no claim should be made for that population.", "specialist_review_needed": ["dietetics/psychiatry for patients on MAOI medications regarding tyramine-containing ferments", "allergy/immunology for suspected histamine intolerance", "food safety/microbiology for any artisanal or small-batch Moroccan traditional ferment (lben, raib, jben) intended for commercial Brain Food production, to ensure ONSSA-compliant controlled fermentation"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "Generally safe for most healthy adults at typical dietary intakes, but the category spans products with meaningfully different risk profiles (fresh low-salt kefir versus heavily salted traditional pickled vegetables versus alcohol-containing kombucha versus high-tyramine aged ferments), so a single unconditional safety pass cannot be given without specifying the exact product and its sodium/alcohol/biogenic-amine profile; commercial production must follow controlled fermentation and hygiene standards (ONSSA-equivalent) to avoid the microbiological risks associated with poorly controlled artisanal fermentation."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Fermented foods as conventional foods (kimchi, sauerkraut, miso, tempeh, kefir, kombucha) are not novel foods given their long history of EU consumption, though some specific fermented ingredients or extracts derived from them could require novel-food assessment if not consumed significantly before 1997 (not individually checked here). No EU health claim exists under Reg. (EC) 1924/2006 for cognition, mood or any brain-related outcome for fermented foods generically or for any specific product in this monograph (kimchi, kefir, kombucha, natto, miso, tempeh); the only EU-authorised claim touching fermented dairy specifically (live yoghurt/fermented-milk cultures and lactose digestion) is covered in yogurt-and-fermented-dairy.json. The word 'probiotic' is treated by the European Commission as an implied health claim requiring authorisation, so even accurately describing a fermented food as containing 'probiotic' cultures is restricted unless a specific strain-level claim has been authorised (none currently exist for a brain/cognitive outcome).", "us": "Fermented foods are conventional foods under FDA's general food-safety framework; kombucha containing meaningful residual alcohol (some batches exceed 0.5% ABV) can trigger alcohholic-beverage labelling/excise requirements depending on final alcohol content, a product-specific compliance issue for any kombucha-based Brain Food offering. No FDA-authorised or FTC-reviewed cognitive, mood or brain claim exists for fermented foods generically; any structure/function claim would require the manufacturer's own competent-and-reliable-evidence substantiation, which the current evidence base (borderline-significant meta-analysis, 'neither convincing nor sufficient' systematic review, and a counter-example in healthy students) would not comfortably support for a cognition or mood claim.", "morocco": "Traditional Moroccan fermented foods (lben, raib, jben, fermented/preserved olives) must be produced in ONSSA-authorised establishments under Law 28-07 to be legally sold; artisanal/informal production outside this framework carries both a legal and a microbiological-safety concern. Nutrition claims are limited to the positive list under Joint Order 281-16, and disease/function claims beyond that list are prohibited under Article 7; no cognition-related claim for any fermented food was identified as approved, so status for any brain-related wording is uncertain and must be confirmed directly with ONSSA before use. Alcohol content in any fermented beverage (e.g., kombucha) is a particularly sensitive regulatory and cultural issue in the Moroccan market and should be verified against applicable national alcohol-product regulations before any such product is marketed there.", "permitted_claims_examples": ["None specific to fermented foods (as distinct from the single dairy-specific lactose-digestion claim covered in yogurt-and-fermented-dairy.json) were identified for any cognitive, mood or brain-related outcome in the EU, US or Morocco."], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "Traditional fermented foods with an established EU/US consumption history (kimchi, kefir, miso, tempeh, natto, kombucha, lben, raib, jben) are not novel foods; isolated bioactive peptides or extracts derived from fermentation (e.g., specific fermented-soy peptide isolates) could require novel-food assessment in the EU or NDI notification in the US if marketed as a concentrated supplement ingredient rather than as the whole traditional food - not independently assessed for any specific such ingredient in this search.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "High: fermented foods are, by definition, already food-format products; the main formulation question for Brain Food is which traditional or modern fermented foods to feature (e.g., Moroccan lben/raib as a dairy-fermented base, or kefir/kimchi/miso as more globally recognised options) and how to standardise their microbial/bioactive content for consistent messaging, since natural fermentation produces batch-to-batch variability unlike a standardised probiotic supplement.", "formats": ["ready-to-drink fermented beverages (kefir, lben/raib-style drinks, kombucha)", "fermented vegetable side dishes/condiments (kimchi-style)", "fermented soy-based powders or foods (following the West 2025 and Hwang 2019 precedent)", "fermented dairy products (cross-reference yogurt-and-fermented-dairy.json for dedicated yogurt/dairy formulation guidance)"], "stability": "Live microbial content in fermented foods can decline over shelf life depending on storage temperature, packaging and pasteurisation status; pasteurised/heat-treated fermented products (common for shelf-stable kimchi, some commercial kefir) will have reduced or no live culture content, which matters if a 'live culture' or psychobiotic positioning is intended versus a flavour/nutrient-only positioning.", "taste": "Fermented foods have strong, acquired flavour profiles (sour, salty, pungent, effervescent) that can be a barrier to adoption for some consumers outside their traditional food culture, but are an asset in markets (including Morocco) where these flavours are already culturally familiar and valued (lben, raib, jben, preserved olives).", "bioavailability_matrix": "Not systematically characterised for fermented-food bioactives generically in this search; fermented-soy isoflavone aglycones are reported in the literature to have different (generally better) bioavailability than their glycoside forms in unfermented soy, which is part of the proposed mechanism for the West 2025 and Murai 2022 findings, but this was not independently re-verified via fulltext in this unit.", "dose_feasibility": "The tested 'doses' in positive trials (e.g., a daily serving of fermented soy powder, 250 mL/day kefir, 100 mL/day fermented beverage) are all realistic, food-compatible serving sizes, so dose feasibility is not a barrier; the barrier is the weakness and inconsistency of the underlying efficacy evidence, not practical deliverability.", "cost_availability": "Most fermented foods in this monograph (kefir, fermented soy products, traditional Moroccan lben/raib/jben, fermented vegetables) are low-to-moderate cost and widely available or locally producible across Morocco, the EU and the US; kombucha and specialty fermented-soy powders are somewhat costlier commercial categories.", "cost_evidence": "Not researched here (no specific supplier pricing was collected in this unit's search)."}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 3, "effect_magnitude": 2, "safety": 6, "reproducibility": 2, "food_suitability": 9, "cost_access": 8, "rationale": "evidence_quality=3: the largest pooled cognition estimate (Marx 2020) is borderline-null and a dedicated 2025 systematic review explicitly concludes evidence is 'neither convincing nor sufficient'; the clearest positive single-trial evidence is in older adults (West 2025) or clinically impaired adults (Hwang 2019 MCI), not the healthy-young-adult/student target population, where one well-conducted comparative study (Karbownik 2022) found an association running in the opposite direction. effect_magnitude=2: pooled effect sizes are small (g~0.16 at best) and individual-trial effects, where positive, are modest. safety=6: generally safe traditional foods, but the category spans meaningfully different risk profiles (sodium in salt-fermented vegetables, alcohol in kombucha, biogenic amines/MAOI interaction, microbiological risk in uncontrolled artisanal fermentation), preventing a higher uniform safety score without product-specific qualification. reproducibility=2: the few positive trials are mostly single, unreplicated studies (fermented soy, Mexican fermented beverage), and a directly contradictory finding exists in the most relevant population (healthy students). food_suitability=9: fermented foods are inherently food-format and culturally resonant, especially for the Moroccan market (lben, raib, jben, olives). cost_access=8: most options are low-cost and locally available across all three target markets."}, "research_gaps": ["No adequately powered, pre-registered trial has tested a single standardised fermented food on validated cognitive outcomes specifically in healthy young adults, students or knowledge workers.", "The counter-intuitive Karbownik 2022 finding (worse mental-health association with higher fermented-food intake in healthy students) has not been explored or replicated in an interventional design.", "Fermented-food composition (live microbial load, specific strains, bioactive peptide content) is rarely standardised or fully characterised across trials, as explicitly flagged by Harsa 2025, making cross-study comparison and dose-response analysis difficult.", "No trial has directly compared a specific Moroccan traditional fermented food (lben, raib, jben) against a placebo or an international comparator (kefir, yogurt) for any brain-relevant outcome.", "Long-term (>12 week) trials isolating a single fermented food's independent brain effect, separate from an overall psychobiotic dietary pattern, are lacking.", "The mechanism by which fermented soy's modified isoflavone/peptide profile might benefit cognition in older women specifically, but not clearly in men or younger adults, has not been directly tested."], "_derived": {"composite_score": 4.4, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": "B", "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": "C", "best_grade_overall": "B", "benefit_domains": {"episodic_memory": "B", "cognitive_decline": "B"}, "n_outcome_rows": 8, "n_studies": 14, "n_verified": 14, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 3, "n_systematic_reviews": 2, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["benefit", "inconsistent", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "prebiotics-and-synbiotics", "name": "Prebiotics and synbiotics", "category": "I", "category_name": "Gut-brain axis", "aliases": ["prebiotic fibers", "galacto-oligosaccharides (GOS)", "B-GOS (Bimuno)", "fructo-oligosaccharides (FOS)", "inulin / oligofructose-enriched inulin", "polydextrose", "synbiotics (probiotic+prebiotic combinations)", "short-chain fatty acid (SCFA) precursors"], "food_sources": ["chicory root, Jerusalem artichoke, leeks, onions, garlic, asparagus, wheat/rye, bananas (native inulin/FOS)", "legumes, oats, lentils (fermentable fibers that yield GOS-like substrates in vivo only indirectly; dietary GOS itself is not a natural macronutrient food component, it is produced industrially from lactose)", "human milk oligosaccharides (infant-specific, not relevant to adult diet)", "isolated prebiotic ingredients added to foods and supplements (chicory inulin, GOS syrup, polydextrose, resistant starch, partially hydrolyzed guar gum)"], "active_constituents": ["inulin-type fructans (inulin, oligofructose)", "galacto-oligosaccharides (GOS, including the proprietary B-GOS/Bimuno blend)", "fructo-oligosaccharides (FOS)", "polydextrose", "partially hydrolyzed guar gum (PHGG)", "colonic short-chain fatty acids (acetate, propionate, butyrate) produced by bacterial fermentation of these fibers - the proposed mediators of any gut-brain effect"], "one_line_verdict": "Prebiotic fibers reliably shift gut bacterial composition (more Bifidobacterium) and have a handful of intriguing small trials on stress hormones and anxiety-related attention, but the largest, best-controlled trials in healthy young adults are null on mood, cognition and anxiety, so a 'prebiotics for the brain' claim is not supported for this population; a modest signal for cognition exists only in older adults and is of low certainty.", "scientific_conclusion": "Prebiotic fibers (chicory inulin, GOS, FOS, polydextrose, partially hydrolyzed guar gum) are non-digestible carbohydrates that are selectively fermented by colonic bacteria, chiefly increasing Bifidobacterium abundance; this microbial shift is well documented and is the one effect that replicates almost every time it is measured [schmidt2015, johnstone2021, mysonhimer2023, johnstone2025, nilochlainn2024]. Whether that shift changes brain function in humans is far less settled. The strongest single acute human signal comes from Schmidt et al. 2015: 3 weeks of 5.5 g/day B-GOS (but not FOS) lowered the salivary cortisol awakening response and reduced attentional vigilance to negative versus positive words in a dot-probe task in 45 healthy volunteers [schmidt2015]; this is a neuroendocrine/attentional-bias biomarker finding, not a memory or reasoning outcome, and has not been replicated at this effect size by the same or other groups. A partial replication in young women (18-25y, n=48) found only a trend-level anxiety reduction restricted to a pre-specified high-trait-anxiety subgroup after 4 weeks of GOS, with no effect in the full sample or on depression, sleep or social anxiety [johnstone2021]. A larger, pre-registered follow-up by the same senior author in 83 healthy young women found no significant effect of 28 days of GOS on the primary outcome (trait anxiety, p=0.443), although GABA/glutamate levels measured by MRS shifted at trend level and reaction times improved in high-anxious participants on tertiary outcomes [johnstone2025]; the escalation from Johnstone 2021's p=0.062 trend to a fully null primary endpoint in the better-powered 2025 trial is a textbook case of effect-size shrinkage with replication. A crossover trial of 5g/day FOS plus 5g/day GOS in 24 healthy adults found no effect on any biological stress/inflammation marker or on DASS-42 mental-health scores, despite confirmed Bifidobacterium increases [mysonhimer2023]. Acutely, a single 5 g dose of oligofructose-enriched inulin improved episodic memory recall and recognition accuracy over 4 hours in 47 adults, alongside improved mood ratings, in a well-controlled crossover design [smith2015] - a single small acute trial, not yet independently replicated, and the same research programme's earlier (2005) chronic trial had found no benefit. The one dietary (not supplement) trial, 'Gut Feelings', randomised 119 adults with moderate psychological distress to a high-prebiotic diet, a probiotic, a synbiotic or placebo for 8 weeks: the prebiotic-diet arm reduced total mood disturbance versus placebo (d=-0.60, 95% CI -1.18 to -0.03, p=0.039) with secondary improvements in anxiety, stress and sleep, while the synbiotic and probiotic-alone arms did not [freijy2022]. On cognition specifically, the same trial's Cogstate battery found no benefit of the prebiotic diet and, if anything, weak Bayesian evidence (97% posterior probability) that the prebiotic diet impaired processing speed (d=0.25); only the probiotic arm showed weak evidence (96% posterior probability) of better working memory [freijy2025] - a result that argues against, not for, prebiotic cognitive enhancement, and illustrates how a mood benefit and a cognition benefit from the same ingredient can diverge. A 4-week whole-diet intervention emphasising prebiotic and fermented foods in 45 healthy adults reduced perceived stress by 32% versus 17% in controls, but the between-group difference was not statistically significant and biological stress markers did not change; only within-subject adherence correlated with stress reduction [berding2023] - a dietary-pattern study, not a single-ingredient trial, reported here and cross-referenced in fermented-foods. Mechanistically, colon-delivered SCFA mixtures (the fermentation end-products of these fibers) blunted the cortisol response to psychosocial stress in 66 healthy men in a rigorous triple-blind trial [dalile2020], supporting SCFAs as a plausible mediator, but a separate trial of extruded wheat bran that raised serum SCFAs produced no psychobiological change [not separately tabulated; cross-reference mechanism], showing the SCFA-to-cortisol link does not reliably translate to whole-fiber foods at achievable doses. In older adults the signal is more consistent but still preliminary: a 12-week, double-blind, twin-controlled trial (PROMOTe, n=72, aged 60+) found the prebiotic (versus placebo) improved a composite cognition factor score (beta=-0.482, 95% CI -0.813 to -0.141, p=0.014) despite no effect on the primary physical-function outcome [nilochlainn2024], and a 2026 systematic review pooling 14 trials (10 probiotic, 2 prebiotic, 2 synbiotic) in adults 60+ reported a significant global-cognition improvement in a sub-group meta-analysis (MD 1.28, 95% CI 0.15-2.40) [birns2026] - promising but drawn mostly from probiotic trials, heterogeneous, and not generalisable to healthy young adults. The most authoritative pooled estimate for prebiotics specifically comes from Marx et al.'s meta-analysis of 5 prebiotic trials (n=355): no significant effect on global cognition (g=0.077, 95% CI -0.091 to 0.246, p=0.369) [marx2020], consistent with Desmedt et al.'s earlier narrative systematic review of 14 prebiotic intervention studies, which found chronic trials (>28 days) more often improved affect/verbal memory than null, but flagged weak methodology and called for better microbiota characterisation [desmedt2019]. In frail older adults, a 13-week inulin+FOS trial improved two frailty criteria (exhaustion, handgrip) but found no effect on cognitive behaviour [buigues2016_crossref, not separately coded]. Children are a separate population: a 3-month prebiotic trial in 7-9 year olds with low reading scores found no effect on any outcome including cognition, anxiety or cortisol [capitao2020], and must not be generalised to adults. Overall: the microbiota-shifting effect of prebiotics is established; the cortisol/attentional-bias effect of B-GOS specifically is a real but small and unreplicated-at-scale finding; the largest, best-powered trials in healthy young adults (the population most relevant to Brain Food) are null for both mood and cognition; and the one positive cognition finding in adults comes from older, not young, participants.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "There is no prebiotic deficiency state: adequately fed people already host fermenting gut bacteria, and typical Western diets provide roughly 2.5-15 g/day of fermentable fiber from normal foods without any isolated 'prebiotic' ingredient. The research question is therefore entirely one of enhancement - adding isolated GOS/FOS/inulin on top of habitual intake to shift microbiota and, hypothetically, mood or cognition - not correction of a shortfall. This differs from fiber adequacy in general (most adults in Morocco, the EU and the US fall short of the ~25-38 g/day total dietary fiber recommendation), where increasing whole-food fiber intake has broad, well-established metabolic benefits independent of any brain claim. The gut-brain literature in this unit is about a narrow, additional psychobiotic hypothesis layered on top of ordinary fiber adequacy, and the evidence for that narrower hypothesis in healthy, non-deficient young adults is weak to null [mysonhimer2023, johnstone2025, freijy2025].", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Marketing of prebiotic supplements for 'gut-brain health', 'stress relief' or 'mental clarity' typically cites the Schmidt 2015 cortisol/attention finding and the general gut-brain-axis literature without disclosing that the largest, most rigorous trials in healthy adults (Mysonhimer 2023, Johnstone 2025, the cognitive arm of Gut Feelings) found no benefit, and that Marx et al.'s meta-analysis found no significant pooled effect on cognition from prebiotic trials. The one dietary RCT with a significant mood benefit (Gut Feelings, prebiotic-diet arm) tested a whole high-prebiotic-food diet in people with moderate psychological distress, not a supplement in healthy, non-distressed young adults, and its own cognitive sub-study found no cognitive benefit and a signal of harm to processing speed. No EU health claim exists for any cognitive, mood or stress outcome for inulin, GOS, FOS or polydextrose; the only authorised EU claims for these fibers concern bowel function (inulin/stool frequency) and blood glucose, not the brain. The honest position for Brain Food is that prebiotic fibers are a legitimate, safe way to support fiber intake and gut microbiota diversity as part of an overall healthy diet, but that a specific brain or mood benefit, while mechanistically plausible and worth continued research, cannot currently be claimed for the target population of healthy young adults and students.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Prebiotic fibers escape digestion in the small intestine and are selectively fermented by colonic bacteria (notably Bifidobacterium and Lactobacillus species), which increases their relative abundance and generates short-chain fatty acids (SCFAs: acetate, propionate, butyrate) as fermentation end-products. SCFAs can signal to the gut via free fatty acid receptors, modulate enteroendocrine and vagal afferent signalling, and at least transiently affect circulating hormones and inflammatory tone. In humans, directly infusing SCFAs into the colon attenuates the cortisol response to psychosocial stress, providing some mechanistic support for the 'prebiotic lowers stress hormones' hypothesis; but increasing SCFAs by feeding a whole fiber food (wheat bran) did not translate into measurable psychobiological change, showing the dose and delivery route achieved by ordinary foods or typical supplement doses may be insufficient to reproduce the colonic-infusion effect. Downstream, proposed routes to the brain include reduced HPA-axis reactivity, vagal signalling, and changes to GABA/glutamate balance (suggested by one MRS study), but no study has shown a complete, measured pathway from an oral prebiotic dose through microbiota change through SCFA increase through a specific brain or cognitive outcome in the same humans.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Oral GOS/FOS/inulin -> resistant to digestion in small intestine -> reaches colon largely intact", "detail": "Well-established pharmacokinetics for these non-digestible oligo/polysaccharides; dose-dependent GI tolerance limits (see safety).", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Colonic bacterial fermentation -> selective increase in Bifidobacterium (and some Lactobacillus) -> SCFA production", "detail": "Consistently reproduced across trials regardless of whether downstream brain/mood outcomes changed [johnstone2021, mysonhimer2023, johnstone2025, nilochlainn2024].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "SCFA absorption into portal/systemic circulation -> signalling via free fatty acid receptors and vagal afferents -> modulation of HPA axis", "detail": "Colon-delivered SCFA infusion attenuated cortisol response to psychosocial stress in 66 healthy men [dalile2020]; but whole-food fiber (wheat bran) raising serum SCFAs produced no such effect, indicating dose/route matters.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Altered HPA reactivity / neurotransmitter balance -> changed emotional processing (attentional bias, GABA/glutamate)", "detail": "B-GOS lowered cortisol awakening response and reduced negative attentional bias in one trial [schmidt2015]; a later, larger trial by a related group found no primary anxiety effect but a trend-level GABA change on MRS [johnstone2025].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Putative downstream effect on measured cognition (memory, attention, executive function)", "detail": "No consistent human evidence; pooled prebiotic effect on global cognition is null [marx2020]; one small acute trial found improved episodic memory [smith2015] unreplicated at scale; one older-adult trial found a composite cognitive benefit [nilochlainn2024].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Animal/preclinical neurochemical changes (hippocampal BDNF, NMDA receptor responses, attentional set-shifting) after prebiotic feeding", "detail": "Rodent studies show increased cortical NMDA responses and improved attentional set-shifting after B-GOS; these inform the human hypotheses but are not evidence of human cognitive efficacy.", "evidence_level": "animal"}], "pathways": ["colonic fermentation and Bifidobacterium enrichment", "SCFA (acetate/propionate/butyrate) signalling via free fatty acid receptors and vagal afferents", "HPA-axis modulation (cortisol awakening response)", "GABA/glutamate balance (preliminary MRS evidence)", "attentional bias to emotional stimuli"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults 18-45y, not clinically anxious or depressed", "dose_or_intake": "5-5.5 g/day B-GOS, FOS, or GOS", "duration": "3-4 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "B-GOS lowered cortisol awakening response and reduced negative attentional bias in one 45-person trial (p<0.05) with no effect from FOS; a partial replication in 48 young women found only a high-anxiety-subgroup trend (p=0.062-0.07); the best-powered replication (n=83) found no significant primary anxiety effect (p=0.443); a 24-person crossover of FOS+GOS found no effect on any mental-health or stress-biomarker outcome.", "effect_estimate": "Cortisol AUC-g group difference p=0.019-0.023 [schmidt2015]; trait anxiety p=0.443, GABA trend p=0.053-0.088 [johnstone2025]; no significant change on DASS-42 [mysonhimer2023]", "certainty_notes": "Downgraded for inconsistency across a chain of studies from the same/overlapping research groups, small samples, multiple subgroup/exploratory analyses, and a clear pattern of effect shrinkage on better-powered replication.", "key_study_ids": ["schmidt2015", "johnstone2021", "johnstone2025", "mysonhimer2023"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults, single-session laboratory testing", "dose_or_intake": "5 g oligofructose-enriched inulin, single dose", "duration": "4 hours", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "A single 5 g dose improved recognition accuracy and immediate/delayed recall versus placebo in a well-controlled crossover trial (n=47); mood was also improved. An earlier chronic trial by the same lead author (2005, not separately tabulated here) found no benefit, so acute and chronic results from the same programme diverge.", "effect_estimate": "Statistically significant improvement in recognition accuracy and recall; exact effect size not reported in abstract/available text", "certainty_notes": "Single small trial, not independently replicated; downgraded for imprecision and lack of replication.", "key_study_ids": ["smith2015"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Pooled across prebiotic RCTs, predominantly healthy or mixed adult samples", "dose_or_intake": "Varied prebiotic doses, 3-12 weeks", "duration": "3-12 weeks (pooled)", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Meta-analysis of 5 prebiotic RCTs (n=355) found no significant pooled effect on global cognition; the cognitive sub-study of the Gut Feelings trial found no benefit of a high-prebiotic diet on any Cogstate domain and weak evidence of processing-speed impairment.", "effect_estimate": "g=0.077, 95% CI -0.091 to 0.246, p=0.369 [marx2020]; prebiotic-diet processing speed d=0.25, 95% CI -0.02 to 0.51, posterior probability of harm 97% [freijy2025]", "certainty_notes": "Best available pooled estimate for this population; downgraded for small total n and short durations, but direction is consistently null-to-negative, not merely imprecise.", "key_study_ids": ["marx2020", "freijy2025", "desmedt2019"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Adults with moderate psychological distress and low baseline prebiotic food intake (not a clinical psychiatric diagnosis)", "dose_or_intake": "High-prebiotic whole-food diet (fruit, vegetables, legumes, whole grains; target >=5 serves prebiotic-rich plant foods/day) vs placebo capsule", "duration": "8 weeks", "grade": "B", "direction": "benefit", "effect_summary": "The 'Gut Feelings' factorial RCT (n=119) found the prebiotic-diet arm reduced total mood disturbance versus placebo with secondary improvements in anxiety, stress and sleep; the probiotic and synbiotic arms did not improve mood.", "effect_estimate": "Cohen's d=-0.60, 95% CI -1.18 to -0.03, p=0.039", "certainty_notes": "Single trial with a moderately-distressed, not healthy, population and a whole-diet (not single-ingredient) intervention; relatively small sample; needs independent replication before generalising, and does not transfer to a single isolated prebiotic supplement claim.", "key_study_ids": ["freijy2022"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Twins aged >=60, community-dwelling, alongside resistance exercise and BCAA supplementation in all arms", "dose_or_intake": "Prebiotic supplement (composition increasing Bifidobacterium), daily", "duration": "12 weeks", "grade": "B", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "In the PROMOTe twin RCT (n=72), the prebiotic arm improved a composite cognition factor score versus placebo despite no significant effect on the co-primary physical outcome (chair-rise time); a subsequent systematic review/meta-analysis pooling probiotic, prebiotic and synbiotic trials in adults >=60 found a significant global-cognition improvement in a sub-group analysis.", "effect_estimate": "beta=-0.482, 95% CI -0.813 to -0.141, p=0.014 [nilochlainn2024]; pooled MD=1.28, 95% CI 0.15-2.40, p<0.05 across a 14-study sub-group (mostly probiotic trials) [birns2026]", "certainty_notes": "Promising single trial plus a heterogeneous pooled estimate dominated by probiotic, not prebiotic, studies; cannot be generalised to healthy young adults or students; co-intervention (exercise, BCAA) in all arms limits isolating the prebiotic's independent contribution.", "key_study_ids": ["nilochlainn2024", "birns2026"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "children_adolescents", "population_detail": "Children 7-9y with low reading scores (not healthy-baseline, not adults)", "dose_or_intake": "Prebiotic supplement, daily", "duration": "3 months", "grade": "E", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "No effect of 3 months of prebiotic supplementation on reading, cognition, sleep, behaviour, mood, anxiety or cortisol in children with low reading scores.", "effect_estimate": "Not reported (fully null proof-of-concept study)", "certainty_notes": "Single small trial in a non-adult, reading-impaired population; must not be generalised to healthy adults or to students; included here only for completeness and null-hunting transparency.", "key_study_ids": ["capitao2020"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "5 g oligofructose-enriched inulin, single dose", "onset": "within 4 hours", "duration_studied": "single 4-hour laboratory session", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "effect_estimate": "significant improvement in recognition accuracy and recall vs placebo; also improved mood, reduced hunger/indigestion", "certainty": "low (single small unreplicated trial)", "safety": "well tolerated at 5 g acute dose", "study_ids": ["smith2015"]}, {"dose": "5.5 g/day B-GOS (Bimuno)", "onset": "measured after 21 days, not acute", "duration_studied": "3 weeks", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "effect_estimate": "lower cortisol awakening response and reduced negative attentional bias vs placebo", "certainty": "low-moderate (single trial, unreplicated at this effect size)", "safety": "no adverse events reported", "study_ids": ["schmidt2015"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "3-4 weeks", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "outcome": "mood_stress, anxiety", "effect_estimate": "null to small trend-level effect; best-powered replication (n=83) null on primary anxiety endpoint", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "small samples, overlapping research groups, multiple subgroup analyses, effect shrinkage on replication", "study_ids": ["johnstone2021", "johnstone2025", "mysonhimer2023"]}, {"duration": "8 weeks", "population": "mixed_adults", "outcome": "mood_stress (distressed, non-clinical population)", "effect_estimate": "d=-0.60 (95% CI -1.18, -0.03) for prebiotic-diet vs placebo on total mood disturbance; no cognitive benefit on secondary analysis", "evidence_quality": "B", "limitations": "whole-diet, not single-ingredient; moderately distressed, not healthy, sample; single trial", "study_ids": ["freijy2022", "freijy2025"]}, {"duration": "12 weeks", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "outcome": "global_cognition", "effect_estimate": "composite cognition factor score improved vs placebo (beta=-0.482, p=0.014); no effect on primary physical-function outcome", "evidence_quality": "B", "limitations": "co-administered with resistance exercise and BCAA in all arms; twin design increases genetic control but sample still modest (n=72); not generalisable to young adults", "study_ids": ["nilochlainn2024"]}, {"duration": "pooled (3-12 weeks)", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "outcome": "global_cognition (meta-analytic)", "effect_estimate": "g=0.077 (95% CI -0.091, 0.246), not significant", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "only 5 trials, n=355, short durations, clinical heterogeneity of populations and cognitive tests", "study_ids": ["marx2020"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Prebiotic fibers (inulin, FOS, GOS, polydextrose, PHGG) are generally recognised as safe at moderate doses and have a long history of food use (native inulin/FOS occur naturally in onions, garlic, chicory, wheat). The principal and well-documented adverse effect is dose-dependent gastrointestinal discomfort - flatulence, bloating, abdominal cramping and, at higher single doses, osmotic diarrhoea - because these fibers are fermented rapidly by colonic bacteria and can draw water into the gut lumen. Comfortable everyday doses are roughly up to 10 g/day for inulin and up to 5 g/day for oligofructose/GOS in most healthy adults, with individual tolerance varying and habituation improving tolerance over repeated exposure; single doses above this threshold (e.g. 10-20 g) substantially increase GI symptoms. No long-term safety signal beyond GI tolerance was identified in this search; no deaths, serious adverse events or regulatory safety alerts were found specific to inulin, FOS, GOS or polydextrose at food/supplement doses. People with irritable bowel syndrome or other FODMAP-sensitive conditions can experience more pronounced bloating and discomfort and should introduce these fibers gradually or avoid high doses (inulin and GOS are classic high-FODMAP ingredients). No specific drug interactions were identified in this unit's searches; theoretical concern exists for altered absorption of co-administered minerals (iron, calcium, magnesium) via changed gut transit and microbiota, though some trials actually showed improved mineral absorption with prebiotic co-administration.", "adverse_effects": ["flatulence and bloating (most common, dose-dependent)", "abdominal cramping", "osmotic diarrhoea at high single doses (>15-20 g)", "exacerbation of symptoms in IBS/FODMAP-sensitive individuals"], "contraindications": ["known fructan/FODMAP intolerance or diagnosed IBS without gradual titration", "active inflammatory bowel disease (not studied in this unit; caution advised, specialist review needed)"], "drug_interactions": ["none definitively established in this unit's search; theoretical interaction with timing of oral mineral or drug absorption due to altered gut transit time has not been systematically studied for these specific fibers"], "upper_limits": "No formal regulatory Tolerable Upper Intake Level (UL) exists for inulin, FOS or GOS as a nutrient; practical GI-comfort ceiling from trial data is approximately 5 g/day oligofructose/GOS and up to 10 g/day native inulin for most healthy adults without gradual titration; EFSA's authorised bowel-function claim for chicory inulin specifies 12 g/day as the effective (not necessarily always comfortable) dose.", "sleep_impact": "No direct evidence in this unit's search that prebiotic fibers themselves disrupt or improve sleep; one GOS-based product trial found a non-significant trend toward improved sleep quality in adults with pre-existing sleep disturbance, and the SCFA-cortisol literature raises a plausible but unconfirmed pathway to altered sleep via HPA-axis modulation.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Trials in this unit extend up to 12 weeks; no multi-year human safety data specific to isolated prebiotic ingredients were identified in this search, though their natural dietary forms (onion, garlic, chicory, wheat) have an extensive history of safe food use at typical dietary intakes.", "daily_use_appropriate": "yes", "special_populations": "Pregnant/lactating women, children, and people with IBS or other functional GI disorders were not systematically reviewed for safety in this unit beyond the Bonnema tolerance trial and the Capitao child trial (which found the GOS-type intervention safe but not efficacious in children); specialist dietetic input is advised for IBS patients given high-FODMAP status of inulin/GOS/FOS.", "specialist_review_needed": ["gastroenterology/dietetics for IBS and other FODMAP-sensitive conditions", "pediatric nutrition if any child-directed product is considered (not supported by current evidence for cognitive claims)"], "hard_safety_gate": "pass", "gate_reason": "Well-tolerated at food-compatible doses (up to ~5-10 g/day) with the main risk being GI discomfort, not a systemic safety concern; no serious adverse events identified in this unit's literature search."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Prebiotic fibers used in foods are conventional food ingredients, not novel foods (native inulin/FOS/GOS have a long history of EU food use). The only authorised EU health claims for these specific fibers under Reg. (EC) 1924/2006 concern bowel function and blood glucose, not the brain: Reg. (EU) 2015/2314 authorises 'chicory inulin contributes to normal bowel function by increasing stool frequency' at a minimum effective intake of 12 g/day of native chicory inulin (this authorisation was time-limited to a single named company, BENEO-Orafti S.A., for 5 years from 1 January 2016; current generic-use status after that period was not independently confirmed in this search and should be verified against the live EU Register before use). A separate EU claim authorises inulin/oligofructose for a lower post-meal blood-glucose rise when substituting for sugars (minimum 30% sugar replacement). No EU health claim exists for cognition, mood, stress or any brain-related outcome for inulin, FOS, GOS or polydextrose; any such wording would require new EFSA substantiation and is not currently available (accessed via WebSearch of legislation.gov.uk mirror of Reg. 2015/2314 and secondary trade-press sources, 2026-10-26).", "us": "FDA's 2018 guidance on dietary fiber (non-binding, exercising enforcement discretion pending formal rulemaking) names inulin and inulin-type fructans, galacto-oligosaccharide, and polydextrose among isolated/synthetic non-digestible carbohydrates that may be declared as 'dietary fiber' on Nutrition Facts/Supplement Facts labels, with polydextrose assigned a caloric value of 1 kcal/g (accessed via WebSearch of FDA guidance summaries and trade press, 2026-10-26; the FDA's original guidance document itself was not independently fetched in this unit and should be verified directly at fda.gov before reliance). No FDA-authorised or FTC-reviewed cognitive, mood or stress claim exists for these ingredients; any such structure/function claim on a US product would require the manufacturer's own competent-and-reliable-evidence substantiation file, which the current human evidence base (mostly null/small/unreplicated trials) would not comfortably support for a 'reduces stress' or 'improves memory' claim.", "morocco": "Prebiotic fiber ingredients (inulin, FOS, GOS, polydextrose) as food ingredients fall under ONSSA's general food-safety and labelling framework (Law 28-07) and the nutrition-claims positive list under Joint Order 281-16; no Morocco-specific dossier or claim approval for these fibers was identified in this search, and disease/function claims beyond the positive list are prohibited by Article 7. Status for any brain/cognitive wording is therefore uncertain and would need direct confirmation with ONSSA.", "permitted_claims_examples": ["EU (Reg. (EU) 2015/2314, status of exclusivity period not independently re-verified): 'Chicory inulin contributes to normal bowel function by increasing stool frequency', conditional on providing at least 12 g/day of native chicory inulin.", "EU: inulin/oligofructose blood-glucose claim when substituting for at least 30% of sugars in a product."], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "Native inulin, FOS and GOS from traditional sources (chicory, onion, garlic, lactose-derived GOS) are not novel foods in the EU and do not require NDI notification in the US at typical food/supplement doses; novel delivery forms, unusual source organisms, or substantially modified/synthetic prebiotic structures would need case-by-case assessment not covered in this search.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "High: inulin, FOS, GOS and polydextrose are widely used, heat-stable (to varying degrees), low-sweetness or mildly sweet food ingredients already incorporated into breads, bars, yogurts, beverages and fiber-fortified foods; they can be added to many Brain Food product formats without major process changes.", "formats": ["fortified bars and baked goods", "beverage/powder sachets (as in the Schmidt and Smith trials)", "fortified dairy/yogurt-alternative bases", "capsule/powder supplements"], "stability": "Inulin and FOS are reasonably heat-stable in baking and moderate processing but can undergo some hydrolysis to simple sugars at high heat/low pH over prolonged periods; GOS is generally stable; polydextrose is highly heat- and acid-stable, which is part of why it is popular as a sugar/fat replacer in baked goods.", "taste": "Inulin and FOS contribute mild sweetness and can mask off-notes; at higher inclusion levels they can affect texture (viscosity, mouthfeel) and may require reformulation; GOS has limited sweetness; polydextrose is nearly tasteless and widely used as a bulking/fat-replacing agent without major sensory penalty.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Not applicable in the traditional sense (these are non-absorbed fibers whose relevant 'delivery' is to the colon, not systemic absorption); the relevant variable is reliable delivery of an effective fermentable dose to the colon, which is well established for all four ingredients at food-compatible doses.", "dose_feasibility": "The studied effective/benefit-associated doses (5-5.5 g/day B-GOS for the cortisol/attention signal; 5 g single dose inulin for the acute memory signal; 10-20 g/day prebiotic mixtures in some other trials) are broadly achievable within a food serving or two without exceeding typical GI-comfort thresholds (~5-10 g/day), making dose-feasibility reasonably good, though the brain-relevant benefit itself remains unconfirmed at these doses in healthy young adults.", "cost_availability": "Inulin, FOS and polydextrose are inexpensive, widely available commodity food ingredients; GOS (especially proprietary blends like Bimuno/B-GOS) is a costlier specialty ingredient; all are readily sourceable for EU/US/Morocco markets.", "cost_evidence": "Not researched here (no specific supplier pricing was collected in this unit's search; see X-series cost/sourcing unit if one exists)."}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 3, "effect_magnitude": 2, "safety": 8, "reproducibility": 2, "food_suitability": 8, "cost_access": 7, "rationale": "evidence_quality=3: best available evidence for the Brain Food target population (healthy young adults/students) is predominantly null (Marx 2020 meta-analysis, Mysonhimer 2023, Johnstone 2025 primary endpoint) with only small, unreplicated or subgroup-level positive signals (Schmidt 2015, Smith 2015, Johnstone 2021 post-hoc); older-adult evidence (PROMOTe, Birns 2026) is more promising but not generalisable. effect_magnitude=2: where effects are statistically significant they are small (d/g ~0.08-0.6, mostly at the low end) and several key findings point toward null or even adverse (processing-speed) directions. safety=8: well-tolerated at food-compatible doses with GI discomfort as the main, manageable risk; no serious safety signal. reproducibility=2: the clearest pattern in this evidence base is shrinking effect sizes on better-powered replication (Johnstone 2021 trend to Johnstone 2025 null), and the largest trials in the target population are null. food_suitability=8: easy to incorporate into food formats at effective doses. cost_access=7: inulin/FOS/polydextrose are cheap and widely available; GOS specialty blends add cost but remain accessible."}, "research_gaps": ["No adequately powered (n>150), pre-registered trial has tested a single prebiotic ingredient on validated cognitive outcomes specifically in students or knowledge workers under real academic/work stress.", "The acute memory finding (Smith 2015) has not been independently replicated at a larger scale or with objective biomarkers.", "The SCFA-cortisol mechanistic pathway (Dalile 2020, colonic infusion) has not been shown to translate into an equivalent effect from an oral, food-compatible prebiotic dose.", "Dose-response relationships between prebiotic intake and any brain-relevant outcome have not been systematically mapped in healthy adults.", "Whole prebiotic-rich diets (as in Gut Feelings) versus isolated supplement ingredients have not been directly compared head-to-head for brain outcomes.", "Long-term (>12 week) human safety and efficacy data specific to isolated prebiotic ingredients for any brain-relevant outcome are lacking."], "_derived": {"composite_score": 4.6, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "pass", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": "C", "best_grade_chronic": "B", "best_grade_young_adults": "C", "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": "B", "best_grade_overall": "B", "benefit_domains": {"episodic_memory": "C", "global_cognition": "B"}, "n_outcome_rows": 6, "n_studies": 14, "n_verified": 14, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 5, "n_systematic_reviews": 3, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["benefit", "inconsistent", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "probiotics-and-psychobiotics", "name": "Probiotics and psychobiotics (strain-specific)", "category": "I", "category_name": "Gut-brain axis", "aliases": ["probiotics", "psychobiotics", "live microbial supplements", "lactic acid bacteria (Lactobacillus, Lacticaseibacillus, Lactiplantibacillus, Levilactobacillus)", "Bifidobacterium", "Bifidobacterium longum 1714 (Zenflore/Novonesis strain)", "Lactiplantibacillus plantarum P8, DR7", "Bifidobacterium breve A1 / MCC1274", "Ecologic Barrier (multispecies, Winclove)", "Lacticaseibacillus paracasei Shirota (Yakult)", "Lacticaseibacillus rhamnosus HN001, GG, CNCM I-3690, JB-1", "Saccharomyces boulardii (yeast probiotic)", "paraprobiotics / heat-killed bacteria (e.g. L. gasseri CP2305) - postbiotic, not live"], "food_sources": ["yoghurt with live cultures (starter cultures L. delbrueckii subsp. bulgaricus and S. thermophilus; these are not the psychobiotic strains tested in trials)", "fermented milks and probiotic drinks (e.g. L. paracasei Shirota fermented milk; L. rhamnosus CNCM I-3690 fermented milk; L. helveticus fermented milk)", "kefir, traditional fermented milks (in Morocco: lben, raib), aged cheeses with live cultures", "fermented vegetables (sauerkraut, kimchi, unpasteurised pickles) - variable, uncharacterised strains and counts", "most trial strains are delivered as freeze-dried capsules or sachets; strain-specific effects cannot be assumed from generic fermented foods"], "active_constituents": ["live microorganisms of a defined strain (genus, species, strain designation) at a stated dose in colony-forming units (CFU), typically 1e9-1e10 CFU/day in mental-health trials", "multi-strain blends (e.g. 8-9 strain Ecologic Barrier; L. helveticus R0052 + B. longum R0175)", "microbial metabolites and cell components implicated mechanistically (short-chain fatty acids, GABA, lactate including D-lactate, cell-wall components); not quantified in the cognitive trials", "in fermented-dairy products, co-delivered bioactive peptides (e.g. lactononadecapeptide in L. helveticus fermented milk) may be the actual active, not the bacteria"], "one_line_verdict": "Probiotic effects on the brain are strain-specific and mostly small: in healthy young adults and students the randomised trials do not show a reliable improvement in memory, attention or academic performance (pooled cognition effects near zero), there is low-certainty evidence for a small reduction in self-reported stress and subclinical anxiety/depression (SMD about 0.2-0.3, with large null trials), possible modest cognitive benefit only in older adults with MCI, and no authorised health claim in the EU.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. Across populations, the only pre-registered meta-analysis restricted to cognitive outcomes found no effect of probiotics on global cognition (11 trials, n=724, g=0.115, 95% CI -0.041 to 0.270) or on any domain [marx2020]; a second meta-analysis found a small overall effect (SMD 0.24) driven by cognitively impaired participants, with no effect in healthy people (SMD 0.15, -0.30 to 0.60) [lv2021]. In healthy young adults the trial base is small (n=22-105 per cognition trial) and mostly null. The well-known B. longum 1714 study used a fixed placebo-then-probiotic order without randomisation (n=22 men) and found lower cortisol output and only a 'subtle' paired-associate learning gain on top of a practice effect [allen2016]; the same laboratory found L. rhamnosus JB-1 had no effect on stress, cognition, EEG or inflammation [kelly2017]. A 4-week multispecies product had no effect on emotion, cognitive control, fMRI or questionnaires, and only a three-way interaction (p=0.039, with a baseline imbalance) suggesting buffering of stress-induced working-memory loss [papalini2019]. GABA-producing lactobacilli did not improve CogState performance [casertano2024]; B. lactis/L. acidophilus did not beat placebo on most tests [czajeczny2023]; S. boulardii did not improve examination grades [karbownik2020]. Neuroimaging changes (fMRI, MEG, EEG) are reported in several small trials [tillisch2013, bagga2018, wang2019, hutchinson2026] but these are biomarkers, inconsistent in location, and not cognition. Mood and stress. Meta-analyses in healthy or working adults report small reductions in subclinical symptoms (SMD 0.34, 0.07-0.61 [mckean2017]; SMD -0.21, -0.34 to -0.09 in 12 workplace trials including open-label designs [benfredj2026]; depression d -0.24 overall, larger in clinical samples [liu2019]). The largest individual trials are null: HN001 in 483 students [slykerman2022press] and in nurses [slykerman2022nurses]; 1714 failed its primary depression outcome in 168 adults [seamans2026]. Positive stressed-adult trials of L. plantarum P8 and DR7 come from one Malaysian group and show effects of about 3 DASS points with lower bounds near zero [lew2019, chong2019]. In depression, CANMAT rates probiotics only as a third-line adjunct [bahji2026]. Older adults and MCI. Positive trials exist (B. bifidum BGN4 + B. longum BORI [kim2021]; B. breve A1 in suspected MCI, RBANS +11.3 points [xiao2020]) but a larger 24-week B. breve trial improved only one subscale [asaoka2022], LGG helped only an impaired subgroup [sanborn2020], and pooled MMSE estimates are very heterogeneous (I2 91%) [silvaolivares2026] or of low certainty [calzadagonzales2025, den2020]. How reproducible. Effects are strain-, dose- and population-specific; most positive trials are industry-funded or use manufacturer-supplied product; the pattern of positive small trials and null large ones is consistent with small-study effects (not formally tested here). Harms reporting is poor [bafeta2018]. What is unknown. No adequately powered, pre-registered trial has shown a cognitive benefit of any strain in healthy young adults, students or knowledge workers; no acute (single-dose) effect has been tested in young adults; no trial has shown that a food matrix (yoghurt) reproduces capsule results for a psychobiotic strain.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "There is no 'probiotic deficiency' in the nutritional sense, and no biomarker identifies who would benefit. The closest analogue to 'deficiency correction' is dysbiosis or a disturbed gut-brain state (depression, irritable bowel syndrome, cognitive impairment, perhaps high stress), and this is where the larger effects cluster: meta-analytic effects on depressive symptoms are larger in clinical than community samples [liu2019], and cognitive effects are seen mainly in people with MCI or Alzheimer's disease [den2020, lv2021, calzadagonzales2025] and in the impaired subgroup of an LGG trial [sanborn2020]. In healthy, unstressed, adequately nourished young adults (the enhancement question) the trials show no reliable cognitive gain [marx2020, kelly2017, papalini2019, casertano2024] and at most a small reduction in self-reported stress [benfredj2026]. Exam-stressed students are a plausible responder group but the largest student trial was null [slykerman2022press]. Evidence from MCI/AD patients and children [rianda2019] must not be transferred to healthy students.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Market claims that probiotics 'boost brain power', 'improve focus and memory', 'feed your second brain' or 'reduce anxiety' generalise from (a) rodent studies of specific strains, (b) small human trials with biomarker or questionnaire outcomes, and (c) patients with depression or MCI. None of these supports a cognition claim for healthy adults. The evidence supports only narrow, strain-specific statements such as 'in a randomised trial in moderately stressed adults, strain X reduced self-reported stress scores by about 3 points over 12 weeks' [lew2019, chong2019], and even these are contradicted for other strains by large null trials [slykerman2022press, slykerman2022nurses]. In the EU, the word 'probiotic' itself is treated by the Commission and several authorities as an implied health claim; no probiotic health claim is authorised, and the only live-culture claim on the EU register concerns lactose digestion by yoghurt starter cultures [efsa2010]. In the US, a 'supports mood/stress' structure/function claim is possible for a dietary supplement, but the FTC requires competent and reliable evidence on the specific strain and dose, and 'reduces depression/anxiety' or 'prevents cognitive decline' would be disease claims. The honest positioning for Brain Food is: fermented dairy as part of a good diet, and, if a supplement is ever considered, one named strain with its own trials, communicated as a gut or general well-being product, not a cognitive enhancer.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Proposed microbiota-gut-brain pathways include vagal afferent signalling, modulation of the HPA stress axis, immune and cytokine signalling, intestinal barrier function, and microbial metabolites (short-chain fatty acids, GABA, tryptophan/serotonin/kynurenine metabolites). These are well supported in rodents for specific strains, but in humans the evidence is limited to inconsistent changes in cortisol, cytokines and brain-imaging signals; ingested strains usually do not durably colonise or reshape the overall microbiota. The translational failure of the JB-1 strain, which worked in anxious mice, illustrates that animal mechanism does not predict human efficacy [kelly2017].", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Oral ingestion of 1e9-1e10 CFU of a defined strain -> partial survival of gastric acid and bile (strain- and matrix-dependent) -> transient presence in the gut, usually without durable colonisation", "detail": "Overall microbiota composition often does not change: no marked change with B. breve MCC1274 for 24 weeks [asaoka2022]; only subtle or uncorrected taxon shifts after 4 weeks of multispecies product [bagga2018]. Dairy matrices may buffer gastric transit; capsule versus food delivery has not been compared for any psychobiotic outcome.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Gut -> HPA axis (cortisol) modulation", "detail": "Lower cortisol output after B. longum 1714 in a non-randomised crossover [allen2016]; no effect on stress hormones with Ecologic Barrier [papalini2019], JB-1 [kelly2017] or L. rhamnosus CNCM I-3690 [wauters2022]; pooled SMD -0.26 in working adults [benfredj2026] but no pooled cortisol effect in healthy volunteers [zhang2020].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Gut -> immune and inflammatory signalling (cytokines, CRP) and gut-barrier function", "detail": "Lower IFN-gamma and TNF-alpha with L. plantarum P8 [lew2019] and DR7 [chong2019]; no cytokine effect with JB-1 [kelly2017]; no CRP effect in workers [benfredj2026]; no effect on small-intestinal permeability (lactulose-mannitol ratio) under public-speaking stress [wauters2022]. Lower hs-CRP and MDA in MCI/AD meta-analysis [den2020].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Vagal afferent signalling and microbial neuroactive metabolites (GABA, short-chain fatty acids, serotonin precursors)", "detail": "Vagus-dependent behavioural effects are shown in rodents (not reviewed here). In humans, fecal GABA/acetylcholine levels did not correlate with outcomes of GABA-producing strains [casertano2024]; plasma serotonin-pathway gene-expression changes with DR7 are exploratory [chong2019].", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Altered brain activity: emotional-processing networks, resting-state connectivity, EEG/MEG oscillations", "detail": "Reduced response of affective/viscerosensory network to emotional faces with fermented milk [tillisch2013]; altered fMRI activation in emotional memory/decision tasks [bagga2018]; MEG theta/beta changes [wang2019]; EEG mobility changes [allen2016]; systematic review of 19 imaging/EEG RCTs (n=762) reports altered resting connectivity and reduced activation to negative stimuli [hutchinson2026]. Regions and directions differ between studies; these are biomarkers, not cognitive outcomes.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Measured human outcomes", "detail": "Cognition in healthy adults: no pooled effect [marx2020, lv2021]; subjective stress and subclinical mood: small, inconsistent benefit [mckean2017, benfredj2026]; MCI/AD: possible small-to-moderate benefit on screening tests with low certainty [den2020, calzadagonzales2025].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["HPA axis / cortisol", "immune-inflammatory signalling (cytokines, CRP)", "intestinal barrier and endotoxin translocation", "vagal afferent signalling (animal evidence)", "microbial metabolites: short-chain fatty acids, GABA, tryptophan-kynurenine-serotonin", "emotional-processing network activity (fMRI/MEG/EEG biomarkers)", "D-lactate production (potential harm pathway in small-intestinal bacterial overgrowth)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy, non-stressed or mildly stressed adults aged 18-40 (students and volunteers), memory, attention, executive and processing tasks", "dose_or_intake": "1e9-1e10 CFU/day, single strains or multispecies blends", "duration": "2-12 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Pooled cognitive effect in healthy participants not significant (SMD 0.15); pooled global cognition across populations not significant (g 0.115). Individual young-adult trials null: JB-1 (n=29, no effect on visuospatial memory, attention switching, RVP); Ecologic Barrier (n=58, no effect without stress); GABA-producing lactobacilli (n=44, CogState unchanged); B. lactis BS01 + L. acidophilus LA02 (placebo improved on 4/5 tests). The only positive signal (B. longum 1714, PAL errors) came from a non-randomised fixed-order design. One meta-analysis reports young-adult verbal ability SMD 0.43 from few trials.", "effect_estimate": "Healthy subgroup SMD 0.15 (95% CI -0.30 to 0.60) [lv2021]; global cognition g 0.115 (-0.041 to 0.270) [marx2020]; young adults verbal ability SMD 0.43 (0.15 to 0.71) [guo2026]", "certainty_notes": "Trials small, heterogeneous strains and tests, multiple outcomes with selective significance; the positive 1714 study is non-randomised (high risk of bias). Downgraded for imprecision and inconsistency; direction of the better-controlled trials is null. No practical benefit for a student can be inferred.", "key_study_ids": ["marx2020", "lv2021", "kelly2017", "papalini2019", "casertano2024", "czajeczny2023", "allen2016", "guo2026"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy women tested before and after an acute laboratory stressor (socially evaluated cold pressor)", "dose_or_intake": "Ecologic Barrier multispecies powder daily (CFU not extracted)", "duration": "28 days", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Probiotic group showed less stress-induced loss of backward digit span than placebo (Time x Group x Session interaction p=0.039; within-group p=0.053), with a marginal baseline imbalance (p=0.077) and no effect on stress hormones or subjective stress. The same lab's exploratory analysis linked the effect to one bacterial genus. Not replicated; JB-1 and 1714 trials did not test stress-induced working memory in the same way.", "effect_estimate": "Interaction F(1,56)=4.48, p=0.039, partial eta-squared 0.07 [papalini2019]; no group difference in raw working memory", "certainty_notes": "Single small trial, manufacturer co-author and co-funding, marginal p-value, possible regression to the mean from baseline imbalance; not replicated. Grade C at most.", "key_study_ids": ["papalini2019", "kelly2017"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "learning", "population": "students", "population_detail": "University and medical students during examination periods; outcome = examination grades or academic performance", "dose_or_intake": "S. boulardii CNCM I-1079 (dose not extracted)", "duration": "30 days", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "The only trial with an academic-performance primary outcome found no effect of S. boulardii on examination grades and no effect on state anxiety, cortisol or metanephrine; pulse rate under stress rose more with the probiotic. No bacterial-probiotic trial with academic performance as primary outcome was found.", "effect_estimate": "Exam performance not significantly different; pulse rate +10.4/min (95% CI 4.2-16.6) vs placebo [karbownik2020]", "certainty_notes": "One small trial (n=50 completers), retrospectively registered; yeast, not bacteria; indirect for other strains.", "key_study_ids": ["karbownik2020"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "students", "population_detail": "Healthy university/medical students facing examinations or public-speaking stress", "dose_or_intake": "L. rhamnosus HN001 capsule; multi-strain capsule (B. coagulans Unique IS2 + 5 strains, ~10e9 CFU) with glutamine twice daily; L. rhamnosus CNCM I-3690 fermented milk twice daily; heat-killed L. gasseri CP2305 tablets", "duration": "4 weeks to one semester (24 weeks for CP2305)", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Largest trial (HN001, n=483, 391 completers) found no difference in stress, anxiety or wellbeing. A manufacturer-authored Indian trial (n=80) reported lower PSS, DASS, STAI and cortisol. L. rhamnosus CNCM I-3690 blunted the rise in state anxiety (p=0.01) without changing cortisol or gut permeability. A 2-week multispecies product had no effect on psychological stress reactivity in 105 students. Heat-killed CP2305 (a paraprobiotic) reduced anxiety and sleep disturbance in 60 medical students.", "effect_estimate": "HN001: no significant difference [slykerman2022press]; CNCM I-3690: STAI increase lower, p=0.01 [wauters2022]; multi-strain: significant reductions, effect sizes not reported in abstract [venkataraman2021]; 2-week blend: no effect [moller2017]", "certainty_notes": "Large independent trial null (COVID context acknowledged), positive trials smaller and industry-funded or industry-supplied; outcomes subjective; strains not interchangeable. Low certainty.", "key_study_ids": ["slykerman2022press", "venkataraman2021", "wauters2022", "moller2017", "karbownik2020", "nishida2019"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy community volunteers without psychiatric diagnosis (subclinical depression, anxiety, perceived stress; cognitive reactivity to sad mood)", "dose_or_intake": "Varied strains and blends, roughly 1e9-1e10 CFU/day", "duration": "3-12 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Meta-analyses report small improvements in subclinical psychological symptoms (SMD 0.34 in 7 studies) and in subjective stress (7 studies, n=1146, no cortisol effect); depression effects in community samples are smaller than in clinical samples. Cognitive reactivity to sad mood (LEIDS-r) was reduced by Ecologic Barrier (n=40), GABA-producing lactobacilli (crossover n=44) and a multistrain product in older adults; a 4-strain blend showed only within-group change with no between-group difference (n=38).", "effect_estimate": "SMD 0.34 (95% CI 0.07-0.61) [mckean2017]; subjective stress reduced, subgroups NS, cortisol NS [zhang2020]; probiotics vs placebo depression d -0.24, anxiety d -0.10 (all samples) [liu2019]; LEIDS-r reduced [steenbergen2015]; no between-group difference [marotta2019]", "certainty_notes": "Pooling of different strains (indirectness), small single-centre trials, subjective outcomes susceptible to expectancy, likely publication bias; several manufacturer-linked authors. Effect size is small (about one fifth to one third of an SD).", "key_study_ids": ["mckean2017", "zhang2020", "liu2019", "steenbergen2015", "casertano2024", "marotta2019"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "knowledge_workers", "population_detail": "Healthy employed adults (office workers, nurses, healthcare workers, surgeons)", "dose_or_intake": "3e6 to >1e11 CFU/day; capsules, yoghurts, fermented milks", "duration": "2-16 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Meta-analysis of 12 workplace trials (n about 3,350) found a small reduction in combined depression/anxiety/stress symptoms and in cortisol, no CRP effect. It included open-label trials. The largest blinded workplace trial (HN001 in nurses) was null for perceived stress. In 12 office workers with sleep complaints, L. paracasei Shirota raised afternoon self-rated attention (VAS) and lowered EEG theta power; this is subjective and biomarker evidence, not measured cognition.", "effect_estimate": "SMD -0.21 (95% CI -0.34 to -0.09); cortisol SMD -0.26 (-0.45 to -0.08) [benfredj2026]; nurses: no difference [slykerman2022nurses]; attention VAS p=0.041 [kikuchihayakawa2023]", "certainty_notes": "Downgraded for risk of bias (open-label studies included), inconsistency of strains, and inconsistency with the largest blinded trial; manufacturer-employee authorship in the office-worker trial. Practical relevance for a knowledge worker: small, uncertain.", "key_study_ids": ["benfredj2026", "slykerman2022nurses", "kikuchihayakawa2023", "chong2019"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Moderately stressed adults (PSS-10 screened), mean age about 31 years, Malaysia; not a high-cognitive-workload or student sample", "dose_or_intake": "L. plantarum P8 1e10 CFU/day; L. plantarum DR7 1e9 CFU/day", "duration": "12 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "P8 (n=103) reduced DASS stress and anxiety at 4 weeks and improved some memory/cognition subtests (verbal learning, social-emotional cognition) with sex differences; DR7 (n=111) reduced stress and anxiety at 8 weeks and improved attention and associative learning only in participants over 30. Many cognitive subtests and subgroups were analysed.", "effect_estimate": "P8: DASS stress MD 2.94 (95% CI 0.08-5.73), anxiety MD 2.82 (0.35-5.30) [lew2019]; DR7: stress p=0.024, anxiety p=0.001, cognitive effect sizes not reported in abstract [chong2019]", "certainty_notes": "Same research group and strain developers for both trials; multiplicity across many CANTAB subtests and subgroups; lower confidence bounds near zero; not independently replicated. Strain-specific; not transferable.", "key_study_ids": ["lew2019", "chong2019"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_fatigue", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Adults 18-70 receiving B. longum 1714: healthy volunteers, adults with poor sleep, adults with mild-moderate depression; outcome = vitality/energy and fatigue scales", "dose_or_intake": "B. longum 1714, 1e9-1e10 CFU/day", "duration": "4-8 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "SF-36 vitality/energy improved versus placebo in the sleep trial (n=89, 8 weeks) and in the low-mood trial (n=168, week 8 p=0.042), and correlated with MEG changes in a 40-person trial, but the primary outcomes were negative or only transiently positive (PSQI global improved equally in both groups; BDI-II not different). Self-reported vitality is not a cognitive test.", "effect_estimate": "Low-mood trial BDI-II week 8 difference -1.43 (95% CI -3.30 to 0.44), p=0.132; PSQI -0.92 (-1.62 to -0.23) at week 4 only [seamans2026]; vitality p<0.05 at 8 weeks [patterson2024]", "certainty_notes": "All 1714 trials authored or funded by the strain owner; primary endpoints negative; positive secondary endpoints at isolated time points (multiplicity). Low certainty.", "key_study_ids": ["patterson2024", "seamans2026", "wang2019", "allen2016"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "sleep_related_cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Adults 18-45 with poor subjective sleep quality; medical students under exam stress", "dose_or_intake": "B. longum 1714 daily; heat-killed L. gasseri CP2305 tablet daily", "duration": "8-24 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "1714 produced a faster improvement in some PSQI components (sleep quality, daytime dysfunction) at week 4 but no difference in PSQI global at 8 weeks and no actigraphy effect. Heat-killed CP2305 improved PSQI and single-channel EEG sleep measures in 60 stressed medical students (a paraprobiotic, not a live probiotic). No trial measured next-day cognitive performance.", "effect_estimate": "PSQI component differences p<0.05 at week 4; global PSQI similar at week 8; actigraphy NS [patterson2024]", "certainty_notes": "Subjective outcomes, industry authorship, no objective sleep or cognitive effect; indirect for cognition.", "key_study_ids": ["patterson2024", "nishida2019"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "neuroplasticity_biomarkers", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults (mostly young women) undergoing fMRI, MEG or EEG before and after probiotic or fermented milk", "dose_or_intake": "Fermented milk with B. animalis subsp. lactis + yoghurt cultures twice daily; multistrain capsules; B. longum 1714 1e9 CFU/day", "duration": "4 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Changes in task-related and resting-state brain activity are reported in most imaging trials (Tillisch, Bagga, Wang, Allen) and summarised in a 19-study systematic review, but regions, directions and tasks differ, behavioural correlates are weak, and one well-designed fMRI trial found no effect without stress. These are biomarkers of altered processing, not evidence of better cognition.", "effect_estimate": "Reduced network response, 49% cross-block covariance, p=0.004 [tillisch2013]; not pooled [hutchinson2026]; fMRI null without stress [papalini2019]", "certainty_notes": "Very small groups (n=11-20 per arm), many analytic choices, industry funding (Danone for Tillisch; strain owner for 1714). Biomarker only; cannot upgrade cognitive grades.", "key_study_ids": ["tillisch2013", "bagga2018", "wang2019", "hutchinson2026", "papalini2019"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Healthy older adults, crossover, testing on the first day of supplementation", "dose_or_intake": "Multistrain probiotic (Winclove), single day", "duration": "1 day", "grade": "D", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Faster reaction times on cognitively demanding executive-function trials on day 1; described by the authors as novel and requiring replication. No acute trial in young adults exists; a 1-day effect is mechanistically unexpected for live bacteria.", "effect_estimate": "-64.91 ms (95% CI -115.70 to -14.15) [eastwood2025]", "certainty_notes": "Exploratory secondary aim, n=30, manufacturer funding and co-author; single trial. Preliminary only.", "key_study_ids": ["eastwood2025"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "alertness", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy young adults and students; single-dose or same-day effects", "dose_or_intake": "not studied", "duration": "single dose", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No randomised trial of a single dose of any probiotic on alertness, attention or memory in healthy young adults was found. The shortest trial in young adults (2 weeks) found no effect on psychological stress reactivity.", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "No direct evidence; any acute claim would be unsupported. The 2-week null trial [moller2017] is chronic by definition and is not used as acute evidence.", "key_study_ids": []}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_aging", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Community-dwelling cognitively normal adults aged about 50-80+", "dose_or_intake": "Strain-specific: B. bifidum BGN4 + B. longum BORI; B. longum BB68S 5e10 CFU; LGG; L. helveticus fermented milk 190 g/day; multistrain Winclove; probiotic milk drink", "duration": "3-12 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Positive trials: mental flexibility and stress improved with BGN4+BORI (n=63) alongside a serum BDNF rise; RBANS total rose 18.9 points with BB68S (n=60, implausibly large); L. helveticus fermented milk improved attention (between-group) in middle-aged adults; L. helveticus IDCC3801 fermented milk improved cognitive-fatigue task performance. Null or negative: LGG had no effect in cognitively intact adults (effect only in an impaired subgroup); 8 weeks of a multistrain product did not improve memory or executive function; a 3-week probiotic milk drink slightly worsened two memory measures. Meta-analysis restricted to older adults: overall cognition SMD 0.40.", "effect_estimate": "Older adults overall cognition SMD 0.40 (95% CI 0.13-0.67), memory SMD 0.51 (0.25-0.78) [guo2026]; RBANS g 0.46 (-0.50 to 1.41), NS [silvaolivares2026]", "certainty_notes": "Small single-country trials, several with implausibly large effects and within-group reporting, industry-linked strains; nulls from independent groups; heterogeneity. BDNF is a biomarker, not cognition. Low certainty.", "key_study_ids": ["kim2021", "shi2022", "sanborn2020", "eastwood2025", "benton2007", "ohsawa2018", "chung2014", "guo2026", "silvaolivares2026"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_mci", "population_detail": "Older adults with suspected or diagnosed MCI and patients with Alzheimer's disease (screening-test outcomes MMSE, MoCA, RBANS, ADAS)", "dose_or_intake": "Mostly 1e9-2e10 CFU/day; B. breve A1/MCC1274 2e10 CFU/day", "duration": "12-24 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Meta-analyses report improvements on cognitive screening tests (SMD 0.37 in 5 RCTs; MMSE MD 4.2 and MoCA MD 1.2 at 12 weeks with low GRADE certainty in 34 RCTs; MMSE g 1.02 with I2 91%). B. breve A1 improved RBANS by 11.3 points in 80 suspected-MCI participants, but the larger 24-week trial of the same strain improved only the orientation subscale. One AD meta-analysis in this area was retracted (2022).", "effect_estimate": "SMD 0.37 (95% CI 0.14-0.61), I2 24% [den2020]; MMSE MD 4.23 (2.77-5.68), MoCA MD 1.21 (0.06-2.36), low certainty [calzadagonzales2025]; MMSE g 1.02 (0.36-1.68), I2 90.84% [silvaolivares2026]; RBANS MD 11.3 (6.7-15.8) [xiao2020]", "certainty_notes": "Downgraded for risk of bias (many small trials, several from regions with data-integrity concerns in this field), inconsistency (I2 up to 91%) and imprecision; the larger replication trial was largely null; manufacturer funding for B. breve trials. This is a patient population and must not be generalised to healthy adults.", "key_study_ids": ["den2020", "calzadagonzales2025", "silvaolivares2026", "xiao2020", "asaoka2022", "lv2021", "ruizgonzalez2021"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Adults with major depressive disorder or mild-moderate depressive symptoms, mostly as adjunct to antidepressants", "dose_or_intake": "Varied strains, typically 1e9-1e10 CFU/day", "duration": "4-12 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Meta-analyses show small-to-moderate reductions in depressive symptoms, larger in psychiatric samples; CANMAT (2026) rates probiotics as a third-line adjunct with inconsistent recent high-quality trials. B. longum 1714 failed its primary BDI-II endpoint in 168 adults with mild-moderate depression.", "effect_estimate": "Psychiatric samples d -0.73 (preliminary), clinical/medical d -0.45 [liu2019]; statistically significant pooled benefit [hofmeister2021]; BDI-II -1.43 (-3.30 to 0.44) [seamans2026]", "certainty_notes": "Outside Brain Food's scope (treatment of disease); heterogeneity in strains and adjunct regimens; mixed results in recent larger trials. Any such claim would be a disease claim.", "key_study_ids": ["liu2019", "hofmeister2021", "bahji2026", "seamans2026"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "children_adolescents", "population_detail": "Infants, children and adolescents (perinatal and childhood supplementation)", "dose_or_intake": "Varied; LGG 1e10 CFU in the single positive trial", "duration": "weeks to 6 months, follow-up to 13 years", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Six of seven RCTs found no cognitive difference; one perinatal LGG trial reported fewer later ADHD/Asperger diagnoses. A later lifespan meta-analysis reports executive-function SMD 0.30 in infants and children from few trials. Not applicable to adult products.", "effect_estimate": "ADHD/AS at 13 y: 17.1% placebo vs 0% probiotic, p=0.008 (single trial) [rianda2019]; executive function SMD 0.30 (0.04-0.56) [guo2026]", "certainty_notes": "Few, small, heterogeneous trials; single positive trial with small numbers. Children are a vulnerable group and outside the adult product scope.", "key_study_ids": ["rianda2019", "guo2026"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "Multistrain probiotic (Winclove), first day of supplementation", "onset": "same day", "duration_studied": "1 day (within an 8-week crossover)", "domain": "executive function reaction time under high demand", "population": "healthy older adults (n=30)", "effect_estimate": "-64.91 ms (95% CI -115.70 to -14.15) [eastwood2025]", "certainty": "very low (single exploratory finding, needs replication)", "safety": "no safety signal reported in abstract", "study_ids": ["eastwood2025"]}, {"dose": "any strain, single dose", "onset": "not studied", "duration_studied": "none", "domain": "alertness, attention, memory", "population": "healthy young adults / students", "effect_estimate": "no trial found", "certainty": "no evidence", "safety": "not applicable", "study_ids": []}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "4-12 weeks", "population": "healthy young adults (18-40)", "outcome": "laboratory cognitive tests (memory, attention, executive function)", "effect_estimate": "no reliable effect; healthy-subgroup SMD 0.15 (-0.30 to 0.60) [lv2021]; JB-1, GABA-lactobacilli and BS01/LA02 trials null", "evidence_quality": "C (low)", "limitations": "small trials, strain heterogeneity, positive 1714 signal from non-randomised design", "study_ids": ["kelly2017", "casertano2024", "czajeczny2023", "allen2016", "papalini2019", "lv2021"]}, {"duration": "4 weeks to one semester", "population": "students under examination stress", "outcome": "perceived stress, anxiety, academic performance", "effect_estimate": "largest trial null (n=483); smaller industry trials positive; exam grades unchanged with S. boulardii", "evidence_quality": "C (low)", "limitations": "subjective outcomes, COVID-period confounding in the largest trial, strain-specific", "study_ids": ["slykerman2022press", "venkataraman2021", "wauters2022", "karbownik2020"]}, {"duration": "2-16 weeks", "population": "healthy working adults", "outcome": "combined depression/anxiety/stress symptoms; cortisol", "effect_estimate": "SMD -0.21 (-0.34 to -0.09); cortisol SMD -0.26 (-0.45 to -0.08) [benfredj2026]; nurses trial null", "evidence_quality": "C (low)", "limitations": "open-label trials pooled; heterogeneous strains and doses (3e6 to >1e11 CFU)", "study_ids": ["benfredj2026", "slykerman2022nurses"]}, {"duration": "12 weeks", "population": "moderately stressed adults (mean ~31 y)", "outcome": "DASS stress/anxiety; CANTAB memory subtests", "effect_estimate": "DASS stress MD 2.94 (0.08-5.73) with P8; selected memory subtests improved", "evidence_quality": "C (low)", "limitations": "single research group, multiplicity, subgroup-dependent cognitive effects", "study_ids": ["lew2019", "chong2019"]}, {"duration": "4-8 weeks", "population": "adults with poor sleep or low mood (B. longum 1714)", "outcome": "PSQI, BDI-II, vitality", "effect_estimate": "primary outcomes not different at study end; transient week-4 PSQI and PHQ-9 differences; vitality improved", "evidence_quality": "C (low)", "limitations": "strain-owner authorship, multiplicity of secondary endpoints", "study_ids": ["patterson2024", "seamans2026"]}, {"duration": "3-12 weeks", "population": "healthy older adults", "outcome": "cognitive tests (RBANS, CERAD, NIH Toolbox)", "effect_estimate": "inconsistent: positive Korean/Chinese trials; null LGG and Winclove trials; slight worsening of memory in a 3-week milk-drink trial; pooled SMD 0.40 [guo2026]", "evidence_quality": "C (low)", "limitations": "implausibly large effects in some trials; heterogeneity; industry strains", "study_ids": ["kim2021", "shi2022", "sanborn2020", "eastwood2025", "benton2007", "guo2026"]}, {"duration": "12-24 weeks", "population": "older adults with suspected MCI / AD", "outcome": "MMSE, MoCA, RBANS, ADAS-Jcog", "effect_estimate": "SMD 0.37 (0.14-0.61) [den2020]; MMSE MD 4.23 low certainty [calzadagonzales2025]; B. breve: RBANS +11.3 in 16 weeks but only one subscale improved in 24-week trial", "evidence_quality": "C (low)", "limitations": "patient population, small trials, high heterogeneity, manufacturer funding", "study_ids": ["den2020", "calzadagonzales2025", "xiao2020", "asaoka2022", "silvaolivares2026"]}, {"duration": "4 weeks", "population": "healthy adults, neuroimaging", "outcome": "fMRI/MEG/EEG activity and connectivity", "effect_estimate": "changes reported in most small trials; not pooled; no consistent region or direction", "evidence_quality": "C for biomarker change; not a cognitive outcome", "limitations": "very small samples, analytic flexibility, industry funding", "study_ids": ["tillisch2013", "bagga2018", "wang2019", "hutchinson2026"]}], "safety": {"summary": "For healthy, immunocompetent adults, commonly used Lactobacillus/Bifidobacterium strains have a long history of use and trials report few adverse events, mostly transient bloating, flatulence or altered bowel habit [doron2015]. However, harms are poorly reported in probiotic RCTs (98% inadequate) so 'no harm reported' is not proof of safety [bafeta2018]. Serious harms are documented in vulnerable people: bacteraemia from the ingested strain in ICU patients [yelin2019], fungaemia risk with Saccharomyces boulardii, increased mortality and bowel ischaemia with enteral probiotics in severe acute pancreatitis [besselink2008], and (FDA warning letter, 29 September 2023) fatal B. longum subsp. infantis sepsis in a preterm infant. A contested observational series linked probiotic use with D-lactic acidosis and 'brain fogginess' in patients with small-intestinal bacterial overgrowth [rao2018]. S. boulardii increased the pulse-rate response to exam stress in students [karbownik2020]. Product quality (strain identity, viable count at end of shelf life, contamination, antibiotic-resistance genes) is a material risk; whole-genome sequencing of the strain is recommended [merenstein2023].", "adverse_effects": ["transient gastrointestinal symptoms: bloating, flatulence, altered stool frequency (common, usually mild)", "bacteraemia/sepsis from the administered strain in critically ill, immunocompromised or preterm patients [yelin2019]", "fungaemia with Saccharomyces boulardii, notably via central venous catheters (documented in the literature; not separately reviewed here)", "increased mortality and bowel ischaemia in predicted severe acute pancreatitis [besselink2008]", "possible D-lactic acidosis and 'brain fog' in small-intestinal bacterial overgrowth (contested) [rao2018]", "higher pulse-rate response to stress with S. boulardii [karbownik2020]", "slightly poorer memory scores in one 3-week trial (possibly chance) [benton2007]", "theoretical: horizontal transfer of antibiotic-resistance genes; excessive immune stimulation [doron2015, merenstein2023]"], "contraindications": ["immunocompromised states (chemotherapy, transplant, high-dose immunosuppressants, advanced HIV)", "critical illness, ICU patients, central venous catheters", "severe acute pancreatitis", "preterm infants and neonates (outside specialist supervision)", "short bowel syndrome or known small-intestinal bacterial overgrowth (D-lactate risk)", "structural heart disease with risk of endocarditis (caution)", "severe allergy to product matrix (milk protein, lactose in dairy formats)"], "drug_interactions": ["antibiotics reduce viability of bacterial probiotics (separate intake by at least 2 hours; general practice guidance, not trial-tested here)", "systemic antifungals inactivate Saccharomyces boulardii", "immunosuppressants increase infection risk (contraindication rather than interaction)", "no established pharmacokinetic interactions with antidepressants; adjunctive use in depression is under study [bahji2026]"], "upper_limits": "No tolerable upper intake level exists for live microorganisms (EFSA/IOM). Trials used up to about 1e11 CFU/day without reported serious harm in healthy adults [kikuchihayakawa2023], but harms reporting is incomplete [bafeta2018].", "sleep_impact": "No adverse sleep effects reported; some strains (B. longum 1714, heat-killed L. gasseri CP2305) were studied for improving subjective sleep with weak, transient effects [patterson2024].", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Trials last 2-24 weeks; no controlled data on cognitive or safety outcomes beyond 6 months in healthy adults. Long-term food use of yoghurt cultures and traditional fermented milks is extensive but uncontrolled.", "daily_use_appropriate": "probably", "special_populations": "Pregnancy and lactation: commonly used strains considered low risk but strain-specific data limited; children: separate evidence base, not to be extrapolated; preterm infants: FDA warning (2023) after a fatal sepsis; older and frail adults: higher infection risk if hospitalised or catheterised; psychiatric patients: probiotics are not a substitute for treatment and depression/anxiety claims would be disease claims; people with SIBO, short bowel or GI surgery: avoid unless supervised.", "specialist_review_needed": ["infectious disease / clinical microbiology review of the specific strain (whole-genome sequence, antibiotic-resistance genes, QPS status)", "gastroenterologist for SIBO/short-bowel and D-lactate considerations", "food microbiology / quality assurance for viable-count stability through shelf life", "regulatory affairs for EU use of the term 'probiotic' per target member state"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "Acceptable for healthy immunocompetent adults when the strain is identified to strain level, genome-sequenced, free of transferable resistance genes and has a safe-use history; requires label warnings and exclusion of immunocompromised, critically ill, catheterised, preterm, short-bowel/SIBO and acute-pancreatitis populations, and recognition that trial harms reporting is inadequate."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "No probiotic health claim is authorised under Regulation (EC) 1924/2006; EFSA has not given a positive opinion on any probiotic-specific claim (FSAI, 'Probiotic health claims', accessed 2026-10-25, https://fsai.ie/business-advice/nutrition/probiotic-health-claims). The Commission's 2007 guidance treats the word 'probiotic' as an implied health claim (European Ombudsman document 197674; FSAI). Several member states nevertheless tolerate 'probiotic' on food supplement labels, generally as a category descriptor and never with a health claim: Spain (AESAN, October 2020), the Netherlands (March 2021), Denmark (May 2021, supplements only), France (DGCCRF, January 2023), and also Italy, Czech Republic, Poland, Greece, Bulgaria according to trade press (NutraIngredients, 9 Jan 2023 and 15 May 2024; ISAPP article on Spain; accessed 2026-10-25). This patchwork is not harmonised and should be re-checked per country before launch. Cognitive, stress or mood claims for probiotics are not authorised; any such claim on an EU product would be unlawful. The only live-culture claim on the register concerns yoghurt starter cultures and lactose digestion [efsa2010]. Strain safety for food use relies on history of use; EFSA's QPS list covers many Lactobacillus/Bifidobacterium species for its own assessments.", "us": "Probiotics are sold as conventional foods (strains require GRAS status) or dietary supplements under DSHEA (strains not marketed before 15 October 1994 require a New Dietary Ingredient notification). Structure/function claims such as 'supports a healthy response to everyday stress' are permitted for supplements with FDA notification and the DSHEA disclaimer; claims to treat or prevent depression, anxiety disorders, MCI or dementia are disease claims and prohibited. FDA's 2018 draft guidance allows voluntary CFU declaration alongside weight (FDA constituent update, accessed 2026-10-25). FTC requires competent and reliable scientific evidence for the specific strain and dose; the 2010 FTC and 39-state settlement with Dannon over Activia/DanActive claims ($21 million to states; cold/flu claims barred without FDA approval) is the leading enforcement precedent (thefdalawblog.com, FTC; accessed 2026-10-25). FDA warned providers on 29 September 2023 about probiotic use in preterm infants after a fatal sepsis case (trade-press reports; accessed 2026-10-25).", "morocco": "Uncertain. Food labelling is governed by Decree No. 2-12-389 of 22 April 2013 (Bulletin officiel no. 6152) under Law 28-07 on food safety, and food businesses need ONSSA sanitary authorisation (UNEP LEAP database; Hespress 2022; accessed 2026-10-25). Food supplements are reported to be registered through a Ministry of Health committee with ONSSA participation (Medias24, January 2023, via search summary). A separate draft decree (implementing Article 30 of Law 27.26, the medicines and pharmacy code) that would reserve certain supplements for pharmacy sale had its government adoption postponed on 22 July 2026 (Medias24 headline via search; page returned HTTP 403, not read). No Moroccan positive list of authorised health claims or specific rule on the term 'probiotic' was found; confirm with ONSSA and the Ministry of Health before any claim. Fermented dairy (yoghurt, lben, raib) is a mainstream Moroccan food category, which favours a food-based route.", "permitted_claims_examples": ["EU (Regulation (EU) No 432/2012 register entry for live yoghurt cultures, wording as quoted by FSAI, accessed 2026-10-25): 'Live cultures in yogurt improve lactose digestion of the product in individuals who have difficulty digesting lactose' - condition: yoghurt or fermented milk containing at least 10^8 CFU live starter microorganisms (Lactobacillus delbrueckii subsp. bulgaricus and Streptococcus thermophilus) per gram. Digestive, not cognitive; exact register wording ('yoghurt or fermented milk') to be re-checked on the EU register."], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "EU: species and strains with significant consumption before 15 May 1997 are not novel; new species or genetically distinct strains may need novel-food authorisation (check the EU Novel Food Catalogue per strain). Heat-killed/postbiotic preparations may also raise novel-food questions. US: NDI notification needed for supplement strains not marketed before 1994; GRAS (self-affirmed or notified) needed for use in conventional foods. Morocco: not researched beyond the general framework.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Moderate. Live cultures fit naturally in refrigerated fermented dairy (yoghurt, fermented milk, lben) and can reach 1e9-1e10 CFU per serving, but the clinically studied psychobiotic strains are proprietary and are not the standard yoghurt starter cultures; adding them requires survival data through shelf life. Ambient products (bars, chocolate, gummies, beverages) generally kill or reduce live counts unless spore-formers or microencapsulation are used.", "formats": ["refrigerated fermented milk or yoghurt with added named strain", "freeze-dried capsule or sachet (format of most trials)", "stick pack mixed into milk (Allen 2016 format)", "tablets of heat-killed (paraprobiotic) bacteria - more stable, but a different product category", "plant-based fermented beverages (viability and strain data needed)"], "stability": "Viability falls with heat, moisture, oxygen and low pH; dairy products need cold chain (approx. 4 C) and a defined end-of-shelf-life CFU count; freeze-dried capsules need moisture-barrier packaging; microencapsulation changes delivery and possibly effects. Label CFU at end of shelf life, not at manufacture.", "taste": "Neutral in capsules; fermented dairy is sour/acidic, acceptable in Moroccan and EU markets; some strains produce off-flavours or post-acidification during storage.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Dairy buffers gastric acid and may improve survival; trials used both capsules and dairy (Tillisch fermented milk [tillisch2013]; LcS and CNCM I-3690 fermented milks [kikuchihayakawa2023, wauters2022]; L. helveticus fermented milk [ohsawa2018]) but no trial compared matrices for a brain outcome. In some fermented milks the putative active is a peptide, not the bacteria [ohsawa2018].", "dose_feasibility": "Studied doses (1e9-2e10 CFU/day; up to 1e11 in LcS milk) can fit into one 100-200 g yoghurt or fermented-milk serving if the strain grows/survives in milk; a capsule easily delivers 1e10 CFU. Feasibility is a strain-specific technical question; effect is not established at any dose for cognition in healthy adults.", "cost_availability": "Generic probiotic yoghurts and supplements are widely available in the EU, US and Morocco; clinically studied strains (e.g. B. longum 1714, L. plantarum P8/DR7, B. breve MCC1274, Ecologic Barrier) are licensed by their owners, which adds cost and IP constraints.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 3, "effect_magnitude": 2, "safety": 7, "reproducibility": 2, "food_suitability": 6, "cost_access": 5, "rationale": "Evidence quality 3: in the target population (healthy young adults, students, knowledge workers) the best evidence for cognition is grade C and null, and for mood/stress grade C with a small effect; stronger signals exist only in MCI/AD and depression (C, out of scope). Effect magnitude 2: cognition effects near zero in healthy adults; mood SMD about 0.2-0.3 on questionnaires. Safety 7: well tolerated in healthy adults, but documented serious harms in vulnerable groups and poor harms reporting. Reproducibility 2: strain-specific findings, mostly single-group or sponsor-linked, large independent trials null, a translational failure (JB-1) and negative primary endpoints (1714 low mood). Food suitability 6: natural fit with fermented dairy, but proprietary strains, cold chain and viability constraints. Cost/access 5: widely available generically; studied strains are licensed; prices not researched."}, "research_gaps": ["Adequately powered (n>=200), pre-registered RCT of a single named strain on objective cognitive outcomes in healthy students or knowledge workers, ideally under realistic stress (exam period)", "Independent replication of the strain-specific positive trials (L. plantarum P8/DR7, B. longum 1714, Ecologic Barrier stress-working-memory effect)", "Head-to-head comparison of capsule versus fermented-dairy delivery of the same strain for a gut-brain outcome", "Dose-response and duration studies; most trials test a single dose for 4-12 weeks", "Responder identification (baseline microbiota, stress level, diet fibre) with pre-specified analyses", "Long-term (>=1 year) trials on cognitive ageing and dementia risk in healthy older adults; currently none", "Systematic adverse-event reporting in probiotic trials; D-lactate and SIBO monitoring in high-dose use", "Publication of registered trials without posted results (e.g. NCT04951687 later published; check others such as NCT04167475/NCT04925440 for full data) and assessment of publication bias in the mood literature", "Moroccan regulatory status of the term 'probiotic' and of supplement health claims"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 4.0, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": "D", "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": "C", "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"episodic_memory": "C", "cognitive_fatigue": "C", "executive_function": "D", "global_cognition": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 16, "n_studies": 55, "n_verified": 55, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 16, "n_systematic_reviews": 17, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["inconsistent", "insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "avocado", "name": "Avocado", "category": "J", "category_name": "Whole foods", "aliases": ["avocado", "Persea americana", "Hass avocado", "avocat (French)", "guacamole", "avocado smoothie / 'jus d'avocat' (Moroccan juice-bar drink)"], "food_sources": ["fresh avocado pulp (Hass and green-skin cultivars)", "guacamole and avocado spreads (often high-pressure processed)", "avocado-milk smoothies", "avocado oil (mostly fat; loses fibre, folate, potassium)"], "active_constituents": ["monounsaturated fat (about 71% of fatty acids, mainly oleic acid; ~6.7 g per half fruit)", "dietary fibre (~4.6 g per half fruit, 68 g)", "potassium (~345 mg per half fruit)", "folate (~60 microg per half fruit; the source abstract misprints mg)", "lutein and zeaxanthin (~185 microg per half fruit)", "vitamin E, vitamin K1, vitamin B6, phytosterols"], "one_line_verdict": "Avocado is a nutritious, weight-neutral fat-and-fibre fruit that boosts carotenoid absorption from accompanying vegetables, but the largest and best-conducted trial (one avocado a day for 6 months, n=241) found no cognitive benefit in adults of any age, and the earlier positive signals came from small Hass Avocado Board-funded studies; it should not be sold as brain food.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. Half a Hass avocado (68 g, 114 kcal) supplies about 6.7 g MUFA, 4.6 g fibre, 345 mg potassium, about 60 microg folate and 185 microg lutein/zeaxanthin [dreher2013], and its fat strongly enhances absorption of carotenoids eaten with it: adding 150 g avocado to a salad multiplied lutein absorption 5.1-fold and beta-carotene 15.3-fold, as did 24 g avocado oil [unlu2005]. Cognitive evidence comes from three trials and one survey, all funded by or authored with the Hass Avocado Board (HAB). The largest and best-conducted, an ancillary study of the multicentre Habitual Diet and Avocado Trial, randomised 251 adults with central obesity (25-84 y, mean 49.6; 65 aged 25-39) to one avocado per day or avoidance for 6 months: compliance exceeded 97%, and there were no group-by-time effects on memory, processing speed, executive function, simple reaction time or working-memory reaction time, no age interaction, and all partial eta-squared values were <=0.016 [lee2026hat]. The parent trial (n=1,008) found no change in visceral fat and only nominal LDL reductions [lichtenstein2022]. Earlier, smaller trials looked positive in their abstracts. Twelve weeks of a daily avocado meal improved Flanker accuracy in 84 adults aged 25-45 with overweight or obesity, independent of serum lutein, with no MPOD change [edwards2020]. In 40 healthy adults over 50, one avocado/day for 6 months raised serum lutein by 25% and macular pigment density within the group, but the full text shows no between-group or group-by-time differences in any CANTAB test; the 'improved sustained attention' was a within-group change [scott2017]. A cross-sectional NHANES analysis by HAB employees reported 0.11-0.15 higher z-scores among avocado consumers aged 60+ [cheng2021]. Food-level lutein (0.5-1.8 mg/day) raises serum lutein but MPOD inconsistently [scott2017, edwards2020, olmedillaalonso2021], and MPOD-cognition links are mainly correlational [garciaromera2022]: a biomarker chain, not proof of effect. Cardiometabolic: avocado modestly lowers total cholesterol (-5.1 mg/dL, low certainty; LDL only in hypercholesterolaemia) without weight gain [jamesmartin2024], and >=2 servings/week was associated with 16% lower CVD risk in US cohorts [pacheco2022]. An editorial by an independent PREDIMED investigator acknowledges the 6-month trial was well conducted and null [ros2026]. Target population: no trial in students; the only young-adult signal (one task, n=84) is contradicted by the larger null trial; no acute cognitive study exists. Conclusion: a good food for diet quality and carotenoid absorption, not a cognitive enhancer.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Avocado does not correct a specific deficiency in typical Moroccan, EU or US diets, although it contributes fibre (where intake is low), potassium, folate and vitamin B6, and its fat improves absorption of lutein and provitamin-A carotenoids from vegetables eaten at the same meal [unlu2005], which could matter for people with low vegetable or carotenoid status. Scott 2017 deliberately recruited low lutein consumers, yet still found no between-group cognitive effect [scott2017]. For enhancement in healthy, adequately nourished adults the best evidence is null across ages 25-84 [lee2026hat]; the positive Flanker result in overweight 25-45-year-olds [edwards2020] has not been replicated. No study has tested deficient groups, students or children, and none should be inferred.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Avocado is widely promoted as a 'brain food' because of lutein, MUFA and folate, and the Hass Avocado Board funded the trials and the NHANES analysis that underpin this message. The abstracts of Scott 2017 and Edwards 2020 emphasise improvements, but in Scott 2017 there were no between-group differences, and the larger HAB-funded HAT ancillary trial found no effect at all. Marketing statements such as 'avocados sharpen focus' or 'boost memory' are not supported. In the EU, nutrient-based claims (e.g. potassium and the nervous system, folate or vitamin B6 and psychological function, 'high fibre') are possible if a serving meets 'source of' conditions, but they describe the nutrient, not the avocado, and cannot be framed as cognitive enhancement. In the US there is no avocado qualified health claim. The honest positioning is 'a satisfying, fibre-rich healthy fat that helps you absorb carotenoids from your salad'.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Avocado's proposed brain mechanisms are indirect: (1) lutein delivered in a fat matrix raises serum lutein and possibly macular/brain lutein, which correlates with cognition in observational studies; (2) MUFA and fibre improve lipid profiles and vascular health; (3) fibre alters gut microbiota; (4) folate, B6 and potassium support normal nervous-system function at adequate intake. Absorption effects are well established; the step from lutein status or lipids to measurable cognition has not been demonstrated in avocado trials.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Avocado ingestion -> absorption of MUFA, lutein and co-consumed carotenoids in chylomicrons", "detail": "150 g avocado or 24 g avocado oil raised lutein absorption 5.1-fold and beta-carotene 15.3-fold from salad in healthy adults [unlu2005].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Increased serum lutein -> macular pigment optical density (MPOD), a proxy of brain lutein", "detail": "Serum lutein +25% and within-group MPD rise over 6 months [scott2017]; serum lutein rose but MPOD did not change after 12 weeks [edwards2020] or 4 weeks of lutein-rich fruit [olmedillaalonso2021].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Brain lutein -> neural efficiency / membrane antioxidant protection", "detail": "MPOD correlates with memory, processing speed and attention in mostly observational studies [garciaromera2022]; causal direction unproven.", "evidence_level": "plausible"}, {"step": "MUFA and fibre -> lower total/LDL cholesterol, vascular function -> cerebrovascular protection", "detail": "Total cholesterol -5.1 mg/dL (low certainty) [jamesmartin2024]; HAT: no effect on visceral fat, nominal LDL reduction [lichtenstein2022]; cohort CVD HR 0.84 [pacheco2022].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Fibre -> gut microbiota and microbial metabolites -> gut-brain signalling", "detail": "Avocado changes gut bacteria and metabolites in RCTs (not reviewed in detail here); any brain effect is speculative.", "evidence_level": "speculative"}, {"step": "Measured human outcome", "detail": "No cognitive effect over 6 months in 241 adults [lee2026hat]; one positive single-task result in 84 overweight adults [edwards2020]; no between-group effect in 40 older adults [scott2017].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["carotenoid (lutein) bioavailability enhancement", "macular/brain lutein status", "MUFA-driven lipid and vascular effects", "fibre-microbiota interactions", "folate, B6, potassium adequacy"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Adults 25-84 y (mean 49.6) with elevated waist circumference, Loma Linda site of HAT; 70% women", "dose_or_intake": "One avocado per day (large Hass) vs avoiding avocados, no other dietary guidance", "duration": "6 months", "grade": "B", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "No group-by-time effect on memory, processing speed, executive function, simple RT or working-memory RT; working-memory RT improved over time in both groups; no age interaction.", "effect_estimate": "All non-significant partial eta-squared <=0.016; e.g. time x group F=0.707 (memory), 0.091 (executive), 3.157 (working-memory RT) [lee2026hat]", "certainty_notes": "Adequately powered (80% for f=0.18), objective tests, >97% compliance, low attrition; downgraded once for single trial, open-label design and indirectness (central obesity only); HAB-funded but null. Moderate certainty of no meaningful effect.", "key_study_ids": ["lee2026hat", "lichtenstein2022"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Adults 25-45 y with overweight/obesity but otherwise healthy (Edwards 2020); 65 HAT participants aged 25-39", "dose_or_intake": "Daily meal with fresh Hass avocado (amount not in abstract) vs isocaloric meal; one avocado/day vs avoidance", "duration": "12 weeks; 6 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Edwards: improved Flanker accuracy (attentional inhibition) and serum lutein, but no change in other tasks' neuroelectric indices or MPOD and no relation to lutein change. HAT: no effect on executive function and no age interaction including the youngest group.", "effect_estimate": "Flanker accuracy improved (effect size not in abstract) [edwards2020]; executive function time x group F=0.091, NS [lee2026hat]", "certainty_notes": "One small positive single-task result vs a larger null trial; both HAB-funded; overweight samples. Inconsistent, imprecise. Not applicable to normal-weight students without new data.", "key_study_ids": ["edwards2020", "lee2026hat"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Healthy adults >50 y (mean ~63) with low lutein intake; NHANES adults >=60 y", "dose_or_intake": "One medium avocado/day (~135 g, 0.5 mg lutein) vs potato or chickpeas; any avocado/guacamole on recall days", "duration": "6 months; cross-sectional", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Scott: both groups improved on memory and spatial working memory over time; no group or group-by-time effects on any CANTAB measure. NHANES: consumers had slightly higher recall and global z-scores. HAT 60-84 subgroup: no age interaction.", "effect_estimate": "No group x time interactions [scott2017]; adjusted z +0.15 (recall), +0.11 (global) cross-sectional [cheng2021]", "certainty_notes": "Trial small (n=40) and underpowered; survey cross-sectional with HAB-employed authors and residual confounding. Trial evidence shows no benefit; observational association does not establish one.", "key_study_ids": ["scott2017", "cheng2021", "lee2026hat"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "other", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Lutein-status biomarkers (serum lutein, macular pigment optical density) in healthy and overweight adults", "dose_or_intake": "0.5 mg/day lutein from one avocado; 1.8 mg/day lutein from fruit including avocado", "duration": "4 weeks to 6 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Serum lutein rises consistently; MPOD rose within the avocado group in one 6-month trial but not in 12-week or 4-week interventions; MPOD links to cognition are observational.", "effect_estimate": "Serum lutein +25% (p=0.001), MPD 0.393 to 0.494 within group [scott2017]; MPOD no change [edwards2020, olmedillaalonso2021]", "certainty_notes": "Biomarker outcomes; small samples; must not be presented as cognitive benefit.", "key_study_ids": ["scott2017", "edwards2020", "olmedillaalonso2021", "garciaromera2022"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "vascular_health", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy, at-risk and hypercholesterolaemic adults (RCTs >=3 weeks); US health professionals (cohorts)", "dose_or_intake": "About 1 avocado/day in trials; >=2 servings/week in cohorts", "duration": "3 weeks to 6 months (RCTs); 30 years (cohorts)", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Small reduction in total cholesterol overall and LDL in hypercholesterolaemic subgroups; no adverse effect on weight; large trial null for visceral fat and metabolic syndrome components; cohorts associate avocado with lower CVD.", "effect_estimate": "TC -5.08 mg/dL (-9.29 to -0.87) [jamesmartin2024]; VAT difference 0.017 L (-0.024 to 0.058) [lichtenstein2022]; CVD HR 0.84 (0.75-0.95) [pacheco2022]", "certainty_notes": "Low to very low certainty per GRADE in the SR; cohorts observational; vascular surrogate, not cognition.", "key_study_ids": ["jamesmartin2024", "lichtenstein2022", "pacheco2022"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Single avocado-containing meal and same-day cognition", "dose_or_intake": "Not studied", "duration": "Single meal", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No acute cognitive trial of avocado was identified; acute avocado meal trials measured satiety, glycaemia and vascular reactivity only.", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "Absence of evidence.", "key_study_ids": []}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "learning", "population": "students", "population_detail": "University students; academic or learning outcomes", "dose_or_intake": "Not studied", "duration": "Not studied", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No study of avocado and learning or academic performance in students was found.", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "Absence of evidence; the closest data (overweight 25-45 y adults) are inconsistent.", "key_study_ids": []}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "150 g avocado or 24 g avocado oil added to a salad", "onset": "Postprandial (9.5 h AUC)", "duration_studied": "9.5 h", "domain": "carotenoid absorption (not cognition)", "population": "Healthy adults (n=11 per study)", "effect_estimate": "Lutein AUC 5.1x, beta-carotene 15.3x vs avocado-free salad", "certainty": "Moderate for absorption; no cognitive endpoint", "safety": "No issues reported", "study_ids": ["unlu2005"]}, {"dose": "Any avocado-containing meal", "onset": "not studied", "duration_studied": "not studied", "domain": "any cognitive domain", "population": "Healthy young adults / students", "effect_estimate": "No acute cognitive trial identified", "certainty": "No evidence", "safety": "Food-level; latex-fruit allergy", "study_ids": []}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "6 months", "population": "251 randomised (241 completed) adults 25-84 y with central obesity", "outcome": "Memory, processing speed, executive function, simple RT, working-memory RT", "effect_estimate": "No group x time effect in any domain; partial eta-squared <=0.016", "evidence_quality": "Moderate", "limitations": "Open-label; one site; obese/overweight only", "study_ids": ["lee2026hat"]}, {"duration": "12 weeks", "population": "84 adults 25-45 y with overweight/obesity", "outcome": "Flanker, Oddball, Nogo tasks with EEG; serum lutein, MPOD", "effect_estimate": "Flanker accuracy improved; serum lutein increased; MPOD and EEG unchanged", "evidence_quality": "Low", "limitations": "Matching design; small; single positive task; HAB-funded", "study_ids": ["edwards2020"]}, {"duration": "6 months", "population": "40 healthy adults >50 y, low lutein intake", "outcome": "CANTAB cognition, serum lutein, MPD", "effect_estimate": "No between-group cognitive effect; serum lutein +25%; MPD rose within avocado group", "evidence_quality": "Low", "limitations": "Small; within-group emphasis in abstract; HAB-funded", "study_ids": ["scott2017"]}, {"duration": "Cross-sectional", "population": "2,886 NHANES adults >=60 y", "outcome": "CERAD recall, animal fluency, DSST, global z", "effect_estimate": "Adjusted z +0.15 (immediate and delayed recall), +0.11 (global)", "evidence_quality": "Very low", "limitations": "Cross-sectional; binary exposure from 2 recalls; HAB-employed authors", "study_ids": ["cheng2021"]}, {"duration": "6 months", "population": "1,008 adults with elevated waist circumference (HAT)", "outcome": "Visceral adipose tissue; cardiometabolic risk factors", "effect_estimate": "VAT difference 0.017 L (-0.024 to 0.058); nominal LDL and total cholesterol reductions; weight unchanged", "evidence_quality": "Moderate", "limitations": "Free-living, no blinding", "study_ids": ["lichtenstein2022"]}, {"duration": "30 years", "population": "110,487 US adults (NHS, HPFS)", "outcome": "Incident cardiovascular disease", "effect_estimate": "HR 0.84 (0.75-0.95) for >=2 servings/week vs none", "evidence_quality": "Low (observational)", "limitations": "Low avocado intake overall; confounding", "study_ids": ["pacheco2022"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Avocado is a safe everyday fruit. Specific concerns are latex-fruit syndrome (30-50% of latex-allergic people react to some fresh fruits including avocado) [wagner2002], case-level reports of reduced warfarin effect [blickstein1991, norwood2015], its energy density (114 kcal per 68 g half, so roughly 230-330 kcal per whole fruit depending on size; weight-neutral when replacing other foods in HAT) [lichtenstein2022], and polyol (sorbitol) content that can trigger symptoms in people with irritable bowel syndrome (not reviewed here). Pesticide exposure concerns in the literature relate to farmworkers, not consumers.", "adverse_effects": ["Allergic reactions, including in latex-fruit syndrome", "Possible reduced INR in warfarin users (case reports)", "GI symptoms in IBS-prone people with large portions (polyols; not reviewed)", "Excess energy if added on top of usual intake"], "contraindications": ["Avocado allergy; natural rubber latex allergy with known fruit cross-reactivity"], "drug_interactions": ["Warfarin (possible antagonism; case-level evidence) - keep intake consistent and monitor INR"], "upper_limits": "No upper limit; studied intake one avocado/day for 6 months [lee2026hat, lichtenstein2022].", "sleep_impact": "None known.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "6 months in RCTs; 30 years in cohorts.", "daily_use_appropriate": "yes", "special_populations": "Suitable in pregnancy and childhood as a food; anticoagulated patients should keep intake stable; latex-allergic people should be warned. No cognitive claims are justified for any group.", "specialist_review_needed": ["Allergist for latex-fruit cross-reactivity labelling", "Clinical pharmacist for warfarin users' guidance", "Food technologist for browning/HPP and Listeria control in ready-to-eat guacamole"], "hard_safety_gate": "pass", "gate_reason": "Conventional fruit with no safety signal at one-a-day intakes for 6 months; allergy and warfarin cautions are manageable by labelling."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Conventional fruit, not novel (avocado seed or leaf extracts may differ; not checked). No avocado-specific authorised claim. Nutrient claims are possible if a serving meets conditions in Reg. (EC) 1924/2006 and Reg. (EU) 432/2012 (wording verified on legislation.gov.uk copy, accessed 2026-10-25): e.g. 'Potassium contributes to normal functioning of the nervous system', 'Folate contributes to normal psychological function', 'Vitamin B6 contributes to normal psychological function', each only for food that is at least a source of the nutrient. From Dreher 2013 values per 68 g, avocado provides about 507 mg potassium/100 g (source threshold 300 mg/100 g), about 88 microg folate/100 g (threshold 30), about 0.29 mg B6/100 g (threshold 0.21) and about 6.8 g fibre/100 g ('high fibre' >=6 g/100 g) - agent calculations to be confirmed by product analysis. Avocado-soybean unsaponifiables are a separate (medicinal) product category.", "us": "Conventional food; FDA's list of qualified health claims (accessed 2026-10-25) contains no avocado claim. Structure/function language on foods must derive from nutritive value and be substantiated (FTC). Whether avocado products meet FDA's updated 'healthy' nutrient-content claim criteria was not checked here.", "morocco": "Conventional fruit under Law 28-07 (ONSSA); labelling Decree 2-12-389; nutrition claims per Joint Order 281-16; no authorised health-claim list (X6 unit). Morocco is an export-oriented producer: about 90,000 t production targeted for 2024/25 with ~10% for the local market and farm-gate prices of 19-20 MAD/kg (Hespress, 1 Feb 2025); 2025/26 production fell below 70,000 t with exports above 50,000 t after heat (Chergui), storms and port closures (FreshPlaza, 13 Apr 2026); Gharb/Loukkos and Rabat-Sale-Kenitra are main zones; water use is contested. Status of any brain claim: not permitted in practice.", "permitted_claims_examples": ["EU (Reg. (EU) 432/2012): 'Potassium contributes to normal functioning of the nervous system' - only for food that is at least a source of potassium (a nutrient claim, not evidence about avocado).", "EU (Reg. (EU) 432/2012): 'Folate contributes to normal psychological function' - only for food that is at least a source of folate."], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "None for avocado pulp or guacamole. Avocado seed powders or extracts, if marketed, may require EU novel-food assessment (not checked).", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Good: the studied dose (one avocado/day, about 135-200 g) is a realistic serving in salads, toast, bowls and the popular Moroccan avocado-milk smoothie, and half an avocado already supplies meaningful fibre, potassium and folate. Best use in Brain Food is as a healthy-fat component that improves carotenoid uptake from vegetables, not as a stand-alone 'brain' product.", "formats": ["fresh whole fruit (retail)", "high-pressure-processed guacamole or avocado spread", "avocado-milk or avocado-yogurt smoothie (traditional Moroccan juice-bar format; watch added sugar)", "salad and grain bowls combining avocado with leafy greens, carrots and tomatoes (carotenoid synergy)", "frozen avocado chunks for smoothies"], "stability": "Highly perishable after cutting: enzymatic browning (polyphenol oxidase) within hours; ripeness window short; ready-to-eat products need HPP, acidification, modified atmosphere and Listeria control; fat can oxidise in processed forms.", "taste": "Mild, buttery, widely liked; texture sensitive to ripeness; pairs with sweet (dates, milk) and savoury applications.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Lipid-rich matrix delivers its own lutein efficiently and multiplies carotenoid absorption from co-eaten vegetables (lutein 5.1x, beta-carotene 15.3x) [unlu2005]; avocado oil gives the absorption effect but lacks fibre and most water-soluble nutrients.", "dose_feasibility": "Fully feasible at food level (one avocado/day in trials). Lutein delivered (~0.5 mg/day) is far below supplement trial doses (10-20 mg/day), so avocado cannot replicate lutein-supplement studies.", "cost_availability": "Imported in most of the EU (Spain and Portugal produce some) and the US (mostly Mexico); in Morocco locally produced but ~90% exported, supply volatile with climate and water constraints.", "cost_evidence": "Moroccan farm-gate price 19-20 MAD/kg in early 2025 (Hespress, accessed 2026-10-25), i.e. roughly 2.6-2.7 MAD per 135 g portion at farm gate; retail prices in Morocco, EU and US not researched here."}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 4, "effect_magnitude": 1, "safety": 9, "reproducibility": 4, "food_suitability": 7, "cost_access": 5, "rationale": "Evidence quality 4: one adequately powered RCT gives moderate (B) certainty of no cognitive benefit in adults with central obesity; other rows are C (small trials, cross-sectional survey) or D (no studies in students or acute settings). Effect magnitude 1: the best trial shows effects near zero (partial eta-squared <=0.016); the only positive result is one task in one small trial. Safety 9: whole fruit with 6-month trial exposure; deductions for latex-fruit allergy and warfarin case reports. Reproducibility 4: the null result comes from the largest trial and is consistent with Scott 2017's between-group data; positive findings were not replicated. Food suitability 7: realistic serving and familiar Moroccan smoothie format, but highly perishable and browning-prone in processed products. Cost/access 5: relatively expensive per serving in EU/US, export-oriented and climate-volatile in Morocco, with water-use concerns."}, "research_gaps": ["RCT of avocado (vs isoenergetic fat source) on cognition in normal-weight healthy young adults and students", "Acute trials of avocado-containing breakfasts on attention and postprandial cognitive performance", "Trials combining avocado with carotenoid-rich vegetables to test whether enhanced lutein absorption translates to MPOD and cognition over >=6 months", "Prospective cohort data linking avocado intake to cognitive decline or dementia incidence (none identified)", "Independent (non-HAB) replication of Edwards 2020", "Results of registered trials: AvoCog NCT06137339 (older adults, status unknown) and NCT05353790 (avocado-mango exchanges in prediabetes listing cognitive change as a condition; an endothelial-function report appeared in JAHA 2026, PMID 41698896, title only seen; cognitive results not found)", "Water footprint and sustainability assessment of Moroccan avocado supply"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 4.8, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "pass", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": null, "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": "C", "best_grade_overall": null, "benefit_domains": {}, "n_outcome_rows": 7, "n_studies": 15, "n_verified": 15, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 3, "n_systematic_reviews": 1, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["inconsistent", "insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "berries-whole-food", "name": "Berries as whole foods (servings, formats, acceptability)", "category": "J", "category_name": "Whole foods", "aliases": ["berries", "blueberries (highbush and wild/lowbush)", "strawberries", "raspberries", "blackberries", "mixed berries", "frozen berries", "freeze-dried berry powder", "berry smoothie", "red fruits (fruits rouges)"], "food_sources": ["fresh blueberries (cultivated): about 57 kcal, 9.96 g total sugars, 2.4 g fibre and 9.7 mg vitamin C per 100 g (USDA FoodData Central SR Legacy 171711, accessed 2026-10-25)", "fresh strawberries: about 32 kcal, 4.89 g total sugars, 2.0 g fibre and 58.8 mg vitamin C per 100 g (USDA FoodData Central SR Legacy 167762, accessed 2026-10-25)", "frozen unsweetened berries (single or mixed), the format used for the mixed-berry smoothie trial [whyte2019]", "freeze-dried whole-fruit powders (20-26 g/day in trials, about 1-2 cups of fresh fruit) [miller2018, miller2021, wood2023, guardino2026]", "berry beverages and juices (higher free-sugar load, larger anthocyanin losses) [nilsson2017, krikorian2010, howard2012]", "Moroccan production: fresh blueberries (season about October-June), raspberries (September-June) and strawberries (November-March) for export (HortiDaily, 9 Oct 2025, citing Morocco World News; accessed 2026-10-25)"], "active_constituents": ["anthocyanins (delphinidin, cyanidin, malvidin, petunidin, peonidin glycosides in blueberry; pelargonidin glycosides in strawberry); concentrations range from 0.7 to 1480 mg/100 g across foods [wu2006]", "proanthocyanidins and flavan-3-ols", "flavonols (quercetin, myricetin)", "phenolic acids (chlorogenic acid) and their gut-microbial metabolites (hippuric, hydroxybenzoic and hydroxycinnamic acids)", "vitamin C (high in strawberries, modest in blueberries)", "dietary fibre and intrinsic sugars (fructose, glucose)"], "one_line_verdict": "Berries are a cheap, safe, well-liked food whose trial doses (about one cup fresh, or 20-26 g freeze-dried powder) are realistic, but their cognitive benefit is limited to small, inconsistent, often industry-funded trials in older adults and mixed acute results in young adults, with no chronic trial in students; a reliable vascular effect and good food fit, not proven brain enhancement, are what a whole-berry product can honestly offer.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. The doses in berry trials are realistic portions: 24 g/day of freeze-dried blueberry equals about one cup (148 g) of fresh fruit [miller2018], 24 g of freeze-dried strawberry about two cups [miller2021], 26 g of freeze-dried wild blueberry about 178 g fresh and 302 mg anthocyanins [wood2023], and a 300 g mixed-berry smoothie about 255 mg anthocyanidins by database calculation [whyte2019]. These doses are about 20-25 times the estimated mean US anthocyanin intake of 12.5 mg/day [wu2006], so the trials test a deliberately high-berry diet. The most consistent effect is vascular: flow-mediated dilation rises by about 0.9-1.1 percentage points [wood2023, he2026]. Cognition in young adults. The evidence is acute and mixed. A single-blind trial of a 300 g mixed-berry smoothie (n=40), funded via the British Summer Fruits Association, found maintained attention accuracy and faster responses over 6 h versus a sugar-matched drink [whyte2019]. Blueberry drinks raised positive affect at 2 h in 21 young adults [khalid2017] and, with an executive-function gain, in 60 emerging adults with depressive symptoms [velichkov2024]. A double-blind dose-ranging crossover (150-450 mg anthocyanins, n=24, mean age 28) found no effect on memory or attention despite lower post-meal glucose [ellis2026]. No chronic whole-berry trial with a cognitive primary outcome exists in healthy young adults or students. The only chronic trial in emerging adults found that depressive symptoms fell more on placebo, by 5.8 BDI-II points [velichkov2024]. Older adults. In 90-day freeze-dried blueberry and strawberry trials (n=37 each), isolated test scores improved [miller2018, miller2021]. A 12-week wild blueberry trial improved immediate recall and task-switch accuracy [wood2023], and a 5-week mixed-berry drink improved one working-memory test [nilsson2017]. A 24-week freeze-dried blueberry trial found no cognitive or biomarker effect [guardino2026], and meta-analyses found no reliable cognitive effect [wang2023, he2026]. Vote-counting reviews are more positive [bonyadi2022, deamicis2022]. Cohorts link higher berry intake with slower cognitive decline [devore2012], lower odds of subjective decline [yeh2021], and lower dementia risk [agarwal2019, jennings2024, lyu2024]. These associations are confounded by healthy-user effects, and some of the authors report blueberry-industry funding. Food realities. Heat destroys 28-59% of anthocyanins, and 6 months of ambient storage destroys a further 62-85% [brownmiller2008]. Juices lose the most [howard2012]. Frozen berries carry recurrent norovirus and hepatitis A risk [bernard2014, enkirch2018], and viral prevalence is higher in berries from African countries [miotti2024]. Unknown: whether a daily berry habit changes cognition in students or knowledge workers.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "There is no deficiency state for anthocyanins or berries, so every cognitive trial tests enhancement or protection against decline, not deficiency correction. Low habitual intake is the norm (US mean about 12.5 mg anthocyanins/day [wu2006]), and trial doses are 20-25 times higher, so even 'adequately nourished' participants are being given a pharmacological-scale polyphenol load from food. The groups that respond are older adults with memory complaints or midlife insulin resistance [krikorian2010, krikorian2023]. In those groups, raised vascular or metabolic risk may play the role that deficiency plays for nutrients. Those findings must not be transferred to healthy young adults, in whom acute results are mixed [whyte2019, ellis2026] and chronic data are absent. Strawberries do correct low vitamin C intake: 150 g provides about 88 mg, above the 80 mg EU reference value (USDA composition). This is genuine nutrient adequacy, but it says nothing about cognition in people already replete in vitamin C. Child trials (single blueberry drinks in 7-10-year-olds) are covered in the blueberry-anthocyanins monograph and are not used here.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Claims such as 'brain berries', 'blueberries boost memory' or 'superfruit for focus' rest on small trials in older adults, on cohorts that cannot separate berries from healthy lifestyles, and on animal work. Several trials and cohort authors are funded by, or have links to, the British Summer Fruits Association, the California Strawberry Commission or the US Highbush Blueberry Council [whyte2019, krikorian2023, jennings2024, lyu2024]. The blueberry-anthocyanins monograph documents further industry links. The evidence supports narrower, honest statements: berries are a nutritious, low-energy fruit; strawberries are high in vitamin C, which carries authorised EU nutrient-function claims; a cup a day fits the MIND-diet pattern; and berry polyphenols improve a blood-vessel function marker in trials. No EU, US or Moroccan authority has authorised a cognitive or brain claim for berries or anthocyanins. The strongest honest product is a convenient, unsweetened, verified-dose whole-berry format, such as frozen or freeze-dried fruit, rather than a 'nootropic' extract, and it should be sold on taste, convenience and diet quality, not memory.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Berry anthocyanins are poorly absorbed intact; most of the dose reaches the colon and is converted to phenolic-acid metabolites that peak in plasma 1-2 h and about 6 h after a meal. Proposed brain effects run through improved endothelial nitric-oxide function and cerebral perfusion, blunting of post-meal glucose, and (in animals) anti-inflammatory and synaptic-signalling actions. In humans only the vascular step (FMD) and the post-meal glucose step are reasonably shown; cerebral blood flow findings are inconsistent and cognitive outcomes are small and inconsistent. Whole fruit adds fibre and vitamin C, and many placebos did not match fibre, which confounds attribution to anthocyanins.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Whole berry (fresh, frozen, freeze-dried) -> anthocyanin dose retained only if heat, storage and juicing losses are limited", "detail": "Thermal processing destroyed 28-59% and 6 months at 25 C a further 62-85% of blueberry anthocyanins [brownmiller2008]; juicing removes skins and seeds and gives the largest losses [howard2012]; anthocyanin content varies from 0.7 to 1480 mg/100 g across foods [wu2006].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Ingestion -> low absorption of parent anthocyanins -> gut-microbial phenolic metabolites in plasma and urine", "detail": "Urinary (poly)phenol excretion rose with 26 g/day freeze-dried wild blueberry [wood2023]; metabolite kinetics are described in the blueberry-anthocyanins monograph.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Metabolites -> endothelial function (FMD) and blood pressure", "detail": "FMD +0.86 percentage points (95% CI 0.56-1.17) after 12 weeks [wood2023]; pooled FMD +1.11% (0.50-1.72) without blood-pressure change [he2026].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Berry polyphenols with a carbohydrate load -> flatter post-meal glucose and insulin", "detail": "Dose-dependent lowering of glucose and insulin at 300 mg anthocyanins or more in young adults [ellis2026]; extended, lower-peak glucose curve versus a sugar-matched control [bell2017]. A biomarker, not cognition.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Vascular and glycaemic changes -> cerebral perfusion and neuronal signalling (anti-inflammatory, BDNF/ERK signalling)", "detail": "Cerebral blood flow unchanged in the 12-week wild blueberry trial [wood2023]; no change in BDNF or NGF receptor in a 12-week red-berry/cocoa trial [garciacordero2021]; anti-inflammatory and synaptic effects mainly from animal studies.", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Measured human cognitive outcome", "detail": "Small, inconsistent improvements on single tasks in older adults [miller2018, miller2021, wood2023, nilsson2017]; null 24-week trial [guardino2026]; mixed acute results in young adults [whyte2019, ellis2026]; no pooled cognitive effect [wang2023, he2026].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["endothelial nitric oxide / flow-mediated dilation", "post-prandial glucose and incretin (GLP-1, PYY, GIP) modulation", "gut-microbial conversion of anthocyanins to phenolic acids", "anti-inflammatory and synaptic signalling (animal data)", "vitamin C supply (strawberries)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults aged 18-35 (means 20-28 y), not selected for complaints; one sample of emerging adults with depressive symptoms reported separately below", "dose_or_intake": "300 g mixed fresh/frozen berries as a 400 mL smoothie (about 255 mg anthocyanidins, database estimate) or freeze-dried wild blueberry drinks giving 150-450 mg anthocyanins", "duration": "single dose, testing 1.5-6 h after intake", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Mixed-berry smoothie maintained attention-task accuracy at 6 h and gave faster responses at 2 and 4 h versus a sugar-matched placebo (n=40, single-blind, between-subjects). A double-blind dose-ranging crossover (n=24) found no effect of 150-450 mg anthocyanins on verbal learning, Corsi span or RVIP at 90 min. A review of 6 young-adult studies reported positive and null findings across domains.", "effect_estimate": "Not reported as standardised effects; many post hoc comparisons (e.g. accuracy at 6 h p=0.003) [whyte2019]; no significant intervention effect at any dose [ellis2026]", "certainty_notes": "Downgraded for risk of bias (single-blind, industry-sponsored smoothie trial, fibre not matched in placebo), inconsistency (double-blind null trial) and imprecision (n 24-40). Practical relevance for a student is unclear: effects were small changes in response speed on attention tasks.", "key_study_ids": ["whyte2019", "ellis2026", "deamicis2022"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy young adults aged 18-21 (n=21, crossover)", "dose_or_intake": "Flavonoid-rich blueberry drink (dose not stated in abstract)", "duration": "single dose, mood at 2 h", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Positive affect (PANAS) rose 2 h after a blueberry drink versus matched placebo; negative affect was unchanged. The same direction was seen in a separate child sample (not used here).", "effect_estimate": "Significant drink x session interaction, effect size not reported in abstract [khalid2017]", "certainty_notes": "One small crossover from a single laboratory; self-rated mood is subjective and is not cognition. Downgraded for imprecision; the only other young-adult sample (with depressive symptoms) is graded separately below.", "key_study_ids": ["khalid2017"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "other", "population_detail": "Emerging adults (mean 20 y) with moderate-to-severe self-reported depressive symptoms (n=60)", "dose_or_intake": "Single wild blueberry drink (dose not stated in abstract)", "duration": "single dose, testing at 2 h", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Positive affect and executive-function accuracy improved at 2 h versus placebo, and the two changes were correlated (r=0.42). The benefit did not carry over to the 6-week phase (see chronic row).", "effect_estimate": "p=0.026 positive affect; p=0.025 executive function accuracy at 2 h [velichkov2024]", "certainty_notes": "Single registered double-blind trial; secondary acute outcomes; symptomatic sample, so it must not be generalised to healthy students.", "key_study_ids": ["velichkov2024"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "other", "population_detail": "Emerging adults (mean 20 y) with moderate-to-severe self-reported depressive symptoms", "dose_or_intake": "Daily wild blueberry drink", "duration": "6 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Primary outcome (BDI-II at 6 weeks) improved more on placebo than on blueberry, by 5.8 points (95% CI 0.8-10.7); anxiety and anhedonia also fell more on placebo; no biomarker differences.", "effect_estimate": "Between-group BDI-II difference 5.8 points favouring placebo (95% CI 0.8 to 10.7, p=0.023) [velichkov2024]", "certainty_notes": "Single registered double-blind trial; result opposite to hypothesis, could be chance, but it is the best chronic evidence in young people and is unfavourable. Mood, not cognition.", "key_study_ids": ["velichkov2024"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "students", "population_detail": "University students, young knowledge workers and healthy adults under 35", "dose_or_intake": "Any daily berry serving", "duration": "2 weeks or longer", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No chronic whole-berry trial with a cognitive primary outcome in healthy students or young workers was found. The only chronic trial in young people had a mood primary outcome and was unfavourable. Evidence for this group rests on extrapolation from acute studies and older adults.", "effect_estimate": "not reported (no trials)", "certainty_notes": "Absence of evidence; any 'study fuel' claim for berries would be unsupported.", "key_study_ids": ["deamicis2022", "velichkov2024"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Healthy adults aged 50-80 without diagnosed impairment", "dose_or_intake": "24 g/day freeze-dried blueberry or strawberry (1-2 cups fresh), 26 g/day freeze-dried wild blueberry (178 g fresh, 302 mg anthocyanins), 20 g/day freeze-dried blueberry, or a 450 g mixed-berry/tomato beverage", "duration": "5 weeks to 24 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Lower task-switch cost and fewer CVLT repetition errors with blueberry (n=37); better task-switch accuracy with wild blueberry (n=61); better working memory after a 5-week mixed-berry beverage (n=40, crossover); null cognitive tests after 24 weeks of freeze-dried blueberry (n=57); pooled executive function SMD 0.21 (-0.05 to 0.46) across blueberry trials.", "effect_estimate": "Switch cost p=0.033, partial eta squared 0.09 [miller2018]; pooled EF SMD 0.21 (95% CI -0.05 to 0.46, I2 42%) [he2026]; working memory p<0.05 [nilsson2017]", "certainty_notes": "Small trials, many outcomes, positive findings on different tasks in each trial (inconsistency), frequent industry links, a null 24-week trial; downgraded for risk of bias, inconsistency and imprecision.", "key_study_ids": ["miller2018", "wood2023", "nilsson2017", "guardino2026", "he2026"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Healthy adults aged 60-80", "dose_or_intake": "24 g/day freeze-dried strawberry (2 cups fresh) or 26 g/day freeze-dried wild blueberry", "duration": "12-13 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Strawberry improved CVLT word recognition and spatial-navigation latency (n=37); wild blueberry improved immediate recall on an auditory verbal learning task (n=61); the 24-week blueberry trial and the chronic-fruit meta-analysis did not support memory benefit.", "effect_estimate": "Word recognition p=0.014, partial eta squared 0.159 [miller2021]; immediate recall p<0.05 [wood2023]; meta-analysis did not support a cognitive effect [wang2023]", "certainty_notes": "Same limitations as executive-function row; outcome selection varies between trials; no trial pre-specified a single memory endpoint with adequate power.", "key_study_ids": ["miller2021", "wood2023", "guardino2026", "wang2023"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "other", "population_detail": "Older adults with early memory changes (n=9) and overweight middle-aged adults with insulin resistance and subjective cognitive decline (n=30 analysed)", "dose_or_intake": "Wild blueberry juice daily; whole-fruit strawberry powder daily", "duration": "12 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Blueberry juice improved paired-associate learning and word-list recall in 9 older adults compared with baseline and with a companion-trial placebo group; strawberry powder reduced memory interference (Cohen's f 0.45) and depressive symptoms (f 0.39) in midlife at-risk adults.", "effect_estimate": "Paired-associate learning p=0.009, word recall p=0.04 [krikorian2010]; memory interference p=0.02, f=0.45 [krikorian2023]", "certainty_notes": "Very small, single-group (Cincinnati) trials, the 2010 trial without concurrent randomised control, strawberry trial funded by the California Strawberry Commission (per companion monograph); downgraded for risk of bias and imprecision. Larger MCI trials are graded in the blueberry-anthocyanins monograph.", "key_study_ids": ["krikorian2010", "krikorian2023", "bonyadi2022"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Adults aged 18-81 across trials of frozen, freeze-dried, juice and extract forms of berries, cherries and citrus", "dose_or_intake": "Various food forms", "duration": "1 week to 6 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "A meta-analysis of 11 trials did not sufficiently support a role for fruits or fruit forms in improving cognition or mood (only cherry juice and psychomotor speed approached significance). Blueberry meta-analysis: no significant effect on executive function or mood. Narrative reviews counting positive outcomes concluded benefit.", "effect_estimate": "Cherry juice reaction time SMD -0.37 (95% CI -0.74 to 0.01) as the only near-significant pooled effect [wang2023]; blueberry EF SMD 0.21 (-0.05 to 0.46) [he2026]", "certainty_notes": "Pooled analyses are null; positive reviews rely on vote counting. Heterogeneous forms and doses (indirectness).", "key_study_ids": ["wang2023", "he2026", "bonyadi2022", "deamicis2022", "garciacordero2021"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_decline", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "16,010 US nurses aged 70 or older (cognitive decline); 49,493 women and 27,842 men (subjective cognitive decline)", "dose_or_intake": "Habitual blueberry and strawberry intake by food-frequency questionnaire (highest categories about 1 serving/week or more)", "duration": "Diet averaged over 14-20 years; cognitive follow-up about 6 years or SCD assessed once", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Higher blueberry and strawberry intake associated with slower global cognitive decline, equivalent to about 1.5-2.5 years of cognitive ageing; strawberries among foods associated with lower odds of subjective cognitive decline.", "effect_estimate": "Blueberry mean slope difference 0.04 SD units (95% CI 0.01-0.07); strawberry 0.03 (0.00-0.06) [devore2012]; anthocyanins OR 0.76 (0.72-0.84) for SCD [yeh2021]", "certainty_notes": "Observational: residual confounding by socioeconomic status and overall diet quality, measurement error, self-reported SCD; cannot establish causation. Grade C as supportive, not confirmatory, evidence.", "key_study_ids": ["devore2012", "yeh2021"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "dementia_risk", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "925 older adults (Rush MAP, 58-98 y); 2,790 Framingham Offspring adults aged 45-82; 121,986 UK Biobank adults aged 40-70", "dose_or_intake": "Strawberry intake (MAP); cumulative blueberry intake (Framingham); 0.5 servings/day of berries within a flavonoid-rich food score (UK Biobank)", "duration": "Mean follow-up 6.7 years (MAP), up to about 20 years (Framingham), 9.2 years (UK Biobank)", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Higher strawberry intake associated with lower Alzheimer's dementia risk; late-life (not midlife) blueberry intake associated with lower dementia risk; consuming at least two of 5 servings tea, 1 serving red wine and 0.5 servings berries per day associated with lower dementia risk.", "effect_estimate": "HR 0.76 (95% CI 0.60-0.96) strawberries [agarwal2019]; AHR 0.62 (0.46-0.84) for the tea/wine/berry combination [jennings2024]; blueberries significant in late life only [lyu2024]", "certainty_notes": "Observational and inconsistent across life stages; berries cannot be separated from combined food scores; several authors received US Highbush Blueberry Council funding [jennings2024, lyu2024].", "key_study_ids": ["agarwal2019", "lyu2024", "jennings2024"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "vascular_health", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults across 14 blueberry RCTs (632 participants), including healthy older adults", "dose_or_intake": "Whole blueberry or freeze-dried powder, e.g. 26 g/day (302 mg anthocyanins)", "duration": "acute to 12 weeks", "grade": "B", "direction": "benefit", "effect_summary": "Flow-mediated dilation improved consistently; blood pressure largely unchanged in the meta-analysis, though 24-h systolic BP fell in one 12-week trial.", "effect_estimate": "Pooled FMD +1.11% (95% CI 0.50-1.72, I2 23%) [he2026]; FMD +0.86% (0.56-1.17) and 24-h SBP -3.59 mmHg (-6.95 to -0.23) [wood2023]", "certainty_notes": "Consistent surrogate effect with low heterogeneity; a biomarker, not a cognitive outcome, and the link to cognition is unproven. Downgraded one level for indirectness to cognition.", "key_study_ids": ["he2026", "wood2023"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "other", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults with normal BMI (mean age about 20-28 y)", "dose_or_intake": "Freeze-dried wild blueberry in a smoothie or drink (several doses; 150-450 mg anthocyanins) with a carbohydrate-rich breakfast or sugar-matched control", "duration": "single dose, 2.5-3 h post-meal", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Post-prandial glucose and insulin fell dose-dependently (significant at 300 mg anthocyanins or more), with increased GLP-1, PYY and GIP but no change in appetite or cognition; an earlier study showed an extended, flatter glucose curve without a significant peak reduction.", "effect_estimate": "Lower glucose and insulin versus control in first hour at medium and high doses [ellis2026]; peak reduction not significant [bell2017]", "certainty_notes": "Metabolic biomarker only; small crossover trials (n 17-24). Shows that berries do not behave like their sugar content alone, which matters for product positioning, but not a cognitive effect.", "key_study_ids": ["ellis2026", "bell2017"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "300 g mixed berries (75 g each strawberry, blueberry, raspberry, blackberry) blended into a 400 mL smoothie; about 255 mg anthocyanidins (USDA-database estimate)", "onset": "2 h (response time), 6 h (accuracy)", "duration_studied": "6 h", "domain": "executive function / selective attention", "population": "healthy young adults 20-30 y, n=40", "effect_estimate": "Accuracy maintained at 6 h vs decline on placebo (p=0.003); faster MANT responses at 2 h and 4 h; standardised effects not reported", "certainty": "low (single-blind, industry-sponsored, between-subjects)", "safety": "No adverse events reported; about 21.6 g fructose plus glucose per serving (matched in placebo)", "study_ids": ["whyte2019"]}, {"dose": "Wild blueberry drink, 150, 300 or 450 mg anthocyanins, with a high-carbohydrate breakfast", "onset": "90 min (cognition); 30-60 min (glucose)", "duration_studied": "3 h", "domain": "episodic memory, spatial working memory, sustained attention; post-prandial glucose", "population": "healthy adults, mean 28 y, n=24 (22 women)", "effect_estimate": "No cognitive effect at any dose; dose-dependent lower glucose and insulin at 300 mg or more", "certainty": "moderate for null cognition in this small sample (double-blind, crossover)", "safety": "Not reported in abstract", "study_ids": ["ellis2026"]}, {"dose": "Flavonoid-rich blueberry drink (freeze-dried wild blueberry)", "onset": "2 h", "duration_studied": "2 h", "domain": "mood (positive affect)", "population": "young adults 18-21 y, n=21 (crossover); emerging adults with depressive symptoms, n=60", "effect_estimate": "Positive affect increased (interaction significant); EF accuracy improved at 2 h in the depressive-symptom sample (p=0.025)", "certainty": "low", "safety": "Not reported", "study_ids": ["khalid2017", "velichkov2024"]}, {"dose": "Freeze-dried wild blueberry powder in a smoothie at several doses, sugar-matched and no-added-sugar conditions", "onset": "30-150 min", "duration_studied": "2.5 h", "domain": "post-prandial glucose (metabolic biomarker)", "population": "healthy young adults, n=17", "effect_estimate": "Extended glucose response and non-significant reduction of peak glucose versus sugar-matched control", "certainty": "low", "safety": "Not reported", "study_ids": ["bell2017"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "90 days", "population": "healthy older adults 60-75 y, n=37", "outcome": "executive function (CVLT repetition errors, task-switch cost); gait and balance", "effect_estimate": "Repetition errors p=0.031 (partial eta squared 0.126); switch cost p=0.033 (0.09); no gait/balance effect", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Small n; many outcomes; freeze-dried 24 g/day (1 cup fresh) vs placebo powder; funding details not determined", "study_ids": ["miller2018"]}, {"duration": "90 days", "population": "healthy older adults 60-75 y, n=37", "outcome": "learning and memory (CVLT word recognition, virtual navigation)", "effect_estimate": "Word recognition p=0.014 (partial eta squared 0.159); navigation latency p=0.020 (0.106); no gait/balance effect", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Small n; secondary-looking outcomes; 24 g/day freeze-dried strawberry equals 2 cups fresh", "study_ids": ["miller2021"]}, {"duration": "12 weeks", "population": "healthy older adults 65-80 y, n=61", "outcome": "FMD (primary), cognition, CBF, BP, microbiome", "effect_estimate": "FMD +0.86% (0.56-1.17); 24-h SBP -3.59 mmHg; better immediate recall and task-switch accuracy (p<0.05); CBF unchanged", "evidence_quality": "moderate for FMD, low for cognition", "limitations": "Cognition secondary; multiple tests; erratum published 2025", "study_ids": ["wood2023"]}, {"duration": "5 weeks per arm (crossover)", "population": "healthy adults 50-70 y, n=40", "outcome": "working memory, selective attention, psychomotor reaction time; lipids, glucose", "effect_estimate": "Better working memory after berry vs control beverage (p<0.05); total and LDL cholesterol reduced; other cognitive tests unchanged", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "450 g fruit/day beverage including tomato; control lacked fibre and polyphenols; 5 of 46 dropped out for compliance difficulties", "study_ids": ["nilsson2017"]}, {"duration": "24 weeks", "population": "cognitively healthy older adults, mean 73.5 y, n=57", "outcome": "serum p-tau181, NfL (primary); GFAP, BP, computerised cognition", "effect_estimate": "No effect on p-tau181 or NfL; diastolic BP reduced; no effect on cognitive tests", "evidence_quality": "moderate for null biomarker result; low for cognition", "limitations": "Small n for cognition; 20 g/day freeze-dried powder", "study_ids": ["guardino2026"]}, {"duration": "12 weeks", "population": "older adults with early memory changes, n=9; midlife insulin-resistant adults with subjective decline, n=30", "outcome": "paired-associate learning, word-list recall; memory interference, depressive symptoms", "effect_estimate": "Paired-associate learning p=0.009; memory interference p=0.02 (f 0.45)", "evidence_quality": "very low to low", "limitations": "Tiny samples; juice format (sugar); single laboratory; strawberry trial industry-funded", "study_ids": ["krikorian2010", "krikorian2023"]}, {"duration": "6 weeks", "population": "emerging adults with depressive symptoms, n=60", "outcome": "BDI-II (primary), anxiety, anhedonia, executive function, serum biomarkers", "effect_estimate": "BDI-II reduction 5.8 points larger on placebo (0.8-10.7)", "evidence_quality": "moderate (registered, double-blind) for lack of benefit", "limitations": "Single trial; mood outcome; possible chance finding", "study_ids": ["velichkov2024"]}, {"duration": "12 weeks", "population": "healthy adults 50-75 y, n=60", "outcome": "neurocognitive battery, BDNF, NGF-R", "effect_estimate": "Tower of London times fell in all arms (cocoa, red berries, combination); no neurotrophin change", "evidence_quality": "very low for berry effect", "limitations": "No placebo arm, so improvements may be practice effects", "study_ids": ["garciacordero2021"]}, {"duration": "habitual intake over 10-20 years", "population": "US and UK cohorts, 925 to 121,986 participants, middle-aged and older", "outcome": "cognitive decline, subjective cognitive decline, dementia", "effect_estimate": "Slower decline (about 1.5-2.5 years of ageing) [devore2012]; SCD OR 0.76 for anthocyanins [yeh2021]; AD HR 0.76 for strawberries [agarwal2019]; dementia AHR 0.62 for tea/wine/berry combination [jennings2024]; blueberries significant in late life only [lyu2024]", "evidence_quality": "low (observational)", "limitations": "Confounding by healthy lifestyle and income; FFQ error; industry COI for some authors; cannot isolate berries", "study_ids": ["devore2012", "yeh2021", "agarwal2019", "jennings2024", "lyu2024"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Fresh, frozen and freeze-dried berries are ordinary foods with no toxicity signal at trial doses (20-26 g powder, about 300 mg anthocyanins, for up to 24 weeks). The material food-safety hazard is microbiological: frozen berries eaten raw (smoothies, yoghurt toppings) are a recurrent vehicle of norovirus and hepatitis A [bernard2014, enkirch2018], with a pooled viral prevalence of about 2% and higher prevalence in berries from African countries [miotti2024]. The Food Safety Authority of Ireland advises boiling imported frozen berries for 1 minute before eating, including in smoothies (FSAI consumer advice page, accessed 2026-10-25). Heating also destroys some anthocyanins [brownmiller2008], so the safer route for a product is validated sourcing and virus testing, not reliance on consumer heating. Juices and sweetened berry products add free sugars. Pesticide residues in berries were not researched here; EU maximum residue levels (Regulation (EC) 396/2005) apply to all supply.", "adverse_effects": ["Norovirus and hepatitis A infection from contaminated frozen berries eaten without heating [bernard2014, enkirch2018, miotti2024]", "Free-sugar load from juices and sweetened products (whole fruit sugars are intrinsic; a 300 g mixed-berry smoothie contained about 21.6 g fructose plus glucose, as matched in the placebo [whyte2019])", "Gastrointestinal discomfort with large fibre-rich servings (the 450 g/day beverage had 5 of 46 dropouts for compliance difficulties [nilsson2017])", "Food allergy or pseudo-allergic reactions to strawberries in sensitised individuals (not reviewed here)", "Possible pesticide residues (EU MRLs under Regulation (EC) 396/2005; residue levels not researched here)"], "contraindications": ["Known allergy to the specific berry", "Raw frozen imported berries for immunocompromised people, pregnant women, older adults in care settings and young children unless heat-treated or from a virus-controlled supply (FSAI advice)"], "drug_interactions": ["No clinically relevant interactions established for whole blueberries, strawberries, raspberries or blackberries at food doses", "Cranberry products and warfarin: see other-berry-polyphenols monograph (small trials do not show an INR effect, but caution is advised)", "Grapefruit-type CYP3A4 inhibition is not established for these berries (not reviewed)"], "upper_limits": "No tolerable upper intake level exists for berries or anthocyanins. Trial exposures without reported harm: 20-26 g/day freeze-dried powder for 12-24 weeks [wood2023, guardino2026]; 450 g/day mixed-fruit beverage for 5 weeks [nilsson2017]. WHO advises limiting free sugars (from juices, not whole fruit) to below 10% of energy (background knowledge, not re-verified here).", "sleep_impact": "None expected; berries contain no stimulants. No trial measured sleep.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Up to 24 weeks in RCTs [guardino2026]; decades of habitual intake in cohorts without safety signals [devore2012, jennings2024].", "daily_use_appropriate": "yes", "special_populations": "Suitable for children, pregnancy and older adults as food. Raw frozen berries carry a viral risk that matters most for pregnant women, immunocompromised people and residents of care homes. People with diabetes should prefer whole fruit over juice and count carbohydrate (about 14.7 g sugars per cup of blueberries, USDA). People on anticoagulants: see cranberry caution.", "specialist_review_needed": ["Food microbiologist (virus control for frozen and freeze-dried berries, especially from Moroccan or other African sources)", "Food safety and regulatory specialist (pesticide residues and MRL compliance for strawberries)", "Dietitian (sugar content of juices and smoothies; diabetes)"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "Fresh berries and validated freeze-dried powders pass. Frozen berries intended to be eaten raw pass only with a documented virus-control programme (good agricultural practice, water quality, testing, or a validated kill step), given repeated EU norovirus and hepatitis A outbreaks and higher viral prevalence in African-origin berries."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Berries, frozen berries and freeze-dried whole-fruit powders are conventional foods, not novel foods. No health claim linking berries or anthocyanins to cognition, memory or brain health is authorised; EFSA's 2010 opinion on antioxidant claims for foods found such effects not substantiated (see blueberry-anthocyanins monograph). Generic Article 13(1) vitamin C claims under Regulation (EU) 432/2012 can be used for foods that are at least a 'source of vitamin C' (15% of the 80 mg NRV per 100 g, i.e. 12 mg/100 g, under Regulation (EC) 1924/2006). Strawberries (58.8 mg/100 g, USDA) qualify; blueberries (9.7 mg/100 g) do not. Freeze-dried products need analysis because vitamin C is lost in processing. These are vitamin C claims, not berry or anthocyanin claims. Fresh strawberries are subject to the EU specific marketing standard for strawberries (Regulation (EU) 543/2011, superseded by Delegated Regulation (EU) 2023/2429 - not verified) and pesticide MRLs (Regulation (EC) 396/2005). EUR-Lex could not be accessed from this session, so the exact register wording should be re-checked.", "us": "Conventional foods; freeze-dried berry powders are food ingredients; extracts are dietary supplement ingredients under DSHEA (NDI status of specific extracts uncertain). No FDA health claim for blueberries, strawberries or anthocyanins exists. The only berry qualified health claim found concerns cranberry and recurrent UTI (see other-berry-polyphenols monograph). Under FTC standards a cognitive structure/function claim would lack competent and reliable evidence for healthy adults. Fresh produce falls under the FDA Produce Safety Rule (FSMA) for growing and harvesting (not re-verified).", "morocco": "Uncertain. Fresh, frozen and freeze-dried berries fall under Law 28-07 on food safety (ONSSA) and labelling Decree 2-12-389. Claims that a food prevents, treats or cures disease are banned. No list of authorised health claims or approval procedure was found (X6 Morocco synthesis). Export berries are certified by ONSSA against importing-country requirements (EU MRLs, phytosanitary rules). Any brain claim should be treated as unauthorised pending legal review.", "permitted_claims_examples": ["EU (Reg. (EU) 432/2012): 'Vitamin C contributes to normal psychological function' - only for food that is at least a source of vitamin C (e.g. fresh strawberries, about 58.8 mg/100 g); a vitamin C claim, not evidence that berries improve cognition. Verify Annex wording before use.", "EU (Reg. (EU) 432/2012): 'Vitamin C contributes to the reduction of tiredness and fatigue' - same condition.", "EU (Reg. (EU) 432/2012): 'Vitamin C contributes to normal functioning of the nervous system' - same condition."], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "Whole fruit, frozen fruit and conventional freeze-dried powders are not novel foods. Purified anthocyanin extracts or novel processes should be checked in the EU Novel Food Catalogue (not verified here); US NDI status of new extracts is uncertain.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "High. Trial doses are normal food portions: about 1 cup (148 g) of fresh blueberries or 24 g freeze-dried powder [miller2018]; 2 cups of strawberries or 24 g powder [miller2021]; 26 g freeze-dried wild blueberry, equal to 178 g fresh and 302 mg anthocyanins [wood2023]; 300 g of mixed frozen berries as a smoothie [whyte2019]. A cup of blueberries supplies about 84 kcal, 14.7 g intrinsic sugars and 3.6 g fibre (USDA FDC 171711). Strawberries supply about half the sugar per 100 g (4.9 g) and six times the vitamin C (USDA FDC 167762).", "formats": ["fresh whole berries (Moroccan season: blueberries about October-June, strawberries November-March)", "frozen unsweetened whole or mixed berries (year-round; virus-controlled supply required if eaten raw)", "freeze-dried whole-fruit powder sachets of 20-26 g (smoothies, yoghurt, overnight oats)", "freeze-dried berry pieces in breakfast cereals or bars (verify anthocyanin retention after baking)", "cold-processed or HPP purees and smoothies (avoid clarified juices and ambient shelf-stable juices)", "berry plus high-flavanol cocoa combinations (concept only; no placebo-controlled evidence [garciacordero2021])"], "stability": "Anthocyanins degrade with heat, oxygen, light, high pH and ambient storage. Thermal processing destroyed 28-59% and 6 months at 25 C a further 62-85% of blueberry anthocyanins [brownmiller2008]; juicing gives the largest losses because skins and seeds are removed [howard2012]. Frozen storage and freeze-drying are the formats used in trials, but their losses were not quantified in the papers reviewed here. Anthocyanin content varies widely by species, cultivar and ripeness [wu2006], so the dose should be stated per serving at the end of shelf life, verified by HPLC.", "taste": "Widely liked sweet-tart flavour; powders mix well into drinks and dairy; colour shifts with pH and stains. Large daily volumes reduce adherence: 5 of 46 participants dropped out of a 450 g/day berry-beverage trial for compliance difficulties [nilsson2017].", "bioavailability_matrix": "Parent anthocyanins are poorly absorbed, and phenolic metabolites appear 1-6 h after intake. Combining berries with a carbohydrate meal lowers post-meal glucose and insulin at 300 mg anthocyanins or more [ellis2026, bell2017]. Whole fruit adds fibre and vitamin C that many placebos did not match. Dairy and protein matrix effects were not reviewed here.", "dose_feasibility": "Feasible. The studied doses (about 250-300 mg anthocyanins) fit in a cup of berries, a 300 g smoothie or a 26 g powder sachet. They are about 20-25 times average US intake [wu2006], so a habitual 'one serving a day' recommendation is a large dietary change, but an achievable one. Juice formats can deliver the dose but carry free sugars and the greatest processing losses.", "cost_availability": "Widely available year-round as frozen fruit. Morocco is a major exporter: about 64,400 t of raspberries worth nearly US$487 million in 2024-25, and 67,300 t of fresh blueberries in 2023-24 (HortiDaily, 9 Oct 2025, citing Morocco World News; accessed 2026-10-25). A search-result summary of bladi.net reported 89,236 t of blueberries for 2025-26, with falling export prices (page not opened; treat as unverified). Moroccan strawberry area fell from about 3,700 ha in 2022 to 2,300 ha in 2025 (HortiDaily). This gives local sourcing options for fresh, frozen or freeze-dried material, but freeze-drying capacity and costs in Morocco were not researched.", "cost_evidence": "US average retail prices in 2023 (USDA ERS Fruit and Vegetable Prices, accessed 2026-10-25): fresh blueberries US$5.13/lb (US$1.73 per cup equivalent), frozen blueberries US$4.20/lb (US$1.39/cup); fresh strawberries US$3.08/lb (US$1.05/cup), frozen US$3.47/lb (US$1.15/cup); fresh raspberries US$7.97/lb (US$2.65/cup), frozen US$6.21/lb (US$2.05/cup); frozen unsweetened mixed berries US$4.45/lb (US$1.47/cup). Retail freeze-dried wild blueberry powder costs about EUR 98/kg, or about EUR 2.35 per 24 g serving (per blueberry-anthocyanins monograph, KoRo listing). Moroccan retail prices were not found."}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 3, "effect_magnitude": 2, "safety": 8, "reproducibility": 3, "food_suitability": 9, "cost_access": 7, "rationale": "Evidence quality 3: no cognitive row in healthy young adults or students exceeds C, the chronic young-adult row is D (no trials), older-adult rows are C (inconsistent), cohorts are C, and only the vascular surrogate reaches B. Effect magnitude 2: pooled cognitive effects are null (EF SMD 0.21, NS), individual positives are single tests with partial eta squared about 0.1, and cohort effects equal about 0.03-0.04 SD over years. Safety 8: an ordinary food with no toxicity signal; deductions for the documented virus risk of raw frozen berries, sugar in juices and pesticide residues in strawberries. Reproducibility 3: positive trials cluster in a few laboratories (Reading, Tufts/USDA, Cincinnati) with frequent commodity-board funding, while independent double-blind trials are null [ellis2026, guardino2026]. Food suitability 9: effective doses are normal portions that are well liked, available frozen year-round and suit many formats; heat and juice processing losses are the main constraint. Cost/access 7: about US$1.05-1.73 per cup equivalent fresh or frozen in the US, strong Moroccan production, but freeze-dried powder costs about EUR 2.35 per serving and fresh berries are seasonal and perishable."}, "research_gaps": ["No chronic (4-12 week) double-blind trial of whole berries on objective cognition in healthy students or young knowledge workers", "No trial in students under exam or high-workload conditions", "Placebos rarely match fibre, colour and taste of whole fruit, so effects cannot be attributed to anthocyanins", "Dose-response for chronic intake and anthocyanin retention in frozen and freeze-dried formats over shelf life is not well quantified", "Moroccan cultivars' anthocyanin content and processing losses are unmeasured", "Independent (non-commodity-funded) replication of the older-adult cognitive findings", "Registered trials without posted results (e.g. NCT04786665 strawberries and cognition, NCT04049162 blueberry plus exercise, NCT01289860 acute blueberry flavonoids in healthy adults) need follow-up for publication bias", "Virus prevalence data for Moroccan export and domestic berries"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 4.8, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": "B", "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"episodic_memory": "C", "cognitive_decline": "C", "dementia_risk": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 13, "n_studies": 28, "n_verified": 28, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 6, "n_systematic_reviews": 4, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["benefit", "inconsistent", "insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "dark-chocolate", "name": "Dark chocolate as a food", "category": "J", "category_name": "Whole foods", "aliases": ["dark chocolate", "bittersweet chocolate", "semisweet chocolate", "high-cocoa chocolate (70%, 85%, 99%)", "cocoa-rich chocolate", "high-flavanol chocolate", "chocolat noir"], "food_sources": ["dark chocolate 70-85% cacao solids: per 100 g about 598 kcal, 42.6 g fat (24.5 g saturated), 24.0 g total sugars, 10.9 g fibre, 11.9 mg iron, 228 mg magnesium, 80 mg caffeine and 802 mg theobromine (USDA FoodData Central SR Legacy 170273, accessed 2026-10-25)", "a 40 g portion of 70-85% dark chocolate therefore gives about 239 kcal, 9.6 g sugars, 9.8 g saturated fat, 32 mg caffeine and 321 mg theobromine (calculated from USDA FDC 170273)", "a 24 g bar of a Japanese high-cacao chocolate used in a trial: 135 kcal, 540 mg cacao polyphenols, 34.8 mg epicatechin, 26.8 mg caffeine and 197.5 mg theobromine [sumiyoshi2019]", "milk chocolate (far lower flavanols; 40.6 mg epicatechin equivalents/100 g in one analysis) and white chocolate (no flavanols, no methylxanthines) [langer2011]", "high-flavanol dark chocolate made with under-fermented or minimally processed cocoa (specialty products)"], "active_constituents": ["flavan-3-ols: (-)-epicatechin, catechin and procyanidin oligomers (DP 1-10); total flavanols 93.5-651.1 mg epicatechin equivalents/100 g across commercial dark chocolates [langer2011]", "methylxanthines: theobromine (about 0.8 g/100 g) and caffeine (about 80 mg/100 g) (USDA FDC 170273) - the constituents responsible for chocolate's acute psychoactivity [smit2004]", "cocoa butter (saturated stearic and palmitic acids, oleic acid)", "added sugar (in most products)", "minerals: iron, magnesium, copper", "contaminants: cadmium and lead from soil and post-harvest handling [abt2018, hands2024]"], "one_line_verdict": "Dark chocolate is a pleasant food whose small acute alertness effect comes mainly from its caffeine and theobromine, whose flavanol dose is unpredictable and usually too low to match positive cocoa trials, whose cognitive trials are small, mostly unblinded and null in older adults, and whose daily use carries real calorie, sugar and cadmium/lead trade-offs, so it is not a credible brain-health product unless reformulated as a verified high-flavanol, low-sugar, metal-tested chocolate sold without cognitive claims.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. Dark chocolate is a variable vehicle, not a standardised dose. Across 12 brands, total flavanols ranged seven-fold (93.5-651.1 mg/100 g), and labelled cocoa content did not predict flavanols (R2 0.41-0.49) [langer2011]. A 41-product survey confirmed this [alanon2016]. A 40 g portion of 70-85% chocolate therefore supplies roughly 40-260 mg of flavanols, below the 500-1000 mg of the positive cocoa-drink trials. It reliably supplies about 240 kcal, 9.6 g sugars, 32 mg caffeine and 320 mg theobromine (USDA). Acute effects. The best-supported acute effect is methylxanthine-driven. The caffeine and theobromine of a 50 g dark bar reproduced the arousal and attention effects of cocoa powder, and white chocolate did not differ from water [smit2004]. In 30 young adults, high-flavanol dark chocolate improved contrast sensitivity, spatial memory and some reaction-time measures compared with white chocolate [field2011]. However, white chocolate lacks both flavanols and methylxanthines. Cocoa drinks in young adults were positive at 520-994 mg [scholey2010] and mostly null at 250 mg [massee2015]. After sleep deprivation, flavanol-rich chocolate preserved working memory in women only [grassi2016]. Chronic effects in young adults. A 30-day trial in 18 undergraduates reported better Stroop and cancellation scores with dark chocolate, analysed within groups only [sumiyoshi2019]. In a 3-week trial, 30 g/day of 85% chocolate reduced negative affect, but 70% chocolate did not [shin2022]. No blinded, adequately powered chronic trial in students exists. Older adults. Two trials using actual chocolate were null: 37 g/day plus a cocoa drink for 6 weeks (n=101) [crews2008], and 50 g/day of chocolate with 410 versus 86 mg flavanols for 8 weeks (n=100) [suominen2020]. In postmenopausal women, 10 g/day of 99% chocolate for 6 months shortened TMT-B by 12 s (d=-0.34, p=0.047) in an unblinded trial [garciayu2022]. Meta-analyses report faster TMT-B (WMD -11.8 to -13.1 s) [shateri2023, arabpour2026], but they pool drinks with chocolate. An umbrella review found no association with cognition or mood [veronese2019]. Cohorts link intake with better cognition [nurk2009, crichton2016] and less decline [moreira2016], but confounding is likely. The country-level Nobel correlation [messerli2012] is ecological. Trade-offs. Chocolate-candy and milk chocolate intake predict weight gain [greenberg2015, liu2024], while dark chocolate was associated with lower diabetes risk [liu2024]. Cadmium and lead are the main concern: 35-43% of 72 US products exceeded California per-serving limits [hands2024], with higher cadmium in high-cocoa and Latin American products [abt2018]. Unknown: whether any chocolate portion helps students beyond a caffeine-theobromine alertness effect.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Chocolate corrects no deficiency relevant to cognition in adequately nourished people. It contains iron (about 4.8 mg per 40 g) and magnesium (about 91 mg per 40 g) (USDA). The non-haem iron in cocoa is poorly absorbed, iron-replete students gain nothing cognitively from extra iron, and the 240 kcal portion makes it a poor iron vehicle compared with legumes or fortified foods. Every cognitive trial of chocolate therefore tests enhancement. The only signal of a 'low-baseline' responder comes from cocoa-extract trials: COSMOS participants with poor habitual diets showed a small memory gain (see cocoa-flavanols monograph). That effect was obtained with 500 mg/day of extract, not chocolate. Acute alertness after chocolate is a methylxanthine (caffeine plus theobromine) effect [smit2004], comparable in kind to a small coffee. This is arousal, not improved memory or intelligence. Mood effects [shin2022, abdoli2024] are self-reported and come from small, partly unblinded trials.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Popular claims that dark chocolate 'boosts brain power', 'improves memory', 'is a superfood for focus' or 'makes you smarter' (often citing the country-level Nobel-laureate correlation [messerli2012]) are not supported. The evidence supports narrower statements. Chocolate contains caffeine and theobromine, which can slightly raise alertness acutely [smit2004]. Specially processed high-flavanol cocoa products can support blood-vessel function at 200 mg flavanols/day, and this is an authorised EU claim that applies only when the flavanol dose is guaranteed. In the US, the FDA's 2023 qualified health claim covers high-flavanol cocoa powder and explicitly excludes chocolate. Two well-sized trials using real chocolate in older adults were null [crews2008, suominen2020]. Labelled cocoa percentage is not a valid proxy for flavanols [langer2011, alanon2016], so '85% = more brain benefit' is unfounded. Daily chocolate also adds energy, sugar and saturated fat, plus measurable cadmium and lead [hands2024]. An honest Brain Food position is that dark chocolate is an enjoyable treat, best in small portions (10-20 g), chosen for low sugar and tested metals. Any functional chocolate would need a verified flavanol dose and a heavy-metal specification, would carry no cognitive claim, and would be marketed for vascular function only where the EU flavanol claim conditions are met.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Chocolate acts through two separate routes. Methylxanthines (caffeine 30-45 mg and theobromine 200-330 mg per 24-40 g) are absorbed within an hour and block adenosine receptors, giving a small, well-characterised arousal effect [smit2004]. Flavanols, if present in sufficient dose (hundreds of mg), are absorbed as (-)-epicatechin and metabolites peaking about 2 h after intake and improve endothelial nitric-oxide function, which may transiently raise cerebral blood flow; this pathway is established for vascular markers but not for durable cognitive benefit. Chocolate processing (fermentation, roasting, alkalisation, conching) lowers and epimerises flavanols, and the fat and sugar matrix adds energy without cognitive benefit. Proposed neurotrophin (BDNF, NGF) and gut-microbiome mechanisms come from small, single trials [sumiyoshi2019, shin2022].", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Chocolate portion -> highly variable flavanol and methylxanthine content", "detail": "Total flavanols 93.5-651.1 mg/100 g across 12 dark chocolates, not predicted by non-fat cocoa solids [langer2011]; % cocoa did not correlate with flavanols across 41 chocolates, and manufacturing epimerises (-)-epicatechin [alanon2016].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Methylxanthines (caffeine + theobromine) -> adenosine receptor antagonism -> arousal", "detail": "Caffeine 19 mg plus theobromine 250 mg (the amounts estimated for a 50 g dark bar) reproduced cocoa powder's effects on energetic arousal and attention [smit2004]; dark chocolate plus cocoa raised pulse rate over 6 weeks [crews2008].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Flavanols (if 200 mg or more) -> plasma epicatechin -> endothelial NO -> FMD and blood pressure", "detail": "Flavanol-rich chocolate preserved FMD (7.0 vs 5.0%) and lowered BP after sleep deprivation [grassi2016]; chocolate improved FMD in pooled RCTs with low-certainty GRADE [veronese2019]; the EU authorised a vascular claim at 200 mg/day (see cocoa-flavanols monograph).", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Vascular effects -> cerebral blood flow and neural efficiency", "detail": "Lower dorsolateral prefrontal fMRI activation during sustained tasks after 635 vs 212 mg cacao polyphenol chocolate (n=26, industry-funded) [sasaki2024]; prefrontal blood flow measured without convincing change [sumiyoshi2019]. Imaging biomarkers, not cognition.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Neurotrophins and gut microbiota -> mood and cognition", "detail": "Plasma NGF rose with 30-day dark chocolate (n=10) [sumiyoshi2019]; 85% chocolate changed microbial diversity, which correlated with negative affect (n=18 vs 14) [shin2022]. Single small trials.", "evidence_level": "speculative"}, {"step": "Measured human cognitive outcome", "detail": "Small acute alertness and attention gains attributable to methylxanthines [smit2004]; inconsistent acute flavanol-specific effects in young adults [field2011, massee2015]; null chronic trials with real chocolate in older adults [crews2008, suominen2020]; modest TMT-B gains in pooled data dominated by cocoa drinks [arabpour2026].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["adenosine A1/A2A receptor antagonism (caffeine, theobromine)", "endothelial nitric oxide / flow-mediated dilation (flavanols)", "cerebral perfusion and prefrontal activation (imaging)", "neurotrophins BDNF and NGF (speculative)", "gut-microbiome modulation (speculative)", "hedonic reward and mood (palatability, expectation)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "alertness", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adult volunteers (UK university samples, n=20 and n=22)", "dose_or_intake": "11.6 g cocoa powder, or caffeine 19 mg plus theobromine 250 mg (amounts in a 50 g dark chocolate bar), or 'milk' and 'dark' chocolate levels of methylxanthines", "duration": "single dose, testing up to about 2-3 h", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Cocoa powder and the matching caffeine+theobromine combination gave identical improvements in energetic arousal and cognitive performance (simple reaction time, RVIP) versus placebo; both milk- and dark-chocolate methylxanthine levels improved performance versus white chocolate, which did not differ from water.", "effect_estimate": "Not reported as standardised effects in abstract [smit2004]", "certainty_notes": "Two small double-blind studies from one laboratory (2004), not replicated with real chocolate bars; downgraded for imprecision. Plausible because the caffeine effect itself is well established (see caffeine monograph). Arousal and attention, not memory.", "key_study_ids": ["smit2004"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy young adults (n=30 crossover for chocolate; n=30-40 for cocoa drinks or tablets)", "dose_or_intake": "Dark chocolate with 720 mg cocoa flavanols vs white chocolate; cocoa drinks with 520 or 994 mg; 250 mg cocoa flavanol tablet", "duration": "single dose, testing 1.5-2.5 h", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "High-flavanol dark chocolate improved visual contrast sensitivity, motion detection time, spatial working memory and some choice reaction-time measures versus white chocolate; cocoa drinks improved serial subtractions (with more errors at the high dose); a 250 mg tablet improved only serial sevens in cycle 1 and mental fatigue.", "effect_estimate": "Not reported as standardised effects [field2011, scholey2010]; mostly NS [massee2015]", "certainty_notes": "Single-blind chocolate trial with a white-chocolate control that also lacks theobromine and caffeine, so flavanols cannot be isolated; small samples; multiple tests; preregistered acute cocoa-drink trials summarised in the cocoa-flavanols monograph were null. Downgraded for risk of bias, inconsistency and imprecision.", "key_study_ids": ["field2011", "scholey2010", "massee2015", "martin2020"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "sleep_deprived", "population_detail": "Healthy adults (n=32) after one night of total sleep deprivation", "dose_or_intake": "Flavanol-rich vs flavanol-poor chocolate bar 2 h before testing (dose not stated in abstract)", "duration": "single dose", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Flavanol-rich chocolate preserved working-memory accuracy after sleep deprivation in women only; it lowered blood pressure and prevented the sleep-loss drop in FMD; no PVT benefit reported in abstract.", "effect_estimate": "FMD 7.0 vs 5.0% (flavanol-rich vs poor); SBP 116.9 vs 120.8 mmHg; working-memory effect size not reported [grassi2016]", "certainty_notes": "Single trial from the L'Aquila group; sex-specific subgroup finding; downgraded for imprecision and possible selective reporting.", "key_study_ids": ["grassi2016"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "other", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy middle-aged adults (n=26), fMRI during sustained executive tasks", "dose_or_intake": "25 g dark chocolate with 635 mg vs 211.7 mg cacao polyphenols", "duration": "single dose, scanning 25-50 min after intake", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "After high-polyphenol chocolate, left dorsolateral prefrontal and inferior parietal activation fell from the first to the second session (interpreted as 'efficiency'); after low-polyphenol chocolate it rose. No behavioural benefit was reported in the abstract.", "effect_estimate": "Interaction effects on BOLD signal only [sasaki2024]", "certainty_notes": "Imaging biomarker, single-blind, manufacturer (Meiji) co-authors and funding; reduced activation is open to opposite interpretations. Not evidence of cognitive improvement.", "key_study_ids": ["sasaki2024"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Japanese undergraduates aged 20-31 (n=18) and Australian young adults (mean 24 y, n=40)", "dose_or_intake": "24 g/day high-cacao dark chocolate (540 mg cacao polyphenols) vs cacao-free white chocolate; 250 mg/day cocoa flavanol tablet vs placebo", "duration": "30 days; 4 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Dark chocolate improved modified Stroop correct answers and digit-cancellation performance from baseline (within-group tests), with effects reported to persist 3 weeks after stopping; white chocolate did not. The 4-week cocoa tablet trial found no sub-chronic cognitive effects. A systematic review of young-adult studies concluded benefit by vote counting.", "effect_estimate": "Within-group Stroop pre-post t(9)=2.93, p=0.017 [sumiyoshi2019]; no significant 4-week effects [massee2015]", "certainty_notes": "Sumiyoshi: n=18, single-blind, no between-group test reported, implausible persistence after washout, theobromine and caffeine confounded, body fat rose slightly at follow-up; Massee: low dose extract. Downgraded for risk of bias and imprecision.", "key_study_ids": ["sumiyoshi2019", "massee2015", "martin2020"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "learning", "population": "students", "population_detail": "University students during term or examination periods", "dose_or_intake": "Any daily chocolate portion", "duration": "2 weeks or longer", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No blinded trial has tested chocolate on learning, exam performance or sustained study capacity in students. The only student sample is the 18-person Sumiyoshi trial.", "effect_estimate": "not reported (no adequate trials)", "certainty_notes": "Absence of evidence; any 'exam chocolate' or 'study snack for memory' claim would be unsupported.", "key_study_ids": ["sumiyoshi2019", "martin2020"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy Korean adults aged 20-30 (n=48)", "dose_or_intake": "30 g/day of 85% or 70% cocoa dark chocolate vs no chocolate", "duration": "3 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "85% chocolate reduced negative affect (PANAS); 70% chocolate did not; gut microbial diversity rose with 85% chocolate and correlated with the change in negative affect.", "effect_estimate": "Negative affect reduced in DC85 only; effect size not reported in abstract [shin2022]", "certainty_notes": "Unblinded no-chocolate control, small arms (14-18), subjective mood outcome, inconsistent dose-response (70% null). Downgraded for risk of bias and imprecision.", "key_study_ids": ["shin2022"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "other", "population_detail": "Menopausal women aged 45-65 (n=60)", "dose_or_intake": "12 g/day of 78% dark chocolate vs 12 g/day milk chocolate", "duration": "8 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Depression scores fell more with dark than milk chocolate; sleep quality and anthropometrics did not differ.", "effect_estimate": "Mean difference -2.3 (95% CI -3.9 to -0.8), Cohen's d -0.54 [abdoli2024]", "certainty_notes": "Single trial; taste likely unblinds participants despite 'triple-blind' label; self-reported depression scale; not cognition.", "key_study_ids": ["abdoli2024"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Cognitively intact older adults (US, n=101; Finland, 65-75 y, n=100, highly educated)", "dose_or_intake": "37 g dark chocolate bar plus 237 mL sweetened cocoa drink daily vs placebo products; 50 g/day dark chocolate with 410 mg vs 86 mg flavanols", "duration": "6 weeks; 8 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "No group by time effects on any neuropsychological, haematological or blood pressure variable (pulse rate rose with chocolate) in the 6-week trial; no differences in verbal fluency, TMT-A or TMT-B, lipids, glucose or weight in the 8-week trial.", "effect_estimate": "TMT-B change -16.1 vs -12.5 s (p=0.66); TMT-A -4.6 vs -4.4 s (p=0.93) [suominen2020]; no significant effects [crews2008]", "certainty_notes": "Two parallel trials of about 100 each, consistent null; downgraded for imprecision (cannot exclude small effects), partial unblinding (more than half guessed allocation [crews2008]) and an active-flavanol comparator (86 mg) [suominen2020], which reduces contrast. Industry co-authors (Fazer) in the Finnish trial.", "key_study_ids": ["crews2008", "suominen2020", "veronese2019"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Middle-aged and older adults in pooled trials of chocolate or cocoa (mostly older; includes postmenopausal women 50-64 y)", "dose_or_intake": "10 g/day 99% chocolate added to diet; pooled chocolate and cocoa drinks (subgroup 500 mg flavanols/day or more)", "duration": "8 weeks to 6 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "In postmenopausal women, 99% chocolate shortened TMT-B by 12 s (d -0.34) with no change in attention, verbal memory, fluency or working memory. Meta-analyses report faster TMT-B and higher verbal fluency with chronic chocolate or cocoa, but working and verbal memory were inconsistent and MMSE unchanged.", "effect_estimate": "TMT-B -12.08 s (95% CI -23.99 to -0.18) [garciayu2022]; EF time WMD -11.77 s (-22.49 to -1.05) [shateri2023]; TMT-B WMD -13.1 s (-17.73 to -8.47) [arabpour2026]", "certainty_notes": "Garcia-Yu unblinded with no-intervention control and borderline p; meta-analyses pool cocoa drinks with chocolate, are dominated by a few precise-looking trials and contrast with null chocolate trials [crews2008, suominen2020]; we judge certainty low despite the authors' 'moderate-high' GRADE rating. A 12-13 s TMT-B gain is small in practical terms.", "key_study_ids": ["garciayu2022", "shateri2023", "arabpour2026", "suominen2020"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_decline", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Portuguese adults 65+ with normal MMSE (n=531, prospective); Norwegian adults 70-74 (n=2,031, cross-sectional); US adults 23-98 (n=968, cross-sectional with prospective check)", "dose_or_intake": "Habitual chocolate intake by FFQ (Norwegian association peaked at about 10 g/day)", "duration": "Median follow-up 48 months (Portugal); cross-sectional (Norway, US)", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Chocolate intake associated with lower risk of a 2-point MMSE decline (only among people with caffeine intake below 75 mg/day); chocolate consumers had better test scores in a dose-dependent pattern up to about 10 g/day; more frequent chocolate intake associated with better global and domain scores cross-sectionally, but no prospective association between earlier cognition and later chocolate intake.", "effect_estimate": "RR 0.59 (95% CI 0.38-0.92) [moreira2016]; better mean scores and lower prevalence of poor performance [nurk2009]; associations largely persisted after adjustment [crichton2016]", "certainty_notes": "Observational, confounding by socioeconomic status and health, crude FFQ exposure, subgroup-only effect in the prospective study; cannot establish causation. Ecological correlations [messerli2012] carry no weight.", "key_study_ids": ["moreira2016", "nurk2009", "crichton2016", "messerli2012"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "vascular_health", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy and at-risk adults in RCTs of chocolate and cocoa", "dose_or_intake": "High-flavanol chocolate or cocoa; EU authorised condition 200 mg cocoa flavanols (DP 1-10) per day", "duration": "90-150 min (acute FMD) and 2-18 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Chocolate consumption improved FMD acutely and over 2-18 weeks in meta-analyses (GRADE low to very low per umbrella review); flavanol-rich chocolate preserved FMD after sleep deprivation; EFSA accepted cause and effect for cocoa flavanols and endothelium-dependent vasodilation at 200 mg/day (cocoa-flavanols monograph).", "effect_estimate": "FMD 7.0 vs 5.0% after sleep deprivation [grassi2016]; pooled FMD positive at 90-150 min and 2-18 weeks [veronese2019]", "certainty_notes": "Graded C for chocolate as sold: the umbrella review rated the chocolate FMD evidence low to very low by GRADE, and ordinary chocolate's flavanol content is unpredictable [langer2011]. Flavanol-verified cocoa products reach B in the cocoa-flavanols monograph. Surrogate outcome; the link to cognition is not established.", "key_study_ids": ["veronese2019", "grassi2016", "langer2011"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "other", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "US cohorts: 192,208 adults (T2D, total chocolate), 111,654 (dark vs milk), 107,243 postmenopausal women (weight)", "dose_or_intake": "Servings of dark, milk or total chocolate; 1 oz (about 28 g) chocolate candy servings", "duration": "3 years (weight) to about 30 years (T2D)", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Cardiometabolic trade-off: 5 or more servings/week of dark chocolate associated with 21% lower T2D risk and no weight gain, whereas milk chocolate was associated with long-term weight gain and no T2D benefit; each extra 1 oz/day of chocolate candy associated with 0.92 kg more 3-year weight gain.", "effect_estimate": "Dark chocolate 5+ servings/week: 21% lower T2D risk (95% CI 5-34%; about HR 0.79, converted by us) [liu2024]; +0.92 kg (0.80-1.05) per 1 oz/day over 3 years [greenberg2015]", "certainty_notes": "Observational; healthy-user bias likely for dark chocolate; some authors received Mars Edge or Mars Symbioscience funding. Relevant to whether a daily chocolate product is defensible on overall health grounds, not cognition.", "key_study_ids": ["liu2024", "greenberg2015"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "Caffeine 19 mg + theobromine 250 mg (50 g dark bar equivalent) or 11.6 g cocoa powder", "onset": "within about 1-2 h", "duration_studied": "up to two post-dose test batteries (study 1) or three (study 2)", "domain": "alertness (energetic arousal), sustained attention (RVIP), simple reaction time", "population": "healthy adults, n=20 and n=22", "effect_estimate": "Identical improvements for cocoa powder and methylxanthines vs placebo; white chocolate equal to water; effect sizes not reported", "certainty": "low", "safety": "Caffeine dose about one-fifth of a cup of brewed coffee; theobromine adds mild stimulation", "study_ids": ["smit2004"]}, {"dose": "Dark chocolate with 720 mg cocoa flavanols vs matched quantity of white chocolate", "onset": "about 2 h", "duration_studied": "single session", "domain": "visual contrast sensitivity, motion detection, spatial working memory, choice reaction time", "population": "healthy young adults, n=30, crossover", "effect_estimate": "Improved contrast sensitivity, motion-direction time and spatial memory; no effect on coherent-motion threshold; sizes not reported", "certainty": "low (single-blind; control lacks methylxanthines)", "safety": "Not reported", "study_ids": ["field2011"]}, {"dose": "Cocoa drink with 520 or 994 mg flavanols vs low-flavanol control (not chocolate)", "onset": "about 1.5 h", "duration_studied": "repeated 10-min cycles of a demanding battery", "domain": "serial subtraction, RVIP, mental fatigue", "population": "healthy adults, n=30, crossover", "effect_estimate": "Both doses improved Serial Threes; 994 mg sped RVIP but increased Serial Sevens errors; fatigue attenuated at 520 mg only", "certainty": "low", "safety": "Not reported", "study_ids": ["scholey2010"]}, {"dose": "250 mg cocoa flavanol tablet vs placebo (not chocolate)", "onset": "same day", "duration_studied": "single session (and 4 weeks)", "domain": "mental fatigue, serial subtraction, RVIP, cognitive battery, CBF", "population": "healthy young adults, mean 24 y, n=40", "effect_estimate": "Only reduced mental fatigue and better Serial Sevens in cycle 1; no other effects", "certainty": "low to moderate for null", "safety": "Not reported", "study_ids": ["massee2015"]}, {"dose": "High- vs low-flavanol chocolate bar 2 h before testing (dose not in abstract)", "onset": "2 h", "duration_studied": "single session after total sleep deprivation", "domain": "working memory, PVT, BP, FMD, PWV", "population": "healthy adults, n=32", "effect_estimate": "Working-memory accuracy preserved in women only; FMD 7.0 vs 5.0%; SBP 116.9 vs 120.8 mmHg", "certainty": "low", "safety": "Not reported", "study_ids": ["grassi2016"]}, {"dose": "25 g dark chocolate, 635 vs 211.7 mg cacao polyphenols", "onset": "25-50 min", "duration_studied": "two 15-min fMRI sessions", "domain": "prefrontal and parietal BOLD activation during executive tasks", "population": "healthy middle-aged adults, n=26", "effect_estimate": "Session-by-chocolate interaction on BOLD; no behavioural outcome reported in abstract", "certainty": "very low for cognition", "safety": "Not reported", "study_ids": ["sasaki2024"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "6 weeks", "population": "healthy older adults, n=101", "outcome": "neuropsychological battery, haematology, BP, pulse", "effect_estimate": "No effects; pulse rate higher with chocolate and cocoa", "evidence_quality": "moderate for null", "limitations": "More than half guessed allocation; flavanol dose not stated in abstract", "study_ids": ["crews2008"]}, {"duration": "8 weeks", "population": "healthy older adults 65-75 y, n=100", "outcome": "verbal fluency, TMT-A, TMT-B; lipids, glucose, weight", "effect_estimate": "No between-group differences (TMT-B p=0.66)", "evidence_quality": "moderate for null", "limitations": "Control chocolate had 86 mg flavanols; highly educated sample; manufacturer co-authors", "study_ids": ["suominen2020"]}, {"duration": "6 months", "population": "postmenopausal women 50-64 y, n=140", "outcome": "attention, executive function, memory, fluency", "effect_estimate": "TMT-B -12.08 s (-23.99 to -0.18), d -0.34; other domains unchanged", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "No placebo, unblinded, multiple outcomes, p=0.047", "study_ids": ["garciayu2022"]}, {"duration": "30 days plus 3-week follow-up", "population": "Japanese undergraduates 20-31 y, n=18", "outcome": "modified Stroop, digit cancellation, NGF, BDNF, prefrontal CBF", "effect_estimate": "Within-group improvements with dark chocolate; NGF and theobromine rose; no BDNF change reported in abstract", "evidence_quality": "very low", "limitations": "Tiny n; no between-group statistics; within-group pre-post tests only", "study_ids": ["sumiyoshi2019"]}, {"duration": "4 weeks", "population": "healthy young adults, n=40", "outcome": "cognitive battery, mood, cardiovascular function", "effect_estimate": "No sub-chronic effects of 250 mg/day cocoa flavanols", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Low dose; small n; extract tablets not chocolate", "study_ids": ["massee2015"]}, {"duration": "3 weeks", "population": "healthy adults 20-30 y, n=48", "outcome": "PANAS mood, gut microbiota", "effect_estimate": "Negative affect reduced with 30 g/day 85% chocolate only", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "No-chocolate control (unblinded); 70% arm null", "study_ids": ["shin2022"]}, {"duration": "8 weeks", "population": "menopausal women 45-65 y, n=60", "outcome": "depression (primary), sleep quality, anthropometry", "effect_estimate": "Depression MD -2.3 (-3.9 to -0.8), d -0.54; sleep and weight unchanged", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Taste unblinding likely; single trial", "study_ids": ["abdoli2024"]}, {"duration": "pooled chronic trials (8 weeks or longer subgroups)", "population": "mixed adults, 16 RCTs and 2,847 participants [arabpour2026]; 7 RCTs [shateri2023]", "outcome": "TMT-A, TMT-B, verbal fluency, memory, MMSE", "effect_estimate": "TMT-B WMD -13.1 s (-17.73 to -8.47); EF time WMD -11.77 s (-22.49 to -1.05); MMSE no effect", "evidence_quality": "low (our judgement)", "limitations": "Pools drinks and chocolate; dominated by a few trials; heterogeneity", "study_ids": ["arabpour2026", "shateri2023"]}, {"duration": "4 years median (prospective); cross-sectional", "population": "older adults, n=531 to 2,031; adults 23-98, n=968", "outcome": "MMSE decline; cognitive test scores", "effect_estimate": "RR 0.59 (0.38-0.92) for decline; better scores among consumers, plateau near 10 g/day", "evidence_quality": "low (observational)", "limitations": "Confounding; FFQ; subgroup effect limited to low-caffeine consumers", "study_ids": ["moreira2016", "nurk2009", "crichton2016"]}, {"duration": "3 to about 30 years", "population": "US cohorts, 107,243 to 192,208 adults", "outcome": "weight change, incident T2D", "effect_estimate": "+0.92 kg/3 y per 1 oz/day chocolate candy; dark chocolate 5+ servings/week HR 0.79 for T2D; milk chocolate linked to weight gain", "evidence_quality": "low (observational)", "limitations": "Residual confounding; self-reported intake; some industry-linked authors", "study_ids": ["greenberg2015", "liu2024"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Dark chocolate is a safe food in small portions, but a daily 'functional' portion raises four issues. (1) Heavy metals: cadmium rises with cocoa content and Latin American origin [abt2018]. 35% of tested US products exceeded California's per-serving cadmium limit and 43% the lead limit, and organic products were not lower [hands2024]. At the EU maximum of 0.80 mg/kg for chocolate with at least 50% dry cocoa solids, 40 g/day gives 32 microgram cadmium, about 3.2 microgram/kg body weight/week for a 70 kg adult. That is above EFSA's tolerable weekly intake of 2.5 microgram/kg/week (EFSA 2011 statement, doi 10.2903/j.efsa.2011.1975, value confirmed via search summary). At the median US concentration (0.239 microgram/g [hands2024]), 40 g/day is about 38% of the TWI. Mean European dietary exposure is already close to the TWI. (2) Energy, sugar and saturated fat: 40 g of 70-85% chocolate gives about 239 kcal, 9.6 g sugars and 9.8 g saturated fat (USDA). Chocolate-candy intake predicts weight gain [greenberg2015], as does milk chocolate [liu2024]. (3) Methylxanthines: about 32 mg caffeine and 321 mg theobromine per 40 g (USDA), raising pulse rate over 6 weeks [crews2008]. (4) Allergen cross-contact (milk, nuts, soy). Chocolate did not provoke headache in a blinded study of chronic headache sufferers [marcus1997].", "adverse_effects": ["Cadmium and lead exposure that can approach or exceed the cadmium TWI at 40 g/day of high-cadmium products [hands2024, abt2018]", "Weight gain with habitual chocolate-candy or milk-chocolate intake (+0.92 kg/3 y per 1 oz/day) [greenberg2015, liu2024]", "Raised pulse rate with daily dark chocolate plus cocoa [crews2008]", "Stimulant effects (caffeine plus theobromine) in sensitive individuals; possible sleep disturbance if eaten in the evening (not directly studied here)", "Gastro-oesophageal reflux symptoms in susceptible people (commonly reported; not reviewed here)", "Allergic reactions to milk, nut or soy cross-contact ingredients"], "contraindications": ["Children and pregnant women: limit cocoa-rich products because of lead and cadmium (neurodevelopmental risk) and caffeine", "People with chronic kidney disease (cadmium accumulation; oxalate and potassium not reviewed here)", "Uncontrolled diabetes or weight-management programmes unless portion and sugar are controlled", "Milk, nut or soy allergy (check cross-contact labelling)"], "drug_interactions": ["Caffeine interactions at higher intakes (CYP1A2 inhibitors such as fluvoxamine or ciprofloxacin raise caffeine levels); clinically minor at 30-45 mg caffeine per 40 g but additive with coffee, tea and energy drinks", "Theobromine and caffeine with MAO inhibitors or stimulant medication (theoretical additive stimulation; not reviewed here)", "No interaction concerns established for flavanols at food doses (see cocoa-flavanols monograph)"], "upper_limits": "No UL for cocoa flavanols; short-term trials used up to 2000 mg/day (cocoa-flavanols monograph). Cadmium: EFSA TWI 2.5 microgram/kg body weight/week (EFSA 2011, value confirmed via search summary). EU maximum levels for cadmium in chocolate are 0.10 mg/kg (milk chocolate under 30% total dry cocoa solids), 0.30 mg/kg (30-50%), 0.80 mg/kg (50% or more), and 0.60 mg/kg for cocoa powder sold to the final consumer. These are from Regulation (EC) 1881/2006 as amended by Regulation (EU) 488/2014, now Regulation (EU) 2023/915, as tabulated by the CBI (Netherlands) buyer-requirements page, accessed 2026-10-25; the EUR-Lex text could not be retrieved. Lead: no EU maximum level for chocolate was identified (not verified). US: California Prop 65 MADLs are 0.5 microgram/day lead and 4.1 microgram/day cadmium [hands2024]. Caffeine: EFSA considers up to 400 mg/day safe for adults and 200 mg/day in pregnancy (EFSA 2015 opinion; background knowledge, not re-read here). 40 g of dark chocolate contributes about 32 mg.", "sleep_impact": "A 40 g portion supplies about 32 mg caffeine (about one-third of a cup of brewed coffee) plus about 320 mg theobromine (USDA FDC 170273); theobromine is a weaker stimulant with a longer half-life. No chocolate trial in this review measured sleep objectively. In menopausal women, 12 g/day of 78% chocolate did not change subjective sleep quality [abdoli2024]. Evening intake of larger portions may disturb sleep in caffeine-sensitive people (not directly studied).", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Trials up to 6 months (10 g/day 99% chocolate) [garciayu2022] and 8 weeks at 50 g/day [suominen2020]; cohorts over decades for weight and diabetes outcomes [liu2024, greenberg2015]. Long-term cadmium accumulation from daily high-cocoa portions has not been studied as an outcome.", "daily_use_appropriate": "probably", "special_populations": "Children: chocolate supplies caffeine and, from high-cocoa products, lead and cadmium, which are a concern for neurodevelopment, so daily high-cocoa products should not be marketed to children. Pregnancy: count caffeine and choose metal-tested products. People with diabetes, obesity or dyslipidaemia: energy, sugar and saturated fat matter more than flavanols. People with reflux or caffeine sensitivity should limit intake. Older adults: no cognitive benefit shown; calories and metals still apply.", "specialist_review_needed": ["Food toxicologist (cadmium and lead exposure modelling for daily portions; origin and supplier specifications)", "Regulatory specialist (EU cadmium limits, Prop 65 labelling for US sales, cocoa product composition rules, claim conditions)", "Dietitian (energy, sugar and saturated fat in a daily product)", "Food chemist (flavanol DP 1-10 assay and stability through conching and storage)"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "Safe as an occasional food. A daily Brain Food chocolate passes only with a cadmium and lead specification well below the EU maximum (so that a 20-40 g daily portion stays well below the cadmium TWI), declared caffeine and theobromine, portion control (about 10-20 g), low added sugar, and no marketing to children."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Chocolate and cocoa products are conventional foods governed by Directive 2000/36/EC. This sets minimum cocoa contents, e.g. 'chocolate' at least 35% total dry cocoa solids; the values are background knowledge and the Directive text was not re-read. Cadmium maximum levels apply: 0.80 mg/kg for chocolate with at least 50% total dry cocoa solids and 0.60 mg/kg for cocoa powder sold to consumers. These are in Regulation (EU) 2023/915, per the CBI summary; EUR-Lex was inaccessible. One authorised Article 13(5) claim is relevant: 'Cocoa flavanols help maintain the elasticity of blood vessels, which contributes to normal blood flow'. Its condition is 200 mg of cocoa flavanols (DP 1-10) per day, from cocoa beverages, dark chocolate or extract (Reg. (EU) 851/2013 and 2015/539, applicant Barry Callebaut; proprietary protection has expired, per the cocoa-flavanols monograph). Most ordinary chocolates cannot guarantee the dose [langer2011]. No cognitive claim for cocoa or chocolate is authorised. Nutrient-function claims (iron, magnesium) are legally available if the product is a 'source' (15% NRV per 100 g): 70-85% chocolate has about 11.9 mg iron and 228 mg magnesium per 100 g (USDA). Using 'Iron contributes to normal cognitive function' on a sugary chocolate would likely be judged misleading in spirit, and nutrient profiles that would restrict claims on HFSS foods were never adopted.", "us": "Chocolate products have FDA standards of identity (21 CFR Part 163, e.g. semisweet/bittersweet chocolate; minimum chocolate-liquor contents not re-verified here). On 3 February 2023 the FDA announced enforcement discretion for a qualified health claim that cocoa flavanols in high flavanol cocoa powder (at least 4% naturally conserved cocoa flavanols) may reduce the risk of cardiovascular disease, with the disclaimer that the evidence is very limited. The FDA page states the claim does not apply to regular cocoa powder or 'other food products made from cacao beans, such as chocolate' (FDA constituent update, accessed 2026-10-25). No federal limit for lead or cadmium in chocolate was identified. California Proposition 65 warnings apply above the MADLs (0.5 microgram/day lead, 4.1 microgram/day cadmium). A December 2022 Consumer Reports test found 23 of 28 dark chocolate bars exceeding a Prop 65-based level for lead or cadmium per 1 oz serving (NPR report, accessed 2026-10-25). Any cognitive structure/function claim would need competent and reliable evidence under FTC standards, which is lacking.", "morocco": "Uncertain. No Moroccan text specific to cocoa and chocolate product composition or naming was found (web search, 2026-10-25). General rules apply: Law 28-07 (ONSSA) and labelling Decree 2-12-389. Claims to prevent or treat disease are prohibited, and no list of authorised health claims exists (X6 Morocco synthesis). Moroccan contaminant limits for cadmium in chocolate were not found; Codex or EU-equivalent specifications should be assumed for imports and exports. Treat any brain or vascular claim as requiring prior consultation with ONSSA or the Ministry of Health.", "permitted_claims_examples": ["EU (Reg. (EU) 851/2013; 2015/539): 'Cocoa flavanols help maintain the elasticity of blood vessels, which contributes to normal blood flow.' Information shall be given that the beneficial effect is obtained with a daily intake of 200 mg of cocoa flavanols. Applies to dark chocolate only if the portion verifiably provides at least 200 mg flavanols (DP 1-10). Wording per cocoa-flavanols monograph; re-verify in the Union Register.", "EU (Reg. (EU) 432/2012): 'Iron contributes to normal cognitive function' - only if the chocolate is at least a source of iron (15% NRV per 100 g). Legally available for most 70-85% chocolates, but likely to mislead consumers about chocolate's own effect; Brain Food should not use it on a confection.", "EU (Reg. (EU) 432/2012): 'Magnesium contributes to normal psychological function' - only if at least a source of magnesium; same caution about misleading use.", "US (FDA enforcement discretion, 3 Feb 2023), high flavanol cocoa powder only, not chocolate: 'Cocoa flavanols in high flavanol cocoa powder may reduce the risk of cardiovascular disease, although the FDA has concluded that there is very limited scientific evidence for this claim.' (wording as summarised from the FDA page; verify exact text)"], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "Chocolate and cocoa are traditional foods. Chocolate enriched with high-flavanol cocoa from conventional processes is not novel. Highly purified (-)-epicatechin or new extraction processes would need checking in the EU Novel Food Catalogue (not verified). Adding vitamins or minerals to chocolate is regulated by Regulation (EC) 1925/2006; national restrictions on fortifying confectionery may apply (not verified).", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Moderate. Chocolate is highly acceptable and portion-controllable, but the flavanol dose is unpredictable: 93.5-651.1 mg/100 g across dark chocolates, not predicted by cocoa percentage [langer2011, alanon2016]. A typical 40 g portion therefore delivers about 40-260 mg flavanols with about 240 kcal. Reaching the EU claim dose of 200 mg in a small portion requires specially processed high-flavanol chocolate (about 10 g per the claim's authorisation conditions, as summarised in the cocoa-flavanols monograph). Reaching the 500 mg/day used in COSMOS or in meta-analysis subgroups [arabpour2026] needs about 77-535 g of ordinary dark chocolate (calculated from [langer2011]), which is impractical.", "formats": ["small (10-20 g) squares of verified high-flavanol, low-sugar dark chocolate", "70-99% dark chocolate (10 g/day 99% chocolate was feasible for 6 months [garciayu2022])", "chocolate coatings or inclusions in bars (dose dilution; flavanol loss during baking not verified)", "high-flavanol cocoa powder drinks as a lower-calorie alternative (see cocoa-flavanols monograph)", "berry plus dark chocolate snacks (concept; no placebo-controlled cognitive evidence [garciacordero2021])"], "stability": "Fermentation, roasting, alkalisation and conching reduce flavanols and epimerise (-)-epicatechin to less bioactive forms [alanon2016]. Alkalised (Dutch-processed) cocoa loses most flavanols (cocoa-flavanols monograph). Flavanol content should be verified per batch by a validated method (DP 1-10, e.g. the AOAC collaborative method), including at end of shelf life. Chocolate is shelf-stable for months, but fat bloom and oxidation limit storage in heat (relevant to Moroccan summers).", "taste": "High-flavanol cocoa is bitter and astringent. In semisweet chocolate, consumers tolerated replacement of up to about 80.7% of non-fat cocoa solids with high-flavanol cocoa powder before group rejection, with no difference between milk- and dark-chocolate likers [harwood2013]. Very high cocoa (85-99%) is accepted by a minority. Sweetening to mask bitterness adds sugar, and some low-sugar sweeteners carry their own labelling issues.", "bioavailability_matrix": "(-)-Epicatechin from chocolate is absorbed, peaking about 2 h after intake (cocoa-flavanols monograph). Whether milk in milk chocolate reduces absorption is debated (not reviewed here). The fat matrix slows gastric emptying. Methylxanthines are well absorbed; theobromine rose in plasma during 30 days of 24 g/day dark chocolate and returned to baseline within 3 weeks of stopping [sumiyoshi2019].", "dose_feasibility": "Methylxanthine (alertness) dose: feasible but small. 40 g gives about 32 mg caffeine plus 321 mg theobromine (USDA), similar to the 19 mg plus 250 mg that was active in [smit2004]. Flavanol dose: 200 mg/day is feasible only with high-flavanol chocolate; 500-900 mg/day is not feasible in a reasonable chocolate portion. Calories: 20 g gives about 120 kcal and 40 g about 240 kcal (USDA). Cadmium: at the EU maximum (0.80 mg/kg), 40 g/day exceeds the cadmium TWI for a 70 kg adult, so portions or specifications must be tighter.", "cost_availability": "Widely available. Cocoa is not grown in Morocco, so all cocoa is imported (sourcing origin affects cadmium [abt2018]). Specialty high-flavanol cocoa ingredients are concentrated in a few suppliers (cocoa-flavanols monograph).", "cost_evidence": "Premium 85% dark chocolate (Lindt Excellence 85%, 100 g) is listed at about EUR 36.90-46.90/kg in Benelux and Austrian online stores (Albert Heijn NL/BE, Gurkerl AT; accessed via search 2026-10-25). That is about EUR 1.50-1.90 per 40 g or EUR 0.75-0.95 per 20 g. Moroccan retail prices and high-flavanol chocolate prices were not researched."}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 3, "effect_magnitude": 2, "safety": 6, "reproducibility": 2, "food_suitability": 5, "cost_access": 6, "rationale": "Evidence quality 3: the best cognitive rows for healthy young adults are C (acute alertness from methylxanthines; inconsistent acute flavanol effects), the student row is D, the older-adult chocolate trials are C null, and cohorts are C. The vascular row is also C for chocolate as sold; it reaches B only for flavanol-verified cocoa products, which are graded in the cocoa-flavanols monograph. Effect magnitude 2: acute alertness is a small caffeine-sized effect; the largest chronic cognitive gain is about 12-13 s on TMT-B (d about 0.3) in unblinded or pooled data, and the two real-chocolate trials in older adults were null. Safety 6: a common food, but daily portions bring cadmium and lead (35-43% of US products exceeded Prop 65 per serving; 40 g/day at the EU limit exceeds the cadmium TWI), 240 kcal and about 10 g sugar per 40 g, methylxanthines and weight-gain associations. Reproducibility 2: positive trials are small, single-laboratory, often single-blind or unblinded and sometimes manufacturer-funded (Meiji, Fazer), while blinded real-chocolate trials are null. Food suitability 5: highly palatable and portionable, but the flavanol dose is unpredictable, cocoa % is not a proxy, and high-flavanol bitterness limits acceptance. Cost/access 6: about EUR 0.75-1.90 per 20-40 g of premium chocolate; specialty high-flavanol products cost more, and cocoa must be imported into Morocco."}, "research_gaps": ["No adequately powered, double-blind trial of a realistic chocolate portion on cognition in students or young knowledge workers, with a control matched for methylxanthines, so that flavanol and methylxanthine effects can be separated", "Trials comparing high-flavanol chocolate with matched low-flavanol chocolate (not white chocolate) at constant caffeine and theobromine", "Dose-response of cadmium and lead exposure from daily chocolate in high consumers, including Moroccan consumers and imported product supply", "Flavanol content of chocolates sold in Morocco and the EU, and losses through conching, storage and hot-climate distribution", "Effects of evening chocolate on objectively measured sleep in students", "Registered trial NCT02996578 (polyphenol-rich chocolate bar and cocoa powder vs low-polyphenol products, cognition, n=16, completed 2018) has no posted results (possible publication bias)", "Independent replication of the Japanese industry-linked imaging and NGF findings"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 3.8, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": "C", "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": "C", "best_grade_sleep_stress": "C", "best_grade_surrogate": "C", "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"alertness": "C", "working_memory": "C", "cognitive_decline": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 13, "n_studies": 28, "n_verified": 28, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 5, "n_systematic_reviews": 4, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["inconsistent", "insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "eggs", "name": "Eggs", "category": "J", "category_name": "Whole foods", "aliases": ["hen's eggs", "whole eggs", "egg yolk", "omega-3 / DHA-enriched eggs", "lutein-enriched eggs", "egg powder", "oeufs (FR)", "bayd (AR)"], "food_sources": ["hen's eggs (whole, boiled, scrambled, omelette)", "egg yolk (concentrates choline, lutein/zeaxanthin, vitamin B12, fat-soluble vitamins and all of the cholesterol)", "nutritionally enriched eggs produced by feeding hens fish oil, algae, marigold extract or selenium (n-3 PUFA-, lutein- or selenium-enriched eggs)", "whole-egg powder used in bakery and food manufacturing"], "active_constituents": ["choline, mostly as phosphatidylcholine (about 150 mg per large egg by standard US composition tables; value commonly cited but not re-verified in this unit because the NIH ODS page returned HTTP 403)", "lutein and zeaxanthin in a lipid-rich, highly bioavailable yolk matrix (331-964 µg per yolk in the eggs used by [wenzel2006], depending on hen feed)", "vitamin B12, riboflavin, vitamin D, selenium, iodine (feed-dependent)", "high-quality protein (tryptophan, tyrosine) and small amounts of DHA (higher in n-3-enriched eggs)", "dietary cholesterol (concentrated in the yolk)"], "one_line_verdict": "Eggs are a cheap, nutrient-dense source of choline and highly bioavailable lutein that reliably raises macular pigment, but no randomized trial shows that eating eggs improves cognition in healthy adults, observational links to slower cognitive ageing are small, inconsistent and partly egg-industry-funded, and for students or young knowledge workers there is simply no evidence.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. Eggs supply choline (mostly phosphatidylcholine), lutein and zeaxanthin in a lipid matrix that is absorbed well, vitamin B12, riboflavin and high-quality protein. The only effects established in randomized or controlled trials are on biomarkers. Six eggs per week for 12 weeks, or 2-4 yolks per day for 5 weeks, raised serum zeaxanthin and macular pigment optical density (MPOD) [wenzel2006, vishwanathan2009]. Pooled across 5 trials (296 participants) the MPOD gain was a weighted mean difference of +0.037 units (95% CI 0.004-0.069) [khalighisikaroudi2021]. MPOD marks retinal (and putatively brain) xanthophyll status; it is not a cognitive outcome. Cognition. No randomized trial of whole eggs has measured cognition in healthy adults. The adult data are: an uncontrolled 8-week pre-post arm (2 eggs/day, 15 Thai military men, reaction time 17% faster) described only in a review [sultan2025]; a 12-week trial of an egg-yolk choline supplement in 41 analysed Japanese adults aged 60-80, run by the manufacturer, with mixed results (better verbal memory, worse processing speed) [yamashita2023]; and a null 36-week trial of an egg-protein hydrolysate [adams2024]. A registered trial of eggs on cognition in 106 adults aged 50-75, funded by the Egg Nutrition Center and completed in 2020, has posted no results (NCT06465888). In children, 9 months of whole-egg powder did not change executive function [oconnor2022], and 1 egg/day for 6 months did not improve development in Malawian infants [prado2020]. Observational evidence in older adults is mixed. A 2026 dose-response pooling of 21 studies (99,453 people, mostly Asian and US) linked about 50 g/day (one egg) to a lower risk of cognitive decline (RR 0.91, 0.83-0.99), with a U-shape [liu2026]. Finnish men showed no association with dementia (HR 0.89 per half egg/day, 0.78-1.01) but better executive test scores [ylilauri2017]. Representative US samples found no association after adjustment [bishop2019, an2022]. Two US cohorts reported large inverse associations with Alzheimer's dementia (HR 0.53 and 0.73), and both were partly funded by the egg industry [pan2024, oh2026]. Chinese cohorts suggest benefit at low-to-moderate intake but not at high intake [li2022, sukik2022, ren2025]. Residual confounding, reverse causation and heterogeneous tests keep certainty low (grade C, older adults). Magnitude and population. If causal, the benefit is small (about a 9% relative risk reduction) and limited to ageing populations. For healthy young adults, students and knowledge workers there are no relevant studies (grade D). Safety context. Up to one egg/day was not associated with cardiovascular disease in a meta-analysis of 1.7 million people [drouinchartier2020]. Pooled US cohorts, however, linked each additional half egg/day to HR 1.06 for CVD through dietary cholesterol [zhong2019]. Four eggs/day did not raise TMAO, whereas choline bitartrate supplements did [wilcox2021]. Egg allergy (challenge-verified in about 0.8% of Europeans) is the main exclusion [spolidoro2023].", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Eggs are best understood as a convenient way to close nutrient gaps, not as an enhancer. Choline intakes in many Western adults fall below the EFSA Adequate Intake of 400 mg/day [efsa2016]. One egg supplies roughly a third of that, and eggs are among the few rich sources for people who eat little meat or liver. Eggs also supply vitamin B12 for lacto-ovo vegetarians, plus lutein. The Chinese data hint that egg intake matters most where diets are poorer: the association with cognition was stronger in people with low iron intake [sukik2022] and in rural older adults [ren2025]. This is consistent with correcting low intake, but it is observational. No trial has tested eggs for enhancement in adequately nourished, healthy young adults, and the egg-yolk choline trial in older Japanese adults was small and contradictory [yamashita2023]. Infant trials in low-income settings, where eggs might plausibly correct deficits, were largely null for development [prado2020], with weaker positive signals [ernyey2024]. These child findings must not be extrapolated to adults. Honest conclusion: for a well-fed student or office worker, eggs add nutritional adequacy but no demonstrated cognitive gain.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Common claims that eggs are a 'brain food', 'boost memory' or 'protect against Alzheimer's' rest on cohort associations in people over 60. Some of those cohorts were analysed with egg-industry money (the American Egg Board funded the AHS-2 analysis [oh2026]; an Egg Nutrition Center grant funded the choline quantification in the Rush Memory and Aging Project analysis [pan2024]). They also rest on mechanistic reasoning about choline and lutein. The defensible statements are narrower. (1) Eggs provide choline, vitamin B12, riboflavin and protein. In the EU, nutrient-function claims such as 'Vitamin B12 contributes to normal psychological function' or 'Choline contributes to normal lipid metabolism' can be used only if the analysed content meets the 'source of' conditions. (2) Eating eggs raises macular pigment, a biomarker of eye health; no EU eye or cognition claim is authorised for lutein. No authorised EU, US or Moroccan claim links eggs, choline or lutein to memory, concentration or dementia prevention, and a 'protects against Alzheimer's' statement would be a prohibited disease claim in the EU and Morocco. The honest marketing position: 'a nutrient-dense whole food that fits a brain-healthy dietary pattern', with no promise of better cognitive performance.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Eggs deliver choline mainly as phosphatidylcholine, which raises plasma choline and betaine. Choline is a precursor of acetylcholine, of membrane phosphatidylcholine and of methyl groups (via betaine) for homocysteine remethylation. Yolk lutein and zeaxanthin are absorbed efficiently with yolk lipids and accumulate in the macula (and, in post-mortem studies, the brain); MPOD is proposed as a proxy for brain lutein. B12, riboflavin and protein amino acids support normal nervous-system function when intake would otherwise be low. Each link is plausible and some are measured in humans (plasma choline, serum xanthophylls, MPOD), but no study has shown that these biomarker changes translate into better cognition in healthy adults. Egg phosphatidylcholine, unlike choline bitartrate, did not raise TMAO.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Whole egg or yolk -> intestinal absorption of phosphatidylcholine, lutein/zeaxanthin (with yolk lipid), B12 and amino acids", "detail": "Serum zeaxanthin rose 36-82% and lutein 16-24% with 2-4 yolks/day [vishwanathan2009]; serum zeaxanthin rose with 6 eggs/week [wenzel2006]; pooled serum lutein +0.150 µmol/L [khalighisikaroudi2021].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Serum xanthophylls -> macular pigment optical density (MPOD)", "detail": "MPOD rose after 5-12 weeks of egg feeding, mainly in people with low baseline MPOD [wenzel2006, vishwanathan2009]; pooled WMD +0.037 [khalighisikaroudi2021].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "MPOD / retinal lutein -> brain lutein -> neural efficiency", "detail": "MPOD correlates with brain lutein in decedent studies (reviewed, not appraised here); causal effect of egg-derived lutein on brain lutein or cognition is not shown.", "evidence_level": "speculative"}, {"step": "Choline/phosphatidylcholine -> acetylcholine synthesis, membrane phospholipids, betaine-mediated methylation", "detail": "Plasma choline rises with egg-yolk choline [yamashita2023]; KIHD links phosphatidylcholine intake to lower dementia risk and better verbal fluency/memory, but this is observational [ylilauri2019]; brain acetylcholine effects in humans are not measured.", "evidence_level": "plausible"}, {"step": "Gut microbial conversion of choline to TMA/TMAO (potential vascular harm)", "detail": "Four eggs/day for 4 weeks did not raise fasting TMAO or platelet reactivity in healthy young adults, while choline bitartrate supplements did [wilcox2021].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Measured human cognitive outcome", "detail": "Only observational associations in older adults (small, inconsistent, U-shaped) [liu2026, sultan2025]; no controlled whole-egg trial in healthy adults; null whole-egg trial in preadolescents [oconnor2022].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["cholinergic neurotransmission (acetylcholine precursor supply)", "membrane phosphatidylcholine synthesis", "one-carbon metabolism (betaine, homocysteine remethylation)", "xanthophyll (lutein/zeaxanthin) accumulation in retina and brain; antioxidant/anti-inflammatory hypotheses", "vitamin B12 / riboflavin adequacy for nervous-system function"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults 18-40, including students and knowledge workers; no relevant controlled trial identified", "dose_or_intake": "not studied (one uncontrolled arm: 2 eggs/day)", "duration": "8 weeks (single uncontrolled arm)", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No randomized or controlled trial of whole eggs on any cognitive outcome in healthy young adults. The only data are the control (regular-egg) arm of a Thai trial in 15 military men (mean age 21), extracted as a pre-post study by a 2025 systematic review: reaction time on a Go/NoGo task fell 17% with no change in false alarms. Practice effects cannot be excluded. A registered egg-cognition trial (NCT06465888, adults 50-75) is unpublished.", "effect_estimate": "not reported (uncontrolled within-group reaction time -17%) [sultan2025]", "certainty_notes": "No comparator, n=15, practice effects, report not indexed in PubMed (known only via the review). Cannot be graded above D.", "key_study_ids": ["sultan2025"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "other", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults; acute (single-meal) cognitive effects of an egg breakfast", "dose_or_intake": "not studied", "duration": "single meal", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "Searches for egg-breakfast or single-meal egg trials with cognitive outcomes found none. Any acute effect of an egg-based breakfast would be confounded by protein/energy content and is not egg-specific.", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "Absence of evidence; no study to grade.", "key_study_ids": []}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_decline", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Community-dwelling adults mostly >=55-65 y in the US, Finland, China and Thailand; cognitively healthy at baseline", "dose_or_intake": "habitual intake from <1 egg/week to >1 egg/day (FFQ, food records or 24-h recalls)", "duration": "2-22 years of follow-up", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Dose-response pooling of 13 longitudinal and 8 cross-sectional studies: about 50 g/day associated with lower risk of cognitive decline, U-shaped (lowest risk at 50-60 g/day). Individual cohorts disagree: KIHD (Finnish men) better executive test scores; Lee 2021 slower memory decline only at intermediate intake; Rancho Bernardo benefits in one sex per analysis (episodic memory in men, category fluency in women); CHNS stronger in low-iron diets; Zhejiang benefit only below 6 eggs/week; HRS and NHANES null after adjustment; two Chinese studies cited in the 2025 review show worse outcomes at the highest intakes.", "effect_estimate": "Pooled RR 0.91 (95% CI 0.83-0.99) [liu2026]; Zhejiang RR 0.82 (0.76-0.89) for 0.1-2.9 eggs/week, no association >=6/week [li2022]; CHNS Q4 vs Q1 beta 0.92 (0.43-1.41) global score points [sukik2022]; HRS no association [bishop2019]", "certainty_notes": "Observational (starts low); downgraded for inconsistency (null in the US national samples, U-shape, sex-specific findings), risk of confounding (healthy-user effects, education, overall diet), reverse causation and outcome heterogeneity. Some analyses are industry-linked. Small absolute effects.", "key_study_ids": ["liu2026", "sultan2025", "ylilauri2017", "bishop2019", "an2022", "lee2021", "kritzsilverstein2023", "kritzsilverstein2024", "sukik2022", "li2022", "ren2025"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "dementia_risk", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Dementia-free middle-aged men (KIHD, 42-60 y), older US adults (Rush MAP mean age 81; AHS-2 Adventists aged 65+ linked to Medicare); Chinese case-control", "dose_or_intake": ">1 egg/week up to >=5 eggs/week vs never/rarely; per half egg/day", "duration": "6.7-21.9 years", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "KIHD: no association with incident dementia or AD. Rush MAP (n=1024, 280 AD cases): >1 egg/week associated with about half the hazard of Alzheimer's dementia, with less AD pathology at autopsy; 39% of the effect was statistically mediated by choline. AHS-2 (n=39,498, 2858 AD): graded inverse association. Guangzhou case-control: daily vs monthly intake inversely associated, with recall-bias risk.", "effect_estimate": "KIHD HR 0.89 (0.78-1.01) per 0.5 egg/day [ylilauri2017]; MAP HR 0.53 (0.34-0.83) for >1/week [pan2024]; AHS-2 HR 0.73 (0.60-0.89) for >=5/week vs never/rarely [oh2026]; case-control OR monthly vs daily 4.82 (1.90-12.27) [igbinigie2024]", "certainty_notes": "Observational, imprecise in MAP, large effect sizes implausible for a single food, and confounding is likely: in AHS-2 the never-egg group probably includes vegans, so this is not a test of eggs alone. Both positive US analyses received egg-industry funding (American Egg Board; Egg Nutrition Center). The only non-industry long-term cohort (KIHD) was null. Grade C at best.", "key_study_ids": ["ylilauri2017", "pan2024", "oh2026", "igbinigie2024", "ylilauri2019"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Healthy Japanese adults aged 60-80 without dementia (egg-yolk choline supplement, not whole eggs)", "dose_or_intake": "300 mg/day egg-yolk choline (capsule/supplement) vs choline-free egg-yolk placebo", "duration": "12 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Verbal memory scores (Cognitrax) improved more with egg-yolk choline at 6 and 12 weeks, but processing speed and symbol-digit coding were worse than placebo at 6 weeks, and physical quality of life was lower. 19 of 60 randomized participants were excluded from the analysis.", "effect_estimate": "Between-group differences reported only as p<0.05; no standardized effect size in the abstract [yamashita2023]", "certainty_notes": "Single small trial run by employees of the manufacturer (Kewpie). Per-protocol analysis after 32% exclusion, multiple outcomes with opposite-direction effects. Tests an egg-derived ingredient, not eggs. Grade C, direction inconsistent.", "key_study_ids": ["yamashita2023"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "other", "population_detail": "Overweight/obese adults aged 60-75 with elevated subjective cognitive failures (egg-protein hydrolysate NWT-03, not whole eggs)", "dose_or_intake": "5.7 g/day egg-protein hydrolysate powder", "duration": "36 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "No overall intervention effect on CANTAB psychomotor speed, executive function or memory, on cerebral blood flow (ASL-MRI) or on serum BDNF. Post-hoc analyses found better multitasking and spatial working memory in women only.", "effect_estimate": "Overall effects not significant; women-only post-hoc: multitasking latency -74 ms (95% CI -134 to -15) [adams2024]", "certainty_notes": "Small (n=44), single trial of a proprietary hydrolysate. The sex-specific findings are post hoc. Not informative about whole eggs.", "key_study_ids": ["adams2024"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "other", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Biomarker: macular pigment optical density (MPOD) in adults 24-80 y, including older statin users with low MPOD; NOT a cognitive outcome", "dose_or_intake": "6 eggs/week to 4 yolks/day; standard or lutein-enriched eggs", "duration": "5-12 weeks per arm (trials up to 1 year in the meta-analysis)", "grade": "B", "direction": "benefit", "effect_summary": "Egg consumption raises serum zeaxanthin (and lutein, less consistently) and MPOD, especially when baseline MPOD is low. Serum LDL cholesterol did not rise in these trials. MPOD is a biomarker of retinal xanthophyll status and only a hypothesised proxy for brain lutein.", "effect_estimate": "MPOD WMD +0.037 (95% CI 0.004-0.069), 5 trials, 296 participants, no detectable heterogeneity [khalighisikaroudi2021]; up to +50% MPOD with 4 yolks/day in low-MPOD older adults [vishwanathan2009]", "certainty_notes": "Graded B for the biomarker only: consistent direction across several small trials with a plausible yolk dose-response, but risk of bias (non-randomized or unblinded designs) and imprecision (pooled CI lower bound 0.004). The effect is an eye biomarker and must not be presented as cognitive benefit.", "key_study_ids": ["khalighisikaroudi2021", "wenzel2006", "vishwanathan2009"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "children_adolescents", "population_detail": "US preadolescents aged 9-13 (whole-egg powder in foods)", "dose_or_intake": "foods formulated with whole-egg powder replacing usual meals/snacks vs milk powder vs gelatin placebo", "duration": "9 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Whole-egg products did not change NIH Toolbox mental flexibility, working memory or inhibitory control. The milk-powder arm improved inhibitory control.", "effect_estimate": "Egg arm: no significant group x time effect; milk arm d = 0.44 (P = 0.049) [oconnor2022]", "certainty_notes": "Single double-blind RCT of moderate size (n=122 analysed). The program was supported by an American Egg Board grant (reported by the university; the paper's funding statement was not read). The null egg result runs against the sponsor's hypothesis, which reduces concern about sponsor bias. Children only; do not extrapolate to adults.", "key_study_ids": ["oconnor2022"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "children_adolescents", "population_detail": "Infants and toddlers in low-income settings (Malawi 6-15 months; Burkina Faso 18-33 months)", "dose_or_intake": "1 egg/day provided to households", "duration": "6 months (plus ~4-month post-trial follow-up in Burkina Faso)", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "In the Mazira RCT (n=660), 1 egg/day for 6 months did not change global development or eye-tracking memory/attention scores, apart from fewer fine-motor delays; effect modification suggested benefit only in less vulnerable households. A post-trial follow-up of Un Oeuf (n=244) found lower odds of gross-motor and personal-social delays among consistent egg consumers, using a parent-reported screener.", "effect_estimate": "Mazira: no difference in primary scores; fine-motor delay 10.6% vs 16.5%, PR 0.59 (0.38-0.91) [prado2020]; Un Oeuf: gross-motor OR 0.13 (P=0.02) among consistent consumers [ernyey2024]", "certainty_notes": "The stronger, blinded RCT was null on its primary outcomes; the positive follow-up is a per-consumption analysis with a parent-report tool. This is an LMIC deficiency context, not applicable to adults.", "key_study_ids": ["prado2020", "ernyey2024"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "vascular_health", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Adults in US, European and Asian prospective cohorts (cardiovascular disease as an indirect determinant of vascular cognitive impairment)", "dose_or_intake": "per half egg/day or per 1 egg/day", "duration": "17.5-32 years", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "The updated meta-analysis (1.7 million participants) found no association of 1 egg/day with CVD overall, and an inverse association in Asian cohorts. Pooled individual data from 6 US cohorts found a small dose-dependent increase in CVD and mortality per half egg/day, explained by dietary cholesterol. Relevant mainly as a safety constraint on recommending high intakes.", "effect_estimate": "RR 0.98 (0.93-1.03) per egg/day, I2 62% [drouinchartier2020]; HR 1.06 (1.03-1.10) per half egg/day, ARD 1.11% [zhong2019]", "certainty_notes": "Observational; the two large analyses conflict. Moderate intake (up to 1/day) appears neutral in most populations; higher intake in people with dyslipidaemia or diabetes is unresolved.", "key_study_ids": ["drouinchartier2020", "zhong2019"]}], "acute_matrix": [], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "21.9 years (cognitive tests at 4 years)", "population": "2497 dementia-free Finnish men aged 42-60 (KIHD)", "outcome": "Incident dementia/AD; Trail Making and Verbal Fluency", "effect_estimate": "Dementia HR 0.89 (0.78-1.01) per 0.5 egg/day; better executive test scores with higher egg intake", "evidence_quality": "Prospective cohort, 4-day food records, register diagnoses; moderate quality", "limitations": "Men only; single baseline diet record; cognitive tests in 480 men only", "study_ids": ["ylilauri2017"]}, {"duration": "mean 15.3 years", "population": "39,498 Seventh-day Adventists aged 65+ linked to Medicare (AHS-2)", "outcome": "Incident Alzheimer's disease", "effect_estimate": "HR 0.73 (0.60-0.89) for >=5/week vs never/rarely; zero intake HR 1.22 (1.11-1.34) vs 10 g/day", "evidence_quality": "Large cohort; claims-based AD diagnosis; low certainty due to confounding", "limitations": "Never-egg group likely enriched for vegans; American Egg Board-funded analysis; FFQ once", "study_ids": ["oh2026"]}, {"duration": "mean 6.7 years", "population": "1024 older adults (mean 81 y), Rush Memory and Aging Project", "outcome": "Alzheimer's dementia; AD pathology at autopsy (n=578)", "effect_estimate": "HR 0.53 (0.34-0.83) for >1 egg/week; AD pathology HR 0.51 (0.35-0.76)", "evidence_quality": "Well-characterised cohort but small and imprecise", "limitations": "Few non-consumers (n=111); industry co-funding (Egg Nutrition Center); implausibly large effect for one food", "study_ids": ["pan2024"]}, {"duration": "2 years", "population": "3835 US adults aged 65+ (Health and Retirement Study)", "outcome": "Working memory, executive function, global mental status and their 2-year change", "effect_estimate": "No association after covariate adjustment", "evidence_quality": "Representative sample; moderate quality", "limitations": "Short follow-up; telephone cognitive tests; FFQ after cognitive baseline", "study_ids": ["bishop2019"]}, {"duration": "6 years", "population": "9028 Chinese adults aged 60+ without cognitive impairment (Zhejiang)", "outcome": "Cognitive impairment by MMSE", "effect_estimate": "RR 0.82 (0.76-0.89) for 0.1-2.9 eggs/week; RR 0.91 (0.84-0.99) for 3.0-5.9/week; no association >=6/week", "evidence_quality": "Large cohort; screening-based outcome", "limitations": "Simple questionnaire for eggs; MMSE-defined impairment; residual confounding by poverty", "study_ids": ["li2022"]}, {"duration": "5-12 weeks per arm", "population": "Adults 24-80 y (women; older statin users with low MPOD)", "outcome": "MPOD (biomarker)", "effect_estimate": "Pooled WMD +0.037 (0.004-0.069); up to +50% in low-baseline older adults with 4 yolks/day", "evidence_quality": "Small RCTs/controlled trials; consistent", "limitations": "Biomarker only; small samples; no cognitive outcomes", "study_ids": ["khalighisikaroudi2021", "wenzel2006", "vishwanathan2009"]}, {"duration": "9 months", "population": "122 US preadolescents aged 9-13", "outcome": "NIH Toolbox executive functions", "effect_estimate": "Whole-egg arm null; milk arm d=0.44 for inhibitory control", "evidence_quality": "Double-blind RCT, moderate size", "limitations": "Children; egg-industry-supported program; egg delivered as powder in processed foods", "study_ids": ["oconnor2022"]}, {"duration": "6 months", "population": "660 Malawian infants aged 6-9 months at enrolment", "outcome": "MDAT development scores, eye-tracking memory and attention", "effect_estimate": "No overall difference; fine-motor delay PR 0.59 (0.38-0.91)", "evidence_quality": "Large blinded-assessor RCT; high quality", "limitations": "Infants in a low-income setting; not generalisable to adults", "study_ids": ["prado2020"]}, {"duration": "follow-up 2-22 years across studies", "population": "21 observational studies, 99,453 people, mainly Asian and US older adults", "outcome": "Cognitive decline/impairment", "effect_estimate": "RR 0.91 (0.83-0.99) at about 50 g/day; U-shaped curve", "evidence_quality": "Meta-analysis of observational studies; low certainty", "limitations": "Mixed designs and outcomes; abstract-level appraisal only; authors' conclusion ('necessary') overstates the data", "study_ids": ["liu2026"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Eggs are a conventional food with a long safety record. The relevant risks are allergy (egg is one of the most common food allergens, especially in children), microbiological hazards (Salmonella in raw or undercooked eggs), and cardiometabolic uncertainty at high intakes because of dietary cholesterol. Up to one egg per day appears neutral for cardiovascular disease in most populations [drouinchartier2020], but pooled US cohorts show small risk increases per extra half egg/day that track dietary cholesterol [zhong2019]. Eggs did not raise TMAO, unlike choline bitartrate supplements [wilcox2021]. Two Chinese studies found worse cognitive outcomes at the highest intakes (U-shape) [ren2025, li2022], which argues against 'more is better'.", "adverse_effects": ["IgE-mediated egg allergy (self-reported 2.4%, challenge-verified 0.8% in Europe), including anaphylaxis [spolidoro2023]", "Salmonella infection from raw or undercooked eggs (homemade mayonnaise, mousses, raw-egg drinks)", "Raised LDL cholesterol in a subset of 'hyper-responders' to dietary cholesterol (not quantified in this unit)", "Possible small increase in CVD risk at higher intakes in US cohorts [zhong2019]"], "contraindications": ["Egg allergy (yolk and white proteins; egg powder and egg-derived ingredients included)", "Raw/undercooked eggs for pregnant women, infants, older adults and immunocompromised people (Salmonella)"], "drug_interactions": ["No clinically relevant pharmacological interactions known for normal egg intake", "People on statins or with familial hypercholesterolaemia: total dietary cholesterol is a dietary-management question for their physician (statin users' LDL did not rise with 2-4 yolks/day for 5 weeks [vishwanathan2009])", "Egg-derived vaccine components (influenza, yellow fever) are a medical, not a dietary, issue"], "upper_limits": "No tolerable upper intake level for eggs. Choline UL 3.5 g/day (US IOM value, widely cited; not re-verified in this unit) is far above dietary intake. Practical guidance from cohort data: up to about 1 egg/day for the general population; individual advice for people with diabetes or dyslipidaemia.", "sleep_impact": "No known effect on sleep; eggs contain no stimulants.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Decades of observational exposure (up to 32 years) in cardiovascular cohorts; controlled feeding trials of 2-4 eggs/yolks/day lasted 4 weeks to 9 months.", "daily_use_appropriate": "yes", "special_populations": "Children: egg allergy is most common in infancy/early childhood. Pregnancy: eggs are a useful choline source (EFSA AI 480 mg/day) but must be fully cooked. People with diabetes: some cohorts suggest higher CVD risk with high egg intake (not reviewed in depth here). Chronic kidney disease: phosphorus and protein load need individual dietetic advice. Vegans: not applicable.", "specialist_review_needed": ["Allergist (allergen labelling and cross-contact controls)", "Food-safety microbiologist (pasteurisation of liquid/powdered egg ingredients)", "Cardiologist/lipidologist if intake above 1 egg/day is promoted"], "hard_safety_gate": "pass", "gate_reason": "Ordinary food with well-characterised hazards (allergen, Salmonella) that are managed by standard labelling and hygiene. Moderate intake is not associated with cardiovascular harm. Products should not encourage intake well above 1 egg/day."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Conventional food (shell eggs under marketing standards and the hygiene package; egg is a mandatory allergen under Regulation (EU) 1169/2011 Annex II). No authorised health claim for eggs, choline, lutein or egg-derived ingredients relates to cognition, memory or dementia; disease-risk claims about Alzheimer's would be prohibited. Authorised nutrient-function claims (Regulation (EU) 432/2012) are usable only if the analysed content meets the 'source of' condition: e.g. vitamin B12 (nervous system, psychological function, reduction of tiredness), riboflavin, choline (lipid metabolism, homocysteine metabolism, liver function; at least 82.5 mg choline per 100 g or per portion), protein, selenium, vitamin D. Iodine 'contributes to normal cognitive function' would require analysed iodine >=15% NRV per 100 g, which depends on hen feed (uncertain). Enriched eggs could carry the DHA brain-function claim only if they reach >=40 mg DHA per 100 g and per 100 kcal (to be verified by analysis). Omega-3 or lutein enrichment through feed is not novel food when using authorised feed additives (not verified case by case).", "us": "Conventional food (FDA; USDA egg products inspection). Egg is a major allergen under FALCPA. No FDA-authorised or qualified health claim links eggs to cognition. Structure/function statements on conventional foods (e.g. 'choline supports brain development') must derive from nutritive value and be truthful and not misleading; FTC requires competent and reliable scientific evidence, which does not exist for cognitive enhancement claims in adults. Eggs are reported to qualify under FDA's updated 'healthy' nutrient-content claim rule (2024); this was not verified in this unit.", "morocco": "Conventional food under Law 28-07 on food safety, enforced by ONSSA (establishment authorisation, hygiene, labelling under Decree 2-12-389 with mandatory Arabic). Disease claims are banned. Nutrition claims follow the positive list of Joint Order 281-16. No authorised list of health claims was found, so any cognition claim is legally uncertain (see X6 regulatory synthesis). Egg products need ONSSA-approved establishments; poultry sector specifics were not researched here.", "permitted_claims_examples": ["Vitamin B12 contributes to normal psychological function. (EU Reg. 432/2012; food must be at least a source of vitamin B12)", "Vitamin B12 contributes to normal functioning of the nervous system. (EU Reg. 432/2012; same condition)", "Choline contributes to normal lipid metabolism. (EU Reg. 432/2012; at least 82.5 mg choline per 100 g, 100 ml or single portion)", "Riboflavin contributes to normal functioning of the nervous system. (EU Reg. 432/2012; food must be at least a source of riboflavin)"], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "None for shell eggs or whole-egg powder (long history of consumption). Egg-protein hydrolysates (e.g. NWT-03) and concentrated egg-yolk choline extracts may need novel-food assessment in the EU or NDI/GRAS review in the US depending on processing and use; not checked case by case.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Excellent as a whole food (boiled eggs, egg-based breakfasts, snack packs). As an ingredient, whole-egg powder works in bakery, pasta and protein snacks, but the only controlled trial of egg powder in foods showed no cognitive effect [oconnor2022].", "formats": ["hard-boiled peeled eggs (ready-to-eat snack packs)", "egg-based breakfast items (omelette cups, shakshuka-style meals relevant to Moroccan cuisine)", "whole-egg or yolk powder in baked goods or protein snacks", "n-3 / lutein-enriched shell eggs (via hen feed)"], "stability": "Shell eggs: several weeks refrigerated. Pasteurised liquid egg and egg powder: long shelf life. Lutein and DHA in yolk are sensitive to oxidation and heat during drying (degree not quantified here).", "taste": "Widely accepted; neutral flavour in formulations. Sulphur notes in reheated boiled eggs limit some ready-to-eat formats.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Yolk lipids make egg lutein/zeaxanthin highly bioavailable relative to its low content [wenzel2006]. Eggs also improve carotenoid absorption from co-eaten vegetables (trial identified in search, not appraised). Egg choline as phosphatidylcholine did not raise TMAO, unlike choline bitartrate [wilcox2021].", "dose_feasibility": "The intakes studied (1 egg/day in cohorts; 6 eggs/week to 4 yolks/day in MPOD trials) are achievable in food. One egg gives about a third of the choline AI. However, there is no cognitive efficacy dose to target, because no trial has shown an effect.", "cost_availability": "Among the cheapest animal-protein foods globally and widely available in Morocco, the EU and the US.", "cost_evidence": "US average retail price for Grade A large eggs about US$2.27 per dozen in August 2026 (officialdata.org republishing BLS series APU0000708111; not verified directly at BLS; accessed 2026-10-25). EU and Moroccan prices not researched here."}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 3, "effect_magnitude": 2, "safety": 8, "reproducibility": 3, "food_suitability": 9, "cost_access": 9, "rationale": "Evidence quality 3: grade C (observational, inconsistent, partly industry-funded) for older adults and D for healthy young adults; grade B applies only to the MPOD biomarker. Effect magnitude 2: at best about 9% lower relative risk of cognitive decline in pooled cohorts; MPOD change small; no cognitive effect in any controlled trial. Safety 8: ordinary food; allergen and Salmonella hazards are well managed; cholesterol caveat above about 1 egg/day. Reproducibility 3: cohort results flip by sex, country and intake level; null results in US national samples and in the best independent cohort. Food suitability 9: it is a food, easy to include. Cost/access 9: inexpensive and ubiquitous."}, "research_gaps": ["No adequately powered RCT of whole eggs on cognition in healthy adults of any age; none in students or knowledge workers.", "The Egg Nutrition Center-funded RCT NCT06465888 (eggs, egg whites, yolks and n-3 eggs vs control; NeuroTracker; adults 50-75; completed March 2020) has no posted or published results, a possible publication-bias signal.", "No acute (single-meal) egg trial with cognitive endpoints.", "Whether egg-induced MPOD increases translate to brain lutein and to cognition is untested in intervention studies.", "Independent (non-industry) replication of the large inverse egg-Alzheimer associations, with analyses separating egg avoiders who are vegan from omnivores who rarely eat eggs.", "Choline status and egg intake in Moroccan adults and students (no data found)."], "_derived": {"composite_score": 5.0, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "pass", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": "B", "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"dementia_risk": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 10, "n_studies": 28, "n_verified": 28, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 12, "n_systematic_reviews": 3, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["benefit", "inconsistent", "insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "olive-oil-as-food", "name": "Extra-virgin olive oil as a dietary fat", "category": "J", "category_name": "Whole foods", "aliases": ["extra-virgin olive oil", "EVOO", "virgin olive oil", "zit zitoun (Moroccan Arabic)", "huile d'olive vierge extra", "Picholine marocaine olive oil", "high-phenolic / early-harvest olive oil"], "food_sources": ["extra-virgin and virgin olive oil (cold mechanical extraction)", "refined olive oil and 'olive oil' blends (refined + virgin; low phenolics)", "olive pomace oil (solvent-extracted; not EVOO)", "table olives (contain some phenolics and MUFA; not covered here)"], "active_constituents": ["oleic acid (C18:1; about 76% of fatty acids in Picholine marocaine virgin oils)", "phenolic alcohols: hydroxytyrosol, tyrosol", "secoiridoids: oleuropein and ligstroside aglycones, oleocanthal, oleacein", "lignans and flavonoids (luteolin, apigenin)", "alpha-tocopherol (vitamin E), squalene, phytosterols, chlorophyll and carotenoids"], "one_line_verdict": "Extra-virgin olive oil is the evidence-preferred culinary fat for cardiovascular health and is associated with lower dementia mortality and slower cognitive decline in older adults, but its cognitive benefit as a single food rests on confounded PREDIMED sub-studies and small MCI trials (grade C), and it has never been tested on cognition in healthy young adults; it belongs in Brain Food as the default fat, not as a brain-boosting product.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. Extra-virgin olive oil (EVOO) is a monounsaturated-fat oil (Picholine marocaine virgin oils: oleic acid 76.1%, about 238 mg/kg secoiridoids plus phenolic alcohols [bouymajane2020]) whose phenolic fraction distinguishes it from refined olive oil and seed oils. Its best-established human benefit is cardiovascular: in PREDIMED (7,447 high-risk Spaniards) a Mediterranean diet supplemented with EVOO (about 1 L/week) reduced major cardiovascular events versus low-fat advice (HR 0.69, 0.53-0.91), a result republished after randomisation irregularities affecting 11.6% of participants [estruch2018], and without weight gain (-0.41 kg, -0.83 to 0.01) [estruch2019weight]. Cognition in older adults. In PREDIMED-Barcelona (447 randomised, 334 retested, median 4.1 y) the EVOO arm had better global (+0.05 vs -0.38 z, p=0.005) and frontal composites than controls, but only 2 of 6 individual tests differed and all composites declined in controls [vallspedret2015]; in PREDIMED-Navarra the EVOO arm had higher post-trial MMSE (+0.62, 0.18-1.05) and clock-drawing scores [martinezlapiscina2013jnnp] and fewer MCI cases (OR 0.34, 0.12-0.97) in a 285-person subsample [martinezlapiscina2013jnha]. These are secondary outcomes of an open-label trial; Navarra lacked baseline cognition; and the contrast is an EVOO-rich Mediterranean package versus low-fat advice, so the oil's own effect cannot be isolated. Replacing other vegetable oils with 20-30 g/day EVOO within a Mediterranean diet (180 older Italians, 1 y) gave a 1.4-point larger ADAS-cog improvement (p=0.024) [mazza2018]. In MCI, MICOIL (n=50, 50 mL/day high- or moderate-phenolic EVOO versus diet advice, 12 months) favoured both EVOO arms [tsolaki2020], and an EVOO versus refined olive oil pilot (n=25, 6 months) improved CDR in both arms while only EVOO reduced blood-brain barrier permeability [kaddoumi2022]; a 2026 meta-analysis of 5 trials (n=747) found SMD 0.29 (0.18-0.41) with low GRADE certainty [simao2026]. Observational. Olive oil >=7 g/day was associated with 28% lower dementia-related death (HR 0.72, 0.64-0.81) over 28 y in 92,383 US adults, independent of diet quality; substitution for margarine/mayonnaise but not butter or other vegetable oils was protective [tessier2024]. Intensive use was linked to less visual-memory decline (OR 0.83, 0.69-0.99) [berr2009], virgin but not common olive oil to better 2-y cognitive change [ni2026], and MUFA to less decline in older women [naqvi2011]. Reviews call the evidence promising but preliminary [fazlollahi2023, kanaan2026]. Target population. No trial has tested olive oil on cognition in healthy young adults or students; the only healthy-adult data are a 9-person crossover showing increased occipital connectivity, a biomarker, with phenol-rich versus regular olive oil [gutierrezromero2026]. In severe major depression EVOO (25 mL/day) improved depression scores versus sunflower oil [foshati2022]. Funding: PREDIMED oils were supplied by olive-oil companies; the Kaddoumi corresponding author co-founded an olive-oil company. Conclusion: use EVOO as the default fat for heart and probably brain ageing within a Mediterranean pattern; single-food cognitive effects are grade C in older adults and unstudied in the young.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Olive oil does not correct a nutrient deficiency in the classical sense: oleic acid is non-essential and the oil supplies only vitamin E in meaningful amounts. Its plausible role is replacement: displacing saturated or trans fats and refined seed-oil-based spreads, which in PREDIMED and US cohorts was associated with cardiovascular and dementia-mortality benefits [estruch2018, tessier2024]. Benefits in trials appeared against a background of cardiovascular risk in older adults [vallspedret2015, mazza2018] or in MCI [tsolaki2020, kaddoumi2022]; these are protection-against-decline effects, not enhancement. For healthy, adequately nourished young adults or students there is no trial evidence of any cognitive enhancement from olive oil, and none should be claimed. In Morocco olive oil is already a staple for many households, so the relevant levers are quality (true extra-virgin rather than oxidised traditional-mill or adulterated oil [elmoudden2020]) and replacing less healthy fats, not adding extra oil on top of the diet.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Claims that olive oil 'boosts memory', 'prevents Alzheimer's' or that high-phenolic or 'high-oleocanthal' oils are brain medicines go far beyond the evidence. The cognitive data are secondary outcomes of one Spanish trial with integrity issues and a diet-package comparator, plus small MCI pilots (n=25-50), one with a declared commercial interest (an author co-founded an olive-oil company); mechanisms (oleocanthal amyloid clearance, BBB repair) come largely from mice. Disease-prevention wording ('reduces dementia risk') is prohibited on foods in the EU and Morocco and would be a disease claim in the US. What can be said legally in the EU: 'Olive oil polyphenols contribute to the protection of blood lipids from oxidative stress' (only for oils with at least 5 mg hydroxytyrosol and derivatives per 20 g, with the 20 g/day information) and the oleic acid/unsaturated-fat cholesterol claim. In the US, FDA qualified health claims for olive-oil MUFA (2004) and high-oleic oils (2018) concern coronary heart disease only. The honest positioning is 'the Mediterranean default fat, with authorised heart-health claims when phenolic content is verified'.", "mechanism": {"summary": "EVOO could support brain ageing through (1) fat replacement and LDL/oxidative-stress lowering that protects cerebral vessels; (2) phenolics (hydroxytyrosol, oleocanthal, oleacein) with antioxidant and anti-inflammatory effects, absorbed and excreted mainly as conjugates; (3) proposed direct effects on amyloid clearance and blood-brain barrier integrity shown in transgenic mice and in one small human MCI study; and (4) gut microbiota modulation. Only route 1 is established in humans; the brain-specific routes are animal or limited human biomarker evidence and do not establish cognitive efficacy.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "EVOO ingestion -> absorption of oleic acid (into lipoproteins and membranes) and of phenolics (hydroxytyrosol, tyrosol) as glucuronide/sulfate conjugates", "detail": "Urinary hydroxytyrosol-glucuronide rose after phenol-rich versus regular olive oil in healthy adults [gutierrezromero2026]; phenol content varies widely by cultivar, harvest and processing [bouymajane2020, elmoudden2020].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Phenolics -> protection of LDL from oxidation; MUFA replacing SFA -> lower LDL cholesterol", "detail": "Basis of the EU-authorised olive-oil polyphenol and oleic-acid claims (Reg. (EU) 432/2012).", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Improved vascular risk profile -> fewer cardiovascular and cerebrovascular events -> protection of brain perfusion", "detail": "PREDIMED EVOO arm HR 0.69 for major CV events [estruch2018]; link to cognition is inferred, not measured directly.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Oleocanthal and other secoiridoids -> amyloid-beta clearance, NLRP3 inhibition, autophagy, BBB tightening", "detail": "Shown in transgenic mouse and cell models (Kaddoumi laboratory and others); in 25 people with MCI, EVOO but not refined olive oil reduced MRI-measured BBB permeability and increased functional connectivity [kaddoumi2022].", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Virgin olive oil -> gut microbiota diversity (e.g. Adlercreutzia) -> proposed gut-brain mediation", "detail": "Observational mediation analysis in 656 PREDIMED-Plus participants [ni2026]; no interventional confirmation.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Measured human outcome", "detail": "Better cognitive composites or MMSE in older adults within an EVOO-rich Mediterranean diet [vallspedret2015, martinezlapiscina2013jnnp]; small MCI trials positive [tsolaki2020, simao2026]; lower dementia-related mortality in cohorts [tessier2024]; no data in healthy young adults.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["fat replacement and LDL lowering", "LDL oxidation protection by phenolics", "anti-inflammatory secoiridoids (oleocanthal COX inhibition, NLRP3)", "amyloid clearance and blood-brain barrier integrity (mainly animal)", "gut microbiota modulation"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Cognitively healthy Spanish adults 55-80 y at high cardiovascular risk (PREDIMED Barcelona and Navarra)", "dose_or_intake": "Mediterranean diet + EVOO about 1 L/week (household supply) vs low-fat advice", "duration": "Median 4.1 y (Barcelona); 6.5 y (Navarra)", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "EVOO-supplemented Mediterranean diet produced better global and frontal composites (Barcelona) and higher MMSE and clock-drawing scores and less MCI (Navarra) than low-fat advice; most individual tests did not differ.", "effect_estimate": "Global composite +0.05 (-0.11 to 0.21) vs -0.38 (-0.57 to -0.18), p=0.005 [vallspedret2015]; MMSE +0.62 (0.18 to 1.05) [martinezlapiscina2013jnnp]; MCI OR 0.34 (0.12-0.97) [martinezlapiscina2013jnha]", "certainty_notes": "Secondary outcomes; open-label; 25% missing follow-up in Barcelona; no baseline cognition in Navarra; randomisation irregularities in the parent trial; whole-diet package (oil effect not isolated); single country and research network. Grade C, consistent with mediterranean-diet.json.", "key_study_ids": ["vallspedret2015", "martinezlapiscina2013jnnp", "martinezlapiscina2013jnha", "estruch2018"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_decline", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Older Italian adults (mean ~70 y) already following a Mediterranean diet", "dose_or_intake": "20-30 g/day EVOO replacing all other vegetable oils", "duration": "12 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Both groups improved ADAS-cog; improvement was larger with EVOO replacement; other tests did not differ.", "effect_estimate": "ADAS-cog change -3.0 +/- 0.4 (EVOO) vs -1.6 +/- 0.4 (control), p=0.024 [mazza2018]", "certainty_notes": "Single trial; 180 randomised but about 110 analysed (per Kanaan 2026); reported EVOO intake similar between groups (26 vs 30 g/day), which undermines the contrast; unblinded; downgraded for risk of bias and imprecision.", "key_study_ids": ["mazza2018", "kanaan2026"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_mci", "population_detail": "Adults with mild cognitive impairment (Greece, USA; mean age ~70)", "dose_or_intake": "50 mL/day high- or moderate-phenolic EVOO (MICOIL); daily EVOO vs refined olive oil (Kaddoumi)", "duration": "6-12 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "MICOIL: both EVOO groups outperformed diet advice on ADAS-cog, digit span and letter fluency, high-phenolic best. Kaddoumi: EVOO and refined olive oil both improved CDR, so the phenolic contribution to clinical scores is unclear. Pooled across 5 trials: small-to-moderate improvement in global cognition, low certainty.", "effect_estimate": "SMD 0.29 (0.18-0.41); MMSE MD 0.42 (0.15-0.68); ADAS-Cog MD 1.45 (0.39-2.51) [simao2026]", "certainty_notes": "Small trials (MICOIL n=50 although registered for 150; Kaddoumi n=25), open or partially blinded designs, MeDi-advice comparators, investigator commercial interest (Kaddoumi); GRADE low per the meta-analysis. MCI evidence must not be transferred to healthy adults.", "key_study_ids": ["tsolaki2020", "kaddoumi2022", "simao2026"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "neuroplasticity_biomarkers", "population": "older_adults_mci", "population_detail": "25 completers with MCI (USA)", "dose_or_intake": "Daily EVOO vs refined olive oil (dose not in abstract)", "duration": "6 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "EVOO reduced MRI-measured blood-brain-barrier permeability and enhanced functional connectivity; refined olive oil did not; both lowered blood Abeta42/40 and p-tau/t-tau ratios.", "effect_estimate": "Regional BBB permeability p=0.00-0.04; connectivity p=0.01-0.05 (paired tests, per Kanaan 2026 summary)", "certainty_notes": "Biomarkers only; very small; many imaging comparisons; corresponding author co-founder of Oleolive LLC. Not a cognitive outcome.", "key_study_ids": ["kaddoumi2022", "kanaan2026"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "dementia_risk", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "US health professionals (mean 56 y), French older adults (Three-City), Spanish older adults with metabolic syndrome, US older women", "dose_or_intake": "Habitual olive oil >7 g/day (US); intensive use for cooking and dressing (France); virgin olive oil intake (Spain); MUFA intake", "duration": "2-28 years", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Higher olive oil intake associated with lower dementia-related death, less visual-memory decline, and better cognitive change for virgin (not common) olive oil; MUFA associated with less cognitive decline in older women.", "effect_estimate": "Dementia death HR 0.72 (0.64-0.81) [tessier2024]; visual memory decline OR 0.83 (0.69-0.99) [berr2009]; MUFA highest vs lowest quartile decline 0.05 vs 0.21 SD (P=0.02) [naqvi2011]", "certainty_notes": "Observational; residual confounding by socioeconomic status and diet; dementia-death outcome depends on death certificates; verbal fluency association non-significant in Three-City. Consistent direction across cohorts, so possible_benefit at grade C.", "key_study_ids": ["tessier2024", "berr2009", "ni2026", "naqvi2011"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "neuroplasticity_biomarkers", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults (n=9; age not stated in abstract)", "dose_or_intake": "Daily phenol-rich EVOO vs regular olive oil (dose not in abstract)", "duration": "Crossover periods (length not in abstract)", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "Increased resting-state occipital functional connectivity with phenol-rich EVOO; interaction with urinary hydroxytyrosol-glucuronide. No cognitive outcome.", "effect_estimate": "not reported in abstract", "certainty_notes": "Pilot of 9 people; imaging biomarker; exploratory. Mechanism-level only.", "key_study_ids": ["gutierrezromero2026"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults 18-35 y, students", "dose_or_intake": "Any EVOO intake", "duration": "Not studied", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No randomised or prospective study of olive oil and cognition in healthy young adults or students was identified; systematic reviews include only older or MCI populations (one restricted to >55 y).", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "Absence of evidence. Older-adult and MCI findings cannot be extrapolated.", "key_study_ids": ["fazlollahi2023", "kanaan2026"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "other", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Single EVOO-containing meal and same-day cognition", "dose_or_intake": "Not studied", "duration": "Single meal", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No acute cognitive trial of an olive-oil meal was identified.", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "Absence of evidence.", "key_study_ids": []}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Adults with major depressive disorder (Iran)", "dose_or_intake": "25 mL/day EVOO vs sunflower oil", "duration": "52 days", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "HAMD-7 declined more with EVOO (P=0.001); BDI-II between-group difference (P=0.021); effect seen in severe but not mild-moderate depression; BDNF and salivary cortisol unchanged.", "effect_estimate": "Between-group P=0.001 (HAMD-7); P=0.021 (BDI-II)", "certainty_notes": "Single small trial (n=73); subgroup-driven; clinical population; not relevant to healthy students' mood without replication.", "key_study_ids": ["foshati2022"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "vascular_health", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Spanish adults 55-80 y at high cardiovascular risk", "dose_or_intake": "Mediterranean diet + EVOO about 1 L/week, ad libitum", "duration": "Median 4.8 y", "grade": "B", "direction": "benefit", "effect_summary": "Fewer major cardiovascular events with EVOO-supplemented Mediterranean diet; no weight gain despite high fat intake.", "effect_estimate": "HR 0.69 (0.53-0.91) [estruch2018]; bodyweight -0.41 kg (-0.83 to 0.01) vs control [estruch2019weight]", "certainty_notes": "Large RCT but open-label with randomisation irregularities (re-analysed); comparator low-fat advice poorly adhered; downgraded once. Vascular surrogate for brain health, not cognition.", "key_study_ids": ["estruch2018", "estruch2019weight"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "Any single EVOO-containing meal", "onset": "not studied", "duration_studied": "not studied", "domain": "any cognitive domain", "population": "Healthy young adults / students", "effect_estimate": "No acute cognitive trial identified", "certainty": "No evidence", "safety": "Food-level", "study_ids": []}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "Median 4.1 y", "population": "447 randomised (334 retested) older Spanish adults at high CV risk", "outcome": "Cognitive composites (memory, frontal, global)", "effect_estimate": "Global +0.05 vs -0.38 z (p=0.005); frontal +0.23 vs -0.33 (p=0.003); memory NS for EVOO", "evidence_quality": "Low (secondary outcome, open-label, attrition)", "limitations": "Diet package; 25% missing; parent-trial randomisation issues", "study_ids": ["vallspedret2015"]}, {"duration": "6.5 y", "population": "522 (JNNP) and 285 (JNHA) PREDIMED-Navarra participants, ~74 y", "outcome": "MMSE, clock drawing, MCI status", "effect_estimate": "MMSE +0.62 (0.18-1.05); MCI OR 0.34 (0.12-0.97)", "evidence_quality": "Low", "limitations": "Post-trial assessment only; no baseline cognition", "study_ids": ["martinezlapiscina2013jnnp", "martinezlapiscina2013jnha"]}, {"duration": "12 months", "population": "180 older Italians on a Mediterranean diet", "outcome": "ADAS-cog with EVOO replacing other oils", "effect_estimate": "-3.0 vs -1.6 points (p=0.024)", "evidence_quality": "Low", "limitations": "Attrition; similar EVOO intake in both arms reported", "study_ids": ["mazza2018"]}, {"duration": "12 months", "population": "50 adults with MCI (Greece)", "outcome": "ADAS-cog, MMSE, digit span, fluency with 50 mL/day high/moderate-phenolic EVOO", "effect_estimate": "Both EVOO groups better than MeDi advice (ADAS-cog p=0.003, digit span p=0.006)", "evidence_quality": "Very low-low", "limitations": "Pilot; under-recruited vs registration (150); APOE imbalance", "study_ids": ["tsolaki2020"]}, {"duration": "6 months", "population": "25 adults with MCI (USA)", "outcome": "BBB permeability, fMRI connectivity, CDR", "effect_estimate": "EVOO reduced BBB permeability; CDR improved with EVOO and refined OO", "evidence_quality": "Very low (biomarker pilot)", "limitations": "Tiny; COI; biomarkers", "study_ids": ["kaddoumi2022"]}, {"duration": "28 years", "population": "92,383 US adults (NHS, HPFS)", "outcome": "Dementia-related death", "effect_estimate": "HR 0.72 (0.64-0.81) for >=7 g/day vs never/rarely", "evidence_quality": "Low (observational)", "limitations": "Death-certificate outcome; health-conscious cohorts", "study_ids": ["tessier2024"]}, {"duration": "4 years", "population": "6,947 French older adults (Three-City)", "outcome": "Cognitive decline (visual memory, verbal fluency)", "effect_estimate": "Visual memory OR 0.83 (0.69-0.99); verbal fluency OR 0.85 (0.70-1.03)", "evidence_quality": "Low", "limitations": "Brief FFQ (use categories only)", "study_ids": ["berr2009"]}, {"duration": "Median 4.8 y", "population": "7,447 Spanish adults at high CV risk", "outcome": "Major cardiovascular events; bodyweight", "effect_estimate": "HR 0.69 (0.53-0.91); weight -0.41 kg", "evidence_quality": "Moderate", "limitations": "Open-label; randomisation irregularities", "study_ids": ["estruch2018", "estruch2019weight"]}, {"duration": "52 days", "population": "73 adults with major depression", "outcome": "Depression scores, BDNF, cortisol", "effect_estimate": "HAMD-7 P=0.001 vs sunflower oil; BDNF and cortisol null", "evidence_quality": "Low", "limitations": "Single small trial; subgroup effect", "study_ids": ["foshati2022"]}], "safety": {"summary": "EVOO at culinary amounts (10-50 g/day) is among the safest foods studied: PREDIMED supplied about 1 L/week per household for ~5 years without weight gain or safety concerns [estruch2018, estruch2019weight]. The main practical risks are quality-related: adulteration or mislabelling with cheaper seed or refined oils, oxidised or high-acidity traditional-mill oils that fail extra-virgin criteria [elmoudden2020], contaminants more typical of pomace oils, and loss of phenolics with heat, light and storage. Its energy density (about 9 kcal/g; ~120 kcal per tablespoon) matters if oil is added on top of rather than instead of other fats.", "adverse_effects": ["Weight gain only if added on top of habitual energy intake (not observed in PREDIMED with ad libitum diets)", "Bitterness/throat pungency of high-phenolic oils (oleocanthal) reducing acceptability", "Rare olive (Ole e 1) allergy, mostly pollen-related; reactions to the oil itself are rare"], "contraindications": ["None for culinary use; documented olive allergy", "Very-low-fat diets prescribed for specific medical conditions (e.g. some pancreatic or lipid disorders) - follow clinical advice"], "drug_interactions": ["No clinically established interactions at food intakes; theoretical additive effects of large phenolic intakes with antihypertensive or antiplatelet drugs are not demonstrated"], "upper_limits": "No upper limit. Studied intakes 20-50 g/day (up to about 1 L/week per household in PREDIMED). Fat intake guidance (e.g. total fat 20-35% of energy) applies.", "sleep_impact": "None known.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "About 5-6.5 years in PREDIMED; decades in cohorts (28 y in NHS/HPFS).", "daily_use_appropriate": "yes", "special_populations": "Suitable in pregnancy, childhood and older age as a culinary fat. People managing weight should substitute rather than add. No cognitive claims transfer from MCI trials to healthy people.", "specialist_review_needed": ["Food chemist / accredited laboratory for phenolic (hydroxytyrosol and derivatives) and IOC quality analysis of each lot", "Food-fraud and authenticity specialist for supply chain (blending, origin)", "Regulatory specialist for EU polyphenol-claim conditions and Moroccan labelling"], "hard_safety_gate": "pass", "gate_reason": "Traditional food with long-term RCT and cohort exposure and no safety signal at culinary intakes; risks are quality and fraud, manageable by specification and testing."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Conventional food; categories, characteristics, labelling and blending rules set by Reg. (EU) 1308/2013 Annex VII Part VIII and Commission Delegated Regulation (EU) 2022/2104 on marketing standards for olive oil (amended by 2023/2598 and 2024/1401; EUR-Lex record checked 2026-10-25, annex limits not read), with conformity checks under Implementing Reg. (EU) 2022/2105 (not re-read). Authorised claims (Reg. (EU) 432/2012, legislation.gov.uk Annex copy accessed 2026-10-25): 'Olive oil polyphenols contribute to the protection of blood lipids from oxidative stress' - only for olive oil containing at least 5 mg hydroxytyrosol and its derivatives (e.g. oleuropein complex and tyrosol) per 20 g of olive oil, with information that the beneficial effect is obtained with a daily intake of 20 g; and 'Replacing saturated fats in the diet with unsaturated fats contributes to the maintenance of normal blood cholesterol levels. Oleic acid is an unsaturated fat.' - only for food high in unsaturated fatty acids. No cognitive or brain claim exists; disease-risk wording (dementia) would require Article 14 authorisation, which does not exist.", "us": "No FDA standard of identity for olive oil (USDA voluntary grade standards exist; not re-read). FDA qualified health claims (FDA QHC list accessed 2026-10-25): 'Monounsaturated Fatty Acids from Olive Oil and Coronary Heart Disease' (1 November 2004; wording about 2 tablespoons/23 g per day replacing saturated fat - exact text not re-read) and 'Oleic Acid and Coronary Heart Disease' (19 November 2018; qualifying level updated 16 July 2020), reported wording: 'Supportive but not conclusive scientific evidence suggests that daily consumption of about 1 1/2 tablespoons (20 grams) of oils containing high levels of oleic acid, when replaced for fats and oils higher in saturated fat, may reduce the risk of coronary heart disease...' (trade press; oils with >=70% oleic acid). No cognitive claim; 'supports brain health' structure/function wording on a conventional food must derive from nutritive value and be substantiated under FTC standards; dementia wording is a disease claim.", "morocco": "Conventional food under Law 28-07; ONSSA performs sanitary control of olive and pomace oils, with INRA, IAV, ENAM and LOARC involved in quality frameworks and SDOQ/organic labels available (Le Matin, 1 Dec 2025, accessed 2026-10-25). Labelling under Decree 2-12-389 (Arabic; disease claims banned); nutrition claims via Joint Order 281-16; no authorised health-claim list found (X6 unit). Market: 2025/26 harvest reported at about 200,000 t oil (+105%), average retail price about 70 MAD/L (range 50-80) after 90-120 MAD/L in the two previous years (Le Matin); the IOC estimate cited by other press was 160,000 t (Hespress via web search). Status of any health claim: uncertain.", "permitted_claims_examples": ["EU (Reg. (EU) 432/2012): 'Olive oil polyphenols contribute to the protection of blood lipids from oxidative stress' - only for olive oil with at least 5 mg hydroxytyrosol and its derivatives per 20 g; consumer informed that the effect is obtained with 20 g/day.", "EU (Reg. (EU) 432/2012): 'Replacing saturated fats in the diet with unsaturated fats contributes to the maintenance of normal blood cholesterol levels. Oleic acid is an unsaturated fat.' - only for food high in unsaturated fatty acids.", "US (FDA qualified health claim, 2018, wording from trade press): 'Supportive but not conclusive scientific evidence suggests that daily consumption of about 1 1/2 tablespoons (20 grams) of oils containing high levels of oleic acid, when replaced for fats and oils higher in saturated fat, may reduce the risk of coronary heart disease.'"], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "None for olive oil. Concentrated olive phenol extracts (hydroxytyrosol) are separately regulated (synthetic hydroxytyrosol is understood to be on the EU novel-food Union list - not re-checked in this unit; see olive-polyphenols monograph, unit G4) and as US supplements could require NDI notification.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Excellent: studied intakes (7-50 g/day; 20 g/day for the EU polyphenol claim) are normal culinary amounts. Brain Food should use EVOO as the default fat in dressings, dips (zaalouk, hummus), bread dipping and baked goods rather than create an 'olive oil shot'. Specify verified extra-virgin grade and, if a claim is wanted, a minimum hydroxytyrosol-and-derivatives content of 250 mg/kg at end of shelf life.", "formats": ["bottled EVOO (dark glass/tin, nitrogen headspace)", "ready-to-use dressings and vinaigrettes with EVOO as sole oil", "Moroccan dips and spreads (zaalouk, taktouka, hummus) made with EVOO", "single-serve 20 g EVOO sachets paired with bread or salads", "EVOO-based bakery (msemen, khobz) replacing margarine or palm fat"], "stability": "Phenolics and aroma decline with storage time, light, heat and oxygen (oleocanthalic acid forms with aging); store <20-25 C in opaque containers; use within 12-18 months of milling; frying reduces phenolics but EVOO is reasonably heat-stable for home cooking.", "taste": "Fruity, bitter and pungent in high-phenolic oils (oleocanthal throat sting), which some consumers dislike; Picholine marocaine oils are typically medium-fruity; traditional-mill (maasra) oils can be fusty/winey if fruit is stored before pressing.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Oil fat improves absorption of fat-soluble vitamins and carotenoids from vegetables eaten with it; hydroxytyrosol and tyrosol are absorbed and excreted mainly as conjugates; phenolic dose depends entirely on the oil lot.", "dose_feasibility": "Fully feasible: 20 g (about 1.5 tablespoons) fits one salad or dip serving; 50 mL/day (MICOIL) is high but culinary. The polyphenol claim requires 5 mg hydroxytyrosol and derivatives per 20 g (250 mg/kg); average Picholine marocaine VOO contained about 238 mg/kg secoiridoids plus phenolic alcohols by a non-official method [bouymajane2020], so lot-by-lot selection and the official method are needed.", "cost_availability": "Morocco is a major producer; prices are volatile with drought (90-120 MAD/L in 2023-2025, about 70 MAD/L average late 2025). In the EU/US EVOO is widely available but premium high-phenolic oils cost several times standard EVOO (not researched).", "cost_evidence": "Moroccan retail average about 70 MAD/L, range 50-80 MAD/L, December 2025 (Le Matin, lematin.ma article 316563, accessed 2026-10-25); at 70 MAD/L, 20 g/day (~0.65 L/month) costs about 45 MAD/month. EU/US prices not researched here."}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 4, "effect_magnitude": 3, "safety": 9, "reproducibility": 3, "food_suitability": 9, "cost_access": 6, "rationale": "Evidence quality 4: cognitive rows are grade C in older adults (PREDIMED sub-studies with integrity issues and a diet-package comparator; small MCI trials; consistent cohorts) and D for healthy young adults; only the cardiovascular row reaches B. Effect magnitude 3: composite differences of about 0.4 SD driven by control decline, <1 MMSE point, SMD 0.29 in MCI, 28% lower dementia-death association; none tested in the target population. Safety 9: long-term trial exposure without safety signals; deductions for fraud/quality and energy density. Reproducibility 3: RCT cognitive findings come from one Spanish network and small MCI pilots; cohorts are directionally consistent but observational. Food suitability 9: studied doses are ordinary culinary amounts and the food is native to Morocco; quality control needed. Cost/access 6: locally produced but price-volatile with drought; high-phenolic oils cost more."}, "research_gaps": ["RCT of EVOO versus refined olive oil or seed oil (matched fat, blinded by masking) on cognition in healthy young adults and students over 8-12 weeks", "Acute trials of EVOO-containing breakfasts versus isoenergetic alternatives on attention and memory", "Dose-response for phenolic content (low vs high hydroxytyrosol oils) on validated cognitive outcomes, independent of industry", "Moroccan cohort or trial data on olive oil quality (traditional mill vs certified EVOO) and cognitive ageing", "Replication of PREDIMED cognitive findings in a trial with baseline cognition, blinded assessment and properly randomised individuals", "Full report of MICOIL (registered for 150) and larger MCI trials with oil-only comparators", "Systematic phenolic profiling of Moroccan commercial EVOO lots against the EU 250 mg/kg claim threshold using the official method"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 5.4, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "pass", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": "B", "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"global_cognition": "C", "cognitive_decline": "C", "dementia_risk": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 10, "n_studies": 20, "n_verified": 20, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 2, "n_systematic_reviews": 3, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["benefit", "insufficient", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "fish-and-seafood", "name": "Fish and seafood", "category": "J", "category_name": "Whole foods", "aliases": ["fish", "seafood", "oily fish / fatty fish", "small pelagic fish (sardines, anchovies, mackerel, herring)", "shellfish", "European pilchard (Sardina pilchardus)", "sardine (FR), sardina (ES), sardin (AR, Morocco)"], "food_sources": ["oily fish: sardines/pilchards, anchovies, Atlantic mackerel, herring, salmon, trout", "lean fish: cod, hake, sea bream, horse mackerel fillet (low in EPA/DHA)", "shellfish: shrimp, mussels, oysters, octopus (iodine, zinc, B12; low-moderate EPA/DHA)", "canned fish (sardines in oil or tomato, mackerel, tuna) and fish spreads", "whole small fish eaten with bones and skin (much more nutrient-dense than fillets)"], "active_constituents": ["EPA and DHA (whole Northwest-African sardine 1.2 g EPA + 0.87 g DHA per 100 g; anchovy 0.54 + 1.0 g per 100 g [aakre2020])", "vitamin D, vitamin B12, iodine, selenium, zinc, calcium (whole small fish), vitamin A (whole fish)", "high-quality protein, taurine", "contaminants co-delivered: methylmercury (mainly large predatory species), cadmium, lead, dioxins/PCBs, histamine (temperature abuse)"], "one_line_verdict": "Regular fish eating (about 1-2+ servings per week) is consistently associated with roughly 20% lower dementia risk and slower memory decline in older adults, but the associations shrink in newer, larger and longer studies, the few food trials show small, mostly child-only effects, and there is no evidence that fish improves cognition in healthy young adults; low-mercury oily fish such as Moroccan sardines is a sound nutrition choice, not a proven cognitive enhancer.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. Fish, particularly small oily species, is a dense source of EPA and DHA, vitamin D, B12, iodine and selenium [aakre2020]. Eating it raises red-cell n-3 status within weeks [teisen2020, kuhn2022]. The cognitive evidence is mainly observational and in older adults. The 2024 dose-response meta-analysis of 35 studies (about 849,000 participants) reported highest vs lowest intake RRs of 0.82 for cognitive impairment/decline (0.75-0.90, I2 61%), 0.82 for dementia (0.73-0.93) and 0.80 for Alzheimer's disease (0.67-0.96) [godos2024], consistent with an earlier meta-analysis (RR 0.80) [bakre2018]. In pooled US/French cohorts, >=4 servings/week slowed memory decline by 0.018 SD/year, a difference equal to that of about 4 years of age [samieri2018]; in CHAP, 1 and >=2 fish meals/week slowed global decline by 10% and 13% [morris2005]. The association is fragile. Within the 2024 meta-analysis, Alzheimer's estimates were null in studies published since 2018 (RR 0.98), in those with >=10 years of follow-up (0.97) and in participants under 70 (0.97). Cognitive-impairment estimates were null when outcomes were clinically examined (0.92, 0.76-1.10). Funnel asymmetry was present [godos2024], and the study was funded by a tuna company. Cohorts that lagged exposure to limit reverse causation [ngabirano2019], followed 43,651 Japanese adults [abe2025], or studied middle-aged Dutch adults [nooyens2018] found no association, and total seafood was unrelated to decline in 5,988 US women [kim2013]. Imaging and autopsy data (larger grey matter [raji2014]; less AD pathology only in APOE4 carriers [morris2016]) are biomarkers, not cognition. Trials of fish meals. In 57 resource-limited South African older adults, 12 weeks of canned pilchards added to a MIND-style diet raised CASI by 2.3/100 points (p=0.04; p=0.07 without imputation) [kuhn2022]. In children and adolescents, 12-16-week Nordic trials gave small effects on attention or processing speed with null primary or intention-to-treat results; overall certainty is low [handeland2017, oyen2018, teisen2020, oconnor2025]. Trials in male forensic inpatients are mixed (better decision-making [hansen2015]; no working-memory effect after 23 weeks of salmon [hansen2018]; better self-rated daily functioning [hansen2014]) and cannot be generalised. Healthy young adults. No fish-food trial exists. A Swedish cohort linked eating fish more than once a week at age 15 to +0.58 stanine at age 18 [aberg2009], with probable residual confounding. Fish-oil supplement trials in young adults are covered in the omega-3 monograph and are largely null. Grade D. Safety. At typical intakes, brain mercury rises with seafood but is not linked to neuropathology [morris2016]. Benefits outweigh risks if high-mercury predatory species are limited, especially in pregnancy and childhood [mozaffarian2006, efsa2015]. Moroccan Atlantic fish tested within EU metal limits [chahid2014].", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "The plausible benefit of fish is correcting low intakes of nutrients that many diets lack: EPA/DHA, iodine, vitamin D and B12. A single 100 g portion of whole sardine or anchovy covers most of the WHO recommended intake for several of these [aakre2020]. This matters for Morocco, where national surveys reported low iodine status in 71% of women (see X6 regulatory synthesis). The trial evidence fits the deficiency-correction interpretation. Effects appeared in resource-limited older adults [kuhn2022], in Danish children with low baseline fish intake, where they rose with the achieved n-3 dose [teisen2020], and in compliance-adjusted rather than intention-to-treat analyses [oyen2018, handeland2017]. For adequately nourished healthy young adults there is no fish-food trial at all. Fish-oil RCTs in that group (omega-3 monograph) show no meaningful enhancement. The defensible position is that fish helps people with low intake reach adequacy; it does not make a well-fed student smarter.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "'Fish is brain food' is old folk wisdom that the observational data partly support for ageing populations. Even there, the effect is modest (about 20% lower relative risk), probably overstated by publication bias and reverse causation, and partly produced by industry-linked authors: the 2024 and 2026 Godos reviews were supported by, or included an employee of, Bolton Food (canned tuna), and the Norwegian FINS trials were funded by the Norwegian Seafood Research Fund with fish donated by Marine Harvest and Pelagia. Claims such as 'boosts memory', 'improves focus for exams', 'raises IQ' or 'prevents Alzheimer's' are not supported in adults and would be unlawful disease claims in the EU and Morocco. What can legally and honestly be said in the EU is narrower: oily fish meeting the conditions can state 'DHA contributes to maintenance of normal brain function' (>=40 mg DHA per 100 g and per 100 kcal; benefit obtained with 250 mg DHA/day), and whole sardines analysed as a source of iodine can state 'Iodine contributes to normal cognitive function'. These are maintenance or deficiency-type claims, not enhancement claims. The honest brand position: low-mercury small oily fish is a cheap, nutrient-dense food that supports normal brain function as part of a healthy diet, and Morocco produces most of the world's sardines.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Fish supplies preformed EPA/DHA, which are incorporated into blood cell and neuronal membranes. DHA is the dominant structural n-3 fatty acid of the brain. Proposed brain mechanisms include membrane and synaptic function, anti-inflammatory lipid mediators, and vascular protection (lower triglycerides, lower coronary mortality). Fish also corrects iodine, vitamin D, B12 and selenium shortfalls, each necessary for normal neural function. Fish also co-delivers methylmercury, a neurotoxicant whose brain levels rise with seafood intake. Only the nutrient-status steps and some biomarker associations are demonstrated in humans; the link to better cognition rests on observational data and small trials.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Fish meal -> absorption of EPA/DHA, iodine, vitamin D, B12, selenium, protein", "detail": "Composition of NW-African sardine and anchovy measured directly [aakre2020]; 12-week fish interventions raised erythrocyte n-3 LCPUFA by 2.3 percentage points in children [teisen2020] and RBC EPA/DPA in older adults [kuhn2022].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Higher circulating DHA -> incorporation into brain phospholipids, synaptic membranes and neurogenesis-related signalling", "detail": "Shown in animal models and inferred from human post-mortem and tracer work (not appraised in this unit); see omega-3-dha-epa monograph.", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "n-3 and fish intake -> cardiovascular and cerebrovascular protection -> fewer vascular contributions to cognitive impairment", "detail": "1-2 servings/week associated with 36% lower coronary death [mozaffarian2006]; in the MAP autopsy cohort ALA, not seafood, was associated with fewer macroinfarcts [morris2016].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Fish intake -> brain structure and AD neuropathology", "detail": "Weekly baked/broiled fish associated with larger hippocampal and precuneus grey matter independent of n-3 estimates [raji2014]; >=1 seafood meal/week associated with fewer plaques/tangles only in APOE4 carriers [morris2016]. Observational biomarkers.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Co-exposure to methylmercury -> brain mercury accumulation (potential neurotoxicity)", "detail": "Brain mercury correlated with seafood meals (rho=0.16) but not with any neuropathology at the intakes observed [morris2016]; developmental neurotoxicity drives pregnancy/child advice [efsa2015].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Correction of iodine, vitamin D, B12 and selenium shortfalls -> normal thyroid and neural function", "detail": "Plausible where intake is low (e.g. iodine in Moroccan women); not tested with cognitive endpoints in fish trials.", "evidence_level": "plausible"}, {"step": "Measured human cognitive outcome", "detail": "Older adults: ~18-20% lower risk of dementia/decline in cohorts, attenuating in newer and larger studies [godos2024]; small RCT benefit in resource-limited elderly [kuhn2022]; children: small, inconsistent attention/processing-speed effects [oconnor2025]; healthy young adults: no trial.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["DHA incorporation into neuronal membranes and synapses", "n-3-derived anti-inflammatory and pro-resolving lipid mediators", "vascular protection (triglyceride lowering, reduced coronary mortality)", "iodine-thyroid axis and vitamin D / B12 adequacy", "methylmercury neurotoxicity (counter-pathway)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults 18-35, including university students and knowledge workers; no fish-food trial identified", "dose_or_intake": ">1 fish meal/week vs <1/week (observational)", "duration": "3 years (adolescence to age 18) in the only cohort", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No randomized trial of fish meals in healthy young adults. A Swedish cohort of 3972 boys found that eating fish more than once a week at 15 predicted higher combined, verbal and visuospatial intelligence at military conscription (age 18). A cross-sectional survey of 115 US college students found unadjusted correlations between seafood intake and learning scores. Fish-oil supplement trials in young adults are mostly null (see omega-3-dha-epa monograph).", "effect_estimate": "+0.58 stanine (95% CI 0.39-0.76) combined intelligence [aberg2009]; Pearson correlation only [pilato2022]", "certainty_notes": "Observational, males only, no baseline cognition adjustment, 52% response; student study unadjusted. Residual confounding by family socioeconomic status and diet quality is likely. Graded D (insufficient).", "key_study_ids": ["aberg2009", "pilato2022"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "other", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults; single-meal effects of fish on cognition", "dose_or_intake": "not studied", "duration": "single meal", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No acute (single-meal) fish trial with cognitive outcomes was found. Acute effects are biologically unlikely because DHA incorporation into membranes takes weeks.", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "Absence of evidence; nothing to grade.", "key_study_ids": []}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "dementia_risk", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Community-dwelling adults mostly >=65 in Europe, North America and East Asia, dementia-free at baseline", "dose_or_intake": "highest vs lowest habitual fish intake (typically >=1-2 servings/week vs rarely); dose-response up to 150 g/day", "duration": "4-15 years of follow-up", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Two meta-analyses report about 20% lower risk of dementia and Alzheimer's disease with higher fish intake. The AD association disappears in studies published since 2018, in studies with >=10 years of follow-up, in large studies (>=5000) and in participants under 70. Asymmetry suggests small-study/publication bias. Individual cohorts: Ohsaki (13,102) modest inverse association; Three-City (4-year lag to reduce reverse causation) and JPHC (43,651) null.", "effect_estimate": "Dementia RR 0.82 (0.73-0.93), AD RR 0.80 (0.67-0.96); AD in studies >=2018 RR 0.98 (0.74-1.28) [godos2024]; RR 0.80 (0.74-0.87) [bakre2018]; Ohsaki Q4 HR 0.84 (0.71-0.997) [tsurumaki2019]", "certainty_notes": "Observational (low starting certainty). Downgraded for likely reverse causation (diet changes in prodromal dementia), confounding (socioeconomic status, overall diet), publication bias and attenuation in the best-designed studies. The main meta-analysis was industry-funded (Bolton Food). Not upgraded for dose-response because CIs are wide. Grade C.", "key_study_ids": ["godos2024", "bakre2018", "tsurumaki2019", "ngabirano2019", "abe2025"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_decline", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Older adults (mostly >=65; Doetinchem 43-70) followed with repeated cognitive testing", "dose_or_intake": "1 to >=4 fish servings/week vs <1/week", "duration": "4-9 years", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "CHAP: 10-13% slower global decline with 1 or >=2 fish meals/week. Five pooled cohorts (23,688): slower memory decline with >=4 servings/week (about 4 years of age), global cognition not significant. Women's Health Study: total seafood null, tuna/dark fish linked to slower verbal-memory decline. Doetinchem (middle-aged): no consistent association. A 2026 review of 25 studies found the most consistent associations for processing speed, executive function and semantic memory, with null results in several fully adjusted models.", "effect_estimate": "Decline -0.088 vs -0.100 SU/year (>=2 meals/week vs <1) [morris2005]; memory decline slower by 0.018 SU/year (0.004-0.032) [samieri2018]; cognitive impairment/decline RR 0.82 (0.75-0.90), I2 61%; clinically examined subgroup RR 0.92 (0.76-1.10) [godos2024]", "certainty_notes": "Observational and inconsistent across cohorts and domains; absolute differences small (0.01-0.02 SD/year); clinically assessed outcomes null in subgroup; industry-linked reviews. Grade C, possible benefit.", "key_study_ids": ["morris2005", "samieri2018", "kim2013", "nooyens2018", "godos2024", "godos2026"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Cognitively intact, resource-limited older adults (mean 72 y, 74% women) in an urban South African retirement centre", "dose_or_intake": "Canned pilchards and fish spread weekly (theoretical +2.2 g n-3 PUFA/day; measured intake about 1.36 g vs 0.72 g/day) on top of a MIND-style enhanced diet", "duration": "12 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "The fish group scored 2.3 points higher on the 100-point CASI than the control group (canned meatballs and soya), significant only in the imputed analysis. RBC EPA and DPA rose.", "effect_estimate": "CASI +2.26 points (p=0.04) with imputation; +2.14 (p=0.07) without [kuhn2022]", "certainty_notes": "Single small open-label RCT (n=57) with a borderline p-value that depends on imputation; co-intervention diet; deficiency-prone population. Not applicable to well-nourished adults.", "key_study_ids": ["kuhn2022"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "neuroplasticity_biomarkers", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Cognitively normal older adults (MRI) and deceased MAP participants (autopsy); biomarkers, not cognition", "dose_or_intake": "weekly baked/broiled fish; >=1 seafood meal/week", "duration": "about 9 years (diet to MRI); about 4.5 years (diet to death)", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Weekly baked/broiled fish associated with larger grey-matter volumes in the hippocampus, precuneus, posterior cingulate and orbitofrontal cortex. Seafood associated with less Alzheimer pathology only in APOE4 carriers. Brain mercury rose with seafood intake but was not linked to pathology.", "effect_estimate": "Neuritic plaques beta -0.69 (95% CI -1.34 to -0.04) in APOE4 carriers [morris2016]; voxel-based associations without a single effect size [raji2014]", "certainty_notes": "Small observational samples (260 MRI; 286 autopsies), cross-sectional brain measures, healthy-user confounding. These are biomarkers and must not be presented as cognitive benefit.", "key_study_ids": ["raji2014", "morris2016"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "processing_speed", "population": "children_adolescents", "population_detail": "Healthy children and adolescents in Norway and Denmark (4-6, 8-9 and 14-15 years)", "dose_or_intake": "Fatty fish lunches 3 times/week or about 300 g/week oily fish vs meat/poultry (or n-3 supplements)", "duration": "12-16 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "FINS-TEENS (n=426): processing speed improved less with meat or supplements than with fish, with low compliance. FINS-KIDS (n=218): no ITT effect on WPPSI-III; benefit only in compliance-adjusted analysis. FiSK Junior (n=199): overall cognitive score trend (+0.17, CI crossing 0), fewer attention errors and a smaller mixing cost. A 2025 systematic review (5 RCTs + 9 cohorts) rated the evidence for improved cognitive development as low certainty.", "effect_estimate": "Processing speed difference -11.8 (-23.3 to -0.4) meat vs fish [handeland2017]; WPPSI change 17.7 vs 17.8, p=0.97 (ITT) [oyen2018]; overall score +0.17 (-0.01 to 0.35) [teisen2020]; GRADE low [oconnor2025]", "certainty_notes": "Small effects, null primary or ITT analyses, multiple outcomes, poor compliance with fish meals, Nordic populations; FINS trials funded by the Norwegian Seafood Research Fund. Children only; not transferable to adults.", "key_study_ids": ["handeland2017", "oyen2018", "teisen2020", "oconnor2025"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Young to middle-aged male forensic inpatients with antisocial traits/psychiatric disorders, many with substance-use histories (USA)", "dose_or_intake": "Fatty fish (Atlantic/farmed salmon) 3 meals/week vs meat/chicken/pork", "duration": "About 23 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Two reports from the same research programme disagree. One found better Iowa Gambling Task performance in the fish group and a Tower of Hanoi gain limited to participants with substance-abuse history on high-working-memory-load problems. A second 23-week trial (n=84) found no effect of salmon on working-memory accuracy or reaction time, which were explained by age and IQ.", "effect_estimate": "Not reported as standardized effects in abstracts; working memory null [hansen2018]; IGT/ToH improvements [hansen2015]", "certainty_notes": "Small unblinded trials in a special population; subgroup-driven positive findings versus a null working-memory trial. Not generalisable to students or workers.", "key_study_ids": ["hansen2015", "hansen2018"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "sleep_related_cognition", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Male forensic inpatients (USA); sleep and self-reported daily functioning", "dose_or_intake": "Atlantic salmon 3 times/week vs alternative meat meals", "duration": "September to February (about 23 weeks)", "grade": "D", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Sleep latency increased in the control group but not in the fish group. The fish group reported better daily functioning. Resting HRV and EPA+DHA changed favourably; vitamin D status did not differ significantly.", "effect_estimate": "Not reported as effect sizes in abstract [hansen2014]", "certainty_notes": "Subjective outcomes in an unblinded single-site trial in a special population; no cognitive test was measured, so this is at most preliminary evidence for sleep/daily functioning (downgraded to D in self-audit).", "key_study_ids": ["hansen2014"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "vascular_health", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Adults in prospective cohorts and trials (coronary outcomes) as an indirect pathway to vascular brain health", "dose_or_intake": "1-2 servings/week of fish, especially oily species (about 250 mg/day EPA+DHA)", "duration": "Years (cohorts)", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Modest fish intake is associated with lower coronary death and total mortality; the benefit-risk balance favours eating fish for adults and for women of childbearing age when high-mercury species are limited.", "effect_estimate": "Coronary death -36% (95% CI 20%-50%); total mortality -17% (0%-32%) [mozaffarian2006]", "certainty_notes": "2006 review mixing cohorts and fish-oil trials; later large fish-oil RCTs were less positive (see omega-3 monograph). Indirect for cognition.", "key_study_ids": ["mozaffarian2006"]}], "acute_matrix": [], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "4-15 years (35 observational studies)", "population": "About 849,000 adults, mostly >=65, worldwide", "outcome": "Cognitive impairment/decline, dementia, Alzheimer's disease", "effect_estimate": "RR 0.82 (0.75-0.90), 0.82 (0.73-0.93), 0.80 (0.67-0.96); AD null in studies since 2018 (0.98) and with >=10 y follow-up (0.97)", "evidence_quality": "Meta-analysis of observational studies; low certainty", "limitations": "Funnel asymmetry; attenuation in better studies; industry funding (Bolton Food)", "study_ids": ["godos2024"]}, {"duration": "Median 3.9-9.1 years", "population": "23,688 adults >=65 in 5 US/French cohorts (88% women)", "outcome": "Decline in global cognition and episodic memory", "effect_estimate": "Memory decline slower by 0.018 SU/year (0.004-0.032) for >=4 vs <1 servings/week; global cognition NS", "evidence_quality": "Pooled cohorts; moderate quality", "limitations": "White participants only; FFQ; small absolute effect", "study_ids": ["samieri2018"]}, {"duration": "Mean 9.8 years with 4-year lag", "population": "5934 French adults >=65 (Three-City)", "outcome": "Dementia and AD", "effect_estimate": "No association for fish after excluding first-4-year cases", "evidence_quality": "Cohort designed against protopathic bias; moderate quality", "limitations": "Brief FFQ; frequency only", "study_ids": ["ngabirano2019"]}, {"duration": "Mean 9.4 years", "population": "43,651 Japanese adults (JPHC)", "outcome": "Disabling dementia", "effect_estimate": "Fish intake not associated in men or women", "evidence_quality": "Large cohort; administrative outcome", "limitations": "Care-certification outcome; high-fish population (little low-intake contrast)", "study_ids": ["abe2025"]}, {"duration": "12 weeks", "population": "57 resource-limited South African older adults", "outcome": "CASI global cognition", "effect_estimate": "+2.26/100 points (p=0.04, imputed); +2.14 (p=0.07, complete case)", "evidence_quality": "Small RCT; some concerns", "limitations": "Open label; co-intervention diet; borderline significance", "study_ids": ["kuhn2022"]}, {"duration": "12 weeks", "population": "426 Norwegian adolescents aged 14-15", "outcome": "d2 attention test", "effect_estimate": "Processing-speed gain 11.8 lower with meat and 13.4 lower with supplements vs fish", "evidence_quality": "RCT with low compliance; some concerns", "limitations": "Taste-driven non-compliance; seafood-industry funding", "study_ids": ["handeland2017"]}, {"duration": "12 weeks", "population": "199 Danish children aged 8-9", "outcome": "Cognitive battery and socioemotional scores", "effect_estimate": "Overall cognitive score +0.17 (-0.01 to 0.35); attention errors -1.9 (-3.4 to -0.3)", "evidence_quality": "Well-run RCT, 99% completion", "limitations": "Children; many outcomes; primary composite not significant", "study_ids": ["teisen2020"]}, {"duration": "16 weeks", "population": "218 Norwegian preschool children aged 4-6", "outcome": "WPPSI-III total raw score", "effect_estimate": "No ITT difference (p=0.97); compliance-adjusted +5.2 points", "evidence_quality": "Cluster-recruited RCT; null primary result", "limitations": "Per-protocol benefit only; seafood-industry funding (FINS program)", "study_ids": ["oyen2018"]}, {"duration": "3 years", "population": "3972 Swedish males (fish at 15, IQ at 18)", "outcome": "Conscription intelligence test", "effect_estimate": "+0.58 stanine (0.39-0.76) for >1 vs <1 fish meal/week", "evidence_quality": "Observational cohort; low certainty", "limitations": "Residual confounding; 52% response; males only", "study_ids": ["aberg2009"]}, {"duration": "23 weeks", "population": "84 young to middle-aged male forensic inpatients", "outcome": "Working memory accuracy and reaction time", "effect_estimate": "No effect of salmon 3 times/week; performance explained by age and IQ", "evidence_quality": "Small RCT; some concerns", "limitations": "Clinical population; unblinded", "study_ids": ["hansen2018"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Fish is a conventional food whose benefits outweigh its risks for adults when high-mercury predatory species are limited [mozaffarian2006, efsa2015]. The main hazards are methylmercury (developmental neurotoxicant; relevant for pregnancy, breastfeeding and young children), persistent organic pollutants in some fatty fish, histamine (scombroid) poisoning after cold-chain failure in sardines, mackerel and tuna [khatouf2024], parasites (Anisakis) in raw or undercooked fish, and allergy [spolidoro2023]. At usual intakes in older adults, higher brain mercury from seafood was not associated with neuropathology [morris2016]. Moroccan Atlantic fish, including sardines and mackerel, tested within EU limits for Hg, Cd and Pb [chahid2014]. Small pelagic species (sardines, anchovies, Atlantic mackerel) are on the FDA/EPA 'Best Choices' list.", "adverse_effects": ["Methylmercury exposure: developmental neurotoxicity at high maternal/child intakes of high-mercury species (shark, swordfish, king mackerel, marlin, bigeye tuna, tilefish)", "Histamine (scombroid) poisoning from temperature-abused sardines, mackerel, anchovies or tuna [khatouf2024]", "Fish and shellfish allergy, including anaphylaxis [spolidoro2023]", "Anisakis infection or allergy from raw/undercooked fish", "Dioxins/PCBs in some fatty fish (risk considered outweighed by benefits [mozaffarian2006])", "High sodium in some canned or salted fish products"], "contraindications": ["Fish or shellfish allergy", "Pregnancy, breastfeeding and young children: avoid high-mercury species; follow national advice on servings", "Raw fish preparations for pregnant women, immunocompromised people and young children"], "drug_interactions": ["No clinically relevant interactions known for normal dietary fish intake (the bleeding-risk discussion for high-dose fish-oil supplements is covered in the omega-3 monograph)", "Histamine-rich fish may provoke reactions in people taking MAO inhibitors or isoniazid (diamine oxidase inhibition); not reviewed in depth here", "Warfarin users: no specific dietary-fish restriction identified in this unit"], "upper_limits": "No upper limit for fish as a food. Methylmercury TWI 1.3 µg/kg bw/week (EFSA). FDA/EPA advice: pregnant or breastfeeding people 8-12 oz (2-3 servings) per week of 'Best Choices'; children 1-11 two age-appropriate servings/week (1-4 oz). EFSA notes that toddlers and children in some countries can exceed the TWI before reaching beneficial intakes unless high-mercury species are limited [efsa2015].", "sleep_impact": "No adverse sleep effect known. One small trial in forensic inpatients suggested better sleep latency and daily functioning with salmon [hansen2014].", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Decades of observational exposure in cohorts; dietary trials of 12 weeks to about 6 months.", "daily_use_appropriate": "probably", "special_populations": "Pregnancy and breastfeeding: seafood is recommended (2-3 servings/week of low-mercury species) for fetal neurodevelopment, with strict avoidance of high-mercury species. Children: smaller portions and low-mercury species. Older adults: benefits likely outweigh risks. People with gout: purine-rich small fish (sardines, anchovies) may need moderation (not reviewed here). Hypertension: watch salt in canned/salted products.", "specialist_review_needed": ["Food toxicologist (methylmercury, Cd, Pb, dioxins, histamine specifications for the chosen species and catch area)", "Allergist (fish/crustacean/mollusc labelling and cross-contact)", "Obstetric/paediatric dietitian for any messaging aimed at pregnant women or children", "Food-safety (HACCP) specialist for cold chain and canning"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "Safe and beneficial as a food if species are restricted to low-mercury small pelagics (sardines, anchovies, Atlantic mackerel, herring) or tested farmed/wild salmon. Products must have contaminant and histamine specifications, cold-chain/HACCP controls and allergen labelling, and must not target pregnant women or children with high-mercury species."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Conventional food (fishery products under the EU hygiene package; contaminant maximum levels for mercury, cadmium and lead apply, e.g. mercury 0.5 mg/kg for most fish and 1.0 mg/kg for listed predatory species under Regulation (EU) 2023/915, as recalled and to be verified against the current annex; histamine criteria for high-histidine species under Regulation (EC) 2073/2005; fish, crustaceans and molluscs are mandatory allergens). Authorised claims that oily fish can carry if conditions are met (Regulation (EU) 432/2012): 'DHA contributes to maintenance of normal brain function' (>=40 mg DHA per 100 g and per 100 kcal; consumer informed that the benefit is obtained with 250 mg DHA/day); 'EPA and DHA contribute to the normal function of the heart' (source of EPA+DHA; 250 mg/day); 'Iodine contributes to normal cognitive function' (food at least a source of iodine, to be established by analysis); vitamin D, B12 and selenium claims. NW-African sardine (0.87 g DHA/100 g, 161 kcal/100 g [aakre2020]) meets the DHA condition by a wide margin. No claim about memory, learning, focus or dementia prevention is authorised; disease-risk claims would need Article 14 authorisation, which does not exist. EFSA advises limiting high-mercury species [efsa2015].", "us": "Conventional food under FDA (Seafood HACCP regulation). Fish and crustacean shellfish are major allergens (FALCPA). The FDA/EPA 'Advice about Eating Fish' (revised October 2021; page current as of 5 March 2024) lists sardines, anchovies, salmon and Atlantic mackerel as 'Best Choices' and king mackerel, marlin, orange roughy, shark, swordfish, tilefish (Gulf of Mexico) and bigeye tuna as 'Choices to Avoid'; it advises 8-12 oz/week for pregnant/breastfeeding people and notes that the Dietary Guidelines recommend at least 8 oz seafood/week for adults (https://www.fda.gov/food/consumers/advice-about-eating-fish, accessed 2026-10-25). A qualified health claim exists for EPA/DHA and coronary heart disease (not re-verified here). No FDA-recognised claim links fish to cognition; structure/function statements must be truthful and not misleading, and the FTC would require competent and reliable evidence for any memory/focus claim, which does not exist for healthy adults.", "morocco": "Conventional food under Law 28-07; ONSSA authorises/approves fishery establishments, runs import/export sanitary controls and operates the national reference laboratory that monitors metals in fish [chahid2014]. Labelling under Decree 2-12-389 (Arabic mandatory); nutrition claims per Joint Order 281-16; disease claims banned; no authorised health-claim list found, so cognition claims are legally uncertain (see X6 synthesis). Morocco holds the world's largest sardine stock: 57% of global sardine catches of about 1.236 million tonnes in 2023 (EU Fishery and Aquaculture Market Observatory, EUMOFA sardine profile, accessed 2026-10-25). Export to the EU requires compliance with EU hygiene and contaminant rules.", "permitted_claims_examples": ["DHA contributes to maintenance of normal brain function. (EU Reg. 432/2012; at least 40 mg DHA per 100 g and per 100 kcal; consumer informed that the beneficial effect is obtained with a daily intake of 250 mg DHA)", "EPA and DHA contribute to the normal function of the heart. (EU Reg. 432/2012; food at least a source of EPA and DHA; benefit with 250 mg EPA+DHA/day)", "Iodine contributes to normal cognitive function. (EU Reg. 432/2012; food must be at least a source of iodine by analysis)", "Vitamin B12 contributes to normal psychological function. (EU Reg. 432/2012; food at least a source of vitamin B12)"], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "None for traditional fish and seafood species or for canning. Novel processing (e.g. new fish-protein hydrolysates or concentrated fish-oil ingredients beyond traditional use) may require EU novel-food or US GRAS/NDI assessment; not relevant for whole canned sardines.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Very good as whole-food products: canned sardines, anchovies and mackerel are shelf-stable, cheap, low-mercury and naturally rich in DHA, iodine, vitamin D and calcium (with bones). The main barrier is acceptability, especially for adolescents and young adults: FINS-TEENS reported low compliance because of taste preferences [handeland2017].", "formats": ["canned sardines/mackerel (in olive oil, tomato, lemon; bone-in for calcium)", "fish spreads and pâtés (used in the South African trial [kuhn2022])", "ready-to-eat lunch bowls or sandwiches with sardine/mackerel", "grilled fresh sardines (Moroccan culinary staple) for food-service", "fish-based snacks (crackers, bites) with careful oxidation control"], "stability": "Canning gives multi-year shelf life; fresh sardines need strict cold chain (histamine rises quickly at 10-22 °C [khatouf2024]). DHA/EPA oxidise in processed and dried fish products (fishy off-flavours); antioxidants and oxygen-barrier packaging are needed.", "taste": "Strong fishy flavour and bones are barriers for some consumers; well accepted in Mediterranean/Moroccan cuisine. Flavoured canned formats and spreads improve acceptance.", "bioavailability_matrix": "EPA/DHA from fish (triglyceride and phospholipid forms) are well absorbed with the meal and raised red-cell n-3 within 12 weeks [teisen2020]. Whole small fish with bones and viscera supply far more iodine, calcium, zinc and vitamin A than fillets [aakre2020].", "dose_feasibility": "Highly feasible. 100 g whole NW-African sardine provides about 1.2 g EPA + 0.87 g DHA [aakre2020], so two 100 g servings/week average about 0.6 g EPA+DHA/day, above the 250 mg/day used for EU claims and in the range of 1-2+ servings/week linked to lower dementia risk. Trial doses (3 fatty-fish meals/week; about 300 g/week) fit ordinary diets.", "cost_availability": "Sardines and anchovies are among the cheapest animal-protein foods in Morocco and southern Europe. Salmon is several times more expensive. Morocco has the largest sardine stock and canning industry.", "cost_evidence": "EUMOFA sardine profile (accessed 2026-10-25): retail 2024 Spain fresh €5.67/kg, canned €10.74/kg; France fresh €6.50/kg, canned €12.50/kg; first-sale 2025 (Jan-Oct) €1.06-1.72/kg (Croatia, Portugal, Spain, France). Moroccan retail prices not researched here."}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 4, "effect_magnitude": 3, "safety": 7, "reproducibility": 4, "food_suitability": 8, "cost_access": 8, "rationale": "Evidence quality 4: grade C for dementia risk and cognitive decline in older adults (large observational literature, two meta-analyses, a few small food RCTs) but D for healthy young adults and no acute data. Effect magnitude 3: about 18-20% lower relative risk in cohorts, attenuating in better studies; RCT effects small (2 CASI points; d-scale effects in children near the threshold of significance). Safety 7: excellent food when low-mercury species are chosen; real hazards (methylmercury for pregnancy/children, histamine, allergy) are well understood and controllable. Reproducibility 4: cohort findings replicate in direction but several large or lag-designed cohorts are null; child RCTs mixed. Food suitability 8: canned small pelagics are practical and shelf-stable; taste acceptability limits young consumers. Cost/access 8: sardines are cheap and abundant, especially in Morocco."}, "research_gaps": ["No randomized trial of fish meals on cognition in healthy young adults, university students or knowledge workers; a 12-week sardine-meal trial with a preregistered cognitive primary endpoint in Moroccan students would be feasible and informative.", "No acute or short-term studies.", "Large cohorts with long lag periods and repeated diet measures are needed to separate causal effects from reverse causation; newer studies show attenuation.", "Few independent (non-industry) syntheses: recent meta-analyses were funded by or included employees of a tuna company; major child trials were funded by a seafood research fund.", "Iodine and vitamin D contribution of whole sardines to deficiency correction in Moroccan women and students has not been tested with functional outcomes.", "Species-specific mercury, cadmium and histamine data for current Moroccan sardine and anchovy supply (most local data are from 2014 or limited samples)."], "_derived": {"composite_score": 5.2, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": "C", "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"dementia_risk": "C", "cognitive_decline": "C", "global_cognition": "C", "processing_speed": "C", "sleep_related_cognition": "D"}, "n_outcome_rows": 10, "n_studies": 28, "n_verified": 28, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 9, "n_systematic_reviews": 4, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["inconsistent", "insufficient", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "leafy-greens-and-vegetables", "name": "Leafy green and other vegetables", "category": "J", "category_name": "Whole foods", "aliases": ["green leafy vegetables (GLV)", "dark green vegetables", "cruciferous vegetables", "spinach, kale, chard, lettuce, rocket/rucola, collards", "Moroccan greens: khobiza/bakoula (mallow, Malva spp.), selq (chard), rejla (purslane), fresh coriander and parsley", "fruit and vegetables (F&V) as an exposure in epidemiology"], "food_sources": ["spinach, kale, Swiss chard, lettuce, rocket (rucola), collard and mustard greens, watercress", "cruciferous vegetables: broccoli, cabbage, cauliflower, Brussels sprouts", "traditional Moroccan cooked greens (bakoula/khobiza from mallow; chard; purslane) and herb-heavy dishes (harira, chermoula, zaalouk with greens)", "other vegetables: carrots, peppers, tomatoes, root vegetables (often grouped with greens in cohort exposures)"], "active_constituents": ["folate (raw spinach roughly 150-200 ug per 100 g; approximate food-composition value, verify against national tables)", "phylloquinone (vitamin K1; leafy greens are the dominant dietary source, several hundred ug per 100 g in spinach/kale)", "lutein and zeaxanthin (carotenoids that accumulate in macula and brain)", "inorganic nitrate (highly variable; rocket and spinach among the highest; converted to nitrite and nitric oxide)", "kaempferol and other flavonols; glucosinolates/sulforaphane in crucifers", "alpha-tocopherol, beta-carotene, magnesium, potassium, dietary fibre"], "one_line_verdict": "Eating about one serving a day of leafy greens is consistently associated in older-adult cohorts with modestly slower cognitive decline and lower dementia risk, but this is observational evidence with residual confounding, the few randomised tests (acute spinach, a children's 12-week vegetable breakfast, the MIND diet trial) were null, and nothing shows that greens sharpen cognition in healthy students or young workers; their honest value is as a safe, nutrient-dense everyday food.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is shown. Prospective cohorts of older adults repeatedly find that higher vegetable intake, and leafy greens in particular, go with slower cognitive decline. In the Rush Memory and Aging Project (n=960, mean 4.7 y) the top quintile of green leafy vegetables (median 1.3 servings/day) declined more slowly by 0.05 standardised units per year, which the authors equate to being 11 years younger [morris2018]; folate, phylloquinone, lutein, nitrate, kaempferol and alpha-tocopherol were each associated with slower decline, but these nutrients are collinear and cannot be separated. Similar findings came from the Chicago Health and Aging Project (vegetables but not fruit, 38-40% slower decline [morris2006]), the Nurses' Health Study (leafy greens 0.05 units over 2 years, about 1-2 years of ageing [kang2005]), CHNS and HRS [huang2024], and Hisayama (27% lower dementia risk in the top vegetable quartile [kimura2022]). Meta-analyses agree on direction: odds ratios of about 0.75-0.80 for cognitive impairment or dementia [jiang2017, mottaghi2018, zhou2022], and a 2026 registered meta-analysis of 222,108 participants reports HR 0.87 (0.82-0.92) for highest vs lowest vegetable intake and HR 0.82 (0.70-0.96) per daily serving of leafy greens in three new cohorts [huang2026]. Systematic review of cohorts concluded the same for vegetables, not fruit [loef2012]. What weakens it. All of this is observational, FFQ-based, mostly in people over 60, and prone to healthy-user confounding and reverse causation; when dementia cases in the first 4 years were excluded the Three-City cohort found no association [ngabirano2019]; Doetinchem found total intake not or inconsistently associated [nooyens2011]; SU.VI.MAX 2 found vegetables associated with worse executive scores [peneau2011]; Mottaghi found the association only in Chinese studies. Randomised evidence is thin and null: an acute spinach (nitrate) meal raised plasma nitrite but did not change cognition or mood (n=30) [bondonno2014]; a 12-week fruit-and-vegetable breakfast did not change IQ in 251 children [li2026]; the 3-year MIND diet trial, which targets daily greens, did not beat a calorie-restricted control diet (difference 0.035 SD) [barnes2023]. A positive sulforaphane trial used an industry supplement, not food [nouchi2021]. In whom and how large. The plausible benefit is slower age-related decline, of a size equivalent to a few years of ageing, in older adults; for healthy young adults and students there are no trials and only a cross-sectional self-report link with lower stress [abbaszadeh2021]. What is unknown. Whether greens themselves (rather than the people who eat them) cause slower decline, which constituent matters, and whether any acute or short-term cognitive effect exists in young people.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Leafy greens are a major source of folate, vitamin K1, lutein, magnesium and nitrate. In people with low folate or raised homocysteine, correcting status may matter for cognition in older age (see folate-b9 monograph); the cohort associations may partly reflect avoidance of such low-intake states rather than enhancement. No trial shows that adding greens to the diet of an adequately nourished young adult improves memory, attention or speed. Biomarker changes (plasma nitrite after spinach [bondonno2014], macular pigment after lutein) are not cognitive outcomes. Evidence from older adults [morris2018, kang2005, huang2026] and from children [li2026] must not be generalised to students or knowledge workers.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Popular claims that 'a salad a day keeps your brain 11 years younger' come from one cohort's model-based equivalence [morris2018] and confuse association with causation; the same lab's randomised MIND trial was null [barnes2023]. 'Spinach boosts focus' has a direct acute test that was null [bondonno2014]. Honest wording for Brain Food: 'leafy greens are part of dietary patterns linked with healthier brain ageing in older adults' (educational, non-claim context), plus authorised nutrient claims where the food qualifies (e.g., folate contributes to normal psychological function; vitamin K contributes to normal blood clotting, EU Reg. 432/2012). An iron cognitive-function claim may be legally available for iron-rich greens in the EU, but spinach iron is poorly absorbed and building a brain message on it would be misleading in spirit. No product should claim cognitive enhancement, dementia prevention or exam performance from vegetables.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Leafy greens deliver a cluster of nutrients (folate, phylloquinone, lutein, nitrate, flavonols, tocopherol, magnesium, potassium, fibre) that could plausibly support brain ageing through homocysteine lowering, vascular nitric-oxide signalling and blood-pressure reduction, carotenoid deposition in neural tissue, vitamin K-dependent proteins and sphingolipids, and anti-inflammatory/antioxidant effects. Human evidence exists for the biomarker steps (plasma nitrite rises acutely; folate lowers homocysteine; lutein raises macular pigment), but the link from these biomarkers to measured cognition rests on cohorts, and the nutrients are too collinear to identify an active constituent.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Leafy greens -> folate, phylloquinone, lutein, nitrate, kaempferol, alpha-tocopherol, fibre, Mg, K", "detail": "Composition is well characterised; Morris 2018 modelled each nutrient separately and found all except beta-carotene associated with slower decline [morris2018].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Dietary nitrate -> oral bacterial reduction to nitrite -> nitric oxide pool", "detail": "A spinach meal raised plasma, salivary and urinary nitrate/nitrite and plasma nitroso species acutely in 30 adults [bondonno2014]; dose-dependent blood-pressure effects are covered in the beetroot-nitrate monograph.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Folate -> lower plasma homocysteine -> possible protection of brain vasculature and white matter", "detail": "Homocysteine lowering is established; cognitive benefit is seen only in older adults with raised homocysteine (see folate-b9 monograph). Not shown for food folate in replete young adults.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Lutein/zeaxanthin -> deposition in retina and brain (macular pigment as surrogate)", "detail": "Biomarker relationships and supplement trials are covered in the carotenoids monograph; dietary lutein was associated with slower decline in MAP [morris2018].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Vitamin K -> gamma-carboxylation of Gas6/protein S and brain sphingolipid metabolism", "detail": "Supported mainly by animal and cell work; human data are observational intake-cognition associations [morris2018].", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Flavonols, glucosinolates (sulforaphane) -> anti-inflammatory/antioxidant signalling (Nrf2)", "detail": "Mostly in vitro and animal; one industry-funded supplement trial in older adults [nouchi2021].", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Better cardiometabolic profile (blood pressure, diabetes risk) -> less vascular brain injury over decades", "detail": "Plausible explanation for long-latency cohort associations; not demonstrated for greens specifically.", "evidence_level": "plausible"}, {"step": "Measured human outcome", "detail": "Slower decline and lower dementia risk in older-adult cohorts [morris2018, kang2005, huang2026]; acute and short-term trials null [bondonno2014, li2026, barnes2023]; no young-adult trials.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["nitrate-nitrite-nitric oxide (vascular)", "one-carbon metabolism / homocysteine", "carotenoid deposition (lutein, zeaxanthin)", "vitamin K-dependent proteins and sphingolipids", "Nrf2 / anti-inflammatory signalling (flavonols, sulforaphane)", "long-term cardiometabolic risk reduction"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_decline", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Community-dwelling adults mostly 60-99 y (MAP, CHAP, NHS, CHNS/HRS, Hisayama, Doetinchem, SU.VI.MAX 2)", "dose_or_intake": "Highest vs lowest intake; GLV median ~1.3 servings/day in top quintile (MAP); total vegetables ~2.8-4.1 servings/day (CHAP)", "duration": "Habitual intake; follow-up 2-13 years", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Most cohorts find slower decline with higher vegetable and especially leafy-green intake (MAP beta 0.05 SU/year, '11 years younger'; NHS 0.05 units over 2 y, ~1-2 years of age; CHAP 38-40% slower; CHNS/HRS 0.011-0.012 units/year). Cross-sectionally (Hordaland, 70-74 y) the association is dose-dependent up to about 500 g/day of fruit and vegetables. Doetinchem was null or inconsistent and SU.VI.MAX 2 found vegetables associated with worse executive function.", "effect_estimate": "MAP: beta 0.05 SU/yr, p=0.0001 [morris2018]; NHS: 0.05 units (95% CI 0.02-0.09) [kang2005]; CHAP: 0.019 SU/yr slower, Q4 vs Q1 [morris2006]; HRS GLV decline beta 0.004 (0.001-0.007) [huang2024]", "certainty_notes": "Observational (starts low); consistent direction across several cohorts but small absolute effects, FFQ measurement error, healthy-user confounding, reverse causation and some null/opposite findings. Not upgraded for dose-response because of collinearity. Indirect to young adults.", "key_study_ids": ["morris2018", "kang2005", "morris2006", "huang2024", "nurk2010", "nooyens2011", "peneau2011"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "dementia_risk", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Adults >=45-65 y at baseline in prospective cohorts and meta-analyses (US, UK, France, Japan, China)", "dose_or_intake": "Highest vs lowest category; per daily serving of leafy greens", "duration": "7-24 years follow-up", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Pooled estimates suggest about 13-25% lower risk of dementia or cognitive impairment with high vegetable intake; leafy greens HR 0.82 per serving in three new cohorts. The Three-City cohort found no association after a 4-year lag to limit reverse causation; Mottaghi found the association only in Chinese studies.", "effect_estimate": "Vegetables HR 0.87 (0.82-0.92), GLV HR 0.82 (0.70-0.96) per serving [huang2026]; F&V OR 0.80 (0.71-0.89), +100 g/d OR 0.87 [jiang2017]; vegetables OR 0.75 (0.70-0.80), AD OR 0.88 (0.76-1.01) [zhou2022]; Q4 vs Q1 dementia -27% [kimura2022]", "certainty_notes": "Observational, heterogeneous exposure definitions, mixing of cross-sectional and cohort data in older meta-analyses; null lagged analysis [ngabirano2019] raises protopathic-bias concern; no RCT with dementia outcomes.", "key_study_ids": ["huang2026", "jiang2017", "mottaghi2018", "zhou2022", "loef2012", "kimura2022", "ngabirano2019"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Adults 65-84 without cognitive impairment, overweight, suboptimal diet (MIND trial); greens are one component of the tested diet", "dose_or_intake": "MIND diet including daily leafy greens and other vegetables vs usual diet, both with mild caloric restriction", "duration": "3 years", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "The only large dietary-pattern RCT that explicitly targets leafy greens found no cognitive or MRI benefit over a control diet with the same counselling and weight loss.", "effect_estimate": "Global cognition difference 0.035 SD (95% CI -0.022 to 0.092), p=0.23 [barnes2023]", "certainty_notes": "Well-conducted trial (low risk of bias) but indirect for greens alone (multi-component pattern; control arm also improved diet), so graded C for this specific question.", "key_study_ids": ["barnes2023"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "30 healthy men and women (age not stated in abstract), crossover", "dose_or_intake": "Nitrate-rich spinach meal (energy-matched) alone or with apple", "duration": "Single meal, testing within hours", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Spinach raised plasma nitrate/nitrite and nitroso species but produced no improvement or deterioration on the CDR computerised battery or mood.", "effect_estimate": "No significant effect on any cognitive or mood measure (values not reported in abstract) [bondonno2014]", "certainty_notes": "Single small trial, abstract only; imprecise; biomarker change without cognitive change.", "key_study_ids": ["bondonno2014"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults 18-35 y, students, knowledge workers", "dose_or_intake": "Not studied", "duration": "Not studied", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No randomised or prospective study has tested leafy greens or vegetables on measured cognition in healthy young adults or students. The only young-adult data found are cross-sectional self-report associations with stress and anxiety.", "effect_estimate": "not reported (no studies)", "certainty_notes": "Absence of evidence; older-adult cohort data are indirect and cannot be transferred.", "key_study_ids": ["abbaszadeh2021"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "students", "population_detail": "181 female university students 18-25 y (Iran), cross-sectional", "dose_or_intake": "Habitual cabbage/vegetable intake (FFQ)", "duration": "Cross-sectional", "grade": "D", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Higher cabbage/vegetable intake was weakly associated with lower self-reported stress and anxiety and better quality of life.", "effect_estimate": "Stress beta -0.04 (p=0.038); anxiety beta -0.02 (p=0.049) [abbaszadeh2021]", "certainty_notes": "Cross-sectional, single small sample, self-report, borderline p-values without multiplicity control; cannot establish direction.", "key_study_ids": ["abbaszadeh2021"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "other", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "US men mean age 51 at baseline (HPFS); outcome is subjective cognitive function 20 years later", "dose_or_intake": "Top vs bottom quintile of total vegetables", "duration": "Diet 1986-2002; SCF assessed 2008 and 2012", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Higher long-term vegetable intake associated with lower odds of poor self-reported cognitive function; intake 18-22 years earlier mattered independently of recent intake.", "effect_estimate": "OR poor SCF 0.66 (0.55-0.80); moderate SCF 0.83 (0.76-0.92) [yuan2019]", "certainty_notes": "Subjective outcome, not measured cognition; observational; large sample and repeated diet measures are strengths.", "key_study_ids": ["yuan2019"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "processing_speed", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "144 healthy older Japanese adults; sulforaphane supplement (broccoli-derived), not whole vegetables", "dose_or_intake": "Sulforaphane supplement daily (dose not stated in abstract)", "duration": "12 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Sulforaphane groups improved processing speed and working memory versus placebo; no added benefit when combined with brain training.", "effect_estimate": "Significant group effects reported; effect sizes not in abstract [nouchi2021]", "certainty_notes": "Single trial funded by a vegetable-product company with an employee co-author; factorial design with many outcomes; supplement, not food; unreplicated.", "key_study_ids": ["nouchi2021"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "children_adolescents", "population_detail": "251 primary-school children 7-11 y, Shanghai, class-randomised", "dose_or_intake": "Daily fruit- and vegetable-enriched breakfast vs energy-matched standard breakfast", "duration": "12 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Diet quality (fibre, carotene) improved but full-scale IQ, other WISC-IV indices, attention symptoms and mood did not change; exploratory fatigue reduction in children <=8 y.", "effect_estimate": "FSIQ change MD -0.63 (95% CI -5.15 to 3.89), p=0.807 [li2026]", "certainty_notes": "Single cluster-randomised trial; wide CI; children only and not transferable to adults.", "key_study_ids": ["li2026"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "Nitrate-rich spinach meal, energy-matched to control (amount not in abstract), alone or with flavonoid-rich apple", "onset": "Plasma nitrate/nitrite and nitroso species raised within hours", "duration_studied": "Single meal; post-meal testing session", "domain": "CDR battery (attention, working and episodic memory, speed) and mood", "population": "30 healthy men and women, randomised crossover", "effect_estimate": "No significant effect on cognition or mood [bondonno2014]", "certainty": "low (single small trial)", "safety": "No adverse effects reported; ordinary food", "study_ids": ["bondonno2014"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "Mean 4.7 years", "population": "960 MAP participants 58-99 y", "outcome": "Rate of global cognitive decline", "effect_estimate": "Top vs bottom GLV quintile: beta 0.05 SU/yr slower (p=0.0001), equated to 11 years younger", "evidence_quality": "observational, well-characterised cohort, FFQ", "limitations": "Residual confounding; collinear nutrients; single FFQ; older volunteers", "study_ids": ["morris2018"]}, {"duration": "Up to 6 years", "population": "3,718 CHAP participants >=65 y", "outcome": "Composite cognitive score change", "effect_estimate": "Vegetables Q4 0.019 SU/yr slower (40%), Q5 0.018 (38%); fruit not associated", "evidence_quality": "observational, population-based", "limitations": "FFQ; confounding; three assessments only", "study_ids": ["morris2006"]}, {"duration": "2-year change after long-term diet (1984 onward)", "population": "13,388 women (Nurses' Health Study)", "outcome": "Telephone cognitive battery decline", "effect_estimate": "GLV top vs bottom 0.05 units slower (95% CI 0.02-0.09); cruciferous 0.04 (0.003-0.07)", "evidence_quality": "observational, large, repeated FFQs", "limitations": "Short 2-year cognitive interval; telephone tests; nurses", "study_ids": ["kang2005"]}, {"duration": "7-13 years (three cohorts) plus meta-analysis", "population": "18,339 adults >=45 y (HRS, Framingham Offspring, Whitehall II); meta n=222,108", "outcome": "Incident dementia", "effect_estimate": "F&V T3 vs T1 HR 0.74 (0.61-0.89); GLV per serving HR 0.82 (0.70-0.96); meta vegetables HR 0.87 (0.82-0.92)", "evidence_quality": "observational, PROSPERO-registered meta-analysis", "limitations": "Cohort-specific dementia definitions; FFQ; residual confounding", "study_ids": ["huang2026"]}, {"duration": "24 years", "population": "1,071 Japanese adults >=60 y (Hisayama)", "outcome": "Incident dementia and AD", "effect_estimate": "Top vs bottom vegetable quartile: dementia -27%, AD -31%; fruit null", "evidence_quality": "observational, long follow-up", "limitations": "Single baseline FFQ; modest size", "study_ids": ["kimura2022"]}, {"duration": "Average 9.8 years with 4-year lag", "population": "5,934 adults >=65 y (Three-City, France)", "outcome": "Incident dementia and AD", "effect_estimate": "No association with raw fruit or cooked fruit and vegetables", "evidence_quality": "observational; designed to limit reverse causation", "limitations": "Brief frequency questionnaire; crude exposure categories", "study_ids": ["ngabirano2019"]}, {"duration": "5 years", "population": "2,613 adults 43-70 y (Doetinchem)", "outcome": "Global cognition, memory, speed, flexibility", "effect_estimate": "Total F&V not or inconsistently associated; vegetables linked to worse baseline speed but smaller decline", "evidence_quality": "observational, midlife", "limitations": "Short follow-up; multiple comparisons", "study_ids": ["nooyens2011"]}, {"duration": "13 years", "population": "2,533 French adults 45-60 y at baseline (SU.VI.MAX 2)", "outcome": "Verbal memory and executive function", "effect_estimate": "F&V positively associated with verbal memory; vegetables negatively associated with executive function (P-trend 0.03)", "evidence_quality": "observational within a supplement trial cohort", "limitations": "Cognition measured once at follow-up; no baseline cognition", "study_ids": ["peneau2011"]}, {"duration": "3 years (RCT)", "population": "604 adults 65-84 y at dementia risk", "outcome": "Global cognition and MRI", "effect_estimate": "MIND diet vs control: 0.035 SD (-0.022 to 0.092), p=0.23", "evidence_quality": "high internal validity; indirect for greens", "limitations": "Multi-component diet; active control diet", "study_ids": ["barnes2023"]}, {"duration": "12 weeks (cluster RCT)", "population": "251 children 7-11 y", "outcome": "WISC-IV full-scale IQ, attention, mood", "effect_estimate": "FSIQ MD -0.63 (-5.15 to 3.89)", "evidence_quality": "randomised, single trial", "limitations": "Children; wide CI; class randomisation", "study_ids": ["li2026"]}], "safety": {"summary": "As ordinary foods, leafy greens and vegetables are among the safest items in the diet; benefits are judged to outweigh contaminant risks by EFSA. Practical issues are the vitamin K interaction with warfarin-type anticoagulants (keep intake stable rather than avoid), nitrate in very high-nitrate leaves and concentrates (infants and nitrate ADI), oxalate in spinach/chard for kidney-stone formers, potassium load in advanced kidney disease, and microbial contamination of raw greens. Concentrated greens powders change the risk profile and need contaminant testing.", "adverse_effects": ["Gastrointestinal bloating/flatulence with large amounts of crucifers or fibre", "Foodborne illness from raw leafy greens (e.g., Shiga-toxin E. coli outbreaks linked to bagged salads); risk is in handling and cold chain", "Possible exceedance of the nitrate ADI with very high rocket/spinach intake or nitrate-concentrated powders [efsa2008nitrate]"], "contraindications": ["Infants: high-nitrate vegetable purees in early infancy carry a methaemoglobinaemia risk (national infant-feeding guidance applies)", "Calcium-oxalate kidney stone formers: limit very high-oxalate greens (spinach, chard) on clinical advice", "Advanced chronic kidney disease: potassium-rich greens may need restriction under renal dietitian guidance"], "drug_interactions": ["Vitamin K antagonists (warfarin, acenocoumarol): large swings in vitamin K intake alter INR; evidence supports stable intake rather than restriction [violi2016]", "No meaningful interaction for direct oral anticoagulants is expected from vitamin K (not researched here)"], "upper_limits": "No tolerable upper intake level for vitamin K has been set by EFSA or the US IOM; folate UL (1,000 ug/day) applies to folic acid, not food folate; nitrate ADI 3.7 mg/kg bw/day [efsa2008nitrate].", "sleep_impact": "None known.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Decades of habitual intake in large cohorts without safety signals at food levels; no long-term data for concentrated greens powders or extracts.", "daily_use_appropriate": "yes", "special_populations": "Pregnancy: greens are encouraged for folate; wash thoroughly. Children: suitable; avoid high-nitrate purees in early infancy. Warfarin users: consistent intake and INR monitoring. Kidney disease and stone formers: individual advice.", "specialist_review_needed": ["Clinical pharmacist or haematologist for anticoagulant messaging", "Food-safety (microbiology) review for any raw or minimally processed greens product", "Toxicologist for nitrate, cadmium/lead and pesticide residues in concentrated powders"], "hard_safety_gate": "pass", "gate_reason": "Ordinary food with long safe use; manageable, well-known cautions (warfarin stability, infant nitrate, oxalate, hygiene). Concentrated powders or sulforaphane extracts would need a separate assessment."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Vegetables are ordinary foods (Reg. 178/2002); no novel-food issue for traditional greens. No authorised health claim links vegetables or greens to cognition, and disease-risk claims about dementia are not permitted. Nutrient-based Article 13 claims from Reg. (EU) 432/2012 can be used if the food meets the condition (at least a 'source of' the nutrient, 15% NRV per 100 g): e.g., folate and normal psychological function (verified in project unit X4), vitamin K and normal blood clotting. Contaminant maximum levels for nitrate in spinach, lettuce and rocket are set in Reg. (EU) 2023/915 (exact current values not re-verified this session). EFSA 2008 retained the nitrate ADI.", "us": "Vegetables are conventional foods. Structure/function claims on conventional foods must derive from nutritive value and require FTC-level substantiation; no cognition claim is substantiated for greens. Authorised health claims in 21 CFR 101.72-101.83 concern cancer, heart disease and neural-tube defects (folate), none cognition (project unit X5). Dietary Guidelines recommend dark-green vegetables.", "morocco": "Ordinary foods under Law 28-07 (ONSSA) and labelling Decree 2-12-389: disease claims banned; Arabic labelling mandatory; nutrition claims limited to the positive list of Joint Order 281-16; health claims defined in law but no authorised list or procedure found (project unit X6), so any brain-health wording is legally uncertain.", "permitted_claims_examples": ["EU (Reg. 432/2012): 'Folate contributes to normal psychological function' - food must be at least a source of folate", "EU (Reg. 432/2012): 'Vitamin K contributes to normal blood clotting' - food must be at least a source of vitamin K"], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "None for traditional vegetables or dried vegetable powders. Isolated sulforaphane or glucoraphanin concentrates would require separate novel-food/food-supplement and NDI analysis (not researched here).", "regulatory_gate": "pass"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Excellent as whole food in meals (salads, cooked Moroccan greens such as bakoula, harira with herbs, soups); limited as an ingredient in shelf-stable products because of perishability, colour and flavour changes.", "formats": ["fresh and cooked greens in ready meals and soups", "frozen chopped spinach/chard", "dried greens powders (spinach, kale, moringa-type blends) for sauces, breads, crackers", "herb-rich condiments (chermoula-style)"], "stability": "Folate and vitamin C are heat- and light-labile (losses with boiling and storage); nitrate is water-soluble and leaches into cooking water; lutein is relatively stable and better absorbed with fat; drying concentrates nitrate, oxalate and contaminants.", "taste": "Bitterness of crucifers and rocket; earthy notes of powders limit inclusion to a few percent in baked goods.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Lutein and vitamin K1 absorption improves with dietary fat (olive oil); spinach iron and calcium are poorly bioavailable (oxalate); cooking increases carotenoid availability but reduces folate.", "dose_feasibility": "The cohort exposure (about 1 serving/day, roughly 1 cup raw or 1/2 cup cooked) is achievable in one meal; because spinach is about 90% water, a powder would need roughly 3-8 g dried greens to approximate one serving (approximate estimate, not measured); no 'cognitive dose' exists because no trial established one.", "cost_availability": "Widely available and inexpensive in Moroccan markets year-round (mallow, chard, spinach, herbs); EU/US supply ubiquitous.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 3, "effect_magnitude": 2, "safety": 9, "reproducibility": 5, "food_suitability": 7, "cost_access": 9, "rationale": "evidence_quality 3: best outcomes are grade C (observational cohorts and meta-analyses in older adults); randomised tests are few and null; grade D (insufficient) for healthy young adults and students. effect_magnitude 2: cohort differences are about 0.01-0.05 SD per year, equivalent to a few years of ageing over a decade, and nothing measurable in the short term. safety 9: ordinary food; cautions for warfarin stability, infant nitrate, oxalate and hygiene. reproducibility 5: direction replicated across many cohorts and continents, but lagged, midlife and Western analyses are often null and one cohort found an opposite executive-function association. food_suitability 7: ideal in meals, harder in shelf-stable products. cost_access 9: cheap and ubiquitous, including in Morocco."}, "research_gaps": ["No randomised trial of leafy-green intake (as food) on measured cognition in healthy young adults or students, acute or chronic", "Food-based RCTs in older adults isolating greens from whole-diet change, with cognitive primary outcomes and >=2 years duration", "Separation of folate, vitamin K, lutein and nitrate effects (collinear in food); biomarker-calibrated intake studies", "Cohorts with lagged analyses to quantify reverse causation; North African cohort data (none found)", "Whether nitrate-rich greens acutely change cerebral blood flow and cognition in young adults (only one null spinach trial)", "Safety and contaminant profile of dried greens powders as product ingredients"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 5.2, "eligibility": "eligible", "safety_gate": "pass", "regulatory_gate": "pass", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": "C", "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"cognitive_decline": "C", "dementia_risk": "C", "processing_speed": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 9, "n_studies": 22, "n_verified": 22, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 8, "n_systematic_reviews": 5, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "legumes", "name": "Legumes (lentils, chickpeas, beans, fava)", "category": "J", "category_name": "Whole foods", "aliases": ["pulses (dry edible seeds of legumes)", "lentils (adass), chickpeas (hummus/hommos), white beans (loubia), fava/faba/broad beans (foul), split peas", "bissara (Moroccan fava or split-pea puree soup), harira (lentil-chickpea soup)", "soybeans and soy foods (tofu, natto, soy milk) - discussed separately because of isoflavones", "beans, peas, lentils (US Dietary Guidelines term)"], "food_sources": ["lentils (brown, green, red), chickpeas, dried fava beans (whole or split), white and red kidney beans, split peas, lupin", "Moroccan staples: bissara, harira, loubia, adass, roasted chickpeas, chickpea-flour street food (karane/calentica)", "soy foods (tofu, natto, soy milk, soy protein) - the main isoflavone source; Moroccan pulses contain little isoflavone", "pulse flours and protein concentrates (chickpea, lentil, fava, pea) used in snacks, pasta and breads"], "active_constituents": ["slowly digestible starch and resistant starch (low glycaemic index)", "dietary fibre including galacto-oligosaccharides (raffinose family)", "plant protein (roughly 20-25% of dry weight; lysine-rich, methionine-limited)", "folate (cooked lentils and chickpeas are among the richest common sources; approximate food-composition values, verify)", "non-haem iron, zinc, magnesium, potassium", "polyphenols (tannins, flavonoids); isoflavones mainly in soy", "anti-nutrients and toxicants: phytate, lectins (raw kidney beans), vicine/convicine (fava), L-DOPA (fava), beta-ODAP (grass pea)"], "one_line_verdict": "Legumes are cheap, safe for most people, low-glycaemic sources of protein, fibre, folate and iron that fit Moroccan food culture, but direct evidence that they improve or protect cognition is sparse and inconsistent (a handful of mainly Asian cohorts and soy-isoflavone trials in older women, the largest of which were null), there is no trial of lentils, chickpeas or fava on cognition in young adults, and fava beans carry a real haemolysis risk for people with G6PD deficiency.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is shown. Pulses reliably lower the glycaemic response of a meal and, over weeks, modestly improve markers of glycaemic control: in 41 trials, pulses alone lowered fasting glucose (SMD -0.82) and, within low-GI diets, HbA1c or fructosamine (SMD -0.28), with large unexplained heterogeneity [sievenpiper2009]. This is a metabolic, not a cognitive, outcome. Cognitive evidence. Cohort data specific to legumes are few and mixed. In a Japanese cohort of 3,739 adults aged 40-64, higher bean intake (including soy) showed a weak inverse association with disabling dementia (HR about 0.78-0.84, P-trend 0.21), significant only for dementia without stroke, and driven by natto rather than tofu [kishida2023]. In 5,691 illiterate Chinese elders, 'always' eating legumes was associated with less 3-year cognitive decline (OR 0.78) [chen2012]; in Japanese women soy and bean intake was associated with lower odds of cognitive impairment [nakamoto2018]; in Cache County the combined 'nuts and legumes' component predicted higher cognitive scores [wengreen2013]; but in the Dutch Doetinchem cohort legume intake was not associated with cognition or its change [nooyens2011]. A dose-response meta-analysis of soy products found OR 0.92 for major neurocognitive disorder but a non-significant OR 1.20 for cognitive impairment, with high heterogeneity [yu2025]. Randomised evidence exists only for soy isoflavones: meta-analyses report small pooled benefits on global cognition and memory (SMD 0.19 [cui2020]; 0.25 [till2026]), but the two largest and longest trials in healthy postmenopausal women were null (WISH, 2.5 years, SMD 0.11, 95% CI -0.13 to 0.35 [henderson2012]; 12 months, 202 women [kreijkamp2004]); positive trials are small (34 men, spatial working memory only [thorp2009]; 61 older adults, industry-funded fermented soy [west2025]). In students, one small 10-week high-soy diet trial reported better memory and mental flexibility [file2001] and has not been replicated. Because lentils, chickpeas and fava contain very little isoflavone, these soy results do not transfer to Moroccan pulses. No trial has tested a legume meal on acute cognition; low-glycaemic-load breakfasts in general give at most a small late-morning memory advantage (SMD 0.16, p=0.05) [gaylor2022]. How large and for whom. Any benefit is plausibly indirect (cardiometabolic, deficiency correction of folate or iron) and small. For healthy young adults the honest grade is 'insufficient'. Safety. Fava beans can trigger acute haemolysis (favism) in G6PD deficiency [luzzatto2018], present in Moroccan newborns [eljanahi2024]; fava also contains L-DOPA relevant to Parkinson's therapy [lambea2021]; undercooked kidney beans cause lectin poisoning [rodhouse1990].", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Legumes are nutritionally valuable mainly as sources of folate, iron, zinc, magnesium, fibre and protein, which matters most where intakes are low (low-income households, vegetarians, women of reproductive age, older adults with poor diets). Correcting iron or folate deficiency can support normal cognitive function (see iron and folate-b9 monographs), but no trial has shown that eating more pulses improves cognition in adequately nourished adults, and pulse iron is poorly absorbed (phytate) unless combined with vitamin C or processed (soaking, sprouting, fermentation). The soy-isoflavone literature concerns oestrogen-receptor effects in postmenopausal women [cui2020, henderson2012], not deficiency correction, and is not transferable to lentils, chickpeas or fava. Evidence from older Asian cohorts [kishida2023, chen2012] must not be generalised to students.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Claims such as 'lentils are brain food', 'chickpeas boost focus' or 'beans prevent dementia' are not supported: the cognitive literature consists of a few inconsistent cohorts and soy-isoflavone trials whose largest studies were null. Defensible messaging is nutritional and factual: 'high in fibre', 'source of protein', 'source of folate/iron' when the product meets EU/Moroccan nutrition-claim conditions, and authorised nutrient health claims such as 'Folate contributes to normal psychological function' or 'Iron contributes to normal cognitive function' where the iron content qualifies (EU Reg. 432/2012) - with the caveat that pulse iron bioavailability is low, so a cognition-themed iron claim on a legume product would be legally possible but scientifically weak. A low-glycaemic 'steady energy' narrative is plausible but not proven for cognition [gaylor2022]. Any fava-containing product sold in Morocco or to Mediterranean consumers needs a clear G6PD (favism) warning in practice.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Pulses could plausibly support brain health indirectly: their slowly digested starch and fibre blunt postprandial glucose and improve glycaemic control; fermentation of their fibre produces short-chain fatty acids that improve next-meal glucose tolerance; they supply folate (homocysteine), iron and zinc (deficiency correction) and plant protein; and as part of plant-rich patterns they lower LDL cholesterol and blood pressure. Soy additionally supplies isoflavones that act on oestrogen receptor beta in brain regions involved in memory. Only the metabolic steps are established in humans; cognitive effects of non-soy pulses are untested.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Pulses -> slowly digestible and resistant starch, fibre (including galacto-oligosaccharides), protein, folate, iron, magnesium", "detail": "Composition well characterised; anti-nutrients (phytate, lectins, vicine/convicine) reduced by soaking and cooking.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Low glycaemic index -> smaller postprandial glucose and insulin excursions; improved fasting glucose and HbA1c over weeks", "detail": "41 trials: pulses alone lowered fasting glucose SMD -0.82; within low-GI diets HbA1c/fructosamine SMD -0.28 [sievenpiper2009].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Stable glucose supply -> possibly better late-postprandial memory and attention", "detail": "Low-GL breakfasts give SMD 0.16 for immediate episodic memory after 120 min (p=0.05); not tested with legume meals [gaylor2022].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Colonic fermentation of pulse fibre -> short-chain fatty acids -> gut hormone and insulin-sensitivity effects; gut-brain signalling", "detail": "Metabolic effects shown for cereal-fibre evening meals in humans; brain effects largely animal (see prebiotics/fermented-food monographs).", "evidence_level": "plausible"}, {"step": "Folate and iron supply -> correction of deficiency -> normal cognitive function", "detail": "Relevant only in deficient or low-status groups (see folate-b9 and iron monographs); phytate limits iron absorption.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Soy isoflavones -> oestrogen receptor beta agonism in hippocampus and prefrontal cortex", "detail": "Mechanism from animal work; human RCTs give small pooled effects but null large trials [cui2020, henderson2012]. Not applicable to lentils, chickpeas, fava.", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Lower LDL cholesterol, blood pressure and diabetes risk -> less cerebrovascular disease over decades", "detail": "Plausible route for cohort associations, e.g., stronger inverse association with dementia without stroke history [kishida2023] is not easily explained by this route; untested.", "evidence_level": "speculative"}, {"step": "Measured human outcome", "detail": "Inconsistent cohort associations with cognitive decline/dementia [kishida2023, chen2012, nooyens2011]; no pulse RCT with cognitive outcomes; soy RCTs mixed.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["glycaemic control (low GI, second-meal effect)", "short-chain fatty acid / gut-brain axis", "one-carbon metabolism (folate)", "iron and zinc status", "oestrogen receptor beta (soy isoflavones only)", "cardiometabolic risk reduction"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "dementia_risk", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Japanese adults 40-64 y at baseline followed into old age (CIRCS); soy-product cohorts in meta-analysis (mostly East Asian)", "dose_or_intake": "Quintiles of bean intake (24-h recall, includes soy); per gram/day soy products", "duration": "Up to ~16 years (59,681 person-years)", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Weak inverse association of bean intake with disabling dementia overall (trend not significant), significant for dementia without stroke and for natto but not tofu; soy-product meta-analysis shows a small inverse association with major neurocognitive disorder but a non-significant opposite estimate for cognitive impairment.", "effect_estimate": "Highest vs lowest beans HR 0.78 (0.62-0.99), P-trend 0.21; non-stroke dementia HR 0.69 (0.51-0.92) [kishida2023]; soy OR 0.92 (0.84-0.99) for major NCD, OR 1.20 (0.83-1.72) for cognitive impairment [yu2025]", "certainty_notes": "Observational, single 24-h recall, few cohorts, mostly soy in East Asian populations (indirect for Moroccan pulses), heterogeneity and inconsistent direction across outcomes.", "key_study_ids": ["kishida2023", "yu2025"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_decline", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Older adults in China (CLHLS, illiterate), Japan (NILS-LSA), USA (Cache County) and middle-aged Dutch adults (Doetinchem)", "dose_or_intake": "Habitual legume frequency ('always' vs rarely); quintiles; per SD", "duration": "3-11 years", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Some cohorts associate legumes with less decline or better scores (CLHLS OR 0.78; Japanese women OR 0.48 per SD of bean intake; Cache County nuts-and-legumes +1.22 3MS points), but Doetinchem found no association for legumes, and legumes are rarely analysed separately from nuts or soy.", "effect_estimate": "AOR 0.78 (0.64-0.96) [chen2012]; OR 0.48 (0.28-0.81) per SD in women [nakamoto2018]; Q5 vs Q1 +1.22 3MS points (P<0.001) [wengreen2013]; null [nooyens2011]", "certainty_notes": "Observational; crude frequency exposures; combination with nuts or soy; small cohorts; inconsistent; publication bias possible because null component analyses are often unreported.", "key_study_ids": ["chen2012", "nakamoto2018", "wengreen2013", "nooyens2011"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Mostly healthy postmenopausal women (mean age ~60) and some older men; soy isoflavone supplements or isoflavone-rich soy protein", "dose_or_intake": "About 60-120 mg isoflavones/day (e.g., 25 g soy protein with 91-99 mg isoflavones)", "duration": "6 weeks to 2.5 years", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Meta-analyses find small pooled improvements in global cognition and memory, but the two largest, longest, low-risk-of-bias trials were null on their primary cognitive outcomes; positive findings come from small or industry-funded trials and secondary outcomes.", "effect_estimate": "SMD 0.19 (0.07-0.32) global, 0.15 (0.03-0.26) memory [cui2020]; composite 0.25 (0.04-0.45), memory 0.33 (0.04-0.62) [till2026]; WISH 0.11 (-0.13 to 0.35) [henderson2012]; JAMA trial null [kreijkamp2004]", "certainty_notes": "Downgraded for inconsistency (large trials null), possible small-study/publication bias, and indirectness to non-soy pulses and to younger people.", "key_study_ids": ["cui2020", "till2026", "henderson2012", "kreijkamp2004", "west2025"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "students", "population_detail": "Healthy young student volunteers of both sexes (UK)", "dose_or_intake": "Supervised high-soy diet (100 mg isoflavones/day) vs low-soy diet (0.5 mg/day)", "duration": "10 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "High-soy diet improved immediate prose recall, delayed pattern matching, 20-min picture recall and rule-shifting; letter fluency and planning improved only in women; no effect on attention.", "effect_estimate": "Significant improvements reported; effect sizes and sample size not in abstract [file2001]", "certainty_notes": "Single small 2001 trial, abstract only, many outcomes, not replicated in young adults; soy-specific (isoflavones) and not applicable to lentils, chickpeas or fava.", "key_study_ids": ["file2001"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "34 healthy men (age range not stated in abstract), crossover", "dose_or_intake": "116 mg isoflavone equivalents/day (capsules)", "duration": "6 weeks per arm", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Spatial working memory improved (18% fewer attempts, 23% fewer errors, 17% less time) but auditory, episodic memory, executive function and visuospatial processing did not change.", "effect_estimate": "Spatial WM P=0.01; other domains null [thorp2009]", "certainty_notes": "Single small trial; one positive outcome among many; supplement rather than food.", "key_study_ids": ["thorp2009"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults 18-35 y, students and knowledge workers eating lentils, chickpeas, beans or fava", "dose_or_intake": "Not studied", "duration": "Not studied", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No cohort or trial has tested non-soy pulses on measured cognition in healthy young adults; reviews of plant-food patterns note the absence of direct evidence.", "effect_estimate": "not reported (no studies)", "certainty_notes": "Absence of evidence; only indirect metabolic rationale.", "key_study_ids": ["rajaram2019"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Adults 20-80 y in low- vs high-glycaemic-load breakfast trials (no legume-specific trial exists)", "dose_or_intake": "Low-GL vs high-GL breakfast (legume meals would be low-GL; untested)", "duration": "Single meal; testing up to ~3-4 h", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No effect of glycaemic load up to 119 min; after 120 min a small advantage for low-GL meals on immediate episodic memory, larger in adults <35 y. Whether a lentil or chickpea meal reproduces this is unknown.", "effect_estimate": "SMD 0.16 (95% CI -0.00 to 0.32), p=0.05, I2=5% after 120 min [gaylor2022]", "certainty_notes": "Indirect for legumes (extrapolated from general low-GL meals); borderline effect; graded D for legume-specific acute cognition.", "key_study_ids": ["gaylor2022"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "other", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Adults with and without diabetes; outcome is glycaemic control (biomarker), not cognition", "dose_or_intake": "Pulses alone or within low-GI/high-fibre diets", "duration": ">=7 days to months", "grade": "B", "direction": "benefit", "effect_summary": "Pulses lower fasting glucose and insulin and, within low-GI or high-fibre diets, HbA1c/fructosamine; heterogeneity high and unexplained.", "effect_estimate": "Fasting glucose SMD -0.82 (-1.36 to -0.27); HbA1c/fructosamine SMD -0.28 (-0.42 to -0.14) [sievenpiper2009]", "certainty_notes": "Many RCTs but high heterogeneity and mixed populations (downgraded one level); metabolic biomarker only - must not be presented as a cognitive benefit.", "key_study_ids": ["sievenpiper2009"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "Low-glycaemic-load breakfast (general; no legume-specific trial)", "onset": "No effect before 120 min; small memory advantage after 120 min", "duration_studied": "Up to ~3-4 h post-meal", "domain": "immediate episodic memory", "population": "Adults 20-80 y (15 studies); larger effect <35 y", "effect_estimate": "SMD 0.16 (-0.00 to 0.32), p=0.05 [gaylor2022]", "certainty": "low; indirect for legumes", "safety": "Ordinary foods; flatulence possible with legume meals", "study_ids": ["gaylor2022"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "59,681 person-years", "population": "3,739 Japanese adults 40-64 y at baseline (CIRCS)", "outcome": "Disabling dementia (insurance-certified)", "effect_estimate": "Highest vs lowest beans HR 0.78 (0.62-0.99), trend P=0.21; without stroke HR 0.69 (0.51-0.92), trend P=0.03; natto inverse, tofu null", "evidence_quality": "observational, single 24-h recall", "limitations": "Beans include soy; one dietary recall; administrative dementia outcome", "study_ids": ["kishida2023"]}, {"duration": "3 years", "population": "5,691 illiterate Chinese adults >=65 y (CLHLS)", "outcome": "MMSE-r decline below 18", "effect_estimate": "'Always' legumes AOR 0.78 (0.64-0.96)", "evidence_quality": "observational, nested case-control", "limitations": "Crude frequency questions; illiterate population; residual confounding", "study_ids": ["chen2012"]}, {"duration": "Longitudinal (NILS-LSA follow-ups)", "population": "776 Japanese adults 60-81 y", "outcome": "Cognitive impairment (MMSE <=23)", "effect_estimate": "Women: OR 0.48 (0.28-0.81) per SD total beans; soy isoflavones 0.55 (0.32-0.93)", "evidence_quality": "observational, 3-day dietary record", "limitations": "Small; findings reported for women only in abstract", "study_ids": ["nakamoto2018"]}, {"duration": "11 years", "population": "3,831 adults >=65 y (Cache County)", "outcome": "Modified Mini-Mental State (3MS)", "effect_estimate": "Nuts and legumes Q5 vs Q1 +1.22 3MS points (P<0.001)", "evidence_quality": "observational", "limitations": "Legumes combined with nuts; level difference not slower decline", "study_ids": ["wengreen2013"]}, {"duration": "5 years", "population": "2,613 Dutch adults 43-70 y (Doetinchem)", "outcome": "Global cognition and domains", "effect_estimate": "Legume intake not associated with baseline or change", "evidence_quality": "observational", "limitations": "Low legume intake range in Netherlands", "study_ids": ["nooyens2011"]}, {"duration": "2.5 years (RCT)", "population": "350 healthy postmenopausal women 45-92 y (WISH)", "outcome": "Global cognition composite (14 tests)", "effect_estimate": "Difference 0.11 SD (-0.13 to 0.35); visual memory factor 0.33 (0.06-0.60) secondary", "evidence_quality": "high (double-blind, ~90% adherence, Class I)", "limitations": "Women only; soy protein supplement, not pulses", "study_ids": ["henderson2012"]}, {"duration": "12 months (RCT)", "population": "202 women 60-75 y", "outcome": "Memory, attention, verbal skills, MMSE", "effect_estimate": "No difference vs milk protein placebo", "evidence_quality": "high (double-blind, population-based)", "limitations": "Started after age 60; soy protein supplement", "study_ids": ["kreijkamp2004"]}, {"duration": "10 weeks (RCT)", "population": "Healthy young students (UK)", "outcome": "Memory, attention, frontal function", "effect_estimate": "High-soy diet improved short- and long-term memory and mental flexibility", "evidence_quality": "low (single small trial)", "limitations": "Unreplicated; sample size not in abstract; soy-specific", "study_ids": ["file2001"]}, {"duration": "12 weeks (RCT)", "population": "61 adults >=65 y (47 completers)", "outcome": "Global cognition, memory, fluency, speed, executive function", "effect_estimate": "Memory improved (p=0.041); global cognition only in post hoc women >=70", "evidence_quality": "low (small, post hoc, industry-funded)", "limitations": "High dropout; post hoc subgroups; fermented soy powder", "study_ids": ["west2025"]}, {"duration": ">=7 days to months (41 RCTs)", "population": "Adults with and without diabetes", "outcome": "Fasting glucose, insulin, HbA1c/fructosamine", "effect_estimate": "FBG SMD -0.82; HbA1c/fructosamine SMD -0.28 (low-GI diets)", "evidence_quality": "moderate (metabolic biomarker)", "limitations": "High heterogeneity; not cognition", "study_ids": ["sievenpiper2009"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Cooked pulses are safe for most people and widely recommended. Specific hazards: favism (acute haemolysis) from fava beans in G6PD deficiency, which occurs in Morocco and around the Mediterranean; L-DOPA in fava relevant to Parkinson's therapy (and, theoretically, MAO inhibitors); lectin poisoning from raw or undercooked kidney beans; neurolathyrism from grass pea monodiets; allergy (soy, peanut and lupin are regulated allergens; chickpea and lentil allergy also reported); gastrointestinal symptoms from galacto-oligosaccharides (relevant in IBS); phytate reducing iron and zinc absorption; and mycotoxins in poorly stored pulses [acuna2022].", "adverse_effects": ["Flatulence, bloating and abdominal discomfort from galacto-oligosaccharides (FODMAPs); reduced by soaking, cooking, canning and gradual introduction", "Acute haemolytic anaemia (favism) after fava beans in G6PD-deficient people, potentially life-threatening in children [luzzatto2018]", "Nausea, vomiting and diarrhoea within 1-7 h after raw or undercooked red kidney beans (lectin) [rodhouse1990]", "Allergic reactions (soy, peanut, lupin, and less commonly chickpea, lentil, pea)", "Neurolathyrism with prolonged high grass-pea intake (famine settings) [lambein2019]"], "contraindications": ["G6PD deficiency: avoid fava beans in all forms (bissara from fava, fava flour, fava protein)", "Known legume allergy (soy, peanut, lupin, others); cross-reactivity possible", "Irritable bowel syndrome on a low-FODMAP diet: portion and processing adjustments"], "drug_interactions": ["Levodopa (Parkinson's disease): fava beans contain L-DOPA and can add to dopaminergic effects or dyskinesias [lambea2021]", "MAO inhibitors: broad beans (pods) are traditionally listed among pressor-risk foods because of L-DOPA/dopamine content (not verified this session)", "Levothyroxine: soy foods may reduce absorption if taken together (not researched here; separate dosing commonly advised)", "Iron supplements and minerals: phytate in pulses reduces non-haem iron and zinc absorption when eaten together"], "upper_limits": "No upper limit for pulses as foods. Folic acid UL (1,000 ug/day) does not apply to natural food folate. Soy isoflavone supplements: EFSA (2015) concluded no adverse effects on breast, thyroid or uterus in postmenopausal women at studied doses (not re-verified this session).", "sleep_impact": "None known; large late legume meals may cause GI discomfort at night.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Lifelong consumption in Mediterranean, Middle Eastern and South Asian diets; cohort studies show no safety signal at usual intakes outside the specific hazards listed.", "daily_use_appropriate": "yes", "special_populations": "Children: favism risk with fava in G6PD deficiency is highest in children; choking risk with whole roasted chickpeas in young children. Pregnancy: pulses are a recommended folate and iron source. Older adults: valuable protein source. Parkinson's patients: fava caution. People on low-FODMAP diets: adapt portions.", "specialist_review_needed": ["Haematologist / clinical geneticist for favism and G6PD labelling strategy in Morocco", "Allergen management specialist (soy, peanut, lupin; precautionary labelling)", "Neurologist or clinical pharmacist for levodopa and MAOI wording", "Food-safety review for lectin inactivation, mycotoxin and pesticide limits in pulse ingredients"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "Pulses are safe staple foods, but fava-containing products require favism (G6PD) and levodopa warnings, kidney-bean products require validated cooking, grass pea should be excluded, and allergen labelling must be correct."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Ordinary foods; no novel-food issue for traditional pulses or conventional pulse flours (novel status of new protein isolates obtained by non-traditional processes not checked). No health claim links pulses to cognition. Nutrition claims under Reg. (EC) 1924/2006 (e.g., 'source of/high fibre', 'source of/high protein') and nutrient health claims under Reg. (EU) 432/2012 (folate, iron, magnesium, protein) apply when conditions are met. Soybeans, peanuts and lupin are mandatory-declaration allergens under Reg. (EU) 1169/2011. Soy isoflavone health claims were not authorised by EFSA (not re-verified this session).", "us": "Conventional foods; pulses and pulse flours are common foods. Soy and peanuts are major allergens requiring declaration (FALCPA). Structure/function claims must derive from nutritive value; no cognition claim is substantiated. A soy-protein/CHD health claim exists in 21 CFR 101.82, but FDA proposed its revocation in 2017; its current status was not verified this session.", "morocco": "Ordinary foods under Law 28-07 and Decree 2-12-389 (allergen declaration in Annex I; Arabic labelling); nutrition claims limited to Joint Order 281-16; no authorised health-claim list or procedure found (project unit X6). No Moroccan rule requiring favism warnings on fava products was found (not specifically searched); voluntary warning recommended.", "permitted_claims_examples": ["EU (Reg. 432/2012): 'Folate contributes to normal psychological function' - food must be at least a source of folate", "EU (Reg. 432/2012): 'Iron contributes to normal cognitive function' - food must be at least a source of iron (bioavailability caveat for pulses)"], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "None for whole pulses, flours, bissara or canned beans. Highly purified isoflavone extracts or new pulse protein isolates would need separate review (EU novel food / US GRAS or NDI) - not researched here.", "regulatory_gate": "pass"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Excellent: culturally central in Morocco (harira, bissara, loubia, adass, roasted chickpeas, karane) and versatile in soups, spreads (hummus), snacks, pasta and baked goods via pulse flours.", "formats": ["ready-to-eat soups (harira, bissara, lentil soup)", "canned or retort-pouch cooked pulses", "roasted chickpea or fava snacks", "chickpea/lentil flour in breads, crackers, pasta and bars", "hummus-type spreads"], "stability": "Dry pulses store for months if kept dry (mycotoxin risk with humidity); cooked products need retort or chilled chain; folate partly lost in cooking water; lectins and trypsin inhibitors destroyed by adequate wet heat.", "taste": "Beany/green notes in flours (reduced by roasting); well accepted in traditional dishes; texture issues at high inclusion in baked goods.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Phytate lowers iron and zinc absorption; soaking, sprouting, fermentation and vitamin C (lemon, tomato, peppers) improve iron uptake; protein complements cereals (lysine) in couscous or bread combinations.", "dose_feasibility": "A typical serving (about 1/2 cup cooked, roughly 90-100 g) provides several grams each of protein and fibre and a substantial share of daily folate (approximate food-composition values); this is the intake level in cohorts. Isoflavone doses studied in soy trials (60-120 mg/day) are not achievable from lentils, chickpeas or fava, which contain little isoflavone.", "cost_availability": "Among the cheapest protein sources; produced and widely sold in Morocco, EU and US.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 2.5, "effect_magnitude": 2, "safety": 7.5, "reproducibility": 3, "food_suitability": 8.5, "cost_access": 9, "rationale": "evidence_quality 2.5: cognitive rows are grade C (few, inconsistent cohorts; soy-isoflavone RCTs with null large trials) or D (no evidence for non-soy pulses in young adults or for acute legume meals); grade B only for a metabolic biomarker (glycaemic control). effect_magnitude 2: any cognitive association is small (about 1 point on a 100-point scale; HR ~0.8 with non-significant trends); soy RCT effects SMD ~0.1-0.25 and not applicable to pulses. safety 7.5: safe staple foods but favism in G6PD deficiency (relevant in Morocco), kidney-bean lectins, allergy and levodopa interaction need controls. reproducibility 3: cohort findings inconsistent across countries; isoflavone positives not replicated in the best trials. food_suitability 8.5: culturally central, versatile, low-GI; GI tolerance and flavour limit some uses. cost_access 9: cheap and locally produced."}, "research_gaps": ["No RCT of lentils, chickpeas or fava beans (as food) on any measured cognitive outcome, acute or chronic, in any adult population", "Acute crossover trials of low-GI legume breakfasts/lunches on late-morning or post-lunch attention and memory in students and knowledge workers", "Cohort analyses separating non-soy pulses from soy and from nuts, with lagged exposure, in Mediterranean and North African populations", "Cognitive effects of legume-based iron/folate improvement in iron-deficient young women (food-based trial)", "National G6PD deficiency prevalence in Morocco and consumer awareness of favism; testing of low vicine-convicine faba varieties", "Publication-bias assessment of soy isoflavone trials (large null trials vs small positive trials)"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 4.7, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "pass", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": "C", "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": "B", "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"episodic_memory": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 8, "n_studies": 22, "n_verified": 22, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 6, "n_systematic_reviews": 4, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["benefit", "inconsistent", "insufficient", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "nuts-walnuts-almonds", "name": "Nuts (walnuts, almonds, mixed nuts)", "category": "J", "category_name": "Whole foods", "aliases": ["tree nuts", "walnuts (Juglans regia)", "almonds (Prunus dulcis)", "mixed nuts", "hazelnuts, pistachios, cashews, pecans, Brazil nuts, pine nuts, macadamias", "peanuts (botanically legumes; included in most nut research definitions)", "nut butters", "amlou (Moroccan almond-argan oil-honey spread)"], "food_sources": ["whole raw or dry-roasted unsalted nuts (typical studied serving 30 g/day; trials range 25-85 g/day)", "nut butters and pastes (peanut butter, almond butter, amlou)", "nut-containing bars, breakfast cereals, baked goods (lower and variable nut content)", "walnuts are the only common tree nut rich in ALA (about 9 g/100 g, i.e. about 2.7 g per 30 g); almonds are rich in vitamin E (about 23.5 mg/100 g) and magnesium (about 270 mg/100 g); Brazil nuts are extremely rich in selenium (composition values per USDA FNDDS as tabulated in the seeds monograph source george2022)"], "active_constituents": ["alpha-linolenic acid (walnuts) and linoleic acid", "monounsaturated fat (oleic acid; almonds, hazelnuts, pecans, macadamias)", "vitamin E (alpha- and gamma-tocopherol)", "polyphenols: ellagitannins/ellagic acid (walnuts, pecans; metabolised by gut microbiota to urolithins), flavan-3-ols and proanthocyanidins (skins)", "fibre, plant protein, arginine, phytosterols", "minerals: magnesium, zinc, copper, selenium (Brazil nuts), folate", "melatonin and serotonin (walnuts, small amounts)"], "one_line_verdict": "Nuts are a safe (except for allergic people), weight-neutral and cardioprotective everyday food with consistent observational links to better cognitive ageing, but randomised trials show no reliable effect on global cognition and only small, unreplicated single-task effects in young adults, so they belong in Brain Food as a nutritious snack base, not as a proven cognitive enhancer.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. Nuts (30-60 g/day of walnuts, almonds or mixed tree nuts) are a nutrient-dense food that does not cause weight gain in RCTs (body weight MD 0.09 kg, 95% CI -0.09 to 0.27) [nishi2021], lowers LDL cholesterol (-0.12 mmol/L across 113 RCTs) [nishi2025] and improves some vascular markers [nijssen2023, coates2020]. Whether they improve cognition is much less clear. In older adults the largest and longest trial (WAHA, n=708, 30-60 g/day walnuts for 2 years) found no effect on global cognition (change -0.072 vs -0.086 SD, P=0.49), with only a post-hoc benefit at one site [waha2020]. Almond trials of 12 weeks to 6 months in healthy, overweight or prediabetic middle-aged and older adults were null [coates2020, mustrarakic2022, adams2026], whereas an open-label, almond-industry-funded trial in Indian adults with prediabetes reported better executive function and processing speed alongside weight loss [gulati2026]. A 16-week crossover of 60 g/day mixed nuts in 28 older adults raised regional cerebral blood flow and improved verbal (+1 word) and visuospatial memory (-4 errors) but not executive function [nijssen2023]. The PREDIMED nut arm (30 g/day mixed nuts within a Mediterranean diet versus low-fat advice) showed a better memory composite [vallspedret2015] and MMSE (+0.57 points) [martinezlapiscina2013], but these test a whole dietary pattern in a trial with documented randomisation irregularities. Systematic reviews call the evidence inconsistent [theodore2021] or could not pool walnut trials [cahoon2021], and the most recent meta-analysis (5 RCTs, n=928) found no significant effect (SMD 0.27, 95% CI -0.65 to 1.19) [moabedi2024]. Cohorts consistently associate nut intake with better cognition or lower dementia risk (UK Biobank HR 0.88, 0.77-0.99 [bizzozeroperoni2025]; 0.13 SD less 2-year decline at 7 or more servings/week [ni2023]; +0.08 SD, equal to about 2 years of ageing [obrien2014]) but cannot exclude healthy-user confounding. In whom and how large for the Brain Food target. For students and young adults the evidence is thin: 8 weeks of walnuts improved one of several outcomes (inferential verbal reasoning +11.2%, d=0.57) with no effect on memory, mood or non-verbal reasoning [pribis2012]; 4 weeks of 30 g/day mixed nuts improved picture-recognition accuracy by 2.3 percentage points and speed by 31 ms but no other task consistently in 79 adults aged 18-49 [haskellramsay2023]; acute walnut or pecan meals produced mixed, task-specific effects including worse memory at 2 h [bell2025, guadagni2025]. In 771 adolescents, 30 g/day walnuts for 6 months had no effect on any primary neuropsychological outcome by intention-to-treat [pinarmarti2023]. Mood, stress and sleep benefits in students and young adults come from open-label or subgroup analyses with self-reported outcomes [herselman2022, zeronrugerio2026, zeronrugerio2025, pribis2016]. How reproducible. Positive findings are usually single secondary outcomes in small trials, often with industry-donated nuts or nut-council-linked investigators; the larger, pre-specified trials are null. What is unknown. No adequately powered, preregistered trial has tested nuts on attention, exam-period cognition or academic performance in students; dose-response, nut type, and the contribution of ALA versus polyphenols remain unresolved.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Nuts do not correct a defined deficiency in the populations studied: no trial selected participants for low vitamin E, magnesium, zinc, selenium or ALA status, and most participants were well-nourished Western or Spanish adults. Any benefit for healthy young adults or students would therefore be an enhancement effect, and that is precisely where the evidence is weakest (single positive tasks among many null outcomes [pribis2012, haskellramsay2023]; null intention-to-treat result in adolescents [pinarmarti2023]). Reviews note that studies in groups at higher risk of cognitive decline tend to report more favourable results [theodore2021], and in PREDIMED the comparator was a low-fat diet, so the observed difference partly reflects what the control group lacked [vallspedret2015]. A nutrient-correction role is plausible for people with low intakes of magnesium or vitamin E, and Brazil nuts can correct selenium deficiency, but these scenarios were not tested in the cognitive trials reviewed here and should not be generalised to well-nourished students. Evidence in older adults, adolescents and people with prediabetes must not be transferred to healthy young adults.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "The walnut's brain-like shape and labels such as 'brain food' or 'nuts for memory' overstate the evidence: the largest RCTs (WAHA, adolescent WALNUTS trial) are null on their primary cognitive outcomes, the most recent meta-analysis is null, and positive results are small, secondary and unreplicated. What can legally be said is narrower. In the EU, 'Walnuts contribute to the improvement of the elasticity of blood vessels' is authorised (Reg. (EU) 432/2012) only for foods providing 30 g walnuts/day, with that intake stated. Nutrient-function claims are available when the product is at least a 'source' of the nutrient: almonds and most tree nuts can qualify for 'Zinc contributes to normal cognitive function' and 'Magnesium contributes to normal psychological function', but these are generic nutrient claims; using them on a 'brain' nut product invites consumers to infer an enhancement effect that the nut trials do not show, and FTC/ASA-style scrutiny would ask for product-specific substantiation of any implied cognitive benefit. In the US, the 2003 qualified health claim for nuts and heart disease (1.5 oz/day, 'Scientific evidence suggests but does not prove...') is the only nut-specific health claim; there is no cognitive claim. Honest positioning: 'a daily handful of unsalted nuts is part of a heart- and brain-healthy diet pattern', supported by cardiometabolic evidence and observational cognitive data, with no promise of sharper thinking.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Nuts deliver unsaturated fats (including ALA in walnuts), vitamin E, polyphenols that gut bacteria convert to urolithins, fibre and minerals. In humans they raise blood ALA and alpha-tocopherol, modestly lower LDL cholesterol and blood pressure, improve endothelial function and, in one small trial, increased regional cerebral blood flow. The hypothesised route to cognition is long-term vascular and metabolic protection of the brain plus anti-inflammatory and antioxidant effects; acute effects might involve postprandial fuel availability. Conversion of ALA to DHA is low (see alpha-linolenic-acid-ala monograph), so a DHA-mediated mechanism is unlikely. Animal studies of walnut peptides and ellagic acid show neuroprotection, but mechanism is not efficacy: the human cognitive endpoints remain largely null.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Daily nut intake (30-60 g) -> absorption of ALA, tocopherols and polyphenol metabolites", "detail": "Red-cell ALA rose by 0.04 percentage points (95% CI 0.03-0.06) after 6 months of 30 g/day walnuts in adolescents [pinarmarti2023]; serum alpha-tocopherol rose 8% with about 85 g/day almonds [mustrarakic2022]; urinary urolithins rose with 40 g/day walnuts [zeronrugerio2026].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Improved cardiometabolic and peripheral vascular function", "detail": "Mixed nuts improved flow-mediated dilation (+1.6 percentage points) and pulse-wave velocity (-0.6 m/s) [nijssen2023]; almonds reduced triglycerides and systolic blood pressure [coates2020]; EU authorised a walnut claim on blood-vessel elasticity at 30 g/day.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Increased cerebral perfusion / brain vascular function", "detail": "Regional CBF +5.0 to 5.6 mL/100 g/min with 60 g/day mixed nuts in 28 older adults [nijssen2023]; no CBF change with 50 g/day almonds in prediabetes [adams2026]. Biomarker only.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Gut microbiota modulation (gut-brain axis)", "detail": "Enrichment of an unclassified Lachnospiraceae taxon with mixed nuts, uncorrelated with cognitive change [haskellramsay2023]; higher microbial diversity in stressed female students eating walnuts [herselman2022]. Causal link to brain function speculative.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Postprandial fuel and neurochemical effects (acute)", "detail": "Walnut breakfast lowered circulating NEFA and slightly increased glucose availability [bell2025]; walnuts raised urinary 6-sulfatoxymelatonin [zeronrugerio2025] and 5-HIAA [zeronrugerio2026], which may simply reflect dietary melatonin/serotonin in walnuts rather than central effects.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Antioxidant, anti-inflammatory and neurotrophic neuroprotection", "detail": "Walnut peptides, walnut oil and ellagic acid reduce oxidative stress and memory deficits in rodent models (seen in search results; not extracted). A walnut meta-analysis found no effect on inflammation or glucose homeostasis markers in humans [cahoon2021].", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Measured human cognitive outcome", "detail": "Global cognition null in the largest trials [waha2020, pinarmarti2023, moabedi2024]; small single-task memory or reasoning effects in small trials [pribis2012, haskellramsay2023, nijssen2023].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["vascular and endothelial function / cerebral blood flow", "lipid lowering (LDL) and blood pressure", "ALA and n-3 status (limited conversion to DHA)", "tocopherol antioxidant status", "ellagitannin to urolithin gut metabolism", "gut microbiota modulation", "postprandial glycaemic and lipid responses", "brain insulin sensitivity (reported in a companion paper of the mixed-nut crossover; not included as a study here)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults aged 18-30 (walnut) and healthy adults (pecan; age range not in abstract), crossover designs", "dose_or_intake": "50 g walnuts in breakfast; 68 g pecans in a shake", "duration": "Single meal, tested 1-6 h after", "grade": "D", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Walnut breakfast gave faster executive-task reaction times over 6 h but worse memory recall at 2 h (better at 6 h) and worse negative affect; pecan meal favoured 8 of 23 measures (attention, processing speed, recall) while control favoured 3 (recognition, numeric working memory).", "effect_estimate": "Not reported as standardized effects in abstracts; P-values per task only (e.g., pecan delayed recall P=0.002; control word recognition P=0.017)", "certainty_notes": "Two small pilot crossover trials (n=32, n=31) with many outcomes and no clear multiplicity control; directions conflict across tasks; pecan comparator was a high-saturated-fat shake, not a neutral meal. Exploratory pilot evidence without a convincing net cognitive benefit: graded D (consistent with the ALA monograph's D for the walnut trial).", "key_study_ids": ["bell2025", "guadagni2025"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Overweight/obese adults on an energy-restricted diet (post-lunch dip paradigm)", "dose_or_intake": "Almond-enriched high-fat lunch (almonds 70-75% of lunch energy) vs high-carbohydrate lunch", "duration": "Single lunch, tested post-lunch", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "The almond lunch attenuated the post-lunch decline in memory by 57.7% relative to a high-carbohydrate lunch; attention declined similarly in both.", "effect_estimate": "57.7% smaller memory decline (P=0.004); absolute scores not in abstract", "certainty_notes": "Single trial; contrast is macronutrient composition (high-fat vs high-carbohydrate), so the effect cannot be attributed to almonds per se; overweight dieting population is indirect for students.", "key_study_ids": ["dhillon2017"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy non-elderly adults 18-49 y (mean 29.9) with low habitual nut intake; college students; healthy young adults (peanut trial)", "dose_or_intake": "30 g/day mixed tree nuts; about 60 g/day walnuts; 25-32 g/day peanuts or peanut butter", "duration": "4 weeks to 6 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Mixed nuts improved picture-recognition accuracy (94.5% vs 92.2%) and speed (757 vs 788 ms), with no consistent effect on word recall, spatial memory or working memory; walnuts did not improve memory in students; peanut products improved immediate memory only in within-group comparisons.", "effect_estimate": "+2.3 percentage points accuracy (P=0.019) and -31 ms (P=0.004) on one task [haskellramsay2023]; memory NS [pribis2012]", "certainty_notes": "Few small trials, multiple outcomes, positive results on one task each and not replicated; peanut trial uses within-group P-values and peanuts are legumes (indirect). Downgraded for imprecision, inconsistency and risk of bias. Practical relevance for a student: negligible to small.", "key_study_ids": ["haskellramsay2023", "pribis2012", "parillimoser2021"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "students", "population_detail": "US college students, healthy young adults (crossover); adolescent data are graded separately and not used here", "dose_or_intake": "About 60 g/day walnuts", "duration": "8 weeks per period", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Walnuts raised inferential verbal reasoning by 11.2% (d=0.57) but did not improve non-verbal reasoning or memory in 64 students.", "effect_estimate": "d=0.567, P=0.009 for one reasoning subtest; other outcomes NS [pribis2012]", "certainty_notes": "Single small student trial with one positive outcome among several, no multiplicity correction reported, not replicated in students. No trial measured academic performance.", "key_study_ids": ["pribis2012"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "students", "population_detail": "University students during exam periods and healthy young adults (mean age 22-24, predominantly female)", "dose_or_intake": "40-60 g/day walnuts; 25-32 g/day peanuts", "duration": "8-16 weeks; 6 months (peanuts)", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Walnuts blunted the exam-period rise in depression and stress scores in an open-label student trial, reduced total mood disturbance by 4.85 POMS points in an open-label crossover, and improved mood only in a male subgroup of a blinded crossover; peanut products lowered anxiety versus a control butter.", "effect_estimate": "DASS-21 depression time x treatment P=0.052 [herselman2022]; TMD -4.85 points, P=0.030 [zeronrugerio2026]; TMD -27.5% in males only, d=0.71, P=0.043, whole sample NS [pribis2016]", "certainty_notes": "Mood is not cognition. Self-reported outcomes in open-label designs (high risk of bias), subgroup-only effects, small samples, multiple scales. Direction fairly consistent but certainty low.", "key_study_ids": ["herselman2022", "zeronrugerio2026", "pribis2016", "parillimoser2021"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "other", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Outcome = sleep quality and daytime sleepiness (not cognition) in healthy young adults (mean 24 y, 85% women)", "dose_or_intake": "40 g walnuts at dinner", "duration": "8 weeks (crossover)", "grade": "D", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Walnuts shortened sleep latency, improved global sleep quality and reduced daytime sleepiness, with higher evening urinary 6-sulfatoxymelatonin.", "effect_estimate": "Sleep latency P=0.001; global sleep quality P=0.002; Epworth sleepiness P=0.002 (effect sizes not in abstract)", "certainty_notes": "Single open-label trial with subjective outcomes; melatonin metabolite rise may reflect dietary melatonin. Exploratory.", "key_study_ids": ["zeronrugerio2025"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "learning", "population": "students", "population_detail": "Academic learning, exam performance or grades in students", "dose_or_intake": "Any", "duration": "Any", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No trial or cohort located that measured learning, exam performance or grades as outcomes of nut intake in students.", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "Evidence gap; extrapolation from reasoning tests or mood scales is not justified.", "key_study_ids": ["pribis2012", "herselman2022"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "children_adolescents", "population_detail": "Healthy adolescents 11-16 y in 12 Barcelona high schools", "dose_or_intake": "30 g/day raw walnut kernels", "duration": "6 months", "grade": "B", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "No intention-to-treat effect on working memory, attention, fluid intelligence, executive function, socio-emotional or ADHD outcomes; adherence-weighted analyses suggested better sustained attention, fluid intelligence and fewer ADHD symptoms.", "effect_estimate": "ITT: all primary endpoints NS; per-protocol attention variability -11.26 ms (-19.92 to -2.60), fluid intelligence +1.78 (0.90-2.67), ADHD score -2.18 (-3.70 to -0.67)", "certainty_notes": "Large randomised trial (n=771) but open-label with limited adherence; per-protocol findings are hypothesis-generating. Children/adolescent evidence must not be generalised to adults.", "key_study_ids": ["pinarmarti2023"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Cognitively healthy community-dwelling adults 50-80 y (normal-weight or overweight, without diabetes or prediabetes); prediabetic trials are graded in the clinical-population row", "dose_or_intake": "30-60 g/day walnuts; 42-85 g/day almonds or almonds at 15% energy", "duration": "12 weeks to 2 years", "grade": "B", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "WAHA (n=708, 2 years) found no effect on the global cognition composite; two almond RCTs in healthy or overweight older adults found no between-group cognitive differences; a meta-analysis of 5 RCTs (mixed adult populations) was null.", "effect_estimate": "WAHA change -0.072 vs -0.086 SD (P=0.491) [waha2020]; pooled SMD 0.27 (-0.65 to 1.19), P=0.57 [moabedi2024]", "certainty_notes": "Consistent null in the largest and longest trials; downgraded once for imprecision and risk of bias (open WAHA design, small almond trials, wide meta-analytic CI). Cannot exclude a small effect (<0.1 SD) or benefit in higher-risk subgroups.", "key_study_ids": ["waha2020", "coates2020", "mustrarakic2022", "moabedi2024", "cahoon2021"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Healthy older adults 60-75 y (crossover, BMI about 28) and middle-aged/older almond consumers", "dose_or_intake": "60 g/day mixed nuts; 85 g/day almonds", "duration": "16 weeks; 6 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Mixed nuts improved verbal memory (+1 word) and visuospatial memory (-4 errors) versus no nuts in 28 adults; almonds improved visual memory only within-group, not versus control; WAHA memory was not improved.", "effect_estimate": "Verbal memory +1 correct (0 to 2), visuospatial -4 errors (-8 to 0) [nijssen2023]; almond between-group NS [mustrarakic2022]", "certainty_notes": "Small, single-blind crossover with confidence intervals touching zero; inconsistent with larger null trials.", "key_study_ids": ["nijssen2023", "mustrarakic2022", "waha2020"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "other", "population_detail": "Older Spanish adults at high cardiovascular risk (PREDIMED), cognitively healthy at entry; intervention was a Mediterranean diet plus nuts versus low-fat advice", "dose_or_intake": "30 g/day mixed nuts (walnuts, almonds, hazelnuts) within a Mediterranean diet", "duration": "4.1-6.5 years", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Memory composite improved relative to control (+0.09 vs -0.17 SD) without significant frontal or global composite differences in Barcelona; MMSE +0.57 and clock drawing +0.33 versus control in Navarra (post-trial assessment only).", "effect_estimate": "Memory composite P=0.04 vs control [vallspedret2015]; MMSE +0.57 (0.11 to 1.03), CDT +0.33 (0.003 to 0.67) [martinezlapiscina2013]", "certainty_notes": "Tests a whole diet against a low-fat comparator, not nuts alone; Navarra had no baseline cognitive testing; high attrition for cognitive follow-up; PREDIMED randomisation irregularities led to the 2018 retraction and republication of the main trial. Indirect for single-food products.", "key_study_ids": ["vallspedret2015", "martinezlapiscina2013"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Adults with prediabetes (Asian Indian 40-60 y; Dutch overweight/obese median 66 y)", "dose_or_intake": "Almonds at 20% energy; 50 g/day almonds", "duration": "24 weeks; 5 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Open-label Almond Board-funded trial reported better executive function and processing speed with almonds plus diet and exercise; a single-blind crossover found no effect on any cognitive outcome or cerebral blood flow.", "effect_estimate": "Stockings of Cambridge beta -2.5 (-4.4 to -0.6) [gulati2026]; all CANTAB outcomes NS [adams2026]", "certainty_notes": "Conflicting small trials; the positive trial is open-label, industry-funded and confounded by weight loss; clinical population is indirect for students.", "key_study_ids": ["gulati2026", "adams2026"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cerebral_blood_flow", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Healthy older adults (mixed nuts) and overweight older adults with prediabetes (almonds)", "dose_or_intake": "60 g/day mixed nuts; 50 g/day almonds", "duration": "16 weeks; 5 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Mixed nuts increased regional CBF in frontal, parietal and prefrontal regions; almonds did not change whole-brain or regional CBF.", "effect_estimate": "+5.0 to +5.6 mL/100 g/min regional CBF (P<0.001) [nijssen2023]; NS [adams2026]", "certainty_notes": "Biomarker, not cognition; two small single-blind crossovers from one group with conflicting results.", "key_study_ids": ["nijssen2023", "adams2026"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "processing_speed", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "US adults 20-59 y (NHANES, cross-sectional) and adults 18-49 (RCT)", "dose_or_intake": "Habitual walnut intake about 10 g/day (observational); 30 g/day mixed nuts (RCT)", "duration": "Habitual; 4 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Walnut consumers aged 20-59 had faster simple reaction time and symbol-digit substitution in cross-sectional data; the 4-week mixed-nut RCT showed no consistent effect on choice reaction time or rapid visual information processing.", "effect_estimate": "-16.4 ms simple reaction time (P=0.03), -0.39 s symbol-digit (P=0.01) [arab2015]; RCT speed tasks NS except picture recognition [haskellramsay2023]", "certainty_notes": "Cross-sectional associations are prone to confounding and reverse causation; the only RCT does not confirm a broad speed effect.", "key_study_ids": ["arab2015", "haskellramsay2023"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "dementia_risk", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Middle-aged and older adults in prospective cohorts (UK Biobank mean 56.5 y; Nurses' Health Study women 70+; PREDIMED-Plus 55-75 y with metabolic syndrome)", "dose_or_intake": "Habitual intake; benefit associations at about 1 handful (30 g)/day or 5-7+ servings/week", "duration": "2-7 years follow-up; decades of exposure", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Daily nut consumption associated with 12% lower incident dementia; higher intake associated with better cognitive status and less 2-year decline in general cognition, but not with slower decline in the Nurses' cohort.", "effect_estimate": "HR 0.88 (0.77-0.99) [bizzozeroperoni2025]; beta 0.13 SD (0.06-0.20) for 7+ servings/week [ni2023]; +0.08 SD (0.00-0.15) [obrien2014]", "certainty_notes": "Observational; residual confounding by socioeconomic status and overall diet quality likely (nut eaters are healthier); effect sizes small; single dietary assessments. RCTs in older adults do not confirm a causal effect on cognition.", "key_study_ids": ["bizzozeroperoni2025", "ni2023", "obrien2014", "theodore2021"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "vascular_health", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Adults of mixed health status (lipid meta-analysis of 113 RCTs); healthy or overweight middle-aged and older adults (vascular function trials)", "dose_or_intake": "Median 45.5 g/day nuts in the lipid meta-analysis; 30-60 g/day in vascular trials (EU walnut claim: 30 g/day)", "duration": "Weeks to months (12-16 weeks in vascular trials)", "grade": "B", "direction": "benefit", "effect_summary": "Nuts lower total and LDL cholesterol, triglycerides and apoB; mixed nuts improved flow-mediated dilation, carotid reactivity and arterial stiffness; almonds lowered systolic blood pressure and triglycerides; the EU authorised a walnut blood-vessel elasticity claim.", "effect_estimate": "LDL-C -0.12 mmol/L (-0.14 to -0.09), TC -0.14 mmol/L (-0.18 to -0.10) [nishi2025]; FMD +1.6 percentage points (1.0 to 2.2), PWV -0.6 m/s (-1.1 to -0.1) [nijssen2023]; SBP and triglycerides reduced (values not in abstract) [coates2020]", "certainty_notes": "Large, consistent RCT evidence for lipids (GRADE moderate/low for most outcomes per the authors; heterogeneity; nut-industry-linked authors); vascular-function trials small. Downgraded from A for indirectness to brain outcomes and author COI. A vascular/cardiometabolic surrogate, not a cognitive outcome.", "key_study_ids": ["nishi2025", "nijssen2023", "coates2020"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "50 g walnuts in a breakfast vs calorie-matched no-nut breakfast", "onset": "Effects tested from 2 h", "duration_studied": "6 h", "domain": "executive function reaction time; episodic memory; mood; EEG", "population": "32 healthy adults aged 18-30", "effect_estimate": "Faster executive-task RTs across the day; memory worse at 2 h, better at 6 h; negative affect worse (effect sizes not in abstract)", "certainty": "Low (pilot crossover, multiple outcomes)", "safety": "No adverse events reported in abstract; disliked by some participants", "study_ids": ["bell2025"]}, {"dose": "68 g pecans in a shake vs isocaloric high-saturated-fat shake", "onset": "1 h", "duration_studied": "4 h", "domain": "attention, processing speed, word recall and recognition, working memory", "population": "31 healthy adults", "effect_estimate": "8 of 23 measures favoured pecans, 3 favoured control", "certainty": "Low (multiplicity; non-neutral comparator)", "safety": "Not reported in abstract", "study_ids": ["guadagni2025"]}, {"dose": "Almond-enriched high-fat lunch vs high-carbohydrate lunch", "onset": "Post-lunch", "duration_studied": "Post-lunch dip period", "domain": "memory and attention", "population": "86 overweight/obese adults on a weight-loss diet", "effect_estimate": "Memory decline 57.7% smaller (P=0.004); attention decline similar", "certainty": "Low (macronutrient confound, single trial)", "safety": "Not reported in abstract", "study_ids": ["dhillon2017"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "8 weeks per arm (crossover)", "population": "64 US college students", "outcome": "Verbal and non-verbal reasoning, memory, mood", "effect_estimate": "Inferential verbal reasoning +11.2% (d=0.57, P=0.009); memory, mood, non-verbal reasoning NS; male-only mood benefit (d=0.71)", "evidence_quality": "Low", "limitations": "Single site, multiple outcomes, impossible to blind walnuts fully, subgroup finding for mood", "study_ids": ["pribis2012", "pribis2016"]}, {"duration": "4 weeks per arm (crossover)", "population": "79 healthy adults 18-49 y", "outcome": "Computerised cognitive battery, mood, microbiota", "effect_estimate": "Picture recognition +2.3 percentage points accuracy, -31 ms; other tasks only order-dependent interactions", "evidence_quality": "Low to moderate", "limitations": "Many outcomes, possible carry-over, short duration, funding not identified in text read", "study_ids": ["haskellramsay2023"]}, {"duration": "16 weeks spanning an exam period", "population": "80 enrolled Australian university students (75% female)", "outcome": "Depression, anxiety, stress, well-being, sleep, microbiota", "effect_estimate": "Control depression/stress rose at exams, walnut group did not; interaction P=0.052 for depression", "evidence_quality": "Low", "limitations": "Open-label, self-report, few men, one cohort during COVID-19", "study_ids": ["herselman2022"]}, {"duration": "6 months", "population": "771 adolescents 11-16 y", "outcome": "Working memory, attention, fluid intelligence, executive function, ADHD symptoms", "effect_estimate": "ITT null for all primary endpoints; per-protocol benefits", "evidence_quality": "Moderate (for the null ITT result)", "limitations": "Open-label, modest adherence, multiple primary endpoints, walnuts donated by industry", "study_ids": ["pinarmarti2023"]}, {"duration": "8 weeks per arm (crossover)", "population": "Healthy young adults (n=76 sleep; n=31 mood), mostly women", "outcome": "Sleep quality and mood", "effect_estimate": "Sleep latency and quality improved; TMD -4.85 points", "evidence_quality": "Low", "limitations": "Open-label, subjective outcomes, urinary metabolites may reflect dietary content", "study_ids": ["zeronrugerio2025", "zeronrugerio2026"]}, {"duration": "6 months", "population": "63 healthy young adults (peanut products)", "outcome": "Memory, anxiety, depression, cortisol", "effect_estimate": "Immediate memory improved within peanut groups; anxiety lower vs control butter", "evidence_quality": "Low", "limitations": "Legume not tree nut; small arms; within-group significance", "study_ids": ["parillimoser2021"]}, {"duration": "2 years", "population": "708 cognitively healthy elders 63-79 y", "outcome": "Global cognition composite", "effect_estimate": "-0.072 vs -0.086 SD (P=0.49); post-hoc Barcelona benefit", "evidence_quality": "Moderate", "limitations": "Open design, site heterogeneity, post-hoc subgroup, walnut-industry funding", "study_ids": ["waha2020"]}, {"duration": "12 weeks to 6 months", "population": "Middle-aged and older adults, healthy or overweight or prediabetic (almond trials)", "outcome": "Working memory, CANTAB battery, mood, CBF", "effect_estimate": "Null in three trials; positive in one open-label industry-funded trial", "evidence_quality": "Low to moderate", "limitations": "Small samples, short durations, heterogeneous controls", "study_ids": ["coates2020", "mustrarakic2022", "adams2026", "gulati2026"]}, {"duration": "16 weeks per arm (crossover)", "population": "28 healthy older adults", "outcome": "CBF, vascular function, CANTAB memory and executive function", "effect_estimate": "Regional CBF +5 mL/100 g/min; verbal memory +1 word; visuospatial memory -4 errors; executive NS", "evidence_quality": "Low", "limitations": "Small, single-blind, many outcomes", "study_ids": ["nijssen2023"]}, {"duration": "4.1-6.5 years", "population": "Older Spanish adults at high cardiovascular risk", "outcome": "Neuropsychological composites, MMSE, clock drawing", "effect_estimate": "Memory composite better vs control; MMSE +0.57", "evidence_quality": "Low", "limitations": "Diet pattern not nuts alone; randomisation irregularities; no baseline cognition in Navarra", "study_ids": ["vallspedret2015", "martinezlapiscina2013"]}, {"duration": "2-7 years follow-up (cohorts)", "population": "Middle-aged and older adults (UK, Spain, US women)", "outcome": "Incident dementia; cognitive change", "effect_estimate": "HR 0.88 (0.77-0.99); beta 0.13 SD; +0.08 SD", "evidence_quality": "Low (observational)", "limitations": "Confounding by healthy lifestyle, single FFQ or 24-h recall exposure", "study_ids": ["bizzozeroperoni2025", "ni2023", "obrien2014"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Nuts are among the safest everyday foods for non-allergic people: long-term trials (WAHA 2 years, adolescent trial 6 months, PREDIMED years) report good tolerance and RCTs show no weight gain [nishi2021]. The principal hazards are IgE-mediated allergy (tree nuts and peanuts are mandatory allergens in the EU and US, reactions can be fatal), choking in young children, selenium excess from Brazil nuts [efsa2023_selenium], mycotoxin (aflatoxin) contamination if sourcing and storage are poor, and added salt or sugar in coated products.", "adverse_effects": ["IgE-mediated allergy including anaphylaxis; European challenge-confirmed prevalence low (hazelnut 0.04%, walnut 0.02%) but self-reported point prevalence 1.8-4.0% [spolidoro2024]", "Gastrointestinal discomfort or bloating at high intakes (fibre, fat)", "Choking/aspiration risk with whole nuts in children under about 5 years and people with dysphagia (general paediatric guidance; not reviewed here)", "Selenosis signs (hair and nail loss) with regular Brazil-nut intake above the selenium UL [efsa2023_selenium]", "Aflatoxin exposure from contaminated nuts (peanuts, pistachios, almonds) if quality control fails (not quantified here)", "High sodium in salted nuts; added sugar in coated nuts"], "contraindications": ["Known tree nut or peanut allergy (and cross-reactivity concerns between tree nuts)", "Whole nuts for young children (use ground nuts or smooth nut butters)", "Oxalate-sensitive kidney-stone formers with very high almond intake (theoretical; not reviewed here)"], "drug_interactions": ["Levothyroxine: walnuts and high-fibre foods are listed in levothyroxine labelling as foods that may reduce absorption; separate dosing (label not re-checked in this unit)", "No clinically important interactions identified for almonds or mixed nuts at food intakes"], "upper_limits": "No UL for nuts as a food. Selenium UL 255 micrograms/day for adults [efsa2023_selenium]: Brazil nuts contain on the order of tens to hundreds of micrograms per nut (highly variable), so a daily mix should contain little or no Brazil nut. Vitamin E UL (EFSA 300 mg/day) is not reachable from nuts. Practical intake ceiling is energy: 30 g provides about 180-200 kcal.", "sleep_impact": "No adverse sleep effect; one open-label trial reported better sleep quality with 40 g walnuts at dinner [zeronrugerio2025]. No stimulant content.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Yes: 2 years at 30-60 g/day walnuts in elders [waha2020], 6 months in adolescents [pinarmarti2023], 4-6.5 years within PREDIMED [vallspedret2015]; well tolerated.", "daily_use_appropriate": "yes", "special_populations": "Allergic individuals must avoid; cross-contact labelling needed for all consumers. Children: whole nuts are a choking hazard; the adolescent walnut trial reported no safety concerns in the abstract. Pregnancy: nuts are normal foods; no specific concern beyond allergy. People on energy-restricted diets: weight-neutral in trials [nishi2021]. Chronic kidney disease: potassium and phosphorus content (dietitian review).", "specialist_review_needed": ["Allergist (allergen management, precautionary labelling, cross-contact in a multi-product facility)", "Food safety/quality specialist (aflatoxin and ochratoxin control, storage, oxidation)", "Paediatric dietitian if products target children (choking and allergy)"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "Safe as food for non-allergic people, but tree nuts and peanuts are major allergens requiring mandatory labelling and cross-contact controls; Brazil-nut content must be limited because of the selenium UL; mycotoxin specifications and child choking risk must be managed."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Nuts are conventional foods (not novel). Authorised Article 13(1) claim (Reg. (EU) 432/2012, Annex wording checked on the legislation.gov.uk copy of the Annex, accessed 2026-10-25): 'Walnuts contribute to the improvement of the elasticity of blood vessels' - may be used only for food which provides a daily intake of 30 g of walnuts, with information that the beneficial effect is obtained with that intake. Nutrient-based claims usable when the nut product is at least a source of the nutrient (15% of NRV per 100 g, Reg. (EC) 1924/2006): e.g. 'Zinc contributes to normal cognitive function', 'Magnesium contributes to normal psychological function', 'Magnesium contributes to normal functioning of the nervous system', 'Vitamin E contributes to the protection of cells from oxidative stress'. Walnuts can qualify for 'source of omega-3 fatty acids' and the ALA cholesterol claim (see ALA monograph). No nut-specific cognitive or brain claim is authorised. Tree nuts (almonds, hazelnuts, walnuts, cashews, pecans, Brazil nuts, pistachios, macadamias) and peanuts are mandatory allergens under Reg. (EU) 1169/2011 Annex II. Contaminant maximum levels (aflatoxins; hydrocyanic acid 35 mg/kg for unprocessed almonds placed on the market for the final consumer under Reg. (EU) 2022/1364, now consolidated in Reg. (EU) 2023/915 - carry-over not verified) apply.", "us": "Nuts are conventional foods (GRAS by common use). FDA issued a qualified health claim for nuts and heart disease on 14 July 2003: 'Scientific evidence suggests but does not prove that eating 1.5 ounces per day of most nuts, as part of a diet low in saturated fat and cholesterol, may reduce the risk of heart disease', applicable to nuts not exceeding 4 g saturated fat per 50 g (almonds, Brazil nuts, cashews, hazelnuts, macadamias, pecans, pine nuts, pistachios, walnuts, peanuts), and a walnut-specific qualified claim in March 2004 (wording from archived FDA pages and trade press; current FDA listing not re-checked). No cognitive claim exists; structure/function language on conventional foods must derive from nutritive value and be truthful and substantiated (FTC competent and reliable evidence). Tree nuts and peanuts are major allergens under FALCPA.", "morocco": "Nuts are conventional foods under ONSSA (Law 28-07 on food safety; labelling Decree 2-12-389 of 22 April 2013, BO 6152). The decree is reported (USDA attaché report) to require allergen information, but the exact Moroccan allergen list was not verified in this unit. No Moroccan list of permitted health claims was located; any brain or cognition claim should be cleared with ONSSA/Ministry of Health and is best avoided. Status: uncertain. Market context: Morocco produced about 163,600 t of almonds in 2024 (FAOSTAT via Helgi Library/Selina Wamucii, accessed 2026-10-25), about 5th worldwide, yet imported a record of over 35,000 t of shelled almonds from the USA in January-November 2024 (FreshPlaza, 30 January 2025), indicating strong domestic demand and supply constraints.", "permitted_claims_examples": ["EU (Reg. (EU) 432/2012): 'Walnuts contribute to the improvement of the elasticity of blood vessels' - only for food providing a daily intake of 30 g of walnuts; consumer must be informed that the beneficial effect is obtained with a daily intake of 30 g of walnuts.", "EU (Reg. (EU) 432/2012): 'Zinc contributes to normal cognitive function' - only for food that is at least a source of zinc (e.g. almonds, cashews, pine nuts per composition data); a generic nutrient claim, not evidence that nuts improve cognition.", "EU (Reg. (EU) 432/2012): 'Magnesium contributes to normal psychological function' - only for food that is at least a source of magnesium.", "US (FDA qualified health claim, 2003): 'Scientific evidence suggests but does not prove that eating 1.5 ounces per day of most nuts, as part of a diet low in saturated fat and cholesterol, may reduce the risk of heart disease.' (wording from secondary/archived sources)"], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "None for common nuts in the EU, US or Morocco. Novel processing (e.g. nut extracts, walnut peptides marketed for memory in Asia) would require novel food assessment in the EU and possibly an NDI notification in the US.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Excellent: the studied dose (30 g/day; 30-60 g in trials) is itself a normal snack serving and needs no concentration. A 30 g sachet of unsalted mixed nuts or an amlou-type spread fits a student snack; the walnut EU claim requires 30 g walnuts per daily intake, which constrains mixes (a mix with 15 g walnuts cannot carry it).", "formats": ["30 g single-serve packs of raw or dry-roasted unsalted mixed nuts (walnuts + almonds + hazelnuts)", "nut and seed snack bars (check nut content per bar; binders add sugar)", "nut butters and amlou (almond, argan oil, honey) - culturally native to Morocco", "nut flours in baked goods (lower dose per serving)", "trail mixes with dried fruit (watch sugar)"], "stability": "Walnuts are rich in PUFA and oxidise readily (rancidity); require low water activity, oxygen barrier or nitrogen-flushed packaging, protection from light and heat, and short shelf life or refrigeration. Almonds and hazelnuts are more stable (MUFA, vitamin E). Aflatoxin prevention requires dry storage and supplier testing.", "taste": "Widely accepted; walnut skins are bitter for some consumers (one acute trial reported worse affect possibly from dislike of the walnut breakfast [bell2025]). Roasting improves flavour but may reduce some polyphenols.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Nutrients are delivered in an intact cell-wall matrix: part of the fat in whole almonds is not absorbed (literature not reviewed here), chewing and grinding (butters) increase release. Ellagitannins require gut microbial conversion to urolithins, with large inter-individual variation. ALA conversion to DHA is low.", "dose_feasibility": "Feasible: 30 g/day equals a handful; 60 g/day (WAHA upper, mixed-nut crossover) is about 360-400 kcal and less realistic daily for students. No concentrate is needed or recommended.", "cost_availability": "Widely available in Morocco (major almond producer, also walnuts in mountain regions), the EU and US. Almonds are cheaper locally than imported walnuts or pistachios. Price volatility follows harvests and drought.", "cost_evidence": "Morocco almonds: FAOSTAT farm-gate price about US$2.12/kg (21.83 MAD/kg, January 2024) and export unit value about US$6.33/kg (2024), both via Selina Wamucii (accessed 2026-10-25); US shelled-almond imports into Morocco Jan-Nov 2024 valued at US$174.5 million for >35,000 t (FreshPlaza), i.e. roughly US$5/kg landed (own calculation). Retail prices for walnuts and mixed nuts were not researched here."}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 4, "effect_magnitude": 2, "safety": 8, "reproducibility": 3, "food_suitability": 9, "cost_access": 6, "rationale": "Evidence quality 4: there are several RCTs including a large 2-year trial, but in the Brain Food target population (healthy young adults/students) the grade is only C (inconsistent, small), the best-supported statements are B for no benefit on global cognition in older adults and adolescents, and dementia associations are observational (C). Effect magnitude 2: positive effects are about 2 percentage points or 30 ms on a single task, one reasoning subtest d=0.57 unreplicated, and null global effects. Safety 8: food-level safety and weight neutrality shown over years; deductions for serious allergy risk, Brazil-nut selenium and mycotoxins. Reproducibility 3: positive findings are not replicated and larger preregistered trials are null; much of the literature has nut-industry funding or donated nuts. Food suitability 9: the effective/studied dose is an ordinary snack serving, culturally native to Morocco (almonds, amlou), with oxidation management for walnuts. Cost/access 6: available everywhere and produced in Morocco, but walnuts and pistachios are relatively expensive per serving and prices are volatile."}, "research_gaps": ["Adequately powered, preregistered RCT of 30 g/day nuts in university students measuring attention, memory and academic performance across an exam period, with a blinded or active comparator", "Head-to-head comparison of walnuts vs almonds vs mixed nuts and a dose-response (15, 30, 60 g/day) in young adults", "Replication of the 4-week mixed-nut picture-recognition effect with prespecified primary outcome and multiplicity control", "Blinded trials on mood and sleep (current evidence open-label and self-reported)", "Independent (non-industry-funded) trials; publication of registered trials with results", "Trials in North African/Moroccan populations and with locally relevant formats (amlou, almonds)", "Mechanistic studies linking cerebral blood flow changes to cognitive outcomes in younger adults", "Long-term cohort analyses with repeated dietary measures and adjustment for overall diet quality in younger populations"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 5.0, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": "C", "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": "B", "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"episodic_memory": "C", "global_cognition": "C", "dementia_risk": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 16, "n_studies": 30, "n_verified": 30, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 15, "n_systematic_reviews": 4, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["benefit", "inconsistent", "insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "seeds", "name": "Seeds (flax, chia, pumpkin, sesame, sunflower)", "category": "J", "category_name": "Whole foods", "aliases": ["flaxseed / linseed (Linum usitatissimum)", "chia seed (Salvia hispanica)", "pumpkin seed / pepitas (Cucurbita pepo)", "sesame seed (Sesamum indicum), tahini", "sunflower seed (Helianthus annuus)", "edible seeds", "nuts and seeds (dietary-guideline category)"], "food_sources": ["whole or ground seeds sprinkled on cereals, yoghurt, salads, breads (typical 10-30 g/day)", "seed butters and pastes (tahini, sunflower butter)", "seed oils (flaxseed oil, sesame oil, pumpkin seed oil) - lose fibre and lignans, keep fat-soluble constituents", "traditional Moroccan foods containing sesame (sellou/sfouf, breads, pastries) and anise/sesame mixes", "composition per 100 g (USDA FNDDS as tabulated by George 2022): ALA about 22.8 g in flax (the table repeats this value for chia, likely a transcription error; chia is usually reported lower); magnesium 550 mg and zinc 7.6 mg in pumpkin seeds; zinc 6.7 mg and magnesium 345 mg in sesame; vitamin E 26.1 mg and selenium 79 micrograms in sunflower seeds [george2022]"], "active_constituents": ["alpha-linolenic acid (flax, chia)", "linoleic acid (sunflower, pumpkin, sesame)", "lignans: secoisolariciresinol diglucoside (flax); sesamin, sesamolin, sesaminol (sesame)", "soluble fibre and mucilage (flax, chia)", "minerals: magnesium, zinc, iron (pumpkin, sesame), selenium (sunflower)", "vitamin E (sunflower)", "tryptophan-rich protein (pumpkin/gourd seed)", "cyanogenic glycosides (linseed) - a hazard, not an active"], "one_line_verdict": "Seeds are nutrient-dense, versatile snack ingredients (ALA, magnesium, zinc, fibre) with modest blood-pressure benefits for flax and chia, but there is essentially no direct human evidence that eating flax, chia, pumpkin, sesame or sunflower seeds improves cognition in anyone, let alone healthy students, so any brain positioning must rest on authorised nutrient claims, not on seed-specific data.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. Seeds are compositionally similar to nuts and are grouped with them in dietary guidelines, yet they have rarely been studied for health outcomes, and almost never for cognition [george2022]. No randomised trial or cohort located in this unit tested whole flax, chia, pumpkin, sesame or sunflower seeds on a cognitive outcome in healthy young adults or students. The human cognitive data that exist concern derived products in older or impaired groups: flaxseed oil providing 2.2 g/day ALA for 12 weeks improved only phonemic verbal fluency (one of many tests) in 60 healthy Japanese older adults, in a trial funded by the oil manufacturer [ogawa2023]; a sesame oil cake extract improved verbal learning scores and lowered plasma amyloid-beta in 70 older adults with memory complaints [jung2021]; and a capsule combining astaxanthin with sesamin improved psychomotor and processing speed in 21 people with MCI [ito2018] and recovery from mental fatigue in 24 healthy adults [imai2018], both FUJIFILM-funded, while sesame lignans with vitamin E did not beat placebo on fatigue in the full sample [takemoto2015]. Ground flaxseed (20 g/day for 4 weeks) reduced self-reported mental fatigue in overweight children but was associated with a smaller height gain [gholami2021], and a deoiled gourd-seed protein plus glucose improved insomnia measures in a small company-linked trial [hudson2005]. None of these isolates the whole seed as eaten, and all are small, short and mostly industry-linked. What is better established is cardiometabolic: meta-analyses show flaxseed lowers blood pressure by about 2-3/2 mmHg (SBP -2.85, 95% CI -5.37 to -0.33; DBP -2.39, -3.78 to -0.99) [ursoniu2016], with similar estimates in an independent analysis [khalesi2015], and an umbrella review of chia meta-analyses reports small reductions in blood pressure, LDL and CRP with low-to-moderate certainty [alyounis2025]. Vascular health is relevant to long-term brain health, but this is a surrogate, not a cognitive effect. ALA from flax or chia is poorly converted to EPA/DHA, and high-dose flaxseed oil does not raise the omega-3 index [lane2022]. In whom and how large. For the Brain Food target population the honest answer is 'unknown': no data. How reproducible. Not applicable for cognition (no replicated finding); the blood-pressure effect is reproduced across meta-analyses. What is unknown. Whether a daily 20-30 g seed serving affects attention, memory, mood or sleep in students; whether magnesium- or zinc-rich seeds help people with low status; long-term safety of high flax intakes in pregnancy.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Seeds are among the richest food sources of magnesium, zinc and (pumpkin) iron [george2022], so their most plausible brain-relevant role is filling nutrient gaps in people with low intakes, for example vegetarians, students with poor diets, or people avoiding meat. That is a deficiency-correction argument, and it has not been tested with seeds as the intervention: no trial selected low-magnesium or low-zinc participants and gave them seeds with cognitive outcomes. In adequately nourished young adults no enhancement effect has been studied at all. The only cognitive-adjacent trials involve older adults with memory complaints or MCI given sesame extracts [jung2021, ito2018], children with overweight given flaxseed [gholami2021], or adults with chronic insomnia given a seed-protein preparation [hudson2005]; none of these can be generalised to healthy students. ALA from seeds does not substitute for preformed DHA [lane2022].", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Popular claims that chia or flax are 'brain superfoods', that pumpkin seeds 'boost serotonin and focus', or that sesame 'protects memory' have no direct support in human trials of the seeds themselves; the cited studies use oils, extracts or capsules (often with other actives such as astaxanthin or vitamin E), in older or impaired people, and are mostly industry-funded. What can lawfully be said in the EU relies on nutrient content: pumpkin and sesame seeds can be 'high in' or 'a source of' zinc, magnesium and iron, enabling authorised claims such as 'Zinc contributes to normal cognitive function', 'Iron contributes to normal cognitive function' and 'Magnesium contributes to normal psychological function'; flax and chia can be 'source of omega-3 fatty acids' and carry the ALA cholesterol claim at 2 g/day. These claims describe the nutrient's normal role, not a seed-specific enhancement, and wording that implies improved focus or memory beyond normal function would mislead and fail FTC-style substantiation. An honest positioning is 'a mineral- and fibre-rich seed mix that contributes zinc and magnesium, which support normal cognitive and psychological function', with no promise of performance gains.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Seeds supply ALA (flax, chia), lignans (flax secoisolariciresinol; sesame sesamin/sesaminol), soluble fibre, tryptophan-rich protein and minerals (magnesium, zinc, iron). Plausible brain-relevant routes are (1) correction of low mineral status, since zinc, iron and magnesium have recognised roles in normal cognitive and psychological function; (2) vascular benefits, notably modest blood-pressure lowering with flax and chia; (3) antioxidant and anti-inflammatory effects of lignans, shown mostly in animals; and (4) tryptophan supply for serotonin/melatonin synthesis, relevant only with carbohydrate co-ingestion and shown only in one small insomnia trial. ALA conversion to DHA is low. None of these pathways has been linked to measured cognitive change after eating whole seeds.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Seed intake (10-30 g/day) -> absorption of ALA, minerals, lignan metabolites; whole flax and chia need grinding or hydration for ALA release", "detail": "Composition data per 100 g [george2022]; high-dose flaxseed oil does not raise EPA+DHA in red cells (omega-3 index) [lane2022].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Mineral repletion (zinc, magnesium, iron) in low-status individuals -> normal neuronal function", "detail": "EU authorised nutrient-function claims recognise these roles; no trial has tested seeds for this purpose.", "evidence_level": "plausible"}, {"step": "Blood pressure and lipid lowering -> better cerebrovascular health over years", "detail": "Flaxseed SBP -1.8 to -2.9 mmHg, DBP -1.6 to -2.4 mmHg [khalesi2015, ursoniu2016]; chia small reductions in BP, LDL, CRP [alyounis2025]. Link to cognition not tested.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Lignans (sesamin, sesaminol, SDG) -> antioxidant / anti-amyloid effects", "detail": "Sesame oil cake extract lowered plasma amyloid-beta in older adults with memory complaints [jung2021]; mechanistic data largely from animal and cell models (not extracted).", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Tryptophan-rich seed protein + carbohydrate -> brain tryptophan uptake -> serotonin/melatonin -> sleep", "detail": "Deoiled gourd seed with glucose improved insomnia measures similar to pharmaceutical tryptophan in a small trial [hudson2005].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Measured human cognitive outcome", "detail": "Only derived products in older/impaired groups: verbal fluency [ogawa2023], verbal learning [jung2021], processing speed [ito2018]; no whole-seed trials in healthy adults.", "evidence_level": "speculative"}], "pathways": ["mineral status (zinc, magnesium, iron)", "blood pressure / vascular function", "ALA (limited conversion to DHA)", "lignan antioxidant and phytoestrogenic activity", "tryptophan availability (with carbohydrate)", "soluble fibre and gut microbiota (not studied for cognition)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy young adults and students (no studies located)", "dose_or_intake": "Any seed intake", "duration": "Any", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No RCT or cohort tested flax, chia, pumpkin, sesame or sunflower seeds on cognition, attention, memory or academic performance in healthy young adults or students.", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "Complete evidence gap; seeds are rarely studied separately from nuts. Do not extrapolate from older-adult extract trials.", "key_study_ids": ["george2022"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "alertness", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Any acute (single-dose) seed effect in healthy adults", "dose_or_intake": "Any", "duration": "Single dose", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No acute cognitive or alertness study of seeds located.", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "Evidence gap.", "key_study_ids": ["george2022"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Healthy Japanese adults 65-80 y; outcome phonemic verbal fluency", "dose_or_intake": "3.7 g/day flaxseed oil (2.2 g ALA) - an oil, not whole seed", "duration": "12 weeks", "grade": "D", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Verbal fluency subscore improved more with flaxseed oil (0.30 vs 0.03 points); no effect on attention, memory, working memory, processing speed or global scales.", "effect_estimate": "Change 0.30 +/- 0.53 vs 0.03 +/- 0.49, P<0.05", "certainty_notes": "Single small manufacturer-funded trial with one positive among many outcomes. Graded C for ALA itself in the ALA monograph; downgraded to D here for indirectness (oil, not seed).", "key_study_ids": ["ogawa2023"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "older_adults_mci", "population_detail": "Older adults (60+) with memory impairment or MCI given sesame-derived extracts", "dose_or_intake": "Sesame oil cake extract (about 1.5 g/day, sesaminol 3.1 mg/g); astaxanthin plus sesamin capsules (doses not in abstract)", "duration": "12 weeks", "grade": "D", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Extract improved verbal learning test scores and lowered plasma amyloid-beta versus placebo; astaxanthin-sesamin improved psychomotor and processing speed on a computerised battery.", "effect_estimate": "Verbal LT between-group P=0.020; composite memory z=2.07 [jung2021]; CNSVS speed improvements, magnitude not in abstract [ito2018]", "certainty_notes": "Two small trials of extracts/composites (astaxanthin confounds attribution), one manufacturer-funded; analysis inconsistencies (withdrawals vs 70 analysed) in jung2021; MCI population not generalisable to students.", "key_study_ids": ["jung2021", "ito2018"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_fatigue", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults with daily fatigue (crossover n=24; parallel trial in fatigued adults)", "dose_or_intake": "Astaxanthin plus sesamin capsules; sesamin/episesamin 10 mg plus vitamin E 55 mg", "duration": "4-8 weeks", "grade": "D", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Astaxanthin-sesamin improved recovery from task-induced mental fatigue with no effect on secondary outcomes; sesame lignans plus vitamin E showed no between-group difference overall, with benefits only in an over-40 subgroup.", "effect_estimate": "Not reported numerically in abstracts", "certainty_notes": "Supplements with co-actives, not seeds; industry involvement; subjective outcomes; subgroup findings.", "key_study_ids": ["imai2018", "takemoto2015"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_fatigue", "population": "children_adolescents", "population_detail": "Children and adolescents with overweight/obesity (Iran)", "dose_or_intake": "20 g/day flaxseed vs 25 g/day puffed wheat", "duration": "4 weeks", "grade": "D", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Mental fatigue decreased with flaxseed (between-group P<0.001, also by ITT); mood (depression, anxiety, stress) unchanged; smaller height increase and smaller BMI reduction with flaxseed.", "effect_estimate": "Mental fatigue between-group P<0.001; height +0.53 vs +1.09 cm (P=0.03)", "certainty_notes": "Single small unblinded-food trial with a subjective outcome in children; growth finding is a possible safety signal of uncertain meaning. Not applicable to adults.", "key_study_ids": ["gholami2021"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "other", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Adults with chronic insomnia; outcome = sleep (objective and subjective)", "dose_or_intake": "Deoiled gourd-seed protein (about 22 mg tryptophan/g protein) with carbohydrate", "duration": "3 weeks", "grade": "D", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Seed-protein tryptophan plus carbohydrate and pharmaceutical tryptophan plus carbohydrate improved insomnia measures; carbohydrate alone did not; the seed preparation reduced time awake.", "effect_estimate": "Not reported numerically in abstract", "certainty_notes": "Single small trial (49 completers), first author linked to the product company, preparation is a defatted seed concentrate with glucose, not whole pumpkin seeds; not replicated.", "key_study_ids": ["hudson2005"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "vascular_health", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Adults with and without elevated blood pressure or cardiometabolic risk", "dose_or_intake": "Flaxseed (whole/ground, oil, lignan extracts); chia seed (various doses)", "duration": "2 weeks to more than 12 weeks", "grade": "B", "direction": "benefit", "effect_summary": "Flaxseed modestly lowers systolic and diastolic blood pressure, more with whole/ground seed and with 12+ weeks; chia shows small reductions in blood pressure, LDL, triglycerides, waist and CRP.", "effect_estimate": "Flax SBP -2.85 (-5.37 to -0.33), DBP -2.39 (-3.78 to -0.99) mmHg [ursoniu2016]; SBP -1.77 (-3.45 to -0.09), DBP -1.58 (-2.64 to -0.52) [khalesi2015]; chia DBP g -0.55 (-0.72 to -0.38) [alyounis2025]", "certainty_notes": "Consistent across meta-analyses but heterogeneous products and populations, small effects, possible publication bias; chia umbrella review rates certainty low to moderate. Surrogate, not cognition.", "key_study_ids": ["ursoniu2016", "khalesi2015", "alyounis2025"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "dementia_risk", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Older adults (no seed-specific cohort data located)", "dose_or_intake": "Habitual seed intake", "duration": "Any", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No prospective cohort reporting seed intake separately in relation to dementia or cognitive decline was located; seeds are usually pooled with nuts or not measured.", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "Evidence gap; nut cohort data (see nuts-walnuts-almonds monograph) cannot be assumed to apply.", "key_study_ids": ["george2022"]}], "acute_matrix": [], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "12 weeks", "population": "60 healthy older Japanese adults", "outcome": "Six cognitive domains", "effect_estimate": "Verbal fluency subscore +0.30 vs +0.03; all others NS", "evidence_quality": "Low", "limitations": "Oil not seed; manufacturer-funded; multiplicity", "study_ids": ["ogawa2023"]}, {"duration": "12 weeks", "population": "70 older adults with memory impairment; 21 with MCI", "outcome": "Verbal learning, plasma amyloid-beta; psychomotor/processing speed", "effect_estimate": "Verbal learning improved (P=0.020); speed measures improved", "evidence_quality": "Very low", "limitations": "Extracts and co-actives; small; industry", "study_ids": ["jung2021", "ito2018"]}, {"duration": "4-8 weeks", "population": "Healthy fatigued adults", "outcome": "Mental fatigue", "effect_estimate": "Improved recovery with astaxanthin-sesamin; no overall effect of sesame lignans plus vitamin E", "evidence_quality": "Very low", "limitations": "Supplements; subjective; industry", "study_ids": ["imai2018", "takemoto2015"]}, {"duration": "4 weeks", "population": "72 children/adolescents with overweight", "outcome": "Fatigue, mood, growth", "effect_estimate": "Mental fatigue reduced; smaller height gain (0.53 vs 1.09 cm)", "evidence_quality": "Low", "limitations": "Short, subjective, unblinded food comparison", "study_ids": ["gholami2021"]}, {"duration": "3 weeks", "population": "57 adults with chronic insomnia", "outcome": "Objective and subjective sleep", "effect_estimate": "Improved with seed-protein tryptophan plus carbohydrate", "evidence_quality": "Very low", "limitations": "Old, small, company-linked, concentrate with glucose", "study_ids": ["hudson2005"]}, {"duration": "2 to more than 12 weeks (meta-analyses)", "population": "1,302 participants in 15 flax trials; about 2,500 in chia meta-analyses", "outcome": "Blood pressure, lipids, CRP", "effect_estimate": "SBP about -2 to -3 mmHg, DBP about -1.6 to -2.4 mmHg (flax); chia small standardized reductions", "evidence_quality": "Moderate", "limitations": "Heterogeneous products; small effects", "study_ids": ["ursoniu2016", "khalesi2015", "alyounis2025"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Seeds are safe everyday foods at culinary amounts (10-30 g/day) for non-allergic people. Specific hazards: sesame is a major allergen with frequent severe reactions [warren2019, patel2016]; raw linseed contains cyanogenic glycosides, for which EFSA applies an ARfD of 20 micrograms cyanide/kg bw and the EU sets maximum levels [efsa2019], though linseed releases cyanide slowly with low blood peaks [abraham2016]; dry chia and flax swell with water and need fluid; large amounts of seeds (especially with shells) can form bezoars [gentile2023]; a small paediatric trial reported smaller height gain with 20 g/day flaxseed [gholami2021].", "adverse_effects": ["Sesame allergy: US convincing IgE-mediated prevalence 0.23%, with severe reactions in 24-37% of those affected [warren2019]; allergy to sunflower, pumpkin, flax and poppy seeds is rarer but reported, sometimes severe [patel2016]", "Cyanide release from raw ground linseed (acute exposure concern at large single portions with active beta-glucosidase) [efsa2019, abraham2016]", "Bloating, flatulence, laxation; risk of intestinal obstruction with high-fibre flax/chia taken without enough fluid", "Seed bezoars / rectal impaction with large quantities, especially unshelled seeds [gentile2023]", "Oesophageal obstruction after swallowing dry chia seeds followed by water (widely reported US conference case; not located in PubMed in this unit)", "Possible smaller height gain in children given 20 g/day flaxseed for 4 weeks (single trial, uncertain) [gholami2021]", "Contamination/adulteration: sesame seed recalls in the EU (ethylene oxide, 2020-2021) and cadmium accumulation in sunflower seeds are known issues (not verified in this unit)"], "contraindications": ["Sesame allergy (and caution in people with multiple food allergies)", "Bowel obstruction, strictures or severe dysphagia (flax, chia)", "Large raw-linseed portions for small children (cyanogenic glycosides; body-weight-based ARfD)"], "drug_interactions": ["Flax/chia fibre and mucilage may reduce absorption of concomitantly taken oral drugs; separate by about 2 h (general fibre precaution)", "Flaxseed and its oil: theoretical additive effect with antihypertensives and antiplatelet/anticoagulant drugs (small BP effect shown; bleeding effect not established)", "Flax lignans (phytoestrogens): theoretical interaction with hormonal therapies; specialist review"], "upper_limits": "No UL for seeds as foods. Cyanide ARfD 20 micrograms/kg bw (EFSA) [efsa2019]: for a 70 kg adult about 1.4 mg cyanide per eating occasion; linseed at the EU maximum for final consumers (150 mg HCN/kg) provides about 1.4-1.5 mg HCN per 10 g of raw ground seed, so a single raw portion of 10 g or more could reach the ARfD in a worst case, whereas slow release from linseed [abraham2016] and heating (which inactivates beta-glucosidase) reduce the hazard; source low-HCN linseed and prefer baked/heat-treated flax in products. Selenium from sunflower seeds is far below the 255 microgram/day UL at normal intakes.", "sleep_impact": "No adverse sleep effects expected; a small company-linked trial suggests seed-protein tryptophan with carbohydrate may improve insomnia [hudson2005].", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Limited: flax and chia trials typically last weeks to months (cardiometabolic meta-analyses); no multi-year safety trials of seed intake; long-term use as traditional foods (sesame, flax) is common.", "daily_use_appropriate": "yes", "special_populations": "Pregnancy and lactation: culinary amounts are normal foods; high-dose flax lignans (phytoestrogens) lack safety data and should be avoided as concentrated supplements. Children: avoid large raw-linseed portions; one trial reported smaller height gain with flaxseed [gholami2021]; whole small seeds are not a choking hazard comparable to nuts but chia must be hydrated. Hormone-sensitive conditions: specialist advice for high flax intakes. People with diverticular or bowel disease: individual advice.", "specialist_review_needed": ["Allergist (sesame as major allergen; multi-seed cross-contact)", "Food toxicologist / quality specialist (hydrocyanic acid in linseed, cadmium, mycotoxins, ethylene oxide history)", "Paediatric dietitian if products target children (flax growth signal, portion sizes)", "Obstetric/endocrine review for concentrated flax-lignan products"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "Safe as culinary ingredients for non-allergic people, but sesame is a major allergen, linseed requires hydrocyanic-acid specifications and portion/heat-treatment controls, and chia/flax need hydration guidance; these are manageable through formulation and labelling."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Flax/linseed, sesame, pumpkin and sunflower seeds are conventional foods. Chia seed (Salvia hispanica) is an authorised novel food on the Union list (Implementing Reg. (EU) 2017/2470), with conditions amended over time, including Reg. (EU) 2020/500 (partially defatted chia seed powders), 2021/668 (chia seeds may also be marketed without pre-packaging) and 2023/2214 (powders, exclusive to the applicant until November 2028); current food-category conditions were not verified here. Sesame seeds are a mandatory allergen (Reg. (EU) 1169/2011 Annex II). Hydrocyanic acid maximum levels (Reg. (EU) 2022/1364, applying from 1 January 2023; now consolidated into Reg. (EU) 2023/915, carry-over not verified): unprocessed whole, ground, milled, cracked or chopped linseed 250 mg/kg, and 150 mg/kg when placed on the market for the final consumer (values from AGROLAB summary, accessed 2026-10-25). No seed-specific health claim is authorised; nutrient claims apply: 'source of omega-3 fatty acids' (>=0.3 g ALA/100 g and per 100 kcal) and the ALA cholesterol claim (2 g/day) for flax and chia; mineral claims for pumpkin and sesame seeds (zinc, magnesium, iron) when 'source' thresholds are met (see permitted claims).", "us": "Seeds are conventional foods (GRAS by common use); chia and flax are widely sold as foods. Sesame became the ninth major food allergen under the FASTER Act, with labelling required on packaged foods and dietary supplements from 1 January 2023 (FDA page, accessed 2026-10-25). No qualified health claim exists for seeds (the 2003 nut claim does not cover seeds). Cognitive structure/function wording on conventional foods must derive from nutritive value and be substantiated (FTC).", "morocco": "Seeds are conventional foods under ONSSA (Law 28-07; labelling Decree 2-12-389). Sesame is traditional in Moroccan foods (sellou, breads, pastries). Whether chia requires any prior authorisation in Morocco, and whether sesame is on Morocco's mandatory allergen list, were not verified. No Moroccan list of permitted health claims was located; status uncertain - avoid cognitive claims and consult ONSSA.", "permitted_claims_examples": ["EU (Reg. (EU) 432/2012): 'Zinc contributes to normal cognitive function' - only for food that is at least a source of zinc (pumpkin and sesame seeds qualify on composition data); nutrient claim, not evidence for seeds.", "EU (Reg. (EU) 432/2012): 'Magnesium contributes to normal psychological function' / 'Magnesium contributes to normal functioning of the nervous system' - only for food that is at least a source of magnesium.", "EU (Reg. (EU) 432/2012): 'Alpha-linolenic acid contributes to the maintenance of normal blood cholesterol levels' - only for food that is at least a source of ALA ('source of omega-3'), with information that the beneficial effect is obtained with a daily intake of 2 g ALA (wording per ALA monograph)."], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "Chia is a novel food in the EU with authorised conditions of use (check current Union list entry before formulating, especially for powders and new food categories). Sesame or flax extracts (sesamin, sesaminol, SDG lignans) concentrated beyond normal food use may require novel food assessment in the EU and NDI notification as dietary ingredients in the US; not assessed here.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Excellent as ingredients: 10-30 g/day fits easily in snack mixes, bars, breakfast toppings and breads. Ground flax and hydrated chia improve nutrient availability. Because there is no cognitive dose to hit, formulation should target nutrient contribution (zinc, magnesium, ALA, fibre) and safety (low-HCN linseed, heat treatment, allergen control).", "formats": ["mixed seed sachets (pumpkin + sunflower + sesame) as a 20-30 g snack", "nut-and-seed bars combined with almonds (pairs with the nuts monograph)", "ground or milled flax in baked goods (heat reduces cyanogenic risk)", "pre-hydrated chia puddings or drinks", "tahini or sesame-almond spreads; traditional sellou-style mixes"], "stability": "Ground flax and chia oxidise quickly once milled (high ALA); use cold-milled, nitrogen-flushed, opaque packaging or whole seeds milled on demand. Whole seeds are stable for months. Sesame and sunflower seeds can turn rancid when roasted and stored warm.", "taste": "Mild and well accepted; flax is slightly bitter when oxidised; chia gel texture divides consumers; roasted pumpkin and sesame seeds are highly palatable.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Whole flaxseed largely passes undigested; grinding is needed for ALA and lignan release. Chia mucilage slows digestion. Mineral bioavailability from seeds is reduced by phytate (roasting, soaking or sprouting partly reduce it). ALA is poorly converted to DHA [lane2022].", "dose_feasibility": "Feasible for nutrient claims: 30 g pumpkin seeds supply about 2.3 mg zinc and 165 mg magnesium (per FNDDS values [george2022]); 10 g ground flax supplies about 2.3 g ALA, meeting the 2 g/day ALA cholesterol-claim intake. No evidence-based cognitive dose exists.", "cost_availability": "Sunflower, pumpkin and sesame seeds are inexpensive and widely available in Morocco, the EU and the US; flax is cheap; chia is mostly imported and costlier.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 2, "effect_magnitude": 1, "safety": 8, "reproducibility": 2, "food_suitability": 9, "cost_access": 8, "rationale": "Evidence quality 2: for any cognitive outcome the grade is D (no studies in young adults; only small extract/oil trials in older or impaired groups); the only B-grade evidence is for blood pressure, a surrogate. Effect magnitude 1: no demonstrated cognitive effect from seeds; cardiovascular effects are about 2-3 mmHg. Safety 8: safe culinary foods with long traditional use; deductions for sesame allergy, linseed cyanogenic glycosides, bezoar/swelling risks and a single paediatric growth signal. Reproducibility 2: no replicated cognitive finding; cognitive-adjacent trials are single, small and largely industry-funded. Food suitability 9: easy to incorporate at 10-30 g with good stability as whole seeds. Cost/access 8: inexpensive and locally available (sesame, sunflower, pumpkin, flax), chia excepted."}, "research_gaps": ["Any RCT of whole seeds (pumpkin, sunflower, sesame, flax, chia) on attention, memory, mood or sleep in healthy young adults or students", "Trials of zinc- and magnesium-rich seed snacks in people with low mineral status (e.g. young women, vegetarians) measuring both status and cognition", "Cohort analyses reporting seed intake separately from nuts in relation to cognitive decline and dementia", "Replication of the pumpkin/gourd-seed tryptophan insomnia trial with whole-food formats and independent funding", "Independent replication of sesame-lignan cognitive findings without co-actives (astaxanthin, vitamin E)", "Clarification of the flaxseed growth signal in children and long-term safety of high flax-lignan intake in pregnancy", "Publication of registered trials (e.g. NCT04645927 flax beverage and memory, completed 2023; NCT07392723 ALA-enriched nutrition in APOE4 carriers)"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 4.3, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": "D", "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": "B", "best_grade_overall": "D", "benefit_domains": {"executive_function": "D", "episodic_memory": "D", "cognitive_fatigue": "D"}, "n_outcome_rows": 9, "n_studies": 17, "n_verified": 17, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 7, "n_systematic_reviews": 3, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["benefit", "inconsistent", "insufficient", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "whole-grains", "name": "Whole grains and oats", "category": "J", "category_name": "Whole foods", "aliases": ["whole grains, wholegrain, whole-grain cereals", "oats, oatmeal, porridge, oat bran", "barley (Moroccan belboula/dchicha barley grits, barley couscous), rye, whole wheat, brown rice, bulgur, millet", "cereal fibre, beta-glucan, arabinoxylan", "Moroccan whole-wheat bread (khobz kamel / 'farine complete'), whole-wheat couscous"], "food_sources": ["oats (rolled oats, oat bran, oat flour)", "barley (pearled vs whole, barley grits, barley bread)", "whole-wheat bread, pasta and couscous; bulgur", "rye kernel and wholemeal rye breads", "brown rice, millet, sorghum, whole maize"], "active_constituents": ["viscous soluble fibre: oat and barley beta-glucan (molecular weight matters for glycaemic effects)", "insoluble and fermentable fibre: arabinoxylan, cellulose, resistant starch", "intact kernel structure (slows starch digestion)", "B vitamins (thiamine, niacin, B6, folate), magnesium, iron, zinc (bran and germ)", "phenolic acids (ferulic acid), avenanthramides (oats), alkylresorcinols (wheat/rye; intake biomarkers)", "gluten proteins in wheat, barley, rye (relevant to coeliac disease)"], "one_line_verdict": "Whole grains, oats and barley reliably blunt post-meal blood glucose (oat beta-glucan about -23%) and are linked to better cardiometabolic health, but evidence that they improve or protect cognition is weak and inconsistent (half of the cohorts null, two small poor-quality brown-rice trials, a null 3-day rye trial on cognition) and there is no trial in healthy students or young workers; they deserve a place in Brain Food as the low-glycaemic base of meals, not as a cognitive ingredient.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is shown. The strongest human evidence is metabolic. Adding oat beta-glucan to carbohydrate meals reduces glucose incremental AUC by 23% (ratio of means 0.77, 0.74-0.81) and insulin by 22% across 103 comparisons, with clear dose-response and a molecular-weight threshold, rated high certainty but funded partly by PepsiCo and run by a contract-research company [zurbau2021]; EFSA accepted cause and effect for oat/barley beta-glucan and blood glucose rise and for cholesterol [efsa2010oat]. Cognitive evidence. A 2023 systematic review using GRADE (funded by General Mills' Bell Institute) judged the association between whole-grain intake and cognition 'inconclusive', with low or very low certainty: of 6 prospective cohorts 3 were null, and only 2 RCTs (processed brown rice in elderly Japanese, poor risk of bias) existed [ross2023]. Since then, the Chicago Health and Aging Project found slower decline with higher whole-grain intake only among African American participants (beta 0.024 SU/year, top vs bottom quintile), not significantly in White participants [liu2023]; Cache County found higher average 3MS scores (+1.19 points of 100) rather than slower decline [wengreen2013]; cross-sectional studies in Norway and China report better scores with moderate whole-grain or high-fibre bread intake and worse with white bread [nurk2010, li2025]. Trials: 2 years of pressurised brown rice (n=52) improved only a frontal sub-item and a processing-time measure [kuroda2019]; 3 days of whole-grain rye evening meals improved insulin sensitivity, SCFA and self-rated mood but not working memory or selective attention (WM 29.6 vs 29.3 correct) in 38 adults aged 52-70 [sandberg2018]; a 4-week multi-component diet that included barley-kernel products improved recognition memory and selective attention, but the grain effect cannot be isolated [nilsson2013]. Acute evidence is indirect: low-glycaemic-load breakfasts give a borderline memory benefit after 120 min (SMD 0.16) that is larger in adults under 35 [gaylor2022]; a guar-enriched (not whole-grain) bread improved late-morning selective attention in middle-aged adults [nilsson2012]; in children and adolescents low-GI cereal or oatmeal breakfasts preserved attention and memory across the morning [ingwersen2007, cooper2012, mahoney2005]. In healthy young adults the only data are a 27% rise in plasma BDNF after a rye-kernel evening meal (n=19, biomarker only) [sandberg2018bdnf] and a null cross-sectional link in female students [abbaszadeh2021]. What else. Gluten intake is not associated with cognition in women without coeliac disease, so gluten-free 'brain' claims are unfounded [wang2021]. Green oat herb extracts (not the grain) improved some tasks in an industry-funded trial [kennedy2020]. Unknown: whether swapping refined for whole grains changes cognition at any age; a large completed whole-grain breakfast trial (NCT04135066) has no published results.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Whole grains supply fibre, magnesium, B vitamins and some iron and zinc; they do not correct any specific deficiency better than other foods, and their phytate lowers mineral absorption. A Moroccan-specific trade-off: mandatory iron (NaFeEDTA) and folic-acid fortification applies to soft-wheat flour but exempts whole ('complete') flour (project unit X6), so a shift from fortified white flour to unfortified whole flour could lower folic-acid intake in women of reproductive age unless compensated. Any cognitive effect in replete adults is unproven; the acute low-GI rationale concerns stable glucose supply, not enhancement, and is best supported in children [ingwersen2007, cooper2012]. Evidence from older adults [liu2023, kuroda2019] and children must not be transferred to students.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "'Slow-release energy for focus' and 'oats for brain power' are not substantiated for adults: adult acute data are indirect and borderline [gaylor2022, nilsson2012], and the only adult whole-grain trial with next-morning cognitive tests was null [sandberg2018]. 'Gluten-free for brain fog' is contradicted in non-coeliac people [wang2021]. 'Oat extract improves memory' refers to green oat herb extracts, not oats [kennedy2020]. What can be said: in the EU, foods meeting the conditions may use the authorised beta-glucan claims (cholesterol: at least 1 g beta-glucan per portion with 3 g/day information; blood glucose: at least 4 g beta-glucan per 30 g available carbohydrate); EFSA found 'whole grain' itself not sufficiently characterised for claims [efsa2010wholegrain]. In the US, the soluble-fibre/CHD health claim (21 CFR 101.81) and a whole-grain FDAMA notification claim exist for heart disease; none concerns cognition. Brain Food should present whole grains as the low-glycaemic, fibre-rich base of meals and avoid any cognitive claim.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Whole grains, particularly oats and barley, slow carbohydrate digestion through viscous beta-glucan and intact kernel structure, lowering postprandial glucose and insulin; their fermentable fibre yields short-chain fatty acids and gut hormones that improve insulin sensitivity at the next meal; over years, higher intake is associated with lower type 2 diabetes and cardiovascular risk, both risk factors for cognitive decline. Better glucoregulation correlates with better cognitive test performance in middle-aged adults, and rye meals raise plasma BDNF, but these are correlations and biomarkers. Direct causal evidence linking whole grains to cognition is lacking.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Whole grains -> viscous beta-glucan (oats, barley), arabinoxylan (rye, wheat), intact kernels, B vitamins, Mg, phenolics", "detail": "Composition established; effects depend on processing (flaking, milling, molecular weight).", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Beta-glucan / kernel structure -> slower starch digestion -> lower postprandial glucose and insulin", "detail": "Oat beta-glucan reduces glucose iAUC 23% and insulin 22%, dose-dependent, MW >300 kg/mol required [zurbau2021].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Colonic fermentation -> SCFA (acetate, butyrate), PYY, GLP-2 -> improved next-morning insulin sensitivity, lower IL-1beta", "detail": "Shown after 3 days of rye-kernel evening meals in 38 adults [sandberg2018].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Better glucoregulation -> better cognitive performance", "detail": "Correlations between insulin sensitivity or glucose tolerance and working memory/attention [sandberg2018, nilsson2012]; causal direction unproven.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Rye-kernel evening meal -> higher fasting plasma BDNF", "detail": "+27% in 19 young adults [sandberg2018bdnf]; peripheral BDNF is not a validated surrogate for brain plasticity or cognition.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Long-term lower T2D and cardiovascular risk -> less vascular brain injury", "detail": "Plausible link underlying cohort associations; not tested for cognition.", "evidence_level": "plausible"}, {"step": "Measured human outcome", "detail": "Cohorts inconsistent [ross2023, liu2023]; RCT cognitive effects null or isolated [sandberg2018, kuroda2019]; acute benefits mainly in children [ingwersen2007, cooper2012].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["postprandial glycaemia and insulinaemia", "colonic fermentation / SCFA / gut hormones", "insulin sensitivity", "peripheral BDNF (biomarker)", "cardiometabolic risk reduction"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_decline", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Community-dwelling older adults (CHAP mean 75 y, 60% African American; Cache County >=65 y) plus cross-sectional Norwegian (70-74 y) and Chinese (mean 64 y) samples", "dose_or_intake": "Highest vs lowest quintile; >3 vs <1 servings/day; per 25 g/day", "duration": "Habitual intake; follow-up 6-11 years (cohorts)", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "GRADE-based review judged evidence inconclusive (3 of 6 cohorts null). CHAP found slower decline only in African American participants; Cache County found a ~1-point higher 3MS level rather than slower decline; cross-sectional studies show better scores with moderate whole-grain intake and worse with white bread.", "effect_estimate": "AA top vs bottom quintile beta 0.024 SU/yr (0.008-0.039); White >3 vs <1 serving beta 0.025 (-0.003 to 0.053) [liu2023]; +1.19 3MS points [wengreen2013]; cross-sectional beta 0.13 SD, MCI OR 0.82 per 25 g/d [li2025]", "certainty_notes": "Observational; inconsistent across cohorts and racial groups; poor reporting of whole-grain intake; high risk of reporting bias noted by the review; review funded by a cereal manufacturer's institute (still concluded inconclusive).", "key_study_ids": ["ross2023", "liu2023", "wengreen2013", "nurk2010", "li2025"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "52 healthy elderly Japanese adults", "dose_or_intake": "100 g/day ultra-high-pressure-processed brown rice vs white rice", "duration": "24 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Improvements limited to one Frontal Assessment Battery sub-item and a processing-time measure (CADi), plus lower apathy; no reported change in global scales.", "effect_estimate": "Not reported in abstract (sub-item differences only) [kuroda2019]", "certainty_notes": "Single small trial rated poor risk of bias in the 2023 GRADE review; selective outcomes; processed product; unreplicated.", "key_study_ids": ["kuroda2019", "ross2023"]}, {"timeframe": "short_term_days", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "38 healthy adults 52-70 y (mean 64, 30 women), crossover", "dose_or_intake": "Whole-grain rye kernel/flour bread with resistant starch as evening meals vs white wheat bread", "duration": "3 consecutive days; tests 11-14 h after last meal", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "No differences in working memory or selective attention accuracy or reaction time despite improved insulin sensitivity, SCFA and gut hormones.", "effect_estimate": "WM correct responses 29.6 vs 29.3 (+1%); selective attention 70.9 vs 70.1 (+1%), P>0.05 [sandberg2018]", "certainty_notes": "Single small crossover, retrospectively registered; short exposure; null result reasonably precise for large effects only.", "key_study_ids": ["sandberg2018"]}, {"timeframe": "short_term_days", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Same 38 adults 52-70 y", "dose_or_intake": "Rye kernel bread + resistant starch evening meals", "duration": "3 days", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Self-rated mood valence and activation were higher the next morning after rye meals.", "effect_estimate": "Valence +9-10% (P<0.05 to P<0.001); activation +7-15% [sandberg2018]", "certainty_notes": "Subjective ratings; participants could identify breads (no blinding); single study.", "key_study_ids": ["sandberg2018"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "44 overweight but healthy adults 50-73 y, crossover", "dose_or_intake": "Multifunctional Nordic-style diet including barley-kernel products and several other 'active' foods vs control diet", "duration": "4 weeks per diet", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Active diet improved recognition memory (RAVLT) and a selective attention/working-memory test; effect of whole grains cannot be separated from other components.", "effect_estimate": "p<0.05 for RAVLT recognition (n=42) and selective attention (n=40); effect sizes not reported in abstract [nilsson2013]", "certainty_notes": "Multi-component confounding; small; single lab; not blinded.", "key_study_ids": ["nilsson2013"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Adults 20-80 y in low- vs high-glycaemic-load breakfast trials (15 studies); no whole-grain-specific trial", "dose_or_intake": "Low-GL breakfast (e.g., whole-grain or oat-based) vs high-GL breakfast", "duration": "Single meal; outcomes up to ~3-4 h", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "No effect within 2 h; small, borderline memory advantage after 120 min, larger in under-35s and good glucose regulators. Consistent with the glucose-and-carbohydrates monograph grade.", "effect_estimate": "SMD 0.16 (95% CI -0.00 to 0.32), p=0.05, I2=5% [gaylor2022]", "certainty_notes": "Borderline effect, heterogeneous meals, indirect for whole grains; subgroup finding not confirmatory.", "key_study_ids": ["gaylor2022"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults 18-35 y, students, knowledge workers eating a whole-grain or oat breakfast or lunch", "dose_or_intake": "Not studied with whole grains specifically", "duration": "Single meal", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No acute trial of a whole-grain or oat meal with cognitive outcomes in young adults was found. The only hints are a non-confirmatory subgroup in the low-GL meta-analysis (larger effect in adults under 35) and a plasma BDNF rise after a rye evening meal without cognitive testing.", "effect_estimate": "not reported (no whole-grain trials); low-GL subgroup <35 y larger but not quantified in abstract [gaylor2022]; BDNF +27% [sandberg2018bdnf]", "certainty_notes": "Indirect (general low-GL meals, biomarker only); subgroup analyses not confirmatory.", "key_study_ids": ["gaylor2022", "sandberg2018bdnf"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "40 healthy adults 49-71 y, crossover", "dose_or_intake": "Guar-gum-enriched white wheat bread (low, sustained glucose) vs white wheat bread at breakfast", "duration": "Single breakfast; tests to 225 min", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Selective attention better in the late postprandial period (75-225 min) after the low-sustained-glucose bread; better glucoregulators performed better overall.", "effect_estimate": "P<0.01 for selective attention test (effect size not in abstract) [nilsson2012]", "certainty_notes": "Single small trial; guar fibre, not whole grain (indirect); same lab as other Lund trials.", "key_study_ids": ["nilsson2012"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "children_adolescents", "population_detail": "Children 6-11 y and adolescents 12-14 y, crossover school studies", "dose_or_intake": "Low-GI breakfast cereal or oatmeal vs high-GI cereal or no breakfast", "duration": "Single breakfast; morning test sessions", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Low-GI breakfasts (including instant oatmeal) reduced the morning decline in attention and memory or improved response times and accuracy versus high-GI breakfast or omission.", "effect_estimate": "Low-GI vs high-GI accuracy maintained (Stroop P=0.039, Sternberg P=0.018, Flanker P=0.014) [cooper2012]; reduced decline in attention and secondary memory [ingwersen2007]; better spatial and short-term memory after oatmeal [mahoney2005]", "certainty_notes": "Small crossover trials, multiple outcomes, unblinded foods, possible cereal-industry involvement not verified; not transferable to adults.", "key_study_ids": ["ingwersen2007", "cooper2012", "mahoney2005"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults 18-35 y, students, knowledge workers", "dose_or_intake": "Not studied in trials", "duration": "Not studied", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No trial or cohort has tested whole grains on measured cognition in healthy young adults; a cross-sectional study of female students found no association of whole-grain intake with self-rated cognitive abilities (per the 2023 review).", "effect_estimate": "not reported (no trials); cross-sectional null reported in a systematic review [abbaszadeh2021, ross2023]", "certainty_notes": "Absence of evidence; self-report measures only.", "key_study_ids": ["abbaszadeh2021", "ross2023"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "neuroplasticity_biomarkers", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "19 healthy adults 20-33 y (mean 25.6), crossover", "dose_or_intake": "Rye-kernel bread (85% whole rye kernels, 50 g available starch) as late evening meal vs white wheat bread", "duration": "Single evening meal or 3 consecutive evenings; fasting sample 10.5 h later", "grade": "D", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Fasting plasma BDNF was 27% higher after rye-kernel evening meals; no cognitive outcomes reported in this paper.", "effect_estimate": "+27% plasma BDNF (p=0.001) [sandberg2018bdnf]", "certainty_notes": "Biomarker only, peripheral BDNF of uncertain meaning; very small; single lab. Not evidence of cognitive benefit.", "key_study_ids": ["sandberg2018bdnf"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "other", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "538 participants (mostly healthy, some with type 2 diabetes or overweight), median age 37 y; outcome is postprandial glycaemia, not cognition", "dose_or_intake": "Oat beta-glucan added to carbohydrate meals (median ~4 g)", "duration": "Single meal, 2-4 h", "grade": "A", "direction": "benefit", "effect_summary": "Oat beta-glucan consistently lowers post-meal glucose and insulin, dose-dependently, provided molecular weight is high; supports the EU authorised glucose-rise claim.", "effect_estimate": "Glucose iAUC ratio 0.77 (0.74-0.81); iPeak 0.72; insulin iAUC 0.78 (0.72-0.85) [zurbau2021]", "certainty_notes": "High certainty per GRADE (103 comparisons), consistent with EFSA's favourable opinion; industry co-funding and contract-lab authorship noted. Biomarker only - must not be presented as a cognitive effect.", "key_study_ids": ["zurbau2021", "efsa2010oat"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "13,494 middle-aged US women without coeliac disease (NHS II, mean 60.6 y); exposure is gluten intake", "dose_or_intake": "Quintiles of energy-adjusted gluten intake (mean 6.3 g/day)", "duration": "Diet 1991-2015; cognition 2014-2019", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Gluten intake was neither beneficially nor harmfully associated with psychomotor speed/attention, learning/working memory or global cognition; findings do not support gluten avoidance for cognition in non-coeliac people.", "effect_estimate": "Global cognition highest vs lowest quintile -0.002 SD (-0.05 to 0.05) [wang2021]", "certainty_notes": "Observational but large and precise null; cognition measured once.", "key_study_ids": ["wang2021"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "132 healthy adults 35-65 y; green oat (Avena sativa) herb extract, not oat grain", "dose_or_intake": "430, 860 or 1290 mg/day green oat extract vs placebo", "duration": "Single dose and 29 days", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Single 1290 mg dose and 4 weeks of 430 or 1290 mg improved Corsi block working memory and multitasking; no mood effect; non-monotonic dose pattern (860 mg less clear).", "effect_estimate": "Significant vs placebo; effect sizes not in abstract [kennedy2020]", "certainty_notes": "Single sponsor-funded trial with sponsor employees as co-authors; extract of aerial parts, not transferable to oats as food.", "key_study_ids": ["kennedy2020"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "Low- vs high-glycaemic-load breakfast (various foods, including cereals)", "onset": "No effect <120 min; small effect after 120 min", "duration_studied": "Up to ~3-4 h", "domain": "immediate episodic memory", "population": "Adults 20-80 y (15 studies); larger effect <35 y", "effect_estimate": "SMD 0.16 (-0.00 to 0.32), p=0.05 [gaylor2022]", "certainty": "low", "safety": "Ordinary foods", "study_ids": ["gaylor2022"]}, {"dose": "Guar-gum-enriched white wheat bread breakfast", "onset": "Late postprandial (75-225 min)", "duration_studied": "225 min", "domain": "selective attention, working memory", "population": "40 healthy adults 49-71 y", "effect_estimate": "Selective attention better, P<0.01 [nilsson2012]", "certainty": "low (single trial, indirect for whole grains)", "safety": "None reported", "study_ids": ["nilsson2012"]}, {"dose": "Low-GI cereal, low-GI breakfast or instant oatmeal vs high-GI cereal or no breakfast", "onset": "30-240 min during school morning", "duration_studied": "One morning per condition", "domain": "attention, secondary/spatial memory, response time", "population": "Children 6-11 y and adolescents 12-14 y", "effect_estimate": "Reduced morning decline; better accuracy vs high GI [ingwersen2007, cooper2012, mahoney2005]", "certainty": "low-to-moderate for children", "safety": "Ordinary foods", "study_ids": ["ingwersen2007", "cooper2012", "mahoney2005"]}, {"dose": "Rye-kernel bread late evening meal (50 g available starch)", "onset": "10.5 h (next morning, fasting)", "duration_studied": "Single and 3 consecutive evenings", "domain": "plasma BDNF (biomarker)", "population": "19 healthy adults 20-33 y", "effect_estimate": "+27% BDNF (p=0.001) [sandberg2018bdnf]", "certainty": "very low for any cognitive inference", "safety": "None reported", "study_ids": ["sandberg2018bdnf"]}, {"dose": "Oat beta-glucan added to carbohydrate meals (median ~4 g)", "onset": "0-120 min", "duration_studied": "2-4 h", "domain": "postprandial glucose and insulin (not cognition)", "population": "538 participants, 103 comparisons", "effect_estimate": "Glucose iAUC -23%, insulin -22% [zurbau2021]", "certainty": "high (biomarker)", "safety": "GI symptoms possible at high fibre doses", "study_ids": ["zurbau2021"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "Mean 6.1 years", "population": "3,326 CHAP participants, mean 75 y, 60% African American", "outcome": "Global cognition, perceptual speed, episodic memory decline", "effect_estimate": "AA top vs bottom quintile: global beta 0.024 (0.008-0.039), speed 0.023, memory 0.028 SU/yr slower; White NS", "evidence_quality": "observational", "limitations": "FFQ; effect modification by race unexplained; residual confounding", "study_ids": ["liu2023"]}, {"duration": "11 years", "population": "3,831 adults >=65 y (Cache County)", "outcome": "3MS", "effect_estimate": "Whole grains Q5 vs Q1 +1.19 points (P<0.001), stable over time", "evidence_quality": "observational", "limitations": "Level difference, not slope; rank-score method", "study_ids": ["wengreen2013"]}, {"duration": "Cross-sectional", "population": "5,020 Chinese adults, mean 64 y", "outcome": "Composite cognition, MCI", "effect_estimate": "Q4 vs Q1 beta 0.13 SD (0.09-0.18); MCI OR 0.82 (0.74-0.91) per 25 g/d", "evidence_quality": "cross-sectional", "limitations": "Reverse causation; square-dance cohort (active volunteers)", "study_ids": ["li2025"]}, {"duration": "24 months (RCT)", "population": "52 healthy elderly Japanese", "outcome": "HDS-R, MMSE, FAB, CADi, apathy", "effect_estimate": "FAB sub-item and CADi time improved; apathy lower", "evidence_quality": "low (poor risk of bias)", "limitations": "Small; selective outcomes; processed brown rice", "study_ids": ["kuroda2019"]}, {"duration": "3 days (crossover RCT)", "population": "38 adults 52-70 y", "outcome": "Working memory, selective attention, mood, metabolic markers", "effect_estimate": "Cognition null (+1%); mood +7-15%; insulin sensitivity and SCFA improved", "evidence_quality": "moderate internal validity, small", "limitations": "Retrospective registration; unblinded; short", "study_ids": ["sandberg2018"]}, {"duration": "4 weeks per diet (crossover RCT)", "population": "44 overweight healthy adults 50-73 y", "outcome": "RAVLT, selective attention", "effect_estimate": "Improved recognition and selective attention (p<0.05)", "evidence_quality": "low for whole-grain inference", "limitations": "Multiple active components; small", "study_ids": ["nilsson2013"]}, {"duration": "Diet 1991-2015; cognition 2014-2019", "population": "13,494 women without coeliac disease (NHS II)", "outcome": "Cogstate psychomotor speed/attention, learning/WM, global", "effect_estimate": "Null for gluten (global -0.002 SD, -0.05 to 0.05)", "evidence_quality": "observational, large, precise", "limitations": "Single cognitive assessment", "study_ids": ["wang2021"]}, {"duration": "29 days (RCT)", "population": "132 adults 35-65 y", "outcome": "Corsi working memory, multitasking, stress response, mood", "effect_estimate": "Improved Corsi and multitasking at 430 and 1290 mg; mood null", "evidence_quality": "low-moderate (single sponsored trial)", "limitations": "Herb extract, not grain; sponsor co-authors", "study_ids": ["kennedy2020"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Whole grains are safe staple foods for most people. Main issues: coeliac disease and wheat allergy (wheat, barley, rye; oats often cross-contaminated), fructan-related symptoms in IBS, contaminants concentrated in bran (inorganic arsenic in brown rice, mycotoxins in poorly stored cereals, especially relevant in North Africa), acrylamide in toasted or baked products, and phytate reducing mineral absorption. A Morocco-specific point is that whole flour is exempt from mandatory iron/folic-acid fortification.", "adverse_effects": ["Bloating, flatulence and altered bowel habit when fibre intake rises quickly", "Gluten-related disorders: coeliac disease (immune enteropathy, extra-intestinal and neurological manifestations), wheat allergy, non-coeliac gluten/wheat sensitivity", "Contaminant exposure: inorganic arsenic (rice bran, brown rice) [su2023]; aflatoxins, ochratoxin A, fumonisins, zearalenone in regional cereals [benhassouna2022]; acrylamide in browned products (not researched here)"], "contraindications": ["Coeliac disease: strict avoidance of wheat, barley, rye; only certified gluten-free oats", "Wheat allergy", "IBS on low-FODMAP diet: limit wheat and rye fructans"], "drug_interactions": ["High-fibre meals may slow or reduce absorption of some oral drugs and of iron/zinc supplements taken at the same time (general pharmacology; separate dosing advised; not researched here)", "People on insulin or sulfonylureas: large changes in carbohydrate quality alter glucose responses (monitoring advice)"], "upper_limits": "No upper limit for whole grains or dietary fibre (EFSA and IOM set none); contaminant limits apply to ingredients (EU maximum levels for mycotoxins and inorganic arsenic in rice; Moroccan limits not checked here).", "sleep_impact": "None known; a rye evening meal improved next-morning mood without sleep data [sandberg2018].", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Decades of habitual intake in large cohorts; consistently associated with lower cardiometabolic disease risk; no long-term safety signal outside the issues listed.", "daily_use_appropriate": "yes", "special_populations": "Children: suitable; limit rice-based products for infants because of arsenic. Pregnancy: in Morocco, replacing fortified white flour with unfortified whole flour may reduce folic-acid intake; folic-acid supplementation guidance still applies. Older adults: fibre beneficial; ensure fluid intake. Coeliac patients: gluten-free whole grains (millet, sorghum, buckwheat, certified oats).", "specialist_review_needed": ["Gastroenterologist/dietitian for gluten and coeliac labelling", "Food toxicologist for mycotoxin, arsenic and acrylamide specifications in bran-rich ingredients", "Regulatory nutritionist for Moroccan fortification rules if a whole-flour product replaces fortified flour"], "hard_safety_gate": "pass", "gate_reason": "Ordinary staple foods; risks (gluten, contaminants) are managed by standard allergen labelling and ingredient specifications."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Ordinary foods. EFSA found 'whole grain' not sufficiently characterised for health claims (2010) [efsa2010wholegrain]. Authorised Article 13 claims in Reg. (EU) 432/2012 include beta-glucan and normal blood cholesterol (>=1 g beta-glucan from oats/barley per portion; inform that 3 g/day gives the effect) and oat/barley beta-glucan and reduced post-meal blood glucose rise (>=4 g beta-glucan per 30 g available carbohydrate per portion); barley grain fibre and faecal bulk is also authorised for high-fibre foods; an Article 14 claim covers oat beta-glucan and cholesterol lowering/CHD risk [efsa2010oat]. Nutrition claims 'source of fibre'/'high fibre' under Reg. 1924/2006. Cereals containing gluten are Annex II allergens (Reg. 1169/2011); 'gluten-free' requires <=20 mg/kg (Reg. 828/2014; not re-read). No cognitive claim exists.", "us": "Conventional foods. Authorised health claim 21 CFR 101.81: soluble fibre from whole oats (oat bran, rolled oats, whole oat flour, oatrim) or barley and coronary heart disease, requiring >=0.75 g soluble fibre per reference amount and stating 3 g/day; FDAMA notification claims link whole-grain foods (>=51% whole grain by weight) with heart disease (and, in the 1999 General Mills notification, certain cancers) - exact current wording not re-verified. Wheat is a major allergen (FALCPA). No cognition health claim; structure/function claims must be truthful and substantiated (FTC).", "morocco": "Cereal products are regulated by Decree 2-19-144 (2019); soft-wheat flour must be enriched with NaFeEDTA and folic acid, but whole ('complete') flour and certain quality/organic small-pack flours are exempt (project unit X6). Labelling under Decree 2-12-389 (Arabic mandatory, allergens Annex I); nutrition claims per Joint Order 281-16; no authorised health-claim list found, so beta-glucan claims authorised in the EU have no confirmed Moroccan equivalent.", "permitted_claims_examples": ["EU (Reg. 432/2012): 'Beta-glucans contribute to the maintenance of normal blood cholesterol levels' - >=1 g beta-glucan from oats, oat bran, barley, barley bran per quantified portion; inform that the effect needs 3 g/day", "EU (Reg. 432/2012): 'Consumption of beta-glucans from oats or barley as part of a meal contributes to the reduction of the blood glucose rise after that meal' - >=4 g beta-glucan per 30 g available carbohydrate in a quantified portion", "US (21 CFR 101.81 model claim): 'Soluble fiber from foods such as (name of source, e.g., oatmeal), as part of a diet low in saturated fat and cholesterol, may reduce the risk of heart disease. A serving of (name of food) supplies __ grams of the (daily amount, 3 g) soluble fiber from (name of source) necessary per day to have this effect.'"], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "None for traditional whole grains, oat bran or barley flours. Concentrated or high-molecular-weight beta-glucan isolates and green oat herb extracts require separate novel-food/food-supplement and NDI/GRAS review (not researched here).", "regulatory_gate": "pass"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Excellent: oats and barley are ideal low-GI bases for breakfast products, bars, breads, soups (Moroccan dchicha/belboula barley soup, barley couscous) and porridges; whole wheat for breads and couscous.", "formats": ["porridge and overnight oats (with dairy or plant milks)", "oat- or barley-based breakfast cereals and bars", "whole-grain breads (barley, rye, whole wheat) and crackers", "whole-grain couscous and barley soups", "beta-glucan-enriched baked goods"], "stability": "Whole-grain flours turn rancid faster (germ lipids) and need shorter shelf life or heat stabilisation; beta-glucan molecular weight is degraded by intensive processing, baking with enzymes and long fermentation, reducing glycaemic benefit [zurbau2021].", "taste": "Oats are well accepted; whole-wheat and barley breads are denser and more bitter; consumer acceptance improves with blends.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Glycaemic benefit depends on beta-glucan dose and molecular weight and on keeping kernels intact (rolled vs instant oats); phytate lowers iron/zinc absorption, partly offset by sourdough fermentation.", "dose_feasibility": "The EU cholesterol claim (>=1 g beta-glucan per portion, 3 g/day) is achievable with roughly 25-40 g oats or oat bran per portion depending on beta-glucan content (oats roughly 3.5-5%, oat bran higher; approximate). The glucose-rise claim (4 g beta-glucan per 30 g available carbohydrate) generally requires oat bran concentrates or beta-glucan-enriched formulations. No cognitive dose exists.", "cost_availability": "Oats, barley and whole wheat are inexpensive staples; barley is traditional and widely grown in Morocco; certified gluten-free oats cost more.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 3, "effect_magnitude": 2, "safety": 8.5, "reproducibility": 3.5, "food_suitability": 9, "cost_access": 9, "rationale": "evidence_quality 3: all cognitive rows are grade C (inconsistent cohorts, small or confounded trials, child-only acute benefits) or D (young adults); grade A applies only to the postprandial-glycaemia biomarker. effect_magnitude 2: cohort differences of ~0.02 SD/year or ~1 point of 100; acute adult effects SMD ~0.16 and indirect; the one adult cognitive trial was null. safety 8.5: staple foods; gluten, IBS and contaminant issues manageable by labelling and specifications. reproducibility 3.5: metabolic effects highly reproducible, cognitive associations not (half of cohorts null; race-specific findings). food_suitability 9: versatile, culturally familiar (barley, whole-wheat couscous, oats) and compatible with authorised EU claims. cost_access 9: cheap and locally produced."}, "research_gaps": ["Randomised trials of whole-grain or oat-based breakfasts vs refined equivalents on late-morning attention and memory in students and knowledge workers (none found)", "Publication of NCT04135066 (Westlake Nutrition and Brain Health Study; whole- vs refined-grain breakfast, n=1,425, completed May 2025, no results posted or published as of 2026-10-25)", "Long-term (>=1 year) whole-grain substitution trials with cognitive primary outcomes in midlife adults", "Explanation of race-specific cohort findings (CHAP) and replication in Mediterranean/North African cohorts", "Whether beta-glucan molecular weight and dose that lower glycaemia also change cognition", "Impact of shifting from fortified white to unfortified whole flour on folate and iron status in Moroccan women"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 5.15, "eligibility": "eligible", "safety_gate": "pass", "regulatory_gate": "pass", "best_grade_acute": "C", "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": "A", "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"executive_function": "C", "sustained_attention": "C", "working_memory": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 14, "n_studies": 22, "n_verified": 22, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 6, "n_systematic_reviews": 2, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["benefit", "inconsistent", "insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "yogurt-and-fermented-dairy", "name": "Yogurt and fermented dairy", "category": "J", "category_name": "Whole foods", "aliases": ["yogurt", "yoghurt", "fermented milk", "kefir", "lben (Moroccan fermented buttermilk)", "raib (Moroccan set fermented milk)", "jben (fresh cheese)", "cheese", "skyr / strained yogurt", "dairy products (milk, yogurt, cheese)"], "food_sources": ["plain set or stirred yogurt (cow, goat, sheep milk)", "drinking yogurt and kefir", "traditional Moroccan lben and raib", "fresh and ripened cheeses", "milk (non-fermented comparator in most cohorts)"], "active_constituents": ["high-quality protein (casein, whey; tryptophan, tyrosine)", "calcium, phosphorus, potassium, magnesium, zinc (enhanced zinc absorption from plant meals)", "iodine (content depends on feed and dairy sanitisers; not verified for Moroccan milk)", "vitamin B12, riboflavin (B2), vitamin A", "live lactic acid bacteria (Lactobacillus delbrueckii subsp. bulgaricus, Streptococcus thermophilus; added probiotic strains in some products)", "fermentation-derived bioactive peptides (e.g. beta-lactolin GTWY, lactononadecapeptide) and fatty acid amides (oleamide) in specific products", "milk fat and milk fat globule membrane polar lipids (in full-fat products)"], "one_line_verdict": "Yogurt and fermented dairy are nutritious, culturally native Moroccan staple foods that help prevent B12, iodine, calcium and protein shortfalls, but the evidence that eating them improves cognition is observational and inconsistent in older adults and almost absent in healthy young adults; the positive trials concern specific industry-developed peptides or probiotic strains, not ordinary yogurt.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. Yogurt and fermented milks supply protein, calcium, riboflavin, vitamin B12 and (variably) iodine; live cultures improve lactose digestion (an EU-authorised claim). For cognition the evidence is mainly observational. A dose-response meta-analysis of 15 prospective cohorts (>300,000 participants, median follow-up 11.4 y) found a non-linear inverse association between dairy intake and cognitive decline or dementia, nadir at about 150 g/day (RR 0.88, 95% CI 0.78-0.99), but the association was confined to Asian cohorts with low intakes, European cohorts were null, and heterogeneity was high [villoz2024]. A 2025 meta-analysis reported RR 0.85 (0.73-0.98) for high versus low intake, better verbal memory and executive function with fermented products and slightly poorer verbal memory with high milk intake [gao2025]. Earlier reviews rated the evidence insufficient (milk and cognitive decline RR 1.21, 0.81-1.82, I2 64%) [lee2018, crichton2010, cuestatriana2019]. Individual cohorts conflict: lower Alzheimer risk with more dairy in Hisayama [ozawa2014]; faster 20-year decline (0.10 z) with >1 glass of milk/day in ARIC [petruskiivleva2017] and with whole-fat milk in PREDIMED-Plus, where yogurt and cheese were neutral [ni2022]; lower dementia risk with high-fat cheese (HR 0.87) and cream in Malmo [du2026] and with cheese in Finnish men (HR 0.72) [ylilauri2022]; a non-significant higher risk with daily cheese in Ohsaki [lu2023]; no dementia association for yogurt or cheese in Sydney [idrus2026]. Confounding by diet quality and socioeconomic status is likely. Randomised evidence. The only RCT of ordinary dairy servings (4 versus 1 serve/day reduced-fat dairy, 6 months, n=38 overweight adults) found only a marginal spatial working-memory benefit [crichton2012rct]. The pooled RCT effect of SMD 0.45 (0.30-0.60) in older adults [giraldocastrillon2026] comes from five small East-Asian, industry-sponsored trials of dairy-derived bioactives (beta-lactolin, L. helveticus lactononadecapeptide fermented milk, probiotic fermented milks, oleamide cheese), not yogurt as such, and that review contains data-extraction errors; examples are an Asahi fermented-milk trial in healthy middle-aged adults (n=60, RBANS attention and delayed memory) [ohsawa2018] and a Kirin beta-lactolin trial (n=30) with EEG P300 changes [kanatome2021]. Target population. In adults aged 25-45 a 4-week single-blind kefir crossover (n=26) improved two relational-memory metrics but not mood or cortisol [cannavale2023]; a Meiji-funded trial (n=66) found acute shifting and updating gains 15-60 min after 10-15 g milk-protein drinks [nouchi2023]; Yakult LcS fermented milk blunted exam-related cortisol rises and physical symptoms in medical students [takada2016]; fMRI changes after a Danone fermented milk in healthy women are a biomarker only [tillisch2013]. In 124 healthy older adults a probiotic milk drink gave no memory benefit and slightly worse scores on two measures [benton2007]. No positive finding is independently replicated; several registered trials have no posted results. Safety signal: dairy intake is associated with higher Parkinson disease risk in men (HR 1.28, 1.05-1.56) [warz2026], supported by Mendelian randomisation (OR 1.70 per serving/day) [domenighetti2022]. Conclusion: a sound nutritious base food and deficiency-prevention vehicle, without adequate evidence of cognitive enhancement in healthy young adults.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "The defensible role of yogurt and fermented dairy is deficiency prevention, not enhancement. Dairy supplies vitamin B12 (see vitamin-b12.json: correcting deficiency matters clinically, but B12 does not enhance cognition in replete people), protein, calcium, riboflavin and, where present, iodine; adding milk or yogurt to a phytate-rich plant diet raised zinc absorption by 50-68% in young Mexican women without impairing iron absorption [rosado2005], which is relevant to low-income Moroccan diets. Moroccan national surveys summarised in the regulatory unit (X6) report widespread low iodine status in women, but the iodine content of Moroccan milk and yogurt was not verified here, so any iodine-based argument needs product analysis. The cohort associations are strongest in Asian populations with low baseline dairy intake and null in high-intake European populations [villoz2024], a pattern consistent with a benefit from moving out of very low intake (or with confounding) rather than with enhancement in replete eaters. The positive RCTs in healthy or subjectively impaired older adults tested specific peptides or probiotic strains [ohsawa2018, kanatome2021, giraldocastrillon2026]; their results cannot be transferred to ordinary yogurt, to students, or to people who already eat dairy daily. In healthy young adults the only cognitive data are one small kefir crossover [cannavale2023] and one acute milk-protein drink trial [nouchi2023]; enhancement in this group is unproven.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Claims such as 'yogurt for the brain', 'gut-brain yogurt', 'probiotic for focus' or 'fermented dairy protects memory' are not supported for ordinary yogurt. The cognitive RCT literature is dominated by small, single-company Japanese trials of proprietary ingredients (Kirin beta-lactolin, Asahi lactononadecapeptide, Yakult LcS, Meiji milk protein drink) and one Danone fMRI study; the meta-analysis that pooled them to SMD 0.45 has extraction errors and should not be quoted. Cohort data are inconsistent and in places point the other way (more milk, faster decline in ARIC and PREDIMED-Plus). In the EU the only dairy-specific authorised claim is lactose digestion by live yogurt cultures; the word 'probiotic' is treated by the Commission as an implied health claim; cognition wording is only possible through nutrient claims (e.g. iodine and normal cognitive function, B12 and normal psychological function) when the product is at least a source of that nutrient, and such claims say nothing about yogurt itself. In the US the only yogurt qualified health claim concerns type 2 diabetes risk (March 2024). The honest positioning is: a protein-, calcium- and B12-rich traditional food that fits a Mediterranean/Moroccan pattern, with no proven brain benefit of its own.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Dairy could influence brain health through four routes: (1) correction or prevention of nutrient shortfalls (B12, iodine, protein, riboflavin) that are known to impair cognition when deficient; (2) cardiometabolic effects (blood pressure, glycaemic regulation, type 2 diabetes risk) that protect cerebrovascular health; (3) live cultures and fermentation products acting on the gut microbiota and vagal or immune gut-brain signalling; (4) specific bioactive peptides or lipids formed during fermentation (beta-lactolin, lactononadecapeptide, oleamide, milk polar lipids). Route 1 is established in deficiency; routes 2-4 rest on limited human biomarker data and animal work and do not establish cognitive efficacy of ordinary yogurt.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Yogurt / fermented milk ingestion -> digestion and absorption of protein, calcium, B12, riboflavin, iodine; lactose partly pre-digested by live cultures", "detail": "Nutrient absorption is well characterised; live cultures improve lactose digestion of the product (EU-authorised claim); milk or yogurt raised zinc absorption from a phytate-rich diet by 50-68% [rosado2005].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Nutrient adequacy (B12, iodine) -> myelination, methylation and thyroid hormone supply to the brain", "detail": "Deficiency of B12 or iodine impairs cognition; benefit of repletion applies to deficient people only (cross-reference vitamin-b12.json); dairy-specific evidence of this route was not studied.", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Live lactic acid bacteria -> transient changes in gut microbiota composition", "detail": "Kefir increased faecal Lactobacillus by 235% versus control milk in healthy adults, but the change did not correlate with memory gains [cannavale2023].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Gut microbes / fermented products -> vagal afferent and HPA-axis signalling -> stress reactivity", "detail": "LcS fermented milk blunted exam-stress salivary cortisol rises in medical students; in rats LcS stimulated gastric vagal afferents and reduced PVN CRF expression [takada2016]; fMRI showed reduced emotional-attention network responses after a fermented milk product [tillisch2013].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Fermentation-derived peptides and lipids (beta-lactolin, lactononadecapeptide, oleamide) -> proposed anti-inflammatory, monoaminergic or microglial actions", "detail": "Mechanisms are described mainly in rodents and in the sponsoring companies' studies; in humans only surrogate EEG (P300) and small cognitive trials exist [kanatome2021, ohsawa2018].", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Dairy -> blood pressure, glycaemic and type 2 diabetes risk -> cerebrovascular health", "detail": "Cardiometabolic associations underlie the FDA yogurt/type 2 diabetes qualified claim; a link from these to cognition in dairy-specific data is not demonstrated.", "evidence_level": "plausible"}, {"step": "Measured human outcome", "detail": "Observational inverse associations with dementia in low-intake Asian cohorts, null in Europe, adverse for high milk intake in two cohorts [villoz2024, petruskiivleva2017, ni2022]; small industry trials of specific products positive [giraldocastrillon2026]; no replicated cognitive benefit of ordinary yogurt in healthy young adults.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["nutrient adequacy (B12, iodine, protein, riboflavin)", "gut microbiota and gut-brain axis (vagal, HPA axis)", "bioactive peptides (beta-lactolin, lactononadecapeptide)", "cardiometabolic and cerebrovascular protection", "milk polar lipids / milk fat globule membrane (not reviewed in detail)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "dementia_risk", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Community-dwelling middle-aged and older adults without dementia at baseline (Asia, Europe, USA, Australia); habitual intake by FFQ or diet records", "dose_or_intake": "Habitual total dairy, about 0-700 g/day; nadir of risk near 150 g/day", "duration": "Follow-up 4.8-30 years (median 11.4)", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Dose-response meta-analysis of 15 cohorts: non-linear inverse association, nadir about 150 g/day; association limited to Asian cohorts with low intake, null in European cohorts. A second meta-analysis found similar pooled estimates. Individual cohorts range from lower AD risk (Hisayama) to faster decline with >1 glass milk/day (ARIC).", "effect_estimate": "RR 0.88 (95% CI 0.78-0.99) at ~150 g/day; RR 0.84 (0.77-0.92) per 1 time/day [villoz2024]; high vs low RR 0.85 (0.73-0.98) [gao2025]; milk high vs low RR 1.21 (0.81-1.82), I2 64% [lee2018]", "certainty_notes": "Observational only (starts low); downgraded for inconsistency (Asia vs Europe, milk vs fermented), high heterogeneity, heterogeneous exposure definitions and residual confounding/reverse causation. Grade C because the direction is not consistent and the effect is small.", "key_study_ids": ["villoz2024", "gao2025", "lee2018", "wu2016", "ozawa2014", "petruskiivleva2017"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_decline", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Older adults (55-90 y) in cohorts separating fermented dairy (yogurt, cheese) from milk", "dose_or_intake": "Yogurt ~90-160 g/day upper quartiles; cheese >=50 g/day high-fat cheese (Malmo); total milk tertiles", "duration": "2-25 years follow-up", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Fermented dairy shows neutral-to-favourable associations (high-fat cheese and cream with lower dementia in Malmo; cheese with lower dementia in Finnish men; yogurt with better memory cross-sectionally in Canada), but yogurt and cheese were neutral in PREDIMED-Plus and Sydney, daily cheese non-significantly harmful in Ohsaki, and whole-fat milk associated with faster 2-year decline in PREDIMED-Plus.", "effect_estimate": "High-fat cheese HR 0.87 (0.78-0.97) [du2026]; cheese HR 0.72 (0.52-0.99) [ylilauri2022]; daily cheese HR 1.28 (0.91-1.79), daily yogurt HR 0.89 (0.74-1.09) [lu2023]; whole-fat milk beta -6.64 (-10.81 to -2.47) global cognition change [ni2022]; no dementia association for yogurt or cheese [idrus2026]", "certainty_notes": "Observational; multiple sub-type analyses invite chance findings; direction varies by product and cohort; full-fat cheese findings conflict with cardiovascular guidance and may reflect confounding. Not usable to claim that fermented dairy protects cognition.", "key_study_ids": ["du2026", "ylilauri2022", "lu2023", "ni2022", "idrus2026", "tessier2021"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Healthy or subjectively impaired older adults (mostly Japan/Korea, mean ~60-70 y) given specific fermented dairy products or dairy-derived bioactives", "dose_or_intake": "Proprietary products: beta-lactolin peptide, L. helveticus fermented milk 190 g/day, probiotic fermented milks, oleamide cheese", "duration": "8-24 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Meta-analysis of 5 small RCTs (n=369) reported a positive pooled effect on global cognition, memory and processing speed. The trials are company-sponsored and product-specific; the review mislabels at least one trial and claims moderate-to-high certainty that is not credible.", "effect_estimate": "SMD 0.45 (95% CI 0.30-0.60) global cognition, 5 RCTs, n=369 [giraldocastrillon2026]; observational OR 0.95 (0.89-1.02) in the same review", "certainty_notes": "Downgraded for risk of bias (industry sponsorship, small samples, unclear allocation concealment in 2 trials), indirectness (specific ingredients, not yogurt; East-Asian older adults) and likely publication bias. Cannot exceed C.", "key_study_ids": ["giraldocastrillon2026", "ohsawa2018", "kanatome2021"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Overweight/obese adults, mean age 52, habitual low dairy intake (<2 serves/day)", "dose_or_intake": "4 serves/day versus 1 serve/day reduced-fat dairy", "duration": "6 months per arm (12-month crossover)", "grade": "C", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "Only RCT of ordinary dairy servings: across memory, processing speed, executive function, attention and reasoning, only spatial working memory was marginally better on the high-dairy diet.", "effect_estimate": "Not reported as effect size in abstract; described as marginal (SoE rated insufficient by Lee 2018)", "certainty_notes": "Single small trial (n=38 completers), many outcomes, marginal single finding, not replicated; insufficient to support a benefit.", "key_study_ids": ["crichton2012rct", "lee2018"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults 25-45 y (n=26) free of GI and mental illness", "dose_or_intake": "8 oz (about 240 mL) kefir-type fermented dairy beverage daily (25-30 billion CFU)", "duration": "4 weeks per arm, crossover", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Kefir improved two relational-memory metrics (misplacement, object-location binding) versus isocaloric lactose-free milk; DASS-42 mood and urinary cortisol unchanged; memory change unrelated to the Lactobacillus increase.", "effect_estimate": "Misplacement p=0.04; object-location binding p=0.03; effect sizes not in abstract", "certainty_notes": "Single small single-blind trial, borderline p-values on a specialised task, no replication; imprecise. Practical relevance for students unknown.", "key_study_ids": ["cannavale2023"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy young adults (n=66), double-blind parallel groups", "dose_or_intake": "Acidified milk drink with 10 g or 15 g milk protein versus 0 g protein drink", "duration": "Single dose; tested 15 and 60 min", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Shifting improved at 15 min with 10 g and 15 g protein (persisting at 60 min with 15 g); updating improved at 60 min; processing speed, inhibition and working-memory capacity not reported as improved.", "effect_estimate": "Not reported as standardized effect in abstract", "certainty_notes": "Single small trial funded by the drink manufacturer (Meiji), several tasks and time points, no replication; tests milk protein dose rather than yogurt or fermentation.", "key_study_ids": ["nouchi2023"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "students", "population_detail": "Healthy medical students preparing for a national examination (three pooled double-blind trials)", "dose_or_intake": "Daily fermented milk containing Lactobacillus casei strain Shirota versus placebo milk", "duration": "8 weeks before the examination", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Exam-related rises in salivary cortisol (a biomarker) and incidence of physical (abdominal, cold) symptoms were suppressed with LcS, but a difference in subjective anxiety, the actual mood outcome, is not reported in the abstract. In older adults a probiotic milk drink did not change mood overall (benefit only in a low-mood subgroup). No cognitive or academic outcomes in students.", "effect_estimate": "Not reported as effect size in abstracts", "certainty_notes": "Strain- and product-specific, sponsor-run (Yakult Central Institute); the positive endpoints are a biomarker and physical symptoms, not mood or cognition; Benton 2007 mood effect only in a post-hoc subgroup. Cross-reference probiotics unit I1.", "key_study_ids": ["takada2016", "benton2007"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Healthy community volunteers, mean age 61.8 y", "dose_or_intake": "Daily probiotic-containing milk drink", "duration": "3 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "No memory benefit; the probiotic group performed slightly worse on two memory measures, which the authors considered a possible chance finding.", "effect_estimate": "not reported in abstract", "certainty_notes": "Single trial (n=124 completers), short duration, abstract-only extraction; adverse direction may be chance. Counterweight to positive company-run fermented-milk trials.", "key_study_ids": ["benton2007"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy middle-aged adults (about 45-64 y; working-age, but not specifically knowledge workers)", "dose_or_intake": "L. helveticus fermented milk drink 190 g/day; beta-lactolin peptide supplement", "duration": "6-8 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "RBANS total, attention and delayed memory improved with fermented milk (between-group difference in attention at 8 weeks); beta-lactolin increased P300 amplitude at two sites and changed verbal fluency scores.", "effect_estimate": "Attention between-group p=0.028 [ohsawa2018]; P300 Cp2 p=0.011, verbal fluency p=0.033 [kanatome2021]", "certainty_notes": "Two small single-company trials (Asahi; Kirin), no independent replication, P300 is a biomarker; product-specific ingredients not present in ordinary yogurt in the same amounts.", "key_study_ids": ["ohsawa2018", "kanatome2021"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "neuroplasticity_biomarkers", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy women (fMRI) and healthy middle-aged adults (EEG)", "dose_or_intake": "Fermented milk with probiotic strains twice daily; beta-lactolin", "duration": "4-6 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "Fermented milk reduced task-related responses of an affective/viscerosensory network and altered midbrain resting connectivity; beta-lactolin increased P300 amplitude. These surrogate changes have no established link to better cognition.", "effect_estimate": "49% cross-block covariance, P=0.004 [tillisch2013]; P300 p=0.011-0.02 [kanatome2021]", "certainty_notes": "Biomarker outcomes, small samples (12 vs 11 vs 13; 30), sponsor involvement (Danone employees; Kirin). Must not be presented as cognitive benefit.", "key_study_ids": ["tillisch2013", "kanatome2021"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_mci", "population_detail": "Chinese adults 65-75 y with mild cognitive impairment", "dose_or_intake": "200 mL A1-beta-casein-free skim milk twice daily versus conventional skim milk", "duration": "90 days", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "A1-free milk improved three cognitive tests more than conventional milk; not linked to glutathione. Compares two milks, not dairy versus none.", "effect_estimate": "Not reported as standardized effect in abstract", "certainty_notes": "Single trial (n=91 analysed); author directs the company marketing the primary test (SCIT) and consulting for The a2 Milk Company; indirect to Brain Food's healthy young target.", "key_study_ids": ["zhang2025"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "learning", "population": "students", "population_detail": "University students; academic performance or learning outcomes", "dose_or_intake": "Any habitual yogurt/fermented dairy intake", "duration": "Not studied", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No trial or cohort was found that measured learning, examination performance or academic outcomes in students in relation to yogurt or fermented dairy.", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "Absence of evidence; only indirect data (stress biomarkers in medical students; one small kefir trial in 25-45 y adults).", "key_study_ids": ["takada2016", "cannavale2023"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "other", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Adults in prospective cohorts and genetic (lactase persistence) Mendelian randomisation: Parkinson disease risk", "dose_or_intake": "Highest vs lowest dairy intake; genetically predicted +1 serving/day", "duration": "Long-term (cohort follow-up; lifelong genetic exposure)", "grade": "C", "direction": "harm", "effect_summary": "Higher dairy intake is associated with higher Parkinson disease risk in men, not women; MR supports causality in men. Absolute risk increase is small because PD is uncommon.", "effect_estimate": "Men HR 1.28 (1.05-1.56), women HR 1.02 (0.80-1.30) [warz2026]; MR OR 1.70 (1.12-2.60) per serving/day, men OR 2.50 (1.37-4.56) [domenighetti2022]", "certainty_notes": "Observational plus MR (single lactase variant, pleiotropy possible); the review authors call the evidence weak. Relevant to safety framing, not to students' short-term use.", "key_study_ids": ["warz2026", "domenighetti2022"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "Acidified milk drink providing 10 g or 15 g milk protein", "onset": "15 min (shifting); 60 min (updating)", "duration_studied": "60 min after a single drink", "domain": "executive_function (task shifting, updating)", "population": "Healthy young adults (n=66)", "effect_estimate": "Not reported as standardized effect in abstract; significant group differences at 15 and 60 min", "certainty": "Low (single small manufacturer-funded trial)", "safety": "No adverse events reported in abstract", "study_ids": ["nouchi2023"]}, {"dose": "Plain yogurt, kefir, lben or cheese as a single serving", "onset": "not studied", "duration_studied": "not studied", "domain": "any cognitive domain", "population": "Healthy young adults / students", "effect_estimate": "No acute cognitive trial of ordinary fermented dairy foods identified", "certainty": "No evidence", "safety": "Food-level; lactose intolerance and milk allergy", "study_ids": []}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "4.8-30 years (median 11.4)", "population": "Adults without chronic disease, 15 cohorts, >300,000 participants", "outcome": "Cognitive decline or incident dementia", "effect_estimate": "RR 0.88 (0.78-0.99) at ~150 g/day; inverse only in Asian cohorts, null in Europe", "evidence_quality": "Low (observational, heterogeneous)", "limitations": "Residual confounding, varied exposure metrics, high heterogeneity", "study_ids": ["villoz2024", "gao2025"]}, {"duration": "20 years", "population": "13,751 US middle-aged adults (ARIC)", "outcome": "Change in global cognitive z-score", "effect_estimate": ">1 glass milk/day: additional decline 0.10 z (95% CI 0.03-0.16) vs almost never", "evidence_quality": "Low (observational)", "limitations": "FFQ at midlife; attrition handled by imputation; possible confounding", "study_ids": ["petruskiivleva2017"]}, {"duration": "2 years", "population": "4,668 Spanish adults 55-75 y with metabolic syndrome (PREDIMED-Plus)", "outcome": "Change in global cognitive function", "effect_estimate": "Whole-fat milk highest tertile beta -6.64 (-10.81 to -2.47); yogurt, cheese, fermented dairy null", "evidence_quality": "Low (observational within trial)", "limitations": "Short follow-up; high-risk population; scaled betas hard to interpret", "study_ids": ["ni2022"]}, {"duration": "8-24 weeks", "population": "Older adults (East Asia), 5 RCTs, n=369", "outcome": "Global cognition with specific fermented dairy bioactives", "effect_estimate": "SMD 0.45 (0.30-0.60)", "evidence_quality": "Low (small industry trials; review extraction errors)", "limitations": "Product-specific; sponsor-run; indirect to yogurt and to young adults", "study_ids": ["giraldocastrillon2026", "ohsawa2018", "kanatome2021"]}, {"duration": "6 months per arm", "population": "38 overweight adults, mean 52 y, low habitual dairy", "outcome": "Multidomain cognition with 4 vs 1 serve/day reduced-fat dairy", "effect_estimate": "Only spatial working memory marginally better", "evidence_quality": "Very low-low (single small RCT)", "limitations": "Small n; many tests; unreplicated", "study_ids": ["crichton2012rct"]}, {"duration": "4 weeks per arm", "population": "26 healthy adults 25-45 y", "outcome": "Relational memory, mood, cortisol with kefir", "effect_estimate": "Two relational-memory metrics improved (p=0.03-0.04); mood and cortisol null", "evidence_quality": "Low (single small single-blind trial)", "limitations": "Small; specialised task; no replication", "study_ids": ["cannavale2023"]}, {"duration": "8 weeks", "population": "Healthy medical students before a national exam", "outcome": "Salivary cortisol, physical symptoms (stress) with LcS fermented milk", "effect_estimate": "Cortisol rise and symptom incidence suppressed vs placebo", "evidence_quality": "Low (sponsor-run, biomarker)", "limitations": "No cognitive outcome; strain-specific", "study_ids": ["takada2016"]}, {"duration": "Long-term / lifelong", "population": "578,736 adults in 8 cohorts; 18,199 in MR consortium", "outcome": "Parkinson disease risk", "effect_estimate": "Men HR 1.28 (1.05-1.56); MR OR 1.70 (1.12-2.60) per serving/day", "evidence_quality": "Low-moderate (observational plus MR)", "limitations": "Sex-specific; single genetic instrument; small absolute risk", "study_ids": ["warz2026", "domenighetti2022"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Yogurt and fermented dairy are safe everyday foods for most people. Relevant risks are cow's milk protein allergy (a mandatory allergen), lactose intolerance (much reduced with live-culture yogurt and aged cheese), microbiological hazards in raw-milk or poorly fermented traditional products (Listeria, Brucella in unpasteurised milk), high added sugar in flavoured yogurts, sodium in cheese, saturated fat in full-fat products, and a modest observational and Mendelian-randomisation signal linking high dairy intake to Parkinson disease in men. Probiotic-enriched products carry a small infection risk in severely immunocompromised people (cross-reference I1).", "adverse_effects": ["Bloating, flatulence or diarrhoea in lactose maldigesters (less with live-culture yogurt)", "IgE-mediated or non-IgE cow's milk protein allergy reactions", "Foodborne infection from raw-milk or unhygienic traditional fermented products (e.g. Listeria, Brucella)", "Higher Parkinson disease risk in men with high dairy intake (HR 1.28 observational; MR OR 1.70 per serving) [warz2026, domenighetti2022]", "Excess free sugar intake from sweetened yogurt drinks"], "contraindications": ["Cow's milk protein allergy", "Galactosaemia", "Severe immunocompromise or central venous catheters for products with added probiotic strains (specialist advice)"], "drug_interactions": ["Calcium in dairy reduces absorption of tetracyclines, fluoroquinolones, levothyroxine and oral bisphosphonates if taken together (separate by 2-4 h)", "Tyramine in aged cheeses with MAO inhibitors (hypertensive reaction); fresh yogurt is low in tyramine"], "upper_limits": "No upper limit for yogurt as a food. Calcium UL 2,500 mg/day (EFSA adults) is not reached by normal dairy intake. Cohort risk nadir for cognition near 150 g/day dairy [villoz2024]; no evidence that more is better.", "sleep_impact": "None known for plain yogurt; no caffeine. Tryptophan content is not shown to affect sleep at food doses.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Decades of habitual intake in cohorts (up to 25-30 years); RCTs of 4 weeks to 12 months.", "daily_use_appropriate": "yes", "special_populations": "Pregnancy: pasteurised products only (Listeria). Children: appropriate food, no cognitive claims transferable. Older adults: useful protein source. People with kidney disease: phosphorus/potassium counselling. Men with family history of Parkinson disease: the PD association is a reason not to promote very high intakes.", "specialist_review_needed": ["Clinical dietitian (lactose intolerance, allergy labelling, sugar reformulation)", "Food microbiologist / ONSSA-approved HACCP for traditional lben/raib production", "Neurologist or epidemiologist for communication of the Parkinson disease signal", "Infectious-disease specialist if probiotic strains are added for immunocompromised consumers"], "hard_safety_gate": "pass", "gate_reason": "A conventional, widely consumed food with long-term exposure data; risks (allergy, lactose, microbiology, sugar) are manageable by labelling, pasteurisation and reformulation. The Parkinson signal argues against promoting high intakes, not against normal servings."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Yogurt and fermented milks are conventional foods (not novel). Authorised Article 13(1) claim in Reg. (EU) 432/2012 (Annex wording checked on legislation.gov.uk copy, accessed 2026-10-25): 'Live cultures in yoghurt or fermented milk improve lactose digestion of the product in individuals who have difficulty digesting lactose', only for yoghurt or fermented milk with at least 10^8 CFU live starter microorganisms (Lactobacillus delbrueckii subsp. bulgaricus and Streptococcus thermophilus) per gram. Nutrient-based claims are usable when the product is at least a 'source of' the nutrient (15% NRV per 100 g, or per portion for single portions, Reg. (EC) 1924/2006), e.g. 'Iodine contributes to normal cognitive function' (verified wording), 'Vitamin B12 contributes to normal psychological function', 'Calcium contributes to normal neurotransmission'. These describe the nutrient, not yogurt, and require analysed content. The Commission treats 'probiotic' as an implied health claim; no probiotic claim is authorised (per X4 regulatory unit). Milk is a mandatory allergen (Reg. (EU) 1169/2011 Annex II). Specific ingredients such as beta-lactolin peptides or lactononadecapeptide would need novel-food assessment if not consumed significantly before 1997 (status not checked).", "us": "Yogurt has a standard of identity (21 CFR 131.200; revised by FDA in 2021 - not re-read here). FDA letter of enforcement discretion, 1 March 2024 (FDA constituent update, accessed 2026-10-25): qualified health claim 'Eating yogurt regularly, at least 2 cups (3 servings) per week, may reduce the risk of type 2 diabetes. FDA has concluded that there is limited information supporting this claim.' (alternative ending 'according to limited scientific evidence'); covers yogurts meeting the standard of identity; no sugar limit stated on the FDA page. No cognitive claim exists; structure/function statements on conventional foods must derive from nutritive value and be truthful and substantiated (FTC competent and reliable scientific evidence). Milk is a major allergen (FALCPA).", "morocco": "Dairy products are conventional foods under Law 28-07 (ONSSA) with labelling under Decree 2-12-389 (Arabic mandatory; disease claims banned, Art. 7); nutrition claims limited to the positive list of Joint Order 281-16; no list of authorised health claims and no approval procedure were found (X6 regulatory unit), so any cognition wording is legally uncertain. Traditional lben and raib must be produced in ONSSA-authorised establishments. Market context: national milk production was reported to have fallen from 2.5 billion litres (2018) to 1.96 billion litres (2023) (challenge.ma article via web search summary, page returned HTTP 403, accessed 2026-10-25; unverified). Status: uncertain for any claim beyond nutrition claims.", "permitted_claims_examples": ["EU (Reg. (EU) 432/2012): 'Live cultures in yoghurt or fermented milk improve lactose digestion of the product in individuals who have difficulty digesting lactose' - only for yoghurt or fermented milk containing at least 10^8 CFU live starter microorganisms (L. delbrueckii subsp. bulgaricus and S. thermophilus) per gram.", "EU (Reg. (EU) 432/2012): 'Iodine contributes to normal cognitive function' - only for food that is at least a source of iodine (a nutrient claim, not evidence about yogurt).", "US (FDA qualified health claim, 1 March 2024): 'Eating yogurt regularly, at least 2 cups (3 servings) per week, may reduce the risk of type 2 diabetes. FDA has concluded that there is limited information supporting this claim.'"], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "None for yogurt, kefir, lben, raib or cheese. Isolated bioactive peptides (beta-lactolin, lactononadecapeptide), novel probiotic strains or milk-fat-globule-membrane concentrates may require EU novel-food authorisation and, as supplements in the US, NDI notification; not checked in this unit.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Excellent as a vehicle: the habitually consumed amounts associated with lowest risk (~150 g/day) equal one ordinary yogurt pot or glass of lben. A plain, low-sugar yogurt or lben combined with nuts, seeds or fruit is a realistic Brain Food breakfast/snack, but the cognitive value rests on overall diet quality and nutrient adequacy, not on the yogurt itself.", "formats": ["plain set or stirred yogurt (125-200 g pots)", "drinking yogurt / lben / kefir (200-250 mL)", "strained high-protein yogurt (10-15 g protein per serving)", "yogurt bowls with nuts, seeds, oats and fruit", "fresh cheese (jben) for savoury snacks"], "stability": "Requires cold chain (2-6 C); live culture counts decline over shelf life (claim requires >=10^8 CFU/g at consumption); post-acidification and syneresis limit shelf life to roughly 3-5 weeks; added probiotic strains need viability validation to end of shelf life.", "taste": "Widely accepted in Morocco (lben, raib, yogurt are staples); plain yogurt is sour, so commercial products are often heavily sweetened; reformulation with fruit and reduced sugar is a design challenge.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Dairy matrix provides highly digestible protein and bioavailable calcium; fermentation lowers lactose; adding yogurt to phytate-rich meals improved zinc absorption [rosado2005]; fat-soluble vitamins depend on fat content.", "dose_feasibility": "Studied intakes fit ordinary servings: 1-4 dairy servings/day [crichton2012rct], 150 g/day risk nadir [villoz2024], 190 g/day fermented milk [ohsawa2018], 240 mL kefir [cannavale2023], 10-15 g milk protein per drink [nouchi2023]. Proprietary peptide doses cannot be achieved with generic yogurt.", "cost_availability": "Yogurt, lben and raib are widely available and inexpensive in Moroccan retail and traditional outlets; domestic milk supply has been under drought pressure (unverified production figures); EU and US supply is abundant.", "cost_evidence": "Retail prices not researched here; production context only (Moroccan milk production reported at 1.96 billion litres in 2023, unverified secondary source)."}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 3, "effect_magnitude": 2, "safety": 8, "reproducibility": 3, "food_suitability": 9, "cost_access": 8, "rationale": "Evidence quality 3: all cognitive rows are grade C or D; dementia associations are observational and inconsistent (Asia vs Europe, milk vs fermented), RCTs are small and mostly test proprietary ingredients; healthy young adults have one small kefir trial and one acute protein-drink trial. Effect magnitude 2: cohort relative risks of 0.85-0.88 likely partly confounded; the one ordinary-dairy RCT found only a marginal single-domain effect; ingredient SMD 0.45 is not transferable. Safety 8: conventional food with decades of exposure; deductions for allergy, lactose, raw-milk microbiology, sugar in flavoured products and the Parkinson signal in men. Reproducibility 3: positive trials are single-company and unreplicated; cohorts conflict. Food suitability 9: the relevant amount is one ordinary serving and the format is native to Morocco (lben, raib). Cost/access 8: cheap and ubiquitous, cold chain required."}, "research_gaps": ["Independent, preregistered RCT of plain yogurt or lben (not proprietary strains) on cognition in healthy young adults and students, 8-12 weeks, with active non-fermented dairy control", "Acute (same-morning) trials of yogurt-based breakfasts versus isoenergetic alternatives on attention and memory in students", "Cohorts in North African populations with low-to-moderate dairy intake (Morocco) linking dairy to cognitive ageing", "Measurement of iodine and B12 content of Moroccan milk, yogurt and lben to judge their deficiency-prevention value", "Publication of results from registered fermented-dairy cognition trials (e.g. NCT05323825, NCT04643080, NCT06311097) that have no posted results", "Independent replication of beta-lactolin and lactononadecapeptide trials outside sponsoring companies", "Clarification of the dairy-Parkinson association and whether fermented dairy differs from milk"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 4.9, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "pass", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": "C", "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": "C", "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": null, "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"global_cognition": "C", "episodic_memory": "C", "executive_function": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 13, "n_studies": 28, "n_verified": 28, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 10, "n_systematic_reviews": 5, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["harm", "inconsistent", "insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "dash-diet", "name": "DASH diet", "category": "K", "category_name": "Dietary patterns", "aliases": ["Dietary Approaches to Stop Hypertension", "DASH eating plan", "DASH-Sodium (DASH combined with sodium reduction)"], "food_sources": ["fruits and vegetables (about 8-10 servings/day in the original feeding trial)", "low-fat dairy products (2-3 servings/day)", "whole grains", "nuts, seeds and legumes", "fish and poultry; limited red meat", "reduced saturated fat, total fat, sweets and sugar-sweetened beverages", "reduced sodium (DASH-Sodium tested about 3.3 g, 2.4 g and 1.5 g sodium/day levels)"], "active_constituents": ["whole-diet pattern rich in potassium, magnesium, calcium and fibre, and low in sodium, saturated fat and added sugar; the blood-pressure effect is attributed to the combined electrolyte and food profile, not to one constituent"], "one_line_verdict": "DASH reliably lowers blood pressure within weeks, and lower blood pressure plausibly protects the ageing brain, but direct evidence that DASH improves cognition is limited to small, mostly older or hypertensive samples where effects were strongest when combined with exercise and weight loss, and cohort associations with dementia are inconsistent, with several large null studies.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. The DASH pattern's primary effect is on blood pressure, demonstrated in controlled feeding trials. In 459 adults with high-normal or stage 1 blood pressure, 8 weeks of the DASH combination diet at constant weight and sodium lowered systolic and diastolic pressure by 5.5 and 3.0 mm Hg more than a typical American control diet (11.4/5.5 mm Hg in hypertensives) [appel1997]. Combined with low sodium, systolic pressure fell 7.1 mm Hg in normotensives and 11.5 mm Hg in hypertensives versus a high-sodium control [sacks2001]. Whether lower blood pressure protects cognition is supported indirectly: intensive drug-based BP control reduced incident MCI (HR 0.81, 0.69-0.95) but not probable dementia (HR 0.83, 0.67-1.04) in SPRINT MIND [williamson2019], a pharmacological rather than dietary test. Direct cognitive trials of DASH are few and small. In ENCORE (n=124 overweight adults with prehypertension or stage 1 hypertension, mean age 52, 4 months), DASH alone improved psychomotor speed (d=0.44, p=0.036) but not executive function-memory-learning; larger gains appeared only when DASH was combined with exercise and caloric restriction (d=0.56) [smith2010]. In ENLIGHTEN (n=160 sedentary adults over 55 with cognitive impairment without dementia, 6 months), DASH did not significantly improve executive function (d=0.30, p=0.059), aerobic exercise did (d=0.32), and the combination did best (d=0.40) [blumenthal2019]; at 1-year follow-up the DASH effect remained marginal (p=0.054) [blumenthal2020]. Observational data are mixed. The Chicago Memory and Aging Project found slower decline per DASH point (0.007 SD/year) [tangney2014], and Cache County reported about 1 point higher 3MS in the top quintile over 11 years [wengreen2013]. In the Nurses' Health Study, long-term DASH adherence was associated with slightly better cognitive level (0.04 SD) but not slower decline [berendsen2017]. WHIMS (n=6425, 9 years) [haring2016], MESA (n=4169) [daniel2021] and ARIC (n=13,630, 27 years) [hu2020] found no association with cognitive decline, performance or dementia. Midlife DASH adherence was associated with fewer subjective cognitive complaints decades later (OR 0.83) [song2024], a self-report outcome. In whom. All cognitive data concern middle-aged or older adults, mostly with elevated blood pressure or overweight. No study in healthy young adults or students was found. How reproducible. BP effects are highly reproducible; cognitive effects are small, confounded by co-interventions and not consistently replicated. A 2025 Cochrane review rates evidence on hard cardiovascular outcomes as low to very low and notes minimal reported adverse effects [bensaaud2025].", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "DASH works by correcting a common dietary imbalance: high sodium and low potassium, magnesium, calcium and fibre intake. BP reductions are largest in hypertensives (11.4 mm Hg systolic) and smaller in normotensives (3.5 mm Hg) [appel1997]. The cognitive rationale is therefore risk-factor correction: lowering vascular burden over decades. Small cognitive gains appear in overweight and hypertensive or cognitively impaired adults, especially with weight loss and exercise [smith2010, blumenthal2019]. For normotensive, healthy young adults there is no evidence of cognitive enhancement, and none is expected over a semester. For Moroccan consumers, the relevant 'deficiency' is high salt intake and sweet tea; the brain-health case is long-term hypertension prevention.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "DASH is a medically endorsed blood-pressure diet; that is its legitimate positioning. Claims that DASH 'sharpens the mind' or 'prevents dementia' exceed the evidence: cognitive trials are small, mostly confounded with exercise and weight loss, and several large cohorts are null [haring2016, daniel2021, hu2020]. Brain Food could credibly support low-sodium, potassium-rich products under authorised blood-pressure claims (EU: potassium 'contributes to the maintenance of normal blood pressure'; US: potassium/sodium health claim) but should not bridge from blood pressure to cognition in marketing. Any implicit 'healthy blood pressure = sharper brain' message would be an unauthorised health claim in the EU.", "mechanism": {"summary": "DASH lowers blood pressure via higher potassium, magnesium, calcium and fibre and lower sodium and saturated fat. Chronic hypertension damages small cerebral vessels (white-matter hyperintensities, microinfarcts) and is a major risk factor for vascular cognitive impairment and dementia. Lowering BP, especially in midlife, is therefore a plausible route to preserving cognition. Short-term cognitive effects, where reported, appear to be mediated by improved fitness and weight loss rather than diet alone [smith2010].", "pathway_map": [{"step": "DASH food pattern -> higher potassium, magnesium, calcium, fibre; lower sodium and saturated fat", "detail": "Controlled feeding achieves this precisely [appel1997, sacks2001]; free-living counselling achieves it partially [blumenthal2019].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Nutrient profile -> lower systolic and diastolic blood pressure within 2-8 weeks", "detail": "-5.5/-3.0 mm Hg overall, -11.4/-5.5 mm Hg in hypertensives [appel1997]; additive with sodium reduction [sacks2001].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Lower blood pressure -> less cerebral small-vessel disease and lower MCI risk", "detail": "Shown with intensive antihypertensive drug therapy (MCI HR 0.81) [williamson2019]; not shown directly for DASH.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Combined DASH with weight loss and exercise -> improved aerobic fitness and insulin sensitivity", "detail": "Cognitive gains in ENCORE were mediated by fitness and weight loss [smith2010].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Measured human cognitive outcome", "detail": "Psychomotor speed d=0.44 with DASH alone [smith2010]; executive function NS with DASH alone [blumenthal2019]; cohorts inconsistent; no young-adult data.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["blood pressure reduction (sodium/potassium balance)", "endothelial function", "cerebral small-vessel disease prevention (hypothesised for DASH)", "weight loss and fitness when combined with exercise", "lipid improvement (total cholesterol, triglycerides)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "vascular_health", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Adults with high-normal BP or stage 1 hypertension (DASH, DASH-Sodium controlled feeding trials, USA)", "dose_or_intake": "DASH combination diet, constant weight, with or without sodium reduction", "duration": "8 weeks; 30 days per sodium level", "grade": "A", "direction": "benefit", "effect_summary": "DASH lowered systolic BP 5.5 mm Hg overall and 11.4 mm Hg in hypertensives; DASH plus low sodium lowered systolic BP 7.1 (normotensive) and 11.5 mm Hg (hypertensive) vs high-sodium control.", "effect_estimate": "SBP -5.5 mm Hg, DBP -3.0 mm Hg vs control (p<0.001) [appel1997]; SBP -11.5 mm Hg (hypertensive, DASH low sodium vs control high sodium) [sacks2001]", "certainty_notes": "Large, rigorously controlled feeding RCTs, replicated, consistent; this is a vascular surrogate, not a cognitive outcome.", "key_study_ids": ["appel1997", "sacks2001", "bensaaud2025"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "processing_speed", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Sedentary overweight/obese adults (mean age 52) with prehypertension or stage 1 hypertension (ENCORE, USA)", "dose_or_intake": "DASH diet counselling alone; or DASH plus weight management (exercise and caloric restriction)", "duration": "4 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "DASH alone improved psychomotor speed vs usual diet; DASH plus weight management improved executive function-memory-learning and psychomotor speed; DASH alone did not improve executive function-memory-learning.", "effect_estimate": "DASH alone psychomotor speed d=0.44, p=0.036; EFML d=0.26, NS; DASH+WM EFML d=0.56, p=0.008 [smith2010]", "certainty_notes": "Single trial, n=124 (about 38-43 per arm), multiple outcomes, unblinded diet; effect confounded by weight loss in the combined arm. NIH-funded.", "key_study_ids": ["smith2010"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "older_adults_mci", "population_detail": "Sedentary adults over 55 (mean 65) with cognitive impairment without dementia and CVD risk factors (ENLIGHTEN)", "dose_or_intake": "DASH counselling, factorial with aerobic exercise", "duration": "6 months (+1-year follow-up)", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "DASH factor effect on executive function not significant (d=0.30, p=0.059); aerobic exercise significant; AE+DASH largest (d=0.40 vs health education). No memory or fluency effect. At 1 year, DASH remained marginal (p=0.054).", "effect_estimate": "DASH d=0.30, p=0.059; AE+DASH d=0.40, p=0.012 [blumenthal2019]; 1-year DASH p=0.054 [blumenthal2020]", "certainty_notes": "Well-designed but small factorial RCT; DASH effect imprecise and not significant; impaired population (not generalisable to healthy adults).", "key_study_ids": ["blumenthal2019", "blumenthal2020"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_decline", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Older adults in prospective cohorts (Chicago MAP, Cache County, Nurses' Health Study, WHIMS, MESA)", "dose_or_intake": "DASH adherence score (various)", "duration": "4-11 years", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Positive in MAP (slower decline) and Cache County (higher 3MS level); NHS associated with level but not decline; WHIMS and MESA null for decline and performance.", "effect_estimate": "0.007 SD/year slower decline per point [tangney2014]; +0.97 3MS points Q5 vs Q1 [wengreen2013]; 0.04 SD level, no decline effect [berendsen2017]; WHIMS p trend 0.23 [haring2016]; MESA null [daniel2021]", "certainty_notes": "Observational, inconsistent, small effects, confounding by SES/education; 'level' associations are cross-sectional in nature.", "key_study_ids": ["tangney2014", "wengreen2013", "berendsen2017", "haring2016", "daniel2021"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "dementia_risk", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Middle-aged (ARIC, mean follow-up 27 years) and older adults (Rush MAP)", "dose_or_intake": "DASH score quintiles/tertiles", "duration": "4.5-27 years", "grade": "D", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Rush MAP: top DASH tertile AD HR 0.61; ARIC (2352 cases) and WHIMS: no significant association with dementia or MCI/probable dementia.", "effect_estimate": "AD HR 0.61 (95% CI 0.38-0.97) [morris2015ad]; ARIC DASH not significant [hu2020]; WHIMS not significant [haring2016]", "certainty_notes": "Largest and longest cohort is null; positive result from a small cohort; observational. Grade D (inconsistent, preliminary).", "key_study_ids": ["hu2020", "morris2015ad", "haring2016"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "other", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Women (mean age 46 at baseline) followed to late life (NYU Women's Health Study); subjective cognitive complaints", "dose_or_intake": "Midlife DASH score, top vs bottom quartile", "duration": "about 30 years", "grade": "D", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Midlife DASH adherence associated with fewer late-life subjective cognitive complaints; subjective outcome, not measured cognition.", "effect_estimate": "OR 0.83 (95% CI 0.70-0.99) for 2+ complaints [song2024]", "certainty_notes": "Observational, self-reported outcome, single cohort; a borderline CI.", "key_study_ids": ["song2024"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy young adults and students", "dose_or_intake": "not studied", "duration": "not studied", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No DASH cognitive trial or cohort in healthy young adults or students was located. BP benefits in normotensive young people are small (DASH lowered SBP 3.5 mm Hg in normotensives in the mixed-age feeding trial).", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "Absence of evidence; long-term vascular rationale only.", "key_study_ids": ["appel1997"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "resistance_to_decline", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Hypertensive adults 50+ without diabetes or stroke (SPRINT MIND); intensive antihypertensive drug therapy, not diet", "dose_or_intake": "SBP target below 120 vs below 140 mm Hg (drugs)", "duration": "median 3.34 years intervention; 5.11 years follow-up", "grade": "B", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Intensive BP control reduced MCI and MCI-or-dementia but not probable dementia; supports the BP-cognition link that DASH would act through, but is indirect for DASH.", "effect_estimate": "MCI HR 0.81 (0.69-0.95); probable dementia HR 0.83 (0.67-1.04) [williamson2019]", "certainty_notes": "Large RCT (A-quality for drug BP lowering) downgraded for indirectness to a dietary pattern; early stopping limited dementia power.", "key_study_ids": ["williamson2019"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "not applicable (dietary pattern)", "onset": "BP effect within 2 weeks of feeding; cognitive acute effects not studied", "duration_studied": "none for cognition", "domain": "any cognitive domain", "population": "any", "effect_estimate": "No acute or short-term (days) cognitive trial of DASH was located.", "certainty": "insufficient", "safety": "not applicable", "study_ids": ["appel1997"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "8 weeks (feeding); 30 days per sodium level", "population": "adults with high-normal BP or stage 1 hypertension", "outcome": "systolic and diastolic BP", "effect_estimate": "-5.5/-3.0 mm Hg; -11.4/-5.5 mm Hg in hypertensives; -11.5 mm Hg with low sodium (hypertensives)", "evidence_quality": "high (A)", "limitations": "Controlled feeding; free-living adherence lower", "study_ids": ["appel1997", "sacks2001"]}, {"duration": "4 months", "population": "overweight adults with high BP, mean age 52", "outcome": "psychomotor speed; executive function-memory-learning", "effect_estimate": "DASH alone speed d=0.44; DASH+WM EFML d=0.56", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Single small trial; co-interventions", "study_ids": ["smith2010"]}, {"duration": "6 months + 1-year follow-up", "population": "older adults with CIND", "outcome": "executive function", "effect_estimate": "DASH d=0.30 (p=0.059); AE+DASH d=0.40", "evidence_quality": "low-moderate", "limitations": "Small; DASH effect NS; impaired population", "study_ids": ["blumenthal2019", "blumenthal2020"]}, {"duration": "4-27 years follow-up", "population": "middle-aged and older adults in cohorts", "outcome": "cognitive decline and dementia", "effect_estimate": "Mixed: slower decline in MAP; null in WHIMS, MESA, ARIC", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Confounding; heterogeneous scores", "study_ids": ["tangney2014", "haring2016", "daniel2021", "hu2020"]}], "safety": {"summary": "DASH is a food-based pattern endorsed for hypertension, with minimal reported adverse effects; long-term safety data from trials are limited (Cochrane 2025) [bensaaud2025]. The main clinical caution is potassium load in people with advanced chronic kidney disease or on drugs that raise potassium (ACE inhibitors, ARBs, potassium-sparing diuretics, some NSAID combinations). Sodium reduction combined with antihypertensives can cause symptomatic hypotension in some older people. High dairy content can be problematic for lactose-intolerant people (common in North Africa).", "adverse_effects": ["Hyperkalaemia risk in advanced CKD or with potassium-retaining medications", "Bloating or gastrointestinal discomfort from increased fibre and dairy (lactose intolerance)", "Possible dizziness/hypotension when BP falls in people already on antihypertensives"], "contraindications": ["Advanced chronic kidney disease or dialysis without dietitian supervision", "Hyperkalaemia history"], "drug_interactions": ["ACE inhibitors, angiotensin receptor blockers, spironolactone/eplerenone and other potassium-sparing diuretics: additive potassium retention", "Antihypertensives: additive BP lowering (may require dose review)", "Lithium: changes in sodium intake alter lithium levels (clinician monitoring needed)"], "upper_limits": "No limit for the pattern; potassium from foods is safe in healthy kidneys; WHO sodium target below 2 g/day.", "sleep_impact": "None expected.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Partly: feeding trials of 8-12 weeks; counselling trials up to 12-18 months; cohorts over decades (observational).", "daily_use_appropriate": "yes", "special_populations": "CKD and potassium-retaining medications: medical/dietitian supervision. Pregnancy: suitable as a healthy pattern; no sodium restriction below recommended intake without advice. Children/adolescents: suitable as a food pattern; no cognitive claims. Lithium users: sodium changes require psychiatric monitoring.", "specialist_review_needed": ["Nephrologist or renal dietitian for CKD", "Prescriber review for people on RAAS inhibitors, potassium-sparing diuretics or lithium"], "hard_safety_gate": "pass", "gate_reason": "Food pattern with established safety in the general population; conditional caution for CKD and potassium-retaining or lithium medications should be stated on product information if potassium-enriched or salt-substitute products are made."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "No claim exists for the DASH pattern. Relevant authorised nutrient claims (EU Register, Regulation (EU) 432/2012) include potassium 'contributes to the maintenance of normal blood pressure' (source of potassium required) and reduction of sodium consumption contributing to maintenance of normal blood pressure (for foods meeting low-sodium/salt conditions). Exact wording and conditions were not re-verified in this unit and should be checked in the EU Register. No cognitive claim is authorised. 'Low sodium/salt' nutrition claims (Annex to Regulation 1924/2006) are available.", "us": "FDA authorised health claim (21 CFR 101.74, sodium and hypertension) is not re-verified here. An FDAMA authoritative-statement claim for potassium-containing foods (2000, Tropicana notification): 'Diets containing foods that are a good source of potassium and that are low in sodium may reduce the risk of high blood pressure and stroke' (eligibility: at least 350 mg potassium and 140 mg or less sodium per serving), per FDA notification page found 2026-10-25. Current status should be confirmed. No cognitive claim is supportable; dementia claims are disease claims.", "morocco": "No DASH-specific regulation; labelling and claims under Law 28-07/ONSSA. Morocco has a national salt-reduction agenda (not verified in this unit). Claim provisions are uncertain.", "permitted_claims_examples": ["EU (source of potassium): 'Potassium contributes to the maintenance of normal blood pressure' (confirm conditions in EU Register)", "US (FDAMA notification, eligible foods): 'Diets containing foods that are a good source of potassium and that are low in sodium may reduce the risk of high blood pressure and stroke' (confirm current status)"], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "None for conventional foods. Potassium-enriched salt substitutes are regulated as food additives/ingredients and carry hyperkalaemia warnings in some jurisdictions (not assessed here).", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Excellent fit for reformulation strategy: low-sodium breads, legume dishes, vegetable-rich ready meals, unsalted nuts, yoghurt-based products. For Morocco, sodium reduction in bread and preserved foods, and potassium-rich vegetable and legume dishes, are the most relevant levers.", "formats": ["reduced-sodium bread and khobz", "vegetable and legume ready meals (low sodium)", "unsalted nut and seed snacks", "plain low-fat yoghurt and fermented milk (lben) products", "fruit-based snacks without added sugar"], "stability": "Sodium reduction affects bread texture, preservation and taste; salt replacers (potassium chloride) can taste bitter and raise potassium-safety issues.", "taste": "Sodium reduction lowers palatability initially; gradual reduction (stepwise) is better accepted.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Whole food; not applicable.", "dose_feasibility": "The studied intervention is a whole diet (8-10 fruit and vegetable servings, 2-3 dairy servings). Products can contribute but not deliver the full pattern; sodium reduction across staple foods is feasible.", "cost_availability": "Fruits, vegetables and legumes are available and relatively affordable in Morocco; low-fat dairy is common; DASH does not depend on expensive specialty items.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 3, "effect_magnitude": 3, "safety": 8, "reproducibility": 3, "food_suitability": 8, "cost_access": 7, "rationale": "Evidence quality 3 (for cognition): grade C for small RCTs in hypertensive/overweight or CIND adults, C/D for inconsistent cohorts, D/insufficient for young adults. Vascular evidence alone would be about 9, but this is a surrogate. Effect magnitude 3: psychomotor speed d=0.44 in one small trial, executive effects NS; cohort associations trivial (0.04 SD) or null. Safety 8: food pattern with caveats for CKD and potassium-retaining drugs. Reproducibility 3: BP effects highly reproducible; cognitive effects not replicated, and large cohorts null. Food suitability 8: reformulation-friendly. Cost/access 7: built on staple foods."}, "research_gaps": ["Any DASH trial with cognitive outcomes in healthy normotensive young adults or students", "Adequately powered trials of DASH alone (without weight loss or exercise) on cognition in midlife hypertensives", "Trials linking DASH-induced BP reduction to MRI small-vessel outcomes and cognition", "Culturally adapted DASH for North Africa (lactose intolerance, bread-based sodium) with BP and cognitive endpoints"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 4.9, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "pass", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": "B", "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": "A", "best_grade_overall": "B", "benefit_domains": {"processing_speed": "C", "resistance_to_decline": "B"}, "n_outcome_rows": 8, "n_studies": 16, "n_verified": 16, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 7, "n_systematic_reviews": 1, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["benefit", "inconsistent", "insufficient", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "high-protein-dietary-patterns", "name": "High-protein dietary patterns", "category": "K", "category_name": "Dietary patterns", "aliases": ["high-protein diet", "protein-rich meals", "protein-rich breakfast", "higher protein-to-carbohydrate ratio", "whey protein supplementation", "dietary protein intake"], "food_sources": ["eggs, dairy (milk, yogurt, cheese), whey", "fish and seafood, poultry, lean meat", "legumes and pulses, soy, tofu", "nuts and seeds", "protein powders (whey, pea, soy)"], "active_constituents": ["protein and its constituent amino acids (tyrosine, tryptophan and other large neutral amino acids, leucine)", "co-nutrients in protein foods (B12, iron, zinc, choline, omega-3 in fish, creatine in meat/fish)"], "one_line_verdict": "Adequate protein matters for older adults' general health and higher protein intake is weakly associated with less subjective cognitive decline, but in healthy young adults a high-protein diet or protein-rich meal has not been shown to improve cognition (the best controlled trial, 0.8 vs 1.6 vs 2.4 g/kg/day for 31 days, was null) and acute meal studies are tiny and inconsistent; protein is a satiety and meal-quality tool, not a brain booster.", "scientific_conclusion": "Acute meals in young adults. The evidence that a protein-rich meal changes cognition within hours comes from two small Swiss crossover studies in 15 male students: pure protein, carbohydrate or fat creams of equal energy produced different patterns, with protein linked to better attention and efficiency but lower overall performance than fat [fischer2001], and a protein-rich (1:4 carbohydrate:protein) meal produced the best short-term memory accuracy while a carbohydrate-rich meal transiently improved attention in the first hour [fischer2002]. In 22 healthy older adults a 300 mL whey drink (774 kJ) improved delayed paragraph recall at 15 min versus a non-energy placebo, as did glucose and fat, suggesting that energy intake rather than protein per se mattered [kaplan2001]. These studies are small, multiple-outcome, not preregistered, compare unrealistic single-macronutrient foods and are not replicated; grade C at most for acute effects, with direction inconsistent. Chronic intake in young adults. The best-controlled trial randomised 39 non-obese young adults to 0.8, 1.6 or 2.4 g/kg/day protein (carbohydrate reduced to compensate) for 31 days including 21 days of 40% energy deficit; there was no effect of protein-to-carbohydrate ratio on cognitive performance, mood or sleep [karl2015]. Eight weeks of whey plus leucine in 30 fit adults improved upper-body strength and lean mass but not cognition [walker2010]. Older adults. A meta-analysis of nine cross-sectional studies (4,929 adults aged 60+) found no association between protein intake and global cognition, with positive associations for memory in three studies [coelhojunior2021]. In 77,335 US health professionals, substituting 5% of energy from carbohydrate with protein was associated with lower odds of subjective cognitive decline (OR 0.89, 0.85-0.94), more strongly for plant protein (OR 0.74, 0.62-0.88) [yeh2022]; a Chinese cohort linked declining animal-protein intake to 48% higher cognitive-impairment risk [xu2022], and a cross-sectional analysis linked low protein intake to higher brain amyloid [fernando2018]. These are observational and partly cross-sectional; reverse causation (people with early decline eat less) is likely. Trials in older adults are mixed: 24 weeks of 2 x 15 g protein drinks in 65 frail elders improved reaction time (68 vs 18 ms) but no cognitive domain [vanderzwaluw2014]; an industry-funded 12-month trial of a whey powder enriched with milk fat globule membrane, taurine and B vitamins in 107 adults with MCI improved MoCA by 1.81 points (0.32-3.30) [li2025], but the multi-ingredient design means protein cannot be isolated. Safety. In healthy adults, higher protein intakes (>=1.5 g/kg or >=20% energy) do not adversely change glomerular filtration rate [devries2018], and an umbrella review found no evidence that higher protein triggers kidney disease or stones, while noting the lack of decades-long data [remer2023]. EFSA set the adult PRI at 0.83 g/kg/day and considered intakes up to about twice the PRI safe [efsa2012]. Practical relevance. For a student or knowledge worker, adequate protein spread across meals supports satiety and stable post-meal glycaemia; the cognitive literature does not justify 'high-protein for focus' claims.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Protein-energy malnutrition and frailty in older adults are the settings in which more protein plausibly matters, and even there trials show at most reaction-time or multi-ingredient effects [vanderzwaluw2014, li2025]. In adequately nourished young adults (who usually already exceed 0.83 g/kg/day), raising protein to 1.6-2.4 g/kg/day for a month did not change cognition, mood or sleep [karl2015], and whey supplementation did not change cognition [walker2010]. Acute meal studies compare unusual single-macronutrient foods and show energy intake, not protein specifically, can transiently affect memory [kaplan2001, fischer2001, fischer2002]. Observational associations in middle-aged and older adults [yeh2022, xu2022] may reflect overall diet quality and reverse causation rather than protein per se.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "'High-protein for brain power', 'protein breakfast for focus' and 'protein prevents memory loss' claims are unsupported. The only EU-authorised protein claims concern muscle and bone ('Protein contributes to a growth in muscle mass', 'to the maintenance of muscle mass', 'to the maintenance of normal bones'), and the 'high protein' nutrition claim requires >=20% of energy from protein. An honest Brain Food position: protein-adequate meals (about 20-30 g protein per main meal) help satiety and steadier energy, and protein foods such as fish, eggs, dairy and legumes bring brain-relevant micronutrients (B12, iodine, choline, omega-3, iron); there is no evidence that more protein than needed improves thinking.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Protein supplies large neutral amino acids (LNAA), including tyrosine (catecholamine precursor) and tryptophan (serotonin precursor), which compete for brain transport; protein-rich meals lower the tryptophan:LNAA ratio and blunt postprandial glycaemic swings compared with carbohydrate-rich meals. Chronically, protein maintains muscle mass, which is linked to frailty and cognitive ageing. None of these mechanisms has been shown to produce a reliable cognitive benefit in healthy adults.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Dietary protein -> digestion to amino acids -> plasma LNAA rise", "detail": "Protein meals change plasma Tyr:LNAA and Trp:LNAA ratios [fischer2002].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Altered LNAA competition at blood-brain barrier -> precursor supply for dopamine/noradrenaline and serotonin", "detail": "Plausible from pharmacology of tyrosine/tryptophan loading; dietary-protein-induced changes in brain neurotransmitter synthesis not directly measured in these trials (see l-tyrosine and tryptophan monographs).", "evidence_level": "plausible"}, {"step": "Reduced postprandial glycaemic and insulin swings vs carbohydrate-rich meals", "detail": "Fischer studies relate stable glucose metabolism and glucagon:insulin ratio to cognitive patterns [fischer2001, fischer2002].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Chronic protein adequacy -> muscle mass maintenance -> lower frailty -> possibly slower cognitive ageing", "detail": "Muscle-brain axis hypotheses reviewed [glenn2019]; low protein intake cross-sectionally linked to brain amyloid [fernando2018].", "evidence_level": "speculative"}, {"step": "Measured human cognitive outcome", "detail": "Null in young adults over 31 days [karl2015] and 8 weeks [walker2010]; acute small inconsistent effects [fischer2001, fischer2002, kaplan2001]; observational associations in older adults [yeh2022, coelhojunior2021].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["large neutral amino acid competition (tyrosine, tryptophan)", "postprandial glycaemia and insulin modulation", "satiety hormones", "muscle-brain axis (speculative)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "students", "population_detail": "15 healthy male university students, morning after overnight fast", "dose_or_intake": "Isoenergetic (1670 kJ) pure-protein or protein-rich (1:4 CHO:protein) test meals", "duration": "3-3.5 h postprandial", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Protein-rich meals linked to better attention/efficiency and memory accuracy after the first hour, but pure protein produced lower overall performance than fat; carbohydrate meals transiently better in first hour.", "effect_estimate": "not reported as standardized effects (abstract-level P values only)", "certainty_notes": "Two small studies from one group, same n=15, many tasks, single-macronutrient foods; unreplicated. Downgraded for imprecision, risk of bias and indirectness.", "key_study_ids": ["fischer2001", "fischer2002"]}, {"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "22 healthy adults aged 61-79 after overnight fast", "dose_or_intake": "300 mL whey drink, 774 kJ", "duration": "15-60 min", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Whey, glucose and fat drinks all improved delayed paragraph recall vs non-energy placebo at 15 min; whey reduced forgetting rate.", "effect_estimate": "P<0.001 for macronutrients vs placebo on delayed recall; magnitude not reported in abstract", "certainty_notes": "Single small study; effect not specific to protein (energy effect); fasted design limits relevance.", "key_study_ids": ["kaplan2001"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Non-obese young adults (21 y), including 21 days of 40% energy deficit; fit adults on whey", "dose_or_intake": "0.8 vs 1.6 vs 2.4 g/kg/day protein; or 19.7 g whey + 6.2 g leucine/day", "duration": "31 days; 8 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "No effect of protein-to-carbohydrate ratio on cognitive performance, mood or sleep; whey+leucine improved strength but not cognition.", "effect_estimate": "Null (no significant diet effect) [karl2015, walker2010]", "certainty_notes": "Two small RCTs (n=39, n=30) consistently null; imprecise, so small effects cannot be excluded. Grade C for no_benefit.", "key_study_ids": ["karl2015", "walker2010"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_decline", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "US health professionals (NHS women, HPFS men), middle-aged to older", "dose_or_intake": "Substitution of 5% energy from carbohydrate with protein", "duration": "~20 years of repeated FFQs", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Higher protein vs carbohydrate associated with lower odds of subjective cognitive decline, strongest for plant protein; beans/legumes, fish, lean poultry protective, hot dogs adverse.", "effect_estimate": "OR 0.89 (0.85-0.94) total protein; 0.74 (0.62-0.88) plant protein [yeh2022]", "certainty_notes": "Observational; subjective outcome; confounded by diet quality; food-source pattern suggests diet quality rather than protein quantity.", "key_study_ids": ["yeh2022", "xu2022"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Adults aged 60+ in cross-sectional studies; frail elderly in RCT", "dose_or_intake": "Habitual protein intake; 2 x 15 g protein drink/day", "duration": "Cross-sectional; 24 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Meta-analysis found no association of protein intake with global cognition (memory positively associated in 3 studies); frail-elderly RCT improved reaction time only.", "effect_estimate": "Global cognition pooled association not significant [coelhojunior2021]; reaction time improved 68 vs 18 ms (P=0.03), no domain effects [vanderzwaluw2014]", "certainty_notes": "Cross-sectional data; one small RCT; reaction time is a single exploratory outcome.", "key_study_ids": ["coelhojunior2021", "vanderzwaluw2014"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_mci", "population_detail": "Chinese adults with MCI, mean age 63", "dose_or_intake": "15 g/day whey powder enriched with MFGM, taurine and B vitamins", "duration": "12 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "MoCA improved more than placebo; DSST also improved.", "effect_estimate": "MoCA MD 1.81 (95% CI 0.32-3.30); DSST MD 2.72 (0.20-5.23) [li2025]", "certainty_notes": "Single industry-funded trial (BYHEALTH employees co-authors); multi-ingredient product so protein effect not isolable; MCI population not generalisable to healthy adults.", "key_study_ids": ["li2025"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "neuroplasticity_biomarkers", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Cognitively normal older adults (AIBL)", "dose_or_intake": "Tertiles of self-reported protein intake", "duration": "Cross-sectional", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "Lower protein tertiles associated with higher odds of high brain amyloid on PET; biomarker only.", "effect_estimate": "OR 12.59 (lowest vs highest tertile) for high amyloid, very wide implied uncertainty [fernando2018]", "certainty_notes": "Cross-sectional, n=162 PET, implausibly large OR suggests imprecision; biomarker not cognition.", "key_study_ids": ["fernando2018"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "1670 kJ protein-rich or pure-protein meal", "onset": "Within 1 h", "duration_studied": "3-3.5 h", "domain": "attention, short-term memory, reaction time", "population": "15 male students", "effect_estimate": "Inconsistent direction by task and time; P values only", "certainty": "C", "safety": "No issues", "study_ids": ["fischer2001", "fischer2002"]}, {"dose": "774 kJ whey drink", "onset": "15 min", "duration_studied": "60 min", "domain": "episodic memory", "population": "22 healthy older adults", "effect_estimate": "Improved delayed recall vs placebo (P<0.001), similar to glucose and fat", "certainty": "C", "safety": "No issues", "study_ids": ["kaplan2001"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "31 days", "population": "Non-obese young adults, energy balance then deficit", "outcome": "Cognitive performance, mood, sleep", "effect_estimate": "No effect of 0.8 vs 1.6 vs 2.4 g/kg/day", "evidence_quality": "C (small RCT)", "limitations": "n=39, military research context", "study_ids": ["karl2015"]}, {"duration": "8 weeks", "population": "Moderately fit adults", "outcome": "Computerised cognition", "effect_estimate": "No difference vs placebo", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "n=30; cognition secondary", "study_ids": ["walker2010"]}, {"duration": "24 weeks", "population": "Frail elderly", "outcome": "Cognitive domains; reaction time", "effect_estimate": "Reaction time improved; domains null", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "n=65; exploratory", "study_ids": ["vanderzwaluw2014"]}, {"duration": "12 months", "population": "MCI", "outcome": "MoCA", "effect_estimate": "+1.81 points vs placebo", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "Industry-funded, multi-ingredient", "study_ids": ["li2025"]}, {"duration": "~20 y (observational)", "population": "US health professionals", "outcome": "Subjective cognitive decline", "effect_estimate": "OR 0.89 per 5% energy protein replacing carbohydrate", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "Self-report, confounding", "study_ids": ["yeh2022"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Higher-protein diets (up to ~1.5-2 g/kg/day) appear safe for adults with healthy kidneys; GFR rises physiologically without evidence of harm. Risks arise in chronic kidney disease, from protein powders (contamination, label inaccuracy) and from red/processed-meat-heavy patterns that carry cardiometabolic and cancer risks.", "adverse_effects": ["gastrointestinal discomfort/bloating with large whey or plant-protein doses", "displacement of fibre-rich carbohydrate foods if protein replaces them", "higher processed/red meat intake if protein comes mainly from those sources (associated with adverse outcomes; hot dogs associated with more SCD in yeh2022)", "contamination risk (heavy metals) in some protein powders (not researched in detail here)"], "contraindications": ["chronic kidney disease (protein restriction usually advised; nephrologist decision)", "inborn errors of amino acid metabolism (e.g. phenylketonuria)", "advanced liver disease with hepatic encephalopathy (specialist management)"], "drug_interactions": ["levodopa: dietary protein (LNAA) competes with absorption and brain uptake; Parkinson's patients may need protein redistribution", "milk/whey calcium can reduce absorption of tetracyclines, fluoroquinolones and levothyroxine if taken together"], "upper_limits": "No formal UL. EFSA (2012) adult PRI 0.83 g/kg/day; intakes up to about twice the PRI considered safe [efsa2012]. EU 'high protein' nutrition claim requires >=20% energy from protein.", "sleep_impact": "No effect of 0.8-2.4 g/kg/day on self-reported sleep in young adults [karl2015].", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Trials up to ~2 years for kidney function; decades in cohorts; no decades-long RCT.", "daily_use_appropriate": "yes", "special_populations": "CKD, diabetes with nephropathy, Parkinson's disease on levodopa, pregnancy (adequacy rather than excess), children (no rationale for high protein), older adults (often under-consume; higher intakes recommended for sarcopenia prevention).", "specialist_review_needed": ["nephrologist/renal dietitian for CKD-risk consumers", "clinical nutritionist for protein-powder product specifications", "food-safety review of protein powder contaminants"], "hard_safety_gate": "pass", "gate_reason": "Protein-adequate meals and moderate high-protein foods are safe for healthy adults; CKD warning advisable for concentrated protein products."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Authorised Article 13(1) claims (Reg. (EU) 432/2012): 'Protein contributes to a growth in muscle mass', 'Protein contributes to the maintenance of muscle mass', 'Protein contributes to the maintenance of normal bones'; condition: food at least a 'source of protein' (>=12% of energy from protein). Nutrition claims (Reg. 1924/2006 Annex): 'source of protein' >=12% energy; 'high protein' >=20% energy. No cognitive claim exists for protein. Wording and conditions from agent knowledge of the register; not re-fetched in this unit.", "us": "Protein is a conventional nutrient; %DV for protein (50 g) requires PDCAAS-corrected declaration when a protein claim is made (21 CFR 101.9, agent knowledge, not re-verified). Whey and plant protein isolates are GRAS/common food ingredients; protein powders may be sold as foods or dietary supplements. Structure/function claims on muscle are common; 'supports focus/memory' would require FTC-level substantiation not provided by the evidence.", "morocco": "Ordinary foods/food supplements under Law 28-07 and Decree 2-12-389 (ONSSA); nutrition claim rules under Joint Order 281-16 as reported in related monographs (not re-verified); no protein-cognition claim framework found.", "permitted_claims_examples": ["EU (food at least a source of protein): 'Protein contributes to the maintenance of muscle mass'", "EU nutrition claim: 'High protein' (>=20% of energy from protein)"], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "None for conventional protein foods, whey or common plant proteins; novel protein sources (insect, mycoprotein, microalgae, precision-fermentation proteins) need individual EU novel-food/US GRAS review (not researched here).", "regulatory_gate": "pass"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "High: protein is delivered by ordinary foods (eggs, dairy, fish, legumes, nuts); protein-forward breakfasts and lunch bowls are easy to design.", "formats": ["protein-containing breakfasts (eggs, yogurt, msemen with eggs/cheese, bissara)", "lunch bowls with legumes/fish", "yogurt/skyr snacks", "protein-enriched bars or drinks (lower priority)"], "stability": "Whey and plant proteins stable in dry formats; Maillard browning and hardening in bars over shelf life.", "taste": "Whole-food protein sources well accepted; high-protein bars and plant isolates can be chalky or bitter.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Animal proteins and soy have high digestibility (PDCAAS/DIAAS); combining legumes with cereals improves amino acid profile.", "dose_feasibility": "20-30 g protein per meal is easily achievable from food servings; the trial doses (1.6-2.4 g/kg/day) are feasible but showed no cognitive benefit.", "cost_availability": "Legumes and eggs are inexpensive; whey isolates and fish more costly.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 3.5, "effect_magnitude": 1.5, "safety": 8.5, "reproducibility": 3, "food_suitability": 9, "cost_access": 8, "rationale": "Evidence quality 3.5: grade C throughout (small trials, observational associations), with the most relevant young-adult trial null. Effect magnitude 1.5: null chronic effects in healthy adults, inconsistent acute effects, small associations. Safety 8.5: safe for healthy kidneys; CKD and levodopa caveats. Reproducibility 3: acute findings from one lab unreplicated; observational signals vary by protein source. Food suitability 9 and cost/access 8: ordinary foods."}, "research_gaps": ["Preregistered crossover trials of realistic mixed breakfasts differing in protein (e.g. 10 vs 30 g) on attention and memory in students across a school morning.", "Adequately powered trials of protein intake on cognition in older adults with low baseline intake, isolating protein from co-ingredients.", "Independent replication of the whey-MFGM MCI trial without industry sponsorship.", "Protein source (plant vs animal) and cognitive ageing using objective tests and reverse-causation-robust designs."], "_derived": {"composite_score": 5.05, "eligibility": "eligible", "safety_gate": "pass", "regulatory_gate": "pass", "best_grade_acute": "C", "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": null, "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"episodic_memory": "C", "cognitive_decline": "C", "global_cognition": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 7, "n_studies": 15, "n_verified": 15, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 5, "n_systematic_reviews": 0, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["inconsistent", "insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "ketogenic-diet-chronic", "name": "Ketogenic diet (chronic)", "category": "K", "category_name": "Dietary patterns", "aliases": ["ketogenic diet", "keto", "KD", "very-low-carbohydrate diet", "low-carbohydrate high-fat (LCHF)", "modified Atkins diet (MAD)", "modified Mediterranean-ketogenic diet", "VLCKD (very-low-calorie ketogenic diet)", "nutritional ketosis"], "food_sources": ["Dietary pattern, not a food: typically <20-50 g carbohydrate/day, 60-80% energy from fat, moderate protein", "Typical foods: meat, fish, eggs, cheese, butter/cream, olive oil, avocado, nuts and seeds, non-starchy vegetables", "Excludes or severely limits bread, couscous, rice, pasta, legumes, most fruit, dates, honey, sugar", "Variants: MCT-supplemented KD; modified Atkins (~20 g net carbs); Mediterranean-ketogenic"], "active_constituents": ["Endogenous ketone bodies: D-beta-hydroxybutyrate, acetoacetate, acetone", "Lowered glucose and insulin; raised free fatty acids", "Diet composition changes (higher saturated fat in many versions, lower fibre/polyphenols from grains, legumes and fruit)"], "one_line_verdict": "A chronic ketogenic diet does not improve cognition in healthy adults (tiny null trials, plus 1-2-year RCTs showing no advantage over low-fat diets), may transiently impair attention and memory during the first days, raises LDL-cholesterol, and shows only small, fragile benefits in MCI/Alzheimer's patients and a borderline mood benefit in treatment-resistant depression - it is a medical diet, not a brain-performance strategy.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. A well-formulated ketogenic diet (roughly 20-50 g carbohydrate per day) reliably raises blood beta-hydroxybutyrate to about 0.5-1 mmol/L and shifts brain fuel use toward ketones; in healthy overweight adults three weeks of ketosis raised global cerebral blood flow by 22% and plasma BDNF by 47% [luong2025]. These are biomarker changes, not cognitive outcomes. In rested healthy adults, the only controlled trials of sustained ketosis are tiny and null: 3 weeks in 11 normal-weight adults changed no cognitive domain, mood or sleep (all d <=0.31) [iacovides2019], and 2 weeks in 8 military personnel likewise [shaw2022]. Larger, longer RCTs in overweight or diabetic adults found no cognitive advantage over low-fat diets after 1 year (n=106) [brinkworth2009] or 2 years (n=115) [kakoschke2021]; mood was worse on low-carbohydrate in one trial [brinkworth2009], better in an Atkins-linked self-report study [mcclernon2007], and no different in the 2-year trial. A 3-arm trial in obesity found keto no better than alternate-day fasting and worse than Mediterranean on one attention test [mela2025]. The induction phase carries a cost. Five days of a high-fat low-carbohydrate diet impaired attention and speed in healthy young men [holloway2011]; ketogenic weight-loss diets worsened Trail Making in week 1 [wing1995] and memory during carbohydrate withdrawal [danci2009]. For a student, the first 1-2 weeks of keto are therefore the riskiest time to start before exams. In impaired populations the signal is more positive but fragile. In MCI, small RCTs report memory gains [krikorian2012, brandt2019], but the most rigorous feasibility trial screened 839 people, randomised 38, achieved ketosis in only 2 and found a non-significant memory difference [buchholz2024]. In Alzheimer's disease the best crossover RCT found a non-significant +2.1 point ACE-III change with better daily function and quality of life [phillips2021]. Meta-analyses report global cognition SMD 0.34 and memory SMD 0.53 in impaired older adults [xi2026] and ADAS-cog -3.4 in AD [rong2024], but they pool whole diets with MCT/ketone supplements, small unblinded trials and high attrition. The ketone-as-alternative-fuel rationale is specific to brains with glucose hypometabolism [croteau2018] and does not transfer to healthy young adults. A modest antidepressant effect in treatment-resistant depression (PHQ-9 -2.2, borderline) came without cognitive change [gao2026]. Epilepsy benefit [martinmcgill2020] is a medical therapy and is unrelated to enhancement. Costs: KDs raise LDL-C (high-certainty, about +8.5 mg/dL on average) [patikorn2023, chang2026], lower intakes of magnesium, calcium, iron and water-soluble vitamins [kenig2019], can reduce muscle mass, and can cause ketoacidosis in lactation [vongeijer2015] or euglycaemic DKA in people with diabetes [dorcely2021]. Bottom line: no evidence that chronic keto improves cognition in healthy adults or students; grade C null at best.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Ketosis does not correct any deficiency; carbohydrate is not an essential nutrient but glucose is the brain's main fuel, and the rationale for ketones as a substitute applies to brains with impaired glucose metabolism (MCI, AD; glucose uptake 7-11% lower) [croteau2018] or to drug-resistant epilepsy [martinmcgill2020]. In healthy, adequately nourished young adults this is an enhancement claim, and the evidence is null [iacovides2019, shaw2022, brinkworth2009, kakoschke2021]. The diet can create deficiencies: magnesium, calcium, iron, potassium and water-soluble vitamin intakes fell below recommendations in a 12-week trial [kenig2019]. Any benefit in MCI/AD must not be generalised to students or workers.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Popular claims such as 'keto gives mental clarity', 'ketones are a superior brain fuel' or 'keto boosts focus' rest on anecdote, rodent work, biomarker studies (CBF, BDNF) [luong2025] and trials in MCI/Alzheimer's patients [krikorian2012, phillips2021]. Controlled trials in healthy adults show no cognitive gain [iacovides2019, shaw2022], long-term RCTs show no advantage over low-fat diets [brinkworth2009, kakoschke2021], and the first week can impair attention and memory [holloway2011, wing1995]. Optimistic vote-count reviews ('80% of studies favourable') [chinnameyyappan2023] mix animal and uncontrolled studies. Self-reported 'feeling sharper' is not measured cognition. No EU authorised health claim exists for ketogenic diets or ketones and cognition. Brain Food should not market keto products for brain performance, and should never imply that keto treats dementia or depression in consumer communication.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Carbohydrate restriction lowers insulin and liver glycogen, driving hepatic fatty-acid oxidation and ketogenesis; BHB and acetoacetate cross the blood-brain barrier via monocarboxylate transporters and can supply part of brain energy. Ketosis raised cerebral blood flow and plasma BDNF in a small healthy-adult trial; in AD/MCI, brain glucose uptake is reduced while ketone uptake is preserved, giving a plausible energy-rescue rationale in impaired brains only. Anti-seizure mechanisms (GABA, adenosine, KATP channels, reduced glycolysis) are established for epilepsy but separate. None of these steps has been shown to produce better cognition in healthy adults.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Carbohydrate restriction (<20-50 g/day) -> low insulin, glycogen depletion -> hepatic ketogenesis", "detail": "BHB rises to ~0.5-1.0 mmol/L within days and is sustained [iacovides2019, phillips2021, luong2025].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Circulating ketones -> MCT transport into brain -> partial replacement of glucose as fuel", "detail": "Brain acetoacetate uptake is preserved in MCI/AD while glucose uptake is 7-11% lower [croteau2018]; cerebral ketone uptake increased after MMKD (and control diet) [neth2020].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Ketosis -> increased cerebral blood flow and plasma BDNF", "detail": "+22% CBF, +47% BDNF after 3 weeks in 11 overweight adults [luong2025]; biomarker only.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Raised free fatty acids during adaptation -> transient impaired energetics/attention", "detail": "5-day high-fat diet lowered cardiac PCr/ATP and impaired attention and speed [holloway2011].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Reduced neuronal excitability (GABA, adenosine, KATP) -> seizure reduction", "detail": "Mechanisms largely animal; clinical efficacy in children with epilepsy [martinmcgill2020].", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Measured human cognitive outcome", "detail": "Null in healthy adults [iacovides2019, shaw2022]; no advantage over low-fat in overweight adults [brinkworth2009, kakoschke2021]; small inconsistent memory gains in MCI/AD [krikorian2012, buchholz2024, phillips2021].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["ketone bodies as alternative brain fuel (MCT1/MCT2 transport)", "insulin lowering / weight loss (confounder of cognitive change)", "cerebral blood flow increase", "BDNF (plasma)", "free fatty acid elevation and transient energetic stress", "gut microbiota changes", "anti-seizure neurotransmitter effects (epilepsy)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "short_term_days", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy young men (Holloway) and overweight/obese women (Wing, D'Anci) in the first days of carbohydrate withdrawal", "dose_or_intake": "High-fat (60-75% energy) diets with near-complete carbohydrate restriction", "duration": "2-7 days (induction)", "grade": "C", "direction": "harm", "effect_summary": "Three small trials found worse attention/speed (5-day high-fat diet), worse Trail Making (week 1 of ketogenic VLED) and worse memory during complete carbohydrate withdrawal, recovering after adaptation or carbohydrate reintroduction.", "effect_estimate": "Not reported as standardized effects; Holloway P<0.01 attention, P<0.001 speed", "certainty_notes": "Small (n=16-21), one non-randomised; consistent direction but imprecise; indirectness (VLED formula, non-ketotic high-fat diet). Downgraded to C.", "key_study_ids": ["holloway2011", "wing1995", "danci2009"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Rested, healthy normal-weight or slightly overweight adults 20s-40s (civilians, military)", "dose_or_intake": "Ketogenic diet ~15% or ~25 g/day carbohydrate, BHB ~1 mmol/L", "duration": "2-3 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Two crossover trials (n=11 and n=8) found no change in vigilance, memory, working memory, executive function, mood or sleep after 2-3 weeks of verified ketosis.", "effect_estimate": "All d <=0.31, all P>=0.39 [iacovides2019]; null [shaw2022]", "certainty_notes": "Very small samples cannot exclude small effects in either direction; consistent null. Indirectly supported by null larger trials in overweight adults. No trial in students.", "key_study_ids": ["iacovides2019", "shaw2022"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Overweight/obese adults, some with type 2 diabetes, aged ~45-58", "dose_or_intake": "Energy-restricted very-low-carbohydrate (14% CHO) or ketogenic diet", "duration": "3 months to 2 years", "grade": "B", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "1-year (n=106) and 2-year (n=115) RCTs found no advantage over low-fat diets for working memory or processing speed; the 3-month Gut trial found equal working-memory gains with keto and ADF vs Mediterranean but worse TMT-A with keto.", "effect_estimate": "No diet x time effect [brinkworth2009]; all P>=0.19 [kakoschke2021]", "certainty_notes": "Reasonable-size RCTs but cognition secondary, limited batteries and high attrition (47% in Kakoschke). Consistent null; downgraded one level for risk of bias.", "key_study_ids": ["brinkworth2009", "kakoschke2021", "mela2025"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Overweight/obese adults on weight-loss diets", "dose_or_intake": "Low-carbohydrate ketogenic vs low-fat diets", "duration": "~6 months to 2 years", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Brinkworth found worse mood trajectory on low-carb; McClernon (Atkins-linked, self-report) found less negative affect and hunger; Kakoschke found no difference.", "effect_estimate": "Interactions P<0.05 in opposite directions across trials", "certainty_notes": "Inconsistent; COI in the positive study; unblinded self-report.", "key_study_ids": ["brinkworth2009", "mcclernon2007", "kakoschke2021"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Adults with treatment-resistant depression", "dose_or_intake": "<30 g/day carbohydrate, prepared meals", "duration": "6 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Well-controlled RCT: PHQ-9 fell 2.2 points more than an equally supported healthy control diet at 6 weeks (borderline), not significant at 12 weeks; no difference in cognitive impairment or other secondaries.", "effect_estimate": "MD -2.18 (95% CI -4.33 to -0.03), d -0.68 [gao2026]", "certainty_notes": "Single trial, unblinded, borderline; clinical population, not generalisable to healthy consumers.", "key_study_ids": ["gao2026"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "vigilance", "population": "sleep_deprived", "population_detail": "Male military personnel kept awake 36 h after 2 weeks of diet", "dose_or_intake": "Iso-energetic KD", "duration": "14 days + 36 h wakefulness", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "KD improved PVT lapses/response times and running-memory CPT during extended wakefulness (n=7).", "effect_estimate": "P<0.05 to <0.01; effect sizes not reported", "certainty_notes": "Exploratory, n=7, many outcomes, unreplicated; downgraded for imprecision and risk of bias (would be D if not randomised).", "key_study_ids": ["henderson2023"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "older_adults_mci", "population_detail": "Older adults with MCI or MCI due to AD; subjective memory complaints", "dose_or_intake": "Very-low-carbohydrate / modified Atkins / modified Mediterranean-ketogenic diets", "duration": "6-12 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Small RCTs report memory gains (Krikorian P=0.01; Brandt per-protocol ES 0.53) but the most recent randomised feasibility trial was non-significant with only 2 of 22 adherent; meta-analysis (including ketogenic supplements) memory SMD 0.53.", "effect_estimate": "SMD 0.53 (0.10-0.96) memory; global SMD 0.34 (0.16-0.52) [xi2026]; MD 1.37 (-0.87 to 4.90) [buchholz2024]", "certainty_notes": "Small, high attrition, poor adherence, weight-loss confounding, pooling with MCT supplements; MCI only. Evidence is for an impaired population and must not be applied to healthy adults.", "key_study_ids": ["krikorian2012", "brandt2019", "buchholz2024", "neth2020", "xi2026"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Patients with Alzheimer's disease", "dose_or_intake": "Modified ketogenic or MCT-supplemented KD", "duration": "12 weeks to 15 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Best-designed crossover RCT: ACE-III +2.1 points (NS) with improved daily function and QoL; uncontrolled KDRAFT ADAS-cog -4.1 in completers; meta-analysis ADAS-cog MD -3.43.", "effect_estimate": "ACE-III +2.12 (SD 8.70), P=0.24 [phillips2021]; ADAS-cog MD -3.43 (-5.98 to -0.88) [rong2024]", "certainty_notes": "Small, unblinded, mixed interventions in meta-analyses; dropout in more severe disease. Patient evidence only.", "key_study_ids": ["phillips2021", "taylor2018", "rong2024"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cerebral_blood_flow", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Cognitively healthy overweight adults; older adults at risk of AD", "dose_or_intake": "Ketogenic diet, BHB ~0.6 mmol/L", "duration": "3-6 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "3-week KD raised global CBF by 22% and plasma BDNF by 47% (n=11); MMKD raised perfusion and ketone uptake but so did the control diet. No accompanying cognitive benefit shown.", "effect_estimate": "CBF +22% (P=0.02), BDNF +47% (P=0.04) [luong2025]", "certainty_notes": "Biomarker outcome only; tiny samples; biomarkers are not cognition and cannot support a cognitive claim.", "key_study_ids": ["luong2025", "neth2020"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "students", "population_detail": "University students / knowledge workers under academic load", "dose_or_intake": "Not studied", "duration": "Not studied", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No trial of a chronic ketogenic diet in students or knowledge workers was found. Indirect evidence (healthy-adult nulls, induction-phase impairments) does not suggest a benefit.", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "No direct evidence; extrapolation from 8-11-person trials only.", "key_study_ids": ["iacovides2019", "holloway2011"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "other", "population": "children_adolescents", "population_detail": "Children (and adults) with drug-resistant epilepsy - medical therapy, outcome = seizures", "dose_or_intake": "Classic 4:1, MCT, modified Atkins diets under medical supervision", "duration": "Months", "grade": "C", "direction": "benefit", "effect_summary": "KDs increase seizure freedom and >=50% seizure reduction in children; adult evidence uncertain; GI adverse effects common.", "effect_estimate": "Seizure reduction RR 5.80 (3.48-9.65); seizure freedom RR 3.16 (1.20-8.35) [martinmcgill2020]", "certainty_notes": "Cochrane rates low to very low certainty. This is therapeutic evidence in a disease and has no bearing on cognitive enhancement in healthy people.", "key_study_ids": ["martinmcgill2020"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "High-fat (75% energy), low-carbohydrate diet", "onset": "Days 1-5", "duration_studied": "5 days", "domain": "attention, processing speed, mood", "population": "healthy young men", "effect_estimate": "Impaired attention (P<0.01), speed (P<0.001), mood (P<0.01)", "certainty": "C (single small crossover RCT)", "safety": "Cardiac PCr/ATP -9%", "study_ids": ["holloway2011"]}, {"dose": "Ketogenic VLED or complete carbohydrate withdrawal", "onset": "48 h to 1 week", "duration_studied": "1-4 weeks", "domain": "executive function (Trail Making), memory", "population": "overweight/obese women", "effect_estimate": "Worse Trail Making in week 1; worse memory during withdrawal, recovers with adaptation/reintroduction", "certainty": "C", "safety": "'Keto flu' period; not adapted state", "study_ids": ["wing1995", "danci2009"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "2-3 weeks", "population": "Healthy adults (n=8-11)", "outcome": "Multi-domain cognition, mood, sleep", "effect_estimate": "Null (d <=0.31)", "evidence_quality": "Low (tiny crossover RCTs)", "limitations": "Underpowered; practice effects", "study_ids": ["iacovides2019", "shaw2022"]}, {"duration": "3 months", "population": "Adults with obesity (cognition n=10-17/arm)", "outcome": "Working memory, Stroop, TMT", "effect_estimate": "L&N improved equally keto vs ADF; TMT-A worse with keto vs Med", "evidence_quality": "Low", "limitations": "Small analysed subset; multiplicity", "study_ids": ["mela2025"]}, {"duration": "1 year", "population": "Overweight/obese adults (n=106)", "outcome": "Working memory, processing speed, mood", "effect_estimate": "No diet effect on cognition; mood better on low-fat", "evidence_quality": "Moderate", "limitations": "Cognition secondary", "study_ids": ["brinkworth2009"]}, {"duration": "2 years", "population": "Adults with obesity and T2D (n=115)", "outcome": "Mood, cognition, QoL", "effect_estimate": "No differences (P>=0.19)", "evidence_quality": "Moderate-low", "limitations": "47% attrition", "study_ids": ["kakoschke2021"]}, {"duration": "6-12 weeks", "population": "MCI / early AD", "outcome": "Memory", "effect_estimate": "Positive small RCTs; latest feasibility trial NS (d 0.57, CI -0.67 to 1.33)", "evidence_quality": "Low", "limitations": "Adherence, attrition, weight-loss confound", "study_ids": ["krikorian2012", "brandt2019", "buchholz2024"]}, {"duration": "12 weeks per period", "population": "Alzheimer's disease (n=26)", "outcome": "ACE-III, ADL, QoL", "effect_estimate": "ACE-III +2.1 NS; ADL +3.1, QoL +3.4", "evidence_quality": "Low-moderate", "limitations": "Unblinded patients/caregivers", "study_ids": ["phillips2021"]}, {"duration": "Median 13 weeks (umbrella)", "population": "Adults", "outcome": "LDL-C", "effect_estimate": "LDL-C increase (high certainty); +8.49 mg/dL average", "evidence_quality": "High for lipid change", "limitations": "No hard CV outcomes", "study_ids": ["patikorn2023", "chang2026"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Short-term ketogenic diets are tolerated by most healthy adults but produce a symptomatic induction phase, raise LDL-C (high certainty), reduce micronutrient intakes and can reduce lean mass. Serious events (ketoacidosis) occur in lactation, with SGLT2 inhibitors and in diabetes. Long-term (>2 y) cardiovascular safety is unknown. Not appropriate as an unsupervised consumer brain-health regimen.", "adverse_effects": ["Induction 'keto flu': headache, fatigue, irritability, impaired attention/memory in week 1 [holloway2011, wing1995, danci2009]", "Constipation, vomiting, diarrhoea (common in epilepsy trials) [martinmcgill2020]", "LDL-C and total cholesterol increase (LDL-C ~+8.5 mg/dL average; large in some individuals) [patikorn2023, chang2026]", "Reduced intakes of Mg, Ca, Fe, K, water-soluble vitamins; serum Ca fall [kenig2019]", "Lean/muscle mass loss [patikorn2023, mela2025]", "Kidney stones / hypercalciuria (children on classic KD) [martinmcgill2020]", "Possible reduced HRV (physiological stress) during induction [shaw2022]", "Ketoacidosis in lactating women [vongeijer2015]; euglycaemic DKA in diabetes on a low-carbohydrate diet [dorcely2021]"], "contraindications": ["Pregnancy and lactation", "Type 1 diabetes or insulin/sulfonylurea-treated diabetes without medical supervision", "SGLT2-inhibitor therapy", "Inborn errors of fat metabolism (carnitine deficiencies, CPT I/II, beta-oxidation defects), pyruvate carboxylase deficiency, porphyria (standard KD therapy contraindications; not individually verified here)", "Pancreatitis, liver failure, gallbladder disease", "History of or current eating disorder", "Familial hypercholesterolaemia or established cardiovascular disease without lipid monitoring", "Children and adolescents outside medical KD programmes"], "drug_interactions": ["SGLT2 inhibitors: euglycaemic ketoacidosis risk (established pharmacology/label warnings; see also [dorcely2021] for diet-associated euglycaemic DKA)", "Insulin and sulfonylureas: hypoglycaemia; dose adjustment needed", "Antihypertensives and diuretics: hypotension/electrolyte loss with natriuresis of early ketosis", "Topiramate, zonisamide, acetazolamide: additive acidosis and kidney stone risk (epilepsy practice; not verified here)", "Valproate: carnitine depletion concerns (epilepsy practice; not verified here)"], "upper_limits": "No UL; relevant limits are for components (saturated fat <10% energy per WHO/EFSA guidance). Lower carbohydrate bound for medical KD ~20 g/day.", "sleep_impact": "No change in subjective sleep over 2-3 weeks in healthy adults [iacovides2019, shaw2022]; induction insomnia anecdotally reported, not established.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Cognition/mood up to 2 years in overweight/diabetic adults [kakoschke2021]; no hard cardiovascular outcome data.", "daily_use_appropriate": "uncertain", "special_populations": "Pregnancy/lactation: avoid (ketoacidosis cases). Children: only in medically supervised epilepsy programmes. People with diabetes on glucose-lowering drugs or SGLT2i: medical supervision. Eating disorders: avoid restrictive diets. Older adults: risk of muscle loss and poor adherence/caregiver burden [taylor2018, buchholz2024]. Ramadan: combining KD with dawn-to-dusk fasting increases ketoacidosis/dehydration risk (no direct study found).", "specialist_review_needed": ["Clinical dietitian", "Endocrinologist/diabetologist", "Cardiologist/lipidologist (LDL monitoring)", "Neurologist for any epilepsy/dementia positioning", "Obstetrician for women of childbearing age"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "Acceptable only as optional, nutritionally complete low-carbohydrate recipes without cognition claims and with explicit exclusions (pregnancy, lactation, diabetes medication, eating disorders, children); a consumer 'keto for brain' programme fails because benefit is unproven while LDL rise and ketoacidosis risks are documented."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "A diet is not itself an authorised product. No authorised health claim in Regulation (EU) No 432/2012 for ketogenic diets, ketones or carbohydrate restriction and cognitive function (checked against knowledge of the register; not re-fetched). Ketogenic formulas for epilepsy are foods for special medical purposes under Regulation (EU) No 609/2013 and Delegated Regulation (EU) 2016/128. Very-low-calorie total diet replacement products fall under Delegated Regulation (EU) 2017/1798. Exogenous BHB salts: EFSA NDA Panel (2022) concluded safety as a novel food was NOT established (EFSA Journal 2022;20(7):7449, doi 10.2903/j.efsa.2022.7449, accessed 2026-10-26). Ketone ester status: not verified (uncertain). MCT oil is a conventional food.", "us": "Diets are not regulated; 'keto' labelling has no FDA definition (uncertain - not verified). Keto-marketed foods are conventional foods; structure/function claims on supplements require DSHEA disclaimer; FTC requires competent and reliable scientific evidence, which does not exist for cognitive enhancement in healthy adults. GRAS notice GRN 001032 for D-beta-hydroxybutyrate (up to 6 g/serving in beverages/bars) was filed 2021 (FDA response not verified). Ketone monoester GRAS status uncertain.", "morocco": "No specific regulation on ketogenic diets identified. Foods fall under Law 28-07 on food safety (ONSSA); nutrition/health claim rules and any registration of keto supplements or BHB products are uncertain and require ONSSA consultation. Not verified from official texts in this unit.", "permitted_claims_examples": [], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "BHB salts: EU novel food safety not established (2022). Ketone esters: EU novel food and US NDI/GRAS status uncertain. Whole-food keto recipes raise no novel-food issue.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Low: the 'intervention' is a whole-diet pattern requiring <20-50 g carbohydrate/day; no single food product delivers it. A low-carbohydrate snack or MCT product does not reproduce the diet and has no cognitive evidence in healthy adults.", "formats": ["Meal plans/recipes (olive oil, nuts, fish, eggs, non-starchy vegetables)", "Low-carbohydrate snacks (not evidence-based for cognition)", "MCT oil/powder (separate evidence base; not a ketogenic diet)", "Medical ketogenic formulas (FSMP, epilepsy only)"], "stability": "Not applicable to a dietary pattern; high-fat products prone to oxidation.", "taste": "Restrictive; Moroccan staples (bread, couscous, msemen, dates, mint tea with sugar) excluded, making cultural adherence hard.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Ketosis depends on whole-day carbohydrate intake; any carbohydrate-containing meal can abolish it.", "dose_feasibility": "Not feasible as a product dose; adherence poor even with intensive coaching (2 of 22 adherent in an MCI trial [buchholz2024]).", "cost_availability": "Higher-cost diet (meat, fish, nuts, cheese) relative to grain-based Moroccan diets; costs not quantified here.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 3, "effect_magnitude": 1, "safety": 4, "reproducibility": 3, "food_suitability": 2, "cost_access": 4, "rationale": "Evidence quality 3: healthy-adult evidence is grade C (tiny null RCTs) with B-level null comparisons vs low-fat; positive signals exist only in MCI/AD (grade C). Effect magnitude 1: no cognitive benefit in target groups; possible induction harm. Safety 4: documented LDL rise, micronutrient gaps, ketoacidosis risks in specific groups. Reproducibility 3: positive findings small and unreplicated; nulls consistent. Food suitability 2: whole-diet intervention, not a product. Cost/access 4: ordinary foods but costlier and culturally difficult in Morocco."}, "research_gaps": ["Adequately powered (n>100) RCT of chronic KD vs Mediterranean diet on cognition in healthy young adults/students", "Trials separating weight loss from ketosis effects in MCI", "Long-term LDL/apoB and cardiovascular outcomes of KD", "Cognitive testing during exam periods or high workload", "Whole-diet KD vs MCT/ketone supplement head-to-head trials", "Culturally adapted (Maghreb) low-carbohydrate patterns and adherence"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 2.8, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": "C", "best_grade_surrogate": "C", "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"vigilance": "C", "episodic_memory": "C", "global_cognition": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 11, "n_studies": 28, "n_verified": 28, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 12, "n_systematic_reviews": 4, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["benefit", "harm", "inconsistent", "insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "low-carbohydrate-diets", "name": "Low-carbohydrate diets (non-ketogenic)", "category": "K", "category_name": "Dietary patterns", "aliases": ["low-carb diet", "low-carbohydrate high-fat (LCHF) diet", "carbohydrate-restricted diet", "Atkins-type diet (induction phase is ketogenic)", "very low-carbohydrate diet (VLCD; often ketogenic)", "high-protein low-carbohydrate diet"], "food_sources": ["meat, fish, eggs, cheese and other protein foods", "non-starchy vegetables, nuts, seeds, avocado, olive oil", "restricted: bread, rice, pasta, couscous, potatoes, sugar, most fruit, legumes (in stricter versions)"], "active_constituents": ["not a constituent: a pattern defined by carbohydrate intake typically 50-130 g/day or <26% of energy (non-ketogenic) versus <50 g/day (ketogenic)", "consequences: lower postprandial glucose/insulin, higher fat and/or protein intake, possible mild ketosis in stricter versions"], "one_line_verdict": "Low-carbohydrate diets do not improve cognition in healthy or overweight adults: weight-loss trials of 8 weeks to 2 years show no cognitive advantage over higher-carbohydrate diets (and one 8-week trial found less improvement in processing speed), the first days of severe carbohydrate restriction or very high fat intake can transiently impair attention, memory and mood, and the EU authorises the claim that carbohydrates (130 g/day) contribute to normal brain function.", "scientific_conclusion": "Terminology first: most published 'low-carbohydrate' cognition trials used very-low-carbohydrate, high-fat diets that are ketogenic or near-ketogenic in their first weeks; genuinely moderate (non-ketogenic, ~50-130 g/day) diets have rarely been tested for cognition. Ketogenic-diet-specific evidence is appraised in the ketogenic-diet-chronic monograph; here we focus on carbohydrate restriction as a weight-loss or lifestyle pattern. Short term (days). In 16 healthy young men, 5 days of a 75%-fat, low-carbohydrate diet impaired attention, speed and mood versus a standard diet (P<0.01 to <0.001) [holloway2011]; 7 days of a 74%-fat diet in 20 sedentary men slowed simple reaction time and reduced power of attention [edwards2011]. In a small self-selected comparison of women starting diets, complete carbohydrate withdrawal impaired memory tasks in the first week, which recovered when carbohydrate was reintroduced, while vigilance was faster [danci2009]; in a 28-day ketogenic versus non-ketogenic very-low-energy formula diet, Trail Making (mental flexibility) worsened mainly in week 1 [wing1995]. These small studies are consistent in direction (transient impairment during adaptation) but are imprecise and some are non-randomised: grade C for harm, short_term_days. Chronic. In 93 overweight adults, 8 weeks of an energy-restricted LCHF diet produced slightly more weight loss but less improvement in speed of processing than a high-carbohydrate low-fat diet (P=0.04 for interaction), with no difference in working memory or mood [halyburton2007]. In 106 overweight adults followed for 1 year, working memory improved similarly on both diets, processing speed did not change, and mood improved more on the low-fat diet [brinkworth2009]. In 115 adults with type 2 diabetes, 52 weeks [tay2016] and 2 years [kakoschke2021] of a low-carbohydrate diet plus exercise produced no cognitive or psychological differences versus a high-unrefined-carbohydrate diet. In 39 non-obese young adults, doubling or tripling protein at the expense of carbohydrate for 31 days had no effect on cognition, mood or sleep [karl2015]; 3 weeks of nutritional ketosis was null in 11 healthy adults [iacovides2019]. Systematic reviews agree that carbohydrate restriction is not worse than other diets for psychological well-being [sindler2024]; a meta-analysis of 8 RCTs found no effect on depression (SMD 0.06, -0.11 to 0.24) or anxiety (SMD 0.19, -0.10 to 0.47), but higher anxiety with <26% carbohydrate in sensitivity analyses (SMD 0.31, 0.10-0.52) [varaee2023]. A meta-analysis of dietary-pattern RCTs in healthy adults listed low-carbohydrate diets among patterns with positive effects, but its pooled estimate (SMD 0.14) mixes Mediterranean, calorie-restriction and lifestyle interventions and cannot isolate carbohydrate restriction [guo2025]. Overall: chronic no_benefit (grade B for overweight/T2D adults, because several medium-sized RCTs up to 2 years agree; grade C for healthy young adults because data are small). Safety and practical relevance: low-carbohydrate diets modestly lower triglycerides and blood pressure but raise LDL-C by ~0.11 mmol/L on average [dong2020], restrict fibre-rich whole grains, legumes and fruit that are associated with good brain ageing, and can cause adaptation symptoms. For a student or knowledge worker, cutting carbohydrate is not a cognitive strategy; avoiding large refined-carbohydrate loads and choosing low-GI carbohydrate is a better-supported message (see glucose-and-carbohydrates and meal-composition-size-and-timing monographs).", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Carbohydrate restriction is neither deficiency correction nor enhancement for the brain: the brain can adapt to ketones and gluconeogenic glucose, but adaptation in the first days to a week is associated with transient decrements in attention, memory, processing speed and mood [holloway2011, edwards2011, danci2009, wing1995]. Once adapted, cognition is no better than on higher-carbohydrate diets in overweight, diabetic or healthy adults [halyburton2007, brinkworth2009, tay2016, kakoschke2021, karl2015]. Any benefits seen in weight-loss trials track weight and glycaemic improvement, which occur with either diet. People with insulin resistance or type 2 diabetes may benefit metabolically, but this is a clinical indication, not cognitive enhancement.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Claims like 'cut carbs for mental clarity', 'no brain fog on low-carb' or 'fat is the better brain fuel' are anecdotal: controlled trials show no cognitive advantage, possible early impairment, and in one trial worse mood than a low-fat diet over a year [brinkworth2009]. In the EU, the authorised claim actually runs the other way: 'Carbohydrates contribute to the maintenance of normal brain function' (daily intake of 130 g from all sources; conditions per Reg. (EU) 1018/2013, documented in the glucose-and-carbohydrates monograph). Brain Food should not market low-carb products for cognition; a defensible position is moderate, low-GI, fibre-rich carbohydrate in balanced meals.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Restricting carbohydrate lowers postprandial glucose and insulin excursions and, below about 50 g/day, induces ketogenesis; the brain shifts partly to ketone oxidation. During adaptation, reduced glucose availability, fluid/electrolyte shifts and higher free fatty acids may impair attention and speed; very high fat intake was associated with reduced cardiac and whole-body energetic efficiency. Proposed benefits (stable glucose, less post-meal dip) have not translated into measured cognitive gains.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Carbohydrate restriction -> lower plasma glucose and insulin excursions; higher fat and/or protein intake", "detail": "Established metabolic consequence; plasma free fatty acids +44% after 5-7 days of 74-75% fat diets [holloway2011, edwards2011].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Below ~50 g/day: hepatic ketogenesis -> ketones supply part of brain energy; gluconeogenesis maintains glucose", "detail": "Physiology established; adaptation takes days to weeks (see ketogenic-diet-chronic monograph).", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Adaptation phase: reduced glucose availability, fluid/electrolyte shifts, possible mitochondrial uncoupling", "detail": "Uncoupling hypothesis from rat work and whole-body efficiency drop in humans [edwards2011]; cardiac PCr/ATP -9% [holloway2011].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Stable glycaemia -> fewer postprandial dips in alertness (hypothesised benefit)", "detail": "Plausible but not demonstrated as a cognitive benefit in chronic trials.", "evidence_level": "plausible"}, {"step": "Measured human cognitive outcome", "detail": "Transient impairment in first days [holloway2011, edwards2011, danci2009, wing1995]; no long-term advantage over higher-carbohydrate diets [halyburton2007, brinkworth2009, tay2016, kakoschke2021].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}], "pathways": ["glucose availability and brain fuel switching", "ketogenesis (in very-low-carbohydrate versions)", "free fatty acid and mitochondrial efficiency effects", "weight loss and glycaemic control (indirect)", "large neutral amino acid / serotonin precursor availability (hypothesised mood effect)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "short_term_days", "domain": "sustained_attention", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy young men (n=16, ~22 y) and sedentary men (n=20)", "dose_or_intake": "74-75% energy from fat, low carbohydrate", "duration": "5-7 days", "grade": "C", "direction": "harm", "effect_summary": "High-fat low-carbohydrate diets impaired attention and speed (and mood) versus standard diets.", "effect_estimate": "Attention P<0.01, speed P<0.001, mood P<0.01 [holloway2011]; simple reaction time slower P<0.01, power of attention reduced P<0.01 [edwards2011]; standardized effects not reported in abstracts", "certainty_notes": "Two small trials from one Oxford group with overlapping authors; consistent direction; imprecise; extreme fat intake limits generalisation to moderate low-carb diets.", "key_study_ids": ["holloway2011", "edwards2011"]}, {"timeframe": "short_term_days", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Women starting weight-loss diets (n=19); women with severe obesity on formula VLED (n=21)", "dose_or_intake": "Complete carbohydrate withdrawal (self-selected); ketogenic vs non-ketogenic VLED", "duration": "1-4 weeks (impairment mainly week 1)", "grade": "C", "direction": "harm", "effect_summary": "Memory impaired during complete carbohydrate withdrawal, recovering with reintroduction; mental flexibility (Trail Making) worse on ketogenic VLED mainly in week 1; vigilance faster in low-carb group.", "effect_estimate": "not reported as standardized effects [danci2009, wing1995]", "certainty_notes": "Very small; one non-randomised; mixed direction across tasks (vigilance faster); downgraded for risk of bias and imprecision.", "key_study_ids": ["danci2009", "wing1995"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "processing_speed", "population": "other", "population_detail": "Overweight/obese adults (~50 y) in energy-restricted weight-loss trials", "dose_or_intake": "Energy-restricted LCHF vs isocaloric HCLF", "duration": "8 weeks; 1 year", "grade": "B", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "8 weeks: speed of processing improved less on LCHF (interaction P=0.04), working memory no difference (P=0.68). 1 year: working memory improved similarly in both arms, processing speed unchanged; mood improved more on low-fat diet.", "effect_estimate": "Time x diet P=0.04 for speed favouring HCLF [halyburton2007]; no diet effect on cognition at 1 year [brinkworth2009]", "certainty_notes": "Two RCTs (n=93, n=106) from the same CSIRO group; consistent no benefit; cognition secondary; attrition. Grade B for absence of benefit.", "key_study_ids": ["halyburton2007", "brinkworth2009"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Adults with obesity and type 2 diabetes (~58 y)", "dose_or_intake": "Very low-carbohydrate (~14% energy) low-saturated-fat diet + exercise vs high unrefined carbohydrate + exercise", "duration": "52 weeks; 2 years", "grade": "B", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "No differences in any of nine cognitive domains at 1 year or psychological/cognitive outcomes at 2 years.", "effect_estimate": "All time x diet P>=0.24 [tay2016]; P>=0.19 [kakoschke2021]", "certainty_notes": "Same trial at two time points (not independent); 47% attrition at 2 y; T2D population (indirect for healthy adults).", "key_study_ids": ["tay2016", "kakoschke2021"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Non-obese young adults (21 y); healthy normal-weight adults (30 y)", "dose_or_intake": "Higher protein replacing carbohydrate (0.8-2.4 g/kg protein); ketogenic 15% CHO", "duration": "31 days; 3 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "No cognitive, mood or sleep effect of lower-carbohydrate higher-protein diets in young adults; 3 weeks of ketosis null on all domains.", "effect_estimate": "Null [karl2015]; all d<=0.31, P>0.39 [iacovides2019]", "certainty_notes": "Small trials (n=39, n=11); imprecise; consistent null.", "key_study_ids": ["karl2015", "iacovides2019"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Adults without mood disorder in RCTs (mostly overweight/obese)", "dose_or_intake": "Low-carbohydrate diets, various definitions", "duration": "Weeks to 2 years", "grade": "B", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "No effect on depression or anxiety overall; possible increased anxiety with <26% carbohydrate; systematic review finds LC diets no worse for well-being.", "effect_estimate": "Depression SMD 0.06 (-0.11 to 0.24); anxiety SMD 0.19 (-0.10 to 0.47); <26% CHO anxiety SMD 0.31 (0.10-0.52) [varaee2023]", "certainty_notes": "8 RCTs, n=590; unblinded; subgroup finding fragile (driven by Australian trials).", "key_study_ids": ["varaee2023", "sindler2024", "brinkworth2009"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "students", "population_detail": "Students and knowledge workers", "dose_or_intake": "Any low-carbohydrate pattern", "duration": "Not studied", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No trial in students or knowledge workers; indirect evidence from healthy young adults is null or shows early impairment.", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "Absence of direct evidence.", "key_study_ids": ["karl2015", "holloway2011"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "74-75% fat, low-carbohydrate diet", "onset": "Within 5-7 days", "duration_studied": "5-7 days", "domain": "attention, reaction time, mood", "population": "Healthy young/sedentary men", "effect_estimate": "Impaired (P<0.01)", "certainty": "C", "safety": "Cardiac PCr/ATP -9%; whole-body efficiency -3%", "study_ids": ["holloway2011", "edwards2011"]}, {"dose": "Complete carbohydrate withdrawal", "onset": "48 h - 1 week", "duration_studied": "3 weeks", "domain": "memory, vigilance", "population": "Women dieting", "effect_estimate": "Memory worse during withdrawal; vigilance faster", "certainty": "C", "safety": "Recovered after reintroduction", "study_ids": ["danci2009"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "8 weeks", "population": "Overweight/obese adults", "outcome": "Working memory, processing speed, mood", "effect_estimate": "Speed improved less on LCHF (P=0.04); memory and mood no difference", "evidence_quality": "B", "limitations": "Cognition at two time points only", "study_ids": ["halyburton2007"]}, {"duration": "1 year", "population": "Overweight/obese adults", "outcome": "Cognition and mood", "effect_estimate": "Cognition no diet effect; mood better on low-fat", "evidence_quality": "B", "limitations": "Attrition; limited battery", "study_ids": ["brinkworth2009"]}, {"duration": "1-2 years", "population": "Type 2 diabetes", "outcome": "Nine cognitive domains; psychological health", "effect_estimate": "No differences", "evidence_quality": "B", "limitations": "Single trial reported twice; attrition", "study_ids": ["tay2016", "kakoschke2021"]}, {"duration": "31 days", "population": "Non-obese young adults", "outcome": "Cognition, mood, sleep", "effect_estimate": "No effect", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "Small", "study_ids": ["karl2015"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Moderate carbohydrate restriction is generally safe for healthy adults, but early adaptation can cause headache, fatigue, constipation and transient cognitive and mood decrements; very low intakes raise LDL-C on average and reduce fibre, whole grains, legumes and fruit. People on glucose-lowering drugs risk hypoglycaemia; SGLT2 inhibitor users risk euglycaemic ketoacidosis with very low intakes.", "adverse_effects": ["adaptation symptoms ('low-carb flu'): headache, fatigue, irritability, reduced attention in first days", "transient impairment of attention, reaction time, memory and mood during the first 1-7 days [holloway2011, edwards2011, danci2009, wing1995]", "LDL-C increase (average +0.11 mmol/L; larger in some individuals) [dong2020]", "constipation and low fibre intake", "possible increased anxiety with <26% energy from carbohydrate [varaee2023]", "reduced high-intensity exercise performance"], "contraindications": ["type 1 diabetes or insulin-treated type 2 diabetes without medical supervision", "SGLT2 inhibitor therapy (euglycaemic ketoacidosis risk at very low intakes)", "pregnancy and breastfeeding", "history of eating disorders", "children and adolescents (growth, school performance)", "familial hypercholesterolaemia / high baseline LDL-C"], "drug_interactions": ["insulin and sulfonylureas: hypoglycaemia risk; doses need adjustment", "SGLT2 inhibitors: ketoacidosis risk", "antihypertensives: blood pressure may fall with weight loss and natriuresis", "lithium: fluid shifts in early restriction may alter levels (theoretical)"], "upper_limits": "No upper limit; EU authorised brain-function claim references 130 g/day carbohydrate from all sources, and EFSA reference intake ranges for carbohydrate are 45-60% of energy (agent knowledge, not re-fetched).", "sleep_impact": "No sleep effect in 3-week ketosis [iacovides2019] or 31-day protein-for-carbohydrate trial [karl2015]; acute carbohydrate-rich evening meals may shorten sleep latency (not assessed here).", "long_term_exposure_studied": "RCTs up to 2 years with cognitive outcomes in overweight/T2D adults; no long-term cognitive data in healthy young adults.", "daily_use_appropriate": "uncertain", "special_populations": "Diabetes on medication, pregnancy, children/adolescents, athletes in high-intensity sports, people with eating disorders, people with dyslipidaemia, students during exam periods (early adaptation impairment).", "specialist_review_needed": ["diabetologist for anyone on glucose-lowering medication", "lipid specialist for high LDL-C", "dietitian for long-term adequacy (fibre, micronutrients)"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "Acceptable as a medical/weight-management choice with supervision; not appropriate to promote to students for cognition given early impairment risk, LDL-C rise and medication interactions."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "No health claim exists for low-carbohydrate diets and cognition. The EU authorised claim 'Carbohydrates contribute to the maintenance of normal brain function' (Reg. (EU) 1018/2013; daily intake of 130 g carbohydrate from all sources; conditions incl. >=20 g carbohydrate per portion and low sugars/no added sugars) contradicts any 'cut carbs for your brain' messaging (details from the project's glucose-and-carbohydrates monograph, which fetched the regulation 2026-10-25). 'Low carbohydrate' is not a harmonised nutrition claim under Reg. 1924/2006 Annex (agent knowledge; national guidance varies; not re-verified). Total diet replacement and weight-control products fall under Reg. (EU) 609/2013 and delegated acts.", "us": "No FDA definition of 'low carb'; FDA has not authorised 'net carbs' nutrient content claims (agent knowledge, not re-verified). Claims of improved focus from low-carb products would need FTC-level substantiation that controlled trials do not provide.", "morocco": "No specific rule on low-carbohydrate claims found; general labelling and claims under Law 28-07, Decree 2-12-389 and Joint Order 281-16 (ONSSA) as reported in related monographs; uncertain.", "permitted_claims_examples": [], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "None for conventional low-carbohydrate foods; sweeteners/fibres used in low-carb products (e.g. allulose, which is not authorised as a novel food in the EU per agent knowledge, unverified) need individual checks.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Low for a brain-performance brand: low-carbohydrate meals are easy to formulate (protein, vegetables, nuts, olive oil) but have no cognitive rationale and conflict with Moroccan staple foods (bread, couscous) and with the EU carbohydrate-brain claim.", "formats": ["moderate-carbohydrate, low-GI meals (preferred alternative)", "protein + vegetable + olive oil bowls", "nut/seed snacks"], "stability": "Not specific", "taste": "Acceptable for savoury meals; low-carb baked goods often rely on sweeteners and fibres with taste and GI-tolerance issues.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Not applicable", "dose_feasibility": "A 50-130 g/day pattern is feasible through meal design, but trials do not justify it for cognition.", "cost_availability": "Higher-protein/fat low-carb meals cost more than grain-based meals.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 5, "effect_magnitude": 0.5, "safety": 6, "reproducibility": 6, "food_suitability": 4, "cost_access": 6, "rationale": "Evidence quality 5: grade B for a consistent absence of chronic benefit in overweight/T2D RCTs; C in healthy young adults. Effect magnitude 0.5: no benefit, possible transient harm. Safety 6: generally safe but adaptation impairment, LDL-C rise and medication interactions (conditional gate). Reproducibility 6: null chronic findings replicate across groups; acute harm from small trials. Food suitability 4: feasible but misaligned with brand purpose and EU claim. Cost/access 6."}, "research_gaps": ["Trials of genuinely moderate (100-130 g/day), non-ketogenic low-GI carbohydrate reduction on cognition in students, with time-of-day testing.", "Time course of adaptation-phase cognitive impairment and who is affected (sex, baseline insulin sensitivity).", "Publication of completed registered trials with cognitive outcomes (e.g. NCT05071287, low-carb/ketogenic vs low-fat with cognitive function listed, completed 2022, no results posted).", "Low-carbohydrate diets and long-term cognitive ageing using objective measures, considering LDL-C trade-offs."], "_derived": {"composite_score": 4.4, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": null, "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": null, "best_grade_overall": null, "benefit_domains": {}, "n_outcome_rows": 7, "n_studies": 14, "n_verified": 14, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 11, "n_systematic_reviews": 2, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["harm", "insufficient", "no_benefit"]}}, {"id": "mind-diet", "name": "MIND diet", "category": "K", "category_name": "Dietary patterns", "aliases": ["Mediterranean-DASH Intervention for Neurodegenerative Delay", "MIND dietary pattern", "MIND score (15-item: 10 brain-healthy, 5 unhealthy food groups)", "MIND-NL (Dutch adaptation)"], "food_sources": ["green leafy vegetables (6 or more servings/week)", "other vegetables (at least 1/day)", "berries (2 or more servings/week; blueberries emphasised in trials)", "nuts (5 or more servings/week)", "beans and legumes (more than 3 meals/week)", "whole grains (3 or more servings/day)", "fish (at least 1/week) and poultry (2 or more/week)", "olive oil as the main cooking fat", "limit: red meat, butter and stick margarine, cheese, pastries and sweets, fried or fast food", "wine (1 glass/day) is a component of the original score; not appropriate for a brain-health brand"], "active_constituents": ["whole-diet pattern: candidate contributors include lutein-zeaxanthin and other carotenoids, folate, vitamin K and nitrate (leafy greens), anthocyanins and flavonoids (berries), vitamin E, ALA and polyphenols (nuts), long-chain omega-3 (fish), fibre, MUFA (olive oil), low saturated fat and low added sugar"], "one_line_verdict": "The MIND diet was derived from, and is consistently associated in, older-adult cohorts with slower cognitive decline and lower dementia risk, but its only large randomised trial (3 years, n=604) found no cognitive or MRI benefit compared with a calorie-restricted control diet, and there is no trial in healthy young adults, so its brain-specific promise remains unproven.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. The MIND score was constructed in 2015 by investigators of the Rush Memory and Aging Project from foods previously linked to dementia, and then tested in the same cohort: the top tertile had a 53% lower incidence of Alzheimer disease (HR 0.47) [morris2015ad], and slower global cognitive decline equivalent to being 7.5 years younger [morris2015decline]. Deriving and testing a score in one cohort inflates effect estimates. Later independent cohorts gave smaller, mixed results. In three cohorts plus a meta-analysis of 11 cohorts (224,049 participants), the top tertile had dementia HR 0.83 (0.76-0.90, I2 35%) [chen2023]. Cognitive-level associations are small: 0.042 SD per SD of MIND score, and annual decline 0.010 SD slower with a CI crossing zero [huang2023]. In Nurses' Health Study women, MIND was associated with verbal memory (0.04 SD) but not with 6-year decline [berendsen2018]. A systematic review found protective associations in 7 of 10 cohorts for dementia but only 2 of 7 for global decline, mostly in North American cohorts [vansoest2024]; another review concluded that RCT results are mixed [tse2025]. Clinicopathological analyses suggest association with less amyloid at autopsy and with better cognition independent of pathology [agarwal2023, dhana2021], but these remain observational. The decisive test, the MIND trial (two US sites, n=604 overweight older adults with a family history of dementia), found a non-significant 3-year difference in global cognition of 0.035 SD (95% CI -0.022 to 0.092) and no MRI differences versus a control diet with the same mild caloric restriction; both groups lost about 5 kg and improved cognition [barnes2023]. Post-hoc analyses found benefit only in participants with BMI 35 or higher (0.040 SD/year) [halloway2025] and among those with the largest increases in MIND score regardless of arm [dhana2026]. These are hypothesis-generating subgroup and per-adherence analyses that do not rescue the primary null. In US POINTER, MIND diet guidance was one element of a structured multidomain package that improved global cognition by 0.029 SD/year versus self-guided lifestyle change; the diet's contribution cannot be isolated [baker2025]. A small Iranian RCT in 37 obese women aged about 48 reported large working-memory and attention gains with a calorie-restricted MIND diet, but it was a per-protocol analysis with unbalanced completion and uncorrected multiple outcomes [arjmand2022]. In whom. Evidence concerns adults over about 50, mostly older Americans. There are no trials in students or healthy young adults. Adherence is socially patterned from adolescence, with lower adherence at lower household income, which is a strong confounder in cohorts [nagata2024]. Unknown: whether MIND outperforms any healthy diet with weight loss, and whether it matters before midlife.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "MIND is a pattern-replacement intervention, not deficiency correction. The original cohorts compared people with low versus high adherence, and low adherence often reflects a generally poor diet and lower socioeconomic status [nagata2024]. In the only large trial, participants were required to have a suboptimal diet at entry. Both arms improved diet and lost weight, and MIND added nothing measurable [barnes2023]. Possible benefit was concentrated in severe obesity (BMI 35 or higher) [halloway2025], suggesting that MIND may help most where metabolic health is poorest, consistent with a correction rather than enhancement model. For healthy, adequately nourished young adults, no evidence of enhancement exists.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "MIND is marketed as 'the brain diet' that 'cuts Alzheimer's risk by 53%' and 'makes your brain 7.5 years younger'. Both figures come from the 2015 derivation cohort [morris2015ad, morris2015decline] and were not confirmed by the randomised trial, which was null [barnes2023]. Independent cohorts suggest roughly a 17% lower dementia risk in the top tertile, which is observational and confounded [chen2023]. Berries, leafy greens and nuts are attractive product ingredients, but no claim that a berry or nut product 'protects the brain' is authorised in the EU, and in the US such dementia language is a disease claim. Defensible statement: 'built around the food groups of the MIND dietary pattern, studied for healthy ageing'. It should be accompanied by the caveat that a 3-year trial did not show cognitive benefit over a healthy calorie-controlled diet.", "mechanism": {"summary": "MIND emphasises foods with plausible neuroprotective constituents: carotenoids and flavonoids from leafy greens and berries, vitamin E and polyphenols from nuts, and omega-3 from fish. It also shares the Mediterranean and DASH vascular benefits (lower blood pressure and LDL, less saturated fat and sugar). Proposed mechanisms are reduced oxidative stress, inflammation and amyloid accumulation, and better cerebrovascular health. Human support consists of biomarker changes (plasma lutein-zeaxanthin and beta-carotene rise with adherence [dhana2026]) and autopsy associations [agarwal2023]. The randomised trial did not show changes in MRI brain measures [barnes2023].", "pathway_map": [{"step": "MIND adherence -> higher intake of leafy greens, berries, nuts, fish; less saturated fat and sweets", "detail": "Achievable: MIND score rose 3.3 points (to 11.0 of 14) in the trial intervention arm vs 0.7 in control [barnes2023]; plasma lutein-zeaxanthin and beta-carotene rose with adherence [dhana2026].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Nutrient intake -> vascular and metabolic improvements (blood pressure, weight, lipids)", "detail": "Inferred from DASH and Mediterranean trials; in the MIND trial weight loss was similar (-5.0 vs -4.8 kg) in both arms because both were calorie-restricted [barnes2023].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Antioxidant/anti-inflammatory nutrients -> less amyloid and tau pathology", "detail": "Autopsy association: MIND score associated with lower global AD pathology and beta-amyloid load (standardised beta about -2); green leafy vegetables strongest [agarwal2023]. Observational; animal data for berry flavonoids not reviewed.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Cognitive resilience independent of pathology", "detail": "Association of MIND with cognition persisted after adjustment for brain pathologies at autopsy (beta 0.111) [dhana2021]; mechanism unknown.", "evidence_level": "speculative"}, {"step": "Measured human cognitive outcome", "detail": "RCT null for global cognition and MRI at 3 years [barnes2023]; possible benefit in BMI 35 or higher (post hoc) [halloway2025]; no data in young adults.", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["carotenoid (lutein-zeaxanthin) and flavonoid supply", "vascular risk reduction (blood pressure, lipids)", "weight loss and insulin sensitivity", "oxidative stress and neuroinflammation (hypothesised)", "amyloid pathology (autopsy association only)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Cognitively unimpaired adults 65-84 with overweight/obesity, suboptimal diet and family history of dementia (US, two sites)", "dose_or_intake": "MIND diet counselling plus mild caloric restriction (MIND score +3.3 points)", "duration": "3 years", "grade": "B", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "No significant difference in global cognition or in four cognitive domains, and no MRI differences, vs a control diet with the same caloric restriction; both groups improved and lost about 5 kg.", "effect_estimate": "Mean difference 0.035 SD (95% CI -0.022 to 0.092), p=0.23 [barnes2023]", "certainty_notes": "Well-conducted NIH-funded RCT with 93% completion; not A because a single trial, control arm also improved diet, mostly well-educated white participants, and practice effects. The null is the most reliable finding on MIND.", "key_study_ids": ["barnes2023"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Older adults with severe obesity (BMI 35 or higher, n=213) within the MIND trial (post hoc effect-modifier analysis)", "dose_or_intake": "MIND diet with caloric restriction", "duration": "3 years", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Among participants with BMI 35 or higher, MIND improved global cognition by 0.040 SD/year more than control; BMI was the only significant modifier among many tested.", "effect_estimate": "0.040 SD/year (SE 0.017), p=0.018 [halloway2025]", "certainty_notes": "Post hoc subgroup; multiple modifiers tested; requires replication.", "key_study_ids": ["halloway2025", "barnes2023"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "MIND-trial participants grouped by achieved change in MIND score (both arms, post hoc)", "dose_or_intake": "Mean increase of 4 points in MIND score (substantial improvers)", "duration": "3 years", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Participants with substantial MIND-score increases had faster cognitive gains than non-improvers, independent of arm; observational within a trial, so the comparison is confounded by adherence behaviour.", "effect_estimate": "beta 0.035 SD/year (95% CI 0.006 to 0.064) [dhana2026]", "certainty_notes": "Per-adherence analysis breaks randomisation (healthy-adherer bias); downgraded to C.", "key_study_ids": ["dhana2026"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "dementia_risk", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Middle-aged and older adults in prospective cohorts (Whitehall II, HRS, Framingham Offspring, Rush MAP and others)", "dose_or_intake": "Highest vs lowest tertile of MIND score; per 3-point increment", "duration": "4.5 years to over 10 years of follow-up", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Derivation cohort: AD HR 0.47 in top tertile; independent cohorts and meta-analysis of 11 cohorts: dementia HR 0.83. Positive in 7 of 10 cohorts in a 2024 review, mostly North American.", "effect_estimate": "Pooled HR 0.83 (95% CI 0.76-0.90), I2 35% [chen2023]; derivation HR 0.47 (0.26-0.76) [morris2015ad]", "certainty_notes": "Observational; derivation-cohort optimism; confounding by SES and education [nagata2024]; RCT did not confirm cognitive benefit. Grade C.", "key_study_ids": ["chen2023", "morris2015ad", "vansoest2024", "tse2025"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_decline", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Older adults in cohorts (Rush MAP, Nurses' Health Study, China Health and Nutrition Survey and others)", "dose_or_intake": "MIND score (continuous or tertiles)", "duration": "3-6 years", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Derivation cohort reported slower decline equal to 7.5 years younger; meta-analysis of 8 cohorts found 0.010 SD/year slower decline (CI crosses zero); NHS found association with level but not decline; 2 of 7 cohorts positive for global decline.", "effect_estimate": "0.010 SD/year (95% CI -0.001 to 0.021) slower decline per SD [huang2023]; beta 0.0092 per point (p<0.0001) [morris2015decline]", "certainty_notes": "Inconsistent across cohorts, small effects, derivation bias. Grade C.", "key_study_ids": ["morris2015decline", "huang2023", "berendsen2018", "vansoest2024"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "40 obese women (mean age 48, BMI 32), Iran", "dose_or_intake": "Calorie-restricted modified MIND diet vs calorie-restricted standard diet", "duration": "3 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Greater gains in working memory, verbal recognition memory and attention, and an MRI increase in inferior frontal gyrus area; per-protocol analysis of 37 completers (22 vs 15).", "effect_estimate": "Working memory +1.37 (95% CI 0.79 to 1.95); attention +3.75 (2.43 to 5.07) [arjmand2022]", "certainty_notes": "Single small trial, per-protocol, unbalanced completion, no multiplicity correction for cognition, outcome assessor blinded; university-funded. Effects implausibly large given the null MIND trial. Grade C at most.", "key_study_ids": ["arjmand2022"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Sedentary older adults 60-79 at risk of decline with suboptimal diet (US POINTER)", "dose_or_intake": "Structured multidomain lifestyle (MIND diet guidelines, exercise, cognitive and social activity, health monitoring) vs self-guided", "duration": "2 years", "grade": "B", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Structured package improved global cognition slightly more than self-guided change, mainly executive function; the diet component cannot be isolated, and the authors say clinical significance is uncertain.", "effect_estimate": "0.029 SD/year (95% CI 0.008-0.050) [baker2025]", "certainty_notes": "Large RCT but indirect for the MIND diet itself; small effect with practice effects in both arms.", "key_study_ids": ["baker2025"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy young adults and students", "dose_or_intake": "not studied", "duration": "not studied", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No trial or cohort of MIND diet and cognition in healthy young adults or students was found; available data concern adolescents' adherence patterns (social gradient) rather than cognitive outcomes.", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "Absence of evidence; extrapolation from older adults is not justified.", "key_study_ids": ["nagata2024"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "neuroplasticity_biomarkers", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Autopsied Rush MAP participants (age at death about 91)", "dose_or_intake": "MIND score from FFQ during life", "duration": "mean 6.8 years of follow-up before death", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Higher MIND score associated with less global AD pathology and beta-amyloid (a biomarker, not cognition); association with cognition persisted after adjustment for pathology.", "effect_estimate": "Global AD pathology beta -0.022 (p=0.034) [agarwal2023]; cognition beta 0.119 (SE 0.040) [dhana2021]", "certainty_notes": "Observational, single cohort (same group that devised MIND), very old population; biomarker outcome. MRI outcomes in the RCT were null [barnes2023].", "key_study_ids": ["agarwal2023", "dhana2021", "barnes2023"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "not applicable (dietary pattern)", "onset": "not studied", "duration_studied": "none", "domain": "any", "population": "any", "effect_estimate": "No acute or short-term (days) trial of the MIND diet was found.", "certainty": "insufficient", "safety": "not applicable", "study_ids": ["vansoest2024"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "3 years", "population": "604 older US adults, overweight, family history of dementia", "outcome": "global cognition, four domains, MRI", "effect_estimate": "0.035 SD (95% CI -0.022 to 0.092), NS; MRI similar", "evidence_quality": "moderate (B) for no benefit over healthy calorie-restricted control", "limitations": "Active control; practice effects; limited diversity", "study_ids": ["barnes2023"]}, {"duration": "3 years (post hoc)", "population": "MIND-trial subgroups (BMI 35 or higher; high adherers)", "outcome": "global cognition slope", "effect_estimate": "+0.040 SD/year (BMI 35+); +0.035 SD/year (substantial improvers vs none)", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Post hoc, breaks randomisation, multiple testing", "study_ids": ["halloway2025", "dhana2026"]}, {"duration": "3 months", "population": "37 obese middle-aged Iranian women", "outcome": "working memory, attention, recognition memory, MRI", "effect_estimate": "Working memory +1.37 (0.79-1.95)", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Per-protocol, small, uncorrected", "study_ids": ["arjmand2022"]}, {"duration": "4.5 years to 10+ years follow-up", "population": "cohorts of middle-aged and older adults", "outcome": "incident dementia / AD", "effect_estimate": "HR 0.83 (0.76-0.90) pooled; HR 0.47 derivation cohort", "evidence_quality": "low (observational)", "limitations": "Confounding; derivation optimism; mostly North American", "study_ids": ["chen2023", "morris2015ad"]}, {"duration": "2 years", "population": "2111 at-risk older US adults", "outcome": "global cognition (multidomain package including MIND guidelines)", "effect_estimate": "0.029 SD/year (0.008-0.050)", "evidence_quality": "moderate but indirect for diet", "limitations": "Diet effect not separable", "study_ids": ["baker2025"]}], "safety": {"summary": "MIND is a food-based pattern with no diet-attributable adverse events in its 3-year RCT (127 vs 153 events, none judged related) [barnes2023]. Component cautions mirror the Mediterranean diet: nut and fish allergy, warfarin-vitamin K interaction with high leafy-green intake, and the original score's daily wine component, which should never be promoted. The trial combined MIND with caloric restriction, which is inappropriate for underweight people, adolescents or people with eating disorders.", "adverse_effects": ["Allergy to nuts, fish or berries in sensitised individuals", "Gastrointestinal effects from increased legumes and fibre", "Alcohol-related harm if the wine component is followed"], "contraindications": ["Known allergy to included foods", "Caloric restriction component contraindicated in underweight, frail older adults at risk of malnutrition, pregnancy and eating disorders"], "drug_interactions": ["Warfarin: high and variable intake of green leafy vegetables (vitamin K) can alter INR", "Alcohol component: sedatives, psychotropics, metformin"], "upper_limits": "No limit for the pattern; component limits (sodium, mercury in fish, alcohol) apply.", "sleep_impact": "None expected.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Yes: 3 years in the MIND trial (n=604) with no diet-related adverse events.", "daily_use_appropriate": "yes", "special_populations": "Pregnancy: suitable without alcohol and with low-mercury fish. Children: no cognitive claims; no caloric restriction. CKD: potassium/phosphorus load may need adjustment. Anticoagulated patients: consistent vitamin K intake.", "specialist_review_needed": ["Dietitian for anticoagulant users and CKD", "Allergen management for nut/berry products"], "hard_safety_gate": "pass", "gate_reason": "Food pattern with 3-year RCT safety data; the alcohol component must be removed from any product communication."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "No health claim exists for the MIND pattern. Component claims (for example walnuts and blood-vessel elasticity at 30 g/day; DHA and normal brain function at 250 mg/day for a fish-derived product; potassium and normal blood pressure) may apply to individual products if conditions are met; none authorises dementia or cognitive-decline language. Reference to 'MIND diet' alongside a product could be read as an implied health claim about the brain and should be avoided or legally reviewed.", "us": "Dietary-pattern descriptions are permitted. 'Reduces risk of Alzheimer's disease or dementia' is a disease claim that is not authorised for foods, and the null RCT undermines FTC substantiation. Structure/function language ('supports healthy brain ageing') on conventional food requires truthful, non-misleading substantiation, which is weak here. Trademark status of 'MIND diet' was not checked.", "morocco": "No specific provision identified; food claims fall under Law 28-07/ONSSA labelling rules, and claim-specific provisions were not verified in this unit (uncertain).", "permitted_claims_examples": [], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "None for conventional foods. Concentrated berry anthocyanin extracts may raise novel food or NDI questions (not assessed here).", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Building-block products are feasible: frozen berry mixes, leafy-green-rich ready meals, nut portions, legume dishes, whole-grain products. The pattern itself is not a product.", "formats": ["berry and nut snack packs (no added sugar)", "leafy-green and legume ready meals", "whole-grain breakfast products with berries and nuts", "fish-based meals"], "stability": "Berries: frozen or freeze-dried to preserve anthocyanins; leafy greens: short shelf life, which favours frozen formats; nuts: oxidation control.", "taste": "Generally acceptable; the pattern restricts butter, cheese and pastries, which may reduce appeal.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Whole food; fat co-ingestion improves carotenoid uptake.", "dose_feasibility": "Weekly targets (6+ servings of leafy greens, 2+ of berries, 5+ of nuts) can be partially supplied by products, but adherence change of about 3-4 score points (as in the trial) requires whole-diet change.", "cost_availability": "Berries are seasonal and expensive in Morocco outside production zones; leafy greens and legumes are cheap and widely available.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 4, "effect_magnitude": 2, "safety": 9, "reproducibility": 3, "food_suitability": 8, "cost_access": 6, "rationale": "Evidence quality 4: a B-grade, well-conducted RCT is null for cognitive benefit, and the positive evidence is C (cohorts, post hoc analyses, one small low-quality RCT); no evidence in young adults. Effect magnitude 2: RCT difference 0.035 SD (NS); independent cohort dementia HR 0.83. Safety 9: food-based, with 3-year trial safety data. Reproducibility 3: derivation-cohort effects not reproduced in the trial; cohort decline findings inconsistent. Food suitability 8: entirely food-based. Cost/access 6: berries and nuts costly; greens and legumes cheap."}, "research_gaps": ["Any trial in healthy young or middle-aged adults or students", "Replication of the BMI 35+ effect-modification signal in a pre-specified trial", "Trials against a habitual-diet (not active) control to separate MIND from general diet improvement", "Validation of MIND outside North America, including North African food cultures (a Moroccan adaptation without alcohol)", "Component trials (leafy greens, berries) with objective cognitive endpoints"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 5.1, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "pass", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": "B", "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": "C", "best_grade_overall": "B", "benefit_domains": {"global_cognition": "B", "dementia_risk": "C", "working_memory": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 9, "n_studies": 16, "n_verified": 16, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 5, "n_systematic_reviews": 5, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["inconsistent", "insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "mediterranean-diet", "name": "Mediterranean diet", "category": "K", "category_name": "Dietary patterns", "aliases": ["MedDiet", "MeDi", "Mediterranean dietary pattern", "Mediterranean-style diet", "PREDIMED diet (MedDiet + extra-virgin olive oil or nuts)", "traditional Moroccan diet (regional variant; not formally equivalent)"], "food_sources": ["vegetables and fruit daily, several portions", "legumes (lentils, chickpeas, beans) several times per week", "whole or minimally refined cereals (bread, couscous, bulgur)", "extra-virgin olive oil as the main added fat (PREDIMED provided about 1 L per week per household)", "nuts and seeds (PREDIMED provided 30 g/day mixed walnuts, almonds, hazelnuts)", "fish and seafood at least twice per week", "moderate dairy (mainly yoghurt and cheese), poultry and eggs", "low intake of red and processed meat, sweets and sugar-sweetened drinks", "wine with meals in some scores (excluded or irrelevant for Muslim-majority and alcohol-free contexts; not a recommendation)"], "active_constituents": ["whole-diet pattern: no single active constituent; candidate contributors include monounsaturated fat (oleic acid), olive-oil phenolics (hydroxytyrosol, oleocanthal), long-chain omega-3 fatty acids from fish, ALA and polyphenols from nuts, folate, fibre, carotenoids and flavonoids from plants, low sodium-to-potassium ratio, low saturated fat and low added sugar", "displacement effect: less ultra-processed food, refined carbohydrate and saturated fat"], "one_line_verdict": "A Mediterranean dietary pattern is consistently associated with modestly lower dementia and cognitive-impairment risk in older-adult cohorts and has some, mostly small and inconsistent, randomised support in older adults at vascular risk, but there is essentially no adequate evidence that adopting it sharpens cognition in healthy young adults or students, and its strongest proven benefit remains cardiovascular.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. The best-established effect of a Mediterranean dietary pattern is cardiovascular: in PREDIMED, 7447 Spanish adults at high cardiovascular risk randomised to a MedDiet supplemented with extra-virgin olive oil (EVOO) or nuts had about 30% fewer major cardiovascular events than a low-fat control (HR 0.69 and 0.72), a result that survived the 2018 withdrawal and re-analysis prompted by randomisation irregularities at two sites [estruch2018, agarwal2019]. For cognition, randomised evidence comes almost entirely from older adults. PREDIMED sub-studies reported better cognition with MedDiet: in Barcelona (n=334 analysed of 447, median 4.1 y) the global composite was 0.05 z (EVOO) vs -0.38 z (control) [vallspedret2015]; in Navarra (n=522) MMSE was +0.62 points higher after 6.5 years, but cognition was measured only at the end and analysed with adjustment rather than as change [martinezlapiscina2013]. Both are secondary analyses of a trial whose randomisation was partly compromised, and JAMA Internal Medicine published a 2018 correction notice on derivative PREDIMED studies. Outside Spain, trials are smaller and mostly null: MedLey (n=137, 6 months, Australia) found no benefit on any domain [knight2016]; NU-AGE (n=1279, 1 year, five European countries) found no between-group difference, only an adherence-stratified (non-randomised) association [marseglia2018]; MedDairy (n=41) and MedPork (n=35) crossovers found processing-speed improvements among many tests, without multiplicity correction [wade2020, wade2019]; MedEx-UK (n=104) reported improved general cognition and memory as secondary outcomes [jennings2024]. Systematic reviews of RCTs describe mostly non-significant, small effects (only 8 of 66 outcomes favoured MedDiet) [raddvagenas2018] or isolated domain effects [loughrey2017]. Observational evidence is larger and consistent in direction: high vs low adherence is associated with lower cognitive impairment (RR 0.60) [psaltopoulou2013], dementia (HR 0.89) and Alzheimer disease (HR 0.70) [fekete2025], and in UK Biobank HR 0.77 independent of polygenic risk [shannon2023]. Influential cohorts were positive [scarmeas2006, scarmeas2009], but several large ones were null or nearly null [feart2009, haring2016, glans2023], and confounding by education, income and overall health behaviour is a major threat. In whom and how large. Effects, where present, are small (fractions of a standard deviation in composite scores) and seen in older adults over years. In young adults the evidence is one 10-day crossover in 24 women with mood and some memory improvements [lee2015], a narrative review of four short trials [esgunoglu2022], and very-low-certainty cross-sectional associations with university grades [peralmartinez2026]. The young-male depression trial AMMEND showed large mood benefit in a clinical population, not cognitive enhancement [bayes2022]. Unknown: whether the pattern causally improves cognition in healthy young or middle-aged adults, which components matter, and whether a traditional Moroccan diet behaves like the Spanish trial diet.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "The Mediterranean diet is a whole-diet pattern, so the relevant contrast is not deficiency correction of a single nutrient but replacement of a poorer diet (high in refined carbohydrate, saturated fat, sugar and processed meat) with a better one. Benefit, where demonstrated, is seen in older adults at elevated vascular risk compared with a low-fat or habitual diet over years [vallspedret2015, martinezlapiscina2013] and is plausibly mediated by vascular health. People who already eat a good diet gain little: MedLey and NU-AGE, in relatively healthy older adults, were null [knight2016, marseglia2018]. For healthy young adults or students with adequate diets there is no trial showing cognitive enhancement; one 10-day crossover in 24 young women reported better mood and some memory recall [lee2015]. The honest framing for Brain Food is 'a better overall diet supports long-term brain and vascular health', not 'a cognitive enhancer'.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Claims such as 'the Mediterranean diet boosts memory', 'prevents Alzheimer's' or 'brain food from the Mediterranean' overstate the evidence. The dementia-risk reductions (about 10-30%) come from observational cohorts subject to confounding, and some large cohorts are null [glans2023, haring2016]. The randomised cognitive findings come mainly from PREDIMED secondary analyses in older Spaniards at high cardiovascular risk, with a randomisation scandal in the parent trial [estruch2018, agarwal2019]. Trials outside Spain are mostly null or show isolated effects. No product can 'contain' the Mediterranean diet. A food that includes olive oil, nuts or legumes does not transfer the pattern's associations. In the EU no health claim exists for the pattern; only component claims (for example olive-oil polyphenols and blood lipids, walnuts and blood-vessel elasticity) are authorised, and none relates to cognition. Defensible communication: 'inspired by the Mediterranean dietary pattern, which is associated with cardiovascular health'. Any 'brain' or 'memory' claim would be non-compliant in the EU and weakly substantiated for FTC purposes.", "mechanism": {"summary": "The proposed brain effects of a Mediterranean diet are mostly indirect and vascular-metabolic: lower blood pressure, better lipid profile, improved endothelial function, lower insulin resistance and reduced cardiovascular events would reduce cerebrovascular injury, a major contributor to late-life cognitive decline and mixed dementia. Secondary hypotheses include reduced systemic inflammation and oxidative stress (olive-oil phenolics, plant polyphenols), omega-3 supply for neuronal membranes, and gut-microbiome changes. Human evidence supports the cardiovascular link; the steps from biomarkers to measured cognitive benefit in healthy people remain largely unproven.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Diet pattern adoption -> changed nutrient intake (more MUFA, fibre, polyphenols, omega-3; less SFA, sugar, sodium)", "detail": "Feasible in trials: MedLey raised MUFA from 13.2% to 19.7% of energy and lowered SFA from 12.1% to 9.1% [knight2016]; MedEx-UK raised adherence by 3.7 points on a 14-point scale [jennings2024].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Nutrient change -> lower cardiovascular risk (blood pressure, endothelial function, major cardiovascular events)", "detail": "PREDIMED re-analysis: HR 0.69 (EVOO) and 0.72 (nuts) for major cardiovascular events vs low-fat control [estruch2018]; cohort associations with lower stroke risk RR 0.71 [psaltopoulou2013].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Lower vascular risk -> less cerebrovascular damage (white-matter lesions, silent infarcts)", "detail": "Plausible and partly supported by blood-pressure trials in other contexts; direct MRI evidence from MedDiet trials is limited and not reviewed in detail here.", "evidence_level": "plausible"}, {"step": "Anti-inflammatory, antioxidant and microbiome effects -> reduced neuroinflammation", "detail": "Inflammatory and microbiome biomarker changes are reported in NU-AGE and MedLey sub-studies; links to neuroinflammation come mainly from animal models (for example EVOO in transgenic mice).", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Measured human cognitive outcome", "detail": "Small benefits in older adults at vascular risk in PREDIMED sub-studies [vallspedret2015, martinezlapiscina2013]; null in MedLey and NU-AGE [knight2016, marseglia2018]; no adequate trial in healthy young adults [lee2015].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["blood pressure and endothelial function (vascular brain health)", "lipid profile and atherosclerosis", "insulin sensitivity and glycaemic control", "systemic inflammation and oxidative stress (olive-oil phenolics, polyphenols)", "omega-3 supply for neuronal membranes", "gut microbiome composition (NU-AGE)", "displacement of ultra-processed foods"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Cognitively healthy older adults (55-80 y) at high cardiovascular risk, Spain (PREDIMED sub-studies)", "dose_or_intake": "MedDiet advice plus 1 L/week EVOO or 30 g/day mixed nuts vs low-fat advice", "duration": "4.1-6.5 years", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Barcelona sub-study: global composite change +0.05 z (EVOO) vs -0.38 z (control), frontal composite +0.23 vs -0.33; Navarra sub-study: MMSE +0.62 points and Clock Drawing +0.51 points higher with EVOO vs control at the end of follow-up.", "effect_estimate": "Global composite 0.05 (95% CI -0.11 to 0.21) vs -0.38 (-0.57 to -0.18), p=0.005 [vallspedret2015]; MMSE adjusted difference +0.62 (0.18 to 1.05) [martinezlapiscina2013]", "certainty_notes": "Downgraded for risk of bias (secondary analyses; 25% missing follow-up in Barcelona; Navarra had no baseline cognition; parent-trial randomisation irregularities and 2018 correction notice), indirectness (Spanish high-risk population, free EVOO/nuts), and inconsistency with null trials outside Spain. Not A or B.", "key_study_ids": ["vallspedret2015", "martinezlapiscina2013", "estruch2018", "agarwal2019", "raddvagenas2018"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_aging", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Healthy older adults outside Spain (Australia, five European countries, UK) without high-risk enrichment or with moderate risk", "dose_or_intake": "Dietitian-supported MedDiet (with or without food provision) vs habitual or low-fat diet", "duration": "8 weeks to 1 year", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "MedLey (n=137, 6 months) null on all domains; NU-AGE (n=1279, 1 year) no between-group difference; MedDairy and MedPork crossovers improved processing speed only, uncorrected for multiplicity; MedEx-UK (n=104, 24 weeks) improved general cognition and memory as secondary outcomes.", "effect_estimate": "MedLey overall adjusted difference +7.99 (95% CI -4.00 to 19.9), p=0.19 [knight2016]; MedPork processing speed difference 0.32 z (0.08 to 0.57) [wade2019]; MedEx-UK general cognition 0.22 (0.05 to 0.35) [jennings2024]", "certainty_notes": "Inconsistent results, small samples, open-label designs, many outcomes without correction; the only large trial (NU-AGE) was null on the randomised comparison. Grade C.", "key_study_ids": ["knight2016", "marseglia2018", "wade2020", "wade2019", "jennings2024", "loughrey2017"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "dementia_risk", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Middle-aged and older adults in prospective cohorts (USA, Europe, UK Biobank), mostly 55+ at baseline", "dose_or_intake": "Highest vs lowest adherence category on MedDiet scores (various scoring systems)", "duration": "4-27 years of follow-up", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Meta-analyses: cognitive impairment RR 0.60 (high adherence); dementia HR 0.89; AD HR 0.70; UK Biobank HR 0.77 (MEDAS). Several large cohorts null (Malmö, 20 y: HR 0.93, 0.75-1.15; WHIMS: no association; Three-City: no association with incident dementia).", "effect_estimate": "Cognitive impairment RR 0.60 (0.43-0.83) [psaltopoulou2013]; dementia HR 0.89 (0.83-0.95), AD HR 0.70 (0.60-0.82) [fekete2025]; HR 0.77 (0.65-0.91) [shannon2023]; Malmö HR 0.93 (0.75-1.15) [glans2023]", "certainty_notes": "Observational, starts low; residual confounding by education, socioeconomic status and health consciousness; heterogeneous scores; publication bias possible; null findings in large well-characterised cohorts. Biologically plausible and consistent in direction, so C rather than D.", "key_study_ids": ["psaltopoulou2013", "fekete2025", "shannon2023", "scarmeas2006", "glans2023", "haring2016", "feart2009"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_decline", "population": "older_adults_mci", "population_detail": "Older adults with mild cognitive impairment (progression to Alzheimer disease), New York multi-ethnic cohort", "dose_or_intake": "Highest vs lowest tertile of MeDi score", "duration": "mean 4.3 years", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "In 482 people with MCI, the highest adherence tertile had 48% lower risk of conversion to AD; a single cohort, not replicated in trials.", "effect_estimate": "HR 0.52 (95% CI 0.30-0.91) for MCI-to-AD conversion [scarmeas2009]", "certainty_notes": "Single observational cohort, wide CI, reverse causation possible (prodromal disease changes eating). Not applicable to healthy young adults.", "key_study_ids": ["scarmeas2009"]}, {"timeframe": "short_term_days", "domain": "episodic_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "24 healthy young women, Australia, randomised crossover", "dose_or_intake": "Switch to a Mediterranean-style diet vs usual diet", "duration": "10 days per condition", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "Improved memory recall and higher self-rated alertness and contentment, less confusion; effect sizes not reported in the abstract; an unblinded, single small study.", "effect_estimate": "not reported (significant p-values for memory recall and mood subscales) [lee2015]; narrative review of 4 short trials found improvements in attention, alertness and contentment [esgunoglu2022]", "certainty_notes": "One small unblinded crossover with many outcomes; subjective alertness is not cognition; very serious imprecision and risk of bias. Grade D: preliminary.", "key_study_ids": ["lee2015", "esgunoglu2022"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "learning", "population": "students", "population_detail": "University students (study-level mean age 19-25 y), 12 cross-sectional studies, 10,931 students", "dose_or_intake": "Self-reported MedDiet adherence (higher vs lower)", "duration": "not applicable (cross-sectional)", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "Higher adherence associated with better objective academic performance (grades, entrance scores), with extreme heterogeneity; the review authors rated certainty very low. Confounding by socioeconomic status, conscientiousness and sleep is likely.", "effect_estimate": "SMD 0.49 (95% CI 0.04 to 0.94), I2 98.2% for objective measures [peralmartinez2026]", "certainty_notes": "Cross-sectional only, no trials; very low GRADE certainty per the review itself; association cannot be read as cognitive enhancement.", "key_study_ids": ["peralmartinez2026"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "clinical_population", "population_detail": "Young men aged 18-25 with moderate-to-severe depression (AMMEND, Australia)", "dose_or_intake": "Dietitian-led MedDiet vs befriending control", "duration": "12 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Large reduction in BDI-II depressive symptoms vs befriending; open-label, single small trial; a clinical population, so it does not show enhancement in healthy students.", "effect_estimate": "BDI-II change difference 14.4 points (95% CI 11.41 to 17.39) [bayes2022]", "certainty_notes": "Open-label with a subjective outcome (high risk of performance bias); single trial; effect implausibly large compared with other diet-depression trials; indirect for cognition.", "key_study_ids": ["bayes2022"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "vascular_health", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Adults 55-80 y at high cardiovascular risk without cardiovascular disease, Spain", "dose_or_intake": "MedDiet + EVOO (1 L/week) or + nuts (30 g/day) vs low-fat advice", "duration": "median 4.8 years", "grade": "B", "direction": "benefit", "effect_summary": "About 30% relative reduction in major cardiovascular events (absolute 3.8% and 3.4% vs 4.4%); the result is robust to excluding 1588 participants with protocol deviations. Cohort meta-analysis: stroke RR 0.71.", "effect_estimate": "HR 0.69 (95% CI 0.53-0.91) EVOO; HR 0.72 (0.54-0.95) nuts [estruch2018]; stroke RR 0.71 (0.57-0.89) [psaltopoulou2013]", "certainty_notes": "A large RCT, downgraded one level for randomisation irregularities and single-country population; indirect for cognition (a vascular surrogate pathway, not a cognitive outcome).", "key_study_ids": ["estruch2018", "agarwal2019", "psaltopoulou2013"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "At-risk older adults in multidomain lifestyle trials in which diet is one of several components (Finland FINGER; US POINTER)", "dose_or_intake": "Dietary counselling (Nordic/MIND-style healthy diet) plus exercise, cognitive training and vascular monitoring", "duration": "2 years", "grade": "B", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Small benefit on global cognition composite for the whole package (FINGER: 0.022 z/year); the diet's isolated contribution cannot be separated.", "effect_estimate": "Between-group difference 0.022 z per year (95% CI 0.002-0.042) [ngandu2015]", "certainty_notes": "High-quality RCT evidence, but indirect for a Mediterranean diet as such (multidomain intervention); a small effect of uncertain clinical relevance.", "key_study_ids": ["ngandu2015"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Cognitively healthy adults (mostly older) in RCTs of various dietary patterns pooled", "dose_or_intake": "Mediterranean, calorie-restricted, low-carbohydrate and healthy-lifestyle diets pooled", "duration": "variable (weeks to years)", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Pooled dietary interventions produced a very small improvement in overall cognition, executive function and processing speed; Mediterranean diet not isolated; young adults poorly represented.", "effect_estimate": "Overall cognition SMD 0.14 (95% CI 0.08-0.20); executive function SMD -0.10 (-0.17 to -0.04, lower = better); processing speed SMD -0.16 (-0.23 to -0.09) [guo2025]", "certainty_notes": "Heterogeneous interventions pooled; PREDIMED dominates; indirect for a specific MedDiet product; practical significance trivial.", "key_study_ids": ["guo2025"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "Single Mediterranean-style meal", "onset": "not studied in eligible trials located", "duration_studied": "not applicable", "domain": "any", "population": "healthy young adults", "effect_estimate": "No acute single-meal cognitive trial of a Mediterranean meal pattern was identified in this search; acute effects of individual components (glucose, caffeine, flavanols) are covered in other monographs.", "certainty": "insufficient", "safety": "not applicable", "study_ids": ["esgunoglu2022"]}, {"dose": "Full Mediterranean-style diet (switch from habitual diet)", "onset": "within 10 days", "duration_studied": "10 days", "domain": "episodic memory, self-rated alertness, mood", "population": "24 healthy young women", "effect_estimate": "Significant improvement in memory recall and self-rated alertness/contentment; effect sizes not reported in abstract", "certainty": "very low (D)", "safety": "no adverse events reported", "study_ids": ["lee2015"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "4.1-6.5 years", "population": "older Spaniards at high cardiovascular risk", "outcome": "global cognition, frontal composite, MMSE", "effect_estimate": "Global composite 0.05 vs -0.38 z (EVOO vs control); MMSE +0.62 (0.18-1.05)", "evidence_quality": "low (C)", "limitations": "Secondary analyses; attrition; no baseline cognition in Navarra; randomisation irregularities in the parent trial", "study_ids": ["vallspedret2015", "martinezlapiscina2013"]}, {"duration": "6 months - 1 year", "population": "healthy older adults, Australia and Europe", "outcome": "multi-domain cognitive battery", "effect_estimate": "Null: MedLey overall +7.99 (-4.00 to 19.9); NU-AGE no between-group difference", "evidence_quality": "moderate for null in this population", "limitations": "Short duration for an ageing outcome; control groups also improved diet", "study_ids": ["knight2016", "marseglia2018"]}, {"duration": "8 weeks per arm (crossover)", "population": "adults 45-80 at cardiovascular risk, Australia", "outcome": "processing speed, mood", "effect_estimate": "Processing speed 0.32 z (0.08 to 0.57) with MedPork; MedDairy processing speed p=0.04; mood improved", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "n=35-41; no multiplicity correction; commodity-funded (Pork CRC for MedPork)", "study_ids": ["wade2019", "wade2020"]}, {"duration": "4-27 years follow-up", "population": "middle-aged and older adults in cohorts", "outcome": "incident dementia, Alzheimer disease, cognitive impairment", "effect_estimate": "Dementia HR 0.89 (0.83-0.95); AD HR 0.70 (0.60-0.82); null in Malmö HR 0.93 (0.75-1.15)", "evidence_quality": "low (observational)", "limitations": "Confounding by SES and education; self-reported diet; heterogeneous scores", "study_ids": ["fekete2025", "shannon2023", "glans2023", "haring2016"]}, {"duration": "12 weeks", "population": "young men 18-25 with depression", "outcome": "depressive symptoms (BDI-II)", "effect_estimate": "-14.4 points (11.41 to 17.39) vs befriending", "evidence_quality": "low", "limitations": "Open-label, subjective outcome, single trial, clinical population", "study_ids": ["bayes2022"]}], "safety": {"summary": "As a whole-diet pattern built from ordinary foods, the Mediterranean diet has an excellent safety profile; large trials (PREDIMED, NU-AGE) report no diet-attributable harm in the abstracts reviewed. Risks are component-specific: tree-nut and fish/shellfish allergy, energy density of olive oil and nuts if not substituted, alcohol (wine) where it is included in scores, mercury in large predatory fish, and sodium in cheese, olives and bread. In Morocco, the traditional pattern carries specific weaknesses: high added sugar (sweet mint tea) and refined bread.", "adverse_effects": ["Allergic reactions to tree nuts, peanuts, sesame, fish or shellfish in sensitised people", "Weight gain possible if olive oil and nuts are added without substitution (not seen in PREDIMED)", "Gastrointestinal discomfort from increased legume and fibre intake (transient)", "Alcohol-related harm if wine is promoted; not recommended for a brain-health brand"], "contraindications": ["Known allergy to nuts, fish, sesame or other included foods", "Alcohol component inappropriate for pregnancy, adolescents, people with alcohol-use disorder, liver disease, many medications, and religious abstainers"], "drug_interactions": ["Warfarin: large changes in leafy green vegetable (vitamin K) intake can alter INR; keep intake consistent and inform the clinician", "Alcohol component interacts with sedatives, metformin, many psychotropics"], "upper_limits": "No upper limit applies to the pattern; component limits apply (for example, fish advice on mercury for pregnancy and children; sodium below 2 g/day WHO guidance).", "sleep_impact": "No adverse effect on sleep expected; no caffeine content per se.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Yes: PREDIMED median 4.8 years (n=7447) and lifelong traditional exposure in Mediterranean populations.", "daily_use_appropriate": "yes", "special_populations": "Pregnancy: suitable with mercury-aware fish choices and no alcohol. Children and adolescents: suitable as a food pattern; no cognitive claims should be made. Chronic kidney disease: potassium and phosphorus load from legumes, nuts and vegetables may need dietitian adjustment. Depression: diet can be an adjunct, not a substitute for treatment [bayes2022]. Eating disorders: avoid restrictive messaging.", "specialist_review_needed": ["Registered dietitian for CKD, anticoagulant users and food allergies", "Allergen-management review for any nut or sesame product"], "hard_safety_gate": "pass", "gate_reason": "Food-based pattern with long-term large-trial exposure and no diet-attributable harm; component allergens and alcohol must be handled through labelling and by excluding alcohol from any brand messaging."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "A dietary pattern is not a food or ingredient and has no authorised health claim under Regulation (EC) 1924/2006. Component claims exist in the EU Register (Regulation (EU) 432/2012), including 'Olive oil polyphenols contribute to the protection of blood lipids from oxidative stress' (at least 5 mg hydroxytyrosol and derivatives per 20 g olive oil) and 'Walnuts contribute to the improvement of the elasticity of the blood vessels' (30 g/day). Wording and conditions were taken from secondary sources found by web search on 2026-10-25 and must be checked against the official EU Register. No claim relating the pattern or its components to cognition, memory or dementia is authorised (DHA 'maintenance of normal brain function' at 250 mg/day is a separate nutrient claim). Generic descriptors such as 'Mediterranean-style' are permitted, but implied brain benefits would count as health claims.", "us": "Dietary patterns can be described in labelling and advertising. The Dietary Guidelines for Americans include a 'Healthy Mediterranean-Style Dietary Pattern' (not re-verified in this unit). No FDA-authorised health claim exists for the pattern. Qualified health claims exist for some components (nuts and walnuts for heart disease, oleic-acid oils); exact current wording was not verified here. Any cognitive or dementia-prevention claim would be a disease claim (not allowed on food) or would need FTC 'competent and reliable scientific evidence', which the current RCT base in healthy adults does not provide.", "morocco": "Morocco co-holds the UNESCO intangible heritage inscription of the Mediterranean diet (2010 inscription; 2013 enlarged listing; emblematic community Chefchaouen), which supports cultural positioning. Food safety and labelling fall under Law 28-07 and ONSSA. Specific provisions on nutrition and health claims were not verified in this unit; status is uncertain. Treat any brain-health claim as requiring prior ONSSA consultation.", "permitted_claims_examples": ["EU (component, olive oil with at least 5 mg hydroxytyrosol and derivatives per 20 g): 'Olive oil polyphenols contribute to the protection of blood lipids from oxidative stress' (wording from secondary sources; confirm in EU Register)", "EU (component, 30 g walnuts/day): 'Walnuts contribute to the improvement of the elasticity of the blood vessels' (wording from secondary sources; confirm in EU Register)"], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "None for conventional foods of the pattern. Concentrated olive-leaf or hydroxytyrosol extracts may raise novel food (EU) or NDI (US) questions and are outside the scope of this monograph.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "The pattern itself cannot be put into one product, but Brain Food can build products that are building blocks of the pattern: legume-based meals, nut and seed mixes, EVOO-based dressings, whole-grain couscous or bread, sardine or mackerel preparations, and low-sugar mint tea alternatives for Morocco.", "formats": ["ready meals or meal kits (tagine with legumes and vegetables, EVOO)", "unsalted mixed-nut portions (30 g)", "high-polyphenol EVOO (claim-eligible if at least 5 mg hydroxytyrosol per 20 g)", "canned oily fish products", "whole-grain couscous and breads", "unsweetened or lightly sweetened mint tea"], "stability": "EVOO phenolics degrade with light, heat and time (claim compliance must hold to end of shelf life); nuts are prone to rancidity (oxygen barrier packaging needed); oily fish requires a cold chain unless canned.", "taste": "Highly palatable and culturally familiar in Morocco and southern Europe; bitterness and pungency of high-phenolic EVOO may limit acceptance.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Whole-food matrix; fat improves carotenoid absorption from vegetables; no supplement-style bioavailability issues.", "dose_feasibility": "The studied 'dose' is a whole diet plus 30 g nuts/day or about 50 g EVOO/day (1 L/week per household). 30 g nuts fits a snack pack; EVOO fits dressings. A single product cannot reproduce the trial intervention.", "cost_availability": "Ingredients widely available in Morocco and the EU; EVOO and nuts are relatively expensive; fish availability varies.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 4, "effect_magnitude": 3, "safety": 9, "reproducibility": 3, "food_suitability": 8, "cost_access": 6, "rationale": "Evidence quality 4: grade C for cognitive outcomes in older adults (PREDIMED sub-studies with integrity concerns, null trials elsewhere), D for young adults; B only for the vascular surrogate. Effect magnitude 3: composite differences of fractions of an SD; observational risk reductions of 10-30% that may be confounded. Safety 9: whole-food pattern with long-term trial exposure; allergen and alcohol caveats. Reproducibility 3: positive RCT results confined to Spanish PREDIMED sites; MedLey and NU-AGE null; cohorts split. Food suitability 8: entirely food-based and culturally native to Morocco, though not reducible to a single product. Cost/access 6: available but EVOO, nuts and fish cost more than refined staples."}, "research_gaps": ["No adequately powered RCT of a Mediterranean diet on cognition in healthy young adults, students or knowledge workers", "No trial of the traditional Moroccan diet (or a low-sugar Moroccan variant) on any cognitive outcome", "Independent replication of PREDIMED cognitive findings with properly randomised designs and baseline cognition", "Component dismantling: which elements (EVOO phenolics, nuts, fish, legumes, sugar reduction) carry any effect", "Pre-registered acute or short-term trials measuring objective attention and memory, not just self-rated alertness", "Mendelian randomisation or negative-control analyses to address socioeconomic confounding in cohorts"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 5.3, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "pass", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": "B", "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": "B", "best_grade_overall": "B", "benefit_domains": {"global_cognition": "B", "dementia_risk": "C", "executive_function": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 10, "n_studies": 28, "n_verified": 28, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 7, "n_systematic_reviews": 8, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["benefit", "inconsistent", "insufficient", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "plant-forward-diets", "name": "Plant-forward, vegetarian and vegan diets", "category": "K", "category_name": "Dietary patterns", "aliases": ["plant-based diet", "vegetarian diet", "lacto-ovo-vegetarian", "vegan diet", "flexitarian / plant-forward diet", "healthful plant-based diet index (hPDI)", "plant-based diet index (PDI)", "unhealthful plant-based diet index (uPDI)", "planetary health diet"], "food_sources": ["legumes and pulses (lentils, chickpeas, beans, fava, soy)", "whole grains", "vegetables, leafy greens and fruit", "nuts and seeds", "plant oils (olive oil)", "in lacto-ovo variants: dairy and eggs", "note: refined grains, sugary drinks, sweets and fried potatoes are also plant foods and score as 'unhealthful' in uPDI"], "active_constituents": ["not a single constituent: a pattern defined by high plant-food and low animal-food intake", "fibre, polyphenols, folate, magnesium, potassium, unsaturated fat (putative beneficial components)", "nutrients at risk of shortfall: vitamin B12, long-chain omega-3 (DHA/EPA), iron, zinc, iodine, choline, creatine, calcium, vitamin D"], "one_line_verdict": "Healthy plant-forward eating (high hPDI) is associated in large cohorts of middle-aged and older adults with roughly 15-25% lower risk of dementia and cognitive impairment, whereas 'unhealthful' plant-based eating and simple vegetarian/non-vegetarian labels show no consistent benefit; there are no randomised trials of plant-based diets with cognitive outcomes in healthy young adults, and the main brain-relevant risk of strict vegan diets (vitamin B12 deficiency) is preventable but real.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is shown. All human evidence linking plant-forward diets to cognition is observational. Meta-analyses of prospective cohorts in middle-aged and older adults find that higher adherence to the healthful plant-based diet index (hPDI) is associated with lower dementia risk (HR 0.85, 95% CI 0.75-0.97, I2 0%, two cohorts [bigras2025]; RR 0.85, 0.76-0.96 [bizzozeroperoni2025]) and lower risk of cognitive decline or impairment (RR 0.74, 0.64-0.85 [bizzozeroperoni2025]; pooled RR 0.74, 0.56-0.97, I2 92% [tsai2026]). The overall plant-based index (PDI), which does not distinguish refined from whole plant foods, is not associated with dementia (HR 1.03, 0.91-1.17 [bigras2025]; HR 1.03, 0.87-1.23 in 180,532 UK Biobank participants [wu2023]), and the unhealthful index (uPDI) is associated with higher risk (HR 1.17, 1.03-1.33 [bigras2025]; 1.29, 1.08-1.53 [wu2023]). Diet quality, not the absence of meat, carries the signal. Dichotomous vegetarian versus non-vegetarian comparisons are inconsistent: a Taiwanese Buddhist cohort found HR 0.67 (0.45-0.996) for dementia [tsai2022], whereas another Taiwanese cohort reported HR 1.68 (1.03-2.75) and the Adventist Health Study-2 found no difference in any cognitive domain (n = 132) [gatto2021]; these are tabulated in the full-text review [bigras2025]. An earlier meta-analysis found no association of vegetarian/vegan diets with cognitive impairment but a higher odds of depression (OR 2.14, 1.11-4.15) [iguacel2021]. In US health-professional cohorts (159,347 people, mean age 44), hPDI was associated with lower subjective cognitive decline (RR 0.76 for 90th vs 10th percentile), but DASH showed stronger associations and the objective difference in global cognition was tiny (z 0.05 for DASH) [chen2026]. A cross-sectional NHANES analysis linked a plant-based macronutrient pattern to better memory and executive function in older adults [ramey2022]. Certainty. GRADE starts low for observational data; residual confounding (education, wealth, health consciousness, religion), reverse causation (prodromal dementia changes diet), FFQ measurement error, high heterogeneity and the small number of contributing cohorts keep the grade at C for dementia/decline in older adults. The systematic review of interventions found no trial demonstrating a causal effect of plant-based diets on cognition [medawar2019], and a 2023 expert review confirms there are no RCT data on cognitive impairment [wang2023]. Young adults and students. No trial or cohort tests cognitive performance in healthy young adults; evidence is insufficient (grade D at best). The nutrient-gap literature is the main young-adult issue: vegans are at risk of B12, DHA, iron, zinc, iodine and choline shortfall, which can harm neurological function when deficiency develops [clementesuarez2025, wang2023]; see the vitamin-b12, iron and omega-3-dha-epa monographs. Creatine is the commonly cited 'vegetarian brain' nutrient, but the largest preregistered trial found no greater cognitive benefit of creatine in vegetarians than omnivores [sandkuhler2023], contradicting a 5-day trial [benton2011]. Practical relevance. Effect sizes are modest relative risks for late-life outcomes measured decades after diet assessment; for a student or knowledge worker the honest message is that a whole-food, plant-rich pattern is compatible with long-term brain health, provided B12 and other gap nutrients are secured, not that going vegan sharpens thinking.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "The plant-forward literature is not about enhancement. The associations with lower dementia risk concern long-term disease prevention in middle-aged and older adults and depend on diet quality (hPDI, not PDI or uPDI) [bigras2025, wu2023]. The clearest causal brain effect in this domain is negative and deficiency-mediated: strict vegan diets without fortified foods or supplements lead to vitamin B12 deficiency, which causes neurological and cognitive damage, and also risk low DHA, iron, zinc, iodine and choline status [clementesuarez2025, wang2023]. Correcting these deficits restores function; nothing indicates that a well-planned plant diet improves cognition in already well-nourished young adults, and creatine supplementation does not preferentially benefit vegetarians in the largest trial [sandkuhler2023]. Children, pregnant women and older adults are distinct populations not addressed by the adult evidence here.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Claims such as 'plant-based food boosts brain power', 'vegan diets improve focus' or 'eat plants to prevent Alzheimer's' overstate the evidence. What can be said honestly: large observational studies associate healthy plant-rich diets (vegetables, legumes, whole grains, nuts, olive oil) with lower risk of later-life cognitive decline and dementia, while plant diets dominated by refined starch and sugar are associated with higher risk. No trial shows a cognitive benefit, and disease-risk-reduction claims for dementia are not authorised in the EU and would be prohibited disease claims for US conventional foods. Brain Food can legitimately market plant-forward meals that also provide B12, iodine, iron and (algal) DHA, using authorised nutrient claims (e.g. 'Vitamin B12 contributes to normal psychological function' when conditions are met), and should not imply vegan diets are cognitively superior.", "mechanism": {"summary": "Proposed pathways are indirect: replacement of processed meat and refined foods by fibre-, polyphenol- and unsaturated-fat-rich foods improves blood pressure, LDL-cholesterol, glycaemia, body weight and inflammation, which are established vascular risk factors for dementia; the gut microbiome has been proposed as a further mediator. Opposing pathways are nutrient shortfalls (B12, DHA, iron, iodine, zinc, choline, creatine). Neither direction has been linked to measured cognitive change in a trial.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Plant-forward diet -> higher fibre, polyphenol, potassium and unsaturated fat; lower saturated fat, haem iron and processed meat", "detail": "Compositional change documented in dietary surveys and trials [wang2023, medawar2019].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Improved cardiometabolic risk factors (weight, LDL-C, HbA1c, blood pressure, inflammation)", "detail": "RCTs of vegetarian/vegan diets show reductions in weight, LDL-C, HbA1c and blood pressure [wang2023]; robust short-to-moderate term benefits on weight, energy metabolism and inflammation [medawar2019].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Lower vascular and metabolic burden on the brain -> slower cognitive ageing / lower dementia incidence", "detail": "Plausible via vascular risk-factor control; supported only by cohort associations with hPDI [bigras2025, bizzozeroperoni2025, wu2023].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Gut microbiome modulation -> gut-brain signalling", "detail": "Microbiome shifts shown; functional 'bottom-up' signalling to cognition remains highly speculative [medawar2019].", "evidence_level": "speculative"}, {"step": "Opposing pathway: inadequate B12, DHA, iron, iodine, zinc, choline -> neurological dysfunction", "detail": "Deficiency-mediated harm is established for B12 and iron in general populations; vegans are at elevated risk if not supplemented [clementesuarez2025].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Measured human cognitive outcome", "detail": "Only observational: lower dementia/cognitive impairment risk with high hPDI; no RCT outcome in any population [medawar2019, wang2023].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["vascular risk factor control (blood pressure, LDL-C, glycaemia)", "anti-inflammatory and antioxidant dietary components", "gut microbiome (speculative)", "nutrient shortfall pathway (B12, DHA, iron, iodine, zinc, choline, creatine)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "dementia_risk", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Cognitively healthy middle-aged and older adults (baseline ~50-80 y) in UK, US, Chinese and Taiwanese cohorts", "dose_or_intake": "Highest vs lowest quartile/quintile of hPDI", "duration": "Follow-up 2-20 years", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Healthful plant-based diet adherence associated with ~15% lower incident dementia; overall PDI null; unhealthful PDI associated with higher risk.", "effect_estimate": "hPDI HR 0.85 (0.75-0.97, I2 0%) [bigras2025]; RR 0.85 (0.76-0.96) [bizzozeroperoni2025]; UK Biobank hPDI HR 0.82 (0.68-0.98), PDI 1.03 (0.87-1.23), uPDI 1.29 (1.08-1.53) [wu2023]", "certainty_notes": "Observational only (start low); few cohorts per pooled estimate; residual confounding and reverse causation likely; consistent direction for hPDI and uPDI gives modest credibility. Not upgraded.", "key_study_ids": ["bigras2025", "bizzozeroperoni2025", "wu2023", "tsai2026"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_decline", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Older adults without cognitive impairment at baseline", "dose_or_intake": "Highest vs lowest PDI/hPDI adherence", "duration": "Follow-up years", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Higher plant-based adherence associated with lower risk of cognitive impairment/decline, with very high heterogeneity.", "effect_estimate": "Cognitive impairment OR 0.68 (0.62-0.75, I2 84%) for hPDI and 0.61 (0.55-0.68, I2 97%) for PDI [bigras2025]; cognitive decline RR 0.74 (0.64-0.85) [bizzozeroperoni2025]; pooled RR 0.74 (0.56-0.97, I2 92%) [tsai2026]", "certainty_notes": "Downgraded for inconsistency (I2 84-97%) and indirectness of MMSE-type outcomes; mostly Chinese cohorts (CLHLS overlap across reviews); possible overlap of studies between meta-analyses.", "key_study_ids": ["bigras2025", "bizzozeroperoni2025", "tsai2026"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "dementia_risk", "population": "vegetarian_vegan", "population_detail": "Self-identified vegetarians vs non-vegetarians (Taiwan Buddhist cohorts, Adventists, South Asian samples)", "dose_or_intake": "Vegetarian (no meat/fish) vs non-vegetarian", "duration": "Follow-up 2.6-10 years", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Dichotomous vegetarian status shows conflicting associations: lower dementia risk in Tzu Chi cohort, higher in another Taiwanese cohort, null in Adventist Health Study-2; earlier meta-analysis found no association with cognitive impairment.", "effect_estimate": "HR 0.67 (0.45-0.996) [tsai2022]; HR 1.68 (1.03-2.75) Fan 2023 as tabulated in [bigras2025]; AHS-2 mild memory impairment OR 1.46 (0.51-4.21) [gatto2021]; no pooled association [iguacel2021]", "certainty_notes": "Inconsistent, imprecise, confounded by religion/lifestyle; exposure classification crude.", "key_study_ids": ["tsai2022", "gatto2021", "bigras2025", "iguacel2021"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_aging", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "US health professionals, mean age 44 at analysis start (NHS, NHSII, HPFS)", "dose_or_intake": "90th vs 10th percentile hPDI", "duration": "Decades of repeated FFQ", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Higher hPDI associated with lower subjective cognitive decline; DASH more strongly; objective cognitive differences very small.", "effect_estimate": "SCD RR 0.76 (0.65-0.85) for hPDI; DASH objective global cognition z +0.05 (0.02-0.09) [chen2026]", "certainty_notes": "Subjective outcome (self-report) in most participants; observational; tiny objective effect size.", "key_study_ids": ["chen2026", "ramey2022"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "General adult populations, cross-sectional and cohort", "dose_or_intake": "Vegetarian/vegan status or hPDI adherence", "duration": "Cross-sectional to years", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Vegetarian status linked to higher depression odds in one meta-analysis, whereas healthy plant-based index adherence linked to lower depression risk in another; unhealthy plant-based index linked to more depression/anxiety.", "effect_estimate": "Vegetarian depression OR 2.14 (1.11-4.15) [iguacel2021]; hPDI depression RR 0.77 (0.67-0.88) cohorts [bizzozeroperoni2025]; UK Biobank hPDI HR 0.92 (0.85-0.99), uPDI 1.15 (1.07-1.24) [wu2023]", "certainty_notes": "Observational, reverse causation likely (people with depression may adopt diets), heterogeneity large; exposure definitions differ.", "key_study_ids": ["iguacel2021", "bizzozeroperoni2025", "wu2023"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy young adults and students", "dose_or_intake": "Any plant-based pattern", "duration": "Not studied", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "No RCT or adequately powered cohort tests cognitive performance in healthy young adults following plant-based diets; systematic review of interventions found no causal cognitive evidence.", "effect_estimate": "not reported", "certainty_notes": "Absence of evidence; extrapolation from late-life dementia cohorts to student performance is not justified.", "key_study_ids": ["medawar2019", "wang2023"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "vegetarian_vegan", "population_detail": "Young adult vegetarians receiving creatine (testing whether a plant-diet gap nutrient limits cognition)", "dose_or_intake": "Creatine 5 g/day (6 wk) or 20 g/day (5 d)", "duration": "5 days - 6 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "A 5-day trial found memory benefit only in vegetarians; the largest preregistered trial (n=123) found no diet-by-creatine interaction.", "effect_estimate": "Diet x supplement interaction not significant (BDS p=0.81) [sandkuhler2023]; positive subgroup in [benton2011]", "certainty_notes": "Inconsistent; see creatine monograph for full appraisal.", "key_study_ids": ["sandkuhler2023", "benton2011"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "Single plant-based vs animal-based meal", "onset": "Hours", "duration_studied": "Not studied for cognition", "domain": "other", "population": "healthy adults", "effect_estimate": "No acute cognitive trial identified; acute effects of meals depend on size and macronutrients (see meal-composition-size-and-timing monograph)", "certainty": "insufficient", "safety": "No acute concerns", "study_ids": []}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "2-20 y follow-up", "population": "Middle-aged/older adults", "outcome": "Incident dementia (hPDI)", "effect_estimate": "HR 0.82-0.85 highest vs lowest", "evidence_quality": "C (observational, consistent for hPDI)", "limitations": "Confounding, reverse causation, few cohorts", "study_ids": ["bigras2025", "bizzozeroperoni2025", "wu2023"]}, {"duration": "Years", "population": "Older adults", "outcome": "Cognitive impairment/decline (PDI/hPDI)", "effect_estimate": "RR/OR 0.61-0.74, I2 84-97%", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "Very high heterogeneity, mostly Chinese cohorts", "study_ids": ["bigras2025", "tsai2026"]}, {"duration": "2.6-10 y", "population": "Vegetarians vs non-vegetarians", "outcome": "Dementia / mild memory impairment", "effect_estimate": "HR 0.67 to 1.68 (conflicting); AHS-2 null", "evidence_quality": "C (inconsistent)", "limitations": "Crude exposure, religious cohorts", "study_ids": ["tsai2022", "gatto2021", "bigras2025"]}, {"duration": "Decades", "population": "US health professionals", "outcome": "Subjective cognitive decline", "effect_estimate": "hPDI RR 0.76 (0.65-0.85)", "evidence_quality": "C", "limitations": "Self-reported outcome", "study_ids": ["chen2026"]}], "safety": {"summary": "Well-planned vegetarian and vegan diets are safe for adults, but strict vegan diets without fortified foods or supplements reliably produce vitamin B12 deficiency over time, with risk of irreversible neurological damage; DHA, iodine, iron, zinc, calcium, vitamin D and choline need planning. Plant-based diets dominated by refined starches and sugars (high uPDI) are associated with worse outcomes.", "adverse_effects": ["vitamin B12 deficiency (megaloblastic anaemia, neuropathy, cognitive and mood disturbance) if unsupplemented", "low iron stores and iron-deficiency anaemia, especially in menstruating women", "low long-chain omega-3 (DHA/EPA) status", "iodine insufficiency where iodised salt or seaweed are not used", "possible lower bone mineral density/fracture risk with low calcium intake", "gastrointestinal discomfort with abrupt increases in fibre/legumes"], "contraindications": ["strict vegan diets in infants and young children without paediatric dietitian supervision", "eating disorders (restrictive diets can mask or reinforce restriction)", "favism (G6PD deficiency) for fava-bean-based dishes"], "drug_interactions": ["metformin and proton pump inhibitors further lower B12 status in vegetarians", "high vitamin K leafy greens: keep intake consistent with warfarin", "high-potassium plant diets with ACE inhibitors/potassium-sparing diuretics in kidney disease"], "upper_limits": "No upper limit for a dietary pattern; supplements used to close gaps follow their nutrient ULs (e.g. iodine 600 ug/day EU adult UL; iron, zinc ULs per EFSA/IOM).", "sleep_impact": "No evidence of sleep effects specific to plant-based diets.", "long_term_exposure_studied": "Decades in cohorts (Adventists, EPIC-Oxford, Tzu Chi, UK Biobank) for disease outcomes; no long-term trials with cognitive endpoints.", "daily_use_appropriate": "yes", "special_populations": "Pregnant and breastfeeding women, infants, children and adolescents need specialist planning (B12, iodine, DHA, iron); older adults on metformin/PPIs need B12 monitoring; people with CKD need potassium/phosphorus advice.", "specialist_review_needed": ["registered dietitian (nutrient adequacy of vegan menus)", "paediatric dietitian for any child-targeted product", "clinical nutrition review of B12 fortification levels"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "Pass for plant-forward meals that include or are paired with reliable B12 (and attention to iodine, iron, DHA); fail for unsupplemented strict vegan programmes marketed for brain health."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Ordinary foods; no novel-food issue for traditional plant foods (novel protein isolates or algal oils require separate checks; algal DHA oils from Schizochytrium are authorised novel foods per general knowledge, not re-verified). No health claim exists for plant-based or vegetarian diets and cognition. 'Vegetarian'/'vegan' labels are not harmonised in EU law (FIC Art. 36 implementing act never adopted; not re-verified). Nutrient claims under Reg. (EU) 432/2012 may be used when conditions are met, e.g. 'Vitamin B12 contributes to normal psychological function' and 'Iron contributes to normal cognitive function' (food at least a source; wording taken from the project's vitamin-b12/iron monographs, not re-fetched here). Dementia risk-reduction claims would need Article 14 authorisation; none exists.", "us": "Conventional foods; 'plant-based' and 'vegan' are not FDA-defined terms. Structure/function statements must derive from nutritive value; claims that plant-based diets prevent dementia are disease claims. FTC substantiation for 'improves focus/memory' would not be met by observational data.", "morocco": "Ordinary foods under Law 28-07 and Decree 2-12-389 (ONSSA); no specific rule on vegetarian/vegan labelling or cognitive claims found; claim framework uncertain (see project unit on Moroccan regulation).", "permitted_claims_examples": ["EU (Reg. 432/2012, if food is at least a source of B12): 'Vitamin B12 contributes to normal psychological function'", "EU (Reg. 432/2012, if food is at least a source of iron): 'Iron contributes to normal cognitive function'"], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "None for whole plant foods. Algal DHA, novel plant protein isolates or fungal proteins need individual novel-food/GRAS checks (not researched here).", "regulatory_gate": "pass"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "High: the 'intervention' is food itself (legume, whole-grain, vegetable and nut-based meals). Moroccan cuisine (bissara, harira with lentils/chickpeas, vegetable tagines, whole-wheat bread, olive oil) is naturally plant-forward and maps onto high hPDI.", "formats": ["ready meals and bowls (legumes, whole grains, vegetables)", "soups (harira, bissara)", "snack packs (nuts, seeds)", "B12/iodine/DHA-fortified plant drinks or spreads"], "stability": "Whole foods: normal shelf-life considerations; B12 (cyanocobalamin) stable in fortified foods; algal DHA prone to oxidation (needs antioxidants/packaging).", "taste": "Generally acceptable; legume and whole-grain textures need culinary development.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Non-haem iron and zinc absorption reduced by phytates (soaking, fermentation, vitamin C help); ALA-to-DHA conversion low (<10%); plant foods contain no reliable B12.", "dose_feasibility": "The 'dose' is dietary pattern adherence (high hPDI), achievable through daily meals; nutrient gaps (B12 2.5-4 ug/day, DHA 250 mg/day) can be closed by fortification in a serving.", "cost_availability": "Legumes, grains and vegetables are low-cost and widely available in Morocco, EU and US.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 3.5, "effect_magnitude": 3, "safety": 8, "reproducibility": 4, "food_suitability": 9, "cost_access": 9, "rationale": "Evidence quality 3.5: grade C, observational only, for late-life dementia/decline; D/insufficient for young adults. Effect magnitude 3: relative risks ~0.75-0.85 for distant outcomes and trivial objective cognitive differences. Safety 8: safe when B12 and other gaps are covered (conditional gate). Reproducibility 4: hPDI/uPDI direction consistent, vegetarian status inconsistent. Food suitability 9 and cost/access 9: ordinary inexpensive foods, culturally native to Morocco."}, "research_gaps": ["No RCT of a plant-based diet with cognitive endpoints in any population; a 6-12 month trial in young adults with B12/DHA-adequate arms is needed.", "Separate effects of diet quality from meat exclusion (hPDI vs vegetarian label) in Western and North African cohorts.", "Cognitive and mood effects of vegan diets in students with verified B12, iron, iodine and DHA status.", "Reverse-causation-robust analyses (lagged exposure, Mendelian randomisation) for plant-based indices and dementia.", "Moroccan/Mediterranean plant-forward meal patterns and cognitive ageing data."], "_derived": {"composite_score": 5.45, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "pass", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": null, "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"dementia_risk": "C", "cognitive_decline": "C", "cognitive_aging": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 7, "n_studies": 14, "n_verified": 14, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 4, "n_systematic_reviews": 4, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["inconsistent", "insufficient", "possible_benefit"]}}, {"id": "time-restricted-eating-and-intermittent-fasting", "name": "Time-restricted eating, intermittent fasting and Ramadan fasting", "category": "K", "category_name": "Dietary patterns", "aliases": ["time-restricted eating (TRE)", "time-restricted feeding (TRF)", "16:8 fasting", "early TRE (eTRE)", "intermittent fasting (IF)", "5:2 diet", "alternate-day fasting (ADF)", "intermittent energy restriction", "Ramadan diurnal intermittent fasting (RDIF)", "Sunnah fasting (Monday/Thursday)", "sawm"], "food_sources": ["Meal-timing pattern, not a food", "TRE: daily eating window typically 6-10 h", "5:2 / ADF: ~500-600 kcal or zero-energy days", "Ramadan: no food or water from dawn (fajr) to sunset (maghrib) for 29-30 days; meals at suhoor (pre-dawn) and iftar (sunset); in Morocco typically harira, dates, milk, eggs, msemen/chebakia, mint tea"], "active_constituents": ["Prolonged post-absorptive state: lower insulin, mild ketosis (BHB ~0.3-0.5 mmol/L after 16+ h)", "Usually reduced total energy intake and weight loss", "Ramadan: daytime fluid restriction, shifted circadian meal timing, reduced/fragmented sleep"], "one_line_verdict": "Time-restricted eating and intermittent fasting neither enhance nor impair cognition in healthy young adults (10-day 16:8 RCT: no objective difference, only a subjective late-morning concentration dip), may modestly help some insulin-resistant or MCI older adults (low certainty), and Ramadan's main cognitive cost comes from about one hour less sleep a night and afternoon alertness dips rather than from the fasting itself.", "scientific_conclusion": "What is demonstrated. Acute fasting is covered in breakfast-vs-fasting.json: across 222 effect sizes, fasted and fed healthy adults perform the same (g 0.02) [bamberg2025], with deficits mainly in longer or more severe fasts [benau2021, oleary2025]. For chronic regimens, the best healthy-adult evidence is a 10-day RCT of 16:8 breakfast-skipping in 121 young adults: no difference in objective cognition (strong Bayesian evidence for the null) or mood, although fasters felt less concentrated before noon [bamberg2026]. A 4-week 16:8 pilot in women was also null [mcleod2026], as was 8 weeks of IF versus calorie restriction in overweight women [teong2021]. The only student trial (6-h early eating window) reported faster TMT-B but was truncated with 50% attrition [mayra2022]. In older insulin-resistant adults, 5:2 IF and a healthy-living diet improved executive function and memory similarly, with IF better on three individual tests [kapogiannis2024]; alternate-day fasting improved Stroop interference versus a Mediterranean diet in a small subset of a trial in obesity [mela2025]. In MCI, a 12-week 15:9 TRE pilot raised MoCA by 1.7 points [wang2025], and a Malaysian cohort linked regular Sunnah fasting with cognitive reversion, but non-fasters smoked far more and were less educated [ooi2020]. Cross-sectional Italian data are too weak to interpret [currenti2021]. Mood is unaffected by TRE in the largest trial (n=197) [claverojimeno2025] and possibly slightly improved for depression in pooled small trials [fernandezrodriguez2023]. The popular 'fasting boosts BDNF' claim is not supported: human studies split evenly [alkurd2024]. Mechanistic narratives about metabolic switching [decabo2019] are largely animal-based. Ramadan. Ramadan differs from TRE because it removes water, shifts meals to night and shortens sleep by about 1 hour (g -0.43) [faris2020]. Moroccan studies show lower subjective alertness and mood at late morning and afternoon, slower reaction time early in the month [roky2000] and objectively shorter sleep latency on the MSLT [roky2003]. Cognitive performance itself is mostly preserved or time-of-day dependent: a lab Ramadan model without sleep change had no cognitive effect [harderlauridsen2017]; athletes were sharper in the morning but worse at afternoon verbal memory [tian2011]; cyclists maintained performance despite disrupted sleep [chamari2016]. Academic data conflict: no penalty at an Indonesian university [nuryakin2022] but lower scores and higher failure among likely fasters in Dutch national exams [hanemaaijer2026]. Ramadan exposure in utero is associated with slightly lower child cognitive scores [pradella2024]. Bottom line: TRE/IF is cognitively neutral for healthy adults and students (neither an enhancer nor a meaningful risk); small possible benefits in metabolically impaired or MCI older adults are low certainty and confounded by weight loss; during Ramadan the practical lever is sleep, hydration and meal quality at suhoor and iftar, not a supplement.", "deficiency_vs_enhancement": "Fasting regimens are neither deficiency correction nor enhancement for healthy adults: the best RCT shows no objective change [bamberg2026, mcleod2026]. Possible benefits appear in people with metabolic dysfunction (insulin resistance, obesity) or MCI [kapogiannis2024, wang2025, mela2025], where weight loss and improved glycaemia are plausible mediators; these must not be generalised to students. Fasting can create transient 'deficits' (hydration, sleep, energy) during Ramadan [faris2020, roky2003]; the nutritional opportunity is to make suhoor and iftar nutritionally adequate (fluids, protein, fibre, slow carbohydrates), not to enhance cognition.", "marketing_vs_evidence": "Claims that IF 'boosts BDNF', 'sharpens focus', 'triggers autophagy in the brain' or 'improves memory' rely on rodent studies and mechanistic reviews [decabo2019]; human BDNF data are inconsistent [alkurd2024] and controlled trials in healthy adults are null [bamberg2026, mcleod2026, teong2021]. Self-reported clarity during fasting is not supported by objective tests, and fasters actually report lower concentration before breaking the fast [bamberg2026]. For Ramadan, marketing 'brain-boosting Ramadan supplements' would be unsupported; evidence supports only general advice on sleep, hydration and balanced suhoor/iftar. Positioning a product as making fasting 'easier' or 'more productive' cannot cite cognitive evidence and must avoid implying medical benefits or religious endorsement.", "mechanism": {"summary": "After ~12-16 h without food, liver glycogen falls and fatty-acid oxidation and mild ketogenesis rise ('metabolic switching'); animal studies link repeated switching to BDNF, mitochondrial biogenesis, autophagy and stress resistance. In humans, the established changes are metabolic (insulin, weight); brain-level changes (BrainAGE, MRS glucose, BDNF) are small, inconsistent or shared with non-fasting healthy diets. Ramadan adds water restriction, circadian meal shift and sleep restriction, which act through sleepiness rather than metabolism.", "pathway_map": [{"step": "Extended daily fast -> glycogen depletion -> lower insulin, mild ketosis", "detail": "Established physiology; BHB correlated with TMT-B in a student pilot [mayra2022].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Energy deficit -> weight loss and improved insulin sensitivity", "detail": "IF/TRE produce weight loss similar to or greater than CR [teong2021, kapogiannis2024, mela2025].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Metabolic switching -> BDNF, autophagy, synaptic plasticity", "detail": "Mostly animal [decabo2019]; human BDNF inconsistent [alkurd2024].", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Improved neuronal insulin signalling / brain aging markers", "detail": "Neuron-derived EV and BrainAGE changes occurred with both IF and a healthy diet [kapogiannis2024].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}, {"step": "Gut microbiota changes -> microglial function", "detail": "Microbiota correlated with cognition in humans; causal data from mice [mela2025].", "evidence_level": "animal"}, {"step": "Ramadan: night eating + water restriction -> shorter, fragmented sleep -> daytime sleepiness", "detail": "TST -~1 h [faris2020]; MSLT shorter [roky2003].", "evidence_level": "established_human"}, {"step": "Measured human cognitive outcome", "detail": "Null in healthy young adults [bamberg2026, mcleod2026]; small selective gains in impaired older adults [kapogiannis2024, wang2025]; Ramadan: afternoon dips, mostly preserved performance [tian2011, harderlauridsen2017].", "evidence_level": "limited_human"}], "pathways": ["metabolic switching / mild ketosis", "insulin sensitivity and weight loss", "BDNF (inconsistent in humans)", "circadian alignment (early TRE)", "gut microbiota-microglia (animal)", "sleep restriction and dehydration (Ramadan)"]}, "evidence_by_outcome": [{"timeframe": "acute", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults fasted hours to ~2 days vs fed (full detail in breakfast-vs-fasting.json)", "dose_or_intake": "Single fasts", "duration": "Single day", "grade": "A", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Meta-analysis of 222 effects: no meaningful fasted vs fed difference; modest reductions with longer fasts and in younger people.", "effect_estimate": "g 0.02 (95% CrI -0.05 to 0.10) [bamberg2025]", "certainty_notes": "Large consistent body; graded A in breakfast-vs-fasting.json; narrative reviews note flexibility deficits with longer/severer fasting [benau2021, oleary2025].", "key_study_ids": ["bamberg2025", "benau2021", "oleary2025"]}, {"timeframe": "short_term_days", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adults ~26 y adapting to 16:8 breakfast-skipping", "dose_or_intake": "16 h daily fast", "duration": "10 days", "grade": "B", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Preregistered-style Bayesian RCT (n=121): no objective cognitive difference (strong evidence for null) and no mood difference; fasters felt less concentrated before noon.", "effect_estimate": "Error OR 0.99 (0.71-1.36), BF01 10,862 [bamberg2026]", "certainty_notes": "Single well-run RCT, online testing; downgraded for single study. 'Feeling less concentrated' is subjective, not measured cognition.", "key_study_ids": ["bamberg2026"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "working_memory", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy adult women", "dose_or_intake": "16:8 TRE", "duration": "4 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Pilot RCT: working memory and attentional control improved equally in both groups; no TRE effect.", "effect_estimate": "No group difference [mcleod2026]", "certainty_notes": "Pilot; sample size unclear; consistent with the 10-day null.", "key_study_ids": ["mcleod2026", "bamberg2026"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "students", "population_detail": "University students", "dose_or_intake": "6-h early eating window", "duration": "4 weeks (truncated)", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "One truncated pilot: TMT-B faster with eTRE (27% vs 6%) but with 50% attrition in the TRE arm.", "effect_estimate": "P=0.02 [mayra2022]", "certainty_notes": "Very low certainty: tiny, truncated, differential attrition, practice effects. Exploratory only.", "key_study_ids": ["mayra2022"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Adults with overweight/obesity (~46-50 y)", "dose_or_intake": "IF at 70% energy; alternate-day fasting", "duration": "8 weeks - 3 months", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "IF vs daily calorie restriction: no cognitive difference (n=46); ADF vs Mediterranean: better Stroop interference in a small analysed subset.", "effect_estimate": "No difference [teong2021]; Stroop P<0.05 (n=10-17/arm) [mela2025]", "certainty_notes": "Small, inconsistent, high risk of bias in the positive trial; weight loss confounds.", "key_study_ids": ["teong2021", "mela2025"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Cognitively intact older adults with insulin resistance and obesity", "dose_or_intake": "5:2 IF", "duration": "8 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Both IF and healthy diet improved executive function and memory; IF better on 3 individual tests but not on the composite.", "effect_estimate": "Composite group x time P=0.10; set shifting P=0.004; long-delay cued recall P=0.048 [kapogiannis2024]", "certainty_notes": "Single small trial, many outcomes without correction; metabolically impaired population.", "key_study_ids": ["kapogiannis2024"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "global_cognition", "population": "older_adults_mci", "population_detail": "Older adults with MCI (China, Malaysia)", "dose_or_intake": "15:9 TRE; Sunnah Monday/Thursday fasting", "duration": "12 weeks (RCT); 36 months (cohort)", "grade": "C", "direction": "possible_benefit", "effect_summary": "Pilot RCT: MoCA +1.7 points vs ad libitum; cohort: more reversion to normal cognition in regular fasters, but heavily confounded.", "effect_estimate": "MoCA beta 1.70 (0.46-2.94) [wang2025]; reversion 24.3% vs 3.7% [ooi2020]", "certainty_notes": "One small RCT without active control; cohort confounded by smoking, education and BMI. MCI only.", "key_study_ids": ["wang2025", "ooi2020"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "cognitive_decline", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Sicilian adults (cross-sectional)", "dose_or_intake": "Self-reported eating window <=10 h", "duration": "Habitual", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "TRF associated with lower odds of cognitive impairment, as was breakfast eating.", "effect_estimate": "OR 0.28 (0.07-0.90) [currenti2021]", "certainty_notes": "Cross-sectional, wide CI, reverse causation and confounding; no prospective data.", "key_study_ids": ["currenti2021"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "mood_stress", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "General and overweight adult populations", "dose_or_intake": "TRE 8-h windows, IF regimens", "duration": "10 days - 12 weeks", "grade": "B", "direction": "no_benefit", "effect_summary": "Largest TRE RCT (n=197) found no change in sleep, depression, anxiety or stress; meta-analysis suggests a small depression improvement but no anxiety/mood effect; no harm signal.", "effect_estimate": "BDI MD 0.2 (-1.0 to 1.3) [claverojimeno2025]; depression SMD 0.41 (0.05-0.76), mood SMD 0.14 (-0.09 to 0.37) [fernandezrodriguez2023]", "certainty_notes": "Moderate: one good RCT plus small-trial meta-analysis; trials exclude people with psychiatric or eating disorders.", "key_study_ids": ["claverojimeno2025", "fernandezrodriguez2023", "bamberg2026"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "neuroplasticity_biomarkers", "population": "mixed_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy and metabolic-disease adults", "dose_or_intake": "CR, ADF, TRE, Ramadan", "duration": "Weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "BDNF rose in 5 studies, fell in 5, unchanged in 6.", "effect_estimate": "Vote count [alkurd2024]", "certainty_notes": "Biomarker only; inconsistent; does not support a 'fasting raises BDNF' claim.", "key_study_ids": ["alkurd2024"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "alertness", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy Muslim adults during Ramadan (Morocco and multi-country)", "dose_or_intake": "Dawn-to-sunset abstinence from food and water", "duration": "~4 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "harm", "effect_summary": "Ramadan shortens sleep by ~1 h/night and increases objective daytime sleepiness (MSLT latency -2 to -6 min), with subjective alertness and mood dips at late morning/afternoon.", "effect_estimate": "TST g -0.43 (-0.64 to -0.22); ESS g -0.06 (negligible) [faris2020]; MSLT -2 to -6 min [roky2003]", "certainty_notes": "Consistent direction across observational pre/post studies (sleep loss moderate; questionnaire sleepiness negligible; MSLT small n=8). Observational design starts low and heterogeneity is high (I2 78%), so graded C despite consistency.", "key_study_ids": ["faris2020", "roky2000", "roky2003", "chamari2016"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "processing_speed", "population": "healthy_young_adults", "population_detail": "Healthy young men and athletes during Ramadan or a Ramadan model", "dose_or_intake": "~14 h daytime fasting", "duration": "~4 weeks", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Lab Ramadan model: no cognitive change; athletes: morning speed/vigilance better but afternoon verbal memory worse; cyclists: no impairment despite disturbed sleep; Moroccan subjects: slower reaction time early in Ramadan.", "effect_estimate": "d 1.3 morning psychomotor, d -0.8 afternoon verbal memory [tian2011]; null [harderlauridsen2017]", "certainty_notes": "Small uncontrolled studies, practice and time-of-day confounding; overall no consistent cognitive deficit, but afternoon dips plausible.", "key_study_ids": ["harderlauridsen2017", "tian2011", "chamari2016", "roky2000"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "learning", "population": "students", "population_detail": "Secondary-school and university students sitting exams during Ramadan", "dose_or_intake": "Ramadan observance (inferred)", "duration": "Exam period", "grade": "C", "direction": "inconsistent", "effect_summary": "Netherlands (Muslim minority): lower exam scores and higher failure among likely fasters; Indonesia (Muslim majority): no effect on university test scores.", "effect_estimate": "Not reported in abstracts [hanemaaijer2026, nuryakin2022]", "certainty_notes": "Natural experiments with inferred fasting; context (school scheduling, social support) likely moderates.", "key_study_ids": ["hanemaaijer2026", "nuryakin2022"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "executive_function", "population": "older_adults_healthy", "population_detail": "Tunisian older adults, active vs sedentary", "dose_or_intake": "Ramadan fasting", "duration": "4 weeks", "grade": "D", "direction": "insufficient", "effect_summary": "Active older adults improved several domains; sedentary declined in associative learning; both had poor sleep.", "effect_estimate": "p 0.005-0.041 [boujelbane2022]", "certainty_notes": "Non-randomised, practice effects, COI of test-platform employees.", "key_study_ids": ["boujelbane2022"]}, {"timeframe": "chronic", "domain": "learning", "population": "children_adolescents", "population_detail": "Offspring exposed to Ramadan in utero", "dose_or_intake": "Maternal Ramadan exposure during pregnancy", "duration": "Pregnancy", "grade": "C", "direction": "harm", "effect_summary": "Children exposed in utero scored slightly lower on cognitive tests; adult disability and chronic-disease indicators higher.", "effect_estimate": "MD -3.10% of a SD [pradella2024]", "certainty_notes": "Low-moderate GRADE; exposure not confirmed fasting; small effect but population-wide relevance.", "key_study_ids": ["pradella2024"]}], "acute_matrix": [{"dose": "Single fast (hours to ~48 h)", "onset": "Same day", "duration_studied": "Single session", "domain": "global cognition", "population": "healthy adults", "effect_estimate": "g 0.02 (-0.05 to 0.10)", "certainty": "A (see breakfast-vs-fasting)", "safety": "Hunger, irritability", "study_ids": ["bamberg2025"]}, {"dose": "Ramadan day (no food or water ~13-16 h)", "onset": "Late morning/afternoon", "duration_studied": "Repeated days within Ramadan", "domain": "alertness, mood, verbal memory", "population": "healthy young adults (Morocco, Singapore)", "effect_estimate": "Subjective alertness and mood lower at 09:00-16:00; afternoon verbal memory d -0.8", "certainty": "C", "safety": "Dehydration and sleep loss contribute", "study_ids": ["roky2000", "tian2011"]}], "longterm_matrix": [{"duration": "10 days", "population": "Healthy adults ~26 y (n=121)", "outcome": "Inhibitory control, interference tasks, mood", "effect_estimate": "Null (BF01 >10,000)", "evidence_quality": "Moderate", "limitations": "Online tasks", "study_ids": ["bamberg2026"]}, {"duration": "4-8 weeks", "population": "Healthy/overweight women; students", "outcome": "Working memory, attention, TMT-B", "effect_estimate": "Null (2 trials); TMT-B faster in truncated student pilot", "evidence_quality": "Low", "limitations": "Pilots, attrition", "study_ids": ["mcleod2026", "teong2021", "mayra2022"]}, {"duration": "8 weeks", "population": "Older insulin-resistant adults (n=40)", "outcome": "Executive function, memory, BrainAGE", "effect_estimate": "Both diets improved; IF better on 3 tests", "evidence_quality": "Low", "limitations": "Multiplicity", "study_ids": ["kapogiannis2024"]}, {"duration": "12 weeks", "population": "Adults with overweight/obesity (n=197)", "outcome": "Sleep, mood, QoL", "effect_estimate": "No differences", "evidence_quality": "Moderate-high", "limitations": "No cognitive tests", "study_ids": ["claverojimeno2025"]}, {"duration": "12 weeks - 36 months", "population": "Older adults with MCI", "outcome": "MoCA / cognitive status", "effect_estimate": "MoCA +1.7; reversion 24% vs 4% (confounded)", "evidence_quality": "Low", "limitations": "Pilot RCT; confounded cohort", "study_ids": ["wang2025", "ooi2020"]}, {"duration": "~4 weeks (Ramadan)", "population": "Healthy young adults", "outcome": "Sleep and daytime sleepiness", "effect_estimate": "TST -~1 h (g -0.43); MSLT latency -2 to -6 min", "evidence_quality": "Moderate (observational)", "limitations": "Heterogeneity", "study_ids": ["faris2020", "roky2003"]}, {"duration": "Exam period during Ramadan", "population": "Students (NL, Indonesia)", "outcome": "Exam scores", "effect_estimate": "Lower in NL minority context; null in Indonesia", "evidence_quality": "Low-moderate", "limitations": "Inferred fasting", "study_ids": ["hanemaaijer2026", "nuryakin2022"]}], "safety": {"summary": "TRE and IF are well tolerated by healthy adults in trials lasting up to 12 months, with hunger (especially at bedtime) and transient concentration dips the commonest effects. Risks concentrate in people with diabetes on glucose-lowering drugs, pregnant or breastfeeding women, adolescents and people with eating-disorder history. Ramadan adds dehydration and sleep loss; IDF-DAR guidelines govern diabetes management.", "adverse_effects": ["Hunger, irritability, lower subjective concentration before breaking the fast [bamberg2026, harderlauridsen2017]", "Higher bedtime hunger; fear of hunger and eating-related stress in qualitative data [vizthum2023]", "Ramadan: ~1 h less sleep, daytime sleepiness, afternoon mood/alertness dips [faris2020, roky2000, roky2003]", "Hypoglycaemia and dehydration risk with medication or heat", "Possible exacerbation of disordered eating in susceptible people (trials exclude them) [vizthum2023]"], "contraindications": ["History of or current eating disorder", "Type 1 diabetes or insulin/sulfonylurea-treated type 2 diabetes without medical plan [hassanein2022]", "Pregnancy and lactation (offspring cognition signal for Ramadan exposure [pradella2024])", "Children and young adolescents (outside religious practice choices)", "Underweight, frailty, sarcopenia", "Unstable psychiatric illness (e.g., bipolar disorder - sleep disruption)"], "drug_interactions": ["Insulin and sulfonylureas: hypoglycaemia; dose and timing adjustment needed [hassanein2022]", "SGLT2 inhibitors: dehydration/ketoacidosis risk during prolonged fasts", "Medications requiring food or fixed daytime dosing (e.g., some antiepileptics, levothyroxine timing, NSAIDs) need rescheduling", "Diuretics/antihypertensives: dehydration and hypotension during Ramadan"], "upper_limits": "No defined limit; trials use 6-10 h eating windows or ~500-600 kcal fasting days; Ramadan fasts 11-18 h depending on latitude and season.", "sleep_impact": "TRE: no change in objective sleep [claverojimeno2025]. Ramadan: total sleep time down ~0.8 h, more awakenings, less deep/REM sleep, increased daytime sleepiness [faris2020, chamari2016, roky2003].", "long_term_exposure_studied": "TRE/IF trials up to 12 months for metabolic outcomes; cognition up to 12 weeks (RCT) and 36 months (cohort, confounded).", "daily_use_appropriate": "probably", "special_populations": "Students: neutral on cognition, but avoid starting a new fasting regimen right before exams; Ramadan exam scheduling matters [hanemaaijer2026]. Diabetes: follow IDF-DAR risk stratification [hassanein2022]. Pregnancy: Ramadan exposure linked to small offspring cognitive and health deficits [pradella2024]. Adolescents and people with eating-disorder risk: IF engagement has been associated with disordered eating in observational studies (not extracted here).", "specialist_review_needed": ["Clinical dietitian", "Diabetologist (Ramadan/IF with diabetes)", "Obstetrician (pregnancy)", "Eating-disorder specialist / psychologist", "Islamic scholarly input for any Ramadan-related communication (cultural, not medical)"], "hard_safety_gate": "conditional", "gate_reason": "Safe for most healthy adults, but any product or programme promoting fasting must screen out diabetes on medication, pregnancy/lactation, eating disorders and adolescents, and Ramadan products must not interfere with diabetes management or encourage fasting against medical advice."}, "regulatory": {"eu": "Meal-timing patterns are not regulated as products and have no authorised health claims. Products positioned for fasting periods (e.g., suhoor drinks) are conventional foods subject to Regulation (EC) No 1924/2006; any cognition claim would require authorisation and none exists for fasting. Generic authorised nutrient claims (e.g., iron, iodine, zinc 'contribute to normal cognitive function'; water 'contributes to maintenance of normal physical and cognitive functions', with conditions) could apply to a nutritionally adequate suhoor product if conditions of use are met (wording from Reg. 432/2012 per project knowledge; re-verify before use).", "us": "No regulation of fasting patterns; foods for Ramadan or fasting are conventional foods. Structure/function claims about 'focus while fasting' would need FTC-grade substantiation, which is lacking.", "morocco": "No specific regulation on fasting products identified. Ramadan nutrition products would be conventional foods under Law 28-07 (ONSSA); claim rules uncertain - require ONSSA verification. Ministry of Health issues annual Ramadan health advice (not verified in this unit).", "permitted_claims_examples": [], "novel_food_or_ndi_issues": "None for the pattern itself; 'fasting mimetic' supplements (e.g., spermidine, novel compounds) would need separate novel food/NDI assessment.", "regulatory_gate": "conditional"}, "formulation": {"food_suitability": "Moderate for a supportive role: the pattern cannot be a product, but suhoor/iftar foods (high-fibre, protein-containing, hydrating options such as dates + milk/yogurt, harira with legumes, eggs, whole grains, water-rich fruit) are a realistic and culturally relevant product space in Morocco - for nutritional adequacy and hydration, not cognitive enhancement.", "formats": ["Suhoor meal kits / slow-release breakfast (oats, legumes, dairy, eggs)", "Iftar hydration (water, electrolyte-balanced drinks without excess sugar)", "Date-and-nut snacks", "Educational content on sleep and caffeine timing during Ramadan"], "stability": "Not applicable to the pattern.", "taste": "Must fit Moroccan Ramadan cuisine; sugar-rich traditional sweets (chebakia) are a nutritional concern.", "bioavailability_matrix": "Not applicable.", "dose_feasibility": "Not a dose; TRE window and Ramadan timing are behavioural.", "cost_availability": "Free pattern; food costs depend on choices.", "cost_evidence": "not researched here"}, "scores": {"evidence_quality": 5, "effect_magnitude": 1, "safety": 7, "reproducibility": 5, "food_suitability": 4, "cost_access": 9, "rationale": "Evidence quality 5: grade A for acute neutrality and grade B for 10-day/mood neutrality in healthy adults, but chronic cognition evidence is C-D. Effect magnitude 1: no benefit in healthy adults; small uncertain effects in MCI/insulin-resistant groups; Ramadan sleep loss is a modest negative. Safety 7: well tolerated with clear exclusion groups. Reproducibility 5: consistent nulls, inconsistent small positives. Food suitability 4: pattern not a product, but Ramadan meal products are realistic for nutrition (not cognition). Cost/access 9: free."}, "research_gaps": ["Adequately powered RCT of chronic TRE on objective cognition in students during exam periods", "Ramadan studies with control groups, objective sleep and cognitive testing in Moroccan students and workers", "Trials testing suhoor composition (protein/fibre/hydration) on afternoon alertness during Ramadan", "Separating weight-loss from fasting-timing effects in MCI trials", "Long-term eating-disorder incidence in adolescents and young adults adopting IF", "Human BDNF/brain imaging studies with cognitive endpoints"], "_derived": {"composite_score": 4.9, "eligibility": "conditional", "safety_gate": "conditional", "regulatory_gate": "conditional", "best_grade_acute": null, "best_grade_chronic": "C", "best_grade_young_adults": null, "best_grade_sleep_stress": null, "best_grade_surrogate": null, "best_grade_overall": "C", "benefit_domains": {"executive_function": "C", "global_cognition": "C"}, "n_outcome_rows": 15, "n_studies": 28, "n_verified": 28, "n_unverified": 0, "n_null_negative": 14, "n_systematic_reviews": 6, "review_status": "unreviewed_draft", "directions": ["harm", "inconsistent", "insufficient", "no_benefit", "possible_benefit"]}}];
